INSERT INTO `irb_application` VALUES (12,3,199,NULL,'Menstrual wellbeing: Pattern of menstrual cycle and disorders and, its effect on health-related quality of life and academic performance of adolescent girls in Jimma zone, south west Ethiopia','2022-01-29','2022-08-21','Four randomly selected districts of Jimma zone','Menstruation is a vital sign of reproductive health and has both clinical and public health importance. It was viewed as an important assessment tool of normal development and the exclusion of pathological conditions for growing girls. Yet this aspect of heath is often still seen as a taboo subject. In LMIC, adolescent girls are often uninformed and unprepared for menarche. As a result, many girls tolerating unnecessary levels of bleeding, pain and other associated symptoms with menstruation by perceiving it as normal traumatic events that adolescent girls should pass through.  A wide range of domains, including education, work, social and family functioning, and general quality of life women can significantly be affected by menstrual related symptoms.. In Ethiopia, despite the potential adverse effects of premenstrual and menstrual symptoms on the health and functioning of girls, the pattern of menstrual cycle and menstrual related problems in adolescents were not well defined. Also, the public health impact and burden of these conditions were still limited. The main aim of this project is to examine Patterns of menstrual cycle and disorders and, measure their effect on health-related quality of life and academic performance of adolescent girls in Jimma zone, south west Ethiopia.\r\nA community based prospective follow-up study will be conducted in the selected districts of Jimma zone.  Nine hundred sixty Adolescent girls who had attained menarche in Jimma zone will be the study population. Both quantitative and qualitative data collection techniques will be used. Quantitative data will be collected using kobo software then exported to STATA 15.0 for analysis. Binary linear regression on HRQL will be performed for each predictor of interest. Stepwise linear regression analyses were performed to identify variables that were most predictive of HRQL. A one-way repeated-measures analysis of variance will be used to assess any differences in HRQL scores between the two groups of girls; those with menstrual disorder and without. For qualitative data thematic analysis will be done using Atlas ti software.  \r\nA total budget of 446,800.00 ETB will be required for the implementation of this project',1,NULL,'2022-03-05 10:07:05',NULL,3,1,NULL,4),(13,1,14,NULL,'Mental health problems in relation to eating behavior patterns, health related quality of life among Jimma zone high school female adolescents: Structural equation modeling','2022-03-01','2022-08-17','Jimma Zone(Jimma ,Serboand Seka)',': Numerous studies in developed country have suggested an association or path between unhealthy eating habits, health related quality of life and mental health problems in adolescents. Unfortunately, the scarcity of data in developing country including Ethiopia makes it difficult to develop appropriate intervention.\r\nOBJECTIVE: To assess mental health problems in relation to eating behavior patterns, nutrient intakes and health related quality of life among high school female adolescent in Jimma zone\r\nMETHODOLOGY: School based cross sectional study design will be used among a sample of 460 female adolescents aged between 10–19 years randomly selected from Jimma zone high schools in 2021. Validated tool will be used to collect data on eating pattern, nutritional status, quality of life and mental health status by following standard operational procedure after conducting pre-test.',1,NULL,'2022-03-15 12:11:43',NULL,2,1,NULL,4),(16,3,144,NULL,'COVID-19 vaccines safety, tolerability and efficacy among cancer patients in Africa: a systematic review and meta-analysis','2022-04-01','2022-06-30','Jimma University','This project is to review COVID-19 vaccines safety, tolerability and efficacy among cancer patients in Africa. The project is going to systematic reviews and meta-analysis for evidence informed decision making. The study wills search different published articles from data-bases.',1,NULL,'2022-03-28 11:05:09',NULL,3,1,NULL,4),(17,3,37,NULL,'Barriers, perception and intention to get vaccinated for COVID-19 vaccines among Adults in Jimma Zone based on Theory of planned behavior: A mixed method study','2022-03-31','2022-04-30','Jimma zone Districts','This project entitled as \" Barriers, perception and intention to get vaccinated for Covid 19 vaccines among Adults in Jimma zone using TPB a mixed method study\" with the total allowed budget of 160,000 ETB',1,NULL,'2022-03-29 22:54:28',NULL,2,1,NULL,4),(20,3,165,NULL,'COVID-19: Response, Recovery and Resilience:  A qualitative exploration in the COvid-19 admitted and recovered patients in Jimma Medical Centre and Shenen Gibe Hospital.','2022-04-02','2022-11-30','South West Ethiopia','Many experts consider both 2020 & 2021 is the infectious years worldwide because of the outbreak of COVID-19 viral disease, which led to a massive public reaction about the pandemic situation. In response to the crisis/pandemic and to ensure the safety of our community different measures were taken by the government. COVID -19 impacts, the victim (patients) need of someone to absolve their worries, physical healthcare, psychological healthcare (emotion, psychological). We are intend to conduct this research as, it is better and an ideal weapon lead to have and build comprehensive societal resilience through exploring the role of covid-19 recovered patients as catalysts of inclusive and resilient societies in crisis response, recovery, and in preparation of future shocks. A cross-sectional qualitative exploratory phenomenological live experience design will be employed to explore the live experience of COVID-19 recovered clients. In-depth interview and FGD guide will be utilized to explore the impacts and role of covid-19 recovered patients in building resilient societies in crisis response, recovery, and in preparation of future shocks.  All patients treated and recovered from COVID center (JMC and Shenen Gibe hospital) considered as source population. The data saturation and maximum variation principles will be strictly follows to explore the aim of the study. ATLAS.ti7.1 will be used for coding; and analysis categories.',1,NULL,'2022-04-02 16:58:32',NULL,2,1,NULL,4),(21,3,80,NULL,'Resilience of public health facilities in the context of crisis management, preparedness and response in Jimma zone, south western Ethiopia','2022-03-12','2022-08-01','Jimma','This study will assess the  resilience (capacity of public health facilities) in the context of crisis for preparation , response and recovery phase. The study will involve both quantitative and qualitative method. Study participans will be  health care professionals, and facilities.',1,NULL,'2022-04-04 11:36:32',NULL,3,3,NULL,4),(24,4,321,NULL,'Effect of Double Duty Interventions on Double Burden of Malnutrition among Secondary School Adolescents in Central Ethiopia: Cluster Randomized Controlled Trial','2022-05-01','2022-11-01','Debre Berhan City, Central Ethiopia','Double burden of malnutrition is an emerging public health problem among adolescents due to the inevitable consequences of nutritional transition. Addressing these two contrasting forms of malnutrition (undernutrition and overnutrition) simultaneously brings an enormous challenge for the food and nutrition policies of developing countries like Ethiopia. Adolescents are the most affected group by malnutrition and ignored group from nutritional interventions. Therefore, double-duty interventions are able to tackle the risk of both nutritional problems among school adolescents simultaneously in an integrated and holistic approach.',1,NULL,'2022-04-06 14:39:36',NULL,2,3,NULL,4),(25,3,136,NULL,'Assessing the prognostic values and Management Guidance of Chest X-ray Score and Clinical parameters in SARS-COV-2 Patients across treatment centers in Jimma and Addis-Ababa, Ethiopia, 2022','2021-12-01','2022-07-01','SARS-COV-2 Patients across treatment centers in Jimma and Addis-Abeba, Ethiopia, 2022','In the fight against the COVID-19 pandemic, there are factors to identify individuals who are at the highest risk for severe illness and death. These are older age, male sex, inflammatory indicators, comorbidities, respiratory rate, SPo2/FiO2 ratio, imaging modalities like CT- scan and chest radiography. The American college of Radiology recommends portable chest radiography to be used in place of CT-scan because of the difficulty in the decontamination process of CT-scan, and due to in-availability of the service in emergency situation like the pandemic and in resource constrained areas. In line with the point made by the American College of Radiology  studies have concluded chest radiography to be a reliable tool and recommended as a first line imaging investigation modality. and some of the studies showed that, Ground Glass Opacity with peripheral distribution, consolidation, pleural effusion, and the chest radio-graph severity score showed a significant correlation with fatal outcome. Thus this study aims to analyze the prognostic value of chest radiographic scoring in relation with other established historical, clinical and biochemical parameters and analyze patient management guidance using their combined value. This study is a retrospective cohort study which will be carried out at Jimma and Addis Ababa COVID-19 treatment centers. Patients records who were admitted to treatment centers from June 2021 to January 2022 with inclusion criteria will be included in the study and collected Data will be entered into EpiData version 3,1 and exported to SPSS version 25 for analysis.',1,NULL,'2022-04-11 16:47:11',NULL,3,1,NULL,4),(37,4,325,NULL,'Risk factors and barriers to resource-appropriate care of lymphoid malignancies in Ethiopia-Multi-centre case control study','2022-05-01','2023-05-01','ADDIS ABABA,JIMMA AND GONDAR','Understanding the epidemiology and risk factors among NHL patients is essential in devising context-appropriate cancer-control strategies. Additionally, patient navigation pathways among lymphoma patients have not been characterized in Ethiopia.  Understanding the barriers in accessing health care among cancer patients and specifically, lymphoma patients is essential to improve proportions of early diagnosis and overall patient outcome in Ethiopia and the wider region. Therefore, this proposed research project aims to understand major risk factors for NHL in Ethiopia  and   also evaluate patient pathways and determinants of delay in order to devise evidence-based and sustainable cancer-control and cancer -care policy for lymphoma patients in Ethiopia and the SSA region.',1,NULL,'2022-04-19 03:35:59',NULL,2,3,NULL,4),(48,4,327,NULL,'Effects of Nutrition Behavioral Change Communication on Feto-Maternal Outcomes among Pregnant Adolescents in West Arsi Zone, Central Ethiopia: Cluster Randomized Controlled Trial.','2022-09-01','2023-09-01','West Arsi, Oromia, Ethiopia','When pregnancy occurs among Adolescents, competition for nutrients happens between the fetus and still growing girl. Pregnant adolescents remain an underserved population and therefore may benefit greatly from receiving prenatal health and nutrition behavioral change intervention.The study aims to assess the effects of nutrition Education intervention packages on Feto-maternal Outcomes among Pregnant Adolescents of West Arsi Zone, Central Ethiopia. A two-arm parallel cluster randomized control trial will be undertaken. The control groups will take the usual service from the health facilities and the Experimental group pregnant Adolescents will take the usual service plus Intensive Nutrition behavioral change intervention using Health belief Model with different teaching materials, and methods, and food preparation demonstration up to delivery. Weight, Height, and MUAC will be measured using standardized and calibrated equipment to assess nutritional status by trained health professionals. A mixed-effects model will be used to analyze birth weight and maternal weight gain data, whereas a generalized estimating equation will be used to analyze the Dietary diversity score and KAP.',1,NULL,'2022-04-19 11:05:27',NULL,2,3,NULL,4),(49,4,327,NULL,'Effects of Nutrition Behavioral Change Communication on Feto-Maternal Outcomes among Pregnant Adolescents in West Arsi Zone, Central Ethiopia: Cluster Randomized Controlled Trial.','2022-09-01','2023-09-01','West Arsi, Oromia, Ethiopia','When pregnancy occurs among Adolescents, competition for nutrients happens between the fetus and still growing girl. Pregnant adolescents remain an underserved population and therefore may benefit greatly from receiving prenatal health and nutrition behavioral change intervention.The study aims to assess the effects of nutrition Education intervention packages on Feto-maternal Outcomes among Pregnant Adolescents of West Arsi Zone, Central Ethiopia. A two-arm parallel cluster randomized control trial will be undertaken. The control groups will take the usual service from the health facilities and the Experimental group pregnant Adolescents will take the usual service plus Intensive Nutrition behavioral change intervention using Health belief Model with different teaching materials, and methods, and food preparation demonstration up to delivery. Weight, Height, and MUAC will be measured using standardized and calibrated equipment to assess nutritional status by trained health professionals. A mixed-effects model will be used to analyze birth weight and maternal weight gain data, whereas a generalized estimating equation will be used to analyze the Dietary diversity score and KAP.',1,NULL,'2022-04-19 13:20:03',NULL,2,3,NULL,4),(50,3,178,NULL,'SARS-CoV-2 specific antibody responses and impact for COVID-19 disease in Health Care Worker and Community Members from Ethiopian related to natural SARS CoV-2 infection and COVID-19 vaccination','2021-11-01','2024-10-31','Jimma and Addis Ababa, Ethiopia','The overall aim of this research project to investigate SARS-CoV-2 specific immune response and correlates of immune protection in the context of circulating SARS-CoV-2 viral variants. In this affiliated study, we aim to investigate these outcomes in Ethiopian health care worker and community members throughout a longitudinal, prospective cohort study. Participants, stratified according to their SARS-CoV-2 infection and vaccination status, will be followed-up at 3-monthly intervals for total duration of two years.',1,NULL,'2022-04-23 14:23:52',NULL,2,3,NULL,4),(51,3,183,NULL,'Effectiveness of an integrated community-based intervention package in improving maternal, neonatal and infant health outcome in Jimma Zone, Southwest Ethiopia: a cluster randomized controlled trial','2022-01-01','2022-08-15','Jimma Zone: Seka district and Dedo district','This project is a community based study applying RCT study design to assess the Effectiveness of an integrated community-based intervention package in improving maternal, neonatal and infant health outcome in Jimma Zone, Southwest Ethiopia. It is a parallel arm study having case and control group in two different districts. Study participants are pregnant women. It is aimed to improve maternal, neonatal and child health outcome.',1,NULL,'2022-04-27 01:04:50',NULL,2,1,NULL,4),(52,1,328,NULL,'Implication of cholera on acute malnutrition, treatment outcome, recovery time, and its continuum of care among cholera-exposed children compare with unexposed children in the community, Ethiopia 2022','2022-05-15','2022-12-15','The study will be conducted throughout Ethiopia at which cholera hotspot area identified by Ethiopian Public Health Institute (EPHI).','The study deals with the interaction of infection and malnutrition, Malnutrition is a common public health problem and it is one of the main reasons for the death of children in developing countries. Infection is one of the main detriments; Cholera outbreaks directly and indirectly associated with malnutrition are the leading causes of morbidity in developing countries. Cholera and malnutrition remain public health challenges in the developing world including Ethiopia; this study is focused on infection with malnutrition and their implication on child health by investigating the burden of cholera on child nutrition, time to recovery, treatment outcomes, and its continuum of care in Ethiopia',1,NULL,'2022-04-27 02:04:05',NULL,2,1,NULL,4),(53,4,328,NULL,'Implication of cholera on acute malnutrition, treatment outcome, recovery time, and its continuum of care among cholera-exposed children compare with unexposed children in the community, Ethiopia 2022','2022-05-15','2022-12-15','The study will be conducted throughout Ethiopia at which cholera hotspot area identified by Ethiopian Public Health Institute (EPHI).','The study deals with the interaction of infection and malnutrition, Malnutrition is a common public health problem and it is one of the main reasons for the death of children in developing countries. Infection is one of the main determinants; Cholera outbreaks directly and indirectly associated with malnutrition are the leading causes of morbidity in developing countries. Cholera and malnutrition remain public health challenges in the developing world including Ethiopia; this study is focused on infection with malnutrition and their implication on child health by investigating the burden of cholera on child nutrition, time to recovery, treatment outcomes, and its continuum of care in Ethiopia',1,NULL,'2022-04-28 17:44:56',NULL,2,1,NULL,4),(54,3,176,NULL,'Exploring the influences and significance of social determinants of health on maternal and child health outcomes in rural districts surrounding Jimma Town, Southwest Ethiopia','2021-01-12','2022-01-08','rural districts surrounding Jimma Town','Exploring the influences and significance of social determinants f health on maternal and child health outcomes in rural districts surrounding Jimma Town, Southwest Ethiopia',1,NULL,'2022-05-03 17:08:49',NULL,2,3,NULL,4),(55,4,334,334,'microbial safety and governance of street foods in selected towns of Ethiopia','2022-06-01','2023-07-20','addis ababa, hawassa, diredawa, Bahirdar and jimma','this research tries to assess the handling practice of street food handlers and also identify the organisms found in the sampled food. Besides to this, it tries to assess the risk of getting disease from salmonella and staphylococcus in the consumption of street foods and explore the governance of street foods',2,NULL,'2022-05-04 13:56:57',NULL,2,1,NULL,4),(56,3,172,NULL,'Readiness for management of domestic violence among health care providers: Pre-experimental study at Jimma Medical Center, Jimma, Ethiopia, 2021.','2021-11-01','2022-05-30','Jimma Medical Center','This is a mega research project of the year 2014 EC. it is to be doner among health care providers of Jimma Medical center',1,NULL,'2022-05-04 14:08:23',NULL,2,1,NULL,4),(57,4,320,NULL,'Risk analysis on nosocomial infection and associated mortality in Jimma Medical Center','2022-02-01','2023-01-30','JImma medical center','Summery \r\nBackground: Infections that are acquired in a hospital or other health care facility are called nosocomial infections (NIs). NIs can be prompted through bacteria, viruses, fungi, or parasites. These microorganisms might also already exist within the patient\'s body or might come from the environment, contaminated clinic equipment, wellbeing care laborers, or other patients.\r\nObjective: This study aims to: 1. Determine the risks to surgical site infection (SSI) and explore determinants of time to SSI after cesarean section (CS) at Jimma Medical Center (JMC), 2. Identify the risk and determinants of time to contract nosocomial infection after neonatal hospital admission, 3. analyze the risk and determinants of Intensive care unit (ICU) death due to nosocomial infection at JMC \r\nMethod: The study will be conducted at JMC. The data will be gathered from primary and secondary sources.  We will prospectively follow all women who will experience elective and emergency CS for objective one. For the second and third objectives, we planned secondary sources from records of the neonatal ward and adult ICU respectively. We will test the hypothesis that there is no significant predictor related to time to SSI after CS. The analysis will be done on the time of events using a log-rank test. Mann-Whitney U-test will be utilized to compare the characteristics of with and without NIs of neonatal patients.\r\nThe work will also use statistical model analysis, such as the survival model, the multistate model, competing for risk model, and joint modeling.\r\nExpected outcome: The expected outcome of the study will be \r\n	Identification of predictors on time to surgical site infection after cesarean section \r\n	Differentiation of predictors on time  to contract nosocomial infection after neonatal hospital admission   \r\n	Determining the risk of  NI associated mortality in patients admitted to adult ICU',1,NULL,'2022-05-15 12:18:01',NULL,2,3,NULL,4),(58,4,360,NULL,'Effect of continuous labour support by a companion of choice on birth outcome, satisfaction and costs-effectiveness among pregnant woman in Addis Ababa, Ethiopia2022: Cluster Randomized Control Trail','2022-05-17','2024-02-08','Addis Ababa, Ethiopia','The World Health Organization (WHO) guidelines recommend companion of woman’s choice during labour and childbirth, to improve labour outcomes and women’s satisfaction with services. As a result, woman who have continuous support during childbirth experience a: 25% decrease in the risk of Cesarean; 8% increase in the likelihood of a spontaneous vaginal birth; 10% decrease in the use of any medications for pain relief; Shorter labors by 41 minutes on average; 38% decrease in the baby’s risk of a low five minute Apgar score; 31% decrease in the risk of being dissatisfied with the birth experience.  In low income countries, specifically in Ethiopia husbands and/or family members are the main companions chosen during labour. However, the effects of continuous labour support as provided by companion of woman\'s choice on satisfaction, labor process and birth outcome have not yet been evaluated in randomized controlled studies. Therefore, the  study aim to evaluate effect of continuous labour support offered by woman’s companion of choice as compared with usual care on clinical outcomes, satisfaction and costs-effetiveness among pregnant woman in Addis Ababa, Ethiopia.',1,NULL,'2022-05-18 09:53:07',NULL,2,3,NULL,4),(59,4,329,320,'Risk analysis on nosocomial infection and associated mortality in Jimma Medical Center','2022-01-02','2023-01-01','JImma medical center','This study aims to: determine the risks to surgical site infection (SSI) and explore determinants of time to SSI after cesarean section (CS) at Jimma Medical Center (JMC), identify the risk and determinants of time to contract nosocomial infection after neonatal hospital admission, and  analyze the risk and determinants of Intensive care unit (ICU) death due to nosocomial infection at JMC. The data will be gathered from primary and secondary sources.  We will prospectively follow all women who will experience elective and emergency CS for objective one. For the second and third objectives, we planned secondary sources from records of the neonatal ward and adult ICU respectively. We will test the hypothesis that there is no significant predictor related to time to SSI after CS. The analysis will be done on the time of events using a log-rank test. Mann-Whitney U-test will be utilized to compare the characteristics of with and without NIs of neonatal patients. The work will also use statistical model analysis, such as the survival model, the multistate model, competing for risk model, and joint modeling.',2,NULL,'2022-05-25 13:04:44',NULL,2,1,NULL,4),(60,4,329,320,'Risk analysis on nosocomial infection and associated mortality in Jimma Medical Center','2022-01-02','2023-01-01','JImma medical center','This study aims to: determine the risks to surgical site infection (SSI) and explore determinants of time to SSI after cesarean section (CS) at Jimma Medical Center (JMC), identify the risk and determinants of time to contract nosocomial infection after neonatal hospital admission, and  analyze the risk and determinants of Intensive care unit (ICU) death due to nosocomial infection at JMC. The data will be gathered from primary and secondary sources.  We will be prospectively gathered from all women who are experiencing elective and emergency C-sections, all neonates who are stayed at least 48h at JMC neonatal ward during the period, and all adult ICU patients who are waited more than 48 hours after admission at ICU select from January 2, 2022, to January 1, 2023. We will test the hypothesis that there is no significant predictor related to time to SSI after CS. The analysis will be done on the time of events using a log-rank test. Mann-Whitney U-test will be utilized to compare the characteristics of with and without NIs of neonatal patients. The work will also use statistical model analysis, such as the survival model, the multistate model, competing for risk model, and joint modeling.',2,NULL,'2022-05-25 13:25:40',NULL,NULL,1,NULL,4);
