INSERT INTO `submission` VALUES (1,12,9,20,'ABSTRACT\r\nBack ground\r\nRickets is a disease of the growing bone, occurs in children only before the fusion of the epiphyses, and is due to un mineralized matrix at the growth plates. Although this problem was largely corrected through public health measures that provided children with adequate vitamin D, rickets remains a consistent problem in our country. Rickets is the disease of growing bone that is unique to children and adolescents. It is caused by a failure of osteoid to calcify in growing person. It commonly occurs due to inadequate sunshine exposure, vitamin D deficiency, dietary deficiency of calcium and phosphorus. It is highly prevalent in Ethiopia in about 41% of children with five years and below with frequent combination with protein energy malnutrition and infectious diseases.\r\nObjectives: To document the prevalence, associative factors and plan intervention for Rickets among under Five Children in Jimma University Medical Centre, Jimma.\r\nMethods: A cross- sectional prospective study will be conducted during the study period to assess the prevalence associative factors of rickets in JUMC, by using convenient and purposive non probability sample technique. Structured questionnaire will be used to assess different variables. Data will be analyzed manually and using scientific calculators. . Finally the finding will be presented using statements, figure and tables.',NULL,'To Assess the Prevalence, Associative Factors and plan Intervention for Rickets among Under Five Children, in Jimma University Medical Centre, Jimma, Southwest Ethiopia','2021-11-18 15:48:11','RESEARCH OUTPUT/OUTCOME\r\nThis study would be first of its kind in Ethiopia where physiotherapeutic intervention would be seen along with medical intervention. The result of this study may help to show the magnitude and associated factors of rickets among under five years old children in the study area. \r\nThis may help health care providers to develop evidence based prevention, assessment and treatment protocols. The study will also bring out attention for consideration of the problem by health care providers to take an appropriate and early intervention especially for physiotherapists.\r\nIdentifying the prevalence and associated factors will create and help to increase the referral\r\nsystem and multidisciplinary approach (MDT) for patients with rickets. It may also be helpful for\r\nthe family to insight some findings which may help them with the symptom management.\r\nBeside that the result of this study may inspire researchers to do further investigations on the\r\nstudy area, and it can be used as a reference in further studies and systematic reviews\r\nFurthermore, it may add its own input for the scientific world. Given the fact that it is important\r\nto determine the prevalence and establish associated factors of rickets among under five years old children.','3f3bcf84d9243cae8062fc8733fa7afb','completed','INTRODUCTION\r\nRickets is a disease of growing bone that is unique to children and adolescents. It is caused by failure of osteoid to calcify in a growing person. Failure of osteoid to calcify in adult is called osteomalacia [1]. Vitamin D deficiency rickets occur when metabolites of vitamin D are deficient. Less commonly, dietary deficiency of calcium and phosphorus may also results in rickets.VitamineD3 is formed in the skin from derivative of cholesterol under the stimulus of ultraviolet, light. Liver oil was the significant sources of vitamin D until early in the 20the century when ergo sterol (vitamin D2) was synthesized from irradiated plant steroids. During the industrial revolution, rickets appeared in epidemic form in temperate zone where the pollution from factories blocked the sun’s ultraviolet rays. Thus rickets was probably the first childhood disease caused by environmental pollution. Natural nutritional source of vitamin D are limited primary to fatty ocean going fish, dairy milk fortified with vitamin D(400IU/L) human milk contains little vitamin D generally less than 20-40IU/L. Therefore, infant who are breast fed are at risk for rickets, especially those who have darkly pigmented skin, which blocks penetration of ultraviolet light [2]. Rickets usually manifested by frontal bossing, craniotabes, delayed closure of fontanels, knobby deformities, bowlegs, knocked knee, Harrison’s groove, rachitic rosary and others [3]. , rickets appeared in epidemic form in temperate zone where the pollution from factories blocked the sun’s ultraviolet rays. Usually rickets is difficult to reverse once it occur but we can avoid further complication by vitamin D (600,000IU stat) supplementation and prevent by early exposure to sunshine ad giving vitamin D rich diet for breast feeding child and pregnant ladies if possible all reproductive age group women. In Britain 30% of immigrants from Indian subcontinent and the West Indies were found to have rickets and the figure rise to 74% when biochemical findings were included in the study. Rickets is quite prevalent in Ethiopia and 41% of children age five year and less than their age and is commonly associated with protein energy malnutrition, infectious diseases of various cause including respiratory tract, anemia, congestive heart failure and gastrointestinal tract risk recurrent diarrhea and mal absorption [4]. The Ethiopian nutrition survey carried out in 1959 showed that approximately 30% of less than one and 30% of one to two year old had rickets. [5-6]. Rickets is a disorder in which mineralization of organic matrix of the skeleton is defective. This disorder is caused by a numbers of different conditions associated with Vit. D deficiency or resistance [7]\r\nRickets, however, is not limited to infancy and early childhood, as evidenced by cases of rickets caused by nutritional vitamin D deficiency being reported in adolescents. Rickets is an example of extreme vitamin D deficiency, with a peak incidence between 3 and 18 months of age. [8]\r\nRickets is common in those parts of Africa where for cultural and social reasons children are not exposed to sufficient sun light. Diets especially of the weaning are extremely deficient in animal fats and oils which may not be properly absorbed due to repeated gastro-intestinal infection .[9]\r\nAmong the Muslim children of North Africa rickets is high because their mothers stay at home during light and those who venture out conceal themselves and their young children beneath cotton wrap. Children, especially those in crowded dark street of the cities receive little sunlight and unable to synthesize sufficient vitamin D for their requirement. In addition, the high fat content of the wheat diets may bind calcium in the quit and so limit its absorption [10].\r\nThe infant are left inside the homes with a baby nurse while the mother goes to work and lack of sunlight and a poor diet loaded to rickets. These beliefs contributed to high incidence of rickets. Due to this reason rickets is quite prevalent in Ethiopia and 41% of children aged 5 years and less are victims of the pathology [11]. Rickets in girls can cause pelvic deformities that are alteration of pelvic shape with marked reduction of the antero-posterior measurement of the brim with some irregular widening of the cavity later on in life due to contracted pelvis leading to obstructed labor which contributes significantly to maternal and prenatal mortality due to obstructed labor [12].\r\nRATIONALE \r\nEven though, different research done on rickets at different area, the prevalence of rickets was not well established in Ethiopia due to the methodological flows of the previous studies. Rickets is common in Ethiopia due to cultural beliefs and educational status of the parents so this study was some associated risk factor. Most of all usually parents of rachitic children won’t bring them to the health institution unless and otherwise they have associated complication, therefore this study tried to pick rachitic children when they came for other complaint.\r\nThe result of this study may help to show the magnitude and associated factors of\r\nrickets among under five years old children in the study area. This may help health care\r\nproviders to develop evidence based prevention, assessment and treatment protocols. The study\r\nwill also bring out attention for consideration of the problem by health care providers to take an\r\nappropriate and early intervention especially for physiotherapists.\r\n Identifying the prevalence and associated factors will create and help to increase the referral\r\nsystem and multidisciplinary approach (MDT) for patients with rickets. It may also be helpful for\r\nthe family to insight some findings which may help them with the symptom management.\r\nBeside that the result of this study may inspire researchers to do further investigations on the\r\nstudy area, and it can be used as a reference in further studies and systematic reviews\r\nFurthermore, it may add its own input for the scientific world. Given the fact that it is important\r\nto determine the prevalence and establish associated factors of rickets among under five years old children.','MATERIAL AND METHODS\r\nStudy area and study period\r\nStudy area\r\nThe study will be conducted in JUMC. JUMC is located in Jimma town, Oromia region, Jimma zone. Jimma town is located 346 kms to south west of the capital of Ethiopia, Addis Ababa. Jimma University specialized hospital is one of the oldest public hospital in the country. Currently it’s the only teaching and referral hospital in the south western part of the country which provides services for approximately 10000 inpatient and 100000 outpatients. The hospital under department of pediatrics has Neonatal Intensive Care Unit (NICU) with 30 beds Emergency OPD with 15 beds, Level I and II wards with 36 beds, Oncology ward with 22 beds, Nutritional Rehabilitation Unit (NRU) 14 beds, Pediatrics Intensive Care Unit (PICU), cardiac ward with 15 beds and Pediatrics Surgery with 22 beds, in total 158 beds.\r\nStudy period \r\nThe study will be conducted from 1st February 2022 to 31st October 2022\r\nStudy design\r\nInstitution based cross-sectional, prospective study design \r\nPopulations\r\nSource population \r\nThe source populations are all pediatric patients admitted to Jimma University Medical Center.\r\nStudy population\r\nAll children in JUMC whose age is under 5 years diagnosed to have rickets and admitted or come to the pediatrics department of JUMC during the study period\r\nSample size and sampling technique\r\nSample size\r\nThe size of the sample was estimated using the confidence interval approach. Depending on the target population (Single population proportion) minimum sample size of 250-300 would be taken for the study.		 \r\nSampling technique: Convenient and purposive non probability sample technique \r\nInclusion criteria\r\n	1 month to under 5 years children visiting JUMC during specified period\r\n	Female and male\r\n	Malnourished children \r\n	Calcium deficiency \r\n	No other relevant or related medical history\r\nExclusion criteria\r\n	Premature birth\r\n	Children on antiepileptic drugs\r\n	Cerebral Palsy \r\n	Age < 1 month\r\nData collection Tool and procedure  \r\nData collection tool\r\nIn this study data will be collected by using semi-structured questionnaire developed from standard different literatures. For data collection, the questionnaire will be prepared in English word and will be translated into local language oromiffa. An English version questionnaire which has closed and open-ended questions will be administered by face to face interview method and physical examination of important part was done on the index children.\r\nData collection procedure\r\nData collection procedure\r\nThe data will be collected by three qualified B.Sc. Nurses, B.Sc. Physiotherapists (one male and\r\none female) and two supervisors with an interview questions by using semi-structured questionnaires \r\nData quality control\r\nTo ensure the quality of the data, the data collectors and supervisors will be trained. The\r\nquestionnaire will be prepared in English language and translate to oromiffa. To check for\r\nconsistency the meaning of the oromiffa will be translated back to English language again. The\r\nsupervisor will have routine checkups for completeness and consistency of the data. Data will be\r\nchecked for its completeness, accuracy and clarity by the principal investigator and supervisors\r\nbefore data entry and data cross-checking will be done before analysis. The data tools will be pre\r\ntested before the actual data collection to check for the accuracy of responses, language clarity,\r\nand appropriateness of the tools. It will be done with 5 % of the total sample size before a\r\nweek of the actual data collection period with the population that is following in a pediatric department of the hospital, which has similar characteristics with the population selected\r\nfor the actual study. For the actual study, the necessary changes will be made based on the\r\nfindings of the pre-test.\r\nData analysis\r\nData will be entered to EPI info 7 then exported and analyzed using SPSS version 23 software.\r\nDescriptive statistics will be done for all variables in the study using statistical measurements\r\nfrequency tables, graphs, percentages, means and standard deviations. Bivariate logistic\r\nregression analysis  and multivariate logistic regression analysis will be used finally variables whose p-value less than 0.05 with 95% CI will consider as significant association and will be  reported by using both p-value and AORs. Multi collinearity will be checked using Variance Inflation Factor (VIF) with a cutoff point < 10 and the overall goodness of fit of the model will checked using Hosmer-Lemeshow goodness of fit test with a cut of point P >0.05','<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">1.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Nelson WE, Vaughan VC, Mackay R, Behrman RE. Nelson textbook of pediatrics1979.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">2.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Kenenisa C, Ewnetu H, Sime H. Retrospective analysis of prevalence of rickets and associated factors among children admitted to pediatric ward in Jimma University specialized hospital. J Pediatr Neonatal Care. 2014;1(7):00044.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">3.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Bereket A. Rickets in developing countries. Endocrine development. 2003;6:220-32.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">4.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Gartner LM, Greer FR. Prevention of rickets and vitamin D deficiency: new guidelines for vitamin D intake. Pediatrics. 2003;111(4):908-10.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">5.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Wondale Y, Shiferaw F, Lulseged S. A systematic review of nutritional rickets in Ethiopia: status and prospects. Ethiopian medical journal. 2005;43(3):203-10.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">6.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Hendrickse R. Diseases of Children in the Subtropics and Tropics. Archives of Disease in Childhood. 1978;53(6):524.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">7.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Lulseged S. Severe rickets in a children&#39;s hospital in Addis Ababa. Ethiopian medical journal. 1990;28(4):175-81.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">8.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Marcdante K, Kliegman RM. Nelson essentials of pediatrics e-book: Elsevier Health Sciences; 2014.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">9.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Sahay M, Sahay R. Rickets&ndash;vitamin D deficiency and dependency. Indian journal of endocrinology and metabolism. 2012;16(2):164.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">10.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Valentine CJ, Wagner CL. Nutritional management of the breastfeeding dyad. Pediatric Clinics. 2013;60(1):261-74.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">11.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Belachew T, Nida H, Getaneh T, Woldemariam D, Getinet W. Calcium deficiency and causation of rickets in Ethiopian children. East African medical journal. 2005;82(3).</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">12.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Braunwald E, Fauci A, Kasper D, Hauser S, Longo D, Jameson L. Harrison&#39;s Principles of Internal Medicine 15th: McGraw-Hill; 1987.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\">&nbsp;</p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\">&nbsp;</p>','To determine the prevalence of rickets in children under 5 years of age and to identify the risk factors of rickets in JUMC.',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,'Rickets, vitamin D deficiency, Osteomalasia,Calcium',10,'<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\"><strong>Study period </strong></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">The study will be conducted from 1<sup>st</sup> February 2022 to 31<sup>st</sup> October 2022</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\">&nbsp;</p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\"><strong>Broad Budget expenses include</strong></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<ul>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Stationary cost</span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Data collector and training expenses</span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Cost of printing and copying</span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Banners and flex expenses</span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Data Analysis </span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Clinical expenses</span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Contingency/ Miscellaneous </span></span></li>\r\n</ul>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\"><strong>Total 250000 Birr</strong></span></span></p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),(3,19,9,18,'Cancer is public health and the leading causes of death in worldwide, particularly in developing countries. The impact of cancer has been reported both in developed and developing countries. According to World Health Organization (WHO) report, the morbidity and mortality increasing throughout the world. Particularly the impact of cancer was high in African countries including Ethiopia. This Impact have been also associated with the development and progression of different cancer which leads to oxidative stress and responsible for producing free radicals in the body. Persistent oxidative stress promotes cell proliferation and leads to the tumors and its angiogenesis. Harmful result of this process is leads to lipid peroxidation, damage to tissue, membrane protein, enzyme, carbohydrate, deoxyribonucleic acid, and inhibition of plasma lipoprotein, lipase activities leads to hyperlipidemia, free fatty acids and electrolyte disorders. But, still the knowledge of biochemical profile is undefined in the setting, which will have the advantage to develop strategies for early screening, diagnosing, therapeutic intervention, particularly in controlling progression to late-stage disease and improving the prognosis of cancer patients. Thus, the aim of this study is to evaluate biochemical profile and associated factors among cancer patients in Jimma Medical Center, Southwest Ethiopia. Data will be collected by face to face interview and venous blood will be collected from cancer patients under aseptic condition. The collected data will be entered into Epidata and exported to SPSS for further analysis. Descriptive statistics such as frequencies, percentage and means will be computed as necessary. Hematology analyzer machine where adjusted and checked by running three (high, normal, low) levels of controls. Bivariable and multivariable logistic regression models will be used.   P-value of <0.05 will be declared as statistically significant. Based on the findings, conclusion will be drawn and recommendations will be forwarded. A total of 575,700.00 Ethiopian birr is required to accomplish the study.','Evaluation of biochemical profile among cancer patients in Jimma Medical Center, Southwest, Ethiopia','EVALUATION OF BIOCHEMICAL PROFILE AND FACTORS ASSOCIATED WITH CANCER PATIENTS IN JIMMA MEDICAL CENTER, SOUTHWEST ETHIOPIA','2021-11-19 10:16:48','Research output/Outcome \r\nThe research output/outcome the project will have the advantage to develop strategies for early screening, diagnosing, therapeutic intervention, particularly in controlling progression to late-stage disease and improving the prognosis of cancer patients.','f3b401c93c0e2f5bf69cc5450d57a030','completed','Cancer is a diseases involving abnormal cell development that invade surrounding tissue or spread throughout the body(1).Cancer is public health and the leading causes of death in worldwide, particularly in developing countries(1,2). Pediatric cancer is uncommon and it is different from adult cancers by growth, metastasis, their treatment and how they are respond to treatment(3). \r\nThe impact of cancer has been reported both in developed and developing countries(4). According to World Health Organization (WHO) report, the morbidity and mortality increasing throughout the world. Particularly the impact of cancer was high in African countries(5). It is estimated to be 21 million people will be diagnosed for tumor by 2030(6).  About 70% of cancer is respond to treatment and survival rate was 80% in western countries(7). But, more than 90% of affected adult cancer are dying from disease in developing counties(8). Similarly, Cancer is an increasing public health burden in Ethiopia(9). According to report published 2022, there are total of estimated 70% death occur from cancer in middle and low income countries(10).\r\nAll factors mentioned above have been also associated with the development and progression of different cancer which leads to oxidative stress and responsible for producing free radicals in the body(11). The release of free radicals is a continuous and physiological cell process(12). However, their excessive production shows harmful effects. Persistent oxidative stress promotes cell proliferation and leads to the tumors and its angiogenesis(13,14). Oxidative stress interfere with physiology of cancer by making genetic instability, alteration in cellular sensitivity to cancer agent, invasion, cell proliferation and promotion of mutation (15). The extent of reactive free radical-induced oxidative damage decreased by the efficiency of the antioxidant process.  Nonetheless, an imbalance between reactive species and anti-oxidant process in the body (16). \r\nThe harmful result of this process is lipid peroxidation, damage to tissue, membrane protein, enzyme, carbohydrate, deoxyribonucleic acid, inhibition of plasma lipoprotein, lipase activities leads to hyperlipidemia, and free fatty acids(17). Moreover, electrolyte disorders are also a very common condition in cancer patients(18). Different strategies have been implemented to prevent cancer. Thereby cancer caused by human papilloma virus and hepatitis B virus prevented by immunization(19). Furthermore, History of alcohol us, occupational exposure, smoking, exposed to radiation, conception of fabricated food, exposed to herbicides/pesticides, viral infection ( HPV, HBV), obesity, family history of cancer,  birth weight, parental age, congenital anomalies, over dose of radiation likes ultrasound, computed tomography during early pregnancy time are some of risk factors related to pediatric cancer prevented  by improving cancer, prevention, screening and treatment(20).\r\nMany options are used for treatment of cancer such as radiation, surgery and chemotherapy.  Consequently, different side effect occurs due to treatment of cancer such as bleeding, anemia, infection(21).  In addition to pathogenesis of cancer, chemotherapies have effect on biochemical profiles. Because, chemotherapies leads to decreases bone marrow blood cell formation facilitate cell death, prevent cell growth through inhibiting different filament function, protein faction and Deoxyribo Nucleic Acid (DNA) synthesis. Furthermore, chemotherapies affect immune system, irreversible hepatic damage(22). \r\nUp to our knowledge study were not done on biochemical profile among cancer patients in Jimma Medical Center. This will have the advantage to develop strategies for early screening, diagnosing, therapeutic intervention, particularly in controlling progression to late-stage disease and improving the prognosis of cancer patients.','Methodology \r\n Study Area and study design \r\nThe study will be conducted in Jimma Medical Center from January 1-October 30, 2022. Hospital is located in Southwest, Jimma Zone, Jimma town, Oromia regional state. It is found in 353 km far from Addis Ababa. It has serving around fifteen million found in Southwest of Ethiopia. It contained different department gynecology, obstetrics, and pediatrics. The ward is staffed with gynecologist, Oncologist, resident of different years (levels), midwifes, nurses and varying number if interns. Oncology Unit has two sub-units, infant oncology and adult oncology. \r\nSources population: All cancer patients admitted at oncology department of Jimma Medical Center will be source population.\r\nStudy Population: All selected cancer patients admitted at oncology department of Jimma Medical Center will be study Population. \r\nInclusion: All pediatrics cancer patients and adult cancer patients that fulfill inclusion criteria will be included \r\nExclusion: Patients who had other diseases and that affect biochemical profile will be excluded. \r\nSample size calculation and sampling procedure.\r\nSample size was determined by using single population proportion formula by considering the following assumptions: p = 24.9% the prevalence of breast cancer. P= 17.5% prevalence of cervical cancer, p= 12.7% prevalence of leukemia in male.  d = 5% the margin of error, Zα/2 = 1.96 at 95% confidence of certainty. Thus, n = [(Zα/2)2 *p (1-p)]/ d2 = 283, considering 10% non-response rate =28 and the taking maximum sample size was 311. The study participants will be selected using consecutive sampling technique from pediatric oncology and Adult oncology after proportionally allocated.\r\nData collection and laboratory methods\r\nThe data will be collected by face to face interview. After data collection tools adapted and modified from different literatures. Questionnaire addressing socio-demographic characteristics, environmental factors and child related risk factors will be collected by using combination of interview by trained B.sc Nurse.\r\nBiochemical analysis\r\nVenous blood will be collected following standard procedure from cancer patients by qualified laboratory professional. Different machine will be used depend on objective of the study and standard operating procedure will be followed in order to maintain quality of laboratory results. \r\nData analysis procedure\r\nThe collected data will be checked for its completeness and coded. It will enter to Epi data version 3.1, and then, it will export to SPSS version 23 for analysis. Data will be coded and entered to EpiData version 3.1 and then exported to SPSS version 25 for analysis. To evaluate the biochemical data descriptive analysis like frequency, percentage and means will be computed as necessary. Bivariate and multivariate logistic regression models will be used to determine the degree of association between the outcome and predictor variables. P-value of <0.05 will be declared as statistically significant.\r\nData Quality assurance \r\nAll the chemicals, and reagent’s expiry date will be checked and control solution will be used to check that the Analyzers, reagents and the test strips are working together and performing the test correctly. The sample will be collected, transported and stored according to the standard guideline until used. Each biochemical test will be performed by experienced laboratory technologists following standard operational procedures. Pretests will be performed for questionaries’ to ensure the validity. Consequently, amendments and corrections were made.\r\nPlan for dissemination and ensuring utilization of findings \r\nThe results of this research project will be presented and submitted to the Research and Postgraduate Coordinating Office of Jimma University. An attempt will be made to present at different conferences and symposiums. Copies of the document will be given to the respective stakeholders so that it will be used as a source of information for possible planning and implementation of health intervention. An effort will be made for possible publication on a reputable journal.','<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">1. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Sung H, Ferlay J, Siegel RL, Laversanne M, Soerjomataram I, Jemal A, et al. Global Cancer Statistics 2020: GLOBOCAN Estimates of Incidence and Mortality Worldwide for 36 Cancers in 185 Countries. CA Cancer J Clin. 2022;71(3):209&ndash;49. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">2. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Sung H, Ferlay J, Siegel RL, Laversanne M, Soerjomataram I, Jemal A, Bray F. Global cancer statistics 2020: GLOBOCAN estimates of incidence and mortality worldwide for 36 cancers in 185 countries. CA: a cancer journal for clinicians. 2021 May;71(3):209-49.</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">3. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Kaatsch P. Epidemiology of childhood cancer. Cancer treatment reviews. 2010 Jun 1; 36(4):277-85. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">4. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Gupta S, Howard SC, Hunger SP, Antillon FG, Metzger ML, Israels T, Harif M, Rodriguez-Galindo C. Treating childhood cancer in low-and middle-income countries. Cancer. Washington DC: The World Bank Group. 2015 Oct 29:121-46.</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">5. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Frenk J. Cancer is on the rise in developing countries. Harvard University Journal. 2009;2. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">6. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Thun MJ, DeLancey JO, Center MM, Jemal A, Ward EM. The global burden of cancer: priorities for prevention. Carcinogenesis. 2010 Jan 1; 31(1):100-10.</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">7. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Saletta F, Seng MS, Lau LM. Advances in pediatric cancer treatment. Translational pediatrics. 2014 Apr;3(2):156. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">8. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Collaborative Group on Hormonal Factors in Breast Cancer. Breast cancer and breastfeeding: collaborative reanalysis of individual data from 47 epidemiological studies in 30 countries, including 50 302 women with breast cancer and 96 973 women without the disease. The lancet. 2002 Jul 20; 360(9328):187-95. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">9. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Endalamaw A, Assimamaw NT, Ayele TA, Muche AA, Zeleke EG, Wondim A, Belay GM, Birhanu Y, Tazebew A, Techane MA, Kassa SF. Prevalence of childhood Cancer among children attending referral hospitals of outpatient Department in Ethiopia. BMC cancer. 2021 Dec;21(1):1-0.</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\">&nbsp;</p>\r\n\r\n<p