Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">8</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top; width:382px\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Grand Total </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top; width:219px\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">170,500.00</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n		</tr>\r\n	</tbody>\r\n</table>',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),(10,14,9,24,'EXCUTIVE SUMMERY\r\nBACKGROUND: Numerous studies in developed country have suggested an association or path between unhealthy eating habits, health related quality of life and mental health problems in adolescents. Unfortunately, the scarcity of data in developing country including Ethiopia makes it difficult to develop appropriate intervention.\r\nOBJECTIVE: To assess mental health problems in relation to eating behavior patterns, nutrient intakes and health related quality of life among high school female adolescent in Jimma zone\r\nMETHODOLOGY: School based cross sectional study design will be used among a sample of 560 female adolescents aged between 10–19 years randomly selected from Jimma zone high schools in 2021. Validated tool will be used to collect data on eating pattern, nutritional status, quality of life and mental health status by following standard operational procedure after conducting pre-test. \r\nANALYSIS STRATEGY: Statistical analysis will be performed with STATA. Normality of data will be analyzed by Kolmogorov-Smirnov test. Normally distributed data will be presented as mean ± SD, and data for variables that will not comply with a normal distribution will be presented as median (interquartile range (IQR), 25; 75 percentile). Multiple logistic regression analysis will be used to examine the association between mental health problems as dependent variable and eating patterns, nutritional status and health related quality of life as independent variables. Furthermore, to provide a sound explanation on how participants’ eating pattern could predict mental health through their quality of life; we will conduct Structural Equation Modeling (SEM).  \r\nBUDGET AND TIMELINE: A total of 446,800 ETB is required to undertake this project with a time period of from January to September , 2022.','Mental health status in relation to eating behavioral pattern and quality of life','Mental health problems in relation to eating behavior patterns, nutrient intakes and health related quality of life among Jimma zone high school female adolescents: Structural equation modeling','2021-11-19 11:27:57','Research outcomes: \r\n-Prevalence of mental health problem will be determined among adolescent in jimma zone\r\n-Eating pattern and Nutritional status of adolescent will be determined\r\n-The association between mental health and eating pattern will be determined then \r\n- The pathways through which dietary pattern is associated with mental health of school adolescents in Jimma Zone will be determined \r\n-The data will be used as baseline for intervention to tackle epidemic of mental health problem from nutritional point of view.','b6c62a8c8a35a3fcb746539a603b95b1','completed','Introduction\r\nMental health problems comprise of a broad range of mental disorders including anxiety disorders, bipolar disorders, depression and eating disorders are generally characterized by some combination of abnormal thoughts, emotions, behavior and relationships with others (1).\r\nMental health problems, as common public health issues among adolescents, have a significant\r\nadverse impact not only on adolescents but also on their parents and families particularly in\r\nrelation to health related quality of life (HRQoL) (2,3). Health-related quality of life (HRQoL)\r\nis a multi-dimensional concept that includes domains related to physical, mental, emotional,\r\nand social functioning (4,5). \r\nAdolescence, as a transitional period between childhood and adulthood, is associated\r\nwith dramatic change and development of the physical, emotional and cognitive function(6).\r\nUnhealthy eating patterns including meal skipping, snacking sugar containing drinks and\r\nsweets, eating away from home, fast food consumption and high intakes of simple sugars, \r\nsaturated fats and unconventional dietary pattern such as adopting vegetarian diet, specific weight loss diet and overall reduction of food intake are common among adolescents (3,6–8). Adequate nutrition and healthy food choices are known to be an important factor in the development of brain and prevention of cognitive disorders; unhealthy eating behaviors are important determinants of mental health problems including hyperactivity disorder, depression and conductive problems in adolescents (9,10).\r\nNumerous studies in developed country have suggested an association between unhealthy eating habits and mental health problems in adolescents (3,10–15). Consequently, several studies have also revealed the association between health related quality of life (HRQoL) and diet; adherence to healthy diets is associated with significant improvements in general physical and psychological health, longevity and lower levels of obesity (16–18). \r\nRecently, studies about dietary patterns have attracted potential attention since dietary patterns provide an overview of diet and facilitate exploration of overall diet and diet-disease relationship that are not revealed when single nutrients or food items are studied (19) but in Ethiopia we don’t have our own validated tool. In this study, mental health problems will be defined as three aspects of mental issues including conduct/oppositional disorders, hyperactivity/ inattention disorders and anxiety/ depressive disorders measured by a self-completed strengths and difficulties questionnaire (SDQ) (20).\r\nAdolescent mental health is inadequately addressed by international development organizations and actions at country level appear to be fragmented(21). Moreover, female adolescents are more prone to develop internalizing mental health problems including emotional problems, depression and anxiety (22). This is because female adolescents use more internalizing mechanisms to face problems (23). The condition is more palpable in students; they encounter with higher stress than others because of greater material expectations, greater pressure on academic institutions and staff, change in environment, and expansion of student numbers. This stress with serious consequences leads to the development of mental problems (24).\r\nAccordingly, Unhealthy eating patterns are most likely to develop internalizing rather than externalizing problems and are more common among girls (25). Teenage girls eat less healthy foods than other population groups; lower self-esteem, more body image dissatisfaction and distortion and thin ideals in the modern society also trigger the unhealthy eating patterns and related mental issues in females (23,26).\r\nNumerous studies in developed country have suggested an association  between unhealthy eating habits, health related quality of life and mental health problems in adolescents. Unfortunately, the scarcity of data in developing country including Ethiopia makes it difficult to develop appropriate intervention.\r\nRational of the study\r\nEthiopia was on an accelerated economic and social development path and the country was developing at 10% growth annually. Such a rapid economic growth requires a physically and mentally healthy population in order to maintain and sustain the achievement of this economic and social development. However, mental illness is the leading non-communicable disorder in terms of burden which affects sustainability of economic growth. Indeed, in a predominantly rural area of Ethiopia, mental illness comprised 11% of the total burden of disease, which out-ranking HIV/AIDS (27).\r\n These surprising statistics show that mental illnesses have been overlooked as a major health priority in Ethiopia and other LMICs, and underscore the need for public health programs targeting mental illnesses with nutritional lens. Thus, understanding the contribution of eating behavioral pattern on mental health and identifying interventions that can minimize the roaming epidemic is of paramount importance. Unfortunately, current scarcity of evidence related to mental health and its relation to eating pattern make it difficult to respond to this need and to propose suitable course of actions. To the best of our knowledge there is no study evaluate the relationship between eating patterns and mental health issues among teenage student girls in Ethiopia. So this study will play paramount importance in closing the research gap. \r\nHypothesis \r\nOur hypothesis is there is direct and indirect association between eating pattern and mental health status\r\nDirect association is eating pattern >>> mental health\r\n\r\nIndirect (path ) is eating pattern>>> quality of life>>> mental health','Materials and methods\r\nStudy area, design and period \r\nSchool based cross sectional study design will be used among a sample of 560 female adolescents aged between 10–19 years randomly selected from Jimma zone high schools in 2021. Multi stage cluster sampling technique will be used, first from 22 woredas in Jimma zone 8 woredas will be selected by lottery methods. After getting number and name of high school in each woreda, 30 % of high school will be included in our study.  Adolescents will be allocated to each school based on proportional number of female adolescent attaining school. Finally each adolescent will be selected by simple random method after developing sampling farm from their roster in each selected schools. \r\nData collection and procedure\r\nAnthropometric assessments\r\nWeight will be measured by calibrated Seca scale (Itin Scale Co., Inc. Germany) with the precision of 0.1 kg and height to the nearest 0.1 cm with a wall scale while subjects wearing light clothes and no shoes. The measurements will be performed two times and the students will be informed about the results of their anthropometric measurements in written forms. BMI will be calculated as weight (kg) divided by height (m) 2. Obesity will be defined as BMI  30 kg/m2 (28).\r\nMeasurement of health-related quality of life (HRQoL)\r\nHealth related quality of life will be  measured using the Short Form 36 (SF-36) questionnaire first developed by Ware et al (29).  The questionnaire was validated in a large population based study\r\namong Ethiopian population by Kebede et al (30). The questionnaire includes 36 items measuring eight dimensions of life quality: Physical Functioning (PF); Role Physical (RP), which refers to role limitations due to physical difficulties; Bodily Pain (BP); General Health (GH); Vitality (VT); Social Functioning (SF); Role Emotional (RE), which refers to role limitations due to emotional difficulties, and Mental Health (MH). The SF-36 has eight scaled scores; the scores are weighted sums of the questions in each section. Scores range from 0 to 100. Lower scores denote more disability and poor HRQoL while higher scores denote less disability and better HRQoL.\r\nPrediction of mental health problems\r\nPrediction of mental health problems will be performed by the Strengths and Difficulties Questionnaires (SDQ), a brief screening questionnaire for psychological problems of children and adolescents. The questionnaire includes 25 items. Each of the 25 items is scored on a three point Liker type scale ranging from 0 to 2. The items are divided into five subscales with five items each resulting in scores of emotional disorders, conduct problems, hyperactive behavior, peer relationships and pro-social behaviors. Finally it will be categorized in to dichotomous variable (unlikely, possible/probable) of each category of mental health problem .The questionnaire was validated by Hoosen et al (20) for use in Ethiopian children and teenagers aged 3 to 18 years old.\r\nMeasurement of eating behavioral pattern\r\nEating behavioral patterns will be measured by eating behavioral pattern questionnaire (EBPQ).\r\nThe questionnaire was firstly developed in Vanderbit University to evaluate dietary fat intake among African- American women (31). The questionnaire includes 51 self-reported items on\r\nhealthy and unhealthy eating behaviors. Every item was rated in a 5 point Likert scale ranging\r\nfrom strongly disagrees to strongly agree. Six eating behavior patterns were assessed in the\r\nEBPQ as follows: low fat eating, snacking and convenience, emotional eating, planning ahead, meal skipping and cultural/ life style behavior. \r\nDietary intake\r\nDietary intake was assessed using a semi-quantitative food-frequency questionnaire (FFQ) of\r\n28 food items adapted to the study area.\r\nStatistical analysis \r\nStatistical analysis will be performed with STATA. Normality of data will be analyzed by Kolmogorov-Smirnov test. Normally distributed data will be presented as mean ± SD, and data for variables that will not comply with a normal distribution will be presented as median (interquartile range (IQR), 25; 75 percentile). Anthropometric indices (weight-for-height or Body Mass Index (BMI)-for-age, height-for-age and weight-for-age) will be calculated using z-score Stata command and the Anthroplus stata macros based on WHO (2006) growth standards for adolescent.  Multiple logistic regression analysis will be used to examine the association between mental health problems as dependent variable and eating patterns, nutritional status and health related quality of life as independent variables. Adjusted odds ratio (OR) and 95% confidence intervals (CI) will be calculated for emotional, conduct, hyperactivity, peer and pro social problems. All potential confounding variables will be entered in the multiple logistic regression analysis to control confounding effect. P values less than 0.05 will be used as statistically significant. Internal consistency, criterion and construct validity will be done to validate behavioral eating patter in our context. Furthermore, to provide a sound explanation on how participants’ eating pattern could predict mental health through their quality of life; we will conducted Structural Equation Modeling (SEM). Accordingly, we hypothesized that poor eating pattern would result in poor quality of life and then poor mental health – which means quality of life is a mediating factor in the relation b/n eating pattern and mental health.  \r\nSEM with maximum likelihood estimation will be fitted to model the above hypothesized relationships. In the measurement models, factor analysis will be employed to estimate the latent variables, dietary pattern, from their construct observed variables. In the structural models, we will estimate both unstandardized and standardized estimates of the direct and indirect effect of eating pattern on mental health through the hypothesized pathways. The need of further model adjustment for relevant variables will be checked by including important covariates. We will evaluate the reliability of the measurement scales and the relative importance of each construct variable in a scale using Cronbach’s alpha coefficient. Model fit will be evaluated by utilizing Chi-Square statistic, Standardized Root Mean Square Residual (SRMR), Tucker-Lewis Index (TLI), Comparative Fit Index (CFI), and Root Mean Square Error of Approximation (RMSEA). As proposed by Hu and Bentler , TLI and CFI values ≥0.95, SRMR values ≤0.08, and RMSEA values ≤0.06 were defined to indicate an adequate fit for the data.','<h1><span style=\"font-size:16pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Cambria&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\"><span style=\"color:#2e74b5\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Reference</span></span></span></span></h1>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt\">1. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; WHO. Depression and Other Common Mental Disorders Global Health Estimates. 2017.</span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt\">2. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Xiaoli Y, Chao J, Wen P, Wenming X, Fang L, Ning L, et al. Prevalence of Psychiatric Disorders among Children and Adolescents in Northeast China. PLoS One. 2014;9(10):1&ndash;9. </span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt\">3. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Oellingrath IM, Svendsen M V, Hestetun I. Eating patterns and mental health problems in early adolescence &ndash; a cross-sectional study of 12 &ndash; 13-year-old Norwegian schoolchildren. Public Health Nutr. 2013;17(11):2554&ndash;62. </span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt\">4. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; US department of health and health service. Health-Related Quality of Life and Well-Being [Internet]. 2010. Available from: https://www.healthypeople.gov/sites/default/files/DefaultPressRelease.</span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt\">5. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Edwards MK LP. Psycho-Socioeconomic bio-behavioral influences on health-related quality of life. Heal Promot Perspect. 2017;7(3):124&ndash;7. </span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt\">6. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Chin Y. MN. Eating behaviours among female Adolescents in Kuantan district, Pahana, Malaysia. Pakistan J Nutr. 2009.</span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt\">7. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Stang J, Kong A, Story M, Eisenberg ME, Neumark-sztainer D. Food and Weight-Related Patterns and Behaviors of Hmong Adolescents. 2007;936&ndash;41. </span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt\">8. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; WHO. Nutrition in adolescence &ndash; Issues and Challenges for the Health Sector. 2017. </span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt\">9. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Weng T, Hao J, Qian Q, Cao H, Fu J, Sun Y, et al. Is there any relationship between dietary patterns and depression and anxiety in Chinese adolescents ? Public Health Nutr. 2011;15(4):673&ndash;82. </span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt\">10. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Lien L, Lien N, Heyerdahl S, Thoresen M, Bjertness E. Consumption of Soft Drinks and Hyperactivity , Mental Distress , and Conduct Problems Among Adolescents in Oslo , Norway. 2006;96(10):1815&ndash;20. </span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt\">11. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Howard AL, Robinson M, Smith GJ, Ambrosini GL, Piek JP, Oddy WH. ADHD Is Associated With a &ldquo; Western &rdquo; Dietary Pattern in Adolescents. 2011.</span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt\">12. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Jacka FN, Kremer PJ, Leslie ER, Berk M, Patton GC, Toumbourou JW, et al. Associations between diet quality and depressed mood in adolescents : results from the Australian Healthy Neighbourhoods Study. 2010;(November 2009). </span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt\">13. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Jacka FN, Kremer PJ, Berk M, Silva-sanigorski AM De, Leslie ER, Pasco JA, et al. A Prospective Study of Diet Quality and Mental Health in Adolescents. PLoS One. 2011;6(9):1&ndash;7. </span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"f