font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\"><span style=\"color:black\">40</span></span></span></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top; width:105px\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\"><span style=\"color:black\">610</span></span></span></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top; width:105px\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\"><span style=\"color:black\">122,000</span></span></span></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n		</tr>\r\n		<tr>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:1px solid black; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top; width:39px\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\"><span style=\"color:black\">Total </span></span></span></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top; width:170px\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\">&nbsp;</p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top; width:126px\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\">&nbsp;</p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top; width:83px\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\">&nbsp;</p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top; width:105px\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\">&nbsp;</p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top; width:105px\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><strong><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\"><span style=\"color:black\">446,800</span></span></span></strong></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n		</tr>\r\n	</tbody>\r\n</table>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\">&nbsp;</p>',1,NULL,NULL,NULL),(11,42,9,24,'In Ethiopia, 59% of infants under 6 months are exclusively breastfed. Contrary to the WHO recommendation, 14% of infants 0-5 months also consume plain water, 1% of them consume non-milk liquids, 8% consume other milk, and 13% consume complementary foods in addition to breast milk. Notably, 6% of infants under age 6 months are not breastfed at all. Exclusive breastfeeding among children under age 6 months has consistently increased from 49% in 2005 to 59% in EMDHS 2019. Regard to child undernutrition in Ethiopia, it shows reduction of child undernutrition between 2005 and 2019. The prevalence of stunting has decreased considerably, from 51% in 2005 to 37% in 2019. Moreover, the prevalence of wasting decreased over the same time period, from 12% to 7%. The percentage of underweight children has consistently decreased from 33% to 21% over this 14-year period. This needs progressive intervention to tackle the problems which happened due to lack of knowledge, practice, and positive attitude on infant feeding. Therefore, this study aimed to Measure the effect of cell phone-based peer counselling on improved breastfeeding practices, complementary feeding, good hygiene and health care practices of mothers living in rural and semi-urban areas of Jimma Zone, Ethiopia.\r\n\r\nA formative assessment will be done using exploratory qualitative research and reviewing of available literatures which can be in the design of study, to identify available resources and to prepare tools for collection of data. The interventions strategies will be developed and implemented in line with national health and nutrition programs and guidelines.  An RCT will be conducted in rural and semi urban areas in Jimma Zone. Healthy pregnant women who are attending antenatal care will be recruited during antenatal services, enrolled at 24-32 weeks of gestation, and randomized to receive either standard of care (SOC) or cell phone based peer support (CPS) up to 12 months postpartum. Effectiveness of the intervention will be measured by comparing intervention and control groups on primary and secondary outcome variables using the relevant statistical tests. T-tests will examine differences in means between the two groups for continuous outcomes. Logistic regression analysis will be performed to examine independent predictors of breastfeeding and complementary feeding. Sub-group analyses will be performed on key socioeconomic factors.',NULL,'Cell Phone Based Infant Feeding Counselling in Jimma Zone, Ethiopia: a randomized controlled trial','2021-11-18 11:29:36','Research output/outcome \r\nInformation that will improve interventions for reducing child undernutrition and improve child health care are central for the work to reduce child mortality and achieve the Sustainable Development Goals. Also, improved child nutrition will translate into enhanced child development which is not only a human right but also imperative for development in general. Furthermore, reduced morbidity and mortality will translate into reduced health care expenses for the society. The results from the PINC study, including the process evaluation and cost-effectiveness analysis, will inform policy makers and local stakeholders of the effectiveness of the described intervention to improve health and nutrition strategies. This will be of importance, not only to the involved countries, but also to other LLMIC. The results and experiences from this study will be disseminated to the local stakeholders as well as global actors in child health and nutrition such as the WHO, UNICEF, the Scaling up Nutrition network and Thousand Days, to the benefit for children globally. The project will contribute to consolidate both national and global knowledge networks in nutrition security and child health that undertakes operations and implementation science research on interventions to improve child nutrition and feeding practices in several priority low-income countries.','0377d381aabc35aaf5f12f4170ff6372','completed','Globally in 2020, the number of children malnourished under five-year age that were stunted, wasted and overweight were 149, 45 and 39 million respectively (1). Annually more than six million children under the age of five die in low- and lower middle-income countries (LLMIC) largely from preventable infectious diseases [1]. Undernutrition is the single most important underlying cause of child death, accounting for an estimated 45% [2].  \r\nThe effectiveness of community delivered breastfeeding education and support in improving child nutrition, and specifically of peer counselling for improving breastfeeding practices, are well-documented [4]. Identifying the means to feasibly scale up such interventions is still a challenge, but recent research conducted through a cell phone based peer counselling intervention offers promising results. In 2010 researchers from the UoT and Egerton partnered to develop and test cell phone based support (CPS) as a novel form of peer counselling for improved IYCF in Kenya, funded by Alive & Thrive and GAIN [5]. In the randomized controlled trial implemented among low-income women in a peri-urban communities, CPS significantly increased the rate of exclusive breastfeeding (EBF) at three months postpartum in comparison with both a traditional peer support group model and the usual hospital standard of care [5]. CPS is thus an innovation that has been successfully tested for feasibility and effectiveness in a single location in Kenya, and it is ready for further enhancement and testing across contexts in Kenya and beyond.\r\nComplementary feeding is critical to child health and nutrition, but the evidence on effective interventions to optimise complementary feeding practices at large scale is still lacking [6]. The PINC study will contribute to establishing the evidence on how to use m-health to effectively scale up breastfeeding support, and at the same time examine how to effectively promote healthy complementary feeding and health care practices, and consequently improve child health and nutrition in LLMIC countries. The intervention’s cost-effectiveness will also be examined. The study uses an innovative and game-changing approach. Furthermore, the study fosters an international collaborative partnership where the capacity of all involved is strengthened. \r\nUndernutrition is a major underlying cause for child mortality [2]. Sub-optimal breastfeeding and complementary feeding practices as well as diseases, in particular diarrhoea, are important determinants for undernutrition [2]. \r\nGlobally, between 1990 and 2011, the proportion of stunted children fell from an estimated 40% to 26% [8]. There are both regional and national differences, following a marked socio-economic trend [8]. The magnitude of undernutrition in both African and Asian countries is alarming, not only because it underlies a large part of the child deaths within the continents, but also because undernutrition during foetal stage and the first two years of life often results in largely irreversible, lifelong cognitive and physical developmental damage [9-13]. \r\nA wealth of evidence supports the WHO and UNICEF recommendation of early initiation of breastfeeding after birth, exclusive breastfeeding of infants until six months of age followed by appropriate complementary feeding with continued breastfeeding until at least two years of age [14]. Breastfeeding is the most effective and inexpensive way to save infant lives [6]. Only 38% of infants aged 0 to 6 months worldwide are exclusively breastfed [15], however randomized intervention trials have showed that counselling or educational interventions increased exclusive breastfeeding by up to 90% from 1-5 months [16]. \r\nIn developing countries early cessation of breastfeeding and introduction of unsound artificial feeding of infants with very dilute milk products is common (17). Only one third of breastfed infants receive nutritionally adequate and safe complementary food [17].\r\nSeveral reviews have documented promising results of interventions such as maternal education for improving complementary feeding, although results are mixed [18-20]. However, this evidence has not been translated into improved outcomes on a national level because there is not sufficient knowledge about what makes these interventions work, at what cost and if they are sustainable. In general there is a need for studies that use formative research and process evaluation and where intermediary behaviour changes are assessed to learn which complementary feeding interventions work [20]. Thus, operational research on how to actually deliver, implement and scale-up these interventions is essential in order to formulate sustainable solutions that will improve uptake of optimal practices and eventually child health outcomes [21, 22]. \r\nThe use of eHealth (the use of Information & Communication Technology for health), and its subset mHealth (use of mobile technology for health), has been promoted as an approach to the development of interventions, which is potentially cost-effective and easily scaled up, and which can be used to reach populations in both low-resource and remote settings [25]. Previous studies using live phone calls to give health messages have been successful in various settings [26], and such services have potential for a great impact on maternal and child health in low- and middle income countries. However, research findings from randomized controlled studies on improving infant feeding and health care practices using mHealth in low-income countries are limited, and there is a need for further studies [26].\r\nExperiences from the RCT of CPS in Kenya showed that the mothers appreciated live phone conversations with the peer counsellors rather than the use of text messaging, since it ensured tailored, personalized counselling (pers. comm, E. Kamau-Mbuthia). Furthermore, the mothers preferred CPS over peer support groups since the former was less time consuming. If proven successful, interventions using CPS will contribute to improved mother and child health through the development of the health systems in low- and middle-income countries. This will work in areas with good cell phone penetration in the communities, and has potential for rapid scaling up at low cost. \r\nIn Ethiopia, 59% of infants under 6 months are exclusively breastfed. Contrary to the WHO recommendation, 14% of infants 0-5 months also consume plain water, 1% of them consume non-milk liquids, 8% consume other milk, and 13% consume complementary foods in addition to breast milk. Notably, 6% of infants under age 6 months are not breastfed at all. Exclusive breastfeeding among children under age 6 months has consistently increased from 49% in 2005 to 59% in EMDHS 2019. Regard to child undernutrition in Ethiopia, it shows reduction of child undernutrition between 2005 and 2019. The prevalence of stunting has decreased considerably, from 51% in 2005 to 37% in 2019. Moreover, the prevalence of wasting decreased over the same time period, from 12% to 7%. The percentage of underweight children has consistently decreased from 33% to 21% over this 14-year period(Ethiopian Public Health Institute (EPHI) & ICF, 2021). This needs progressive intervention to tackle the problems which happened due to lack of knowledge, practice, and positive attitude on infant feeding.','Study setting, design, population, sampling and statistical analysis \r\n Jimma Zone is one of the 20 administrative Zone in Oromia Regional state and it is found 354 Km to South West of Addis Ababa. The capital town of the Zone is Jimma, it is administratively divided in to 20 rural districts and one urban administrative. There are 512 rural and 43 urban kebeles. There are 122 Health canters and 565 health posts. The total population of the zone is estimated to be 3,518,260 from 2007 census data projection. The current project will be implemented in rural and semi urban area of the zone\r\nStudy Design \r\nThe PINC study will comprise intervention mapping include several steps and both evaluation and RCT can be included. These steps are a formative assessment, an RCT, a process evaluation and a cost-effectiveness analysis. \r\nFormative assessment\r\nA formative assessment will be done using exploratory qualitative research and reviewing of available literatures which can be in the design of study, to identify available resources and to prepare tools for collection of data.  The interventions strategies will be developed and implemented in line with national health and nutrition programs and guidelines. \r\nFindings of formative assessment will be also used to identify the terms which are commonly used in the community for infant feeding practices like provision of colostrum, onset of lactation and initiation of complementary feeding practises to develop key messages on infant feeding. Additionally, the formative assessment will focus   on assessing maternal understanding of the appropriateness and cultural adaptation, ‘the systematic modification of an evidence-based treatment (EBT) or intervention protocol to consider language, culture, and context in such a way that it is compatible with the client’s cultural patterns, meanings, and values’ of the Breastfeeding Intentions Scale (BFIS) [29] and the Breastfeeding Self Efficacy Scale (BFSES) [30] will be tested to scale up the intervention in Jimma Zone, Ethiopia. \r\nRandomized Controlled Trial (RCT)\r\nAn RCT will be conducted in rural and semi urban areas in Jimma Zone. Healthy pregnant women who are attending antenatal care will be recruited during antenatal services, enrolled at 24-32 weeks of gestation, and randomized to receive either standard of care (SOC) or cell phone based peer support (CPS) up to 12 months postpartum. \r\nA suite of baseline and post-natal indicators will be compared between the two allocation groups: SOC and CPS. Recruited and trained field workers will conduct a total of six interviews with the mothers, at the hospital (the first interview), over the phone and at home (the final interview) to collect the data (Table 2). Anthropometric measurements (weight and length) will be collected at birth and at 12 months to compare growth between the two groups.\r\nThe primary outcomes \r\nDifference between mothers in the intervention and control group (after adjusting for potentially confounders) regarding:\r\n(1) Breastfeeding initiation within first hour of delivery and provision of colostrum, (2) exclusive breastfeeding to 3 and 6 months, (3) time of initiation of complementary food, (4) Dietary diversity score and food frequencies of complementary feeding at 9 and 12 months, and (5) continued breastfeeding (BF) to 9 and 12 months\r\nSecondary outcomes \r\nDifference between mothers in the intervention and control group (after adjusting for potentially confounders) regarding:\r\n1) Maternal knowledge and attitudes on optimal infant feeding practices (at 12 months), and 2) Difference in hygiene (hand washing practices) of the mothers between the intervention groups and control groups. \r\n Table 1. Interview schedule for data collections of control (SOC) and intervention (CPS) groups\r\n	Interview 1	Interview 2	Interview 3	Interview 4	Interview 5	Interview 6\r\nReproductive stage	24-32 weeks gestation	1 week postpartum	3 months postpartum	6 months postpartum	9 months    postpartum	12 months     postpartum\r\nLocation	Hospital// Health centre/Health post	Phone	Phone	Phone	Phone	Home\r\nDuration	30 min	30 min	45 min	45 min	45 min	60 min\r\nIndicators and outcomes	Maternal and household background information\r\nIntention to BF\r\nMaternal weight/height\r\nClinical info	Infant feeding practices\r\nHealth care seeking practices\r\nMorbidity pattern\r\nHygiene\r\nBirth weight/length	Infant feeding practices\r\nHealth care seeking practices\r\nMorbidity pattern \r\nHygiene\r\nVaccination\r\n	Infant feeding practices\r\nHealth care seeking practices\r\nMorbidity pattern \r\nHygiene\r\nVaccination\r\n	Infant feeding practices\r\nHealth care seeking practices\r\nMorbidity pattern \r\nHygiene\r\nVaccination\r\n	Infant feeding practices\r\nHealth care seeking practices\r\nMorbidity pattern \r\nHygiene\r\nVaccination\r\nMaternal knowledge on infant feeding\r\nInfant weight/ length\r\nIntervention Strategies\r\nThe control group (SOC) will receive the standard infant feeding and health care support provided by nursing and other staff at the ante-natal and health clinics as per infants and young child feeding guideline. \r\nTo implement the intervention components (CPS), trained peer leaders (TPL=20, 1 in each rural district) will be recruited through a thorough process. Potential candidates are experienced mothers who have themselves breastfed, and preferably have training and experience as BF/IYCF counsellor. The pre-selected candidates will complete training to support EBF, appropriate complementary feeding (CF), hygiene and health care behaviour of others. The final selection of TPLs will be based on their skills in counselling on the topics in question, in handling various situations and in treating all clients with respect. The selected TPLs will support women randomized to CPS from late pregnancy (24-32 weeks) to 12 months postpartum. The TPLs will receive salary based on hours of work, and it will be a part-time job. A “call centre” will be established where the TPLs will access a designated phone from which they call. The participating mothers will be designated a TPL who will be assigned to call them every 2nd week until 12 months postpartum on a predetermined schedule. The TPLs will use prepared check lists with progressive questions regarding feeding, health care and hygiene suited the infant’s age, and based on previous calls. TPLs will keep a log for each mother to ensure that each call builds on the previous conversations. Trained supervisors will oversee that the scheduled CPS takes place and will supervise when needed. Participants assigned to CPS will be provided with the cell phone number of a specific TPL who they can call whenever they need BF or CF assistance. Features for sending messages at no cost is often offered from the mobile service provider which will enable the mothers to contact the TPL. \r\nThe participating mothers will not be provided with cell phones, airtime or any additional cell phone accessories.\r\nProcess evaluation\r\nThe PINC study will apply the program implementation pathways (PIP) analysis described by Nguen et al [32], where the mechanisms through which interventions are expected to achieve impact will be mapped. We will examine the content of the communication between the mothers and the TPL during individual cell phone calls, which will be documented using call logs. We will examine participant flow for potential biases at enrolment and through dropout. Furthermore, we will examine process indicators to (i) evaluate the quality of delivery of the CPS intervention to identify any potential threats to the validity of findings; and (ii) investigate pathways through which such support may have strengthened maternal efforts to meet national recommendations on exclusive breastfeeding and adequate complementary feeding by group.\r\nCost-effectiveness analysis\r\nIn this task we will do cost analysis using participant-level data collected as a part of the RCT [33]. The aim is to estimate the cost per counselled women and use these estimates to do cost-effectiveness analysis related to the main outcomes. To estimate the cost we will collect data regarding cost of counselling per mother (time used for the counsellor, equipment, air time etc.); and time used on counselling for the counselled women. If the RCT cannot provide sufficient cost data, we will supplement with model simulations based on relevant literature [34]. Uncertainty will be handled by using deterministic and probabilistic sensitivity analysis [35].\r\nIf the quality of the collected data allows it, we will also do cost-effectiveness analysis including more long-term health outcomes. By using modelling and parameter values from literature, we can estimate the long term effect from the intermediate outcomes collected in the RCT [36]. \r\nParticipants - recruitment, inclusion and exclusion criteria \r\nCalculation of the sample size was performed with increase in EBF for six months as the primary outcome. To detect an increase of EBF from about 35% to 50% with a power of 90%, a total of 448 mothers are needed in each setting, assuming a significance level of 5%. Assuming a drop-out rate of 20% and cluster design effect of 1.5, a total of 560 women should be included, randomized into 280 women per group (SOC and CPS). \r\nAll women attending at least one of the four recommended antenatal visits at the health facilities in the study area and who is at 24-32 weeks gestation, will be eligible to participate in the study. These women will be asked if they are interested to participate prior to screening and enrolment. \r\nInclusion criteria: 1) Household ownership of a cell phone; 2) Anticipated local residence for the next 15 months; 3) Pregnancy verified by a health care provider; gestation age 24 - 32 weeks (based on hospital record of Estimated Due Date, EDD); 4) Intention to breastfeed; 5) Aged 18 - 45 years; 6) no history of mental illness, 7) no current health condition that contraindicates breastfeeding.\r\nExclusion criteria: 1) Infant born prior to 37 weeks of gestation and/or with low birth weight (<2500 g); 2) Prior preference for group allocated; 3) Diagnosis with chronic illness such as HIV, diabetes or hypertension; 4) Unwilling to give detailed directions to their home address.\r\nFive maternal screening questions asked at recruitment will establish inclusion criteria 1-6.  Recruitment will be conducted over a period of 6-9 months in order to arrive at the desired sample size.\r\nRandomisation – sequence, allocation, blinding\r\nRandom numbers will be generated using a computer program. All volunteering women meeting the inclusion criteria at each recruitment session (i.e., antenatal clinic session) will be allocated random numbers by a member of the study team. Randomisation will be done in blocks of 2 or 4 to ensure equal allocation to both control and intervention groups. Allocation will be concealed. During data collection, the field workers will be blinded from knowing the allocation of the study participants.\r\nStatistical analyses\r\nEffectiveness of the intervention will be measured by comparing intervention and control groups on primary and secondary outcome variables using the relevant statistical tests. ANOVA and t-tests will examine differences in means between the two groups for continuous outcomes. Linear and Cox regression will be performed to examine independent predictors of breastfeeding and complementary feeding. Sub-group analyses will be performed on key socioeconomic factors. \r\nCapacity building (inclusion of PhD and/or MSc students) \r\nCompetence building is of high value to this mega project. The PhD and Master’s students have been identified, and these will first work as research assistants in the projects and thereafter use data from the RCTs in the PhD and Master’s thesis. The PhD students will benefit from the competencies of qualified researchers in several disciplines and methodological approaches. Also, master’s students will do their Master thesis in this project. \r\nEthical considerations \r\nThe study protocol will be submitted to the Jimma University Institutional Review Board (IRB) and Oromia Regional Health Bureau for assessment and approval. The participants’ names will be anonymized when data are used for research purposes. The study will be conducted in full accordance with the World Medical Association Declaration of Helsinki. Informed consent will be obtained from all participants. \r\nThe included mothers will receive at least the same standard of care as the references group. There are no elements of the intervention posing a health threat to mothers or children. The communication between the TPLs and the mothers will be monitored to ensure that a respectful and empowering counselling approach is used. \r\nCell phone is the technology most accessible to the poor populations, as they are increasingly cheap. Thus, using cell phone communication in  health interventions is a good way of including the most vulnerable and to narrow social gaps in health [25]','<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"color:black\"><a name=\"_ENREF_1\"><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">1.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; UN Inter-agency Group for Child Mortality Estimation, <em>Levels and trends in child mortality</em>. 2013, UNICEF: New York.</span></span></a></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"color:black\"><a name=\"_ENREF_2\"><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">2.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Black, R.E., et al., <em>Maternal and child undernutrition and overweight in low-income and middle-income countries.</em> Lancet, 2013. <strong>382</strong>(9890): p. 427-51.</span></span></a></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"color:black\"><a name=\"_ENREF_3\"><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">3.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; UN Commission on Life-Saving Commodities for Women and Children, <em>Commissioner&#39;s report. </em>2012.</span></span></a></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"color:black\"><a name=\"_ENREF_4\"><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">4.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Chapman, D.J., et al., <em>Breastfeeding peer counseling: from efficacy through scale-up.</em> J Hum Lact, 2010. <strong>26</strong>(3): p. 314-26.</span></span></a></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"color:black\"><a name=\"_ENREF_5\"><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">5.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Sellen D et al. <em>A randomized controlled trial indicates benefits of cell phone based peer counseling to support exclusive breastfeeding in Kenya. IUNS 20th International Congress of Nutrition, Granada (Spain), September 15-20, 2013.</em> in <em>IUNS 20th International Congress of Nutrition</em>. 2013. Granada (Spain).</span></span></a></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"color:black\"><a name=\"_ENREF_6\"><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">6.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Bhutta, Z.A., et al., <em>Evidence-based interventions for improvement of maternal and child nutrition: what can be done and at what cost?</em> Lancet, 2013.</span></span></a><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"> <strong>382</strong>: p 452-477.</span></span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"color:black\"><a name=\"_ENREF_7\"><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">7.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Liu, L., et al., <em>Global, regional, and national causes of child mortality: an updated systematic analysis for 2010 with time trends since 2000.</em> Lancet, 2012. <strong>379</strong>(9832): p. 2151-61.</span></span></a></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"color:black\"><a name=\"_ENREF_8\"><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">8.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; UNICEF, WHO, and World Bank, <em>Levels and trends in child malnutrition. Joint child malnutrition estimates</em>. 2012, United Nations International Children&rsquo;s Fund/World Health Organization/World Bank: New York/Geneva/Washington DC.</span></span></a></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"color:black\"><a name=\"_ENREF_9\"><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">9.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Grantham-McGregor, S., et al., <em>Developmental potential in the first 5 years for children in developing countries.</em> Lancet, 2007. <strong>369</strong>(9555): p. 60-70.</span></span></a></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"color:black\"><a name=\"_ENREF_10\"><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">10.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Adair, L.S., et al., <em>Associations of linear growth and relative weight gain during early life with adult health and human capital in countries of low and middle income: findings from five birth cohort studies.</em> Lancet, 2013. <strong>382</strong>(9891): p. 525-34.</span></span></a></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"color:black\"><a name=\"_ENREF_11\"><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">11.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Chang, S.M., et al., <em>Early childhood stunting and later fine motor abilities.</em> Dev Med Child Neurol, 2010. <strong>52</strong>(9): p. 831-6.</span></span></a></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"color:black\"><a name=\"_ENREF_12\"><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">12.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Cheung, Y.B. and P. Ashorn, <em>Continuation of linear growth failure and its association with cognitive ability are not dependent on initial length-for-age: a longitudinal study from 6 months to 11 years of age.</em> Acta Paediatr, 2010. <strong>99</strong>(11): p. 1719-23.</span></span></a></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"color:black\"><a name=\"_ENREF_13\"><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">13.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Gandhi, M., et al., <em>Height gain during early childhood is an important predictor of schooling and mathematics ability outcomes.</em> Acta Paediatr, 2011. <strong>100</strong>(8): p. 1113-8.</span></span></a></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"color:black\"><a name=\"_ENREF_14\"><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">14.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; WHO/UNICEF, <em>Global strategy on infant and young child feeding</em>. 2003, World Health Organization: Geneva.</span></span></a></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,s