n-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"color:black\"><a name=\"_ENREF_32\"><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">32.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Nguyen, P.H., et al., <em>Program impact pathway analysis of a social franchise model shows potential to improve infant and young child feeding practices in Vietnam.</em> J Nutr, 2014. <strong>144</strong>(10): p. 1627-36.</span></span></a></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"color:black\"><a name=\"_ENREF_33\"><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">33.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Drummond, M., et al., eds., <em>Methods for the Economic Evaluation of Health Care Programmes</em>. Third ed. 2005: Oxford University Press.</span></span></a></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"color:black\"><a name=\"_ENREF_34\"><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">34.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Briggs, A.H., K. Claxton, and M. Sculpher, <em>Decision modelling for health economic evaluation. Handbooks in health economic evaluation</em>. 2006, Oxford: Oxford University Press.</span></span></a></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"color:black\"><a name=\"_ENREF_35\"><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">35.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Claxton, K., <em>Exploring Uncertainty in Cost-Effectiveness Analysis.</em> PharmacoEconomics, 2008. <strong>26</strong>(9): p. 781-798.</span></span></a></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"color:black\"><a name=\"_ENREF_36\"><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">36.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Jacklin, P., et al, <em>Modelling the cost effectiveness of interventions to promote breastfeeding, in NICE Maternal and Child Nutrition Programme 2007</em>. 2007, NICE: London.</span></span></a></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"color:black\"><a name=\"_ENREF_37\"><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">37.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; United Nations, <em>A new global partnership: Eradicate poverty and transform economics through sustainable development. The report of the high level panel of eminent persons on the Post-2015 Development Agenda</em>. 2013, United Nations: New York.</span></span></a></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<ol start=\"38\">\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"color:black\"><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">Central Statistical Agency (CSA) [Ethiopia] and ICF. 2017. 2016 Ethiopia Demographic and Health Survey Key Findings. Addis Ababa, Ethiopia, and Rockville, Maryland, USA. CSA and ICF</span></span></span></span></span></li>\r\n</ol>','To assess the effect of Cell Phone Based Infant Feeding Counselling in Jimma Zone, Ethiopia: a randomized controlled trial',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,2,'Cell phone based,Infant Feeding Counselling ,Malnutrition',10,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),(12,72,9,13,'Ambulance is an essential component of prehospital Emergency medical service (EMS) that used to transport patient and EMS crew, and equipment for patient care. Internal patient compartment layout of the ambulances should facilitate efficient clinical care and ensure the safety of both patients and EMS provider. The recruitment costs of the vehicles also vary based on the level of advancement. Basic Life Support (BLS) ambulance is only capable of simply transporting a patient to health facility and is limited to a stretcher and basic first aid kit for essential first aid, and dominantly used in Ethiopia. In standard life support (SLS) ambulances professionals can do can do intermediate care such as clearing of the airway and support blood circulation. Ensuring a balance between patient care and EMS crew safety is a significant challenge for the EMS service. Evidences have shown that the interior design of ambulances has several faults, which directly affect the quality of life-saving treatment and found to endanger staff in the event of an accident. Hence, proper patient compartment design should consider health, comfort, and performance. Therefore, the aim of this project is to evaluate design challenges with BLS ambulance, to optimize comfort and safety of patient and EMS team during prehospital care provision by remodelling and retrofitting patient compartment in already existing BLS to SLS ambulance using locally available resources and to evaluate its feasibility, among ambulance providers in Jimma city. \r\nTo accomplish this project a total of 220,500 ETB is required; and this will be addressed with the current grant, to be completed in the duration of 6 months.',NULL,'Feasibility of retrofitting Basic patient transporter ambulances to standard life support unit with locally available resources among ambulance providers in Jimma city, Ethiopia','2021-11-18 15:58:13','Present study will identify the design challenges with commonly used basic patient transporter (BLS) ambulances in Ethiopia, and its influence on patient and professional safety. The study will identify possible solutions to design challenges and implementation strategy with locally available resources. Immediate output will be consumed to retrofit two BLS ambulance units to SLS units at study area, and generate further adaptation strategy (evolution) for wider implementation in Ethiopia.','1071a3f7de87605da0920ea159898fde','completed','Prehospital emergency care is an integral part of continuum of emergency care and confers a survival benefit to injured and ill persons[1]. It plays an essential role in emergency medical care by providing on-scene stabilization and safe, rapid transportation by trained ambulance crews to an appropriate health facility [2-4]. Ambulance is an essential component of prehospital Emergency medical service (EMS) that used to transport patient and EMS crew, and equipment for patient care [5].  \r\nEvery day hundreds ambulances on the road and several of patients arrives emergency department of hospitals using ambulance, study from Ghana shows the proportion of ambulance arrival accounts for 14.8% in Ghana[6] and 22.4% in Jimma[7]. As the emergencies of different cases vary, the facilities required in ambulances and the level of performance also vary based on the clinical conditions of the patients [8]. Furthermore, internal patient compartment layout of the ambulances should facilitate efficient clinical care and ensure the safety of both patients and EMS provider [9].The recruitment costs of the vehicles also vary based on the level of advancement [5]. \r\nBased on the level of advancement in patient care and equipment availability in patient compartment ground ambulances mainly categorized into three; including Advanced life support (ALS), Standard life support (SLS) and Basic life support (BLS) ambulances [5, 8]. BLS ambulances are primarily used for basic medical purposes, mainly for non-emergency transportation such as visiting a physician, routine examinations or any lab investigations[5, 8]. It also used transport patients from one health facilities to another or to home[5, 8]. BLS ambulance is only capable of simply transporting a patient to health facility and its facility limited to a stretcher and basic first aid kit for essential first aid[5, 8]. Land Cruiser Hardtop Ambulance Vehicle is a BLS ambulance that dominantly used in public health facilities to transport patients from accident site to health facility and between health facilities in Ethiopia [10, 11]. The ALS ambulances are usually used in transportation critically ill emergency cases, and capable of providing required medical monitoring and ALS care with the help of trained EMS professional[12]. ALS ambulance unit has facilities for monitoring vital signs, for giving drug therapy, IV therapy, oxygen and also cardiac monitoring[12, 13]. SLS ambulances are intermediate level ambulance whose facilities come in between the ALS and BLS ambulances[13]. It usually has those equipment and facility to carry the patient safely into the hospital[12, 13]. In SLS ambulances professionals can do can do clearing of the airway and support blood circulation, though more advanced care are not possible[12, 13].\r\nEnsuring a balance between patient care and EMS crew safety is a significant challenge for the EMS service [9, 14]. Evidences have shown that the interior design of ambulances has several faults, which directly affect the quality of life-saving treatment and found to endanger staff in the event of an accident[15]. As a result, EMS workers face occupational fatality rates that are more than double that of the general population and that comparable to other emergency personnel [16]. Due to the nature of their work, providing critical patient care in rapidly moving ambulances EMS providers also face high injuries [17, 18]. Much of workplace related injuries attributed to the deficient design and layout of patient compartments, which are typically not designed to allow EMS provider to perform their patient care tasks while remaining seated and restrained[14, 19]. \r\n \r\nFigure 1: Traditional ambulance layout (Type I/III) (Left) [9]. Layout of LC Hardtop BLS Ambulance in Ethiopia (Right)[11]\r\nIn EMS, both patient safety and care provider safety are essential requirements to improve safety, and to optimize EMS worker performance in the patient compartment of an ambulance[16]. Hence, future patient compartment design should consider health, comfort, and performance [19]. According to Letendre and Robinson, accessing equipment and patients to be the most physically demanding activities, while caring patient in cardiac arrest in ambulance[20]. To improve the patient´s and providers´ safety, it has been suggested that ambulances should possess the following main compartments which include: (1) utility cabinet capable of storing all the necessary medicines, drugs, and equipment; (2) monitor/defibrillator located on a shelf in the cabinet, near the paramedic’s seat; (3) stretcher, located on the side fastened to the floor of the vehicle, (4) passenger bench a 5-paramedic’s seat, located at the head of the stretcher and close to the monitor/defibrillator[15]. Overall, any ambulance dispatched to provide care should have essential types of equipment that should be used to provide emergency cares that involves.-Airway, ventilation, and oxygenation, circulatory support utilities, infection control, medications and medication delivery, patient packaging, evacuation, and heat-loss prevention miscellaneous items[12, 13].\r\nBLS ambulances which lacked essential types of equipment are dominantly used in Ethiopia[21]. Consequently, ambulances are being used only for patient transportations leaving aside other essential emergency care that would save patient life. Moreover, entire providers ran ambulances because they owned the only vehicles capable of carrying patients horizontally. In addition, there are no standards that address performance, ergonomics or safety in ambulance patient compartments for health facilities when procuring ambulances, in Ethiopia. In addition, there is no study indicating whether design and layout of patient compartment in existing ambulances can allow EMS provider to perform their tasks safely, possess essential compartment and ensure patient safety. Further, there are no evidences showing the applicability and feasibility retrofitting ambulance resources at our hand in order to improve efficiency and to make patient compartments safer for EMS personnel and patients, and that enable the effective delivery of patient care in Ethiopia. Therefore, the aim of this project is to evaluate design challenges with BLS ambulance, to optimize comfort and safety of patient and EMS team during prehospital care provision by remodeling and retrofitting patient compartment in already existing ambulance using locally available resources and to evaluate its feasibility, among ambulance providers in Jimma city.','3.1	Study settings\r\nThe study will be conducted from February 30, to July 30, 2022, among public organizations offering ambulance service in Jimma city. The preliminary assessment of this project will involve all public ambulance service providers and government hospitals owning ambulances. The implementation will be carried out at Aayyoo Ambulance, Jimma Medical Center (JMC). Aayyoo Ambulance is the only professional Emergency Medical Service (EMS) provider in Jimma city. It established under JMC and has been offering prehospital care service since October 2020, in Jimma city. Aayyoo ambulance, the only professional EMS provider in Jimma, possess one ALS, three SLS and seven BLS ambulances to provide 24/7 service in the city[22]. Shenen gibe hospital and Jimma city health office possess 2 BLS ambulances each. Land Cruiser Hardtop (BLS) Ambulances are dominantly used ambulances among public health facilities in Jimma city, to transport patients from accident site to health facility and between health facilities. \r\n3.2	Study design \r\nMixed approach of quantitative and qualitative methods will be employed to evaluate existing facility and the feasibility of solution interventions. The study involves two phases including phase of preliminary assessment and interventions. \r\n3.3	Study population\r\nStudy population involves health care workers working on ambulances (including health professionals and drivers), emergency medicine experts and administrative officials.\r\n3.4	Sample size and sampling technique\r\nAll available health professionals and drivers working on ambulance service, emergency medicine experts and officials administering the ambulance service will be involved in the study using purposive sampling technique.\r\n3.5	Data collection tools and procedures \r\nQualitative data\r\nFocus group discussion (FGD) will be conducted to gain a broader understanding of the issues involved in ambulance safety, from a variety of stakeholder viewpoints. Three FGDs will be conducted to identify existing design challenges and suggestions for the improvement of working the environment within the patient compartments in the BLS ambulances. Each FGD involves 6 to 10 participants incorporating EMS professionals, drivers of ambulance, emergency medicine experts and administrative officials. The FGD will be conducted in Afaan Oromoo and moderated by experienced investigator, audio taped, and supplemented by note taking. Physical observation of ambulance units also will be done to obtain clear picture of patient compartment in two BLS units at Aayyoo ambulance. The observation will be supported by field note taking.  \r\nQuantitative data \r\nTo evaluate the design challenges and safety of patient’s compartment with existing BLS ambulances we adapted structured questionnaire from previous similar published literatures[9, 14-16, 19]. The questionnaire contains two parts, first participant’s biographic data; second part items to evaluate the patient’s compartment in terms of the working environment, ergonomic design and the safety of the crew and patients. This contains 20 questions categorized under five essential design issues that are most important across both safety and functionality of the patient’s compartment; this includes Seating and restraints, Workspace, Storage, Equipment, Communication, and Ingress and egress. The participants will be asked to rate each item using three three-point Likert scale involving agree, neutral and disagree in terms of the safety and functionality points of view. \r\n3.6	Developing Design and implementation strategy\r\nThe findings from quantitative and qualitative data (FGD, and physical observations) will be synthesized to identify priority area for safety and functionality of the patient’s compartment in BLS ambulance, and strategic requirements to retrofit to standard life support unit. Modified Delphi consultation will be used to identify strategic requirements and design modeling to retrofit BLS to standard life support unit. The Delphi process will involve EMS professionals, Emergency medicine experts and automotive experts. This modified Delphi methods is used to facilitate a dialogue and knowledge sharing among such a diverse group of participants. The modified Delphi consultation will involve the following rounds;\r\nRound one: A draft version of needs and priority area with design challenges that identified by aforementioned quantitative and qualitative data will be established, and sent to participants to review and appraise the needs and priority areas until reaching consensus. This would help to assess and identify the collective priorities for the design requirements of patient compartments in ambulances. Furthermore it will identify gaps in current practice, establish consensus on technical issues related to ambulance design, and review and prioritize future design requirements. The findings here will enable the project team to develop a set of needs and requirement for further cycles. \r\nRound two: Requirements of future patient compartment design in terms of working environment, ergonomic design and the safety of the crew and patients will be drafted. Then requirements will be sent to participants for further appraisal, and to make assessment. Participants will be requested to assess the requirements from the safety, functionality, and the combined points of view, as well as to evaluate its applicability with locally available resources and the cycle continues until reaching consensus. The suggested potential topics would include requirements for Seating/restraint and communications, Work environment, General equipment and storage, and Special equipment and storage. Hence, a three-point Likert scale involving Essential, Conditional and Low importance interventions if implemented, as described in the next Table 1. \r\nTable 1: The Likert scale to review and prioritize the need for improvement \r\nScale  	Descriptions  \r\nEssential	•	Would significantly improve patient and EMS crew safety if implemented. \r\n•	Would significantly improve patient care if implemented.\r\nConditional	•	Would improve patient and EMS crew safety if implemented, but not to a significant degree.\r\n•	Would improve patient care if implemented, but not to a significant degree.\r\nLow	•	Could improve patient and EMS crew safety to some degree if implemented, but not an important requirement at this time.\r\n•	Could make patient care somewhat easier if implemented, but not an important requirement at this time.\r\nRound three: Based on selected requirements a set of design concepts that represent three-dimensional graphical models will be developed by participants who are automotive expert. Then modeling of the potential designs for retrofitting will be sent to participants to identify further needs, requirements and suggestions about future ambulance patient compartment modeling. This will be a stage to ensure that identified potential design would satisfy patient needs and professional needs.\r\nRound four: The suggested model for retrofitting will be compared against the national standards requirements for ambulances in Ethiopia and similar literatures. \r\nRound five: Finally, the design model will be developed along implementation strategies, to retrofit patient compartment in the BLS ambulance to SLS ambulance. \r\n3.7	Application of the findings /Interventions/\r\nIntervention will be carried to evaluate the feasibility and applicability of potential model to retrofit patient compartment design in BLS ambulance, that developed by this study. Two BLS ambulances owned by Aayyoo Ambulance dispatch center will be selected and retrofitted using a potential design model developed with aforementioned Delphi process. Implementations of the designed model will be carried-out by accredited Workshop enterprise using requirement specification documents under supervision of investigator team in Jimma city. The retrofitted model ambulance unit will be compared against initial requirement specification documents for accuracy of interventions. The same participants involved in Delphi method will be invited to validate whether a retrofitted model would satisfy patient needs and professional needs. All human and material costs invested per each ambulance unit will be obtained and recorded. \r\n3.8	Data analysis \r\nQuantitatively collected data will be entered and cleaned using Epi-data version 3.1 and exported to SPSS Version 23.0 for statistical analysis. Descriptive statistics, including frequencies and percent will be used to summarize the finding. Qualitatively obtained information including audio records and field notes from FGDs and physical observations will be translated and transcribed. Then translated transcribed text will be imported to AtlasTi 7 to facilitate the coding process. Then a coded data will be organized by sub-themes and triangulated with quantitative data to provide explanations of numerical findings. Data regarding interventions feasibility will be recorded for human and material costs of interventions. Then total intervention costs invested per each ambulance unit will be obtained. The feasibility analysis of the interventions will be done by comparing intervention costs against contemporary market costs to recruit standard ambulance in Ethiopia. Finally, the findings synthesized to generate the standard recommendations document incorporating requirements, validated model and implementation strategy to retrofit patient compartment of BLS ambulance to SLS ambulance unit will be generated. \r\n3.9	Quality Assurance\r\nTools for data collection tools including questionnaire and FGD guides will be translated to local languages (Afan Oromo and Amharic). Data collectors and supervisors will be trained about the objectives of the project and data collection procedures for respective studies. The data collection procedure will be closely supervised by data collection supervisors and investigatory team members. All FGDs will be supplemented by interviewer note taking to prevent loss of information as results of faulty of audio recording instrument. Discussions will be held among investigator team to create common understandings and consensus on how the data codes or categories developed for analysis. Both the forward and backward translations of audio records and field note transcriptions will be done by different individuals to ensure the consistency between original and translated transcribed texts. Double entry a technique and a text-to-speech program read of the data to prevent data entry blunder and to examine data further for mistakes through checking consistency with transcribed texts.\r\n3.8 Ethical Consideration \r\nThe study protocols and procedures will be submitted to Jimma University, Institute of Health IRB to obtain approval and ethical clearance of the study. Official letter of recommendation will be obtained from Institute of Health Research and Postgraduate Coordinating Office, Jimma University. Further, the copy of the approved research proposal will be brought to administrative official of the organization to obtain further recommendation to carry out the study. \r\nPrior to starting actual data collection procedure, the study participants will be explained the purpose of the study to obtain their consents and cooperation to participate in the study. The information sheet will be supplied to every participant’s prior to their enrolment to the study. Further, the participant’s participation forms and roles, length of participations, and rights withdraw from study without any penalty at any time were explained on the information sheet, and  it will be read aloud by the interviewer or participant self-read depend on their preference. Subsequently, FGD participants will be requested for informed oral consents, and as well permission for audio records. Otherwise all participation in the study will be on the behalf of voluntary participation. Participants’ names will not be written on any of data collection formats. Further, no any organization or research participant will be identifiable from any publications and or reports of this study.','<p>[1].&nbsp;&nbsp; &nbsp;Hsia RY, Thind A, Zakariah A, et al. Prehospital and emergency care: Updates from the disease control priorities, version 3 World J Surg. 2015.10.1007/s00268-015-2997-5.<br />\r\n[2].&nbsp;&nbsp; &nbsp;Mould-Millman N-K, Dixon JM, Sefa N, et al. The state of emergency medical services (ems) systems in africa. Prehosp Disaster Med. 2017;32(3):1-11.10.1017/S1049023X17000061.<br />\r\n[3].&nbsp;&nbsp; &nbsp;Stein C, Mould-Millman N-K, Vries SD, et al. Access to out-of-hospital emergency care in africa: Consensus conference recommendations. African Journal of Emergency Medicine. 2016;6:4.http://dx.doi.org/10.1016/j.afjem.2016.08.008.<br />\r\n[4].&nbsp;&nbsp; &nbsp;Mould-Millman N-K, Naidoo R, Vries SD, et al. Afem out-of-hospital emergency care workgroup consensus paper: Advancing out-of-hospital emergency care in africa-advocacy and development. Afr J Emerg Med. 2014;4(2):5.<br />\r\n[5].&nbsp;&nbsp; &nbsp;Gunderson M. Principles of ems system design. In: David C. Cone, Jane H. Brice, Theodore R. Delbridge, et al., editors. Emergency medical services: Clinical practice and systems oversight. Principles of ems system design. II. 3 ed. USA: John Wiley &amp; Sons, Inc.; 2021 p. 16.<br />\r\n[6].&nbsp;&nbsp; &nbsp;Mould-Millman N-K, Rominski S, Oteng R. Ambulance or taxi? High acuity prehospital transports in the ashanti region of ghana. African Journal of Emergency Medicine. 2014;4:5.<br />\r\n[7].&nbsp;&nbsp; &nbsp;Ahmed AA, Ibro SA, Melkamu G, et al. Length of stay in the emergency department and its associated factors at jimma medical center, southwest ethiopia. Open Access Emergency Medicine. 2020;12:8.http://doi.org/10.2147/OAEM.S254239.<br />\r\n[8].&nbsp;&nbsp; &nbsp;Kobusingye OC, Hyder AA, Bishai D, et al. Emergency medical services. In: Jamison DT BJ, Measham AR, editor. Disease control priorities in developing countries: World Bank; 2006.<br />\r\n[9].&nbsp;&nbsp; &nbsp;Deogratias Kibira, Y Tina Lee, Jennifer Marshall, et al. Simulation-based design concept evaluation for ambulance patient compartments. Simulation: Transactions of the Society for Modeling and Simulation International. 2015;91(8):24.https://10.1177/0037549715592716.<br />\r\n[10].&nbsp;&nbsp; &nbsp;Ambulance manufacturer in ethiopia Dazzle; 2021 [cited 2021 13/11]. Available from: https://dazzleuae.com/ambulance-manufacturer-in-ethiopia/.<br />\r\n[11].&nbsp;&nbsp; &nbsp;Land cruiser 78 hardtop ambulance: Toyota Gibraltar Stockholdings Ltd; 2021 [cited 2021 13/11]. Available from: https://www.toyota-gib.com/eng/models/heavy-duty-utility-type/ambulance/land-cruiser-78-hardtop-ambulance-HZJ78-RJMRS-A3.html.<br />\r\n[12].&nbsp;&nbsp; &nbsp;Lyng J, Adelgais K, Alter R, et al. Recommended essential equipment for basic life support and advanced life support ground ambulances 2020: A joint position statement. Pediatrics. 2021;147(6).10.1542/peds.2021-051508.<br />\r\n[13].&nbsp;&nbsp; &nbsp;Anon. Equipment for ground ambulances prehospital emergency care 2014.<br />\r\n[14].&nbsp;&nbsp; &nbsp;Avery L, Jacobs A, Moore J, et al. Ambulance patient compartment human factors design guidebook. 2014.<br />\r\n[15].&nbsp;&nbsp; &nbsp;Byran E, Gilad IJIjoos, ergonomics. Design considerations to enhance the safety of patient compartments in ambulance transporters. 2012;18(2):221-31.<br />\r\n[16].&nbsp;&nbsp; &nbsp;Dadfarnia M, Lee YT, Kibira D, et al. Requirements analysis for safer ambulance patient compartments. 2013;16:601-10.<br />\r\n[17].&nbsp;&nbsp; &nbsp;Green JD, Ammons DE, Isaacs AJ, et al., editors. Creating a safe work environment for emergency medical service workers. ASSE professional development conference and exhibition; 2008: OnePetro.<br />\r\n[18].&nbsp;&nbsp; &nbsp;Maguire BJ, Smith S. Injuries and fatalities among emergency medical technicians and paramedics in the united states. Prehospital and disaster medicine. 2013;28(4):376-82.10.1017/s1049023x13003555.<br />\r\n[19].&nbsp;&nbsp; &nbsp;Ferreira J, Hignett SJAe. Reviewing ambulance design for clinical efficiency and paramedic safety. 2005;36(1):97-105.<br />\r\n[20].&nbsp;&nbsp; &nbsp;Letendre J, Robinson D. Evaluation of paramedic&#39;s tasks and equipment to control the risk of musculoskeletal injury. CUPE Internal Report, 2000.<br />\r\n[21].&nbsp;&nbsp; &nbsp;EMA. Ethiopia&rsquo;s free rural ambulance service &lsquo;halves&rsquo; pregnancy-related deaths Addis Ababa, Ethiopia: Ethiopian Midwifery Association; 2019 [cited 2019 Agust, 01]. Available from: http://midwives.org.et/203?language=am.<br />\r\n[22].&nbsp;&nbsp; &nbsp;Jimma aayyoo ambulance Jimma: Jimma University; 2021 [updated December 08, 2021; cited 2021 October 10, 2021]. 1:[Official website of Aayyoo Ambulance]. Available from: https://www.ayo.ju.edu.et.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>&nbsp;</p>','The main goal of this study is to explore design challenges to basic patient transporter ambulance and feasibility of redesigning to standard life support ambulance with locally available resources in Jimma city, Ethiopia.',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,2,'Emergency Medical Service,Prehospital care,Ambulance',10,'<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:10pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt\">The project will take </span><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt\">6 months to be accomplished, February 2021 to July 2021. </span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<table cellspacing=\"0\" class=\"Table\" style=\"border-collapse:collapse; margin-left:-50px; width:80.02%\">\r\n	<tbody>\r\n		<tr>\r\n			<td colspan=\"10\" style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:1px solid black; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:1px solid black; height:19px; vertical-align:bottom; width:100%\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:center\"><span style=\"font-size:10pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><strong><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt\">Timeline</span></strong></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n		</tr>\r\n		<tr>\r\n			<td rowspan=\"2\" style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:1px solid black; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:19px; vertical-align:bottom; width:24%\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:center\"><span style=\"font-size:10pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><strong><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt\">Activities </span></strong></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td colspan=\"9\" style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:19px; vertical-align:bottom; width:76%\">\r