e; height:16px; vertical-align:bottom; width:7%\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\">&nbsp;</p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:16px; vertical-align:bottom; width:8%\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\">&nbsp;</p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:16px; vertical-align:bottom; width:8%\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\">&nbsp;</p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:16px; vertical-align:bottom; width:7%\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\">&nbsp;</p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:16px; vertical-align:bottom; width:8%\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\">&nbsp;</p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:16px; vertical-align:bottom; width:7%\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\">&nbsp;</p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:16px; vertical-align:bottom; width:6%\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\">&nbsp;</p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:16px; vertical-align:bottom; width:8%\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\">&nbsp;</p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:16px; vertical-align:bottom; width:13%\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\">&nbsp;</p>\r\n			</td>\r\n		</tr>\r\n		<tr>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:1px solid black; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:19px; vertical-align:bottom; width:24%\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:10pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt\">Write Up the manuscript for publication</span></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:19px; vertical-align:bottom; width:7%\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:10pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">&nbsp;</span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:19px; vertical-align:bottom; width:8%\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:10pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">&nbsp;</span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:19px; vertical-align:bottom; width:8%\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:10pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">&nbsp;</span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:19px; vertical-align:bottom; width:7%\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:10pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">&nbsp;</span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:19px; vertical-align:bottom; width:8%\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:10pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">&nbsp;</span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:19px; vertical-align:bottom; width:7%\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:10pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">&nbsp;</span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:19px; vertical-align:bottom; width:6%\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:10pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">&nbsp;</span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:19px; vertical-align:bottom; width:8%\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:10pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">&nbsp;</span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:19px; vertical-align:bottom; width:13%\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:10pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">&nbsp;</span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n		</tr>\r\n	</tbody>\r\n</table>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\">&nbsp;</p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\">&nbsp;</p>',1,NULL,NULL,NULL),(13,60,9,17,'Abstract / Executive summary\r\n\r\nBackground: HIV/AIDS has defined its fourth decade in the last June 2021 while being a leading public heath challenge to human kind. The lion share of this burden is in Sub-Saharan Africa where the disease is daunting a massive impact on health outcomes and life expectancy. In Ethiopia, currently, more than 600,000 peoples are estimated to be living with HIV. More than sixty percent of adults living with HIV in Ethiopia are estimated to be Sero-discordant. HIV transmission within Sero-discordant couples can contribute substantially to the overall burden of disease and challenging the reduction of new HIV infection strategies nationally however, the prevalence rate   sero-dicordance and associated challenges of these couples is not well investigated particularly in our study area Jimma Zone.\r\nObjective: To assess Prevalence, challenges, Coping Strategies, and social support of Sero-discordant couples in Jimma Zone, Southwest, Ethiopia.\r\n\r\nMethods: A facility-based cross-sectional study design using quantitative and qualitative method of data collection will be conducted from November 20, 2021, to December 20, 2022. The data will be collected through face to face interviews using English Version (translated to local language) structured and pretested questionnaire. The data will be cleaned and checked for completeness and consistency. The data generated will be analyzed using Statistical Package for Social (SPSS version 25). Data exploration will be done before running the statistical analysis. Descriptive statistics will be used to summarize the data. Multivariate logistic regression will be used to identify factors associated with dependent variables separately. Adjusted odds ratio and 95% CI will respectively be used to measure the strength of associations. In the final model, the p-value and CI for an OR at 5% level of significance will be used to declare statistical significance. Hosmer and Lemeshow\'s test of significance will be used to test model fitness.','To identify the major(social, psychological, sexual) challenges for HIV positive sero-discordant couples 3.	To assess coping strategies of HIV positive Sero-discordantcoupleswithin the familie','Prevalence rate of HIV positive Sero-discordance,associated Challenges, Coping strategies and social support among Clients on HIV/AIDS care and treatment services in Jimma Zone, Southwest Ethiopia','2021-11-18 12:22:23','Determine the prevalence rate of  sero-discordant couples currently on the long term HIV/AIDS care and treatment services in the Jimma zone public health facilities -this will help to design the appropriate services tailored to theses clients and help as base line  to plan and  forecast the future prevalence of HIV among these group \r\n identify the major social, psychological, sexual and clinical   challenges for HIV positive sero-discordant couples:so that care treatment services will  be designed in a way that consider or prevent these major challenges','8c24f39c1be810bb71293390822c44a0','completed','Chapter one: Background and Rationale of the study \r\nHIV/AIDS has defined its fourth decade in the last June 2021 while being a leading public heath challenge to human kind .Human immunodeficiency virus (HIV) infection remains the leading cause of morbidity and mortality throughout the world.(1).HIV /AIDS continues to be a major global health priority despite the promising progress that has been made so far in preventing the disease(2).Globally more than 78 million people were infected and more than 36 million were dead due to HIV/AIDS so far. At the end of 2020, more than 38 million people were living with HIV, about 1.5 million [1.0 million–2.0 million] people worldwide newly acquired HIV infection in the same year which includes sero-discordant people. Newly infection declined by 23% when compared with 2010. AIDS-related deaths have also been reduced by 60% since the peak in 2004. In  the year 2020, 680 000 [480 000–1.0 million] people died from HIV-related causes globally compared to 1.1 million in 2010(3).\r\n HIV/AIDS is one of the world’s most fatal infectious diseases – particularly across Sub-Saharan Africa where the disease has had a massive impact on health outcomes and life expectancy (4).Sub Saharan African has been the epicentre since the existence of HIV/AIDS by bearing more than 60% of the global burden.(5).As one of the sub-Saharan countries, the case in Ethiopia is not different, Ethiopia has a generalized HIV epidemic with 590,000 PLWH aged 15 or older, for an estimated HIV prevalence among adults of 1.1%, although estimates vary substantially across the country(6). and the annual HIV transmission from the infected partner to negative partner is high; this is because of the high prevalence of a discordant couple (7).\r\nCouples are said to be HIV-affected when either one or both partners are infected with HIV. Partners are categorized as HIV-concordant when both partners are HIV infected(8). HIV discordance refers to a situation where one of the partners is HIV positive while the other is HIV negative. Hard discordance refers to a situation where the male is the positive partner while soft discordance is a situation where the female is the positive partner(9). The majority of new infections of HIV are through the heterosexual transmission of HIV among the serodiscordant(10).A recent study suggests that a large proportion of new HIV infections in countries with mature epidemics occur within discordant partnerships, making discordance a major contributor to the spread of HIV in Africa(11).HIV transmission within serodiscordant couples can contribute substantially to the overall burden of disease. In high prevalence areas like sub-Saharan Africa, approximately half of HIV-positive persons have negative partners, and in low prevalence settings, this proportion may be as high as 75%(12, 13). The risk of HIV transmission from seropositive partner to negative partner is very high among discordant couples who do not take protective measures(14). Therefore, most discordant couples become concordant positive eventually.\r\nThis population constitutes the target group for HIV prevention strategies.One of the difficulties with HIV-discordant couples is that the increased risk for infection comes from within stable relationships rather than from outside sexual partners. It is important to provide couples with information that being in a monogamous stable relationship does not mean that partners are not at risk for HIV transmission(15). Preventing new HIV infections through identification of groups at high risk, supplemented by provision of care and support for infected people, has been a widely accepted intervention approach in fighting HIV and AIDS(16).\r\nEthiopia have a similar HIV/AIDS epidemic burden, epidemiologic patterns, mode of transmission like other sub-Saharan Africa. The current Ethiopian national HIV prevalence is estimated to be 0.9%, urban are more affected than rural areas (2.9% versus 0.4%) while females are twice as affected as male (1.2% versus 0.6%). Out of the 11 regional states and city administrations, seven have HIV prevalence of 1% and above. One of the main challenges contributing to new HIV incidence is considerable proportion of HIV  Sero discordance couples    among the major challenges, particularly couples where one person is living with HIV and the other is HIV-negative, the HIV-negative partner is at continuous risk of infection (10% annual risk)(17). \r\nSerodiscordantencounter is still the dominant mode of HIV transmission in Ethiopia. Although the HIV/AIDS prevalence is higher among females, there is a high disparity between males and females to access HIV testing, treatment, and care services, where males are less likely to access HIV/AIDS treatment care than females.\r\nHowever Couples may have poor understanding of what it means to be serodiscordant, Misconceptions are a major barrier to managing and stopping the spread of AIDS, and they cause a negative attitude towards people stricken by this serious disease that might result in grave harm to their physical and emotional spirit(18).\r\nDespite of these facts, many people in stable relationships do not know the HIV status of their partner, and many people with a HIV - positive partner do not know their own status. Only 40% of HIV-positive individuals worldwide know their outcome and approximately 50% of people living with HIV in relationships are estimated to be part of discordant couples, but such discordant couples’ needs in the world are not readily available. According to Meta-analysis study carried out to determine the prevalence of HIV discordance in high HIV burden 10 African countries from 16296 participants showed a 23.52% point prevalence of HIV discordance. By the same study, the lowest and highest prevalence was observed with higher variation in the prevalence of discordance across different African countries 16.5% in northern Tanzania and 49% in SSA. This could contribute to the high incidence of Sero conversion particularly in Ethiopia where disclosures status is expected to be very law(19)\r\n However, with an estimated 669,000 People living with HIV (PLHIV) and majority sero-discordant , of which 44,229 are <15 years of age,  HIV/AIDS  is still  a heavy burden on the country (20).\r\nThe introduction of antiretroviral treatment (ART) has dramatically improved the survival of people living with HIV. Expansion of HIV care and treatment programs has been a priority for governments in high burden countries including Ethiopia(21). Since the introduction of antiretroviral treatment, Ethiopia has achieved considerable success in scaling up HIV care and treatment services. These accomplishments were made through a multi-pronged national strategy based on decentralization of services, provision of free ART, involvement of lay peer educators and task shifting to nurse-provided care, as well as collaboration with partner organizations(20). \r\nAs of the end of 2019, 25.4 million people with HIV (67%) were accessing antiretroviral therapy (ART) globally. That means 12.6 million people are still waiting. The HIV treatment access is the key to the global effort to end AIDS as a public health threat. People with HIV who are aware of their status, take ART daily as prescribed, keep an undetectable viral load can live long and healthy lives (3).\r\nThe success of HIV Care and treatment as program requires a life time commitment of the patients who are taking ART to be remaining on treatment and followed up regularly while critically considering their partner HIV status. Clients with sero-discordant status requires strong and close follow up which takes into account their challenges and copying mechanism.\r\nIn this regard the health system is expected to address these groups based on practical research findings which will guide how to deal with sero-discordant couples without exposing each other and lead their health partnership by addressing comprehensively their personal, psychological, social and other challenges. So that, the heath system will achieve its vision of seeing AIDS free generation in 2030 by reducing new HIV infections and elimination of mother to child transmission of HIV.\r\nThe care and treatment services for HIV/AIDS clients have been improving from time to time in terms of access, coverage and quality of care. However there are still remaining challenges that are hindering the success of the services of which the client related challenges like being sero-discordant status which are not well studied particularly in Jimma Zone. A conservative estimate of about 60% of new HIV infections occur in HIV sero-discordant couples as 30% of married HIV positives have negative spouse. Although HIV-discordant couples are at 10% annual risk of acquiring HIV infection and large number of new HIV infections occur in stable partnerships, most HIV prevention programs focus on reducing casual sexual partners, condom use, and increasing fidelity among married partners.Hence this study will try to determine the prevalence rate those sero discordant and identify their social, psychological, , fertility ,and clinical  challenges  of these couples. \r\nThe finding primarily will help the Zone health department and regional health bureau to design sero discordant related strategies that will contribute to the care and treatment success of the HIV/AIDS services.\r\nIt will also help as base line for other national strategies which will focus on these sero-discordant couple groups\r\nconceptual framework considers :\r\n1 client personal and demographics factors\r\n2.copying strategy factors \r\n3.social support factors \r\n3.health facilities services related factors -then at the center sero-discordant \r\nthe figure is included in the attached mother document of this proposal','Chapter Three: Methods/Approaches \r\n3.1 Study area and period  \r\nThe study will be conducted in public health centers and hospitals of Jimma zone from December 20, 2022 to March 30, 2022. Jimma Zone is the largest zone Oromia regional state. According to the new national and regional HIV related estimates for 2020, the estimated PLHIV in the zone is 3742. However, currently, there are 2992 peoples are on ART services at the end of first quarter of 2014 Ethiopian fiscal year. Currently sixteen public health centers and three hospitals are actively providing ART services in the Zone.\r\nThe study will be conducted in public health centers and hospitals of Jimma zone from November 20, 2021, to December 20, 2022. Jimma Zone is the largest and one of the zones that is hit by HIV pandemic as of other zones in the region and nation. It has boundaries North– West Showa, South -   SNNP, West - East Wollega and Iluababor, East– South West Showa and SNNP and has Area = 199,316.18 KM2 it is 354 kms far from capital city Addis Ababa. Jimma zone has various public health facilities including public hospitals, health centers, and health posts.  \r\n3.2 Study design: Facility based cross-sectional study design which will use quantitative and qualitative methods of data collection will be implemented.\r\n3.3 Population\r\n3.3.1 Source population\r\nAll HIV patients who (residing at least 6 months) living in Jimma zone will be included in the sample during the data collection period.\r\n3.3.2 Study population\r\nAll HIV-positive patients who are on ART during the study period. \r\n3.3.3 Study unit\r\nThe study unit of this study will be Sero- discordant couples who are receiving AR services on follow-up-care in the zone \r\n3.4 Eligibility criteria\r\n3.4.1 Inclusion Criteria\r\nParticipants who are reproductive age groups (>18 years old), HIV Positive couples receiving ART, and those living in the zone (>6 months) will be included in the study.\r\n3.4.2 Exclusion criteria\r\nThose who are under the age of 18, seriously sick clients, and Sero-positive concordant will be excluded from the study.\r\n3.5 Sample Size Determination and Sampling Procedures\r\n3.5.1 Sample Size Determination\r\nThe sample size (n) required for the study will be calculated using the formula to estimate a single population proportion formula by considering the following assumptions. Zα/2=critical value for normal distribution at 95% confidence level which equals 1.96 (Z value at alpha=0.05),d=margin of error 0.05, and P= 50 % because estimation proportion of challenges, Coping Strategies, and social support among HIV positive Couples attending ART is unknown, design effect 1.5. Based on the formula of the sample size, the representative sample size will be 384. Then the sample size will be multiplied by design effect (384×1.5=576) and a 10% none respondent rate will be added to 576. The final sample size will be 634.\r\n3.5.2 Sampling Procedure\r\nIn Jimma zone, there are 18 districts (Weredas), for the present study the districts will be obtained from Jimma zone health office; the representative 4 woredas (4districts) will be selected using purposeful sampling techniques, based on their annual report of HIV/AIDS prevalence.After getting district the number of public health facilities (public hospitals, health centers, and health post)with their ART service will be obtained from each district health office. Then our sample size will be allocated proportionally for public hospitals, health centers, and health posts based on their ART service capacity (figure-1).\r\n3.6 Study Variables\r\n3.6.1 Dependent Variables \r\n•	Sero-positive couples  \r\n3.6.2 Independent Variables \r\n•	Socio-demographicand related factors:Age, Sex, Religion, Residences, Marital status, Educational status, living condition, income, Employment \r\n•	PsychologicalFactors:Perceived stigma, coping skills, social support, personal behavior \r\nCoping strategies,Adherence status,clinical condition,Adherence status\r\n•	Health facility-related factors:Availability of ART services, availability of health facilities, utilization of health services, quality of ART services\r\n3.7 Data Collection Tools and Techniques\r\n3.7.1 Questionnaire\r\nData will be   collected with a structured questionnaire and will administer by face-to-face interviews. Necessary medical data were reviewed from patient records, the questionnaire will be adapted from tools developed by WHO training material forcouples HIV testing and counselling including antiretroviral therapy for treatment and prevention in Sero-discordant couple(22, 23),stepwise assessment tools which will be used to collect information on HIV stigma scales (24), coping strategies for dealing with HIV AIDS stigma(25), and developed after an extensive review of the literature and similar study tools(26-29)will be adapted carefully. \r\n3.7.2 Data Collection Procedure\r\nThe data will be collected in face-to-face interviews by well-trained and experienced data collectors. During face-to-face interviews, appropriate distances will be kept between data collectors and interviewees; personal protective equipment (PPE) such as facemasks and gloves will be worn by all data collectors and participants, and alcohol and sanitizers will be used. \r\n3.8 Data Quality Assurances\r\nConceptual and operational definitions of terms will be used according to the objective of the study. The questioner will prepare in English and translate to dominant local languages Amharic and Afan Oromo and back to English by two different language expertise.  Training will be given to data collectors. Facilitators and supervisors will be assigned to control and guide the data collection and sample collection process, and by so doing it will increase the chances of consistency in data collection.\r\n3.9 Data Processing and Analysis \r\nAfter collecting data, it will be checked for completeness and consistency. Data will be entered into computer using the EpiData manager and exported to SPSS version 25 for statistical analysis. Data exploration will be done before running the statistical analysis. Descriptive statistics will be used to summarize the data. Multivariable logistic regression will be used to identify factors associated with dependent variables separately. Adjusted odds ratio and 95% CI will respectively be used to measure the strength of associations. In the final model, the p-value and CI for aOR at 5% level of significance will be used to declare statistical significance. Hosmer and Lemeshow\'s test of significance will be used to test model fitness.  \r\n3.	Research Project Teams: \r\nThis study will be conducted by multidisciplinary professionals, each research project team will have their responsibility to achieve the goal of the research and be enthusiastic to accomplish the vision of the University.\r\n4.	Capacity Building:\r\nFour post-graduate students will participate in the project for the preparation of the final year graduation paper.\r\n5.	Ethical Consideration:\r\nEthical clearances will be obtained from Jimma University IRB/committee, concerned administrative offices will be communicated with a formal letter and written consent will be obtained from study participants. Findings will be communicated in aggregated form and individual information will be kept confidential.\r\n7. Plan for dissemination and ensuring utilization of findings\r\nThe research result report will be submitted to the Institute of Health research and innovation office. The result will be communicated to Oromia regional state, HIV/AIDS  coordination office Jimma zone. Efforts will be done to disseminate the result through poster, seminar presentations, and publishing on reputable journals.','<p><strong>REFERENCES</strong></p>\r\n\r\n<p>1. Girum T, Wasie A, Worku AJBid. Trend of HIV/AIDS for the last 26 years and predicting achievement of the 90&ndash;90-90 HIV prevention targets by 2020 in Ethiopia: a time series analysis. 2018;18(1):1-10. 2. Hailemariam TG, Kassie GM, Sisay MMJBPH. Sexual life and fertility desire in long-term HIV serodiscordant couples in Addis Ababa, Ethiopia: a grounded theory study. 2012;12(1):1-12. 3. Organization WH. Global progress report on HIV, viral hepatitis and sexually transmitted infections, 2021: accountability for the global health sector strategies 2016&ndash;2021: actions for impact: web annex 2: data methods. 2021. 4. Mann J. AIDS in the world II: global dimensions, social roots, and responses: Oxford University Press on Demand; 1996. 5. Dellar RC, Dlamini S, Karim QAJJotIAS. Adolescent girls and young women: key populations for HIV epidemic control. 2015;18:19408. 6. Parcesepe A, Tymejczyk O, Remien R, Gadisa T, Kulkarni SG, Hoffman S, et al. HIV-related stigma, social support, and psychological distress among individuals initiating ART in Ethiopia. 2018;22(12):3815-25. 7. Awad SF, Chemaitelly H, Abu-Raddad LJJIJoID. Estimating the annual risk of HIV transmission within HIV sero-discordant couples in sub-Saharan Africa. 2018;66:131-4. 8. Mehra B, Bhalla P, Rawat D, Kishore JJIjoph. A study of HIV-concordant and-discordant couples attending voluntary counselling and testing services at a tertiary care center in North India. 2015;59(4):306. 9. Nnebue C, Anaekwe A, Anaekwe CJEjohs. Sociodemographic Correlates of HIV Discordant and Concordant Couples in Anambra State, Nigeria. 2017;27(4):363-72. 10. Buchacz K, van der Straten A, Saul J, Shiboski SC, Gomez CA, Padian NJJoaids. Sociodemographic, behavioral, and clinical correlates of inconsistent condom use in HIV-serodiscordant heterosexual couples. 2001;28(3):289-97. 11. Guthrie BL, de Bruyn G, Farquhar CJCHr. HIV-1-discordant couples in sub-Saharan Africa: explanations and implications for high rates of discordancy. 2007;5(4):416-29. 12. Chemaitelly H, Cremin I, Shelton J, Hallett TB, Abu-Raddad LJJSti. Distinct HIV discordancy patterns by epidemic size in stable sexual partnerships in sub-Saharan Africa. 2012;88(1):51-7. 13. Muessig KE, Cohen MSJCHAR. Advances in HIV prevention for serodiscordant couples. 2014;11(4):434-46. 14. Bantigen K, Kitaw L, Negeri H, Kebede M, Wassie A, Bishaw K, et al. Rate of HIV Seroconversion Among Seronegative Male Partners Living with HIV Positive Women in Addis Ababa, Ethiopia, 2019: A Retrospective Cohort Study. 2021;13:125. 15. Tadesse MJBrn. Assessment of HIV discordance and associated risk factors among couples receiving HIV test in Dilla, Ethiopia. 2014;7(1):1-12. 16. De Walque DJP, review d. Sero‐discordant couples in five African countries: implications for prevention strategies. 2007;33(3):501-23. 17. Kibret GD, Ferede A, Leshargie CT, Wagnew F, Ketema DB, Alebel AJIdop. Trends and spatial distributions of HIV prevalence in Ethiopia. 2019;8(1):1-9. 18. Seid A, Ahmed MJHA. What are the Determinants of Misconception About HIV Transmission Among Ever-Married Women in Ethiopia? 2020;12:441. 19. Gelfew M, Desta M, Mengist B, Amaha H, Hailie D, Bewket BJIJoWsHC. Prevalence of HIV sero_discordance among couples in Sub Saharan Africa, 2019, systematic review and Meta analysis. 2020;5(1):18-24. 20. Ethiopia FMoH. National Consolidated Guidelines for Comprehensive Hiv Prevention, Care and Treatment. FMOH Ethiopia Addis Ababa; 2018. 21. WHO WHOJG. UNAIDS: Global Report: UNAIDS report on the global AIDS epidemic. 2010;10. 22. World Health Organization %J Geneva SWDPS. Couples HIV testing and counselling including antiretroviral therapy for treatment and prevention in serodiscordant couples: recommendations for a public health approach. 2012. 23. Organization WH. Interim WHO clinical staging of HVI/AIDS and HIV/AIDS case definitions for surveillance: African Region. World Health Organization; 2005. 24. Reinius M, Wettergren L, Wiklander M, Svedhem V, Ekstr&ouml;m AM, Eriksson LEJH, et al. Development of a 12-item short version of the HIV stigma scale. 2017;15(1):1-9. 25. Brown L, Macintyre K, Trujillo LJAe, prevention. Interventions to reduce HIV/AIDS stigma: what have we learned? 2003;15(1):49-69. 26. Gitonga MM, Ballidawa J, Ndege S. Challenges and coping strategies among couples of mixed HIV status presenting at a large comprehensive care centre in Eldoret, Kenya. 2012. 27. Rispel L, Cloete A, Metcalf C, Moody K. Exploring coping strategies and life choices made by HIV-discordant couples in long-term relationships: insights from South Africa, Tanzania and the Ukraine. 2009. 28. Beckerman NLJJoHA, Services S. Serodiscordant couples in the age of combination therapies: challenges and coping patterns. 2002;1(3):67-80. 29. Cherayi SJL. Life of discordant couples living with HIV in Puducherry, India: Psychological distress and coping strategies. 2013;1.</p>','To assess Prevalence, challenges, Coping Strategies, and social support of Sero-discordant couples in Jimma Zone, Southwest, Ethiopia',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,2,'Sero-discordant couples, Jimma Zone, ART patients, coping strategies, challenges',10,'<p>Key milestones(Activities) Months (November 20, 2021, to May 27, 2022) Responsible bodies S. No. 02 04 06 08 10 12 1 Topic selection PI, Co-investigators 2 Proposal writing PI, Co-investigators 3 proposal submission PI, Co-investigators 4 Proposal defense and Comment correction and second draft submission PI, Co-investigators 5 Train data collector and data collection PI, Co-investigators, and Data collectors 6 Data cleaning and entry PI, Co-investigators 7 Data analysis and report writing PI, Co-investigators 8 Report submission PI, Co-investigators</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),(14,97,9,17,'Background: The COVID-19 pandemic, which was first reported in Wuhan, Hubei Province of China in December 2019 has become a global health disaster. According to Federal Ministry of Health (FMOH), in Ethiopia, the first case was confirmed on March 13, 2020. Currently, in Ethiopia more than 367,857 COVID-19 cases were recorded, among these more than 6,572 patients were died due to COVID-19. Thus, reducing COVID-19 morbidity and mortality through enhancing the practice of COVID-19 preventive measures are the key. In Ethiopia the COVID-19 preventive and control measures are not strictly applicable, in addition the communities are negligence in the prevention and control measures of COVID-19 from time to time. Hence this research will be used as a baseline for the practice of COVID-19 preventive measures, its associated factors and acceptance level of vaccine in Jimma town.\r\nObjective: The main aim of this finding is to assess the current practice of COVID-19 preventive measures, its associated factors and the acceptance level of the vaccine among residents of Jimma town. \r\nMethods: A cross-sectional study will be carried out from December 30- 2021, January 30, 2022 in Jimma town. The outcome variable for this finding will be three key COVID-19 preventive measures such as wearing of face mask, handwashing with soap and water or with alcohol-based hand sanitizer, and social distancing which are binary outcome, hence three multivariable logistic regression analysis will be implemented and data will be analyzed using SPSS version 23 software.  \r\nExpected Outcome: To determine the current practice of COVID-19 preventive measures are based on COVID-19 protocols among the residents of Jimma town. To identify the factors significant associated with the practice of COVID-19 preventive measures among the residents in Jimma town and proceeding the results of this finding prepare well-designed policy brief.\r\nWork plan and Budget: This project will be implemented with a maximum of one year from December 30, 2021 to November 30, 2022 with a total budget of 177, 948 (one hundred seventy-seven thousand and nine hundred forty-eight) birr will be coasted.',NULL,'Current practice of COVID-19 preventive measures, associated factors and the acceptance level of vaccine among residents of Jimma town, Southwest Ethiopia: a community-based study',NULL,'5. Research Outcome/Output\r\n•	To know the current practice of COVID-19 preventive measures among the residents in Jimma town. \r\n•	To identify the factors significantly associated with the practice of COVID-19 preventive measures among the residents in Jimma town and proceeding the results of this finding prepare well-designed policy brief.','3cf6a47ed760c4b2b98f467af09a126b','completed','Background \r\nThe COVID-19 pandemic, which was first reported in Wuhan, Hubei Province of China in December 2019 has become a global health crisis [1,2]. The pandemic has led to increased COVID-19 morbidity and mortality [3]. The pandemic has also had devastating effects on the world’s economy with collapse of many businesses and loss of many jobs [5,6]. The educational sector has also been disrupted, with some schools adopting to new ways of teaching and learning [7]. \r\nCOVID-19 is a disease caused by a novel coronavirus named SARS-CoV-2 [8,9]. “SARS-CoV-2 is a RNA virus, which is characterized by symptoms such as fever or chills, cough, difficulty breathing, fatigue, muscle or body aches, headache, new loss of taste or smell, sore throat, congestion or runny nose, nausea or vomiting, and diarrhea” [8,10]. Development of these symptoms may appear 2–14 days after exposure to the virus [8,9]. Coronavirus can be transmitted via animal-to-human and human-to-human interactions [9], Whereas human-to-human transmission routes include droplet inhalation, coughing, and sneezing, contact transmission modes include feco-oral, nasal, and eye mucous membrane contacts [11–13]. \r\n Practicing preventive measures such as handwashing with soap and water, wearing of face mask, social distancing, covering of the mouth and nose when coughing, and avoiding touching of the face can prevent transmission of COVID19 infection [14].\r\nThe federal ministry of health confirmed the first case of COVID-19 in Ethiopia was reported on March 13, 2020. According to worldometers, in Ethiopia a total of 367,857  COVID-19 cases were recorded, among these 6,572 patients were died. In response to the global pandemic, the government of Ethiopia adopted several control strategies to reduce the morbidity and mortality associated with COVID-19.\r\n“Key among these strategies included testing, tracing, and treatment (“3 Ts”) approach; lockdowns in some parts of the country; and ensuring that all Ethiopians practice COVID-19 preventive measures” [16]. In Ethiopia the COVID-19 preventive and control measures are not strictly applicable, in addition the communities are negligence in the prevention and control measures of COVID-19 from time to time. Hence this research will be used as a baseline for the practice of COVID-19 preventive measures and its associated factors in Jimma town.\r\n1.2 Rationale \r\nThe practice of COVID-19 preventive measures plays a great role for the mitigation of the pandemic. For better practice of the preventive measures of the pandemic, institutional as well as individual efforts are the backbone to control the spread of COVID-19. \r\nIn Jimma zone, many stack holders like Jimma university, Jimma zone health bureau, Jimma town administrative, Jimma Emergency Operation Center and others are playing their roles in the mitigation of COVID-19 pandemic. The stack holders mainly address the activities based on international COVID-19 protocol among these awareness creation on the pandemic like handwashing, keeping the social distancing, mask wearing and giving the successive training on COVID-19.\r\n Hence, this research will be conducted to answer whether the practice of COVID-19 preventive measures are based on COVID-19 protocol among the residents of Jimma town and the factors associated with the practice of COVID-19 preventive measures in Jimma town.','2. Methods and Materials \r\n2.1 Study design and settings\r\nA cross-sectional study will be conducted via structured questionnaire from December 30, 2021 to January 30, 2022 in Jimma town. Jimma town is the largest city in southwestern Oromia Region, Ethiopia. It has a latitude and longitude of 7°40′N 36°50′E. \r\nSource Population:  All residents of Jimma town are the source population for this study. \r\nStudy Population: residents who are randomly selected will be the study population for this study.  \r\nStudy Unit: Individuals who reside in Jimma town and fulfill the inclusion criteria. \r\nInclusion and Exclusion criteria: Residents who live in the kebele for more than six months will be included in the study. \r\n2.2 Sample size calculation and Sampling procedures \r\n2.2.1 Sample size calculation \r\nThe minimum sample size required for the study will be estimated based on the assumption that 50% of the resident will observe the practice of COVID-19 preventive measures, using a 5% margin of error with a 95% CI, a minimum sample size will be calculated using single proportion formula:\r\nn=(〖〖(z〗_(α⁄2)))〗^2 p(1-p))/d^2 ,\r\nWhere, \r\nn is the minimum sample size,\r\np is the proportion of the residents will observe the practice of COVID-19 preventive measures which    is 50%,\r\nd is the margin of error which is 5%, then the minimum sample size will be 384.\r\nBut, our sampling techniques will be multistage sampling, then the sample size will be multiplied by the design effect which is 2 and after adding 10% non-response rate the final sample size for this study will be 845. \r\n2.2.2. Sampling procedures \r\nMulti-stage sampling will be carried out using proportional allocation to select the study participants from the households, First, 30% of the kebeles nearly, four kebeles will be selected randomly and then in the kebeles representative households will be selected using systematic random sampling as shown in Figure 1. \r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nFigure 1: Schematic illustration of sampling.\r\n\r\n2.3 Study Variables\r\nDependent Variable: The primary outcomes of interest will be included three key COVID-19 preventive measures, these are:  wearing of face mask, handwashing with soap and water or with alcohol-based hand sanitizer, and social distancing. Wearing of face mask will be dichotomized as “1” for a resident who always or often wears a face mask irrespective of the type of face mask and “0” for those who reported wearing the face mask sometimes or not at all. Handwashing with soap and water or hand sanitizing will also dichotomize as “1” for a resident who always or often washes his/her hands with soap and water or sanitizes the hands with alcohol-based hand sanitizer and “0” for residents who either do not wash/sanitize their hands or sometimes hand wash/sanitize. Social distancing is defined as maintaining at least one-meter distance [14]. It will dichotomize as “1” for resident who practiced it always or often and “0” for resident who did not practice it or practiced it sometimes.\r\nIndependent Variables: The independent variables will be included in this study age, gender (male and female), educational level (no education, primary education, and secondary or higher education, health insurance (yes or no), income, family number, occupation. \r\n2.4 Data collection procedures\r\nStructured questionnaires will be adapted from similar studies and translated to both Amharic and Afaan Oromo languages if it is necessary. Two supervisors and eight data collectors will be involved after they will be trained on the data collection procedures. The data will be collected via interview keeping the physical distancing between the interviewer and the respondents.  \r\n2.5 Data quality management\r\nA 5% of the sample size will be used for pretest out of the study area. In addition, training will be given for data collectors and supervisors. Besides, the questionnaire primarily available in English language will be translated to local languages (Amharic and Afaan Oromo) if it is necessary. The questionnaire will also be checked for completeness and data will be entered using EpiData software (using validation approach); and will be analyzed in version 23 SPSS software. Also, there will be strong supervision, coaching and mentoring of the data collectors and supervisors by the investigation team.\r\n\r\n2.6 Data analysis\r\nDescriptive statistics will be used to report the characteristics of the study population. We will use three multivariable logistic regression models to assess the relationship between the independent variables of interest and each of the outcomes while controlling simultaneously for all our covariates.','<h1><span style=\"font-size:16pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri Light&quot;,sans-serif\"><span style=\"color:#2e74b5\"><a name=\"_Toc87519454\"><strong><span style=\"font-size:14.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">References</span></span></strong></a><strong> </strong></span></span></span></h1>\r\n\r\n<ol>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"background-color:white\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">Zhou, P., Yang, X.L., Wang, X.G., Hu, B., Zhang, L., Zhang, W., Si, H.R., Zhu, Y., Li, B., Huang, C.L. and Chen, H.D., 2020. A pneumonia outbreak associated with a new coronavirus of probable bat origin.&nbsp;nature,&nbsp;579(7798), pp.270-273.</span></span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span sty