e=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">3</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top; width:281px\">\r\n			<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Transport costs</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top; width:321px\">\r\n			<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">15,000</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n		</tr>\r\n		<tr>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:1px solid black; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top; width:40px\">\r\n			<p>&nbsp;</p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top; width:281px\">\r\n			<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Sub-total </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top; width:321px\">\r\n			<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">245,000</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n		</tr>\r\n		<tr>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:1px solid black; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top; width:40px\">\r\n			<p>&nbsp;</p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top; width:281px\">\r\n			<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Contingency (10%)</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top; width:321px\">\r\n			<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">24,500</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n		</tr>\r\n		<tr>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:1px solid black; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top; width:40px\">\r\n			<p>&nbsp;</p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top; width:281px\">\r\n			<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Grand total </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top; width:321px\">\r\n			<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><strong><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">269,500.<s>00</s></span></span></strong></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n		</tr>\r\n	</tbody>\r\n</table>\r\n</div>\r\n\r\n<div style=\"page-break-after:always\"><span style=\"display:none\">&nbsp;</span></div>',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),(16,71,9,20,'Background: Congenital anomaly is one of the global public health problem, especially in low and middle income countries. A number of life lost due to congenital anomalies annually. Those who are living with congenital anomalies are suffering from disabilities, psychosocial problems, economic and educational impacts. Indeed, it also significantly affect the family’s life, healthcare systems, and societies\r\nObjective: Understanding comprehensive knowledge, experiences, stigma and discriminations related congenital anomalies among married women in Jimma Zone, Ethiopia, 2021/2022\r\nMethods: Both quantitative and qualitative study will be conducted to address the objective of this project. A survey will be conducted among married women of reproductive age groups in Dedo and Kersa districts. Qualitative study conducted with mothers or caregivers of children with congenital anomaly, health extension workers, health care providers working on maternal and child health care unit, religious’ leaders married males and married females. A structured tool will be used for survey, while interview guides will be used for qualitative. Descriptive and inferential analyses will be performed as necessary.\r\nBudget: A total of 353,600 ETB is needed to complete this project.',NULL,'Understanding comprehensive knowledge, experiences, stigma and discriminations related congenital anomalies among married women of Jimma Zone, Ethiopia: Implication for child health','2021-11-18 15:01:23','Research output/outcome\r\nThis research project has several pertinent outcomes. Knowing   women’s level of knowledge, perceptions and identifying its predictors enable the local managers and health care providers to act up on such a sensitive issue (congenital anomalies). These results in increased comprehensive knowledge of women about congenital anomalies. This study provide good chance for the women to hear about congenital anomalies, enable them to think of it and gather information and plan toward its prevention which results in reduced prevalence of congenital anomalies. In addition, exploring experiences of mothers or caregivers of children with congenital anomalies and stigma and discrimination related to congenital anomalies  give input for the district health offices, women’s and children’s affairs to plan and act up on this neglected but serious health issue (which is congenital anomalies) which results in improved psychosocial supports. The findings of this project will be presented for health care providers at different levels (health posts (health extension workers), health centers and district health office) and women’s and children’s affairs to work on it.  Moreover, the project will impact working as interdisciplinary university staffs and students.','fe933c12e51d73cf6aaf10da47e20926','completed','Background and rationale\r\nCongenital anomaly is anatomical or physiological abnormalities that occur while the fetus is in uterus and it can be detected during pregnancy, at birth, or sometimes may only be noticed latter during infancy (1).  Even though there are different types of CAs, mainly classified in two based on their visibilities: Visible type of CAs such as spina bifida, cleft lips, cleft palates, exomphalos, laparoschisis/ congenital fissure, and limb defects. There are also invisible types of CAs such as atrial septal defect, persistent ductus arteriosus and esophageal atresia (2). CAs occur commonly during the first two months; usually, before a woman notice she is pregnant.\r\n  Congenital anomalies are one among the causes of disease burden which account for 6% of all births globally (3). Evidence showed that nearly 8 million children are born with major CAs every year, and more than 90% of them occur in LMICs (4). The incidence of CAs in resource limited countries ranges from 1 to11per 1000 births (5). Little is known about CAs in Ethiopia. A cross-sectional study conducted in Addis Ababa and Amhara region showed that the magnitude of birth defects was found to be 1.9% and out of this, orofacial accounts 34.2%, neural tube accounts30.8%, and upper and lower extremities accounts 12.8% of the total identified CAs (6). Another descriptive cross-sectional study from the same area showed that the proportion of congenital anomaly was 1.99% (199 per 10,000) children. Of the total CAs, neural tube defects ranked first (40.3%), orofacial clefts ranked second (23.3%), and muscular-skeletal system anomalies ranked third (23.1%) (7).\r\nThe exact cause of CAs remains unclear for approximately half of all congenital anomalies (1). However, a number of factors affect organs formation and end up with congenital anomalies. These factors include maternal micronutrient deficiency, maternal chronic diseases, a previous history of CA-affected pregnancy( family history of CA), infections during early pregnancy, obesity, chemical and heat/radiation exposure during early pregnancy, genetic, parental education, maternal ages, occupations, smoking, alcohol consumption, obesity, caffeine, medications, and exposure to radiation and hyperthermia during early pregnancy (8, 9). Evidence from some studies indicated that genetic and environmental factors attribute a significant part in causing CAs during the first trimester (10). \r\nCongenital anomalies have a number of negative consequences. CAs affect all organ systems. However mainly it affects the musculoskeletal, cardiovascular, and gastrointestinal systems (11).  CAs are the major cause of newborn/infant morbidity, mortality, and lifelong disability in addition to adding to the burden of the health care system (12) Evidence showed that globally, CAs attributed to over 400 000 fetal deaths, about 20–30% of infant deaths, 2.5 million under-five deaths, and further 2 million children surviving with significant disability (13). These negative consequences are commonly seen in LMICs.  For instance, evidence showed that 95% of deaths among children with CA worldwide occur in resource-limited (12). Indeed, CAs cause physical disabilities, mental disabilities, abortion and stillbirths (14). CAs have also serious psychosocial (self-esteem, personal interactions, and work and recreational opportunities), economic, educational, and cosmetic effects (15). Furthermore, congenital anomalies also significantly affect the family’s life, healthcare systems, and societies in general (16).  With all these burdens, the world is far from achieving the Sustainable Development Goals three (SDGs-3) (17). Thus, there is a need for researches on the area of CAs, identifications of gaps, and strong interventions. \r\nThe majority of CAs can be prevented when preconception care and antenatal care are well focused and quality services are provided. Thus, early risk identification, counseling, promoting access to earlier diagnosis, and management of the identified risks are the foundation for preventing CAs and improving the health of the mother, her child, and future generations (6). Preconception care such as vaccination, adequate intake of folic acid supplementation, dietary diversity, and adequate antenatal care has paramount roles in the prevention of congenital anomalies. For instance, the risk of neural tube defect is reduced by 50% to 70% when women get folic acid supplementation during preconception period (18).\r\nEven though CAs are highly prevalent in LMICs, little is known about CAs in Ethiopia. Some of the existing evidence were facility-based and have limited scope (19). Thus, our project aimed to assess the married women’s knowledge, perceptions, and experiences about CAs and its associated factors in the Jimma zone using a mixed-method study. Findings from this project are important for policymakers, health planners, governmental and nongovernmental institutions, local managers, and  , , frontline health care providers in designing interventional strategies towards improving child health and reducing or preventing CAs and its negative consequences. It also provide women and communities at large to have clear and comprehensive understanding about congenital anomalies, and improve their health seeking behaviors.','Methodology\r\nStudy area and Period\r\nThe study will be conducted from January 2021 to September 2022 in two selected districts of Jimma Zone (Dedo and Kersa districts).  Recent report gained from Jimma zone health office showed the total populations of the zone were 3,543,625.  There are a total of 753,924 households in the zone. Of the total populations, reproductive age groups accounts 799,783. \r\nCurrently, the zone has 9 public hospitals (1 teaching hospital, 2 general hospitals, 121 health centers and 513 health posts, respectively. Dedo and Kersa districts have a total population of 232,126 and 241,030, respectively. Women of reproductive age groups were 51,300 in Dedo and 53,268 in Kersa district, respectively. Dedo district has a total of 36 kebeles (3 urban and 33 rural), while Kersa has and 32kebeles (2 urban and 30 rural).  \r\nStudy design\r\nFor objective 1:  An exploratory sequential mixed study will be conducted to assess women’s knowledge and perceptions about congenital anomalies. To address these objectives, we will start with qualitative study to elicit ideas, insight and generate items as congenital anomalies are new at community level in our country. Then, community based cross-sectional study will be conducted to assess married women’s knowledge and perceptions.\r\nFor objective 2: A phenomenological study will be conducted among mothers of children with congenital anomalies.\r\nFor objective 3:  We aimed to carry out a descriptive qualitative study with different groups of individuals.\r\nSource and study population\r\nFor objective 1: the source populations are all married women of reproductive age of the Dedo and Kersa districts.  The study populations are randomly selected women who fulfill inclusion criteria.  Married women who lived at least for six months in the selected districts prior to data collection will be included in the study. Those who are critically ill, diagnosed mental illness and unable to communicate will be excluded.\r\nFor objective 2 and 3: Mothers of children with congenital anomalies (whether the child is alive or not) are the parent populations. In addition, key informants such as health extension workers, health care providers working on maternal and child health care will be interviewed. On top of these, focused group discussions (with females and males) will also be conducted to address objective 4.\r\nSample size \r\nFor objective 1: A single population proportion formula; n = [(Z α/2) 2 *p (1-p)]/d2, is used to determine the sample with the following assumptions:1.96 standard score for 95% confidence level, 50% proportion of congenital anomalies (since no studies in Ethiopia that can address the same objectives), 5% margin of error, 10% non-response rate and 1.5 design effect. After putting the numbers in the formula, the final sample size become 633.\r\nFor objective 2 and 3We proposed to conduct the key informant interview with 23 mothers or caregivers of children with congenital anomaly, 8 health extension workers, 6 health care providers working on maternal and child health care unit and 2 religious’ leaders. Married males and married females from both rural and urban will be participated in the focused group discussions. In general we aimed to conduct of 39 key informant interviews and 8 focused group discussions.  However, the actual sample size depends on the saturation.\r\nSampling Technique \r\nFor objective 1: The Dedo and Kersa districts are purposely selected considering districts with high prevalence of CAs based information obtained from Jimma zone health office. Detailed sampling procedure is conducted as follows: Initially, kebele stratification is done based on residency (rural and urban). Then, five urban kebeles (3 from Dedo and 2 from Kersa district) are purposely included.  Then, 12 rural kebeles (6 from Dedo and 6 from 	Kersa district) are selected using simple random sampling technique. Finally, proportional allocation to the sample size is made for the 17 selected kebeles. \r\n For rural kebeles the sampling frame will be obtained from the family folder. Then, simple random sampling technique is used to identify the study participants.  For urban kebeles, the total number house hold in kebeles bare used to develop the sampling frame. Then, simple random sampling technique will be used to identify the households. \r\nFor objective 2 and 3:  The participants will be purposively selected. The recruitment will be based on place of residency (rural and urban), types of congenital anomalies and working unit for health care providers (those working on maternal and child health care unit) and referent others (religious leaders) will be considered. Accordingly, the key informants will be 23 mothers or caregivers of children with congenital anomaly, 8 health extension workers, 6 health care providers working on maternal and child health care unit and 2 religious’ leaders. In addition, 8 focused group discussions will be conducted with married males and females.   \r\nData collection instruments and procedures\r\nFor objective 1: Relevant literatures will be reviewed to adapt a structured questionnaire will developed by reviewing articles and guidelines and then pre-tested.  Reliability and validity of tool will assured.  We will hire 9   data collectors and 5 supervisors.\r\nFor Objective 2 and 3:  The project team will develop interview and discussion guides considering the objectives of the study. Voice recorder and field notes will be used. Five qualitative data collectors (experts) will be recruited to collect the data. \r\nAnalysis \r\nFor Objective 1:  We will use STATA version 16.0 software for analysis. Descriptive analysis will be done as necessary. Logistic regression analysis will be performed to identify predictors of women’s knowledge and perceptions about congenital anomalies.  .\r\nFor Objective 2 and 3: Atlas ti software version 7.0.71 will be used. Thematic analysis approaches will be followed.','<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><strong><span style=\"font-size:14.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">Reference</span></span></strong></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">1. WHO.: Congenital anomalies WHO 2016. <span style=\"color:#0563c1\"><u><a href=\"http://www.who.int/mediacentre/factsheets/fs370/en/\" style=\"color:#0563c1; text-decoration:underline\">http://www.who.int/mediacentre/factsheets/fs370/en/</a></u></span>. Accessed 9,November 2021. 2016.</span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">2. Prajapati V, Kacha AR, Kakkad KM, Damor PB, Nandaniya AM: Study of congenital malformation in neonates born at tertiary care hospital. <em>National J Community Med </em>2015, 6(1):30-34. </span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">3. &nbsp;WHO methods and data sources for global burden of disease estimates 2000-2016. <em>Global health estimates technical paper WHO/HIS/IER/GHE/20184 Geneva: WHO </em>2018.</span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">4. Sitkin NA, Ozgediz D, Donkor P, Farmer DL: Congenital anomalies in low-and middle-income countries: the unborn child of global surgery. <em>World journal of surgery </em>2015, 39(1):36-40.</span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">5. Salih MA, Murshid WR, Mohamed AG, Ignacio LC, de Jesus JE, Baabbad R, El Bushra HM: Risk factors for neural tube defects in Riyadh City, Saudi Arabia: Case-control study. <em>Sudanese journal of paediatrics </em>2014, 14(2):49.</span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">6. Taye M, Afework M, Fantaye W, Diro E, Worku A: Magnitude of birth defects in central and Northwest Ethiopia from 2010-2014: a descriptive retrospective study. <em>PloS one </em>2016, 11(10):e0161998.</span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">7. Taye M, Afework M, Fantaye W, Diro E, Worku A: Congenital anomalies prevalence in Addis Ababa and the Amhara region, Ethiopia: a descriptive cross-sectional study. <em>BMC pediatrics </em>2019, 19(1):1-11.</span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">8. </span><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">Flores A, Vellozzi C, Valencia D, Sniezek J: Global burden of neural tube defects, risk factors, and prevention. <em>Indian journal of community health </em>2014, 26(Suppl 1):3.</span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">9. Kishimba RS, Mpembeni R, Mghamba J: Factors associated with major structural birth defects among newborns delivered at Muhimbili National Hospital and Municipal Hospitals in Dar Es Salaam, Tanzania 2011&ndash;2012. <em>Pan African Medical Journal </em>2015, 20(1).</span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">10. Malik S, Cleves M, Zhao W, Correa A, Hobbs C: National Birth Defects Prevention Study. Association between congenital heart defects and small for gestational age. <em>Pediatrics </em>2007, 119(4):e976-e982.</span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">11. Egbe A, Uppu S, Lee S, Stroustrup A, Ho D, Srivastava S: Congenital malformations in the newborn population: a population study and analysis of the effect of sex and prematurity. <em>Pediatrics &amp; Neonatology </em>2015, 56(1):25-30.</span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">12. ICBDSR W: Birth defects surveillance a manual for programme managers. <em>World Health Organization, Geneva </em>2014.</span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">13. Modell B, Darlison M, Moorthie S, Blencowe H, Petrou M, Lawn J: Epidemiological methods in community genetics and the Modell Global Database of Congenital Disorders (MGDb). 2016.</span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">14. Ahmed AM, El Kader S, El Hamid A, Gaafar M: Assessment of risk factors for fetal congenital anomalies among pregnant women at Cairo University Hospitals. <em>J Am Sci </em>2011, 7(12):899-907.</span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">15. Sachdeva S, Nanda S, Bhalla K, Sachdeva R: Gross congenital malformation at birth in a government hospital. <em>Indian J Public Health </em>2014, 58:54-56.</span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">16. Tesfay FA, Aga FB, Teshome GS: Determinants of neural tube defect among children at zewditu memorial hospital, addis ababa, ethiopia a case control study. <em>International Journal of Africa Nursing Sciences </em>2021:100318.</span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">17. Howden-Chapman P, Siri J, Chisholm E, Chapman R, Doll CN, Capon A: SDG 3: Ensure healthy lives and promote wellbeing for all at all ages. <em>A guide to SDG interactions: from science to implementation Paris, France: International Council for Science </em>2017:81-126.</span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">18. Salih MA, Murshid WR, Seidahmed MZ: Epidemiology, prenatal management, and prevention of neural tube defects. <em>Saudi medical journal </em>2014, 35(Suppl 1):S15.</span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">19. Chen G, Song X, Ji Y, Zhang L, Pei L, Chen J, Liu J, Li C, Zheng X: Prevention of NTDs with periconceptional multivitamin supplementation containing folic acid in China. <em>Birth Defects Research Part A: Clinical and Molecular Teratology </em>2008, 82(8):592-596.</span></span></span></p>','Understanding comprehensive knowledge, experiences, stigma and discriminations related congenital anomalies among married women in Jimma Zone, Ethiopia, 2021/2022',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,2,'Congenital anomaly, women, child health, Jimma',10,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),(17,58,9,20,'Summary\r\nIn Ethiopia, the pooled prevalence of perinatal depression is 25.8%, which is almost two-fold of the pooled global prevalence. This indicates the need for prompt interventions. World Health Organization (WHO) directed that antenatal care providers have good opportunities to identify and refer mothers who are at risk and provide psychosocial interventions.  However, in Ethiopia, there is no assessment and interventions targeted to this population at maternal health care service. Objective: - To assess the effect of social support to improve pregnancy healthy lifestyle and prevent perinatal depressions in primary health care facilities by primary health care providers. \r\nMethods: Based on the research objectives a cluster randomized control trial and institutional-based cross-sectional study design will be carried out among 640 (12-20Wks) gestational age pregnant mothers. For trial study 320 in intervention, and 320 in the control group with an average cluster size of 20 will be applied. The unite of randomization is a health center so, among 122 health centers in Jimma, 32 non-adjacent health centers (clusters) will be selected and 16 clusters in each arm will be assigned by using an SPSS-generated random number sequence. By taking the three consecutive monthly reports of each facility, the average monthly load of ANC clients within specified gestational age (12-20wks) will be taken and ‘by systematic random sampling ‘every Kth pregnant woman will be selected until the required sample size fulfilled. Data will be collected by face-to-face interview at 12-20 weeks (baseline) and 6-weeks postpartum period. Well-validated questioner-9, healthy pregnancy lifestyle, and functional social support tool will be used to assess perinatal depression, healthy pregnancy lifestyle, and social support status of the women in two measurement times. \r\nData analysis will be done by using Epi data manager 4.4.1 and SPSS- 25 statistical software packages.  Based on the nature of outcome variable, multivariable logistic regression and generalized estimating equation (GEE) will be used to see the effect of the intervention on major outcome variables. Statistical significance will be assessed using, odds ratios with 95% confidence intervals and p- values less than 0.05.   Finally, results will be presented by using text, tables, and graphs.\r\nWork plane and budget: - The project will take about a 6-month duration with an estimated total budget of 425,532- birr.',NULL,'The effect of social support to improve healthy pregnancy life style and prevent perinatal depression in primary health care facilities, in Jimma, Oromia, Ethiopia   A Cluster Randomized Controlled trial','2021-11-19 15:49:17','Project Out Come /Impact \r\nPrimary outcome: - \r\nGenerate data: - \r\n•	On the practice of mothers on pregnancy healthy lifestyle and associated risk factors social\r\n•	Social support and associated risk factors\r\n•	Perinatal depression and associated risk factors    \r\n•	Gives ways of assessing and preventing maternal depression at primary health care facility by the primary health care provider.\r\n•	Improve family participation in maternal and child health care\r\n•	Create a link between maternal and mental health services.\r\n•	Prevent perinatal depressions and improve pregnancy healthy lifestyle  \r\nSecondary outcome \r\n•	Improvements in utilization of preventive maternal and child health care service, and exclusive breastfeeding \r\n•	Improve healthy promoting lifestyles of the mother and pregnancy outcome\r\n•	 Improve child development\r\n•	Reduce intimate partner violence and decrease marital dysfunction which is critical for the child, mother, family, society, and the country as well.','4d06cf344555531c173d351578234955','completed','BACKGROUND AND RATIONALE  \r\nGlobally perinatal depression ranges from 15% to 65%[1] and shows significant heterogeneity across nations, nations with significantly higher rates of income inequality, maternal mortality, infant mortality, or women of childbearing age working ≥40 hr. a week have higher rates of perinatal depressions [2]. Similarly in the lower- and middle-income countries, its pooled prevalence was  34.0% and 22.7% respectively[3]. Estimates from East Africa indicate up to 39 %[4, 5].  In Ethiopia,  the pooled prevalence is  25.8%[5]. Specifically, in Jimma, it’s about 35.6%[6]. \r\nPerinatal depression refers to psychiatric disorders that are prevalent during pregnancy and as long as 1 year after the birth or adoption of a baby[7]. It occurs at a critical time, impairs a women’s function and affects optimal development of offspring’s[8, 9]. It has  direct relationship with health promoting behaviors of pregnant women’s such as:- nutrition[10-13], sleep status[14], health responsibilities [8, 9], and self-care activities [49], Furthermore, it increases the relative risk of preterm birth by 39%, low birth weight by 49% and intrauterine growth restriction by 45% [15]. And linked to higher rates of spontaneous abortion, prolonged labor, and operative deliveries[16].  Moreover, it associates with maternal anemia [17], gestational diabetes[18],preclamcia[19], and urinary tract infections[20] which results poor pregnancy outcomes.  \r\n\r\nFurthermore, it affects maternal-infant bonding which results impaired child neurodevelopment[21-23], early cessation of breastfeeding[24-26], receiving fewer preventive health services like vaccinations[8, 9], child abuse, and intimate partner violence [27-29]. WHO indicates that treating the depression of mothers leads to improved growth and development of the newborn, and reduces the likelihood of diarrhea and malnutrition in children [30]. The Sustainable Development Goal agenda under goal 3: highlights the importance of continued attention to maternal and newborn health including mental health [31]. So, as one of the countries which have adapted these goals, it is one of the most important areas that we have to work on it.  Because a high number of pregnant women are suffering from these problems. \r\n\r\nEffective antidepressant and psychotherapeutic interventions are present for the treatment of PPD, but still, PPD is a significantly untreated medical problem in lower- and middle-income countries including Ethiopia. Safety of drugs during pregnancy and breastfeeding((FDA, category C and D)[32, 33],  stigma [33], compliant with psychotherapeutic treatment, being west-centric and not in tune with different cultural and religious beliefs(attrition rate > 15-40%)[34] and finally, shortage of mental health professionals [35]  are the most frequently mentioned problems. \r\nCurrent evidence comes with a good opportunity which indicates: - The prenatal period offers maternal health care workers a unique opportunity to identify mothers at risk for emotional problems, marital difficulties, poor social support, and poor coping behavior[35-37]. Furthermore, Evidence indicates that even if an individual has biological vulnerability, it is not sufficient to manifest the disorder and requires psychosocial stressors[38, 39]. And almost all psychosocial risk factors are common for both antepartum and post-partum depressions[4, 40]. Moreover, DSM-5 acknowledges, up to 40% of episodes begin during pregnancy[41]. Correspondingly, WHO, the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists, and Postpartum support international recommends psychosocial support in the antenatal period by lay providers [28, 30, 42-44]. So based on this all evidence, if we work by maternal health care provider on psychosocial factors during early pregnancy, we can effectively prevent perinatal depressions.\r\n History of psychiatric illness, life stress, and poor social support are three constellations of risk factors which strongly related to PPD[45]. Moreover, positive social support is often instrumental in buffering the effects of psychiatric vulnerability and life stress.: which appeared to protect against PPD by dampening increases in biological factors during pregnancy and life stress[39].  Lack of social support and perceived social isolation from partner, family or community have three-fold increase risk of perinatal depressions and can compromise both the mother and infant wellbeing[46-50]. Similarly,  antenatal depression together with a prior history of depression and a low level of partner support were the strongest independent predictors of postnatal depressions[45] and women who have low intimate partner support during pregnancy has the chance of developing persistent depression after delivery increased more than seven-folds[4]. As far as my knowledge is concerned, no preventive interventional study done in Ethiopia targeted this problem. So, the current study assesses the effect of social support to improve pregnancy healthy lifestyle and prevent perinatal depressions in primary health care facilities, by primary health care providers, in Jimma, Ethiopia. This will help policymakers and any concerned stakeholders to prepare necessary resources and flourish programs, and policy development. It will also be an insight for other investigators who want to do further research in this regard.','METHODOLOGY \r\nStudy area and period \r\nThe study will be conducted in primary health care facilities found in Jimma. It takes a maximum of 6-month duration from early pregnancy (12-20wks of GA) up to 6-wks post-partum period tentatively from January 30/2022 to July, 30/2022.   \r\nStudy design\r\nBased on the research questions/objectives, for the first three objectives (assessment of perinatal depression, social support, and healthy pregnancy lifestyle) institution-based cross-sectional study design will be applied. For the last two objectives, Cluster-Randomized Controlled trials with parallel-group, single-blind and two-arm interventions will be applied to assess the effect of social support to improve mothers’ pregnancy healthy lifestyle and prevent perinatal depression in a primary health care facility, in Jimma, Ethiopia.   \r\nPopulation \r\n Source Population: All pregnant women (12-20 GA) in Jimma, primary health care facilities (health center). \r\nStudy population and Participant Eligibility Criteria: - The study participant will be those who are systematically selected, eligible pregnant mothers whose gestational age is between 12-20 weeks in selected health centers in Jimma. For the first three objectives (assessment of perinatal depression, social support, and healthy pregnancy lifestyle) all mothers whose GA  12-20wks and selected by systematic sampling technique will participate.  Whereas for the last two objectives (the effect of social support to improve HPLT and PPD) depression status of mothers will be additional eligibility criteria.  And depression status of mothers will be assessed by a locally validated tool, patient health questioner-9 (PHQ-9)[51]. Mothers who have moderate-severe depression (PHQ-9≥10) in both groups will be referred to a nearby hospital, psychiatric outpatient department to be further evaluated and treated by a district clinician(psychiatrist). Furthermore, mothers who passed the above two criteria but, who are already on treatment for previous mental illness, have a hearing disability, and are severely ill who need hospitalization will also be excluded from the study.  Trial flow detail (Fig 1).   \r\n\r\n\r\n Sample size calculation \r\nFirst, the sample size was calculated for all objectives and the largest sample size was used for all objectives. In Ethiopia, there is no intervention targeted to perinatal depressions. Due to lack of study to estimate the effect of a targeted intervention on outcome variables the author tried to find a study done in low and middle income and different ethnic group women from a different country and taken as a base for this study to estimate the effect of the intervention, on outcome variables. Generally, the study shows up to 27% reduction in the prevalence and symptoms of depressive episodes on 6-month post-partum period [37, 52-55]. In addition to the above experience on intervention outcome, the author tries to consider different local situations like resource, facility (like busy clinic), health care providers competency and our mothers’ status at least 12% (Effect size 0.12) reduction of PPD expected from the study. finally, the sample size was calculated based on the recommendations of sample size calculations for cluster randomized controlled trials [56, 57], by using the following assumptions: proportion of post-natal depression is 21.9% based on a previous study[58],  at least 12% (Effect size 0.12), a number of clusters available–32 (16 clusters per arm), with 95% confidence interval and 80% power, the intra-cluster correlation coefficient of 0.04849[59], The sample size were calculated to determine the number of observations required per cluster, for two-sample comparison of proportions (using normal approximation), using G-power. Assuming individual randomization, the sample size per arm is 158. Then allowing for cluster randomization, the average cluster size required is 20, and the final sample size is 640 pregnant mothers (320 in intervention, and 320 in control). For the first three objectives (assessment of perinatal depression, social support, and healthy pregnancy lifestyle) all 640 will be taken. \r\nRandomization and Sampling Technique: - Because the unit of randomization is a health center and, in our setup only in the hospital, where the psychiatrist is found,  32 non-adjusted health centers (clusters) which have similar baseline characteristics, which are provided by midwives/nurses, have similar lifestyle,  culture and have transportation access for mothers who have moderate-severe depression to go nearby hospitals for further diagnosis and treatment by district clinician (psychiatrist) were selected from 122 health centers in Jimma. Then by using a simple randomization method and SPSS-generated random number sequence 16 clusters (health center) in each arm (1:1 allocation) were assigned to the intervention and control group. Based on randomization results Wolda, Sheki, Toba, Yabu, Asendabo, Seka, Sekoru, Sadi, Choche, Nada, Bulbul, Kochi, Sombo, Deneba, Office, and Kalach health centers were assigned as the intervention group. Whereas -Chira, Yachi, Limu Genet, Atinago, Sebo, Shabe, Ambuye, Bilida, Gembe, Babu, Haro, Jimma H/C, Kisha, Natri, Kore, and Buyo kechema health centers were assigned in the control group. \r\nFinally, by taking the three consecutive monthly reports of each facility, the average monthly load of ANC clients within specified gestational age (12-20wks) will be taken and ‘by systematic random sampling technique participant mothers will be selected by every ‘’kth’’ until the required sample size to be fulfilled. If the selected mother not fulfilled the eligibility criteria and unwilling to participate, the next mother will be included in the study.  Figure 1 trial flow of the study\r\nScreening and Enrollment: Study participant screening will pass two stages; first mothers\' gestational age will be assessed (calculated based on their last normal menstrual period (LMNP) and fundal height (combination result will be taken) or directly taken from the card.  Then mothers whose gestational age is 12-20 weeks will be assessed for their depression status by a locally validated tool, patient health questioner-9 (PHQ-9)[51]. Mothers who are normal (score 0-4) and mild (score 5-9) by PHQ-9 will be included in the study.  The rest with moderate-severe depression (PHQ-9≥10) in both groups will be referred to a nearby hospital psychiatric outpatient department, to be further evaluated and treated by a district clinician(psychiatrist). Trial flow detail (Fig 1).   \r\n\r\n\r\nOperational definitions\r\nPerinatal depression: - Perinatal depression will be assessed by PHQ-9 which comprises of 9-questions, each having scores as 0-3, with minimum and maximum scores as 0 and 27 respectively. The minimum cut of score for diagnosis of perinatal depression is eight (8)[51]. Score (0-4) normal, (5-9) mild, (10-14) moderate, (≥15) severe depressive symptoms[60, 61]. \r\nHealthy pregnancy lifestyle \r\nFor a healthy pregnancy lifestyle; health-Promoting Lifestyle Profile two (HPLP II) will be used, it has 52 items with 6 subscales. [62]The items included on each scale are as follows: health responsibility (9) item, physical activity (8) item, Nutrition (9) item, Spiritual growth/psychological wellness (9) item, Interpersonal relationship (9) and stress management (8) item. A score for overall health-promoting lifestyle is obtained by calculating the mean of the individual\'s responses to all 52 items (84). \r\nSocial support: -Functional social support will be used to assess the social support status of pregnant women. It has 14 items, sum scores for all 14 items. by keeping scores on a continuous scale. Higher scores indicate higher social support [63].\r\nData collection process and quality control\r\nData will be collected using a well validated and pre-tested structured questionnaire.  The questionnaire used to collect data will be designed by reviewing relevant literatures and related standardize tools that includes assessment of perinatal depression[51], healthy pregnancy life styles[64], socioeconomic and demographic characteristics, social support[65], intimate partner violence and others. The validity and reliability of each questioner will be addressed as necessary.  \r\n\r\nThe data will be collected by 2 psychiatric nurses and 2 midwives who work in different health facilities other than the study area and are blind to the hypothesis of the study, participant, and cluster recruitment.  They are recruited based on their sex, professions, and experience, preferably, who has good communication skills, and knows very well the local language and culture will be selected. Training of data collectors will be undertaken by the investigator and psychiatrist for 2 days. The questionnaire will be translated into Afan Oromo and Amharic languages by experts, and also translated back to English. A pretest will be done on 5 % of pregnant women in facilities other than the study area. The data collection will be supervised by two supervisors: the investigator and MSc psychiatrist. During data collection, the filled questionnaires will be checked carefully on the spot and daily basis for their completeness, accuracy, and clarity. Any error, ambiguity, incompleteness, or other problem encountered will be early identified, communicated, discussed, and solved before starting next day activities.  Data will be collected two times: -baseline (12-20) wks of gestational age), and end line 6-weeks post-partum. \r\nIntervention \r\nTraining Materials and Content of the Intervention Package\r\nThe intervention package, technique, and flow will be derived from different well-validated guidelines, manuals, and handbooks that were prepared for the prevention and management of perinatal depressions.  First, the development of the intervention package is based on the following guidelines WHO, mhGAP Intervention Guide in non-specialized health settings by non-specialized professionals,[35] NICE, management of antenatal and postnatal mental health: clinical management and service guidance[66] and Interventions to Prevent Perinatal Depression: United States Preventive Service Task Force.[43] Second, the psychoeducation intervention techniques derived from  Clinical Practice Guidelines for Psychoeducation in Psychiatric Disorders: General Principles of Psychoeducation [67, 68] interpersonal psychotherapy based skill[69] and Minimum necessary intervention to maintain a postpartum depression prevention program in prenatal clinics serving for low-income women [70] \r\nLastly, adaptation will be carried out in three steps: - (1) analysis of the literature review; (2) evaluation of available handbooks and manuals targeting perinatal depression prevention. (3) Utilization of comments and discussions with researcher’s experts in the area, clinicians (psychiatrists, psychologists and non-specialized health care providers like midwife, nurse, and/ or gynecologist), users and carers, in order to identify the most relevant information which will be included in the intervention.  Then an ad-hoc manual with a detailed description of each session of the intervention is prepared.\r\nTotally 3 prenatal group sessions lasting 60 to 90 minutes will be administered for all intervention groups (two sessions mothers and one session for family) every week [55, 68, 71, 72]. And in each group up to 8-10 women or  family  will be participated based on guideline, available resources and time[68].\r\n PROPOSED CONTENT OF THE TRAINING SESSION\r\n1.	The participants became familiar with each other and the structure of the sessions. In the continuation of the same session, - \r\n•	Definition forms of social support\r\n•	Importance of social support to manage stress and improve health life style \r\n•	How/Ways to get social support \r\n2.	Group discussion and experience sharing on how to get social support.\r\n3.	Discussion with partner or family about the Importance of social support during pregnancy and once the baby born and its benefit to manage stress. And their role.\r\n\r\nAdministration of interventions \r\nThe intervention will be delivered for mothers or family by midwife or nurses, who are working on ANC clinic and recruited from the study site for the purpose of resource management and sustainability of the study. It’s difficult to blind providers about participant and intervention but they are unaware of the study hypothesis and assessment tools. One midwife from each facility totally 16 midwives will be recruited. Female providers will be selected because of mothers may discuss their problem and concern without fear and social stigma for their female health care providers that know their cultural barrier and understand their psychosocial problem clearly. Training for health care provider working at ANC clinic (midwifes and nurses) will be given by psychiatrist and investigator for 2 days. The intervention package given for the provider includes all content of interventions sessions mentioned above plus, how to establishing referral system from health center ANC to the psychiatry department of the nearby hospital for women with moderate- severe depressions. And ways of   monitoring the intervention to decrease the dropout rate of mothers from the intervention and how to motivate mothers to follow strictly the sessions up to end line data collection time.\r\nControl arm \r\nParticipant in the control arm will receive the existing routine care (Focused antenatal care).  The routine care has no any assessment and management or prevention programs in relation to perinatal depression (maternal mental health).  \r\nMonitoring and Evaluation: \r\nAs indicated above depression will be followed by PHQ-9 by assessing the women at 12-20wks, and end-line assessment will be done by 6-weeks post-partum. Log frame will be developed to pinpoint objectives and activities. Supervision will be done every day by psychiatrist and principal investigator. Women’s address will be taken like phone number, kebele, to follow and encourage to finish all sessions.  It can be done by taking women’s address (like telephone number, kebele, house no), and by communicating with health extension worker in each kebele. Because health extension workers know each pregnant woman found in their working kebele.  If the women missed one session, the midwife will find her by recorded address and by the help of health extension worker then try to help women to finish the rest of the sessions. Finally, when the women gave birth, delivery may be attended by other provider but the women still followed by the same provider until 6 weeks post-partum. By using EPI (expanded program on immunization) as advantage end line data collection will be done at EPI room. In-depth interviews will be conducted with relevant personnel in ANC clinic and women to identify workable solutions found in the implementation of intervention. Providers and women’s satisfaction by the intervention will be assessed.\r\n\r\nSustainability of the intervention: \r\nThe intervention will be done by participating facility MCH, hospital psychiatric clinic or OPD and woreda MCH coordinator offices through the entire study period. Providers working at MCH clinic   and managers in the facility will be trained on how to handle the program, with their routine activity, the benefit of the program for mothers, and how it facilitates their work to meet their plans on maternal and child health and service utilizations.   And all possible efforts will be done by discussing with them to own the program for sustainability. Before leaving the project; it should be handed over to the office by forming a strong linkage with these responsible stakeholders of the facility. The referral system will be linked the ANC clinic (health center) with the psychiatric clinic through the existing government structure, ‘’available referral paper’’ so that the program will be sustain. By available referral system pregnant women whose PHQ-9 score≥10 will be referred to psychiatric out patient department to get medical treatment.  Even if the referral system presents previously in any facility in Ethiopia, the link between psychiatric and ANC clinic are not well known or working due to women are not assessed for psychosocial problem at ANC follow-up so we can say its new idea which doesn’t exist in Ethiopia.\r\n Data Analysis\r\nAfter data collection, the data will be cleaned, edited, coded and entered into the computer using Epi data manager 4.4.1. The analysis will be done by using SPSS- 25 statistical software packages. Descriptive statistics will be used to check for missing variables, errors, and outliers. Any error identified at this time will be corrected by the revision of the original questionnaire.  Descriptive analyses will be carried out, including frequencies, mean and standard deviation to describe the study population concerning to socioeconomic and demographic characteristics, psychosocial, obstetric and other relevant variables. Based on the research questions multivariable logistic regression will be applied for the first three objectives (assessment of perinatal depression, social support and healthy pregnancy life style and associated risk factor). \r\nData from the trial will be analyzed by intention to treat analysis at both cluster and individual level. Outcome’s variables will be compared between intervention and control groups by generalized estimating equation with binary outcome variable, by taking account of clustering. \r\nStatistical significance will be assessed using the chi-square test, odds ratios with 95% confidence intervals and p- values less than 0.05. The analysis includes the following independent variables: time of survey (baseline/end line), group status (intervention /control) and interaction between time and intervention status. The model controls: - complications during labor and delivery such as neonatal loss or admission, breast feeding difficulty, and mode of delivery. Based on previous study antenatal psychosocial risk, socio-economic and demographic factors such as marital status, parity, age, education, and income will be included in the model as covariate variables.','<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\">1.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Dadi, A.F., et al., <em>Global burden of antenatal depression and its association with adverse birth outcomes: an umbrella review.</em> BMC Public Health, 2020. <strong>20</strong>(1): p. 173.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\">2.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Hahn-Holbrook, J., T. Cornwell-Hinrichs, and I. Anaya, <em>Economic and Health Predictors of National Postpartum Depression Prevalence: A Systematic Review, Meta-analysis, and Meta-Regression of 291 Studies from 56 Countries.</em> Front Psychiatry, 2017. <strong>8</strong>: p. 248.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\">3.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Fekadu Dadi, A., E.R. Miller, and L. Mwanri, <em>Antenatal depression and its association with adverse birth outcomes in low and middle-income countries: Step 4: Review practice against evidence-based audit criteria.</em> PLoS One, 2020. <strong>15</strong>(1): p. e0227323.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\">4.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Ongeri, L., et al., <em>Demographic, psychosocial and clinical factors associated with postpartum depression in Kenyan women.</em> BMC Psychiatry, 2018. <strong>18</strong>(1): p. 318.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\">5.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Getinet, W., et al., <em>Prevalence and Risk Factors for Antenatal Depression in Ethiopia: Systematic Review.</em> Depress Res Treat, 2018. <strong>2018</strong>: p. 3649269.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\">6.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Negewo, A.N., <em>Prevalence of Depression among Pregnant Women Attending ANC Follow up Mother&#39;s at Higher Two Health Center, Jimma Teaching Health Center, Shenen Gibe Hospital and JUSH, Jimma Town, Oromia Regional State, South West Ethiopia.</em> Clinics in Mother and Child Health, 2019. <strong>16</strong>(1).</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\">7.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; O&#39;Hara, M.W. and K.L. Wisner, <em>Perinatal mental illness: definition, description and aetiology.</em> Best Pract Res Clin Obstet Gynaecol, 2014. <strong>28</strong>(1): p. 3-12.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\">8.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Masters, G.A., et al., <em>Utilization of Health Care Among Perinatal Women in the United States: The Role of Depression.</em> J Womens Health (Larchmt), 2020. <strong>29</strong>(7): p. 944-951.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\">9.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Bitew, T., et al., <em>Antenatal depressive symptoms and utilisation of delivery and postnatal care: a prospective study in rural Ethiopia.</em> BMC Pregnancy Childbirth, 2017. <strong>17</strong>(1): p. 206.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\">10.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Sparling, T.M., et al., <em>The role of diet and nutritional supplementation in perinatal depression: a systematic review.</em> Matern Child Nutr, 2017. <strong>13</strong>(1).</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\">11.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Shi, D., G.H. Wang, and W. Feng, <em>Nutritional assessments in pregnancy and the risk of postpartum depression in Chinese women: A case-control study.</em> Medicine (Baltimore), 2020. <strong>99</strong>(33): p. e21647.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\">12.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Hsu, M.C., C.Y. Tung, and H.E. Chen, <em>Omega-3 polyunsaturated fatty acid supplementation in prevention and treatment of maternal depression: Putative mechanism and recommendation.</em> J Affect Disord, 2018. <strong>238</strong>: p. 47-61.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\">13.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Avalos, L.A., et al., <em>Prenatal Depression and Diet Quality During Pregnancy.</em> J Acad Nutr Diet, 2020. <strong>120</strong>(6): p. 972-984.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\">14.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Bais, B., et al., <em>The Impact of Objective and Subjective Sleep Parameters on Depressive Symptoms during Pregnancy in Women with a Mental Disorder: An Explorative Study.</em> Int J Environ Res Public Health, 2019. <strong>16</strong>(9).</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\">15.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Grote, N.K., et al., <em>A meta-analysis of depression during pregnancy and the risk of preterm birth, low birth weight, and intrauterine growth restriction.</em> Arch Gen Psychiatry, 2010. <strong>67</strong>(10): p. 1012-24.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\">16.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Eastwood, J., et al., <em>The Impact of Antenatal Depression on Perinatal Outcomes in Australian Women.</em> PLoS One, 2017. <strong>12</strong>(1): p. e0169907.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\">17.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Azami, M., et al., <em>The association between anemia and postpartum depression: A systematic review and meta-analysis.</em> Caspian J Intern Med, 2019. <strong>10</strong>(2): p. 115-124.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\">18.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Azami, M., et al., <em>The association between gestational diabetes and postpartum depression: A systematic review and meta-analysis.</em> Diabetes Res Clin Pract, 2019. <strong>149</strong>: p. 147-155.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\">19.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Malakouti, J., et al., <em>Relationship between Health Promoting Lifestyle and Perceived Stress in Pregnant Women with Preeclampsia.</em> J Caring Sci, 2015. <strong>4</strong>(2): p. 155-63.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\">20.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Liu, J.M., et al., <em>Antepartum urinary tract infection and postpartum depression in Taiwan - a nationwide population-based study.</em> BMC Pregnancy Childbirth, 2018. <strong>18</strong>(1): p. 79.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\">21.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Leigh, B. and J. Milgrom, <em>Risk factors for antenatal depression, postnatal depression and parenting stress.</em> BMC Psychiatry, 2008. <strong>8</strong>: p. 24.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\">22.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Oyetunji, A. and P. Chandra, <em>Postpartum stress and infant outcome: A review of current literature.</em> Psychiatry Res, 2020. <strong>284</strong>: p. 112769.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\">23.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Olsson, C.A., et al., <em>Preconception depression and anxiety symptoms and maternal-infant bonding: a 20-year intergenerational cohort study.</em> Arch Womens Ment Health, 2021. <strong>24</strong>(3): p. 513-523.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\">24.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Pope, C.J., et al., <em>Breastfeeding and postpartum depression: Assessing the influence of breastfeeding intention and other risk factors.</em> J Affect Disord, 2016. <strong>200</strong>: p. 45-50.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\">25.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Pope, C.J. and D. Mazmanian, <em>Breastfeeding and Postpartum Depression: An Overview and Methodological Recommendations for Future Research.</em> Depress Res Treat, 2016. <strong>2016</strong>: p. 4765310.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\">26.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Wouk, K., A.M. Stuebe, and S. Meltzer-Brody, <em>Postpartum Mental Health and Breastfeeding Practices: An Analysis Using the 2010-2011 Pregnancy Risk Assessment Monitoring System.</em> Matern Child Health J, 2017. <strong>21</strong>(3): p. 636-647.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\">27.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Carroll, J.C., et al., <em>Effectiveness of the Antenatal Psychosocial Health Assessment (ALPHA) form in detecting psychosocial concerns: a randomized controlled trial.</em> CMAJ, 2005. <strong>173</strong>(3): p. 253-9.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\">28.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; <em>&lt;WHO _ Maternal mental health.pdf&gt;.</em></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\">29.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Midmer, D., <em>The ALPHA Form: Assessing Antenatal Psychosocial Health,Provider&rsquo;s Guide 3rd Edition.</em> <a href=\"http://dfcm19.med.utoronto.ca/research/alpha/default.htm\" style=\"color:blue; text-decoration:underline\">http://dfcm19.med.utoronto.ca/research/alpha/default.htm</a>, 2005.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\">30.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; WHO, <em>Mental health Maternal mental health</em>. 2019, WHO: Geneva. p. 1-3.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\">31.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; UN, <em>Indicators for Sustainable Development Goals,</em>, in <em>A report by the Leadership Council of the Sustainable Development Solutions Network</em>. 2014. p. 1-72.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\">32.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Yonkers, K.A., et al., <em>Antidepressant use in pregnant and postpartum women.</em> Annu Rev Clin Psychol, 2014. <strong>10</strong>: p. 369-92.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\">33.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; O&rsquo;Connor E et al, <em>Screening for Depression in Adults: An Updated Systematic Evidence Review for the U.S. Preventive Services Task Force.</em> Evidence Synthesis No. 128. AHRQ Publication No. 14-05208-EF-1. Rockville, MD: Agency for Healthcare Research and Quality; .Screening 2016: p. 137-140.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\">34.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Davies, T. and C. Lund, <em>Integrating mental health care into primary care systems in low- and middle-income countries: lessons from PRIME and AFFIRM.</em> Glob Ment Health (Camb), 2017. <strong>4</strong>: p. e7.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\">35.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Meena Cabral de Mello, V.C.-M., Natalie Drew, Daniela Fuhr, Michelle Funk, Sandra Gove, Suzanne Hill,Jodi Morris, Mwansa Nkowane, Geoffrey Reed, Dag Rekve,Robert Scherpbier, Rami Subhi, Isy Vromans, Silke Walleser., <em>mhGAP Intervention Guide for mental, neurological and substance use disorders in non-specialized health settings.</em> WHO:GENEVA, 2010.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\">36.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Verpe, H., et al., <em>Early postpartum discharge: maternal depression, breastfeeding habits and different follow-up strategies.</em> Scand J Caring Sci, 2019. <strong>33</strong>(1): p. 85-92.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\">37.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Rahman, A., et al., <em>Interventions for common perinatal mental disorders in women in low- and middle-income countries: a systematic review and meta-analysis.</em> Bull World Health Organ, 2013. <strong>91</strong>(8): p. 593-601I.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\">38.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Agius, C.G.M., <em>The Stress-Vulnerability Model How Does Stress Impact On Mental Illness At The Level Of The Brain And What Are The Consequences?</em> Psychiatria Danubina, 2010. <strong>22</strong>(2): p. 198&ndash;202 </span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\">39.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Hahn-Holbrook, J., et al., <em>Placental Corticotropin-Releasing Hormone Mediates the Association Between Prenatal Social Support and Postpartum Depression.</em> Clin Psychol Sci, 2013. <strong>1</strong>(3): p. 253-264.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\">40.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Faisal-Cury, A. and P.R. Menezes, <em>Antenatal depression strongly predicts postnatal depression in primary health care.</em> Braz J Psychiatry, 2012. <strong>34</strong>(4): p. 446-50.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\">41.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Jacques, N., et al., <em>Prenatal and postnatal maternal depression and infant hospitalization and mortality in the first year of life: A systematic review and meta-analysis.</em> J Affect Disord, 2019. <strong>243</strong>: p. 201-208.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\">42.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nb