e of Patient Health Questionnaire-9 and Edinburgh Postnatal Depression Scale for screening antepartum depression.</em> J Affect Disord, 2014. <strong>162</strong>: p. 1-7.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\">61.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; van Heyningen, T., et al., <em>Comparison of mental health screening tools for detecting antenatal depression and anxiety disorders in South African women.</em> PLoS One, 2018. <strong>13</strong>(4): p. e0193697.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\">62.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; <em>&lt;English_HPLPII.pdf&gt;.</em></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\">63.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Wilkins, L.W., <em>The Duke-UNC Functional Social Support Questionnaire.</em> MEDICAL CARE2018.<strong> Vol. 26, No. 7</strong>.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\">64.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; S.N. Walker, K.S., N. Pender, , <em>LIFESTYLE PROFILE II.</em> 1995.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\">65.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Stephanie L. Martin1, Z.B., Moshood O. Omotayo1, Yohannes Kebede2, Gretel H. Pelto1, Rebecca J. Stoltzfus1, and Katherine L. Dickin1, <em>&ldquo;I Can&rsquo;t Answer What You&rsquo;re Asking Me. Let Me Go, Please.&rdquo;: Cognitive Interviewing to Assess Social Support Measures in Ethiopia and Kenya.</em> journals.sagepub.com/home/fmx, 2017: p. 1-16.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\">66.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; NICE, <em>Antenatal and postnatal mental health: clinical management and service guidance.</em> <a href=\"file:///C:/Users/Lenovo/Desktop/www.nice.org.uk/guidance/cg192\" style=\"color:blue; text-decoration:underline\">www.nice.org.uk/guidance/cg192</a>, 2014.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\">67.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Li, Y., et al., <em>Path model of antenatal stress and depressive symptoms among Chinese primipara in late pregnancy.</em> BMC Pregnancy Childbirth, 2016. <strong>16</strong>(1): p. 180.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\">68.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Sarkhel, S., O.P. Singh, and M. Arora, <em>Clinical Practice Guidelines for Psychoeducation in Psychiatric Disorders General Principles of Psychoeducation.</em> Indian J Psychiatry, 2020. <strong>62</strong>(Suppl 2): p. S319-S323.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\">69.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Klerma, W.M.K.J., <em>Interpersonal Psychotherapy: The Model.</em> Improving acess to psychological therapies, 2010.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\">70.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Johnson, J.E., et al., <em>Protocol for the ROSE sustainment (ROSES) study, a sequential multiple assignment randomized trial to determine the minimum necessary intervention to maintain a postpartum depression prevention program in prenatal clinics serving low-income women.</em> Implement Sci, 2018. <strong>13</strong>(1): p. 115.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\">71.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Caron Zlotnick, S.L.J., Ivan W. Miller,Teri Pearlstein, Margaret Howard,, <em>Postpartum Depression in Women Receiving Public Assistance: Pilot Study of an Interpersonal-Therapy-Oriented Group Intervention.</em> Am J Psychiatry, 2001.<strong> 158:</strong>: p. 638&ndash;640.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\">72.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Sylvester, E.D.B.a.A.N., <em>Postpartum Depression: What Counselors Need to Know.</em> VISTAS Online, 2013.</span></span></p>','To assess the effect of social support to improve pregnancy healthy lifestyle and prevent perinatal depressions in primary health care facilities, in Jimma, Oromia, Ethiopia, 2021.',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,2,'Perinatal depression, antenatal care, postnatal depression, healthy life style',10,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO `submission` VALUES (18,83,9,26,'Title 	Development and validation of patient health-literacy framework for healthcare settings: Towards improved healthcare errors and patient empowerment systems\r\nThematic area	1.	Information and communication technologies for health\r\n2.	Determinants of health and health behaviors\r\nProject objectives and outcomes\r\nObjectives 	Description of expected outcomes \r\n1.	To explore and develop patient health literacy system, platforms, contexts, opportunities and challenges in public-private hospitals and PHC in Jimma zone	This objective aims to investigate key dimensions of patient health literacy framework by considering health care systems, communication platforms, opportunities and challenges of advancing health literacy, treatment outcomes and reducing errors\r\n2.	To assess patient health literacy status in public and private hospital/PHC settings of Jimma zone 	This objective will mainly assess patients’ cognitive, behavioral or operational, and affective, system, and context-specific attributes of health literacy. The assessed dimensions emanate from mix of emerging models and findings of qualitative explorations of the current study. \r\n3.	To describe health care errors in public and private hospitals/PHC of Jimma zone	To assess patient and healthcare workers-related health care errors of omission and commission, describe patterns based on types and statuses of health workers, across and clinics and wards\r\n4.	To develop and validate patient health literacy framework for health care settings 	This objective will be the main and ultimate purpose of the study, that is, to conduct principal component analysis for validating a comprehensive patient health-literacy framework (PHLF). Predictive capacity of the PHLF will be assessed against overall satisfaction with health care, health care errors and intention to adhere to treatment regimen\r\nName of the PI	1.	Dr. Yohannes Kebede (PhD, MPH)\r\nDepartment & Faculty of the PI	Health, Behavior and Society, Faculty of Public Health \r\nList of the Co-applicants 	2.	Mr. Firanbon Teshome (member)\r\n	3.	Mohammed Jemal (member)\r\n	4.	Dr. Netsanet Workneh (member)\r\n	5.	Abebe Abera (member)\r\nBudget requested	391,100 Birr \r\nDuration of the project (indicate start and end month and year)	 January 01, 2022 to December 30, 2022',NULL,'Development and validation of patient health-literacy framework for healthcare settings: Towards improved healthcare errors and patient empowerment systems','2021-11-19 15:33:08','Sno	Project phases 	Descriptions\r\n1	Phase 1: Obj 1 and 3	Phase 1: qualitatively explores health-literacy platforms and system (obj 1) and healthcare errors (portion of obj 3)\r\n	Outputs	•	The current health-literacy system for hospitals/health centers assessed\r\n•	Forms, process, and reasons of healthcare errors explored\r\n	Outcomes	•	Draft/hypothesized patient health-literacy framework developed\r\n•	Quantitative tools for assessing patient health-literacy framework finalized\r\n•	Draft diagram of healthcare errors omitted/committed by patients, providers and structures drawn\r\n•	Quantitative tools for assessing healthcare errors finalized \r\n2	Phase 2: obj 2, 3, and 4	Phase 2: quantitatively assesses patients’ health-literacy status and healthcare errors in hospital and health center settings\r\n	Outputs	•	Patients’ health-literacy status assessed \r\n•	Healthcare errors in hospital/health center settings assessed\r\n	Outcomes	•	Forms, pattern, and factors of patient health-literacy described\r\n•	Patient health-literacy framework validated\r\n•	Forms, patterns, distribution, and factors for healthcare errors: patient-related, provider-related and system-related identified\r\n•	Linkages between PHLF and healthcare errors (undiagnosed, on-adherence, misuses, etc) established','c78dc3bfaac2b5cb78cec9c3c59e976d','completed','World is currently shifting to health-literacy approach for behavior change. Behavior change has many approaches that unfolded overtime- which includes IEC, BCC, SBCC and Health-literacy. Health Literacy is  degree to which individuals have the capability to get, process, and understand basic health information and services required to make appropriate health decisions (1–3).\r\nAmong different models of Health literacy Bakers model of Health Literacy is a well-known model. According to this the first domain is individual capacity which has two subdomains; which are reading fluency and is prior knowledge. According to this model Health literacy is one of the different factors (e.g., culture and social norms, health care access) that leads to getting of new knowledge, more positive attitudes, greater self-efficacy, positive health behaviors, and better health outcomes (2).\r\nAccording to different literatures patients with low health literacy level  was associated with more hospitalizations, greater use of emergency care, high mortality rate, high risk of medication error, higher health care cost; lower receipt of preventive screening service, suboptimal medications adherence, dearth of skills to easily access health information, less likely to engage in active discussions about their health options, have difficulty in comprehending health care information, experience misunderstanding about their disease, and communicate ineffectively with healthcare providers (4–8)\r\nmedical errors which are commonly known as Health care Errors is defined as an act that does not achieve its desired outcome (either of omission or commission), the failure of a planned action to be completed as intended (an error of execution), the deviation from the plan to achieve an objective that may potentially cause harm to the patient (an error of planning). (9). Medical error is a threat to a strong patient-health professional relationship and may also lead to poor quality health service delivery and can cause temporary or permanent harm or death and high economic burden on the health care system (10,11).  \r\nHealth Literacy and Medical errors are associated either directly or indirectly through poor coordination of care, lack of training, inadequate knowledge, inadequate perception of risks, Poor Physician-Patient engagement, poor nurse-physician communication. (12,13). Health Literacy is a new topic which has more recently been viewed as an emerging concept in the delivery of safe, quality health care and become increasingly crucial for social, economic and health development (3,14). \r\nDespite its huge importance in reducing health care errors and improving the quality of the healthcare system, to the best of the investigators knowledge there is no context specific Patient Health literacy Framework. Therefore, this study fills this gap by developing new or refining and validating context specific patient health-literacy framework (PHLF) in hospital and primary health care settings of Jimma zone.','4.1. Project setting and coverage\r\nAs indicated in the project objectives above, this project will be aimed to reduce the probabilities of healthcare errors in healthcare settings- hospital contexts whereby patients are empowered through organized platforms that support health-literacy framework. The project will cover Jimma, Mana and Kersa districts of Jimma zone. Three selected public and private hospitals and two health centers: specifically, Jimma University Medical Center, Oda Ulle-private hospital and Seka hospital, Yebu health center, Serbo health center) will be targeted.  Jimma zone is located in the Southwest direction 356 K.M away from the national capital, Finfinnee. With an area of 50.52 KM2, Jimma zone has 21 districts, and 42 urban and 513 rural gandas. The total population of Jimma Zone is estimated to be 3.2 million, with the majority of the population living in rural areas. The districts selection will further be discussed with zonal health department. The selection of the districts was made based on a couple of criteria: public and private healthcare setting with primary and specialized/referral services with high patient and that have high empowerment opportunities with few available resources if targeted. The selected public hospitals are (primary and specialized), and private hospitals, health centers provide primary health care and are nearest to the community. Patients and health workers from ranges of wards and clinics available in those selected hospitals and health centers. \r\n4.2. Study design\r\nThe current study employs mixed method and has two phases. Phase 1: exploratory qualitative study; and Phase 2: quantitative survey to assess healthcare errors, and health-literacy status. \r\nPhase 1: During phase 1 qualitative study, we will explore systems, platforms, dimensions, opportunities and challenges for health-literacy for healthcare setting. We will conduct checklist-based observation to assess available platforms and opportunities required to establish locally appropriate health-literacy system. Then, in-depth interviews will be conducted among selected patients and health workers from selected wards to understand dimensions of health-literacy and experiences pertaining to healthcare errors. Emerging health-literacy models will be adapted as eliciting concepts during phase 1 exploratory qualitative inquiry so that context specific and additional dimensions will be explored toward development of comprehensive health-literacy model for hospital/health center healthcare settings. Hence, objective 1, qualitative part of objective 3 will be addressed during this phase 1 exploration. \r\nPhase 2: during this phase quantitative survey will be conducted to address objectives 2 and 4, and part of objective 3. Hence, patients and providers surveys will be conducted to quantify health care errors and assess patients’ health literacy status. The survey tools will be informed by qualitative findings.   Finally, PHLF will be developed and validated. \r\n\r\n4.3.	Project target population and sample \r\nPopulation: The project will be targeted at (1) healthcare providers at PHCU/hospitals, (2) clients of health centers and hospitals and (3) health center/hospital facilities.  First, healthcare workers will be assessed to assess healthcare errors omitted/committed and their engagements in advancing patients’ health literacy. Patients/clients will be assessed for level of health literacy and their roles in healthcare errors, and finally facilities will be assessed to understand platforms, opportunities and challenges for advanced patient health-literacy. \r\nSample: Accordingly, to address objective 1 and part of objective 3 (during phase 1): five health facilities will be observed; tentatively 15 in-depth interviews will be conducted with patients and health workers from different wards. For phase 2 surveys, a total of 844 patients (determined using a single population formula, p=50%, 10% non-response rate, design effect of 2) and 422 health workers-nurses, general practitioners, anesthetists, etc. (determined using a single population formula, p=50%, 10% non-response rate) will be recruited.  \r\n\r\n4.4. Eliciting conceptual frameworks for patient health-literacy and healthcare errors \r\n4.4.1. Eliciting conceptual for PHLF\r\nThere is no patient health-literacy framework ever developed and validated. Hence, the project only utilizes emerging models as eliciting conceptual frameworks instead of using then as conceptual framework. The project will have two separate concepts: health-literacy and healthcare errors. Theoretically, improved health outcomes and healthcare errors will be expected out of strong patient health-literacy framework established into healthcare settings.  Hence, developing and validating health-literacy framework will be the main aim of the current project. In fact, since our aim is mainly to develop and validate PHLF, we only use the emerging frameworks as eliciting concepts that are to be developed further based on elicitation exploratory qualitative study- in the phase 1 of the project. \r\nGenerally speaking, health literacy, in its first emerging framework (Baker’s framework, 2006, figure 1) attempts links individual capacity with access to and interpretation of health information, acquirement of new knowledge, attitude and practice that lead to improved health outcomes. \r\n      \r\n     Figure 1: Baker’s et.al (2006) measure of health literacy\r\n   \r\n    As a brief summary, the central concepts of many health-literacy models emerged later include: communication skills, basic knowledge and belief about health topics, cultural and linguistic factors, and public health and healthcare system demands i.e to influence people’s ability to adopt healthier behaviors, respond appropriately to health-related information, share information with healthcare providers, manage their own health care, and navigate the intricacies of healthcare systems.  Accordingly, some models consider basic education (individual capacity, experience, reading/numeracy skills); empowerment as a process through tailored education for health-knowledge and communication-negotiation skills acquirement that leads to increased interactive health literacy; and empowerment as outcome finally results in engagement in social actions, change in behaviors and practices, and participation in changing social norms and practices i.e an increased critical health literacy. Some models consider information processing theory (IPT) central to health-literacy given the ability to access, understand, appraise and apply health information in order to become knowledgeable,  competent/skilled (increased knowledge and understanding of how to manage health conditions) and motivated to take actions toward keeping one’s and families’ health by producing informed options, making informed decisions (active involvement in consultations), and adhering and participating in healthcare, disease prevention, and health promotion. Therefore, equity, participation, health behavior and health service use are some of the main continuum indicators of individual to standard determinants for information access, processing and applying.  Some models consider education system, culture and society, and health system as the three potential intervention points for health outcomes and costs. \r\n      \r\nBroder, et.al, 2017 health literacy framework will be utilized as eliciting concept for this project, (extended Baker’s framework). This framework has pulled several dimensions into three main concepts: \r\n1.	Cognitive attributes: The mental abilities and actions that enable a person to think, learn and process information are attributed to this category. These include: knowledge, basic or functional health -related skills, appraisal and evaluation, comprehension and understanding, critical thinking \r\n2.	Behavioral or operational attributes:  All dimensions referring to actions that take place outside of the individual’s mind were assigned to be behavioural attributes These include: seeking and accessing information, communication and interaction, application of information, and citizenship\r\n3.	Affective and conative attributes This category includes dimensions of health literacy that evolve around the experience of feeling or emotions (affective attribute) or describe personality traits and mental stages that influence how individuals strive towards action and direct their efforts (conative attributes). These include: self-awareness and reflection, self-control and regulation, self-efficacy, interest and motivation. \r\n3.4.2. Eliciting conceptual framework for healthcare errors\r\nIt has already been introduced in the background that health literacy determines health outcomes, reduces healthcare errors, hospitalization rates, help people adapt health behaviors, adhere to healthcare, prevent diseases and promote health. This project aims to find out links between errors and health literacy status also. The follow diagram (Figure 2) provides general flow of healthcare errors in the management of health condition at healthcare settings. \r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n \r\n4.5. Data management and analysis\r\n This project will produce both qualitative and quantitative data on health literacy and healthcare errors. Qualitative data will be analyzed using ATLAS ti.7.5.18 to build on eliciting concepts of and develop hypothetical patient health-literacy framework and healthcare error dimensions.  Additionally, the qualitative data will inform refinement of quantitative surveys. Opening coding method will be used to thematically analyze the qualitative data. Quantitative data of healthcare errors and patient health-literacy status will presented using descriptive statistics (mean, median, range, standard deviations) with 95% confidence intervals. Inferential statistics (chi-square, t-tests, ANOVA) will be used to assess differences in proportions and means and patterns, forms and distribution of healthcare errors and health-literacy status. The validation of hypothesized PHLF will be performed by using principal component analysis (PCA) using Eigen value of 1 at factor loading cutoff value >0.4. Reliability analysis will be done for emergent components of PHLF. Correlation and regression analysis will be done to assess relationship and effect between healthcare errors and health-literacy status.','<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">1<span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\">. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Welfare F. Social and Behavior Change Communication ( SBCC ) Training for Information , Education , and Communication ( IEC ) Officers. 2013. </span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\">2. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Baker DW. The meaning and the measure of health literacy. J Gen Intern Med. 2006;21(8):878&ndash;83. </span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\">3. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Parnell TA. Health Literacy: History, Definitions, and Models. Heal Lit Nurs. 2018;3&ndash;31. </span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\">4. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Jayasinghe UW, Harris MF, Parker SM, Litt J, van Driel M, Mazza D, et al. The impact of health literacy and life style risk factors on health-related quality of life of Australian patients. Health Qual Life Outcomes [Internet]. 2016;14(1):1&ndash;13. Available from: http://dx.doi.org/10.1186/s12955-016-0471-1</span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\">5. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Berkman ND, Sheridan SL, Donahue KE, Halpern DJ, Crotty K. Low health literacy and health outcomes: An updated systematic review. Ann Intern Med. 2011;155(2):97&ndash;107. </span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\">6. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Morrison AK, Gibson C, Higgins C, Gutzeit M. Health Literacy&ndash;Related Safety Events: A Qualitative Study of Health Literacy Failures in Patient Safety Events. Pediatr Qual Saf. 2021;6(4):e425. </span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\">7. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Tilahun D, Abera A, Nemera G. Communicative health literacy in patients with non-communicable diseases in Ethiopia: a cross-sectional study. Trop Med Health. 2021;49(1). </span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\">8. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Aboumatar HJ, Carson KA, Beach MC, Roter DL, Cooper LA. The impact of health literacy on desire for participation in healthcare, medical visit communication, and patient reported outcomes among patients with hypertension. J Gen Intern Med. 2013;28(11):1469&ndash;76. </span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\">9. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Grober ED, Bohnen JMA. Defining medical error. Can J Surg. 2005;48(1):39&ndash;44. </span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\">10. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Baldwin DWC, Daugherty SR, Rowley BD. Unethical and unprofessional conduct observed by residents during their first year of training. Vol. 73, Academic Medicine. 1998. p. 1195&ndash;200. </span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\">11. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; 6 do clinical clerks suffer.pdf. </span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\">12. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Liu C, Wang D, Liu C, Jiang J, Wang X, Chen H, et al. What is the meaning of health literacy? A systematic review and qualitative synthesis. Fam Med Community Heal. 2020;8(2):1&ndash;8. </span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\">13. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Endalamaw A, Dessie G, Biresaw H, Belachew A, Amare D, Workineh Y, et al. Medication Errors in Ethiopia: Systematic Review and Meta-Analysis. 2020;(June). </span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\">14. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Office of Disease Prevention and Health Promotion. Health Literacy | Healthy People 2020 [Internet]. Office of Disease Presentation and Health Promotion. 2020. p. 26&ndash;9. Available from: https://www.healthypeople.gov/2020/topics-objectives/topic/social-determinants-health/interventions-resources/health-literacy</span></span></span></p>','3.1. Goal \r\nThe ultimate aim of this project will be to develop and validate comprehensive/general (non-specific) and locally appropriate patient health-literacy framework that workout to improve healthcare errors and patients’ outcomes in public-private health settings providing primary and specialized healthcare. \r\n3.2 General objective\r\nThe general objective of the study will be to assess the current statuses of health care errors, patients’ health-literacy and opportunities and challenges to finally develop and validate comprehensive patient health-literacy framework',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,2,'Health-literacy,Patient health-literacy,Patient health-literacy framework,healthcare errors',10,'<h1><span style=\"font-size:16pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri Light&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"color:#2f5496\"><a name=\"_Toc88053112\"><strong><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"color:black\">9. Project duration and time schedule:</span></span></strong></a><strong> </strong></span></span></span></h1>\r\n\r\n<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\">The proposed duration of the project will be 1 year (Dec, 2021-Dec 2022) (table below)</span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<table cellspacing=\"0\" class=\"MsoTableGrid\" style=\"border-collapse:collapse; border:none; width:100.0%\">\r\n	<tbody>\r\n		<tr>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:1px solid black; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:1px solid black; vertical-align:top; width:27%\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><strong>Activities </strong></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:1px solid black; vertical-align:top; width:6%\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><strong><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt\">Nov-Dec&nbsp; </span></strong></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:1px solid black; vertical-align:top; width:5%\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><strong><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt\">Jan </span></strong></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:1px solid black; vertical-align:top; width:5%\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><strong><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt\">Feb </span></strong></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:1px solid black; vertical-align:top; width:6%\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><strong><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt\">Mar</span></strong></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:1px solid black; vertical-align:top; width:6%\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><strong><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt\">Apr<