ht:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:17px; vertical-align:top; width:315px\">\r\n			<ul>\r\n				<li><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\">Patients&rsquo; survey </span></span></span></li>\r\n			</ul>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:17px; vertical-align:top; width:124px\">\r\n			<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\">145,200</span></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n		</tr>\r\n		<tr>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:18px; vertical-align:top; width:315px\">\r\n			<ul>\r\n				<li><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\">Health workers&rsquo; survey</span></span></span></li>\r\n			</ul>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:18px; vertical-align:top; width:124px\">\r\n			<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\">102,600</span></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n		</tr>\r\n		<tr>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:1px solid black; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top; width:66px\">\r\n			<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\">3</span></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top; width:315px\">\r\n			<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\">Logistics </span></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top; width:124px\">\r\n			<p style=\"margin-left:48px\">&nbsp;</p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td rowspan=\"3\" style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top; width:132px\">\r\n			<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\">36,700</span></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n		</tr>\r\n		<tr>\r\n			<td rowspan=\"2\" style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:1px solid black; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:17px; vertical-align:top; width:66px\">\r\n			<p>&nbsp;</p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:17px; vertical-align:top; width:315px\">\r\n			<ul>\r\n				<li><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\">Transport </span></span></span></li>\r\n			</ul>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:17px; vertical-align:top; width:124px\">\r\n			<p style=\"margin-left:24px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\">28,700</span></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n		</tr>\r\n		<tr>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:21px; vertical-align:top; width:315px\">\r\n			<ul>\r\n				<li><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\">Duplications </span></span></span></li>\r\n			</ul>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:21px; vertical-align:top; width:124px\">\r\n			<p style=\"margin-left:24px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\">8,000</span></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n		</tr>\r\n		<tr>\r\n			<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:1px solid black; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top; width:505px\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:center\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><strong><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\">Grand total</span></strong></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top; width:132px\">\r\n			<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><strong><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\">391,100 birr</span></strong></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n		</tr>\r\n	</tbody>\r\n</table>',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),(19,121,9,20,'Background and Rationale:  Pelvic floor disorder affects millions of women throughout the world and adversely affects the quality of life of a woman. They often experience discomfort, low self-esteem, mood swings, and feelings of helplessness. The reported prevalence rates of UI in women vary considerably due to differences in target population. Evidence indicates that some risk factors for pelvic floor disorder are predictable, modifiable and can be reduced through preventive and corrective measures which related with the following pregnancy and child birth characteristics, multiple pregnancies, excessive pregnancy weight gain, instrumental delivery, newborn weight (>4,000 g), and excessive duration of the second stage of lobar. \r\nObjective: The goal of this project is to improve compliance with evidence-based criteria regarding Pelvic Flour Muscle Training (PFMT) during Pregnancy and Post natal care in Jimma Zone Hospitals.  \r\nMethodology:  The study will be conducted in Jimma Zone hospitals (Seka Primary Hospital, Omo Nadda Primary Hospital and Agaro General Hospital). This evidence implementation project will be conducted using the Joanna Briggs Institute (JBI) Practical Application of Clinical Evidence System and Getting Research into practice audit and feedback tool. The JBI PACES and GRiP framework for promoting evidence-based healthcare involves three phases of activity. Phase I: Establishing a Project Team and Performing a Baseline Audit, Phase II: Implementation of Best Practice and Phase III: Post Implementation Audit. The simple data analysis will be done by excel sheet and will be presented by graphs and tables. \r\nTimeline: January 01 to December 30/2022\r\nBudget: This project required 527,400.00 EBirr budget.','Compliance to Women’s Pelvic Floor Muscles Exercise Training during Pregnancy and Postpartum','Compliance to Women’s Pelvic Floor Muscles Exercise Training during Pregnancy and Postpartum at Hospitals in Jimma Zone, Southwest Ethiopia: A Best Practice Implementation Project','2021-11-19 13:32:05','Research Outcome \r\nThe research will have the following outcome \r\n1.	All staff working at MCH unit will get training on the best practice regarding PFMT after this project.\r\n2.	The challenges or barriers related with PFMT will be addressed after this project.\r\n3.	The women can be benefited from this project by enhancing her health by applying PFMT which will prevent urinary incontinences, utero-vaginal prolapse.\r\n4.	The clinical audit team will be established in the hospitals after completion of this project.','3d3015a6228ed5b1d140ce6c30ce5f70','completed','Background and Rationale  \r\nPelvic floor disorder affects millions of women throughout the world and adversely affects the quality of life (QoL) of a woman (1, 2). They often experience discomfort, low self-esteem, mood swings, and feelings of helplessness (3, 4). Fear of urinary loss and offensive odor have a negative impact on socialization and contribute to social isolation and changes in sexual activity(5); they may even trigger anxiety disorder or depression(3).  It  includes stress urinary incontinence (SUI), urgency urinary incontinence (UUI), overactive bladder (OAB), pelvic organ prolapses (POP), and fecal or anal incontinence (FI, AI)(6). \r\nThe reported prevalence rates of UI in women vary considerably due to differences in target population because it occurs during different stages of female life such as during pregnancy, early postpartum period and during menopause. Any form of pelvic floor disorder prevalence ranges from 5% -69% (7), from which pelvic organ prolapse accounts (5-10%), Urinary incontinence (30-60%), and anal incontinence (11-15%)(5-10). In Ethiopia population based -study indicates 20.5% women reported at least one type of pelvic floor disorder and from which 49.6% reported two or more pelvic floor disorders (11).\r\nPregnancy and childbirth are independent factors that are strongly associated with the onset or worsening of UI (up to 30–50%) in women of childbearing age (1, 5).  Physiologic and anatomic changes during pregnancy, that can result in weak pelvic floor muscles (PFM), and poor pregnancy and child birth care causes worsening of the problems(1). Vaginal delivery has been strongly implicated in the etiology of pelvic floor disorders (1). Similarly, studies indicate that of all prolapse procedures 99% had at least one vaginal delivery and of all incontinence procedures 95% had at least one vaginal delivery(5). Similarly high fertility rate, a difference in lifestyle, environmental and genetic factors, body mass index [BMI], were the most common factors that associated with pelvic floor disorders specially in older women(1, 12, 13). Even if  the women in low-income countries including Ethiopian women are more vulnerable to the above risk factors(8, 11), low attitude towards PFD in the community, being ashamed, embarrassed and fear of being discrimination led to hiding the problem and decrease help-seeking behavior women but the pelvic floor disorder is worsen  in these countries(14).\r\nEvidence indicates that some risk factors for pelvic floor disorder are predictable, modifiable and can be reduced through preventive and corrective measures which related with the following pregnancy and child birth characteristics––multiple pregnancies, excessive pregnancy weight gain, instrumental delivery, newborn weight (>4,000 g), and excessive duration of the second stage of lobar(15, 16).  Based on this evidence, in our clinical set up some of preventive and correctional measures such as skill birth attendance, elective and emergency cesarean section, avoid smoking and constipation and maintaining a normal BMI) has been practiced to alleviate the problem of pelvic floor disorders.  Due to high number of populations served by primary health care facilities and gave birth through vagina or instrumentally in Ethiopia: and low access or lack of adequate health infrastructures and equipment in the facility for cesarean sections, low institutional delivery coverage allows the problem to continue in similar path.  \r\nEvidence indicate that it is important to improve preventive measures and identify new and better management modalities in order to improve the health of individuals afflicted and reduce the costs for society(6, 17). Pelvic floor muscle trainings are effective preventive and conservative treatment methods that are not practiced in our clinical set ups.  It’s done with discussion with pregnant women as part of their routine antenatal and post natal care(6). It is a supervised treatment that involves muscle‐clenching exercises to strengthen the PF muscles. It is a substantial part of the conservative treatment of SUI and MUI, and it is proposed as the first line of treatment for pelvic floor disorders. Women who exercised their pelvic flour muscles in a systematic and consistent manner over time retained stronger and better coordinated muscles (18). This muscle strength contributed to greater urethral pressure in situations of increased intra-abdominal pressure thus decreasing the risk of urinary loss. \r\nA systematic review study showed that women who underwent PFMT during pregnancy had a lower risk of developing UI at the end of pregnancy and in the first 3–6 months postpartum. Postnatal UI can be reduced by 29% or even by 37% in women who exercise their PF muscles during pregnancy (19).The effects of postnatal PFMT on the prevalence of incontinence in the general population of mothers are not clear; it is likely that greater benefit will be achieved with prevention and treatment approaches that target groups of women who are \"at high risk\" of developing incontinence or who have incontinence(20). Furthermore, PFMT provides women with an understanding of their anatomy and control of their musculature. In situations where abdominal hyper-pressure triggering urinary loss can help them avoid or decrease the loss, or in emergency contexts, rapid contractions of the PF muscles can inhibit detrusor contraction (21). But in Ethiopia there is no assessment of high-risk women and training of women’s pelvic floor muscles during pregnancy and postpartum period.','Methodology \r\nThe study will be conducted in Jimma Zone hospitals. Jimma Zone has seven hospitals (1. Agaro General Hospital, 2. Limmu Genet Hospital, 3. Dimtu Primary Hospital, 4. Shenen Gibe General Hospitals, 5. Omo Nadda Primary Hospital, 6. Seka Primary Hospital and Setema Primary Hospital). So that three hospitals were selected (Seka Primary Hospital, Omo Nadda Primary Hospital and Agaro General Hospital) by lottery method. All health care professionals involved in the care of women’s during pregnancy and post-partum are the study population. This evidence implementation project will be conducted using the Joanna Briggs Institute (JBI) Practical Application of Clinical Evidence System and Getting Research into practice audit and feedback tool. These tools will be employed to promote evidence-based healthcare using audit and re-audit standard criteria concomitant to team-based analysis of the organizational and client barriers and identification of strategies to overcome these barriers.  The JBI PACES and GRiP framework for promoting evidence-based healthcare involves three phases of activity.\r\nQuantitative Data Collection \r\nPhase 1: Establishing a Project Team and Performing a Baseline Audit Based On Criteria Informed By the Evidence\r\nThe project team will be organized based on team members’ position, willingness and agreement to participate and implement the project, and provide their support for the successful accomplishment of the project. Then by using the four- evidence-based clinical audit criteria, baseline assessment will be evaluated to see whether the health professionals’ clinical practice in pelvic flour muscle training (PFMT) met the evidence-based criteria provided in the JBI evidence summary or not. \r\nEvidence- based audit criteria: \r\n1.	Health professionals received education on PFMT.\r\n2.	Women receive PFMT during the antenatal care period.\r\n3.	 An assessment is done to identify women at high risk of developing urinary incontinence after childbirth.\r\n4.	Women at high risk of developing urinary incontinence receive PFMT in the postpartum period.  \r\nFor the first criteria, all registered health professionals currently working at maternal and child health care services (antenatal, delivery, postnatal, Expanded Program on Immunization, and family planning) will be audited. \r\nFor the 2nd and 3rd criteria’s, data will be collected by observational technique using the JBI-PACES software from all 50 professionals working at MCH units in the selected Hospitals. For the fourth criteria based on the risk assessment check list all women who are at high risk of developing urinary incontinency will receive PFMT in the postpartum period. So the professionals will be audited for this service at post-partum care for the fourth criteria. \r\nQualitative Data Collection \r\nTo explore barriers and facilitators for engaging health professionals in PFMT qualitative data will be collected. About 12 key informant interviews will be conducted from senior staff, MCH focal person and chief executive director/medical director of all selected hospitals.\r\nData Analysis and Presentation\r\nThe simple data analysis will be done by excel sheet and will be presented by graphs and tables for quantitative and thematic analysis will be conducted for qualitative data. Based on the baseline audit finding the following phase two implementation of best practice will be conducted.    \r\nPhase Two: Implementation of Best Practice\r\nAt the implementation phase, guided by the PACES Getting Research into Practice (grip) framework tool, the project team will discuss the results of the baseline data. Then, the possible barriers to the practice of training of women pelvic floor will be explored. Based on the identified barriers, key strategies and subsequent actions will be developed and set by the team and other stakeholders. Based on the identified barrier the project team will prepare training on best practice implementation on the standard checklists for PFMT. After implementation which is training delivered for the health professionals in the selected hospitals, the post audit will be conducted after three months of best practice implementation. \r\nPhase Three: Post Implementation Audit \r\nIn the follow up audit, the same tool (JBI PACES tool) will be used for data collection again. Then the data will be analyzed in the same way and will be presented for the staff. If there are unsolved gaps, the team will give support to fill the identified gaps again.','<ol>\r\n	<li><span style=\"font-size:14pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\"><span style=\"color:black\"><a name=\"_Toc88089841\"> References</a> </span></span></span></li>\r\n</ol>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">1.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Bodner-Adler B, Kimberger O, Laml T, Halpern K, Beitl C, Umek W, et al. Prevalence and risk factors for pelvic floor disorders during early and late pregnancy in a cohort of Austrian women. Arch Gynecol Obstet. 2019;300(5):1325-30.</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">2.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Zhou F, Xue K, Liu Y, Zhuo L, Tu S, Palmer MH. Toileting behaviors and factors associated with urinary incontinence in college-aged female students in China. Int Urogynecol J. 2020;31(5):961-71.</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">3.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Yaakobi T, Handelzalts JE, Peled Y, Levy S, Wiznitzer A, Krissi H. Direct and Indirect Effects of Personality Traits on Psychological Distress in Women With Pelvic Floor Disorders. Female Pelvic Med Reconstr Surg. 2017;23(6):412-6.</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times