h:52px\">\r\n			<p style=\"margin-left:7px\"><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\">7</span></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:16px; vertical-align:top; width:118px\">\r\n			<p><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\">Training </span></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:16px; vertical-align:top; width:101px\">\r\n			<p style=\"margin-left:7px\"><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\">15</span></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:16px; vertical-align:top; width:68px\">\r\n			<p style=\"margin-left:7px\"><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\">200</span></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:16px; vertical-align:top; width:75px\">\r\n			<p><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\">3000</span></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:16px; vertical-align:top; width:81px\">\r\n			<p style=\"margin-left:9px\"><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\">9000</span></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:16px; vertical-align:top; width:78px\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:center\">&nbsp;</p>\r\n			</td>\r\n		</tr>\r\n		<tr>\r\n			<td colspan=\"7\" style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:1px solid black; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:28px; vertical-align:top; width:572px\">\r\n			<p style=\"margin-left:7px; text-align:center\"><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\">Total&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;798000<strong>&nbsp;et birr</strong>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n		</tr>\r\n	</tbody>\r\n</table>',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),(21,24,9,21,'Depression is among the most common mental disorders that causes significant morbidity and mortality. Beside pharmacotherapy, alternative intervention mechanisms like music therapy are found to be powerful options to treat depression. While listening to music, dopamine and serotonin hormones are released and this helps to minimize stress, anxiety, and depression. Since music sounds are freely available everywhere with minimal cost, it is an alternative solution to reduce treatments costs.  \r\nPreliminary study that we conducted at Jimma University showed that the “Begena” (the biblical Harp of David) instrumental sound has a potential healing capability on a depressed subject. However, the study lacked control groups and was limited to small sample size.  Thus, it requires further investigation for “Begena” instrumental sound to be used as an alternative intervention mechanism in the clinical setup. \r\nWe aim in this project to conduct a large-scale study so as to investigate the therapeutic effects of “Begena” sound on depressed subjects at study setting. Healthy subjects will also be enrolled as a control group. \r\n\r\nA control intervention for randomized clinical trial will be followed by selecting individuals randomly with case, those who are using proposed treatment option (Begena instrumental sound therapy) VS subject treated with existing solution medication therapy for the same clinical condition.','Frequency Domain EEG Neural Signal Analysis Study','The Neural Effect of Instrumental “Begena” Sound Therapy on Depressive Disorder','2021-11-18 16:55:13','4.	Research Outcome for the Community\r\nEthiopian chordophone’s instrumental sounds were traditionally used by people to meditate themselves since ancient time. For example, Begena has been used in ancient Israel to heal King Solomon’s nerve and heal his insomnia. Skillfully prepared instrumental music sound therapy can have several benefits to help people with variety illnesses including depression. Hence this research aims to introduce a new source of instrumental sound that created in a skillful way to the field of MT.  \r\n\r\nIn doing so, the proposed research has very high significant not only on mental health but also used as an assistive treatment of other chronic disease like heart disease, diabetes, and hypertension.  It will be one of natural treatment option in JU health facilities that can avoid adverse side effect of the medication, nearly available, with very low cost and can be given no requirement of specialist at home or in health facilities.','69f0d5d46c384a565744477d668d01dd','completed','1.	Background and Rationale \r\nMental depression is a common mental health disorder that affects people’s emotional, cognitive, physical, and social wellbeing regardless of the state of their immune system, leading to underemployment and reduced productivity [1]. \r\nEven though the root cause of depression is not clearly understood, experts believe that change in hormone levels, including serotonin is considered as a cause [5]. The level of this hormone inside the brain becomes unbalanced due to physical, social and emotional factors, including: Loss of loved ones, drug or substance use, previous history of mental illness, and academic load in the university study [1, 2, 5 ].\r\nCurrently this illness has different severity stage and can develop from low-depression to self- suicide (self –killing). Currently this stage are clinically diagnosed either using standard patient health questionnaire (PHQ-9) score test (e.g. scores 5, 10, 15, and 20 represented mild, moderate, moderately severe, and severe depression level, respectively) or using an electroencephalogram (EEG) machine[6,7].\r\n\r\nWhen it get worse, it lead to self –suicide, causing 800,000 people death per year globally as well as it increase the risk of getting chronic disease. According to the World Health Organization (WHO) current report, 332 million people, about 4.4% of the world population of all age types, are suffering from depression [2].\r\n“It accounts for 6.5 % of the burden of diseases in Ethiopia”[3]. A study was conducted on 354 staff members of Jimma University and showed that 22.9% of the total participants were in the depressed states [1].  A 2019 study result that participate 760 Jimma university students indicates that 58.4% of the sampled students have been affected and 83.8% of students with this problem required treatment help [4].\r\n\r\nIn Ethiopia, almost all treatment options for depression stick on either psychotherapy or pharmacotherapy, which are not only least cost effective options but also carry associated side effects.  As music therapy is not costly and is easily available, it hastens recovery as most people in Ethiopia are having low socioeconomic status to get the drugs and the psychotherapy. Instrumental “Begena” sound at binaural beat frequency range is presented here as an alternative therapy option with a quicker healing capability on depressed subjects and its use could be promoted easily among the community as it is widely listened by the believers of Orthodox Christianity.  \r\nWhat is Begena Instrument? Begena is one of Ethiopian traditional instrument is characterized by a very specific buzzing sound. It is a chordophone instrument with ten strings, belonging to the lyre family of instruments. \r\nPractically, it is common to see this effect within a subject who plays with or listen to it. It produces a low bass heart-warming vibrational sound and can attract people’s attention, putting them into a new emotional mood. Due to this reason, it’s one of the lovely traditional instruments in Ethiopia and common to see people spending time to practice and learn how to play it. Hence, this research proposal aims to investigate the neural effect of the Begena instrumental sound therapy.','A control intervention for randomized clinical trial will be followed by selecting individuals randomly with case, those who are using proposed treatment option (Begena instrumental sound therapy) VS subject treated with existing solution medication therapy for the same clinical condition. This research project will be conducted in Jimma university medical center from December 20 2021 to September 20, 2022.','<h1><span style=\"font-size:14pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Cambria,&quot;serif&quot;\"><span style=\"color:#365f91\"><a name=\"_Toc87799003\">Reference</a> </span></span></span></h1>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Cambria&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">[1]&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; D. Press, &ldquo;Depression , anxiety , stress , and their associated factors among Jimma University staff , Jimma , Southwest Ethiopia , 2016 : a cross-sectional study,&rdquo; pp. 2803&ndash;2812, 2017.</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Cambria&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">[2]&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; WHO, &ldquo;Depression and Other Common Mental Disorders Global Health Estimates,&rdquo; pp. 3&ndash;4, 2017.</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Cambria&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">[3]&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; B. Duko, M. Erdado, and J. Ebrahim, &ldquo;Prevalence and factors associated with depression among hospital admitted patients in south Ethiopia : cross sectional study,&rdquo; <em>BMC Res. Notes</em>, pp. 10&ndash;13, 2019, doi: 10.1186/s13104-019-4109-3.</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Cambria&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">[4]&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Y. Gebreegziabher, E. Girma, and M. Tesfaye, &ldquo;Help-seeking behavior of Jimma university students with common mental disorders: A cross-sectional study,&rdquo; <em>PLoS One</em>, vol. 14, no. 2, pp. 1&ndash;18, 2019, doi: 10.1371</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Cambria&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">[5]&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; A. J. Gelenberg <em>et al.</em>, &ldquo;Treatment of Patients With Major Depressive Disorder,&rdquo; no. May, 2010.</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Cambria&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">&nbsp;[6]&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; K. Words, &ldquo;Computer-Aided Diagnosis of Depression Using EEG Signals,&rdquo; vol. 599489, pp. 329&ndash;336, 2015, doi: 10.1159/000381950.</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Cambria&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">[7]&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; D. P. X. Kan and P. F. Lee, &ldquo;Decrease alpha waves in depression: An electroencephalogram(EEG) study,&rdquo; <em>2015 Int. Conf. BioSignal Anal. Process. Syst. ICBAPS 2015</em>, p. 158: 159, 2015, doi: 10.1109/ICBAPS.2015.7292237.</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px; text-align:justify\">&nbsp;</p>','To evaluate instrumental “Begena” sound therapy effectiveness in alleviating mental depression symptoms based on EEG-based neural effect analysis study',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,2,'EEG,Begena,Frequency-domain,Depressive-disorder ,Neural-Signal',10,'<h1>Research Project Time Schedules</h1>\r\n\r\n<table cellspacing=\"0\" class=\"MsoTableLightGridAccent1\" style=\"border-collapse:collapse; border:none; margin-left:-29px; width:690px\">\r\n	<tbody>\r\n		<tr>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:3px solid #4f81bd; border-left:1px solid #4f81bd; border-right:1px solid #4f81bd; border-top:1px solid #4f81bd; height:52px; vertical-align:top; width:150px\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Cambria&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Milestone </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:3px solid #4f81bd; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid #4f81bd; border-top:1px solid #4f81bd; height:52px; vertical-align:top; width:138px\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-si