#1f497d; border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:17px; vertical-align:top; width:48px\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\">&nbsp;</p>\r\n			</td>\r\n		</tr>\r\n		<tr>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:2px solid black; border-left:2px solid black; border-right:2px solid black; border-top:none; height:10px; vertical-align:top; width:43px\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">9</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"border-bottom:2px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:10px; vertical-align:top; width:252px\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Monitoring and evaluation</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"background-color:white; border-bottom:2px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:10px; vertical-align:top; width:96px\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Investigator </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"background-color:#1f497d; border-bottom:2px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:10px; vertical-align:top; width:48px\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\">&nbsp;</p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"background-color:#1f497d; border-bottom:2px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:10px; vertical-align:top; width:54px\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\">&nbsp;</p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"background-color:#1f497d; border-bottom:2px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:10px; vertical-align:top; width:60px\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\">&nbsp;</p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"background-color:#1f497d; border-bottom:2px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:10px; vertical-align:top; width:54px\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">&nbsp;</span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"background-color:#1f497d; border-bottom:2px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:10px; vertical-align:top; width:60px\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\">&nbsp;</p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"background-color:#1f497d; border-bottom:2px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:10px; vertical-align:top; width:54px\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\">&nbsp;</p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"background-color:#1f497d; border-bottom:2px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:10px; vertical-align:top; width:54px\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\">&nbsp;</p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"background-color:#1f497d; border-bottom:2px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:10px; vertical-align:top; width:54px\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\">&nbsp;</p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"background-color:#1f497d; border-bottom:2px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:10px; vertical-align:top; width:48px\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\">&nbsp;</p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"background-color:#1f497d; border-bottom:2px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:10px; vertical-align:top; width:54px\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\">&nbsp;</p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"background-color:#1f497d; border-bottom:2px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:10px; vertical-align:top; width:48px\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\">&nbsp;</p>\r\n			</td>\r\n		</tr>\r\n	</tbody>\r\n</table>',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),(23,95,9,20,'2.	Executive Summary \r\nBackground: Continuum of Care (CoC) is a global framework designed to mitigate maternal mortality and morbidity and under-five mortality. Understanding the levels, retentions, and dropouts along the completion of the continuum of care is very mandatory to design effective strategies. However, little is known about the maternity continuum of care (ANC 4+, SBA, and Postnatal contacts) in the study setting. Thus, this project aimed to assess the level of completion along the continuum of care for maternal, neonatal, and child health services and its determinant factors among postpartum women during COVID-19 pandemic time in Jimma Zone, Southwest, Ethiopia from March to April, 2022.\r\nMethods: A community-based mixed study design linked to health facility data will be employed from March to April 2022 in the Jimma zone. Multistage cluster sampling will be used to reach study subjects. Data will be obtained from 842 postpartum mothers from 6 weeks to 6 months after their last birth. For a quantitative method, data will be collected via face-to-face interviewers. Questionnaires and observational checklists (for health facilities) will be developed by reviewing different literatures, WHO guidelines, and other national protocols. Data will be entered into Epidata version 3.1 and exported to Statistical Package for Social Sciences (SPSS) version 25 for analysis. Multilevel logistic regression analysis will be fitted to identify the predictors in the completion of each segment of the continuum of care. For a qualitative part, 5-10 individuals for focus group discussions (FGDs) and 30 key informants for in-depth interviews will be conducted. The analysis will be done using the atlas ti.7.1. \r\nWork plan and budget: The overall study will be conducted from December to August,2022.The total budget required for this project is 385,000 ETB.','Level of completion along the continuum of care for maternal, neonatal, and child health services and its determinant factors','Assessment of Level of Completion along the Continuum of Care For Maternal, Neonatal, and Child Health Services and Its Determinant Factors among Postpartum Women During COVID-19 Pandemic Time in Jimma Zone, Southwest, Ethiopia: Mixed Study Design.','2021-11-19 16:19:17','Research outcome/Impact\r\nThe following points are the expected outcome of this project:\r\n1.	It generates basic knowledge about the level of maternity continuums of care in the study settings.\r\n2.	It directs policymakers and other stakeholders to pay more attention to the continuum of care rather than separate entities to promote, protect and provide maternal and child health care services.\r\n3.	It helps maternity health care providers to understand the dropouts, retention, and promoting factors to complete along continuums of maternity care.\r\n4.	It helps to understand the current maternal health care services when the world is overwhelmed with the global pandemic period and local instability issues.\r\n5.	In addition to the above point, it can be used as baseline data for those who are interested to conduct further research on this area of concern.','0ad3d6279e203763f7486ca09fe87a7a','completed','3.	Rationale and Background\r\nDespite the World Health Organization (WHO) recommending to end the preventable causes of maternal mortality and morbidity. Globally, around 808 women die every day from preventable causes of maternal death, all almost 99% occurs in developing countries, of this 68% are reported in Subsaharan Africa [1,2]. In response to this, World Health Organization implemented a global agenda which is known as Sustainable Development Goal (SDG) three /3. According to this goal, every country aims to reduce maternal mortality and morbidity to less than 70 per 100,000 live births and or no countries to have more than two times this target by 2030 [3].\r\nIn the past one and half decades,according to EDHS data from 2000 to 2016 Ethiopia has made significant signs of progress in reducing maternal mortality(MM) from 871 to 412 per 100,000 live births, under-five mortality from 166 to 67 per 1000 live births, also neonatal mortality from 49 to 29 per 1000 live births. But this still represents an unacceptably high number of neonatal and maternal mortality and morbidities in Ethiopia [4]. To achieve the SDG agenda, the government of Ethiopia has made the health sector transformation plan of 2021-2025 GC to decrease maternal mortality from 401 to 279, under-five mortality from 59 per 1000 live births to 44. And also reduce neonatal mortality from 33 per 1000 live births to 21 by the end of 2025 [5].\r\nTo reach these global and national targets, WHO recommended the completion of maternity continuum of care (CoC) as an important tool to mitigate maternal mortality and morbidity and also neonatal mortality. On the other hand, it is an important strategy to improve maternal and child health[6].Continuum of Care (CoC) is a global framework introduced in 1970 for providing continuum care for aging people [7]. However, recently many scholars have defined continuum of care (CoC) as a continuation of care from pregnancy to the postpartum period. And the complete continuum of care is considered when a woman receives; at least four antenatal care (ANC), assisted by skilled birth attendants during childbirth and receives at least one postnatal visit within six weeks [8-11]. The continuity of care (CoC) has two key domains, time and place, continuum of care from pre-pregnancy, pregnancy, childbirth, postpartum and neonatal health, is the time-domain of continuity of care (CoC). Whereas, integration of care at households, community, facility, and referral services is a place domain of continuity of care (CoC) [8,12].\r\nThe extent to which a woman completes maternal health services is an important indicator to provide, promote, and protect maternally and child health care [6,8]. Furthermore, evidence-based data indicated that at 99% universal coverage of continuity of care (CoC), it could avert approximately 41-72% of neonatal mortalities worldwide [13]. Also, other evidence showed that if delivery is assisted by skilled birth attendants and received postnatal checkups in the first week after birth, it could avert maternal mortality by 80% and neonatal mortality by 66%.[ 14]  \r\nIn addition to the above research, evidence-based data and WHO recommendations as continuity of care (CoC) are tools to promote, protect and improve maternal and neonatal health. However, a small proportion of women in Ethiopia completed the continuum of a care pathway. For instance, a study in the Gamo zone showed that 42(9.7%) of participants had completed the continuum of maternity care [10].\r\nAnother study from Northwest Ethiopia found that around 47% of women have completed the continuum of care. However, this study may overestimate the report because this study considered women who had at least one ANC visit for the first level continuum of care [11]. In contrast, the most recent WHO recommendation is that every pregnant woman receives a minimum of eight Antenatal contacts for a positive pregnancy experience and to reduce perinatal mortality and morbidities. But still, it has not been implemented in Ethiopia [15].\r\nAccordingly, previous studies conducted in Ethiopia in general and Jimma Zone in particular primarily focused on isolated units of maternal health care services. However, studying each maternal health care service separately does not ensure every woman for receiving key elements of care starting from preconception to postpartum period. Also, the previous study mainly concentrated on individual-related factors by overlooking households, community, health facilities-related factors. However, considering multilevel factors could help to design suitable strategies.\r\nTo fill the above-listed gaps, this study aimed to assess the level of completion along the continuum of care for maternal, neonatal, and child health services and its determinant factors among postpartum women in Jimma Zone, Southwest, Ethiopia.','5.	Description of approaches/Methods\r\n5.1 Study area and design\r\nThis project will be done in Jimma Zone, Southwest, Ethiopia. Jimma zone is found in Oromia regional state and is located 352 km away from the capital city of the country, Ethiopia. According to the projection of the 2007 population census, the zone has an estimated population of 3,261,371 (49.9% women) in the year 2017. Of the total females, 23.1% were women of childbearing age (WCBA), which suggests a total of 753,377 WCBA residing in the zone. The zone has 21 districts,46 small urban and 512 rural kebeles. In addition ,Jimma zone has 770 health institutions, including health centers (n = 110), health posts (n = 486), private clinics (n = 156),public hospitals (one referral, six district hospitals, and one zonal hospital).The study period for this project will be from March to April,2022. \r\n5.2 Design \r\nA community-based cross-sectional mixed study design linked to health facility data will be employed.\r\n5.3 Population\r\nThe Source of the population for this study will be all mothers who started ANC at public health facilities and stayed for 6weeks to 6 months after their most recent childbirth. And the study population of this study will be all women who initiated ANC at health facilities and stayed 6weeks to 6months after giving their most recent birth and living in randomly selected woreda and kebele of Jimma Zone for a minimum six months\r\n5.4 Sample size determination and sampling technique\r\nThe sample size will be determined by using single population proportion formula with the following assumption proportion of completion maternity of care from 6weeks to six months 47% [11], 5% margin of error, 1.96 (CI),10% nonresponse, and assuming 2 design effects, the final sample size is 842. Multistage cluster sampling will be used. First, six (considering 30 % representativeness ) woreda will be selected by lottery methods. Second, two kebele from each eligible district(a total of 12 kebele) will be again selected by lottery methods. The final sample size will be proportionally allocated based on the population size of the kebeles. Preliminary census will be conducted for residence of the selected kebeles to get the number of mothers who have recent births and stayed from 6weeks to 6 months. A systematic sampling technique will be used to select household  Those households that have more than one eligible participant, will be selected by lottery method. The outcome variable of this project will be the completion of the continuum of care (ANC 4+, skilled birth attendants, and postnatal contacts).\r\nFor the qualitative study, 30 key informant interviews will be purposefully selected based on the richness of the information from the significant community members such as health extension workers, health development army leaders, traditional birth attendants, kebele leaders, etc.,). Four focused group discussions (FDGs) will be conducted on the key informant members like health extension workers, health development army leaders, women who gave birth, and their husbands. The participants of the FDGs will be consisting of 5 to 10 individuals.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nThe continuum of care will be considered to be complete when the following key points are met:\r\n1)	Women who received ANC 4 + during their most recent pregnancy\r\n2)	Attendance by skilled health care providers during labor and deliver\r\n3)	Received at least one postnatal contact within six weeks after delivery. Continuum of care is considered to be incomplete when one of the above three key component is missed.\r\n5.5 Data collection, analysis procedure and quality management\r\nThe questionnaire will be prepared by extensive search of different relevant literatures and considering the WHO recommended components of each continuum of care (Antenatal care, Skilled Birth Attendants, and Postnatal care components). The translation will be done by language experts to the local language (Afaan Oromo), then back into English to ensure consistency. The Afaan Oromo version will be used for data collection. A pretest will be done on 10% of the calculated sample size before actual data collection in out of the study settings on certain communities. The modification will be taken based on the result of the pretest. To carry out data collection at least a total of 25 data collectors and 10 supervisors will be recruited. The selected data collectors and supervisors will be taken a 2-day training on how to fill the questionnaire. Information on the purpose of the study, ethical concerns, and prevention measures of Covid-19 will be given. Data completeness and consistency will be checked, cleaned, and complied with daily.The collected data will be coded, entered to data manager version 4.6, and exported to SPSS version 23 for analysis. Bivariate logistic regression analysis will be used to show the significance of the association. The variable that shows the significant association in a bivariate analysis at p-value <0.2 will be entered into multivariable logistic regressions to control for confounding and the significance of association will be determined at 95% confidence interval and p-value <0.05. The qualitative data will be analyzed using the atlas ti.7.1. A thematic analysis approach through which the codes, categories, and themes can be developed from the data will be employed. The data will be transcribed verbatim, translated to English; the language through which the analysis will be carried out. Reading and re-reading of the data will be carried out to understand and extract the important concepts from the data, and line-by-line coding will be done. The codes will be merged to develop the categories, and the categories will be, in turn, merged to form themes. Then, the findings will be presented with themes, categories, and quotations from the data.\r\n5.6 Plan for dissemination and ensuring utilization of findings \r\nThe findings of this project will be disseminated to governmental and non-governmental stakeholders and policymakers. Finally, it will be submitted for publication in a peer-reviewed journal.','<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><strong><em><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\"><span style=\"color:#365f91\">References</span></span></span></em></strong></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\">&nbsp;</p>\r\n\r\n<ol>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">WHO, UNFPA, World Bank. The United Nations Population Division. Trends in maternal mortality: 1990 to 2015. 2015.</span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><a href=\"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/maternal-mortality\" style=\"color:blue; text-decoration:underline\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/maternal-mortality</span></span></a></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\"><span style=\"color:#333333\">UN. Sustainable Development Goals. 17 Goals to transform our world. New York: United Nations; 2015. http://www.un.org/ sustainable development/sustainable-development-goals/ [accessed 11 November 2016]. </span></span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\"><span style=\"color:#333333\">Central Statistics Agency [Ethiopia] and ICF .2016 .Ethiopian Demographic and Health Survey 2016.Addis Ababa ,Maryland, USA,CSA and ICF.</span></span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\"><span style=\"color:#333333\">National Health sector transformation plan of Ethiopia from 2021-2025</span></span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\"><span style=\"color:#333333\">Singh K., Story W.T., and Moran A.C., Assessing the continuum of care pathway for maternal health in South Asia and Sub-Saharan Africa. Maternal and child health journal, 2016. 20(2): p. 281&ndash;289. https:// doi.org/10.1007/s10995-015-1827-6 PMID: 26511130.</span></span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Duner A., Blomberg S., and Hasson H., Implementing a continuum of care model for older people&mdash; results from a Swedish case study. International Journal of Integrated Care, 2011. 11.</span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Kerber K.J., et al., Continuum of care for maternal, newborn, and child health: from slogan to service delivery. The Lancet, 2007. 370(9595): p. 1358&ndash;1369.</span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Wang, W. and R. Hong, Levels and determinants of continuum of care for maternal and newborn health in Cambodia-evidence from a population-based survey. 2015.</span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Haile D, Kondale M, Andarge E, Tunje A, Fikadu T, Boti N (2020) Level of completion along continuum of care for maternal and newborn health services and factors associated with it among women in Arba Minch Zuria woreda, Gamo zone, Southern Ethiopia: A community based cross-sectional study. PLoS ONE 15(6): e0221670</span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Asratie MH, Muche AA, Geremew AB (2020) Completion of maternity continuum of care among women in the post-partum period: Magnitude and associated factors in the northwest, Ethiopia. PLoS ONE 15(8): e023798</span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">WHO. A global review of the key interventions related to reproductive, maternal, newborn and child health (RMNCH). Geneva, Switzerland. 2011.</span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Darmstadt GL, Bhutta ZA, Cousens S, Adam T, Walker N, de Bernis L; Lancet Neonatal Survival Steering Team. Evidence-based, cost-effective interventions: how many newborn babies can we save? Lancet. 2005 Mar 12-18;365(9463):977-88. doi: 10.1016/S0140-6736(05)71088-6. PMID: 15767001).</span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">World Health Organization. Newborns: reducing mortality &ndash; Fact sheet, January 2016. Geneva: World Health Organization; 2016</span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">World Health Organization (WHO). WHO Recommendations on Antenatal Care for a Positive Pregnancy Experience: Summary. Geneva, Switzerland: WHO; 2018. Licence: CC BY-NC-SA 3.0 IGO.</span></span></span></span></li>\r\n</ol>','The overall goal of this project will be an assessment of the level of completion along the continuum of care for maternal, neonatal, and child health services and its determinant factors among postpartum women during COVID - 19 pandemic time in Jimma Zone, Southwest, Ethiopia ,from March to April ,2022.',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,2,'Keywords: Continuum of Care, Antenatal care, Skilled Birth Attendants, postpartum women,  Jimma zone.',10,'<p><strong><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\"><span style=\"color:#0e101a\">Work plan and budget</span></span></span></strong><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\"><span style=\"color:#0e101a\">: The overall study will be conducted from December to August,2022.The total budget required for this project is </span></span></span><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">385,000 </span></span><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\"><span style=\"color:#0e101a\">ETB</span></span></span></p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),(24,67,9,25,'Background: Currently, the rapid emergence of antimicrobial resistance against most antibiotics caused a need for finding new strategies to reverse the problems. Using traditional medicinal plants and their synergistic effects in combination with common antibiotics can be used as the source of research ideas to change the current treatment strategies.\r\nObjective: The present project is aimed to assess the knowledge and practice of traditional healers on the potential of Ethiopian medicinal plants against infectious diseases; to investigate in vitro antibacterial potential of five Ethiopian medicinal plants fractions (Zingiber officinale, Thymus vulgaris, Allium sativum, Mangifera indica, and Eucalyptus globulus) and in vitro and in vivo synergistic effect with selected antibiotics against some antibiotic-resistant bacterial strains.\r\nMethod: A cross-sectional descriptive study design will be conducted to assess the knowledge and practice of traditional healers in the Jimma Zone. The experimental study will be conducted in Jimma University and Jimma University Medical Center laboratories from February 2022 to January 2023.\r\nExpected outcome: The study results will provide evidence on the contribution of medicinal plants to reverse antimicrobial resistance and their synergistic effects in combination with antibiotics to reverse antimicrobial resistance. Additionally, it will generate data for further large-scale, well-designed in vivo studies (clinical trials) to provide more conclusive proof of their efficacy.\r\nBudget: The estimated budget to accomplish this project is 691,100 ETB.',NULL,'Investigating antibacterial potential of traditional medicinal plants and synergistic effect in combination with antibiotics: A new approach to reverse antimicrobial resistance','2021-11-19 15:09:10','1.	evaluating the knowledge and practice of traditional healers on the use of common Ethiopian traditional medicinal plants against infectious diseases\r\n2.	generating data or pieces of evidence on the in vitro antibacterial potential of common Ethiopian traditional medicinal plants against antibiotic-resistant and non-resistant bacterial strains and its use for developing new  antibiotics\r\n3.	generating data or pieces of evidence on the in vitro and in vivo synergistic effect of five Ethiopian medicinal plants fractions in combination with selected antibiotics against antibiotic-resistant bacteria and its contribution in reversing antimicrobial resistance \r\n4.	generating data for further large-scale, well-designed in vivo studies (application of clinical trial) to provide more conclusive proof of their efficacy','d4092210edbe81a7dbfe542b2462ea37','completed','The discovery of antibiotics has been one of the essential medical interventions in the 20th century. It plays a critical role in reducing mortality and morbidity caused by infectious diseases. So, rational use is required for safe and effective use for a long time.  However, there is inappropriate and irrational use of antibiotics. They can lead to the emergence and prevalence of resistant microbial populations. Now, the emergence and spread of antimicrobial resistance (AMR) is growing and posing a threat to global health. It created an unprecedented challenge to translational medicine and can make most of the antibiotics useless in therapeutics in the future [1-5]. \r\nIn 2050, the spread of AMR would lead to 10 million people dying every year [6]. Based on several recent reports, increased resistance of pathogens such as Pseudomonas aeruginosa, Acinetobacter baumannii, Enterobacteriaceae, Enterococcus faecium, Staphylococcus aureus, Helicobacter pylori, Salmonellae, Neisseria gonorrhoeae, Streptococcus pneumonia, Haemophilus influenza, Shigella species, Klebsiella pneumonia, and Escherichia coli need immediate concern [7-9]. WHO indicated that they are highly developing resistances against many antibiotics such as tetracycline, chloramphenicol, chloramphenicol, cloxacillin, gentamicin, ciprofloxacin, cefoxitin, vancomycin, carbapenem, erythromycin, ampicillin, cefotaxime and ceftazidime, and others [10-12]. Specifically, different studies reported the resistance of E.coli (from urine) to ciprofloxacin, gentamicin, tetracycline; Staphylococcus aureus to ampicillin, cotrimoxazole, gentamicin, amoxicillin, erythromycin, penicillin; Pseudomonas aeruginosa (from an ear infection) to tetracycline and gentamicin; Shigella species to cotrimoxazole and norfloxacin are reported [13].\r\nTherefore, the emergence and spread of pathogenic and resistant bacteria to many antibiotics (multidrug-resistant strains) need to find alternative chemotherapeutic agents to combat [7,14,15]. However, the design and development of new antibiotics is costly, time-consuming, and has been dramatically decreased [1,2,6,]. Additionally, combining antibiotics can make their adverse effects more pronounced [17]. Therefore, there is an urgent need to eradicate the infection, defeat the problems of resistance and side effects of the antimicrobial drugs that are currently used. Particularly, considering the development of botanicals to counter drug-resistant microbes is essential [18].\r\nSeveral researchers have seen the future, using herbal medicine could improve the current treatment strategies and be supplemental to overcome bacterial resistance [10,11,19-22]. It can be used to develop resistant-modifying agents. Accordingly, various evidences have demonstrated extracts of traditional medicinal plants such as Trigonella foenum-graecum (fenugreek), Nigella sativa (black cumin), Zingiber officinale (Ginger), Licorice, myrrh gum, Rosmarinus officinalis (rosemary), Thymus vulgaris (thyme), Citrus aurantium (bitter orange), eucalyptus extract, mango extract, and  Allium sativum (garlic) extract have antibacterial effects against several bacteria. These bacteria are Escherichia coli, Pseudomonas aeruginosa, Klebsiella pneumoniae, Staphylococcus aureus, Bacillus subtilis, Salmonella typhi, Streptococcus species, Enterococcus species, and others [11,23,24,25,26]. Specifically, Allium sativum or garlic had antibacterial effect against multidrug-resistant E.coli; Ocimum basilicum against Staphylococcus aureus, Zingiber officinale against Pseudomonas aeruginosa and Helicobacter pylori, Piper longum against Shigella [27,28].\r\nParticularly, synergistic effects of herbal medicine are being used as a source of research ideas and shifted researchers’ sights [2,16,25]. Synergy can occur through a variety of mechanisms, including pharmacodynamic synergism, pharmacokinetic synergism, and targeting disease resistance mechanisms [29,30]. Finally, it results in increased killing rate, potentiating of the drug, prevention of drug elimination, a better effect in vivo, etc. Moreover, it reduces the side effects produced by high doses of antibiotics [14,31]. \r\nIncreasing evidence has demonstrated that herbal extracts and antibiotics can have synergistic effects against antibiotic-resistant bacteria. In the future, these combinations of traditional medicinal plant extracts and antibiotics can provide a promising strategy or novel idea for combating antibiotic resistance. Particularly in China, many traditional Chinese medicines have flourished for thousands of years in clinical practice and have been documented as effective in treating various infectious diseases [4,11,32-34].\r\nIn Ethiopia, many hundreds of plants are used as herbal natural products and supplements as the primary mode of healthcare, especially for infectious diseases management [35]. Highly experienced traditional healers are a primary source of them and their use. These healers are generally older people. Their cumulative knowledge should be recorded and otherwise could be lost. Moreover, there has been no study done to investigate the synergistic effects of herbal medicine to solve the problem of AMR in Ethiopia. Thus, the present project is aimed to investigate in vitro antibacterial potential of five Ethiopian medicinal plants fractions (Zingiber officinale, Thymus vulgaris, Allium sativum, Mangifera indica, and Eucalyptus globulus) and in vitro and in vivo synergistic effect in combination with selected antibiotics (cloxacillin, gentamicin, ciprofloxacin, vancomycin, and ampicillin) against some antimicrobial-resistant bacteria chosen from the WHO antibiotic-resistant priority list and prevalence in Jimma University Medical Center. Additionally, it aimed to assess the knowledge and practice of traditional healers on selected traditional medicinal plants against infectious diseases in Jimma Zone, Southwest Ethiopia.','Methodology\r\n	Materials, bacteria and animal \r\n	Equipment and reagents: Mueller–Hinton broth and agar, Whatman filter paper, methanol, ethanol (98% of purity), dimethyl sulphoxide (99% of purity), hexane, ethyl acetate, etc. \r\n	Antimicrobial agents: cloxacillin, gentamicin, ciprofloxacin, vancomycin, ampicillin\r\n	Herbal medicine:  Zingiber officinale (Ginger), Thymus vulgaris (thyme), Allium sativum (garlic), mango and eucalyptus\r\n	Bacteria: Pseudomonas aeruginosa, Staphylococcus aureus, Streptococcus pneumonia, Haemophilus influenza, E.coli\r\n	Animal: Mice \r\n	Methods \r\n	Study area and period:\r\nThe study will be conducted in Jimma Zone, which is located 352 kms from the capital Addis Ababa in southwest of Ethiopia.  Jimma Zone is divided in to 15 woredas with an agro-ecological setting of highlands (15%), midlands (67%) and lowlands (18%). It has more than 2.5 million population size. The study period will be from February 2022 to January 2023.\r\n	Study design\r\n	For objective 1: A cross-sectional descriptive study design will be conducted. \r\n	For objective 2-4: Experimental study will be conducted in experimental laboratories (Pharmaceutics Laboratory and Pharmacology Laboratory of School of Pharmacy, Microbiology lab of Jimma University Medical Center (JUMC). \r\n	Population (For objective 1): \r\n	Source population:  All traditional healers in Jimma Zone.\r\n	Study population: Traditional healers living and actively practicing traditional healing using traditional medicinal plants in 15 woredas of Jimma Zone. \r\n	Inclusion and exclusion criteria (For objective one) \r\n	Inclusion criteria: Traditional healers actively practicing traditional treating of infectious diseases using traditional medicinal plants. \r\n	Exclusion criteria: Traditional healers who are not willing to respond. \r\n	 Sampling procedure and sample size estimation\r\n	For objective 1:  All traditional healers of fifteen (15) woredas of Jimma Zone will be purposively recruited as study participants. From them, all volunteers and who are actively practicing the traditional treatment of infectious diseases using traditional medicinal plants will be considered as study sample.\r\n	For objective 2-4: \r\n	Traditional medicinal plant selection and collection \r\nEthiopian medicinal plants will be selected following a review of various kinds of literature and data from traditional healers concerning their promising efficacy to treat infectious diseases. According to data from the pieces of literature review Zingiber officinale (Ginger), Thymus vulgaris (thyme), Allium sativum (garlic), Mangifera indica (mango), and eucalyptus have the promising antimicrobial potential [1,2,4,6,8,11,14,16,18,19,27,31]. Considering these data and availability in the study area [48], they are proposed for study and will be confirmed after data from the traditional healers. \r\nThe fresh parts of the aforementioned plants will be collected from the study area. After collection, taxonomic identities of the medicinal plants will be determined by a botanist from the Biology Department of Jimma University. The fresh plant materials will be carefully washed under running water to remove dust particles, air-dried at room temperature, and put in the shade in an aerated place till complete drying. Then they will be homogenized to a fine powder in a mechanical grinder and stored in airtight bottles. Each sample will be labeled with the date of collection and locality. \r\n	Antibiotics selection and collection\r\nAntibiotics will be purposively selected based on the emergency of AMR against them. Using data from WHO emergence and spread of AMR priority list and other study reviews [12,15,17,18,38] as a source, antimicrobials such as cloxacillin, gentamicin, ciprofloxacin, vancomycin, and ampicillin are proposed for study. In the study area, their bacterial resistance profile will be confirmed by conducting a pilot study in JUMC. They will be purchased from Ethiopian pharmaceutical industries such as Ethiopian Pharmaceuticals Manufacturing Factory, Addis Pharmaceutical Factory, or pharmacies.\r\n	Bacteria strains selection and collection\r\nBacteria strains will be selected based on various studies finding on their resistance development and effectiveness of medicinal plants against them, WHO antibiotic-resistant priority list [9,16-26,39-42], and their prevalence and availability in Jimma University Medical Center. Accordingly, five bacterial such as Pseudomonas aeruginosa, Staphylococcus aureus, Streptococcus pneumonia, Haemophilus influenza, and Escherichia coli are proposed. Their resistance profile will be confirmed by conducting a pilot study in JUMC. These bacteria control strains (ATCC) will be obtained or purchased from the Microbiology Laboratory of JUMC. Their antibiotic-resistant bacteria strains will be collected from infectious sputum, urine, ear infection, or other body fluid samples of critically ill patients [13] in JUMC by experienced laboratory technologists. Following collection, bacterial species will be sub-cultured into Standard Mueller-Hinton agar and/or broth (MHA and MHB) and incubated at 37 oC for 18 - 24 hours in the Microbiology Laboratory of School of Medical Laboratory, Jimma University. All isolates will be cultured and identified accordingly (using Gram staining) [10]. \r\n	Data collection tools\r\n	For objective 1\r\nA semi-structured questionnaire will be developed. Its contents can be classified into sessions containing socio-demographic characteristics, knowledge, and practice of traditional healers on the potential of medicinal plants such as Zingiber officinale, Thymus vulgaris, Allium sativum, Mangifera indica, and Eucalyptus globulus) against infectious diseases. The investigators will train eight supervisors and thirty data collectors. During fieldwork, traditional healers will be searched by using data from concerned bodies such as kebele leaders, health bureau, and local people. Before data collection, the traditional healers will be asked for voluntarism to respond to the interview. Trained healthcare professionals will collect the data via face-to-face interviews with voluntary traditional healers. \r\n	For objective 2- 4: Experimental tests \r\n	Extract preparation\r\nExtraction will be done using known standard procedures in Pharmaceutics Laboratory of School of Pharmacy, Jimma University. Extraction solvents are hexane (to obtain non-polar), ethyl acetate (to obtain amphiphilic), and ethanol (to obtain polar) [37]. Accordingly, each weighed-air-dried plant powder will be macerated or percolated with solvents (hexane, ethyl acetate, and ethanol separately) at room temperature for 24 hours. Then the extracts or fractions will be filtered through Whatman filter paper no.1. The filtrates will be evaporated at 40 °C in a water bath to complete dryness to give the plant extract. The extracts will then be kept in sterile bottles under refrigerated conditions [36].\r\n	Percentage yields\r\nThe dry extract will be weighted and the yields of extracts will be calculated using the following formula for each solvent.\r\n% yield=(mass of dried extract )/(mass  of dried plant part)\r\n	In vitro susceptibility testing\r\n	Determination of zone of inhibition \r\nThe antibacterial activity of the plant extracts against antibiotic-resistant bacteria and non-resistant bacteria will be tested using the agar well diffusion method. Nutrient agar as a media will be prepared according to the manufacturer’s instructions. It will be sterilized and carefully transferred into sterile Petri dishes and will be allowed to solidify after which the plates will be placed in the drier to remove excess moisture.  Then nutrient agar plates will be inoculated with each bacterial organism.\r\nFor sensitivity testing, different stock solutions such as 1,000 µg/ml, 2,000 µg/ml, 5,000 µg/ml, 8,000 µg/ml, and other concentrations will be prepared by suspending plant extracts in solvent (DMSO), accordingly. \r\nSix millimeters disc will be prepared by punching Whatman No. 1 filter paper. Then putting in the bottle, it will be sterilized in an oven at 160 oC for 15 min and allowed to cool. Then it will be soaked in stock solutions of medicinal plant extract alone and plant extract in combination with selected antibiotics, separately and dried for 30 minutes under aseptic condition. Gentamycin disk will be used as positive control and 6 mm DMSO impregnated Whatman paper will be used as a negative control. Finally, the plates will be incubated at 37 oC for 18 - 24 hrs. After incubation, the plates will be observed for the presence of clear zones of growth inhibition as evidence of antimicrobial activity. The zones, where present, the diameter will be measured and recorded in millimeters (mm) using a meter rule [38].\r\n	Determination of Minimum inhibitor concentration \r\nMinimal inhibitory concentrations (MICs) will be determined using the microplate dilution method for both antibiotic-resistant and non-resistant bacteria. The stock solution concentration used for zone of inhibition test will be prepared by dissolving the extracts in DMSO [47].\r\nDissolved plant fractions (100 μL) and gentamicin (100 μL) will be sequentially diluted two-fold with distilled water in a 96-well plate. Then, 100 μl of bacterial suspension (standardized to McFarland standard No. 0.5) will be added to each well. The sealed plates will be incubated at 37 °C for 24 h.  40 μl of p-iodonitrotetrazolium violet (INT) will be added to the microplate wells and incubated at 37 °C for 30–45 min to indicate bacterial growth. A pink/red color indicates presence of bacterial growth, and inhibition of bacterial growth is indicated by clear wells. Gentamicin will be used as a positive control (50 μg/ml) for gram-negative bacteria and oxacillin for gram-positive bacteria while the extract dissolving solvents (DMSO) will be used as negative controls. The assay will be performed in quadruplicates and repeated three times.\r\n	Determination of minimum bactericidal concentration\r\nMinimum bactericidal concentration (MBCs) will be  determined  by  standardized  broth  microdilution  techniques  with  starting  inoculums  of  5  ×  105 cfu/ml  after adjusting the turbidity with McFarland standard according to Clinical and Laboratory Standards Institute (CLSI)  guidelines  and  incubated  at  35oC  for  24  hours [39-41]. Visual growth inhibition with 0.1 mL aliquots from drug dilution wells will be plated onto MHA media for MBCs assay. The lowest drug concentration that yielded three or fewer microorganism colonies will be recorded as the MBC. They will be determined in triplicate, using concentrations ranging up to 20-48 μg/mL for all the fractions [41].\r\n	In vitro synergistic testing against antibiotic-resistant bacteria\r\nThe synergy potential of the selected antibiotics and medicinal plant combination will be measured by fractional inhibitory concentration with the checkerboard method. The  FIC  of  the  combination  will be  calculated  by  dividing  the MIC of  the  plant extract-antibiotic  combination  by  the  MIC  of plant extract or antibiotic  alone. The  FICI will be  obtained  by  adding  the FIC  of  plant extract  and  that  of  antibiotic.  \r\nThe FIC index was calculated with the following formulas [39,40,42]\r\nFICantibiotic=(MIC of antibiotic in combination)/(MIC of antibiotic alone)\r\nFICplant extract=(MIC of plant extract in combination)/(MIC of plant extract  alone)\r\n                         Then,    FICI = FICantibiotic+ FICplant extract \r\n	Animal toxicity study for the plant extracts\r\nAcute toxicity study will be carried out using the limit test dose of 2000mg/kg as has been described by Organization for Economic Cooperation and Development (OECD) guideline [49]. A total of five female  mice  will be fasted for  4  h and  will be administered  with  the  limit dose  2000  mg/kg of the crude extract of the plant extract.  The animals will then  be observed  individually for mortality and overt  signs  of toxicity during the  first 30 min, periodically during the first 24 hrs, with special  attention  given  during  the  first 4h  and  daily  afterward for  a total  of  14  days.  Any change in behaviors (breathing, alertness, diarrhea, motor activity, convulsions, restlessness, and coma) and mortality will be checked carefully.\r\n	In vivo synergistic testing against antibiotic-resistant bacteria\r\nMice will be obtained or purchased from the College of Agriculture and Veterinary Medicine, Jimma University, and use in the study. The study will be conducted in the Pharmacology Laboratory of the School of Pharmacy. The mice will be infected with selected bacteria (based on in vitro study data) and will be randomized into three groups. The first group will be served as the control and receive distilled water; the second group will receive conventional antibiotics alone, whereas the third group will receive a combination of antibiotics and plant extract after they develop an infection. After treatment, the sample will be taken from the study mice, and the results will be analyzed with microbiological cultures, and the combined effect will be evaluated [44,45].\r\n	Study variables\r\n	Dependent variables: \r\n	knowledge of traditional healers\r\n	practice of traditional healers\r\n	antimicrobial resistance\r\n	antibacterial effect\r\n	synergistic effect\r\n	Independent variables:\r\n	antibiotics \r\n	bacteria type\r\n	medicinal plant type\r\n	experience of traditional healers \r\n	Data analysis and interpretation \r\nData of medicinal plant extracts tested for one or more antibacterial activity and synergistic effects along with antibiotics will be included in the analysis. \r\nData will be analyzed using statistical tools like Pearson correlation odds ratio, Chi-square/Fisher’s exact test accordingly. Differences at p < 0.05 will be considered to be significant.\r\nFor herbal antimicrobial, the inhibition zone will be interpreted as an indicator of the sensitivity of the bacteria to the tested herbal antimicrobial. Accordingly, the test results will be interpreted based on inhibition zone diameter measured in mm as per guidelines of CLSI (where so ever applicable) as resistance, mildly susceptible, moderately susceptible, and highly susceptible [26,37,41,45,46]. The FICI results will be interpreted as follows: FICI< 0.5 indicates synergy, 0.5–0.75 indicates partial synergy, 0.76–1indicates an additive effect, 1–4 indifferent (or no effect); > 4 antagonistic [25,39,40,42).\r\n	Abbreviations:\r\n	AMR:  Antimcirobial resistance\r\n	CLSI: Clinical and Laboratory Standards Institute guidelines\r\n	FIC: fractional inhibitory  concentration  \r\n	FICI: fractional inhibitory  concentration  indices\r\n	MBC: Minimum bactericidal concentration \r\n	MIC: Minimum inhibitory concentration\r\nOperational definition\r\nInfectious disease: These diseases caused by microbes which includes urinary tract infection, respiratory infection, wound infections, ear infections, ophthalmic infection, etc.\r\nMedicinal plant (herbal extract): A plant or plant part or an extract or mixture of these used to maintain health and to prevent, alleviate, or cure disease\r\nSynergy: It occurs when the combination of two or more things produces outcomes greater than the sum of the individual parts [4].\r\nTraditional healers: They are healers who are experienced and known in the community, kebele or neighbors by giving traditional medicinal plants.','<h1><span style=\"font-size:14pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Cambria,serif\"><span style=\"color:#365f91\"><a name=\"_Toc88144695\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">References</span></span></a></span></span></span></h1>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">1. <span style=\"background-color:white\">Gill EE, Franco OL, Hancock RE. Antibiotic adjuvants: diverse strategies for controlling drug-resistant pathogens.&nbsp;<em>Chem Biol Drug Des</em>. 2015;85(1):56-78.</span></span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"background-color:white\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">2. </span></span></span><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">Mundy, Lorna, Pendry, Barbara, Rahman, Mukhlesur. Antimicrobial resistance and synergy in herbal medicine.Journal of Herbal Medicine. </span></span><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><a href=\"http://dx.doi.org/10.1016/j.hermed.2016.03.001\" style=\"color:blue; text-decoration:underline\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\">http://dx.doi.org/10.1016/j.hermed.2016.03.001</span></a></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">3.<span style=\"background-color:white\"> Habtom GK. Perceptions and attitudes of modern and traditional medical practitioners about traditional medical practice in Eritrea. <em>Int J Complement Alt Med</em>. 2018;11(1):6-19.&nbsp;</span></span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">4. Bao, Zhang, Liu <em>et al.</em>Synergistic effects of anti-MRSA herbal extracts combined with antibiotics. <em>FutureMicrobiol. </em>(2020); 15(13), 1265&ndash;1276.</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">5. </span></span><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">Davey, P., M.H. Wilcox, W. Irving and G. Thwaites, 2015. Antimicrobial Chemotherapy. 7th Edn., Oxford University Press, United Kingdom, Pages: 432.</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">6. </span></span><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><a href=\"https://www.microbiologyresearch.org/search?value1=Qiqi+He&amp;option1=author&amp;noRedirect=true\" style=\"color:blue; text-decoration:underline\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"background-color:white\">Qiqi He</span></span></a></span><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">, 2019. <span style=\"background-color:white\">Antibacterial activity of traditional herbal medicine. </span></span></span><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1099/acmi.ac2019.po0266\" style=\"color:blue; text-decoration:underline\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"background-color:white\">https://doi.org/10.1099/acmi.ac2019.po0266</span></span></a></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">7. <span style=\"background-color:white\">Snow Setzer M, Sharifi-Rad J, Setzer WN. The search for herbal antibiotics: an in-silico investigation of antibacterial phytochemicals. <em>Antibiotics (Basel).</em> 2016 Sep 12;5(3):30.</span></span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">8. Nakazawa H, Kikuchi Y, Honda T, Isago T, Nozaki M. Enhancement of</span></span> <span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">antimicrobial effects of various antibiotics against methicillin-resistant</span></span> <span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">Staphylococcus aureus (MRSA) by combination with fosfomycin. <em>J Infect</em></span></span><em> </em><em><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">Chemother</span></span></em><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">. 2003;9:304&ndash;9</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">9. </span></span><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><a href=\"https://www.testtargettreat.com/en/home/news-events/who_published_list_bacteria.html\" style=\"color:blue; text-decoration:underline\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\">https://www.testtargettreat.com/en/home/news-events/who_published_list_bacteria.html</span></a></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">10. Bhoj Raj Singh, Akanksha Yadav, Dharmendra Kumar Sinha and Obli Rajendran Vinodh Kumar, 2020. Potential of Herbal Antibacterials as an Alternative to Antibiotics for Multiple Drug Resistant Bacteria: An Analysis.&nbsp;<em>Research Journal of Veterinary Sciences, 13: 1-8.</em></span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><em><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"background-color:#deeaf8\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">11. </span></span></span></em><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">Kuok <em>et al</em>. Synergistic antibacterial effects of herbal extracts and antibiotics on methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus: A computational and experimental study. Experimental Biology and Medicine 2017; 242: 731&ndash;743</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">12. </span></span><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><a href=\"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/antimicrobial-resistance\" style=\"color:blue; text-decoration:underline\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\">https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/antimicrobial-resistance</span></a></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">13. <span style=\"background-color:white\">Mulu W, Abera B, Yimer M, Hailu T, Ayele H, Abate D. Bacterial agents and antibiotic resistance profiles of infections from different sites that occurred among patients at DebreMarkos Referral Hospital, Ethiopia: a cross-sectional study. BMC research notes. 2017 Dec; 10(1):1-9.</span></span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">14. Bhardwaj et al.,</span></span><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"> Potential of Herbal Drug and Antibiotic Combination Therapy: A New Approach to Treat Multidrug Resistant Bacteria.</span></span><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"> Pharm Anal Acta 2016, 7:11</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">15. Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC). Antibiotic Resistance Threats in the United States; CDC: Atlanta, GA, USA, 2013.</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">16.<span style=\"background-color:white\"> Su T, Qiu Y, Hua X, Ye B, Luo H, Liu D, Qu P, Qiu Z. Novel Opportunity to Reverse Antibiotic Resistance: To Explore Traditional Chinese Medicine With Potential Activity Against Antibiotics-Resistance Bacteria. Front Microbiol. 2020 Dec 22;11:610070.&nbsp;</span></span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">17. Garnacho-Montero J, <em>et al</em>. Clinical efficacy and safety of the combination of colistin plus vancomycin for the treatment of severe infections caused by carbapenem-resistant Acinetobacter baumannii. Chemotherapy 2013;59:225&ndash;31</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">18. P.D. Gupta, T.J. Birdi. Development of botanicals to combat antibiotic resistance.&nbsp; <em>Journal of Ayurveda and Integrative Medicine</em> 8 (2017) 266e275</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">19. </span></span><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">Basappa, K, and J. Venugopal, 2013. Natural alternatives to antibiotic agents. <em>Asian J. Biomed. Pharmaceut. Sci</em>.</span></span><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"background-color:white\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"> Volume 3, Issue 25</span></span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">20. </span></span><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">Ionescu, M.I., 2017. Are herbal products an alternative to antibiotics? Bact. Pathogen. Antibact. Cntrl. Sahra, 10.5772/intechopen.72110</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">21. </span></span><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">Buhner, S.H., 2012. Herbal antibiotics: Natural alternatives for treating drug-resistant bacteria. 2nd Edn., Storey Publishing, USA, Pages: 480.</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">22. </span></span><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">Chandra, H., P. Bishnoi, A. Yadav, B. Patni, A.P. Mishra and A.R. Nautiyal, 2017. Antimicrobial resistance and the alternative resources with special emphasis on plant based antimicrobials-A review. Plants, Vol. 6. 10.3390/plants6020016.</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">23. Stephen Harrod Buhner. Herbal antibiotics. </span></span><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">Natural alternatives for treating drug-resistant bacteria. Edwards Brothers Malloy. 2012</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">24. </span></span><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">Ravi BS et al. Antibacterial effects of natural herbal</span></span><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"> extracts on <em>Streptococcus mutans</em>: Can they be potential additives in dentifrices? <em>International Journal of Dentistry</em>. Volume 2017</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">25. Md Shahadat Hossan et al (2018) Antibacterial effects of 18 medicinal plants used by the Khyang tribe in Bangladesh, Pharmaceutical Biology, 56:1, 201-208</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">26. Gonelimali FD et al. Antimicrobial properties and mechanism of action of some plant extracts against food pathogens and spoilage microorganisms. Front. Microbiol. 9:1639.</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">27. <span style=\"background-color:white\">Bhardwaji M, Singh BR, Sinha DK, Kumar V, PrasannaVadhana OR, Varan Singh S. Potential of herbal drug and antibiotic combination therapy: Areviewapproche to treat multidrug resistant bacteria. Pharm. Anal. Acta. 2016;7(11):523.</span></span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">28. </span></span><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"background-color:white\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">Chandra H, Bishnoi P, Yadav A, Patni B, Mishra AP, Nautiyal AR. Antimicrobial resistance and the alternative resources with special emphasis on plant-based antimicrobials&mdash;a review. Plants. 2017 Jun;6(2):16.</span></span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">29. </span></span><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">Lindsay K. Caesar and Nadja B. Cech. Synergy and antagonism in natural product extracts: when 1 + 1 does not equal 2. Nat. Prod. Rep., 2019, 36, 869-888</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">30. </span></span><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"background-color:white\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">Boucher HW, Talbot GH, Bradley JS, Edwards JE, Gilbert D, Rice LB, Scheld M, Spellberg B, Bartlett J. Bad bugs, no drugs: no ESKAPE! An update from the Infectious Diseases Society of America. Clin Infect Dis. 2009 Jan 1;48(1):1-12.</span></span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"background-color:white\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">31. </span></span></span><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">Nascimento GGF, Locatelli J, Freitas PC, Silv GL (2000) Antibacterial activity of plant extracts and phytochemicals on antibiotic-resistant bacteria. <em>Braz J Microbiol</em> 31: 247-256.</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">32. </span></span><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">An J, Zuo GY, Hao XY, Wang GC, Li ZS. Antibacterial and synergy of a flavanonol rhamnoside with antibiotics against clinical isolates of methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus (MRSA). Phytomedicine. 2011;18:990&ndash;3</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">33. Zuo GY, Han ZQ, Hao XY, Han J, Li ZS, Wang GC. Synergy of aminoglycoside antibiotics by 3-Benzylchroman derivatives from the Chinese drug Caesalpinia sappan against clinical methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus (MRSA). Phytomedicine 2014;21:936&ndash;41</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">34. Zuo GY, Zhang XJ, Yang CX, Han J, Wang GC, Bian ZQ. Evaluation of traditional Chinese medicinal plants for anti-MRSA activity with reference to the treatment record of infectious diseases. Molecules. 2012;17:2955&ndash;67</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">35. </span>