an style=\"font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">Betts JW, Murphy C, Kelly SM, Haswell SJ. Synergistic antibacterial effects of theaflavin in combination with ampicillin against hospital isolates of Stenotrophomonasmaltophilia. Journal of microbiology, biotechnology and food sciences. 2012 Jan 1; 2(3):1068-78.</span></span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">39.</span></span><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"background-color:white\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"> An J, Zuo GY, Hao XY, Wang GC, Li ZS. Antibacterial and synergy of a flavanonolrhamnoside with antibiotics against clinical isolates of methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus (MRSA). Phytomedicine. 2011 Aug 15; 18(11):990-3.</span></span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">40.</span></span><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"background-color:white\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"> Zuo GY, Han ZQ, Hao XY, Han J, Li ZS, Wang GC. Synergy of aminoglycoside antibiotics by 3-Benzylchroman derivatives from the Chinese drug Caesalpiniasappan against clinical methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus (MRSA). Phytomedicine. 2014 Jun 15; 21(7):936-41.</span></span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">41.</span></span><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"background-color:white\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"> Zuo GY, Zhang XJ, Yang CX, Han J, Wang GC, Bian ZQ. Evaluation of traditional Chinese medicinal plants for anti-MRSA activity with reference to the treatment record of infectious diseases. Molecules. 2012 Mar; 17(3):2955-67.</span></span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"background-color:white\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">42. Kuok CF, Hoi SO, Hoi CF, Chan CH, Fong IH, Ngok CK, Meng LR, Fong P. Synergistic antibacterial effects of herbal extracts and antibiotics on methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus: A computational and experimental study. Experimental Biology and Medicine. 2017 Apr; 242(7):731-43.</span></span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"background-color:white\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">43. Lee YS, Han CH, Kang SH, LEE SJ, Kim SW, Shin OR, SIM YC, LEE SJ, CHO YH. Synergistic effect between catechin and ciprofloxacin on chronic bacterial prostatitis rat model. International journal of urology. 2005 Apr;12(4):383-9.</span></span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"background-color:white\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">44. Muinat FA, Ahmed OK. Antimicrobial and toxic potentials of the aqueous. development. 2014; 6(7):46.</span></span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"background-color:white\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">45.</span></span></span><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"> CLSI, 2006. Methods for antimicrobial dilution and disk susceptibility testing of infrequently isolated or fastidious bacteria. Clinical and Laboratory Standards Institute, Wayne, PA., USA.</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"background-color:white\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">46. </span></span></span><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">Clinical and laboratory standards lnstitute, 2014. Performance standards for antimicrobial disk susceptibility tests. 24<sup>th</sup> informational supplement, Document M100-S24 and M11 A8.Clinical and Laboratory Standards Institute, Wayne, Pennsylvania.Pages 249. ISBN 1-56238-805-3.</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">47. </span></span><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">Eloff, N.J., 1998. A sensitive and quick microplate method to determine the minimal inhibitory concentration of plant extracts for bacteria. Planta Medica 64, 711&ndash;713. Minimal bactericidal concentration (MBC).</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"background-color:white\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">48. Jima TT, Megersa M. Ethnobotanical Study of Medicinal Plants Used to Treat Human Diseases in Berbere District, Bale Zone of Oromia Regional State, South East Ethiopia.&nbsp;<em>Evid Based Complement Alternat Med</em>. 2018;2018:8602945. Published 2018 Jul 17. doi:10.1155/2018/8602945</span></span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">49. </span></span><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">Organization of Economic Co-operation and Development (2008) The OECD guidelines for testing of chemical: Acute oral toxicity- fixed dose procedure. OECD publishing, France</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\">&nbsp;</p>','The present project is aimed to assess the knowledge and practice of traditional healers on the potential of Ethiopian medicinal plants against infectious diseases; to investigate in vitro antibacterial potential of five Ethiopian medicinal plants fractions (Zingiber officinale, Thymus vulgaris, Allium sativum, Mangifera indica, and Eucalyptus globulus) and in vitro and in vivo synergistic effect with selected antibiotics against some antibiotic-resistant bacterial strains chosen from the WHO antibiotic-resistant priority list.',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,2,'traditional medicinal plant,synergistic effect,antimicrobial resistance,antimicrtobial resistant-bacteria,antimicrobial effects',10,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),(25,87,9,12,'Abstract\r\nBackground: In the last decade, resilience has emerged as a key concept for health systems in global health. Building on work from other disciplines, health systems resilience is generally understood as the capacity of a system to absorb adapt or transform in order to maintain essential functions when faced with shocks and stresses. Hospital preparedness to cope with disasters is an important part of health system programs to reduce loss and disabilities.\r\nObjective: To assess resilience of Public health facilities in the context of crisis management, preparedness and response in Jimma zone, south western Ethiopia, 2021.\r\nMethods: Health institution based cross-sectional study design complemented with a qualitative method will be conducted. The study will be included all (9) public hospitals (five primary hospitals (Dimtu, Seka, Nada, Dedo and Setema), three general hospitals (Agaro, Limmu Genet and Shenen Gibe) and one referral hospital (Jimma medical center)) found in Jimma zone. A pretested structured questionnaire will be used to collect data for quantitative part and for the qualitative study part, a semi-structured  guide  will  be  developed  considering  the  research  questions  or  objectives and data will be collected through key informant. The number of KIs will dependent on information saturation, i.e., the interviews will be ended as soon as the new interviewee reiterated what had already been stated. Descriptive  statistics  like  frequencies,  percentages,  mean,  and  standard deviations  will  be  carried  out. The variables with a p-value < 0.25 in the  bivariable  analysis  will  be  taken  as  candidates  for  multivariable  analysis.  Finally, multivariable logistic regression analysis will be done. The adjusted odds ratio (AOR) with respective  95%  CI  and  a  p-value  of  less  than  0.05  will  be  taken  to  declare  statistically significant.  The qualitative data will be analyzed using the atlas ti.7.1.  A  thematic  analysis  approach through  which  the  codes,  categories,  and  themes  can  be  developed  from  the  data  will  be employed. The data will be transcribed verbatim, translated to English; the language through which the analysis will be carried out.\r\nWork Plan and Budget: A gross of 440,000 Ethiopian Birr is required to undertake this study.','Level of resilience of Public health facilities  preparedness for crisis management','RESELIENCE OF PUBLIC HEALTH FACILITIES IN THE CONTEXT OF CRISIS MANAGEMENT, PREPAREDNESS AND RESPONSE IN JIMMA ZONE, SOUTH WESTERN ETHIOPIA',NULL,'Research outcome/impact\r\n	Level of public health facilities preparedness for crisis management will be identified.\r\n	Public health facilities crisis management and emergency preparedness plan, and response strategies will be assessed.\r\n	Public health facilities level of preparedness on the continuity of essential health service and surge capacity will be determined.\r\n	Leadership capacities and coordination on crisis management and emergency preparedness will be assessed.','7bc7379757c936e02cf11ab4c6ef5632',NULL,'Back ground and rationale\r\nThe United Nation define Resilience as it is “the ability of individuals, households, communities, cities, institutions, systems and societies to prevent, resist, absorb, adapt, respond and recover positively, efficiently and effectively when faced with a wide range of risks, while maintaining an acceptable level of functioning and without compromising long-term prospects for sustainable development, peace and security, human rights and well-being for all”(1).\r\n Resilience is a key factor in the SDGs and a central mechanism for making progress in pursuing the sustainable development agenda. The SDGs call for global mobilization to tackle issues of widespread public concern. The elimination of poverty, hunger, climate change, disease, food insecurity, and environmental degradation, the strengthening of preparedness for man-made and natural disasters and the sustainability of the planet’s ecosystems are some of the global challenges included in the 2030 agenda and its 17 SDGs and 169 associated targets. The SDGs have greatly increased the profile of resilience. Sustainable development requires sustainable societies, and resilience comprises a key element (2). \r\n In the last decade, resilience has emerged as a key concept for health systems in global health. Building on work from other disciplines, health systems resilience is generally understood as the capacity of a system to absorb adapt or transform in order to maintain essential functions when faced with shocks and stresses. Hospital preparedness to cope with disasters is an important part of health system programs to reduce loss and disabilities. In fact, the international slogan of preparedness for coping with emergencies and disasters, especially hospital preparedness, is the main program in disaster management at the national level, especially in disaster-prone countries (3). On other hand, effective management of these destructive and damaging events depends on predicting the problems associated with these events and planning to respond to them effectively. Disasters and crises can strike at any time. They can strike at any time, wreaking havoc on communities and resulting in significant human misery and loss of life. Individuals and communities become even more vulnerable when national and local systems, particularly health systems, are ill-prepared to deal with a crisis. A crisis\'s abrupt rise in demand for crucial health services often overwhelms health systems and organizations, leaving them unable to offer the lifesaving interventions required(4).\r\nIn the past quarter-century nearly 4 million lives have been lost and trillions of dollars in related damages have been seen worldwide due to natural disasters. Besides their devastating effects on human lives and economy, disasters place heavy burdens on health care facilities. There have been countless cases of health infrastructures from sophisticated hospitals to small but vital health centers that have suffered this fate(5).\r\nDisease outbreaks and public health crises result in significant human suffering, death, and financial damage. As seen in recent epidemics of cholera, drug-resistant tuberculosis, Ebola virus illness, and Zika virus disease, such occurrences strain already frail health systems. The current COVID-19 pandemic is putting the world\'s health systems to the test, including well-resourced systems in Europe and North America, where health institutions and public health agencies are overburdened, diagnostics are in short supply, triage systems are failing, personal protective equipment is inadequate, and front-line health workers are at risk of disease and death(6).\r\nThe Covid-19 pandemic reminded African countries that population health is the foundation on which success in social, economic and development progress is built. Most countries have poor health infrastructure and weak health systems that are unable to provide quality care to patients. Covid-19 has further worsened the already fragile foundation of Africa’s health services and caused sharp decline in the provision of key life-saving essential health services. Across the region, patients have struggled to access essential services including maternal and child care services, chronic care for diabetes and HIV/AIDS, treatment of confirmed malaria cases and childhood vaccination programs. Some 14 countries evaluated by WHO have recorded a 50% decline in services within 3 months compared to the same period in 2019. Ten months of the Covid-19 pandemic have wiped away an entire decade of economic development resulting in 50 million Africans who will fall below the poverty line of living on $1.5 a day(7).\r\nCountries must prioritize investing in strengthening health infrastructure in order to improve pandemic preparedness and the resilience of health systems by having widely accessible primary healthcare services, an adequately skilled workforce and quality health products needed for effective disease management provided at a cost that people can afford. Without resilient health systems with adequate health infrastructure, any economic progress African countries make is extremely fragile and unsustainable(7). \r\nEthiopia has made impressive strides to strengthen public health systems over the past decade. However, results have been uneven, with both quality of services and equitable access remaining as challenges. Recurring drought, conflict and disease outbreaks often hamper the country’s progress towards its goal to provide high quality health services to all of its citizens(8).\r\nIn a recent study done in selected hospitals of western Ethiopia shows that the overall level of emergency and disaster preparedness in the selected hospitals was weak with an average calculated preparedness score of 45.6%(9). This study is to look into the public hospitals\' preparedness for crisis management in the Jimma zone. Nine public hospitals in Jimma zone are among the prospective institutes to be studied by this study.\r\nHospitals and health institutions are critical in delivering health services to their communities, especially in times of crisis and emergency. In large-scale calamities, well-prepared and disaster-resilient hospitals are needed to handle the increased demand for life-saving services, which can soon overrun the surge capacity and functional safety of hospitals and the health system as a whole. To enhance emergency preparedness to respond effectively to the most likely catastrophe situations, as well as boost overall health system preparedness using an all-hazards strategy by strengthening hospital crisis management capabilities. In addition to make sure sustainability of routine essential health care services despite emergencies or disasters.\r\nDespite the fact that it is worthy of intellectual attention and resources, few research on Ethiopia\'s health sector disaster preparedness have been conducted. In this study, we shall describe the state of crisis management readiness in Jimma zone public hospitals. This will serve as a benchmark for measuring future progress on the impact of crisis preparedness interventions and initiatives, as well as a roadmap for developing crisis readiness and response plans. It will also be utilized as an input for any interested body that requires a researcher\'s reference.','Description of approaches/Methods\r\nStudy setting\r\nThe study will be conducted in public hospitals in Jimma zone, Oromia regional state, southwestern Ethiopia. Jimma zone is one of the largest zones in the regional state of Oromia and located 352 km away from the capital city of the country, Addis Ababa. It is categorized administratively into 21 districts. The zone has 603 public health facilities, including hospitals (one referral, five primary hospitals, and three general hospital), health centers (n=110) and health posts (n= 486). \r\nStudy design and period\r\nHealth institution based cross-sectional study design complemented with a qualitative method will be conducted from January, 2021to January, 2022.\r\nSource population:\r\nAll public health facilities, healthcare professionals and documents used to manage health facilities in jimma zone will be source population.\r\nStudy population\r\nAll hospitals, health professionals and documents fulfilled eligibility criteria \r\n\r\nEligibility criteria\r\nAll primary, general and referral hospitals which give services at least for one year before data collection period, voluntary healthcare professionals and complete documents used for hospital management will be included in study.\r\nSample size determination \r\nThe study will be included all (9) public hospitals (five primary hospitals (Dimtu, Seka, Nada, Dedo and Setema), three general hospitals (Agaro, Limmu Genet and Shenen Gibe) and one referral hospital (Jimma medical center)) found in Jimma zone. There are 2131 health professionals and 1694 support and administrative staff working in public hospitals of jimma zone. \r\nThe sample size required for the health workers will be calculated by using a single population proportion formula.\r\nn=Z^2 P(1-P)/d^2\r\nWhereas n= desired sample size, Z=level of significance at 95% confidence interval, p=maximum expected proportion (0.5) because no previous study; d= margin of error (5%)\r\n((1.96)^2 (0.5)(0.5))/(0.05)^2 =384\r\nSince the number of population is less than 10,000, the sample size will be adjusted. Therefore, the corrected sample size will be calculated as follows \r\nnf=n/(1+n/N)\r\nWhere, nf =desired sample size n = sample from infinite population, N =population size (2131 health professionals and 1694 support and administrative staff working in public hospitals of jimma zone).  \r\nnf=384/(1+384/3825)=350\r\nAdding 10% for non-response rate, the final sample size will be calculated as:\r\nn=350*0.1+350=385\r\nThen, we will be allocated this sample to both health professionals and administrative staffs.   Accordingly, 215 health care professionals and 171 administrative workers will be included from 9 hospitals.\r\nFor key informants (KI), a  purposive  sampling  technique  will be  used  to  recruit  study  participants  from  each hospitals. The participants will be selected based on their role and service year in the area of the issue under investigation.  The number of interviewees will be   determined based on assumed information saturation. Interviews will be completed when the participants began repeating what has been said.\r\nData collection procedure (instrument, personnel and technique)\r\nThe World Health Organization and regional office for European tools for studying strengthening health-system emergency preparedness will be adapted to collect data about Public health facilities preparedness for crisis management (reference to Toolkit for assessing health-system capacity for crisis management).Data will be collected by self-administered questioners and reviewing documents. For the qualitative study part, a semi-structured  guide  will  be  developed  considering  the  research  questions  or  objectives and data will be collected through key informant. The number of KIs will dependent on information saturation, i.e., the interviews will be ended as soon as the new interviewee reiterated what had already been stated. Also, the selection of the participants will be taken into account the service year and the role in the health facilities management. COVID-19 transmission prevention methods and precaution will be strictly followed during data collections. The data will be collected by seven data collectors (4BSc in Nurses and 3 Public Health) and supervised by 2 MPH professionals. \r\nData collection procedures\r\nStructured questionnaires and checklists will be developed to capture important data form documents. For qualitative part: In-depth face-to-face interviews will be conducted with key informants (KIs). The number of KIs will dependent on information saturation, i.e., the interviews will be ended as soon as the new interviewee reiterated what had already been stated. Also, the selection of the participants will be taken into account the service year and the role in the health facilities management.\r\nData analysis technique\r\nData quality assurance\r\nPretest will be conducted on 5% of the sample size at the hospitals of Buno Bedelle zone. The purpose of pretest is to ensure the accuracy of the tools and to determine the estimated amount of time needed to complete the tools. Data collectors of relevant backgrounds will be recruited to gather all relevant data after taking training on the objective of the study and data collection procedure.   \r\nData management and statistical analysis\r\nThe collected data will be checked for completeness, clarity, and accuracy then it will be entered into Epidata version 4.4.1 and exported a statistical package for social science (SPSS) version 23.  The exported data will be explored to check outliers and missing values and, finally analyzed. Descriptive  statistics  like  frequencies,  percentages,  mean,  and  standard deviations  will  be  carried  out. The variables with a p-value < 0.25 in the  bivariable  analysis  will  be  taken  as  candidates  for  multivariable  analysis.  Finally, multivariable logistic regression analysis will be done. The adjusted odds ratio (AOR) with respective  95%  CI  and  a  p-value  of  less  than  0.05  will  be  taken  to  declare  statistically significant.  The qualitative data will be analyzed using the atlas ti.7.1.  A  thematic  analysis  approach through  which  the  codes,  categories,  and  themes  can  be  developed  from  the  data  will  be employed. The data will be transcribed verbatim, translated to English; the language through which the analysis will be carried out. Reading and re-reading of the data will be carried out to understand and extract the important concepts from the data, and line-by-line coding will be done. The codes will be merged to develop the categories, and the categories will be, in turn, merged to form themes. Then, the findings will be presented with themes, categories, and quotations from the data.\r\nPlan for dissemination and ensuring utilization of findings \r\nThe  results  of  this  research  project  will  be  presented  and  submitted  to  the  Research  and Postgraduate Coordinating Office of Jimma University. An attempt will be made to present at different conferences and symposiums. Copies of the document will be given to the respective stakeholders  so  that  it  will  be  used  as  a  source  of  information  for  possible  planning  and implementation of health intervention. An effort will be made for possible publication on a reputable journal.','<p>&nbsp;</p>\r\n\r\n<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><strong><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">References</span></span></strong></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">1. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Chief Executives Board for Coordination Report of the High-Level Committee on Programmes at its thirty-fourth session. 2017. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">2. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; 70/1. Transforming our world: the 2030 Agenda for Sustainable Development Transforming our world: the 2030 Agenda for Sustainable Development Preamble. 2015. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">3. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Charney RL, Rebmann T, Esguerra CR, Lai CW, Dalawari P. Public perceptions of hospital responsibilities to those presenting without medical injury or illness during a disaster. J Emerg Med. 2013 Oct;45(4):578&ndash;84. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">4. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Khankeh HR, Zavareh DK-. Why the Prominent Improvement in Prehospital Medical Response in Iran Couldn&rsquo;t Decrease the Number of Death Related Road Traffic Injuries. J Trauma Treat. 2012;01(04). </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">5. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Eyre A. LITERATURE AND BEST PRACTICE REVIEW AND ASSESSMENT: IDENTIFYING PEOPLE&rsquo;S NEEDS IN MAJOR EMERGENCIES AND BEST PRACTICE IN HUMANITARIAN RESPONSE [Internet]. 2006. Available from: www.wcmt.org.uk/public/reports/60_1.pdf</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">6. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Durski KN, Osterholm M, Majumdar SS, Nilles E, Bausch DG, Atun R. Shifting the paradigm: Using disease outbreaks to build resilient health systems. Vol. 5, BMJ Global Health. BMJ Publishing Group; 2020. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">7. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; WHO. COVID-19 hits life-saving health services in Africa &mdash; WHO [Internet]. 2020. Available from: https://thestreetjournal.org/covid-19-hits-life-saving-health-services-in-africa-who/</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">8. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Ethiopia U. USAID Ethiopia Fact Sheet - Building Resilience - October 2020. 2020. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">9. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Woyessa AH, Teshome M, Mulatu B, Abadiga M, Hiko N, Kebede B. Disaster preparedness in selected hospitals of Western Ethiopia and risk perceptions of their authorities. Open Access Emerg Med. 2020;12:219&ndash;25. </span></span></span></span></p>','to assess resilience of Public health facilities in the context of crisis management, preparedness and response in Jimma zone, south western Ethiopia',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,'resilience,crisis,response,preparedness',0,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),(26,27,9,20,'Background: Labour pain is as severe as the pain of broken bone. Even though different pharmacological labor pain management methods such as epidural analgesia, pethidine, and others are available, they are associated with significant adverse effects on both fetuses and laboring women. Thus, developed countries use laboring in warm water as effective labor pain management with its much more positive effect on the progress of labor and feto-maternal condition.  \r\nObjectives: To assess the effectiveness of laboring in warm water as non-pharmacological management of labor pain; effect on  fetal and maternal outcomes; and associated factors among laboring women in Jimma town public health facilities, 2021/2022\r\nMethodology: This study will be conducted in two hospitals and four health centers of Jimma town by using the randomized controlled trials. 312 study participants will be recruited half as a control group and half as an interventional group. The control group will pass through conventional labor while the intervention group will experience the full duration of their labor in warm water contained in a large bathing pool. The standard tool will be used to collect data from both the control and the interventional group from the beginning of labor to the end of the fourth stage of labor. Data will be entered by Epidata version 4.2 and analyzed by SPSS version 23.  \r\nWork plan and budget. This study will be conducted from December/2021 to June/2022 and it requires 518,017.5 ETB total budgets.','How effective laboring in warm water is in managing labor pain?','Effectiveness of laboring in warm water as non-pharmacological management of labor pain; effect on fetal and maternal outcomes; and associated factors among laboring women in Jimma town public health facilities: Randomized controlled trial','2021-11-18 18:16:30','This research will have the following four major output/outcomes.\r\n\r\n	The effectiveness of laboring in warm water in managing labor pain will be revealed and it will be used as effective non-pharmacological labor pain management in the future. \r\n	Also, the effect of laboring in warm water on fetal and maternal outcomes will be identified and necessary precaution methods will be made depending on the result for future practice. \r\n	In addition, factors associated with the effectiveness of laboring in warm water in managing labor pain will be identified thereby directing appropriate arrangements to be undertaken for better outcomes in the future.\r\n	 Moreover, factors associated with the effect of laboring in warm water on the fetal and maternal outcomes will be revealed. Thus,   necessary arrangements will be made in the future to maximize positive outcomes.','43bd162f5f72698ac6dd94b7a7258a57','completed','Background\r\nEven though the severity of labor pain varies from woman to woman and from pregnancy to pregnancy, it is equated with bad aching pain and pain of broken bone (1). Labor pain occurs as a result of uterine muscle contraction, shortening, thinning, and opening of the cervix that intensifies progressively during labor making labor pain more severe over time until delivery (2).  Significant pregnant women experience exaggerated fear of labor pain that results in prolonged labor, greater use of pain relievers, unnecessary emergency cesarean delivery, and many more negative consequences for both mother and fetus/newborn (3). \r\n\r\nLaboring in warm water is a non-pharmacological technique of labor pain management where the laboring woman’s abdomen is totally submerged in warm water in the tub or large bathing pool (4). Laboring in warm water reduces the effect of gravity and enables a woman to move freely and be comfortable.  Warm water minimizes the production of catecholamine such as adrenalin and promotes the release of natural hormones such as endorphin to make the laboring woman feel relaxed and cope with labor pain (5). \r\n\r\nIn opposite to a concern that water birth may lead to neonatal water inhalation, and increment of the rate of admission to neonatal intensive care unit, evidence shows no increased risk to mother and neonate. But laboring in warm water during the first stage of labor minimized the use of epidural/spinal/para-cervical block (6).\r\n1.2 Rationale\r\nEven if pharmacological pain relievers such as epidural analgesia can be used as labor pain management, they are associated with adverse effects and negative childbirth experiences (1). Immersion in warm water during labor is important non-pharmacological labor pain management and is associated with a reduced risk of cesarean delivery. Also, it is associated with more safe spontaneous vaginal delivery (5). Laboring in warm water help laboring mothers to feel relaxed, manage contractions, and minimize the need for medical interventions (7). In addition to reducing labor pain and the rate of emergency cesarean delivery, water immersion during labor also reduces the duration of labor and the rate of stress urinary incontinence during the post-partum period. Also, it results in no increased risk of poor neonatal outcomes (8). \r\nDifferent randomized controlled trial studies revealed that laboring in warm water resulted in minimized laboring women\'s anxiety, and reduced fetal mal-presentation. Also, it resulted in a good rate of cervical dilation which is proportional to average labor augmentation. Overall, warm water immersion during labor optimizes physiologic labor and lead to increased satisfaction of laboring women with the overall labor experience (9). \r\nEven though laboring in warm water is widely practiced with good users’ preference in developed countries (6); it is not being practiced in most developing countries including Ethiopia yet. But since our country, Ethiopia, is progressing to be among developed countries, such best practices should be commenced to make institutional delivery service interesting thereby maximizing the institutional delivery service utilization to the pre-settled goal. Thus, commencing such an interesting project upholds our countries health service delivery status to the next upper step.','4. Methodology \r\n4.1 study area and period \r\nThis study will be conducted in Jimma town public health facilities. Jimma town is one of the big towns of the Oromia region.  It is found in the southwest direction from the capital city of the country (Addis Ababa) at a distance of 356 kilometers. There are six (two hospitals and 4 health centers) public health facilities in Jimma town. These six public health facilities are Jimma medical center, Shonen Gibe general hospital, Jimma Health Center, Jimma Higher two Health Center, Bacho Bore Health Center, and Mender Kochi Health Center. According to the statistics obtained from each Jimma town public health facility, totally all the facilities provide delivery service for four hundred ten (410) pregnant women per month which mean that annually the public health facilities in Jimma town provide delivery services for four thousand nine hundred twenty \r\n(4920) pregnant women on average. This study will be conducted from February 01 to May 30 /2021.\r\n4.2 study design \r\nA randomized controlled trial study design will be used to conduct this study.\r\n4.3 Population\r\n4.3.1 Source population\r\nThe source population of this study will be all laboring mothers admitted to the labor ward of Jimma town public health facilities.\r\n4.3.2 Study population\r\nThe study population will be all laboring women admitted to Jimma town public health facilities and who are within the first and second stage of labor with the normal progress of labor and normal feto-maternal condition and selected randomly as experimental and control group.  \r\n4.3.3 Inclusion and exclusion criteria\r\nLaboring women within the first and second stages of labor with normal labor progress, and normal feto-maternal condition will be included. But laboring women who had heart disease, hepatitis B, dermatitis, any open wound, candidates for augmentation of labor, or any abnormality for which the women should be closely monitored will be excluded from the study.    \r\n4.4 Sample size estimation and Sampling procedure \r\nThe sample size is calculated by single population proportion formula (n= (zα/2)2(p(1-p)/d2  with the assumption of P- Proportion of effectiveness of laboring in warm water in managing labor pain taken as 50 percent because of lack of relevant previous study. z- Critical value at 95% confidence level thus 1.96; d-margin of error: taken as 5%. n=384.16.\r\nThus, since the total laboring women within the study period (4 months, February to May)of this study is estimated to be 1640, which is less than 10,000; correction formula (nf=N*n/N+n) is used to further reduce the sample size. Thus, nf= 1640*384.16/1640 + 384.16; nf= 312. This means that 156 laboring women will be selected as an interventional group and the rest half 156 laboring women will be selected as a control group. The sample size will be proportionally allocated for the six public health facilities in Jimma town based on the client flow each health facilities have.  Both the interventional and the control group will be selected by using a systematic random sampling method.  The study participants will be assigned to either the interventional or control group purely randomly. \r\n4.5 Data collection tools and data collection procedures\r\nThe standard tool will be adopted from a previous study and will be used for data collection. Data will be collected on socio-demographic characteristics, obstetric factors, conditions during labor, labor pain perception scale, fetal and maternal outcomes, and other relevant characteristics. The questionnaire will be prepared in English and will be translated into both Afan Oromo and Amharic languages by the language experts and will be translated back to English to assure the consistency. The study participants will be closely followed from their admission to the end of the fourth stage of labor. Necessary data will be obtained by the time the laboring women are not having a uterine contraction and are not under evaluation by their caregivers. The data collectors will be 12 (2 from each health facility) BSc holder midwives after they will be trained on the objective of the study, data collection tool, method of data collection, and how to care for laboring women in the warm water bathing pool. Six (one per each facility) supervisors (MSc holder midwives) will be trained on the overall process of data collection and will supervise from selection of study participants to the end of data collection and checking the completeness of the filled questionnaire. The principal investigator will closely follow the overall process and make necessary facilitation.  \r\n4.6.	Data management and statistical analysis \r\nCollected data will be checked for completeness, coded, and entered into Epi-data software version 4.2 and will be exported to Statistical Package for Social Science (SPSS) version 23 for analysis. Data will be analyzed descriptively and binary logistic regression analysis will be used to determine the association of independent variables with outcome variables. Variables with a p-value <0.2 in the bivariate analysis will be transferred to multivariable analysis. P-value at <0.05 and adjusted odds ratio at 95% confidence interval will be used to ascertain the association between independent and outcome variables.\r\n\r\n4.7	Data quality assurance \r\nTo ensure the quality of data, a standard questionnaire will be used for data collection. Training will be given to data collectors and supervisors for two days duration. The functionality of every accessory material such as water boiler and gauge of temperature, availability of water, and cleanliness of bathing pool will be monitored and maintained by facilitation of the principal investigator to keep every process of the experiment smooth. Filled questionnaires will be checked for completeness and questionnaires with incomplete parts and inconsistent responses will be excluded. During data entry, Epi-data version 4.2 will be used to minimize errors. Finally, the multivariable analysis will be done to control potential confounding variables.\r\n4.8	Plan for dissemination and ensuring utilization of findings\r\nThe result of this study will be submitted to Jimma University Institute of Health postgraduate research directorate office, and to public health facilities of Jimma town to show the finding and to initiate necessary intervention after then.  Efforts will be made to present at big conferences and workshops at the national or regional or university level. Finally, the report of this study will be published in reputable international or national journals.    \r\n\r\n4.9.Ethical considerations \r\n    Ethical approval will be obtained from Jimma University Institute of Health Institutional Review Board. Participation in the study will be based on the willingness of the study participants. Written informed consent will be obtained from each study participant. The anonymity of the study participants and confidentiality of their information will be assured throughout.','<ol>\r\n	<li><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\">Mater Mothers&rsquo; hospital patient information.&nbsp; Available from: <a href=\"http://brochures.mater.org.au/brochures/mater-mothers-hospital/labour-and-birth-warm-water-immersion-and-water-bi.aspx\" style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;; color:blue; text-decoration:underline\">http://brochures.mater.org.au/brochures/mater-mothers-hospital/labour-and-birth-warm-water-immersion-and-water-bi.aspx</a>. Accessed on November 10, 2021.</span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\">ACNM. Coping with Labor Pain. Journal of Midwifery and women&rsquo;s health. 2020. Volume 65. Issue 3. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1111/jmwh.13127\" style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;; color:blue; text-decoration:underline\">https://doi.org/10.1111/jmwh.13127</a></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\">Gunilla S, Marie B, Sofie P, Louise A, Marie B, and Ann J. Effects of continuous midwifery labour support for women with severe fear of childbirth. BMC Pregnancy and Childbirth (2015) 15:115 DOI 10.1186/s12884-015-0548-6 </span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\">Valentina F, Sara M, Serena D, Alessandro S, Livia G, Giulia M, Emanuela S, and Antonio R. The childbirth experience: obstetric and psychological predictors in Italian primiparous women. BMC Pregnancy and Childbirth (2019) 19:419. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1186/s12884-019-2561-7\" style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;; color:blue; text-decoration:underline\">https://doi.org/10.1186/s12884-019-2561-7</a></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\">Mirjam L, Rachel R, John T, Marian K, and Jennifer H. Immersion in water for pain relief and the risk of intrapartum transfer among low-risk nulliparous women: secondary analysis of the Birthplace national prospective cohort study. BMC Pregnancy and Childbirth 2014, 14:60 <a href=\"http://www.biomedcentral.com/1471-2393/14/60\" style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;; color:blue; text-decoration:underline\">http://www.biomedcentral.com/1471-2393/14/60</a></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">Cluett ER, Burns E, Cuthbert A. Immersion in water during labor and birth. Cochrane Database Syst Rev. 2018 May 16;5(5):CD000111. doi: 10.1002/14651858.CD000111.pub4. PMID: 29768662; PMCID: PMC6494420.</span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\">N</span><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\">HS. Hull University teaching hospital. </span><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\">What you need to know about giving birth in water.Communications Team7th February 2018. Available on: </span><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><a href=\"https://www.hey.nhs.uk/news/2018/02/07/need-know-giving-birth-water\" style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;; color:blue; text-decoration:underline\">https://www.hey.nhs.uk/news/2018/02/07/need-know-giving-birth-water</a></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\">Liu, Y., Liu, Y., Huang, X. et al. A comparison of maternal and neonatal outcomes between water immersion during labor and conventional labor and delivery. BMC Pregnancy Childbirth 14, 160 (2014). <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1186/1471-2393-14-160\" style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;; color:blue; text-decoration:underline\">https://doi.org/10.1186/1471-2393-14-160</a></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\">Jenna Sh. Systematic Review of Hydrotherapy Research.&nbsp; Does a Warm Bath in Labor Promote Normal Physiologic Childbirth? J Perinat Neonat Nurs (2017). <span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">[1]</span></span> Volume 31 Number 4, 303&ndash;316 </span></span></span></li>\r\n</ol>','To assess the effectiveness of laboring in warm water as non-pharmacological management of labor pain; effect on  fetal and maternal outcomes; and associated factors among laboring women in Jimma town public health facilities, 2021/2022',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,2,'Effectiveness,laboring in warm water,labor pain, Jimma town',10,'<p><span style=\"font-size:12px\">This mega research project needs a total of <span style=\"font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">518,017.5 ETB and it will be taken place from December 16/2021 to June 30/2022.</span></span></p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),(27,81,9,19,'The quality of milk along the dairy value chain and its determinants has been investigated by several researchers. Milk quality can also be determined by the health of the lactating animal, the hygiene of the cow, and the environment. On the other hand, the development of antibiotic resistance through pathogenic microorganisms is becoming an increasingly important challenge and not been fully studied in dairy industry in Ethiopia. Both reduced product quality and the development of antibacterial resistance affect i) food security and the incidence of infectious diseases, and ii) the current unmet demand for Ethiopian dairy products.\r\nTherefore, the purpose of this study is to assess (1) the microbial quality of milk along the entire milk value chain, taking into account the above-mentioned under-researched areas, based on the health of the lactating animal (2) antimicrobial resistance profiles of isolates and farmer knowledge, attitude & perception towards the use of microbial drugs and (3) the effect of careless use of antimicrobials on the development of bacterial resistance in lactating cows.\r\nA cross-sectional study will be conducted to assess milk quality and prevalence of asymptomatic mastitis, and a drug susceptibility test of bacterial isolates from February to March 2022. The collection of data and samples will be including: i) milk sample, ii) swab sample, iii) asymptomatic mastitis test, iv) water sample, v) and questionnaire survey. The collected samples will be analyzed for total aerobic mesophilic bacteria and total coliforms using standard methods. S. aureus and E. coli will be isolated and antimicrobial susceptibility testing will also be performed on Mueller Hinton agar medium (MHA) using the Kirby-Bauer agar disk diffusion method.\r\nThis study will shed light on the current knowledge gap among farmers, traders and consumers regarding proper livestock and hygienic handling of milk. It will also show the milk quality. In addition, this study will also reveal the impact of traditional careless use of antimicrobials in lactating cattle on animal and human product quality and infection prevention.',NULL,'Microbial Quality and Antimicrobial Sensitivity of Staphylococcus aureus and Escherichia coli isolates from Raw Cow Milk in Jimma town and surrounding districts, Southwestern Ethiopia','2021-11-18 18:38:00','This research project has the following expected outcomes: \r\nI.	Show the microbial quality compliance of milk and the percentage of milk samples for each microbial level will be determined\r\nII.	Indicate the sanitary condition of animal husbandry and limitation in antimicrobial drug utilization in association with resistance development of bacterial isolates\r\nIII.	Show the prevalence of subclinical mastitis among milk cows so that it helps the stakeholders to take appropriate measures\r\nIV.	Help to identify the gap (knowledge, attitude and practice) in quality assurance in the dairy value chain related to actors in the industry (milkers, distributors and sellers)\r\nV.	The findings of this study may contribute to the prevention and control of milk-borne diseases.\r\nThe project output can help to ensure a sustainable solution for the food insecurity (increase livestock and asset production) and health problems of animals and humans (infection prevention and control) in developing countries like Ethiopia. It can show clearly where should be the intervention area and how the different professionals including agriculturalists, Environmentalist, Public health officers and Veterinarians integrativelly involves in the solution.','a14661898d470f4de6b1fe1bc4c1fc4a','completed','Among food items which are nutritionally rich, cow milk that contains water, carbohydrates, proteins, fats and minerals is mentioned. It contains anti-pathogenic agents, immunoglobulins that protect the newly born (Kanyeka, 2014; Zahara & Zinewi, 2015; Megersa et al., 2019). Global communities depend and consume milk and its products largely. In East African countries more than 75% of the milk product is sold raw (Bertu et al., 2010; Oliver et al., 2011; Tolosa et al., 2016). Raw milk consumption is also common in Ethiopia at the home or village level (Zahara & Zinewi, 2015), but some educated consumers practice cooking at the home level (Bertu et al., 2010; Tolosa et al., 2016). Yet, the traditional boiling process may not be effective due to time-temperature abuse or inadequate heating. Even it may activate some homophile groups. So reducing the pollution from farm to consumption is important along with consumers practice (boiling). Consumption of raw milk contaminated with bacteria leads to the development of various food infections & poisonings. Raw milk is an important vehicle for infecting humans with milk-derived pathogens (Kanyeka, 2014). In this regard members of the community primarily sensitive people, including pregnant women, the elderly, infants and people with chronic cases are at risk of the problem (Khudor et al., 2012; Mamo et al., 2016; Tegegne & Tesfaye, 2017; Guya et al., 2019; Berhanu et al., 2021). In Ethiopia, heavy consumption of raw milk is common (Tegegne & Tesfaye, 2017), and children mainly consume more from other groups in Jimma (Tolosa et al., 2016). \r\nNinety-eight percent of milk in Jimma produced by smallholders (Tolosa et al., 2016). As the population and urbanization increase alarmingly, parallel to that, the demand for milk consumption also increasing (Zebib & Zewdu, 2020; Berhanu et al., 2021). To gratify this unmet demand, smallholder dairy farms are growing in number (Tolosa et al., 2016) but the quality is under question.\r\nFrom the very beginning, milk and dairy products are suitable media for the growth and reproduction of various microorganisms, unless proper handling is used (Zahara & Zinewi, 2015; Eshetu, et al., 2020). This is due to its inherent characteristics such as high water activity, moderate pH (6.4–6.6) an ample supply of nutrients (Sisay, et al., 2005; Adams & Moss, 2008) and the hot and humid climatic condition of tropical regions also allows micro-organisms to spoil milk like other food products (Workiye, 2012). Milk can get microbial contaminants in two main ways. The first is direct contact with sources of contamination such as milking practices, milk processing, water for hygiene, inadequate temperature control, and equipment used in dairy environments, and the second is infected dairy cows (S.P. Oliver et al., 2005; Kanyeka, 2014; Ayele et al., 2017; Islam et al., 2018; Berhe, et al., 2020). \r\nBoth of these sources are a challenge for dairy farms in developing countries including Ethiopia. Inflammation of udder is a widespread disease that affects dairy cows. The prevalence of mastitis, especially asymptomatic mastitis, is high and can result in significant financial losses on the industry. This is a major challenge since no visible sign can be seen in the udder or milk and is not well known to Ethiopian farmers (Fesseha et al., 2021). However, many infectious and environmental pathogens such as Staphylococcus aureus, Escherichia coli, Streptococcus, Mycoplasma., and Corynebacterium bovis are responsible for this cattle infection (Abebe et al., 2016; Fesseha et al., 2021) because the nipple end was exposed to an organic litter source and a moist muddy bay (Gashaw & Gebrehiwot, 2018). In general, milk-rotting microorganisms and milk-borne pathogens are very different and can include bacteria, yeast, mold and bacteriophage  (Ahmedsham, et al., 2018). These food-borne pathogenic microorganisms include S. aureus, pathogenic E. coli, Salmonella spp, Listeria monocytogenes, Brucella abortus, Mycobacterium spp., Campylobacter spp., Leptospira spp., Clostridium spp., Pseudomonas aeruginosa and Proteusspp. (Kanyeka, 2014; Abebe et al., 2016; Berhe et al., 2020; Eshetu et al., 2020). Various studies have reported that up to 90% of breast diseases are due to these pathogenic microorganisms (Gwandu et al., 2018).\r\nThough entire stage of the dairy value chain is exposed to the spoilage or pathogenic microorganisms, the quality of raw milk is deteriorating along the value chain. There are several factors that affect quality. These include the education status of dairy workers, the attitudes of dairy workers, and the water quality used to wash the contact surfaces of dairy products and the hands of dairy workers (Berhanu et al., 2021).\r\nEvaluation of milk quality along the milk value chain from milking to retail or milking to consumption has been done so far in our research environment (Tolosa et al., 2016; Korma, et al., 2018; Berhanu et al., 2021). Milk quality can also be determined from the beginning by the health of the cow and the cleanliness / hygiene of the cow (Zahara & Zinewi, 2015; Martin, et al., 2019). As an indicator, staphylococcal food poisoning is widespread in Ethiopia and is a major concern for milk contamination due to the high prevalence of cases of mastitis in animals, primarily caused by S. aureus (Tegegne & Tesfaye, 2017). Milk contamination is not only due to these causes, but also to the milking process and the hygiene of the milker\'s hands. If milk quality problems occur immediately after milking, mixing in a storage tank can contaminate all products. This has led to widespread economic and health crises around the world (Kanyeka, 2014; Kuma, et al., 2015). Twenty-five percent of the world\'s food product is perishing solely by microbial spoilage. In Ethiopia, more than 25% of milk and its products are lost during milking until consumption (Zahara & Zinewi, 2015).\r\n\r\nOn the other hand, the biggest challenge in the dairy industry is the detection of mastitis and antibacterial residues in dairy products, especially in sub-Saharan Africa (Kenyeka, 2014; Ssajjakambwe et al., 2017). Animal and human disease caused by microscopic etiological agents especially those caused by bacteria are treated by antimicrobial drugs. However, some bacteria may show complete or partial resistance to various antibiotics (IDF, 2017). The presence of antimicrobial residues in milk is a major cause of resistance-developing microorganisms and the incidence is increasing (Kanyeka, 2014; Gwandu et al., 2018; Zebib & Zewdu, 2020). \r\nIn dairy farming, these antimicrobials are utilized for two purposes; therapeutically to treat infection and prophylactically to prevent onset of infection (Oliver et al., 2011; Ruegg, 2013). The utilization of these drugs for food-producing animal has several gains. These benefits include healthier and more productive animals, minimized mortality, production of high-quality, abundant and nutritious food, and minimizing costs for human consumption (Oliver et al., 2011). However, recently, the problem of bacterial resistance to antibiotics is worsening at a frightening rate from time to time and become a challenge. This disturbing phenomenon occurs naturally in bacteria by natural selection or natural selection, but can be caused by improper use of antibiotics in human, animal and plant medicine (IDF, 2017; Kalayu et al., 2020) mainly by over or underuse (Ayukekbong et al., 2017; Jankowiak et al., 2020). In addition to hindering the selection of suitable antibiotics for veterinary medicine (Kanyeka, 2014), it poses a serious threat to human health and milk consumers and poses a major challenge to modern medicine and allergy risk (Tadesse & Dabassa, 2012; Jankowiak et al., 2020; Zebib & Zewdu, 2020). It also affects the proper production of dairy products (affecting milk pH and flavor production, reducing milk coagulation and miss-aging cheese) (Zebib & Zewdu, 2020).\r\nThe impact of food microbial spoilage or contamination and traditional improper/ misuse of drugs or antibiotics for lactating cows on milk qualities, animal and human health and future treatment effectiveness is overlooked in integration. The spoilage or contamination of milk and development of a resistant strain of pathogenic microorganisms will have an impact on 1) Health of the animal since diseases caused by these strains cannot be cured by the drugs, 2) Quality of milk due to high contamination starting from the mammary gland, 3) Milk supply for the consumer because of loss of the product attributed to proliferation and spoilage versus our rapidly growing population. Thus, this leads to food security problem. Consequently, it will affect 4) human (consumers) health (foodborne infection and intoxication) and 5) the health care system at large.\r\n\r\nTherefore, this study is aimed (1), to assess the microbial quality of cow milk along the whole milk value chain by considering the above-underexplored areas starting from the health status of the lactating animal (2), to assess the antimicrobial resistance profile of isolates and farmers KAP towards drug utilization and (3), assess the impact of non-prudent utilization of antimicrobial drugs for lactating cows to bacterial resistance development. This helps us to know and critically recommend appropriate corrective measures to improve milk quality thereby food security and safeguard the health of our community.','3.1.	Study area\r\nThe city of Jimma is the capital of Jimma Zone in the region of Oromia province in Ethiopia and is surrounded by four rural districts: Kersa, Dedo, Seka and Mana. Jimma zone has a total population of 2.6 million. From this 88.7% the residents live in rural districts. It has a total of 521,506 households. The average maximum and minimum temperatures of the area is 25-30°C and 7-12°C respectively with annual precipitation ranging from 1,200 to 2,800 mm per year (Central Agricultural Census Commission, 2002). For Jimma town, the average altitude above sea level is about 1780 m with annual precipitation and temperature ranging from 1400 to 1900 mm 6 OC to 31 OC respectively.  The area is known for its coffee and livestock production (Tolosa et al., 2016). It is estimated that there are 1,718,284 cattle in the zone (CSA, 2007).\r\n3.2.	Study Design and Duration \r\nFrom February to March 2022, a cross-sectional study design will be conducted to evaluate milk quality, prevalence of asymptomatic mastitis, and drug susceptibility testing of bacterial isolates. Source and study population\r\n3.3.	Source population \r\nAll milk producer, distribution and retailer centers which are found in Jimma town and surrounding four district (Kersa, Dedo, Seka and Mana) will be the source populations.\r\n3.3.1.	Study population \r\nThe study population for milk, water & swab sample on microbial contamination will be all randomly selected dairy farms, milk distributors and retailers. For KAP assessment selected milk handlers and dairy farmers will be included. The sample size will be proportionally allocated to the 4 districts and Jimma town.\r\n3.3.2.	Inclusion and exclusion criteria\r\n3.3.2.1.	 Inclusion criteria \r\nThe inclusion criteria of the study participants included the availability of milk & milk caw during the time of sample collection.\r\n3.3.2.2.	 Exclusion criteria \r\nNon-volunteer milk production house, enterprises, distributors, retailers and milk handlers will be excluded from the study \r\n3.4.	Sample size and sampling techniques \r\n3.4.1.	Sample Size determination for water, milk and swab (equipment & teat) microbial examination \r\nThe sample size is estimated as described by Kothari 2009 using the following formula:\r\n \r\nWhere n = estimated sample size, Z = standard normal score, p = prevalence of milk contamination (unsatisfactory quality) by and microbes which is 32% from previous study (Berhanu et al., 2021), q = probability of having no microbial contamination in the sample and d = precision level (acceptable error). The estimate is desired to be with a 5% margin of error and 95% confidence interval. By considering 10% non-respondent rate, the required sample size for this study will be 239. Therefore, 239 milk (80 milk producer, 80 distributor & 80 retailer), 239 water 239 equipment swab and 80 teats (only at dairy farm) swab samples will be collected.\r\n3.4.2.	 Sample Size determination for dairy farmers KAP, milk handler KAP assessment and hand swab \r\nThe average over all estimate of KAP level of milk handlers at the study area for milk microbial contamination is 55% according to Berhanu et al. (2021). The estimate is desired to be with a 5% margin of error and 95% confidence interval. Therefore, the required sample size for this study is determined using the following statistical formula.\r\n \r\nWhere \"n\" is the minimum number of sample size, \"Z\" is the standard normal score, \"p\" is the prevalence value, \"q\" = 1-p and \"d\" is a marginal error. At 95% confidence interval Z=1.96 and marginal error is 5%. Considering a 10% non-response rate, the final sample size is calculated to be 420. A simple random sampling will be used for the selection of study subjects.\r\n3.4.	Study variables\r\n3.4.1.	Dependent variables\r\n	The microbial load of raw cow’s milk\r\n	Prevalence of subclinical mastitis among milk cow\r\n	Antimicrobial susceptibility of S. aureus and E. coli isolates\r\n3.4.2.	Independent variables\r\n	Socio-demographic variables\r\n	Sanitary condition around animal husbandry\r\n	Cleanliness of equipment and utensils \r\n	Water quality (bacteriological) \r\n	KAP of actors along the different dairy value chain towards proper antimicrobial drug utilization and milk handling and care\r\n3.5.	 Data collection and sampling\r\n3.5.1.	Milk sampling \r\nMilk samples will be collected at various points in the milk supplier\'s value chain. As raw milk quality begins to decline on the farm (Gashaw & Gebrehiwot, 2018), approximately 250 ml of milk will be aseptically taken from the lactating cow\'s udder (Amentie, et al., 2016). In addition, milk samples will be taken from milk storage tanks at all stages of the milk supply chain (farms, distribution centers, retail stores) using sterile universal plastic bottles and a sterilized 50 ml syringe according to Berhanu et al (2021). When opening the sample bottle prior to taking the sample, a flame will be used to create a relatively sterile zone. Hands of sampler and the outside of the milk sample bottle are also disinfected with 78% ethyl alcohol (Gemechu, 2016). The samples are then labeled and placed in a refrigerator (4 °C) to prevent and stop microbial growth and reproduction (Amakelew, et al., 2015) and transported as soon as possible to Jimma university (JU) Institute of health, faculty of public health Environmental Health Science & Technology department laboratory for immediate further processing within 4 hours.\r\n3.5.2.	Swab sample\r\nCotton swab samples (100cm2) will be obtained using sterile cotton swabs; the outside of the nipple, the milker\'s hand, and the milk contact equipment throughout the milk value stream will be sampled for about 20 sec. (Ayele et al., 2017; Berhanu et al., 2021)\r\n3.5.3.	Water sampling \r\nWater used for washing cow teats and milk storage equipment, and for rinsing after washing will be taken using a sterilized 250 ml polyethylene bottle. As a result, the samples will be labeled and shipped in a cool box to JU Institute of health, faculty of public health Environmental Health Science & Technology department laboratory for immediate further processing within 4 hours.\r\n3.5.4.	Questionnaire & checklist-based survey\r\nThe KAP of dairy farmers on animal husbandry handling, antimicrobial drug utilization and milking process and milk handlers on proper milk handling (hand washing practices, washing & disinfection of milk contact surfaces and etc.) will be assessed by using structured questionnaires & checklist. In addition, the animals conditions (cow breed, age, parity and lactation stage, management) and environmental factors such as litter, ventilation, frequency of barn cleaning, potting wash and use of shared towels and other conditions may affect the hygienic quality of the raw milk will be evaluated (Tegegne & Tesfaye, 2017). The questionnaires is prepared by modifying from Getahun & Gebre-Selassie (2003), Haile (2015), Mainda et al. (2015), Tolosa et al. (2016), Tegegne & Tesfaye 2017 and Islam et al. (2018). The tools will be firstly written in English and then translated into Oromifa. Face-to-face interviews and observation will be the modalities of survey assessment.\r\n3.5.5.	Subclinical Mastitis Test\r\nCalifornia Mastitis Test (CMT) will be used to assess the subclinical mastitis according to (Bonfoh et al., 2003; Abebe et al., 2016; Ssajjakambwe et al., 2017; Fesseha et al., 2021). The CMT results will be scored as 0. 1, 2 and 3 according to Abebe et al. (2016). Based on gel formation, 0, 1, 2, and 3 indicates negative, weakly positive, distinct positive and strongly positive respectively. All 1, 2 & 3 scores concided as indicators of positive result (Abebe et al., 2016).\r\n3.6.	Sample analysis\r\n3.6.1.	Determination of total mesophilic bacterial count\r\nFollowing standard procedures and methods, the milk sample will be analyzed for Total Coliform Count (TCC), Total Aerobic Mesophilic Bacterial Count (TAMBC), S. aureus according to Korma et al (2018) and E. coli. according to Mainda et al. (2015).\r\n3.6.2.	Isolation and identification of S. aureus and E. coli\r\nStaphylococcus aureus isolation and identification will be performed on milk samples taken from bovine udder as illustrated by Ayele et al (2017); Tegegne & Tesfaye, (2017) and Kalayu et al (2020). E. coli is isolated and identified from bovine nipple samples according to standard procedures described by Disassa et al (2017).\r\n3.6.3.	Antimicrobial susceptibility test\r\nMueller Hinton agar disc diffusion method (MHA) will be used to conduct antimicrobial susceptibility test for S. aureus and E. coli on commonly used antibiotics namely: vancomycin, penicillin G, tetracycline, sulfametoxazole trimethoprim, ciprofloxacin, naridixic acid, amoxicillin, cefoxitin, erythromycin, and streptomycin (Oliver et al., 2011; Mainda et al., 2015; Ayele et al., 2017; Kalayu et al., 2020; Grima et al., 2021) by Kirby-Bauer agar disk diffusions method matching tests to 0.5 McFarland turbidity standards. Finaly the result will be interpreted following the guideline of the Clinical and Laboratory Standards Institute (CLSI, 2014) by measuring the diameter of the zone of inhibition. Results will be reported as susceptible, moderate, or resistant according to an interpretation of the diameter of the complete bacterial growth-inhibiting zone using modified Kirby Bower disk diffusion techniques (WHO, 2003). \r\n3.7.	 Data management and analysis\r\n3.7.1.	Quality assurance\r\nSampling and analysis in the investigated laboratory will be in strict accordance with standard operating procedures (SOPs). The data and samples collected in the fields will be coded and visually checked for errors before entering the database. S. aureus (ATCC 25923) and E. coli (ATCC 25922) control strains will be used to verify the proper functioning of the sterile medium and isolation mechanism, to test the inhibitory properties & their growth and to verify the quality of antibiotic plates for antibiotic susceptibility testing.\r\n3.8.	Data analysis\r\nComputer coding will be pre-tested, and data entry and analyses will be done using SPSS (Version 25). All data will be validated by a series of logical and range checks and statistical analyses will include both descriptive and analytical methods. For categorical variables of interest, the differences between categories will be compared by the χ2 test and the probability of < 0.05 will be considered to be statistically significant. Finally, backward stepwise regression analyses will be performed simultaneously adjusting for confounding variables for identifying factors that independently influenced the outcome. Different regression models (logistic and linear regression) will be applied to determine the association between the dependent and independent variables.\r\n3.9.	 Plan for dissemination of findings\r\nThe study findings will be presented to different workshops, scientific conferences (national and international). Moreover, effort will be made to publish this work in peer-reviewed journals. In addition, copies of the reports will be presented to the stalk holders considered in the study, Jimma zone health office, and other concerned organizations directly or indirectly involved in food safety, AMR and infection prevention & control issues.','<p style=\"margin-left:32px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"><span style=\"color:black\">Abebe, R., Hatiya, H., Abera, M., Megersa, B., &amp; Asmare, K. (2016). Bovine mastitis : prevalence, risk factors and isolation of Staphylococcus aureus in dairy herds at Hawassa milk shed, South Ethiopia. <em>BMC Veterinary Research</em>, <em>12</em>(270), 1&ndash;11. https://doi.org/10.1186/s12917-016-0905-3</span></span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:32px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"><span style=\"color:black\">Adams, M. R., &amp; Moss, M. O. (2008). <em>Food Microbiology</em> (Third Edit). University of Surrey, Guildford, UK: The Royal Society of Chemistry, Thomas Graham House, Science Park, Milton Road, Cambridge CB4 0WF, UK.</span></span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:32px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"><span style=\"color:black\">Ahmedsham, M., Amza, N., &amp; Tamiru, M. (2018). Review on milk and milk product safety, quality assurance and control. <em>International Journal of Livestock Production</em>, <em>9</em>(4), 67&ndash;78. https://doi.org/10.5897/IJLP2017.0403</span></span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:32px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"><span style=\"color:black\">Amakelew, S., Eshetu, M., Animut, G., &amp; Gebeyew, K. (2015). Microbial Quality of Cow Milk in Dawa Chefa District, Amhara Region, Ethiopia. <em>J Adv Dairy Res</em>, <em>3</em>(2), 1&ndash;4. https://doi.org/10.4172/2329-888X.1000135</span></span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:32px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"><span style=\"color:black\">Amentie, T., Kebede, A., Mekasha, Y., &amp; Eshetu, M. (2016). Microbiological Quality of Raw Cow Milk across the Milk Supply Chain in Eastern Ethiopia. <em>East African Journal of Sciences</em>, <em>10</em>(2), 119&ndash;132.</span></span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:32px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"><span style=\"color:black\">Ayele, Y., Gutema, F. D., Edao, B. M., Girma, R., Tufa, T. B., Beyene, T. J., &hellip; Beyi, A. F. (2017). Assessment of Staphylococcus aureus along the milk value chain and its public health importance in Sebeta, central Oromia, Ethiopia. <em>BMC Microbiology</em>, <em>17</em>(141), 1&ndash;7. https://doi.org/10.1186/s12866-017-1048-9</span></span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:32px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"><span style=\"color:black\">Ayukekbong, J. A., Ntemgwa, M., &amp; Atabe, A. N. (2017). The threat of antimicrobial resistance in developing countries : causes and control strategies. <em>Antimicrobial Resistance and Infection Control</em>, <em>6</em>(47), 1&ndash;8. https://doi.org/10.1186/s13756-017-0208-x</span></span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:32px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"><span style=\"color:black\">Berhanu, L., Gume, B., Kassa, T., Sena, L., Tegegne, D., Getnet, M., &hellip; Tiku, S. (2021). Microbiol quality of raw cow milk and its predictors along the dairy value chain in Southwest Ethiopia. <em>International Journal of Food Microbiology</em>, <em>350</em>, 1&ndash;7. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ijfoodmicro.2021.109228</span></span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:32px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"><span style=\"color:black\">Berhe, G., Wasihun, A. G., Kassaye, E., &amp; Gebreselasie, K. (2020). </span></span></span><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"><span style=\"color:black\">Milk-borne bacterial health hazards in milk produced for commercial purposes in Tigray, northern Ethiopia. <em>BMC Public Health</em>, <em>20</em>(894), 1&ndash;8.</span></span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:32px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"><span style=\"color:black\">Bertu, W. J., Dapar, M., Gusi, A. M., Ngulukun, S. S., Leo, S., &amp; Jwander, L. D. (2010). Prevalence of Brucella antibodies in marketed milk in Jos and environs. <em>African Journal of Food Science</em>, <em>4</em>(2), 62&ndash;64.</span></span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:32px; text-align:justify\"><span style=