left:32px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"><span style=\"color:black\">Guya, M. E., Adugna, M. M., &amp; Mummed, Y. (2019). Milk Production, Marketing and Quality in Meta District of Eastern Hararghe Milk Production, Marketing and Quality in Meta District of Eastern Hararghe Zone, Ethiopia. <em>Journal of Agricultural Science</em>, <em>11</em>(5). https://doi.org/10.5539/jas.v11n5p535</span></span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:32px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"><span style=\"color:black\">Gwandu, S. H., Nonga, H. E., Mdegela, R. H., Katakweba, A. S., Suleiman, T. S., &amp; Ryoba, R. (2018). Assessment of Raw Cow Milk Quality in Smallholder Dairy Farms in Pemba Island Zanzibar, Tanzania. <em>Veterinary Medicine International</em>, <em>2018</em>, 1&ndash;9.</span></span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:32px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"><span style=\"color:black\">Haile, S. (2015). <em>Quality Assessment of Cattle Milk in Adea Berga and Ejerie Districts of West Shoa Zone, Ethiopia</em>. Haramaya University, Haramaya.</span></span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:32px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"><span style=\"color:black\">IDF. (2017). <em>Guidance on Antimicrobial Resistance from the Dairy Sector</em>.</span></span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:32px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"><span style=\"color:black\">Islam, M. A., Roy, S., Nabi, A., Solaiman, S., Rahman, M., Huq, M., &hellip; Ahmed, N. (2018). Microbiological quality assessment of milk at different stages of the dairy value chain in a developing country setting. <em>International Journal of Food Microbiology</em>, <em>278</em>, 11&ndash;19. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ijfoodmicro.2018.04.028</span></span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:32px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"><span style=\"color:black\">Jankowiak, E., Kubera, Ł., Małecka-adamowicz, M., &amp; Dembowska, E. (2020). Microbiological air quality in pharmacies and an antibiotic resistance profile of staphylococci species. </span></span></span><em><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"><span style=\"color:black\">Aerobiologia</span></span></span></em><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"><span style=\"color:black\">, <em>36</em>(4), 551&ndash;563. https://doi.org/10.1007/s10453-020-09651-x</span></span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:32px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"><span style=\"color:black\">Kalayu, A. A., Woldetsadik, D. A., Woldeamanuel, Y., Wang, S., Gebreyes, W. A., &amp; Teferi, T. (2020). </span></span></span><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"><span style=\"color:black\">Burden and antimicrobial resistance of S . aureus in dairy farms in Mekelle, Northern Ethiopia. <em>BMC Veterinary Research</em>, <em>16</em>(20), 1&ndash;8.</span></span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:32px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"><span style=\"color:black\">Kanyeka, H. B. (2014). <em>Assessment of Microbial Quality of Raw Cow&rsquo;s Milk and Antimicrobial Susceptibility of Selected Milk-Borne Bacteria in Kilosa and Mvomero Districts, Tanzania</em>.</span></span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:32px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"><span style=\"color:black\">Khudor, M. H., Abbas, B. A., &amp; Idbeis, H. I. (2012). Detection of Enterotoxin Genes of Staphylococcus Aureus Isolates from Raw Milk. <em>Bas.J.Vet.Res</em>, <em>11</em>(1), 254&ndash;264. https://doi.org/10.33762/bvetr.2012.54852</span></span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:32px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"><span style=\"color:black\">Korma, H., Nurfeta, A., &amp; Negera, E. (2018). Microbiological quality and safety of milk production and marketing in Hawassa district, Ethiopia. <em>African Journal of Microbiology Research</em>, <em>12</em>(25), 587&ndash;594. https://doi.org/10.5897/AJMR2018.8901</span></span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:32px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">Kothari, C. R., 2009. Research Methodology. Methods and Techniques.3rd Ed. New Delhi. India. 401pp.</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:32px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"><span style=\"color:black\">Kuma, A., Tolossa, D., &amp; Abdisa, M. (2015). Assessment of Raw Milk Microbial Quality at Different Critical Points of Oromia to Milk Retail Centers in Addis Ababa. <em>Food Science and Quality Management</em>, <em>38</em>, 1&ndash;10.</span></span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:32px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"><span style=\"color:black\">Mainda, G., Bessell, P. R., Muma, J. B., Mcateer, S. P., Chase-, M. E., Gibbons, J., &hellip; Bronsvoort, B. M. (2015). Prevalence and patterns of antimicrobial resistance among Escherichia coli isolated from Zambian dairy cattle across different production systems. Nature Publishing Group. https://doi.org/10.1038/srep12439</span></span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:32px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"><span style=\"color:black\">Martin, N. H., Kent, D. J., Evanowski, R. L., Hrobuchak, T. J. Z., &amp; Wiedmann, M. (2019). Bacterial spore levels in bulk tank raw milk are influenced by environmental and cow hygiene factors. <em>Journal of Dairy Science</em>, <em>102</em>(11), 9689&ndash;9701. https://doi.org/10.3168/jds.2019-16304</span></span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:32px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"><span style=\"color:black\">Megersa, R., Mathewos, M., &amp; Fesseha, H. (2019). Isolation and Identification of Escherichia coli from Dairy Cow Raw Milk in Bishoftu Town, Central Ethiopia. <em>Archives of Veterinary and Animal Sciences Copyright</em>, <em>1</em>(1), 1&ndash;7.</span></span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:32px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"><span style=\"color:black\">Oliver, S. P., Jayarao, B. M., &amp; Almeida, R. A. (2005). Foodborne Pathogens in Milk and the Dairy Farm Environment: Food Safety and Public Health Implications. <em>Foodborne Pathogens and Disease</em>, <em>2</em>(2), 115&ndash;129.</span></span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:32px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"><span style=\"color:black\">Oliver, S. P., Murinda, S. E., &amp; Jayarao, B. M. (2011). Impact of Antibiotic Use in Adult Dairy Cows on Antimicrobial Resistance of Veterinary and Human Pathogens : A Comprehensive Review. <em>Foodborne Pathogens and Disease</em>, <em>8</em>(3). https://doi.org/10.1089/fpd.2010.0730</span></span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:32px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"><span style=\"color:black\">Ruegg, P. (2013). Antimicrobial Residues and Resistance: Understanding and Managing Drug Usage on Dairy Farms. Kansas City.</span></span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:32px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"><span style=\"color:black\">Sisay, T., Alemayehu, E., &amp; Gizaw, E. (2005). Examination of Bacteriological and Physical Quality of raw milk. <em>Ethiop J Health Sci</em>, <em>15</em>(2), 177&ndash;185.</span></span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:32px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"><span style=\"color:black\">Ssajjakambwe, P., Bahizi, G., Setumba, C., Kisaka, S. M. B., Vudriko, P., Atuheire, C., &hellip; Kaneene, J. B. (2017). Milk Hygiene in Rural Southwestern Uganda : Prevalence of Mastitis and Antimicrobial Resistance Profiles of Bacterial Contaminants of Milk and Milk Products. <em>Veterinary Medicine International</em>, <em>2017</em>, 1&ndash;7.</span></span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:32px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"><span style=\"color:black\">Tadesse, T., &amp; Anbessa Dabassa. (2012). Prevalence and Antimicrobial Resistance of Salmonella Isolated from Raw Milk Samples Collected from Kersa District, Jimma Zone, Southwest Ethiopia. <em>J. Med. Sci</em>, <em>12</em>(7), 224&ndash;228.</span></span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:32px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"><span style=\"color:black\">Tegegne, B., &amp; Tesfaye, S. (2017). Bacteriological milk quality : possible hygienic factors and the role of Staphylococcus aureus in raw bovine milk in and around Gondar, Ethiopia. <em>International Journal of Food Contamination</em>, <em>4</em>(1), 1&ndash;9. https://doi.org/10.1186/s40550-016-0046-2</span></span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:32px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"><span style=\"color:black\">Tolosa, T., Verbeke, J., Piepers, S., Tefera, M., Getachew, Y., Supr&eacute;, K., &amp; Devliegher, S. (2016). Milk production, quality, and consumption in Jimma ( Ethiopia ): Facts and producers &#39;, retailers &#39;, and consumers &#39; perspectives. <em>Preventive Veterinary Medicine</em>, <em>124</em>, 9&ndash;14. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.prevetmed.2015.12.016</span></span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:32px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"><span style=\"color:black\">WHO. (2003). <em>Basic laboratory procedures in clinical bacteriology</em> (2nd Editio). Geneva.</span></span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:32px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"><span style=\"color:black\">Workiye, M. (2012). <em>Study on Milk Chemical Composition, Bacteriological` Quality and Handling Practices in Debre Libanose District, North Shewa Zone Oromia Region</em>.</span></span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:32px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"><span style=\"color:black\">Zahara, L., &amp; Zinewi, H. (2015). The Microbial properties of dairy products in Ethiopia : A Review. <em>International Journal of Dairy Science and Technology</em>, <em>2</em>(1), 88&ndash;94.</span></span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:32px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"><span style=\"color:black\">Zebib, H., &amp; Zewdu, A. (2020). Nutritional Quality, Level of Chemical Contaminants and Adulterants in Milk and Dairy Products in Ethiopia : A Review. <em>Modern Chemistry</em>, <em>8</em>(4), 64&ndash;72. https://doi.org/10.11648/j.mc.20200804.13</span></span></span></span></span></p>','To assess the microbial quality and antimicrobial sensitivity of S. aureus and E. coli isolates from raw cow milk in Jimma town and surrounding districts, Southwestern Ethiopia, 2022',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,2,'milk quality, antimicrobial resistance, Staphylococcus aureus, Escherichia coli, milk handler, Jimma',10,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),(28,106,9,21,'Executive summary\r\nBackground: According to global estimate approximately 172 million people have been affected due to armed conflict attack consequence. Furthermore, in countries affected by armed conflict more than 240 million children have been vulnerable to fragile humanitarian crisis associated with paramount increased psychosocial and psychiatric outcomes. Psychiatric outcomes among internally displaced people can be triggered by war, witnessed trauma, atrocity, severe famine condition, and shock. Subsequently, internally displaced people exposed to emergencies suffer from of psychological distress, depression, anxiety, post-traumatic stress, gender-based violence. To deescalate the ongoing psychiatric impacts among IDPs, evidence is crucial for decision making. Despite these enormous impacts on IDPs, there is no solid data assessed comprehensively that shows the magnitude of psychiatry outcomes among IDPs in Ethiopia.\r\nObjective: Psychiatry related outcomes and associated factors among Internal Displaced Peoples in North Wollo Following the North Ethiopia War, Ethiopia,2021\r\nMethods:  Mixed approach design will be implemented. The project will be implemented from December 2021 to June 2022. Regarding tools validated, structured, and well-designed questionnaire will be used in order to collect robust data among 1285 sample populations. To reach study participants multistage and simple random sampling technique following interviewer-administered face-to-face interview data collection approach will be used. Concerning qualitative data collection techniques; different interviewing technique such as IDI, KII, and FGD will be approached in order to triangulate information and have quality data among participants. Statistical packages such as EpiData version 3.1 and STATA version 14 will be executed for data management and vigorous analysis respectively. Texts, tables, graphs will be used to present results, likewise proportion, mean, percentage, standard deviation odds ratio will be used to summarize the result. To assess psychiatric related outcomes different form of descriptive analysis will be performed accordingly. To identify risk factors supposed associated with psychiatric outcomes, simple and multivariable linear regression statistical modeling will be used. Furthermore, to declare statistical association or significancy, 𝞫 Coefficient with 95% CI at P value >=0.05 will be reported. \r\nTimeline and Budget: This project will run from December 2021 to June, 2022 by an estimated cost of 511,500 ETB.','Psychiatry related outcomes and associated factors among Internal Displaced Peoples in North Wollo','Psychiatric outcomes and risk factors associated among internal displaced people in North Wollo, exposed to North Ethiopia War, North East Ethiopia,2021: Mixed approach','2021-11-19 18:58:23','The status of IDPs depression level will be made available for intervention \r\n	Proportion of Gender-based-violence will be known among IDPs and get available \r\n	Stakeholders will get understood prevalence of traumatic disorder among IDPs \r\n	stakeholders will have understood proportion of anxiety among IDPs \r\n	Situation of IDPs toward Psychosocial support will be known and be accessible for intervention \r\n	Predictors factors of aforementioned psychiatric outcomes will get understood and will get produced for intervention \r\n	In-depth understanding or exploring psychiatric related outcomes and the impact of the war','186bbc22ee7ce295f32a7d10616325a8','completed','1. Introduction\r\n1.1. Background \r\nPsychiatric illness could be stated as severe but preventable types of illness that has been misperceived across the world, while it is the most important area of health. Usually it has been  defined as fundamental behavioral, biological and psychological disturbance[1]. War or emergency situation has worsened psychiatric outcomes.  Approximately 1 billion people live with psychiatric disorder, stunningly more than 75% of people with disorder do not get treatment in low-income countries. Alarmingly, a person dies by suicide every 40 seconds. In the world over 160 million peoples are found in dire condition and need humanitarian assistance because of conflict. During armed conflict situation the psychiatric disorder could be double and 1 in 5 conflict affected people have Psychiatric condition[2-9].  \r\nPsychiatric outcomes among Internally Displaced People (IDPs) triggered by traumas experienced during war time. IDPs exposed to emergencies or war get suffered from psychological distress as a traumatic event experienced during war has a capacity to aggravate symptoms of psychiatric outcomes [10, 11].\r\nAccording to global estimate approximately 172 million people have been affected due to armed conflict attack consequence. Furthermore, in countries affected by armed conflict more than 240 million children have been vulnerable to fragile humanitarian crisis associated with paramount increased psychosocial and psychiatric outcomes [12, 13]. Armed conflicts are accompanying to psychiatry outcome or mental health consequences that disproportionately burden vulnerable segments of populations such as elderly people, disable people, women and children[14]. \r\nAlthough global estimates of psychiatry outcomes varied between countries, data revealed   that depression (28%); anxiety (26.9%); post-traumatic stress symptoms (24.1%); stress (36.5%); psychological distress (50.0%); and for sleep problems (27.6%).  Moreover, the magnitude of Psychiatric disorder such as PTSD, depression, and anxiety tends to be higher among IDPs and peoples exposed armed war situation[15, 16].    \r\nFactors that trigger the psychiatric outcome during war and war related displacement are exposure to armed conflict, lack of livelihoods, education and development, violence, poverty and/or persecution, barriers to accessing health care, poor living conditions, separation from family members, unemployment, assimilation difficulties, challenges to cultural, religious, and gender identities, social isolation[15, 17-21]. \r\n1.1.1. Depression\r\nDepression is a common and serious mental disorder that negatively affects the way of thinking, emotional expression and behavior of individuals. It is also manifested by decreased energy, low self-steam and poor concentration and can decrease a person’s ability to function both at home and at work[22].\r\nsystematic review conducted on psychosocial problems in IDPs who were exposed to war showed that pooled estimate depression (43%) [23]. A study conducted among  IDPs  in different countries revealed that;  in Colombians IDPs by Armed Conflict reported 41% level of depression[24], in Ukraine prevalence of depression (25%)[25] and similar study in Ukraine figure out that  3.3% IDPs  developed long-term depressive reaction [26], Vietnamese refugees in Norway depression (18%) and stated that  rates of psychiatric disorder had not diminished 3 years later [27, 28].  Studies in Darfur showed 38%[29] and in south Darfur 13.5%[30] depression among IDPs, in Nigeria  the mean score of depression was 18.1+5.01[22] and  in Kaduna, North Western Nigeria reported that 59.7% [31] had depression, Another study conducted Sri Lanka reported 22.2% level of depression among IDPS in war related area [32]. Get displaced can exacerbate pre-existing Psychiatric outcomes and also contribute to new outcomes such as PTSD or depression. According to the review of expert opinion; around 30% of displaced people due to Armed war conflict suffer from either PTSD or depression. As per scholars have  figured out, globally about  14.4 million IDPs are likely to be affected as consequence  of armed conflict war[33]. Studies indicated that factors such as sex, education, marital status, exposure to violence, time since displacement, age , and childhood trauma  were associated factors with depression among IDPs following war[24, 29-31, 34].\r\n1.1.2. Anxiety\r\nAnxiety disorder could be stated as feel excessive, tension, and unrealistic worry with little or no reason. When someone is anxious, he/she experiences physical feelings and worrying thoughts and this can make it hard to do even simple tasks and so they begin to avoid things and it can affect behavior, thought, feeling and physical body[1, 4].\r\naccording to the report from different literatures Anxiety is a common mental health problem in IDPs next to PTSD. A study conducted in Ukraine reported that 13.3%[26]  and 20.2% [25] IDPs developed anxiety disorder[26]. Whereas systematic review study on psychosocial problems in IDP showed that pooled estimate of magnitude of Anxiety among those who were exposed to war was 27%[29]. Another study conducted on Prevalence of War-Related Mental Health Conditions  in Sri Lanka reported that magnitude of anxiety was 32.6% [32]. \r\n1.1.3. Gender Based Violence (GBV)\r\nAny form of violence targeted toward women because they are women is stated as gender-based violence (GBV). Internationally it has been agreed that GBV is a deliberate, prearranged form of human right violation and terrifying acts against women. As it has been estimated among three women one could likely get experienced either form of GBV throughout their lifetime. During crisis, conflict and times of displacement the threat of GBV significantly get increased in alarming manner for women and girls[35, 36]. It comprises domestic violence including intimate or spouse violence, sexual harassment, Psychological and emotional abuse, sex tracking, harmful traditional practice, physical violence, forced prostitution, discriminatory practices based on gender. Any form of GBV has been made or acting against women could be well reason out as it is because of power inequalities between women and men[35-39].  Women and adolescent girls exposed to IDPs environments are more vulnerable for IDP setting induced gender-based violence such as abduction, rape, and women trafficking are the most commonly reported violence that women have experienced. The impacts of gender-based violence may range from immediate to long-term multiple sexual, physical, and Psychiatric outcomes for women and girls, including death. As per publish articles an adolescent girl dies as a result of violence, every 10 minutes, somewhere in the world[2, 4, 19].\r\n Globally, as the result of either intimate partner or non-partner induced sexual or physical violence, more than 35% of women have victimized. This is more pronounced when women are in a place where there are crises, conflict and displacement including internal displacement[38, 40-42]. Women exposed to conflict areas have been faced threat of sexual violence, rape, and forced abortion by Armed actors preparators. Armed conflict affected GBV such as sexual violence, physical violence, and early marriage through looting of resources, death of husband, and rape [42-44].  \r\nWomen exposed into war zones are suffering due to increased violence, and global commitments remain largely unmet. Among IDPs suffer the impact of armed conflict, it is fact that women and adolescence girls are the first to lose their right [45-48]. Factors associated with GBV were identified as level of education, age, employment status, residency, health condition, marital status, number of children, drug use, and infidelity[49].\r\nIn Ethiopia there is limited studies that shows the magnitude and factors associated with GBV among people exposed to war or internally displaced following the war. The purpose is to understand why armed group commit sexual violence and understand the consequences of being sexual harassed by armed group.\r\n1.1.4. Psychosocial Support\r\nPsychological support (PSS) has become one of important concept or eventually become a focus of interest for agencies working in in ongoing and post-conflicts during the last few decades[50].  Psychosocial support is a process of facilitating resilience for psychological and social problems characterized in most individual and collective experiences of displacement and war. which is accompanied by several main stress factors; such as security issues, economic constraints, breakdown of social economic structures, and or modification of social roles, atrocities, persecution violence, and discrimination, destruction of social fabric, direct exposure to violent acts or witnessed trauma acts[51, 52]. Psychosocial related crisis is initiated from armed conflict or war, subsequently, exposed or let higher number of populations particularly vulnerable group of population  to be found on severe and critical situation [53, 54].\r\n Factors such as; age, social support, occupation, stress, gender, level of education, disability, depression, anxiety, and PTSD were common factors supposed to be associated with psychosocial support [36, 37].\r\n1.1.5. Post-Traumatic Stress Disorder (PTSD)\r\nPTSD is one of the most important mental health problems characterized by either experience, witnesses, confronts murder or a serious grievance, war, invoking a response of fear, helplessness or horror. According to systematic review and meta-analysis pooled report that has been considered in 40 countries data that ranges from 3 to 88%, pooled estimate of PTSD was 30.6% [55-57].\r\nIn many global regions the number of IDPs is exponentially growing due to conflicts. Such as conflicts has been undergone and or undergoing in Easter Europe, Middle east, and Africa have contributed to groups of population being displaced and affected by mental health disorders at alarming rate, and making provision of humanitarian support extremely difficult.  One global report estimated highest number of estimates which was 38 million IDPs just only by one year.  This report has indicated as international internal displacement following to conflict will terrifyingly increasing [6, 58]. At the end of 2020, IDMC reported that about 55milion IDPs across the world, of this 48 million are due to conflict and violence and more than 23 million IDPs are aged under 18. Internal displacement emergencies spread to previously unaffected areas of sub-Saharan Africa including Ethiopia (6.8 million)[59]. \r\nIn east Africa like Uganda (74%)[60],  in Sri Lanka (56%)[61], Likewise study conducted in Ethiopia among IDPs revealed 58.4%[62]. Prevalence of PTSD in Study conducted in Syria refuge Lebanon showed 35.4% and 27.2% respectively [63, 64], in north west Nigeria 42.2%[65]. According to research conducted by IDMC In Nigeria and Ethiopia, more than a third of participants were reported conditions such as feeling worried, get nervous, get angry or sad. Being exposed to traumatic events would likely to increase food insecurity, and likelihood of having sever form of mental health issues among IDPs. According to scholars factors such as  ethnicity, education, sex, income, living condition and social provision, age  and marital status [1, 33, 57].\r\nIn North part of Ethiopia armed conflict has been started starting on November 2020. Following the North Ethiopia war millions of Ethiopia have been internally displaced, exposed for war and or different trauma, faced with depression, anxiety, gender-based violence, psychological distress and similar Psychiatric outcomes. The burden and severity of the problem supposed to be very serious and life treating in North Wollo, due to the fact that the area is very prone for drought, majority of the residences are food insecure and highly dependent on humanitarians’ assistance such as on PSNP. Furthermore, the health service coverage, infrastructure coverage, and education coverage are too low.  Given the mentioned problems; The current northern Ethiopia war makes everything so worsen among resident of North Wollo, nevertheless there is no solid data that comprehensively assessed the magnitude of the problem related psychiatry outcomes among IDPs. Therefore, this project has planned to determine Psychiatry related outcomes and identify associated risk factors among internal displaced peoples in North Wollo following North Ethiopia war.\r\n1.2. Rational or justification of the study \r\nPsychiatry related outcomes such as PTSD, depressing, psychological stress, and anxiety in IDPs can be triggered by war.  Despite these enormous impacts on IDPs, there is no solid data that comprehensively assessed the magnitude of the problem related psychiatry outcomes among IDPs in Ethiopia. Hence, the finding will support IDPs who actually have faced the problem, and will have importance to stakeholders working in psychiatric related outcome management, and or humanitarian assistance organizations by showing the magnitude of PTSD, Depression, Anxiety, GBV, and Psychosocial support. Furthermore, the study will support Jimma University by showing important gaps for the community service or outreach service to strengthen across affected area of the country.  In addition, finding that will be generated from this study could contribute a paramount knowledge to scientific community and fill the identified gap. The last but not the least, it may help to initiate comprehensive intervention to the management of PTSD, Depression, Anxiety, GBV, Psychosocial Support in IDPs following the North Ethiopia war.','Methods \r\n	 Study area and Period\r\nThe project will be executed in Amhara Regional State (ARS), North Wollo Zone areas where IDP settings are located. Recently, more than one million peoples are internally displaced and approximately more than 5 million peoples are found in dire condition following the north Ethiopia war. The project will be implemented from December 2021 to June 2022.\r\n	Study Design\r\nMixed approach design will be implemented. Cross-sectional epidemiological study design and Phenomenological qualitative study approach will be implemented for quantitative and qualitative study, respectively.   \r\n	Source Population\r\nAll people who are displaced and returned from North Wollo zone will be the source population.\r\n	Study Population\r\nAll people who are displaced and returned from North Wollo zone and available in the study area during study period, will be study populations.\r\n	Eligibility Criteria\r\n	Inclusion Criteria\r\nAll peoples who were displaced recently following North Ethiopia war will be included in the assessment\r\n	Exclusion Criteria\r\nParticipants who will be an able to provide the information following acute sever illness will be excluded.  \r\n	Study variables \r\nOutcome variables: Psychiatric outcomes; PTSD, Depression, Anxiety, GBV, Psychosocial support\r\nIndependent variables;\r\n	Scio-economic and demographic factors:  sex, education, marital status, age, livelihood, poverty, living condition, separation from family member, occupation, religion, social isolation, residency, number of children, family size, disability, socioeconomic deprivation, family cohesion, Income source,\r\n	Clinical related factors: depression, anxiety, childhood trauma, PTSD, GBV, health condition, history of mental illness, suicidal ideation, injury condition\r\n	Environmental related factors: time since displacement, exposure to violence, stressful life events, health care access, persecution, infidelity, perceived sense of safety, social protection services, school interruption, information, racism\r\n	 psychosocial and Behavioral related factors: drug use, social support, stress, psychological distress, substance use problems, discrimination, culture, self-esteem, perceived stigma \r\n	Operational Definitions\r\n	PTSD: To measure PTSD PCL-5 20 items questionary with a score of ≥ 31 cutoff point will be used.\r\n	Social Support: Poor Social Support: a score of “3-8” on Oslo Social Support Scale; Moderate Social Support: a score of “9-11” on Oslo Social Support Scale; Strong Social Support: a score of “12-14”.\r\n	Current substance use: using at least one of a specific substance for non-medical purpose within the last three months (alcohol, chat, tobacco, others).\r\n	Gender Based Violence among female: when a women score greater than ore equals to one against either sexual violence, physical violence checklist score will indicate the presence of GBV.\r\n	Sample Size Determination and Sampling Procedure\r\n	Sample Size Determination\r\n Single population proportion formula was used to calculate the sample size. The assumptions were; proportion of the outcome variable  that was taken from study conducted in south Ethiopia which was 58.4%[62],  4% level of precision (d) to increase level of precision, ∝/2= significance level at ∝= 0.05.\r\n                                                             n=((Z α/2)^2 p(1-p))/d^(2 ) \r\nn=((1.96)^2 0.584(1-0.584))/〖0.04〗^(2 ) \r\nN= 584, \r\nThen multiple by the design effect 2 give as 584*2=1,168, By adding 10% non-response the final sample size used for the assessment will be 1285.\r\n	Sampling Procedure\r\nTo reach study participants multistage and simple random sampling technique following interviewer-administered face-to-face interview data collection approach will be used. The sampling frame will be secured from IDP s coordinators and other government officials; by having the sampling frame participants will be selected using computer generated random method by using open epi statistical software.\r\nFor qualitative part: Data will be collected using more than one data collection technique in order to have in-depth information of the problem. Qualitative data collection techniques; different interviewing technique such as IDI, KII and FGD will be executed in order to triangulate information and have quality data. Qualitative participants will be approached purposively and recruiting participants will continue until theoretical saturation is achieved. This all-qualitative approach will data collection procedures will be audio recorded given the permission has get secured from participants for audio record before the initiation of the interview. Semi structured   interview guide will be used. All the IDIs, KII and FGD will conduct by Amharic language, for validity of the interview guide it will be translated from English to Amharic and vice versa.  \r\nThe translation will be done by the research team which is consists researchers with different discipline including senior psychiatrist, mental health and public health professionals. All the FGD and IDIs data will be collected by researchers who have experience of qualitative data collection, transcriptions, translation and who have qualitative research training. \r\nData source\r\nFor KII; Regional focal points, Zonal Focal points, woreda Focal points, IDPs Coordinators, Religious leaders, woreda and Kebele leaders, and Humanitarian assistance organization focal persons will be selected purposively based on their proximity to the crisis. For FGD; will be conducted among Female groups, adolescent girl groups, Male groups, group that contain both females and males; whereas for IDI individuals will be recruited in order to have their in-depth lived experience. \r\n	Data Collection Instruments\r\nValidated & designed assessment tools will be considered in data collection; PTSD will be measured using PCL-5 checklist which has a 20 items self- report questionnaire with rating scale of 0-4 for each symptom will be used. A total symptom severity score ranges from 0-80 and PCL-5 cutoff >=31 score will indicate the presence of  PTSD [66, 67].  The tool used to measure anxiety, depression and stress is called DASS-21 which contain 21 items which has been designed  and every DASS -21 scales has 7 items divided in to subscales with similar cut-off score [68, 69].\r\nIn addition daily stress life events will be measured using  a tool called List of Threatening Experiences (LTE) [70].  Social support will be measured using Oslo 3 items tool and categorized as Poor Social Support: a score of “3-8” on Oslo Social Support Scale; Moderate Social Support: a score of “9-11” on Oslo Social Support Scale; Strong Social Support: a score of “12-14”[71]. ASSIST will be used to assess substance use and if an individual is positive for either of (tobacco, alcohol, Cannabis, Cocaine, hallucinogen, and etc..) will be considered as substance user.  \r\n	Data Quality Assurance\r\nTo assure the quality of the data the questionnaires will be translated into local language Amharic and translate back to in English language. Five days of training will be given for data collectors and supervisor to assure the quality of data. Pre-test of all structured questionnaires will be performed. Data will be collected by five psychiatry professions and will be supervised daily by one Epidemiologist and one Biostatistician. The necessary feedbacks will provide back daily for the data collectors throughout the study period. The quality of data will also check during data entry, analysis, and interpretation.\r\nFor Qualitative part: Interview guide will be developed by research team and the content which should be included with the interview guide will be decided by research team. For the quality of the data qualitative researcher will be trained for five (5) days about data collection, participant recruitment, data transcription and translation. During training session mock interview will be conducted and finally debriefing will be considered with qualitative researchers and supervisors. Ten (10) qualitative researchers (of these 5 males and 5 female) will be recruited including FGD facilitator and note taker. \r\n	Method of Data Analysis\r\nStatistical packages, EpiData version 3.1 will be used to enter data and STATA version 14 will be executed for data management and vigorous analysis. To assess psychiatric related outcomes different form of descriptive analysis will be performed accordingly. Texts, table and graph will be used for data presentation and mean, standard deviation and percentage will be used for data summarization. To identify risk factors supposed associated with psychiatric outcomes, simple and multivariable linear regression statistical modeling will be used. Furthermore, T test, ANOVA will used to compare means accordingly. To diagnosis the statistical model which will be fitted with the data associated linear regression model assumptions will be evaluated. Furthermore, to declare statistical association or significancy, 𝞫 Coefficient with 95% CI at P value >=0.05 will be reported. whereas qualitative audio records or data will be transcribed into local language using verbatim approach and translated into English then coded and categorized using thematic approach. Double check up will be done for the precision of transcription.  For qualitative data handling, coding, management and in order to categorize in to higher order of category, AtlasTi qualitative software will be used. The coding will start with descriptive label. Coding could be done by two coders and compared to increase the rigor. Then, there will be refining, expanding and rejecting codes. Finally, themes will be generated by inductive analysis. \r\n	Ethical Consideration\r\nEthical approval will be obtained from Institutional review Board (IRB), Institute of Health, Jimma University. Supporting letter will be obtained from Jimma University and Amhara Regional Health Bureau. The objectives of the project will be explained for any concerned body and participants at each level. Written informed consent will be secured from all participants before the start of the interview. For children aged 9-18 years or for adolescent assent will be taken from their Guardians. Participants will be informed the rights to withdraw or to not answer for some part of the questionaries when they are not interested. The participation will be volunteer based. The confidentiality of the information will be maintained.\r\n	Plan for dissemination and ensuring utilization of findings\r\nIn our dissemination plan, primarily the terminal report of the project will be communicated to the above aforementioned Jimma University, Institute of Health, Health Research and Postgraduate Directing Office. Furthermore, the project findings will be disseminated to Amhara Regional Health Bureau, to humanitarian assistance organization, Federal Ministry of Health for timely actions and rational allocations of resources. In connection and collaboration with the identified stakeholders working in this project area, we will discuss on fund raising to communicate our project finding and introduce appropriate comprehensive intervention with. The last but not the least, the findings will be published with reputable peer reviewed international journals.','<ol start=\"3\">\r\n	<li><span style=\"font-size:14pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri Light&quot;,sans-serif\"><span style=\"color:#2e74b5\"><a name=\"_Hlk88149877\"></a><a name=\"_Toc88172186\"> <span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">References</span></span></a></span></span></span></li>\r\n</ol>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><a name=\"_ENREF_1\">1.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Edition FJAPA: <strong>Diagnostic and statistical manual of mental disorders</strong>. 2014.</a></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><a name=\"_ENREF_2\">2.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; World Bank: <strong>Mental health: lessons learned in 2020 for 2021 and forward</strong>. FEBRUARY 11, 2021.</a></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><a name=\"_ENREF_3\">3.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; WHO: <strong>One in five people have mental disorders in post-conflict settings</strong>. 2019.</a></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><a name=\"_ENREF_4\">4.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Charlson F, van Ommeren M, Flaxman A, Cornett J, Whiteford H, Saxena SJTL: <strong>New WHO prevalence estimates of mental disorders in conflict settings: a systematic review and meta-analysis</strong>. 2019, <strong>394</strong>(10194):240-248.</a></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><a name=\"_ENREF_5\">5.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Fiskin A, Miglani M, Buzza CJPA: <strong>Implications of global mental health for addressing health disparities in high-income countries</strong>. 2018, <strong>48</strong>(3):149-153.</a></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><a name=\"_ENREF_6\">6.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Roberts B, Browne JJGph: <strong>A systematic review of factors influencing the psychological health of conflict-affected populations in low-and middle-income countries</strong>. 2011, <strong>6</strong>(8):814-829.</a></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><a name=\"_ENREF_7\">7.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Organization WH: <strong>World Mental Health Day: an opportunity to kick-start a massive scale-up in investment in mental health</strong>. 27 August 2020.</a></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><a name=\"_ENREF_8\">8.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; WHO: <strong>Suicide key facts: World Health Organization Report</strong>. 17 June 2021.</a></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><a name=\"_ENREF_9\">9.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Cantor D, Swartz J, Roberts B, Abbara A, Ager A, Bhutta ZA, Blanchet K, Madoro Bunte D, Chukwuorji JC, Daoud N<em> et al</em>: <strong>Understanding the health needs of internally displaced persons: A scoping review</strong>. <em>Journal of Migration and Health </em>2021, <strong>4</strong>:100071.</a></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><a name=\"_ENREF_10\">10.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Marquez PV: <strong>Mental Health Among Displaced People and Refugees: Making the Case for Action at the World Bank Group</strong>. In<em>.</em>: World Bank; 2016.</a></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><a name=\"_ENREF_11\">11.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; <strong>Evaluation of UNHCR&rsquo;s Emergency Response to the influx of Syrian Refugees into Turkey</strong>. 2014-2015.</a></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><a name=\"_ENREF_12\">12.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Children St: <strong>Save the Children&rsquo;s End of Childhood Report 2018.</strong> 2019.</a></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><a name=\"_ENREF_13\">13.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; disasters Bcfroteo: <strong>People affected by conflict: humanitarian needs in numbers.</strong> 2013.</a></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><a name=\"_ENREF_14\">14.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Mahdis Kamali, Mariella Munyuzangabo, Fahad J Siddiqui, Michelle F Gaffey, Sarah Meteke, Daina Als, Reena P Jain, Amruta Radhakrishnan, Shailja Shah, Anushka Ataullahjan<em> et al</em>: <strong>Delivering mental health and psychosocial support interventions to women and children in conflict settings: a systematic review</strong>. <em>BMJ Global Health </em>2020.</a></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><a name=\"_ENREF_15\">15.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; WHO: <strong>Mental health and forced displacement</strong>. 31 August 2021.</a></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><a name=\"_ENREF_16\">16.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; WHO: <strong>World Mental Health Day &ndash; The state of global mental health</strong>. October 8th, 2021.</a></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><a name=\"_ENREF_17\">17.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; World Health O, Unhcr: <strong>mhGAP Humanitarian Intervention Guide (mhGAP-HIG): clinical management of mental, neurological and substance use conditions in humanitarian emergencies</strong>. Geneva: World Health Organization; 2015.</a></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><a name=\"_ENREF_18\">18.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; WHO: <strong>Promoting the health of refugees and migrants: draft global action plan, 2019&ndash;2023</strong>. 23 May 2019.</a></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><a name=\"_ENREF_19\">19.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; WHO: <strong>mhGAP Intervention Guide for mental, neurological and substance use disorders in non-specialized health settings Version 2.0</strong>. 2019.</a></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><a name=\"_ENREF_20\">20.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Roberts B, Makhashvili N, Javakhishvili JJIaG-TLSoH, Medicine T: <strong>Hidden burdens of conflict: issues of mental health and access to services among internally displaced persons in Ukraine</strong>. 2017.</a></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><a name=\"_ENREF_21\">21.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Duncan H, Popp I: <strong>World migration report 2018</strong>. In<em>.</em>: Chapter; 2017.</a></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><a name=\"_ENREF_22\">22.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Ronald C.N. Oginyi OSMaONJ: <strong>Depression, Psychological Distress, Social Support and Coping Strategies as Predictors of Psychological Well-Being Among Internally Displaced Persons</strong>. 2017.</a></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><a name=\"_ENREF_23\">23.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Kadir A, Shenoda S, Goldhagen J, Pitterman S: <strong>The Effects of Armed Conflict on Children</strong>. <em>Pediatrics </em>2018:e20182586.</a></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><a name=\"_ENREF_24\">24.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Richards A, Ospina-Duque J, Barrera-Valencia M, Escobar-Rinc&oacute;n J, Ardila-Guti&eacute;rrez M, Metzler T, Marmar C: <strong>Posttraumatic stress disorder, anxiety and depression symptoms, and psychosocial treatment needs in Colombians internally displaced by armed conflict: A mixed-method evaluation</strong>. <em>Psychological Trauma: Theory, Research, Practice, and Policy </em>2011, <strong>3</strong>(4):384.</a></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><a name=\"_ENREF_25\">25.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Irina Kuznetsova, Oksana Mikheieva, Jon Catling, John Round, Svitlana Babenko: <strong>The Mental Health of Internally Displaced People and the General Population in Ukraine</strong>. 2019.<