:39px\">\r\n			<p>5.</p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top; width:254px\">\r\n			<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Times,&quot;serif&quot;\">&nbsp;Stationary </span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top; width:230px\">\r\n			<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Times,&quot;serif&quot;\">37,000.00&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top; width:115px\">\r\n			<p>&nbsp;</p>\r\n			</td>\r\n		</tr>\r\n		<tr>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:1px solid black; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top; width:39px\">\r\n			<p>&nbsp;</p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top; width:254px\">\r\n			<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Times,&quot;serif&quot;\">Total </span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top; width:230px\">\r\n			<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Times,&quot;serif&quot;\">494,090.00</span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top; width:115px\">\r\n			<p>&nbsp;</p>\r\n			</td>\r\n		</tr>\r\n		<tr>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:1px solid black; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top; width:39px\">\r\n			<p>&nbsp;</p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top; width:254px\">\r\n			<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Times,&quot;serif&quot;\">Contingency 10%</span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top; width:230px\">\r\n			<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Times,&quot;serif&quot;\">49,409.00</span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top; width:115px\">\r\n			<p>&nbsp;</p>\r\n			</td>\r\n		</tr>\r\n		<tr>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:1px solid black; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:13px; vertical-align:top; width:39px\">\r\n			<p>&nbsp;</p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:13px; vertical-align:top; width:254px\">\r\n			<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Times,&quot;serif&quot;\">Total Budget</span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:13px; vertical-align:top; width:230px\">\r\n			<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Times,&quot;serif&quot;\"><strong><u><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">543,499.00</span></span></u></strong></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:13px; vertical-align:top; width:115px\">\r\n			<p>&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;&nbsp;</p>\r\n			</td>\r\n		</tr>\r\n	</tbody>\r\n</table>\r\n\r\n<p>&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;&nbsp;</p>\r\n\r\n<p>&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;&nbsp;</p>\r\n\r\n<p>&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;&nbsp;</p>',1,NULL,NULL,NULL),(30,152,9,21,'Schizophrenia is a chronic and severe psychiatric disorder often associated with cognitive impairment and affective, mainly depressive, symptoms. It affects nearly 20 million people around the globe. Antipsychotic medication is recommended for the management of acute psychotic episodes and the prevention of recurrences and relapses in such patients. Despite their well-known efficacy, antipsychotic medications are not without adverse effects. Owing to such unwanted effects, it is desirable and recommended to monitor and measure the concentration of antipsychotic drugs in blood plasma by a technique called Therapeutic Drug Monitoring (TDM). A hospital-based longitudinal study will be conducted at Jimma Medical Center and Ethiopian Public Health Institute on 300 patients with schizophrenia who can be stratified into two groups based on their exposure to substances.',NULL,'Therapeutic drug monitoring of antipsychotic drugs prescribed to adult patients with schizophrenia: A novel comparative experimental study to assess treatment outcome, prognosis and resistance pattern among substance users and non-users.','2021-11-19 09:19:35','1.	To assess magnitude of treatment outcome among substance user and non-user schizophrenic patients\r\na)	Enables to predict the prognosis of patients with schizophrenia\r\nb)	Helps to reduce length of hospitalization\r\nc)	Helps to prevent the development of resistant schizophrenia\r\nd)	Helps to prevent complications of schizophrenia\r\n2.	To compare the magnitude of treatment outcome among the two groups\r\na)	Helps to identify the impact of substance use on treatment outcome\r\nb)	Helps to identify which groups experience poor treatment outcome\r\nc)	Enables researchers to perform further studies in such groups\r\n3.	To assess the adequacy of antipsychotic drug dose in blood plasma in the two arms\r\na)	Enables to predict the desired amount of drug dose in blood to provide better outcome\r\nb)	Adds evidence about the plasma concentration of antipsychotic drugs specific to Ethiopian patients \r\nc)	Helps to identify variations from the expected plasma concentrations known by experimental recommendations\r\n4.	To compare the rate of drug concentration among the two groups\r\na)	Helps to identify differences in plasma drug concentration between the two groups\r\nb)	Helps to examine the effect of substance use on plasma drug concentration of antipsychotic drugs\r\n5.	To assess factors responsible for sub optimal drug dose in blood plasma\r\na)	Enables policy makers to devise a strategy in solving identified factors\r\nb)	Helps as a source of evidence for various health care facilities\r\nc)	Helps as a source of data for other researchers \r\n6.	To analyze drug resistance pattern in schizophrenic patients\r\na)	Provides a novel principle towards drug resistance among psychiatric patients which was previously not known\r\nb)	Shifts the principle of drug resistance philosophy from antibiotics to other medications used for non-communicable diseases\r\nc)	 Helps physicians to select safe medications based on their resistance profiles \r\n7.	To assess the impact of substance use on pharmacokinetics of antipsychotic drugs\r\na)	 Provides solid evidence on the effect of smoking on metabolism of antipsychotic medications \r\nb)	Provides data on the effect of substance use on treatment outcome \r\nc)	Provides information on the prognosis of schizophrenic patients who are concurrent substance users\r\n8.	To assess the impact of substance use on drug-drug interactions \r\na)	 Enables physicians to choose drugs that doesn’t interact with addictive substances \r\nb)	Adds evidence towards prognosis of schizophrenic patients who are concurrent substance users\r\nc)	Adds evidence towards drug resistance secondary to substance use among schizophrenic patients who are concurrent substance users','b8c66d39ba3bf94d0edf766f7aa27c4a','completed','Schizophrenia is a chronic and severe psychiatric disorder often associated with cognitive impairment and affective, mainly depressive, symptoms. It affects nearly 20 million people around the globe. It is correlated with significant disability and might influence educational and occupational performance. In addition, people with schizophrenia are 2-3 times more likely to die early than the general population (1,2,3).\r\nThe national prevalence of schizophrenia Ethiopia is not known even though few localized studies reported incidences and prevalence. For instance, a community based study in Butajira found that the estimated prevalence of psychosis was 4.7/1000. In another study conducted in Addis Ababa, a weighted lifetime and one month prevalence of schizophrenia were 0.4% and 0.3% respectively (4,5).\r\nSchizophrenia is a treatable mental illness. Antipsychotic medication is recommended for management of acute psychotic episodes and prevention of recurrences and relapses in such patients (6). Despite their well-known efficacy, antipsychotic medications are not without adverse effects. Owing to such unwanted effects, it is desirable and recommended to monitor and measure the concentration of antipsychotic drugs in blood plasma by a technique called Therapeutic Drug Monitoring (TDM) (7,8).\r\n TDM of antipsychotic drugs involves measurement of drug serum concentrations followed by interpretation of results. It is a powerful tool that allows patient selective treatment for the specific needs of individuals. It can help in monitoring medication compliance, dose modification, reducing the risk of overdose and in cost-effectiveness in the management of various disorders including schizophrenia (9,10).\r\nBenefits of using TDM for patients being treated with antipsychotics include: ensuring adherence, quantitatively adjusting dosages for medication interactions or genetic variations, ensuring an adequate trial of a medi¬cation before considering it a treatment failure. Evaluating the level of antipsychotic drugs in blood/serum also is a well-accepted technique of determining real treatment failure than depending on the clinical judgment of the treating psychiatrist (11).\r\nIn the past decade, there has been a desire  in designing  adequate treatments for patients with a schizophrenia and a comorbid substance use disorder. According to a study conducted in Spain, a decrease in cigarette smoking, alcohol, cocaine or cannabis use led to an improvement of overall psychiatric symptoms (12). More importantly, a study has shown that substances including cigarette and alcohol enhance the elimination of antipsychotic drugs by inducing a cytochrome P450 enzyme thus leading to treatment failure (9). \r\nIn countries like Ethiopia, the principle of therapeutic drug monitoring in general is a new principle. When it comes to mental illnesses, treating physicians rely on worsening or improvement of signs and symptoms for their clinical decision making. This type of monitoring cannot identify drug toxicity and drug resistance. \r\nIn addition, majority of studies conducted abroad were among patients with schizophrenia receiving a second generation/ atypical antipsychotic medications. Unfortunately, majority of patients in Ethiopia still rely of the first generation/typical antipsychotic medications that are well known in their adverse effects including extrapyramidal symptoms, tardive dyskinesia and neuroleptic malignant syndrome which might occur partly due to their excess plasma concentration.\r\nTherefore, the present study aims to conduct a therapeutic drug monitoring on both first generation and second generation antipsychotic medications as a tool to assess treatment outcome, prognosis and resistance pattern. Summary of gaps in Ethiopia and study area is presented in Table 1.\r\n Uses of therapeutic drug monitoring\r\n	Decreases occurrence of drug toxicity\r\n	Improves adherence to antipsychotic medications \r\n	Helps to identify pseudopharmacoresistance problems \r\n	Explains the effect of substance use on plasma concentration of antipsychotic drugs\r\n	Helps in the diagnosis of treatment-resistant schizophrenia (13)','Study area and period \r\nThe present study will be conducted at two sites. Patients will be selected and sociodemographic, clinical and blood sample will be obtained from adult patients admitted to psychiatric ward of Jimma Medical Center.  The collected blood sample will be analyzed for drug concentration at the Ethiopian Public Health Institute (EPHI) in Addis Ababa. The study will be conducted between February 1 and October 30, 2022.\r\n3.2 Study design \r\nA hospital based longitudinal study will be employed \r\n3.3 Population \r\n3.3.1 Study population \r\n For sub research question 1, 4 and 9 – adult patients with schizophrenia who are neither substance users nor had history of substance use\r\nFor sub research question 2,5 and 8 - adult patients with schizophrenia who are currently substance users or had a history of substance use in the past 6 months.\r\n3.4 Eligibility criteria \r\n3.4.1 Inclusion criteria\r\n•	Age ≥18 years old\r\n•	Willingness to participate in the study \r\n3.4.2 Exclusion criteria\r\n•	Patients with other comorbid psychiatric conditions\r\n•	Patients with treatment resistant schizophrenia (because such patients may receive more than 2 antipsychotic medications)\r\n•	Patients who are receiving other drugs for mental illness such as depression and anxiety (because drug interaction can affect plasma concentration of antipsychotic drug) \r\n•	Patients who doesn’t have a care giver at the time of data collection\r\n3.5 Sample size estimation and sampling procedure\r\nAt baseline, study participants will be grouped in two groups. Group-1 will include 50 adult patients with schizophrenia without a history of substance use. Similarly, Arm-2 will include 50 adult patients with schizophrenia with a history of substance use. All study participants who were available during data collection period and who fulfill the inclusion criteria will be included in the study. \r\n3.6 Data collection technique\r\n3.6.1 Data collection tools\r\nTogether with patients’ medical chart, a semi structured questionnaire will be developed from different literatures to identify risk factors for poor treatment outcome. The questionnaire will be translated to Amharic language and Afaan Oromo. Data will be collected either by self-administered form (for patients or care givers who are literate) or using interview based form (for illiterate participants). Data on clinical and medication related variables will also be collected using a checklist.\r\n3.6.2 Data collectors and supervisors  \r\nTwo nurses and two laboratory technicians will be involved in the data collection process. The first nurse will collect sociodemographic data of the patients. The second will be involved in collecting clinical and medication related information.  Regarding the laboratory process, the two laboratory technicians will draw blood from patients and store the collected blood based on a good laboratory practice. About 3 mL of blood will be drawn from patients after they take their routine medications. Two of the research project team members will supervise data collectors during the whole process. \r\n3.6.3 Laboratory measurement \r\nTherapeutic drug monitoring will be performed for five antipsychotic medications namely fluphenazine, risperidone, haloperidol, clozapine and olanzapine. The laboratory test will be performed at the Ethiopian Public Health Institute (EPHI) Addis Ababa, Ethiopia. Technique employed to perform therapeutic drug monitoring is High Performance Liquid Chromatography – Mass Spectroscopy (HPLC – MS). The therapeutic reference range of the medications to be analyzed in the blood plasma is presented in Table 2. \r\nindependent two samples t test will be used to compare the plasma concentrations of antipsychotic drugs between the two treatment groups stratified by their exposure to substances i.e. substance users and substance non users. In case where Levene’s test for variance is greater than 0.05, equal variance will be assumed, otherwise unequal variance will be assumed.\r\nBy classifying groups into two, a one sample t test will be used to compare the plasma concentration of antipsychotic medications with the standard therapeutic plasma concentration provided by guidelines.\r\nPredictors of poor treatment outcome will be identified by fitting logistic regression model, after identifying candidate variable in binary logistic regression model with selection p ≤ 0.25 and all variable’s whose 95 % CI is not inclusive of one will be considered significant. The Hosmer–Lemeshow test will be done for goodness of fit of the model.\r\nA p value of < 0.05 will be considered statistically significant. Data will be expressed as mean ± SD for continuous variables and as frequencies for categorical variables and will be presented in narration, tables and figures.\r\n3.8 Quality assurance\r\nThe data collected on the structured questionnaire will be checked for its completeness, and piloting of research will be done in Jimma health center. For laboratory investigations, Standard quality control protocols will be performed. All instruments will be operated and quality controlled according to the manufacturer’s instructions and normal and abnormal controls will be run. No analysis will be done if controls are out of range.\r\n3.9 Plan for dissemination and ensuring utilization of findings\r\nPrior to dissemination of the result, results of patients with abnormal finding will be communicated with their physicians for possible management and all patients will be given their laboratory result on their next visit.  The result of this study will be communicated to the Jimma university Research directorate and national ministry of Health. It will also be published on peer reviewed reputable journals. The result of the study will also be communicated to the hospitals through journal articles, newspapers, bulletins and pamphlets in major languages spoken in Ethiopia.\r\n3.10 Operational definitions\r\n	Antipsychotic medications – medications used for management of schizophrenia and for the purpose of this study include fluphenazine, haloperidol, clozapine, olanzapine and risperidone. \r\n	Substances – are chemicals that interfere with the mechanism of action of antipsychotic medications and include smoking, alcohol, Khat and cannabis\r\n	Substance users – adult schizophrenic patients who use cigarrete, alcohol, khat and cannabis.\r\n3.11 COVID-19 related protocols\r\nWe are in the era of the pandemic. We should keep ourselves, health professionals and patients from Covid-19. Training will be given for data collectors about prevention of COVID-19. Research team members will disseminate face masks for all data collectors and data collectors will disseminate them to study participants. In addition, all data collectors will be provided sanitizers. In addition to the face masks, the data collectors will train patients to keep social distancing, wear face masks regularly and use sanitizers.\r\nEthical considerations\r\nThe study protocol will receive an approval from the Institutional Review Board of the Institute of Health Sciences, Jimma University and will be conducted as per International Conference for Harmonization, Good Clinical Practice guidelines. All patients will provide a written informed consent to participate. In order to keep confidentiality of patient information, each patient sample will be coded and only patient initials will be used. \r\nInformation will be first given to study participants/ care givers about the nature of the research including the following:-\r\n	To let them know that their participation in the study is entirely voluntary.\r\n	To assure them that if they choose not to participate, the services they get from the hospital will continue.\r\n	To ask data collectors about words they don’t understand when filling/answering questions \r\n	To talk to anyone they feel comfortable and to take time  with before deciding to take part in the study\r\n	To let them know that about 3mL of blood will be drawn from their arm after they take their medication (the time differs depending on the type of medication they are taking).\r\n	 To let them know that they may change their mind and stop participating even if they agreed to participate earlier. \r\n	To let them know that the information they provide is kept confidential. \r\n	To let them know that the knowledge we get from this study by including them as our respondents will benefit all patients.\r\n	To let them know that they can meet data collectors and supervisors anytime they need.','<ol>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"background-color:white\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"><span style=\"color:black\">Vos T, Abajobir AA, Abate KH, et al. Global, regional, and national incidence, prevalence, and years lived with disability for 328 diseases and injuries for 195 countries, 1990&ndash;2016: a systematic analysis for the Global Burden of Disease Study 2016. The Lancet. 2017 Sep 16;390(10100):1211-59.</span></span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"background-color:white\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"><span style=\"color:#222222\">World Health Organization. Report of the international pilot study of schizophrenia. World Health Organization; 1973.</span></span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\">&nbsp;<span style=\"background-color:white\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"><span style=\"color:black\">Laursen TM, Nordentoft M, Mortensen PB. Excess early mortality in schizophrenia. Annual review of clinical psychology. 2014 Mar 28;10:425-48.</span></span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"background-color:white\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"><span style=\"color:black\">Kebede D, Alem A, Shibre T, et al. Onset and clinical course of schizophrenia in Butajira-Ethiopia. Social Psychiatry and Psychiatric Epidemiology. 2003;38(11):625-31.</span></span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"background-color:white\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"><span style=\"color:black\">Kebede D, Alem A. Major mental disorders in Addis Ababa, Ethiopia. I. Schizophrenia, schizoaffective and cognitive disorders. Acta Psychiatrica Scandinavica. 1999;100:11-7.</span></span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"background-color:white\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"><span style=\"color:black\">Muscatello MR, Bruno A, Pandolfo G, et al. Emerging treatments in the management of schizophrenia&ndash;focus on sertindole. Drug design, development and therapy. 2010;4:187.</span></span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"background-color:white\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"><span style=\"color:black\">Newcomer JW. Second-generation (atypical) antipsychotics and metabolic effects. CNS drugs. 2005;19(1):1-93.</span></span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"background-color:white\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"><span style=\"color:black\">Herrmann N, Lanctot KL. Do atypical antipsychotics cause stroke? CNS drugs. 2005;19(2):91-103.</span></span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"background-color:white\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"><span style=\"color:black\">Grundmann M, Kacirova I, Urinovska R. Therapeutic drug monitoring of atypical antipsychotic drugs. Acta pharmaceutica. 2014;64(4):387-401.</span></span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"background-color:white\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"><span style=\"color:black\">Kane JM, Correll CU. Past and present progress in the pharmacologic treatment of schizophrenia. The Journal of clinical psychiatry. 2010;71(9):1-5</span></span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"background-color:white\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"><span style=\"color:black\">Meyer JM. Is monitoring of plasma antipsychotic levels useful? Current Psychiatry.2015;14(11):16-9.</span></span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Palatino LT Std&quot;,serif\"><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt\"><span style=\"background-color:white\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"><span style=\"color:black\">San L, Arranz B, Martinez-Raga J. Antipsychotic drug treatment of schizophrenic patients with substance abuse disorders. European Addiction Research. 2007;13(4):230-43.</span></span></span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"background-color:white\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"><span style=\"color:black\">Hiemke C, Bergemann N, Clement HW, et al. Consensus guidelines for therapeutic drug monitoring in neuropsychopharmacology: update 2017. Pharmacopsychiatry. 2018;51(01/02):9-62.</span></span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"background-color:white\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"><span style=\"color:black\">McCutcheon R, Beck K, D&#39;Ambrosio E, et al. Antipsychotic plasma levels in the assessment of poor treatment response in schizophrenia. Acta Psychiatrica Scandinavica. 2018;137(1):39-46.</span></span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"background-color:white\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"><span style=\"color:black\">BPharm RM, Peterson GM, McLean S, et al. Monitoring plasma levels of fluphenazine during chronic therapy with fluphenazine decanoate. Journal of clinical pharmacy and therapeutics. 1995;20(2):55-62.</span></span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"background-color:white\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"><span style=\"color:black\">Coryell W, Miller DD, Perry PJ. Haloperidol plasma levels and dose optimization. American Journal of Psychiatry. 1998;155(1):48-53.</span></span></span></span></span></li>\r\n</ol>','To perform therapeutic drug monitoring of antipsychotic drugs prescribed to adult patients with schizophrenia to help assess treatment outcome, prognosis and resistance pattern among substance users and non-users.',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,2,'Schizophrenia,Ethiopia,Therapeutic drug monitoring,Antipsychotic medications',10,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),(31,23,9,15,'In developing countries the exponential development into ICT and system based technology has had significant effect upon business & infrastructure distribution structures of the 20th century global economy. Improving the quality of the health sector and transforming this sector to system based technology are the crucial activity for achieving the development goal.  Practicing and improving quality management of health centers are contributed the improvement of health related services. \r\nThe proposed Hospital Management System (HMS) delivers the use of modernized actions, control, and financial control, for effective patient care, improved proﬁtability and enhanced administration. \r\nThe existing system of hospital management system is used a manual system until now. This system of management has had many problems including large weighting time of patients to gat service, ambiguity to identify patient’s history, absence of well-organized database, Lack of security of data, consumes large volume of paper work, manual based work, the absence of higher officials direct role. Besides a computerized system is needed to avoid all these limitations and working more accurately. Web-based Hospital Management Systems offer benefits of efficient compliance & tracking, efficient procedures, the quality of the patient’s service, strict financial containment and extended efficiency. Therefore we are highly initiated to develop secure web-based hospital management system.','❖	To provide management information system report on demand to management for better decision making.','DEVELOPING WEB-BASED HOSPITAL MANAGEMENT SYSTEM FOR TWO SELECTEDD HOSPITALE’S IN JIMMA ZONE, OROMIA REGION SOUTH WEST ETHIOPIA','2021-11-19 09:20:25','It provides the top management at a single point of control.\r\n	It  handles activities of major departments in a hospital like:\r\n	Well organized Secure data. \r\n	Ensure data accuracy’s. \r\n	Administrator controls the entire system. \r\n	Minimize manual data entry. \r\n	Greater efficiency. \r\n	User friendly and interactive.\r\n	Minimum time required.','afeb2f5467206dea924287d34255b493','completed','Hospital Management System(HMS)  is the new innovated system  that comprising social and professional  functions and practices that occur within different health organizations in order to achieve proposed goals through humans and the other resources (1). Monitoring  and evaluation of  the overall activities  in hospitals  information about diseases and information about patient care   is  too important [(2) ].  The knowledge  and skills   of  health professionals, semi-professionals and professional’s personnel are mobilizes in hospitals  to provide highly personalized personnel services to patients[(3)]. \r\nThe system  should   be improved not only on the quality of data produced, rather  on evidence of the sustained use of data to improve the over all health system performance, upgrading  the qualities of health systems in terms of data availability and use often requires interventions that address a wide range of significant determinants of quality a health  performance. We all are visited at least ones in a certain hospital or health centers specially governmental hospitals either for treatment or collecting data for conducting research. In our visit it is visual that we have seen the manual handling of the record of all the patients’ information. We have  looking from  this method of recording  facing a number of problem like weighting time, losing of our previous charts, absence of well-organized or completed data and soon is time consuming ,highly prone to error. So our team decided to limit those problems and develop this web-based hospital management system. The purpose of this project is to automate the process of day to-day activities in the hospital. This system will make the complicated process as simple as possible using automated hospital management system on two selected hospitals. The system are designed that user can make further expansion without much eﬀorts in other hospitals and can be readily used by non-programming personal.','2.1. Project planning and scheduling \r\nOur project team describes this Project planning schedules by using Gantt charts. The chart uses to plan and subsequently report progress within the project environment. Initially, the project scope is defined and the appropriate methods for completing the project are determined. So by following this step, the durations for the various tasks necessary to complete the work are listed and grouped into a work breakdown structure. \r\n2.3. Relevance of Hospital information System \r\n  The most important necessity and reason for hospitals management systems automation are inadequacy of manual procedures (5). Due to extensive changes in medical technology and increased expectation of patients hospitals that lack hospital management Systems will not be able to compete with other hospitals. According to   (6), the hospital information systems give comfortable and quick access to patient data.  \r\n2.5 Hardware tools required are:\r\n	Personal computer (PC): almost all tasks of our project are performed on the computer.\r\n	Flash disk: required for data movement to store & transfer data from one PC to another PC.\r\n	Disks (CD, DVD): necessary for the movement of relevant data and for backup and recovery mechanisms.\r\n	Network cable: since our system is web based, it is a very necessary requirement. It also helps us to extract relevant information about our project from the internet.\r\n	Server: to store the data.\r\n	Stationeries (pen, paper): for writing all necessary documentations associated with the project.\r\n	Notebook: to take notes during data collection and for other documents.\r\n   2.6. Software tools required are:\r\n✔	MS-Access: to create and design the database which used to store the information of the customers & the employees of the organization.\r\n✔	Ubuntu Operating system: will be used for the system since it is readily available in laboratories.\r\n✔	Browsers: -since our system is web based, it is a very necessary requirement.\r\n✔	PHP: -To design the backend of the system whole application.\r\n✔	MYSQL :-for designing the database\r\n✔	Microsoft office Word 2013 :-for documenting the corresponding deliverables associated with the project\r\n✔	Vscode: For writing a code or program of the system.\r\n✔	Ubuntu Server: - To test the system by running\r\n✔	Ubuntu server:-To implementation the code and to design interface\r\n3.	Scope of this projects\r\nThis project aims to develop web-based hospital management system in two selected hospitals in Jimma zone Oromia region Ethiopia. This application contains login form, patient registration, doctor registration. Hospital Management application allow patients to edit their information like patient name, contact number, address, disease from which he is suffering from etc. It can be used in any Hospital, Clinic, Dispensary or Pathology labs for maintaining patient details and their test results.\r\n\r\n\r\n4.	SYSTEM IMPLEMENTATION \r\nImplementation is the phase where objectives of physical operations of the system turned into reality, which means a real working model. The crucial phase in the system development life cycle is the successful implementation of the new system design. The process of converting a new system into an operational one is known as system implementation. This includes all those activities that take place to convert from an old manual system to a new technical system. System implementation used to:\r\n	Reduce the number of workers that are required to do the task.\r\n	Decision making purpose using the reports those are generated from the system.\r\n	Accessing information in a fast way.\r\n	Giving work satisfaction for administrator, physicians, nurses and other staffs','<ol>\r\n	<li>&nbsp;</li>\r\n</ol>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">1. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Gupta A, Niranajan A. Hospital Management and Control System. Eur J Mol Clin Med. 2020;7(6):1079&ndash;85. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">2. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Haux R, Ammenwerth E, Winter A, Brigl B. Strategic information management in hospitals: an introduction to hospital information systems. Springer Science &amp; Business Media; 2004. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">3. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Zabel KL, Biermeier-Hanson BBJ, Baltes BB, Early BJ, Shepard A. Generational differences in work ethic: Fact or fiction? J Bus Psychol. 2017;32(3):301&ndash;15. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">4. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Adebisi OA, Oladosu DA, Busari OA, Oyewola Y V. Design and implementation of hospital management system. Int J Eng Innov Technol(IJEIT). 2015;5(1). </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">5. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Pinaire J, Az&eacute; J, Bringay S, Cayla G, Landais P. Hospital burden of coronary artery disease: Trends of myocardial infarction and/or percutaneous coronary interventions in France 2009&ndash;2014. PLoS One. 2019;14(5):e0215649. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">6. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Ghosh D, Guha R. What are we &lsquo;tweeting&rsquo;about obesity? Mapping tweets with topic modeling and Geographic Information System. Cartogr Geogr Inf Sci. 2013;40(2):90&ndash;102. </span></span></span></span></p>','The general objective of this project is to develop a web based secure hospital management system for two selected general hospitals in Jimma zone.',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,2,'Hospital management system, Automated, Jimma',10,'<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\">&nbsp;</p>\r\n\r\n<ol>\r\n	<li><span style=\"font-size:13pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Cambria,&quot;serif&quot;\"><span style=\"color:#4f81bd\"><a name=\"_Toc83731265\"></a><a name=\"_Toc87280580\"> Study work plan</a> </span></span></span></li>\r\n</ol>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><a name=\"_2et92p0\"></a></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"color:#4f81bd\"><strong><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Table- </span></span><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">2</span></span><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">-Project planning schedules reflecting proposed time frames, activities and outputs/deliverables of hospital management system </span></span></strong></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<table align=\"center\" cellspacing=\"0\" class=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse:collapse; border:none; width:659px\">\r\n	<tbody>\r\n		<tr>\r\n			<td rowspan=\"2\" style=\"background-color:#95b3d7; border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:1px solid black; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:1px solid black; height:11px; vertical-align:top; width:43px\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><strong><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">No.</span></strong></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td rowspan=\"2\" style=\"background-color:#95b3d7; border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:1px solid black; height:11px; vertical-align:top; width:307px\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif