</span></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:13px; vertical-align:top; width:384px\">\r\n			<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">4,120 </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n		</tr>\r\n		<tr>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:1px solid black; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:37px; vertical-align:top; width:277px\">\r\n			<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Stationary &amp;others</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:37px; vertical-align:top; width:384px\">\r\n			<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">20,472 </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n		</tr>\r\n		<tr>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:1px solid black; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:71px; vertical-align:top; width:277px\">\r\n			<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Personnel</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n			<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">salary</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:71px; vertical-align:top; width:384px\">\r\n			<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">469,800</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n			<p>&nbsp;</p>\r\n			</td>\r\n		</tr>\r\n		<tr>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:1px solid black; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:71px; vertical-align:top; width:277px\">\r\n			<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Reagents</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:71px; vertical-align:top; width:384px\">\r\n			<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><strong><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">539,445</span></span></strong></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n		</tr>\r\n		<tr>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:1px solid black; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:34px; vertical-align:top; width:277px\">\r\n			<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><strong><u><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Total </span></span></u></strong></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:34px; vertical-align:top; width:384px\">\r\n			<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><strong><u><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">1,057,837 </span></span></u></strong></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n		</tr>\r\n	</tbody>\r\n</table>',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),(34,114,9,18,'Background and rationale:  World Health Organization describes the term diabetes as a group of metabolic disorders characterized and identified by the presence of hyperglycemia in the absence of treatment. The prevalence of diabetes is unacceptably rising in both developed and resource limited country like Ethiopia. \r\nObjective: To assess the prevalence, uncontrolled, undiagnosed and burden of Diabetic Mellitus and associated factors among adults of Jimma zone, Southwest Ethiopia, 2021\r\nMethods and Material: Community based comparative cross sectional study will be employed on among adult (>18) population in Jimma zone from January 02 to September 20, 2022. Sample size for the study is calculated by using Epi-Info ver.7 for comparative cross sectional study and 2212(1106 urban and 1106 rural) population will be included in the study. A multistage cluster random sampling strategy will be used to select study participants from urban and rural residents. This study will use the WHO NCD STEPS instrument which consisted of three steps; socio-demographic and behavioural characteristics, physical measurement, and biochemical measurement. The demographic and behavioural risk factors will be collected through face-to-face interviews using an interviewer-administered pretested structured questionnaire, anthropometric and biochemical measurements will be collected by trained and experienced data collectors. The data will be entered and cleaned using Epi-Info version 7 and will be analysed using R software version 4.3.1. Descriptive statistics will be computed. Variables with p-value < 0.25 in the univariate binary logistic regression analysis will be fitted in the multiple logistic regression model. Adjusted odds ratio with a 95% confidence interval and value < 0.05 will be used to declare a significant association. The goodness of fitness of the model will be checked by likelihood ratio test.',NULL,'Prevalence, uncontrolled, undiagnosed  and burden of Diabetic Mellitus and associated factors among adults of Jimma zone, Southwest Ethiopia, 2021: Comparative cross-sectional study','2021-11-19 10:58:57','Mainly, this study helps study participants to know their diabetes mellitus status, risk factors and burdens of the disease. And also, it will provide guides for policy makers and stakeholders with updated information about prevalence of undiagnosed and uncontrolled diabetes mellitus for future planning and interventions. Individuals with undiagnosed diabetes confirmed during the study are encouraged to be treated and linked with health center. The result will also give an insight on diabetes mellitus and can be utilized as a benchmark for future researchers.','79c4615faffc925834ba168fb582b0fe','completed','According to World Health Organization (WHO), the term diabetes describes a group of metabolic disorders characterized and identified by the presence of hyperglycemia in the absence of treatment(1). The heterogeneous aetio-pathology of diabetes mellitus includes defects in insulin secretion, insulin action, or both, and disturbances of carbohydrate, fat and protein metabolism. It may present with characteristic symptoms such as thirst, polyuria, blurring of vision, and weight loss. The most severe clinical manifestations are ketoacidosis or a non-ketotic hyperosmolar state that may lead to dehydration, coma and, in the absence of effective treatment, death(1).  The majority of diabetes cases fall into two broad etiological categories (2). \r\nFour diagnostic tests for diabetes are currently recommended. Accordingly, people with fasting plasma glucose(FBG) values of ≥ 7.0 mmol/L (126 mg/dl), 2-hour post-load plasma glucose(OGTT) ≥ 11.1 mmol/L (200 mg/dl) (5), glycated haemoglobin (HbA1c) ≥ 6.5% (48 mmol/L); or a random blood glucose ≥ 11.1 mmol/L (200 mg/ dl) in the presence of signs and symptoms are considered to have diabetes(3). \r\nThe global prevalence of DM in adults over the age of 18 increased from 4.7% in 1980 to 8.8% in 2017(4, 5).  In 2015, an estimated 4.0million death were directly caused by DM(4).  World Health Organization predicts that DM will be the seventh leading cause of death in 2030, affecting 628.6 million people worldwide by 2045(6).  Today, about 1 in 11 adults worldwide  have diabetes, 90% of whom have type 2 DM(7). According to International Diabetes Federation Atlas report (IDFA) in 2019, approximately 463 million adults (20-79 years) were living with diabetes; by 2045 the magnitude  will rise to 700 million and 79.5% of adults with diabetes were living in low- and middle-income countries, about 75.9% (351.7 million) people were  working age group(4). There were 2,567,900 cases of diabetes in Ethiopia in 2015, which account for 5.2% of the population(7). Specifically in Ethiopia, 3.8% (4.0% males and 3.6% females) of the population had diabetes, accounting for 1% of overall mortality in the country(5).\r\nA 2016 national STEPS survey report showed that 5.9% (6.0% men and 5.8 m) of Ethiopians had elevated blood glucose levels and 98.4% had at least one risk factor for DM(8).\r\n\r\nAs a result of increased urbanization and lifestyle changes in developing countries, the prevalence of diabetes is high, which is associated with the risk of macro/microvascular complications(9). It is a major cause of blindness, kidney failure, heart attacks, stroke, amputation, and death(10). The negative impacts of DM are unacceptably high as there are effective public health and clinical interventions including a healthy diet, regular physical activity, maintaining normal body weight, and avoiding tobacco use(11). \r\nThe burden of DM has an economic impact that affects the health care system and puts an additional burden on  affected people and their families(12). Globally, the estimated loss of gross domestic product to DM, including direct and indirect costs, is $ 1.7 trillion while, LMIC suffers a total loss of $ 800 billion. Besides the financial burden on the health care system and the national economy, it brings catastrophic personal costs through loss of income due to disability and premature death(5).\r\nPrior community-based studies conducted elsewhere reported the prevalence of DM to range from 1.4% to19.1% (13-16). Different study identified factors associated with DM like; age, marital status, level of education, occupation, positive family history of DM, fruit and vegetable consumption, physical activity,  obesity, smoking, and alcohol consumption(14, 16-19). Physical activity and over-weight have not received much importance in the daily life of the urban population in Africa.  While, with the progressive improvement in infrastructure, the adoption of western lifestyle, and the rising number of ageing population, people in urban areas are more prone to developing diabetes(20). So far, most of the diabetes mellitus studies in Ethiopia were institution-based and few studies focused on urban setting. \r\n Hence, population-based epidemiological information from both  urban  and rural populations is essential to understand the picture of DM in Ethiopia and to help expand service availability and appropriate clinical intervention(21, 22). In addition, prior evidence accumulated regarding the prevalence of previously undiagnosed and uncontrolled DM was limited despite being an important public health intervention targeting morbidity, mortality, and improvement in the quality of life(23-25).  However, to the best of our knowledge, there are no recent population-level studies in Jimma zone concerning the magnitude of DM and associated factors which is useful for policy makers to craft contextual interventions.  Therefore, this study aimed to assess the prevalence, uncontrolled, undiagnosed and burden of DM and associated factors among urban adults in Jimma zone, south west Ethiopia.','3.1. Study area and period \r\nThe study will be conducted from January 02 to September 20, 2022 among the adult population aged 18 years and above living in Jimma zone Oromia Regional State south west Ethiopia. Jimma Zone has 21 districts and 2 urban administrative. The zone has 2986957 total populations from this, 2794110 were rural dwellers and 192847 were urban dwellers. Jimma town is one the urban administrative town in the zone, and located at 352 km from Addis Ababa to the southwest. Its astronomical location is 7º 4’ north latitude and 36º 5’ east longitude. The town was founded in 1837 and one of the reform towns in the region and has a city administration, municipality and 17 Kebele (the smallest administrative units in Ethiopia). The Jimma town had estimated total population of 169446(26).\r\n 3.2. Study Design and Source of Population\r\nA community-based comparative cross-sectional study design will be conducted. All adult (>18) year who lived in Jimma zone will be source population.\r\n 3.2.1. Study Population\r\nAll selected adults (>18) from randomly selected kebele who lived in Jimma zone for at least six months prior to the survey will be included, as well as those who will be available during the study period. \r\n3.3. Inclusion and exclusion criteria \r\n3.3.1. Inclusion criteria\r\ni.	All adult (>18) years’ samples residents who had lived at least 6 months in the zone prior to the data collection will be included. \r\n 3.3.2. Exclusion Criteria\r\ni)	Individuals who were taking any drug with possible impact on glucose metabolism (e.g., steroids, B-blockers, and thiazide diuretics) other than antidiabetic mellitus drugs were excluded to avoid false positive prediabetes or diabetes mellitus.\r\nii)	Pregnant women were excluded from the study to avoid the possible impact of pregnancy on anthropometric and laboratory parameters.\r\niii)	However, adults who were severely ill, adults who will not be willing to participate and unable to communicate during the study period will be excluded from the study.\r\n 3.4. Sample Size determination\r\nSample size was calculated using Epi-Info ver.7 for comparative cross-sectional study with the assumption of the prevalence of diabetic’s mellitus  among urban 5.1% and that of rural 2.1%,  from study conducted in Gondar(18). Considering, 95% confidence interval, and 80% detection power, 1:1 ratio for urban and rural. After multiplying with a design effect of 1.5 and adding 10% non-response rate the final sample size will be,2212( 1106 for urban and 1106 for rural).                                             \r\n   Sampling techniques\r\nA multistage cluster random sampling strategy will be used to select study participants from urban and rural locations. From urban administrative, Jimma town is purposively selected. From this, six (35%) kebele will be selected by simple random sampling. From selected kebele, listing of house hold will be made to prepare frame list and corresponding sample size will be selected using systematic random sampling technique. From 21 districts in Jimma zone, 7(33%) district will be selected by lottery method. From this, one (1) rural kebele is selected from each district by lottery method and listing of house hold will be made to prepare frame list and corresponding sample size will be selected using systematic random sampling technique. For both urban and rural cluster, from the selected households if more than one individual fulfil eligibility criteria, only one person is selected randomly by data collectors. Households are allocated to selected kebele by proportionate allocation and from each selected kebele by using the following formula. \r\nni=(n*Ni)/N \r\nWhere   ni=sample size of each selected kebele\r\n             n=total sample size \r\n            Ni=total number of household in each selected kebele\r\n            N=total number of household in all selected kebele\r\nTable 1: proportionate allocation for selected kebele in Jimma town (Urban cluster)\r\nS/n	Town	Kebele	House hold	Sample size\r\n1	Jimma	Ginjo	5451	286\r\n2	Jimma	H/Mentina	3312	174\r\n3	Jimma	Hora Gibe	387	20\r\n4	Jimma	Bosa Kito	3448	181\r\n5	Jimma	B/Bore	6658	350\r\n6	Jimma	Ifa Bula	1813	95\r\nTotal 		21,069	1,106\r\n\r\nTable 2: proportionate allocation for rural clusters\r\nDistrict Name	Selected Kebele	population size	Sample size\r\nOmo nada	Tolibeyam	988	204\r\nLimu kosa	Dambi Gabana	612	127\r\nGomma	Omo Beko	456	94\r\nTiro Afata	Dacha Gibe	564	117\r\nSetama	Chafata	787	163\r\nShebe Sombo	Urgeyi	915	189\r\nMana	Bilida	1022	212\r\nTotal		5344	1,106\r\n    3.6. Data collection instruments\r\nThis study will use the WHO NCD STEPS instrument which consisted of three steps for measuring the risk of NCD risk factors. The first step consists of a collection of core and expanded socio-demographic and behavioural characteristics of the study population. The second step involves core and expanded physical measurement, and the third step consists of biochemical measurement.\r\nData will be collected by using questionnaires. The English version questionnaire will be developed and translated to Afaan Oromo and Amharic, widely spoken language in the study setting by language expert. To check for consistency of the questionnaires, back translation to the English language will be made before commencing actual data collection. The training will be given to nurses from nearby health facilities who are willing to participate in the study on the study objectives and the STEPS survey procedure.  And those trained nurses will be involved in collecting data under supervision of investigators. Five(5%) pre-test will be conducted in a sub-district that was not included in the study to check that the data collectors and responders understood the questions translated to the local languages and also measures consistency in measurement among the data collectors. Accordingly, necessary adjustments will be made before the actual data collection according to the feedback from the pre-test. The demographic and behavioural risk factors will be collected through face-to-face interviews using an interviewer-administered questionnaire. Each participant will be questioned for age, sex, educational status, marital status, occupation type, physical activity, history of raised blood pressure and diabetes, fruit and vegetable intake, alcohol consumption, and smoking habit.  \r\n3.7.	  Variable Measurement\r\nA digital glucometer will be used to measure capillary blood sugar after participants were asked about the time lapsed from the last meal. Capillary blood samples will be collected three times on different occasions (for three consecutive days) from a single study participant, and the average of the three measurements will be used for analysis to minimize errors. We will use the International Diabetes Association’s (IDA) definition to define DM: fasting blood glucose (FBG) ⩾126mg/dL or random blood glucose (RBG) >200mg/dL(27). \r\nIn addition, subjects who were taking DM medications during the survey will be identified as previously diagnosed with DM(28). Previously undiagnosed DM will be defined as participants who had not had their blood sugar tested before and not taking DM medications during the survey and had an FBG ⩾126 mg/dL or RBG >200mg/dL(27). Similarly, uncontrolled DM (or poor glycemic control) will be defined when FBG ⩾126mg/dL or RBG >200mg/dL among those who were taking DM medication during the survey(29).\r\nBlood pressure (BP) will be measured using a semi-automatic BP monitor (Microlife BP A50; Microlife AG, Widnaiu, Switzerland) three times, each 3–5min apart, from the left arm while the participant was in a sitting position, and the average value of the last two measurements will be used to define hypertension; persistent systolic BP ⩾140mmHg or diastolic BP ⩾90 mmHg or reported use of antihypertensive medication(30).\r\nAnthropometric measurement will be carried out following standard procedures and using calibrated instruments(31). Weight will be measured using a standard digital scale and a stadiometer to measure height, and the results will be recorded to the nearest 0.5cm. Body mass index (BMI) will be defined as a person’s weight in kilograms divided by the square of the person’s height in meters (kg/m2) and categorized as follows: BMI <18.5, underweight; BMI 18.5–24.9, normal; and BMI ⩾25, overweight and obese(32). The digital BP apparatus will be used to measure BP. Abdominal obesity was defined as waist circumference: in males >94cm and females >80cm(33).\r\nTo measure physical activity, the WHO global recommendation on physical activity will be used. Accordingly, the combination of moderate- and vigorous-intensity activity accumulating at least 600 metabolic equivalent (MET)- minutes per week will be used as a cut-off to define physically active (achieved 600 or more MET-minutes per week) versus inactive adults (achieving less than 600 MET minutes per week)(33, 34).\r\nCurrent tobacco smoke was defined when a participant reported that he or she smoked tobacco products daily during the data collection period. Similarly, participants who drank alcohol in the past 30days of the data collection period will be considered as current alcohol users(33).\r\n3.8.	Variables of the study \r\nDependent Variables\r\nThe dependent variables for this study are the undiagnosed and the uncontrolled diabetes mellitus\r\nIndependent Variables \r\n•	Socio demographic characteristics are age, sex, educational status, marital status, occupation type, religion and residence.\r\n•	Behavioural factors includes current smoking and alcohol consumption status, number of sitting hours per day, frequency of eating vegetables, fruit and fatty meal, type of oil used, and physical activity. \r\n•	A physical measurements variable includes waist circumference, blood pressure, weight, and height. \r\n Data Quality Assurance\r\nPrior to the start of the survey, data collectors will be updated on proper measurement and sample data collection. Data will be collected during the real field data collection period, and regular field supervisions will be carried out to monitor the field work on the spot. Following the collection of blood samples, plasma will be separated and stored at 20°C before being analyzed. The HumaStar 80 chemical analyzer will be calibrated with an AutoCal calibrator, and quality control samples of normal (HumaTrol N) and pathological (HumaTrol P) will be performed each day before testing samples. The machine\'s and reagents\' manufacturer\'s instructions will be properly followed. \r\nData Processing and Analysis\r\nThe data will be entered and cleaned using Epi-Info version 7 and analysed using R software version 4.3.0. Descriptive statistics in the form of means for continuous variables and percentages for categorical variables will be made. Then, the findings will be presented by tables and texts. Variables with 0.25 values in the univariate binary logistic regression analysis will be fitted in the multiple logistic regression model. Adjusted odds ratio (AOR) with a 95% confidence interval (CI) and value < 0.05 in the multiple logistic regression model will be used to declare a significant association with the outcome. The goodness of fitness of the model will be checked by likelihood ratio test.','<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><a name=\"_ENREF_1\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">1.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Organization WH. Classification of diabetes mellitus. 2019.</span></a></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><a name=\"_ENREF_2\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">2.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Association AD. Diagnosis and classification of diabetes mellitus. Diabetes care. 2009;32(Supplement 1):S62-S7.</span></a></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><a name=\"_ENREF_3\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">3.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Organization WH. Report of a World Health Organization Consultation. Use of glycated haemoglobin (HbA1c) in the diagnosis of diabetes mellitus. Diabetes Res Clin Pract. 2011;93:299-309.</span></a></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><a name=\"_ENREF_4\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">4.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Saeedi P, Petersohn I, Salpea P, Malanda B, Karuranga S, Unwin N, et al. Global and regional diabetes prevalence estimates for 2019 and projections for 2030 and 2045: Results from the International Diabetes Federation Diabetes Atlas. Diabetes research and clinical practice. 2019;157:107843.</span></a></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><a name=\"_ENREF_5\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">5.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Roglic G. WHO Global report on diabetes: A summary. International Journal of Noncommunicable Diseases. 2016;1(1):3.</span></a></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><a name=\"_ENREF_6\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">6.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Organization WH. Guidelines on second-and third-line medicines and type of insulin for the control of blood glucose levels in non-pregnant adults with diabetes mellitus: