/p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"background-color:white; border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top; width:217px\">\r\n			<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">419,480</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n		</tr>\r\n		<tr>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:1px solid black; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top; width:39px\">\r\n			<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">7</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top; width:308px\">\r\n			<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Contingency cost (10%)</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top; width:217px\">\r\n			<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">41948</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n		</tr>\r\n		<tr>\r\n			<td style=\"background-color:white; border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:1px solid black; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top; width:39px\">\r\n			<p>&nbsp;</p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"background-color:white; border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top; width:308px\">\r\n			<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Grand Total cost</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"background-color:white; border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top; width:217px\">\r\n			<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">461428</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n		</tr>\r\n	</tbody>\r\n</table>',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),(35,166,9,19,'Antimicrobial resistance (AMR) is a growing problem in the 21st century and one of the most serious jeopardies to global public health. There is no sufficient data on antimicrobial resistance profile of antibiotics in Ethiopia particularly in JMC. A cross sectional study will be employed on 300 patients admitted to three wards of Jimma Medical Center namely pediatrics, internal medicine and surgery. Clinical samples such as wound swab, urine, sputum, and stool will be collected from hospitalized patients with evident bacterial infection depending on the type of infection. Then the collected samples will be detected and isolated by a standard microbiological technique. After isolation of the different microbial strains antimicrobial susceptibility test will be conducted using different standard techniques. A zone of inhibition will be measured and the results will be interpreted as sensitive, resistant, or intermediate based on resistance data interpreted according to Clinical and Laboratory Standards Institute',NULL,'Assessment of Ceftazidime Resistant Microbial strains in different wards of Jimma Medical Center : A threat to the last resort antibiotic','2021-11-19 09:45:28','1. To assess the prevalence of Ceftazidime resistance 	•	\r\nAdds new evidence regarding prevalence of Ceftazidime resistance\r\nUsed as source of information for further searchers\r\nHelps us look for other alternative treatments\r\n2. To identify the predominant  bacterial strains found at different wards of JMC\r\nHelps to treat patients empirically\r\nHelps in early initiation of treatment \r\nTo decrease morbidity and mortality\r\nTo decrease late complications of bacterial infection\r\n3. To identify the common bacterial strains that are resistant to Ceftazidime\r\nTo be cautious in selecting antibiotics for those strains\r\nHelps physicians to select safe medications based on their resistance profiles\r\n4.To perform drug use evaluation of Ceftazidime	•	\r\nTo know about the indications of Ceftazidime\r\nTo identify the common drug therapy problems \r\n5. To assess factors associated with drug resistance\r\nTo devise a strategy in solving identified factors\r\nHelps as a source of evidence for various health care facilities\r\n6. To assess prescribing pattern of Ceftazidime	•	\r\nTo assess irrational drug use','f55a2aaad3a6823bd5e66c6d8bf068ee','completed','Antimicrobial resistance (AMR) is a growing problem in the 21st century and one of the most serious jeopardies to global public health [1]. Moreover, the percentages of organisms exhibiting AMR, especially resistance to multiple antibiotics, are continuingly increased [2]. Resistant microorganisms lead to an increase in morbidity and mortality since it increases the risk of inappropriate therapy [3,4]. Antimicrobial resistance (AMR) is a serious global health challenge that impacts all countries and all people, regardless of their wealth or status.   It is predicted that by 2050 there will be more than ten million deaths per year attributed to AMR. Further, it is predicted that the greatest number of these deaths will be in developing countries. [5]\r\nRepresentative data on the extent of the problem in low-and middle-income countries are relatively scarce, but high levels of resistance are increasingly being reported worldwide. Data on AMR among local pathogens help define the best possible treatment for individual patients [6, 7]. However, the proportion of resistant bacteria can vary from one area to another [8], and in many health facilities there are no local data on resistance patterns [9]. There is no sufficient data on antimicrobial resistance profile of antibiotics in Ethiopia particularly in JMC. Therefore, the present study aims to screen the antimicrobial resistance profile of Ceftazidime among hospitalized patients at different wards of JMC.','3.	Design and methods\r\n	 \r\nStudy area and sample collection\r\nThe present study will be conducted at three wards of Jimma medical center namely pediatrics, internal medicine, and surgical wards. Clinical samples such as wound swab, urine, sputum, and stool will be collected from hospitalized patients with evident bacterial infection depending on the type of infection. Then the collected samples will be detected and isolated by a standard microbiological technique. After isolation of the different microbial strains antimicrobial susceptibility test will be conducted using different standard techniques. A zone of inhibition will be measured and the results will be interpreted as sensitive, resistant, or intermediate based on resistance data interpreted according to Clinical and Laboratory Standards Institute [9] \r\nStudy design and period\r\nA hospital based cross-sectional study will be conducted between February 1 and October 30, 2022.\r\nPopulation \r\nSource population\r\nAll patients admitted to pediatrics, internal medicine, and surgical wards of JMC.\r\nStudy population \r\n All patients admitted to pediatrics, internal medicine, and surgical wards of JMC who fulfill the inclusion criteria.\r\nEligibility criteria \r\nInclusion criteria\r\n•	Patients who will be on Ceftazidime during sample collection.\r\n•	Willingness to participate in the study \r\nExclusion criteria\r\n•	Patients admitted to ICU\r\n•	Patients who discontinued Ceftazidime previously\r\nSample size estimation and sampling procedure\r\nSamples will be taken from 300 study participants admitted to JMC, All patients who were available during data collection period and who fulfill the inclusion criteria will be included in the study. \r\nData collection technique\r\nData collection tools\r\nInformation about socio-demographic characteristics of the patients, clinical variables, and medication related variables will be collected using semi structured questionnaire which will be developed from different literatures to. Data on clinical and medication related variables will also be collected using a checklist.\r\nData collectors and supervisors  \r\nTwo nurses and four laboratory technicians will be involved in the data collection process. The two nurses will collect socio demographic, clinical and medication related information of the patients. The two laboratory technicians will collect sample from patients. Two of the research project team members will supervise data collectors during the whole process. \r\nAntimicrobial Susceptibility Testing\r\nAntimicrobial susceptibility testing will be done using disk diffusion technique according to Kirby–Bauer method using S. aureus ATCC 25923 and as quality control strains [10]. Accordingly, at least three to five well-isolated colonies of the same morphological type will be selected from an agar plate culture and transferred into Muller Hinton broth and incubated at 37∘C for 24 hours. The turbidity of the suspension will be adjusted with sterile saline to obtain turbidity optically comparable to that of the 0.5 McFarland standards. Then, the swab will be streaked over the entire surface of the freshly prepared Mueller Hinton agar plate. The antimicrobial disks will be applied to the plates within 15 minutes after inoculation. The plates will then be incubated at 37∘C for 24 hours. A zone of inhibition will be measured and the results will be interpreted as sensitive, resistant, or intermediate based on resistance data interpreted according to Clinical and Laboratory Standards Institute [9]. The antimicrobial agent to be tested is Ceftazidime (CAZ) (30 𝜇g).\r\nData management and statistical analysis\r\nData entry will be made using Epi data version 4.6. Data analysis will be performed using Statistical Package for Social Science (SPSS) version 23. A multivariable logistic regression analysis will used to assess factors associated with Ceftazidime resistance. All variables will be initially tested for an association with Ceftazidime resistance. Those variables demonstrating a bivariate association with at least a marginal significance of (P<0.25) will be included in a multivariable model.  Adjusted odds ratio will be used to measure strength of association. A p-value of <0.05 (with 95% confidence interval) will be considered to be statistically significant.  Data will be expressed as mean ± SD for continuous variables and as frequencies and percentages for categorical variables and the result will be presented by using narrative, tables, graphs and charts.\r\nQuality assurance\r\nTo ensure data quality, reliability of data extraction forms will be checked by doing pre test on 5% of the sample size, training will be given for data collectors. Accuracy and completeness of data will be checked daily after data collection time. Staining reagents, culture medias, and antibiotic discs will be checked for their normal shelf life before use. All culture plates and antibiotic discs will be stored at recommended refrigeration temperature after being prepared and sterilized by autoclaving at 121∘C for 15 minutes. The standard reference bacterial strains will be tested as a positive control on the biochemical tests and agar plates with antibiotic discs. Proper sample collection and handling will be done by experienced nurses who will work at each ward unit.\r\nPlan for dissemination and ensuring utilization of findings\r\nAfter data interpretation, appropriate recommendation will be made and the results of the study will be communicated to the Jimma university Research directorate and national ministry of Health. The result of the study will also be communicated to the hospitals through journal articles, newspapers, bulletins and pamphlets in major languages spoken in Ethiopia. Attempts will be made to present findings on national scientific conferences and to publish on peer reviewed reputable journals.\r\n4.Ethical and environmental considerations\r\nPrior to data collection ethical approval will be obtained from institutional review board (IRB) of Jimma University, College of Health Sciences. Verbal consent from participants will be obtained before the interview. In addition, a written informed consent will be granted from study participants prior to initiation of the study. In order to keep confidentiality of patient information, each sample will be coded and only patient initials will be used. The objective of study will be pa made clear to patients and concerned bodies including JMC.\r\nInformation will be given to study participants/ care givers to let them know that sample will be taken from their urine, wound swab, stool or sputum depending on the type of bacterial infection. Patients will be informed that they may change their mind and stop participating even if they agreed to participate earlier.','<ol>\r\n	<li><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"color:black\">World Health Organization, Essential Drugs Monitor: Antimicrobial Drug Resistance: GlobalThreat,WorldHealthOrganization, Geneva, Switzerland, 2000. </span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"color:black\">Y. Pfeifer, A. Cullik, and W.Witte, &ldquo;Resistance to cephalosporins and carbapenems in Gram-negative bacterial pathogens,&rdquo; International Journal of Medical Microbiology, vol. 300, no. 6, pp. 371&ndash;379, 2010.</span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"color:black\">A. Kapil, &ldquo;The challenge of antibiotic resistance; need to contemplate,&rdquo; Indian Journal of Medical Research, vol. 121, no. 2, pp. 83&ndash;91, 2005. </span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"color:black\">C. L. Ventola, &ldquo;The antibiotic resistance crisis&mdash;part 1: causes and threats,&rdquo; P&amp;T, vol. 40, no. 4, pp. 277&ndash;283, 2015. </span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"color:black\">Saha SK, Hawes L, Mazza D. Improving antibiotic prescribing by general practitioners: a protocol for a systematic review of interventions involving pharmacists. BMJ Open 2018; 8:e020583. doi:10.1136/ bmjopen-2017-020583</span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"color:black\">S. Ganesh Kumar, C. Adithan, B. N. Harish, S. Sujatha, G. Roy, and A. Malini, &ldquo;Antimicrobial resistance in India: A review,&rdquo; Journal of Natural Science, Biology and Medicine, vol. 4, no. 2, pp. 286&ndash;291, 2013.</span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"color:black\">A. P. Johnson, &ldquo;Surveillance of antibiotic resistance,&rdquo; Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society B: Biological Sciences, vol. 370, no. 1670, 2015.</span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"color:black\">G. Karam, J. Chastre, M. H.Wilcox, and J. Vincent, &ldquo;Antibiotic strategies in the era of multidrug resistance,&rdquo; Critical Care, vol. 20, article 136, 2016.</span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"color:black\">A. A. Abejew,A. A.Denboba, and A.G. Mekonnen, &ldquo;Prevalence and antibiotic resistance pattern of urinary tract bacterial infections in Dessie area, Northeast Ethiopia,&rdquo; BMC Research Notes, vol. 7, no. 1, article 687, 2014.</span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;,sans-serif\"><span style=\"color:black\">R. C Franklin, A. W. Matthew, A. Jeff, N. D. Michael, M. E. George, and J. F.Mary, Performance Standards for Antimicrobial Disk Susceptibility Tests, 11th edition, 2012.</span></span></span></li>\r\n</ol>','To assess Ceftazidime resistant microbial stains in different wards of Jimma Medical Center',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,2,'Antimicrobial resistance,ceftazidime,microbial strains,Jimma Medical Center',10,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO `submission` VALUES (36,61,9,20,'Background: Hypertensive disorders of pregnancy are rising in Ethiopia. However, prevalence and risk factors of hypertensive disorders of pregnancy and their health seeking behavior of pre-eclampsia is not well understood in Jimma Zone. Hence, these studies will try to assess the prevalence of hypertensive disorders, health seeking behavior, and living experience of pregnant women with pre-eclampsia among pregnant women in Jimma Zone, South Western Ethiopia in 2022.\r\nMethods: Community based cross sectional study will be conducted to assess hypertensive disorders among 847 pregnant women by employing the WHO STEP wise approach. Likewise, in order to study the level of knowledge, attitude and practice and associated factors of pregnant women with hypertensive disorders will be done on sample of 254 pregnant women by using interviewer administered semi structured questionnaire. In addition, phenomenological study will be conducted to explore the experience of pre-eclamptic pregnant women by using semi structured interview guide. For quantitative data, EPI Info version 3.7.1 and STATA version 14.0 will be used for data entry and analysis respectively. Binary logistic regression analysis will be done to assess possible associations. Variables with P-value of < 0.25 in bi-variable logistic regression analysis will be used in multi-variable analysis. P-value < 0.05 will be used to declare significance of association. For the qualitative, the collected data will be coded, categorized and analyzed by employing six step thematic analyses via ATLAS ti version 8.0 software.\r\nTimeline and budget breakdown: The study will be conducted from February – June 30/ 2022 with total expected budget of 426, 720 ETB.','Hypertensive disorders among pregnant women','Hypertensive Disorders among Pregnant Women and their Health Seeking Behavior for Pre-eclampsia:-A community Based Cross sectional study in Jimma Zone, South Western Ethiopia.','2021-11-19 10:17:59','For the first objective: Community based prevalence of hypertensive disorders its possible factors responsible for the problem will be determined\r\nFor the second objective: the health care seeking behavior of pregnant women towards pre-eclampsia will be escalated.\r\nFor the third objective: Experience about the health care seeking, danger signs and symptoms and complications, management and prevention and control of pre-eclampsia will be explored','2e22ef4694a7a57c167c91c2aa412e4b','completed','Background: Worldwide, chronic non communicable diseases (CNCDs) are major public health problem in the last half a century. One of the most common NCDs problems among pregnant woman are Hypertensive Disorders of Pregnancy (HDP). Even if there are variations in defining hypertensive disorders of pregnancy; HDP is defined commonly hypertension that occurs during pregnancy. Originating from blood vessels it is characterized by systemic manifestations. The causes are combination of both genetic and acquired factors (1). Globally, Hypertensive disorders of pregnancy (HDP) consists of around 14·0% from all causes of maternal death occurred between the year 2003 to 2009. In Sub-Saharan Africa, HDPs are the second leading cause of maternal death and complications(2). The prevalence of HDP is on the raise in Ethiopia. In northern Ethiopia, the prevalence of HDP had increased the past five years from 1.4% in 2013 to 4% in 2017(3). According to the report of systematic review and meta-analysis conducted in Ethiopia during 2018, the overall pooled prevalence of HDP was 6.1% and the highest prevalence was reported from Southern Nations, Nationalities and Peoples’ Region (SNNPR) which was 10.0%. Likewise, the magnitude of pregnancy induced hypertension and pre-eclampsia/eclampsia were 6.3% and 5.5% respectively. The study further reported that the pooled prevalence of pregnancy-induced hypertension in Ethiopia was 6.1%  (4) . \r\nOne of the main factors that hinders the prevention and control of HDP was low knowledge of pregnant women towards it. For instance in Tanzania, the  knowledge of  pregnant women towards pre-eclampsia and its forms was low (5). Similarly, in northern Ethiopia normo-tensive pregnant women have better knowledge than pregnant women with HDP (6). Ethiopia has introduced various efforts to halt NCDs including HDP and had improved access to care of chronic illnesses for communities in various corners of the country and launched strategic framework for the prevention and control of chronic non communicable diseases and entailed in programs (7-9). \r\nHDP are largely preventable and the number of premature deaths caused by them can be greatly reduced [10]. In addition to a number of  international, regional and national efforts to prevent and control HDP ; it is also included in the ‘Strategy for Women’s and Children’s Health’ launched by the UN Secretary-General in September, 2010 (11). Correspondingly , UN has called a subject of high level meeting on chronic non-communicable diseases in Moscow in 2011; and WHO had called to drew attention to the neglect of chronic diseases and introduced new action plan for prevention and control of non-communicable diseases in 2013(12-13]. Above all, intra-muscular administration of magnesium sulfate has huge importance in hindering eclamptic complication (14).\r\nSimilarly, Ethiopia has various strategies to prevent and control HDP and to reduce maternal and perinatal mortality in to 199 per 100, 000 live births and still births from 18 to 10 per 1000 live births by year 2020 (15-16). Although Ethiopia had identified preeclampsia as major cause of maternal death; factors and outcomes related to pre-eclmpsia were not deeply understood in Ethiopia. This has made the prevention and control of pre-eclampsia more difficult (17). In addition to the above mentioned national efforts, there are training of  health care providers for the management of pregnanacy hypertension. Hence, the aim of this community Level Interventions for Pre-eclampsia (CLIP), a cluster randomized trial in Ethiopia is to reduce the maternal morbidity and perinatal mortality and major morbidity by 20% in Jimma Zone, South western Ethiopia.\r\nRationale of the Study\r\nThere are substantial indirect maternal deaths secondary to HDP worldwide and in Ethiopia. Many researches reports form international organizations showed that there is a significant increment on the prevalence of pre-eclampsia among pregnant women especially in least developed countries.  And addressing causes of maternal death are crucial to improve the health of the mother and newborn and for future wellness of the generation. To widen detail understanding of risk factors, health seeking behavior of pregnant women towards pre-eclampsia and management of pre-eclampsia and to address the changing needs of pregnant women with HDP; in Ethiopia requires detailed epidemiologic data. \r\nStudies have suggested that identification of risk factors of HDP among general populations has contribution in reducing the prevalence and its adverse consequences. This study will contribute to the reduction of HDP by ascertaining those factors that are modifiable by appropriate public health interventions. It further identifies issues that hinder health seeking behavior of pre-eclampsia. \r\nThe research community will also better understand the prevalence and risk factors of hypertensive disorders of pregnancy in rural Ethiopia and above all, this research is designed with the intention of establishing a basis for better women\'s health services, serves as basis for other future studies on HDP among pregnant women and their adverse consequences, and appropriate intervention options for HDP in pre pregnancy, pregnancy and post-natal period that will help to halt the vicious cycle of poverty and give opportunity to intervene during the period of pregnancy in Jimma Zone in South western Ethiopia.','1.	Materials and Methods\r\na.	Study area and period: \r\nThe current study will be conducted in Jimma Zone. Jimma Zone is located in Oromia regional state in South West of Ethiopia. Jimma town is the capital and administrative center of the Zone and is located at a distance of 350 Km away from the capital of Ethiopia- Addis Ababa. The study area is situated between 1689 and 3018 meter above sea level and receives an average rainfall between 1200 and 2400 mm per annum. The maximum and minimum annual temperature of the area is 28.8 and 11.8 OC, respectively (CSA, 2005). Jimma Zone has plenty of year-round evergreen wetlands that support biodiversity, livestock, and socioeconomic activities. \r\nb.	Study Design:\r\nCross sectional study design for the first and second objective and phenomenological \r\nc.	Populations\r\nAll pregnant mothers resided in Jimma Zone will be the source population for the current study. The study populations for the first, second and third objectives will be pregnant women who are aged 15 and more years and who have HDP. The forth will use pre-eclamptic pregnant women with the same character. Pregnant women who have hearing or speaking problems or an intellectual disability and pregnant women who are unable to communicate during the interview will be excluded from the study. \r\nd.	Sampling procedure and sample size\r\nSample size\r\nThe sample size for the first two objectives will be calculated using single population proportion formula and by taking the largest sample. Indeed, we will employ multi stage stratified sampling by considering 10% non response rate. Hence, a sample of 847 pregnant women will be recruited. For the third objective twenty purposely selected pre-eclamptic pregnant women, having varied character and its complications will be interviewed. \r\ne.	Measurement and Data Collection\r\nFor the first two objectives interview administered data collection technique will be used. The data will be collected by seven diploma nurses with previous experience in data collection, who speaks local language (Afan Oromo language) fluently and who have an ample experience. WHO STEPS wise Questionnaire which is validated nationally by EPHI in 2015 will be used to measure socio demographic variables like: Age, education status, occupation, income, residence and number of households, obstetric factors: Gravida & Parity and risk factors: Unhealthy use of alcohol, diets, physical inactivity and tobacco use are among modified risk factors of hypertensive disorders of pregnancy. A structured interview questionnaire that is developed from reviewed literatures will be used to knowledge, attitudes and practice of pregnant women towards hypertensive disorders.\r\n\r\nHypertension will be measured by sphygmomanometer with two consequent measurements. Nurses who have at least diploma in nursing profession will measure the blood pressure of pregnant women. Hypertension will be defined according to the seventh report of the internationally recognized Joint National Committee as systolic BP ≥140 mmHg and/or diastolic BP ≥90 mmHg. Firstly, the pregnant women will be allowed to sit and rest for five minutes. The cuff of the sphygmomanometer should be at heart level. An average of at least two measurements will be taken between 1-2 minutes apart. In the first visits, BP should be measured in both arms to assess for possible differences. Secondly, the higher value of blood pressure (BP) will be taken as a reference. If the two measurements have a difference of more than 10mmHg, blood pressure will be measured for the third time. The diagnosis of HDP will be made in the upcoming visits of pregnant women \r\n\r\nLikewise, data for the third objective will be semi structured interview guide that is developed by reviewing literature to explore the living experience of pre-eclamptic pregnant women. Data will be collected by a research assistant that have rich experience in collecting qualitative data, which have first degree in nursing profession, who have served clinically for at least two years and who are fluent in Afan Oromo language. Twenty pre-eclamptic pregnant women with various characteristics will be included in the study. The sample size will be further determined by the saturation of ideas during the interview.\r\n5   Data Management and Statistical Analysis \r\nPlan for Data Analysis\r\nFor Quantitative Data; Statistical data will be entered using Epi data version 3.1. After that, data will be exported to STATA version 14.0. The data will be cleaned by calculating frequencies, sorting and visualization, and analyzed using the STATA version 14.0 software. Binary logistic regression analysis will be used to measure possible association between dependent and independent factors. Variance inflation factor will be used to check multi-co linearity and before drawing conclusions based on logistic regression model, Hosmer-Lemeshow goodness of fit test will be checked in order to check the goodness of fit (P- value > 0.05). Prevalence and risk factors for hypertensive disorders and health seeking behavior among pregnant women will be analyzed using bi-variable logistic regression analysis and multivariable logistic regression analysis. Variables with P-value of < 0.25 in bi-variable logistic regression analysis will be used in multi-variable analysis. P-value < 0.05 will be used to declare significance of association.\r\n6   Data Quality Assurance for all Objectives\r\nFor Quantitative Data\r\nFor the first objective standard WHO STEPWISE questionnaire will be used and for the second and third objectives, semi structured questionnaire that is developed by reviewing literature will be utilized.  For all; appropriate training will be given on how to obtain consent, collect data and management of problems during field work. In addition, appropriate training will be given for data collectors and supervisors. Likewise, these questionnaires will be pre tested on 5% population out of the study area. During the process of data collection; strong daily follow-up of data collectors and supervisors and proper feedback will be done on daily basis.  Data will be checked every day for completeness. Discussions will be made with the data collectors and supervisors to address any problems that will occur during the process of the interview on daily basis. The feedbacks will be given accordingly on the same day. Necessary correction will be made immediately. Any problem encountered during the process of the study will be corrected and managed appropriately. After data collection, data will be cleaned by sorting, visualizations, frequency and manually. Furthermore, the quantitative data will be entered in to Epi data 3.1 and exported by using STATA version 14.00.\r\nFor Qualitative Data\r\nTrustworthiness\r\nObjective three have qualitative data componenets. Trustworthiness of these qualitative data will be ensured by employing Lincoln’s and Guba’s four criteria’s namely credibility, dependability, confirmability and transferability (24). Before collecting the data FGDs, KIIs and in depth interview guides will be developed by searching literatures for guiding the interviews. Appropriate translations and back translations will be made by English language and Afan Oromo language experts respectively to maintain consistency. To maintain credibility; thick descriptions, triangulations, reflexivity, peer examinations, interview techniques and coherence structure will be arranged; time and nominated sampling of the respondents will be also taken in to account during the interview. All the interviews will be recorded in local language and transcribed verbatim and translated to English. In order to assure transferability; comparison with demographic data, time and nominated sample and thick descriptions will be performed. For dependability; dependability audit, code recode and reliability test will be done. Finally to ensure the confirmablity; confirmablity audit, triangulations, reflexivity and thick descriptions will be performed. The collected data will be coded, categorized and analyzed by employing six step thematic analyses via ATLAS ti version 8.0 software. \r\n7   Plan for Dissemination\r\nThe results of the study will be presented to department of population and family health, Institute of Health, Jimma University, and will be communicated to Jimma Zone health department and respective Woredas and Kebelles. The findings will be also disseminated to different organizations that will have contribution to improve the status of women’s and child health. Five publications will be made and efforts will make to present the results on scientific conferences/symposiums. The results of the findings will be published on local, national and /or international journals.\r\n8   Ethical Considerations  \r\nEthical clearance will be obtained from Jimma University institutional review board and Oromia region health bureau. Letter of permission will be obtained from Jimma Zone and respective district administration offices and the respective Kebelles (the lowest administration unit in Ethiopia). The purpose of the study will be explained to the study participants before the study. Verbal and written consent will be secured and confidentiality of the information will be ensured. The interview will be conducted in separate, quite and silent room. The results of the study will be communicated to the respective Woreda health offices and subsequent pregnant women residing in those Woreda. Health information and promotion will be conveyed to the study participants.','<table cellspacing=\"0\" class=\"Table\" style=\"border-collapse:collapse; border:none; width:100.0%\">\r\n	<tbody>\r\n		<tr>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:1px solid black; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:1px solid black; height:47px; vertical-align:top; width:100%\">\r\n			<ol>\r\n				<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style