9px; vertical-align:top; width:79px\">\r\n			<h4>&nbsp;</h4>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"background-color:black; border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:39px; vertical-align:top; width:57px\">\r\n			<h4>&nbsp;</h4>\r\n			</td>\r\n		</tr>\r\n		<tr>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:1px solid black; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:39px; vertical-align:top; width:193px\">\r\n			<p><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\">Submission of report</span></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:39px; vertical-align:top; width:79px\">\r\n			<p>&nbsp;</p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:39px; vertical-align:top; width:79px\">\r\n			<p>&nbsp;</p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:39px; vertical-align:top; width:79px\">\r\n			<p>&nbsp;</p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:39px; vertical-align:top; width:71px\">\r\n			<h4>&nbsp;</h4>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:39px; vertical-align:top; width:79px\">\r\n			<h4>&nbsp;</h4>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:39px; vertical-align:top; width:64px\">\r\n			<h4>&nbsp;</h4>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:39px; vertical-align:top; width:71px\">\r\n			<h4>&nbsp;</h4>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:39px; vertical-align:top; width:79px\">\r\n			<h4>&nbsp;</h4>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"background-color:black; border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:39px; vertical-align:top; width:57px\">\r\n			<h4>&nbsp;</h4>\r\n			</td>\r\n		</tr>\r\n	</tbody>\r\n</table>',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),(37,18,9,17,'Executive summary \r\n A coronavirus disease (COVID-19) is a very contagious disease caused by the SARS-CoV-2 virus which was emerged in 2019. The COVID-19 pandemic continued to wreak havoc across the world being a major global public health challenge. Since its first foot step on the glob in late 2019, the SARS-CoV-2 is relentlessly impacting nearly every aspect of the world’s community in unprecedented way which necessitated a holistic substantial response by public health authorities at all levels. Even though very few studies have been done in Ethiopia to identify predictors of COVID-19 mortality, as to the knowledge of the investigators, no prospective follow-up study has been done using representative sample size, and the available pieces of evidence are inconclusive. Therefore, the current study is aimed to identify predictors of mortality, time to recovery and its predictors among COVID-19 patients hospitalized to COVID-19 treatment centers in the Jimma zone. \r\nObjective:  To Assess Incidence of Mortality, Time to Recovery and Their Predictors among COVID-19 Cases Admitted To COVID-19 Treatment Centers in The Jimma Zone: Facility-Based Prospective Cohort Study\r\nMethods: Facility-based prospective cohort study will be done among patients admitted to COVID-19 treatment centers in the Jimma zone. Data entry will be done using Epi-Data manager 4.1 and will be exported Stata version 14 for further data processing and analysis. Descriptive statistics will be used to summarize descriptive data. Cox recession model will be used for analytical data analysis. Adjusted hazard ratio and 95% CI will be to measure strength of association and its statistical significance. \r\nWork plan and budget: the project will be undertaken from January 01 to December 30/2022 with a total budget of  692550.00',NULL,'Incidence of Mortality, Time to Recovery and Their Predictors among COVID-19 Cases Admitted To COVID-19 Treatment Centers in The Jimma Zone: Facility-Based Prospective Cohort Study','2021-11-19 10:21:02','Research Output/Outcome \r\nThe research will identify independent predictors of mortality, determine median time to recovery from the disease, and identify independent predictors of early recovery from the disease. This will enhance evidence-based intervention of the disease and will ultimately minimize the rate of death from the disease among hospitalized patients in the study area and similar settings','553da9cb2d647749b31c3ccd9d3ed4b1','completed','A coronavirus disease (COVID-19) is a very contagious disease caused by the SARS-CoV-2 virus which was emerged in 2019(1,2). Coronaviruses are named for their appearance: the virus’s outer layers are covered with spike proteins which surround the virus like a crown (2).  The virus causing the disease can be directly transmitted from an infected person to a susceptible host through coughing, sneezing, speaking, singing, and breathing(1–3). Anyone can get sick with the virus and become seriously ill or die at any age. Most people infected with the virus experience mild to moderate respiratory illness and may recover without seeking special treatment(1,3). A person infected with COVID-19 may have a wide range of symptoms like fever or chills, cough, shortness of breathing, fatigue, muscle or body aches, headache, loss of taste or smell, sore throat, congestion or runny nose, nausea or vomiting, and diarrhea which may appear within 2-14 days of exposure to the virus(2,4).  The COVID-19 cases are classified as suspected, probable, and confirmed cases based on clinical and epidemiological evidences. A person with a positive Nucleic Acid Amplification Test (NAAT) who is positive for SARS-CoV-2 Antigen-RDT, and meet either the probable case definition or suspect case definition is classified as a confirmed case of COVID-19(3). Even though many things are yet not discovered about the disease, one can prevent the transmission of the disease through keeping a physical distance, wearing a mask, frequently washing hands, getting vaccinated, and isolating a person with the symptoms of the disease(1,5). The WHO’s and others’ reports have shown that equitable access to safe and effective vaccines is critical to end the COVID-19 pandemic(6–8).\r\nThe COVID-19 pandemic continued to wreak havoc across the world being a major global public health challenge. Since its first foot step on the glob in late 2019, the SARS-CoV-2 is relentlessly impacting nearly every aspect of the world’s community in unprecedented way which necessitated a holistic substantial response by public health authorities at all levels(9–13). As of October 31, 2021, over 246 million confirmed cases and nearly 5 million deaths have been reported. Over 3 million new cases were reported during the week 25 to 31 October 2021. The European Region (192.3 new cases per 100 000 population) and the Region of the Americas (71.8 new cases per 100 000 population) were the regions reporting the highest weekly case incidence rates per 100 000 population.  The same two regions reported the highest weekly incidences of death which were 2.6 and 1.5 per 100 000 population respectively  (14). A systematic review and meta-analysis done among 26,542 COVID-19 patients showed that the overall prevalence of death among the patients was 8.8%(15). On the other hand, a comprehensive systematic review and meta-analysis of 77 studies and 38,000 patients showed that the overall prevalence of death among hospitalized COVID-19 cases was 20% and 23% in the US and Europe, and 11% for China(16).  The prospective cohort studies done among hospitalized patients in Italy and England  showed that the overall rate of mortality among the patients was 29.7% and 36% respectively(17,18). The COVID-19 pandemic is rapidly evolving across the continent of African. The disruptions to supply chains and domestic production as a result of the pandemic combined with weak external demand, the sharp fall in commodity prices, and disruption to key sectors’ services such as tourism are putting the people’s routine jobs and livelihoods at devastating risk. The recent estimates showed that, globally, the COVID-19 could push 40 to 60 million people into extreme poverty, 27 million of whom live in Sub-Saharan Africa. In the Horn of Africa, conflicts and famine have triggered the displacement of millions of people within countries and across borders. Today, there are more than 2.7 million refugees and over six million internally displaced people in this part of the world. The countries of the Horn of Africa were badly hit by the pandemic as they were straggling with scaling up their responses to a recent displacement crisis triggered by conflicts and droughts(11,19). According to the latest World Bank economic analysis for the country, Ethiopia seems to have been spared from the worst outcomes and is showing some signs of recovery. The phone surveys conducted by the World Bank in collaboration with the Jobs Commission and the Central Statistical Agency showed  that most companies that had  been temporarily closed at the onset of the pandemic are now reopened (20).  However, according to the WHO’s report on 9 November, 2021, in Ethiopia, from 3 January 2020 to 8 November 2021, there have been 367,210 confirmed cases of COVID-19 with 6,542 deaths, reported to WHO(21). \r\nDifferent studies have shown that  old age, being hypertensive, history of coronary heart disease, active cancer, being diabetic patient, having chronic liver disease, male sex, presence of malignancy, chronic obstructive lung disease, immunosuppressive condition, shortness of breathing, multiorgan dysfunction, sepsis, and chronic neurological disease were commonly associated with mortality among COVID-19 patients(16,17,29,30,18,22–28). Even though very few studies have been done in Ethiopia to identify predictors of COVID-19 mortality, as to the knowledge of the investigators, no prospective follow-up study has been done using representative sample size, and the available pieces of evidence are inconclusive. Therefore, the current study is aimed to identify predictors of mortality, time to recovery and its predictors among COVID-19 patients hospitalized to COVID-19 treatment centers in the Jimma zone.','Study setting and Period\r\nThe study will be conducted at the COVID-19 treatment centers in the Jimma Zone; Jimma University Medical Center, Shenen Gibe General Hospital, Limmu Genet Hospital, and Agaro Hospital. In the past eight months, 222 and 72 COVID-19 cases were admitted to the Jimma University Medical Center and Shenen Gibe General Hospital respectively. Limmu Genet General Hospital and Agaro General Hospital are the newly identified treatment centers for Covid-19 by the Oromia Regional Health Bureau.  The study will be conducted from February 01 to September 30/2022 (Eight months). \r\n Study Design, Population, Sample Size, and Sampling Techniques \r\nTable 1: Study design, population, sample size, and sampling techniques\r\nSpecific objectives 	Study design	Source population	Study population	Sample size	Sampling techniques\r\n1	Prospective follow-up study	All COVID-19 cases admitted to COVID-19 treatment center	All COVID-19 cases admitted to COVID-19 treatment center during the study period	All admitted COVID-19 patients will be included indiscriminately 	We will include all\r\n2	Prospective follow-up study	All COVID-19 cases admitted to COVID-19 treatment center	All COVID-19 cases admitted to COVID-19 treatment center during the study period	All admitted COVID-19 patients will be included indiscriminately 	We will include all\r\n3	Prospective follow-up study	All COVID-19 cases admitted to COVID-19 treatment center	All COVID-19 cases admitted to COVID-19 treatment center during the study period	All admitted COVID-19 patients will be included indiscriminately 	We will include all\r\n \r\nData collection tools and techniques \r\nData collection tools will be developed based on the reviews of different relevant literature. Data will be collected by reviewing patients’ logbooks and patients’ charts, and using face-to-face interview \r\nPlan for data processing and analysis\r\nThe collected data will first be checked for completeness and consistency and coded manually. Data entry will be done using EpiData manager version 4.1. After entering data into a computer, it will be exported to SPSS version 25 for further data processing and statistical analysis. Descriptive statistics will be used to summarize descriptive data. Cox proportional hazard regression model will be used for the analytical study. Bivariate cox regression will be fitted to identify candidate variables (P-Value <=25%) for the multivariable proportional hazard Cox regression. Multivariable proportional hazard cox regression model will be fitted to identify independent predictors of mortality, and to identify independent predictors of early recovery among COVID-19 cases admitted to the COVID-19 treatment center in the Jimma zone. Adjusted hazard ratio (AHR) and 95% CI will respectively be used to measure the statistical associations and their statistical significance. Kaplan meier and Log-rank test will be used for the comparison of survival functions. Multicollinearity between the independent variables will be checked using variable inflation factor. Breslow-Day Tarone’s test of homogeneity will be used to assess interactions between dependent variables. If an interaction is detected between the independent variables, either the stratum specific value would be reported or the interaction term will be included in the final model.   Confidence interval will be used to declare statistical significance in the final model \r\nEthical Considerations\r\nEthical approval will be obtained from ethical review board of Institute of Health, Jimma University.   Letter will be sought from the Jimma University Medical Center, Jimma Zone and Jimma Town Health offices to get permission from selected Hospitals which dedicated for COVID-19 treatment center. Prior to data collection, written consent will be obtained from each study participant. Confidentiality of individuals’ information will be maintained in all steps of the research.','<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">1. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; World Health Organization (WHO). Coronavirus disease. Geneva, Swizerland; Washington D.C, the United states; 2021. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">2. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Johns Hopkins Medicine. What Is Coronavirus? Baltimore, Maryland; 2019. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">3. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; World Health Organization (WHO). WHO COVID-19 : Case Definitions. Geneva, Swizerland; 2020. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">4. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; (CDC) C for DC and P. COVID-19 Symptoms of COVID-19 Difference between COVID-19 &amp; Flu. Atlanta, Giorgia, United States; 2021. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">5. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; World Health Organization (WHO). Advice for the public : Coronavirus disease ( COVID-. Geneva, Swizerland; Washington D.C, the United states; 2019. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">6. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; World Health Organization (WHO). COVID-19 vaccines. Geneva, Swizerland; Washington D.C, the United states; 2019. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">7. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; (CDC) C for DC and P. When You &rsquo; ve Been Fully Vaccinated. Atlanta, Giorgia, United States; 2021. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">8. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Bernal L, Nejmoa DOI. Effectiveness of Covid-19 Vaccines. 2021;2:2108891. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">9. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; (CDC) C for DC and P. COVID-19 CDC Public Health Science Agenda for COVID-19 Goal of The CDC Public Health Science Agenda Priority Area I . COVID-19 Disease Detection , Burden , And. Atlanta, Giorgia, United States; 2021. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">10. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; World Bank. World Bank Group COVID-19 Crisis Response. Washington DC; 2020. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">11. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; World Bank. World Bank &rsquo; s Response to COVID-19 ( Coronavirus ) in Africa. Vol. 19. Atlanta, Giorgia, United States; 2020. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">12. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; World Bank. Social Sustainability and Inclusion COVID-19 ( Coronavirus ) Response. Vol. 19. Washington DC; 2021. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">13. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; World Bank. Assessing the impact and policy responses in support of private-sector firms in the context of the COVID-19 pandemic 1. Atlanta, Giorgia, United States; 2020. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">14. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; World Health Organization (WHO). COVID-19 Weekly Epidemiological Update. Geneva, Swizerland; 2021. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">15. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Kim W, Han JM, Lee KE. Predictors of Mortality in Patients with COVID-19 : A Systematic Review and. Korean J Clin Pharm. 2020;30(3):169&ndash;76. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">16. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Dorjee K, Kim H, Id EB, Dolma R. Prevalence and predictors of death and severe disease in patients hospitalized due to COVID-19 : A comprehensive systematic review and meta-analysis of 77 studies and 38 , 000 patients. PLoS One [Internet]. 2020;15(12):1&ndash;27. Available from: http://dx.doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0243191</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">17. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Bellan M, Patti G, Hayden E, Azzolina D, Pirisi M, Acquaviva A, et al. Fatality rate and predictors of mortality in an Italian cohort of hospitalized COVID ‑ 19 patients. Nat Res [Internet]. 2020;10(20731):1&ndash;10. Available from: https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-020-77698-4</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-