/td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:10px; vertical-align:top; width:40px\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\">&nbsp;</p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:10px; vertical-align:top; width:40px\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\">&nbsp;</p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:10px; vertical-align:top; width:40px\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\">&nbsp;</p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:10px; vertical-align:top; width:40px\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\">&nbsp;</p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:10px; vertical-align:top; width:40px\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\">&nbsp;</p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:10px; vertical-align:top; width:40px\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\">&nbsp;</p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"background-color:#a8d08d; border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:10px; vertical-align:top; width:40px\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\">&nbsp;</p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:10px; vertical-align:top; width:40px\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\">&nbsp;</p>\r\n			</td>\r\n		</tr>\r\n		<tr>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:1px solid black; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:10px; vertical-align:top; width:56px\">\r\n			<ol start=\"6\">\r\n				<li style=\"text-align:justify\">&nbsp;</li>\r\n			</ol>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:10px; vertical-align:top; width:131px\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">Dissemination workshop</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:10px; vertical-align:top; width:40px\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\">&nbsp;</p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:10px; vertical-align:top; width:40px\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\">&nbsp;</p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:10px; vertical-align:top; width:40px\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\">&nbsp;</p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:10px; vertical-align:top; width:40px\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\">&nbsp;</p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:10px; vertical-align:top; width:40px\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\">&nbsp;</p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:10px; vertical-align:top; width:40px\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\">&nbsp;</p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:10px; vertical-align:top; width:40px\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\">&nbsp;</p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:10px; vertical-align:top; width:40px\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\">&nbsp;</p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:10px; vertical-align:top; width:40px\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\">&nbsp;</p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:10px; vertical-align:top; width:40px\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\">&nbsp;</p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:10px; vertical-align:top; width:40px\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\">&nbsp;</p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"background-color:#a8d08d; border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:10px; vertical-align:top; width:40px\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\">&nbsp;</p>\r\n			</td>\r\n		</tr>\r\n	</tbody>\r\n</table>',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),(38,36,9,20,'Executive summary\r\nBackground: Sexual shame is a sensitive issue that gets in the way of communicating with people about topics related to sexuality. Due to such a negative perception of sexuality, sexual communication with family, friends, and/or significant other can be considered as challenging.\r\nObjective: the aim of this mega  research project is to assess correlations between Sexual Shame, Sexual Communication, and Perceived Social Support among High School Students at Jimma Town Southwest Ethiopia\r\nMethod: A facility based cross- sectional study will be conducted among Jimma town high school students. High schools will be selected using simple random sampling techniques and the participants will be selected using systematic random sampling techniques. A total of 423 high school students will be recruits for this study. The coded data will be checked, cleaned and entered into Epi data version 3.1 and then analyzed by SPSS window version 21. An independent-samples t-test to examine statistical significance between sexual shame without unanswered optional items       scores and sexual shame with mean scores. This could help decide which sexual shame data to use for further data analyses including hypotheses. To examine correlations between sexual shame, sexual communication, and perceived social support, the data will be analyzed using Pearson’s produce-moment correlation. To examine the statistical significance of sexual shame, a non- parametric one-way between-subject ANOVA will be carried. Variables with p-value less than ≤0.05 will be used as cutoff point for statistical significance.\r\nTotal budget requested: A total 239,150.00ETB','Ashamed to Talk About Sex: Correlations between Sexual Shame, Sexual Communication, and Perceived Social Support among High School Students','Ashamed to Talk About Sex: Correlations between Sexual Shame, Sexual Communication, and Perceived Social Support among High School Students at Jimma Town Southwest Ethiopia','2021-11-19 10:04:18','As high school students are a productive age people sexual shame related problem should be mitigated and they have to attentively follow their education. After determining magnitude of shame scores, sexual talk, communications perceived social support; intervention will be made according to the weight of the problem. This study will identify factors associated with sexual shame and perceived social support. From this point of view we will give training based on gaps identified from the survey and we will monitor by following whether they got awareness or not after providing necessary materials (filling gaps). Lastly we will expect productive educated young population with in the study area (Jimma town).','43fdb64666a79a1ef3077d0a58b7c25e','completed','According to a 2012 survey with 2,092 participants (1,046 parents and 1,046 adolescent children), 50% of adolescents feel uncomfortable talking about sex with parents, and 19% of parents report being uncomfortable discussing sex topics with their children (1). Another survey carried out in 2019 with 2,000 American adults showed that 61% of the participants talk about sex with significant others, 60% with best friends, 40% with friends, 28% with co-workers, and 26% with roommates (2). As these statistics show, people differ in preferred conversational partners for discussing sex and sexuality, but discomfort with discussing sex-related topics is still common between individuals and certain social circles.\r\n\r\nSexual shame occurs when people feel ashamed because of their sex and sexuality such as “past or current sexual thoughts, behaviors, and experiences” (3). Some researchers argue sexual shame cause one to become socially withdrawn and psychologically distressed (4). Because of the painful nature of sexual shame, it is also strongly related to mental health issues such as depression and anxiety (5).When people experience sexual stigma and feel shame, they are more likely to conceal their sexuality, which may influence their comfort with and frequency of sexual communication. Concealment is a behavioral and affective response to sexual stigma against one’s sexuality. This response is associated with self-condemnation, a lack of self-acceptance, and an awareness of one\'s non-normative sexuality. By decreasing distress and increasing physical safety, concealment plays an important role in distinguishing between public-self and private-self and protecting individuals who feel sexually and socially vulnerable due to stigma, social alienation, prejudice, or discrimination (6, 7). Sexual communication is an interpersonal communication approach that can provide conversational partners with opportunities to learn about human sexuality. However, when sexual shame increases, concealment also increases  Thus, many people who experience sexual stigma gain a sense of discomfort and get discouraged from disclosing their sexuality and sex concerns to others (8).\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n \r\nPerceived social support is an anticipated emotional and material support from others, such as family, friends, and/or significant others.  For example, a socially supportive friend may share their personal experiences about the relevant issues (9). Open conversational partners (such as family) openly accept one’s sex and sexuality, it facilitates more open exposure to and frequent contact between conversational partners, which can be a great tool in combating sexual stigma. Such openness, comfort and acceptance are associated with reduced negative emotions, including sexual shame (10). \r\n\r\nThe purpose of this study is to assess how sexual shame, sexual communication and perceived social support are related. Analyzing such correlations can help to better understand how likely sexual shame is to affect people’s frequency of sexual communication and how perceived social support is associated with levels of sexual shame and frequency of sexual communication. This study is new since it investigates the relationships between sexual shame, sexual communication, and perceived social support, three variables that have not been studied before. In addition, most studies do not address a wide range of sex and sexuality topics, such as sexual fantasies, motivations, attitudes, behaviors, relationships, and activities, but is limited in topics of sexual and reproductive health issues (11,12,13). Thus, it is important to go beyond those issues and to discuss a wider variety of sex and sexuality topics. This study could also shed light on more comprehensive and vulnerable populations. The goals of the current study are (1) to examine whether sexual shame is associated with the frequency of sexual communication with others such as family, friends, and/or significant others, (2) to address whether levels of sexual shame are negatively correlated with people’s perceptions of social support, and (3) to identify whether levels of engagement in sexual communication are positively associated with people’s perceptions of social support. Furthermore, the wide variety of sexuality topics also provides insights to the frequency of which these topics are discussed with different conversational partners: family, friends, or significant others.','4.1. Study area and Design \r\nThe study will be conducted in selected high schools of Jimma town.\r\n4.1.1 Study design: - Facility based cross-sectional study design will be conducted. \r\n4.2 Inclusion and Exclusion criteria\r\nStudents who are seriously ill, that can’t respond will be excluded.\r\n4.3 Sampling and Sampling techniques\r\n4.3.1 Sample size \r\nSample size calculation is assuming a maximum prevalence of 50%, because as far as our knowledge we haven’t got previous findings in the area so we have applied simple proportion sample size calculation formula bay using confidence interval (CI) of 95%, with 5% margin of error. Considering, an additional 10% for any error in questionnaire filling, then the calculation was as follows.\r\nn=([Z_(α/2) ]^2 pq)/d^2 \r\nn=([1.96]^2 0.5*0.5)/〖(0.5)〗^2 =384\r\n With 10% non-response rate total sample size will be 423; the study subject is allocated in each high school from private and public by using proportional allocation formula.\r\n4.3.2 Source population:\r\nAll high school students of Jimma town.\r\n4.3.3 Study population \r\nAll high school students of Jimma town who can fulfill the inclusion criteria.\r\n4.3.4 Sampling techniques\r\n In Jimma Zone, there are 52 high schools among them 8 private and 44 public high schools. On average 550 students are there in each high schools. High school will be selected by simple random sampling. Then, from each high school we will select study subjects by using systematic random sampling. In-depth interview will do with 10 students to substantiate quantitative data.  \r\n4.4 Study variables\r\n4.4.1 Dependent variables: \r\n	Sexual shame score.\r\n4.4.2 Independent variables:-\r\n	Socio demographic and economic factors\r\n	Perception on social support, sexual talk, communications, sexual experience.  \r\n	Attitude status towards sexual experience.  \r\n4.5 Data collection procedures (instrument, process, data quality control)\r\n4.5.1 Data collection instrument\r\nThe instrument was adapted from different literatures. Shame score questionnaire will be taken from study conducted in different literatures and will be analyzed. \r\n4.5.2 Data collection process \r\nData will be collected by Oromiffa version questionnaire by self-administered questionnaire method and in-depth interview will be conducted. One day training will be given for both data collectors and supervisors for detail information about questionnaire before data collection.\r\n4.6. Data analysis procedure\r\nThe coded data will be checked, cleaned and entered into Epi data version 3.1 and then analyzed by SPSS window version 21. An independent-samples t-test to examine statistical significance between sexual shame without unanswered optional items       scores and sexual shame with mean scores. This could help decide which sexual shame data to use for further data analyses including hypotheses. To examine examining correlations between sexual shame, sexual communication, and perceived social support, the data will be analyzed using Pearson’s produce-moment correlation. To examine the statistical significance of sexual shame, a non- parametric one-way between-subject ANOVA will be carried. Variables with p-value less than ≤0.05 will be used as cutoff point for statistical significance.\r\n4.7 Data quality management\r\nStandardized and validated questionnaire will be prepared in English and translated to oromioffa version and re translated back to English. This questionnaire will be translated by language experts. Questionnaire will be pre-tested on 5% of population a week before actual data collection period in other non-selected high schools of the town. After pre-test modification will be done accordingly. Data will be checked and entered into Epi-data version 3.1 by creating double data entry template. Any ambiguity will be addressed by communicating with the data collectors and supervisors.\r\n	 Dissemination plan\r\nThe result of the study will be communicated to Jimma University and to concerned bodies in the study areas. Attempts will be made to publish the findings in scientific journal. \r\n8.	Ethical Considerations \r\n\r\nBefore data collection, ethical clearance letter will be obtained from ethical review board of Jimma University Institute of Health. The letter will be submitted to Jimma town education Department for permission. The respondents will be informed and their oral consents will be obtained. The respondents’ right to refuse or withdraw from participating in the interview at any time will be  fully respected and the information provided by each respondent will be kept confidential by making each questionnaire coded and not sharing personal information of any patient to the third party.','<ol>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Planned Parenthood. (2012). <em>Half of all teens feel uncomfortable talking to their parents about sex while only 19 percent of parents feel the same, new survey shows</em>. Retrieved from </span></span><a href=\"https://www.plannedparenthood.org/about-us/newsroom/press-releases/half-all-teens-feel-uncomfortable-talking-their-parents-about-sex-while-only-19-percent-parents\" style=\"color:blue; text-decoration:underline\">https://www.plannedparenthood.o