style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\"><span style=\"color:black\">Transportation&nbsp; for data collectors and supervisors</span></span></span></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top; width:118px\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\"><span style=\"color:black\">5,000</span></span></span></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top; width:138px\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\">&nbsp;</p>\r\n			</td>\r\n		</tr>\r\n		<tr>\r\n			<td rowspan=\"2\" style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:1px solid black; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top; width:49px\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\">&nbsp;</p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top; width:252px\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\"><span style=\"color:black\">Total </span></span></span></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top; width:118px\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><strong><u><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\"><span style=\"color:black\">239,150</span></span></span></u></strong></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top; width:138px\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\">&nbsp;</p>\r\n			</td>\r\n		</tr>\r\n		<tr>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top; width:252px\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\"><span style=\"color:black\">Grand total </span></span></span></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top; width:118px\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><strong><u><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\"><span style=\"color:black\">239,150</span></span></span></u></strong></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top; width:138px\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\">&nbsp;</p>\r\n			</td>\r\n		</tr>\r\n	</tbody>\r\n</table>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:24px; text-align:justify\">&nbsp;</p>',1,NULL,NULL,NULL),(39,77,9,17,'10. Abstract / Executive summary\r\nThe COVID-19 has been caused tragic events by disrupting lives, social welfare and global economy and declared by WHO as the serious public health concern and recognized as worldwide contagious disease (pandemic). Population growth is fundamentally interesting from both the point of ecology and\r\nphysics as different factors contributed to the trajectory of a population density and dynamics. Among factors affecting population growth rate, the outbreak of epidemic diseases is very crucial. The epidemic scholars have been already predicted that the pandemic Covid-19 has been affecting the world population. Worldwide, mortality levels are certainly affected by the Covid-19 pandemic and recorded as the largest decline in life expectancy since the 1918–1919 due to influenza pandemic and World War II. Throughout history, spikes in mortality owing to events such as wars, famines, and pandemics were followed by changes in fertility trends. How the COVID-19 pandemic will affect fertility has an implication for the rate of population aging, shaping future health challenges and economic growth potential across the globe. There are frequent claims that the ongoing pandemic and the lockdown measures will result in a baby boom where couples spend more time with each other to procreate and favors a critical advantage for fertility. From opposite wing, studies also revealed that the pandemic stressful situation imposing negative impacts on sexual health and reproductive outcomes (reduce lipido, impotence, menstrual disturbances and reduce fertility). Thus, the present study aims to address this discrepancy of literatures concerning impact of pandemic on population growth by assessing its double-edged sword on mortality and fertility as indicators of population growth rate and effect of the pandemic on sexual practices among couples in Jimma town.\r\nA community based population statistical survey will be conducted in Jimma town (or among some selected kebeles if budget and/or time constrained). From Jimma town about six kebeles (Ginjo Guduru, Bosa Addis, Awetu Mendera, Mendera Koci, Hermata Mentina and Becho Bore) will be selected deliberately for the fact that they harbor crowded populations and also host significant institutions (health institutions, university, religious organizations, business areas, markets and etc). \r\nFor another objective of the study (effect of the pandemic on sexual practices among couples in Jimma town), a total of 400 couples from the selected kebeles of Jimma town by taking p=50% and 10% non response rate. \r\nData will be collected for two months  by structured questionnaire via face to face interview at household level by experienced data collectors. The double-edged sword effect of Covid-19 on population growth will be assessed by recording the number of pregnant mothers, born children and dead individuals during the pandemic (from April 11, 2020 since Ethiopia declared national lockdown and other preventive measures). All members of the household who lived for more than six months will be included to the study and assessed for intended outcomes (pregnancy, birth and death). Population growth rate will be computed by calculating birth rates and mortality rate and their net difference to evaluate double-edged sword of Covid-19. The effect of Covid-19 on the sexual practices will be assessed by comparing the extent to sexual related practices before and during the pandemic with 5 point likert scales (1-vey less, 2-less, 3-similar, 4-high, 5-very high) for both sex. The collected data will be entered in to Epidata version 4.3.1 and finally exported to SPSS version 25 for further analysis. The finding of the study will be reported using tables, figures and narration. The mean score of likert scales will be compared by independent t-test analysis during and before the pandemic. P-value of 0.05 will be declared as statistically significant.  A total of 350,000.00 Ethiopian birr is required to accomplish the study.',NULL,'Does Covid-19 impose a double-edged sword on population growth? A case of Jimma town (or some selected kebeles); A population statistical survey, and effect of the pandemic on sexual practices among couples in Jimma town, Jimma, Southwest Ethiopia.','2021-11-19 14:36:06','Research outcome/impact\r\nThe double-edged sword effect of Covid-19 on population growth will be assessed and the finding of the study will be served as an input of reproductive health implication and mortality effect of Covid-19 and anticipated for the direction of management/policy.','a347f6ae32fa7dda3dda21d0c5ac962c','completed','The COVID-19 has been caused tragic events by disrupting lives, social welfare and global economy and declared by WHO as the serious public health concern in January 30th , 2020) and recognized as worldwide contagious disease (pandemic) in March 11th , 2020 [1,2]. The time evolution of population growth is fundamentally interesting from both the point of ecology and\r\nphysics as different factors contributed to the trajectory of a population density and drives dynamical pattern in population growth rate [3]. Among factors affecting population growthrate, the outbreak of epidemic diseases is very crucial [4].\r\nThe epidemic scholars have been already predicted that the pandemic Covid-19 will affect the world population and its impact on specific population growth dynamic [5–10]. Worldwide, mortality levels are certainly affected by the Covid-19 pandemic and recorded as the largest decline in life expectancy since the 1918–1919 due to influenza pandemic and World War II [11–17]. Throughout history, spikes in mortality owing to events such as wars, famines, and pandemics were followed by changes in fertility, resulting in fewer births in the short term and recuperation in subsequent years. Economic and social change triggered by a pandemic is also likely to influence child bearing intentions and fertility [18].\r\nThe main quest raised ‘how the Covid-19 pandemic will affect fertility’ has an implication for the rate of population aging, shaping future health challenges and economic growth potential across the globe. There are frequent claims that the ongoing pandemic and the lockdown measures will result in a baby boom where couples spend more time with each other, more likely to procreate and favors a critical advantage for fertility which considered as a symbolic meaning, believing that a new births become a positive reframing mechanism, signaling a return to normality based on lessons learned from previous unexpected mortality shocks [18–21].\r\nLiteratures were inconsistent and disparity in regards to impact of Covid-19 on sexual health/fertility which is an important aspect of general wellbeing, with important consequences on the population’s daily lives where fertility and birth trends in response to the pandemic varies according to socioeconomic conditions of population [22–25]. Studies also recommended the safe practice of sexual act during the pandemic to boost immune system and overcome anxiety and depression[24,26]. \r\nBut, in contrary to this some studies reported that the sexual practice behaviors (sexual desire, frequency of sex) were reduced during the pandemic due to anxiety, stress and depression. The prolonged school closures and mandated physical distancing also imposes a heavier burden on parents’ time, and lowers desired fertility and childbearing postponements during the lockdown measures [27,28]. Thus, the present study is aimed to address this discrepancy of literatures concerning impact of pandemic on population growth by assessing its double-edged sword on mortality and fertility as indicators of population growth rate and effect of Covid-19 on sexual related practices.','A community based population statistical survey will be conducted in Jimma town (or among some selected kebeles that harbor crowded populations and also hosts significant institutions (health institutions, university, religious organizations, business areas, markets and etc)). Initially the study is proposed to be conducted among entire Jimma town. But, if budget and/or time constrained some kebeles will be selected (Ginjo Guduru, Bosa Addis, Awetu Mendera, Mendera Koci, Hermata Mentina and Becho Bore kebeles) purposely due to fore mentioned reasons. \r\nData will be collected for two months by structured questionnaire via face to face interview at household level in Jimma town (or some selected kebeles). Experienced data collectors will be hired (who lives in specific kebeles, know the demography of the area well and have good communication skills) to facilitate the work. \r\nThe double-edged sword effect of Covid-19 on population growth will be assessed by recording the number of pregnant mothers, born children and dead individuals at household levels during the pandemic (since April 11, 2020 when Ethiopia declared national lockdown and other preventive measures). All members of the household who lived there for more than six months will be included to the study and assessed for intended outcomes (pregnancy status, birth and death). As pregnancy can be manifested well within first trimesters, any pregnancy before April, 2020 will be excluded. It is assessed by a simple question “Is there any household member, who became pregnant after April, 2020?” Children born after 9 months of declared lockdown (from December, 2020) will be included and any member of household who dead due to any cause since April 11, 2020 will be recorded. Population growth rate will be computed by calculating birth rates and mortality rate and their net difference to evaluate double-edged sword of Covid-19.\r\nFor another objective of the study (effect of the pandemic on sexual practices among couples in Jimma town), a total of 400 couples from the selected kebeles of Jimma town by taking p=50% and 10% non response rate. The effect of Covid-19 on the sexual practices will be assessed by comparing the extent to sexual related practices before and during the pandemic with 5 point likert scales (1-vey less, 2-less, 3-similar, 4-high, 5-very high) for both sex. \r\nThe collected data will be entered in to Epidata version 4.3.1 and finally exported to SPSS version 25 for further analysis. The finding of the study will be reported using tables, figures and narration. The mean score of likert scales for different sexual practices will be compared by independent t-test analysis during and before the pandemic. P-value of <0.05 will be declared as statistically significant.','<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">[1]&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Jee Y. WHO International Health Regulations emergency committee for the COVID-19 outbreak. Epidemiol Health 2020;42.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">[2]&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Jernigan DB, COVID C, Team R. Update: public health response to the coronavirus disease 2019 outbreak&mdash;United States, February 24, 2020. Morb Mortal Wkly Rep 2020;69:216.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">[3]&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Biswas D, Roy S. Analyzing COVID-19 pandemic with a new growth model for population ecology. ArXivPrepr ArXiv200412950 2020.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">[4]&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Wu Z, McGoogan JM. Characteristics of and important lessons from the coronavirus disease 2019 (COVID-19) outbreak in China: summary of a report of 72 314 cases from the Chinese Center for Disease Control and Prevention. Jama 2020;323:1239&ndash;42.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">[5]&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Wang N, Fu Y, Zhang H, Shi H. An evaluation of mathematical models for the outbreak of COVID-19.PrecisClin Med 2020;3:85&ndash;93.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">[6]&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Chowdhury R, Heng K, Shawon MSR, Goh G, Okonofua D, Ochoa-Rosales C, et al. Dynamic interventions to control COVID-19 pandemic: a multivariate prediction modelling study comparing 16 worldwide countries. Eur J Epidemiol 2020;35:389&ndash;99.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">[7]&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Tuli S, Tuli S, Tuli R, Gill SS. Predicting the growth and trend of COVID-19 pandemic using machine learning and cloud computing. Internet Things 2020;11:100222.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">[8]&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Ivanov D. Predicting the impacts of epidemic outbreaks on global supply chains: A simulation-based analysis on the coronavirus outbreak (COVID-19/SARS-CoV-2) case. Transp Res Part E LogistTransp Rev 2020;136:101922.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">[9]&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Wang P, Zheng X, Li J, Zhu B. Prediction of epidemic trends in COVID-19 with logistic model and machine learning technics. Chaos Solitons Fractals 2020;139:110058.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">[10]&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; L&oacute;pez L, Rodo X. A modified SEIR model to predict the COVID-19 outbreak in Spain and Italy: simulating control scenarios and multi-scale epidemics. Results Phys 2021;21:103746.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">[11]&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Arthi V, Parman J. Disease, downturns, and wellbeing: Economic history and the long-run impacts of COVID-19. Explor Econ Hist 2021;79:101381.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">[12]&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Hamidi S, Sabouri S, Ewing R. Does density aggravate the COVID-19 pandemic? Early findings and lessons for planners. J Am PlannAssoc 2020;86:495&ndash;509.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">[13]&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Goldstein JR, Lee RD. Demographic perspectives on the mortality of COVID-19 and other epidemics. ProcNatlAcadSci 2020;117:22035&ndash;41.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">[14]&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Gonz&aacute;lez FAI, Dip JA, London S. Long-Lasting Effects of Pandemics: The Case of the 1918 Influenza Pandemic in Argentina. Spat Spatio-Temporal Epidemiol 2021:100409.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">[15]&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Nichols CM, Bristow N, Ewing ET, Gabriel JM, Montoya BC, Outka E. Reconsidering the 1918&ndash;19 Influenza Pandemic in the Age of COVID-19. J Gilded Age Progress Era 2020;19:642&ndash;72.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">[16]&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Ghislandi S, Muttarak R, Sauerberg M, Scotti B. News from the front: Excess mortality and life expectancy in two major epicentres of the COVID-19 pandemic in Italy. MedRxiv 2020.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">[17]&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Drezner DW. The Song Remains the Same: International Relations After COVID-19. Int Organ 2020:1&ndash;18.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">[18]&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Aassve A, Cavalli N, Mencarini L, Plach S, Bacci ML. The COVID-19 pandemic and human fertility. Science 2020;369:370&ndash;1.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">[19]&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Chattu VK, Chami G. Global Health Diplomacy Amid the COVID-19 Pandemic: A Strategic Opportunity for Improving Health, Peace, and Well-Being in the CARICOM Region&mdash;A Systematic Review. SocSci 2020;9:88.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">[20]&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Balbo N, Kashnitsky I, Melegaro A, Mesl&eacute; F, Mills MC, Valk D, et al. Demography and the Coronavirus Pandemic. 2020.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">[21]&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Anser MK, Yousaf Z, Khan MA, Voo XH, Nassani AA, Alotaibi SM, et al. The impacts of COVID-19 measures on global environment and fertility rate: double coincidence. Air Qual Atmosphere Health 2020;13:1083&ndash;92.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">[22]&nbsp;&nbsp;&nb