order-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:1px solid black; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:1px solid black; vertical-align:top; width:78px\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"><strong>S.N</strong></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:1px solid black; vertical-align:top; width:334px\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"><strong>Category</strong></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:1px solid black; vertical-align:top; width:213px\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"><strong>Total cost (in ETB)</strong></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n		</tr>\r\n		<tr>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:1px solid black; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top; width:78px\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">1.</span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top; width:334px\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">Stationery cost</span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top; width:213px\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">25,000.00</span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n		</tr>\r\n		<tr>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:1px solid black; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top; width:78px\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">4.</span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top; width:334px\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">Personnel cost</span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top; width:213px\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">300,000.00</span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n		</tr>\r\n		<tr>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:1px solid black; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top; width:78px\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">5.</span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top; width:334px\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">Transportation cost</span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top; width:213px\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">25,000.00</span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n		</tr>\r\n		<tr>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:1px solid black; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top; width:78px\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\">&nbsp;</p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top; width:334px\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"><strong>Total cost</strong></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top; width:213px\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"><strong>350,000.00</strong></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n		</tr>\r\n	</tbody>\r\n</table>',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),(40,161,9,16,'Abstract / Executive summary\r\nBackground: Mining has a potential hazard to the workers. Coal mining was associated with a broad range of health problems over the long run. Among many, coal mining lung disease, occupational stress and musculoskeletal disorder were frequent problems identified so far. In developed countries, the focus has been given to the health of the workers. However, the concern of occupation-related disease was almost neglected in developing countries including Ethiopia. Therefore, the main aim of this study is to assess the availability, utilization of safety measures, screening of lung disease, musculoskeletal disorder, and associated factors among coal miners in Jimma Zone, southwest Ethiopia.\r\nMethodology: The cross-sectional study will be conducted in the Jimma Zone coal mining area (at Botor Xolay and Dedo District). About 384 coal miners will be included in the study after proportional allocation was performed for all the coal mining areas. All the workers on mining during data collection will be included in the study and those with overt lung disease before the start of mining and less than the six-month experience of coal mining will be excluded. Data will be collected by interview administered semi-structured questionnaire by trained data collectors. A questionnaire was structured by relevant literature review and measurement will be conducted by strict adherence to recommended guidelines and standards. The respiratory symptom will be assessed by the English version of the American Thoracic Society Division of Lung Disease questionnaire (ATS-DLD-78A).  Occupational stress will be assessed by the English version of the Effort–Reward Imbalance Questionnaire (ERI), and mental health will be assessed by Symptom Checklist-90(SCL-90). A lung function test will be conducted by using a portable digital spirometer (SP10, Italy). Blood pressure will be measured by using a digital sphygmomanometer (NUTEC, CDC). Weight will be recorded by using digital weight balance (TANITA 380, Japan). Data will be entered into Epi data version 4.1 and exported into SPSS version 23 for analysis. Descriptive variables will be expressed in terms of frequency and percentages. Chi-square, independent samples t-test, binary and multivariate logistic regression will be conducted to identify predictors of lung function, availability of safety measures, and external body injury. Quality of data will be assured by conducting training for data collectors, conducting pretests, and ensuring continuous supervision during data collection. All measures will be taken to prevent the spread of COVID-19 during data collection. The total budget required for this study will be 467,500.00 ETB.','SAFETY MEASURES AND MINING RELATED  DISEASES','HEALTH SAFETY MEASURES, MINING RELATED LUNG DISEASES, MUSCULOSKELETAL DISORDERS, OCCUPATIONAL STRESS AND ASSOCIATED FACTORS AMONG COAL MINERS IN JIMMA ZONE','2021-11-19 11:40:29','4.13. Research output or outcome\r\nThis project is intended to identify occupation-related lung, musculoskeletal, and stress.  As an output, it will identify the level of protection of coal mining workers against major occupation-related diseases. Moreover, it will help to identify the early effect and associated factors which can further predispose. Having adequate and quality results from this project will immediately help participants to know their status regarding the screened problems. In addition, the finding of this project will actually motivate all the stakeholders and concerning body to give major focus on the health of its workers. In long run, the finding will be published and this will help in','1b8c23daa9908433822f2fb598b257b4','completed','Introduction \r\nMining is one of the most dangerous and risky areas which could be associated with strict risk screening and early management of mining-related disease (1).  Mining hazard has potential risk to well being and health of the workers. To successfully manage health risks, identification of the health hazard and its source is mandatory(2).  Coal mining is one of the mining areas having been associated with multiple health problems; lung disease is one of these which results in several disabilities. Many diseases have been identified with coal mining dust, from which lung problem is of immense importance. These include interstitial lung disease, pneumoconiosis, emphysema, Chronic Obstructive Pulmonary Diseases (COPD), chronic bronchitis, silicosis, fibrosis, noise-induced hearing loss, musculoskeletal disorders, and accidents(3–5).  \r\nThese diseases were mainly due to exposure to reparatory dust particles including silicates, carbon, and quartz(6,7). Carbon exposure affects the lung in many ways. It can result in lung inflammation and persistent emphysema(8,9). Respiratory silica and silicates were identified to be associated with pneumoconiosis and pulmonary fibrosis(10,11). Quartz exposure was implicated in lung cancer(12). For successful mining, adequate and continuous work on both the mental and physical health of workers is recommended. In a real scenario, the majority of miners face occupation-related stress as a major determining factor of mental health. In addition,   assessment of musculoskeletal disorder best signifies the level of workers’ physical health(13–15). According to a report from a comprehensive systematic review and meta-analysis, musculoskeletal disorder among miners was very high and the recommendation was forwarded for continuous supervision and screening of those disorders for the increment of productive abilities of the workers(16).\r\nIn our country Ethiopia, mining is one of the top priorities of the development-related sector. Besides this, occupational health related to this mining was a little bit neglected. Special focus and screening should be done in a higher-risk occupations like coal mining (17–19).  Coal mining-related lung disease can vary from the smallest airway damage to complete lung distraction. Mitigating this problem can be possible if and only if there are adequate protective measures, strict use of those measures by workers, and continuous screening of these lung diseases in the exposed group in advance to identify any occupation-related lung diseases as early as possible (4,20). \r\nMortality and morbidity related to coal mining are more prevalent among those with increased age, long duration of work, type of mining having exposure to underground digging, educational status, adherence to safety measures, and several works related and environmental factors(21). According to evidence from systematic review and meta-analysis, effort and techniques to control dust effectively and surveillance of respiratory health are highly recommended(22). Physical activity was identified to be important in reducing lung disease in coal miners if started at a low black carbon level (23).  \r\nIn developed countries, guidelines were developed by the government to exposure to reparable min dust. In meantime, occupation exposure limits were varied from country to country. In the United States of America (USA), the limit for occupational exposure to coal dust was 1.5mg/m3and for crystalline silica is 0.005 mg/m3(6). In low and middle-income countries there was no guideline developed so far to protect the health of workers in mining occupation and the limit of hazard exposure was not well identified and this will further impact the health of miners in developing countries(24–26).  Mining without adequate assurance of occupational health and environmental health cannot be sustained. For a mining occupation to be successful in any continent and country, having been focused on the health of both environments should be a policy as well as a priority area in the mining industry(27,28).   \r\nAs far as the knowledge of the researchers is concerned, there was no published study done on safety measures and lung disease screening among coal miners in Jimma Zone. So this study will close this gap. In this project, a comprehensive assessment of available health safety measures will be well explored as they limit the majority of mining-related diseases. Moreover, the screening will be conducted to early identify occupational stress, lung disease, musculoskeletal disease burden, and a possible risk that aggravates all these conditions will be explored.','Design and methods\r\n4.1. Study area and period\r\nThe studies will be conducted among adults aged eighteen years or older conducting coal mining in Jimma Zone, In the zone, there are two mining areas which are located in Dedo district and Xolay. According to information from zonal administration, about 400 micro each contains an average of 15 workers have been conducting mining in Tolay, additionally about 20 micro each contains an average of 55 workers have been conducting mining in Dedo. Accordingly, a total of about 7100 workers were performing mining in the Zone. The study will be commenced from February 2022 to May 2022.\r\n4.2. Study design\r\nA facility-based cross-sectional study design will be employed.\r\n4.3. Populations:\r\nSource population\r\n All adults aged 18 years and above on coal mining in Jimma Zone.\r\nStudy population\r\nAdults aged 18 years and above were selected for the study from the coal mining area in Jimma Zone.\r\nInclusion and exclusion criteria\r\nAll adults conducting mining in the Jimma Zone coal mining area and aged 18 years or older will be included. The individual with overt lung disease before the coal mining exposure and those with less than six months of coal mining experience will be excluded. \r\n4.4. Sampling procedure and Sample size estimation\r\nIndividuals will be enrolled in the study by using proportional allocation to all the coal mining areas in Jimma Zone. Accordingly, there are two coal mining areas, one in Xolay having 6000 workers and the other in the Dedo district having 1100 workers. Based on the proportional allocation rule, the calculated sample size will be proportionally allocated for both mining areas.   \r\n       \r\nn=([Zα/2]2 *p [1-p]  )/d2\r\np= Expected population proportion of the respiratory symptoms. Since there was no previous study on the prevalence of respiratory symptoms among the coal mining communities in Ethiopia, a prevalence of 50% will be taken to estimate the sample size.\r\n	Q= 1-p\r\n	The level of confidence, α = 0.05 (95%) will be utilized to estimate the prevalence of respiratory symptoms.\r\n	D = degree of absolute precision/ error tolerated=0.05 (5%) \r\n	Zα/2=Standard normal variable at 95% Confidence level (1.96).\r\nn=([Zα/2]2 *p [1-p]  )/d2\r\n\r\nn=([1.96]2 *0.5 [1-0.5]  )/((0.05)2) =384\r\nBy conducting a minimization formula (since the total population was less than 10,000) and by adding a five percent non-response rate, the final sample size will be 383.\r\nNf=n/(1+n/N) = 365, and by adding five percent non response rate, the final sample size will be 383. \r\n4.5.Data collection tools (questionnaire / variables; biological measurements / lab investigations; anthropometric measurements; others)\r\nData regarding demographic factors and behavioral risk factors will be collected using World Health Organization standardized structured questionnaire for developing countries. The respiratory symptom will be assessed by the English version of the American Thoracic Society Division of Lung Disease questionnaire (ATS-DLD-78A).  Occupational stress will be assessed by the English version of the Effort–Reward Imbalance Questionnaire (ERI), and mental health will be assessed by Symptom Checklist-90(SCL-90). Height will be measured using a stadiometer installed with scale and weight will be recorded after measuring the patient\'s barefoot to the nearest 0.1kg and wearing the light clothes using a weight balance (TANITA 380, Tokyo, Japan). During the height measurement, the participant’s shoes and any hats or hair ornaments were removed. Subjects faced away from the wall with the heels together and the back as straight as possible. The head, shoulders, buttocks, and heels touched the vertical stand. The participant steps away from the wall and the height measurement is recorded to the nearest 0.1 cm. From weight and height measurements, body mass index was calculated. Waist circumference (in centimeter) was measured at the midpoint between the lowermost rib and the iliac crest. Hip circumference was measured at the greater trochanter of the femur on a horizontal plane, and these circumferences were used to calculate a waist-to-hip ratio., \r\nBlood pressure (BP) was measured all three times in the left arm with a digital sphygmomanometer (NUTEC, BP09, and CDC). Three seated BP measurements were taken for each subject spaced five minutes apart. The BP was taken using a mercuric sphygmomanometer from the left upper arm after the participant was seated quietly for at least 5 min (average of three measurements). Blood pressure (BP) was measured using a standard adult arm cuff of Aneroid type and a digital sphygmomanometer after 5 min rest. To improve the reliability of measurement three readings were taken with 5 min intervals and the average of the three readings was recorded as the final BP of the patient. But if the difference between the two readings was greater than 5 mmHg, a third measurement was taken and the average of the three readings was recorded as the final BP of the participants. \r\nSpirometer measurement\r\nLung function tests will be examined using a portable digital spirometer (SP10, Gima S.p.A, Gessate, Italy). Firstly, testee deep inspires, then seals the lips around the mouthpiece and blasts all air out as forcefully as possible; the exhalant gas transforms to rotary airflow by a turbine, then makes the blade rotate. The reception part of the infrared pair diodes (one is for infrared emission, the other is reception) towards the blade is used for receiving the infrared ray, when the blade rotates, the received ray strength of the reception diode will be different as the difference of the blade angle, so form the various signal of same proportion in reception diode, which forms acquisition signal by SCM after processing. At last, various parameters to be measured formed\r\nfrom the information which was processed by the microprocessor, and displayed from the screen.\r\n4.6. Study variables \r\nDependent variable\r\nFor objective one: availability of lung health safety measures\r\nFor objectives two and three: level of utilization of lung health safety measures\r\nFor objective four: respiratory disease symptoms\r\nFor objective five: pulmonary function status\r\nFor objective six : Musculoskeletal disorder\r\nFor objective seven: Occupational stress \r\n Independent variables\r\n	Socio-demographic characteristics \r\n	Lifestyle Characteristics and Dietary Practices \r\n	Physical and laboratory measurements\r\n	Work-related factors\r\n	Health safety measure assessment-related factors \r\n	Respiratory symptom Assessment related variables \r\n	Pulmonary function test assessment-related variables\r\n	Musculoskeletal disorders assessment-related factors \r\n	 Occupational stress assessment-related factors\r\n4.7. Operational definition\r\n	Hypertension-Systolic blood pressure ≥ 140 mmHg or those with diastolic blood pressure ≥ 90 mmHg.\r\n	Abdominal or central adiposity: This is measured using waist circumference and waist to hip ratio. The cut-off point for central obesity is a waist circumference of ≥94 cm for males and ≥80cm for females.\r\n	Body mass index\r\n• Underweight - BMI < 18.5 kg/m2\r\n• Normal weight - BMI ≥ 18.5 to 24.9 kg/m2\r\n• Overweight - BMI ≥ 25.0 to 29.9 kg/m2\r\n• Obesity - BMI of 30.0 to 34.9 kg/m2\r\n	Current smoker–an individual who was smoking at least half a pack during the last year of the study period. \r\n	Regular Chat chewing– an individual who was chewing khat daily in the last one month of the study period.\r\n	Physical Activity: For the purposes of this study, we consider physical activity as any activity done by the muscles in a systematic, structured, and repetitive manner in order to maintain body fitness. In accordance with WHO, MET (Metabolic Equivalent) will be used in the analysis of physical activity. MET is defined as the ratio of the work metabolic rate to the resting metabolic rate. One MET is defined as 1 kcal/kg/hour and is equivalent to the energy cost of sitting quietly, around 3.5 ml/kg/min. A person not meeting any of the following criteria is considered being physically inactive: Three or more days of vigorous‐intensity activity of at least 10 minutes per day; OR Five or more days of moderate‐intensity activity or walking of at least 30 minutes per day; OR  Five or more days of any combination of walking, moderate‐ or vigorous intensity activities achieving a minimum of at least 600 MET‐minutes per week (WHO STEPS manual)	\r\n4.8. Data management and statistical analysis\r\n	Data will be entered into EPI data manager version 4.0.2 then will be exported to SPSS (Version 22.0) for statistical analysis. Frequency distributions of socio‐ demographical, lifestyle, clinical, behavioral characteristics, work-related factors, respiratory symptoms, and lung function tests of the participants will be first explored by percentage (%) and frequencies. Continuous variables will be expressed as mean ± standard deviation values. Chi‐Square tests will be used to evaluate the differences in the distribution of categorical variables for study groups. Chi-square (χ2) analyses will be used to compare differences in proportion and independent samples t-test analysis will be conducted to determine e factors associated with lung function test. Binary and multivariate logistic regression analyses will be done to determine factors associated with lung function tests and respiratory symptoms.\r\n      Normality of continuous variables will be checked using graphic methods (Histograms with normality curves and QQ-plots) and models will be selected depending on the type of dependent variable (Logistic regression for binary viable, linear regression for continuous normally distributed variable) and multicollinearity will be checked.\r\n4.9. Quality assurance\r\n    Data will be double entered into EPI data manager version 4.0.2, Trained Nurses; Medical Laboratory technicians will be involved in the collection of socio-demographic, behavioral, clinical, and physical characteristics from the individuals. Proper functioning of instruments, laboratory reagents, and technical performance will be checked by using quality control samples. Standard operating procedures (SOPs) will be followed starting from sample collection up to result reporting. All laboratory procedures were handled by laboratory technologists. Before data analysis cleaning was done. Adequate precautions will be made to ensure COVID-19 spread during data collections. \r\n4.10. Plan for dissemination and ensuring utilization of findings\r\nThe result of this study will be communicated to the Jimma University Institute Research Directorate. An effort will be made to publish the result in peer-reviewed reputable journals. The result of the study will also be communicated to the hospitals through journal articles, newspapers, bulletins, and pamphlets in major languages spoken in Ethiopia.\r\n4.11. Ethical and environmental considerations\r\n	The study will be performed in accordance with the principles of the Declaration of Helsinki, and the Ethics Committee of Jimma University In the statute of Health Research Board will be approved the study protocol. Written informed consent will be obtained from all subjects prior to the start of the study. A letter of supports will be obtained from the Health Institute of Jimma University and the nature of the study will be fully explained to the study participant. After getting permission from the study participants. Each study participant will be informed about the research and reassured that confidentiality of information would be maintained during data collection, analyses, interpretation, and publication of results. After screening study participants, those individuals with abnormal pulmonary function tests and respiratory symptoms will be referred to the hospital for further management ahead of development complications. \r\n4.12. Capacity building \r\nThree masters students will be involved in the project (one from Physiology, one from Anatomy, and one from Psychiatry will be involved in the project for capacity building)','<h1><span style=\"font-size:16pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\"><a name=\"_Toc87925180\"><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">References</span></span></a> </span></span></h1>\r\n\r\n<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">1. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Afolayan DO, Onwualu AP, Eggleston CM, Adetunji AR, Tao M, Amankwah RK. Safe Mining Assessment of Artisanal Barite Mining Activities in Nigeria. Mining. 2021;1(2):224&ndash;40. </span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">2. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; How can occupational safety and health be managed? (Labour administration and inspection) [Internet]. [cited 2021 Nov 5]. Available from: https://www.ilo.org/global/topics/labour-administration-inspection/resources-library/publications/guide-for-labour-inspectors/how-can-osh-be-managed/lang--en/index.htm</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">3. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Laney AS, Weissman DN. Respiratory Diseases Caused by Coal Mine Dust. J Occup Environ Med Am Coll Occup Environ Med. 2014 Oct;56(0 10):S18&ndash;22. </span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">4. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Lu C, Dasgupta P, Cameron J, Fritschi L, Baade P. A systematic review and meta-analysis on international studies of prevalence, mortality and survival due to coal mine dust lung disease. PLOS ONE. 2021 Aug 3;16(8):e0255617. </span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">5. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Respiratory Diseases [Internet]. [cited 2021 Nov 5]. Available from: https://www.cdc.gov/niosh/mining/topics/respiratorydiseases.html</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">6. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Go LH, Cohen RA. Coal Workers&rsquo; Pneumoconiosis and Other Mining-Relat