one; vertical-align:top; width:282px\">\r\n			<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Reagent and Medical equipment costs </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top; width:138px\">\r\n			<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">60,000.<s>00 </s>ETB</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n		</tr>\r\n		<tr>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:1px solid black; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top; width:102px\">\r\n			<ol start=\"4\">\r\n				<li>&nbsp;</li>\r\n			</ol>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top; width:282px\">\r\n			<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Data processing costs </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top; width:138px\">\r\n			<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">45,000.<s>00 </s>ETB</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n		</tr>\r\n		<tr>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:1px solid black; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top; width:102px\">\r\n			<p style=\"margin-left:48px\">&nbsp;</p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top; width:282px\">\r\n			<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Sub &ndash;total </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top; width:138px\">\r\n			<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">425,000.<s>00 </s>ETB</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n		</tr>\r\n		<tr>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:1px solid black; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top; width:102px\">\r\n			<ol start=\"5\">\r\n				<li>&nbsp;</li>\r\n			</ol>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top; width:282px\">\r\n			<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Contingency (10%)</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top; width:138px\">\r\n			<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">42,500.<s>00 </s>ETB</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n		</tr>\r\n		<tr>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:1px solid black; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top; width:102px\">\r\n			<p>&nbsp;</p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top; width:282px\">\r\n			<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><strong><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Total </span></span></strong></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top; width:138px\">\r\n			<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><strong><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">467,500.<s>00 </s>ETB</span></span></strong></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n		</tr>\r\n	</tbody>\r\n</table>\r\n\r\n<p>&nbsp;</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),(41,70,9,25,'Background: Malaria is a serious infectious disease that remains to be the burden of public health in\r\ndeveloping countries with unacceptably high levels of death. It is adversely affecting not only the\r\nhealth of the peoples but also economic development and quality of life. Computer-based lead\r\ncompounds discovery can shorten the time required and cutting down the cost for the drug\r\ndevelopment, the otherwise slow and resource intensive process. \r\nObjective:  The aim of this study is to identify potential  lead compounds to combat malaria using\r\nenvironmental, phytochemical and in silico methods towards the new development of anti-malarial\r\nagents. \r\nMethods: This is a laboratory-based experimental study to be carried out after identifying top five\r\nplants with anti-malarial and mosquito repellent activities from study conducted in Ethiopia. The\r\nplants will be selected based in vivo maximum percent chemo-suppression and percentage of\r\nrepellency. Isolation will be carried out using column and preparative thin layer chromatography\r\n(PTLC). Spectroscopic methods (ESI–MS, 1D and 2D-NMR, IR, GC-MS) will be used to characterize\r\nthe chemical structures of the compounds. Repellence will be tested by means of an arm-in-cage\r\nassay. AutoDock Vina will used to screen potential compounds by molecular docking using two\r\ncrystal structures of Plasmodium enzymes. In vivo anti-malarial activity will be evaluated for the\r\ncompounds with the highest binding free energy score using Peters’ 4-day suppressive test against P.\r\nberghei in mice. The in vivo anti-malarial data will be analysed using Prism – GraphPad. The results \r\n will be presented as Mean ± standard error of mean. The means will be compared by analysis of\r\nvariance), and post-test analysis will be performed. Probability level at (p<0.05) will be  considered\r\nsignificant.','Malaria Prevention and Treatment','Integrated Approach to the Mitigation of Malaria: Environmental, Phytochemical and in silico Methods towards the New Development in Anti-Malarial agents.','2021-11-19 10:17:20','Compile and document the phytochemistry, safety and efficacy of experimentally studied medicinal plants for malaria treatment in Ethiopia. Build the library of lead compounds with anti-malarial and mosquito repellent activity.Build the library of potential lead compound with anti-malarial activity.Identify anti-malarial lead compound for further pre-clinical investigation.','fd8452e80b141af53056599da17ce26d','completed','2.1. Background and Rationale\r\nMalaria is a serious infectious disease that remains to be the burden of public health in developing countries with unacceptably high levels of death 1,2. According to the world Health Organization(WHO), there were approximately 229 million cases and 409,000 deaths globally in 2019 3. The highest prevalence reported in Africa (82%), followed by Southeast Asia (10%) 3,4. Malaria has had profound effects on human lives, particularly in the sub-Saharan African 5. It is adversely affecting not only the health of the peoples but also economic development and quality of life 6,7. The disease is caused by five protozoan pathogens of the Plasmodium spp: Plasmodium falciparum and Plasmodium vivax, are the two major species that co-exist in Ethiopia accounting  for about 60 and 40 % of malaria cases, respectively 8,9. It causes serious complications, such as hypoglycaemia, acute renal failure, severe anaemia and cerebral malaria among infected individuals 10. Malaria is spread by the sting of Plasmodium spp.-infected female mosquitoes of the Anopheles genus 11. \r\nIn Ethiopia, malaria control has been one of the major components of health care system as it is one of the leading causes of morbidity and mortality 12. The changing topographical and climatic features are contributing to the cyclical and unpredictable malaria transmission pattern in Ethiopia, which is typically characterized by regular focal, recurring, and widespread epidemics 8. It is projected that nearly 70% of the total population are living in areas at risk of malaria 13. \r\nOne promising way to fight malaria is the integrative approaches that includes the use of vector control products, medicine, and  vaccines 14. Nevertheless, the plasticity of the mosquito and the Plasmodium parasite has led to escalating resistance to medicines and insecticides. Resistance to artemisinin-based combination therapies (ACTs) has been identified in five countries in Southeast Asia. The spread of these strains to Africa or the Indian subcontinent could be disastrous 15. The resistance in malaria parasites is assumed to have appeared through mutations in the active sites of drug targets or from the biochemical alterations in the drug receptors 16. Encouragingly, the current deployment of malaria vaccine is considered as further important step towards reducing the disease burden by providing partial protection against malaria in young children 17.  \r\n2.2. Targeted Antimalarial Drug Discovery\r\nOwing to the expansion of resistance toward the existing therapies, a rigorous drug discovery to uncover new natural leads using innovative approaches will speed up the development endeavours of new anti-malaria therapeutics 18. An ideal antimalarial should be reasonably priced, have good oral bioavailability, short treatment regimens, tolerated by the patient, and stable at room temperature 19. There have been substantial advancements in the development of in vitro and in vivo experiments that generate data used to inform decisions about the potential merit of new compounds that would be a valuable tool across all stages of drug development 20.\r\nOne of the current approaches for reducing the time needed and cutting down the cost for the discovery is to incorporate computer-based techniques like in silico docking s, pharmacophore-based searches, and neural networking 18,19. Computer-based methods have also been integrated in the prediction of the possible metabolic routes of drug molecules, as well as forecast their pharmacokinetic profiles 21,22. The absorption, distribution, metabolism, excretion, and toxicity (ADMET) profile of a potential drug molecule should be known if it has to stand the chances of entering the market. Hence, appraising such information for lead compounds in advance would help reduce molecules of unnecessary profiles and ultimately reduce the cost of drug discovery 23.\r\nWHO acknowledged that most of the currently existing plant-derived pharmaceuticals applied in modern  medicines  resembles their traditional usage in the native cultures 24. The use of plant-derived compounds for mosquito control has been used since time memorials. The development of artemisinin derivatives from the traditionally used Chinese plant Qinghao, Artemisia annua L., also reaffirms the potential of ethnobotanicals to provide effective drugs for the treatment of malaria 25. Likewise, in Ethiopia, there are documentations of medicinal plants used traditionally for the treatment of malaria in different parts of the country 26. However, most of the studies carried on the medicinal claims of anti-malarial or mosquito repellent activities in Ethiopia are limited only to ethnobotanical studies, plant crude extracts or solvent fractions of secondary metabolites, potentially possessing the anti-malarial activities 27. The number of studies that have had the active natural compounds isolated and the toxicity determined are very limited to the best of our knowledge. These approaches are far from attaining the active or the leads compounds towards the final goal of identifying the anti-malarial agents. \r\nTherefore, the aim of this study is to bring phytochemicals of anti-malarial agents (published in trusted scientific journals from Ethiopian origin) towards its final goal through isolating, chemical characterization, in silico modelling and bioactivity assays (Figure 1). In silico compound activity profiling approach can also substitute for preliminary structure–activity relationship studies because both potency and selectivity are revealed simultaneously, further hastening the process of drug development. This could have an enormous impact on the otherwise slow and resource intensive practice of the traditional drug development processes.','2.5. Methods  and Materials \r\n2.5.1. Study Design\r\nThis project will be a laboratory-based experimental study to be carried out at Jimma University department of Pharmacy and Chemistry, Addis Ababa University department of Chemistry  and Ethiopia Public Health Institute (EPHI).  \r\n2.5.2. Plant Materials\r\nThe plant material to be used in this study will be identified after systematic literature review on commonly used traditional medicinal plants for the treatment of malaria by using a web-based literature search strategy. Accordingly, top five plants for anti-malarial and mosquito repellent activity based on in vivo maximum percent chemo-suppression and percentage of repellency will be selected. Search for published journal articles will be attained using scientific databases such as including Google Scholar, Science Direct, Pub Med, Web of Science. and  African Journals online. In addition, unpublished MSc/PhD thesis from local university websites and Google search engine will be included. \r\nInclusion criteria \r\n•	Unpublished MSc/PhD thesis with in vivo anti-malarial activity conducted at any time in Ethiopia that has already passed the academic evaluation. \r\n•	Peer reviewed full length experimental studies with in vivo anti-malarial activity published.\r\nExclusion criteria\r\n•	Ethno-botanical and ethno medicinal articles on anti-malarial medicinal plants conducted in Ethiopia without experimental in vivo anti-malarial activities. \r\n•	Plants without scientific names \r\nThe following key terms will be used: Ethiopia/Ethiopian plants/Ethiopian medicinal plants/Ethiopian anti-malarial plants, Malaria/Ant malarial/ Anti-malarial plants, in vivo anti-malarial activity, Traditional Medicine/Traditional medicinal plants, Medicinal Plants/Medicinal herbs, Ethno botany/Ethno botanical, Ethno pharmacology/ Ethno pharmacological survey and Ethiopian traditional medicinal plant, mosquito repellent, insect repellent. \r\n 2.5.3. Chemicals and Instruments \r\nAll chemical used in this study will be of analytical grade unless otherwise stated. The Chromatographic separation will be performed by preparative TLC (PTLC) Silica gel 60 F254 (0.5 mm thick), Silica gel 60 (0.063–0.200 mm) will be used for column chromatography (70–230 mesh),Sephadex G-10, Sephadex LH-20. Trisodium citrate, Giemsa, pure chloroquine phosphate, tetramethylsilane, n-butanol, Hexane, Ethyl acetate, DMSO, MeOH, CDCl3, CD3OD will be used in the experiment. Spectroscopic methods will be used to characterize the structures:  NMR (1D and 2D-NMR), IR, LC‐MS, GC-MS\r\n2.5.4. Extraction and Fractionation\r\nThe air-dried powdered parts of the plant identified in the previous section (section 4.3) will be extracted by different solvents described with highest activity as described in the selected papers. The removal of the organic solvent will be attained by rotary evaporator and the water extract will be freeze dried.\r\n2.5.5. Preparative Thin Layer Chromatography for Isolation\r\nThe PTLC will be carried out using a mixture of solvent with best Rf values identified during the regular TLC evaluation.  The chromatograms will be visualized by ultraviolet light (UV) at wavelength 254 nm or chemical methods. The major band will be carefully scrapped off the plates, will be washed with a mixture of chloroform and methanol (1:1), and then filtered and concentrated to dryness under reduced pressure. \r\n2.5.6. NMR \r\nThe NMR spectra will be obtained at room temperature using 400 MHz spectrometer at 400 MHz for 1H and 100 MHz for 13C using deuterated solvents. Chemical shifts will be reported relative to the residual solvent peaks. \r\n2.5.7. Mass Spectrometry \r\nMass spectra will be recorded using the positive and negative modes on TQ Orbitrap XL mass spectrometer\r\n2.5.8. Repellence Test\r\nThe repellence will be tested by means of an arm-in-cage assay techniques 28,29, in which subjects insert their product-treated arm into a cage with a fixed number of blood unfed mosquitoes and the time elapsed to the first bite will be recorded. \r\n2.5.9. In-silico Molecular Docking Studies\r\nDocking study will be carried out on two crystal structures of Plasmodium enzymes plasmepsin II (Protein Data Bank; PDB: 4CKU) and l-lactate dehydrogenase (pfLDH) [PDB: 1LDG], using AutoDock Vina. The ligand will be separated from the protein. The polar hydrogen atoms and Kollman charges will be added to the protein, and the water molecules will be eliminated. The final file will be minimized by UCSF Chimera software and saved in PDBQT format for further analysis. \r\n2.5.10. Preparation of the Ligands\r\nThe structure of the identified compounds will be drawn by chemdraw and imported into OpenBabel software and subjected to energy minimization. The energy minimization will be performed with the universal force field (UFF) using the conjugate gradient algorithm. The minimized compounds will be transformed to PDBQT format for further analysis.\r\n\r\n2.5.11. Experimental Animals and Parasite \r\nSwiss albino mice of either sex will be used in the experiment. All the experiments will be conducted in accordance with internationally accepted laboratory animal use and care guideline 30  and will be  approved by the Institutional Review Board of Jimma University. The chloroquine- sensitive Plasmodium berghei ANKA strain will used for antimalarial assay. The parasites will be maintained in the laboratory by serial blood passage from the infected mice to the non-infected ones on a weekly basis.\r\n2.5.12. Antimalarial Activity of Extracts \r\nThe in vivo antimalarial activity of the compounds with highest binding free energy (from docking experiments) will be investigated using the classic 4-day suppressive test to evaluate the chemosuppressive effect of the isolated compounds during early infection. The percent of the inhibition of parasitemia (percent of suppression) and the survival time will be taken as parameters for the determination of activity of the test substances throughout the assay. Moreover, changes in body (rectal) temperature, and body weight loss will be recorded.','<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">1. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; WHO. Global technical strategy for malaria 2016-2030. <em>World Heal Organ</em>. Published online 2015:1-35. http://apps.who.int/iris/bitstream/10665/176712/1/9789241564991_eng.pdf?ua=1</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">2. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Phillips MA, Burrows JN, Manyando C, Van Huijsduijnen RH, Van Voorhis WC, Wells TNC. Malaria. <em>Nat Rev Dis Prim</em>. 2017;3.</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">3. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; World Health Organization. WHO Guidelines for malaria - 2021. <em>WHO Guildlines Malar</em>. 2021;1:210.</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">4. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; WHO WH organization. WORLD MALARIA REPORT 2020 Global messaging. Published online 2020:1-6.</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">5. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Al-Awadhi M, Ahmad S, Iqbal J. Current status and the epidemiology of malaria in the middle east region and beyond. <em>Microorganisms</em>. 2021;9(2):1-20.</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;,&qu