><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Amene,&nbsp; student1</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"background-color:white; border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\">&nbsp;</p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"background-color:yellow; border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\">&nbsp;</p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"background-color:yellow; border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\">&nbsp;</p>\r\n			</td>\r\n		</tr>\r\n		<tr>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:1px solid black; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top; width:519px\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Summary&nbsp; report to research &amp; Post graduate coordinating office</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top; width:104px\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Jimma</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top; width:131px\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">All </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\">&nbsp;</p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\">&nbsp;</p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"background-color:#00b050; border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\">&nbsp;</p>\r\n			</td>\r\n		</tr>\r\n		<tr>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:1px solid black; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top; width:519px\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Dissemination of research output and Publication</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top; width:104px\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">National, international </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top; width:131px\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">All </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\">&nbsp;</p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\">&nbsp;</p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"background-color:#00b050; border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\">&nbsp;</p>\r\n			</td>\r\n		</tr>\r\n	</tbody>\r\n</table>',1,NULL,NULL,NULL),(42,55,9,17,'Background: Water, sanitation and hygiene are essential for preventing and managing diseases including neglected tropical diseases which affect over 1 billion people among the poorest communities. WASH is a broad term that encompasses the facilities, behaviors, practice and services that prevent diseases caused by contact with human wastes that are the source of many infectious diseases including diarrheal diseases, schistosomiases, trachoma and ascariases infection Objective of this study will be to assess the implementation of Water, Sanitation and Hygiene towards prevention and control of Selected Neglected Tropical diseases and associated factors in Jimma Zone, Southwest, Ethiopia, 2021\r\nMethod:A community-based cross-sectional study design will be used, employing both quantitative and qualitative methods, from February 1st to March 30th, 2022 in six randomly selected districts of the Jimma Zone in southwestern Ethiopia on 844 households. Descriptive statistics will be displayed using tables and graphs, and bivariate logistic regression will be used to identify candidate variables for multivariate logistic regression. An odds ratio of 95% CI will be used to measure the strength of the association. In multivariable logistic regression analysis, 4,444 variables with a P value of 0.05 or less will be considered significant factors.\r\nBudget and work plan:the activities will be accomplished within the provided timeline by using 327,894.00 ETB',NULL,'Implementation of Water, Sanitation and Hygiene towards Prevention and Control of Selected Neglected Tropical Diseases and Associated Factors in Jimma Zone, Southwest, Ethiopia','2021-11-19 11:03:14','Implementation of the research project findings will:\r\n•	Increased personal hygiene and sanitary services utilization\r\n•	Improve knowledge in prevention of NTDs\r\n•	Strengthening community participation in NTD prevention and management efforts\r\n•	Create Safe and supportive environment to prevent NTDs\r\n•	Environmental sanitation status of all targeted community will be improved and community will declare sustain open defecation free zone\r\n•	Personnel hygiene status of the target community will be improved and the prevalence of trachoma and other communicable disease will be reduced\r\n•	Decreased incidence and prevalence of infectious diseases, neglected tropical diseases, and other WASH-related diseases','9fee774be7cb71c06f243f703e70fb43','completed','Neglected Tropical Diseases (NTD) is a group of infectious diseases found in the world\'s poorest countries in Africa, Asia and Latin America. These diseases are classified as neglected because they have not been adequately addressed by public health interventions or drug and diagnostics developers (Huang et al., 2014). Today, NTDs cause unacceptable human suffering and death in all affected regions. In sub-Saharan Africa, for example, the impact of NTDs as a whole is comparable to the global burden of both malaria and tuberculosis. Despite the terrible suffering and permanent disabilities provoked by them, they are often less visible than other diseases and receive little attention from governments and are therefore often forgotten, which is the main reason why they are called neglected (Mitra & Mawson, 2017).\r\nVarieties of intervention were made across the world to reduce the burden on NTDs with different strategic approaches. Trachoma is one from the most know NTD infections, the causative agent for trachoma is bacterial infection of the eye spread through person to person contact with contaminated hands, clothing and eye-seeking flies. Trachoma is prevalent in 1 to 9 years children, and the disease is known to have the chance of reemerging. Now adays, trachoma is a public health problem in 44 countries and accounts for 1.9 million people blindness or moderately to severely visually impaired because of trachoma and some 200 million people are confirmed to live in endemic areas that need interventions (Amare et al., 2019). \r\nWater, sanitation and hygiene are essential for preventing and managing diseases including neglected tropical diseases which affect over 1 billion people among the poorest communities. However, water, sanitation and sanitation education (WASH), attitude and behavioral changes, and infrastructure improvements can be used to achieve sustainable management, eradication, or eradication of neglected tropical diseases. (NTDs) (Herricks et al., 2017). Globally, WASH is the best strategy designed to fight NTD. Coordination and interconnection between disease control program and WASH sector is crucial to fight the burden of NTD. (WHO, 2019 ; Negussu et al., 2017  ).\r\nWASH is a broad term that encompasses the facilities, behaviors, practice and services that prevent diseases caused by contact with human wastes that are the source of many infectious diseases including diarrheal diseases, schistosomiases, trachoma and ascariases infection (Martin et al., 2018). Different sanitation facility can be utilized for breaking the transmission of these infectious disease including latrines. For illustration improper utilization of latrine leads to dramatic impact on health, economic growth, personal security, and dignity especially for women and girls and results an avoidable death or impairment (Leshargie et al., 2018).\r\nAround the world, about 2.4 billion people do not have access to proper hygiene and more than 946 defecate in the field. (UNICEF and WHO, 2015). The presence of toilets or other facilities to dispose of human feces may have an indirect positive impact on the risk of developing trachoma. Effective fly control can be achieved through latrine coverage, proper sanitation and consistent latrine utilization throughout the community. Improving environmental hygiene is important to reduce the spread of disease outbreaks. The personal hygiene of the community especially, during the childhood are recommended to control the face flies (Musca sorbens) which are important fly of trachoma transmission (Lee et al., 2007).\r\nOn the other hand improving clean and safe water for drinking purpose and access to improved sanitation a cheap way to improve public health and save lives (Maggie and Montgomery, 2007). Different study conducted on  trachoma and drinking water shows, the prevalence of active trachoma was related with utilization of water from unprotected source and  lower household water consumption (Ayelgn et al., 2021).\r\nIn addition, many cases of active trachoma were associated with personal hygiene. (Tafesse et al., 2014). Personal hygiene which comprises hand hygiene, face cleaning, clothing washing and bathing (Ogden et al., 2013; Bp et al., 2020; Churko et al., 2021). Good hygiene is an important barrier to combat the transmission the disease even though developing such practices in the community is difficult and require different approaches to come up with behavioral change (Hassan, 2012; Tidwell et al., 2020). \r\nHygiene and sanitations are needed for sustained reductions in trachoma disease burden (Stocks et al., 2014) form the SAFE (surgery to treat trichiasis (S), mass drug distribution (A), face hygiene (F), and environmental sanitation (E)) are strategies in a comprehensive WHO elimination program (Wohlgemuth, 2017; Churko et al., 2021). Specially it is well known and indisputable that handwashing and facial cleanness are crucial elements for the prevention of trachoma (Tidwell et al., 2019; Churko et al., 2021). They are feasible to apply, low in cost and simple for implementation and effective way for disease prevention (Wichaidit et al., 2019). \r\nHowever, the disease can also transmit through mechanical vector to the individuals unless there is continuous face washing following hand washing in the presence of causative agent in the surrounding environment. Hence the integration of face washing with the hand washing behavior change program is supper important (Tidwell et al., 2019). \r\nSo far there are many studies that address the effect of hand and face washing up on the prevention of trachoma cases but their integrative effect is not well studied. For example, an individual having a clean face and no visible ocular discharge is associated with a 68% reduction in risk of infection with C. trachomatis, the bacteria that causes trachoma. Regular washing of clothing and bedding with soap is also important in preventing further spread of the disease (Ogden et al., 2013).\r\nTherefore, the purpose of this study is to evaluate i) the use of toilets and related factors as a bridge to trachoma prevention ii) safe water availability and associated factors iii) the condition and importance of proper personal hygiene (hand and face washing) & integration of face washing with the hand washing behavioral change program for the prevention and elimination of trachoma case.','4.1 Study area and period\r\nThe survey will be conducted from February 1st to March 30th, 2022 in six randomly selected districts of the Jimma Zone in southwestern Ethiopia. The Jimma Zone is one of the Oromia zones, 356 km southwest of Addis Ababa, the capital of Ethiopia. There are 21 Districts with a total of 561 Kebeles in Jimma Zone. Concerning health facilities, there was one tertiary hospital, 2 General Hospitals, 6 primary hospitals, 122 health centers, and 513 health posts in the Zone.\r\n4.2 Study design\r\nA community-based cross-sectional study design will be used, employing both quantitative and qualitative methods, to undertake this study. The qualitative method will be used to support the findings of quantitative results in order to explore and understand what will be behind mere quantitative figures.\r\n4.3 Source population\r\nFor quantitative part, all households in the zone will be considered as source population.  For qualitative part WASH focal person, Health Developmental Army and Health Extension workers will be considered as a source population.\r\n4.4 Study populations\r\nFor the quantitative part, all households in the zone that can meet the selection criteria. For qualitative part WASH focal person, Health Developmental Army and Health Extension workers will be the study population. \r\n4.5 Inclusion and exclusion criteria\r\nInclusion: People over 18 years old who have lived in Kebele for more than 6 months.\r\nExclusion:People who are seriously ill during data collection will be excluded from the study.\r\n4.6 Sample size determination and sampling procedures\r\nSample size determination\r\nSingle population formula will be used assuming 95% confidence interval and 50% proportion (P) due to lack of such study, and a precision of 5% between the sample and the 10% non- response rate is taken. The formula for calculating the sample size (n) is:  n= [(z1-α/2)2p(1-p)]/d2 the sample size will be 422 adding 10% non-response and design effect of 2 the final sample size will be 844. \r\nParticipant Recruitment for Qualitative Study\r\nThe participants for key informant interview will be recruited by considering individuals who have depth information about WASH such as WASH focal person, Health Developmental Army and Health Extension workers. \r\nSampling procedures for quantitative study\r\nDistricts (primary level) and Kebeles (secondary level) will be selected in a multi-stage sampling process. Six (30%) districts will be randomly selected from the zone. From each selected district, 30% of Kebeles will be randomly selected. The total number of households will be determined by each selected Kebele and will be distributed in proportion to the Kebele. Next, assign the number of participants in proportion to the number of households. Individual households will be identified using a systematic random sample. After the total number of households is determined, the interval \"K\" is calculated. Random start/the first household will be selected by lottery method and then the study participant (HH) will be selected by every Kthinterval.\r\nSampling Technique for Qualitative study\r\nPurposive/Judgmental sampling technique will be employed in order to select key informants who will be able to provide detailed information about WASH. The investigators judged to include the key informants based on their experiences and practice.\r\n4.7 Study variable\r\nDependent variable\r\n•	Prevalence of trachoma\r\n•	Availability and utilization of Latrine\r\n•	Availability and microbial quality of safe water\r\n•	Personal hygienic practice.  \r\nIndependent variable\r\n•	Socio demographic characteristics (age, marriage status, education level, occupation, monthly income).\r\n•	Housing conditions\r\n4.8 Data collection tools and personnel\r\nData will be collected using semi-structured interviewer administered questionnaire that will be used from different literatures. The questionnaire will be translated into AfanOlomo by a language expert before the data is collected. Data will be collected by health professionals who have a good experience so far and they will be supervised during the data collection process. Interviewer guide (probes) with open ended questions for qualitative developed after reviewing different literatures and will be translated into Afan Oromo language before data collection. Observation checklist and FGD guide will be used for qualitative parts. Different chemicals and reagents, sample collection materials and laboratory setup will be adjusted before data collection for microbial quality analysis of water sample.\r\n4.9 Data Quality Control\r\nTo assure the data quality high emphasis will be given in designing data collection tool. Surveys are first written in English and then translated into Afan Oromo\'s language for ease of understanding by respondents. Structured data collection tool will be utilized. The effectiveness and clarity of the tool has been tested on 5% of individuals. The required correction will be employed accordingly. Two days of training will be provided to data collectors and supervisors on the purpose of the survey, the method of data collection, the tools and importance of the survey. During data collection, each data collector\'s issues and directions will be monitored and necessary corrections will be made. The integrity of the collected questionnaires will be checked and both the data collector and the manager take corrective action in the field. Daily meetings will be held between data collectors, supervisors, and chief auditors to discuss potential issues and assess the progress of data collection. Pretests will be performed on 5% of the total sample before data is collected to ensure that how our respondent react to the tool and make necessary adjustment as needed. Water samples will be collected through sterile glass and transported to the laboratory in a cool box for analysis within a day. The chemicals and reagents used in microbial analysis will be that of analytically grade.\r\n4.10 Data processing and analysis\r\nThe collected data will be checked, coded, entered into epidata and exported to SPSS version 23 for analysis. Descriptive statistics will be displayed using tables and graphs, and bivariate logistic regression will be used to identify candidate variables for multivariate logistic regression. Candidate variables will be entered into multivariate logistic regression analysis. An odds ratio of 95% CI will be used to measure the strength of the association. In multivariable logistic regression analysis, 4,444 variables with a P value of 0.05 or less will be considered significant factors.\r\nFor qualitative part;the tape-recorded data will be transcribed and translated word by word by the Principal Investigator. Then, thematic analysis approaches will be used and the principal investigator code, categorize and thematize the data by using Atlas ti software version 7.0.71.\r\n4.11 Dissemination of finding\r\nThe findings will be presented to Jimma University scientific community and the result will be submitted to Jimma University research office. The results will also be communicated to zones, districts and other affected organizations so that research recommendations can be incorporated and applied into the planning process. Attempts will be made to publish in peer-reviewed national and international journals.','<p style=\"margin-left:32px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">Amare, A., Worku, T., Ashagirie, B., Adugna, M., &amp; Getaneh, A. (2019). Bacteriological profile , antimicrobial susceptibility patterns of the isolates among street vended foods and hygienic practice of vendors in Gondar town , Northwest Ethiopia : a cross sectional study. <em>BMC Microbiology</em>, <em>19</em>(120), 1&ndash;9.</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">Ayelgn, K., Guadu, T., &amp; Getachew, A. (2021). Low prevalence of active trachoma and associated factors among children aged 1 &ndash; 9 years in rural communities of Metema District , Northwest Ethiopia : a community based cross-sectional study. <em>Italian Journal of Pediatrics</em>, <em>47</em>(114), 1&ndash;8.</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:32px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">Bp, S. K., Reddy, M. A., Paul, P., Das, L., Jc, D., Kurian, B. P., Ghosh, S., &amp; BN, R. (2020). Importance of understanding the need of personal hygiene : A comprehensive review. <em>International Journal of Research in Pharmacy and Pharmaceutical Sciences</em>, <em>5</em>(6), 56&ndash;61.</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:32px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">Churko, C., Asfaw, M. A., &amp; Zerdo, Z. (2021). Knowledge , Attitude , Practices and Associated Factors Towards Trachoma Among People Living in Arba Minch Zuria District , Gamo Zone , Southern Ethiopia. <em>Clinical Ophthalmology</em>, <em>15</em>, 3075&ndash;3085.</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:32px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">Hassan, B. A. R. (2012). Importance of Personal Hygiene. <em>Pharmaceutica Analytica Acta</em>, <em>3</em>(8), 4172. https://doi.org/10.4172/2153-2435.1000e126</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:32px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span 