 Ferede, A., Sintayehu, M., Dessie, G., Boneya, D. J., Birhanu, M. Y., &amp; Kibret, G. D. (2018). Household latrine utilization and its association with educational status of household heads in Ethiopia: A systematic review and meta-analysis. <em>BMC Public Health</em>, <em>18</em>(1), 1&ndash;12. https://doi.org/10.1186/s12889-018-5798-6</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:32px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">Maggie A. Montgomery, M. E. (2007). in Developing. In <em>ENviRoNmENtAl SCiENCE &amp; tEChNologY</em>.</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:32px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">Martin, N. A., Hulland, K. R. S., Dreibelbis, R., Sultana, F., &amp; Winch, P. J. (2018). Sustained adoption of water, sanitation and hygiene interventions: systematic review. <em>Tropical Medicine and International Health</em>, <em>23</em>(2), 122&ndash;135. https://doi.org/10.1111/tmi.13011</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:32px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">Mitra, A. K., &amp; Mawson, A. R. (2017). Neglected tropical diseases: Epidemiology and global burden. <em>Tropical Medicine and Infectious Disease</em>, <em>2</em>(3). https://doi.org/10.3390/tropicalmed2030036</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:32px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">Ogden, S., Gallo, K., Davis, S., McGuire, C., Meyer, E., Addiss, D., &amp; Haddad, D. (2013). <em>WASH and the Neglected Tropical Diseases: A manual for WASH implementers</em>.</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:32px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">Stocks, M. E., Ogden, S., Haddad, D., Addiss, D. G., Mcguire, C., &amp; Freeman, M. C. (2014). Effect of Water , Sanitation , and Hygiene on the Prevention of Trachoma : A Systematic Review and Meta- Analysis. <em>PLoS Medicine</em>, <em>11</em>(2). https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pmed.1001605</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:32px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">Tafesse, F., Desse, G., Bacha, K., &amp; Alemayehu, H. (2014). Microbiological quality and safety of street vended raw meat in Jijiga town of Somali Regional State, southeast Ethiopia. <em>African Journal of Microbiology Research</em>, <em>8</em>(48), 3867&ndash;3874. https://doi.org/10.5897/AJMR2014.7136</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:32px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">Tidwell, J. B., Fergus, C., Gopalakrishnan, A., Sheth, E., Sidibe, M., Wohlgemuth, L., Jain, A., &amp; Woods, G. (2019). Integrating Face Washing into a School-Based , Handwashing Behavior Change Program to Prevent Trachoma in Turkana , Kenya. <em>Am. J. Trop. Med. Hyg.</em>, <em>101</em>(4), 767&ndash;773. https://doi.org/10.4269/ajtmh.19-0205</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:32px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">Tidwell, J. B., Gopalakrishnan, A., Unni, A., Sheth, E., Daryanani, A., Singh, S., &amp; Sidibe, M. (2020). <em>Impact of a teacher-led school handwashing program on children &rsquo; s handwashing with soap at school and home in Bihar , India</em>. PLoS ONE. https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0229655</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:32px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">WHO. (2019). <em>WASH and Health working together</em>.</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:32px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">Wichaidit, W., Steinacher, R., Okal, J. A., Whinnery, J., Null, C., Kordas, K., Yu, J., Pickering, A. J., &amp; Ram, P. K. (2019). Effect of an equipment-behavior change intervention on handwashing behavior among primary school children in Kenya : the Povu Poa school pilot study. <em>BMC Public Health</em>, <em>19</em>(647), 1&ndash;12.</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:32px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">Wohlgemuth, L. (2017). From clean hands to clean faces: Working with the WASH sector to end trachoma. <em>The International Coalition for Trachoma Control</em>.</span></span></span></span></p>','To assess Water, sanitation and hygiene implementation towards prevention and controlling of selected neglected tropical diseases and related factors in Jimma Zone, southwestern Ethiopia',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,2,'Jimma zone, NTD,Trachoma,water,hygiene,sanitation',10,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),(43,160,9,26,'Project summary\r\nBackground: The term workaholism was first coined by Oates, which is non-chemical addictions of the 21st century, a multifaceted, fame-gaining concept in modern times defined as “the tendency towards an excessive interest in work and stimulates irrational motivation to invest most of one’s time and effort to work, which impairs other important life activities” and think about work anytime and anywhere. This project aims to assess workaholism and its outcomes for the efficiency and effectiveness of health care organizations.\r\nApproaches and Methods: The study will be conducted in Jimma Zone public health facilities, and the study will be conducted from January to December 2022, using a Cross-sectional, facility-based research design using both quantitative and qualitative methods. The study population will be all health care professionals working in Jimma zone public facilities. The final sample size will be determined based on the objectives of the project for each research question, using a Proportionate stratified simple random sampling technique will be employed. The population of interest for FGD and In-depth interviews will be purposively selected. A valid and reliable tool will be developed or adopted according to the research questions. Senior health care professionals will be recruited to conduct quantitative data collection. Qualitative study and supervision will be conducted by MSc, Mph, or MD professionals. The data will be entered into Epi data manager 4.6 statistical software and then exported to SPSS version 25.0. descriptive statistics and inferential analysis will be used according to the nature of the data and variables. \r\nBudget and work plan: The time frame is from January to December 2022, and the budget will be 412,500 ETH birr.','Workaholism','Workaholism and its outcomes -A 21st Century addiction among Health-Care Professionals in Jimma zone public Health facilities','2021-11-19 10:48:24','⮚	Knowledge will be generated on the level of health care professionals workaholism\r\n⮚	Evidence will be generated to what extent workaholism affect work-related outcomes \r\n⮚	Evidence will be generated to what extent workaholism affects the quality of Performance','ef494cef98ac382db76e6ece3397d557','completed','Work is a central component in life that satisfies the fundamental needs of humans, including survival, relatedness to others, and self-determination. However, some people are seemingly driven by internal and external forces to work excessively and compulsively (1,2).\r\nThe term workaholism was first coined by Oates, which is non-chemical addictions of the 21st century, a multifaceted, fame-gaining concept in modern times defined as “the tendency towards an excessive interest in work and stimulates irrational motivation to invest most of one’s time and effort to work, which impairs other important life activities” and think about work anytime and anywhere (2–4).\r\nIn recent years there has been increasing attention towards the phenomenon of workaholism which may lead to positive outcomes and negative outcomes such as professional expertise, career satisfaction, better career prospects, stress, burnout, guilt and anxiety, emotional instability, irritability, negative feeling,  relationship problem, interpersonal relations, sleep disorder, less autonomous, higher commitment and retention, work-family conflict, work-life conflict,  poor coping abilities, low job, and life satisfaction, personal happiness, mental distress, attention deficit hyperactivity disorder, work-related obsessive-compulsive disorder, and depression, emotionally or cognitively exhausted, incivility, productivity, poor mental and physical health, disillusion, impulse-control disorder, and more significantly its negative effects on workaholics’ families(2,5–12). \r\nAny organization\'s survival depends heavily on the people employed inside the organization. These workers \' feelings, thought, attitudes, and actions have a far-reaching influence on whether the organization can accomplish its goals and objectives (13,14). \r\nIn recent decades, there has been an increasing body of literature that focuses on workaholism due to now day changes in the global economy, such as Restructuring, globalization, diversity, competitiveness, downsizing, re-engineering, aging populations and environmental degradation have led to the organizational realization that in the 21st century, where workers have become demoralized, alienated and unable to cope with the compartmentalized nature of their jobs, existing systems and policies are no longer sufficient(15).\r\nThe modern organization is facing employee-related issues such as stress, burnout, work-family conflict, work-life conflict, inadequate coping skills, low job and life satisfaction, mental illness, incivility, poor mental and physical health, violence, and corruption are tackled by the modern company. There is a common perception that for organizations to succeed in the 21st century, their representatives and workers need to knock into their work environment, which enhances organizational performance, profitability and effectiveness directly and indirectly (16,17).\r\nThe modern organization is facing employee-related issues such as stress, burnout, work-family conflict, work-life conflict, inadequate coping skills, low job and life satisfaction, mental illness, incivility, poor mental and physical health, violence, and corruption are tackled by the modern company. There is a common perception that for organizations to succeed in the 21st century, their representatives and workers need to knock into their work environment, which enhances organizational performance, profitability, and effectiveness directly and indirectly','Description of approaches/Methods\r\nStudy setting\r\nThe study will be conducted in Jimma Zone. The zone is divided into 21 districts and one town administration. There are Eight hospitals (one referral and teaching hospital, one general hospital, and six primary hospitals), 115 health centers, and 520 health posts in the Jimma zone. There are more than 2500 health care workers of all types in Jimma zone public health facilities.\r\nStudy design: \r\nCross-sectional, facility-based research design using both quantitative and qualitative methods.\r\nPopulation\r\nThe study population will be all health care professionals (Physicians, Nurses, Midwives, laboratories, pharmacies, and others) working in Jimma zone public hospital, southwest Ethiopia.\r\nSample \r\nFor a quantitative study, the final sample size will be determined based on the objectives of the project for each research question.  For qualitative study Hospital administrators, directors, or representatives of all types of professions will be involved and the sample size will be determined based on data saturation. \r\nSampling procedure\r\nA proportionate stratified simple random sampling technique will be deployed to select study participants from Health facilities according to their professional category. The population of interest for FGD and In-depth interviews will be purposively selected. \r\nInstrument\r\nTo assess the level of the Workaholism scale will be utilized and for each specific objective, a valid and reliable tool will be developed or adopted according to the research questions.  \r\nData collection procedure\r\nSenior health care professionals will be recruited to conduct quantitative data collection. Qualitative study and supervision will be conducted by MSc, Mph, or MD professionals.\r\nData Analysis.\r\nThe data will be entered into Epi data manager 4.6 statistical software and the generated data will be exported to SPSS version 25.0 and will be cleaned by calculating frequencies and sorting. descriptive statistics including mean and standard deviation (SD) will be used to describe the sample. Finally, the inferential analysis will be done according to the nature of the data and variables. \r\n  Quality assurance\r\nTo assure the quality first valid tool will be utilized. Three days of training including the pretest will be given to data collectors and supervisors on the objectives of the study, data collection tools, and research ethics. Supervision will be conducted by supervisors and research teams to monitor the overall data collection process. \r\nPlan for dissemination and ensuring utilization of findings \r\nThe findings of this will be disseminated to governmental and non-governmental stakeholders and policymakers. Finally, it will be submitted for publication in a peer-reviewed journal. \r\nCapacity building (inclusion of Ph.D. and/or MSc students)\r\nThree MPH / MSc students will be included\r\nEthical considerations\r\nEthical clearance will be obtained from the Institutional Review Board of Jimma University, Institute of health. Ethical issues within the study will be taken into consideration when carrying out the study. The purpose, merit, and demerits of the study will be explained and informed consent will be obtained from study participants. \r\nproject work plan\r\nTable 1: Project work plan of Workaholism and its outcomes -A 21st Century addiction among Health-Care Professionals in Jimma zone public Health facilities, 2021/2.\r\nActivity	Oct	Nov	Dec	Jan	Feb	Mar	Apr	May	June	July 	Aug 	Sep\r\nPreparation and submission of the concept note, and proposal												\r\nApproval by reviewers												\r\nSecuring funds												\r\nData collector selection, Pre-test, data collection												\r\nData entry and analysis												\r\nReport writing and submission												\r\nDissemination to stakeholders												\r\nSubmission to peer review journal												\r\n\r\nProposed budget\r\nTable 2. The proposed budget of Workaholism and its outcomes -A 21st Century addiction among Health-Care Professionals in Jimma zone public Health facilities, 2021/2.\r\nS.N	List of items	Total price(Birr)	Remark\r\n1	Human resource cost 	300,000	\r\n2	Cost of supplies, stationeries, and transportation	75,000	\r\n3	Contingency (10%)	37,500	\r\n	Total 	412,500','<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">1. Ravoux H, Pereira B, Brousse G, Dewavrin S, Cornet T, Mermillod M, et al. Work addiction test questionnaire to assess workaholism: Validation of French version. J Med Internet Res. 2018;20(2). </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">2. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Raphael Snir D, Schou Andreassen C, Hetland J, Pallesen S. Psychometric assessment of workaholism measures. J Manag Psychol. 2013;29(1):7&ndash;24. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">3. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Griffiths MD. Workaholism -A 21st-century addiction. 2018;(November). </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">4. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Andreassen CS, Griffiths MD, Hetland J, Kravina L, Jensen F, Pallesen S. The prevalence of workaholism: A survey study in a nationally representative sample of Norwegian employees. PLoS One. 2014;9(8):1&ndash;10. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">5. &nbsp;&nbsp;&