yle=\"background-color:#f2dbdb; height:15px; vertical-align:top; width:105px\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:left\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"><span style=\"color:black\">10,000.00</span></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n		</tr>\r\n		<tr>\r\n			<td colspan=\"6\" style=\"height:16px; vertical-align:top; width:507px\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:left\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">Data collection and supervision costs</span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"height:16px; vertical-align:top; width:102px\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:left\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">200,000.00</span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n		</tr>\r\n		<tr>\r\n			<td colspan=\"6\" style=\"background-color:#f2dbdb; height:20px; vertical-align:top; width:507px\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:left\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"><span style=\"color:black\">Total Stationary Materials Budget</span></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"background-color:#f2dbdb; height:20px; vertical-align:top; width:102px\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:left\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"><span style=\"color:black\">10,000.00</span></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n		</tr>\r\n		<tr>\r\n			<td colspan=\"6\" style=\"height:14px; vertical-align:top; width:507px\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:left\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">Logistic and transport cost</span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"height:14px; vertical-align:top; width:102px\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:left\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">40,000.00</span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n		</tr>\r\n		<tr>\r\n			<td colspan=\"6\" style=\"background-color:#f2dbdb; height:15px; vertical-align:top; width:507px\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:left\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"><span style=\"color:black\">Total cost</span></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"background-color:#f2dbdb; height:15px; vertical-align:top; width:102px\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:left\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"><span style=\"color:black\">280,000.00</span></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n		</tr>\r\n		<tr>\r\n			<td colspan=\"6\" style=\"height:15px; vertical-align:top; width:507px\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:left\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">Contingency cost 10%</span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"height:15px; vertical-align:top; width:102px\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:left\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">28,000.00</span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n		</tr>\r\n		<tr>\r\n			<td colspan=\"7\" style=\"background-color:#f2dbdb; height:14px; vertical-align:top; width:508px\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:left\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"><span style=\"color:black\">Grand Total cost in<strong> </strong>ETB</span></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"background-color:#f2dbdb; height:14px; vertical-align:top; width:101px\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:left\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"><span style=\"color:black\">308,000.00</span></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n		</tr>\r\n	</tbody>\r\n</table>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:left\">&nbsp;</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),(46,31,9,19,'Bacterial infections due to multiple drug resistance (MDR) strains are major global health security problem that is a significant cause of morbidity and mortality in developing countries (1). Multidrug resistance was defined as acquired resistance to at least one agent in three or more antimicrobial categories (2). Bacterial adherence and biofilm formation on surfaces has many serious consequences for public health as well and is a major virulence factor contributing to the chronicity of infections (3).\r\nIn Ethiopia, like other developing countries, it is difficult to evaluate the burden of bacterial pathogens due to multiple antimicrobial resistance because of the limited scope of studies and lack of coordinated epidemiological surveillance systems (1,4). On the other hand, there is a critical need to search and study other alternative antimicrobial agents such as bacteriophages: bacterial viruses.\r\nTherefore the aim of the present study is to isolate and characterize bacteriophages that can distract multidrug-resistant Pseudomonas aeruginosa, Acinetobacter baumannii, and Klebsiella pneumoniae in planktonic form as well as in biofilm condition. These bacteria is planned to be isolated from ill cases in Jimma Medical Center ver a specified time. Specific bacteriophages against the bacteria will be recovered from wastewater (sewage) sources from within and outside of the hospital compounds following standard protocol (5,6). Characterization of the bacteriophages will be done including adsorption rate, pH and heat stability, one-step growth curve, latent time, burst size, structural proteins, and genome size. The working team shall make effort to communicate abroad laboratories inorder to visualize bacteriophages in a more detailed technicque including transmission electron microscopy which is vital for classifying them.  \r\nIn this study, various clinical samples will be collected from a total of 382 cases and will be investigated for Pseudomonas aeruginosa, Acinetobacter baumannii, and Klebsiella pneumoniae using standard bacteriological methods (7). Furhtermore, their antimicrobial susceptibility profile will be performed using disk diffusion method (8). It is assumed that the research approach can incite novel and effective bacteriophages candidate isolation for future use in the prevention and control strategy against MDR bacteria.','Lytic bacteriophages against multidrug resistant Pseudomonas, Klebsiella and Acinetobacter in Jimma.','In vitro assessment of Bacteriophage Isolates against Multidrug Resistant Pseudomonas aeuruginosa, Klebsiella pneumoniae and Acinetobacter baumanni at Jimma Medical Center','2021-11-19 10:59:11','1. Brings information to the health professionals and other scholars that even multiple drug resistant bacteria can be destructed or treated with a natural remedy that can be brought out from the activity of bacteriophages  \r\n2.Brings direction and technique to isolate bacteriophages for other resistant bacteria to drugs, disinfections, or physical conditions.','b01ff2e59238cfc482b332a7ce8ddefb','completed','Bacterial infections and associated multiple drug resistance (MDR) features are a major global health security crisis as they are becoming the causes of increased morbidity and mortality in many developing countries (1,2). MDR is defined as acquired bacterial resistance to at least one agent in three or more antimicrobial categories (3). The world recognized ESKAPE (Enterococcus faecium, Staphylococcus aureus, Klebsiella pneumonia, Acinetobacter baumannii, Pseudomonas aeruginosa, and Enterobacter species) pathogens, as a documented antimicrobial resistant strains within healthcare facilites worldwide, and they are critically significant in costing human life, and bringing financial pressure (4,5). In Ethiopia, like other developing countries, it is difficult to evaluate the burden of bacterial pathogens due to antimicrobial drug resistance in the clinical settings, related to burden on morbidity, mortality, cost it incur, etc, though several small scale studies showed the increased prevalence of MDR bacteria specially gram negatives including Psuedomonas aeruginosa, Klebsiella pneumoniae and Acinetobacter baumannii (1–3,6–10). World Health Organization (WHO) in 2017 has published lists of bacteria with a ‘critical’ priority for which new antibiotics are urgently needed. The three listed bacteria are carbapenem-resistant Acinetobacter baumanni, Psuedomonas aeruginosa, and members of Enterobactericeae (Gram negative enteric bacteria) including Klebsiella pneumoniae (5). Specifically, the burden of these bacteria in the medical facility setting as a cause of nosocomial infection as well as their distribution around patient environments were not highlighted because of limited scope of studies, lack of culture facilities along with technical competency to identify and lack of coordinated epidemiological surveillance systems in Ethiopia.\r\nSince new antimicrobial drugs development are at their sluggish pace in the past over two decades, there is an urgent need to search and develop for alternative agents worldwide (11).  In this regard, bacteriophages, which are bacterial viruses, are one of the economical gifts of nature which are found abundantly in most environments. They are studied as an alternative agent of therapy from inanimate or animate surfaces (12). These bacterial viruses exist in various (clean or effluent) water sources. They are said to be numerically the most prominent biological entities on earth, with an estimated population size of 1030 to 1031 virus particles. This absolute count is said to be 10 or more times higher than the total bacterial counts on the planet Earth (12).\r\nThe aim of the present study is therefore to assess MDR P. aeruginosa, A. baumannii, and Klebsiella pneumoniae from clinical as well as hospital environmental samples by use of standard microbiological methods (13,14). Thus, these bacteria will be used as a host to isolate and characterize specific bacteriophages that will destruct the bacteria by lysis. This study will attempt to recover novel candidates usable in effective therapeutic and in infection prevention and control strategy against MDR P. aeruginosa, A. baumannii, and Klebsiella pneumoniae bacteria so as to reduce healthcare assocated infections.\r\nThe main driver to search & develop novel therapeutic option for the MDR bacteria such as P. aeruginosa, A. baumannii, and Klebsiella pneumoniae infections are the declining efficacy of conventional antimicrobial therapies. Furthermore, the bacteria advance progressively to of become resistant to numerous antimicrobial agents is likely to continue. These bacteria resistance is also known to other different disinfectants and weak bleaching agents used in healthcare facilities (6,15). P. aeruginosa, A. baumannii, and Klebsiella pneumoniae have resistance rate that vary in different geographical areas of the world with time. As a result, continuous inspection of healthcare facilities is needed to understand the distribution of the bacteria near to patient’s environment of Jimma Medical Center (JMC) as well as their level of antimicrobial resistance.  \r\nThe resultant knowledge about viruses attacking Psuedomonas aeruginosa, Klebsiella pneumoniae and Acinetobacter baumannii in the JMC setting will help to develop alternative biological control methods using bacteriophages in the future. This process will be one of its first kinds to study in Jimma in particular and in Ethiopia in general. As such, isolation of virulent bacteriophages from different sources against MDR Psuedomonas aeruginosa, Klebsiella pneumoniae and Acinetobacter baumannii is not a complex protocol. However, handling and detail characterization of candidate bacteriophages are something that are required to prepare them for optimal utilization and treatment of inanimate objects at laboratory experimental setting and later at healthcare facility levels in the future where those bacterial strains are widespread (16,17). \r\nThis study approach can be an economical direction to limit the occurrence of nosocomial or healthcare associated infections in Jimma. Furthermore, this study approach and protocols can be easily taken high to advance on other similar prevalent drug resistant bacteria important in different infections in different healthcare facilities in Ethiopia.','Study Area and Period\r\nThe study will be conducted in Jimma town specifically in Jimma Medical Center. The space for the study will be at Microbiology Laboratory of the hospital and department of medical laboratory sciences, Jimma University. Jimma town is located 350 kms southwest of Addis Ababa. The total projected population of Jimma town from 2007 central statistical agency (CSA) census report is 120,960, of whom 60,824 are males and 60,136 females. The current population may be double of these figures. The town has two governmental hospitals and four health centers which provides health care services for Jimma. Jimma Medical Center (JMC) is one of the oldest public hospitals in the country. It was established in 1930 E.C during the Italian occupation for the service of their soldiers. It became the only teaching and referral hospital in the southwestern part of the country. It provides services for approximately 17,500 inpatient and 164,000 outpatient attendances in a year.  It has a bed capacity of 523 and a total of more than 1461 staffs of both supportive and professionals (https://www.ju.edu.et/jimma-university-specialized-hospital-JMC). This study is planned to be under taken from October 2016 to July, 2017. \r\nStudy design \r\nCross-sectional study design will be employed in all bacterial and their specific bacteriophage isolation whereas experimental study design will be employed to identify and characterize efficient candidate phage against the MDR bacteria in this study.\r\nPopulation 	\r\nSource population\r\nAll patients attending at Jimma Medical Center for bacterial isolation and Jimma town sewage points.\r\nStudy population\r\nInpatients and patient environments of JMC and sewage (waste water) points of the center. \r\nInclusion and exclusion criteria\r\nInclusion criteria:-\r\n•	All inpatients specimens requested for culture and willing to participate will be included.\r\n•	Selected and highly contaminated and polluted waste water sources from sluggish streams in Jimma town and from JMC sewage box points will be included.\r\nExclusion criteria\r\nPatients refused to allow their samples to be included in the study will be excluded. \r\nFor the environmental samples, area with unlikely contaminated sources by human or animal wastes will be excluded.\r\nSample size determination: \r\na.	Patients \r\nThe minimum sample size of patients for isolation, and  identification of the bacteria (P.a, K. pn, & A.b) is calculated using single population proportion formula, considering the frequency of the frequent infections from urinary tract infected cases (P) of  46 %  from our own 3 years retrospective study (unpublished), with 95% confidence level and 5% margin of error\r\n \r\nN = eggs which should be sampled\r\nZ = the standard normal deviation corresponding, 95% of    Confidence level   = 1.96\r\nP = prevalence (46%)\r\nd = the degree of accuracy desired (0.05)\r\n \r\n  N = 382 sample size.\r\nDifferent equipment at the patient environment including bed side table, bed frame, wall bulb switch, IV stand, oxygen control valve, locker handle circuits of mechanical ventilation, ventilator screen, ventilator screen button and pulse oximetry will be included to isolate the bacteria.\r\nSample size for isolation and identification of bacteriophages against the two bacterial genera will depend on assessing from five sluggish stream sources that have human and animal excreta contamination in the town will be used to collect as well as another five sewage samples from Jimma University main campus and The University Hospital septic points will be used by convineint sampling method.\r\nData Collection \r\nDemographic data (Patients)\r\nDemographic information will be collected using semi-structured questionnaire (Annexed). Trained health workers (nurses) will be used to collect the data. \r\nLaboratory data\r\na. Isolation and identification of the bacteria: - All type of specimens will be inoculated on recommended agar media. After incubation for 24 to 48 hrs at 370C aerobically, the plates will be examined, and bacterial species will be identified following standard procedure (7, 26). \r\nb.	Isolation of bacteriophages;- \r\nIsolation of bacteriophages specific against E. coli and Klebsiella species will be carried out from sewage/wastewater sample according to the standard protocol described by Twest and Kropinski (27). Sewage samples will be collected in sterile 200ml containers from randomly selected points. The samples collected will be processed by the enrichment method in Medical Microbiology Laboratory of Jimma University main campus. \r\nBriefly, 50 mL of each sample will be centrifuged to remove particulate materials. The supernatants will be filter sterilized by passing through a 0.45-micrometer membrane filter. The filtrate (50 ml) along with log phase grown bacterial strains will then be mixed with equal volume of sterile double strength Luria/Nutrient broth in a Erlenmeyer flask. The flask will be incubated with the indicator (host) strains while shaking. Next day, the mixture will be centrifuged and the supernatant containing phage will be passed through a 0.45 micrometer pore membrane filter under aseptic condition. The pellet will be discarded whereas the filtered will be held for further assessment of bacteriophage activity or enrichment. Then after whenever there is phage activity, phage characterization will be done according to Adams 1959 (5).\r\nAntimicrobial susceptibility Testing\r\nAntimicrobial susceptibility of the isolates will be done using Kirby disk diffusion techniques according to Clinical Laboratory Standards Institute (CLSI) guidelines (28).\r\nBriefly:- From a pure culture 3-5 selected colonies of bacteria will be  taken by using a sterile inoculation loop and transferred to a tube containing 5 ml sterile normal saline and  will be mixed gently to a homogenous suspension and the turbidity of the suspension will be  adjusted to a McFarland standard 0.5. A sterile cotton swab will be dipped into the suspension and the excess suspension will be removed by gentle rotation of the swab against the surface of the tube. The swab will be then streaked evenly over the entire surface of Mueller Hinton agar.\r\nThe inoculated plates will be left at room temperature to dry for 3-5 minutes and antibiotics discs will be placed using sterile forceps in an equidistant fashion on the surface of a plate. Various antimicrobial drugs will be used. The plates will be incubated at 37oC for 24 hours. A diameter of the zone of inhibition around the disc will be measured to the nearest millimetre using a calliper.  The isolates will be classified as sensitive, intermediate and resistant according to the standardized table supplied by the Clinical and Laboratory Standards Institute (28). E. coli (ATCC 25922) and P. aeruginosa (ATCC 27853) that will be sensitive to all antimicrobials will be used for quality control. \r\nAdherence and Biofilm formation testing\r\nAll the isolates will be preserved in 15% glycerol broth and stored at -200C for in vitro biofilm formation evaluation. The isolated organisms will be tested for their ability to form biofilm, according to the method described by Stepanovic and his coworkers as well as Christensen et al. (18,19) using flat-bottomed 96-well clear polystyrene micro-titer plate and measuring optical density which is proportional to the concentration/ weight of the bacteria adhered using automatic ELISA reader. \r\nData management and statistical analysis\r\nThe data will be checked for completeness, and then entered into Microsoft Excel software for edition, cleaning and then exported to Origin Pro 8 Software for analysis and graph. Participants’ demographic information will be described by using descriptive statistics. During the data analysis different percentage, frequency, comparisons and summarization will be done. Bivariate and multivariate logistic regression analysis will be done to identify risk factors for multiple drug resistant as well as the bacterial infection provides difference in magnitude among inpatients and their environment. Students t-test will be used to estimate the mean ± Standard Deviation for bacteriophage types and biocontrol experiments. P-value less than 0.05 will be taken as statistically significant at 95% confidence level.\r\nQuality assurance \r\nTo ensure the quality of data different quality control activities will be involved, including Pre-testing of questionnaire, standardization of procedures and providing training for data collectors and periodic supervision will be conducted. Sterility check will be performed to avoid the possibility of contamination. All reagents will be checked for their expiry date and prepared according to the manufacturer’s instruction. The data will be   kept for rechecking and 5% of the tests will be repeated randomly to check the accuracy and reliability of the working procedure and performance of the data collectors to minimize technical and observer bias.  One-day training will be given for data collectors.','<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Bookman Old Style&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">1. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Founou RC, Founou LL, Essack SY. Clinical and economic impact of antibiotic resistance in developing countries: A systematic review and meta-analysis. PLoS One [Internet]. 2017 Dec 21 [cited 2020 Sep 1];12(12). Available from: https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC5739407/</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Bookman Old Style&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">2. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; World Health Organization. Antimicrobial resistance: global report on surveillance [Internet]. Geneva: World Health Organization; 2014. Available from: https://apps.who.int/iris/handle/10665/112642</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Bookman Old Style&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">3. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Magiorakos A-P, Srinivasan A, Carey RB, Carmeli Y, Falagas ME, Giske CG, et al. Multidrug-resistant, extensively drug-resistant and pandrug-resistant bacteria: an international expert proposal for interim standard definitions for acquired resistance. Clin Microbiol Infect. 2012 Mar;18(3):268&ndash;81. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Bookman Old Style&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">4. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; De Oliveira DMP, Forde BM, Kidd TJ, Harris PNA, Schembri MA, Beatson SA, et al. Antimicrobial Resistance in ESKAPE Pathogens. Clin Microbiol Rev [Internet]. 2020 May 13 [cited 2021 Oct 16];33(3):e00181-19. Available from: https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC7227449/</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Bookman Old Style&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">5. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; WHO. WHO publishes list of bacteria for which new antibiotics are urgently needed [Internet]. 2017 [cited 2021 Oct 15]. Available from: https://www.who.int/news/item/27-02-2017-who-publishes-list-of-bacteria-for-which-new-antibiotics-are-urgently-needed</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Bookman Old Style&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">6. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Awoke T, Teka B, Seman A, Sebre S, Yeshitela B, Aseffa A, et al. High Prevalence of Multidrug-Resistant Klebsiella pneumoniae in a Tertiary Care Hospital in Ethiopia. Antibiotics (Basel). 2021 Aug 20;10(8):1007. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Bookman Old Style&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">7. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Bekele T, Tesfaye A, Sewunet T, Waktola HD. Pseudomonas aeruginosa isolates and their antimicrobial susceptibility pattern among catheterized patients at Jimma University Teaching Hospital, Jimma, Ethiopia. BMC Res Notes [Internet]. 2015 Sep 28 [cited 2021 Oct 15];8:488. Available from: https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC4587897/</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Bookman Old Style&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">8. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Nwadike VU, Ojide CK, Kalu EI. Multidrug Resistant Acinetobacter Infection and Their Antimicrobial Susceptibility Pattern in a Nigerian Tertiary Hospital ICU. Afr J Infect Dis [Internet]. 2014 [cited 2021 Oct 15];8(1):14&ndash;8. Available from: https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC3957209/</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Bookman Old Style&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">9. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Petti CA, Polage CR, Quinn TC, Ronald AR, Sande MA. Laboratory medicine in Africa: a barrier to effective health care. Clin Infect Dis. 2006 Feb 1;42(3):377&ndash;82. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Bookman Old Style&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">10. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Solomon FB, Wadilo F, Tufa EG, Mitiku M. Extended spectrum and metalo beta-lactamase producing airborne Pseudomonas aeruginosa and Acinetobacter baumanii in restricted settings of a referral hospital: a neglected condition. Antimicrobial Resistance &amp; Infection Control [Internet]. 2017 Oct 23 [cited 2021 Oct 15];6(1):106. Available from: https://doi.org/10.1186/s13756-017-0266-0</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Bookman Old Style&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">11. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Kutter E, De Vos D, Gvasalia G, Alavidze Z, Gogokhia L, Kuhl S, et al. Phage therapy in clinical practice: treatment of human infections. Curr Pharm Biotechnol. 2010 Jan;11(1):69&ndash;86. </span></sp