order-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top; width:188px\">\r\n			<p style=\"margin-left:1px; margin-right:1px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><a name=\"_Toc472579731\"></a><a name=\"_Toc472426326\"></a><a name=\"_Toc471742856\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\"><span style=\"color:black\">Dissemination of findings </span></span></span></a></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top; width:48px\">\r\n			<p style=\"margin-left:1px; margin-right:1px; text-align:justify\">&nbsp;</p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top; width:42px\">\r\n			<p style=\"margin-left:1px; margin-right:1px; text-align:justify\">&nbsp;</p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top; width:42px\">\r\n			<p style=\"margin-left:1px; margin-right:1px; text-align:justify\">&nbsp;</p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top; width:48px\">\r\n			<p style=\"margin-left:1px; margin-right:1px; text-align:justify\">&nbsp;</p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top; width:48px\">\r\n			<p style=\"margin-left:1px; margin-right:1px; text-align:justify\">&nbsp;</p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top; width:48px\">\r\n			<p style=\"margin-left:1px; margin-right:1px; text-align:justify\">&nbsp;</p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"background-color:white; border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top; width:54px\">\r\n			<p style=\"margin-left:1px; margin-right:1px; text-align:justify\">&nbsp;</p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"background-color:black; border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top; width:66px\">\r\n			<p style=\"margin-left:1px; margin-right:1px; text-align:justify\">&nbsp;</p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:none; border-left:none; border-right:none; border-top:none; width:0px\">\r\n			<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\">&nbsp;</span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n		</tr>\r\n	</tbody>\r\n</table>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\">&nbsp;</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),(49,131,9,18,'Background: The total number of people with diabetes in the world is projected to rise from 366 million in 2011 to an estimated 552 million by 2030. Every ten seconds a person dies from diabetes related causes. The prevalence of diabetes in Ethiopia estimated to 2-3 % of the entire population. Management adequacy and treatment outcomes of diabetic patients weren’t investigated among patients with diabetes mellitus in Jimma zone. Because of this therapeutic success with current medication is unknown. The herbal medicines used for treatment of diabetes are not also well documented in Ethiopia. Furthermore, the safety and effectiveness of such plants is not studied and documented. Therefore, the current research project will focus on evaluating treatment adequacy and glycemic control among adult patients living with diabetes mellitus as well as assessment of the practice of herbal medicine use in managing disease state and in vivo anti-diabetic activity evaluation and charectrization of most widely used medicinal plants in Jimma zone.\r\nMethods: This project employed different methodological and data collection approach based on specific objectives. For evaluation of glycemic control, management adequacy, and its determinants; a health facility based cross-sectional study will be carried out. For gathering and documentation of herbal medicines used for treatment of diabetes mellitus a survey will be conducted among traditional medical practitioners from selected woreda’s of Jimma zone. In vivo experimental design will be employed for anti-diabetic activity evaluation and characterization of selected medicinal plants.\r\nWork and budget plan: The entire project will be conducted from January 2022 to December 2022 and the total budget of the project is 673,774.5 ETB.',NULL,'Investigation of diabetic management adequacy and treatment outcomes among diabetic patients as well as in vivo antidiabetic activity evaluation and characterization of selected medicinal plants in Jimma zone','2021-11-19 12:15:09','Significance of the Study\r\nHistorical records reveal that in traditional medical systems, a disease similar to type 2 diabetes was treated with plant extracts [21, 22]. There have been many studies on hypoglycaemic plants and a great variety of compounds have been isolated (alkaloids, glycosides, terpenes, flavonoids, etc.). Several modern drugs including the anti-diabetic drug metformin (a biguanide) is a derivate of an active natural product [23]  and also natural products are known to be safe. \r\nManagement adequacy and treatment outcomes of diabetic patients weren’t investigated among patients with diabetes mellitus in Jimma zone. Because of this therapeutic success with current medication is not known. On the other hand, Ethiopia is a country known for a diversity of flora. Many Ethiopian populations use plants for treatment of ailments in their communities [12]. The herbal medicines used for treatment of diabetes are not well documented. Furthermore, the safety and effectiveness of such plants is not studied and documented. The research outcome of management adequacy helps to have an understanding about current therapeutic success. The gathering and documentation of herbal medicines in the study area will serve as a pool for further searching of novel antidiabetic drugs.\r\nThe in vivo antidiabetic activity evaluation part of the project will also help the communities in particular and Ethiopia as whole by three folds. First if the plants proved to be safe and effective it is easily accessible and affordable to the communities and hence they can be benefited by using them from their localities as phytomedicines. Secondly, if the plants are not safe or effective or both it will help to draw the attention of the public and the authorities to the ineffective use of medicinal plants which in such a serious case like the management of diabetes may adversely affect the health of the consumer and complicate the user’s health condition without any clear benefit.\r\n Thirdly, the study involves the search for bioactive phytochemicals. The success of such study will contribute for discovery of lead compounds which will be further developed to new anti-diabetic drugs through synthetic chemistry. On the other hand, the effectiveness of in vivo antidiabetic activity of the investigational crude plants may also be used as a base for the development of medicinal plants in to phytomedicines to be used clinically after thorough clinical investigation about its safety and effectiveness. In either of these cases, the success of the research project will contribute for the health and economic development of the country.','3fec756530bb123de1b94c4b4363b507','completed','Introduction\r\nDiabetes mellitus (DM) is a group of metabolic disorders characterized by hyperglycemia, hypertriglyceridemia and hypercholesterolemia resulting from defects in insulin secretion or action or both [1]. It is initially characterized by a loss of glucose homeostasis with disturbances of carbohydrate, fat and protein metabolism [2]. Diabetes is a known major risk factor for cardiovascular disease, metabolic syndrome, dyslipidemia, and end-stage renal disease [3]. Optimal glycemic control is a primary requirement for improvement of the quality of life of diabetic patients as uncontrolled glucose level leads to different diabetic complications which is classified as micro and macro vascular complications [4]. \r\nDiabetes is one of the major causes of premature death worldwide. Every ten second a person dies from diabetes related causes mainly from cardiovascular complications [5]. A few years ago, the concern of the “diabetes epidemic” was restricted to the developed countries, while the other parts of the world were not considered as threatened. Unfortunately, the picture has moved and nowadays no country escapes the diabetes invasion [6]. The total number of people with diabetes in the world is projected to rise from 366 million in 2011 to an estimated 552 million by 2030 [7]. This is mainly due to a tremendous increase of 171% in developing countries, while it will increase only of 41% in developed countries [6]. This clearly shows that diabetes is becoming the major public health problem of developing countries.\r\nThere is no population-based prevalence study for Ethiopia and national estimates is based on studies done in neighboring countries with similar socioeconomic situations and accordingly, the prevalence estimate stands at 2-3 % of the Ethiopian population [8] . A community based study available from one of the urban center in the southwestern part of Ethiopia has indicated the prevalence of T2DM to be 5.3% in those older than 40 years of age. The prevalence of participants with impaired glucose regulation, i.e. impaired fasting glucose and impaired glucose tolerance, was found to be 14.8% which was further indicative of the impending problem [9].  \r\nMedicinal plants have been used in traditional health care systems since prehistoric times and are still the most important health care source for the vast majority of the population around the world [10].  According to the World Health Organization (WHO), about 65-80% of the world’s population in developing countries depends essentially on plants for their primary healthcare due to poverty and lack of access to modern medicine [11]. \r\nEthiopia is a country characterized by a wide range of climatic and ecological conditions possessing enormous diversity of flora and fauna, including wide range of potentially useful medicinal plants. Traditional medical practices are common in Ethiopia in which about 80% of the population in the country use plant-based traditional medicine as their major primary healthcare system [12]. Generally, the majority of Ethiopians rely on indigenous remedies for a numerous socio-cultural and economic reasons.\r\nStatement of the Problem\r\nDiabetes mellitus was assumed to be the disease of developed countries for so long time.  Unfortunately, the picture has moved and nowadays no country escapes the diabetes invasion [6].  The management adequacy and treatment outcomes as well as determinants of diabetes in Jimma zone in particular and in Ethiopia in general are not well established. Furthermore, even if the community in Ethiopia uses herbal medicines for the treatment of diseases with similar sign and symptoms of diabetes, there is no documentation of such herbal medicines. Moreover, the scientific communities are unaware about these herbal medicines and hence cannot conduct a research on them to develop in to medicine or to phytomedicines or to protect the community from any toxic effects associated with the uses of such herbal medicines.\r\nPatients with diabetes require optimal glycemic control and prevention of diabetes complications to improve their quality of life. Pharmacological therapy, as monotherapy or combination therapy is often necessary to achieve glycemic control in the management of diabetes. However, it has been reported that neither sulfonylureas nor biguanide can significantly alter the rate of progression of hyperglycemia in type 2 diabetic patients [13]. This means that currently available drugs for the treatment of diabetes are not effective enough to control blood glucose level and thereby diabetic complications. Furthermore, the currently used antidiabetic drugs have several side effects. For example, sulfonylureas are known for hypoglycemia and weight gain as the main side effects [14]. Weight gain, edema, anemia, pulmonary edema, and congestive heart failure are among the risk of the PPARγ analogue, thiazolidinedione’s [15]. Thus, the management of diabetes without any side effects is still a challenge. Therefore, development of new antihyperglycemic agents with safety and effectiveness is an urgent need [16].\r\n Studies have been demonstrated that oxidative stress, mediated mainly by hyperglycemia-induced generation of free radicals, contributes to the development and progression of diabetes and its complications [17, 18]. On the other hand, although insulin resistance is crucial to the pathogenesis of the disease, the associated atherogenic lipoprotein phenotype considerably enhances the risk [19]. Additionally, lipid accumulation in the liver has been shown to play a key role in insulin resistance and alleviation of this condition was suggested to be one mechanism to improve insulin sensitivity [20]. Hence, searching for medicinal plants having antidiabetic, lipid modifying ability and antioxidant activity is very useful as it is to mean shooting two birds with a single bullet.','Study design and methodological approach\r\nThis project employed different methodological and data collection approach based on specific objectives. These are described in the following work packages linked to the different specific objectives.\r\nWork package1: Evaluation of glycemic control, management adequacy, and its determinants\r\n	Overall study design\r\nThe main objective of the study is to evaluate the diabetes management adequacy (clinical inertia), glycemic control at selected hospitals found in Jimma zone. A health facility based cross-sectional study will be carried out from January, 2022 to- March, 2022. \r\n \r\n	Populations\r\n	Source populations\r\nAll patients living with diabetes mellitus and on follow up in all health facilities in Jimma zone, southwest Ethiopia.\r\n	Study population\r\nAll diabetic patients who are on follow up in selected health facilities in Jimma zone and fulfill the inclusion criteria. \r\n	Inclusion and exclusion criteria\r\nAll adult patients aged ≥18 years and on antidiabetic medication for at least 6 months and on follow up at diabetic clinic will be included. Diabetic patients admitted to inpatient ward, living with other major co-morbidities (like mental illness related problems) and on antidiabetic medication for less than 6 months will be excluded. \r\n	Sample size and sampling techniques\r\nThe sample size is calculated using a single population proportion (Kish & Lisle) formula, by considering, 95% confidence level, 5% margin of error and estimated proportion of patients with treatment intensification is not known in the study are(thus, P=50%) participants will be selected according to systematic random sampling method. \r\nn=((Zα/2)^2*p*(1-p))/d^2             =((1.96)^2*0.5*(0.5))/〖0.05〗^2           = 384\r\nTaking 10% non-response rate: The final minimum sample size will be =422\r\n	n= desired sample sizes.             \r\n	Zα/2 = critical value at 95% CI which equals to 1.96 (z -value at α=0.05)\r\n	p = proportion of patients with treatment intensification.\r\n	d=margin of error (0.05) \r\n	Research endpoints (dependent Variables) \r\n	Glycemic Control and disease state management adequacy\r\nFasting/random blood sugar will be measured and recorded on each follow up day of the patients. Glycemic control and disease state management adequacy will be assessed based on patient clinical condition, review current and previous medication dose was at each patient visit, laboratory investigation and standard treatment guidelines [24] \r\n	Hospital admission\r\nThe frequency of hospital admission and/or the total length of time spent in hospital during the study period will be assessed. Hospitalization will be assessed through review patient chart report, hospital case record, and asking the patient during follows up.\r\n	Quality of Life\r\nQuality of life will be evaluated using the brief version of the World Health Organization Quality of Life instrument (WHOQOL-bref), a 26-item generic instrument derived from the complete WHOQOL-100 version [25]. This scale has been validated in many countries, including Ethiopia, and has adequate psychometric characteristics such as internal consistency, discriminating validity and reliability\r\n	Diabetic self-care behavior and herbal medicine usage\r\nThe revised version of the Summary of Diabetes Self-Care Activities (SDSCA) questionnaire originally developed by Schmitt et al [26] will be used to measure participants’ self-care practice and behaviors. The practice of herbal medicine usage as complementary to modern medicine will also be investigated from diabetic patients. \r\n	Co-variates (Independent Variables)\r\n	Sociodemographic variables: age, sex, marital status, educational status, financial support,    social support, occupation, residence, family history.\r\n	Disease related variables: stage of diabetes, Symptoms of diabetic complications, comorbidities.\r\n	Drug related variables: Duration of therapy, number of antidiabetic drugs\r\n	Set up related variables: frequency of follow up visits, distance to the health facility.\r\n	Behavioral related (exercise, alcohol, chat, smoking coffee and other drug uses, self-care adherence).\r\n	Laboratory related (blood glucose, lipid profile, organ function tests and other relevant specific lab tests).\r\n	Data collection instrument and procedure\r\n	Data Collection Instrument\r\nStructured data collection questionnaire developed by world health organization (WHO) on stepwise approach to chronic disease risk factor surveillance in developing countries [27] will be used with modifications according to study objective in addition to questionnaire which will be developed by investigators. Current national and international clinical practice guidelines (CPG) [28] will also be used to assess the clinical care and treatment outcomes related to diabetes mellitus.\r\n	Data Collection Procedure\r\nTrained data collectors will interview the study participants and review patient charts and medical records for the respective information after all data collection tools are pre-tested. Trained laboratory professionals will perform necessary laboratory investigations (biochemical) upon requests. Other diagnostic work up will be performed by trained professionals, when necessary.  All available and important recorded diagnostic workup and laboratory result(s), including clinical signs and symptoms, diagnosis of each medical problem(s) will be taken. \r\n	Data Quality Control and assurance \r\nThe data collection questionnaires and formats will be pretested in equivalent health facilities. The necessary modification will be made to the tools based on pretesting outcomes. Before entry to SPSS for analysis, data will be cleared, categorized, compiled and coded and also checked for completeness and accuracy. Any erroneous, ambiguous and incomplete data will be excluded.\r\n	Data management and statistical analysis \r\nData will be entered into computer using Epi-Data version 3.1 and exported to the Statistical package for Social Science (SPSS) version 22.0 for analysis. Differences between mean values will be evaluated using Student’s t test while proportions will be compared using the Pearson’s Chi-square test. Multivariable logistic regression analyses will be used to assess the crude and adjusted effect of seemingly significant predictors of the target outcome. Categorical and continuous data will be expressed as percentages and mean ± standard deviation respectively. Descriptive statistics will be applied for the analysis of patient characteristics, including means, standard deviations (SD), medians, and percentiles and categorical variables will be analyzed by using the chi-square test. A p-value of <0.05 will be considered to be statistically significant.\r\n \r\n	Ethical Considerations\r\nEthical clearance & approval will be obtained from IRB of Jimma University. An official letter stating the purpose of the study will be written by Jimma University and presented to the Jimma zone health Office. The study health facilities will be communicated through the official letters from the zonal health office. A written informed consent will be taken from each study participant after clear orientation of the study objective. The raw data will not be made available to any one and not used as the determinant of the subjects. All steps in data collection and compilation will be conducted and supervised by the principal investigator. Strict confidentiality will be assured through anonymous recording and coding of questionnaires and placed in safe place. The patient has full right not to participate and as well as leave the study at any time during the study time. This study will not impose any significant risk on participants. There will be blood sample withdrawal for lab analysis and/or investigation for patients as previous routine care; this might cause some patient discomfort otherwise there will not be any significant risk. The researchers will not add any procedure for the research purpose, rather evaluate for risk and need for investigation(s) and order after clinical evaluation.\r\nWork Package 2: Gathering and documentation of Herbal Medicines Used for Treatment of Diabetes Mellitus \r\nThe study area, study design and periods, data quality control and ethical considerations are the same as above\r\nPopulations\r\nSource Populations\r\nAll herbal medical practitioners and diabetic patients who have follow up in health facilities in Jimma zone\r\nStudy populations\r\nHerbal medical practitioners living in selected woreda’s and diabetic patients who have follow up in selected health facilities of Jimma zone.\r\n \r\nStudy Variables\r\nDependent Variables\r\nHerbal medicines used for treatment of diabetes mellitus, parts of plants used for treatment of diabetes mellitus, methods of preparation of herbal medicines, route of administration of herbal medicines, and duration of therapy, \r\nIndependent Variables\r\nSociodemographic characteristics of herbal medical practitioners and diabetic patients, work experience of herbal practitioners\r\nData Collection Tools and Methods\r\nInformation regarding the herbal medicines used for the treatment of diabetes mellitus is collected from the herbal medical practitioners of randomly selected werada’s of Jimma zone using pre-made data collection format. The sample of each herb will be collected and deposited at herbarium of Jimma University after verification of its authenticity.\r\nWork Package 3:  Evaluation of Antidiabetic Activity of Selected Medicinal Plants\r\n	Methods and Materials \r\n	Chemicals and Reagents\r\nThe following solvents (ethanol, petroleum ether, ethyl acetate, n-butanol, methanol, chloroform) and chemicals (streptozotocin, citric acid, sodium citrate, anhydrous sodium sulphate, silica, tween 80) all of analytical grade will be purchased from the local vendors. The standard antidiabetic drugs namely, glibenclamide and metformin, will be purchased from local pharmacies. Insulin ELISA kit, triglycerides determination kit, total cholesterol levels determination kit, glucometer with glucose oxidase kit will also be purchased from local vendors.  \r\n	Plant Material\r\nThe plant materials will be collected from areas where the plants traditionally used for the treatment of diabetes. Taxonomical identification of the plants specimens will be confirmed and voucher specimens will be deposited at Jimma University Herbarium.\r\n\r\n	Experimental Animals\r\nSprague Dewelly rats of male sex weighing 200–250g will be used for the study. The animals will be maintained at room temperature and12-h light–dark cycles. The animals will be randomized into experimental and control groups and housed in group of three in sanitized polypropylene cages containing sterile paddy husk as bedding. They will have free access to standard pellets as basal diet and water ad libitum. Animals will be habituated to laboratory conditions for 72 h prior to experimental protocol to minimize if any of non-specific stress. \r\n	Method of Extraction and Purifications\r\n	Crude Extract\r\nThe plant materials will be shade dried and powdered. The powder will be extracted three times by Ultrasonic-assisted extraction with 95% ethanol at room temperature for 1hour. The extracts will be filtered using Whatman filter paper. The filtrates will be combined and concentrated under reduced pressure at 40 oC using rotary evaporator and stored in a refrigerator for use in subsequent experiments. The dried plant material supposed to have essential oil will be subjected to steam distillation using Clevenger-type apparatus[29]  to obtain the essential oil. The essential oils will be dried over anhydrous Na2SO4 and stored at 4 oC in the dark.\r\n	 Fractionation \r\nThe fractionation of ethanolic extract will be done according to the method described by Chakrabarti et al. (2002) [30]. Briefly, to the concentrated ethanolic extract a volume of water (500 ml) will be added and fractionated with petroleum ether (4x600 ml), chloroform (4x/600 ml), ethyl acetate (4x/600 ml) and n-butyl alcohol (4x/600 ml). The percentage yields of petroleum ether, chloroform, ethyl acetate and n-butanol extracts will be calculated. The anti-diabetic activity of each fraction will be evaluated. \r\n	Chromatographic Isolation of Active Fractions\r\nThe solvent fractions with greater activity will be  subjected to fractionation and purification using a range of chromatographic techniques such as Column Chromatography (CC) over silica gel, Preparative Thin Layer Chromatography (prep-TLC) and gel filtration over sephadex, to obtain pure compounds. \r\n	 Structural Determination \r\nThe structure of pure compounds will be established using a combination of spectroscopic methods such as Infra-Red (IR), Ultra Violet (UV), 1H and 13C Nuclear Magnetic Resonance (NMR), and Mass Spectrometry (MS). The isolated compounds will also be screened for their in vivo activity. The compounds with moderate activity will be then chemically modified so as to enhance their activities. Furthermore, standardization and formulation including their toxicity will be performed following appropriate regulatory pathways for plant-based medicines.\r\n	Acute Toxicity Study\r\nThe acute toxicity study will be carried out according to OECD guidelines–425. Healthy adult Wistar albino rats of either sex, starved overnight will be divided into two groups, each consisting of five rats and one group will be orally fed with 2000mg/kg of the extracts while the other fed with vehicle. The animals will be observed continuously for 2 h under the following profiles [31].\r\n	Behavioral profile: Alertness, restlessness, irritability, and fearfulness.\r\n	Neurological profile: Spontaneous activities, reactivity, touch response, pain response and gait.\r\n	Autonomic profile: Defecation and urination.\r\n	After a period of 24 hours, 72 hours and 14 days they will be observed for any lethality or death.\r\n	 Induction of Experimental Diabetes\r\nDiabetes will be induced by intraperitoneal administration of streptozotocin dissolved in 0.1M citrate buffer, pH 4.5, in a dose of 65 mg/kg [32].  Three days after the injection, the blood glucose levels will be measured and the animals with blood glucose levels higher than 300 mg/dl will be considered as diabetic. To overcome the hypoglycemia which occurred during the first 24 h following the STZ administration, diabetic rats will be orally given 5% glucose solution [33]. \r\n	 Determination of Hypoglycaemic Effect on Acute Administration\r\nDiabetic animals will be fasted for 12 h (water ad libitum). Test samples will be given orally using oral gastric gavages. The blood glucose concentrations will be measured in all animals at the beginning of the study and the measurements will be repeated 1/2, 1, 2, 4 h and 6 h after the initial of the experiment  [34]. \r\n	 Prophylactic Activity against STZ Challenge\r\nFor determination of prophylactic activity, normal animals will be treated with test extracts at a dose of 500 mg/kg (p.o.) for 30 days. After monitoring blood glucose levels on day 30, animals will be challenged to streptozotocin at a dose of 75 mg/kg (i.p.). Blood glucose levels after 48 h will be monitored for induction of hyperglycemia along with untreated control animals (fed with distilled water) challenged with STZ [35].\r\n	Evaluation of Chronic Effects of Extracts in Streptozotocin-induced Diabetic Rats\r\n The experimentally diabetic induced animals will be randomly assigned into six groups of six animals each and will receive the following treatments: \r\n	Group I: Normal control + distilled water\r\n	Group II: Diabetic + distilled water\r\n	Group III: Diabetic + different doses of plant extracts\r\n	Group IV: Diabetic + Standard anti diabetic drug\r\n The freshly prepared solutions of the extracts will be orally administered daily for 30days. Body weights and blood glucose level analysis will be done weekly on overnight fasted animals [36, 37].  After 30 days of treatment, rats will be anesthetized by ether, their blood will be collected from heart in ordinary tubes for serum total cholesterol, triglycerides and high-density lipoprotein analysis [38].\r\n	 Biochemical Measurement \r\n	Determination of Blood Glucose Levels\r\n Blood glucose concentration (mg/dl) will be determined using a Glucometer-elite commercial test, based on the Glucose oxidase method. Blood samples will be collected from the tip of tail at the defined time patterns [39].\r\n	Determination of Insulin Concentration\r\nSerum insulin levels will be measured by the microplate ELISA method using a commercial kit [40].\r\n	Determination of Plasma Lipid Levels\r\nPlasma triglycerides and total cholesterol levels will be measured using colorimetric method with commercially available kits. Absorbance will be measured at 500 nm [41] Blood will be collected from heart in ordinary tubes and will be allowed to clot, and then the clotted blood will be centrifuged at 2500rpm for 5min. Serum total cholesterol, triglycerides and high-density lipoprotein will be estimated using commercial kits. Low-density lipoprotein and very low-density lipoprotein will be calculated using the following formula [38].\r\n	 Insulin Resistance Calculation\r\nFasting plasma insulin–glucose ratio (FIGR) and insulin resistance indices (FIRI) will be calculated according to the formulae earlier used by Harati et al. and Shalam et al. (2006)[42]:\r\n	FIGR = fasting insulin/fasting glucose;\r\nFIRI = (fasting insulin X fasting glucose)/25.','<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><a name=\"_ENREF_1\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">1.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Muhit, M.A., et al., <em>Cardiovascular disease prevalence and prescription patterns at a tertiary level hospital in Bangladesh.</em> Journal of Applied Pharmaceutical Science, 2012. <strong>2</strong>(3): p. 8.</span></a></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><a name=\"_ENREF_2\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">2.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Tripathi, B.K. and A.K. Srivastava, <em>Diabetes mellitus: complications and therapeutics.</em> Medical science monitor, 2006. <strong>12</strong>(7): p. RA130-RA147.</span></a></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><a name=\"_ENREF_3\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">3.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Nishizawa, Y., et al. <em>Roles of metabolic and endocrinological alterations in atherosclerosis and cardiovascular disease in renal failure: another form of metabolic syndrome</em>. in <em>Seminars in nephrology</em>. 2004. Elsevier.</span></a></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><a name=\"_ENREF_4\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">4.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Fowler, M.J., <em>Microvascular and macrovascular complications of diabetes.</em> Clinical diabetes, 2011. <strong>29</strong>(3): p. 116-122.</span></a></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><a name=\"_ENREF_5\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">5.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Susan van, D., et al., <em>The global burden of diabetes and its complications: an emerging pandemic.</em> European Journal of Cardiovascular Prevention &amp; Rehabilitation, 2010. <strong>17</strong>(1_suppl): p. s3-s8.</span></a></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><a name=\"_ENREF_6\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">6.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Virally, M., et al., <em>Type 2 diabetes mellitus: epidemiology, pathophysiology, unmet needs and therapeutical perspectives.</em> Diabetes &amp; metabolism, 2007. <strong>33</strong>(4): p. 231-244.</span></a></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><a name=\"_ENREF_7\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">7.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Whiting, D.R., et al., <em>IDF diabetes atlas: global estimates of the prevalence of diabetes for 2011 and 2030.</em> Diabetes research and clinical practice, 2011. <strong>94</strong>(3): p. 311-321.</span></a></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><a name=\"_ENREF_8\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">8.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Tran, A., et al., <em>Prevalence of metabolic syndrome among working adults in Ethiopia.</em> International journal of hypertension, 2011. <strong>2011</strong>.</span></a></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><a name=\"_ENREF_9\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">9.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Zekewos, A., et al., <em>Prevalence of diabetes mellitus and associated factors in Southern Ethiopia: a community based study.</em> Ethiopian journal of health sciences, 2018. <strong>28</strong>(4).</span></a></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><a name=\"_ENREF_10\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">10.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Uprety, Y., et al., <em>Traditional use of medicinal plants in the boreal forest of Canada: review and perspectives.</em> Journal of ethnobiology and ethnomedicine, 2012. <strong>8</strong>(1): p. 1-14.</span></a></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><a name=\"_ENREF_11\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">11.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Xavier, T.F., M. Kannan, and A. Auxilia, <em>Traditional Medicinal Plants Used in the treatment of different skin diseases.</em> International Journal of Current Microbiology and Applied Sciences, 2015. <strong>4</strong>(5): p. 1043-1053.</span></a></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><a name=\"_ENREF_12\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">12.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Seid, M.A. and B.A. Tsegay, <em>Ethnobotanical survey of traditional medicinal plants in Tehuledere district, South Wollo, Ethiopia.</em> Journal of Medicinal Plants Research, 2011. <strong>5</strong>(26): p. 6233-6242.</span></a></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><a name=\"_ENREF_13\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">13.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Krentz, A.J. and C.J. Bailey, <em>Oral antidiabetic agents.</em> Drugs, 2005. <strong>65</strong>(3): p. 385-411.</span></a></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><a name=\"_ENREF_14\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">14.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Cheng, A.Y. and I.G. Fantus, <em>Oral antihyperglycemic therapy for type 2 diabetes mellitus.</em> Cmaj, 2005. <strong>172</strong>(2): p. 213-226.</span></a></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><a name=\"_ENREF_15\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">15.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Cheng, A.Y. and I.G. Fantus, <em>Effect of thiazolidinediones on lipid profile.</em> Cmaj, 2005. <strong>173</strong>(4): p. 344-345.</span></a></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><a name=\"_ENREF_16\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">16.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Ray, A., <em>Beyond debacle and debate: developing solutions in drug safety.</em> Nature Reviews Drug Discovery, 2009. <strong>8</strong>(10): p. 775-779.</span></a></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><a name=\"_ENREF_17\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">17.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Rahimi, R., et al., <em>A review on the role of antioxidants in the management of diabetes and its complications.</em> Biomedicine &amp; Pharmacotherapy, 2005. <strong>59</strong>(7): p. 365-373.</span></a></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><a name=\"_ENREF_18\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">18.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Khan, A.N., et al., <em>Role of antioxidant in oxidative stress and diabetes mellitus.</em> Journal of pharmacognosy and phytochemistry, 2015. <strong>3</strong>(6): p. 217-220.</span></a></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><a name=\"_ENREF_19\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">19.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Naskar, S., et al., <em>Evaluation of antihyperglycemic activity of Cocos nucifera Linn. on streptozotocin induced type 2 diabetic rats.</em> Journal of ethnopharmacology, 2011. <strong>138</strong>(3): p. 769-773.</span></a></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><a name=\"_ENREF_20\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">20.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Xu, M., et al., <em>MicroRNA‐190b regulates lipid metabolism and insulin sensitivity by targeting IGF‐1 and ADAMTS9 in non‐alcoholic fatty liver disease.</em> Journal of cellular biochemistry, 2018. <strong>119</strong>(7): p. 5864-5874.</span></a></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><a name=\"_ENREF_21\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">21.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Attele, A.S., et al., <em>Antidiabetic effects of Panax ginseng berry extract and the identification of an effective component.</em> Diabetes, 2002. <strong>51</strong>(6): p. 1851-1858.</span></a></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><a name=\"_ENREF_22\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">22.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Dey, L., A.S. Attele, and C.-S. Yuan, <em>Alternative therapies for type 2 diabetes.</em> Alternative medicine review, 2002. <strong>7</strong>(1): p. 45-58.</span></a></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><a name=\"_ENREF_23\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">23.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Andrade-Cetto, A. and M. Heinrich, <em>Mexican plants with hypoglycaemic effect used in the treatment of diabetes.</em> Journal of ethnopharmacology, 2005. <strong>99</strong>(3): p. 325-348.</span></a></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><a name=\"_ENREF_24\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">24.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Association, A.D., <em>15. Diabetes care in the hospital: standards of medical care in diabetes&mdash;2020.</em> Diabetes Care, 2020. <strong>43</strong>(Supplement 1): p. S193-S202.</span></a></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><a name=\"_ENREF_25\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">25.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Organization, W.H., <em>The world health organization quality of life (WHOQOL)-BREF</em>. 2004, World Health Organization.</span></a></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><a name=\"_ENREF_26\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">26.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Schmitt, A., et al., <em>The Diabetes Self-Management Questionnaire (DSMQ): development and evaluation of an instrument to assess diabetes self-care activities associated with glycaemic control.</em> Health and quality of life outcomes, 2013. <strong>11</strong>(1): p. 1-14.</span></a></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><a name=\"_ENREF_27\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">27.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; WHO, <em>World Health Organization. Chronic diseases and health promotion: stepwise approach to surveillance (STEPS) 2012, Available at: </em></span></a><a href=\"http://www.who.int/chp/steps/manual/en/index.html\" style=\"color:#0563c1; text-decoration:underline\"><em><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">http://www.who.int/chp/steps/manual/en/index.html</span></em></a><em><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\"> accessed on July 12, 2017.</span></em><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\"> 2012.</span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><a name=\"_ENREF_28\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">28.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Association, A.D., <em>Introduction: standards of medical care in diabetes&mdash;2020</em>. 2020, Am Diabetes Assoc.</span></a></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><a name=\"_ENREF_29\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">29.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Eidi, M., A. Eidi, and H. Zamanizadeh, <em>Effect of Salvia officinalis L. leaves on serum glucose and insulin in healthy and streptozotocin-induced diabetic rats.</em> Journal of ethnopharmacology, 2005. <strong>100</strong>(3): p. 310-313.</span></a></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><a name=\"_ENREF_30\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">30.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Chakrabarti, R., et al., <em>Antidiabetic and hypolipidemic activity of Helicteres isora in animal models.</em> Journal of Ethnopharmacology, 2002. <strong>81</strong>(3): p. 343-349.</span></a></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><a name=\"_ENREF_31\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">31.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Dandekar, P., et al., <em>Toxicological evaluation of pH-sensitive nanoparticles of curcumin: acute, sub-acute and genotoxicity studies.</em> Food and Chemical Toxicology, 2010. <strong>48</strong>(8-9): p. 2073-2089.</span></a></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><a name=\"_ENREF_32\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">32.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Orhan, G., et al., <em>Synergy tests by E test and checkerboard methods of antimicrobial combinations against Brucella melitensis.</em> Journal of clinical microbiology, 2005. <strong>43</strong>(1): p. 140-143.</span></a></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><a name=\"_ENREF_33\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">33.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Orhan, E., et al., <em>Effects of plant growth promoting rhizobacteria (PGPR) on yield, growth and nutrient contents in organically growing raspberry.</em> Scientia Horticulturae, 2006. <strong>111</strong>(1): p. 38-43.</span></a></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><a name=\"_ENREF_34\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">34.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Orhan, I.E., <em>Centella asiatica (L.) Urban: from traditional medicine to modern medicine with neuroprotective potential.</em> Evidence-based complementary and alternative medicine, 2012. <strong>2012</strong>.</span></a></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><a name=\"_ENREF_35\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">35.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Singh, S.N., et al., <em>Effect of an antidiabetic extract of Catharanthus roseus on enzymic activities in streptozotocin induced diabetic rats.</em> Journal of Ethnopharmacology, 2001. <strong>76</strong>(3): p. 269-277.</span></a></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><a name=\"_ENREF_36\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">36.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Pandit, R., A. Phadke, and A. Jagtap, <em>Antidiabetic effect of Ficus religiosa extract in streptozotocin-induced diabetic rats.</em> Journal of ethnopharmacology, 2010. <strong>128</strong>(2): p. 462-466.</span></a></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><a name=\"_ENREF_37\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">37.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Ojewole, J., <em>Antiinflammatory and analgesic effects of Psidium guajava Linn.(Myrtaceae) leaf aqueous extract in rats and mice.</em> Methods and findings in experimental and clinical pharmacology, 2006. <strong>28</strong>(7): p. 441-446.</span></a></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><a name=\"_ENREF_38\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">38.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Feshani, A.M., S.M. Kouhsari, and S. Mohammadi, <em>Vaccinium arctostaphylos, a common herbal medicine in Iran: Molecular and biochemical study of its antidiabetic effects on alloxan-diabetic Wistar rats.</em> Journal of ethnopharmacology, 2011. <strong>133</strong>(1): p. 67-74.</span></a></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><a name=\"_ENREF_39\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">39.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Orhan, D.D., et al., <em>Evaluation of the hypoglycemic effect and antioxidant activity of three Viscum album subspecies (European mistletoe) in streptozotocin-diabetic rats.</em> Journal of ethnopharmacology, 2005. <strong>98</strong>(1-2): p. 95-102.</span></a></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><a name=\"_ENREF_40\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">40.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Irudayaraj, S.S., et al., <em>Antidiabetic and antioxidant activities of Toddalia asiatica (L.) Lam. leaves in streptozotocin induced diabetic rats.</em> Journal of Ethnopharmacology, 2012. <strong>143</strong>(2): p. 515-523.</span></a></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><a name=\"_ENREF_41\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">41.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Zeggwagh, N.-A., et al., <em>Study of hypoglycaemic and hypolipidemic effects of Inula viscosa L. aqueous extract in normal and diabetic rats.</em> Journal of ethnopharmacology, 2006. <strong>108</strong>(2): p. 223-227.</span></a></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><a name=\"_ENREF_42\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">42.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Adeneye, A.A., <em>The leaf and seed aqueous extract of Phyllanthus amarus improves insulin resistance diabetes in experimental animal studies.</em> Journal of Ethnopharmacology, 2012. <strong>144</strong>(3): p. 705-711.</span></a></span></span></p>','Evaluation of treatment adequacy and glycemic control among adult patients living with diabetes mellitus as well as assessment of the practice of herbal medicine use in managing disease state and in vivo antidiabetic activity evaluation and characterization of most widely used medicinal plants in Jimma zone.',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,2,'Diabetes, management adequacy, medicinal plants, treatment outcome',10,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),(50,25,9,15,'Executive summary\r\nBackground: Despite booming of internet use in Ethiopia, consumers still value and use more the traditional sources of health information/communication with their doctors. Furthermore , Although online access to and use of health information can play a key role in effectively increasing people`s knowledge and also help in health-related decision making, enormous amount of population may have more difficulties in benefiting from online health information \r\nObjectives: To assess Internet based health related information utilization and its associated factors among chronic illness patients in Jimma University medical Center.\r\nMethodology: Facility based cross sectional study design with qualitative method will be employed among 423 of chronic patients on follow up in study area (Jimma university Medical center). Study subjects will be selected using systematic sampling technique at kth interval. Data will be collected using face to face interview using semi structured and unstructured questionnaire and analyzed using SPPSS version 24 and . The collected data will be analyzed using descriptive statistics and logistic regression (Bivariate and Multiple logistic regressions). The statistical significance of the variables will be determined at P< 0.05 for the final model.\r\nWork plan and Budget: The study will be conducted from December16, 2021 to August 31, 2022 and a total of   350,287ETB will be required to accomplish this project.',NULL,'INTERNET BASED HEALTH RELATED INFORMATION UTILIZATION AND ITS ASSOCIATED FACTORS AMONG CHRONIC ILLNESS PATIENTS IN JIMMA UNIVERSITY MEDICAL CENTER','2021-11-19 18:07:50','1.Generate new knowledge to the field of electronic health information and point out the deterrents for its utilization \r\n2.The findings obtain from this study could be helpful for the stakeholders and government who are working on the e-health related projects.\r\nMake health care related decisions\r\nImprove patient physician communication\r\nEmpower patients for  self-efficacy in disease management\r\nImprove the treatment outcomes of the patients\r\n3.Generate new knowledge to the field of electronic health information and point out the deterrents for its utilization \r\n Change the way patients access health related information \r\n Help the patients to learn about their illness \r\nIt supplements a national strategic plan of e- health\r\nImprove the quality of patients with chronic illness\r\n4.New knowledge will be generated','763ad0c54cf1bf619e3057e16025be87','completed','1.1	Background\r\nDespite booming of internet use in Ethiopia, consumers still value and use more the traditional sources of health information/communication with their doctors(1).\r\nAlthough online access to and use of health information can play a key role in effectively increasing people`s knowledge and also help in health-related decision making, enormous amount of population may have more difficulties in benefiting from online health information (2).\r\nIn spite of the evident access of information communication technology accompanied by use of the Internet in sub-Saharan Africa, especially among the chronic disease patients, not many studies have revealed the use of Internet for health purposes. A huge number of Studies in this area are largely focused on the industrialized countries (5-10) there is a dearth of research in Ethiopia done in this field. The few works on Ethiopia have narrowly focused on health professionals and youths in the higher education. It is thus not clear how patients with chronic diseases, a significant and recognizable segment of the population, with necessarily wide access to modern information technologies (mobile phones, desktop computers, laptops, and tablets) use the Internet for seeking health information and support.\r\nA chronic illness is defined as a condition that lasts one year or more which requires ongoing medical attention and limits a person’s daily activities and which includes Heart disease ,Hypertension, Stroke ,Chronic respiratory diseases ,Diabetes ,Arthritis , Asthma  and  Cancer(11). \r\nIn 2013  World Health Organization estimate  between 2013 -2020 globally 63% death occurs and more than 36 million people die annually from non-communicable diseases such as cardiovascular diseases, cancers, chronic respiratory diseases and diabetes(12).Successful management of most chronic health conditions involves self-care on an outpatient basis. This requires an integration of a variety of information and support resources available to consumers, and not completely relying on healthcare professionals (13). \r\nChronic illnesses are greatest challenging health harms in the world, and their burden in community is increased from time to time. An effective controlling chronic health situation involves self-care. This requires an integration of a variety of information and support resources accessible to clients (14).\r\nAccordingly, Internet based health information access and use are essential for optimal health outcomes especially in chronic patient (15).Internet access has become increased to a variety of health related evidence. The evolution of Web 2.0 has delivered a more sharing setting where users develop and disseminate online content (16). \r\nInternet based information and communication technologies become gradually accepted in healthcare as a means to improve health termed eHealth. According to study conducted on Patient and healthcare professional eHealth literacy and needs for systemic sclerosis support showed that majority of Patients used the internet predominantly for private purposes and 8.8% of them never used it to search for health information (17). E-Health literacy is defined as “one’s ability to search, find, understand, and appraise health information from electronic sources and apply the knowledge gained for addressing or solving a health problem.”  \r\n\r\nAccording to the Lily model, eHealth literacy is a set of six basic skills (traditional literacy, health literacy, information literacy, scientific literacy, media literacy and computer literacy. Is a function of different set of skills, it is highly affected by multidimensional factors including sociocultural, economical and behavioral factors (18, 19). \r\nIn Florida almost 90% of older Web 2.0 users 89.1% reported using popular Web 2.0 websites, such as Facebook and Twitter, to find and share health information (20). Additionally based on the findings from USA, the main factors not to utilize internet based health information are Sociodemographic / economic characteristics (3). These deterrents were also identified by different scholars.\r\nAccording to survey carried out among patients with diagnosed chronic illnesses and treated in three health care facilities in Krakow and Poland, 60.3% use Internet for health information (21). The conducted in University of Gondar comprehensive specialized hospital indicate that 47.0% chronic patients use daily internet, 81.2% browsing for social media and 95.8% of reported that their major means of internet use was smartphone(22). \r\nTherefore, the aim of this study is to assess the utilization of internet-based health information and its contributing factors among chronic illness patients in Ethiopia for seeking online health information and support.','4.	Methodology \r\n4.1 study area and period \r\nThe Project will be conducted in Jimma university Medical center. It is one of the specialized hospital in Oromiya Regional State which is serving more than total population of 2,486,155.Currently it is the only teaching and referral hospital in the southwestern part of the country, providing services for approximately 15,000 inpatient, 160,000 outpatient attendants, 11,000 emergency cases and 4500 deliveries in a year coming to the hospital from the catchment population of about 15 million people. The center has an annual out-patient case load of 160,000 and 45,000 in-patients. It provides services to a diverse population from three regional states, namely Oromiya, Southern Nations Nationalities and Peoples, as well as Gambella. The study will be conducted from December 17, 2021to August31, 2022.\r\n4.2 study design \r\nFacility based cross sectional study design with qualitative study will be employed\r\n4.3 Population\r\n4.3.1 Source population\r\nAll patients with chronic disease attending Jimma medical center\r\nAll health care providers who are working in the chronic units\r\n4.3.2 Study population\r\nAll sampled patients with chronic disease attending Jimma Medical center\r\nAll purposively selected health care providers \r\n4.3.3 Inclusion and exclusion criteria\r\nInclusion criteria \r\n•	Age greater than 18 years\r\n•	Being literate\r\n•	Having at least one chronic disease \r\n•	Able to use the internet \r\nExclusion criteria \r\n\r\n	Critically ill patients at the time of data collection \r\n\r\n4.4 Sample size estimation and Sampling procedure \r\nThe sample size was calculated using a single population proportion formula with considering the following assumptions:\r\n \r\nWhere n is the minimum sample size required, p is estimated proportion of internet-based health related information utilization, z is the standard value of confidence level of α = 95% and d is the margin of error between the sample and the population. For this study p = 50 %, as far as there is no study conducted on prevalence of internet-based health information utilization among chronic disease patients in Ethiopia. Hence, with the following assumptions, the sample size for this study is calculated as follow; \r\n \r\nBy adding non response rate, 10%, on to the calculated sample size (384), then the final sample size was 423.\r\nSystematic random sampling method will be used to select the study participants from the public hospital namely; Jimma Medical center. The previous six months clients’ flow to the two health facilities for chronic care will be reviewed from registration book to estimate the expected number of patients that will come for follow up. Therefore, the total number of chronic patients on follow up in the last six months was 15,655 for Jimma medical center. Thus, the average number of chronic patients on follow up in a one month would be 2609.The calculated sample size, 423, will be recruited from the study hospital. Individual patients will be selected by using systematic random sampling technique (every 3th) and the first sample will be selected by lottery method. The study sample constituted patients with cancer, cardiovascular diseases (hypertension, heart failure, hyperlipidemia, coronary artery disease, and arrhythmia), hypo/hyperthyroidism, diabetes mellitus, chronic renal failure, chronic stomach and intestinal diseases, rheumatoid arthritis, systemic lupus erythematous, liver failure, chronic obstructive pulmonary disease, HIV/AIDs, TB, epilepsy and anemia\r\nRegarding the qualitative part, participants will be selected purposively based on their experience, exposure and professional category. A minimum of 12 KII health care providers will be part of the study. \r\n4.5 Data collection tools and data collection procedures\r\nThe data will be collected by using a modified, semi structured and pretested questionnaire which will be adapted from varies published literature. The questionnaires have the following parts, The first part included questions related to the patient’s sociodemographic factors such as age, gender, educational status, marital status, patient’s chronic diseases, and the number of chronic diseases. The second part has questions related to using internet characteristics and their opinions on using the internet to access health-related information such as skill, frequency, reasons for using the internet, whether the respondent used the internet to access health-related information or not and reasons for not using, preferred internet resources, whether they found information obtained from the internet is useful or not, and finally, the approach of health care professionals to their internet using experiences, etc.\r\nThe questionnaires will be prepared originally in English and translated to local language Afan Oromo and Amharic by experts who have BA in both languages for purpose of data collection. \r\nThe data will be collected by four nurses/midwives under two supervisors working in the hospitals. They collected data for a period of one month. The data collection process will last approximately 20-30 minutes.\r\nThe qualitative data will be collected using unstructured questionnaire with face-to-face in-depth interview from the selected health care providers. This data collection process will take a maximum of two hours. \r\n\r\n4.6.	Data management and statistical analysis \r\nThe collected data will be checked for its completeness and cleaned before entry into computer. Then, data will be coded, cleaned, edited, and entered into EPI data version 3.1 to minimize logical errors and design skipping patterns. Then, the data will be exported to SPSS window version 24 for analysis. Descriptive analysis will be done by computing proportions and summary statistics. Then, the information will be presented by using simple frequencies, summary measures, tables and figures. Bi-variate analysis and multivariate analysis will be done to see the association between each independent variables and outcome variables by using binary logistic regression. The assumption for binary logistic regression will be cheeked. The goodness of fit will be checked by Hosmer-Lemeshow statistic and omnibus testes. All variables with p<0.25 in the Bivariate analysis will be included in the final model of multivariate analysis in order to control all possible confounders. Multico-linearity test will be  carried out to see the correlation between independent variables by using the standard error and collinearity statistics (variance inflation factors >10 and standard error >2 will be  considered as suggestive of existence of multi co-linearity).The direction and strength statistical association was measured by odd ratio with 95%CI.Adjusted odds ratio along with 95%CI will be  estimated to identify predictors using multivariate analysis in binary logistic regression. In this study, P-value<0.05 will be considered to declare a result statistically significant. The qualitative data will be analyzed using a thematic analysis.\r\n4.7	Data quality assurance \r\nIn order to assess the internal validity of the questionnaires, expert opinions from two nursing faculty and computer science department will be taken. After making the necessary recommended changes, the final versions of the questionnaires will be prepared. In order to assess the comprehensibility of the questionnaires, a pre-test will be performed with 5%patients who are not including in the sampling.\r\n4.8	Plan for dissemination and ensuring utilization of findings\r\nThe findings of this project will be submitted to governmental and non-governmental stakeholders and policy makers. Finally, it will be submitted for publication on a peer reviewed journal.\r\n5.	Ethical considerations \r\n    Ethical clearance will be obtained from the Institutional Review Board (IRB) of Jimma University Institute of health sciences. Permission letter is going to be obtained from Jimma University Medical Hospital after the objectives of the study is explained. Written consent will be obtained from selected participant to confirm willingness to participate in the study before the interview. Privacy and confidentiality will be ensured throughout the process of the study. The study participants will be told that they have the right to refuse the consent or withdraw from the study has not altered or put at risk their access to care.','<ol>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Dumitru RC, B&uuml;rkle T, Potapov S, Lausen B, Wiese B, Prokosch H-U. Use and perception of Internet for health related purposes in Germany: results of a national survey. International Journal of Public Health. 2007;52(5):275-85.</span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Jacobs W, Amuta AO, Jeon KC. Health information seeking in the digital age: An analysis of health information seeking behavior among US adults. Cogent Social Sciences. 2017;3(1):1302785.</span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Cohen RA, Adams PF. Use of the Internet for health information: United States, 2009. NCHS data brief, no 66. Hyattsville, MD: National Center for Health Statistics. 2011</span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Bujnowska-Fedak MM. Trends in the use of the Internet for health purposes in Poland. BMC Public Health. 2015 Feb 27;15:194. doi: 10.1186/s12889-015-1473-3. PMID: 25886280; PMCID: PMC4349300.</span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Paslakis G, Fischer-Jacobs J, Pape L, Schiffer M, Gertges R, Tegtbur U, Zimmermann T, N&ouml;hre M, de Zwaan M. Assessment of Use and Preferences Regarding Internet-Based Health Care Delivery: Cross-Sectional Questionnaire Study. J Med Internet Res. 2019 May 16;21(5):e12416. doi: 10.2196/12416. PMID: 31099338; PMCID: PMC6542248.</span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Dumitru RC, Ganslandt T, Prokosch HU. German healthcare consumer&#39;s perception of the Internet as a source of health related information. AMIA AnnuSymp Proc. 2006;2006:224-8. PMID: 17238336; PMCID: PMC1839556.</span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Bujnowska-Fedak MM, Węgierek P. The Impact of Online Health Information on Patient Health Behaviours and Making Decisions Concerning Health. Int J Environ Res Public Health. 2020 Jan 31;17(3):880. doi: 10.3390/ijerph17030880. PMID: 32023828; PMCID: PMC7037991.</span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Takahashi Y, Ohura T, Ishizaki T, Okamoto S, Miki K, Naito M, Akamatsu R, Sugimori H, Yoshiike N, Miyaki K, Shimbo T, Nakayama T. Internet use for health-related information via personal computers and cell phones in Japan: a cross-sectional population-based survey. J Med Internet Res. 2011 Dec 14;13(4):e110. doi: 10.2196/jmir.1796. PMID: 22169526; PMCID: PMC3278096.</span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Sch&auml;fer M, Stark B, Werner AM, Tibubos AN, Reichel JL, Pfirrmann D, Edelmann D, Heller S, M&uuml;lder LM, Rigotti T, Letzel S, Dietz P. Health Information Seeking Among University Students Before and During the Corona Crisis-Findings From Germany. Front Public Health. 2021 Jan 25;8:616603. doi: 10.3389/fpubh.2020.616603. PMID: 33585388; PMCID: PMC7873736.</span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Sedrak MS, Soto-Perez-De-Celis E, Nelson RA, Liu J, Waring ME, Lane DS, Paskett ED, Chlebowski RT. Online Health Information-Seeking Among Older Women With Chronic Illness: Analysis of the Women&#39;s Health Initiative. J Med Internet Res. 2020 Apr 9;22(4):e15906. doi: 10.2196/15906. PMID: 32271152;</span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Goodman RA, Posner SF, Huang ES, Parekh AK, Koh HK. Defining and measuring chronic conditions: imperatives for research, policy, program, and practice. Prev Chronic Dis. 2013</span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">World Health Organization Global action plan for the prevention and control of non-communicable diseases 2013 -2020</span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">J. G. Anderson. Consumers of ehealth: patterns of use and barriers. Soc Sci Comp Rev. 2004;22:242-8</span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Benson Oweghoro at el Knowledge, Access and Use of Internet-Based Health Information for Personal Healthcare among Employees of the Foremost Nigerian University. American Journal of Health Research. Health Information Technology in Developing Nations: Challenges and Prospects Health Information Technology. Vol. 3, No. 1-1, 2015</span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Lee HY, Jin SW, Henning-Smith C, Lee J, Lee J Role of Health Literacy in Health-Related Information-Seeking Behavior Online: Cross-sectional Study J Med Internet Res 2021;23(1):e14088</span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Benetoli A, Chen TF, Aslani P. How patients&rsquo; use of social media impacts their interactions with healthcare professionals. Patient Education and Counseling </span></span><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><a href=\"http://dx.doi.org/10.1016/j.pec.2017.08.015\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\"><span style=\"color:#0563c1\">http://dx.doi.org/10.1016/j.pec.2017.08.015</span></span></span></a></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Kocher A, Simon M, Dwyer AA, et al. Patient and healthcare professional eHealth literacy and needs for systemic sclerosis support: a mixed methods study. RMD Open 2021 </span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Norman C, Skinner H. eHealth Literacy: Essential Skills for Consumer Health in a Networked World. J Med Internet Res 2006;8(2):9</span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Griebel L, Enwald H, Gilstad H, Pohl A-L, Moreland J, Sedlmayr M. eHealth literacy research-quo vadis? Inform Health Soc Care. 2018;43(4):427&ndash;42</span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Tennant B, Stellefson M, Dodd V, et al. eHealth literacy and Web 2.0 health information seeking behaviors among baby boomers and older adults.&nbsp;<em>J Med Internet Res</em>. 2015;17(3)</span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Duplaga ,A cross-sectional study assessing determinants of the attitude to the introduction of eHealth services among patients suffering from chronic conditions BMC Medical Informatics and Decision Making 15:33, 2015</span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Shiferaw et al. E-health literacy and associated factors among chronic patients in a low-income country: a cross-sectional survey BMC Medical Informatics and Decision Making&nbsp; 20:181,2020</span></span></li>\r\n</ol>','To assess Internet based health related information utilization and its associated factors among chronic illness patients in Jimma University medical Center.',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,2,'Internet based Health information, utilization,chronic illness patients',10,'<p>The budget required for this budget is&nbsp; 350,287 and the project will be&nbsp; undertaken from December 16,2021 to August 31,2022</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),(51,74,9,20,'10. Abstract / Executive summary \r\nObstetric violence should be viewed as a structural barrier that is a determinant factor in maternal mortality in sub-Saharan Africa and all maternal health projects should now integrate the issue of obstetric violence. It affects women throughout their sexual and reproductive lives that results gy-necological problems. Objective of this study is obstetric violence and associated factors among women’s give birth in Jimma zone public health facilities. Ethiopia, 2022. \r\nMethods: Institutional based cross-sectional study design employing both quantitative and qualita-tive methods will be conducted among women who give birth in Jimma zone public health facilities. The largest sample size which applies for all study objectives. For qualitative study purposive sampling technique will be used and sample size will be based on the saturation of the information to be reached. Data will be collected using semi-structure interviewer-administered questionnaire. Bi-variable and multivariable logistic regressions models will be used to identify factors associated with the outcome variables. Thematic analysis assisted by Atlas.ti 8 will be used for qualitative data. The finding of the study will be presented using texts, tables and figures as appropriate.\r\nKeywords: obstetric violence, Jimma zone, non-dignified care, non-consented care\r\n11. Start date: February 1,2022	12. End date: January 31,2023','obstetric violence experience by women during childbirth','Obstetric violence    among women’s give birth in Jimma zone public health facilities.','2021-11-19 12:12:55','1.	The non-consented care that women faced at health facilities during childbirth will be determined in order to design interventions.\r\n2.	The extent of physical violence faced by women during childbirth health facility will be determined.\r\n3.	Factors associated with obstetric violence will be identified among postnatal mothers provide intervention based on the finding. \r\n4.	To explore the neglected care that women experienced during childbirth in order to provide possible preventive methods.\r\n5.	To provide information for the confinement of women encounter after childbirth at health facility for intervention \r\n6.	The degree of non -dignified care that women face at health facility during give birth will be identified provide possible suggestion..','c6736bef34152eeadd25ebe685f4dea1','completed','Obstetric violence (OV) specific type of violation women’s rights in medical practic during childbirth processes. During childbirth in a facility, laboring women may be subjected to various forms of obstetric violence (1). Obstetric violence is both a human rights violation and a major public health issue (2). This violation of rights in obstetric practice occurs both in the public and in the private sector during care re-lated to pregnancy and birth, embedded in a multifaceted framework of institutional and gender violence (3).\r\nPhysical abuse, non-consented care, non-confidential care, non-dignified care, discriminated care, neg-lected care, and confinement in a health facility are the seven categories of obstetric violence (4,5). \r\nHowever, obstetric violence against women as a gendered expression of violence and abuse during child-birth has not received the same attention as other contexts in which violence against women takes place. Despite that Universal Rights of Childbearing Women charter highlighted that every women has the right to get dignified, respectful sexual and reproductive health care, many women around the globe experience neglectful, abusive and disrespectful care during facility based child birth(6).\r\n A recent qualitative study synthesis indicates that a lot of women experience different kind of violence during child birth. Violence of women during child birth is manifested by broad range of disrespectful and abusive care during child birth (7). \r\nIn spite of Ethiopian ministry of Health promotes the provision of compassionate and respectful care in health facilities, recent evidences show that a lot of women experience one or more of mistreat-ment during their stay in health facilities for child birth. Those includes but not limited to physical abuse (slapping, pinching, beating…), verbal abuse (yelling, shouting,..),discrimination based on their back ground and other kind of disrespectful care(8). Three out of four Ethiopian women had been faced to at least one kind of obstetric violence during labor and delivery (9).\r\nLiteratures indicate that both health care providers and policy makers primary concerns survival of    mother and fetus during childbirth process in developing country. Neither policy makers nor health care provider consider the experience of women during facility based child and its consequences on future of their health care seeking behavior(10). In some instances women has recalled and described their hospital child birth experience as “Dehumanized birth” where they were treated as a thing or machine not as a human (11).\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nGenerally, obstetric violence is seen as a structural barrier that is a decisive factor in maternal mor-tality in Sub-Saharan Africa and around the world. It affects women throughout their sexual and re-productive life, resulting in gynecological issues. Obstetric violence is a global, national, and aca-demic concern because of the significant impact it has on childbearing women throughout their lives. Despite the different effects of obstetric violence around the world, relatively little study have been done on the obstetric violence experienced by women during childbirth. To the best of the in-vestigators\' knowledge, no well identified study on obstetric violence among women during child-birth has been done in Ethiopia. As a result, for evidence-based and context-specific interventions for women experiencing obstetric violence. To fill the gap this study is aimed to assess Obstetric vi-olence and associated factors among women’s give birth in Jimma zone public health facilities Southwest Ethiopia, 2022.','4 Methodology\r\na)	Study area and period; \r\nThis study will be conducted in public health facilities found in the Jimma Zone, from April 1, 2022 to May 30, 2022. Jimma zone is located in the southwest direction from the capital city of Ethiopia, Addis Ababa. Jimma Zone is one of the largest zones of Oromia region. The zone has 21 districts. Jimma town is the capi-tal city of the zone. In the zone, there are eight hospitals (one referral and teaching hospital, one general hospital, and six primary hospitals), 115 health centers, and 520 health posts. More than 15 million people are served by public health facilities each year, including people from border zones and South Sudan, with the Jimma Medical Center accounting for the lion\'s share.\r\nb. Study design\r\nFacility based cross-sectional study design both quantitative and qualitative data collection methods will be employed.\r\n   c. Population\r\ni. Source population\r\nQuantitative study: All women who give birth in Jimma zone public health facilities during study period.\r\nQualitative study: Purposively selected women who give birth in Jimma zone public health facilities\r\nii. Study population\r\nAll sampled women who give birth in Jimma Zone public health facilities and willing to participate will be study population.\r\n    iii. Inclusion and exclusion criteria\r\n Inclusion: All women who give birth in Jimma zone public health care facilities during the data collection period and willing to participate will be included..  \r\n Exclusion criteria; Critically ill and unable to respond, and un in the study will be excluded.\r\n       d. Sampling technique\r\nSystematic sampling technique will be used for quantitative method and participants for qualitative methods will be selected purposively until data saturation.\r\nE. Data collection tools and method\r\nQuantitative data	\r\n The questionnaire will be prepared in English language. It will be translated to the local language (Afaan Oromo and Amharic), and then back to English by language expert to check its consistency.Face to face interview will be conducted at exit from health facilities for quantitative methods and in-depth interview will be conducted for qualitative one. Before actual data collection, a pre-test will be conducted at non se-lected health facilities and necessary modifications will be made accordingly.\r\n\r\n\r\n  \r\nQualitative \r\nFor qualitative data tape recorder will be used in order to capture their opinion fully after informing about the objective of the study. A semi-structured questions guide will be used to lead the discussion. The data will be collected by in-depth interview. The data was collected simultaneously with quantitative one\r\nf. Data quality control\r\nBefore the actual data collection the questionnaire will be pretested on 5% of the sample size one week be-fore the actual data collection at one health care facility other than those selected. Two days training will be given for data collectors and supervisors. The data will be checked for completeness and accuracy at each day. The supervisors  will make an ongoing checkup each day during the data collection to ensure the quali-ty of data by checking filled questionnaires\r\ng. Data management and statistical analysis\r\nThe questionnaire will be double-checked for accuracy, cleaned, coded, and entered into EPI data statistical software version 3.1 before being exported to SPSS windows version 23 for analysis. The Bivariate analysis will be used to identify candidate variables for multivariable analysis. Then variables found to have p-values of less than 0.25 will be taken to Multivariable Logistic regression for controlling the possible effects of confounders. Finally, variables that will have a p-value ≤ 05 will be described as significant and Adjusted OR with 95% CI will be determined to see the strength of the associations.\r\n\r\n         \r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n      h. Ethical consideration\r\nEthical approval will be obtained from Jimma University Institute of Health Institutional ethical review board. Permission letter will be obtained from Jimma zone health beareu and informed verbal consent will be obtained from respective study participants. Study participants will be provided comprehensive informa-tion about nature, objectives, benefits, potential risks of the study and participants right to refuse and with-draw participation in the study will be assured. Written informed consent will be obtained from study par-ticipants before the interview Privacy and confidentiality of collected information will be ensured. During data collection,COVID-19 preventive measures like wearing face-mask will be strictly applied by data col-lectors.','<h3 style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Cambria,&quot;serif&quot;\"><span style=\"color:#4f81bd\"><span style=\"font-size:14.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">&nbsp;&nbsp; &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<a name=\"_Toc88148687\">References</a> </span></span></span></span></span></h3>\r\n\r\n<ol>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><a name=\"_bookmark0\"></a><a name=\"_bookmark2\"></a>GIRE , Obstetric Violence, (In spanish)</span></span></li>\r\n</ol>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">https://www.gire.org.mx/nuestros-temas/violencia-obstetrica</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<ol start=\"2\">\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"background-color:white\">Jardim DM, Modena CM. Obstetric violence in the daily routine of care and its characteristics. Revista latino-americana de enfermagem. 2018 Nov 29;26.</span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"background-color:white\"><span style=\"color:#403d39\">Funda&ccedil;&atilde;o Perseu Abramo e Sesc. Mulheres Brasileiras e G&ecirc;nero nos Espa&ccedil;os P&uacute;blico e Privado 2010; [access 1 Sep 2019]. Dispon&iacute;vel em: </span></span><a href=\"https://fpabramo.org.br/publicacoes/publicacao/pesquisa-mulheres-brasileiras-e-genero-nos-espacos-publico-e-privado-2010/\" style=\"color:blue; text-decoration:underline\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"background-color:white\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\"><span style=\"color:#0000ee\">&raquo; https://fpabramo.org.br/publicacoes/publicacao/pesquisa-mulheres-brasileiras-e-genero-nos-espacos-publico-e-privado-2010/</span></span></span></a></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">Meghan A. Bohren JPV, Erin C. Hunter,Olha Lutsiv,Suprita K. Makh. The Mistreatment of Women during Childbirth in Health Facilities Globally. PLoS Medicine. 2015.</span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"background-color:white\"><span style=\"color:#333333\">Reis V, et al. Respectful maternity care, 2012.&nbsp;</span></span></span></span></li>\r\n</ol>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><a href=\"https://www.k4health.org/sites/default/files/RMC%20Survey%20Report_0.pdf\" style=\"color:blue; text-decoration:underline\"><strong><span style=\"background-color:white\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\"><span style=\"color:#004b83\">https://www.k4health.org/sites/default/files/RMC%20Survey%20Report_0.pdf</span></span></span></strong></a><span style=\"background-color:white\"><span style=\"color:#333333\">. 2012</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<ol start=\"6\">\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">Madiba RMaS. Disrespect and Abuse Experienced by Women duringChildbirth in Midwife-Led Obstetric Units in Tshwane District, South Africa. International journal of enviromental health and public health research. 2020.</span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">Meghan A Bohren HM, Bukola Fawole,Thae Maung Maung, Mamadou Dioulde Balde,Ernest Maya, Soe Soe Thwin. How women are treated during facility-based childbirth in four countries. Lancet. 2019.</span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">Ephrem D.shifearw Y-k, Thomas vanden akker. mistreatement of women in public health facilities of Ethiopia. reproductive health. 2019.</span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"background-color:white\">Mihret MS. Obstetric violence and its associated factors among postnatal women in a Specialized Comprehensive Hospital, Amhara Region, Northwest Ethiopia. BMC research notes. 2019 Dec;12 (1):1-7.</span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">Anteneh Asefa AM, Samson Gebremedhin,Ephrem Tekle. Mitigating the mistreatment of childbearing women.&nbsp; BMJ open. 2020.</span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><a name=\"_bookmark5\"></a>Ernest T Maya KA-B, Phyllis Dako-Gyeke,Caroline Badzi,Joshua P Vogel. Women&rsquo;s perspectives of mistreatmentduringchildbirth at health facilities in Ghana. Reproductive Health Matters. 2018.</span></span></li>\r\n</ol>','Assessment of obstetric violence experience by women during childbirth in Jimma zone public health institutions, 2022.',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,2,'Keywords: obstetric violence, Jimma zone, non-dignified care, non-consented car',10,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),(52,137,9,18,'Background: Chronic kidney disease (CKD) is a global public health problem and is currently one of the fastest growing causes of death in the world. It is associated with increased risk of death, enormous financial costs, and cardiovascular events. The major risk factors for CKD in Africa are high levels of infectious diseases, increased incidence of non-communicable diseases such as diabetes and hypertension, exposure to environmental pollutants, and increased urbanization. Early detection, screening campaigns, health promotion, and readily available, often inexpensive, appropriate interventions can improve outcomes. However, many patients with CKD arrive late in the health care due to inadequate awareness, the cost of health care and traditional beliefs about the cause and nature of the disease. Even though there are studies on prevalence of CKD among people with other chronic diseases in Ethiopia, there is limited information among general population. In addition, screening of CKD is rarely performed in Ethiopia among general population and most of the CKD cases remained undiagnosed. Also, there is limited information on the CKD awareness, prevalence, associated factors, risk perceptions and health care seeking behavior among communities in Ethiopia, including the study area. Therefore, the purpose of this study is tried to solve this gap at Jimma zone, Southwest Ethiopia.\r\nMethods: Community-based cross-sectional study supported by qualitative findings will be conducted from February -May 2022 among adults in Jimma zone, Southwest Ethiopia. 844 households will be recruited using systematic random sampling technique. Data will be collected by face-to face interview using structured questionnaire, blood pressure and anthropometric measurements and laboratory investigations will be done. Binary logistic regression will be used for data analysis.\r\nTimeline and budget: The study will be conducted within one year period with a total budget of 490,952 ETB.',NULL,'Assessment of magnitude, related factors  of chronic kidney diseases and understanding community awareness and health seeking behavior towards it among adult residents in Jimma Zone, Southwest Ethiopia; Mixed study design','2021-11-19 11:43:51','Early identification and treatment of the chronic kidney diseases reduces the burden of diseases from community. It also assesses associated factors and communities’ awareness, perception and health seeking behavior regarding chronic kidney disease. So, the findings from current study will provide scientific information for developing strategies and intervention to reduce chronic kidney diseases.','c7c38dfd8849d02209f950bf8530bdee','completed','Chronic kidney disease (CKD) is defined as an abnormality in the function or structure of the kidney, lasting more than 3 months and progressing, causing irreversible damage to the kidney(1).It is an emerging public health problem globally and currently one of the fastest growing causes of death in the world and requires special attention.It is associated with an increased risk of death and increased cardiovascular events(2–4).\r\nIn 2017, 697.5 million CKD cases occurred globally, killing 1.2 million people each year as a result of costly treatment(5).According to the global burden of disease 2015 study, CKD was the 17th  leading cause of global years loss of life and in 2016 there were 21 million new cases of CKD(6,7).In addition, it is estimated that approximately 2.3-7.1 million adults will die prematurely by 2030 due to lack of access to renal replacement therapy(8). \r\nIn developing  countries, the magnitude is much greater (9)and evidences show that an estimated incidence of CKD in developing countries is4 times higher than in developed countries.In Africa the prevalence of CKD in general population ranges from 2% to 41% andit usually affects working age between twenty and fifty years with  high mortalitybecause oflimited facilities(10–12).\r\nCKD has long term health  impacts such as cardiovascular disease, kidney failure, mineral and bone disorders, frequent hospitalizations and premature death(13,14).The cost of treatment far exceeds the cost of prevention and is a significant financial burden. For instance,management of end-stage CKD includes hemodialysis which is financially burdensome for most people andless than 3% of people in need of renal replacement therapy get it (8,15).\r\nAlthough the exact origin of CKD is unknown, research suggest that it is caused by a number of risk factors.The main risk factors for CKD in Africa are high levels of infectious diseases, increased incidence of non-communicable diseases such as diabetes and hypertension, exposure to environmental pollutants, and increased urbanization(12,16).The causes of CKD vary by settings. Diabetes and high blood pressure are the most common causes affecting almost5-7% of the world\'s population and are more common in developing countries(17). In addition, chronic kidney disease of unknown cause is a main health problem, especially in developing countries(18)and  its exact cause is unknown, various multifactorial causes like heat stress, chronic dehydration, agrochemicals, intense manual labor, low altitude, consumption of high fructose beverages, frequent use of self-prescribed non-steroidal anti-inflammatory medications and low income have been proposed for its development(19,20).\r\nEvidences suggest  that early detection, screening campaigns, health promotion, and suitable interventions can stop CKD altogether, slow its  progression or even reverse the process of the disease totally(21,22).Moreover, early detection and treatment of possible risk factors are readily available and often inexpensive. However, many patients with CKD arrive late in the health care and have poor outcomes. As reported by many authors, this may be due to inadequate awareness, the cost for health care and traditional beliefs about the cause and nature of the disease(23–25).\r\nDespite the availability of interventions, the burden of CKD and its associated risk factors in developing countries is still understudied. This could be owing to a lack of awareness among the general public, health-care personnel, and government and other funders, leading to the mistaken belief that CKD is not a serious problem(26,27). Furthermore, research reveal that the general public has misconceptions about CKD and its risk factors, as well as poor health-seeking behavior (28).\r\nThe majority of people with CKD seek medical help late in life, often in advanced stages of disease. Because the early stages of CKD are usually asymptomatic, the disease\'s burden is mostly undiagnosed and difficult to quantify. When more than half of the renal functional mass has been gone, the symptoms begin to appear(29). Thus, population-based prevention strategies appear to be the best and cheapest solution for early detection of modifiable risk factors and early detection of it(30).\r\nEven though there are studies on prevalence of CKD among people with other chronic diseases in Ethiopia, there is limited information among general population. In addition, CKD screening is rarely performed in the general population of Ethiopia, and most CKD cases are undiagnosed. Generally, CKD is an important cause of death and disability worldwide, but, there is limited information on the CKD awareness, prevalence, associated factors, risk perceptions and health care seeking behavior among communities in Ethiopia, including the study area(31–33).Hence, screening for CKD, increasing community awareness towards the disease and risk factors has a positive effect on the effective management of CKD(34).Furthermore, understanding risk perception and health care seeking behavior of people towards CKD is essential to take appropriate interventions. Therefore, the purpose of this study is tried to solve this gap at Jimma zone, Southwest Ethiopia.','Study setting, design and study period\r\nCommunity-based cross-sectional study supported by qualitative findings will be conducted among adults in Jimma zone, Southwest Ethiopia. According to the data obtained from zonal health department, currently, the zone has a total population of 3,759,072 and 21 districts. The study will be conducted within one year period.\r\nPopulation\r\nAll adults aged ≥18 year who lived in Jimma zone will be our source population, whereas adults aged ≥ 18 years living in the selected districts during the study period and fulfill the eligibility criteria will be our study population. Participants with previously known chronic kidney disease, pregnant women and those unable communicate due to severe illness will be excluded from the study\r\nSample size determination\r\nThe sample size is calculated using the single population proportion formula and the assumptions considered are: P =50%, significance level 5% and margin of error 5%.  Our final sample size is 844 after accounting for a design effect (2) and a 10% non-response rate.\r\nSampling procedure\r\nA multi-stage sampling procedure will be used in this study. Out of 21 districts in Jimma Zone, six districts (≈30%) will be selected randomly. Then, the total sample will be allocated proportionally to selected districts. Again,30% of kebeles from selected districts will be selected randomly. Similarly, the sample size will be allocated to each randomly selected kebeles in the districts. Finally, systematic random sampling technique will be used to select the study participants. The total number of household (HH) in each selected kebeles will be obtained and it will be divided to sample size to get sampling interval (Kth interval). The first HH will be selected using lottery method and the participant selection will continue every Kth interval until the required sample achieved. In case of more than one eligible subject with in HH, lottery method will be used to the select the participant.\r\nA qualitative study to explore and gain deeper understanding of the awareness, perceived susceptibility, barriers and facilitators of health seeking behavior will be conducted. 18 key informant interviews will be purposefully recruited based on the richness of the information from health care providers, and community members. Two focus group discussions (FDGs) will be conducted with community members, and consisting of 6-10 men and women, will be carefully selected.\r\nData collection procedure	\r\nQuantitative data collection tools will be developed after reviewing various relevant literature and the Guidelines for Global Outcomes to Improve Kidney Disease (KDIGO)(35).Data will be collected by face to face interview of the study participants. For the qualitative study part, a semi-structured guide will be developed considering the research questions or objectives.Data will be collected by trained and experienced health proffessionals.\r\nAnthropometric measurements\r\nParticipants\' weight will be measured in kilograms (kg) to the nearest 0.1kg with light clothing and shoes off, and height will be measured to the nearest 0.1 centimeters using stadiometer. Body mass index will be determined by dividing weight in kg by the square of height. After participants have rested for at least 5 minutes, their blood pressure will be measured. It will be measured twice and the average of the two readings will be taken. Using fixed tension, the waist circumference will be measured halfway between the lowest costal margin at the mid-clavicular line and the anterior superior iliac spine. The greatest circumference of the hip will be measured at the level of the femoral trochanters. The shortest peri-waist length between the inferior costal margin and the iliac crest will be used to determine the waist-hip ratio.\r\nLaboratory investigation\r\nSerum creatinine, Complete blood count (CBC), random blood sugar (RBS) and urine analysis measurements will be done. After preparing the required materials and written consent obtained from participants, blood sample will be collected and processed for biochemical analysis by trained laboratory technician. Antecubital vein will be disinfected with 70% alcohol and tourniquet will be applied. Then 5mls of blood will be collected to Ethylenediaminetetraacetic acid (EDTA) and serum separator tube (SST) from each participant following standard operating procedure (SOP). The EDTA sample will be mixed well to prevent clotting. The SST sample will be stayed for 30 minutes and serum will be separated from collected blood sample by centrifugation at 4000 rpm for 10minutes. Serum will also be stored at -20oC for later use in the analysis of biochemistry. Biochemical parameters such as creatinine and urea levels will be determined by using Roche cobas automated analyzer. The complete blood count will be analyzed by Beckman coulter (UniCel® DxH 800) hematological analyzer machine which is currently a standard panel nationally. Five ml of urine specimen will also be collected with clean urine cup, leak-proof, screw-capped urine collection container and urine albumin will be determined by using dipsticks.\r\nGlomerular filtration rate (GFR) will be estimated using the CKD- EPI equation (36). CKD will be classified according to the KDIGO classification system and defined based on the estimated GFR and presence of albuminuria(37).\r\nData processing and analysis\r\nData will be double-checked for accuracy and entered into Epi-data 3.1 before being exported to SPSS version 20 for analysis. Participants\' socio-demography and clinical characteristics will be presented using descriptive statistics such as mean, percentage, and standard deviation. Bivariate analysis will be done to check a relationship between independent variables and CKD, and variables with a p-value of less than 0.25 will be regarded for multivariate logistic regression. Finally, for independent predictors of CKD, we will apply multivariate logistic regression, with a p-value of 0.05 considered statistically significant.\r\nThe qualitative data will be analyzed using the atlas ti.7.1. A thematic analysis approach through which the codes, categories, and themes can be developed from the data will be employed. The data will be transcribed verbatim, translated to English; the language through which the analysis will be carried out. Reading and re-reading of the data will be carried out to understand and extract the important concepts from the data, and line-by-line coding will be done. The codes will be merged to develop the categories, and the categories will be in turn merged to form themes. Then, the findings will be presented with themes, categories, and quotations from the data.\r\nData quality management\r\nTo ensure uniformity, the questionnaire will be translated from English to Afan Oromo and Amharic, and then back to English. Data collectors and supervisors will get two days of training on the study\'s purpose and data collection processes. Prior to data collection, 5% of the total sample will be pretested to validate the data collecting tool\'s clarity and integrity. Grammar, sequence, and timing modifications and changes will be made based on the results of pretests before the actual data collection began. The data collected will be checked daily for integrity, accuracy, clarity and consistency. Biochemical parameters will be measured and analyzed following standard procedures.\r\nThe trustworthiness of the qualitative findings will be ensured through different techniques. First, a semi-structured questionnaire will be developed concerning the research questions. Second, a diversified group of individuals will be recruited from different study settings. Third, peer debriefing will be done daily. Forth, member checking will be done with key study participants to check the interpretations of the findings, and their critique or comments will be incorporated. Fifth, at the end of each interview, major thematic areas will be presented to the study participants for checking the interpretations, and comments. Sixth, an audit trail will be done to verify the study findings.','<ol>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Whaley-Connell AT, Sowers JR, Stevens LA, McFarlane SI, Shlipak MG, Norris KC, Chen SC, Qiu Y, Wang C, Li S, Vassalotti JA. CKD in the United States: kidney early evaluation program (KEEP) and National Health and Nutrition Examination Survey (NHANES) 1999-2004. American journal of kidney diseases. 2008 Apr 1;51(4):S13-20.</span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Levey AS, Atkins R, Coresh J, Cohen EP, Collins AJ, Eckardt KU, Nahas ME, Jaber BL, Jadoul M, Levin A, Powe NR. Chronic kidney disease as a global public health problem: approaches and initiatives&ndash;a position statement from Kidney Disease Improving Global Outcomes. Kidney international. 2007 Aug 1;72(3):247-59.</span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">J. DiPiro, R. Talbert, G. Yee, G. Matzke, B. Wells, and L. Posey, Pharmacotherapy , McGraw-Hill Publishing, New York, NY, USA, 2011.</span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Webster AC, Nagler EV, Morton RL, Masson P. Chronic kidney disease. Lancet. 2017;389(10075):1238&ndash;1252.</span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Bikbov B, Purcell CA, Levey AS, Smith M, Abdoli A, Abebe M, Adebayo OM, Afarideh M, Agarwal SK, Agudelo-Botero M, Ahmadian E. Global, regional, and national burden of chronic kidney disease, 1990&ndash;2017: a systematic analysis for the Global Burden of Disease Study 2017. The Lancet. 2020 Feb 29;395(10225):709-33.</span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Xie Y, Bowe B, Mokdad AH, Xian H, Yan Y, Li T, Maddukuri G, Tsai CY, Floyd T, Al-Aly Z. Analysis of the Global Burden of Disease study highlights the global, regional, and national trends of chronic kidney disease epidemiology from 1990 to 2016. Kidney international. 2018 Sep 1;94(3):567-81.</span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Wang H, Naghavi M, Allen C, Barber RM, Bhutta ZA, Carter A, Casey DC, Charlson FJ, Chen AZ, Coates MM, Coggeshall M. Global, regional, and national life expectancy, all-cause mortality, and cause-specific mortality for 249 causes of death, 1980&ndash;2015: a systematic analysis for the Global Burden of Disease Study 2015. The lancet. 2016 Oct 8;388(10053):1459-544.</span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Liyanage T, Ninomiya T, Jha V, Neal B, Patrice HM, Okpechi I, Zhao MH, Lv J, Garg AX, Knight J, Rodgers A. Worldwide access to treatment for end-stage kidney disease: a systematic review. The Lancet. 2015 May 16;385(9981):1975-82.</span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Norton JM, Moxey-Mims MM, Eggers PW, Narva AS, Star RA, Kimmel PL, Rodgers GP. Social determinants of racial disparities in CKD. Journal of the American Society of Nephrology. 2016 Sep 1;27(9):2576-95.</span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-alig