			<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Subtotal</span></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:9px; vertical-align:top; width:229px\">\r\n			<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">446,320</span></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n		</tr>\r\n		<tr>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:9px; vertical-align:top; width:313px\">\r\n			<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Contingency cost (10%)</span></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:9px; vertical-align:top; width:229px\">\r\n			<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">446,32</span></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n		</tr>\r\n		<tr>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:13px; vertical-align:top; width:313px\">\r\n			<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Grand Total cost in ETB</span></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:13px; vertical-align:top; width:229px\">\r\n			<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">490,952</span></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n		</tr>\r\n	</tbody>\r\n</table>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\">&nbsp;</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO `submission` VALUES (53,147,9,15,'Executive summary\r\nBackground: Hypertension is the leading risk factor for cardiovascular diseases. Unhealthy lifestyles such as physical inactivity, sedentary behaviour (prolonged sitting) and unhealthy dietary practices are the major behavioural risk factors of hypertension and hypertension-related complications. World Health Organization recommends a total risk approach of using lifestyle interventions as the best solution to control hypertension specially in countries with  limited resources. Application of mHealth-based lifestyle interventions is strongly recommended to promote healthy lifestyles and control hypertension in settings with limited resources. However, there is a scarcity of evidence on the effects of mHealth-based lifestyles interventions on high blood pressure, risk of cardiovascular diseases, complications related to hypertension and quality of life in Ethiopia.\r\nObjective: This study aims to examine the effect of mHealth-applications on blood pressure, lifestyle behaviours and quality of life among hypertensive patients in Jimma Medical Center (JMC), South-west of Ethiopia.\r\nMethods: Double-blinded, parallel-group, randomized controlled superiority trial with longitudinal repeated measures will be conducted among hypertensive patients who are receiving treatment in the chronic follow-up unit of the centre in JMC. In the study, 89 patients will be randomized to receive mHealth-based lifestyle interventions by using short text messages, mobile apps, wearable devices and counselling (intervention group) while 89 patients will receive routine care (control group). We have developed a total of 54 health messages based on the theory of planned behaviour to increase knowledge, change attitude so that healthy lifestyle practices are increased by patients. These messages will be used during counselling sessions throughout intervention periods and will also be sent to each patient in the intervention group via short messages. Mobile app and wearable devices will be used to track physical activities (steps per day, energy expenditure in calories). Questionnaires and health messages will be pre-tested in 5% of the patients. Outcome (blood pressure, 10-year cardiovascular risk, quality of life) and predictor data (socio-economic, lifestyles, dietary, metabolic and psychosocial) will be collected 4 times (baseline, 3rd, 6th and 9th months) by following each patient for nine months (i.e. 712 participants on longitudinal bases).  A linear mixed-effects (fixed and random) model will be used to determine predictors of blood pressure and quality of life. A generalized logistic regression model will be used to determine predictors of 10-year CVDs risk. Models assumptions will be checked and an appropriate correlation matrix will be selected accordingly.\r\nResult: The findings of the study for primary and secondary outcomes will be presented using Consolidated Standards for Reporting Trial (CONSORT) guideline.',NULL,'Application of mHealth technologies to control hypertension, improve lifestyle behaviours and quality of life: a randomized controlled trial','2021-11-19 11:56:47','Specific objectives and epected outputs/outcomes \r\n1.	To measure the effect of mobile app and short text messaging interventions on blood pressure 	\r\n•	Increased utilization of mHealth apps for the management of hypertension  \r\n•	Reduced high blood pressure-related complications (heart, stroke, PAD, CKD, Retinopathy, neuropathy)\r\n•	Improved health-seeking behaviour and adherence to anti-hypertension medications and follow-ups\r\n2.	To examine the effect of mobile app and short text messaging interventions on physical activities and dietary practices\r\n•	Increased knowledge on healthy physical activities regarding types, health benefit, duration and frequency of performance per day/week\r\n•	Increased knowledge on healthy dietary intake regarding their health benefits, contents, amount and frequency dietary intake \r\n•	Changed attitudes towards physical activities and dietary practices \r\n•	Increased daily adherence to recommended healthy physical activities and healthy dietary practices\r\n•	Improved mental health-related problems (stress and depression etc)\r\n3.	To determine the effects of mobile app and short text messaging interventions on quality of life	\r\n•	Increased functional/physical, family and social functioning\r\n•	Improved financial/work-related situations\r\n4.	To determine the effect of  mobile app and short text messaging interventions on 10-year cardiovascular risk	\r\n•	Improved/reduced body weight (obesity/weight)\r\n•	Reduced serum cholesterols and blood glucose \r\n•	Reduced risk hypertension-related complications\r\n•	Risk-based stratification and management \r\n5.	To identify end line predictors of controlled blood pressure, 10-year CVDs risk and quality of life	•	Risk-based planning and design of interventions','82b3877af668c38935a289aea62cd4ee','completed','Background: Cardiovascular diseases and hypertension are the leading causes of global deaths (1-3) with two-third of these deaths occurring in lower-and-middle income countries (4). Morbidities, mortalities, and economic impacts due to these diseases are increasing every year (3). Despite the availability of effective medications and other public health measures, hypertension remains a serious medical condition globally (5). \r\nIn developing countries, the prevalence of hypertension is predicted to rise by 80% (1.15 billion) in 2025 (5) including in Ethiopia (6). Evidence from Global Burden of Disease showed high systolic blood pressure and dietary risks are the two leading risk factors of cardiovascular diseases contributing to more than 50% of disability-adjusted years (DALYs) in Ethiopia (7), and this burden is predicted to increase until 2025 (8). Unhealthy lifestyles, such as physical inactivity, sedentary behaviour (prolonged sitting) and unhealthy dietary practices are the major behavioural risk factors of hypertension and cardiovascular complications related to hypertension (9, 10). More than 20% of adults were physically inactive in 2010 (3, 11) and global daily intake of unhealthy diets exceed the optimal level of daily intake (12). Evidence from different sources depicted that unhealthy lifestyle are associated with increased risk of morbidity and mortality due to hypertension and cardiovascular diseases (1, 2, 9, 10, 13-17). \r\nOnce an individual develops hypertension and cardiovascular complications, lifelong medications, complex surgeries and renal dialysis may be required to save the lives of the patients (3). However, such treatments in resource-constrained settings are not affordable for most of the individual patients (3). The World Health Organization and national blood pressure education program (of the US) recommends total risk-approach of using affordable lifestyle interventions (physical activity and dietary intervention) as the best solution to control hypertension and complications related to hypertension in settings with limited resources (3, 13).\r\nPhysical inactivity is defined as an insufficient level of physical activity required to meet the physical activity recommendations (11, 13). According to the recent physical activity and sedentary behaviour guideline of the World Health Organization(13), adults should perform regular moderate-intensity aerobic physical activity for 150-300 minutes per week to vigorous-intensity aerobic physical activity for 75-150 minutes per week or an equivalent combination of moderatetovigorous-intensity activity throughout the week(13). However, this recommendation does not specify the level of severity of the diseases; for instance, patients with myocardial infarction (severe chest pain), patients with heart failure, stroke and chronic renal failure may not perform similar recommendations (13). Even though there are different types of aerobic physical exercise, walking, the most popular and the preferred type of exercise, has shown reduced systolic and diastolic blood pressures (18).  However, its effect is not well explored in hypertensive patients with hypertension-related complications in Ethiopia. \r\nSedentary behaviour, a recently recognized risk factor of chronic diseases, is defined as any waking behaviour characterized by an energy expenditure of 1.5 metabolic equivalents (METs) or less while sitting, reclining or lying down (13, 19). Even though there is no clearly quantified threshold of sedentary time, the World Health Organization recommends adults with chronic conditions to limit the amount of time spent being sedentary and to replace sedentary time with physical activity of any intensity (13, 20).  Large longitudinal studies and meta-analysis of cohort studies in adults indicated a dose-response relationship between sedentary behaviour and all-cause and CVDs mortality. Adults who reported sitting almost all of the time to watch TV for at least 4 hours had experienced an increased risk of all-cause and CVDs mortality regardless of their intensity of physical activity (15, 16, 21-23).  This indicates interventions targeting sedentary behaviour should also consider physical activity recommendations to fully mitigate cardiovascular health risks due to these behaviours (21). However, this important risk factor is not included in the guideline for the management of major non-communicable diseases in Ethiopia (24).\r\nSimilarly, adults are advised to practice healthy dietary recommendations including reducing salt (less than 1 teaspoon), replacing saturated and trans-fatty acids with unsaturated fatty acids, including fresh fruits and vegetables in meals and snacks, limiting consumption of fried, pre-processed and sugary foods and drinks (10). Evidence verified that healthy dietary advice improves cardiovascular risk factors (25) and is hence effective to prevent hypertension, reduce the 10-year risk score for cardiovascular diseases and reduce complications related to hypertension alone or in combination with other lifestyle interventions (26-28).   \r\nRecently mHealth applications are strongly recommended to promote physical activities and reduce sedentary time in settings with limited resources (29). Mobile Health (mHealth) which is the extended component of eHealth is defined as the use of mobile phones, patient monitoring devices, personal digital assistances and other wireless devices to support medical and public health practices (30, 31). These applications are used to collect health-related data of healthy individuals and patients to provide appropriate health care services (32). Wearable devices (33), short text messages (30, 31) and mobile-based walking apps (34) are some of the examples of mHealth application technologies. \r\nWearable device (fitness gadgets) has been used by individuals to self-monitor their sports performances. Recently, however, it has been widely used by researchers to quantify, promote and monitor physical activities (steps per day, distance travelled, energy expenditure) (33) sedentary time and heart rates (35). Mobile-based pedometer apps which are downloadable onto mobile phones from Google Play Store are also used to track walking steps and are widely used in epidemiological studies involving children, adolescents and adult population (34).  On the other hand, short text message is the most widely utilized mHealth application for smoking cessation, to improve medication adherence and reduce weight in the general population and patients (in clinical settings) (36, 37) as it can reach a wider population with low cost to improve intended health outcomes (30, 31). \r\nRationale: In low-income countries including Ethiopia, evidence of association of physical activity, dietary practices with hypertension and complications are mainly based on observational cross-sectional studies from participants’ self-reports (6, 38-40). This indicates  that evidence from quality studies are required to achieve a reduction of overall mortality related to hypertension and cardiovascular diseases (3). More importantly, evidence on sedentary behaviour, an independent risk factor of hypertension and hypertension-related complications regardless of other risk factors (41, 42), are limited in Ethiopia. \r\nSecondly, even though there are adequate interventional studies conducted globally on the effect of lifestyles interventions on hypertension (43), 10-year risk of cardiovascular diseases (44) and complications related to hypertension (45), most of these findings are from developed countries limiting the applications of these interventions to the Ethiopian context. Moreover, these findings are also heterogeneous due to the short duration of lifestyle interventions and using single interventions (e.g. only diet intervention or only physical activity interventions or only counselling). \r\nThirdly, recently there is a rapid growth of mHealth applications in health researches that address unhealthy lifestyles (46). As evidenced by systematic reviews, there are promising results of mHealth applications to promote healthy lifestyles, reduce sedentary behaviour (47-49), reduce high blood pressure,10-year cardiovascular risk (50-52), and complications related to hypertension (50, 53). However, even though Ethiopia has applied mHealth applications in areas of maternal health programs (54), utilization of mHealth technologies to promote healthy lifestyles, prevent and control chronic non-communicable diseases is limited especially in clinical settings. Due to these reasons, the application of multiple lifestyle interventions for longer follow-up duration (at least 6 months) is indispensable by using studies with rigorous methods and objectively measured lifestyle behaviours to support real-life-scenario especially in primary health care facilities in Ethiopia (55-57). To fill these gaps and to examine the effects of mHealth-based lifestyle interventions in clinical settings, we have designed a randomized controlled trial to address the following goal and objectives.','Methodology\r\nStudy setting: The study will be conducted in Jimma Medical Center located in Jimma town, Oromia, Southwest of Ethiopia. Jimma town is one of the towns of Jimma Zone located 352 KM Southwest of Addis Ababa, the capital. The Center is providing health care services for non-communicable diseases including hypertension and other non-communicable diseases for approximately 20 million populations in South-west Ethiopia. Currently, a total of N patients are receiving treatments and are on follow-up in chronic care units of which N are hypertensive patients The study will be conducted for one year (from Feb 2022 to Feb 2023).\r\nStudy design: This is a double-blinded, parallel-group, randomized controlled superiority trial with a 1:1 allocation ratio (fig 1).\r\nPopulation \r\nSource population: will be all adult patients (18-64 years) diagnosed with a systolic blood pressure of ≥ 140 mmHg and/or diastolic blood pressure ≥ 90 mmHg and who are receiving treatment for hypertension in Southwest Ethiopia. \r\nStudy population: will be all adult patients diagnosed with a systolic blood pressure of ≥ 140 mmHg and/or diastolic blood pressure ≥ 90 mmHg and who are receiving treatment for hypertension in the chronic follow-up unit of Jimma Medical Center (JMC\r\nInclusion and exclusion criteria\r\nInclusion criteria: Patients living in the catchment area of the JMC (in Southwest Ethiopia) and willing to continue treatment follow-up at JMC, willing to participate in the trial, with or without hypertension-related complications regardless of their body mass index will be included in the study.\r\nExclusion criteria: Patients who cannot perform intervention packages because of physical and mental conditions (e.g. serious mental illness, end-stage heart diseases, stroke and renal diseases), a person with a disability and pregnant woman will be excluded from the study. Moreover, patients in a control group with a similar disease in the neighbourhood of a patient in an intervention group will be excluded from the study to reduce the risk of information contamination. \r\nSample size and sampling procedure\r\nSample size: Sample size calculation for the trial is based on the primary hypothesis that states “hypertensive patients who receive counselling and mHealth-based healthy lifestyle interventions will have a clinically meaningful reduction in mean systolic blood pressure of at least 5mmHg compared to hypertension patients on usual care (µ1- µ2 =5 mmHg) with the standard deviation of 10 mmHg, power of 95% and 5% level of significance Moreover, Considering 4 data points (dp) to collect data and ICC=0.6) and 20% dropout rate, a total of 712 participants (89 for each group) on longitudinal base will be followed.\r\nSampling procedure: Patients will be recruited from the chronic care unit of JMC. The physician who will also ascertain the outcome will conduct screening using a checklist based on inclusion and exclusion criteria. A sampling frame containing lists of eligible patients will be prepared to identify participants of the study using computer-generated random numbers.  \r\nVariables\r\nPrimary outcome: Blood pressure (mean blood pressure). \r\nSecondary outcomes: 10-year risk of cardiovascular disease and quality of life. \r\nPredictors: Socio-demographics, knowledge, attitude, subjective norm, perceived behaviour control, lifestyle behaviours, dietary practices, metabolic risk factors and psychosocial factors. \r\n \r\nFigure 1: Patient flow (modified from CONSORT diagram) (58).\r\n\r\nMeasurement method and questionnaires\r\nBlood pressure	Blood pressure (BP) will be measured by using calibrated sphygmomanometer using WHO STEPs (Stepwise approach to surveillance) manual (59). Before measuring BP, the patient should take a rest for 5-10 minutes in a seated position comfortably on a chair putting both legs on flat ground without crossing them. The arm with the cuff should rest on a flat table at about the same height as the heart/chest. The cuff should be placed on a bare upper arm of 1.2 to 2.5 centimetres from the inner part of the elbow and the patient should be instructed not to talk during measurements. To reduce the risk of information bias, BP should be measured twice in both arms taking 3-5 minutes between measurements. Use the arm with the higher BP reading (difference of 15mmHg) for subsequent measurements.\r\n\r\n10-year CVDs risk:This is a composite variable used to estimate or predict the risk of having cardiovascular risks (myocardial infarction/heart attack, angina, ischemic stroke or transient attack) within the next 10 years for a person whose age is 25-84 years when it’s compared to a healthy person with the same age, sex and ethnicity. This risk score will be estimated by using the QRISK3 risk prediction algorithm (60). The algorithm utilizes various clinical and socio-demographic characteristics, family history of heart attack/angina in less than 60 years, metabolic and behavioural factors.\r\n\r\nPhysical measurements: Weight, height, waist and hip circumferences will be measured based on the WHO STEP manual (59).  Weight: is measured using a calibrated weighing scale. The patient should wear light clothes, remove footwear, and stand placing arms on each side. Values of the measurements will be recorded to the nearest decimal point. Height: is measured using a height measuring board. The patient is expected to remove the shoe and cover of the head putting feet together, heels against the backboard, knees straight and eyes at the same level as ears and stand straight facing forward. The measurement value is recorded in centimeters to the nearest one decimal point. Waist circumference: is measured by locating and marking the mid-point (using tape) between the inferior margin of the last rib (lowest point) and the crescent of the ilium (top of the hip bone). Using located mid-point (the narrow part of the waist), wrap the tape horizontally across the back and front of the patient to measure waist circumference. Measurement values are recorded using a centimetre to the nearest one decimal point.  Hip circumference: s measured from the level of the greater trochanter (on the largest part of the buttock). The patient is expected to stand feet together, place arms at each side with palms of hands facing inwards. Measurement values are recorded using a centimetre to the nearest one decimal point.\r\n\r\nBiochemical measurements: A 5-10 ml samples of fasting (12 hrs) blood sample will be collected from each patient to determine total cholesterol (TC), high-density lipoprotein (HDL), low-density lipoprotein (LDL), triglycerides, serum creatinine, protein, sodium, potassium and fasting blood glucose and HbA1C using standard laboratory methods. Blood samples will be immediately frozen following cold chain standards (at -20°C) until it will be analyzed within 72 hours and will be frozen at -80°C for further analysis.. Laboratory variations will be controlled using reference samples that are created in the laboratory. Necessary quality control and external standardization will be conducted using national laboratory criteria.  \r\n\r\nLifestyles: The physical activity of the patient will be measured based on the intensity of the physical activity recommended by the World Health Organization (13). The intensity of physical activity (sedentary behaviour, light, moderate and vigorous) will be measured at baseline, 3rd, 6th and 9th months (each time for seven days) by using energy expenditure. Energy expenditure will be measured by using a smart watch or mobile-based app. Total calories that will be measured for seven days will be divided by the weight of the patient to determine the energy expenditure in metabolic equivalent (61, 62). Moreover, the intensity of the physical activity will be assessed using International Physical Activity Questionnaire (IPAQ) (63). The total sedentary time during weekdays and weekends will be measured for nine domains using the Sedentary Behaviour Questionnaire (63). Moreover, the alcohol and smoking status of the patient will be measured using WHO STEPs instrument version 3.2 (64). \r\n\r\nDietary practice:	Dietary intake (fruit-vegetable, grains, legumes, root/tubers, fail and oil, sodium) will be measured directly from patients by using a food frequency questionnaires developed from food frequency questionnaires (65), WHO STEP version 3.2 (64) and Ethiopian context  (66). The amount of sodium will be determined using 24 hrs urine analysis. \r\n\r\nPsychosocial: Health-related Quality of life (HrQoL) will be measured directly from patients based on 8 domains related to physical functioning (physical and social functioning, physical and emotional limitation) and wellbeing (body pain, general and mental health and vitality). A short-form questionnaire with 36-items will be used to measure HrQoL. Stress and depression will be assessed using Perceived Stress Scale with 10 items (PSS-10) and the Patient Health Questionnaire of nine items (PHQ-9) (67) respectively.\r\n \r\nDescription of intervention packages \r\nCounselling: We have developed health messages based on the theory of planned behaviour (TPB) (68) using guidelines for physical activity  (9, 13) and healthy eating (10), hypertension lifestyle management (24). According to this theory, attitudes towards the behaviour, subjective norms and perceived behaviour control determines intention (readiness) and behaviour (69). At baseline, we will assess healthy lifestyle practice levels to immediately classify patients into the different levels of healthy lifestyles practice (very poor, poor, medium and high). . Based on these, the counsellor will have a discussion to increase knowledge and to change three constructs to increase intention to perform healthy lifestyles. The subsequent counselling sessions will be conducted to increase adherence to healthy lifestyles following each patient’s monthly routine follow-up schedules. We will use daily and weekly outputs of the mobile app to counsel each patient on physical activity and sedentary behaviour. Online videos on physical activities will be utilized to motivate (encourage) patients and physical exercise experts will also demonstrate how to perform physical activity exercises during follow-up.\r\nSpecifically, patients will be counselled to perform the following PA and dietary practices\r\n•	Healthy physical activity interventions: Based on his/her ability, each patient will be counselled to perform moderate (e.g. brisk walking, push/sit-ups, yoga exercise, video workouts) to vigorous (jogging, jumping jacks, running, fast uphill walking, and skipping rope) intensity physical activities. If the patient prefers to perform one or several moderate-intensity physical activities, s/he should perform for 30-60 minutes per day for five days. If the patient prefers to perform one or several vigorous-intensity physical activities, s/he should perform for 15-30 minutes per day for five days. The patient can also perform by combining moderate and vigorous PAs at a time.\r\n•	Interrupt prolonged sitting: the patients will also be counselled to limit sitting time by interrupting prolonged sitting of more than 20 minutes and replacing with PA of any intensity.\r\n•	Healthy dietary practice interventions: Patients will be counselled to practice healthy diet intake:  increasing fruit-vegetable, vegetable oils, whole grains, legumes, roots/tubes. Moreover, patients will be counselled to limit fat, saturated fatty acids, processed foods and salt.\r\nShort text messages: Each patient will receive healthy lifestyle messages that support patients to increase knowledge, change attitude and intention to perform the behaviour. Short text messages will be sent to every patient by using Jimma University’s SMS text messaging system/centre.  We have developed a total of 54 health messages to address physical activity, dietary practice and sedentary behaviour (12 messages to increase knowledge, 12 messages to change attitude and 12 messages to change practices,9messages to change subjective norm and 9 messages to promote perceived behaviour control).. At a time, three messages that address each lifestyle will be sent three times per week (Monday, Wednesday and Saturday) at 9:00 am. The principal investigator will receive similar health messages to verify the messages are delivered to each patient. \r\nWearable device (smart-watch): we will use a smart-watch to track and quantify the physical activity of each patient, including the number of steps per day, calories (energy expenditure in kcal) to monitor adherence to moderate to vigorous-intensity aerobic physical activity recommendations.  The device also provides a weekly summary of steps and calories in absolute numbers and graphs. The patient should wear the smart-watch on the wrist during working hours (8:00 am until the sleeping time at night). The patients should not remove their watch during working hours and before sleeping time except for safety reasons. However, the patients are expected to remove the device whenever they are going to sleep.\r\nMobile-based app: We will download and install a pedometer app from the Google play store onto the smartphone of each patient. Therefore, the patient who will not be able to perform such exercises will be instructed to start walking at least 4000 steps per day (which is equivalent to 30 minutes of brisk walking) (70). To have better health outcomes, a patient can also increase walking steps up to 10,000 per day (70, 71). After installing the app, we will verify whether it is counting steps and energy. The patient should carry his/her mobile whenever performing any physical activity that involves walking. Each patient will be instructed to charge his mobile in the morning before going for his/her daily routine activities or at lunch or night time. We will monitor the physical activities of each patient for whole intervention periods (9 months) as the app displays daily, weekly and monthly activities (steps and calories) graphically and in absolute number.\r\nIntervention procedure\r\nRandomization: An independent statistician will generate sequential random in a block size of six using the block randomization technique based on a 1:1 allocation ratio. Patients who fulfil inclusion criteria will be randomized to either intervention or control group. Patients in the intervention group will be assigned to receive counselling and mHealth-based lifestyle interventions in addition to  routine health care. Patients in the control group will continue receiving only routine care. The routine care provided in JMC for hypertensive patients includes pharmacologic treatments, non-pharmacologic treatments lifestyle advice), follow-up and clinical monitoring for hypertension and hypertension-related complications. \r\nAllocation: A list of eligible patients will be communicated to a physician who is not part of the investigating to assign patients to intervention and control group using randomly generated sequential alpha-numeric mixed codes each with a length of 4. The physician will register the group to which the patient is assigned. Data collectors, laboratory technologists, physicians (outcome ascertainer), clinical nurses (counsellors) and other health care professionals who will involve in the treatment of the patients will not have access to randomization details until the study is completed. Details of randomization implementation will be kept confidential and secured in a password-protected computer with the principal investigator. However, approving authorities such as Institutional Review Board (IRB) can have access to randomization details whenever required. \r\nBlinding: Laboratory technologists, data collectors and outcome ascertainers will access only patients and the code of the patients during data collection. Even though investigating team can access patients and patients’ data, they will not be involved in the data collection process. Even though it’s not possible to fully blind patients, we will try to minimize information contamination by providing similar advice at baseline and installing a mobile app for both groups. Counsellors (because they provide counselling), and patient supervisors (because they install the mobile app and provide detailed advice on how to utilize the app) will not be blinded ().  \r\nData collection methods and procedure \r\nData collection techniques:  Face-to-face interviewer-administered questionnaires will be used to collect data on socio-demographics, household assets (wealth index), lifestyles (physical activity, dietary practice and sedentary behaviour), psychosocial (depression, stress), and quality of life. Physical and biochemical measurements will be conducted to collect metabolic risk factors (cholesterol, weights, height, electrolytes, urine sodium, fasting blood glucose, waist and hip circumferences) using checklists. A checklist will be used to collect clinical data related to complications/organ damages (heart and renal, peripheral arterial diseases) and comorbidities (diabetes) from the medical records of the patients.\r\nBaseline data collection: Trained data collectors (physician and clinical nurse) will collect outcomes and risk factors data at baseline. Moreover, seven-day physical activities of each patient will be measured at baseline using the smart-watch and mobile app.\r\nFollow-up visits: A physician and patient supervisor will administer intervention packages immediately after baseline data collection is completed. Outcomes and risk factors data will be collected from each patient at 3rd month, 6th month and 9th month follow-up visits using a similar procedure as in baseline assessment. Each patient will be followed for 9 months. The patient supervisor (PS) will follow each patient in the field (by reminding them of days of counselling and next data collection time). PS will also support patients by addressing any difficulties related to intervention packages and other issues (related to our study). Moreover, we will provide a reminder card for the next counselling and data collection date. Moreover, whenever a patient visits the centre, a patient supervisor will facilitate contact between data collectors, counsellors and physicians.  \r\nPre-test\r\nInstruments and Intervention packages:  All questionnaires and intervention packages will be pre-tested on 5% of non-participating hypertensive patients. During the pre-test, each patient will receive 45 translated health messages in Afan Oromo or Amharic every morning via designed SMS text messages. The physical activity of each patient will also be measured using the smartwatch and mobile-based app for seven days. Feedback will be collected from each patient to improve content and language clarity of health messages and other challenges related to using the smartwatch and mobile app. Moreover, an experienced physician will evaluate health messages in terms of contents, language clarity and number of texts. Feedback will be collected by experts to improve the questionnaire and health messages. \r\nOperational definitions: will be detailed in the full proposal.\r\nData quality control\r\nData collectors and supervisors will be given extensive training on physical measurements, interview techniques and how to collect blood samples, transport and store them for biochemical analysis. The pre-test of the instruments and intervention packages will be conducted before data collection. Questionnaire and health messages will be translated to local languages by local language experts. The findings on the pre-test will be used to modify tools and health messages. Data collectors and supervisors will participate in the pre-test study to improve the data collection and supervision process. Supervisors will strictly follow the data collection process, check the filled questionnaires on daily basis during data collection periods. Data entry errors will be eliminated by using epi-data version 3.1 for double entry verification. \r\nData analysis plan\r\nData processing: Data will be entered into epi-data version 3.1 and analyzed using STATA version 13. All descriptive statistics will be computed to explore data before analysis. The intention to treat analysis (ITT) approach will be used to analyse the whole data. Normality distribution of outcome variable and continuous covariates will be checked using graphs, plots and statistical tests. Normality of variance of the intercept and slope, residues (error) will also be checked. \r\nModel specification for primary outcome: since this is a longitudinal study with repeated measures of 4 data points, the outcome data of the individual subject that will be measured at different data points are correlated one another (ICC).  To account for this correlation between measurements, we will include each participant (“subject”) in the analysis to increase statistical power and to have a representative sample of the population. The evidence of correction (ICC) between repeated observations will also be explored to select an appropriate covariance structure for the analysis. We also expect that follow-up time for each subject will also not be the same. Therefore, we will include “time” as an interaction term with other covariates and will be included in the analysis. To control the effect of missing and effects of clustering, fixed effects and random effects model with random intercept and random slope model will be fitted using maximum likelihood and restricted maximum likelihood estimation method respectively. \r\nFixed effects model will be used to estimate overall population intercept and overall population slope for each covariate (exposures and potential confounders). Using a stepwise likelihood ratio test, covariates will be identified for the next model. The findings of the fixed effects model will be presented using coefficients, standard errors and 95%CIs for the coefficients. For the random intercept and slope model, individual subjects will be considered in the regression model to account for the effect of subjects on their repeated measurements (i.e. individual variability in blood pressure at baseline and across time after intervention). Individual-specific intercept and slope, the variance of intercept, slope, residues, and correlation structure (ICC) will be estimated. Compound symmetric variance and/or unstructured variance matrix will be used to model the covariance structure of random intercept and slope. Effects of mHealth on 10-years CVDs risk will be analysed using a generalized logistic regression model. Effects of mHealth on quality of life and the predictors will be treated as a continuous variable and analysed using a linear mixed model. Other variables such as wealth index and dietary practice will be analysed using principal component analysis (PCA). \r\nEthical consideration \r\nThe study will be conducted as per Ethiopian national ethics guidelines. Ethical clearance will be received from the IRB of Jimma University. The trial will also be registered in the Clinical Trials Registry. Verbal and written informed consent will be received from each eligible patient before randomization. There is no potential harm that will happen to patients except they will feel minimal pain during blood collection and time during data collection. The potential benefit and harm of participating in the study will be explained for each patient verbally and in a written information sheet using the language of the patient. Blood samples collected from each patient will only be utilized for research purposes. Patient-specific codes will be utilized on data collection instruments and blood samples to keep the confidentiality of the patients. Male and female data collectors will collect physical measurements; waist and hip circumferences, in a private room.  The patient supervisor will strictly follow patients by calling them every month. Patients will also be informed to report if there are any health concerns related to their participation in the study (especially on physical activity and dietary preferences). If there are any health encounters to patients, (e.g. falling accident during physical activity), they will be advised to immediately visit JMC so that s/he will be linked to treatment facilities and to investigate the cause of the accident and provide further counselling. Trial participation has no effect on the patient’s management and treatment process and the participation in the trial is totally based on the full willingness of the patient.','<h1 style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:14pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Cambria,&quot;serif&quot;\"><span style=\"color:#365f91\">References </span></span></span></h1>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">1.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Kearney PM, Whelton M, Reynolds K, Muntner P, Whelton PK, He J. Global burden of hypertension: analysis of worldwide data. Lancet (London, England). 2005;365(9455):217-23.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">2.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Whelton PK, He J, Appel LJ, Cutler JA, Havas S, Kotchen TA, et al. Primary prevention of hypertension: clinical and public health advisory from The National High Blood Pressure Education Program. Jama. 2002;288(15):1882-8.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">3.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Organization WH. Global status report on noncommunicable diseases 2014: World Health Organization; 2014.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">4.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; World Health Organiztion W. Hyertension. available at <a href=\"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/hypertension\" style=\"color:blue; text-decoration:underline\">https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/hypertension</a> August 2021.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">5.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Organization WH. A global brief on hypertension: silent killer, global public health crisis: World Health Day 2013. World Health Organization; 2013.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">6.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Amare F, Hagos B, Sisay M, Molla B. Uncontrolled hypertension in Ethiopia: a systematic review and meta-analysis of institution-based observational studies. BMC cardiovascular disorders. 2020;20(1):129.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">7.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Ali S, Misganaw A, Worku A, Destaw Z, Negash L, Bekele A, et al. The burden of cardiovascular diseases in Ethiopia from 1990 to 2017: evidence from the Global Burden of Disease Study. International health. 2020;13(4):318-26.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">8.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbs