</span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:2px solid black; border-left:2px solid black; border-right:none; border-top:none; height:35px; width:173px\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"><span style=\"color:black\">May 29, 2022</span></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:2px solid black; border-left:2px solid black; border-right:2px solid black; border-top:none; height:35px; width:183px\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"><span style=\"color:black\">June 10, 2022</span></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n		</tr>\r\n	</tbody>\r\n</table>\r\n\r\n<h2 style=\"margin-left:24px\">&nbsp;</h2>\r\n\r\n<h1 style=\"margin-left:24px\"><span style=\"font-size:14pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">5. Expected Outputs/Outcomes and significance </span></span></h1>\r\n\r\n<table cellspacing=\"0\" class=\"Table\" style=\"border-collapse:collapse; width:645px\">\r\n	<tbody>\r\n		<tr>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:2px solid black; border-left:2px solid black; border-right:2px solid black; border-top:2px solid black; height:33px; width:253px\">\r\n			<p style=\"margin-left:50px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"><span style=\"color:black\"><strong>Process indicator</strong></span></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:2px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:2px solid black; border-top:2px solid black; height:33px; width:212px\">\r\n			<p style=\"margin-left:50px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"><span style=\"color:black\"><strong>Outcome indicator</strong></span></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:2px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:2px solid black; border-top:2px solid black; height:33px; width:180px\">\r\n			<p style=\"margin-left:2px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"><span style=\"color:black\"><strong>Impact indicator</strong></span></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n		</tr>\r\n		<tr>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:2px solid black; border-left:2px solid black; border-right:2px solid black; border-top:none; height:142px; vertical-align:top; width:253px\">\r\n			<ul>\r\n				<li><span style=\"font-size:10pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">Adoption of the system institute wide.</span></span></span></span></li>\r\n				<li><span style=\"font-size:10pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">Users /researchers feedback for using the system and </span></span></span></span></li>\r\n			</ul>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:2px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:2px solid black; border-top:none; height:142px; vertical-align:top; width:212px\">\r\n			<ul>\r\n				<li><span style=\"font-size:10pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">Reduction of burden of colleges regarding to proposal collection and editorial management</span></span></span></span></li>\r\n				<li><span style=\"font-size:10pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">Report management facilitate efficiency for submission and publication of terminal report</span></span></span></span></li>\r\n			</ul>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:2px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:2px solid black; border-top:none; height:142px; vertical-align:top; width:180px\">\r\n			<ul>\r\n				<li><span style=\"font-size:10pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">Reduction of work load , paper work and timelines for editors and researchers.</span></span></span></span></li>\r\n			</ul>\r\n			</td>\r\n		</tr>\r\n	</tbody>\r\n</table>',1,NULL,NULL,NULL),(56,30,9,19,'Antimicrobial medications has protected millions of lives and avoided indescribable suffering, however, the surge of antimicrobial resistant pathogens puts a multifactorial challenge in patient care process, including increase in mortality, expenditure of health-care budget, direct and indirect patient cost, compromise patient quality of life, and reduces the option of using the available antimicrobials. Pediatrics is special populations who are highly vulnerable to infectious disease; as a result antimicrobials are the most widely prescribed agents for the pediatric patients globally. Similarly, inappropriate antimicrobial prescriptions are common in pediatrics a patient which is the major factors for antimicrobial resistance. \r\nThe expansion of digital aid use in healthcare system simplifies the challenges faced by health care providers, including the emergency of antimicrobial resistance.  As a result, computer support antimicrobial prescribing plays an important role in guiding the decision-making processes such as an appropriate selection of antimicrobials, dose, safety, and drug resistance profile. Despite evidences from high-income countries revealed paramount benefit of digital health care, including computer aided prescription; studies in low-income countries are limited. Therefore, the aim of this quasi-experimental preliminary study is to assess the impact of pocket sized computer aid antibiotic prescription on antimicrobial resistant and treatment outcome among patients admitted at the pediatric ward of Jimma medical center, Ethiopia. The finding of the study will serve as an input for developing local Antimicrobial Stewardship program based on the local microbial profile and a baseline for developing nation-wide computer aid prescriptions to tackle antibiotic resistance.',NULL,'The impact of pocket-sized computer aid antimicrobial prescription on antimicrobial resistant and treatment outcome among patients admitted at the pediatric ward of Jimma medical center; A quasi-experimental study design','2021-11-19 18:52:57','Overall, the primary justification for assessing the impact of computer aided prescription in pediatrics patient and its subsequent effect on the occurrence of adverse patient outcomes is to gather important data and analyze it to compare the routine prescription trend versus computer aided prescription rate of in-hospital complication and its effect change on in-hospital complication, mortality, and length of hospitalization as well as antibiotic prescription pattern. Appropriate prescription and rational use of antibiotics are a timely issue to tackle AMR worldwide; it needs a special concern in low-income countries, where the burden is more than double. In Ethiopian antimicrobial stewardship program in place with an overall objective of achieving rational use of antimicrobials and reducing the burden of AMR including mortality, complications and length hospitalization.  Furthermore, computer-assisted antibiotics prescribing significantly reduce mortality, disease complication, volume of antibiotic usage, length of hospital stay and antibiotic resistance. Our study will help to gather important information on the impact of intervention such as: \r\n	Helps to know the magnitude of change in antimicrobial resistant profile.  \r\n	Helps to know the magnitude of change in rate of in-hospital complication \r\n	Helps to know the magnitude of change in hospital mortality \r\n	Helps to know the change in-length of hospitalization\r\n	Helps to know  change in antibiotic prescription pattern','1b8d7e9ab9add255f3de626270679b0f','completed','Pediatrics are highly vulnerable to infectious disease as result of immature immunity, behavioral characteristics such as inadequate hygiene, frequent mouthing of hands and objects, drooling, and direct contact with children during play, and the breakdown of their normal defenses by disease, invasive procedures or therapy (1). As a result, antimicrobials are the most widely prescribed agents across the world for the pediatric patients (2). Despite antimicrobial saves millions of lives and avoids indescribable sufferings, the growing burden of antimicrobial resistance(AMR) results patient care process in danger (3,4).  \r\nAccording to the World Health Organization (WHO), AMR is one of the top 10 global public health threats facing human health (5). The various factors are contributed to AMR incidence, including inadequate diagnostic procedure, inappropriate use of antimicrobials for patient care process and agriculture purpose (6). According to WHO report, more than two-third of prescriptions are antimicrobial, of which, 30%-50% of them are irrational, which substantially increases AMR (7–9). \r\nAntimicrobial resistance threatens health care services and patient outcomes such as increased complications, prolonged hospital stays, adverse drug effects, health care costs, and death (7–9). Furthermore, without pressing action, the AMR causes serious concern over public health, we are in the direction for a post-antibiotic era, in which common infections and minor wounds can easily kill anyone (7).  Annually, WHO reports that more than 25,000 people death and an estimated cost of 1.5 billion euros wasted as a result of AMR in developed countries. Even if there is no concert data on the impact of antimicrobial resistance in low- and middle-income (LMI) countries, the burden is estimated to be a double in in LMI countries (10). \r\nGlobally, pediatrics populations are at high risk of having inappropriate antimicrobials prescription (2) because of misuse of it in viral respiratory tract infections, overuse of broad spectrum antibiotic in respiratory tract infections and urinary tract infections (11). The rate of inappropriate antibiotic prescription are varied across the countries, for instance, a study from United states of America, 21.0% (12), Spain, 51.9% (13), and turkey, 56.10% (14) of prescriptions were inappropriate. \r\nIn Ethiopia, on average, antimicrobials covered 50% of the hospital’s budget and contributed 56% all prescribed medications, and which was as high as 86% in pediatric ward of Jimma medical center. The appropriateness of  antibiotics prescription varies across different setups in our country (15–17). A study from Tigray revealed 28.3% (18), 10.1% from Shenen Gibe Hospital (19), and 58.07% from Dessie Referral Hospital (20) of patients antibiotics prescription were inappropriate.  \r\nIn view of all the problems listed above, AMR requires an immediate intervention to ameliorate its impact using different approaches, such as prescriber’s education and training as well as clinical pharmacist’s incorporation in the patient care process.  However, the burden of inappropriate antimicrobials prescription and related AMR are not decreased as expected (21).  As a result, WHO recommends and warns the urgent behavioral change towards antimicrobial prescriptions and use to tackle antibiotic resistance (5). \r\nAs gold opportunities, emerging health information technology simplifies the challenges faced by health care providers. Computer support prescription is one of emerging technology, which is the use of any software designed to facilitate treatment decisions where the characteristics of certain patients are adapted to a computer database to facilitate individual patient treatment used by prescribers (22–24). As a result, computer aid antibiotic prescription plays an important role in optimizing antimicrobial use such as  antibiotics appropriate selection , dose, safety, and drug resistance profile and improving patient outcomes (25). Studies showed that computer-assisted antimicrobial prescription significantly reduce mortality, disease complication, volume of antibiotic usage, length of hospital stay, and antimicrobial resistance (21,24–26). \r\nIn our country, despite almost all prescribers have smartphones and/ or tablets, as far as our knowledge is concerned, there is no data on utilizing this material for medication prescription to improve rational antibiotic utilization and tackling AMR at health facilities. Therefore, this study will assess the impact of pocket-sized computer aid prescription on AMR and in-hospital complication, mortality and length of hospital stay among pediatrics patients admitted to the pediatric ward of Jimma medical center. The finding of the study will serve as an input for developing local Antimicrobial Stewardship program based on the local microbial profile and a baseline for developing nation-wide computer aid prescriptions to tackle antibiotic resistance','Method\r\nStudy setting and period\r\nThe study will be conducted at the pediatric ward of the Jimma Medical Center (JMC). JMC is one of the oldest public referral hospitals in Ethiopia, which was established in 1930. It is located in Jimma town, 352 km South West of Addis Ababa. JMC is the only teaching and referral hospital in the South Western part of Ethiopia with bed capacity of 659. It provides services for approximately 15,000 inpatient and 160,000 outpatient clients a year with a catchment population of about 15 million people. The study will be conducted over six months January to June, 2022 (the first 3 month for control period and the next 3 month for the intervention period).\r\nStudy design \r\nA prospective facility based quasi-experimental study design (i.e. Posttest only nonequivalent groups design) will be conducted. Pediatrics patients recruited before delivering computer aid prescription for prescribers will considered as control group, while patients enrolled after delivering computer aid prescription will considered as treatment group. \r\nPopulation\r\nSource population\r\nAll pediatric patients diagnosed with infectious diseases at pediatric ward of JMC. \r\nStudy population\r\nAll pediatrics patients diagnosed with infectious diseases at pediatric ward of JMC and fulfilling the inclusion criteria.\r\nInclusion criteria\r\n	Patient age ≤ 15 years,\r\n	Patients diagnosed with infectious disease and taking at least one antibiotic,\r\n	Patients guard/parents willing to participate in the study\r\nExclusion criteria	\r\n	Patients taking antibiotics without any infectious disease diagnosis. \r\n	Patients participated in pre-interventional study period will not participate in the post-intervention period, \r\n	Patients taking other antimicrobials such as antivirals and antifungals only,\r\n	Repeated admission (same patient admitted multiple times in either of study period) during the study period.\r\nSample size estimation and sampling technique\r\nThe sample size(n) required for the study are calculated using a double population proportion formula(27) with the following assumption considering confidence level of 95%, α = 0.05, the critical value (Z) =1.96), power of 80% ( Z β), the likely proportion of in antimicrobial resistance rate in pediatric ward Before intervention (p1= 54.5%)(28) and the likely sample proportion of Antimicrobial resistance rate in pediatric ward after the intervention ((p2=48.8%) (29). \r\nn=(2〖(Z α/2+Z β)〗^2 p (1-p))⁄((p1-p2)2 )\r\nn=(2〖(1.96+0.842)〗^2 0.517(1-0.517))⁄((0.545-0.488)^2=) 625\r\nThe minimum sample size required for each group (control and interventional group) will be 625 to detect whether the stated difference exists between the two proportions. A consecutive sampling technique will be used to recruit the needed sample size for both control and intervention period.\r\nInterventional materials and implementation \r\nAntibiotic prescription support software/application will be developed that contain common infectious disease type, pathogens identified, antibiotic susceptibility and resistance profiles of common bacteria isolated from each patients in the pediatric ward. The system has two components: Web application and Android application. The web application is used by system administrators or investigators to feed initial data and to update existing information on common type of infectious disease, common pathogens identified in each infectious disease, respective antibiotic susceptibility and resistant profile from the control group participants. An Android application will be easily downloaded, installed, and used by prescribers in their pocket sized tablet or smartphone device. The system allowed the user to browse common type of infectious disease, common pathogens identified in each infectious disease, respective antibiotic susceptibility and resistant data with the Android application from his/her smartphone/tablets. When large amounts of information are present, they will be displayed across several pages to reduce unimportant scrolling on long pages. Synchronization will be used for updating information saved on the devices and gathering usage logs that specified what decision support will be used and when. Before the interventional study period started, the training will be given to all health professionals who are responsible to prescribe antibiotics on how to use the designed system.\r\nData collection tool and procedure \r\nThe data collection tool will be designed after reviewing relevant literatures (15,16). The tool will have five parts: Part I: Socio-demographic variables; Part II: Clinical variables; Part III: Medication-related variables; Part IV: bacterial cultures and susceptibility profile and Part V: Outcome variables.  All clinical endpoints of patients on daily basis from admission to discharge, and prescribed antibiotics profile and laboratory tests results of the patient will be recorded. The data will be collected by seven data collectors (2 B.Pharm in pharmacy, 2 BSc nurse, and 3 Medical Laboratory professionals) and supervised by 2 MPH professionals. All eligible patients’ will be enrolled at admission to the ward and followed till discharge. Patients will be followed strictly during their hospital stay and all relevant data will be collected from the patient chart, laboratory results, and patient’s caregiver`s interview. Pathogen culture or susceptibility tests will be done for each patient followed during both control and intervention period. For in-hospital mortality time when the event occurred will be recorded. Prescription related indictors will be measured according to WHO developed formula and compared to acceptable standard level (30).\r\nStudy Variables\r\nCovariates/independent Variables\r\nSocio-demographic variables:-sex, age, weight, and residence.\r\nClinical variables:-length of hospital stay (LOS), referral information, medical history, previous history of hospital admission, diseases (types and number), surgical procedure during hospitalization, laboratory tests (ESR, complete blood count, and others), vital signs (PR, RR, body temperature), anthropometric findings, complications, and pediatrics admission unit (NRU, oncology, surgery, NICU, level-l and ll).\r\n\r\nMedication-related variables:-previous history of antibiotic use, antibiotic dosage regimen of medications (at admission, during hospital stay, and at discharge), number of medications (at admission, during hospital stay, and at discharge).\r\nCulture and susceptibility finding:-Pathogens identified, to which antibiotic resistant and to which susceptible. \r\nOutcomes/dependent variables\r\nThe outcomes of the study are:\r\n	Change in the magnitude of antimicrobial resistant \r\n	Change in the duration of hospitalization \r\n	Change in the proportion of in-hospital complications\r\n	Change in the proportion of in-hospital mortality\r\n	Change in the proportion of antimicrobial prescription pattern\r\nData management and statistical analysis\r\nData will be entered into Epi data version 4.2.0.0 and exported to STATA 15.0 for analysis. Categorical variables will be described using frequency and percentage. Parametric continuous variables will be described using mean and standard deviations (SD), whereas for non-parametric continuous variables, median and percentiles will be used. Two samples Z test will be employed to determine the rate of in-hospital complication and mortality difference among interventional and control group.  Two-sample student T-test will be conducted to see the difference in length of hospitalization among the interventional and control group. All variables will be used in the bivariate logistic regression. P-value < 0.25 will consider as a cut-off point to select variables on binary logistic regression for multivariable logistic regression to determine independent determinants of clinical outcomes. A two-tailed p-value < 0.05 will be considered statistically significant. Defined Daily Dose (DDD) per patient, where DDDs will be measure the effect of an intervention on antibiotics use, examine changes in antibiotic utilization over time and make international comparisons. \r\nData Quality assurance\r\nThe following will be made to assure data quality\r\n	Participants will be made familiar with the data collection tool and procedure through training.\r\n	Pre-test will be conducted prior to the actual data collection and the tool will be amended accordingly.\r\n	Data completeness will be checked regularly.\r\n	Before analysis, data will be checked for completeness and accuracy. \r\nOperational definition\r\nPocket sized computer: Refers smartphones and tablet that can be handled in pocket. \r\nComputer aid prescription: using an application support to prescribe antibiotics for the patient. \r\nTreatment outcome: Are patient outcomes recorded during hospitalization period at pediatrics ward such as in-hospital complication, mortality, and length of hospitalization. \r\nPlan for dissemination and ensuring utilization of findings \r\nThe result of the study will be presented to the Institute of health, JUMC, Ministry of Health, and as well to concerned professional associations, like Ethiopian pharmaceutical association (EPA), Ethiopian public health association and others. Attempts will be made to present findings on both national and international scientific conferences and to publish on peer reviewed reputable journal.\r\nOperational definition\r\nPocket sized computer: Refers smartphones and tablet that can be handled in pocket \r\nComputer aid prescribing: Prescribes using computer data to prescribe antimicrobial treatment for his/her patients \r\nPatient clinical treatment outcome: Are the results occurred due to treatment intervention during hospitalization period \r\nEthical considerations\r\nEthical approval &amp; clearance will be obtained from Institutional Review Board (IRB) of Jimma University. A written informed consent will be requested from each study participant. Upon collection of data and completion of the study, all raw data will be kept strictly confidential. The patient will be informed their full right not to participate and as well as leave the study at any time during the study period.','<p style=\"text-align:justify\">&nbsp;</p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">1. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Moore DL. Essentials of paediatric infection control. Paediatr Child Health (Oxford). 2001;6(8):571&ndash;9. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">2. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Karataş Y, Khan Z. Antibiotic usage in the pediatric population: The need for effective role of parents and prescribers. Guncel Pediatr. 2021;19(1):135&ndash;40. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">3. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Aminov RI. The role of antibiotics and antibiotic resistance in nature. Environ Microbiol. 2009;11(12):2970&ndash;88. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">4. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Filice GA, Drekonja DM, Thurn JR, Rector TS, Hamann GM, Masoud BT, et al. Use of a Computer Decision Support System and Antimicrobial Therapy Appropriateness. Infect Control Hosp Epidemiol. 2013;34(6):558&ndash;65. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">5. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; World Health Organization. Antibiotic resistance [Internet]. [cited 2021 Nov 19]. Available from: https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/antibiotic-resistance?__cf_chl_captcha_tk__=JaidXVpcMo69yE2fZ4vm6Be5_BkxhkYGC..hpGR9W4A-1637304447-0-gaNycGzNCKU</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">6. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Disease National institue of allergay and infectious. Causes of Antimicrobial (Drug) Resistance [Internet]. [cited 2021 Nov 19]. Available from: https://www.niaid.nih.gov/research/antimicrobial-resistance-causes</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">7. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Zucca M, Savoia D. The post-antibiotic era: Promising developments in the therapy of infectious diseases. Int J Biomed Sci. 2010;6(2):77&ndash;86. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">8. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; World Health organization. Global Report on Surveillance 2014. WHO 2014 AMR Rep [Internet]. 2014;1&ndash;8. Available from: http://www.who.int/drugresistance/documents/AMR_report_Web_slide_set.pdf</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">9. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Zarb P, Amadeo B, Muller A, Drapier N, Vankerckhoven V, Davey P, et al. Identification of targets for quality improvement in antimicrobial prescribing: The web-based ESAC point prevalence survey 2009. J Antimicrob Chemother. 2011;66(2):443&ndash;9. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">10. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Wozniak TM, Barnsbee L, Lee XJ, Pacella RE. Using the best available data to estimate the cost of antimicrobial resistance: A systematic review. Antimicrob Resist Infect Control. 2019;8(1):1&ndash;12. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">11. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Miao R, Wan C, Wang Z, Zhu Y, Zhao Y, Zhang L, et al. Inappropriate antibiotic prescriptions among pediatric inpatients in different type hospitals. Med (United States). 2020;99(2). </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">12. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Bethany A Wattles , Kahir S Jawad , Yana Feygin , Maiying Kong , Navjyot K Vidwan , Michelle D Stevenson MJS. Inappropriate outpatient antibiotic use in children insured by Kentucky Medicaid. Infect Control Hosp Epidemiol [Internet]. 2021; Available from: https://doi.org/10.1007/s12630-019-01405-9</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">13. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Croche Santander B, Campos Alonso E, S&aacute;nchez Carri&oacute;n A, Marcos Fuentes L, Diaz Flores I, Vargas JC, et al. Appropriateness of antibiotic prescribing in paediatric patients in a hospital emergency department. An Pediatr&iacute;a (English Ed. 2018;88(5):259&ndash;65. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">14. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Oğuz E, Bebitoğlu BT, Nuhoğlu &Ccedil;, &Ccedil;ağ Y, Hodzic A, Temel F, et al. Evaluation of antibiotic use among hospitalised patients in a paediatric department of a training hospital in Turkey. Int J Clin Pract. 2021;75(3):0&ndash;3. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">15. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Kebede HK, Gesesew HA, Woldehaimanot TE, Goro KK. Antimicrobial use in paediatric patients in a teaching hospital in Ethiopia. PLoS One. 2017;12(3). </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">16. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Desta Z, Abula T, Gebre-Yohannes A, Worku A. Drug prescribing patterns for outpatients in three hospitals in north-west Ethiopia. Vol. 16, Ethiopian Journal of Health Development. 2002. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">17. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Woldu MA, Suleman S, Workneh N, Berhane H. Retrospective study of the pattern of antibiotic use in Hawassa University referral hospital pediatric ward, southern Ethiopia. J Appl Pharm Sci. 2013;3(2):93&ndash;8. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">18. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Yehualaw A, Taferre C, Bantie AT, Demsie DG. Appropriateness and Pattern of Antibiotic Prescription in Pediatric Patients at Adigart General Hospital, Tigray, Ethiopia. Biomed Res Int. 2021;2021. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">19. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; T A, M M. Retrospective Drug Use pattern of Antibiotics in Pediatric Ward of Shenan Gibe Hospital, Oromia Region, Ethiopia. J Antibiot Res. 2017;1(1):1&ndash;10. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">20. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Getachew E. Antibiotic prescribing pattern in a referral hospital in Ethiopia. African J Pharm Pharmacol. 2013;7(38):2657&ndash;61. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">21. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Yourman L, Concato J, Agostini J V. Use of computer decision support interventions to improve medication prescribing in older adults: A systematic review. Am J Geriatr Pharmacother. 2008;6(2):119&ndash;29. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">22. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Baysari MT, Lehnbom EC, Li L, Hargreaves A, Day RO, Westbrook JI. The effectiveness of information technology to improve antimicrobial prescribing in hospitals: A systematic review and meta-analysis. Int J Med Inform [Internet]. 2016;92:15&ndash;34. Available from: http://dx.doi.org/10.1016/j.ijmedinf.2016.04.008</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">23. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Calloway S, Akilo H, Bierman K. Impact of a clinical decision support system on pharmacy clinical interventions, documentation efforts, and costs. Hosp Pharm. 2013;48(9):744&ndash;52. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">24. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Curtis CE, Al Bahar F, Marriott JF. The effectiveness of computerised decision support on antibiotic use in hospitals: A systematic review. PLoS One. 2017;12(8):1&ndash;15. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">25. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Randell R, Mitchell N, Dowding D, Cullum N, Thompson C. Effects of computerized decision support systems on nursing performance and patient outcomes: A systematic review. J Heal Serv Res Policy. 2007;12(4):242&ndash;9. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">26. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Holstiege J, Mathes T, Pieper D. Effects of computer-aided clinical decision support systems in improving antibiotic prescribing by primary care providers: A systematic review. J Am Med Informatics Assoc. 2014;22(1):236&ndash;42. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">27. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Charan J, Biswas T. How to calculate sample size for different study designs in medical research? Indian J Psychol Med. 2013;35(2):121&ndash;6. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">28. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Abera A, Tilahun M, Tekele SG, Belete MA. Prevalence, Antimicrobial Susceptibility Patterns, and Risk Factors Associated with Enterococci among Pediatric Patients at Dessie Referral Hospital, Northeastern Ethiopia. Biomed Res Int. 2021;2021. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">29. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Arya SC, Agarwal N, Agarwal S. Reduction of broad-spectrum antibiotic use with computerized decision support in an intensive care unit. Int J Qual Heal Care. 2006;18(5):389. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">30. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; World Health Organization. How to investigate drug use in health facilities: Selected drug use indicators. WHO/DAP/931. 1993;1(1&ndash;97):92. </span></span></span></span></p>','To assess the impact of pocket-sized computer aid antimicrobial prescription on antimicrobial resistant and treatment outcome among patients admitted at the pediatric ward of Jimma medical center',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,2,'pocket-sized computer aid antimicrobial prescription,Jimma medical center,pediatrics,antimicrobial resistance',10,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),(57,29,9,18,'Diabetes mellitus is a chronic disease marked by prolonged hyperglycemia. The basis for the occurrence of diabetes mellitus is insulin secretion defects or resistance in peripheral insulin action or both. Diabetes mellitus is nowadays epidemic both in developed and developing countries affecting urban and rural residents of worldwide. Diabetes mellitus is a long-standing and critical disorder that has a negative role in the lives and wellbeing of individuals, families, and societies globally. It becomes one of the condition top-ranking responsible for mortality in adults. The prevalence of diabetes mellitus in Sub-Saharan countries is increased from time to time. Ethiopia is found in the top five among Sub-Saharan countries having high numbers of diabetes mellitus cases. Human beings had been utilized plants in different forms to combat diseases and promote health as far as the establishment of humanity. The nutritional approach is one of the strategies for the management of diabetes mellitus which is supportive in lessening diabetic complications danger. Green coffee has chlorogenic acids and anti-inflammatory effects. DM has no effective modern treatments. The only available treatment is lifelong modern medicine having associated health side effects and their costly prices. This paves way for herbal therapy with no or least side effects to control and treat DM.  In Ethiopia, there is a scarcity of information about the effects of green coffee on various health complications, including diabetes mellitus in both human subjects and animal models. Therefore, the present study is planned to investigate the anti-diabetic effect of hydro-methanolic extract of green coffee beans in STZ-induced diabetic mice. In Ethiopia, there is a scarcity of information about the effects of green coffee on various health complications, including diabetes mellitus in both human subjects and animal models. Therefore, the present study is planned to investigate the anti-diabetic effect of hydro-methanolic extract of green coffee beans in STZ-induced diabetic mice. The collected data will be entered into Epidata and exported to SPSS for further analysis. ANOVA will be applied to determine the anti-diabetic effect of hydro-methanolic extract of green coffee among different groups of mice (control groups, exposure groups, and treatment groups with different crude extracts) at different time intervals. A p-value of <0.05 will be declared as statistically significant. Based on the findings, conclusion will be drawn and recommendations will be forwarded. A total of 429,000.00Ethiopian birr is required to accomplish the study.','anti-diabetic effect of hydro-methanolic extract of green coffee beans in STZ-induced diabetic mice','Evaluation of anti-diabetic effect of hydro-methanolic extract of green coffee beans in STZ-induced diabetic mice','2021-11-19 12:15:29','The research outcome of the project will have the advantage to develop therapeutic strategies and in controlling progression and complication of the disease. Furthermore, it provides information for clinical trial on diabetes mellitus.','8960395dfd41ab20c7140300e6f0ea17','completed','Diabetes mellitus is a chronic disease marked by prolonged hyperglycemia. The basis for the occurrence of diabetes mellitus is insulin secretion defects or resistance in peripheral insulin action or both (1). Diabetes mellitus is nowadays epidemic both in developed and developing countries affecting urban and rural residents of worldwide  (2, 3). Based on pancreatic beta cell status diabetes mellitus is classified into four types: - type 1 diabetes mellitus, type 2 diabetes mellitus, gestational diabetes mellitus and other types of diabetes mellitus (4). Diabetes mellitus is developed by various complex mechanisms with multifactorial pathways. Course of diabetes mellitus is determined by interplay among genes, lifestyle, environment and oxidative stress (5).  \r\n\r\nDiabetes mellitus is a long-standing and critical disorder that has a negative role in the lives and wellbeing of individuals, families, and societies globally. It becomes one of the condition top-ranking responsible for mortality in adults (6). Now a day diabetes mellitus arrived at threatening levels in which almost half a billion are living with diabetes mellitus worldwide. In 2019, 463 million people had diabetes mellitus and by 2030 this figure will be predicted close to 578 million. More than four million people died as a result of this disease and related condition in 2019 in which their ages were between 20 and 79. Out of 463 million people with diabetes mellitus, more than 75% were in working age (7).  The prevalence of diabetes mellitus in Sub-Saharan countries is increased from time to time. Ethiopia is found in the top five among Sub-Saharan countries having high numbers of diabetes mellitus cases (6). The impact of diabetes mellitus is mostly as result of its complications. All chronic complications of DM are once developed are irreversible and cause significant death (8,9). Untreated diabetes mellitus causes micro-vascular and macro-vascular complications. The occurrence of these complications are attributed by unrelenting hyperglycemia which results in overproduction of reactive oxygen species and formation of pro-inflammatory cytokines (10).  \r\n\r\nHuman beings had been utilized plants in different forms to combat diseases and promote health as far as the establishment of humanity. The nutritional approach is one of the strategies for the management of diabetes mellitus which is supportive in lessening diabetic complications danger. Coffee, representing the fruit of the coffee plant, belongs to the Rubiaceae family. It is known for   its characteristic aroma and taste has  exciting and health endorsing property (11). (12). Impacts of coffee on the health are attributed by decreasing aggravating disease and their complications through preventing antioxidant and anti-inflammatory reaction before it intensify degenerative disorders (13). \r\n\r\nGreen coffee beans of all coffee species are rich of many bioactive substances. Caffeine, trigonelline, phenols and diterpenes are the most abundant bioactive compounds in the coffee. Health benefits of coffee consumption is attributed by the presence of these bioactive compounds (14,15). An up-to-date meta-analysis of human studies in which regular and moderate coffee consumption showed reducing effects on diabetes mellitus (16). Also, many animal studies showed that green coffee has a protective role against diabetes mellitus (17). This protective effect of green coffee is through regulating hepatic glucose metabolism by improving insulin sensitivity, and regulation of key enzymes of glucose and lipid metabolism, like glucokinase, glucose-6-phosphatase, fatty acid synthase and carnitine palmitoyltransferase (18,19). \r\n\r\nChlorogenic acid is most abundant phenols found in green coffee. The antioxidant effect of green coffee is due to plentiful presence of this chlorogenic acids (20). Beside this CGA has anti-inflammatory and blood glucose disposal during oral glucose tolerance test raising effects (21). Competitive inhibition of the glucose-6-phosphate translocase enzyme by CGAs is a proposed hypothesis through which it protects against T2DM in mice liver microsomes. This enzyme catalyzes reactions in both glycogenolysis and gluconeogenesis, which takes part in the regulation of blood glucose levels and its homeostasis (22).\r\n\r\nDM has no effective modern treatments. The only available treatment is lifelong modern medicine having associated health side effects and their costly prices. This paves way for herbal therapy with no or least side effects to control and treat DM.  In Ethiopia, there is a scarcity of information about the effects of green coffee on various health complications, including diabetes mellitus in both human subjects and animal models. Therefore, the present study is planned to investigate the anti-diabetic effect of hydro-methanolic extract of green coffee beans in STZ-induced diabetic mice.','4	METHODS AND MATERIALS\r\n4.1	Study Area\r\nThe study will be carried out at Biochemistry Department Laboratory, Jimma University, Jimma Southwest Ethiopia. \r\n4.2	Study Period and Duration\r\nThe study will be undertaken for a period of 6 weeks from December 2021 to January 2022. \r\n4.3	Study Design\r\nA post-test experimental study will be conducted on the mice model to investigate anti-diabetic effect of hydro-methanolic extract of green coffee beans in STZ-induced diabetic mice. \r\n4.4	Reagents and Chemicals  \r\nIn this study the following reagents and chemicals will be used: ethanol (70% to 90%), methanol, distilled water, chloroform, kidney function test kit, liver function test kit, 10% formalin, paraffin wax, xylene solution, sodium citrate, di-ethyl ether, H & E stain, 5% glucose solution, Glibenclamide, streptozotocin (sigma Aldrich Company, India).\r\n4.5	Equipments\r\nIn this study the following equipment and materials will be used: Whatman filter paper No.1, Erlenmeyer flasks (500mL), magnetic stirrer, beakers, filtering cloth, mortar and pestle, oral gavage (for oral feeding of mice), lyophilizer (OPR-FDU-5012, Korea), desiccator, refrigerator, Rotary evaporator (Heizbad Hei-VAP, Germany), Human star 100 Chemistry analyzer, heater, one touch glucometer, scissors, HD colored camera, test tube, volumetric flask (5000mL), light microscope, electronic balance (A & D Company Limited, Tokyo Japan), syringe (1mL, 3mL), micropipettes(10, 200 and 1000μL), metallic spatula, centrifuge (GEMMY INDUSTRIAL CORP., Taiwan), stainless and plastic cage, centrifuge tube (15mL and 50mL), microtome and measuring cylinder (1000mL).\r\n4.6	Study Variables\r\n	Independent variable\r\n	Green coffee beans administration \r\n	Dependent variables\r\n	Blood glucose levels\r\n	Liver function test levels\r\n	kidney function test levels\r\n	pancreas function test levels\r\n	lipid profile levels\r\n	biomarker of oxidative stress\r\n	histopathology of kidney, liver and pancreas\r\n4.7	Sample Size Determination\r\nSample size determination will be based on the \"resource equation method,” which recommends that each treatment group of the experimental animal should be at least 5 animals (23). The study will be conducted on 6 groups of mice. Therefore, the total sample size will be 6 groups × 10 rats= 60 mice. \r\n4.8	Experimental Animals\r\nA total of 60 adult male Swiss albino mice of 7-8 weeks old will be procured from Tropical and Infectious Diseases Research Center of Jimma University. The experimental mice will be placed in plastic cage with stainless steel cover and will be housed in the animal laboratory with optimum temperature (22±2 ºC), optimum ventilation, and 12 hour light-dark cycle. The cages will be kept clean throughout the experiment. All mice will be provided with free access to standard chow and water ad libithum. Female mice will be excluded from the study because of their cyclic hormonal variations that can affect the biochemical levels.\r\n4.9	Experimental Protocol\r\nThe mice will be randomly assigned into six groups. Each mouse in the given group will be identified by giving a number on its tail by a permanent marker. The mice will be grouped and treated as follows: \r\nGroup I (Normal control group): will receive saline orally.\r\nGroup II (Negative control group): will receive saline orally.\r\nGroup III (Treatment group): will treated with 100 mg/kg BW/day of green coffee. \r\nGroup IV (Treatment group): will treated with 200 mg/kg BW/day of green coffee.  \r\nGroup V (Treatment group): will treated with 300 mg/kg BW/day of green coffee.  \r\nGroup VI (Positive control group): will treated with 5 mg/kg BW of Glibenclamide (24).\r\n4.10	Induction of Experimental Diabetes Mellitus\r\nDiabetes will be induced by intraperitoneal injection of STZ at a dose of 120mg/kg of mice body weight prepared in 0.1M sodium citrate buffer in a volume of 20ml/kg of body weight. Before diabetes induction, the animals have fasted for 12 hours. Confirmation of diabetes will be done a week after STZ treatment, using one-touch glucometer and blood sample will be obtained from tail puncture of the mice, and mice with fasting glycaemia over 250mg/dl included in the study as diabetic mice (25).\r\n4.11	Preparation of Green Coffee Beans Solution and Dosage Calculation\r\nCoffee used as a treatment in the present study will be packed green coffee beans and purchased from Ethiopian Commodity Exchange in Jimma town. Coffee beans are crushed and changed into powder at different particle sizes. This will be done by mortar and pestle. The coarse powder of green coffee bean powder will be macerated in 80% methanol (V/V) with ratio of 1:10 (weight of coffee bean powder in grams to extraction solvent in milliliter) for three days with mechanical shaking twice a day. Then the extract will be filtered first using filtering cloth to separate coarse powder and then by Whatman filter paper No.1 to separate fine powders from the filtrate. Then the filtrate will be evaporated to dryness by rotary evaporator and will be concentrated further by water bath at 40 ºC. Then the filtrate will be taken to thermostatic oven at 40 ºC for overnight to evaporate the remaining methanol. After that, the final extract free of methanol will be taken to deep freezer to get solid consistency and will be dried by freeze dryer (lyophilizer). This final dried extract will be weighed following packing into glass to avoid contamination from atmospheric air (26).\r\n\r\nThe final extract will be kept in a refrigerator at 4 ºC until it will be used for experimentation. 100 mg/kg, 200 mg/kg and 300 mg/kg of green coffee extract in solution of 1 ml/kg will be given by oral gavage.\r\n\r\n4.12	Data Collection \r\n4.13	Assessment of Food and Liquid Consumption \r\nFood (g/group/day) and liquids (ml/group/day) will be measured and given to the rat according to their assigned group every day. Standard chow of 100g for each group and 200ml of tap water for the normal control group will be given.\r\n4.14	Blood Sample Collection, Serum Preparation and Storage\r\nAt the end of eighth week, the mice will fast overnight and anesthetized with diethyl ether. Blood sample will be collected by cardiac puncture and the mice will be sacrificed by cervical dislocation. Serum will be prepared by transferring blood sample into serum separator tube (SST) and will be left for 30 minutes at room temperatures to clot. Next, the clotted blood sample will be centrifuged at 2000rpm for 15 minutes. At the end, the serum will be transferred into the necked tube and will be stored at -80 ºC deep freezer until analysis will be performed.\r\n4.15	Laboratory Tests \r\nKidney function status, liver function status, pancreas function status, blood glucose levels, lipid profile, oxidative stress biomarker will be evaluated to determine anti-diabetic effects of green coffee beans on experimental animals.\r\n\r\n4.16	Histopathological Study of kidney, liver and pancreas\r\nTo evaluate restoring effect of green coffee beans against STZ effects histopathological examination of kidney, liver and pancreas will be performed. To perform this, tissue will be taken from each mouse after sacrificed and will be put to preserving tissue which contains 10% formalin. Then the tissue will be taken out from preservative and will be washed by ethanol subsequently with different concentration starting from 70% to 100% and xylene solution will be used to remove ethanol from tissue and replace this ethanol with fluid that readily miscible with paraffin wax which enhance the tissue to embed easily with the wax to form tissue blocks.\r\nNext, the tissue block will be sectioned by microtome and that section will be immersed in a water bath at 40 0C and the unfolded section will be taken and will be dried by putting in an oven at 56 0C for 15 minutes. Then the dried section will be stained with hematoxylin-eosin stain after removal of paraffin wax by using ethanol with a descending concentration and will be examined by using light microscope under different magnification. After examination photo will be taken by using microscope camera and the pictures will be read and interpreted by pathologist.\r\n4.17	Data Entry and Analysis\r\nAll data will be entered into epi data version 4.6 and exported to SPSS version 25 for statistical analysis. Normality distributions will be assessed by the Shapiro-Wilks test and plots histogram. A one-way analysis of variance (ANOVA) will be done to determine statistical differences among all groups of the study. Pairwise comparisons will be conducted by Tukey post hoc multiple comparison tests. Also, Pearson bivariate correlation will be used. The results of the data will be presented as mean±standard deviation (SD). The p-values < 0.05 will be considered statistically significant.\r\n4.18	Quality Assurance\r\nGreen coffee beans solution will be freshly prepared by the principal investigator daily. All chemicals and reagents used in this experiment will be of analytical grade. Each biochemical test will be performed by experienced laboratory technologists following standard operational procedures (SOPs). Negative and positive control samples will be run to assure the validity of the test results. In addition, data entry and clearance will be performed by Epi-data before analysis by SPSS.  \r\n4.19	Ethical Consideration\r\nThe ethical clearance will be obtained from JU Research and Ethical Review Committee. All experimental activities will be carried out per recommendations from the declaration of nationally and internationally conventional standards for the employment of experimental animals, and code of ethics of animal experiments, which comply with scientific and ethical guidelines.\r\n4.20	Utilization and Dissemination of the Results\r\nThe results of this research will be presented and submitted to the Research and Postgraduate Coordinating Office of Jimma University. An effort will be made to present at different stakeholders in different formats, including face-to-face presentations, publishing in peer-reviewed journals and presentations at scientific meetings.','<p style=\"marg