ne; height:31px; vertical-align:top; width:46px\">\r\n			<p>&nbsp;</p>\r\n			</td>\r\n		</tr>\r\n		<tr>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:1px solid black; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:30px; vertical-align:top; width:193px\">\r\n			<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Maiandra GD&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">Final Report writing &nbsp;</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:30px; vertical-align:top; width:46px\">\r\n			<p>&nbsp;</p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:30px; vertical-align:top; width:46px\">\r\n			<p>&nbsp;</p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:30px; vertical-align:top; width:46px\">\r\n			<p>&nbsp;</p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:30px; vertical-align:top; width:46px\">\r\n			<p>&nbsp;</p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:30px; vertical-align:top; width:46px\">\r\n			<p>&nbsp;</p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:30px; vertical-align:top; width:46px\">\r\n			<p>&nbsp;</p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:30px; vertical-align:top; width:46px\">\r\n			<p>&nbsp;</p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:30px; vertical-align:top; width:46px\">\r\n			<p>&nbsp;</p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:30px; vertical-align:top; width:46px\">\r\n			<p>&nbsp;</p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"background-color:#4f81bd; border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:30px; vertical-align:top; width:46px\">\r\n			<p>&nbsp;</p>\r\n			</td>\r\n		</tr>\r\n		<tr>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:1px solid black; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:30px; vertical-align:top; width:193px\">\r\n			<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Maiandra GD&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">Monitoring and evaluation </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"background-color:#4472c4; border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:30px; vertical-align:top; width:46px\">\r\n			<p>&nbsp;</p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"background-color:#4472c4; border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:30px; vertical-align:top; width:46px\">\r\n			<p>&nbsp;</p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"background-color:#4472c4; border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:30px; vertical-align:top; width:46px\">\r\n			<p>&nbsp;</p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"background-color:#4472c4; border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:30px; vertical-align:top; width:46px\">\r\n			<p>&nbsp;</p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"background-color:#4472c4; border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:30px; vertical-align:top; width:46px\">\r\n			<p>&nbsp;</p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"background-color:#4472c4; border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:30px; vertical-align:top; width:46px\">\r\n			<p>&nbsp;</p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"background-color:#4472c4; border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:30px; vertical-align:top; width:46px\">\r\n			<p>&nbsp;</p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"background-color:#4472c4; border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:30px; vertical-align:top; width:46px\">\r\n			<p>&nbsp;</p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"background-color:#4472c4; border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:30px; vertical-align:top; width:46px\">\r\n			<p>&nbsp;</p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"background-color:#4472c4; border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:30px; vertical-align:top; width:46px\">\r\n			<p>&nbsp;</p>\r\n			</td>\r\n		</tr>\r\n	</tbody>\r\n</table>',1,NULL,NULL,NULL),(61,130,9,26,'Project summary\r\nBackground: Workplace bullying, violence, and incivility are multifaceted problem/phenomenon defined as “repeated offensive, abusive, intimidating, or insulting behavior, abuse of power, or unfair sanctions that make recipients upset and feel humiliated, vulnerable, or threatened, creating stress and undermining their self-confidence. which are real and complex phenomena in the workplace and remain widespread across all care settings affecting every country. This project aims to assess Workplace incivility, Violence, and Bullying behaviors among Health-Care Providers and its outcome in Jimma zone public Health facilities. \r\nApproaches and Methods: The study will be conducted in Jimma Zone public health facilities, and the study will be conducted from January to December 2022, using Cross-sectional, facility-based study design using both quantitative and qualitative methods. The study population will be all health care professionals working in Jimma zone public facilities. The final sample size will be determined based on the objectives research question, using Proportionate stratified simple random sampling technique will be employed. The population of interest for FGD and In-depth interviews will be purposively selected. A valid and reliable tool will be developed or adopted according to the research questions. Senior sociologist and psychologist professionals will be recruited to conduct quantitative data collection. Qualitative study and supervision will be conducted by MSc, Mph, or Ph.D. professionals. The data will be entered into Epi data manager 4.6 statistical software and the exported to SPSS version 25.0. descriptive statistics, and the inferential analysis will be used according to the nature of the data and variables. \r\nBudget and work plan: The time frame is from January to December 2022, and the budget will be 467,500 ETH birr.','Workplace incivility, Violence, and Bullying behaviors','Workplace incivility, Violence, and Bullying behaviors among Health-Care Providers and it’s outcome in Jimma zone public Health facilities.','2021-11-19 14:45:49','⮚	Knowledge will be generated on the level of health care professionals Workplace incivility, Violence, and Bullying behaviors.\r\n⮚	Evidence will be generated to what extent Workplace incivility, Violence, and Bullying behaviors affect patient care outcomes \r\n⮚	Evidence will be generated to what extent Workplace incivility, Violence, and Bullying behaviors affects the quality of Performance','6873c0337042feaf7b4b2a417ad5c8a1','completed','Workplace bullying, violence, and incivility are multifaceted problems/phenomena defined as “repeated offensive, abusive, intimidating, or insulting behavior, abuse of power, or unfair sanctions that make recipients upset and feel humiliated, vulnerable, or threatened, creating stress and undermining their self-confidence.”(1,2) which are real and complex phenomena in the workplace and remain widespread across all care settings affecting every country (3,4). \r\nFor centuries, some form of incivility, bullying, or violence has touched far too many members of the health professions. They affect every specialty, occur in virtually every practice and academic setting, and extend into every educational and organizational level of the profession which may be directed from the top down (employers against employees), from the bottom up (employees against employers), or horizontally (employees against employees) (1,5,6). \r\nA study in America, 43% of health care workers have been verbally and/or physically threatened by a patient or family member of a patient while at work, of those behaviors 13% of days away from work were the result of violence, and this rate has increased in recent years. Hospitals and healthcare organizations experience additional consequences that led to increased costs related to turnover, absenteeism, and decreased work performance staff treatment for depression and/or anxiety, which is estimated at $379,500 for every percentage point per year for each employee experiencing workplace incivility, bullying, and violence which foster medical errors, contribute to poor patient satisfaction and adverse patient outcomes and increase the cost of care (8–11).\r\nAn overview of relevant literature indicates that incivility, bullying, and workplace violence are concerns health care field, and beyond and that health care professionals ignore or tolerate incivility and bullying because of fear or lack of knowledge(12,13). However, that’s also a reason to leave or plan to leave the profession. Other negative effects include decreased job satisfaction, reduced organizational commitment, decreased personal health which may result in physical symptoms such as headaches, interrupted sleep, and other mores (14). Those actions may also be associated with psychological conditions, including heightened levels of psychological stress, anxiety, irritability, and depressive symptoms that may progress to a posttraumatic stress disorder, and added direct and indirect costs to employers(15,16). \r\nIf confidence and competence decrease because of incivility, bullying, and workplace violence, this result can adversely affect the quality of patient care and care outcomes. Such results may interfere with clinical judgment to the extent that health care providers\' performance is affected. Other studies report an increase in patient safety-related errors as well as an increase in patient ignorance, delayed treatment, and medical errors(17).\r\nRelationships damaged by bullying and bullying can have an impact on unhealthy workplaces and ultimately have a negative impact on the quality and safety of the care provided. Increasing frequency and rates of persistent behavior have been proven to have negative effects on workplace satisfaction, workforce retention, and health care professionals\' psychological and physical health, as well as inferred implications on patient care quality and risk of poor health outcomes(18,19). \r\nThere is a lack of research specifically related to Workplace incivility, Violence, and Bullying behaviors among Health-Care Providers and its outcome in Ethiopia. This study addresses this gap by identifying the magnitude and its outcome. A clearer picture of the real scope of Workplace incivility, Violence, and Bullying behaviors and its outcome could inform policymakers and create better opportunities to improve the quality of care and create a conducive working environment. Moreover, the finding from the study will be going to help both employees and employers. The findings from this study may help as baseline data for further studies, health care institutions, and policymakers. Therefore, Identifying the correlation between Workplace incivility, Violence, and Bullying behaviors among Health-Care Providers and its outcome is important in the process of managing and building appropriate solutions and recommendations.','Description of approaches/Methods\r\nStudy setting\r\nThe study will be conducted in Jimma Zone. The zone is divided into 21 districts and one town administration. There are eight hospitals (one referral and teaching hospital, one general hospital, and six primary hospitals), 115 health centers, and 520 health posts in the Jimma zone. There are more than 2500 health care workers of all types in Jimma zone public health facilities.\r\nStudy design: \r\nCross-sectional, facility-based research design using both quantitative and qualitative methods.\r\nPopulation\r\nThe study population will be all health care professionals (Physicians, Nurses, Midwives, laboratorist, pharmacists, and others) working in Jimma zone public hospitals, southwest Ethiopia.\r\nSample \r\nFor a quantitative study, the final sample size will be determined based on the objectives of the project for each research question, and for qualitative study Hospital administrators, directors, or representatives of all types of professions will be involved and the sample size will be determined based on data saturation. \r\nSampling procedure\r\nA proportionate stratified simple random sampling technique will be deployed to select study participants from Health facilities according to their professional category. The population of interest for FGD and In-depth interviews will be purposively selected. \r\nInstrument\r\nTo assess the level of Workplace incivility, Violence, and Bullying behaviors scale will be utilized and for each specific objective, a valid and reliable tool will be developed or adopted according to the research questions.  \r\nData collection procedure\r\nSenior health care professionals will be recruited to conduct quantitative data collection. Qualitative study and supervision will be conducted by MSc, Mph, or MD professionals.\r\nData Analysis.\r\nThe data will be entered into Epi data manager 4.6 statistical software and the generated data will be exported to SPSS version 25.0 and will be cleaned by calculating frequencies and sorting. descriptive statistics including mean and standard deviation (SD) will be used to describe the sample. Finally, the inferential analysis will be done according to the nature of the data and variables. \r\n  Quality assurance\r\nTo assure the quality first valid tool will be utilized. Three days of training including the pretest will be given to data collectors and supervisors on the objectives of the study, data collection tools, and research ethics. Supervision will be conducted by supervisors and research teams to monitor the overall data collection process. \r\nPlan for dissemination and ensuring utilization of findings \r\nThe findings of this will be disseminated to governmental and non-governmental stakeholders and policymakers. Finally, it will be submitted for publication in a peer-reviewed journal. \r\nCapacity building (inclusion of Ph.D. and/or MSc students)\r\nThree MPH / MSc students will be included\r\nEthical considerations\r\nEthical clearance will be obtained from the Institutional Review Board of Jimma University, Institute of health. Ethical issues within the study will be taken into consideration when carrying out the study. The purpose, merit, and demerits of the study will be explained, and informed consent will be obtained from study participants.','<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">Reference</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">1. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Vessey JA, DeMarco R, DiFazio R. Bullying, harassment, and horizontal violence in the nursing workforce the state of the Science. Annu Rev Nurs Res. 2010;28(1):133&ndash;57. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">2. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Broome BA. Dealing with Sharks and Bullies in the Workplace. ABNF J. 2008;19(1). </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">3. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Crawford CL, Chu F, Judson LH, Cuenca E, Jadalla AA, Tze-Polo L, et al. An Integrative Review of Nurse-to-Nurse Incivility, Hostility, and Workplace Violence: A GPS for Nurse Leaders. Nurs Adm Q. 2019 Apr;43(2):138&ndash;56. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">4. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Saltzberg CW. Balancing in moments of vulnerability while dancing the dialectic. Adv Nurs Sci. 2011;34(3):229&ndash;42. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">5. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Hader R. Workplace violence survey 2008: Unsettling findings. Nurs Manag (Harrow). 2008;39(7):13&ndash;9. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">6. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Hutchinson M, Wilkes L, Jackson D, Vickers MH. Integrating individual, work group and organizational factors: Testing a multidimensional model of bullying in the nursing workplace. J Nurs Manag. 2010;18(2):173&ndash;81. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">7. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Ciocco M. Fast Facts on Combating Nurse Bullying, Incivility, and Workplace Violence: What Nurses Need to Know in a Nutshell [Internet]. 1st ed. New York, NY: Springer Publishing Company; 2017 [cited 2021 Nov 10]. Available from: http://connect.springerpub.com/lookup/doi/10.1891/9780826138187</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">8. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Becher J, Visovsky C. Horizontal violence in nursing. Medsurg Nurs. 2012;21(4):210. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">9. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Dempsey C, Reilly B. Nurse engagement: What are the contributing factors for success. OJIN Online J Issues Nurs. 2016;21(1):2. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">10. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Gomaa AE, Tapp LC, Luckhaupt SE, Vanoli K, Sarmiento RF, Raudabaugh WM, et al. Occupational traumatic injuries among workers in health care facilities&mdash;United States, 2012&ndash;2014. MMWR Morb Mortal Wkly 