=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\"><strong>Note: Q1</strong>: January-April; <strong>Q2</strong>: May-August; <strong>Q3</strong>: September- December</span></span></h1>\r\n\r\n<h1 style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:14pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\"><a name=\"_Toc88164924\">Budget breakdown</a></span></span></h1>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">A total of 500,000.00 Ethiopian birrs is required to accomplish the study.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:left\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\"><strong>Table 2: Budget breakdown. </strong></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:left\">&nbsp;</p>\r\n\r\n<table cellspacing=\"0\" class=\"MsoTableGrid\" style=\"border-collapse:collapse; border:none; margin-left:13px\">\r\n	<tbody>\r\n		<tr>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:1px solid black; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:1px solid black; vertical-align:top; width:78px\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">S.N</span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:1px solid black; vertical-align:top; width:334px\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Category</span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:1px solid black; vertical-align:top; width:213px\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Total cost (in ETB)</span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n		</tr>\r\n		<tr>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:1px solid black; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top; width:78px\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">1.</span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top; width:334px\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Stationery cost</span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top; width:213px\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">5,000.00</span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n		</tr>\r\n		<tr>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:1px solid black; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top; width:78px\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">3.</span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top; width:334px\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Personnel cost</span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top; width:213px\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">400,000.00</span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n		</tr>\r\n		<tr>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:1px solid black; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top; width:78px\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">4.</span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top; width:334px\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Transportation cost</span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top; width:213px\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">95,000.00</span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n		</tr>\r\n		<tr>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:1px solid black; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top; width:78px\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\">&nbsp;</p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top; width:334px\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\"><strong>Total cost</strong></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top; width:213px\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\"><strong>500,000.00</strong></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n		</tr>\r\n	</tbody>\r\n</table>',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),(63,183,9,20,'Executive summary\r\nBackground: Maternal, newborn, infant’s morbidity and mortality are unacceptably high in sub-Saharan Africa including Ethiopia. Despite considerable efforts made in maternal and child health care, poor maternal, neonatal and child health problems remained a significant public health concern globally and particularly in low and middle-income countries. Community-based Interventions and strategies for improving maternal, newborn, and child health care have been recommended through a continuum of care approach. However, few efforts have been made to identify synergies and integrate different intervention packages across the country. \r\nObjective: This study aims to assess the effectiveness of an integrated community-based intervention package in improving maternal, neonatal and infant health outcomes in Jimma Zone, Southwest Ethiopia: a cluster randomized controlled trial.\r\nMethods: This is a parallel-arm, single-blind, cluster randomized controlled trial conducted in the Dedo and Seka Chekorsa districts of the Jimma zone. After excluding 10 kebeles from each district to be considered as a buffer zone, we will assign 26 kebeles to the intervention arm and 26 to the control arm. A total of 624 pregnant women in their third trimester who reside in the kebeles assigned to the intervention clusters will be identified and enrolled (312 in intervention and 312 in control groups). The intervention includes Behavioral Change Communication, Home gardening, animal husbandry Intervention and male involvement. Various multidisciplinary professionals and experts regularly monitor the overall process of the research and intervention activities. The effect of the intervention in comparison with the routine care will be assessed by fitting mixed-effects linear regression models for the continuous outcomes and mixed-effects linear probability models for the binary outcomes. In all analyses, adjustment will be made for clustering at the kebele level and covariate. All tests will be two-sided and the level of significance will be set at alpha < 0.05.\r\nBudget: A total of 874,120.00 ETB will be required.','a cluster randomized controlled trial','Effectiveness of an integrated community-based intervention package in improving maternal, neonatal and infant health outcome in Jimma Zone, Southwest Ethiopia: a cluster randomized controlled trial','2021-11-19 17:09:05','Expected outputs/outcomes\r\n	Reduction in maternal, neonatal, and infant morbidity\r\n	Improved health-seeking behaviour\r\n	Increased knowledge on danger signs\r\n	Improved attitude on danger signs\r\n	Improved neonatal care practice\r\n	Improved health-seeking behaviour\r\n	Increased knowledge and attitude on optimal feeding practices\r\n	Increase in the prevalence of early initiation, exclusive breastfeeding, timely introduction of complementary food, feeding frequency and dietary diversity\r\n	Improved mothers dietary practice\r\n	Decreased prevalence of stunting, wasting, underweight\r\n	Improved body mass index \r\n	Improved household food security status\r\n	Increased utilization of skilled delivery and postnatal care\r\n	Improved birth-preparedness and complication-readiness plan\r\n	Improved women empowerment','7ac876b381e7220567111f88e45da8aa','completed','1.	Background and Rationale\r\nMaternal health refers to the health of women during pregnancy, childbirth, and the postnatal period. Each stage should be a positive experience, ensuring women and their babies reach their full potential for health and well-being (1,2). On the other hand, the health and well-being of women, newborns, and children are inherently linked(3). When mothers are malnourished, ill, or receive insufficient care, their newborns are at increased risk of disease and premature death (4). An integrated approach that includes community-based care as an essential component has the potential to substantially improve maternal, newborn, and child health outcomes (5).   \r\n Maternal mortality is concentrated in Sub-Saharan Africa with a maternal mortality ratio estimated to be nearly 546 per 100,000 live births, which is 50 times higher than in industrialized countries.  Approximately 99 percent of all newborn deaths also occur in LMIC (1). The majority of maternal, prenatal and neonatal deaths in developing countries occur at home immediately after childbirth while child’s risk of dying is highest during the first 28 days of life (1,2).   \r\nDespite considerable progress made in maternal and child health care, poor maternal, neonatal and child health care remained as a significant public health problem globally and particularly in low- and middle-income countries (LMICs) (6). The reasons are multi-factorial, including poverty; poor health status of women; illiteracy; lack of information regarding the availability of health services/providers; lack of control on household resources and decision making authority; poor ante natal and obstetric care, both within the community and health facilities; absence of a trained attendant at delivery; inadequate referral system for emergency obstetric care; and absence of/poor linkages of health centers with the communities (2). The majority of maternal and neonatal deaths could be prevented with early recognition and proper implementation of required skills and knowledge by incorporating the community in the intervention programs (5).  \r\nIntegrating essential interventions that have an impact on maternal, neonatal and child health outcomes could have great potential for accelerating progress towards achieving the SDG target 3.2 and 2.2 by considering interdependent relationship between maternal, neonatal and child health (1,7). The integration of these interventions needs to span not only in time period (pre-pregnancy, pregnancy, childbirth, and the postnatal period). The delivery of essential interventions can have profound translational and intergenerational impacts with important implications for the long-term well-being of mother, newborns and children (1).   \r\nReview of literature shows that different community-based intervention packages have an effect on reducing maternal, neonatal and child morbidity (5,8,9,10). Likewise, substantial evidence suggests community-based interventions are an important platform for improving health outcomes, for coordination of care from preconception to delivery and the health of the child which can lead to profound benefits for the health and well-being of women, neonates and child. It also helps to improve subsequent pregnancy and child health outcomes (5).\r\n A recent review of preconception risks and interventions shows that community-based intervention is associated with a lower neonatal morbidity rate, and a significant increase in antenatal care (ANC), and breastfeeding rates (11). Furthermore, evidence from another interventional review done on community-based intervention shows that, community-based intervention correlate to significant reduction in maternal morbidity and neonatal mortality. It also increased the referrals to health facility for pregnancy related complication by 40%, and improved the rates of early breastfeeding by 94% (5).   \r\nFurthermore, male involvement in maternal and child health care programs is increasingly recognized globally (12,13). It is known that men in developing countries are the chief decision-makers, influencing women\'s access to maternal health services, infants as well as their health outcomes. Different literature shows that male involvement is positively associated with maternal and child health outcomes however, (14) male involvement is found very low. \r\nDifferent literatures show that increasing awareness and knowledge of male regarding maternal and child health have benefits on increasing health care-seeking of women during pregnancy, birth and postnatal period (8,9,10,15), infant feeding practices (16,17) and health care seeking for childhood illness (18,19). \r\nBehavior change communication (BCC) is a communication strategy which encourages community to change their behaviors in order to adopt healthy behavioral practices. BCC is one of the key interventions for improving maternal health related knowledge of mothers ‘including pregnancy related knowledge of danger signs (20). Moreover, Behavioral change communication (BCC) package had increased highly the chances of mother to care for their neonates. There is also a significant improvement on general care of children (21).  \r\nOn the other hand, in rural settings, where most households are engaged in smallholder farming, homestead food production (HFP) interventions offer one of the most promising pathways towards improving livelihoods, the diversity of agricultural outputs, food security, and child nutrition (22). Small-scale, nutrition-sensitive agricultural interventions equip smallholder farmers with the tools and skills to cultivate home gardens and raise livestock. Home gardens promote production of nutrient-rich fruits and vegetables that can grow well in local conditions. Household members, particularly focusing on women, are trained in cultivating the crops and raising livestock. Animal husbandry has an effect on health by improving the availability of animal product origin that could ensure a supply of important nutrients for their own families (23).   \r\nCountries have made meticulous efforts to address the major contributing causes and factors for maternal, neonatal and child mortality (24,25,26). As Yet numerous research gaps exist; the studies available for review are mostly program evaluations without comprehensive and high-quality study designs (2).  Moreover, researchers of articles also suggested to encourage evidence that evaluate community-based intervention packages reduce morbidity for women, mortality and morbidity for child, and improves care-related outcomes particularly in low- and middle-income countries. In-line with this, our study aims to produce evidence on effectiveness of community based mixed interventions in improving maternal and child health outcome. Therefore, this study aims to assess the effectiveness of an integrated community-based intervention packages such as homesteading, animal husbandry and male partner involvement with behavior change communication in improving maternal, neonatal and infant health outcomes.','Methodology\r\nStudy setting: The study will be conducted in the Dedo and Seka Chekorsa districts of the Jimma zone, Oromia region, Southwest Ethiopia. Both districts have 36 Kebeles, of which 3 of the Dedo and 2 of Seka Chekorsa are urban kebele (27,28). Dedo district has a total population of 237,844 while Seka Chekorsa district has a total population of 296,440 populations. (27,28).  The dominant climate type of Dedo and Seka Chekoresa districts are “Weyna dega” and “Dega”, respectively. Approximately 83.3% of the population of Dedo and 94.4 % of the population of Seka Chekorsa district rely on agriculture to survive. Both districts are known for their cash crop production. The main crops grown in these districts include maize, grains, and coffee while fruits grown in both areas include avocado, mango, and apple. Based on the 2013E.C report, there were 8486 and 9436 pregnant women in Dedo and Seka Chekorsa districts, respectively. In the Dedo district, 39 %, 40 %, and 40 % of women received prenatal care, skill delivery, and post-natal care, respectively (29) while 75%, 68 %, and 68 % of women received prenatal care, skill delivery, and post-natal care the Seka Chekorsa district, respectively. There are One hospital, eight health centers and nine private clinics in the Dedo district whereas 1 public hospital, 9 public health centers, 16 private health facilities are found in Seka Chekorsa district (30). Previously, NGOs were working in the area of Maternal, child health and nutrition in both districts. However, all of them are phased out currently (29,30). The study will be conducted from February 2022 to March 2023 GC. \r\nStudy design: A parallel-arm, single-blind, cluster randomized controlled trial with a 1:1 ratio will be employed.\r\nPopulation \r\nSource population: All pregnant women who reside in the Dedo and Seka chekorsa districts.\r\nStudy population: All pregnant women from selected kebeles in the Dedo district who fulfil the inclusion criteria.  \r\nInclusion and exclusion criteria: The inclusion criteria during pregnancy include pregnant women in their third trimester who have lived in the selected kebeles for more than 6 months, have no plans to leave during the intervention period and are willing to be visited by data collectors and supervisors After delivery, exclusion criteria include serious illness or clinical complications requiring hospitalization, maternal death, newborn death, abortion, stillbirth, twin pregnancy, preterm birth, or any child congenital abnormality that may interfere with breastfeeding. \r\nSample size and sampling procedure: The sample size was determined with the following assumptions: to detect an increase a minimum acceptable diet from 11 to 31% [17], with 95% CIs and 80% power, assuming an intra-cluster correlation coefficient of 0·2 for a cluster size of 10, it was calculated that we will need 52 clusters. Adding 20% of the sample size for loss to follow-up, the final sample size is 624 pregnant women (312 in intervention, and 312 in control groups).\r\nRandomization and masking: First, we selected two districts that have similar characteristics and are adjacent to each other. Both districts have a total of 72 kebeles (the smallest administrative unit), 36 in each district. Then, we chose 20, 10 from each district, kebeles on the boundary of the two districts to act as a buffer zone, to prevent information contamination between the intervention and control clusters. Finally, 26 kebeles in the Dedo district will be assigned to the intervention group while 26 kebeles in the Seka Chekorsa district are assigned to control clusters (Figure 1). Allocation concealment will not be done for study participants, as they would certainly know if they are in the intervention group or not. However, data collectors will be blinded to the allocation assignment by not being informed about it, not being part of the trial implementers, and not being inhabitants of any of the kebeles. Moreover, data analysts will be blinded to group allocation.\r\nRecruitment: All pregnant women in their third trimester who reside in the kebeles assigned to the intervention clusters will be identified and enrolled in the study using the updated Health Extension Workers’ antenatal care logbook. Pregnant women will also be identified through the one-to-five network to reduce the possibility of missing them. Then, all the identified pregnant women in their third trimester in both the intervention and control groups will be enrolled in the study as a cohort.\r\nVariables   \r\nPrimary outcomes: Neonatal care practices, infant feeding practices, and infant nutritional status. \r\nSecondary outcomes: Birth preparedness and complication readiness plan, maternal, neonatal, and infant morbidity rate, maternal feeding practice, maternal and household food security, skilled delivery, postnatal care, and women’s empowerment. \r\nCovariates: Economic status, maternal education, male involvement, age, residence\r\nFigure 1 Trial profile\r\nMeasurements\r\nBirth preparedness and complication readiness plan: The following five items will be considered to generate a composite variable for birth preparedness and complication readiness plan. These are; plan to save money, plan to arrange transport, plan to give birth at a health facility, plan to be attended by a skilled attendant and plan to arrange blood donor. If a pregnant woman responds three or above will be considered as “well prepared” otherwise “not well prepared” (31)\r\nKnowledge and attitude of key danger signs: Knowledge of key danger signs will be measured by using the standard indicators identified by Maternal and neonatal health devising of JHEPIGO that included 3 key danger signs of pregnancy, 4 key danger signs of labour and delivery, 3 key danger signs of the postpartum period and 4 key danger signs of newborns. Attitude towards birth preparedness and complication readiness will be measured by using a composite indicator of 7 questions on a Likert’s scale (31).\r\nNeonatal care practice: Neonatal care practice will be measured by using a composite score (index) of 12 questions adapted from the minimum neonatal care package of WHO such as cord care, thermal care, breastfeeding initiation, immunization on date of birth and others. All the 12 questions will have a response category of ‘Yes’ or ‘No’, then the score of ‘1’ will be given for ‘Yes’ or appropriate practice and ‘0’ was given for ‘No’ or inappropriate practice. Then total score (index) for each respondent will be determined by using PCA. Then, the mean score will be determined and mothers scoring above or equal to the mean will be labelled as having “Good neonatal care practice” and otherwise “Poor neonatal care practice.”\r\nMaternal morbidity: In the current project, maternal health problems include maternal nutritional problems, mental distress and anemia.\r\nAnemia: The maternal anemia status will be assessed by measuring haemoglobin level at baseline and end line. A blood sample will be collected via finger-prick with disposable lancet and haemoglobin level will be determined by laboratory technicians using a portable digital hemoglobin meter (HemoCue AB Angelholm, Sweden). A highly sensitive and specific haemoglobin measuring method will be used. level will be adjusted for altitude depending on tabulated values proposed by different researchers (32) and recommended by WHO and CDC (33). \r\no	Anaemia in pregnancy: haemoglobin level below 11g/dl during pregnancy\r\no	Mild anaemia: haemoglobin level from 9–10.9 g/dl\r\no	Moderate anaemia: haemoglobin level from 7–8.9 g/dl\r\no	Severe anaemia: haemoglobin level from 4–6.9 g/dl\r\no	Very severe anaemia: haemoglobin level below 4 g/dl \r\nMaternal mental distress questionnaire: To measure maternal psychological distress, Kessler Psychological Distress Scale (K6), will be used. This screening tool has been validated in the Ethiopian setting and found to have a cut-off score of 5 or more, with a sensitivity and specificity of 84.2% and 82.7%, respectively. It is a six-item questionnaire intended to yield a global measure of psychological distress based on questions about anxiety, depressive and somatic symptoms that a mother has experienced in the most recent 4 weeks period. In this method, five points are given for any answer of ‘all of the time’ down to zero point in a linear formulation for ‘none’ of the time. This results in individual K6 scores being restricted to the range of 0 to 24 inclusive(34).\r\nInfant morbidity: Mothers were asked to recall infant morbidity during the two weeks before the end line follow-up. Diarrhea was defined as three or more liquid or semisolid stools within a day. Fever was determined by the mother’s report. Acute respiratory infection was defined as a combination of fever and cough. \r\nAnthropometric measurements:  The maternal anthropometric will be determined by MUAC and weight measurement at baseline and after delivery. For children, weight and length will be measured at infancy period, at 6 months and, 9 months to generate Z scores of weight for height, weight for age and height for age. Length will be measured using a length board at a precision of 0.1 cm. Mothers’, Neonate’s and child’s weight will be measured to the nearest 1.0 g using UNICEF SECA weighing scales with light clothing with shoes removed (for the mother). MUAC will be measured to the nearest 0.1cm on the left arm using non-stretchable MUAC tape.  Height, weight and MUAC measurements will be done in duplicate (35) \r\nPregnant mother, Infant and Child Feeding Practice: \r\nMeasuring individual dietary intake for pregnant mothers: Twenty-four hours recall for dietary diversity score, Animal Source Food (ASF) and meal frequency will be taken at base line in the third trimester and after delivery. The WHO Indicator Guide For measuring household and individual dietary diversity will be used (36)\r\nEarly initiation of breastfeeding: Women will be asked how soon after birth they put their infant to the breast to determine how early they started breastfeeding. Initiation of breastfeeding within one hour will be considered as optimal early initiation.\r\nExclusive breastfeeding: is defined as feeding the infant only breast milk and no other food or drink, including water, while enabling the baby to receive ORS, drops, and syrups (vitamins, minerals and medicines) for the first six months of life. Women will be asked how many months they had exclusively breastfed their index child, as well as if they are currently providing their infants with any food or drink other than breast milk, to determine EBF practice. If they say \"yes\" to the second question, they will be asked the age at which they first started eating or drinking. Therefore, we will use the questions above to determine if the child had been exclusively breastfed since birth.\r\nOptimum complementary feeding practice will be assessed by using 24 hours dietary recall to generate meal frequency of three meals/day for 6-8 months and four times/day for children aged 9 months and above respectively), minimum dietary diversity: the consumption of four or more foods within 24 hour, the minimum acceptable diet: is a composite indictor of minimum dietary  diversity and minimum   and the timely introduction of solid, semi-solid or soft foods  at 6–8 months(35). The 24 hours dietary recall will be taken at infancy, at 6 months and at 9 months of child age.\r\nMeasuring individual dietary intake for pregnant mothers: Twenty-four hours recall for dietary diversity score, Animal Source Food (ASF) and meal frequency will be taken in the third trimester. The WHO Indicator Guide For measuring household and individual dietary diversity will be used (36)\r\nMeasurement of Food security: Food security will be assessed using food security questions based on the Household Food Insecurity Access Scale (HFIAS). Responses to the food insecurity questions will be summarized on a scale to provide a continuous indicator of the degree of a household’s food insecurity. Cut-off points on the scale enable categorical classification of whether households are food secure or not (37) \r\nHousehold food access: Household food access will be assessed by the questions from the FANTA Diet Diversity Score questionnaire (38)\r\nWomen’s empowerment: A composite index for measuring women empowerment which include: freedom of mobility, decision making power, autonomy, ownership of household assets, and contribution to family income, reproductive rights, exposure to information, and participation in the development program will be employed. Under each of these indicators, a number of sub-indicators will be considered. Composite empowerment indices will be developed to measure the overall level of women empowerment in the context of Ethiopia(39) \r\nIntervention description\r\nBCC Intervention description: The intervention will be provided through Women Developmental army (WDA) leaders to roll out a set of integrated community-based interventions covering life stages from pregnancy to age one. WDA leaders will be recruited from the selected kebeles based on the following criteria: being volunteer, literate and who have breastfeeding experience. A nutritionist and reproductive health expert will provide the training for WDA leaders, including four days of classroom instruction and three days of field-based training. The intervention will have 3 phases: In phase I, the BCC key message will address frequency and diversity of diet during pregnancy and lactation, birth preparedness and complication readiness, the importance of early initiation of breastfeeding, discouragement of bottle feeding and pre-lacteal feeding, and importance of delivery in a health facility. The second phase of the intervention will cover the neonatal period and will extend to the index child’s first birthday. During this phase, BCC key message will cover the importance of exclusive breastfeeding, timely initiation of complementary foods, frequency, diversity, consistency, responsive feeding, growth monitoring and WASH practice. The BCC message will be delivered to the recruited mothers who are assigned to the intervention groups through WDA leaders for nine months. The message will be imparted through gatherings and home visits. Moreover, we will use Broadcast (local radio, local announcements using megaphones) and print media (Leaflets, pamphlets and posters) in Afan Oromo (local language) to deliver the BCC messages. Each BCC session will last 40 – 60 minutes. Therefore, women in the intervention group will receive two home visits during pregnancy and five home visits after delivery. Women in the control group will continue to receive the standard care.\r\nHome gardening and animal husbandry Intervention: The home-gardening intervention will engage the existing community workforce of agricultural extension workers (AEWs). Female AEWs will be selected based on their work performance and commitment. A senior agricultural professional will provide training for selected AEWs for 3 days about the intervention according to the training manual on homestead gardens and animal husbandry. Pregnant women in the intervention kebeles will receive a one-time free supply of seeds and chicken to establish their home gardening and poultry production around their compound. The project will provide seeds of micronutrient dense vegetables and fruits like carrots, spinach, pepper, tomatoes, cauliflower, cabbage, red beets and broad bean, tubers (potatoes) and locally bred chicks whose eggs are available to feed children before their two years of age to address the micronutrient-rich and animal source foods. The AEWs will provide ongoing agricultural training on agricultural activities along with IYCF-focused BCC sessions by WDA for pregnant women in the intervention group. Discussion, demonstration, and re-demonstration will be used to convey the message to the selected mother in the intervention wings. Each session will last for 40-60 minutes. The recruited mothers from intervention groups will receive two home visits during pregnancy and 5 home visits after delivery.  \r\nMale involvement intervention: In this study, BCC will be given to husbands of women in the interventions clusters by agricultural extension workers and WDA on the following components: knowledge on danger signs in each of the phases (pregnancy, childbirth, and the postpartum period), actions taken on birth preparedness and complication readiness, men escorting their wives to ANC, accompanying women to the place of delivery, involving mothers in decision making, diverse production, nutrition, maternal, infant and young child feeding, and the necessity of support of a partner in the productive and child care duties. Throughout the intervention periods, the recruited husbands will have 2 home visits during pregnancy and 5 visits after delivery. The intervention activities will be monitored monthly by project staff throughout the 9 months intervention periods. \r\nControl group: Pregnant women and newborn babies living in the control zones will continue to benefit from the routine maternal and child health care available, which consisted of antenatal clinics, access to free facility delivery, postpartum checkups, infant welfare clinics, and immunization clinics.\r\nData collection methods: Data will be collected in four phases. At baseline (Phase one), data on basic socio-demography, obstetric history, economy, birth preparedness and complication readiness, maternal nutrition (dietary intake, nutritional status), decision making/autonomy, maternal morbidity and treatment-seeking behaviors, food security, will be collected. Second (phase two), just at the end of the post-natal period (within 42 days after delivery), maternal health service utilization (delivery place and attendant and postnatal care), conditions of labour, BF initiation, neonatal characteristics and neonatal care practices will be collected. Similarly, pregnancy outcomes will be seen for mothers within the first six weeks. For dead neonates, infants and mothers, Verbal Autopsy will be conducted within 15–30 days of death to identify causes of death. Thirdly (Phase three), end of 6th month of birth, data on infant care practices (IYCF, nutritional status of infants and mothers, immunization, illness and treatment-seeking behaviors), WASH practice, infant survival and causes of infant death will be collected. Lastly (phase four) at 9th-month data related to, infants’ morbidity complementary feeding and nutritional status of infants will be measured. Trained interviewers will conduct face-to-face interviews using structured questionnaires. Interviews are expected to last approximately 1 hour and will be conducted in a quiet, private space at the homes of the women. If selected women are absent from their homes, interviewers will visit households up to three different days/times to attempt to interview them before the woman will be replaced with another randomly selected woman. Experiences of women development army leaders, agricultural extension workers, and study participants will be assessed qualitatively after the trial is completed. We will conduct four Focus group discussions with WDA leaders and AEWs and twelve in-depth interviews with study participants. Both the FGDs and IDIs will continue until information saturation is reached. \r\nData collectors and supervisors: Ten females, who had completed 10th grade or above will be recruited, trained. The training will be provided for two weeks using mixed methods like a lecture, role-play, mock interview, and practice in classroom and field settings intensively. All study data will be obtained by trained interviewers who will be unaware of the study hypotheses and recorded maternal responses on standardized forms. The verbal autopsy (Vas) will be conducted by two experienced females to be selected and trained from the specific kebele, who are familiar with the culture, setting of the residents. The data collection process will be strictly supervised by the research team and trained supervisors. To control the quality of data, in addition to training, pretest, supervision and use of local languages, the inter-item consistency of the indicators to measure the composite score of wealth index, BP & CR and others will be checked by using Chronbach-alpha at 0.7 cut-off points. Data will be collected and entered and edited separately by each study site and finally will be summed up together to a central data coordinating center.\r\nLoss to follow up: Retrospective data review will be used to identify mothers who are Lost to follow up (LTFU). It will be categorized based on the point of follow up at which the mother will be lost. Some mothers will be LTFU before delivery. For these subjects, only basic demographic data will be available. Other subjects will be LTFU between delivery and the 42-day visits (LTFU-after). For these subjects, only demographic and delivery outcomes will be available. Mothers missing both visits will be considered to have been LTFU-before delivery.\r\nPretest: The questionnaire will be pre-tested on 5% of the same source population other than sampled population. Based on the pre-test, questions will be revised and edited. \r\nData management: We will establish a data management Committee consisting of various multidisciplinary professionals to regularly monitor the overall process of the research and intervention activities. The committee will meet every three months to discuss with the research team the performance of research activities and review the progress data analysis plan.\r\nData analysis: Double data entry will be performed using Epidata version 4.6 and statistical analysis will be done using Stata version 16. Descriptive statistics will be used to summarize demographic characteristics, the proportion of food security, feeding practices, morbidity, women empowerment, knowledge, attitude and practice scores. Anthropometric measurement data analysis will be performed using WHO Anthro software plus. Moreover, mean values of anthropometric and empowerment data in the intervention and control groups will be compared using Student’s t-test. The effect of the intervention in comparison with the routine education will be assessed by fitting mixed-effects linear regression models for the continuous outcomes and mixed-effects linear probability models for the binary outcomes. In all analyses, the adjustment will be made for clustering at the kebele level and covariate. All tests will be two-sided and the level of significance will be set at alpha < 0.05. All FGDs and interview audio files and notes will be translated to English, and then transcribed verbatim. Reading and re-reading will be done to code, categorize, and organize into themes and sub-themes. Finally drawn key quotes will be elaborated.\r\nEthical consideration: Ethical clearance for this study will be sought from Jimma University Research Ethics Review Committee. Before initiation of the study, approval will be obtained from the participating communities through sensitization meetings. Oral information will be given to all eligible participants in the local languages before obtaining written informed consent. In addition, a written information sheet prepared in their local language will be given to all respondents. There are no major risks associated with any of the examinations. No monetary reimbursements will be provided to study participants nor to the communities participating in the study. Culturally appropriate methods for tracking enrolled women to ensure completeness of reporting will be employed in each site. Mothers and Children found ill at any examinations will be referred to the nearby health facility for diagnosis and treatment. Pregnant women with Hgb level < 7g/dl will be referred to the nearby hospital.\r\nCapacity building: The data that will be generated from this study will be enormous and diverse. Therefore, we will invite six masters’ students to take part in this process which will give them the opportunity to gain skills in conducting research as well as use the data for their thesis. The team and the students will publish the findings in scientific peer-reviewed journals.','<h2><span style=\"font-size:13pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Cambria,&quot;serif&quot;\"><span style=\"color:#4f81bd\"><a name=\"_Toc88121790\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\"><span style=\"color:black\">Reference</span></span></span></a> </span></span></span></h2>\r\n\r\n<ol>\r\n	<li><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\"><span style=\"color:black\">ORGANIZATION, W. H. 2019. Maternal mortality: evidence brief. World Health Organization</span></span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\"><span style=\"color:black\">Black RE, Laxminarayan R, Temmerman M, Walker N. Reproductive, Maternal, newborn, and child health: disease control priorities, (volume 2). 2016.</span></span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\"><span style=\"color:black\">Lassi ZS, Musavi NB, Maliqi B, Mansoor N, de Francisco A, Toure K, et al. Systematic review on human resources for health interventions to improve maternal health outcomes: Evidence from low- and middle-income countries. Hum Resour Health [Internet]. 2016;14(1). Available from: http://dx.doi.org/10.1186/s12960-016-0106-y</span></span></span></span></span></li>\r\n</ol>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px\">&nbsp;</p>\r\n\r\n<ol start=\"4\">\r\n	<li><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\"><span style=\"color:black\">RONSMANS, C., CHOWDHURY, M. E., DASGUPTA, S. K., AHMED, A. &amp; KOBLINSKY, M. 2010. Effect of parent&#39;s death on child survival in rural Bangladesh: a cohort study. <em>The Lancet,</em> 375<strong>,</strong> 2024-2031.</span></span></span></span></span></li>\r\n</ol>\r\n\r\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\r\n\r\n<ol start=\"5\">\r\n	<li><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\"><span style=\"color:black\">LASSI, Z. S. &amp; BHUTTA, Z. A. 2015. Community‐based intervention packages for reducing maternal and neonatal morbidity and mortality and improving neonatal outcomes. <em>Cochrane database of systematic reviews</em>.</span></span></span></span></span></li>\r\n</ol>\r\n\r\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\r\n\r\n<ol start=\"6\">\r\n	<li><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\"><span style=\"color:black\">UNFPA 2013. maternal health in africa. This Fact Sheet was prepared in January 2013 for the Summit of CARMMA (Campaign on Accelerated Reduction of Maternal, New Born and Child Mortality in Africa) in Addis Ababa. </span></span></span><a href=\"https://www.unfpa.org/sites/default/files/resource-pdf/EN_Maternal%20Health%20in%20Africa_factsheet_web.pdf\" style=\"color:blue; text-decoration:underline\"><em><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\"><span style=\"color:black\">https://www.unfpa.org/sites/default/files/resource-pdf/EN_Maternal%20Health%20in%20Africa_factsheet_web.pdf</span></span></span></em></a></span></span></li>\r\n	<li><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\"><span style=\"color:black\">STORY, W. T., LEBAN, K., ALTOBELLI, L. C., GEBRIAN, B., HOSSAIN, J., LEWIS, J., MORROW, M., NIELSEN, J. N., ROSALES, A. &amp; RUBARDT, M. 2017. Institutionalizing community-focused maternal, newborn, and child health strategies to strengthen health systems: a new framework for the Sustainable Development Goal era. <em>Globalization and health,</em> 13<strong>,</strong> 1-13.</span></span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\"><span style=\"color:black\">Gross K, Alba S, Glass TR, Schellenberg JA, Obrist B. Timing of antenatal care for adolescent and adult pregnant women in south-eastern Tanzania. BMC pregnancy and childbirth. 2012;12(1):1-12.</span></span></span></span></span></li>\r\n</ol>\r\n\r\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\r\n\r\n<ol start=\"9\">\r\n	<li><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\"><span style=\"color:black\">Olayemi O, Bello F, Aimakhu C, Obajimi G, Adekunle A. Male participation in pregnancy and delivery in Nigeria: a survey of antenatal attendees. Journal of biosocial science. 2009;41(4):493-503.</span></span></span></span></span></li>\r\n</ol>\r\n\r\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\r\n\r\n<ol start=\"10\">\r\n	<li><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\"><span style=\"color:black\">Brunson J. Confronting maternal mortality, controlling birth in Nepal: The gendered politics of receiving biomedical care at birth. Social science &amp; medicine. 2010;71(10):1719-27.</span></span></span></span></span></li>\r\n</ol>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px\">&nbsp;</p>\r\n\r\n<ol start=\"11\">\r\n	<li><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\"><span style=\"color:black\">Robert E Black, Ramanan Laxminarayan, marleen Temmerman NW. 3,Disease Control Priorities: Reproductive, Maternal, Newborn and Child Health. Dis Control Priorities Third Ed. 2016;2:163&ndash;6.</span></span></span></span></span></li>\r\n</ol>\r\n\r\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\r\n\r\n<ol start=\"12\">\r\n	<li><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\"><span style=\"color:black\">DAVIS, J., VYANKANDONDERA, J., L1516UCHTERS, S., SIMON, D. &amp; HOLMES, W. 2016. Male involvement in reproductive, maternal and child health: a qualitative study of policymaker and practitioner perspectives in the Pacific. <em>Reproductive health,</em> 13<strong>,</strong> 1-11.</span></span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\"><span style=\"color:black\">NKWONTA, C. A. &amp; MESSIAS, D. K. 2019. Male Participation in Reproductive Health Interventions in Sub-Saharan Africa: A Scoping Review. <em>International perspectives on sexual and reproductive health,</em> 45<strong>,</strong> 71-85.</span></span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\"><span style=\"color:black\">CRAYMAH, J. P., OPPONG, R. K. &amp; TUOYIRE, D. A. 2017. Male involvement in maternal health care at Anomabo, central region, Ghana. <em>International journal of reproductive medicine,</em> 2017.</span></span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\"><span style=\"color:black\">Abosse Z, Woldie M, Ololo S. Factors influencing antenatal care service utilization in hadiya zone. Ethiopian Journal of Health Sciences. 2010;20(2).</span></span></span></span></span></li>\r\n</ol>\r\n\r\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\r\n\r\n<ol start=\"16\">\r\n	<li><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\"><span style=\"color:black\">Sharma M, Petosa R. Impact of expectant fathers in breast-feeding decisions. Journal of the American Dietetic Association. 1997;97(11):1311-3.</span></span></span></span></span></li>\r\n</ol>\r\n\r\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\r\n\r\n<o