;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\"><span style=\"color:black\">WHO, Chan M. Haemoglobin concentrations for the diagnosis of anaemia and assessment of severity. Geneva, Switz World Heal Organ [Internet]. 2011;1&ndash;6. Available from: http://scholar.google.com/scholar?hl=en&amp;btnG=Search&amp;q=intitle:Haemoglobin+concentrations+for+the+diagnosis+of+anaemia+and+assessment+of+severity#1</span></span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\"><span style=\"color:black\">KESSLER RC, GREEN JG, GRUBER MJ, SAMPSON NA, BROMET E, CUITAN M, et al. Screening for serious mental illness in the general population with the K6 screening scale: results from the WHO World Mental Health (WMH) survey. Int J Methods Psychiatr Res [Internet]. 2010;17 Suppl 1(Supplement 1):S78&ndash;82. Available from: </span></span></span><a href=\"http://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/mpr.247/abstract\" style=\"color:blue; text-decoration:underline\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\"><span style=\"color:black\">http://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/mpr.247/abstract</span></span></span></a></span></span></li>\r\n	<li><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\"><span style=\"color:black\">Cashin K, Oot L. Guide to Anthropometry: A Practical Tool for Program Planners, Managers, and Implementers. Food Nutr Tech Assist III Proj (FANTA)/ FHI 360. 2018;1&ndash;231. </span></span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\"><span style=\"color:black\">UNICEF &amp;WHO. Indicators for assessing infant and young child feeding practices. World Heal Organ [Internet]. 2010;WHA55 A55/:19. Available from: http://apps.who.int/iris/bitstream/handle/10665/44306/9789241599290_eng.pdf?sequence=1%0Ahttp://whqlibdoc.who.int/publications/2008/9789241596664_eng.pdf%5Cnhttp://www.unicef.org/programme/breastfeeding/innocenti.htm%5Cnhttp://innocenti15.net/declaration.</span></span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\"><span style=\"color:black\">Coates J, Swindale&nbsp; a, Bilinsky P. Household Food Insecurity Access Scale (HFIAS) for measurement of food access: indicator guide. Washington, DC Food Nutr Tech &hellip;. 2007;(August):Version 3</span></span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\"><span style=\"color:black\">windale A, Bilinsky P. Household Dietary Diversity Score (HDDS) for measurement of household food access: Indicator guide. Food Nutr Tech Assist &hellip;. 2006;11.</span></span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\"><span style=\"color:black\">Biswas TK, Kabir M. Measuring women&rsquo;s empowerment : Indicators and measurement techniques. Soc Change [Internet]. 2004 Sep 1;34(3):64&ndash;77. Available from: https://doi.org/10.1177/004908570403400305</span></span></span></span></span></li>\r\n</ol>','To assess the effectiveness of an integrated community-based intervention package in improving maternal, neonatal and infant health outcome in Jimma Zone, Southwest Ethiopia: a cluster randomized controlled trial',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,2,'community-based intervention, maternal, neonatal, infant, health outcome, randomized controlled trial',10,NULL,1,NULL,NULL,NULL),(64,111,9,20,'Summary \r\nBackground:  The role of social support during and after the pregnancy period has been of particular interest as a possible coping strategy for difficulties arising from the many challenges that pregnancy brings. Even though social support had great role in maternal and child health during and after pregnancy, there is no enough evidence about the impact of social support during pregnancy on the progress of labour, birth outcome (maternal and neonatal), maternal competence, postpartum depression, and anxiety among women in  Ethiopia\r\nObjective: The objective of this project is to assess the impact of perceived social support during pregnancy on the progress of labour and delivery, birth outcome (maternal and neonatal), maternal competence, postpartum depression, and anxiety among pregnant women in Jimma zone hospitals/2021\r\nMethod and martial: The study will be conducted in Jimma zonepublic hospitals using prospective cohort design on randomly selected pregnant women Data will be  entered into Epi- data 4.2 and exported (SPSS) version 23 for statistical analysis for cleaning and analysis. Logistic regression model will be used to analyze the data. Tables, charts, and texts will be used to report the result.\r\nBudget:The required budget for this study is 473,000 ETB',NULL,'IMPACT OF PERCEIVED SOCIAL SUPPORT DURING PREGNANCY ON THE PROGRESS OF LABOUR, BIRTH OUTCOME (MATERNAL AND NEONATAL), MATERNAL COMPETENCE, POSTPARTUM DEPRESSION, AND ANXIETY AMONG WOMEN IN JIMMA ZONE HOSPITALS','2021-11-19 13:55:38','8	Research outcome/impact\r\nThe finding from this research project will contribute the following outcomes \r\n•	The magnitude and associated factors of social support during pregnancy will be determined\r\n•	The impact of perceived social support on the progress of labour and delivery will be explored \r\n•	The effect of social support on the outcome of pregnancy, both maternal and neonatal outcome will be evaluated\r\n•	The level of maternal competency will be assessed \r\n•	The magnitude of post-partum depression will be determined \r\n•	The impact of social support during pregnancy on postpartum depression will be identified \r\n•	Level anxiety maternal during post-partum period be explored\r\n•	Information on the relationship between social support and maternal anxiety will be sought\r\n•	Evidence will be generated to take action for with low social support','d0a0b950952216cd60290a87005b20f4','completed','3.	Background and rationale\r\nPregnancy is a period of women’s lives and is defined as a period of developmental crisis in which complex processes like biological, physiological, social, and spiritual changes are experienced. Biological predisposition or psychosocial sensitivity of the women during pregnancy, birth, and the postpartum period areeasier to reveal depression(1), difficulties in maintaining the care for their health and the newborn (2),and acquisition of new skills related to childcare(3,4). Especially first-time mothers are at known risk for psychological distress in the postpartum period(5).\r\nSocial support is a multidimensional construct and it has expressed using various scales to reflect functional and structural dimensions (6,7): with six dimensions, namely attachment (parallels with emotional), social integration (parallels with companionship), reassurance of worth (parallels with positive feedback), reliable alliance (parallels with instrumental support), guidance (parallels with informational support), and opportunity for nurturance(7)(8).\r\nPregnancy outcome is the result of events that occurred from conception to the end of pregnancy, and this can be the maternal outcome or neonatal outcomes, such as low birth weight preterm birth, and stillbirth(9). During their first pregnancy, women may have no relevant health knowledge or experience with delivery, and fear of childbirth may lead to adverse outcomes for mothers and babies so social support is a very important influential factor for prenatal(10).\r\nPregnant women receiving low social support are at risk of substances use, developing mental illness, and adverse birth outcomes(11). The study in Iran evidenced  that low Social support was associated with pregnancy side effects and outcomes(12,13). Social support during pregnancy is thought to contribute to better pregnancy and birth outcomes(14).\r\nMaternal role competence is a mother’s skills and interactions in the care of her infant, a constituent of maternal role attainment (MRA), and maternal identity as a major component in the process(15). Achieving maternal-role competency is considered an important element of maternal adaptation. However, motherhood represents a principal transition in a woman’s life, indicating one’s capability to provide care for another human being as a mother(16). Developing maternal competence in first-time mothers has a significant impact on neonates’ growth psychosocial development and psychological development and social support can be an important element for becoming a new mother even many women have difficulty finding the energy to care for themselves, their infants, and their families(17). One of the effective factors on the maternal role was social support  (18).\r\nMany kinds of literature show that between 7 and 80per cent of women experience some form of postpartum distress from the more common ‘blues’ to depression(5). WHO reports that worldwide about 10% of pregnant women and 13% of women after childbirth experience depression(19).Depression and  anxiety in pregnancy and the postpartum period are associated with poor long-term maternal and child health outcomes while  Social support has been shown to mitigate the effects of maternal depression  and anxiety(20)\r\nliterature indicates that social support affects maternal during pregnancy and post-partum period anxiety; the study in Iran revealed that 37% of the total variance of pregnancy anxiety is justified by perceived stress and social support(21).Study conducted among pregnant women in Australia, 7.1% of pregnant women reported low social support(11).Even though Social support plays an important role, during pregnancy and childbirth (22), on pregnancy and infant outcome(23), a mediating role between antenatal depression among healthy primipara women(10).\r\nMost studies were conducted in high-income countries, and little is known in low-income countries including Ethiopia, while  Specific and contextualized data on social support during distinct health events are needed to improve social support interventions(24).Ethiopia is doing a lot to decrease maternal and neonatal morbidity and mortality and to achieve sustainable development goals. However, there is no literature, which addresses the impact of perceived social support on maternal and child health. Therefore this study aims to assess the impact of perceived social support during pregnancy on the progress of labour and delivery, birth outcome (maternal and neonatal), maternal competence, postpartum depression, and anxietyamong pregnantwomen in Jimma zone hospitals.','5.	Description of approaches/Methods\r\n	Study setting: Study will be conducted in Jimma zone hospitals. The study will be conducted in Jimma zone public hospitals. Jimma zone constituted of 21 woredas and 2 city administrations. \r\n	 There are six districts and one referral hospital in Jimma zone, Namely Shenengibie, Seka, Agaro, Limugenet, Senitema, Omonada, and Jimma Medical Center and Proportional allocation of the sample size will be done after sampled Hospitals \r\nStudy design and study population\r\nStudy design:  A prospective cohort study design will be conducted\r\nSource population: All pregnant women attending antenatal care service in Jimma zone hospitals\r\nStudy population:The study population will be all pregnant women who complete 32 weeks and attend antenatal care services in Jimma zone hospitals. \r\nInclusion Criteria\r\nAll pregnant women will be included in the study  \r\nExclusion criteria \r\nPregnant women with previous mental or physical complications/ disease will be excluded\r\nSample size and sampling procedure\r\nSample size determination\r\nThe sample size will be determined by using single population proportion formula by considering 50% since there is no previous work on the topic of interest and a marginal error of 5% between the sample and the population at 95% confidence level. \r\nThe formula for single population proportion:\r\n \r\nn = 384\r\nWhere: - n = Sample size, Zα/2 = Confidence level at 95% = 1.96, P = 50%\r\nd = margin of error of 5%\r\nIn addition, considering the design effect and lost to follow up we need to make the sample size double; therefore the final sample size will be (384×2)= 768 , finally it will be allocated proportionally for each study site.Separate sample size will be calculated for each objective\r\n\r\nSampling procedure:Study participants will be selected by systematic random sampling technique. \r\nData management and statistical analysis\r\n\r\nData will be entered into Epi data version 4.2 for coding and avoiding errors during data entry. The coded data will then be exported to the statistical package for social science (SPSS) version 23 for statistical analysis. Descriptive analysis like frequencies, percentages, means, and standard deviationswill be computed to explore socio-demographic, obstetric, and other health-related characteristics and psychosocial characteristics of study participants. Multinomial Logistic regression will be used to find out the association between outcome and explanatory variables. The model goodness of fit will be tested by using Hosmer-Lemeshow and Omnibus test. The strength of association will be evaluated using an odds ratio at a 95% confidence interval and P-value < 0.05 will be considered to declare significant associations. \r\n6.	Study variables\r\n6.1 Dependent variables\r\n•	Progress of Labour\r\n•	Pregnancy outcome(maternal and neonatal)\r\n•	Maternal competence\r\n•	Post-partum depression \r\n•	Post-partum anxiety\r\n6.2	Independentvariables\r\nLevel of social support Social support during pregnancy \r\nSociodemographic characteristics: Age, income, educational status, employment/Occupation, marital status, level of income, residency \r\nObstetrics related characteristics: Gestational age, abortion/stillbirth history, previous history of labor and delivery, current pregnancy complication, complications, number of ANC visits in the current pregnancy, less understanding of the delivery process, acceptance of the current pregnancy, the length of hospital stay. Parity, unplanned pregnancy, unwanted pregnancy, birth outcome, duration of labor\r\nPsychosocial and facility-related characteristics:baby father support, health personal approach, history of abuse and violence, worrying about the wellbeing of the fetus, preference of baby sex\r\nOperational definition \r\nSocial support:  the “information that leads the individual to believe that they are cared for, loved, esteemed and that they belong to a social network with mutual duties”(6).\r\nPerceived social support: the scale contains 12 items that are rated on a 5-point scale ranging from ‘strongly disagree’ to ‘strongly agree’. Possible scores range from 12 to 60. Higher total scores indicate greater perceived social support.\r\nParental competence: measured by a 5 point Likert Parental Sense of Competence Scale containing 17 items and using the total sum scores, higher scores will indicate higher parenting competency. The mean of the total sum scores will be used as a cutoff point for categorizing parental competence.\r\nAdverse birth outcomes: are defined as the presence of at least one or more of the following conditions in recent pregnancy such as low birth weight, macrosomia, preterm birth, or stillbirth(25). \r\nPost-partumdepression:is depression associated with having a baby often begins during pregnancy, which will be measured using Edinburgh Postnatal Depression Scale (EPDS) contains 10 items with 3 point Likert(26).\r\nPost-partum anxiety is a fear or nervousness during the post-partum period about what might happen which will be measured using validated Postpartum Specific Anxiety Scale (PSAS) with 16-item (27).\r\n\r\nQuality assurance\r\nData quality will be assured before, during, and after data collection. Before the actual data collection, the validity of the questionnaire will be checked. A pretest will be done on 5 % of the sample size in hospitals that will not be included in the actual study to determine clarity and internal consistency of the questionnaire and corrections and modifications will be taken according to the result. The one-day training will be givento data collectors and supervisors about the methodology and questionnaire by the investigator.    \r\nDuring the data collection period, the purpose of data collection and the importance of the study will be informed to the participants to generate quality data. The collected data will be checked for completeness and consistency by trained supervisors and investigators through close follow-up and necessary corrective actions will be made accordingly. \r\n7	Capacity building \r\nInclusion of Ph.D. and MSc students\r\n9.	Ethical consideration\r\nEthical approval and clearance will be obtained from the institutional review board (IRB) of Jimma university institute of health and additional permission will be obtained from the respective hospitals. Moreover, the purpose of the study will be explained to the study participants and verbal informed consent will be taken before data collection starts.  Confidentiality of the data will be insured. In addition, the respondents’ right to refuse or withdraw from filling out the questionnaire will be fully realized and the information provided by each respondent will be kept strictly confidential. \r\nPlan for dissemination and ensuring utilization of findings \r\nThe findings of this will be submitted to governmental and non-governmental stakeholders and policymakers. Finally, it will be submitted for publication in a peer-reviewed journal.','<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><strong><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">References </span></span></strong></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">1. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Aytac SH. The Effect of Social Support on Pregnancy and Postpartum Depression. 2020;13(1):746&ndash;58. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">2. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Silveira J, Cardoso C, Langendorf TF, Maris S, Padoin M, Mombaque W. Social support perceived by pregnant and puerperal women with HIV : A cross-sectional study *. 2021; </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">3. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Perun MB. Maternal identity of women in the postpartum period. J Educ Cult Soc. 2020;4(1):95&ndash;105. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">4. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Ngai FW, Chan SWC. Stress, maternal role competence, and satisfaction among Chinese women in the perinatal period. Res Nurs Heal. 2012;35(1):30&ndash;9. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">5. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Machado TDS, Chur-hansen A, Due C. First-time mothers &rsquo; perceptions of social support : Recommendations for best practice. 2020; </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">6. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Cutrona &amp; Russell. Social Provisons Scale.Pdf. 1984. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">7. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; A. D, A. K. Social support, stress, and depressive symptoms among the elderly. Turk Geriatr Derg [Internet]. 2010;13(2):30. Available from: http://geriatri.dergisi.org/pdf.php3?id=493%0Ahttp://ovidsp.ovid.com/ovidweb.cgi?T=JS&amp;PAGE=reference&amp;D=emed12&amp;NEWS=N&amp;AN=71033287</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">8. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Milgrom J, Hirshler Y, Reece J, Holt C, Gemmill AW. Social Support &mdash; A Protective Factor for Depressed Perinatal Women ? 2019; </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">9. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Outcomes AB, States U. Adverse Birth Outcomes. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">10. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Zhou X, Liu H, Li X, Li F, Zhang S, Zhang S. Mediating effects of social support between antenatal depression and fear of childbirth among nulliparous woman. 2021;10(6):6399&ndash;409. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">11. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Bedaso A, Adams J, Peng W, Sibbritt D. Prevalence and determinants of low social support during pregnancy among Australian women : a community-based cross-sectional study. 2021;1&ndash;11. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">12. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Abdollahpour S, Ramezani S, Khosravi A. Perceived Social Support among Family in Pregnant Women. 3(21):879&ndash;88. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">13. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Iranzad I, Bani S, Hasanpour S, Mohammadalizadeh S. Perceived Social Support and Stress among Pregnant Women at Health Centers of Iran- Tabriz. 2014;3(4):287&ndash;95. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">14. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Title P, Gardner K, Douglas K, Sector P. ACT HEALTH VACATION STUDY PROGRAM. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">15. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Sindiani AM, Saeieh SE, Rahimzadeh M, Yazdkhasti M, Kim THM, Connolly JA, et al. Nursing-Theory-Utilization-Application. J Affect Disord [Internet]. 2014;7(1):1&ndash;7. Available from: http://dx.doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0240239</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">16. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Shrestha S, Adachi K, Petrini MA, Shrestha S. Maternal Role : A Concept Analysis. 2018;(4). </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">17. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Kelbore W, Yasin H, Kolle D. Maternal Role Competence and its Associated Factors among Mothers Who Attended Immunization Clinics at Public Health Facilities of Arba Minch Town, Southern Ethiopia : A Cross-Sectional Study. Int J Sci Basic Appl Res. 2020;53(CI):129&ndash;38. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">18. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Gao L ling, Chan SW chi, Sun K. Effects of an interpersonal-psychotherapy-oriented childbirth education program for Chinese first-time childbearing women at 3-month follow up: Randomised controlled trial. Int J Nurs Stud. 2012;49(3):274&ndash;81. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">19. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Jiang L. Peer Groups, Social Support, and Well-being : Evidence from a Large Online Maternity Community. 2021; </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">20. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; D SMP. PT US CR. J Affect Disord [Internet]. 2019; Available from: https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jad.2019.03.083</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">21. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Basharpoor S, Heydarirad H, Daryadel SJ, Heydari F. The Role of Perceived Stress and Social Support among Predicting Anxiety in Pregnant Women. 2017;27(2):9&ndash;16. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">22. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Deconinck T, Capron A, Hirsch C, Ghorbaniasl G. Prediction of near- and far-field noise generated by contra-rotating open rotors. Vol. 11, International Journal of Aeroacoustics. 2012. 177&ndash;196 p. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">23. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Alio AP, Salihu HM, Mcintosh C, August EM, Weldeselasse H, Sanchez E, et al. The Effect of Paternal Age on Fetal Birth Outcomes. Am J Men&#39;s Health. 2012;6(5):427&ndash;35. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">24. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Edmonds JK, Paul M, Sibley LM. Type, Content, and Source of Social Support Perceived by Women during Pregnancy : Evidence from Matlab , Bangladesh. 2011;29(2):163&ndash;73. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">25. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Tamirat KS, Sisay MM, Tesema GA, Tessema ZT. Determinants of adverse birth outcome in Sub-Saharan Africa: analysis of recent demographic and health surveys. BMC Public Health. 2021;21(1):1&ndash;10. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">26. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Kleiman K. F. Edinburgh Postnatal Depression Scale (EPDS ). Ther Postpartum Woman. 2021;347(3):302&ndash;3. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">27. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Davies SM, Christiansen P, Harrold JA, Silverio SA, Fallon V. Creation and validation of the Postpartum Specific Anxiety Scale Research Short-Form (PSAS-RSF). Arch Women&#39;s Ment Health. 2021;957&ndash;69. </span></span></span></span></p>','The objective of this project is to assess the impact of perceived social support during pregnancy on the progress of labour and delivery, birth outcome (maternal and neonatal), maternal competence, postpartum depression, and anxiety among pregnant women in Jimma zone hospitals/2021',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,2,'Key Words: Social support, Pregnancy, Maternal Competence, Pregnancy outcome, progress of labour, postpartum depression, post-partum anxiety,Jimma zone, Ethiopia',10,'<ol start=\"9\">\r\n	<li><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><strong><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Timeline: </span></span></strong></span></span></li>\r\n</ol>\r\n\r\n<table cellspacing=\"0\" class=\"Table\" style=\"border-collapse:collapse; border:none; margin-left:-1px; width:6