=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Cost of supplies, stationeries, and transportation</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:23px; vertical-align:top; width:130px\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">80,000</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:23px; vertical-align:top; width:135px\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\">&nbsp;</p>\r\n			</td>\r\n		</tr>\r\n		<tr>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:1px solid black; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:30px; vertical-align:top; width:43px\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">3</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:30px; vertical-align:top; width:352px\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Contingency (10%)</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:30px; vertical-align:top; width:130px\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">43,000</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:30px; vertical-align:top; width:135px\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\">&nbsp;</p>\r\n			</td>\r\n		</tr>\r\n		<tr>\r\n			<td rowspan=\"2\" style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:1px solid black; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:23px; vertical-align:top; width:43px\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\">&nbsp;</p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td rowspan=\"2\" style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:23px; vertical-align:top; width:352px\">\r\n			<p style=\"margin-left:8px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Total </span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td rowspan=\"2\" style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:23px; vertical-align:top; width:130px\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><strong><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">473,000</span></span></strong></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:23px; vertical-align:top; width:135px\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\">&nbsp;</p>\r\n			</td>\r\n		</tr>\r\n		<tr>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:5px; vertical-align:top; width:135px\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\">&nbsp;</p>\r\n			</td>\r\n		</tr>\r\n	</tbody>\r\n</table>\r\n\r\n<p>&nbsp;</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),(65,140,9,25,'Them: Traditional and complementary Medicine\r\nTitle of the project: Challenges, opportunities, and opinion to integrate traditional medicine into the national health care system: Moving towards Universal Health Coverage.\r\nSub-research questions: \r\n	To explore opinion, and concerns of traditional medicine practitioners on integration of traditional medicine into the health care system\r\n	To explore opinion of modern health care practitioners on integration of traditional medicine into the health care system\r\n	To explore the opinion of the community members on integration of traditional medicine in to the modern healthcare system\r\n	To explore challenges and opportunities in integrating traditional medicine into the health care system\r\nName of PI: Mr. Azmeraw Bekele (MSc.) Pharmaceutical supply chain management, Faculty of health sciences\r\nList of Co-Applicants: \r\n1.	Tidenek Mulugeta \r\n2.	Tadesse Gudeta\r\n3.	Bekele Boche\r\n4.	Bezawit Alem\r\nBudget: 258,500 ETB\r\nDuration of the Project: January 01, 2022 to June 30 2022','Challenges, opportunities, and opinion to integrate traditional medicine into the national health care system','Challenges, opportunities, and opinion to integrate traditional medicine into the national health care system: Moving towards Universal Health Coverage.','2021-11-19 14:08:43','The findings of the project will help to better foresee the challenges and opportunities related to the integration of TM into the modern healthcare system. More importantly, policymakers will be more informed about traditional healers\' readiness to be incorporated into the health system, as well as modern health professionals\' willingness to cooperate and practice with traditional healers. In general, the findings of this project will be used as a significant input and directive in framing and moving forward with the integration of TM into the national modern healthcare system.','620651f7012894885dc639701977527a','completed','Primary health care is crucial to achieving universal health coverage (UHC), which ensures that all people and communities have access to the healthcare services they want and need without having to pay a high price (1,2). Since the Declaration of Alma-Ata in 1978, traditional medicine has been acknowledged as a vital primary health care resource for many communities and as a component of achieving “health for all” (3). The World Health Organization (WHO) defines traditional medicine as \"the total of knowledge, skill, and practices based on theories, beliefs, and experiences indigenous to various cultures, whether explicable or not, used in health maintenance as well as the prevention, diagnosis, improvement, or treatment of physical and mental illness\" (4).  It is a form of primary health care, which is practiced in many parts of the world, most notably in Africa, Asia, and the Pacific (5). \r\nThere has recently been a surge of interest in Traditional Medicine (TM) and its application to public health in both developed and developing countries (6). TM has many advantages, including easy accessibility, extensive public acceptance, little side effects, and contribution to economic progress (7–9). Traditional practitioners are sometimes the first and only health providers accessible in some places, and traditional herbal medicines are employed for primary health care (10). In the quest for healthier populations, the involvement of TM practitioners in teaching individuals, families, and communities on health promotion, illness prevention, public health issues, and appropriate care-seeking can also be leveraged (11). \r\nTM is used by more than 80% of the world\'s population, either alone or in combination with other treatments (3). Similarly, TM and Traditional Health Practitioners (THPs) constitute the major source of health care for 80% of the people in Sub-Saharan Africa (12). In Ethiopia, traditional medicine is used by around 80% of the population. TM popularity, preference, and use in Ethiopia have not declined, even though primary healthcare coverage is better than it has ever been (9,13–15). \r\nSince the WHO first advocated TM in developing nations in 1978, there has been a growing interest among developing countries in incorporating it into their national healthcare systems. For instance, as of 2018, 109 WHO Member States, including Ethiopia reported having a TM legislative or regulatory framework (16,17). The integration of TM with the national healthcare system may offer opportunities to improve five critical health-system features for achieving UHC: quality, efficiency, equity, accountability, sustainability, and resilience (18). Ethiopia and other countries in the process of integrating TM into their national healthcare systems typically have a lengthy history of traditional medicine use but have yet to achieve the level of support and institutionalization seen in well-established countries (3,19). The degree of integration with national healthcare systems varies greatly across and between countries. Integration has been theorized, characterized, and classified in various ways at the health system, service delivery, and consumer level (Fig:1) (20,21). Intolerant, tolerant, inclusive, or integrative healthcare systems are influenced by local contextual elements at the health system level, that is, the level of official government recognition: intolerant, tolerant, inclusive, or integrative healthcare systems. Integration at the health-systems level, in turn, has an impact on integration at the service-delivery level. Integration at the service delivery level can take the form of multiple providers of different modalities collaborating or a single \'integrative\' professional providing both conventional and TM therapy (22, 23). Consumers, regardless of the system-level model of integration, experience varied degrees of TM and biomedicine integration because they seek healthcare services based on their requirements, access, and ideologies. Individual settings have distinct sociocultural, economic, and political conditions that facilitate or obstruct T&M inclusion into healthcare systems. In this context, each country must determine its path to T&M integration, with varying degrees of recognition of the role TM may play in achieving UHC (24).\r\nIn Ethiopia, there is a wealth of literature on the varied incarnations of TM practices for a wide range of health problems, the spectrum of TM practices, and the community\'s preference to utilize TM. For example, research from northern Ethiopia found that 70.9 % of study participants had used traditional medicine (25). Furthermore, a survey conducted in western Ethiopia indicated that 74.22 %, 23.83 percent, and 238.8 % of the study subjects utilized traditional medicine, believed TM was more effective than modern medicine and favored it over modern medicine, respectively (26). Likewise, according to a study done in southwest Ethiopia, a substantial majority of study participants (81%) practiced TM (9). Regarding the integration of TM with modern healthcare systems, available evidence from Ethiopia found that both health professionals (86%) and traditional healers (97%) supported TM-modern healthcare collaboration (27). \r\nThe Ethiopian government, as part of its national heritage, also strongly promotes and encourages traditional medicine through its policies (28). Despite these shreds of evidence on the importance and use of TM, as well as policy commitments, the traditional medicine of Ethiopia remained unintegrated to the national health care system. Therefore, this study aims at exploring the challenges and opportunities of system integration from the perspectives of different stakeholders.','Study area and period\r\nJimma zone is one of 20 zones in the Oromia National Regional State located 346 km southwest of the capital, Addis Ababa. Jimma town is the capital of the Jimma zone. Jimma zone has twenty districts and one hundred twenty-five public health facilities. In Jimma town, there are seven public health facilities. Generally, there are 512 health posts in Jimma town and Jimma zone. This zone has a total population of 2,486,155, of which 1,250,527 are males and 1,235,628 females (29). The data collection will be conducted from February to March 2022.\r\nStudy design and population \r\nA qualitative exploratory study approach will be employed to understand the stakeholders’, TM practitioners’, health workers\' community, and community leaders\' opinions on the integration of traditional medicine into the national health care system.\r\nPopulation Source Population: \r\nAll traditional and modern medical practitioners including health extension workers, community members, community leaders, and health bureau officers at federal, regional, and zonal levels will be the source population. \r\nStudy population\r\nIn addition to Jimma town, at least three districts will be identified and included in the study. Later on, two kebeles will be selected from each district randomly. Purposively selected traditional medicine practitioners, healthcare professionals (Medical doctors, Health officers, nurses, physiotherapists, midwives, pharmacists, and health extension workers) from different levels of health facilities, stakeholders (officers from FMOH, regional and Zonal health bureau), community members and community leaders (religious leaders, influential individuals, and elders) fulfilling the eligibility criteria will be the study population. \r\nEligibility criteria \r\nInclusion criteria \r\nOfficers of the health bureau at all levels (Federal to district) will be considered if they have a direct connection to the issue. Traditional medicine practitioners who have the highest year of experience among their colleagues will be given priority for admittance. Being well renowned in their respective communities will be the inclusion criteria for traditional medicine practitioners and community leaders. Community members (residents) who are greater than eighteen years from the selected districts and kebeles will be included in the study. Health professionals from different disciplines will be selected from different levels of healthcare delivery facilities.  Health extension workers will be recruited for in-depth interviews from the selected kebeles depending on their year of experience.\r\nExclusion criteria \r\nParticipants who are unable to respond and individuals with serious illnesses will be excluded.\r\nSample size determination and sampling procedure \r\nPractitioners, community leaders, and community members from Jimma town, and the selected districts and kebeles will be chosen through purposeful sampling. Religious diversity will be taken into account to capture the diverse perspectives of participants. Health bureau officers who have a direct connection with the issue from the federal, regional, and zonal, and district levels will be purposively selected. Health workers from various domains (Medical doctors, Health officers, nurses, physiotherapists, midwives, pharmacists, and health extension workers) will be selected on purpose. Tentatively, 10 in-depth interviews with traditional medicine practitioners, 4 FGD with community members, 3 FGD with health professionals, 3 in-depth interviews with health extension workers, 6 in-depth interviews with community leaders, and religious leaders (3 with each of them), 5 in-depth interviews with officers from zonal to federal health bureaus. Saturations of ideas will be the basis for the determination of the size of the participants for interviewees and FGDs. The data will concurrently be collected and coded to decide on the nature of the subsequent data to be collected. Generally, the following participants will take part in the study\r\n•	 Health practitioners: physicians, midwives, nurses and midwives, physiotherapists, pharmacists,  health officers, and health extension workers\r\n•	Traditional medicine practitioners \r\n•	Community members\r\n•	Community leaders and\r\n•	Concerned officers of health bureau from Federal to district\r\nData collection Method and Procedure \r\nData Collection Method\r\nThe data will be collected through focus group discussions (FGDs) and in-depth interviews, and key informant interviews. An open-ended guide will be utilized to facilitate the discussion and interviews. The responses and discussions will be recorded using a digital voice recorder accompanied by note-taking. A natural setting will be maintained to build rapport with the participants. The two commonly spoken local languages, Amharic and Afan Oromo, will be used for discussion. Data collectors having experience in qualitative data collection and who are fluent in these two languages will be recruited. A team of qualitative researchers having MSC and above in health professions will review transcripts against records and supervise the whole process. \r\nData Collection Procedure\r\nFocus group discussions (FGDs) with Modern medical practitioners\r\nTo explore the opinions of modern practitioners on the integration of traditional medicine into the national health care system, in-depth interviews and FGDs will be conducted. Each FGD will comprise 6-12 participants. Health professionals including physicians, health officers, nurses, physiotherapists, midwives, and pharmacists, will be included. \r\nFocus group discussions (FGDs) with community members\r\nTo explore the community’s opinion, and concerns on the integration of TM into the national healthcare system, four FGD will be conducted (two with males and two with females)\r\nIn-depth interviews with community leaders, religious leaders, traditional medicine practitioners, health extension workers, and health bureau officers\r\nIn-depth, interviews will be conducted with purposefully selected health bureau officers, community leaders, religious leaders, traditional medicine practitioners, and health extension workers. Views of the responsible officers will be explored on the challenges and opportunities of system integration. Purposefully selected officers at Federal MOH, Oromia health bureau, and Jimma zone health bureau will take part in the study. The in-depth interview with traditional practitioners aims at exploring opinions of traditional medicine practitioners, and their concerns regarding the integration and associated challenges opportunities, and concerns. Community leaders and religious leaders interviewees will be picked from, elders, religious leaders, and influential individuals.  Health extension workers will be selected for in-depth interviews from the selected kebeles to explore their opinions and existing enablers (opportunities) regarding the integration of TM into the health system. The interview will be conducted by qualitative researchers with health professional backgrounds having MSC and above.\r\nData analysis\r\nThe data will be transcribed with verbatim transcription and translated into English after the audio record. The coding and analysis of the data will be assisted by ATLAS.ti 7.1.4. Software. Repetitive readings will be made to get accustomed to the data. Then, codes will be assigned and inter-code consistencies will be checked. The codes will be clustered into categories and themes will be organized by linking related categories. The whole research team will review the coded transcripts and generate codes to reach to consensus. Finally, the themes will be summarized and opinions among interviewees will be quoted to explain some important expressions. \r\nData Quality assurance \r\nData collectors will be trained for two days. To maintain trustworthiness, discussions, and interviews will be conducted with participants who have relevant experience and expertise. The data collection tools will be prepared in English, and then translated to Amharic and Afan Oromo finally it will be back-translated into English by another independent translator to check for consistency. Member checking will be done at the end of data collection by summarizing major thematic areas that have emerged during the interview and FGD. Team members of the research will also review and give their comments on the report. To ensure transferability, the whole research process, will be explained extensively. To ensure dependability, the methodological approaches followed will be clearly described. A detailed chronology of research activities will be audited by the research team. \r\nPlan for dissemination and ensuring utilization of findings\r\nThe study\'s findings will be submitted to the Research and Postgraduate Coordinating Office of Jimma University. It will be attempted to present in various conferences and symposiums. Copies of the document will be distributed to the relevant stakeholders so that they can utilize it as a resource for planning and implementing health interventions. An attempt will be made to have the paper published in a reputable journal.','<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">1. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Rodin J, De Ferranti D. Universal health coverage: The third global health transition? Lancet [Internet]. 2012;380(9845):861&ndash;2. Available from: http://dx.doi.org/10.1016/S0140-6736(12)61340-3</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">2. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; World Health Organization. Universal Health Coverage: Moving Towards Better Health. 2016;1&ndash;92. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">3. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; World Health Organization. Traditional and Complementary Medicine in Primary Health Care. 2018;1&ndash;11. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">4. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Negahban A, Maleki M, Abbasian A. Elements of integrating traditional and complementary medicine into primary healthcare: A systematic review. J Clin Diagnostic Res. 2018;12(12):IE05-IE11. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">5. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; C.K Ong, G. Bodekar, C. Grundy, G Burford K shein. 9241562862_Map.Pdf. 2005. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">6. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Unnikrishnan Payyappallimana. Role of Traditional Medicine in Primary Health Care: An Overview of Perspectives and Challenges. Yokohama J Soc Sci [Internet]. 2010;14(6):57-78. Available from: http://s3.amazonaws.com/academia.edu.documents/11039208/Role_of_Traditional_Medicine_in_Primary_Health_Care_A