uot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Transcription</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top; width:204px\">\r\n			<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">58,000</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n		</tr>\r\n		<tr>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:1px solid black; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top; width:127px\">\r\n			<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">4</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top; width:300px\">\r\n			<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Transportation</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top; width:204px\">\r\n			<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">15,500</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n		</tr>\r\n		<tr>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:1px solid black; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:17px; vertical-align:top; width:127px\">\r\n			<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">5</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:17px; vertical-align:top; width:300px\">\r\n			<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Stationary</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:17px; vertical-align:top; width:204px\">\r\n			<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">2,500</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n		</tr>\r\n		<tr>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:1px solid black; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:38px; vertical-align:top; width:127px\">\r\n			<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">6</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:38px; vertical-align:top; width:300px\">\r\n			<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Contingency budget</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:38px; vertical-align:top; width:204px\">\r\n			<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">25,850 ETB</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n		</tr>\r\n		<tr>\r\n			<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:1px solid black; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top; width:427px\">\r\n			<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><strong><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Grand total</span></span></strong></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top; width:204px\">\r\n			<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><strong><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">284, 350 ETB</span></span></strong></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n		</tr>\r\n	</tbody>\r\n</table>\r\n\r\n<p>&nbsp;</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),(67,7,9,19,'Antimicrobial resistance is recognized as a One Health challenge because of the rapid emergence and dissemination of resistant bacteria and genes among humans, animals and the environment. The need for a One Health approach to address antimicrobial resistance is now firmly established at the international level and is included in the action plans of many countries around the world. However, there are significant challenges in implementation of antimicrobial stewardship mainly due to lack of coordination and collaboration of the human-animal and environmental sectors, limited political commitment and low awareness and engagement among stakeholders, limited technical and financial capacity of sector offices. Therefore, this study aim to analyze the existing gaps in polices and strategies; and implementation status among human, animal and environment sectors in Ethiopia. A cross sectional study will be conducted between November 2021 to October 2022. All federal ministerial offices involved in public health, agriculture and veterinary services, environment will be included in this study. Moreover, a total of 623 actors from healthcare workers, veterinarians, farmers, development agents, water and wastewater treatment plant operators, conservationists, municipality workers, environmental health experts, research institutions, regulatory bodies, academicians and policy makers will be included in this study. Furthermore, 20 FDGS, 40 KII, expert elicitation and workshop will be conducted to explore qualitative information. FAO assessment framework will be used to identify the existing policy and implementation gaps. A total of 528,036 ETB is required to carry out this study.',NULL,'Analysis of policies and implementation gaps in Antimicrobial stewardship among human, animal and environment sectors in Ethiopia. A One Health Approach','2021-11-19 15:01:37','5.	Research output/outcome \r\nIncreasing the interdisciplinary coordination to curbing AMR can help promote science-based decision making; reduce unnecessary duplication among the sectors responsible for the health of humans, animals and the environment\r\nOutput 1: Systems for AMR will be strengthened among human, animal and environmental sectors in Ethiopia\r\nActivities:\r\na.	Develop guidelines in reference to the implementation of Antimicrobial stewardship \r\nb.	Building awareness among the policy-makers for coordinated action across the sectors, updating medical and allied health sciences curriculum for rational use of medicines including antimicrobials, and adequate resourcing with budget and human resources\r\nOutput 2: Systems for optimized use strengthened in human, animal and environment sectors  \r\nActivities:\r\n2.1: Scale up enforcement of regulation along the distribution chain of antimicrobials\r\n2.3: Develop a reporting system and database to support antimicrobial consumption in humans, animals and environment \r\n2.4: Scale up implementation of national AMS guidelines \r\n2.5: Improve reporting on Antimicrobial use (AMU) in animals\r\n2.6: Building capacity of county veterinary services on implementation of the National Action Plan (NAP) and prudent use of antimicrobials\r\nOutput 3: Improved collaboration and partnership among human, animal and environmental sectors \r\nActivities:\r\n3.1: Improve the capacity of each sector based on the identified gaps \r\n3.2: Publication/development of newsletters and peer articles on AMR progress and NAP implementation\r\n3.3: Development of policy brief','cb0f787cb7c739c69b3a40f6b5b3323a','completed','1.	Background and rationale \r\nAntimicrobials and antibiotics are the cornerstone of modern medicine and are necessary treatments for conditions ranging from certain skin infections to warding off secondary infections after major surgeries. Antimicrobial resistance (AMR) is one of the biggest global challenges to human development placing the sustainable development goals (SDGs 1, 2, 3, 6 and 10) at risk if left unchecked. An estimated 700,000 people die each year from drug resistant infections and this number is expected to rise to 10 million by 2050 (World Bank, 2017). According to World Bank estimates about 24 million people will be pushed into extreme poverty as a result of AMR (Adeyi et al. 2017). AMR expected to cause losses to the global economy as much as 3.4 trillion USD by 2030 and 100 trillion USD annually by 2050 (World Bank, 2017). \r\n\r\nAntimicrobial resistance is recognized as a One Health challenge because of the rapid emergence and dissemination of resistant bacteria and genes among humans, animals and the environment (Robinson et al. 2016). Accordingly, Global and National Action Plans (NAPs) to tackle AMR, a tripartite alliance of the World Health Organization (WHO), the Food and Agricultural Organization (FAO) and the World Organization for Animal Health (OIE) set policies and strategies for the implementation of antimicrobial stewardship (FAO, 2021). However, the implementation status of policies and strategies in developing countries such as Ethiopia is largely unknown.  \r\n\r\nHumans, animals, and the environment are the reservoirs that contribute and allow AMR to propagate in interconnected ecosystems. The irrational use of antimicrobials especially for self treatment, over-prescription by medical practitioners and illegal access to antimicrobial medication without a prescription are the key drivers of antimicrobial resistance (Haque et al. 2019). In addition, improper management of pharmaceutical waste could be one of the mechanisms by which resistant genes can transfer among pathogens in the environment (Bengtsson-Palme et al. 2018). On the other hand, the routine use of antibiotics for growth promotion, prophylactic and therapeutic purposes in animal production systems can lead to the emergence of resistant bacteria (Hao et al. 2020). Therefore, multi-sectoral, multi-disciplinary, and multi-institutional and thus need a coordinated response of the three sectors, adopting a comprehensive approach such as that of One Health. A One Health approach can build connections and communication channels across sectors to collaborate on antimicrobial stewardship and the implementation of programs, policies and legislation (McEwen and Collignon 2018). The need for a One Health approach to address antimicrobial resistance is now firmly established at the international level and is included in the action plans of many countries around the world. However, there are significant challenges in implementation of antimicrobial stewardship mainly due to lack of coordination and collaboration of the human-animal and environmental sectors, limited political commitment and low awareness and engagement among stakeholders, limited technical and financial capacity of sector offices. \r\n\r\nEthiopia is a potential hotspot for the emergence of AMR due to the high burden of infectious diseases. Poor sewage and water treatment systems, limited enforcement of regulations to penalize non-compliance, and the relatively unregulated access to antimicrobials for humans and high antimicrobial usage for livestock are the major factors contributed to AMR (FMHACA, 2015). Although, Ethiopia has policies and strategies for the prevention and containment of AMR (FMHACA, 2015), its implementation status is unknown. Therefore, this study aim to analyze the existing polices and strategies; and implementation status among human, animal and environment sectors in the country. The findings of this study will help to enhance the implementation of antimicrobial stewardship','1.	Methods and materials\r\n1.1	Study area and period\r\nThis study will be conducted in Federal ministerial offices, Addis Ababa city administration and Oromia regional state between December 2021 to October 2022.  \r\n1.2	Study design \r\n	\r\nA cross sectional study will be conducted to assess the existing policies and implementation gaps in antimicrobial stewardship among human, animal and environmental health sectors \r\n1.3	Study population \r\nAll federal ministerial offices involved in public health, agriculture and veterinary services, environment, forest and climate change, wild life conservation and natural resources management will be included in this study. Moreover, healthcare workers, veterinarians, farmers, development agents, water and wastewater treatment plant operators, conservationists, municipality workers, environmental health experts, research institutions, regulatory bodies, academicians and policy makers will be included in this study\r\n1.4	Sample size and sampling techniques \r\nAntimicrobial stewardship related policies and legislations of Addis Ababa city administration, Oromia regional state and various federal ministerial offices including  ministry of Health, ministry of Agriculture and Natural resources, Environment, forest and climate change commission, ministry of water irrigation and electricity and Ethiopia Wildlife conservation authority will be  evaluated. On the other hand, we estimated sample size as described by Kothari 2009 for policy implementation gaps among policy actors. Accordingly, a total of 623 actors from healthcare workers, veterinarians, farmers, development agents, water and wastewater treatment plant operators, conservationists, municipality workers, environmental health experts, research institutions, regulatory bodies, academicians and policy makers will be included in this study. The number of policy actors in each institution will be estimated and samples will be proportionally allocated to each institutions and professional category.  \r\n1.5	Data collection  \r\n\r\nWe will use FAO assessment framework to evaluate the implementation status of policies and strategies (Table 1). Data will be collected though desk review, document review, survey, FDG and KII. A total of 623 participants will be involved for interviewing. In addition, 20 FDGS, 40 KII, expert elicitation and workshop will be conducted to obtain the required information.  FAO assessment framework will be used to identify the existing policy and implementation gaps. This Framework’s review process helps reveal gaps in national AMR and AMU policies and can help assess the compatibility of various types of national policy with international standards for addressing AMR. The primary focus of the Framework is on policies for which national authorities are typically responsible and provides insights into ways that government agencies responsible for addressing AMR can improve their policy approach to ensure that interventions are well justified, timely and effective. A special emphasis of the Framework is given to stakeholder engagement and multi-sectoral coordination. The Framework raises questions and makes recommendations for addressing specific policy issues and provides case studies of policy interventions which can be adapted to fit various national contexts. In addition, a standardized assessment tool will be used to assess perception and policy implementation gaps among policy actors. \r\n1.6	Impact statement \r\n1.6.1	Beneficiary analysis \r\nThe primary beneficiaries for this study are public health, veterinary medicine, agriculture and environmental practitioners, patients, farmers, the general public and policy makers who have the potential to influence, develop, implement and reviewing policies and strategies. \r\n1.6.2	Engagement strategy \r\nThe main aim of the Stakeholder engagement is to identify and engage every important Stakeholder at local, regional, and national level to ensure that the outcomes of the project will address the gaps in awareness, practices, policies, strategies in antimicrobial stewardship. Stakeholders in antimicrobial use include physicians, nurses, pharmacists, patients, medical doctors, pharmaceutical companies, regulators, and policy makers. The list of stakeholders and their role is shown in Table 2\r\n\r\n1.7	Data management and analysis\r\nAnalysis of qualitative data was performed using Microsoft Office, Word Processing and Excel Office 16 using the guide by Braun and Clarke, 2006. Qualitative thematic analysis was performed for qualitative data\r\n1.8	Ethical consideration \r\nEthical clearance will be obtained from Institutional Review Board of Institute of Health, Jimma University and National Research Ethics Committee. Written consent will be obtained from Federal, regional and Addis Ababa city administration offices. Additionally, an informed written consent will be obtained from the respondents and all the information obtained from each study participant will be confidential.','<h1><span style=\"font-size:14pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\"><span style=\"color:#0d0d0d\"><a name=\"_Toc88224848\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\">References</span></a> </span></span></span></h1>\r\n\r\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px\"><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\"><span style=\"color:#0d0d0d\">Adeyi&nbsp; et al. (2017). Drug-resistant infections : a threat to our economic future (Vol. 2) : final report (English). Washington, D.C. : World Bank Group. </span></span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px\"><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\"><span style=\"color:#0d0d0d\">Bengtsson-Palme J, Kristiansson E, Larsson DGJ (2018)&nbsp; Environmental factors influencing the development and spread of antibiotic resistance. FEMS Microbiol Rev.&nbsp; 42(1)</span></span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px\"><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\"><span style=\"color:#0d0d0d\">FAO (2021). Evaluation of FAO&rsquo;s role and work on antimicrobial resistance (AMR) </span></span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px\"><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\"><span style=\"color:#0d0d0d\">&nbsp;FMHACA (2015). Strategy for the prevention and containment of antimicrobial resistance for Ethiopia. </span></span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\"><span style=\"color:#0d0d0d\">Haque et al. (2019). Self-medication of antibiotics: investigating practice among university students at the Malaysian National Defence University. &nbsp;Infection and Drug Resistance&nbsp;12 1333&mdash;1351.&nbsp; </span></span></span><a href=\"https://doi.org/10.2147/IDR.S203364\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\"><span style=\"color:#0d0d0d\">https://doi.org/10.2147/IDR.S203364</span></span></span></a></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\"><span style=\"color:#0d0d0d\">Hao TTH, Yidana Z, Smooker PM, Coloe PJ (2020). Antibiotic use in food animals worldwide, with a focus on Africa: Pluses and minuses. Journal of Global Antimicrobial Resistance 20, Pp170-177.</span></span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\"><span style=\"color:#0d0d0d\">McEwen S A and Collignon PJ (2018). Antimicrobial Resistance: a One Health Perspective. Microbiol Spectr. &nbsp;doi: 10.1128/microbiolspec.ARBA-0009-2017.</span></span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px\"><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\"><span style=\"color:#0d0d0d\">Robinson,&nbsp; TP. et al. (2016). Antibiotic resistance is the quintessential One Health issue. Trans. R. Soc. Trop. Med. Hyg. 110, 377 &ndash; 380. (doi:10.1093/trstmh/ trw048)</span></span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px\"><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\"><span style=\"color:#0d0d0d\">World Bank Group (2017). A final report on drug-resistant Infections. A threat to our economic future. </span></span></span></span></span></p>','To evaluate the existing policies and strategies in Antimicrobial stewardship among human, animal and environment sectors in Ethiopia.',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,2,'Antimicrobials, Antimicrobial resistance, One health, Stewardship',10,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),(68,76,9,15,'Executive summery \r\nBackground: \r\nThere is empirical evidence that Comprehensive Sexuality Education (CSE) plays a major role in improving sexual and reproductive health and right (SRHR) outcomes. However, there is no evidence indicating the most effective channels for CSE delivery in Ethiopia. A landscape analysis indicated that there are four main CSE delivery channels; school-based, traditional mass media, peer-to-peer approaches, and digital media. In Ethiopia, the first three: school-based, traditional mass media, peer-to-peer approaches were implemented for decades, however, according to recent data indicated that still high burden of unmet needs. Digital tools can help fill these gaps. Digital media are increasingly influencing the lives of young people. Around the world, adolescents and young people are using digital media as a platform to learn, experience, and communicate.\r\nObjective: \r\nThis project aimed to develop mobile application which will helps users to find age appropriate sexual and reproductive health information and services.\r\nMethodology: \r\nInnovation - In this project, we have proposed an innovative and comprehensive sexual education digital platform for youths primarily women in Ethiopia.  Using user-friendly mobile application (termed “ Booru foyya’aa gochuuf” - which means prepare for a better tomorrow in local language  - is a youth-driven, holistic mobile app to increase sexual and reproductive health for Ethiopian youth 15-24 years old. The proposed system has comprised of several features such as; shorten and clear guidelines, question answering on vague instances, audio and video supported tutorials, and anonymous chatting services with counselors. The mobile application will be designed in user-friendly manner to increase the usability among youths. The proposed application enables youths to be aware of about unprotected sex, unwanted pregnancy, adverse pregnancy outcomes, use of sexual contraceptive methods, menstrual cycle related issues and others.A preliminary user requirement gathering, analysis and data modeling pipelines will be employed to develop the mobile application. One of the most frequently used software development design pattern approach such as; Model View Controller (MVC) will be used for the application development paradigm. All of the software application will be developed to support voice engagement and text display in the specified languages (Amharic and Afaan Oromo).\r\nAnalysis strategy:\r\nTo compare pre/post knowledge difference, we will use Mc-Nemar test for categorical variable and t-test for continues variables. All potential confounding variables will be entered in the final model to control confounding effect. P values less than 0.05 will be used as statistically significant.\r\nBudget and timeline: \r\nA total of 693,955.5 Ethiopian birr is required to undertake this project with a time period of January to October, 2022.','Digital health services for  youth`s in Jimma town','Digital Health services for youth`s to support sexual and reproductive health and rights (SRHR) in Jimma Town: Mobile –based app. A quasi-experimental study','2021-11-19 15:15:41','Research output/outcome\r\nOutput of our project (mobile app) is to meet all adolescent’s needs for modern contraception, pregnancy-related and newborn care information, and treatment for the major curable STIs. Moreover, the project outcome is to decrease the morbidity of unintended pregnancies and related complications, all of which take a tremendous toll on youth’s lives, physical and mental well-being, and productivity. Finally, our new technology based information dissemination will be scaled-up in large at country level after proofing its effect.','319304bfdef154ce6bdfa5908ac660df','completed','Introduction \r\nGlobal challenge on sexual and reproductive health 	\r\nSexual and reproductive health and rights have far-reaching implications for individuals, families and society. Health and well-being depend on people’s ability to plan pregnancies and births; obtain high-quality care before, during and after a pregnancy; and prevent and treat HIV and other STIs. People who experience gender-based violence, infertility and reproductive cancers also rely on sexual and reproductive health care. A lack of access to these essential services jeopardizes individuals’ health and lives and, in turn, negatively affects families and social and economic development more broadly (1).\r\nRecognizing the linkages between these fundamental aspects of individual health and broader development efforts, several global initiatives of the past decade have advanced parts of the sexual and reproductive health and rights agenda. For example, many of the 17 Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs)—especially Goals 3, 4 and 5, which aim for improved health, education and gender equality, respectively—depend on improvements in sexual and reproductive health and rights (2).\r\nSexual and reproductive health care encompasses a broad range of services that ensure people can decide whether and when to have children, experience safe pregnancy and delivery, have healthy newborns, and have a safe and satisfying sexual life. These services are important investments both because they enhance individual well-being and allow people to exercise their sexual and reproductive rights, and because they have far-reaching benefits for societies and for future generations (1).\r\n\r\nIn 2018, the Guttmacher-Lancet Commission on Sexual and Reproductive Health and Rights outlined an essential package of sexual and reproductive health interventions to which all people should have access (3). This proposed package includes the global initiatives, such as Family Planning 2020 (4) and the UN’s Global Strategy for Women’s, Children’s and Adolescents’ Health (5). New estimates for 2019 show that sexual and reproductive health services fall well short of needs in low and middle income countries (LMICs). Approximately 218 million women of reproductive age (15–49) in these countries have an unmet need for modern contraception—that is, they want to avoid a pregnancy but are not using a modern method. About half (49%) of pregnancies in LMICs—111 million annually—are unintended  (1).\r\nFurthermore, an estimated 133 million women of reproductive age in LMICs need but do not receive treatment for one of the four major curable STIs—chlamydia, gonorrhea, syphilis or trichomoniasis. If all women infected with chlamydia or gonorrhea were given effective and timely treatment, cases of pelvic inflammatory disease and infertility caused by these STIs would be eliminated (1).\r\nAdolescents, in particular, have substantial unmet needs for sexual and reproductive health care. For example, women aged 15–19 who want to avoid a pregnancy have much higher unmet need for modern contraception than do all women of reproductive age who want to avoid a pregnancy (43% vs. 24%). Adolescent women face many barriers to obtaining contraceptive care, including fear of exposing that they are sexually active (if they are unmarried) and social pressure to have a child (if they are married). Adolescents in LMICs have an estimated 21 million pregnancies each year, 50% of which are unintended (1,3).\r\nAdolescence, the period in which young people transition from childhood to adulthood, is a critical time to invest in sexual and reproductive health. In LMICs, many young women become sexually active, marry and start childbearing between the ages of 15 and 19, often without the support of policies and programs that would enable them to make informed and voluntary decisions to protect their health and exercise their sexual and reproductive rights (4,6).\r\nInternational commitments regarding adolescent sexual and reproductive health emphasize both the unique challenges that people face at this point in the life course, as well as the longer-term benefits of investing in effective services for adolescents (4,7).\r\nIn addition, the 2030 Agenda for Sustainable Development explicitly aims for a world in which the voices of the most vulnerable are heard and the needs of the most vulnerable are met (2). To achieve these targets and reach the most disadvantaged groups of adolescents for whom information is limited, and whose needs are often overlooked (8–10) . The SDGs also call for countries to attain universal health coverage, ensuring that quality services are accessible and affordable to everyone. The groups of adolescents for whom information is limited, and whose needs are often overlooked (4). Thus, contraceptive, pregnancy-related and newborn health information and services are among a wide range of interventions necessary to fully support adolescents’ health and rights (11).\r\nOnly an estimated 55 per cent of girls and women are able to make their own decisions about sex, contraception and health care, according to recent data (12), and too few adolescents have access to comprehensive sexuality education. \r\nThe timely provision of comprehensive information and life skills training in sexual and reproductive health (SRH) is essential for adolescents to lead healthy sexual lives, exercise their rights and avoid negative health outcomes. While sexuality education is just one component in a multifaceted approach to addressing and ultimately improving the sexual and reproductive lives of young people, it provides a unique opportunity for adolescents to gain knowledge and skills, explore their attitudes and values, and practice the decision-making and other life skills necessary for making healthy, informed choices about their sexual lives (13–17).\r\nAbstinence-only education programs have shown little evidence of improving sexual and reproductive health outcomes (6,18). By comparison, comprehensive sexuality education programs that recognize sexual activity during adolescence as normative behavior and that focus on human rights, gender equality and empowerment, have been shown to increase knowledge, self-confidence and self-esteem; positively change attitudes and gender and social norms; strengthen decision-making and communication skills and build self-efficacy; and increase the use of condoms and other contraceptive methods(6,16,19,20).  \r\nComprehensive sexuality education is a rights-based and gender-transformative approach, whether in school or outside of school. It is most effective when comprehensive sexuality education is taught over several years by integrating age-appropriate information that accounts for the developing capacities of young people. According to standards established by the United Nations and other international agencies, CSE should cover a range of topics in five key categories identified by the study team: sexual and reproductive physiology, prevention of HIV and other STIs, contraception and unintended pregnancy, values and interpersonal skills, and gender and sexual and reproductive rights (1).\r\nBut it also goes beyond information, helping young people to explore and nurture positive values regarding their sexual and reproductive health and rights. This education includes discussions about family life, relationships, culture and gender roles, and also addresses human rights, gender equality, bodily autonomy and threats such as discrimination, sexual abuse and violence (1).\r\nTaken together, these programmes help young people develop self-esteem and life skills that encourage critical thinking, clear communication, and responsible decision-making and respectful and empathetic behavior (1).\r\nAccording to the international technical guidance on sexuality education (16), comprehensive sexuality education must be:\r\n•	Scientifically accurate\r\n•	Incremental \r\n•	Age - and developmentally - appropriate\r\n•	Curriculum based \r\n•	Comprehensive \r\n•	Based on a human rights approach\r\n•	Based on gender equality \r\n•	Culturally relevant and context appropriate \r\n•	Transformative\r\n•	Able to help develop life skills needed to support healthy choices\r\n\r\n\r\nKey facts about comprehensive sexuality education (16).\r\n1.	Comprehensive sexuality education does not lead to earlier sexual activity or riskier sexual behavior. \r\n2.	In fact, these programmes reduce risky behaviors: About two thirds two of evaluations show reductions in targeted risky behaviors. About 60 per cent of programmes had a positive effect on at least one behavioral or biological outcome, such as increased condom use or reduced unplanned pregnancies.\r\n3.	Studies of abstinence-only programmes are either inconclusive or show abstinence-only education to be ineffective at improving health outcomes.\r\n4.	Delivering high-quality comprehensive sexuality education requires training and support.\r\n5.	Addressing gender and power issues also leads to better health outcomes.\r\n6.	To be most effective, curricula must be tailored to the specific context and needs of young people.\r\n7.	Engaging parents and communities as part of this education is critical. Sexuality education is most effective when school-based programmes are complemented by community-based initiatives.\r\nChallenge in Ethiopia \r\nIn Ethiopia, adolescent sexual and reproductive health and rights (SRHR) remains a pressing concern. Among adolescent women aged 15-19 in Ethiopia, 930,000 want to avoid a pregnancy: 600,000 (64%) whose need for modern methods is met and 330,000 (36%) who have an unmet need, which means  that about 1 in every 3 teen pregnancy is unwanted -  they would use modern contraception if they had access to it, but they don’t. Moreover, among women aged 15-19 in Ethiopia, 3,600,000 give birth each year and many do not receive needed care (1).\r\nTherefore, increasing awareness of the benefits of contraceptive services and building the capacity\r\nof adolescents to make their own decisions about using contraceptives is vital (21). Interventions with supporting evidence include combining educational programs in schools and communities with youth-friendly services, health center outreach activities and media campaigns (22). Activities that integrate improvements in service quality with efforts to generate community support for adolescent sexual and reproductive health are effective at increasing uptake of sexual and reproductive health services (23).\r\n\r\nThe timely provision of comprehensive information and life skills training in sexual and reproductive health (SRH) is essential for adolescents to lead healthy sexual lives, exercise their rights and avoid negative health outcomes. There is empirical evidence that Comprehensive Sexuality Education (CSE) plays a major role in improving SRHR outcomes. However, there is no evidence indicating the most effective channels for CSE delivery in Ethiopia. A landscape analysis indicated that there are four main CSE delivery channels; school-based, traditional mass media, peer-to-peer approaches, and digital media. In Ethiopia, the first three: school-based, traditional mass media, peer-to-peer approaches were implemented for decades, however, according to recent data indicated that still high burden of unmet needs (1).\r\nDigital tools can help fill these gaps(24). This project aimed to develop mobile application which will helps users to find age appropriate sexual and reproductive health information and services. The project will test the application meets the needs of young people, the extent to which this channel is an effective method to deliver Comprehensive reproductive health Education and assess level of informed decision by adolescents (24).\r\nDigital media are increasingly influencing the lives of young people. Around the world, adolescents and young people are using digital media as a platform to learn, experience, and communicate(24). Digital platforms present an important opportunity to better understand and address young people’s sexual and reproductive health(24). .In Ethiopia, young people use smartphones, tablets and computers to engage in diverse online activities, such as social networking, instant messaging/texting, and browsing websites/search engines. This connectivity creates positive opportunities to access sexual and reproductive health information.  \r\n \r\nRationale \r\nIf all women in LMICs wanting to avoid a pregnancy were to use modern contraceptives and all pregnant women and their newborns were to receive care at the standards recommended by the World Health Organization, the impacts would be dramatic: Unintended pregnancies would drop by 68%, unsafe abortions would drop by 72%, and maternal deaths would drop by 62%. In addition, if all women infected with chlamydia or gonorrhea were given effective and timely treatment, cases of pelvic inflammatory disease and infertility caused by these STIs would be eliminated (1).\r\nAround the world, young people don’t have access to accurate and age appropriate Family Planning and Reproductive Health [FP/RH] information. Since FP/RH education is rarely comprehensive, adolescents naturally turn to the internet and social media for information, much of which is inaccurate, prescriptive, or difficult to understand. Our mobile app puts engaging, evidence-based information into the palm of their hands which is stepping up to the challenge of getting appropriate information in friendly manner (25).\r\nFP/RH information and services are often only designed for married women(3). Even though Ethiopian law allows youth to get appropriate information on FP/RH care adolescents remains highly stigmatized. Cultural norms, provider bias, or a lack of information about a need for FP/RH care makes finding youth-friendly services difficult for unmarried young people. \r\nInvesting in sexual and reproductive health services for adolescents, who are at a critical point in their lives, has far-reaching effects (26,27). The 2016 Lancet Commission on Adolescent Health and Wellbeing recommended increasing investments in young people to yield a “triple dividend of benefits”: for adolescents now, for their future adult lives and for the next generation of children (11). Different study supported this recommendation; it examined the benefits and costs of progressively scaling up interventions related to the health, education and well-being of adolescents to high levels of coverage by 2030 (26).  It found that a package of 66 health interventions—including services related to contraception, safe abortion and post abortion care, and HIV prevention and treatment—would cost $4.50 per capita per year when averaged across 40 LMICs for the period 2015–2030. The value of the economic and social benefits resulting from this large-scale investment in adolescents was estimated to be substantial, ranging from six to 17 times the costs of the interventions.','Materials and methods\r\nStudy area, design and period \r\nSecondary school based quasi-experimental study design will be conducted among 200 adolescents aged 15-19 years from February to August, 2022 in Jimma town, Jimma zone. In Jimma town there are two preparatory school namely jimma high school and Jimma University community school. The two schools will be randomly assigned to intervention and control groups. Students included in both the intervention and control groups will be from grade 11 and 12.  For the intervention group newly developed android application will be installed to their mobile phone and two days training will be given on how to use it. For the control group they will continue their usual activity. \r\nInnovation \r\nIn this project, we have proposed an innovative and comprehensive sexual education digital platform for youths primarily women in Ethiopia.  Using user-friendly mobile application (termed “ Booru foyya’aa gochuuf” - which means prepare for a better tomorrow in local language  - is a youth-driven, holistic mobile app to increase sexual and reproductive health for Ethiopian youth 15-24 years old. The proposed system has comprised of several features such as; shorten and clear guidelines, question answering on vague instances, audio and video supported tutorials, and anonymous chatting services with counselors. The content of guidelines and frequently asked questions could be accessed offline to reduce youths challenge in weak internet connectivity areas. However, when youths want to chat and feel their physical presence with anonymous counselors; they need to connect using their cellular data or broadband internet. The mobile application will be designed in user-friendly manner to increase the usability among youths. The proposed application enables youths to be aware of about unprotected sex, unwanted pregnancy, adverse pregnancy outcomes, use of sexual contraceptive methods, menstrual cycle related issues and others. Moreover, the proposed system could also possibly reduce adverse pregnancy outcomes and abortions followed by lack of proper knowledge about sexual education among youths.  \r\nA preliminary user requirement gathering, analysis and data modeling pipelines will be employed to develop the mobile application. One of the most frequently used software development design pattern approach such as; Model View Controller (MVC) will be used for the application development paradigm. In addition, for the software development we will use state of the art MobileFirst web application development frameworks and tools such as flutter mobile application toolkit, Django web application development framework. The mobile application will be developed using flutter mobile application development toolkit. Flutter is a cross platform mobile application development environment. Same logic, same UI can be developed once and can be compiled and deployed for android and IOS devices. In addition, it has good flexibility on UI development; it supports different plugins (dependency packages) for adding interactive features; and will help us to design user friendly mobile application. For website application development we will develop both front end and back-end applications. \r\nFor front end web application development, we will use state of the art tools such as HTML5, CSS, JavaScript, jQuery, AJAX and bootstrap. And for back end we will use Django web development frame work. Django is a python-based web development framework. These can help us to integrate image processing and deep learning classifiers to our web application. \r\nAll of the software application will be developed to support voice engagement and text display in the specified languages (Amharic and Afaan Oromo). In addition, we will include audio-video data and chatting modules to enable youths easily catches tutorials and ask questions whenever they are in need of it. All the content development and key messages are aligned with our culture and in line with existing national priorities to improve reproductive health.  \r\nSample size calculation and study population \r\nThis quasi–experimental study aimed at applying mobile application based sexual and reproductive health education as intervention, and used improvement in health seeking behavior among students as a major outcome variable. In addition, to get an adequate sample size for both the intervention and control groups, two population proportion sample size calculation formula, with 5 % type I error and 80 % power was used. Accordingly, n1 has represented students selected for the intervention group and n2 represented students selected for the control group. Moreover, for the prevalence of condom use among intervention group during post intervention period (P1), 0.53 was taken from a similar study conducted in Adiss Abeba, Ethiopia (28). For the prevalence of condom use among the comparison or control group during post intervention (P2), 0.31 was taken from the same study (28).Thus, r or ratio of n2, n1 = 1 and it gives N= 152. Prevalence of condom use was given the larger sample size from different comprehensive reproductive use components. Finally, 10 % contingency was added for possible refusal to participate in the study and also to maximize the sample size as much as possible for better validity. As a result, the calculated sample size was 168 and 84 students from one secondary school for the intervention group and another 84 students for the control group from other purposively selected secondary school.\r\nIn Jimma town we have two secondary school namely jimma high school and jimma university community school. Lottery method will be used to select intervention and control school. After selecting school, all eligible students aged 15-24 having smart phone will be selected by simple random after developing study frame from student roster. \r\n\r\nIntervention group: user-friendly mobile app will be installed to their mobile and one day training will be given on how to use the app.\r\nControl group: nothing will be given\r\nData collection and quality control\r\nData will be collected from the intervention and control group students using self-administered and similar questionnaire during both pre and post intervention periods. The questionnaire have several sections with various Socio-demographic and reproductive related behaviors. The questionnaire will be initially prepared in English language and translated to Amharic and afaan oromo version then back to English to maintain its consistencies in meanings and senses, by two individuals with expert knowledge in both Amharic/Afaan oromo and English languages. The questionnaire will be pre- tested in one secondary schools in adjustment district. Then, the required corrections in language and content will be done for better clarity and more understandability. The pre and post intervention data collections will be facilitated by six BSc holder nurses under the supervisions of two senior health professionals (a health officer with long work experience)\r\nAnalysis strategy \r\nThe collected data from both the intervention and control group students during pre and post intervention periods will be entered first into Epi-Info software version 3.5.4 for cleaning. and then exported to STATA software version 16.0 for analysis. Normality of data will be analyzed by Kolmogorov-Smirnov test. Normally distributed data will be presented as mean ± SD, and data for variables that will not comply with a normal distribution will be presented as median (interquartile range (IQR), 25; 75 percentile). Dependent variables will be comprehensive knowledge of reproductive health. To compare pre/post knowledge difference we will use Mc-Nemar test for categorical variable and t-test for continues variables. All potential confounding variables will be entered in the final model to control confounding effect. P values less than 0.05 will be used as statistically significant.','<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">Reference </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">1. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Elizabeth A. Sully, Ann Biddlecom, Jacqueline E. Darroch, Taylor Riley, Lori S. Ashford, Naomi Lince-Deroche, Lauren Firestein RM. Adding It Up: Investing in Sexual and Reproductive Health 2019, New York: Guttmacher Inst. 2020; </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">2. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; United Nations. Transforming Our World: The 2030 Agenda for Sustainable Development. A New Era Glob Heal. 2018; </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">3. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Starrs AM, Ezeh AC, Barker G, Basu A, Bertrand JT, Blum R, et al. Accelerate progress&mdash;sexual and reproductive health and rights for all: report of the Guttmacher&ndash;Lancet Commission. Lancet. 2018;391(10140):2642&ndash;92. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">4. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; FP2020. WOMEN AT THE CENTER Annual Report [Internet]. 2021. Available from: https://familyplanning2020.org/arc-of-progress</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">5. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Child Every Woman Every. THE GLOBAL STRATEGY FOR WOMEN&rsquo;S, CHILDREN&rsquo;S AND ADOLESCENTS&rsquo; HEALTH (2016-2030) [Internet]. 2016. Available from: www.everywomaneverychild.org</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">6. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Underhill K, Montgomery P, Operario D. Sexual abstinence only programmes to prevent HIV infection in high income countries: Systematic review. Br Med J. 2007;335(7613):248&ndash;52. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">7. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; UNFPA. How our future depends on a girl at this decisive age. State World Popul. 2016; </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">8. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Woog V, K&aring;gesten A. The Sexual and Reproductive Health Needs of Very Young Adolescents In Developing Countries. Guttmarcher Inst [Internet]. 2017;(May):10&ndash;1. Available from: https://www.guttmacher.org/fact-sheet/srh-needs-very-young-adolescents-in-developing-countries</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">9. &