ot;Calibri&quot;,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"><span style=\"color:black\">Provide user training and deliver user manual&nbsp; </span></span></span></span></li>\r\n			</ul>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:87px; vertical-align:top; width:54px\">\r\n			<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"><span style=\"color:black\">30</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:87px; vertical-align:top; width:96px\">\r\n			<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"><span style=\"color:black\">724</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:87px; vertical-align:top; width:96px\">\r\n			<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"><span style=\"color:black\">65,160.00</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n		</tr>\r\n		<tr>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:1px solid black; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:87px; vertical-align:top; width:36px\">\r\n			<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"><span style=\"color:black\">9</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:87px; vertical-align:top; width:125px\">\r\n			<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"><span style=\"color:black\">Field data collection </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:87px; vertical-align:top; width:102px\">\r\n			<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"><span style=\"color:black\">10</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:87px; vertical-align:top; width:222px\">\r\n			<ul>\r\n				<li><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"><span style=\"color:black\">Data collection</span></span></span></span></li>\r\n			</ul>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:87px; vertical-align:top; width:54px\">\r\n			<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"><span style=\"color:black\">30</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:87px; vertical-align:top; width:96px\">\r\n			<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"><span style=\"color:black\">305</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:87px; vertical-align:top; width:96px\">\r\n			<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"><span style=\"color:black\">91,500</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n		</tr>\r\n		<tr>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:1px solid black; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:24px; vertical-align:top; width:36px\">\r\n			<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,sans-serif\">&nbsp;</span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td colspan=\"5\" style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:24px; vertical-align:top; width:599px\">\r\n			<p>&nbsp;</p>\r\n\r\n			<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,sans-serif\"><strong><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"><span style=\"color:black\">Sub total </span></span></strong></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:24px; vertical-align:top; width:96px\">\r\n			<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"><span style=\"color:black\">612,780</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n		</tr>\r\n		<tr>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:1px solid black; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:13px; vertical-align:top; width:36px\">\r\n			<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,sans-serif\">&nbsp;</span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td colspan=\"5\" style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:13px; vertical-align:top; width:599px\">\r\n			<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,sans-serif\"><strong><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"><span style=\"color:black\">Other direct costs </span></span></strong></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:13px; vertical-align:top; width:96px\">\r\n			<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"><span style=\"color:black\">48,130.00</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n		</tr>\r\n		<tr>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:1px solid black; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:24px; vertical-align:top; width:36px\">\r\n			<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,sans-serif\">&nbsp;</span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td colspan=\"5\" style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:24px; vertical-align:top; width:599px\">\r\n			<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,sans-serif\"><strong><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"><span style=\"color:black\">Total </span></span></strong></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:24px; vertical-align:top; width:96px\">\r\n			<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"><span style=\"color:black\">660, 910</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n		</tr>\r\n		<tr>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:1px solid black; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:24px; vertical-align:top; width:36px\">\r\n			<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,sans-serif\">&nbsp;</span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td colspan=\"5\" style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:24px; vertical-align:top; width:599px\">\r\n			<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,sans-serif\"><strong><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"><span style=\"color:black\">Contingency (5%)</span></span></strong></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:24px; vertical-align:top; width:96px\">\r\n			<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"><span style=\"color:black\">33,045.5</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n		</tr>\r\n		<tr>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:1px solid black; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:24px; vertical-align:top; width:36px\">\r\n			<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,sans-serif\">&nbsp;</span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td colspan=\"5\" style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:24px; vertical-align:top; width:599px\">\r\n			<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,sans-serif\"><strong><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"><span style=\"color:black\">General Total</span></span></strong></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:24px; vertical-align:top; width:96px\">\r\n			<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,sans-serif\"><strong><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"><span style=\"color:black\">693,955.5</span></span></strong></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n		</tr>\r\n	</tbody>\r\n</table>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\">&nbsp;</p>',1,NULL,NULL,NULL),(69,33,9,20,'Background: More than one fourth of the global population is aged between 10 and 24, with more than eighty five percent (86%) living in developing countries. These young people are tomorrow’s parents. The health decision they make today have impact on health and wellbeing of their communities and of their countries for a decade to come. In Ethiopia, regardless of socio-economic status or educational background, open discussion about sexual and reproductive health (SRH) remains a cultural taboo, specifically, among young people and unmarried adolescents.\r\nObjectives: Understanding health needs, Social and Gender influences on Youth reproductive health and their intention to utilize reproductive health services in Jimma zone, Ethiopia.  \r\nMethods: A mixed method approach will be used to address the objectives. Eight hundred fourth four (844) adolescents and youths will be included in the study using a simple random sampling technique. Indeed, twenty-eight focus groups and twenty-two in-depth interviews will be recruited purposively. The quantitative data will be exported to STATA version 16.0 and ATLAS version 7.5 soft wares will be used analyses of survey and qualitative, respectively. The primary outcomes will be summarized and advanced statistical modelling such multi-level mixed-effects logistic regression modelling will be computed whenever appropriate. Inductive approach of thematic analysis will be performed for the qualitative data. \r\nBudget: The total proposed budget for this project is 388,485 ETB',NULL,'Understanding health needs, Social and Gender influences on Youth reproductive health and their intention to utilize reproductive health services in Jimma zone, Ethiopia 2021','2021-11-19 15:21:19','The expected results from this study improves the community attitude regarding the adolescent and youth reproductive health service. Also, it solves non-medical health problem of young people which deep rooted in the society by assessing social and gender norms that influence the adolescent and youth health service and increases the utilization of reproductive health services by addressing the determinants that affects intention of adolescent and youth to utilize sexual and reproductive health services.','efa728e356cd545612e6584c475e9a13','completed','There is overlaps of adolescent and youth. Adolescents are individuals of age group range 10-19 years while youth are in the age groups 15-24 years. Even though the definition classification is mostly universal, different continents or nations adopt different age ranges for youth based on changes in demographic, economic, and socio-cultural circumstances (1).  \r\n There are physical, social roles, relationships, expectations, emotional and intellectual changes observed during adolescence which  provide the foundation for functioning as an adult (2). Worldwide, the basic sexual and reproductive health needs of adolescents and youth can be compromised due to different challenges especially in low and middle income countries where the violation of basic human rights commonly seen. It can also be due to lack of knowledge and awareness about puberty, sexuality,  access, culture and  norms which can have significant implications on young people’s health and welfare as well economic development and poverty reduction (3). \r\nResearch in 2019 indicates that nearly 12 million girls aged 15-19 years and nearly 800,000 girls under 15 years give birth each year in developing regions. Early pregnancy increases the likelihood of contracting an STI, dropping out of school, experiencing mental health issues and developing life-threatening childbirth-related complications which is the leading cause of death for 15-19 years old girls globally (4) \r\nIn Ethiopian, teen age pregnancy was highly prevalent which accounts for 13-28% among adolescent and youth.  Evidence showed that female youth were 5 times more likely to be vulnerable to sexually transmitted infections than males in Ethiopia(5). Findings also indicated that the utilization of reproductive health services in the existing health care system by young people was very low which results in unwanted pregnancies, abortions and  other complications (5).\r\nDespite improved gender laws and policies, informal gender biases dominate most levels of the government, preventing women’s access to needed services. Social norms such as expectations that women and girls are responsible for doing domestic chores and nursing sick family members can expose women and girls to greater health risks (6). There is undeniable evidence showing that the gender roles and norms we adopt as we develop from childhood to adulthood and the unequal access to power and resources between men and women, strongly impact on our health. Naturally , men and women have different health needs, but livelihood  and socially credited roles arising from prevailing social and cultural patterns also contribute  a part in the health picture (7) \r\nThe time during adolescent and youth period is crucial, in which society begin to take on gender roles in all features of their lives and accordance with societal expectations. Therefore adolescents and youth exposure to risk behaviors and protective factors for SRH depends somewhat on the cultural, religious and gender norms in context of their society. (8).  \r\nThe growing attention to the health of adolescents and youth in Ethiopia is, for the following reasons, first, this group covers a significant proportion (33.8%) of the country’s population. Second, the adolescent and youth population are implications for social, political, and economic development. Third, healthy adolescents and youth are a key asset and resource, with great potential to support their families, communities and the nation both at present and in the future as actors in social change, not simply beneficiaries of social programs (9).\r\nEven though, efforts made to improve access to youth reproductive health services, the utilization of the service is among adolescent and youth population particularly, females are highly affected by reproductive health problems and still it is one of the focus areas for the future sustainable development goal which, contains a number of targets specially, provision of reproductive health ,including family planning, information and education, and the integration of reproductive health into national strategies and programs enshrined in target 3.7  (11)\r\nExploring health needs adolescent and youth and to investigate social and gender influence is vital as dealing with the public dimensions of adolescent and youth problem. Prior studies demonstrated the predictors of  low service utilizations of sexual reproductive health such as parental factors such as strong socio-cultural such as, taboo and culture barrier that families  do not discuss about sex to their children  (12,13). However, these studies are limited in scope (e.g., didn’t address the influence of multiple relationships between individual, social, resources, and institutional). Secondly, limited evidence on priority health needs of adolescent and youth and their intention to utilize reproductive health service. \r\nTherefore, in order for adolescent and youth health programs to remain focused, and to make policy impact on adolescent and youth health, aggregate data must be available at local and national levels to prioritize the health need services by decision makers in terms of intervention.','4.	Method \r\n4.1 Setting: The study will be implemented in seven districts of Jimma zone. The Zone has a population of 2.4 million residing in an area of 18,413 sq. km making the population density about 130.7 persons/km2 (10). There are seven hospitals, out of two are general and the others are primary hospitals, 121 Health centers (HCs), and 513 health posts (HPs).  According to 2007 national census, the total population and households of the zone were 3,543,625 and 753,924 respectively, of whom 799783 (22.1%) are reproductive age group and adolescent youth population group accounts 1,248,498 (34.5 %). \r\n4.2 Study design: \r\nFor objective 1 and 2: A descriptive qualitative study will be conducted.\r\nFor objective 3: A sequential exploratory mixed-method study will be conducted among adolescent and youth to measure the intention toward reproductive health service utilization. Accordingly, qualitative study will be conducted with the primary target audiences (adolescent and youth) to explore the silent beliefs related to reproductive health service. Then, quantitative study will be conducted to identify predictors of   adolescent and youths’ intention to utilization reproductive health service. \r\nFor objective 4: A community based cross-sectional study will be conducted\r\n4.3 population:\r\nFor objective 3 and 4: All adolescents and youth aged 15 to 24 years in selected districts of Jimma zone are source population. The Study population are randomly selected adolescent and youth in the selected districts of Jimma Zone who fulfill the inclusion criteria. All adolescents and youth who reside in the selected districts for at least 12 months before the study period will be included. Those who are critically ill during the study period, known psychological problems and unable to communicated will be excluded.\r\nFor objective 1 and 2:  Adolescents and youth, parents, health extension workers and health care providers working on Adolescents and youth reproductive health clinics and private pharmacies, teachers and religious leaders.\r\n4.3.1.	Sample size determination \r\nFor objective 3 and 4:  The sample size is calculated using single population proportion considering the following assumptions: 50% proportion of adolescents and youth intention to utilize reproductive health, 5% marginal error, and 95% confidence level, design effect 2 and 10% non-response rate. After putting the numbers in place the final sample size become 844. \r\nFor objective 1 and 2: A total of 50 participants will be involved in the qualitative study. Specifically, 28 focused group discussions/FGDs (10 with adolescent and youth female, 10 with adolescent and youth male, 4 with fathers and 4 with mothers); and 22 key informant interviews/KIIs (8 with health extension workers, 5 with health care providers working at public health institutions, 3 with health care providers working at private health institutions, 4 with teachers and 2 with religious leaders).\r\n4.3.2. Sampling technique: A multistage sampling technique is used to select seven districts among the total of twenty-one districts of Jimma zone by lottery method. The districts will be further stratified by rural & urban kebeles to ensure representativeness of the study population. One urban district and six rural district is selected. The sample size for each selected district and kebele will be determined by proportional. A list of households (HHs) in the selected kebeles will be obtained from the community health information system which is available at health post. To select the households, simple random sampling technique will be used. \r\n4.5 Data collection \r\nFourteen (14) data collectors will collect the data, who know local language and adolescents’ and youths’ contexts. The quantitative data are collected through interviewer administered tool for the first objective of this study. Qualitative inquiry assistants will be recruited based on their previous experience in the qualitative study.  Qualitative data collectors will be guided by interview guides developed by project teams. Voice recording and field note taking will be well focused. Open-ended semi-structured and probe questions will be used to initiate the interview and discussions. \r\n4.6 Data management and analysis a Plan: The quantitative data will be exported to STATA 16.0 (Stata Corporation, College Station, Texas, USA) and the primary outcomes will be summarized and advanced statistical modelling such multi-level mixed-effects logistic regression modelling will be computed whenever appropriate. Thematic analysis will be used for the qualitative data, using ATLAS version 7.5 software\r\n4.7. Outcome Variable: Intention to utilization of reproductive health service (objective 3) and utilization of reproductive health service (objective 4)                            \r\n4.8. Explanatory variables: individual, social, institutional and resource level variables \r\nIndividual level factors:  Attitude, Belief, self-efficacy developing body, aspiration, intention behavior, skill, and age  \r\nSocial level factors: social and peer network, family configuration, social capital and support, positive deviant\r\nInstitution level factors: policies and laws, educational system, government structure, economic policies, religion institution, service and infrastructure.\r\nResource level factors: services, infrastructure and livelihood','<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"><span style=\"color:black\">1. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Chandra-Mouli V, Bloem P, Ferguson J. The World Health Organization&rsquo;s work on adolescent sexual and reproductive health. Bundesgesundheitsblatt - Gesundheitsforsch - Gesundheitsschutz. 2013;56(2):256&ndash;61. </span></span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"><span style=\"color:black\">2. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; J.W.Kinaro et al. Socio-Cultural Barriers Influencing Utilization of Sexual and Reproductive Health (SRH) Information and Services among Adolescents and Youth 10 - 24 Years in Pastoral Communities in Kenya. Adv Sex Med. 2019;09(01):1&ndash;16. </span></span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"><span style=\"color:black\">3. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Seema Jayachandran. Social norms as a barrier to women&rsquo;s employment in developing countries. IMF Econ Rev https//doi.org/101057/s41308-021-00140-w [Internet]. 2020;(0123456789):No. 27449. Available from: http://www.nber.org/papers/w27449</span></span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"><span style=\"color:black\">4. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Jones N, Presler-marshall E, Baird S, Hicks J, Chuta N, Gezahegne K. Adolescent health , nutrition , and sexual and reproductive health in Ethiopia. 2019;(May). </span></span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"><span style=\"color:black\">5. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Central Statistical Agency Addis Ababa E. UNICEF Ethiopia. Ethiopia Demographic and Health Survey. Addis Ababa, Ethiopia, and Rockville, Maryland, USA: CSA and ICF.. Addis Ababa, Ethiopia, and Rockville, Maryland, USA: CSA and ICF. 2016. 249 p. </span></span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"><span style=\"color:black\">6. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Asia-Pacific Gender in Humanitarian Action Working Group. The COVID-19 Outbreak and Gender : Vol. 27, Gender in Humanitarian Action. 2019. </span></span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"><span style=\"color:black\">7. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Dr Ann Weber. Gender norms and health: insights from global survey data. Lancet. 2019;393(10189):2455&ndash;68. </span></span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"><span style=\"color:black\">8. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; D. Azuh et al. Socio-Cultural Factors of Gender Roles in Women&rsquo;s Healthcare Utilization in Southwest Nigeria. Open J Soc Sci. 2015;03(04):105&ndash;17. </span></span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"><span style=\"color:black\">9. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Health M. NATIONAL ADOLESCENT SEXUAL AND REPRODUCTIVE. 2015; </span></span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"><span style=\"color:black\">10. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Mary Manandhar et at. Gender, health and the 2030 Agenda for sustainable development. Bull World Health Organ. 2018;96(9). </span></span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"><span style=\"color:black\">11. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; UNICEF. Strategy for Health. Strateg Heal 2016-2030. 2016;1&ndash;56. </span></span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"><span style=\"color:black\">12. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Ayehu et al. Young people&rsquo;s parental discussion about sexual and reproductive health issues and its associated factors in Awabel woreda, Northwest Ethiopia. Reprod Health [Internet]. 2016;13(1):1&ndash;8. Available from: http://dx.doi.org/10.1186/s12978-016-0143-y</span></span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"><span style=\"color:black\">13. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Khanal SP. Multi-level Barriers for Utilization of Youth Friendly Reproductive Health Services (YFRHS) among Youths. J Heal Promot. 2020;8(June):95&ndash;108. </span></span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"><span style=\"color:black\">14. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; J. Pulerwitz et al. Proposing a Conceptual Framework to Address Social Norms That In fl uence Adolescent Sexual and Reproductive Health. J Adolesc Heal [Internet]. 2019;64(4):S7&ndash;9. Available from: https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jadohealth.2019.01.014</span></span></span></p>','Understanding health needs, social and gender influences on youth reproductive health and their intention to utilize reproductive health services in Jimma zone, Ethiopia, 2021',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,2,'Adolescent, Youth, health needs, Social and Gender influences, Jimma',10,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),(70,176,9,20,'Background: Social determinants of maternal and child health is a condition in which pregnant mothers live, get education and conduct their daily routine activities. Evidence indicates that high disparities were observed in maternal and child health care between and within countries that attributed to the social determinants of health. For instance, recent estimates of United Nations Mortality showed that Ethiopia is one among the six countries accounted for half of the estimated global number of stillbirths and of global live births. Although maternal and child health were largely affected by the triple role of being woman: reproductive, productive, and the community role, evidence in this area that is how social determinants of maternal and child health affect maternal and child health outcomes were neither well examined, nor well understood in Jimma rural community. \r\nObjective: Understanding the influences, and significance of social determinants of health on maternal and child health outcomes in Jimma Zone Southwest Ethiopia \r\nMethods: An explanatory mixed-method design will be conducted among 422 randomly selected mothers having who have at least one pregnancy history in the last 2 years in four selected districts of Jimma Zone. A structed interviewer administered questionnaire will be used for quantitative data followed by the qualitative data. Two in-depth interviews per each religious and community leader per district give a total of 16 in-depth KII to be conducted. In addition, two FGDs will be conducted per Kebele (two men’s group and two women’s group) totaling 8 FGDs, each containing 8 to 10 participants. Primary outcomes of the project are maternal and child health outcomes such as perinatal morbidity (preterm birth) and mortality (still birth, neonatal death), pregnancy outcomes such as obstetric danger signs during pregnancy, delivery and post-partum. The main explanatory variables will be the social determinants of health such as social gradient, working condition and social exclusion. For quantitative data open data kit (ODK) will be used and analyzed using STATA version 16.0. Advanced statistical methods such as Structural equation modelling (SEM) and Multivariable logistic regression will be used to estimate the effect and causal pathways. Qualitative data will be summarized by using ATLAS ti version 7.5. for explanatory analysis.\r\nTimelines: This project will be conducted from December, 2021-to June, 2022\r\nBudget: Overall 556,229.10 ETB (five hundred fifty-six two hundred twenty-nine birr and ten cents) will be required to accomplish the project in 8 months.','The influences of social determinants of maternal and child health in Jimma Zone, Ethiopia','Understanding the influences and significance of social determinants of health on maternal and child health outcomes in Jimma Zone Southwest Ethiopia','2021-11-19 16:38:05','This project aims to improve maternal and child health outcomes through understanding the influences and significance of social determinants of health in Jimma Zone Southwest Ethiopia','6f216193e6c5cfae27affb74a1973427','completed','Back ground: \r\nThe structural determinants and conditions of daily life constitute the social determinants of health and are responsible for a major part of health inequities between and within countries (1).  Research shows that clinical care is only one factor that impacts population health and that a collection of other factors including the natural and built environment where people live, education economic stability, food, and community and social context grouped under the term social determinants of health (SDOH), have significantly more influence on care utilization, outcomes, and population health. Together, these factors account for 60% of preventable mortality which can be addressed through the life-course approach to maternal health care services (2). \r\nPolicy and program attention are shifting towards addressing the social determinants of maternal, newborn, and child health (MNCH) for the achievement of sustainable development goal 3 target of reducing maternal mortality ratio to 70 per 100,000 live births by 2030, the global agenda. Developing regions account for approximately 99% (302,000) of the global maternal deaths and 98% (2.64 million) of the global neonatal deaths in 2015. Sub-Saharan Africa alone accounted for roughly 66% (201,000) of global maternal deaths. Eleven up to sixty seven percent of sub-Saharan Africa’s newborn deaths could be prevented with high coverage of care. A well-functioning maternal and child health care services should take into account the client and provider satisfaction and maximize efficiency in resource limited settings (3, 4).\r\nProblem statement\r\nDespite the progress in the Maternal and Child Health care services utilization, for instance, the proportion of Antenatal Care (ANC) increased only from 27 to 62%, delivery care from 6 to 26% and PNC from 5 to 17%, maternal and child health inequities still a major challenge in Ethiopia. Difference in utilization of services is a common phenomenon whereby mothers from the poor, rural, and less educated class are less likely to utilize the health care services. Only 17% of women and 13% of newborns received a postnatal check within the first 2 days of birth (7, 8). \r\nUnderstanding the community’s social determinants and behaviors of the vital services for maternal, newborn and child health is therefore imperative not only towards reducing inequity in health but also towards designing and implementing better strategies so as to improve the general public health perspective. Identification of communities which are greatly contributing to overall disparity in health and a well laid out follow-up mechanism from pregnancy through child’s immunization program could serve towards improving maternal and infant health outcomes and equity (9).\r\nCurrently Ethiopia is witnessing high home delivery/low institutional delivery rates, an alarmin maternal mortality rate (even if there is a remarkable decline from the previous figures in the last two decades), and high neonatal mortality and low MCH service utilization. Prior studies demonstrated the determinants of  low service utilizations of specific maternal health care such as ANC (10-12). However, first, these studies are limited in scope (e.g., didn’t take the social determinants of health in to account). Secondly, evidence on the social determinants of maternal, newborn and child care study were lacking. Thus, it is essential to characterize and identify the social determinant of maternal health care in designing tailored interventions. Increased emphasis has to be given to the need for standards of care, and the mechanisms which address the barriers to provision and use of quality care; understanding the life course of a women is critical to improving health services and to improve quality of care, which in turn could improve the utilization of the services by the community. \r\nRationale of the study \r\nProgress on coverage of lifesaving interventions for maternal and child health care is uneven. Skilled care during childbirth, including emergency care for mothers and newborns, is critical: about 2 million lives a year are lost to complications occurring during labor and delivery (13). Evidence has shown that poor quality of facility-based care for these women and newborns is one of the major contributing factors for their elevated rates of morbidity and mortality. In addition, women who perceive the quality of facility-based care to be poor may choose to avoid facility-based deliveries, where life-saving interventions could be available. In this context, understanding the underlying factors that impact the quality of facility-based services and assessing the significant determinants to improve the quality of care represent critical inputs for the improvement of maternal and newborn health. This study greatly contributes in strengthening linkages for social support between women, men, families and communities and with the health care delivery system. Improving quality of care, health services and health provider interactions with women, men, families and communities. This study also intends to advocate for increased funding for reproductive and maternal, newborn and child health, maximize financial and technical support for large-scale implementation of priority strategies and interventions and encourage accountability of key actors to keep their promises. Therefore, this study will be carried out to improve the utilization of maternal, new born and child health care service (MNCHCS) through cogent policy and program recommendation of effective interventions will be designed from the findings of the study. Furthermore, the result of the study will be very important for policy makers, health planners, executive bodies and front-line implementers in the area.','Setting: The study will be conducted in four districts of Jimma zone. \r\nSource population: Mothers who gave birth in the last two years in the selected districts of Jimma Zone\r\nStudy population: Randomly selected mothers who have pregnancy history in the last two years in the selected districts of Jimma Zone fulfilling inclusion criteria during the study period.\r\nInclusion/exclusion criteria: mothers who were 2 months and above after giving live or still birth and reside at least 6 months in the study area will be included. However, those who have intellectual disability and unable communicate will be excluded.\r\nSample size determination: Epi Info version 7.5 was used to identify minimum sample size by using assumptions of 95% confidence level, d=0.05% (margin of error), p=0.5 (proportion of mothers and children having favorable health outcomes), and 10% non-response rate. Finally, the sample size will be 422 mothers linked with them under five children.\r\nStudy design and sampling. A sequential explanatory mixed-method study design will be conducted among women and their families. This approach will be used to explore social determinants of maternal health from various levels and perspectives and how these influence maternal health and child outcomes. \r\n1.	A first quantitative study will use the quantitative study that links mothers and under five children. A woman having under five children will be interviewed in the two selected districts in Jimma Zone. This will help examine how maternal and child health affected by social determinants and identify determining factors in various maternal health contexts, collecting information about socio-demographic and socio-economic characteristics, obstetric characteristics, sexual and reproductive health outcomes.\r\n2.	A second qualitative study will use in-depth key informant interviews (KII) to explore individual, household, and community data on social determinants and perceptions of age at puberty, fertility and marital dynamics, pregnancy, health and injuries, mental health, sexual and reproductive health, and their life-style behaviours toward maternal health service utilization, pregnancy and birth outcomes, sociocultural and intergenerational impacts of reproductive health outcomes. Health care providers, religious leaders and community leaders will be purposively selected for interviews. Two in-depth interviews per each religious and community leader per district give a total of 16 in-depth KII to be conducted.\r\n3.	A third qualitative study will conduct focus group discussions (FGDs) to uncover people’s perceptions and behaviours and unpack influences on their material and social circumstances, life aspirations, social trajectories, age at pregnancy, fertility and marital dynamics, health and injuries, mental health, socio-cultural and impacts of reproductive health outcomes, sexual and reproductive health, food security and nutrition, and their lifestyle behaviours toward maternal health service utilization, early childhood development, and pregnancy and birth outcomes. Two FGDs will be conducted per Kebele (consists of around 500 households) (two men’s group and two women’s group) totalling 8 FGDs, each containing 8 to 10 participants.\r\nIn general Objectives number 1 will be a pure qualitative and done using FGDs and KIIs; whereas the rest objectives number 2, 3, and 4 will be answered by both quantitative and qualitative methods.\r\nData Management Plan: A structured questionnaire prepared in local language will be used. All data collection forms will be checked for completeness and consistency by the research team and collected data will be exported to STATA 16.0 (Stata Corporation, College Station, Texas, USA). The primary outcomes will be summarized and advanced statistical modelling such as path analysis and multi-level mixed-effects logistic regression modelling will be computed whenever appropriate. Qualitative data will be analyzed thematically using ATLAS ti version 7.5 software.\r\nData quality assurance: Data collection will also follow standard procedures; training will be given for interviewers on data collection procedure, ethical procedure and interview techniques; quality control checks will be employed using lots quality assurance sampling techniques. The research team will closely supervise the data collection process. For qualitative study, responsible supervisors will keep records of the interviews process on daily basis. \r\nPrimary outcomes: maternal and child health outcomes such as perinatal morbidity (preterm birth) and mortality (still birth, neonatal death), pregnancy outcomes such as obstetric danger signs during pregnancy, delivery and post-partum\r\nExplanatory variables: \r\nSocial determinants: individual and household-level factors: Individual (i.e., physiological, psychological/stress, health behaviors) and household-level (i.e., social support) indicators, and healthcare access within the context of the eco-social theory.\r\nSocial determinants: community-level factors: Housing stability, neighborhood and environmental safety, and food accessibility impact health outcomes.\r\nSocial determinants: cultural and societal-level factors: Cultural, societal, and political forces influence discriminatory practices, economic instability, and poor educational and occupational opportunities lead to health inequity.\r\nVariable Measurement \r\nSocial determinants of health: Religion and three factors will be selected purposively from the WHO lists of social determinants of health namely, social gradient, work condition and social exclusion: \r\nSocial gradient will be generated from the combination of education and household wealth. It will be categorized under three different ladder as high, middle, and lower ladders of gradient.\r\nWork condition will be generated from the variable measuring occupation and categorised into three groups by the physical stress associated with the type of work. Less physically stressful work will be defined as work entailing professional service or clerical work, while physically stressful work consists of agricultural, manual and other unskilled task.\r\nSocial exclusion will measure the proportions of respondents prevented from full participation in household decisions. This will be proxied by the level of women’s autonomy based on women’s participation in decision about own health, visit to friends/relatives and purchase of household large items. finally grouped as low, moderate, and higher.','<ol>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">CSDH (2008). Closing the gap in a generation: health equity through action on the social determinants of health. Final Report of the Commission on Social Determinants of Health. Geneva, World Health Organization.</span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">Daniel Young. </span></span><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">The Network for Public health law: Addressing Social Determinants of Maternal and Child Health through Medicaid Managed Care: accessed from </span></span><a href=\"https://www.networkforphl.org/the_network_blog/2019/04/25/1134/\" style=\"color:blue; text-decoration:underline\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">https://www.networkforphl.org/the_network_blog/2019/04/25/1134/</span></span></a><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"> on 28/08/2019</span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">Kerber KJ, Graft-johnson JE De, Bhutta ZA, Okong P, Starrs A, Lawn JE. Continuum of care for maternal , newborn , and child health : from slogan to service delivery Lancet. 2007 Oct 13;370(9595):1358-69. 2007;370.</span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">De Graft-Johnson J, Kerber K, Tinker A, Otchere S, Narayanan I, Shoo R. The continuum of care &ndash; reaching mothers and babies at the crucial time and place. Oppor Africa&rsquo;s Newborns. 2006;23&ndash;36. </span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">Maternal and Perinatal Morbidity and Mortality Review Committee. Maternal deaths in the Maldives: 2009&ndash;2011. The Maldives Government; 2011.</span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">Adam T, Lim SS, Mehta S, Bhutta ZA, Fogstad H, Mathai M, Zupan J, Darmstadt GL: Cost effectiveness analysis of strategies for maternal and neonatal health in developing countries. BMJ 2005, 331(7525):1107.</span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">&nbsp;CSA [Ethiopia] and ORC Macro Ethiopia Demographic and Health Survey 2000. Addis Ababa, Ethiopia and Calverton, Maryland, USA Central statistical Agency and ICF International; 2001. </span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">Central Statistical Agency (CSA) [Ethiopia] and ICF. 2016. Ethiopia Demographic and Health Survey 2016. Addis Ababa, Ethiopia, and Rockville, Maryland, USA. CSA and ICF. </span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">Gebre E, Worku A, Bukola F. Inequities in maternal health services utilization in Ethiopia 2000 &ndash; 2016 : magnitude , trends , and determinants. 2018;1&ndash;9. </span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">Gurmessa Tura. Antenatal care service utilization and associated factors in metekel zone , northwest Ethiopia. Ethiop J Health Sci. Vol.19, No. 2 July 2009 </span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">Tsegay Y, Gebrehiwot T, Goicolea I, Edin K, Lemma H, Sebastian MS. Determinants of antenatal and delivery care utilization in Tigray region, Ethiopia: a cross-sectional study. Int J Equity Health 2013;12:30. Available from: </span></span><a href=\"http://www.pubmedcentral.nih.gov/articlerender.fcgi?artid=3658893&amp;tool=pmcentrez&amp;\" style=\"color:blue; text-decoration:underline\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">http://www.pubmedcentral.nih.gov/articlerender.fcgi?artid=3658893&amp;tool=pmcentrez&amp;</span></span></a><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"> endertype=abstract</span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">Bahilu T, Tewodros B, Mariam AG, Dibaba Y. Factors affecting antenatal care utilization in yem special woreda, southwestern Ethiopia. Ethiop J Heal Sci Vol 2o, No 2 July 2010</span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-fami