on, Ethiopia. The Pan African Medical Journal. 2016;25(Suppl 2). </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:24px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">20. Alehegn BG, Mulunesh TT, Yilkal TA, Abebaw AG. Sexual and reproductive health services utilization and associated factors among preparatory school students in Mecha District, Northwest Ethiopia: cross sectional study. J Gynecol Women&rsquo;s Health. 2018;11(5): </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:24px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">21. Gebreyesus H, Teweldemedhin M, Mamo A. Determinants of reproductive health services utilization among rural female adolescents in Asgede-Tsimbla district Northern Ethiopia: a community based cross-sectional study. Reproductive health. 2019 Dec;16(1):1-0. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:24px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">22. Manganello JA. Health literacy and adolescents: a framework and agenda for future research. Health education research. 2008 Oct 1;23(5):840-7. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">23. Ghanbari S, Ramezankhani A, Montazeri A, Mehrabi Y. Health literacy measure for adolescents (HELMA): development and psychometric properties. PloS one. 2016 Feb 16;11(2):e0149202</span></span></p>','Young reproductive health problems, health care seeking behavior, risky sexual behaviors, service utilization and associated factors in Jimma zone, Ethiopia: Mixed study design',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,2,'health seeeking behaviour  , Reproductive health problem , reproductive health service utilization',10,'<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><strong><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">Table 3</span></span></strong><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">: Budget break down of the project </span></span><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">young reproductive health problems,</span></span><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"> health care seeking behavior</span></span><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">, risky sexual behaviors, service utilization and associated factors in Jimma zone, Ethiopia</span></span><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"> Southwest.</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<table cellspacing=\"0\" class=\"MsoTableGrid\" style=\"border-collapse:collapse; border:none; margin-left:1px; width:618px\">\r\n	<tbody>\r\n		<tr>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:1px solid black; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:1px solid black; height:20px; vertical-align:top; width:315px\">\r\n			<ol>\r\n				<li><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">Data collectors training cost</span></span></span></span></li>\r\n			</ol>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:1px solid black; height:20px; vertical-align:top; width:303px\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">15,000.00</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n		</tr>\r\n		<tr>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:1px solid black; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:16px; vertical-align:top; width:315px\">\r\n			<ol start=\"2\">\r\n				<li><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">Pilot test </span></span></span></span></li>\r\n			</ol>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:16px; vertical-align:top; width:303px\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">12,000.00</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n		</tr>\r\n		<tr>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:1px solid black; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:9px; vertical-align:top; width:315px\">\r\n			<ol start=\"3\">\r\n				<li><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">Budget for stationery materials</span></span></span></span></li>\r\n			</ol>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:9px; vertical-align:top; width:303px\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">5,000.00</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n		</tr>\r\n		<tr>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:1px solid black; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:11px; vertical-align:top; width:315px\">\r\n			<ol start=\"4\">\r\n				<li><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">Data collection cost</span></span></span></span></li>\r\n			</ol>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:11px; vertical-align:top; width:303px\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">150,000.00</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n		</tr>\r\n		<tr>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:1px solid black; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:6px; vertical-align:top; width:315px\">\r\n			<ol start=\"5\">\r\n				<li><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">Transportation cost</span></span></span></span></li>\r\n			</ol>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:6px; vertical-align:top; width:303px\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">20,000.00</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n		</tr>\r\n		<tr>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:1px solid black; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:9px; vertical-align:top; width:315px\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Subtotal</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:9px; vertical-align:top; width:303px\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">20500.00</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n		</tr>\r\n		<tr>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:1px solid black; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:9px; vertical-align:top; width:315px\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Contingency cost (5%)</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:9px; vertical-align:top; width:303px\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">10250.00</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n		</tr>\r\n		<tr>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:1px solid black; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:13px; vertical-align:top; width:315px\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Grand Total cost in ETB</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:13px; vertical-align:top; width:303px\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">215,250.00</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n		</tr>\r\n	</tbody>\r\n</table>',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),(72,171,9,18,'Background: Cardiovascular disease (CVD) is the leading cause of death in the world, with 17.5 million fatalities each year. Surrogate markers for predicting atherosclerosis, cognitive impairment, and CVD risk have been proposed using carotid intima–media thickness (CIMT) and carotid plaques evaluated by carotid ultrasound. CIMT is being investigated as a surrogate marker for diagnosing subclinical atherosclerosis in order to assess risk and track the progression of atherosclerosis in order to guide medical intervention. However, the importance carotid ultrasound examination of CA to see carotid lesions in Ethiopia has not been studied so far. It is particularly importance in resource constrained settings, like our setting, where invasive diagnostic modalities have become a luxury. \r\nObjective: the aim of this study is to evaluate the magnitude of carotid atherosclerosis (CA) detected by carotid duplex ultrasonography and related risk factors among adults visiting Jimma university medical center.\r\nMethods: From January to May 2022, a hospital-based cross-sectional study will be undertaken among individuals aged 18 and above who visit Jimma University Medical Center. The study\'s 422 participants will be chosen using a systematic random sampling technique. Data will be collected by face-to-face interview using structured questionnaire, blood pressure, anthropometric measurements, Untrasonography, echocardiography and electrocardiographic will be measured by trained operators. Descriptive statistics employed and multivariable logistic regression analysis will be used. To declare statistical significance, get an adjusted odds ratio with a respective 95 percent confidence interval and a p-value of less than 0.05.\r\nTime line and Budget: The study will be conducted within one year period with a total budget of 652,300 ETB',NULL,'CAROTID ATHEROSCLEROSIS DETECTED BY ULTRASONOGRAPHY AS A SURROGATE MARKER OF ATHEROSCLEROSIS AMONG ADULTS VISITING JIMMA UNIVESITY MEDICAL CENTER, SOUTHWEST ETHIOPIA','2021-11-19 15:42:48','In middle-aged and older populations, early risk-reduction efforts to prevent the progression of carotid artery disease should be explored. This study will be of particular importance for early prevention of CVD as well as intervention through screening of CIMT. It has been well attested in other settings.\r\nIn addition, short term and low cost training of available health professionals will impact the management of patients in resource limited setting.','910b620710062f3000d9bf926827b097','completed','Cardiovascular disease (CVD) is the number one cause of death worldwide, accounting for 17.5 million deaths per year(Nangia, Singh et al. 2016). In 2012, the World Health Organization (WHO) estimated that one third of all deaths globally were attributable to CVD, and 7.4 million of those deaths were from ischemic heart disease. Coronary heart disease mortality is decreasing in many developed countries, but is increasing in developing and transitional countries, partly as a result of increasing longevity, urbanization, and lifestyle changes(Sharathkumar, Soucie et al. 2011).\r\nAtherosclerosis is a systemic vascular disease that raises the risk of life-threatening cardiovascular illnesses (CVDs), such as coronary artery disease and ischemic stroke (Faxon, Creager et al. 2004). Carotid atherosclerosis (CA) has been linked to the occurrence of new ischemic stroke in asymptomatic patients with other risk factors such as smoking, low physical activity, hypertension, diabetes mellitus, or hyperlipidemia, according to studies (Wang, Wang et al. 2016). These modifiable risk factors are responsible for more than 90% of the global stroke burden, and addressing them could cut the global stroke burden by a third (Feigin, Roth et al. 2016). CA is also linked to cognitive impairment, which has an impact on daily activities. However, because the atherosclerotic process can begin at a young age and develop silently over decades, asymptomatic atherosclerosis is frequently overlooked. Early detection and treatment of atherosclerosis may help to prevent cognitive impairment as well as reduce CVD morbidity and death (Wang, Li et al. 2018).\r\nChronic kidney disease (CKD) is an independent predictor of poor cardiovascular outcomes, and patients with CKD have accelerated atherosclerosis and a higher risk of myocardialinfarction, despite the fact that cardiovascular disease is still the leading cause of death (Subbiah, Chhabra et al. 2016). \r\nCoronary artery disease (CAD) is still the leading cause of death in Western countries, with atherosclerosis serving as the primary pathophysiology for both CAD and cerebrovascular illness. Until recently, the only way to detect luminal stenosis was to use invasive methods. The degree of luminal stenosis in the carotid arteries, as determined by noninvasive imaging such as ultrasonography, computed tomography angiography (CTA), or magnetic resonance. In CAD, angiography is now employed for preoperative assessment rather than traditional angiography (Jashari, Ibrahimi et al. 2013, Khajavi, Khademi et al.). \r\nThis is due to the fact that carotid arteries are positioned more superficially than coronary arteries. Carotid evaluation in CAD and vice versa has become clinically important in order to precisely identify patients who could benefit from aggressive preventive therapy as well as prompt treatment due to the shared underlying atherosclerotic pathology in the arterial systems. Because of the very accurate imaging techniques that have recently been developed, invasive imaging of the carotid arteries has become less appealing for routine imaging and surveillance, and is now reserved for interventional therapy. The quantity and composition of carotid plaque (CP), notably the lipid necrotic core, are linked to the severity of CAD stenosis (Bytyçi, Shenouda et al. 2021).  \r\nDiabetes mellitus is associated with a high risk of cardiovascular disease (CVD) which is the most common cause of mortality in people with diabetes. CVD accounts for more than 80% of deaths in people with diabetes. A two- to fourfold increased risk of CVD in people with diabetes compared with the background population has been reported by various research groups. People with diabetes have a 150 percent to 400 percent greater risk of stroke. People taking diabetic drugs were three times more likely to develop cerebrovascular illness in the Multiple Risk Factor Intervention Trial (MRFIT) than those who did not take diabetes medications.\r\nDiabetes is linked to an increase in the occurrence and severity of peripheral vascular disease. As a result, not only does atherosclerosis develop at an earlier age in diabetics, but it is also more widespread and severe than in those without diabetes. Peripheral vascular disease is two to four times more likely in those with diabetes. The examination of carotid intima-media thickness (CIMT) and endothelial function of the brachial artery flow-mediated dilatation by ultrasonography has been employed as a surrogate marker of CVD in persons with diabetes (Katakami, Kaneto et al. 2014).\r\nCarotid intima–media thickness (CIMT) and carotid plaques, both of which may be evaluated by carotid ultrasound, have been proposed as surrogate markers for predicting cognitive impairment and CVD risk. CIMT is being investigated as a surrogate marker for diagnosing subclinical atherosclerosis, risk assessment, and monitoring atherosclerosis progression for medical intervention (Gardener, Caunca et al. 2017). Several population studies have used carotid ultrasound to examine CA; nevertheless, the epidemiology of carotid lesions in Ethiopia has yet to be investigated. Furthermore, doppler Ultrasound is available around the clock and with short duration of training of health care professionals we can tackle the giant CVD and its consequences. It is particularly importance in resource constrained settings, like our setting, where invasive diagnostic modalities have become a luxury.Therefore, the aim of this study is to determine the extent of carotid atherosclerosis (CA) discovered by carotid duplex ultrasonography, as well as related risk factors, among people who visit Jimma University Medical Center.','Study area and period\r\nThe study will be conducted among adults aged ≥18 who will visit Jimma university medical center over a period of one year from Jan 2022 to Feb 2023.\r\nPopulation\r\nSource population: All adults aged ≥ 18 years visiting JUMC for medical services \r\nStudy population: adults aged ≥18 years visiting JUMC for medical services during the study period and fulfill the eligibility criteria. Adults with established or symptomatic cardiac disease and unstable due to severe illness will be excluded from the study.\r\nStudy Design\r\nHospital based cross sectional study \r\nSample size determination\r\nThe following assumptions are used to compute sample size using the single population proportion formula: prevalence of carotid atherosclerosis (50%), 95% confidence interval, margin of error (5%), and non-response rate (10%).By using the following formula\r\n                 n= (Zα/2)2 P (1-P)\r\n                                    d2\r\nAs a result, 422 people will be included in this study.\r\nSampling procedure\r\nThe current study will be conducted among adults ≥ 18 years visiting outpatient medical department in JUMC. The average number of adults visiting JUMC medical department for medical service annually will be obtained from health management information system unit to estimate total number of adult population (≥ 18 years) coming for medical service Systematic random sampling technique will be used to selected study participants. Sampling interval (Kth interval) will be calculated by dividing total adults ≥ 18 years to sample size. The first participant will be selected by lottery method and sampling will be continued every Kth interval until the required sample size reached.\r\nData collection procedures\r\nData will be collected using interviewer administered structured questionnaire developed after reviewing different literatures and anthropometric measurements and laboratory investigation (Ultrasonographic, electrocardiographic and echocardiographic assessment) will be done. The questionnaire willinitiallyprepared in English language and it will be translated to AfaanOromoo and Amharic languages and back translated to English to check consistency. The components of the questionnaire include socio-demographic variables (age,sex, marital status, educational status, religion, residence, income, height, weight and blood pressure) lifestyle related variables (smoking, physical activity, alcohol consumption, work stress, and coffee and salt consumption), co-morbidity related variables (personal and family history of stroke, diabetes mellitus, hypertension and kidney disease) and clinical related variables (current drug use, ).Data will be collected by trained and experienced health professionals and supervised by principal investigator. \r\nHeight of the participants will be measured to the nearest 0.5cm  using stadiometer and weight will be measure to the nearest 0.1kg participants  wearing light clothe and barefoot. Blood pressure readings (by sphygmomanometer) will be measured twice and the average of the two readings will be considered.\r\nLaboratory tools and measurements\r\nElectrocardiographic (ECG)\r\nTwelve lead ECG, which is noninvasive procedure, will be done by a trained personnel after explaining the procedure to the participant and taking consent from the participant. Tools required for the procedure are 12-lead ECG machine, ECG electrodes, ECG paper, gel and tissue paper or gauze. The ECG machine calibration will be checked by the operator and information of the patient will be filled in. Then, the participant is required to remove clothes above the waist and from extremities. Jewelries and watches may need to be removed. The participant will be requested to relax and lay down supine on examining bed with legs uncrossed and arms lying freely by the side of the body. Leads will be placed in their respective places and the ECG display screen will be observed for at least ten seconds for a clear and good tracing to make sure there are no artifacts. The image will be printed out on ECG paper and will be interpreted by cardiologist or Internist.\r\nCarotid artery Ultrasonography for measurement of Carotid Intima-Media Thickness (CIMT)\r\nCarotid intima media thickness measurement will be done by cardiologist or (trained Internist) after procedure explained to the participant and consent obtained from participant. It is noninvasive study with no radiation risk and complication. The materials will be used are ultrasound machine, high frequency linear transducer, ultrasound gel and examination bed. The participant will be asked to lie down supine on examining bed with slight neck extension and rotated at 45 degree away from the side being examined, and asked to remove any jewelry that may get on the way of examination. Then common carotid artery intima- media thickness will be measured by B-mode ultrasound using high frequency linear transducer.  The measurement will be taken at far wall of the common carotid artery at different angles and average measurement will be taken.The mean value >75th percentile for specific age, sex and side will be used. Values above 1mm will be considered abnormal.\r\nEchocardiography\r\nTrans thoracic Echocardiography is a noninvasive and safe study with no radiation risk and it will be done by cardiologist after taking consent from the participant. Before the procedure the necessary materials such as echo machine, gel, examination table and tissue paper will be prepared. Then, the participant will be asked to remove clothes from upper body and lie down on left lateral position on the examining bed. The information of the participant will also entered and measurements will be taken in a standard way and results printed out in a tabular form.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nStudy variables\r\nDependent variable is Carotid atherosclerosis while independent variables include socio-demographic variables (age,sex, marital status, educational status, religion, residence, income, height, weight and blood pressure) lifestyle related variables (smoking, physical activity, alcohol consumption, work stress, and coffee and salt consumption), co-morbidity related variables (personal and family history of stroke, diabetes mellitus, hypertension and kidney disease) and clinical related variables (current drug use, ).\r\nData quality management\r\nTraining will be given for data collectors on study objectives, sampling procedures and how to interview and measure anthropometric parameters, BP, ultrasonography, electrocardiography and echocardiography. Pre-test will be done on five percent of the sample size in JUMC which will not be included in final analysis. The instruments for measuring BP, weight and height will be calibrated against the standard before measuring each participant. Untrasonographic, electrocardiographic and echocardiographic assessment will be done following the standardized steps and carried out by trained operator. The data collection process will be supervised by principal investigator on daily basis. \r\nData processing and analysis\r\nThe data will be double-checked for accuracy, coded, cleaned, and entered into Epidata software 3.1 before being exported to SPSS version 25 for further analysis. For continuous variables, descriptive statistics such as mean, median, and standard deviation will be used, whereas for categorical variables, frequency tables and percentages will be used. At a p-value of less than 0.25, bivariable analysis will be used to select candidate variables for multivariable analysis. To find the independent predictors of the outcome variable, a multivariable logistic regression analysis will be used. To declare statistical significance, the adjusted odds ratio (AOR) with respective 95 percent CI and P-value 0.05 will be employed. Text, table, and graph will be used to present the findings.\r\nDissemination of the findings\r\nThe findings from present study will be presented and submitted to Jimma University research office. The findings will also communicate with JUMC and other concerned bodies. The findings will be considered for publication in peer-reviewed journals.','<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Bonithon-Kopp, C., P. J. Touboul, C. Berr, C. Magne and P. Ducimetière (1996). &quot;Factors of carotid arterial enlargement in a population aged 59 to 71 years: the EVA study.&quot; <u>Stroke</u><strong>27</strong>(4): 654-660.</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Byty&ccedil;i, I., R. Shenouda, P. Wester and M. Y. Henein (2021). &quot;Carotid atherosclerosis in predicting coronary artery disease: a systematic review and meta-analysis.&quot; <u>Arteriosclerosis, Thrombosis, and Vascular Biology</u><strong>41</strong>(4): e224-e237.</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Faxon, D. P., M. A. Creager, S. C. Smith Jr, R. C. Pasternak, J. W. Olin, M. A. Bettmann, M. H. Criqui, R. V. Milani, J. Loscalzo and J. A. Kaufman (2004). &quot;Atherosclerotic Vascular Disease Conference: Executive summary: Atherosclerotic Vascular Disease Conference proceeding for healthcare professionals from a special writing group of the American Heart Association.&quot; <u>Circulation</u><strong>109</strong>(21): 2595-2604.</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Feigin, V. L., G. A. Roth, M. Naghavi, P. Parmar, R. Krishnamurthi, S. Chugh, G. A. Mensah, B. Norrving, I. Shiue and M. Ng (2016). &quot;Global burden of stroke and risk factors in 188 countries, during 1990&ndash;2013: a systematic analysis for the Global Burden of Disease Study 2013.&quot; <u>The Lancet Neurology</u><strong>15</strong>(9): 913-924.</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Gardener, H., M. R. Caunca, C. Dong, Y. K. Cheung, M. S. Elkind, R. L. Sacco, T. Rundek and C. B. Wright (2017). &quot;Ultrasound markers of carotid atherosclerosis and cognition: the Northern Manhattan Study.&quot; <u>Stroke</u><strong>48</strong>(7): 1855-1861.</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Grant, P. J. and F. Cosentino (2019). &quot;The 2019 ESC Guidelines on diabetes, pre-diabetes, and cardiovascular diseases developed in collaboration with the EASD: new features and the &lsquo;Ten Commandments&rsquo; of the 2019 Guidelines are discussed by Professor Peter J. Grant and Professor Francesco Cosentino, the Task Force chairmen.&quot; <u>European heart journal</u><strong>40</strong>(39): 3215-3217.</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Jashari, F., P. Ibrahimi, R. Nicoll, G. Bajraktari, P. Wester and M. Y. Henein (2013). &quot;Coronary and carotid atherosclerosis: similarities and differences.&quot; <u>Atherosclerosis</u><strong>227</strong>(2): 193-200.</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Johri, A. M., D. W. Chitty, M. Matangi, P. Malik, P. Mousavi, A. Day, M. Gravett and C. Simpson (2013). &quot;Can carotid bulb plaque assessment rule out significant coronary artery disease? A comparison of plaque quantification by two-and three-dimensional ultrasound.&quot; <u>Journal of the American Society of Echocardiography</u><strong>26</strong>(1): 86-95.</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Kanber, B. (2014). <u>Identifying the vulnerable carotid plaque by means of dynamic ultrasound image analysis</u>, University of Leicester.</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Karpenko, A., S. Bugurov, P. Ignatenko, V. Starodubtsev, I. Popova, K. Malinowski and P. Musialek (2021). &quot;Randomized controlled trial of conventional versus MicroNet-covered stent in carotid artery revascularization.&quot; <u>Cardiovascular Interventions</u><strong>14</strong>(21): 2377-2387.</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Katakami, N., H. Kaneto and I. Shimomura (2014). &quot;Carotid ultrasonography: a potent tool for better clinical practice in diagnosis of atherosclerosis in diabetic patients.&quot; <u>Journal of diabetes investigation</u><strong>5</strong>(1): 3-13.</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Khajavi, L., G. Khademi, M. Mehramiz, A. Norouzy and M. Safarian (2018). &quot;Association of dysglycemia with mortality in children receiving parenteral nutrition in pediatric intensive care unit.&quot; <u>Turkish Journal of Pediatrics</u><strong>60</strong>(2): 134-141.</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Krejza, J., M. Arkuszewski, S. E. Kasner, J. Weigele, A. Ustymowicz, R. W. Hurst, B. L. Cucchiara and S. R. Messe (2006). &quot;Carotid artery diameter in men and women and the relation to body and neck size.&quot; <u>Stroke</u><strong>37</strong>(4): 1103-1105.</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Larsson, A. C. and S. Rosfors (2021). &quot;Diameter‐based measurements of the degree of carotid artery stenosis using ultrasonography.&quot; <u>Clinical Physiology and Functional Imaging</u><strong>41</strong>(2): 217-220.</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Li, H., X. Xu, B. Luo and Y. Zhang (2021). &quot;The Predictive Value of Carotid Ultrasonography With Cardiovascular Risk Factors&mdash;A &ldquo;SPIDER&rdquo; Promoting Atherosclerosis.&quot; <u>Frontiers in Cardiovascular Medicine</u>: 907.</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Lorenz, M. W., J. F. Polak, M. Kavousi, E. B. Mathiesen, H. V&ouml;lzke, T.-P. Tuomainen, D. Sander, M. Plichart, A. L. Catapano and C. M. Robertson (2012). &quot;Carotid intima-media thickness progression to predict cardiovascular events in the general population (the PROG-IMT collaborative project): a meta-analysis of individual participant data.&quot; <u>The Lancet</u><strong>379</strong>(9831): 2053-2062.</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Mantella, L. E., K. N. Colledanchise, M.-F. Hetu, S. B. Feinstein, J. Abunassar and A. M. Johri (2019). &quot;Carotid intraplaque neovascularization predicts coronary artery disease and cardiovascular events.&quot; <u>European Heart Journal-Cardiovascular Imaging</u><strong>20</strong>(11): 1239-1247.</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Nangia, R., H. Singh and K. Kaur (2016). &quot;Prevalence of cardiovascular disease (CVD) risk factors.&quot; <u>medical journal armed forces india</u><strong>72</strong>(4): 315-319.</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Naqvi, T. Z. and M.-S. Lee (2014). &quot;Carotid intima-media thickness and plaque in cardiovascular risk assessment.&quot; <u>JACC: Cardiovascular Imaging</u><strong>7</strong>(10): 1025-1038.</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">N&auml;slund, U., A. Lundgren, D. Vanoli and M. Norberg (2019). &quot;Is intima-media thickness a predictor for cardiovascular risk?&ndash;Authors&#39; reply.&quot; <u>The Lancet</u><strong>394</strong>(10196): 381.</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">&Oslash;ygarden, H. (2017). Carotid intima‐media thickness and prediction of cardiovascular disease, Am Heart Assoc.</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Paraskevas, K. I., H. H. Sillesen, T. Rundek, E. B. Mathiesen and J. D. Spence (2020). Carotid Intima&ndash;Media Thickness Versus Carotid Plaque Burden for Predicting Cardiovascular Risk, SAGE Publications Sage CA: Los Angeles, CA.</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Piepoli, M. F. (2017). 2016 European Guidelines on cardiovascular disease prevention in clinical practice, Springer.</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Rodriguez-Macias, K. A., L. Lind and T. Naessen (2006). &quot;Thicker carotid intima layer and thinner media layer in subjects with cardiovascular diseases: An investigation using noninvasive high-frequency ultrasound.&quot; <u>Atherosclerosis</u><strong>189</strong>(2): 393-400.</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calib