pler U/S cost&hellip;&hellip;&hellip;...360,000</span></span></span></span></span></li>\r\n</ol>\r\n\r\n<ol start=\"5\" style=\"list-style-type:lower-roman\">\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\"><span style=\"color:black\">Stationary materials &hellip;&hellip;&hellip;&hellip;&hellip;&hellip;&hellip;&hellip;.10,000</span></span></span></span></span></li>\r\n</ol>\r\n\r\n<ol start=\"5\" style=\"list-style-type:lower-roman\">\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\"><span style=\"color:black\">Transportation cost &hellip;&hellip;&hellip;&hellip;&hellip;&hellip;&hellip;&hellip;..11,000</span></span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\" value=\"5\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\"><span style=\"color:black\">Total &hellip;&hellip;&hellip;&hellip;&hellip;&hellip;&hellip;&hellip;&hellip;&hellip;&hellip;&hellip;&hellip;...593,000</span></span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\" value=\"5\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\"><span style=\"color:black\">10% contingency &hellip;&hellip;&hellip;&hellip;&hellip;&hellip;&hellip;&hellip;&hellip;.59,300</span></span></span></span></span></li>\r\n</ol>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><strong><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\"><span style=\"color:black\">Total budget summary&hellip;&hellip;&hellip;&hellip;&hellip;&hellip;&hellip;&hellip;652,300</span></span></span></strong></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\">&nbsp;</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO `submission` VALUES (73,156,9,19,'Summary of the Proposal \r\nBackground: Worldwide approximately 43.3 million individuals suffer from blindness and about 295 million persons have a significant visual impairment in 2020 with about 90% occurring in developing countries. Bacteria are the most common cause of ocular infections globally and contribute to 50%-70% of external ocular infections in Ethiopia. Despite the fact that antimicrobial resistance is increasing rapidly in the country, there is a paucity of data related to antibiotic susceptibility patterns and treatment outcomes of external ocular infections.\r\nAim: This study is aimed at evaluating a bacterial profile, antibiotic resistance pattern, and treatment outcomes of patients with external ocular infections at JMC Ophthalmology Clinic, Ethiopia \r\nMethods: Facility-based prospective longitudinal study will be conducted at Jimma Medical Center (JMC) Ophthalmology Clinic from January 2022 to December 2022. Patients with signs and symptoms of external ocular infections who will visit the Ophthalmology Clinic during the study period will be included irrespective of age and gender. All specimens will be collected by an ophthalmologist and microbiological tests will be performed following the standard procedures and laboratory guidelines. Data on socio-demographic, medical history, and clinical data will be collected by trained ophthalmic nurses and residents from the patient medical records and by interviewing patients or caregivers. The data on clinical outcomes will be collected by following patients on daily basis during their stay at the Ophthalmology clinic and weekly for 90-days post-enrollment. The data will be entered into Epidata version 4.2. Then the data will be exported to STATA version 15 for statistical analysis. Bivariate and multivariate logistic regression analyses will be conducted to identify factors associated with poor clinical outcomes among patients with EOIs.\r\nResults and Discussions: The findings of this study will be summarized and discussed using scientifically sound justifications.\r\nBudget: The total budget required for the present study is 500,000 ETB',NULL,'Bacterial Etiology, Antibiotic Resistance Pattern and Treatment Outcome of External Ocular Infections among Patients Attending Ophthalmology Clinic of Jimma Medical Center, Jimma, Ethiopia','2021-11-19 16:15:07','Study outcomes and measurement tool \r\nThe outcomes of the present study are the proportion of bacterial pathogens, antibiotic resistance patterns, and clinical outcomes of EOIs. Identifications of bacterial pathogens and their antibiotic susceptibility pattern will be performed according to the standard operation protocols (SOPs) and following the Clinical and Laboratory Standards Institute (CLSI) guideline. The clinical outcome of the two most common presentations of EOIs conjunctivitis and keratitis will be assessed based on evidence-based recommendations(23,24). The patients will be followed from admission to 90-days to capture the full events such as cure/recovery, complications, reinfections, visual acuity based on the specific disease presentation. Detailed clinical outcome assessment is provided under Annex-I','1dac34f0b65fb9f4b06fce6313a02948','completed','Worldwide approximately 43.3 million individuals suffer from blindness and about 295 million persons have a significant visual impairment in 2020(1). Globally, the disability-adjusted life years (DALYs) due to blindness and vision loss were increased from 12.4 million during 1990 to 22.6 million in 2019(2). Blindness and visual impairment are associated with huge financial impact globally with the estimated potential productivity loss of 410.7$ billion annually(3). Sub-Saharan Africa (SSA) disproportionately carries a high number of blindness and vision impairment(4). Ethiopia is one of the countries with the world\'s highest prevalence of blindness (1.6%) and visual impairment (3.7%) with more than 80% is avoidable cases (either preventable or treatable)(5,6). According to World Health Organization (WHO) report, effective prevention or treatment has not been given for more than one-third of people with visual impairment(7). \r\nBacteria are the most common cause of ocular infections globally(8) and contribute to 50%-70% of external ocular infections in Ethiopia(9). Common presentations of external ocular infections are conjunctivitis, keratitis, blepharitis, dacryocytitis and orbital or periorbital cystitis(9). Unless appropriately managed, these ocular infections could cause vision impairment and blindness(10,11). Previous studies in Ethiopia reported that 46 %-67% of patients with EOIs had a positive culture for one or more bacteria(12–15). Accordingly, staphylococcus ssp. and streptococcus pneumonia (s. pneumonia) are frequently isolated gram-positive bacteria while E. coli, K. Pneumonia, and S. aeruginosa, are the most common gram-negative bacteria associated with EOIs(9,12–15)\r\nIn Ethiopia, most of the bacterial isolates from the different clinical samples are resistant to the most frequently used antibiotics(16).  Particularly, Methicillin-Resistant Staphylococcus aureus (MRSA) is extremely resistant to common antibiotics used in Ethiopian healthcare including ophthalmology(12,16). From the ophthalmology perspective, Staphylococcus aureus is the leading cause of ocular infections in Ethiopia and other settings(12)(13)(14). Furthermore, the steadily increasing prevalence of antibiotic-resistant P. aeruginosa and extended beta-lactamases producing Enterobacteriaceae (E.coli and K. pneumonia) poses a substantial challenge on ophthalmologists who are responsible to prescribe empiric antibiotics in resource-limited setting such as Ethiopia(17)(18). Moreover, these resistant bacteria compromise patient care and negatively affect visual outcomes(19). Hence, studies that characterize antibiotic susceptibility patterns in ocular infections with particular focus on MDR organisms are urgently needed in order to tailor empiric antibiotic treatment for ocular infections and where possible, to amend local treatment guidelines accordingly(20–22)\r\nAlthough there were studies on antimicrobial susceptibility pattern in ocular infections in different parts of Ethiopia including Jimma(12–15) the trend of antibiotic resistance vary from setting to setting and from time to time. Understanding this rapid emergence and dynamic nature of antimicrobial resistance, WHO and Ethiopian Food and Drug Authorities (EFDA) recommend continuous surveillance of antimicrobial resistance(20–22). Despite the aforementioned studies describing the common bacterial etiology of EOIs, they did not address the increasing burden of MDR particularly with staphylococcus ssp. and S. aeruginosa. Furthermore, as far as our knowledge concerned, none of these studies describe the common antibiotics regimens used for treatment of EOIs and they did not evaluate treatment outcome of patients with EOIs comprehensively. Hence, this study is aimed to assess bacterial profile, antimicrobial resistance, and treatment outcome of patients with EOIs attending Jimma Medical Center (JMC) ophthalmology clinic, Ethiopia.','Study design and area \r\nThe facility-based prospective longitudinal study will be conducted at Jimma Medical Center (JMC) Ophthalmology Clinic from January 2022 to December 2022. JMC ophthalmology clinic is the only referral and tertiary care eye center that provide services for about 15 million population catchment in southwestern Ethiopia. The clinic is run under the Ophthalmology department that has Ophthalmologists, ophthalmology residents, and optometrists. The ophthalmology clinic has inpatient wards that provide inpatient services, operation rooms, outpatient departments,s and ophthalmology pharmacies. The clinic uses the hospital microbiology lab which provides microbiological tests including culture, gram-stains, and a wide range of chemical tests.\r\nStudy population\r\nAll patients who will visit Ophthalmology Clinic for ocular infections during the study period will be considered as a source population. Patients with signs and symptoms of external ocular infections who will visit the Ophthalmology Clinic during the study period will be included irrespective of age and gender. Patients who received systemic or topical antibiotics 2 weeks before visit and patients/caregivers who refuse to participate in the study will be excluded from this study.\r\nSample size and sampling technique\r\nWe have calculated the sample size using a single population proportion formula n = z2 p (1-p)/ d2; where: n = the number of study participants; Z = Standard normal distribution = 1.96 at 95% confidence interval (CI), P = the prevalence of culture-confirmed bacterial EOIs among ophthalmic patients at Jimma that was 46.1%(13); d = margin of error taken as 5%. The sample size calculated based on the above formula was 382 and adding 15% for non-response rate the final sample size was 439. All eligible study participants will be enrolled consecutively until the final sample size is reached. \r\nData collection \r\nSocio-demographic and clinical data\r\nThe data will be collected using structured questionnaire developed after reviewing previous literature. The data collection tool contains socio-demographic part, clinic data, microbiological findings, and outcome parts.  Data on socio-demographic, medical history and clinical data will be collected by trained ophthalmic nurses and ophthalmology residents from patient medical record and by interviewing patients or caregivers. The data on clinical outcomes will be collected by following patients on daily basis during their stay at Ophthalmology clinic and weekly for one month after discharge. Based on their specific infections patients will be followed for 90-days post enrollment according to follow-up schedule decided by treating Ophthalmologist. Patients unable to attend the follow-up will be contacted using phone call. All microbiological data will be collected following the standard procedures and laboratory guideline as described in detail below.\r\nSpecimen Collection and handling \r\nAll patients with EOIs clinically suspected for bacterial infections will be requested for specimen collection and the sample will be collected by ophthalmologist from the external part of the eye like eyelids, conjunctiva, Cornea and lacrimal sac according to the nature of the disease. Eye discharge will be collected by gently rubbing sterilized cotton swab over the lower conjunctival sac. In the case of dacryocystitis, the specimen will be collected by puncturing of the lacrimal sac below the medial canthal ligament. In the case of keratitis or corneal ulcer, specimen will be taken using the slit lamp microscope. Tear film contents, lashes deposit, and corneal punctuate erosions will be swabbed in the case of ulcerative blepharitis. At least two samples will be collected from each participants. The first for gram-stain and the other for culture test. We will follow the standard operating procedure (SOP) for specimen collection, handling and transportation during the study period(25).\r\nCulture and bacterial identification\r\nThe collected specimens will be inoculated on MacConkey agar, chocolate agar plates, Mannitol salt agar, and blood agar plates (all media from Oxoid, Hampshire, UK)(13). The culture media will be incubated at 37Co aerobically for 48 h. To facilitate Co2 tensions, the chocolate agar plates will be incubated in a candle jar. Initial examination for bacterial growth will be conducted after 24 hours and media with no bacterial growth will be further incubated for 24h. According to the hospital SOP sub culture will be conducted for media with mixed bacterial colony growth. Bacterial identification will be done by gram-stain and colony examination that will be followed by a series of biochemical tests(12,13) \r\nAntibiotic susceptibility test\r\nAntibiotic susceptibility test will be conducted on each isolated bacteria using the disc-diffusion technique on Mueller Hinton agar (MHA)(26). But, to test antibiotic sensitivity of fastidious bacteria we will use MHA that contain 5% defibrinated sheep-blood. About three to five bacterial colony will be picked and emulsified in 5ml of normal saline which will be further adjusted to a 0.5 McFarland standard solution. The antibiotic-impregnated disk will be put on the inoculated agar plates using sterile forceps and it will be incubated at 37Co over 24 hours. The zone of inhibition will be measured and the zone diameters will be categorized in consonance with the Clinical and Laboratory Standards Institute (CLSI) guideline as susceptible, intermediate, and resistant(26). Bacteria isolate which has resistance to at least one medication in ≥3 different antibiotic classes will be considered as MDR(27).\r\n\r\nQuality control\r\nThe data collection tool used to assess the socio-demographic characteristics and clinical data will be tested on 5% of the sample size before the actual data collection and all relevant modification will be made accordingly. The data collection tool will be translated into local languages (Afan Oromo and Amharic) and will back-translated to English to maintain consistency. All specimens will be collected and handled according to the SOPs for external ocular sample collection. The quality of culture and reagents will be checked regularly by inspecting for the color change, cracks, over moisture,   hemolysis, and expiry date. The sterility of the culture will be checked by incubating at least one un-inoculated plate from each batch. Standard strains like S. aureus (ATCC 25923), E. coli (ATCC 25922), and P. aeruginosa (ATCC 27853) will be used to ensure the quality and performance of the media(26). Two individuals will enter data independently to keep data entry quality.\r\n\r\nData analysis and interpretation\r\nAfter checking the completeness of each questionnaire, the data will be entered into Epidata version 4.2. Then the data will be exported to STATA version 15 for statistical analysis. Descriptive statistics will be used to summarize antimicrobial susceptibility findings and common clinical features of EOIs. Bivariate and multivariate logistic regression analyses will be conducted to identify factors associated with poor clinical outcomes among patients with EOIs. Adjusted odds ration (OR) with 95% confidence interval (CI) will be calculated to determine independent predictors of poor clinical outcomes. Independent variables with a p-value <0.05 will be accepted as statistically significant.\r\nEthical considerations \r\nEthical approval will be obtained from the Institutional Review Board (IRB) of Jimma University Institute of Health before initiating the study. Permissions to conduct the study will be obtained from the Ophthalmology department before initiating patient enrollment. Any modification to the study protocol will be communicated to the IRB for endorsement. The project team will discuss the study purpose, procedures, follow-up, and data handling procedure, and participant privacy with caregivers or patients in local languages. After reassuring the participants/caregivers that they have the right to refuse participation or withdraw from the study at any time, the project team will receive written informed consent from all enrolled patients/caregivers. All the data collected will be used for the research purpose and only the study team will have access to both the paper-based and data stored on the computer. \r\nDissemination plan \r\nThe finding of this will be disseminated to all concerned bodies including JMC, Ethiopian Food, and drug administration Authority (EFDA), Ministry of Health (MoH) to update on current antimicrobial resistance in ocular infections. The results of this study will be presented at relevant scientific conferences and we will publish in the reputable journals to share our findings with scientific communities','<ol>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><strong><span style=\"font-size:14.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">REFERENCES </span></span></strong></span></span></li>\r\n</ol>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">1. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Bourne RRA, Steinmetz JD, Saylan M, Mersha AM, Weldemariam AH, Wondmeneh TG, et al. Causes of blindness and vision impairment in 2020 and trends over 30 years, and prevalence of avoidable blindness in relation to VISION 2020: The Right to Sight: An analysis for the Global Burden of Disease Study. Lancet Glob Heal. 2021;9(2):e144&ndash;60. </span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">2. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Abbafati C, Abbas KM, Abbasi-Kangevari M, Abd-Allah F, Abdelalim A, Abdollahi M, et al. Global burden of 369 diseases and injuries in 204 countries and territories, 1990&ndash;2019: a systematic analysis for the Global Burden of Disease Study 2019. Lancet. 2020;396(10258):1204&ndash;22. </span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">3. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Patricia A, Ramke J, Cairns J, Butt T, Zhang JH, Muirhead D, et al. EClinicalMedicine Global economic productivity losses from vision impairment and blindness. EClinicalMedicine [Internet]. 2021;35:100852. Available from: https://doi.org/10.1016/j.eclinm.2021.100852</span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">4. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Ackland P, Resnikoff S, Bourne R. World blindness and visual impairment: despite many successes, the problem is&nbsp; growing. Community eye Heal. 2017;30(100):71&ndash;3. </span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">5. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Cherinet FM, Tekalign SY, Anbesse DH, Bizuneh ZY. Prevalence and associated factors of low vision and blindness among patients attending St . Paul &rsquo; s Hospital Millennium Medical College , Addis Ababa , Ethiopia. BMC Ophthalmol. 2020;(2018):10&ndash;2. </span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">6. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Tegegn MT, Assaye AK, Mersha GA. Proportion, Causes and Associated Factors of Blindness Among Adult Patients&nbsp; Attending Tertiary Eye Care and Training Center in Ethiopia. Clin Optom. 2021;13:83&ndash;91. </span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">7. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; WHO. World Health Organization. World report on vision. [Internet]. Geneva; 2019. Available from: https://www.who.int/publications/i/item/97892 41516570</span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">8. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Deepthi KG, Prabagaran SR. Microbial Pathogenesis Ocular bacterial infections : Pathogenesis and diagnosis. Microb Pathog. 2020;145(June 2019). </span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">9. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Teweldemedhin M, Gebreyesus H, Atsbaha AH, Asgedom SW. Bacterial profile of ocular infections : a systematic review. BMC Ophthalmol. 2017;1&ndash;9. </span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">10. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Roodhooft JMJ. Leading causes of blindness worldwide. Bull Soc Belge Ophtalmol. 2002;(283):19&ndash;25. </span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">11. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Burton MJ. Prevention, treatment and rehabilitation. Community eye Heal. 2009 Dec;22(71):33&ndash;5. </span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">12. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Getahun E, Gelaw B, Assefa A, Assefa Y, Amsalu A. Bacterial pathogens associated with external ocular infections alongside eminent&nbsp; proportion of multidrug resistant isolates at the University of Gondar Hospital, northwest Ethiopia. BMC Ophthalmol. 2017 Aug;17(1):151. </span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">13. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Diriba K, Kassa T, Alemu Y, Bekele S. In Vitro Biofilm Formation and Antibiotic Susceptibility Patterns of Bacteria from&nbsp; Suspected External Eye Infected Patients Attending Ophthalmology Clinic, Southwest Ethiopia. Int J Microbiol. 2020;2020:8472395. </span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">14. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Ayehubizu Z, Mulu W, Biadglegne F. Common bacterial causes of external ocular infections, associated risk factors and&nbsp; antibiotic resistance among patients at ophthalmology unit of Felege Hiwot Referral Hospital, Northwest Ethiopia: a cross-sectional study. J Ophthalmic Inflamm Infect. 2021 Mar;11(1):7. </span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">15. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Teweldemedhin M, Saravanan M, Gebreyesus A, Gebreegziabiher D. Ocular bacterial infections at Quiha Ophthalmic Hospital, Northern Ethiopia: an&nbsp; evaluation according to the risk factors and the antimicrobial susceptibility of bacterial isolates. BMC Infect Dis. 2017 Mar;17(1):207. </span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">16. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Reta A, Bitew Kifilie A, Mengist A. Bacterial Infections and Their Antibiotic Resistance Pattern in Ethiopia: A&nbsp; Systematic Review. Adv Prev Med. 2019;2019:4380309. </span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">17. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Diriba K, Awulachew E, Gemede A, Anja A. The magnitude of extended-spectrum beta-lactamase- producing Enterobacteriaceae from clinical samples in Ethiopia: a systematic 