ng Enterobacteriaceae from clinical samples in Ethiopia: a systematic review and meta-analysis. Access Microbiol. 2021;3(3). </span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">18. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Subedi D, Vijay AK, Willcox M. Overview of mechanisms of antibiotic resistance in Pseudomonas aeruginosa: an ocular perspective. Clin Exp Optom. 2018;101(2):162&ndash;71. </span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">19. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Schulte AJ, Agan BK, Wang H-C, McGann PT, Davies BW, Legault GL, et al. Multidrug-Resistant Organisms from Ophthalmic Cultures: Antibiotic Resistance and&nbsp; Visual Acuity. Mil Med. 2020 Aug;185(7&ndash;8):e1002&ndash;7. </span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">20. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; World Health Organization (WHO). Global Action Plan on Antimicrobial Resistance. [Internet]. world health organization (WHO). Geneva; 2015. Available from: https://www.who.int/antimicrobialresistance/%0Aglobal-action-plan/en/</span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">21. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; EFDA. Strategy for the Prevention and Containment of Antimicrobial Resistance for Ethiopia 2015-2020 [Internet]. Addis Ababa; 2015. Available from: https://www.who.int/publications/m/item/ethiopia</span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">22. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Ibrahim RA, Teshal AM, Dinku SF, Abera NA, Negeri AA, Desta FG, et al. Antimicrobial resistance surveillance in Ethiopia: Implementation experiences and&nbsp; lessons learned. Afr J Lab Med. 2018;7(2):770. </span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">23. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Azari AA, Barney NP. Conjunctivitis: A Systematic Review of Diagnosis and Treatment. JAMA [Internet]. 2013 Oct 23;310(16):1721&ndash;30. Available from: https://doi.org/10.1001/jama.2013.280318</span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">24. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Lin A, Rhee MK, Akpek EK, Amescua G, Farid M, Garcia-Ferrer FJ, et al. Bacterial Keratitis Preferred Practice Pattern&reg;. Ophthalmology. 2019;126(1):P1&ndash;55. </span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">25. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Sharma S. Diagnosis of infectious diseases of the eye. Eye (Lond). 2012 Feb;26(2):177&ndash;84. </span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">26. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Vandepitte J, Verhaegen J, Engbaek K, Rohner P, Piot P HC. Basic laboratory procedures in clinical bacteriology: [Internet]. 2nd Editio. Geneva: WHO; 2003. Available from: www.who.int/iris/handle/10665/42696.</span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">27. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Magiorakos A-P, Srinivasan A, Carey RB, Carmeli Y, Falagas ME, Giske CG, et al. Multidrug-resistant, extensively drug-resistant and pandrug-resistant bacteria: an&nbsp; international expert proposal for interim standard definitions for acquired resistance. Clin Microbiol Infect&nbsp; Off Publ Eur&nbsp; Soc Clin Microbiol Infect Dis. 2012 Mar;18(3):268&ndash;81. </span></span></span></p>','This study is aimed at evaluating a bacterial profile, antibiotic resistance pattern, and treatment outcomes of patients with external ocular infections at JMC Ophthalmology Clinic, Ethiopia',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,2,'Antimicrobial resistance,Antibiotic Susceptibility ,Ocular Infection,MRSA,Multidrug resistance',10,NULL,1,NULL,NULL,NULL),(74,124,9,14,'Background: A clearly defined framework for an emergency supply chain is essential to effectively address health logistic needs in a timely manner. Maintaining efficient health logistics management framework provides health care leaders with an ability to identify and mobilize the necessary resources. Effective supply of health logistics during crisis often requires logistical networking, adaptability, and creativity. \r\nObjective: this study introduces an emergency health logistic framework that could be adapted to different local contexts so that it could be possible to respond rapidly to disaster related health logistic needs.\r\nMethod: A grounded theory led approach will be employed to inductively develop a resilient emergency health logistic management framework. The study will make its focus on drought affected areas of Bale Zone, Oromia region. The health logistic response status, challenges and preparedness will be qualitatively explored to build up the framework. Key informant interviews and FGDs will be conducted with stakeholder organizations, drought affected community members and community leaders. The data management will be assisted by ATLAS.ti 7.1.4. Thematic analysis will be employed to analyze the data and thematic proximity will be checked by considering the frequency of co-occurring codes in each emerging theme. The strength of association will be shown using co-occurrence coefficient (C-coefficient) value. Finally, the emerging dimensions will be used to construct an emergency health logistic framework.\r\nBudget and Time Frame: To conduct the study a total of 611,200 Birr is required. The study starts on January 2022 and ends up on July 2022.',NULL,'Development of Emergency Health Logistics Management Framework: Improving the Health Logistic Access of Drought Affected Areas','2021-11-19 16:18:39','The project\'s findings will evaluate the status health logistic in the affected areas and come up with an emergency health logistic management framework. The developed framework will serve as a valuable guidance to design context-specific health logistic interventions that ensure the resilience of the health logistic system and contributes to health logistic access during humanitarian disasters.','d778b2d532c19554011dbb8e44359175','completed','Ethiopia has been suffering from crop pests, floods, drought-induced famines, landslides, and wars resulting large casualties and significant societal damages. Drought-induced famine has been Ethiopia\'s deadliest disaster event for many years, causing immense suffering to millions of Ethiopians, especially rural populations, and it remains a national challenge (1,2). Such humanitarian disasters always result a public health emergency, necessitating an immediate action towards fulfilling the health logistic needs of the society in the afflicted communities. (3,4)\r\nEmergency health logistics management is a current point of discussion as disasters may occur anytime at any place with so many negative consequences. Moreover, it is becoming a growing concern due to the occurrence of several infectious diseases like COVID 19. In the event of a public health emergency, quick emergency supplies are critical to reduce the loss of life.(5-7)\r\n Associated with this, different humanitarian aid organizations are stretching their hands to the afflicted areas. Despite this, emergency health logistic management remained a key challenge which may not be easily addressed like that of any material logistics. The challenges of emergency health logistics management can be characterized in the following main aspects. First, the timeliness of relief supply and distribution is difficult to control in the emergency context. Second, the resource management for emergency logistics remains challenging. Lastly, accurate demand information is required but it is difficult to get (8,9).\r\nA clearly defined framework for an emergency supply chain is essential to effectively address health logistic needs in a timely manner (10). Recently, humanitarian emergencies are emerging as a challenge to the traditional approach of disaster response. When disasters occur, most likely the supply chains will be broken, exposing the needy in the most vulnerable position. More than 70% of the expenses of humanitarian response are associated with health supply chain. By 2030, the cost of humanitarian disaster related response is estimated to reach $20 billion per year.(11)\r\nOne vital aspect of disaster management is having a resilient health logistics management system. In most disaster exposed part of the world, little evidences are available regarding health care logistics involvement in planning for disaster management. (12)\r\nMaintaining efficient health logistics management framework provides health care leaders with an ability to identify and mobilize the necessary resources. Effective supply of health logistics during crisis often requires logistical networking, adaptability, and creativity. Managers should foresee where supplies are, where they need to go, what supplies will be needed the most, how often, and through what mechanisms affected individuals can receive (13-15).\r\nIt is essential to understand country specific threats and potential resources to get out of humanitarian crisis with minimal negative consequences. Because of dissimilarity in country contexts, country specific dimensions should be identified to decide on the design of emergency supply chain. Understanding the locally existing health threats will also allow countries to know the types of items and forecast quantities in the event of an emergency, as well as to decide whether to stock some of these items a head of time. (10,16)\r\nDespite the a contextualized health logistics framework  determines the survival of many vulnerable individuals, to  the  best search  of  the  investigators,  there  is  no  customized emergency health logistic framework in the study area. Therefore, this study introduces an emergency health logistic framework that could be adapted to different local contexts so that it could be possible to respond rapidly to disaster related health needs.','Study area and period\r\nThe study will make its focus in drought affected areas of Bale Zone Oromia Region. Bale Zone is one of the areas hit by droughts. Bale Zone is located in Oromia Regional State\'s southeast corner. It contains a variety of climate zones, from semi-arid to afro-alpine moorland. The Oromia region\'s livelihood profile revealed that the zone is divided into two livelihood systems: pastoral and agro-pastoral livelihood zones. Pastoralism and agro-pastoralism are the dominant land use regimes in the zone, and animals are the community\'s most valuable asset. Bale has a higher risk of drought than many other zones, and has had a string of rain failures in the last nine years. The zone has twenty districts with a total population of 2,105,481 among which 1,893,055 89.91) living in the rural areas (17, 18). \r\nStudy design and population\r\nGrounded theory guided approach will be employed to inductively develop a resilient emergency health logistic management framework by qualitatively exploring individual experiences. \r\nPopulation\r\nSource Population:\r\nAll community leaders and drought affected individuals in the study area as well as governmental and nongovernmental stakeholders working on health logistics will be the source population.\r\nStudy Population:\r\nPurposively selected community leaders including, religious leaders, Kebele administrators and influential individuals as well as victims of the drought (Especially household leaders) will participate in the study. Moreover, purposively selected officers from the following concerned stakeholders will take part in the study.\r\n•	Disaster Risk Management Commission from federal to local level, \r\n•	Public Health Emergency Management and health logistic officers from federal to Woreda level\r\n•	Concerned nongovernmental organizations’ officers \r\nEligibility criteria\r\nInclusion Criteria\r\nOfficers who work on emergency health logistic issues, community leaders who are well known by the community and drought affected community household leaders who are above 18 years old will be enrolled for the study.\r\nExclusion Criteria\r\nParticipants who cannot respond and communicate due to medical reasons will be excluded from the study\r\nSample size and sampling procedure\r\nKey informant interviews will be conducted with concerned stakeholder organization officers (both governmental and nongovernmental), community leaders and drought affected individuals. Tentatively, 8 KIIs with concerned governmental organizations, 5 with nongovernmental organization officers, 5 with community leaders and 7 with drought affected community members will be conducted.  The exact number of key informant interviews will be determined based on the saturation of ideas during data collection. These key informants will be selected considering their responsibility at their respective organizations. Both strategic and operational level officers will be considered for key informant interview. The study will focus on three districts with higher number of drought affected households. To further uncover some unforeseen issues, FGDs will be conducted with drought affected community members from these selected districts. Temporarily, six separate FGDs will be conducted. Each FGD will have eight to twelve participants. All participants will be purposively selected considering their responsibility in their households. From a single household, one individual will purposively selected to take part in the FGD. \r\nData collection Procedure and tools\r\nData collection Procedure\r\nThe KIIs with officers of stakeholder organizations, community leaders and drought affected community members will focus at examining the preparedness, status of the response and the existing challenges. To triangulate findings from KII and to obtain richer information on the status of emergency health logistic response, FGDs will be conducted with drought affected community members. Experienced qualitative data collectors with relevant educational background will be recruited and trained for two days. Each FGD will have one facilitator and one recorder. During data collection, natural settings will be preferred. Afan oromo will be utilized for discussion and interview with community participants and leaders. All FGDs and KIIs will be recorded using digital voice recorder and backup note takings.\r\n\r\n\r\nData collection Tool\r\nFGDs and interview guides will be developed after review of relevant literatures. A pilot study will be conducted in nearby drought affected kebele which will be excluded from the actual study later on. Expert opinions will also be incorporated and all the necessary amendments will be made to refine the data collection tool. \r\nData management and statistical analysis\r\nThe collected data will be transcribed with verbatim transcription and translated into English by language experts. The data management will be assisted by ATLAS.ti 7.1.4. Software. Repetitive readings will be made to get adapted with the data. Then, codes will be assigned and inter-code consistencies will be checked. The codes will be clustered into categories (sub-themes) and themes by linking related ideas. The whole research team will review the transcripts and generated codes to reach on to consensus. The emerging themes will be the bases to identify dimensions of the emergency health logistics management framework. Thematic proximity will be checked by considering the frequency of co-occurring codes in each emerging theme. The strength of association will be shown using co-occurrence coefficient (C-coefficient) value. Finally, the emerging dimensions will be used to construct an emergency health logistic framework.\r\nData quality assurance\r\nData collectors will be trained for two days. To maintain trustworthiness, discussions and interviews will be conducted with participants who have relevant experience and expertise. The data collection tools will be prepared in English, and then translated to Amharic and Afan Oromo finally it will be back-translated into English by another independent translator to check for consistency. Member checking will be done at the end of data collection by summarizing major thematic areas that have emerged during the interview and FGD. Team members of the research will also review and give their comment on the report. To ensure transferability, the whole research process, will be explained extensively. To ensure dependability, the methodological approaches followed will be clearly described. A detailed chronology of research activities will be audited by the research team.\r\n\r\n3.	Ethical Consideration\r\nEthical approval will be requested from Jimma University Institutional Review Board. And Formal permission will be secured from all relevant offices. Each participant will be informed verbally and consent will obtained. Culture and norms of the community will be respected.','<ol>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"background-color:white\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Zeleke TT, Giorgi F, Diro GT, Zaitchik BF. Trend and periodicity of drought over Ethiopia. International journal of climatology. 2017 Nov;37(13):4733-48.) (Teshome A, Zhang J. Increase of extreme drought over Ethiopia under climate warming. Advances in Meteorology. 2019 Aug 25;2019.)</span></span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"background-color:white\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Lautze S, Aklilu Y, Raven-Roberts A, Young H, Kebede G, Leaning J. Risk and vulnerability in Ethiopia: Learning from the past, responding to the present, preparing for the future. Report for the US Agency for International Development. Addis Ababa, Ethiopia. 2003 Jun.</span></span></span><span style=\"font-family:MillerText-Roman,&quot;serif&quot;\"><span style=\"color:black\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">).</span></span></span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"background-color:white\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Hou C, Jiang H. Methodology of emergency medical logistics for multiple epidemic areas in public health emergency. Plos one. 2021 Jul 26;16(7):e0253978.</span></span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"background-color:white\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Govindan K, Mina H, Alavi B. A decision support system for demand management in healthcare supply chains considering the epidemic outbreaks: A case study of coronavirus disease 2019 (COVID-19). Transportation Research Part E: Logistics and Transportation Review. 2020 Jun 1;138:101967.</span></span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"background-color:white\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Zhang Y, Ding Q, Liu JB. Performance evaluation of emergency logistics capability for public health emergencies: perspective of COVID-19. International Journal of Logistics Research and Applications. 2021 Apr 14:1-4.</span></span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"background-color:white\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Oloruntoba R, Babatunde S, Agho K. A systematic review of humanitarian logistics models for medical and healthcare products in humanitarian emergencies in Africa. Final Project Report of a Seed Corn Funded Project by the Humanitarian Innovation Initiative (HI2) the Watson Institute for International and Public Affairs, Brown University, USA. August. 2018 Mar 19.</span></span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"background-color:white\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Yui-yip L, Zhang J, Ng AK, Roozbeh P. Implications of a Pandemic Outbreak Risk: A Discussion on China&#39;s Emergency Logistics in the Era of Coronavirus Disease 2019 (COVID-19). Journal of International Logistics and Trade. 2020;18(3):127-35.</span></span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"background-color:white\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Jiang Y, Yuan Y. Emergency logistics in a large-scale disaster context: Achievements and challenges. International journal of environmental research and public health. 2019 Jan;16(5):779.</span></span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"background-color:white\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Kov&aacute;cs G, Falagara Sigala I. Lessons learned from humanitarian logistics to manage supply chain disruptions. Journal of Supply Chain Management. 2021 Jan;57(1):41-9.</span></span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">USAID. BEST PRACTICES IN SUPPLY CHAIN PREPAREDNESS FOR PUBLIC HEALTH EMERGENCIES. Atecchnical report 2018</span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">The vital role played by logistics during humanitarian crises </span></span><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"background-color:white\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">https://www.weforum.org/agenda/2020/02/the-vital-role-played-by-logistics-during-humanitarian-crises/ accessed on</span></span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"background-color:white\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">VanVactor JD. Health care logistics: who has the ball during disaster?. Emerging health threats journal. 2011 Jan 1;4(1):7167.</span></span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"background-color:white\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Kov&aacute;cs G, Falagara Sigala I. Lessons learned from humanitarian logistics to manage supply chain disruptions. Journal of Supply Chain Management. 2021 Jan;57(1):41-9.</span></span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"background-color:white\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Salam MA, Khan SA. Lessons from the humanitarian disaster logistics management: A case study of the earthquake in Haiti. Benchmarking: An International Journal. 2020 Mar 21.</span></span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"background-color:white\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Y&aacute;&ntilde;ez-Sandivari L, Cort&eacute;s CE, Rey PA. Humanitarian logisti