tyle=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">558,080</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n		</tr>\r\n		<tr>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:1px solid black; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top; width:127px\">\r\n			<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">8</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top; width:300px\">\r\n			<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Contingency (10%)</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top; width:204px\">\r\n			<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">55,808</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n		</tr>\r\n		<tr>\r\n			<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:1px solid black; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top; width:427px\">\r\n			<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><strong><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Grand total</span></span></strong></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top; width:204px\">\r\n			<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><strong><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">613,880 ETB</span></span></strong></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n		</tr>\r\n	</tbody>\r\n</table>\r\n</div>\r\n\r\n<div style=\"page-break-after:always\"><span style=\"display:none\">&nbsp;</span></div>',1,NULL,NULL,NULL),(75,35,9,20,'Abstract / Executive summary(Maximum 0.5 pages)\r\nBackground: The causes of maternal death and individual, environmental and health systems related gaps contributing to maternal death in Ethiopia remain unclear(1). therefore the purpose of this study is to investigating the risk factors and outcomes of Pregnancy induced hypertension among pregnant women attending ANC follow-up in hospitals found in Jimma zone\r\nObjective: The main aim of the present study is to advances in assessing the risk factors of Pregnancy induced hypertension among pregnant women attending ANC follow-up in hospitals found in Jimma zone, Ethiopia: 2014.\r\nMethodology: A prospective longitudinal study will be performed conducted with longitudinal assessment of maternal risk factors and blood pressure level at different trimesters of pregnancy','What is the association between sociodemographic status and Pregnancy induced hypertension?  What is the association between maternal Biochemical and physical factors on Pregnancy induced hypertension? What is the association between maternal behavioral f','Investigating the risk factors and outcomes of Pregnancy induced hypertension among pregnant women attending ANC follow-up in hospitals found in Jimma zone, South West of Ethiopia, 2014.','2021-11-19 19:11:13','By conducting this specific study we will identify the consequence of different biochemical disruption that occurs due to pregnancy at different specific time of occurrence. So, based on this study our finding will help:\r\n	Early detection of hypertensive disorders of pregnancy that help reduces complication due to the disease. \r\n	Come out with evidence on effects trimester specific hormonal changes and its effect on developments of hypertensive disorders of pregnancy and to set management and improvements policy. \r\n	Policy implications for countries in stimulating the development of more effective strategies to reduce maternal mortality and morbidity associated with hypertensive disorders of pregnancy. \r\n	It paves the way for researcher for more detailed investigations of the problem and assessment of birth outcomes.','23e5b00cc1fb607f96d026568e3e7132','completed','I.	Background and Rationale\r\n Pregnancy induced hypertension (PIH) refers to one of the four conditions: 1, pre-existing hypertension, 2, gestational hypertension and preeclampsia 3, pre-existing hypertension plus superimposed gestational hypertension with proteinuria and 4, unclassifiable hypertension(1). Disorders of pregnancy induced hypertensive are a major health problem in the obstetric population as they are one of the leading causes of maternal and prenatal morbidity and mortality(3). Worldwide, 10 % of all pregnancies are complicated by hypertension, with preeclampsia and eclampsia being the major causes of maternal and prenatal morbidity and mortality(4). Other study done in Zimbabwe the prevalence of PIH was 19.4%(5). \r\nEven those PIH leading cause of maternal death and a major contributor of maternal and perinatal morbidity, the mechanisms responsible for the pathogenesis of PIH have not yet been fully elucidated. Studies during the past decade, however, have provided a better understanding of the potential mechanisms responsible for the pathogenesis of PIH(6). It is evident that the “maternal constitution,” including genetic, behavioral, and metabolic factors, influences the maternal response to the abnormal placentation of preeclampsia(7). Placental ischemia is thought to lead to widespread activation/dysfunction of the maternal vascular endothelium that results in enhanced formation of endothelin and thromboxane, increased vascular sensitivity to angiotensin II, and decreased formation of vasodilators such as nitric oxide and prostacyclin(8).\r\nAbnormal maternal-foetal circulation due to poor placentation in early pregnancy makes the placenta hypoxic, and in response, a series of proinflammatory factors are released from the placenta that damage maternal endothelial cells; consequently, vascular resistance is increased, which as a result leads to maternal hypertension development(9). Inappropriate adaptation of maternal physiology may lead to complications of pregnancy, such as preeclampsia, fetal growth restriction, fetal overgrowth and preterm birth. Maternal adaptations to pregnancy are largely mediated by the placenta (10). \r\nMaternal thyroid hormones are critical to fetal neurodevelopment and change significantly during pregnancy. Therefore, thyroid status is frequently assessed during pregnancy, both to evaluate suspected thyroid abnormalities and to monitor the status of pre-existing thyroid disease(11,12). Thyroid dysfunction without any primary thyroid disease may result in various qualitative or quantitative changes of triglycerides, phospholipids, cholesterol, and other lipoproteins(13). The placenta also produces human placental lactogen (hPL), which stimulates maternal lipolysis and fatty acid metabolism. As a result, this conserves blood glucose for use by the fetus. It can also decrease maternal tissue sensitivity to insulin, resulting in gestational diabetes(14).The altered endocrine milieu associated with obesity (increased levels of insulin, androgens, and leptin)(15). \r\nThe increased estrogen in pregnancy leads to increase corticosteroid binding globulin production and in response the adrenal gland produces more cortisol. The net effect is an increase of free cortisol. This contributes to insulin resistance of pregnancy and possibly striae(16). Existing studies suggest that maternal pregnancy stress may influence perinatal outcomes including gestational weight gain by disrupting the hypothalamic-pituitary-axis resulting in elevation of cortisol levels (17).\r\nIn Ethiopia one in five pregnant women has history of khat chewing(18). the practice of chewing khat go beyond the life of the pregnant women and affect the neonate by reduced daily food intake, anemia, low birth weight and to the extent of maternal and infant death(19,20). The add up of all these factor may have strong prediction in resulting pregnancy induced hypertension so the purpose of these study will be to investigating those risk factors and outcomes of pregnancy induced hypertension among pregnant women attending ANC follow-up in hospitals found in Jimma zone.','A prospective longitudinal study will be performed conducted with longitudinal assessment of maternal risk factors and blood pressure level at different trimesters of pregnancy','<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Gudeta TA, Lema TB, KItila SB. Pregnancy induced hypertension and associated factors among pregnant women receiving antenatal care service at jimma town public health facilities, South West Ethiopia. Journal of Gynecology and Womens Health. 2018;10(4). </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">2. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Kintiraki E, Papakatsika S, Kotronis G, Goulis DG, Kotsis V. Pregnancy-Induced hypertension. :13. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">3. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Gudeta TA, Regassa TM. Pregnancy Induced Hypertension and Associated Factors among Women Attending Delivery Service at Mizan-Tepi University Teaching Hospital, Tepi General Hospital and Gebretsadik Shawo Hospital, Southwest, Ethiopia. Ethiopian Journal of Health Sciences [Internet]. 2019 [cited 2021 Nov 5];29(1). Available from: https://www.ajol.info/index.php/ejhs/article/view/187185</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">4. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Hofmeyr GJ, Lawrie TA, Atallah &Aacute;N, Torloni MR. Calcium supplementation during pregnancy for preventing hypertensive disorders and related problems. Cochrane database of systematic reviews. 2018;(10). </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">5. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Muti M, Tshimanga M, Notion GT, Bangure D, Chonzi P. Prevalence of pregnancy induced hypertension and pregnancy outcomes among women seeking maternity services in Harare, Zimbabwe. BMC Cardiovasc Disord. 2015 Oct 2;15(1):111. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">6. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Datta S, Kodali BS, Segal S. Maternal Physiological Changes During Pregnancy, Labor, and the Postpartum Period. In: Datta S, Kodali BS, Segal S, editors. Obstetric Anesthesia Handbook: Fifth Edition [Internet]. New York, NY: Springer; 2010 [cited 2021 Nov 5]. p. 1&ndash;14. Available from: https://doi.org/10.1007/978-0-387-88602-2_1</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">7. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Roberts JM, Bell MJ. If we know so much about preeclampsia, why haven&rsquo;t we cured the disease? Journal of reproductive immunology. 2013;99(1&ndash;2):1&ndash;9. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">8. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Granger JP, Alexander BT, Bennett WA, Khalil RA. Pathophysiology of pregnancy-induced hypertension. American journal of hypertension. 2001;14(S3):178S-185S. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">9. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Zygula A, Kosinski P, Wroczynski P, Makarewicz-Wujec M, Pietrzak B, Wielgos M, et al. Oxidative Stress Markers Differ in Two Placental Dysfunction Pathologies: Pregnancy-Induced Hypertension and Intrauterine Growth Restriction. Oxidative Medicine and Cellular Longevity. 2020 Jun 30;2020:e1323891. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">10. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Napso T, Yong HEJ, Lop