2 diabetes mellitus patients at St. Paul&rsquo;s Hospital, Addis Ababa, Ethiopia. BMC research notes. 2018;11(1):1&ndash;6. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">15. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Salzer L, Tenenbaum-Gavish K, Hod M. Metabolic disorder of pregnancy (understanding pathophysiology of diabetes and preeclampsia). Best practice &amp; research Clinical obstetrics &amp; gynaecology. 2015;29(3):328&ndash;38. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">16. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Shen H, Liu X, Chen Y, Biwei HE, Cheng W. Associations of lipid levels during gestation with hypertensive disorders of pregnancy and gestational diabetes mellitus: a prospective longitudinal cohort study. BMJ open. 2016;6(12):e013509. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">17. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Kubo A, Ferrara A, Brown SD, Ehrlich SF, Tsai A-L, Quesenberry Jr CP, et al. Perceived psychosocial stress and gestational weight gain among women with gestational diabetes. PLoS One. 2017;12(3):e0174290. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">18. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Bifftu BB. Prevalence of khat chewing during pregnancy in Ethiopia: a systematic review and meta-analysis. Preprint Posted online June. 2020;8. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">19. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Tesfay K, Abera M, Wondafrash M, Tesfaye M. Effect of Khat Use During Pregnancy on the Birth Weight of Newborn in Jimma, Ethiopia. International Journal of Mental Health and Addiction. 2019;17(6):1432&ndash;41. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">20. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Manzar MD, Salahuddin M, Sony P, Maru TT, Pandi-Perumal SR, Moscovitch A, et al. Sleep disturbances and memory impairment among pregnant women consuming khat: An under-recognized problem. Annals of thoracic medicine. 2017;12(4):247. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">21. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Egeland GM, Skurtveit S, Staff AC, Eide GE, Daltveit AK, Klungs&oslash;yr K, et al. Pregnancy-related risk factors are associated with a significant burden of treated hypertension within 10 years of delivery: Findings from a population-based Norwegian Cohort. Journal of the American Heart Association. 2018;7(10). </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">22. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Gudeta TA, Regassa TM. Pregnancy Induced Hypertension and Associated Factors among Women Attending Delivery Service at Mizan-Tepi University Teaching Hospital, Tepi General Hospital and Gebretsadik Shawo Hospital, Southwest, Ethiopia. Ethiopian journal of health sciences. 2019;29(1):831&ndash;40. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">23. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Walle TA, Azagew AW. Hypertensive disorder of pregnancy prevalence and associated factors among pregnant women attending ante natal care at Gondar town health Institutions, North West Ethiopia 2017. Pregnancy Hypertension. 2019;16(September 2018):79&ndash;84. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">24. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Mekonnen W, Hailemariam D, Gebremariam A. Causes of maternal death in Ethiopia between 1990 and 2016: Systematic review with meta-analysis. Ethiopian Journal of Health Development. 2018;32(4):225&ndash;42. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">25. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Gudeta TA. Pregnancy Induced Hypertension and Associated Factors among Pregnant Women Receiving Antenatal Care Service at Jimma Town Public Health Facilities, South West Ethiopia. Journal of Gynecology and Womens Health. 2018;10(4):1&ndash;9. </span></span></span></span></p>','The main aim of the present study is to investigating the risk factors of Pregnancy induced hypertension among pregnant women attending ANC follow-up in hospitals found in Jimma zone, Ethiopia: 2014.',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,2,'pregnancy induced hypertension,maternal outcome ,perinatal outcome',10,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),(76,214,9,12,'Assessment of pattern of diseases in children admitted to JMC and their outcome will show the common diseases causing morbidity and mortality; so that intervention will be prioritized accordingly. In addition assessment of outcomes will indirectly assess the quality of care given. This will be an observational descriptive study; we will also assess the care givers’ satisfaction at the time of discharge from the hospital.  Children less than 15 year of age including neonates, adolescents and pediatric surgical cases will be enrolled after getting caregivers’ consent. This study is necessary for evidence based interventions concerning improvement of pediatric care, to reduce in hospital mortality, prevent acute or long lasting complications. In the process; this study will also help the department to improve documentation, patient follow up and capacity building by motivating the investigators to get involved in further research activities in addition to clinical work.',NULL,'Quality improvement project to increase elective operation theater efficiency at JUMC','2021-11-19 16:22:54','Outcome measures:\r\nFirst case incision on time rate = Total # of First case surgeries that started before or at the agreed up time/# of all first case surgeries \r\nOT utilization rate = Total # of surgeries performed per table per day/expected # of surgeries per table per day\r\nCancellation rate = Total # of cancelled surgeries/Total # of planned surgeries\r\n\r\nThe result of this study will elucidate the factors associated with poor efficiency of the operation theater and determine which intervention idea brings improvement.','b519b5f8d493dfd5632dab1573220cd3','completed','Operation theaters are some of the expensive units of hospitals which need to be effective and efficient to minimize unnecessary expenses and be productive with the limited resource. Study done in Singapore showed that operating rooms contribute to almost two-thirds of a hospital’s revenue and account for about 40% of the hospital’s total expenses which include manpower costs.(1) Another study conducted in Australia showed that, due to the pressure in health from requirements of aging population, introduction of expensive medical technologies and greater community expectations for access to health services, public hospitals are required to provide better care by being more efficient and reducing wasteful spending. (2)\r\nEfficiency improvement in the OT can improve OT productivity, decrease patient waiting time to surgery and increase patient satisfaction. Numerous factors in the perioperative setting influence OT productivity, such as surgical scheduling accuracy, starting on time, minimising procedure time variation (but taking in procedure complexity), turnover time, inter-operative delays and bed management. In addition, the training of junior surgical and anaesthetic staff may affect OT productivity.(2) First case starting time determines the utilization of the operating table and thus the first patient is termed the golden patient. Multiple factors related to HR, equipment, consumables and others can delay the first case incision time. This intern may lead to cancellation of the last cases. Cancellation may also arise from poor pre-operative preparation, unforeseen infrastructural challenges, or over planning.\r\nAs we are living in a resource limited country, efficient utilization of the operating tables are paramount. It is estimated that over five million surgical interventions are needed in Ethiopia each year to adequately serve the needs of the population. But estimates show that no more than 200,000 surgeries (4%) are actually performed annually.(3) Improving the efficiency of surgery was the first objective of the 6th strategic pillar of The Safe Surgery 2020 (SS2020) project which ended in 2020. OT efficiency indicators include Rate of First Elective Case On-Time Theater Performance and  Rate of Cancellation of Elective Surgery.(4)\r\nEven though some factors may be beyond control, majority are predictable and preventable. Thus running QI project to improve the efficiency of the OT focusing on these three metrics (FCIT, OT utilization and cancellation) is expected to show significant improvement which intern improve the surgical volume, bed occupancy rate and waiting time for surgery.','Study area and population: \r\nJimma University Institute of Health has 800 beds and gives service to more than 20 million catchment population. It has 12 operating theaters with 15 operating tables.The project will be conducted at Jimma University Institute of Health (JUMC) departments (units) of Ophthalmology, General Surgery, Pediatric Surgery, Orthopedic surgery, Gynecology/Obstetric Surgery, and Hepatobiliary Surgery. \r\n\r\nStudy period:\r\nProject duration shall be one year. \r\n\r\nSource population and study population:\r\nAll patients who get operated at regular operating tables of  Ophthalmology, General Surgery, Pediatric Surgery, Orthopedic surgery, Gynecology/Obstetric Surgery, and Hepatobiliary Surgery during the study period will be used as study population \r\n\r\nDue to the inclusion of all operated patients, no sampling technique will be used.\r\n\r\nBaseline data collection:\r\nBaseline data for FCIT, OT utilization and cancellation will initially be collected for 1 month. \r\nData collection format will be prepared for FCIT, OT utilization and OT cancellation on day of surgery. Data collectors will be given training on how to complete the format. Using the PDSA cycle, intervention ideas will be tested separately and analyzed to assess the outcome every 10 data point. \r\n\r\nData will be visualized using run charts and assessed for improvement.','<ol>\r\n	<li>Hong Choon Oh, Tieng Beng Phua, Shao Chuen Tong, Jeremy Fung Yen Lim. Assessing the performance of operating rooms: what to measure and why, Proceedings of Singapore Healthcare, volume 20, Number 2, 2011</li>\r\n	<li>ACI Surgical Services Taskforce; Operating Theatre Efficiency Guidelines: A guide to the efficient management of operating theatres in New South Wales hospitals. December 2014; ISBN: 978-1-76000-132-2, pp5-6</li>\r\n	<li>Surgical care systems strengthening: developing national surgical, obstetric and anaesthesia plans. Geneva: World Health Organization; 2017. Licence: CC BY-NC-SA 3.0 IGO</li>\r\n	<li>Sehrish Bari, Joseph Incorvia, Katherine R. Iverson, Abebe Bekele, Kaya Garringer, Olivia Ahearn, Laura Drown, Amanu Aragaw Emiru, Daniel Burssa, Samson Workineh, Ephrem Daniel Sheferaw, John G. Meara &amp; Andualem Beyene (2021) Surgical data strengthening in Ethiopia: results of a Kirkpatrick framework evaluation of a data quality intervention, Global Health Action, 14:1, 1855808, DOI: 10.1080/16549716.2020.1855808)</li>\r\n</ol>','The goal of this project is to improve the efficiency of JUMC OT by improving First case incision time (FCIT), OT utilization, and cancellation on the day of surgery at Ophthalmology, General Surgery, Pediatric Surgery, Orthopedic surgery, Gynecology/Obstetric Surgery, and Hepatobiliary Surgery units and departments.',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,2,'FCIT,OR efficiency,OT utilization,OR cancellation',10,'<p><em>Budget</em> (outline just major line items and gross budget, detailed breakdown is not required at this stage)</p>\r\n\r\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\r\n\r\n<table cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\">\r\n	<tbody>\r\n		<tr>\r\n			<td style=\"vertical-align:top\">\r\n			<p>S. no</p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"vertical-align:top\">\r\n			<p>Activities</p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"vertical-align:top\">\r\n			<p>Estimated cost</p>\r\n			</td>\r\n		</tr>\r\n		<tr>\r\n			<td style=\"vertical-align:top\">\r\n			<p>1</p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"vertical-align:top\">\r\n			<p>Data collection</p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"vertical-align:top\">\r\n			<p>210,000</p>\r\n			</td>\r\n		</t