:23.0px\">\r\n			<p>&nbsp;</p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1.0px solid #000000; border-left:1.0px solid #000000; border-right:1.0px solid #000000; border-top:1.0px solid #000000; height:27.0px; vertical-align:top; width:24.0px\">\r\n			<p>&nbsp;</p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1.0px solid #000000; border-left:1.0px solid #000000; border-right:1.0px solid #000000; border-top:1.0px solid #000000; height:27.0px; vertical-align:top; width:23.0px\">\r\n			<p>&nbsp;</p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1.0px solid #000000; border-left:1.0px solid #000000; border-right:1.0px solid #000000; border-top:1.0px solid #000000; height:27.0px; vertical-align:top; width:23.0px\">\r\n			<p>&nbsp;</p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1.0px solid #000000; border-left:1.0px solid #000000; border-right:1.0px solid #000000; border-top:1.0px solid #000000; height:27.0px; vertical-align:top; width:23.0px\">\r\n			<p>&nbsp;</p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1.0px solid #000000; border-left:1.0px solid #000000; border-right:1.0px solid #000000; border-top:1.0px solid #000000; height:27.0px; vertical-align:top; width:24.0px\">\r\n			<p>&nbsp;</p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1.0px solid #000000; border-left:1.0px solid #000000; border-right:1.0px solid #000000; border-top:1.0px solid #000000; height:27.0px; vertical-align:top; width:23.0px\">\r\n			<p>&nbsp;</p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1.0px solid #000000; border-left:1.0px solid #000000; border-right:1.0px solid #000000; border-top:1.0px solid #000000; height:27.0px; vertical-align:top; width:23.0px\">\r\n			<p>&nbsp;</p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1.0px solid #000000; border-left:1.0px solid #000000; border-right:1.0px solid #000000; border-top:1.0px solid #000000; height:27.0px; vertical-align:top; width:23.0px\">\r\n			<p>&nbsp;</p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1.0px solid #000000; border-left:1.0px solid #000000; border-right:1.0px solid #000000; border-top:1.0px solid #000000; height:27.0px; vertical-align:top; width:24.0px\">\r\n			<p>&nbsp;</p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"background-color:#bfbfbf; border-bottom:1.0px solid #000000; border-left:1.0px solid #000000; border-right:1.0px solid #000000; border-top:1.0px solid #000000; height:27.0px; vertical-align:top; width:27.0px\">\r\n			<p>&nbsp;</p>\r\n			</td>\r\n		</tr>\r\n	</tbody>\r\n</table>',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),(77,115,9,16,'Low back pain (LBP) is a common syndrome that causes disability and absence from work. Although there are several causes, the most common type of back pain is nonspecific. Exercises are often used to treat back pain, but there is controversy regarding its effectiveness. The current project aimed to assess the prevalence and risk factors of nonspecific LBP among nurses in relation to gender differences and to determine the effect of standardized exercises training on pain improvement and the quality of life among nurse with nonspecific LBP at two public hospitals found in Jimma town Ethiopia.  A gender based comparative cross-sectional study will be conducted at two public hospitals in Jimma town. Semi-structured self-administered questionnaire which includes socio-demographic, organizational, personal and life style, work related and psychosocial) characteristics will be developed on the basis of literature review. Standard Nordic musculoskeletal questionnaires (an important instrument for clinical use applied universally and will be adapted to Ethiopia. The current project has two phases. The first phase will be a cross sectional assessment of prevalence LBP and risk factors. Base on the baseline evaluation a randomized controlled trial which involved the standardized exercise training intervention will be conducted. For evaluation of health related quality of life  the World Health Organization Quality of Life-brief (WHOQOL-BREF) will be used.',NULL,'PREVALENCE OF LOWER BACK PAIN AND QUALITY OF LIFE AMONG NURSES: THE GENDER PERSPECTIVE AT TWO PUBLIC HOSPITALS IN SOUTH WEST ETHIOPIA','2021-11-19 16:47:28','To date, existing LBP research in Ethiopia few and has focused on prevalence and associated risk factors(12, 13) and there has been no research examining LBP related to gender differences in the Ethiopian population context. Since in the African context, including Ethiopia women tend to do more household responsibilities compared to men so the different genders may be exposed to different causative agents for LBP. Furthermore, still no study has revealed the standardized exercises training for improving LBP and quality of life in Ethiopia specifically Jimma. Thus, this study will help for addressing the prevalence and risk factors of LBP in relation to gender difference, moreover the effect of standardized exercises training for improving LBP and quality of life. Based on the result sensitize the nurses about their problem, adopt preventive measures to reduce LBP and improving the quality of life of nurses with LBP participating in this study in addition the finding from this study will contribute knowledge to the existing literatures.','ba3a9a6425ab74f12f74f8d3e059688c','completed','Lower back pain (LBP) is a major cause of morbidity in high-, middle- and low-income countries. Itis identified as the most common complaints in nurses and globally the prevalence of LBP in nurses is about 50-80%(1, 2).Occupation plays possibly the biggest role in the occurrence of LBP on nurses because in providing a care, nurses are subjected to lift and transport patients or equipment i.e. nearly 20 % of nursing jobs have a lot of physical activity, and this activity is often in difficult environment particularly in developing nations where lifting aids are not available. So LBP remained the leading cause of activity limitation, work absence, and early retirement. Also causing direct and indirect economic burden on individual sufferer, society and governments(3, 4). Evidences shown that the prevalence of LBP among African nations, higher than the global LBP prevalence reported, this point out LBP is a growing problem among nurses and also a public health concern among developing nations in Africa(5). \r\nLBP prevalence estimate from studies: a study done in America(2),Switzerland(6), Hong Kong(7),Iran(8)showed that the overall prevalence of LBP among nurses was 52%, 76%, 40.6% and 61.2% respectively. While researches in Sub-Sahara Africa, A study done in South Africa(9), Tunisia(10),and Nigeria(11)showed that the overall prevalence of LBP among nurses was 59%, 58.1%, and 82.7% respectively. Few studies were done in Ethiopia; the prevalence of LBP among nurses was 45.8% in Addis Ababa(12)and 64% in Gondar (13). LBP is complex conditions with several factors contributing to its occurrence.Studies have been identified a wide-ranging factors associated with LBP among nurses and categorized as follows.\r\nPsychosocial factors such as job satisfaction and job stress(12, 14, 15), poor psycho-social work environment,  lack of social support from colleagues, psychological distress(16-19),have been reported to be associated with LBP. \r\nPhysical factors organizational and ergonomics factors such as high workloads specially nurses in Africa, carrying out a broad range of tasks and working in settings where no other health workers are available(3, 20)Moreover manual handling of materials, monotonous tasks, repetitive movements, long-standing, bending of the back, and carrying heavy objects(9, 11, 21-24),unavailability of lifting equipment(10, 22) have been reported to be associated with LBP. \r\nIndividual factors such as age, gender, high work experience, obesity, educational level, marital status (more married nurses were suffering from LBP than unmarried nurses),number of pregnancies, poor physical condition(12, 22)poor knowledge of back care ergonomics (24)were significantly associated with LBP.\r\n2.	RELEVANCE AND SIGNIFICANCE OF THE STUDY\r\nAmong different factors the effect of gender on the prevalence of LBP has produced conflicting results i.e. evidence of review studies(25, 26)have found that women more affected by LBP than men, in contrary another  review of studies revealed a reverse gender pattern i.e. being male was associated with having LBP higher than female(27). Since the effect of LBP not only cause discomfort and impaired the quality of life for individual nurse, but also is costly for the employers and the society(28). As we know human resources are the major fuel for work of any organization there healthiness are also not debatable. In line to this the health of nurses is a major concern for health organization as they are the direct caregivers to the patient, and their emotional and physical stability is needed for effective patient care for maintaining their standards in relation to patient safety. Thus it is important to identify the factors that influence the risk of developing LBP separately for men and women and adopt strategies for effective prevention, intervention and reduction of LBP.\r\nIn developed nations various interventions has been taken to reduce the impact, and findings from different studies revealed, non-pharmacological therapy or therapeutic exercises is recognized for the management of LBP and also its significance to improve the quality of life. Moreover evaluating QOL is documented as an important to represent clinical effectiveness in clinical trials of therapeutic exercises (29-31).Studies of the most effective types of exercise therapy for chronic or acute LBP are still controversial in literature; however, therapeutic exercises are probably the most widely used conservative treatment. Exercise standardization, as well as duration, frequency and time of evaluation are necessary to reduce the risk of misinterpretation in determining the most appropriate modality for a given population affected by nonspecific LBP. Exercises that are done incorrectly can be harmful to their health (32)','This project employed different methodological and data collection approach based on specific objectives. These are described in following work packages linked to the different specific objectives.\r\nWork package 1: To answer research question 1 and 2: \r\n4.1.	STUDY DESIGN AND STUDY AREA: \r\nA gender based comparative cross-sectional study will be conducted at two public hospitals (Jimma Medical Center (JMC), and Shenen Gibe Hospital, in Jimma town.\r\n4.2.	SOURCE AND STUDY POPULATION\r\nCurrently 686 nurses are working in two public hospitals of Jimma town i.e. Jimma Medical Center (N=623, Female 357 and Male 266) and Shenen Gibe Hospital (N= 63, Female 43 and Male 19) \r\n4.3.	SAMPLE SIZE DETERMINATION \r\nTo answer the 1st and 2ndresearch question the sample size is determined by two population proportion formula using Open Epi online software considering the following parameters. The sample is calculated considering 95% Confidence level, 80% power, 1:1 ratio of female to male gender, 64% prevalence of LBP among all nurses (13), and to detect an OR of 2 (measure of effect), needs 354 sample of nurses. Finally by considering 10% non-response, the required sample size is 394 nurses (197male and 197 female nurses).\r\n4.4.	SAMPLING TECHNIQUES AND PROCEDURE \r\n	The sampling frame of nurses will be prepared by their list from the two public Hospital’s (i.e. Jimma Medical Center and Shenen Gibe Hospital) Human Resource director. The study samples that have been determined in the sample size determination will be allocated proportionally based on the total nurses in each hospital. Finally, simple random sampling technique will be applied to select the study participants from prepared list from two hospitals (Jimma Medical Center and Shenen Gibe Hospital) and distributed according to their proportional number of nurses in two hospitals. A 1:1 proportion of male and female nurse participants will be selected.\r\n4.5.	Data collection tools and procedure\r\nSemi-structured self-administered questionnaire which includes socio-demographic, organizational, personal and life style, work related and psychosocial) characteristics will be developed on the basis of literature review. Standard Nordic musculoskeletal questionnaires (an important instrument for clinical use applied universally and will be adapted to Ethiopia \r\nWork package 2: To answer research question 3: \r\n4.6.	STUDY DESIGN AND STUDY POPULATION\r\nA randomized controlled trial and compared the standardized exercise training intervention group and control group. A number of nurses with LBP from the result of research question one.\r\n4.7.	DATA COLLECTION METHODS AND TOOLS \r\nRandomized controlled single-blind design will be followed for 10 weeks with participants from both groups (standardized exercise training group and non-exercise group). The World Health Organization Quality of Life-brief (WHOQOL-BREF) questionnaire English version will be used to assess the QOL of the participants and the assessments for QOL will be performed at two points of time at baseline and after 6 weeks. All the study tools will be used after pre-test in 5% of the sample size.\r\n4.8.	ETHICAL CONSIDERATIONS\r\nThe study will be conducted in accordance with the principles of the Declaration of Helsinki and the International Council on Harmonization Guidelines for Good Clinical Practice. Ethical clearance & approval will be obtained from the Institutional Review Board (IRB) of Institute of Health, Jimma University, Ethiopia. In the process, the following detailed ethical issues will be considered. These includes, the voluntary nature of participation, obtaining written consent, after a necessary explanation about the purpose, benefit and risk of the study and also their right on decision of whether or not participating in the study. Ensuring confidentiality (by using code instead of name of the participants in the questionnaire) and the privacy of any information given by the participant will be protected.','<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><a name=\"_ENREF_1\">1.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Gen&ccedil; A, Kahraman T, G&ouml;z E. The prevalence differences of musculoskeletal problems and related physical workload among hospital staff. Journal of back and musculoskeletal rehabilitation. 2016;29(3):541-7.</a></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><a name=\"_ENREF_2\">2.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Budhrani-Shani P, Berry DL, Arcari P, Langevin H, Wayne PM. Mind-body exercises for nurses with chronic low back pain: an evidence-based review. Nursing research and practice. 2016;2016.</a></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><a name=\"_ENREF_3\">3.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Lin L-Y, Juan C-W. A systematic review of the literature: risk factors and strategies for occupational hazards in hospitals. 中華職業醫學雜誌. 2015;22(2):71-88.</a></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><a name=\"_ENREF_4\">4.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Steenstra I, Verbeek J, Heymans M, Bongers P. Prognostic factors for duration of sick leave in.</a></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><a name=\"_ENREF_5\">5.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Morris LD, Daniels KJ, Ganguli B, Louw QA. An update on the prevalence of low back pain in Africa: a systematic review and meta-analyses. BMC musculoskeletal disorders. 2018;19(1):1-15.</a></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><a name=\"_ENREF_6\">6.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Corona G, Amedei F, Miselli F, Padalino M, Tibaldi S, Franco G. Association between relational and organizational factors and occurrence of musculoskeletal disease in health personnel. Giornale italiano di medicina del lavoro ed ergonomia. 2005;27(2):208-12.</a></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><a name=\"_ENREF_7\">7.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Yip Yb. A study of work stress, patient handling activities and the risk of low back pain among nurses in Hong Kong. Journal of advanced nursing. 2001;36(6):794-804.</a></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><a name=\"_ENREF_8\">8.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Azizpour Y, Delpisheh A, Montazeri Z, Sayehmiri K. Prevalence of low back pain in Iranian nurses: a systematic review and meta-analysis. BMC nursing. 2017;16(1):1-10.</a></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><a name=\"_ENREF_9\">9.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Dlungwane T, Voce A, Knight S. Prevalence and factors associated with low back pain among nurses at a regional hospital in KwaZulu-Natal, South Africa. Health SA Gesondheid. 2018;23.</a></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><a name=\"_ENREF_10\">10.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Boughattas W, El Maalel O, Maoua M, Bougmiza I, Kalboussi H, Brahem A, et al. Low back pain among nurses: prevalence, and occupational risk factors. Occupational Diseases and Environmental Medicine. 2017;5(1):26-37.</a></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><a name=\"_ENREF_11\">11.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Muhammed AF, Ladan AM, Musa HA, Garba AM. Work related risk factors for lower back pain among nurses in Ahmadu Bello University Teaching Hospital (ABUTH), Zaria-Nigeria. IOSR Journal of Nursing and Health Science (IOSR-JNHS). 2015;4(3).</a></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><a name=\"_ENREF_12\">12.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Belay MM, Worku A, Gebrie SA, Wamisho B. Epidemiology of low back pain among nurses working in public hospitals of Addis Ababa, Ethiopia. East and Central African Journal of Surgery. 2016;21(1):113-31.</a></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><a name=\"_ENREF_13\">13.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Mekonnen TH, Yenealem DG. Factors affecting healthcare utilization for low back pain among nurses in Gondar town, northwest Ethiopia, 2018: a cross-sectional study. BMC research notes. 2019;12(1):1-6.</a></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><a name=\"_ENREF_14\">14.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Yip VYB. New low back pain in nurses: work activities, work stress and sedentary lifestyle. Journal of advanced nursing. 2004;46(4):430-40.</a></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><a name=\"_ENREF_15\">15.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Davis KG, Heaney CA. The relationship between psychosocial work characteristics and low back pain: underlying methodological issues. Clinical biomechanics. 2000;15(6):389-406.</a></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><a name=\"_ENREF_16\">16.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Smith D, Leggat P. Musculoskeletal disorders in nursing. Australian Nursing Journal: ANJ, The. 2003;11(1):19-21.</a></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><a name=\"_ENREF_17\">17.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Feyer A-M, Herbison P, Williamson AM, de Silva I, Mandryk J, Hendrie L, et al. The role of physical and psychological factors in occupational low back pain: a prospective cohort study. Occupational and environmental medicine. 2000;57(2):116-20.</a></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><a name=\"_ENREF_18\">18.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Moussa MMM, El-Ezaby HH, El-Mowafy RI. Low back pain and coping strategies&rsquo; among nurses in Port Said City, Egypt. Journal of Nursing Education and Practice. 2015;5(7):55-62.</a></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><a name=\"_ENREF_19\">19.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Eriksen W, Bruusgaard D, Knardahl S. Work factors as predictors of intense or disabling low back pain; a prospective study of nurses&rsquo; aides. Occupational and environmental medicine. 2004;61(5):398-404.</a></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><a name=\"_ENREF_20\">20.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Shieh S-H, Sung F-C, Su C-H, Tsai Y, Hsieh VC-R. Increased low back pain risk in nurses with high workload for patient care: A questionnaire survey. Taiwanese Journal of Obstetrics and Gynecology. 2016;55(4):525-9.</a></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><a name=\"_ENREF_21\">21.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Jahani A, Kalantary S, Alitavoli A. An application of artificial intelligence techniques in prediction of birds soundscape impact on tourists&rsquo; mental restoration in natural urban areas. Urban Forestry &amp; Urban Greening. 2021;61:127088.</a></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><a name=\"_ENREF_22\">22.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Adhikari S, Dhakal G. Prevalent Causes of Low Back Pain and its Impact among Nurses Working in Sahid Gangalal National Heart Centre. Journal of Nepal Health Research Council. 2014;12(28):167-71.</a></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><a name=\"_ENREF_23\">23.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Wong T, Teo N, Kyaw M. Prevalence and risk factors associated with low back among health care providers in a District Hospital. Malaysian Orthopaedic Journal. 2010;4(2):23-8.</a></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><a name=\"_ENREF_24\">24.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Sikiru L, Hanifa S. Prevalence and risk factors of low back pain among nurses in a typical Nigerian hospital. African health sciences. 2010;10(1):26.</a></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><a name=\"_ENREF_25\">25.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Hoy D, Bain C, Williams G, March L, Brooks P, Blyth F, et al. A systematic review of the global prevalence of low back pain. Arthritis &amp; Rheumatism. 2012;64(6):2028-37.</a></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><a name=\"_ENREF_26\">26.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; W&aacute;ng YXJ, W&aacute;ng J-Q, K&aacute;pl&aacute;r Z. Increased low back pain prevalence in females than in males after menopause age: evidences based on synthetic literature review. Quantitative imaging in medicine and surgery. 2016;6(2):199.</a></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><a name=\"_ENREF_27\">27.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Fatoye F, Gebrye T, Odeyemi I. Real-world incidence and prevalence of low back pain using routinely collected data. Rheumatology international. 2019;39(4):619-26.</a></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><a name=\"_ENREF_28\">28.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Charney W, Schirmer J. Nursing injury rates and negative patient outcomes&mdash;connecting the dots. AAOHN journal. 2007;55(11):470-5.</a></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><a name=\"_ENREF_29\">29.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Shamsher S, Prabha T, Sethuraman K, Shamsher S. Incorporating physical exercise as part of lifestyle: The way to combat non-specific chronic low back pain. SBV Journal of Basic Clinical and Applied Health Science. 2018;1(1):153-62.</a></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><a name=\"_ENREF_30\">30.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Broonen J-P, Marty M, Legout V, Cedraschi C, Henrotin Y. Is volition the missing link in the management of low back pain? Joint Bone Spine. 2011;78(4):364-7.</a></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><a name=\"_ENREF_31\">31.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Henchoz Y, So AK-L. Exercise and nonspecific low back pain: a literature review. Joint Bone Spine. 2008;75(5):533-9.</a></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><a name=\"_ENREF_32\">32.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Lizier DT, Perez MV, Sakata RK. Exercises for treatment of nonspecific low back pain. Brazilian Journal of Anesthesiology. 2012;62(6):838-46.</a></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\">&nbsp;</p>','To estimate the prevalence and risk factors of nonspecific LBP among nurses in relation to gender differences and to determine the effect of standardized exercises training on pain improvement and  the quality of life among nurse with nonspecific LBP at two public hospitals  found in Jimma town Ethiopia',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,2,'Quality of life,Low back pain,standardized exercise training',10,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),(78,200,9,18,'Background: Many patients have multiple chronic conditions, therapeutic regimens often involve multiple medications and frequent daily dosing. In developed countries, medication regimen complexity is associated with an increase in hospital readmissions, adverse drug effects, and medication non-adherence. However, little is known from developing countries, in particular Ethiopia.\r\nObjective: This project/study will aim to assess the level of patients’ medication regimen complexity and its effect on medication non-adherence and hospital readmission among chronic NCDs patients attending follow-up care at JUMC.\r\nMethods: A retrospective study will be employed. All illegible patients with chronic NCDs who were admitted between September 2019 and September 2020 will be included. The patients will be retrospectively followed for one year to capture the study outcomes. Data will be collected using structured questionnaires. The collected data will be cleaned and entered into Epidata version 3.1 and then transported to SPSS version 25 for analysis. Descriptive statistics will be performed to describe the findings. Multivariate logistic regression will be performed to assess the effect of MRC on hospital readmission and medication no-adherence. \r\nResult: The findings of this study/project are expected to address the research questions, and they will be presented in the form of a narrative along with frequency, percentages, mean, medians, tables, and figures. \r\nConclusion: The result of the study will be concluded based on the findings','Effect of medication regimen complexity on medication non-adherence and readmission','Assessment of the patient medication regimen complexity (MRC) and its effects on medication non-adherence and unplanned hospital readmission among patients with chronic non-communicable diseases (NCDs) attending the chronic follow-up care at JUMC','2021-11-19 17:13:20','The outcomes of this study will be unplanned readmission to the hospital ward and medication non-adherence.','0de88453abed882a9b18535fb1b456da','completed','Effective medications are central to the prevention and management of chronic diseases and their complications. Because many patients have multiple chronic conditions, therapeutic regimens often involve multiple medications and frequent daily dosing. Such regimen complexity may undermine effective chronic disease management (1). In 2004, George et al developed the Medication Regimen Complexity Index (MRCI) for quantifying drug regimen complexity (2).  The MRCI tool does not simply assess the number of medications a patient is taking; it also assesses points for the formulation (inhalers are more complex than a tablet), dosing frequency (more times per day is more complex), and any additional directions the patient needs to follow (e.g. take 1 hour before meals) (3). The MRCI includes weighted components of (A) dosage form, (B) dosing frequency, and (C) additional administration instructions. Component A incorporates a weighting scheme for dosage form (e.g., tablet vs spray vs gel), route of administration, and, in some instances, body part. Component B includes 23 weights ranging from 0.5 for a once-daily PRN (as needed) to 12.5 for a medication that is prescribed to be taken every 2 hrs. Component C provides weights for 10 additional directions a patient may need to follow in adhering to a prescribed regimen. MRC is used for quantifying drug regimen complexity with potential applications in both practice and research (2,4). \r\nStatement of the problem\r\nReadmission rates have been considered a hospital quality measure and have been shown to reflect dimensions of quality of patient care (5,6). Twenty-nine (29%) of Americans took five or more medications daily, and 119,000 deaths are annually occurring from prescription medication-related issues. One million emergency department (ED) visits and 280,000 hospitalizations each year from avoidable complications are caused by drugs or drug interactions (7). The estimated annual cost of drug-related morbidity and mortality resulting from non-optimized medication therapy was $528.4 billion, which was equivalent to 16% of total US health care expenditures in 2016 (8). The Medicare Payment Advisory Commission (MedPAC) has estimated that 12% of readmissions are potentially avoidable. Preventing 10% of these readmissions could save Medicare $1 billion (9). \r\nComplex medication regimens are troublesome to patients and physicians due to the resulting problems of nonadherence, therapeutic failure, and adverse drug reactions (10). A more recent meta-analysis of 12 studies from developed countries confirmed that MRC among patients with chronic diseases is associated with an increase in hospitalization, hospital readmissions, and medication nonadherence (11). A specific systematic review on the effects of MRC on medication non-adherence revealed higher MRC was associated with non-adherent in 28 studies among patients with different chronic diseases (12). \r\nPatients with higher MRC scores at admission have increased potential adverse drug events (ADEs), 30-day hospital readmissions, and reduced adherence (13,14). In a study by Wimmer et al in 2016, there was a significant statistical difference in the score of MCRI between patients who were readmitted and non-readmitted (12 vs. 7.5, P<0.01). MRC was significantly associated with unplanned hospitalization (15). High MRCI scores increased the odds of potential ADEs and unplanned 30-day hospital readmission. The odds of experiencing unplanned 30-day readmission were 5.45 times greater for those with a discharge MRCI score of 22 or above (16). A study by S. Lalic et al reported a 10-unit increase in MRCI was associated with time to first hospitalization (hazard ratio (HR) 1.17; 95% CI 1.06 - 1.29), the number of hospitalizations (Incidence rate ratio (IRR) 1.15; 95% CI 1.06 - 1.24), and hospital days per person-year (IRR 1.19; 95% CI 1.16 - 1.23) (17). In patients with Heart failure (HF), MRCI ≥15 was significantly associated with 30-day rehospitalization and HF patients with coronary artery disease were more likely to be readmitted 30 days after discharge (18). Moreover, Bazargan et al reported patients with a low complexity index were two times more likely to adhere to the dosage regimen of their medications compared to those with a high drug regimen complexity index (19). \r\nStudies have also shown, the level of the patient MRC has an association with other clinical outcomes. A study finding revealed diabetic patients with complex antidiabetic medication regimens had a higher level of diabetes-related distress (20). Moreover, blood pressure management was poor in hypertensive patients with higher MRCI scores (21). In HIV-infected patients, a higher level of MRC has negatively affected the quality of life of the patients (22). A study also reported higher MRCI was associated with mortality (15).\r\nTo the best of our knowledge of the topic, there are only two studies published from Africa. A more recent study from Egypt showed reducing medications regimen complexities led to a significant improvement of HbA1c (23). A study report from Debre Tabor, Ethiopia reported the prevalence of MRCI among type 2 diabetes mellitus patients is high (35.6%). Patients with lower MRCI scores were more adherent to their medication compared with patients with higher MRCI. In a similar way, high diabetes-specific MRCI was associated with poor glycemic control (24). Therefore, this study/project is designed to determine the patterns and effects of MRC on medication non-adherence and unplanned hospital readmission among patients with chronic NCDs attending chronic follow-up care at JUMC, Southwest Ethiopia. \r\nSignificance of the study\r\nIt is important for healthcare providers to account for the patient’s difficulties in relation to their medications, particularly for patients on long-term medications for chronic diseases. Identifying patients at risk for medication complexity through screening tools is important to reduce the consequences of complex regimens such as non-adherence and readmission to hospital; which in turn could save healthcare costs. Pre-admission screening with the MRCI can help identify patients requiring higher pharmaceutical care input. The MRCI is potentially useful for identifying patients who may benefit from Medication Therapy Management (MTM). Simplifying and optimizing medication use through comprehensive medication management, enhanced discharge planning, and discussing with healthcare providers and patients can reduce the MRC. The findings of this project may alert healthcare professionals particularly pharmacists to properly identify patients with complex medication regimens to appropriately organize the medications and counsel the patients and help health policymakers to devise appropriate counseling strategies for patients with complex medication regimens. The findings of this project/study may also be a baseline for the researchers.','Methods and materials\r\nStudy setting and period: This study will be conducted at Jimma University Medical Center (JUMC), a chronic follow care clinic. The study will be expected to be conducted as per the schedule set by the project administration if it will be accepted. \r\nStudy design: 12 months retrospective follow-up study \r\nSource populations: All patients with chronic NCDs on follow-up care at JUMC\r\nStudy populations: Patients with chronic NCDs who were admitted between September 1, 2019 and September 1, 2020 and on follow-up care at JUMC\r\nInclusion criteria: Patients who were prescribed at least one drug and age greater than 18 years \r\nExclusion criteria: Patients who lost from follow-up care, transferred to other sites, died before readmission, and incomplete medical records \r\nSample size and sampling techniques\r\nAll patients with chronic NCDs who were admitted between September 1, 2019 and September1, 2020, on follow-up care and who fulfilled the inclusion criteria will be included. \r\nOutcomes\r\nThe outcomes of this study will be medication non-adherence and unplanned readmission to the hospital ward. The first readmission will be used to analyze the effects of MRC on readmission rather than the number of hospitalizations because medication changes are often made during hospitalization. In this project/study, those patients who will readmit to the hospital due to causes unrelated to the index NCDs such as trauma or accidents will not be included in the analysis. \r\nParticipants’ follow-up and outcome measure \r\nPatients with chronic NCDs, who were admitted between September 1, 2019 and 2020 and on follow-up at chronic follow-up care will be included. The patients’ baseline clinical data including medications will be collected. Then, the patients will be followed for one year to capture the study outcomes. We believe that an adequate number of patients could readmit within 12 months. The medications collected at the baseline will be categorized according to the Anatomical Therapeutic Chemical Classification System recommended by the World Health Organization (25). Medication regimen complexity will be quantified using the validated 65-item Medication Regimen Complexity Index (MRCI), which was validated in different counties. The score consists of the following three sections: A (dosage forms), B (dosing frequency), and C (additional directions). Each section’s MCRI score is combined to determine the total score (2). In adult patients, the cut point of the higher score for MRCI is greater than 13.0 and 15.5 for elders, respectively (26). If an adverse drug reaction is reported, a Naranjo causality assessment will be made (27). Medication non-adherence will be determined using “proportion of days covered (PDC)”, which is an appropriate way to measure adherence in patients taking polytherapy. PDC is based on the percentage of days the patient has medication available. The formula to estimate the PDC = (total days covered)/ (total days in period) × 100. The patient was considered to be adherent to his/her medications when the PDC percentage is greater than equal to 80.0% (28). \r\nData collection procedures and instrument\r\nData collection tools will be developed after reviewing related articles and organizational guidelines on the related topics. Validated data collection (MRCI) (2) will also be adapted and the tool will be pretested before starting the actual data collection. Naranjo causality assessment will be applied if there was documented ADRs. Then, data will be collected using trained three Pharmacists and three Nurses under the supervision of two supervisors. \r\nData quality assurance\r\nRoutinely, two research supervisors will check the completeness of the collected data.\r\nEthical clearance: The ethical clearance will be obtained from Jimma University Institutional Review Board (IRB). The official letter from the Institute of Health will be sent to the Director of JUMC. The patients’ data will be kept confidential by not writing the name of the patient and not sharing data with other third parties.\r\nAnalysis: The collected data will be checked, cleaned, and entered into Epidata version 3.1 and transferred to SPSS version 25 for analysis. Descriptive statistics will be performed and the findings will be presented in frequency/percentage, means (SD), and median (IQR) based on the distribution of the variables. The findings are expected to be compared using either chi-squares/fishers, or student-T-test/Mann-Whitney U Test. Bivariate and then multivariate logistic regressions will be used to determine factors associated with unplanned readmission. Adjusted odds ratio along with the 95%CI will be used to describe the findings from regression analysis. Two-sided P< 0.05 will be used to declare the statistical significance. \r\nEthical considerations: Ethical clearance will be obtained from Jimma University IRB. The official letter from the institute of health will be sent to the Director of JUMC. The patients’ data will be kept confidential by not writing the name of the patient and not sharing data with other third parties.','<p style=\"text-align:justify\">&nbsp;</p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">1. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Choudhry NK, Fischer MA, Avorn J, Liberman JN, Schneeweiss S, Pakes J, et al. The implications of therapeutic complexity on adherence to cardiovascular medications. Arch Intern Med. 2011;171(9):814&ndash;22. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">2. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; George J, Phun YT, Bailey MJ, Kong DCM, Stewart K. Development validation of the medication regimen complexity index. Ann Pharmacother. 2004;38(9):1369&ndash;76. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">3. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Clay PG. Medication Regimen Complexity Indices: A tool to focus MTM efforts? J Am Pharm Assoc [Internet]. 2014;54(6):664. Available from: http://dx.doi.org/10.1331/JAPhA.2014.14542</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">4. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; McDonald M V., Peng TR, Sridharan S, Foust JB, Kogan P, Pezzin LE, et al. Automating the medication regimen complexity index. J Am Med Informatics Assoc. 2013;20(3):499&ndash;505. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">5. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Press MJ, Scanlon DP, Ryan AM, Zhu J, Navathe AS, Mittler JN, et al. Limits of readmission rates in measuring hospital quality suggest the need for added metrics. Health Aff. 2013;32(6):1083&ndash;91. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">6. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Rumball-Smith J, Hider P. The validity of readmission rate as a marker of the quality of hospital care, and a recommendation for its definition. N Z Med J. 2009;122(1289):2009. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">7. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Center for Health Care Strategies. Addressing Medication Complexity Through Community-Based. Available from: https://www.chcs.org/resource/addressing-medication-complexity-through-community-based-strategies/</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">8. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Watanabe JH, McInnis T, Hirsch JD. Cost of Prescription Drug&ndash;Related Morbidity and Mortality. Ann Pharmacother. 2018;52(9):829&ndash;37. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">9. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; McIlvennan CK, Eapen ZJ, Allen LA. Hospital readmissions reduction program. Circulation. 2015;131(20):1796&ndash;803. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">10. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Center for Health Care Strategies. Understanding and Addressing Medication Complexity [Internet]. 2019 Jun. Available from: https://www.chcs.org/resource/understanding-and-addressing-medication-complexity/</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">11. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Alves-Concei&ccedil;&atilde;o V, Rocha KSS, Silva FVN, Silva R de OS, Cerqueira-Santos S, Nunes MAP, et al. Are Clinical Outcomes Associated With Medication Regimen Complexity? A Systematic Review and Meta-analysis. Ann Pharmacother. 2020;54(4):301&ndash;13. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">12. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Pantuzza LL, Ceccato M das GB, Silveira MR, Junqueira LMR, Reis AMM. Association between medication regimen complexity and pharmacotherapy adherence: a systematic review. Eur J Clin Pharmacol. 2017;73(11):1475&ndash;89. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">13. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Lenz M, Clark JA, Gates BJ. Medication Regimen Complexity in Patients Receiving Consultant Pharmacy Services in Home Health Care. Sr Care Pharm. 2020 Feb;35(2):81&ndash;4. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">14. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Willson MN, Greer CL, Weeks DL. Medication Regimen Complexity and Hospital Readmission for an Adverse Drug Event. Ann Pharmacother. 2014;48(1):26&ndash;32. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">15. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Wimmer BC, Bell JS, Fastbom J, Wiese MD, Johnell K. Medication Regimen Complexity and Polypharmacy as Factors Associated With All-Cause Mortality in Older People: A Population-Based Cohort Study. Ann Pharmacother. 2016;50(2):89&ndash;95. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">16. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Schoonover H, Corbett CF, Weeks DL, Willson MN, Setter SM. Predicting potential postdischarge adverse drug events and 30-day unplanned hospital readmissions from medication regimen complexity. J Patient Saf. 2014;10(4):186&ndash;91. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">17. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Lalic S, Sluggett JK, Ilom&auml;ki J, Wimmer BC, Tan ECK, Robson L, et al. Polypharmacy and Medication Regimen Complexity as Risk Factors for Hospitalization Among Residents of Long-Term Care Facilities: A Prospective Cohort Study. J Am Med Dir Assoc [Internet]. 2016;17(11):1067.e1. Available from: http://dx.doi.org/10.1016/j.jamda.2016.08.019</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">18. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Colavecchia AC, Putney DR, Johnson ML, Aparasu RR. Discharge medication complexity and 30-day heart failure readmissions. Res Soc Adm Pharm. 2017;13(4):857&ndash;63. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">19. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Bazargan M, Smith J, Yazdanshenas H, Movassaghi M, Martins D, Orum G. Non-adherence to medication regimens among older African-American adults. BMC Geriatr. 2017;17(1):1&ndash;12. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">20. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Luzuriaga M, Leite R, Ahmed H, Saab PG, Garg R. Complexity of antidiabetic medication regimen is associated with increased diabetes-related distress in persons with type 2 diabetes mellitus. BMJ Open Diabetes Res Care. 2021;9(1):1&ndash;6. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">21. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Wakai E, Ikemura K, Kato C, Okuda M. Effect of number of medications and complexity of regimens on medication adherence and blood pressure management in hospitalized patients with hypertension. PLoS One. 2021;16(6 June):1&ndash;10. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">22. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Contreras-Mac&iacute;as E, Guti&eacute;rrez-Pizarraya A, RobustilloCort&eacute;s MA, Morillo-Verdugo R. High level of medication regimen complexity index correlate with worse quality of life in people living with HIV. Rev Esp Quimioter. 2021;34(2):93&ndash;9. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">23. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Abdelaziz TS, Sadek KM. Effect of reducing medication regimen complexity on glycaemic control in patients with diabetes. Rom J Intern Med. 2019;57(1):23&ndash;9. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">24. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Ayele AA, Tegegn HG, Ayele TA, Ayalew MB. Medication regimen complexity and its impact on medication adherence and glycemic control among patients with type 2 diabetes mellitus in an Ethiopian general hospital. BMJ Open Diabetes Res Care. 2019;7(1). </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">25. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; WHO. Anatomical Therapeutic Chemical ( ATC ) Classification Classi