right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:30px; vertical-align:top; width:360px\">\r\n			<p><span style=\"font-size:10pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\">Contingency (10%)</span></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:30px; vertical-align:top; width:132px\">\r\n			<p><span style=\"font-size:10pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\">51,000</span></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:30px; vertical-align:top; width:115px\">\r\n			<p>&nbsp;</p>\r\n			</td>\r\n		</tr>\r\n		<tr>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:1px solid black; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:25px; vertical-align:top; width:54px\">\r\n			<p>&nbsp;</p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:25px; vertical-align:top; width:360px\">\r\n			<p style=\"margin-left:8px\"><span style=\"font-size:10pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\">Total </span></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:25px; vertical-align:top; width:132px\">\r\n			<p><span style=\"font-size:10pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\">561,000</span></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:25px; vertical-align:top; width:115px\">\r\n			<p>&nbsp;</p>\r\n			</td>\r\n		</tr>\r\n	</tbody>\r\n</table>',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),(89,1,14,18,'dsadasdasd','Subtitle','asdasddas','2022-05-24 11:39:29','sdad','b7a23d7528def98b08b2c664718b025f','completed','dsaasd','sasad','<p>sadsadasd</p>','sadds',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,NULL,10,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'Proposal-628c99c186b29444013812.docx'),(90,143,13,19,'Antimicrobial resistance (AMR) poses a serious threat to public health today. The main driver of AMR is the misuse of antimicrobials medicines globally. In Ethiopia, almost half (49%) of antimicrobials used were inappropriate and most bacterial isolates have shown significant resistance to available drugs. To mitigate this problem, the Ethiopian Federal Ministry of Health and other stakeholders have been working on the implementation of antimicrobial stewardship (an intervention designed to ensure effective and responsible antimicrobial use) in Ethiopian hospitals. However, antimicrobial stewardship is not fully functional in the implemented hospital and it is not fully implemented in most Ethiopian hospitals. Since the traditional approach failed to curb the AMR, innovative ways to counteract the current challenges are greatly needed. Therefore the present technology transfer project is aimed to implement a digital antimicrobial stewardship mobile application and to evaluate its impact on antimicrobial utilization and patient clinical outcomes.\r\nThe project team will develop an antimicrobial stewardship mobile application that can be used on all versions of smartphones by healthcare professionals (HCPs). The mobile app will incorporate different materials on antimicrobial stewardship and infectious disease management into an integrated user-friendly interface for use at a point of care. This innovation will provide clinical decision support, and increase access to evidence-based guidelines, hospital antibiograms, and data entry to mention a few. This will enhance the prescriber’s adherence to evidence-based guidelines and minimize overconsumption of antimicrobials and the emergency of resistant pathogens.\r\nThe present study will be conducted at Jimma University Medical Center (JUMC) from Jun 30 2022 to June 30, 2023. The project has two phases: the pre-implementation phase and the digital stewardship phase. During the pre-implementation phase, the standard practice of the hospital related to antimicrobial stewardship and infectious disease prevention and management will continue as it is. The project team will work on developing the app and collecting baseline antimicrobial use indicators and patient outcomes. During the digital stewardship phase, we will test the app and launch it officially for use by clinicians. The impact of digital antimicrobial stewardship on antimicrobial utilization and patient outcomes will be evaluated by comparing pre-defined indicators between the two phases. \r\nAfter evaluating the impact of the app, we will hand over the mobile app to the Drug Information Center of JUMC. The institution can generate revenue by making subscription-only access (maybe at an institution level or end-user level) and customizing the content for other sub-Saharan African countries.  Since this innovation uses the available local-resource and resolves serious public health threats, it has a high potential to attract the government and private sector to invest and implement it.','Antimicrobial Stewardship','Evaluating the impact of an Antimicrobial Stewardship Smartphone App at Jimma University Medical Center, Jimma, Ethiopia','2022-05-14 17:48:19','The current project will improve appropriateness of antimicrobial prescription by providing clinicians with clinical decision support at point of care and easy access to up-to-date guidelines. In addition, this project will promote rational use of antibiotics which will further decrease unnecessary antibiotic consumption and related costs. Moreover, through promoting rational use of antibiotics, the current project will decrease length of hospital stay, intensive care admission, in-hospital mortality and re-admission. Finally, it will decrease incidence of resistant pathogens and hospital acquired infections (for detailed outcome explanation and definition see table 1 in main document).','3074c06b525903ca930e511c45164d97','completed','Antimicrobials are medications used to treat diseases caused by microbes (bacteria, viruses, fungi, and parasites)1. Antimicrobial Resistance (AMR) occurs when microbes change gradually and become unresponsive to the drugs designed to kill them1,2. AMR is one of the serious and urgent global public health problems since it affects the healthcare, veterinary, and agriculture industries1–3 AMR is associated with high mortality, frequent healthcare visits, prolonged hospital stay, and a huge economic burden on healthcare3. For instance, each year in the United State of America, at least 2.8 million people are infected with the antimicrobial-resistant microbe and about 35,000 people die as a result with an overall cost of up to $55 billion annually2,4. The World Bank indicated that AMR would upraise the rate of poverty and disproportionately affect low-income countries compared to the rest of the world5. \r\nThe main driver of AMR is the misuse of antimicrobials both in animal and human medicine globally and antimicrobial misuse is disproportionately higher in low and middle-income countries (LMIC)2,6,7 Worldwide up to 50% of antimicrobials used in the hospital were inappropriate8. Similarly, almost half (49%) of antimicrobials used in Ethiopian hospitals were inappropriate9. In addition, the pooled prevalence of multidrug resistance (MDR) strains was about 60% and the majority of bacterial isolates have shown significant resistance to commonly used antibiotics in Ethiopia9,10. \r\nTo increase the appropriate prescription and utilization of antimicrobials the Federal Ministry of Health (FMOH)11 and Ethiopian Food and Drug Administration (EFDA)12 have developed and implemented antimicrobial stewardship (AMS) program which is crucial for treatment success and prevention of AMR to a broader scale. However, this essential program is not fully implemented in most healthcare institutions13. This is largely due to a lack of regularly updated guidelines, a lack of easily accessible stewardship material at the point of care, and a unified platform that enhances healthcare professional communication14. Therefore the present technology transfer project is aimed to implement a digital antimicrobial stewardship mobile application and to evaluate its impact on antimicrobial utilization and patient clinical outcomes.\r\n        The Proposed Innovation \r\nThe project team will develop an innovative mobile app that incorporates different materials on antimicrobial stewardship and infectious disease management into an integrated user-friendly interface for use at a point of care. The app can be used on all versions of smartphones and by all healthcare professionals providing direct patient care. The app contents will be drafted in collaboration with the hospital antimicrobial stewardship committee and infection prevention and control committee using the national treatment guideline and stewardship practical guidelines. The project team will further restructure the guideline based on World Health Organization (WHO) anatomical classification into a series of treatment algorithms that simply clinical decisions and suggest first-line and alternative treatment at a point of care (Fig 1). \r\nThe app will enable all healthcare professionals to access real-time data about hospital antibiograms, evidence-based treatment guidelines, hospital and national antibiotic formulary, national antibiotic resistance survey reports, therapeutic drug monitoring (TDM) protocol for selected antibiotics, and clear criteria regulating the dispensing of restricted antimicrobials. Moreover, nurses can get access to parenteral antimicrobial preparation and administration protocol with the timing of de-escalation to oral antibiotics. Furthermore, the app will enable the clinical pharmacist to document and report interventions on antibiotic consumption data (DDD) and laboratory professionals to report susceptibility data (Fig 2).\r\nOn scale-up, the app can be linked with the WHO microbiological database software (WHONET)15 and it will reduce time, cost, and human resources to compile and report antimicrobial resistance surveillance reports. In addition, we will automate the app to alert clinicians when to review, switch, or discontinue antibiotics. Generally, the present innovation will provide locally customized antimicrobial stewardship guidelines, clinical decision support, data entry, and alerting prescribers on antibiotics prescriptions which promote prudent use of antimicrobials and reduce the emergence of resistant pathogens. \r\nAs far as our knowledge is concerned, this innovation is the first of its kind in JUMC or Ethiopian hospitals. The present innovation will significantly change an approach toward AMR prevention and control from paper-based and physically limited to a fully accessible resource by all HCPs at a point of care. It will save time and resources needed to access, develop, distribute and use guidelines by end-users as well as healthcare providers. In addition, since all the content of the app, will focus on common infections and accounts for local resistance patterns it will reduce further emergencies of resistant pathogens and potentially, improve patient quality of care. Furthermore, by supporting real-time data collection, it will promote service integration at different levels of healthcare, and support the detection of outbreaks, infectious disease dynamics, and antimicrobial consumption patterns. This will enable health policymakers and healthcare providers to implement strategies to prevent, treat and control infectious diseases timely.','5	5.1	Study area and duration \r\nThis study will be conducted at Jimma University Medical Center (JUMC) from Jun 30, 2022, to Jun 30, 2023. JUMC is a tertiary care hospital affiliated with Jimma University and it has more than 1500 HCPs. Since the hospital is a teaching hospital more than 100 nurses, 300 medical interns (rotation based), 60 residents, 20 Clinical Pharmacy master students (rotation based), 20 internists, and more than 10 sub-specialists are available in the medical ward of JUMC. As a key stakeholder in this project, the hospital has an Antimicrobial Stewardship Committee (ASC) and Infection Prevention and Control (IPC) committee composed of a multidisciplinary team. For the implementation of digital antimicrobial stewardship, the availability of smartphones, internet access, and computers are essential. Regarding this infrastructure, all junior doctors have tablet computers (windows phones) on top of smartphones and 97.5 % HCPs at JUMC have smartphones from our preliminary survey. Furthermore, JUMC has its own IT staff which supports the installation and maintenance of computers as well as data management. Furthermore, the hospital has a drug information center (DIC) equipped with more than 50 latest desktop computers with internet access. Lastly, almost all the physicians’ offices are equipped with desktop computers and internet access. So, we believe that the availability of these infrastructures will increase the feasibility of this project.\r\n5.2	Study design and phase \r\nThe pre-post study design will be conducted at JUMC, Ethiopia from Jun 30, 2022, to June 30, 2023. The present study will be conducted in two phases: the pre-implementation phase and the digital antimicrobial stewardship phase (mobile app phase).\r\n5.2.1	Pre-implementation phase \r\nDuring this period all standard care and paper-based antimicrobial stewardship will be continued as it is.  During this phase, the project team will recruit data collectors to assess the baseline antimicrobial consumption and key patient-related parameters. Detailed stewardship material will be drafted in collaboration with ASC and IPC. All antimicrobial stewardship materials will be endorsed by ASC before launching the app for use. The hospital antibiogram will be developed by compiling the record of microbiological test results over 6 months. Lastly, we will promote the use of the app banners and TV screens at different colliders of the hospital.\r\n5.2.2	Digital antimicrobial stewardship phase\r\nThis phase will be from December 1, 2022, to May 31, 2023. During this phase, all healthcare professionals can access clinical decision support on common infectious diseases evidence-based guidelines, hospital antibiogram, therapeutic drug monitoring for selected antibiotics, and hospital formulary using the antimicrobial stewardship App. Nurses have access to the database for parenteral antibiotic preparation and administration protocol and criteria for IV to oral shift. In addition, hospital pharmacists will enter data on antimicrobial stewardship-related interventions and data on antimicrobial consumption. Laboratory professionals will enter data related to drug susceptibility and positive culture results (Fig 2). Except for the digitalization of antimicrobial stewardship, other standards of care stay as it was pre-implementation. \r\n5.3	Data collection procedure \r\nDuring this phase, all patients who will be admitted to the general medical ward of JMC and receive systemic antibiotics will be enrolled.  All data regarding antimicrobial prescription including consumption, antimicrobial cost, resistance pattern, hospital, and ICU stay, in-hospital mortality, and re-admission rate will be collected from active patient’s medical records. The antimicrobial consumption data will be reported as a defined daily dose (DDD) based on the World Health Organization (WHO) collaborating center for Drug Statistics Methodology30. Furthermore, hospital clinical pharmacists will assess the appropriateness of antimicrobial utilization and they will identify drug-related problems (DRPs) based on Pharmaceutical Care Network Europe (PCNE) classification version 8.0231 To assess the impact of the digital antimicrobial stewardship mobile app and the result will be compared with those collected during the pre-implementation phase. In addition, we will assess the healthcare professional’s experience and usability of the app by using the System Usability Scale (SUS)32\r\n5.4	Statistical analysis\r\nThe data will be entered into Epidata version 4.2 and Stata v 16 will be used for data analysis. Baseline characteristics of patients and use of antimicrobial drugs will be summarized using proportion and chi-square test. Antimicrobial consumption, cost, appropriateness of antimicrobial treatment, and other clinical outcomes rate will be compared between the 2 study phases of the project using appropriate statistical methods.\r\n5.5	Dissemination \r\nResults will be disseminated to healthcare professionals and researchers via presentations at national and international conferences and publications in peer-reviewed journals. Furthermore, we plan to have a workshop with interested healthcare workers after the end of the study to disseminate experiences and findings. The public will be informed through press releases. General project data and metadata will be included in the supplementary data of published articles if needed, and/or shared through university repositories','<ol>\r\n	<li><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">World health organization (WHO). Fact Sheets, Antimicrobial resistance. Published 2020. Accessed October 5, 2021. https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/antimicrobial-resistance </span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">CDC. Antibiotic / Antimicrobial Resistance (AR / AMR). Published 2020. Accessed October 5, 2021. https://www.cdc.gov/drugresistance/about.html</span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">Michael CA, Dominey-Howes D, Labbate M. The antimicrobial resistance crisis: Causes, consequences, and management. <em>Front Public Heal</em>. 2014;2(SEP):1-8. doi:10.3389/fpubh.2014.00145</span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">Dadgostar P. Antimicrobial resistance: implications and costs. <em>Infect Drug Resist</em>. 2019;12:3903-3910. doi:10.2147/IDR.S234610</span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">Wang W, Arshad MI, Khurshid M, et al. Antibiotic resistance : a rundown of a global crisis. <em>Infect Drug Resist</em>. Published online 2018:1645-1658.</span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">Klein EY, Van Boeckel TP, Martinez EM, et al. Global increase and geographic convergence in antibiotic consumption between 2000 and 2015. <em>Proc Natl Acad Sci U S A</em>. 2018;115(15):E3463-E3470. doi:10.1073/pnas.1717295115</span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">Pokharel S, Raut S, Adhikari B. Tackling antimicrobial resistance in low-income and middle-income countries. <em>BMJ Glob Heal</em>. 2019;4(6):4-6. doi:10.1136/bmjgh-2019-002104</span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">CDC. <em>Antibiotic Resistance Threats in the United States, 2019</em>.; 2019. doi:http://dx.doi.org/10.15620/cdc:82532</span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">Muhie OA. Antibiotic Use and Resistance Pattern in Ethiopia: Systematic Review and Meta-Analysis. <em>Int J Microbiol</em>. 2019;2019. doi:10.1155/2019/2489063</span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">Tsegaye A, Mulubrahan A, Enkosilassie M, Mengistu H. The burden of antimicrobial resistance at tertiary care hospital , southern Ethiopia : a three years &rsquo; retrospective study. <em>BMC Infect Dis</em>. 2019;1:6-13.</span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">Ethiopia. Strategy for the Prevention and Containment of Antimicrobial Resistance for Ethiopia. world health organization (WHO). Accessed August 2, 2021. https://www.who.int/publications/m/item/ethiopia</span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">Gerba H. Ethiopian Food, Medicine and Healthcare Administration and Control Authority. <em>Korean Natl Inst Food Drug Saf Eval</em>. Published online 2018. http://www.nifds.go.kr/brd/m_95/down.do?brd_id=board_mfds_411&amp;seq=30934&amp;data_tp=A&amp;file_seq=4</span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">FMHACA. <em>A Practical Guide to Antimicrobial Stewardship Program in Ethiopian Hospitals</em>.; 2018. https://www.ghsupplychain.org/sites/default/files/2019-03/Guide to Antimicrobial Stewardship Program in Hospitals.pdf</span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">Gebretekle GB, Mariam DH, Abebe W, et al. Opportunities and barriers to implementing antibiotic stewardship in low and middle-income countries: Lessons from a mixed-methods study in a tertiary care hospital in Ethiopia. <em>PLoS One</em>. 2018;13(12):1-15. doi:10.1371/journal.pone.0208447</span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">WHO. WHONET 5.6. Published online 2021. https://www.whonet.org/software.html</span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">Stryjak J. <em>The Mobile Economy 2020</em>.; 2020. https://data.gsmaintelligence.com/research/research/research-2020/the-mobile-economy-2020</span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">Statista. Number of mHealth apps available in the Google Play Store from 1st quarter 2015 to 1st quarter 2021. Published 2021. Accessed October 6, 2021. https://www.statista.com/statistics/779919/health-apps-available-google-play-worldwide/</span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">Yardley L, Choudhury T, Patrick K, Michie S. Current Issues and Future Directions for Research Into Digital Behavior Change&nbsp; Interventions. <em>Am J Prev Med</em>. 2016;51(5):814-815. doi:10.1016/j.amepre.2016.07.019</span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">Grand View Research. mHealth Apps Market Growth &amp; Trends. Published 2021. Accessed October 10, 2021. https://www.grandviewresearch.com/press-release/global-mhealth-app-market</span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">Firstline. Spectrum Mobile Health Inc, dba Firstline Clinical. Published 2021. Accessed October 10, 2021. https://firstline.org/</span></span></span><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">Induction Healthcare. Induction Guidance App. Accessed October 5, 2021. https://inductionhealthcare.com/guidance/</span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">Luke SP Moore, Esmita Charani Enrique, Castro-Sanchez, Myriam Gharbi, Mark J Gilchrist AHH. Delivering antimicrobial stewardship through a bespoke mobile-health application_REAIM analysis of the 3 year impact. In: <em>European Congress of Clinical Microbiology and Infectious Diseases</em>. ; 2015. https://www.researchgate.net/publication/276267447_Delivering_antimicrobial_stewardship_through_a_bespoke_mobile-health_application_REAIM_analysis_of_the_3_year_impact</span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">&nbsp;Helou RI, Foudraine DE, Catho G, Peyravi Latif A, Verkaik NJ, Verbon A. Use of stewardship smartphone applications by physicians and prescribing of antimicrobials in hospitals: A systematic review. <em>PLoS One</em>. 2020;15(9 September):1-14. doi:10.1371/journal.pone.0239751</span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">Charani E, Gharbi M, Moore LSP, et al. Effect of adding a mobile health intervention to a multimodal antimicrobial stewardship programme across three teaching hospitals: An interrupted time series study. <em>J Antimicrob Chemother</em>. 2017;72(6):1825-1831. doi:10.1093/jac/dkx040</span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">Doyle D, McDonald G, Pratt C, et al. Impact of a mobile decision support tool on antimicrobial stewardship indicators in St. John&rsquo;s, Canada. <em>PLoS One</em>. 2021;16(6 June):1-10. doi:10.1371/journal.pone.0252407</span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">Shawki MA, AlSetohy WM, Ali KA, Ibrahim MR, El-Husseiny N, Sabry NA. Antimicrobial stewardship solutions with a smart innovative tool. <em>J Am Pharm Assoc</em>. Published online 2021:1-9. doi:10.1016/j.japh.2021.04.013</span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">Tuon FF, Gasparetto J, Wollmann LC, Moraes TP de. Mobile health application to assist doctors in antibiotic prescription &ndash; an approach for antibiotic stewardship. <em>Brazilian J Infect Dis</em>. 2017;21(6):660-664. doi:10.1016/j.bjid.2017.08.002</span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">Young HL, Shihadeh KC, Skinner AA, et al. Implementation of an institution-specific antimicrobial stewardship smartphone application. <em>Proc Int Astron Union</em>. 2018;39(8):986-988. doi:10.1017/ice.2018.135</span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">Markley JD, Pakyz A, Bernard S, et al. A survey to optimize the design of an antimicrobial stewardship smartphone app at an academic medical center. <em>Am J Infect Control</em>. 2017;45(3):317-320. doi:10.1016/j.ajic.2016.09.026</span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">Organization World Health (WHO). ATC index with DDDs.</span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">Europe. PCN. Classification for drug related problems. Accessed August 1, 2021. https://www.pcne.org/upload/files/230_PCNE_%0Aclassification_V8-02.pdf.</span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">&nbsp; J. S. Measuring usability with the System Susability Scale (SUS). Accessed August 5, 2021. https://measuringu.com/sus/</span></span></span></li>\r\n</ol>','To implement an antimicrobial stewardship mobile application and to evaluate its impact on antimicrobial utilization and patient clinical outcomes at JUMC, 2022/23',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,'Digital Health, Antimicrobial resistance,Stewardship,mHealth,Infectious disease',10,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'Proposal-627fbfa332228635846535.docx'),(91,115,14,19,'Background: Cancer patients are prone to serious infection complications with 10-fold relative risk compared to non-cancer patients which is results from a complex interplay between the hosts’ underlying immunodeficiency and the nature of treatment practices they experience, prophylaxis use, and application of invasive procedures with a significant morbidity and mortality worldwide. The increased risk of bacterial infections in the cancer patient is further compounded by the rising trends of antibiotic resistance in commonly implicated organisms all over the world.\r\nAim: This study is aimed to determine bacterial profile, antibiotic resistance pattern of isolates, treatment outcomes of infections and associated factors among cancer patients with infections admitted to Jimma Medical Center. \r\nMethods: Facility-based prospective longitudinal study will be conducted at Jimma Medical Center (JMC) from August 2022 to August 2023. Cancer Patients with sign and symptoms of infections who will visit JMC during the study period will be included. All specimens will be collected by laboratory technician and microbiological test will be performed following the standard procedures and laboratory guidelines. Data on socio-demographic, medical history and clinical data will be collected by trained oncology nurses and residents from patient medical record and by interviewing patients or caregivers. The data on clinical outcomes will be collected on daily basis during their stay at JMC and for 90-days post enrolment. Pretest will be done to ensure the validity of the questionnaires. The data will be entered into Epidata version 4.6. Then the data will be exported to SPSS version 23.0 for statistical analysis. Bivariate and multivariate logistic regression analyses will be conducted to identify factors associated with poor clinical outcomes of infections among cancer patients with infections. Variables with a p-value of < 0.05 will be considered statistically significant\r\nResults and Discussions: The findings of this study will have an important contribution to informing clinicians of local resistance patterns which enable them to tailor empiric antibiotic treatment of cancer patient infections. This study will further strengthen the national antimicrobial surveillance and reduce the emergence of resistant pathogens by promoting the prudent use of antibiotics.\r\nBudget: The total budget required for the present study is 582,603 ETB',NULL,'Bacterial Profile, Antibiotic Resistance Pattern of isolates, Treatment Outcomes of infections and associated factors among cancer Patients with infections admitted to Jimma Medical Centers.','2022-05-23 15:02:05','Objectives and outcomes\r\nGeneral objective:  This study is aimed at evaluating bacterial profile, Antibiotic Resistance Pattern, Treatment Outcomes and associated factors among cancer Patients with infections admitted to Jimma University Medical Centers, in Jimma town, Southwest Ethiopia\r\nSpecific objectives (sub research questions)	Expected outcomes (the impact or changes in the field that the project will have contributed to)\r\n1. To isolate and identify common bacterial pathogens among cancer patients with infections admitted to JMC, Southwest Ethiopia \r\n		if the clinician has adequate information on the spectrum of microorganisms which cause infection among cancer patients they select appropriate drug, which indirectly saves healthcare costs and preventing patients’ suffering\r\n	will help the patients to get the best available treatment option for infection resulting in better quality of life and adding active working man to the country’s economy\r\n2. To evaluate antibiotic susceptibility pattern of common bacterial isolates from cancer patients  with clinical suspected infections		Will enhance health professionals to choice alternative cost effective drugs to overcome patients suffering and reduce hospitalization costs \r\n	will reduce incidence of AMR and ultimately result better clinical outcomes\r\n	To reduce treatment failure leading to chronic problems, Adverse effects of treatments and relapse of the infection after treatment\r\n	will provide an outline for management strategies and will be influential in healthcare decision making\r\n	It also improve the quality of life of Cancer patients, reduce unnecessary hospitalization costs ,Saves patients suffering, healthcare costs, morbidity and mortality\r\n	Increases the government’s plan to health coverage for all by reducing AMR  associated economic loss though professionals job loss, morbidity and mortality \r\n3. To determine treatment outcome of infections among cancer patients at JMC patients	\r\n	Will determine treatment outcome of infections of cancer patients in terms of survival (mortality rate), degree of health or recovery, pain level achieved or extent of return to physical activity \r\n4.To identify factors associated with treatment outcome of infection in cancer patients at  Jimma medical center		Will identify factors associated with treatment outcome of infection in cancer patients.\r\n	Helps health policy makers to design strategies for the control and prevention of factors associated with treatment outcome of infection in cancer patients','44fedcf0182504396228013318374146','completed','1.	1.	Background and Rational\r\nCancer is a leading cause of death worldwide, with more than half of the cases occurring in developing nations(Jemal et al., 1999) . About 10% to 50% of patients with solid tumors and more than 80% of patients with hematological malignancies may develop febrile neutropenia after treatment(Vara et al., 2021). Cancer patients have a 10-fold greater risk of serious infection complications than non-cancer patients, which are related to prolonged hospital stays, higher healthcare costs, and significant morbidity and mortality(Prabhash et al., 2010). Infection risk in cancer patients is characterized by a combination interaction between both the host\'s underlying immunodeficiency and the nature of treatment they undergo (such as surgical intervention, radiation therapy, chemotherapy), prophylaxis use, and the use of invasive procedures (such as central venous catheter and urinary catheter) with a rising rate of morbidity and mortality worldwide which is a medical emergency that requires the use of broad-spectrum antibiotics (Zembower, 2014)(Freifeld et al., 2011).\r\nBacteria are the most common cause of infections in cancer patients worldwide (Zembower, 2014), contributing for 86.6 percent of infections in Ethiopian cancer patients (Arega et al., 2018). Previous studies in Ethiopia showed that 19.4 % to 86.6 % of cancer patients had positive bacteria cultures(Fentie et al., 2018) (Tigabu et al., 2020)(Arega et al., 2018). As a result, staphylococcus ssp. and coagulase negative staphylococci are the most frequently isolated gram-positive bacteria, while E. coli, Pseudomonas aeruginosa, Citrobacter spp., Enterobacter spp., and Klebsiella pneumonia are the most common gram-negative bacteria associated with infections in cancer patients (Fentie et al., 2018)(Tigabu et al., 2020)(Arega et al., 2018). The rising trends of antibiotic resistance in frequently implicated pathogens all over the globe combine the increased risk of bacterial infections in cancer patients, posing a major threat to patient treatment as it leads to serious complications. The rising trends of antibiotic resistance in commonly implicated organisms around the world compound the increased risk of bacterial infections in cancer patients, posing a major threat to patient treatment, as it leads to severe morbidity and high mortality rates, increased duration of hospital stay, and a significant financial burden on the patient and healthcare system (Arega et al., 2018)(Bhat et al., 2016)(Reta et al., 2019).\r\nThe majority of bacterial isolates from various clinical samples in Ethiopia are resistance to the most commonly used antibiotics (Saleem et al., 2019). Methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus (MRSA) is particularly resistant to antibiotics often used in Ethiopian healthcare, particularly oncology (Fentie et al., 2018)(Arega et al., 2018)(Saleem et al., 2019). Staphylococcus aureus is the most common cause of infection in Ethiopia and other countries, according to oncology specialists  (Fentie et al., 2018)(Tigabu et al., 2020)(Arega et al., 2018)(Fazeli et al., 2018)(Uso & Taher, 2021)(Perez et al., 2014)(Nazneen et al., 2016) (la Hoz & Cortés, 2020). Furthermore, the rising prevalence of antibiotic-resistant P. aeruginosa and extended beta-lactames-producing Enterobacteriaceae (E.coli and K. pneumonia) poses a significant challenge for oncologists in resource-limited settings such as Ethiopia (Tigabu et al., 2020),(Parikh & Bhat, 2015)(Islas-Muñoz et al., 2018)(Shrestha et al., 2021). Furthermore, high antibiotic use, frequent hospitalization, invasive procedures, broad-spectrum antibiotic use, prolonged hospital stays, and chemotherapy have been identified as major risk factors for infections in cancer patients, making them vulnerable to multi drug resistance (MDR) bacteria strains (Parikh & Bhat, 2015)(Islas-Muñoz et al., 2018). Despite the fact that rational antibiotic use has been shown to decrease AMR(Shrestha et al., 2021)(Islam et al., 2021), frequent and irrational antibiotic use has been linked to an increase in MDR bacteria in cancer patients (Tverdek et al., 2012)(Ariffin et al., 1997).\r\nFurthermore, major surgeries and cancer chemotherapies would be jeopardized without effective antibiotics for infection prevention and treatment, putting patients at even greater risk. Infections in cancer patients are frequently treated using established guidelines that take into account local microbiology and antibiotic sensitivity patterns of potential causative agents (Freifeld et al., 2011) (Arega et al., 2018). Because the spectrum and drug resistance profile of pathogens that cause infections in such patients have shown significant fluctuations in different geographical areas, time points, and even from one hospital to another(Ramphal, 2004) (Montassier et al., 2013) (EFMHACA, 2018), this could be made more evidence-based if the clinician has adequate information on the spectrum of microorganisms and antibiotic susceptibility patterns prevalent in that particular setting. Because of the rapid development and dynamic nature of antibiotic resistance, the WHO and Ethiopian Food and Drug Authorities (EFDA) recommend that antimicrobial resistance should be continuously monitored (Maillard et al., 2020)(Gerba, 2018)(Ibrahim et al., 2019)(Jorgensen & Ferraro, 2009). Despite this, practically all cancer patients with fever episodes in our study setting start empirical antibacterial treatments, and possible regimen changes are made depending on the patients\' clinical responses. Despite the growing burden of cancer, the burden of infection in cancer patients, their causative pathogens (Bacterial profiles), and antibiotic resistance trends remain scant in Ethiopia and Africa at large.. \r\nBecause cancer patients\' immune systems are impaired, infection can lead to other life-threatening disorders, prompt and precise detection of infection from urine and blood samples of cancer patients is critical to enhance their health.  As a result, knowing AMR patterns for infection is important for informing clinical practice and stewardship in the use of antibiotics in cancer patients.  Such evidence is essential for promoting the development of clinical guidelines, aiding clinicians in selecting effective empirical therapies, providing good epidemiological profiles to compare our situation to others, and making policy recommendations for expanding the AMR stewardship program in support of optimizing antibiotic use in Ethiopia(EFMHACA, 2018). To the best of our knowledge, the real burden, antimicrobial resistance pattern, treatment outcomes of infections, and associated risk factors of treatment outcomes of infections among cancer patients with infections remain unknown at Jimma Medical Center. As a result, the goal of this study is to determine the bacterial profile, antibiotic resistant, infection treatment outcomes, and risk factors in cancer patients with infections at Jimma Medical Center in southwest Ethiopia.\r\n.','3.	3.	METHODOLOGY\r\nStudy design, area and period\r\nFacility-based prospective longitudinal study will be conducted at Jimma Medical Center (JMC)  from August 2022 to August 2023. The hospital is located in Jimma town, which is 345 km from Addis Ababa, the capital city of Ethiopia. JMC is the only referral and tertiary care oncology center that provide services for about 15 million population catchment in southwestern Ethiopia. The oncology unit has an inpatient wards that provides inpatient services, radiation therapy, and oncology pharmacy. While there were averages of 428 out patients having follow up at oncologic unit in JMC for six months. Currently, oncologic unit of JMC run by three senior oncologist specialists, three general practitioners (GP), 6 oncology nurses (two MSc in oncology nursing and four BSc nurses) and medical physicist (from Health Management Information System (HMIS) of JMC in 2014 E.c). It has 14 beds.\r\nStudy population\r\nAll cancer patients who will visit JMC for infections during the study period will be considered as a source population. Patients with sign and symptoms of infections who will visit JMC during the study period will be included. Patients who received systemic or topical antibiotics 2 weeks before visit and patients/caregivers who refuse to participate in the study will be excluded from this study.\r\n        Sample size and sampling technique\r\nWe have calculated the sample size using a single population proportion formula n = z2 p (1-p)/ d2; where: n = the number of study participants; Z = Standard normal distribution = 1.96 at 95% confidence interval (CI), P = prevalence of culture confirmed infections among cancer patients that was previously done in Ethiopia at Gondar that was 19.4%(Fentie et al., 2018)); d = margin of error taken as 5%. The sample size calculated based on the above formula was 240 and adding 15% for non-response rate the final sample size was 276. All eligible study participants will be enrolled consecutively until the final sample size reach. \r\n Study outcomes and measurement tool\r\nThe outcomes of the present study are proportion of bacterial pathogens, antibiotic resistance pattern, clinical outcomes of infections and factors associated with treatment outcome of infections among cancer patients. Identifications of bacterial pathogens and their antibiotic susceptibility pattern will be performed according to the standard operation protocols (SOPs) and following the Clinical and Laboratory Standards Institute (CLSI) guideline . Clinical outcome of the infections among cancer patients will be assessed based on evidence-based recommendations (Freifeld et al., 2011), (Montassier et al., 2013). The patients will be followed from admission to 90-days to capture the full events such as cure/recovery, complications, reinfections, death.\r\n  Socio-demographic and clinical data \r\nThe data will be collected using structured questionnaire developed after reviewing previous literature. The data collection tool contains socio-demographic part, clinic data, microbiological findings, and outcome parts. Data on socio-demographic, medical history and clinical data will be collected by trained oncologic nurses and oncology residents from patient medical record and by interviewing patients or caregivers. The data on clinical outcomes will be collected on daily basis during their stay at JMC and weekly for one month after discharge. Based on their specific infections patients will be followed for 90-days post enrollment according to follow-up schedule decided by treating Oncologist. Patients unable to attend the follow-up will be contacted using phone call. All microbiological data will be collected following the standard procedures and laboratory guideline as described in detail below. \r\n  Specimen collection and handling\r\nBlood samples (5 mL) will be drawn from two different sites of peripheral vein aseptically (disinfecting with 70% alcohol and 2% tincture of iodine) by experienced laboratory technician prior to any antibiotic use. The collected blood samples will be then transferred into culture bottles of sterile tryptic soy broth (Oxoid Ltd., Basingstoke, UK) (Arega et al., 2018). Bottles will be incubated at 37°C for 7 days and observed for signs of bacterial growth (turbidity, hemolysis, clot formation) on a daily bases for up to 7 days. Urine specimens will be collected by a laboratory technologist by instructing the patients to collect approximately 10 ml to 15 ml midstream urine in clean, leak-proof sterile plastic cups.  For catheterized patients, the sample is taken by placing a thin rubber tube (called a catheter) through the urethra into the bladder.  First, the area around the urethra will be thoroughly washed with a germ-killing (antiseptic) solution. The tube is inserted into the urethra. The urine drains into a sterile container, and the catheter is removed. We will follow the standard operating procedure (SOP) for specimen collection, handling and transportation during the study period (la Hoz & Cortés, 2020).\r\nUrine culture and identification\r\n Each urine samples will inoculated onto a Cysteine-Lactose-Electrolyte Deficient agar (CLED) (Oxoid Ltd., England) by using a calibrated, sterile, nonreusable plastic loop 1 μl (0.001 ml) and incubated aerobically at 37°C for 18 to 24 hours to check the growth, and urine cultures were considered as significant bacteriuria when colony forming units (CFUs) were ≥105 /ml of voided urine, and a single colony will picked and suspended in nutrient broth and then sub-cultured onto blood agar plate and MacConkey agar plate, finally incubated at 37°C for 24 hours for further identification. Bacterial identification will then done using standardized biochemical tests, namely, indole production, Oxidase, H2S production, gas production, lactose fermentation, hydrolysis of urea, citrate utilization, lysine decarboxylation, and motility test for Gram-negative bacteria and for Gram positive bacteria, catalase and coagulase tests. \r\nBlood culture and identification \r\nGram staining will be performed from the blood culture broths that show visible growth. Evidence for growth will be consider based on the appearance of colonies growing on top of red cells, turbidity, gas bubbles, or hemolysis. In addition, blind subculturing will be done if a visible growth is not detected within 24 hours. Terminal subculturing will be done for bottles that did not show visible growth within 7 days. Based on clues from Gram-staining results, we sub-cultured organisms on chocolate agar, blood agar and MacConkey agar. We will incubate bacterial cultures aerobically at 37°C for up to 24–48 hours and the SDA for 2 weeks. Gram-positive bacteria were identified using coagulase and catalase tests. Enterobacteriaceae isolates and non-fermentative Gram-negative bacilli were identified using analytical profile index (API20E) and API-NE identification kits (BioMérieux, Lyon, France), respectively. \r\n       Antimicrobial susceptibility testing \r\nAntibiotic susceptibility testing of bacterial isolates will be done by Kirby Bauer disc diffusion method using Muller Hinton agar (MHA) plate (Oxoid Ltd.)(Jorgensen & Ferraro, 2009)(Clinical and Laboratory Standards Institute, 2012) (Stratton, 1984).Three-to-five pure colonies from a fresh non-selective agar plate will be selected and transferred to 5 mL of 0.85% sterile normal saline and thoroughly mixed to make the suspension homogenous. The turbidity will adjust visually with sterile normal saline to match with a 0.5 McFarland standard. Using a sterile cotton applicator stick, the suspension will be distributed uniformly over the entire surface of a MHA plate using a sterile swab. The inoculated plates will leave at room temperature to dry for 3–5 minutes. Using sterile forceps, the antibiotic discs will placed on MHA by considering 24 mm distance between each disk and 15 mm from the border. \r\nThe antibiotics discs will be tested include Ciproﬂoxacin (CIP,5µg), trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole (SXT,1.25/23.75µg), Nalidixic acid (NA,30 μg ), Meropenem (MEM, 10 μg ), cefotaxime (CTX,30µg), ceftazidime (CAZ,30µg), tetracycline (TTC,30 µg)  nitrofurantoin (F,30µg), norﬂoxacin (NOR,10µg), ceftriaxone (CRO,30µg), amoxicillin-clavulanic acid (AMC,20/10µg), ampicillin (AMP,10µg), gentamicin (CN,10µg), erythromycin (E,15µg), Penicillin (PEN,10µg), Clindamycin (CL,10µg), Cefoxitin (Fox,30 μg) and chloramphenicol (C, 30µg). Of these, Nalidixic acid (NA,), Meropenem (MEM, 30 μg ), cefotaxime (CTX,30µg), ceftazidime (CAZ,30µg), tetracycline (TTC; 30 µg)  can be used only for Gram-negative bacteria, whereas erythromycin (E,15µg), penicillin (PEN,10µg), and Clindamycin (CL,10µg) can be used only for Gram-positive bacteria; however, the rest of the antibiotics were used for both isolates. The criteria used to select the antimicrobial agents tested will be based on the CLSI recommendations and local frequent prescriptions for the management of infections in the study area in line with CLSI guide line.The inoculated plates will be incubated for 24–48 hours at 37°C. Isolates will classified as sensitive, intermediate and resistant based on diameters of zones of inhibition measured by a caliper. The standardized table supplied by the Clinical Laboratory Standard Institute was used for estimation of zone of inhibition(Clinical and Laboratory Standards Institute, 2012). \r\n        Quality control\r\nPretest will be done to ensure the validity of the survey tools. Training is going to be arranged for data collectors on the objective of the study, each item of the questionnaires, their responsibility, and obligations. During data collection the investigators will make tight supervision on whether the data collectors adhere to the research protocol or not and make immediate correction. The collected data will also be double checked for completeness. The reliability of the study findings will guaranteed by implementing quality control measures that include sterility and performance checks throughout the whole process of the laboratory work. We will use Staphylococcus aureus (ATCC 25923), Escherichia coli (ATCC 25922), and Pseudomonas aeruginosa (ATCC 27853) as reference strains for quality control of culture and antimicrobial susceptibility testing. \r\nData analysis\r\nData will be coded, cleaned compiled and entered in to Epi-data 4.6, and exported to the SPSS version 23.0 for analysis. A chi-square test will performed to check the adequacy of cells before performing binary logistic regression. Bivariate analysis will be conducted and variables having a p-value less than 0.25 will considered for multi-variable logistic regression analysis, variables with a p-value of < 0.05 will be considered as statistically significant.\r\n Ethical considerations. \r\nEthical clearance & approval will be obtained from the Institutional Review Board (IRB) of Institute of Health, Jimma University.  The letter will be communicated to concerned officials. Permissions to conduct the study will be obtained from Oncology department before initiating patient enrollment. Any modification to the study protocol will be communicated to the IRB for endorsement. In the process, the following detailed ethical issues will be considered. These includes, the voluntary nature of participation, obtaining written consent, after a necessary explanation about the purpose, benefit and risk of the study and also their right on decision of whether or not participating in the study. In the questionnaire ,Ensuring confidentiality of participants by using code instead of name of patients. During blood collection for culture the necessary precautions will be commenced for study subjects. Moreover, confidentiality of the data is going to be strictly maintained throughout the course of the study. The laboratory result will be given to the attending physician and treated accordingly. \r\nPlan for dissemination and ensuring utilization of findings \r\nThe research is the first to be conducted in Jimma  Medical Center. The finding of this study will be disseminated for all concerned bodies including JMC, Ethiopian Food and drug administration Authority (EFDA), Ministry of Health (MoH) to update on current antimicrobial resistance in cancer patients with infections. The results of this study will be presented on relevant scientific conferences and we will publish on reputable journal to share our findings for scientific communities.\r\nOperational definition and definition of terms \r\nFebrile neutropenia (FN): Febrile neutropaenia (FN) is defined as an oral temperature of\r\n>38.3°C or two consecutive readings of >38.0°C for 2 h and an absolute neutrophil count (ANC) of <0.5 × 109 /l, or expected to fall below 0.5 × 109 (Klastersky et al., 2016).\r\nSensitive (S): Sensitive bacterial isolates are inhibited by the usual achievable concentration of antimicrobial agent monitored (Maillard et al., 2020)(Gerba, 2018)(Ibrahim et al., 2019). \r\nIntermediate (I): bacterial isolates with antimicrobial agent minimum inhibitory concentrations (MICs) that approach usually attainable blood and tissue levels and for which response rates may be lower than for susceptible isolates monitored (Maillard et al., 2020)(Gerba, 2018)(Ibrahim et al., 2019). \r\nResistant (R): bacterial isolates are not inhibited by the usually achievable concentrations of the agent with normal dosage schedule monitored (Maillard et al., 2020)(Gerba, 2018)(Ibrahim et al., 2019).\r\nMultiple drug resistant (MDR): was defined as a bacterial isolate, which is resistant to one or more antibiotics in three or more classes of antimicrobials agents (Freifeld et al., 2011)\r\nTreatment outcome: all events that are occurred during hospital stay that includes death, improved and discharged, referred out, self-discharge and complications. \r\nPoor outcome: events which happen during hospital stay and include death, self-discharge and referred out.\r\nGood outcome: events which occurred during hospital stay, patients improved with vital signs and discharged.\r\nEastern Cooperative Group (ECOG) Performance Status Scale: describes a patient’s level of functioning in terms of their ability to care for themself, daily activity, and physical ability (walking, working, etc.)(Azam et al., 2019)\r\n       Table 1. ECOG Performance Status Scale\r\n\r\n       Table 1. ECOG Performance Status Scale\r\nGRADE	\r\n	ECOG PERFORMANCE STATUS\r\n0	Fully active, able to carry on all pre-disease performance without restriction\r\n1	Restricted in physically strenuous activity but ambulatory and able to carry out work of a light or sedentary nature, e.g., light house work, office work\r\n2	Ambulatory and capable of all self-care but unable to carry out any work activities; up and about more than 50% of waking hours\r\n3	Capable of only limited self-care; confined to bed or chair more than 50% of waking  hours\r\n\r\n4	Completely disabled; cannot carry on any self-  care; totally confined to bed or chair\r\n5      Dead','<ol>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Arega, B., Woldeamanuel, Y., Adane, K., Sherif, A. A., &amp; Asrat, D. (2018). Microbial spectrum and drug-resistance profile of isolates causing bloodstream infections in febrile cancer patients at a referral hospital in Addis Ababa, Ethiopia. <em>Infection and Drug Resistance</em>, <em>11</em>, 1511&ndash;1519. https://doi.org/10.2147/IDR.S168867</span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Ariffin, H., Ariffin, W., Peng, L. H., &amp; Parasakthi, N. (1997). Septicaemia in paediatric cancer patients: A 5-year surveillance study in University Hospital, Kuala Lumpur, Malaysia. <em>Journal of Tropical Pediatrics</em>, <em>43</em>(5), 279&ndash;281. https://doi.org/10.1093/tropej/43.5.279</span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Azam, F., Latif, M. F., Farooq, A., Tirmazy, S. H., Alshahrani, S., Bashir, S., &amp; Bukhari, N. (2019). Performance Status Assessment by Using ECOG (Eastern Cooperative Oncology Group) Score for Cancer Patients by Oncology Healthcare Professionals. <em>Case Reports in Oncology</em>, <em>12</em>(3), 728&ndash;736. https://doi.org/10.1159/000503095</span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Bhat, V., Gupta, S., Kelkar, R., Biswas, S., Khattry, N., Moiyadi, A., Bhat, P., Ambulkar, R., Chavan, P., Chiplunkar, S., Kotekar, A., &amp; Gupta, T. (2016). Bacteriological profile and antibiotic susceptibility patterns of clinical isolates in a tertiary care cancer center. <em>Indian Journal of Medical and Paediatric Oncology</em>, <em>37</em>(1), 20&ndash;24. https://doi.org/10.4103/0971-5851.177010</span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Clinical and Laboratory Standards Institute. (2012). <em>Performance standards for antimicrobial disk susceptibility tests: Approved standard - Eleventh edition</em> (Vol. 32, Issue 1). https://doi.org/M02-A11</span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">EFMHACA. (2018). <em>A Practical Guide to Antimicrobial Stewardship Program in Ethiopian Hospitals</em>.</span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Fazeli, H., Moghim, S., &amp; Zare, D. (2018). Antimicrobial Resistance Pattern and Spectrum of Multiple-drug-resistant Enterobacteriaceae in Iranian Hospitalized Patients with Cancer. <em>Advanced Biomedical Research</em>, <em>7</em>(1), 69. https://doi.org/10.4103/abr.abr_164_17</span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Fentie, A., Wondimeneh, Y., Balcha, A., Amsalu, A., &amp; Adankie, B. T. (2018). Bacterial profile, antibiotic resistance pattern and associated factors among cancer patients at university of Gondar Hospital, northwest Ethiopia. <em>Infection and Drug Resistance</em>, <em>11</em>, 2169&ndash;2178. https://doi.org/10.2147/IDR.S183283</span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Freifeld, A. G., Bow, E. J., Sepkowitz, K. A., Boeckh, M. J., Ito, J. I., Mullen, C. A., Raad, I. I., Rolston, K. V., Young, J. A. H., &amp; Wingard, J. R. (2011). Clinical practice guideline for the use of antimicrobial agents in neutropenic patients with cancer: 2010 Update by the Infectious Diseases Society of America. <em>Clinical Infectious Diseases</em>, <em>52</em>(4). https://doi.org/10.1093/cid/cir073</span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Gerba, H. (2018). Ethiopian Food, Medicine and Healthcare Administration and Control Authority. <em>Korean National Institute of Food and Drug Safety Evaluation</em>. http://www.nifds.go.kr/brd/m_95/down.do?brd_id=board_mfds_411&amp;seq=30934&amp;data_tp=A&amp;file_seq=4</span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Ibrahim, R. A., Teshale, A. M., Dinku, S. F., Abera, N. A., Negeri, A. A., Desta, F. G., Seyum, E. T., Gemeda, A. W., &amp; Keficho, W. M. (2019). Erratum: Antimicrobial resistance surveillance in Ethiopia: Implementation experiences and lessons learned (African Journal of Laboratory Medicine (2019) 8:1 DOI: 10.4102/ajlm.v8i1.1109). <em>African Journal of Laboratory Medicine</em>, <em>8</em>(1), 1&ndash;4. https://doi.org/10.4102/ajlm.v7i2.770</span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Islam, &nbsp;Md. Rafiqul, Akhter, F., Khatun, N., &amp; Rahman, M. (2021). Bacterial Causes of Infections and Their Antimicrobial Sensitivity Pattern in Cancer Patients. <em>EAS Journal of Radiology and Imaging Technology</em>, <em>3</em>(2), 29&ndash;35. https://doi.org/10.36349/easjrit.2021.v03i02.001</span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Islas-Mu&ntilde;oz, B., Volkow-Fern&aacute;ndez, P., Ibanes-Guti&eacute;rrez, C., Villamar-Ram&iacute;rez, A., Vilar-Compte, D., &amp; Cornejo-Ju&aacute;rez, P. (2018). Bloodstream infections in cancer patients. Risk factors associated with mortality. <em>International Journal of Infectious Diseases</em>, <em>71</em>, 59&ndash;64. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ijid.2018.03.022</span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Jemal, A., Bray, F., &amp; Ferlay, J. (1999). Global Cancer Statistics: 2011. <em>CA Cancer J Clin</em>, <em>49</em>(2), 1,33-64. https://doi.org/10.3322/caac.20107.Available</span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Jorgensen, J. H., &amp; Ferraro, M. J. (2009). Antimicrobial susceptibility testing: A review of general principles and contemporary practices. <em>Clinical Infectious Diseases</em>, <em>49</em>(11), 1749&ndash;1755. https://doi.org/10.1086/647952</span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Klastersky, J., de Naurois, J., Rolston, K., Rapoport, B., Maschmeyer, G., Aapro, M., Herrstedt, J., &amp; on behalf of the ESMO Guidelines Committee. (2016). Management of febrile neutropaenia: ESMO clinical practice guidelines. <em>Annals of Oncology</em>, <em>27</em>(Supplement 5), v111&ndash;v118. https://doi.org/10.1093/annonc/mdw325</span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">la Hoz, A., &amp; Cort&eacute;s, J. A. (2020). Bacterial and Atypical Infections in Critically Ill Cancer Patients. In J. L. Nates &amp; K. J. Price (Eds.), <em>Oncologic Critical Care</em> (pp. 1379&ndash;1400). Springer International Publishing. https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-319-74588-6_123</span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Maillard, J. Y., Bloomfield, S. F., Courvalin, P., Essack, S. Y., Gandra, S., Gerba, C. P., Rubino, J. R., &amp; Scott, E. A. (2020). Reducing antibiotic prescribing and addressing the global problem of antibiotic resistance by targeted hygiene in the home and everyday life settings: A position paper. <em>American Journal of Infection Control</em>, <em>48</em>(9), 1090&ndash;1099. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ajic.2020.04.011</span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Montassier, E., Batard, E., Gastinne, T., Potel, G., &amp; De La Cocheti&egrave;re, M. F. (2013). Recent changes in bacteremia in patients with cancer: A systematic review of epidemiology and antibiotic resistance. <em>European Journal of Clinical Microbiology and Infectious Diseases</em>, <em>32</em>(7), 841&ndash;850. https://doi.org/10.1007/s10096-013-1819-7</span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Nazneen, S., Mukta, K., Santosh, C., &amp; Borde, A. (2016). Bacteriological trends and antibiotic susceptibility patterns of clinical isolates at Government Cancer Hospital, Marathwada. <em>Indian Journal of Cancer</em>, <em>53</em>(4), 583&ndash;586. https://doi.org/10.4103/ijc.IJC_34_17</span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Parikh, P., &amp; Bhat, V. (2015). Urinary tract infection in cancer patients in a tertiary cancer setting in India: microbial spectrum and antibiotic susceptibility pattern. <em>Antimicrobial Resistance and Infection Control</em>, <em>4</em>(S1), 2015. https://doi.org/10.1186/2047-2994-4-s1-p221</span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Perez, F., Adachi, J., &amp; Bonomo, R. A. (2014). Antibiotic-resistant gram-negative bacterial infections in patients with cancer. <em>Clinical Infectious Diseases</em>, <em>59</em>(Suppl 5), S335&ndash;S339. https://doi.org/10.1093/cid/ciu612</span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Prabhash, K., Medhekar, A., Ghadyalpatil, N., Noronha, V., Biswas, S., Kurkure, P., Nair, R., &amp; Kelkar, R. (2010). Blood stream infections in cancer patients: A single center experience of isolates and sensitivity pattern. <em>Indian Journal of Cancer</em>, <em>47</em>(2), 184&ndash;188. https://doi.org/10.4103/0019-509X.63019</span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Ramphal, R. (2004). Changes in the etiology of bacteremia in febrile neutropenic patients and the susceptibilities of the currently isolated pathogens. <em>Clinical Infectious Diseases</em>, <em>39</em>(SUPPL. 1), 25&ndash;31. https://doi.org/10.1086/383048</span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Reta, A., Bitew Kifilie, A., &amp; Mengist, A. (2019). Bacterial Infections and Their Antibiotic Resistance Pattern in Ethiopia: A Systematic Review. <em>Advances in Preventive Medicine</em>, <em>2019</em>(Cdc), 1&ndash;10. https://doi.org/10.1155/2019/4380309</span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Saleem, Z., Hassali, M. A., Hashmi, F. K., Godman, B., &amp; Bhutta, O. A. (2019). A repeated point prevalence survey of antimicrobial use in specialized cancer care hospital of Pakistan: findings and implications. <em>Hospital Practice (1995)</em>, <em>47</em>(3), 149&ndash;154. https://doi.org/10.1080/21548331.2019.1628614</span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Shrestha, G., Wei, X., Hann, K., Soe, K. T., Satyanarayana, S., Siwakoti, B., Bastakoti, S., Mulmi, R., Rana, K., &amp; Lamichhane, N. (2021). Bacterial profile and antibiotic resistance among cancer patients with urinary tract infection in a national tertiary cancer hospital of Nepal. <em>Tropical Medicine and Infectious Disease</em>, <em>6</em>(2), 1&ndash;10. https://doi.org/10.3390/tropicalmed6020049</span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Stratton, C. W. (1984). Susceptibility testing revisited. <em>Progress in Clinical Pathology</em>, <em>9</em>, 65&ndash;100.</span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Tigabu, A., Ferede, W., Belay, G., &amp; Gelaw, B. (2020). Prevalence of Asymptomatic Bacteriuria and Antibiotic Susceptibility Patterns of Bacterial Isolates among Cancer Patients and Healthy Blood Donors at the University of Gondar Specialized Hospital. <em>International Journal of Microbiology</em>, <em>2020</em>. https://doi.org/10.1155/2020/3091564</span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Tverdek, F. P., Rolston, K. V., &amp; Chemaly, R. F. (2012). Antimicrobial stewardship in patients with cancer. <em>Pharmacotherapy</em>, <em>32</em>(8), 722&ndash;734. https://doi.org/10.1002/j.1875-9114.2012.01162.x</span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Uso, R. S., &amp; Taher, C. A. (2021). <em>RESEARCH PAPER BACTERIAL PROFILE , ANTIBIOTIC RESISTANCE PATTERNS , AND ASSOCIATED FACTORS AMONG HEMATOLOGICAL MALIGNANT</em>.</span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Vara, A., Prathyusha, Y. H., &amp; Suguneswari, G. (2021). Bacteriological profile of clinical isolates in cancer patients with febrile neutropenia in a tertiary care hospital in Kurnool, Andhra Pradesh. <em>Indian Journal of Microbiology Research</em>, <em>8</em>(2), 139&ndash;141. https://doi.org/10.18231/j.ijmr.2021.028</span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Zembower, T. R. (2014). Epidemiology of infections in cancer patients. In <em>Cancer Treatment and Research</em> (Vol. 161). https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-319-04220-6_2</span></span></span></span></li>\r\n</ol>','General objective for this project is aimed to determine bacterial profile, Antibiotic Resistance Pattern of isolates, Treatment Outcomes of infections and associated factors among cancer Patients with infections admitted to Jimma Medical Centers, in Jimma town, Southwest Ethiopia',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,'Key words: Cancer patients, bacterial profile, antibiotic resistance pattern, Clinical outcomes, Multidrug resistance, risk factors, Jimma University, Ethiopia.',10,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'Proposal-628b77bd2b23e429279141.docx'),(92,159,13,17,'Background: Disseminated intravascular coagulation (DIC) is life-threatening disease characterized by activation of uncontrolled and disseminated coagulation. About one-third to half of sepsis patients admitted to intensive care unit are reported to have DIC and it is also part of sepsis pathogenesis. However, data regarding the magnitude of DIC in sepsis patients in developing countries is limited. This is mainly due to the diagnostic challenges related to DIC. Though various diagnostic scores are developed by different organizations, its applicability is hampered by the issue of accessibility to common settings in developing countries. Cognizant of this, the international society on thrombosis and hemostasis developed a new and simpler criteria to diagnose sepsis-induced coagulopathy. However, this criteria is not yet evaluated in low-resource countries.\r\nObjective: The aim of this study is to determine the magnitude, and outcomes of disseminated intravascular coagulation and to validate sepsis-induced coagulopathy criteria in adult sepsis patients at Jimma Medical Center intensive care unit from september1, 2022 to August 30, 2023.\r\nMethods: a prospective longitudinal study will be conducted by consecutively involving 131 adult sepsis patients in JMC intensive care unit. A structured checklist and questionnaire will be used to collect background information and clinical data of the patients. Blood samples will be collected at day 1, day 3, and day 7 of admission using citrated (3 ml), EDTA (4 ml), and serum separator tube (SST) (3 ml) for coagulation analysis (prothrombin time, fibrinogen, D-dimer), complete blood count (platelet count), and chemistry analysis (bilirubin, creatinine, and lactate), respectively. Sepsis and septic shock diagnosis will be made based on the sepsis-3 definition. Data will be analyzed using SPSS version 25. Differences between the groups will be compared with the t-test. Multivariable stepwise logistic regression analysis will be used to explore predictors of ICU mortality in patients with DIC. Survival analysis will be performed using Cox proportional hazard regression analysis. Receiver operating characteristic (ROC) will be used to see the diagnostic and prognostic ability of the SIC diagnostic criteria. \r\nTimeline: The study will be conducted from september1, 2022 to August 30, 2023.\r\nBudget: A total of 1,433,372 ETB will be needed to undertake this research work',NULL,'Disseminated Intravascular Coagulation and Validation of Sepsis-Induced Coagulopathy Criteria in Adult Sepsis Patients at Jimma Medical Center Intensive Care Unit','2022-05-28 15:35:26','The complexity of sepsis relies on consequent organ failure through various mechanisms including coagulopathy. DIC is the severe form of coagulopathy that exacerbates the outcome of sepsis patients. However, the magnitude and possible outcomes of this condition with respect to sepsis are not well studied. This is mainly due to the diagnostic complexity it bears. The diagnostic criteria proposed by ISTH so far are not being used in developing countries like Ethiopia due to its resource intensiveness. Finding simpler and easily applicable diagnostic tools is highly warranted to alleviate the existing challenges. The SIC criteria is simple, easily accessible and sepsis-oriented tool to diagnose coagulopathy in sepsis. Validation of this criteria in resource limited countries like Ethiopia will be valuable to inform policy makers so as to integrate the tool in routine clinical practice.','45aba9da60b7ebf07fcc4e7cf9874bcb','completed','Disseminated intravascular coagulation (DIC) is a life-threatening disease characterized by activation of uncontrolled and disseminated coagulation. According to the international society on thrombosis and hemostasis (ISTH) scientific subcommittee, DIC is defined as an acquired syndrome characterized by the intravascular activation of coagulation with loss of localization arising from different causes. It can originate from and cause damage to the microvasculature, which if sufficiently severe, can produce organ dysfunction (1). About one-third to half of sepsis patients admitted to intensive care unit (ICU) are reported to have DIC (2)(3). Sepsis is the major cause of morbidity and mortality in ICU whereby DIC is part of the pathogenesis (4)(5).\r\nActivation of coagulation in sepsis is meant to protect host against infection, that might turn out to be detrimental to the host in cases of over-activation. A crosstalk between inflammation and coagulation is believed to be a crucial point in the pathogenesis of sepsis whereby inflammatory cytokines such as IL-6 induce activation of coagulation, whereas protease activated receptors (PARs) are activated by coagulation proteases to initiate signaling and subsequent cytokine production (6). \r\n\r\nTissue factor is also the key initiator of thrombin formation in sepsis (7). There is an increased expression of tissue factor on monocyte during severe gram-negative sepsis (8). Tissue factor expressed on extracellular vesicles following cellular injury also further fosters the coagulation process (9). In addition to activation of coagulation, suppression of fibrinolysis is another crucial point in sepsis pathogenesis (10). Activation of platelets, a cell recognized forefront in hemostasis, is also an important feature in sepsis. Thrombocytopenia is one of important findings in DIC in addition to coagulation abnormalities (10).\r\nData regarding the magnitude of DIC in sepsis patients in developing countries is scarce. This is mainly due to the diagnostic challenges related to DIC. Varied diagnostic criteria of DIC have been developed by nationally and internationally affiliated organizations. \r\nThe first diagnostic criteria was proposed by the Japanese Ministry of Health and Welfare (JMHW) in 1976 which involves global coagulation tests, the underlying diseases, and clinical symptoms (11). Overt and non-overt DIC criteria was then established by ISTH in 2001 comprising D-dimer, fibrinogen assay, platelet count, and prothrombin time (PT) ratio (12). The Japanese association for acute medicine (JAAM) has also developed diagnostic criteria for DIC in 2006 by including systemic inflammatory response syndrome (SIRS) and eliminating the fibrinogen level in contrast to the ISTH overt-DIC criteria (13).\r\nOvert-DIC criteria established by ISTH have been adopted as the gold standard for the diagnosis of DIC (14). However, its application best fits to patients who are at advanced (possibly irreversible) stage (15); and the scoring system comprises laboratory parameters which are not readily accessible in common settings such as fibrinogen/fibrin degradation products, D-dimer and fibrinogen assays (Table 1). Moreover, the SIRS category that makes up the JAAM DIC criteria is no longer used in sepsis-3 definition, necessitating other criteria which best fits with the new definition.\r\nCognizant of these challenges, the ISTH produced new diagnostic criteria for sepsis-induced coagulopathy (SIC) in 2017, which is based on platelet count, prothrombin time ratio and sequential organ failure assessment (SOFA) score (16). The SOFA score is computed from dysfunction of the respiratory, cardiovascular, hepatic and renal systems whereby a score of 2 or more within each of these systems was defined as organ dysfunction (17). \r\nPrevious studies have examined the diagnostic and prognostic utility of the SIC score. A study involving 332 sepsis patients showed the comparability of the SIC score with ISTH overt-DIC score and at times there was a difference, the SIC score preceded the ISTH overt-DIC score (18). Significantly higher mortality rate was also reported for ISTH overt-DIC (32.5%) as compared to SIC (23.1%).\r\nAnother validation study compared the JAAM DIC criteria with SIC. The diagnostic performance of SIC was found to be comparable with that of the JAAM DIC. Similar performance was also reported with respect to predicting 28-day mortality (19). The SIC criteria gain preference as compared to JAAM DIC due to its simplicity.\r\nHowever, the performance of SIC criteria in resource limited countries is not yet evaluated. Validation of the SIC criteria in developing countries like Ethiopia where there is no DIC diagnostic criteria in use apparently, is valuable mainly due to its ease of applicability and its comparable performance with previous criteria.','3.1.	Study area and period\r\nThe study will be conducted at Jimma Medical Center (JMC). JMC is one of the oldest hospitals in Ethiopia and it is the only teaching and referral hospital in southwest Ethiopia with 800 bed capacity and a catchment population of over 20 million people. JMC has a total of 12 beds in the medical and surgical ICUs, with an estimated annual admission of 350-400 patients. One-third to half of ICU admitted patients are estimated to have sepsis or septic shock. Sepsis is the 2nd common cause of admission and septic shock is the 1st common cause of death in JMC ICUs, according to a 2020 report from the unit. The study will be conducted from September 1, 2022 to August 30, 2023.\r\n3.2.	Study Design\r\nA prospective longitudinal study design will be conducted. The prevalence of DIC among ICU admitted sepsis adults will be determined and the same patients will also be followed for possible outcomes. Patients with DIC will be followed with repeated phlebotomy to see the diagnostic and prognostic value of DIC diagnostic criteria.\r\n3.3.	Study Population\r\nThose patients who will be diagnosed to have sepsis and septic shock, according to Sepsis-3 definition (SOFA score of 2 or more) (20), will be involved in the study to determine the prevalence and outcomes of DIC. The prevalence of DIC will be determined based on the ISTH DIC score, JAAM DIC score, and SIC score; and these patients will be followed to see the prognostic value of each score.\r\n3.4.	Eligibility criteria\r\n3.4.1.	Inclusion criteria:\r\nAll sepsis patients aged 18 years or older who are admitted to ICU and stayed for at least 24 h will be included. \r\n3.4.2.	Exclusion criteria: \r\nPatients who are on anticoagulant therapy before participation, and have history of fulminant hepatitis, decompensated liver cirrhosis, serious liver disorders, hematological malignancy, and treatment on chemotherapy will be excluded from a study. \r\n\r\n3.5.	Sample size estimation and sampling procedure\r\nSample size is computed based on single population proportion sample size calculation using the following formula:\r\nn= (z α/2)2 (p) (1-p)/d2\r\nWhereby, \r\n            n=sample size \r\nP= 50% as the prevalence of DIC is not known in Ethiopia \r\n            α =critical value at 95% confidence interval of certainty (1.96) \r\n            d=margin of sampling error to be tolerated which is 5% \r\nThe calculated sample size will be 384. Since the annual total number of sepsis cases at JMC ICUs is estimated to be 200, the final corrected sample size is 131. Consecutive sampling technique will be carried out to select 131 adult sepsis patients admitted to ICU to determine the prevalence and outcomes of DIC. To obtain 131 sepsis individuals by making use of SOFA score as a diagnostic tool, about 350 ICU adult patients have to be studied as the hospital is not currently using SOFA score as a routine diagnostic tool.\r\n3.6.	Variables\r\n3.6.1.	Dependent Variables\r\nDIC\r\nOutcomes (Discharge, Death)\r\n3.6.2.	Independent variables\r\nSocio-demographic characteristics: Age, Sex, Educational status, Employment status, marital status, residence, monthly income, family size\r\nClinical characteristics: Etiologic agents, source of infection, comorbidities, medication, medical procedures (use of mechanical ventilator, catheter, central line use), time of antibiotic administration, length of stay (LOS) in ICU, LOS in hospital\r\n3.7.	Data collection tools\r\nA structured checklist and questionnaire will be used to collect background information and clinical data of the patients.\r\n3.8.	Specimen collection, processing, and analysis\r\nBlood samples will be collected at day 1, day 3, and day 7 of admission using citrated (3 ml), EDTA (4 ml), and serum separator tube (SST) (3 ml) for coagulation analysis (prothrombin time, fibrinogen, D-dimer), complete blood count (platelet count), and chemistry analysis (bilirubin, creatinine, and lactate), respectively. Blood samples at day 2, day 4, and day 8 are also particularly important to see platelet dynamics within 24 hrs. to compute the JAAM criteria. Arterial blood samples (1 ml) for arterial blood gas analysis (ABG) will also be obtained. A glascow coma score (GCS) will be obtained by the physician.\r\n3.8.1.	Coagulation tests\r\nAfter transporting the sample to hematology laboratory, Platelet poor plasma (PPP) will be prepared from citrated tube sample to analyze PT/INR and fibrinogen levels using a routine coagulation analyzer in the study area. The D-dimer will also be analyzed using a latex-based immunoassay analyzer.\r\n3.8.2.	Complete blood count\r\nPlatelet count will be determined as part of the CBC using impedance and light scatter-based hematology analyzer from EDTA anticoagulated blood sample. \r\n3.8.3.	Organ function tests\r\nNon-anticoagulated blood sample from SST will be used to analyze creatinine, bilirubin, and lactate using an automated clinical chemistry analyzer.\r\n3.9.	Data management and statistical analysis\r\nData will be checked for its completeness and entered Epidata version 3.1 (Epidata Association, Odense Denmark) and exported to Statistical Package for Social Sciences (SPSS)version 25 software (IBM Corporation, USA) for analysis. Continuous variables will be presented as the means ± standard deviations (SDs) or medians [interquartile ranges (IQRs)], and categorical variables will be presented as percentages. Differences between the groups will be compared with the t-test, one-way analysis of variance or Wilcoxon rank-sum test for continuous variables and the chi-squared or Fisher’s test for categorical variables. Multivariable stepwise logistic regression analysis will be used to explore predictors of ICU mortality in patients with DIC. Survival analysis will be performed using Cox proportional hazard regression analysis. The correlation of DIC criteria with disease severity scores such as Acute Physiology and Chronic Health Evaluation (APACHE) II score and the SOFA score will be tested by Pearson’s correlation coefficients. Receiver operating characteristic (ROC) will be used to see the diagnostic and prognostic ability of the SIC diagnostic criteria. \r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n3.10.	Quality Assurance\r\nTraining will be given to the data collectors by the principal investigator before the actual data collection started. Manufacturer instructions and standard operating procedures will be strictly followed during specimen collection and all other laboratory procedures.\r\n3.11.	Plan for dissemination\r\nThe finding of this study will be submitted to the respective schools of Jimma University and JMC. The findings of the study will also be published in peer-reviewed scientific journals. It will also be presented on different scientific conferences in Ethiopia and internationally.','<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><strong><span style=\"font-size:14.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">References</span></span></strong></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">1. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Sakr Y, Jaschinski U, Wittebole X, Szakmany T, Lipman J, &Ntilde;amendys-Silva SA, et al. Sepsis in Intensive Care Unit Patients: Worldwide Data From the Intensive Care over Nations Audit. Open Forum Infect Dis [Internet]. 2018 Dec 1 [cited 2021 Sep 12];5(12).&nbsp;</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">2. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Iba T, Levy JH, Raj A, Warkentin TE. Advance in the Management of Sepsis-Induced Coagulopathy and Disseminated Intravascular Coagulation. J Clin Med [Internet]. 2019 May 22 [cited 2021 Jun 19];8(5):728.</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">3. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Wada H, Trachil J, Di Nisio M&nbsp; et al. The Scientific Standardisa-tion Committee on DIC of the International Society on Throm-bosis Haemostasis. Guidance for diagnosis and treatment in DICfrom harmonization of the recommendations from three guide-lines. J Thromb Haemost. 2013;11(11):761&ndash;7. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">4. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Saito S, Uchino S, Hayakawa M, Yamakawa K, Kudo D, Iizuka Y, et al. Epidemiology of disseminated intravascular coagulation in sepsis and validation of scoring systems. J Crit Care. 2019 Apr 1;50:23&ndash;30. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">5. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Gando S, Shiraishi A, Yamakawa K, Ogura H, Saitoh D, Fujishima S, et al. Role of disseminated intravascular coagulation in severe sepsis. Thromb Res. 2019 Jun 1;178:182&ndash;8. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">6. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Levi M, van der Poll T. Coagulation and sepsis. Vol. 149, Thrombosis Research. Elsevier Ltd; 2017. p. 38&ndash;44. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">7. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; F.B. Taylor Jr., A. Chang, W. Ruf, J.H. Morrissey, L. Hinshaw, R. Catlett&nbsp; et al. Lethal E. coli septic shock is prevented by blocking tissue factor with monoclonal antibody. Circ Shock. 1991;33(3):127&ndash;34. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">8. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; B. Osterud TF. Increased tissue thromboplastin activity in monocytes of patients with meningococcal infection: related to an unfavourable prognosis, . Thromb Haemost . 1983;49(1):5&ndash;7. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">9. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; T I, H O. Role of extracellular vesicles in the development of sepsis-induced coagulopathy. J intensive care [Internet]. 2018 Oct 19 [cited 2021 Oct 7];6(1).</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">10. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Iba T, Levy JH, Raj A, Warkentin TE. Advance in the Management of Sepsis-Induced Coagulopathy and Disseminated Intravascular Coagulation. J Clin Med [Internet]. 2019 May 22 [cited 2021 Jun 18];8(5):728.</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">11. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Taylor J, Toh CH, Hoots WK, Wada H, Levi M. Towards definition, clinical and laboratory criteria, and a scoring system for disseminated intravascular coagulation: On behalf of the scientific subcommittee on Disseminated Intravascular Coagulation (DIC) of the International Society on Thrombosis and . Vol. 86, Thrombosis and Haemostasis. Schattauer GmbH; 2001. p. 1327&ndash;30. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">12. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Gando S, Saitoh D, Ogura H, Mayumi T, Koseki K, Ikeda T, et al. Natural history of disseminated intravascular coagulation diagnosed based on the newly established diagnostic criteria for critically ill patients: Results of a multicenter, prospective survey. Crit Care Med. 2008;36(1):145&ndash;50. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">13. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Yamakawa K, Umemura Y, Murao S, Hayakawa M, Fujimi S. Optimal Timing and Early Intervention With Anticoagulant Therapy for Sepsis-Induced Disseminated Intravascular Coagulation. Clin Appl Thromb [Internet]. 2019 Mar 5 [cited 2021 Jun 19];25.&nbsp;</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">14. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Gando S, Saitoh D, Ogura H&nbsp; et al. Japanese Association for Acute Medicine Disseminated Intravascular Coagulation (JAAM DIC) Study Group, Japanese Association for Acute Medicine Disseminated Intravascular Coagulation (JAAM DIC) Study Group. Natural history of disseminated intravascular coa. Crit Care Med. 2008;36(1):145&ndash;50. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">15. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; S G, D S, H O, T M, K K, T I, et al. Disseminated intravascular coagulation (DIC) diagnosed based on the Japanese Association for Acute Medicine criteria is a dependent continuum to overt DIC in patients with sepsis. Thromb Res [Internet]. 2009 Mar [cited 2021 Sep 24];123(5):715&ndash;8.&nbsp;</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">16. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Iba T, Di Nisio M, Levy JH, Kitamura N, Thachil J. New criteria for sepsis-induced coagulopathy (SIC) following the revised sepsis definition: A retrospective analysis of a nationwide survey [Internet]. Vol. 7, BMJ Open. BMJ Publishing Group; 2017 [cited 2021 Jun 19].&nbsp;</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">17. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; JL V, A&nbsp; de M, F C, R M, J T, PM S, et al. Use of the SOFA score to assess the incidence of organ dysfunction/failure in intensive care units: results of a multicenter, prospective study. Working group on &ldquo;sepsis-related problems&rdquo; of the European Society of Intensive Care Medicine. Crit Care Med [Internet]. 1998 [cited 2021 Sep 24];26(11):1793&ndash;800.&nbsp;</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">18. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; T I, M A, M DN, S G, H A, K S, et al. Newly Proposed Sepsis-Induced Coagulopathy Precedes International Society on Thrombosis and Haemostasis Overt-Disseminated Intravascular Coagulation and Predicts High Mortality. J Intensive Care Med [Internet]. 2020 Jul 1 [cited 2021 Sep 19];35(7):643&ndash;9.&nbsp;</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">19. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Iba T, Arakawa M, Levy JH, Yamakawa K, Koami H, Hifumi T, et al. Sepsis-Induced Coagulopathy and Japanese Association for Acute Medicine DIC in Coagulopathic Patients with Decreased Antithrombin and Treated by Antithrombin. Clin Appl Thromb. 2018;24(7):1020&ndash;6. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">20. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Singer M, Deutschman CS, Seymour CW, Shankar-Hari M, Annane D, Bauer M, et al. The Third International Consensus Definitions for Sepsis and Septic Shock (Sepsis-3). Acta Med Okayama [Internet]. 2012 Feb 23 [cited 2021 Aug 3];66(4):299&ndash;305.</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">21. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Vincent J-L, Moreno R, Takala J, Willatts S, De Mendon&ccedil;a A, Bruining H, et al. The SOFA (Sepsis-related Organ Failure Assessment) score to describe organ dysfunction/failure. Intensive Care Med 1996 227 [Internet]. 96AD [cited 2021 Sep 18];22(7):707&ndash;10.&nbsp;</span></span></span></span></p>','The aim of this study is to determine the prevalence, and outcome of disseminated intravascular coagulation and to validate sepsis-induced coagulopathy criteria in adult sepsis patients at Jimma Medical Center intensive care unit from September 1, 2022 to August 30, 2023.',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,'Sepsis,Sepsis-Induced Coagulopathy ,Disseminated intravascular coagulation,validation',10,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'Proposal-629214dc30f9c862866375.docx'),(93,349,13,20,'Evidence based practice during intra-partum care is very important in reducing maternal and new born mortality and morbidity. According to the WHO Quality of Care Framework for maternal and newborn health, evidence-based practice is a key quality of care component. So the objective of this study was to assess evidence based intra-partum practice and associated factors among obstetric care providers in Jimma zone public hospitals, south west Ethiopia. Facility based mixed study design (observational and qualitative study) will be conducted among obstetric care providers in Jimma zone public hospitals, south west Ethiopia. For qualitative study, purposive sampling technique will be used and sample size will be based on the saturation of the information to be reached. Data will be collected using an observational checklist and an interviewer guide. Bi-variable and multivariable logistic regression models will be used to identify factors associated with the outcome variables. Thematic analysis assisted by Atlas. ti 8 will be used for qualitative data. The finding of the study will be presented using texts, tables and figures',NULL,'Evidence Based Intra-partum Care practice, common obstetrical care mal practice and associated factors among obstetric care providers in public hospitals of Jimma zone','2022-05-16 10:20:37','It provides information which could be used as a base to design tailored interventions.  \r\nKnowledge gaps will be identified to design interventions. \r\nBarriers of evidence-based intrapartum care will be identified in order to put a best available recommendation \r\nEvidence will be generated to improve the accessibility of resources to implement evidence-based practice during intrapartum care.','a0b22fa19cffb265f7dc2572d3027755','completed','Globally, around 300,000 women die every year during child birth. In countries with a good health care facility, modern technology and evidence based health care practice, the chances of dying during pregnancy, in childbirth, or in the days and weeks after delivery are very low. However, in developing countries maternal death during pregnancy and child birth remained as a major health problem issue. Even though, most global maternal and newborn deaths are avoidable with quality health care provision, a large proportion of all maternal deaths take place in hospitals(1).\r\nQuality of care is a focus area for improvement to reduce avoidable mortality and morbidity among mothers and new-born babies. According to the WHO Quality of Care Framework for maternal and newborn health, evidence-based practice is a .key quality of care component(2). Two entities underpin the implementation of evidence-based care; firstly, is the provision of ‘recommended’ practices, which have evidence of effectiveness and generally facilitate the physiological process of birth. The second is the avoidance of ‘not recommended’ practices, which are often invasive medical interventions, and have proved ineffective or harmful if provided in a routine manner(3). \r\nHowever, studies have identified gaps between recommended and actual practices. Essential routine monitoring and assessment during labor as well as key practices are not sufficiently conducted, compounded by inappropriate infrastructures and supplies. Mistreatment and abusive actions, including unnecessary interventions, are also common in health facility deliveries. A cross-sectional study from Sweden reveals a 22.7% proportion of using evidence in intra-partum care, while 78% of women had received care concordant with evidence in Iranian social security hospitals (4-6).Moreover, another institution based study in California, United States of America has also revealed that received evidence based intra-partum care(7).\r\nA multi-institutional Californian study reveals age, profession, and qualification, and current position of care providers, including sex, age, qualification, employment status were  associated with evidence-based practice(7).Several studies also shown that age, sex, profession, qualification, experience, access to internet, workshop, conferences, searching for the Cochrane database had significant statistical  association  with evidence-based intra-partum practice (7-10).\r\n\r\nTherefore, to counteract maternal and newborn death, evidence based intrapartum-care is the likely tactic given principally based on timely and competent care which demanded in advance by women(6).Even though delivery assisted by skilled provider is the most important factor in reducing maternal mortality, recent analysis on emergency obstetrics has showed that obstetric care providers had insufficient knowledge and poor skill of diagnosing PPH(Post-Partum Hemorrhage) and birth asphyxia(11).\r\nIn fact, evidence based decision making has been advocated to bring about quality of health service(3). Even though evidence based practice has been advocated across the health care authorities and facilities in Ethiopia, there is little to no evidence showing how it looks on the ground. So to authors knowledge, there is limited research conducted on magnitude of evidence based intra partum practice and associated factors in Ethiopia, particularly in the Jimma zone .So the aim of this study is to assess evidence-based intrapartum practice and associated factors among obstetric care providers in Jimma zone public hospitals, Southwest Ethiopia..','4.	Design and methods \r\na)	Study area and period; \r\nThe study will be conducted in nine (9) public hospitals found in Jimma zone, south west Ethiopia from June 1/2022 –May 20 /2023. Jimma Zone is located 352 KMs South west of the capital Addis Ababa. The population projection of 2020/2021 of the zone is 3,090,112.There are nine (9) public hospitals namely Shenengibe, Limmu gannet hospital , Seka hospital , Dedo Hospital , Dimtu Hospital , Omo Nada Hospital ,Agaro Hospital ,Satamma Hospital  and JUMC. \r\nStudy design; \r\nThe study will be a mix-methods study design which consist of direct observation of intrapartum practice during the women in second and third stages, as well as semi-structured interviews and focus group discussion with care providers. .  \r\nb)	Population\r\ni.	Source population\r\nQuantitative study: All obstetric care providers in Jimma zone public health institutions. \r\nQualitative study: Purposively selected obstetric care providers in Jimma zone public health institutions. \r\nii. Study population\r\n All obstetrics care providers who gave care for laboring mothers in labour and delivery unit during study period.   \r\n       iii. Inclusion and exclusion criteria\r\nInclusion: Obstetric care providers who are currently working in labor ward.\r\nExclusion criteria; Obstetric care providers on annual leave during the study period.\r\na.	Sample size estimation and sampling procedure\r\ni.	Sample size \r\nThe sample size will be determined for specific objective of this study and the largest sample size which applies for all objectives were considered. Sample size will be calculated using a single population proportion formula.                       \r\n                           n= (Z α/2)2 p (1-p) /d2    \r\nWhere,	   \r\n   n= sample size\r\nZ1- α /2 = confidence level corresponding to 95% CI = 1.96 \r\nP = 50 %(which give maximum sample size)\r\nd= 0.05, is the margin of the sampling error to be tolerated.\r\nBased on the above assumption the total sample size will be 384.\r\nii. Sampling procedure \r\nAll obstetrics care providers who were available during the data collection period and give obstetric care at least for one laboring mother will be considered as study participant due to smaller number of obstetric care providers.\r\nData collection tools and technique\r\nIntrapartum practices by health care providers during second and third stage of labor will be directly observed by using adapted WHO model of intrapartum care checklist. Semi-structured interviews will be conducted with obstetric care providers to explore their perception on evidence based intrapartum care. \r\nStudy variables\r\nDependent variable \r\n 	Evidence based intrapartum practice.\r\nIndependent variable\r\n  Socio-economic  factors:  -  Age,  sex,  employment status, profession, qualification,\r\nPosition, marital status, experience.\r\nOrganizational  factors:  -  access  to  computer  &  internet,  managerial  motivation, \r\nconference,  and  interactive  EBP  skill  building  workshops,  cooperation,  regular \r\nMentoring, patient condition, role clarity, source of health information.\r\nIndividual factors: \r\nUse of computer for reading, internet, and documentation, Searching for journals,  Cochrane  database, WHO  Reproductive Health Library, Use of  Scientific journals,  textbooks, colleagues, and conference\r\nfor health information source; Participating  on  conference,  training,  seminar,   Usage  of  health  information  for  patient  condition  improvement, clinical research,  knowledge and attitude.\r\n\r\n\r\nOperational definition\r\n Evidence based intrapartum  practice:  Obstetric care  provides who scored greater than or \r\nEqual to the median value of practice related questions of intrapartum practice.\r\nObstetric care providers: A certified health personnel who provide care for the woman during \r\nLabor and delivery.\r\nAdequate knowledge:  - Obstetric care provides who scored greater than or equal to the median value of knowledge related questions of intrapartum practice.\r\nFavorable attitude: Obstetric care provides who scored greater than or equal to the median \r\nValue of attitude related questions of intrapartum practice.\r\n5.	Data management and statistical analysis \r\n	The questionnaire will be checked for completeness, cleaned, coded, and entered into EPI data statistical software version 3.1 and exported to SPSS windows version 23 for analysis. Frequencies, proportions, and summary statistics will be used to describe the study population in relation to relevant variables and presented using texts, tables, and figures. The Bivariate analysis will be employed to identify candidate variables for multivariable analysis. Then variables found to have p-values of less than 0.25 will be taken to Multivariable Logistic regression for controlling the possible effects of confounders. Finally, variables that will have a p-value ≤ 05 will be described as significant and Adjusted OR with 95% CI will be determined to see the strength of the associations. Hosmer and Lemeshow test will be used to check the fitness of the model. For qualitative data, the data will be transcribed verbatim and translated into English by the language experts. Then the data will be analyzed using thematic content analysis Assisted by Atlas.ti 8. Codes will be identified for key concepts that represent recurring themes which will then be categorized, grouped and organized to draw out key themes.\r\n6	Quality assurance\r\nQuality of data will be assured through careful design, translation, and back retranslation of the questionnaire. A pre-test will be conducted on 5% of the sample size to ensure clarity, logical sequence, skip pattern of the questionnaire and amendment will be done based on the findings of the pre-test. Sign language translators will be recruited to collect data from participants with hearing impairments. Two days of training will be given to data collectors and supervisors before the actual data collection day. The overall supervision will be carried out by the project members. During data collection, the questionnaire will be reviewed and checked for completeness and consistency by supervisors every day and necessary feedback will be offered to data collectors the next morning. The trustworthiness of qualitative data will be ensured by triangulation of findings, and rich description of the study methods.\r\n2.	Plan for dissemination and ensuring utilization of findings\r\n	The finding of the study will be presented on different Workshops. It will be disseminated to the stakeholders at the Zonal and Woreda’s levels. Besides, the research findings will be presented in different national and international scientific conferences. The finding will be published in peer-reviewed reputable journals.\r\n\r\n3.	Ethical and environmental considerations \r\nEthical clearance will be obtained from the Institutional Review Board of Jimma University; Institute of Health. Letter of cooperation will be written to Jimma Zone health office. Permission to undertake the study will be secured from all relevant authority in the zone and hospitals. Study participants will be provided comprehensive information about nature, objectives, benefits,  potential risks of the study and participants right to refuse and withdraw participation in the study will be assured . Written informed consent will be obtained from study participants before the interview. A written informed consent from their parent/ guardian will be obtained and assent for participants below the age of 18 will be obtained. Participation will be completely on a voluntary basis and confidentiality of the information guaranteed by secret code and kept anonymously. The hard copy of the collected data will be kept in locked cabinet and secured by password on the computer. Privacy and confidentiality of collected information will be ensured.','<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">1.Baskett T, Arulkumaran S. Intrapartum Care for the MRCOG and Beyond: Cambridge University Press; 2011.</span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">2.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Raven JH, Tolhurst RJ, Tang S, Van Den Broek N. What is quality in maternal and neonatal health care? Midwifery. 2012;28(5):e676-e83.</span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">3.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Organization WH. WHO recommendations on intrapartum care for a positive childbirth experience: World Health Organization; 2018.</span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">4.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Sandin‐Boj&ouml; AK, Kvist LJ. Care in labor: a Swedish survey using the Bologna Score. Birth. 2008;35(4):321-8.</span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">5.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Osungbade K, Oginni S, Olumide E, Owoaje E. Clinical audit of intra-partum care at secondary health facilities in Nigeria. Nigerian Journal of Clinical Practice. 2010;13(2).</span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">6.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Aghlmand S, Akbari F, Lameei A, Mohammad K, Small R, Arab M. Developing evidence-based maternity care in Iran: a quality improvement study. BMC pregnancy and childbirth. 2008;8(1):1-8.</span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">7.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Brown CE, Ecoff L, Kim SC, Wickline MA, Rose B, Klimpel K, et al. Multi‐institutional study of barriers to research utilisation and evidence‐based practice among hospital nurses. Journal of clinical nursing. 2010;19(13‐14):1944-51.</span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">8.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Hoogenboom G, Thwin MM, Velink K, Baaijens M, Charrunwatthana P, Nosten F, et al. Quality of intrapartum care by skilled birth attendants in a refugee clinic on the Thai-Myanmar border: a survey using WHO Safe Motherhood Needs Assessment. BMC pregnancy and childbirth. 2015;15(1):1-9.</span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">9.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Jeng B. Practices and quality of intrapartum care in the main referral hospital of The Gambia 2008.</span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">10.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Kardong‐Edgren S. Using evidence‐based practice to improve intrapartum care. Journal of Obstetric, Gynecologic, &amp; Neonatal Nursing. 2001;30(4):371-5.</span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">11.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Otolorin E, Gomez P, Currie S, Thapa K, Dao B. Essential basic and emergency obstetric and newborn care: from education and training to service delivery and quality of care. International Journal of Gynecology &amp; Obstetrics. 2015;130:S46-S53.</span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">12.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; MESSELE A. COLLEGE OF MEDICINE AND HEALTH SCIENCE DEPARTMENT OF MIDWIFERY: COLLEGE OF MEDICINE AND HEATLH SCIENCES, UNIVERSITY OF GONDAR; 2015.</span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">13.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Akuze J, Annerstedt KS, Benova L, Chipeta E, Dossou J-P, Gross MM, et al. Action leveraging evidence to reduce perinatal mortality and morbidity (ALERT): study protocol for a stepped-wedge cluster-randomised trial in Benin, Malawi, Tanzania and Uganda. BMC health services research. 2021;21(1):1-14.</span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">14.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Mukamurigo J, Dencker A, Nyirazinyoye L, Ntaganira J, Berg M. Quality of intrapartum care for healthy women with spontaneous onset of labour in Rwanda: A health facility-based, cross-sectional study. Sexual &amp; Reproductive Healthcare. 2019;19:78-83.</span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">15.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Carr CA, Schott A. Differences in evidence‐based care in midwifery practice and education. Journal of Nursing Scholarship. 2002;34(2):153-8.</span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">16.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Devoe LD. The future of intrapartum care: navigating the perfect storm&mdash;an obstetrician&#39;s odyssey. American Journal of Obstetrics &amp; Gynecology. 2009;201(1):100-4.</span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">17.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Monebenimp F, Tenefopa M, Koh VM, Kago I. Competence of health care providers on care of newborns at birth in a level-1 health facility in Yaound&eacute;, Cameroon. Pan African Medical Journal. 2012;11(1).</span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">18.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Jha P, Christensson K, Svanberg AS, Larsson M, Sharma B, Johansson E. Cashless childbirth, but at a cost: a grounded theory study on quality of intrapartum care in public health facilities in India. Midwifery. 2016;39:78-86.</span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">19.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Matsui M, Saito Y, Po R, Taing B, Nhek C, Tung R, et al. Knowledge on intrapartum care practices among skilled birth attendants in Cambodia&mdash;a cross-sectional study. Reproductive health. 2021;18(1):1-11.</span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">20.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Rota A, Antolini L, Colciago E, Nespoli A, Borrelli SE, Fumagalli S. Timing of hospital admission in labour: latent versus active phase, mode of birth and intrapartum interventions. A correlational study. Women and Birth. 2018;31(4):313-8.</span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">21.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Rooks JP. Evidence‐based practice and its application to childbirth care for low‐risk women. Journal of Nurse‐Midwifery. 1999;44(4):355-69</span></span></p>','To assess Evidence Based Intra-partum Care practice, common obstetrical care mal practice and associated factors among obstetric care providers in public hospitals of Jimma zone',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,'Intra-partum,evidence based ,obstetric care providers, evidence based intrapartum care',10,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'Proposal-627f8e78416cd699331870.docx'),(94,342,13,20,'Background: Each year, an approximately 2.4 million newborns die globally. More than 85% of the deaths are attributed to three clinical conditions: prematurity, asphyxia and infection. Ethiopia is ranked the fourth among 20 countries with the highest neonatal mortality. National data suggests that many newborns are dying while high impact cost effective interventions are in place. The World Health Organization acknowledged absence of research assessing the impact of neonatal interventions as a serious gap. Likewise, there is a gap in knowledge regarding the impact of neonatal interventions in reducing neonatal death in southwest Ethiopia. \r\nObjectives: This research mainly seeks to evaluate the impact of neonatal interventions on reducing neonatal death in southwest Ethiopia, 2022/23. Thereby identifying what proportion of neonatal lives were being saved as a result of particular interventions and what were the bottle necks of the interventions for saving neonatal lives. \r\nMethods: The study will be conducted in southwest Ethiopia, from June,2022 to May, 2023. A randomly selected 422 newborns and 422 health care providers will be considered as a study subjects. Data will be collected by trained data collectors using interviewer administered questionnaire and structured participatory observation. Data will be entered in to EpiData version 4.2 and analyzed using Statistical Package for the Social Sciences (SPSS window version 25) software. Decision about neonatal mortality and associated clinical condition will be determined when p-value is less than 0.05 at 95% confidence interval. Impact of neonatal intervention will be analyzed and determined using Maternal and Neonatal Directed Assessment of Technologies (MANDATE), a mathematical model. \r\nBudget: This research project is estimated to cost a total of 593,176 ETB.',NULL,'Neonatal interventions: a mathematical modelling of their potential impact on reducing neonatal death in southwest Ethiopia, a perspective to sustainable development goal','2022-05-14 14:02:13','The outputs of this research project are \r\n	Magnitude of neonatal mortality\r\n	Clinical conditions associated with neonatal mortality\r\n	Case fatality rate of each clinical condition\r\n	The penetration and utilization of interventions\r\n	The proportion of lives saved as a result of the intervention \r\n	Proportions of lives saved if a particular intervention is improved under ideal conditions','02a0b8e78694cb260de6962b240e990c','completed','Each year, an approximately 2.4 million newborns die, amounting to 6,700 newborn deaths every day (WHO, 2022). The death mostly arises from condition that is treatable with appropriate interventions (Lawn et al., 2014). Nearly 99% of neonatal death occurs in developing countries (Masaba & Mmusi-Phetoe, 2020). Compared to a child born in Europe, a child in sub-Saharan Africa (SSA) is 10 times more likely to die in the first month of life (UNICEF, 2015).\r\nEthiopia is ranked the fourth among 20 countries with the highest Neonatal Mortality (NM) in the world (WHO, 2022). The progress made in Ethiopia over the past three decades in reducing neonatal mortality has been too slow. The number of neonatal deaths decreased only by 42% from 1990 and become stagnant since 2005 (FMOH, 2015b).  The current rate is nearly three times more than what has been targeted to achieve in the health sector transformation plan I (FMOH, 2015a).\r\nImproving the survival chances of babies remains an urgent global challenge. The united nation has set an ambitious target; neonatal mortality in all countries should not exceed 12 deaths per 1,000 live births (LB) by 2030 (UN, 2015). However, Ethiopia is significantly off track to meet the target on ending the preventable deaths of newborns (FMOH, 2019). According to latest estimates, with the current rate of NM reduction Ethiopia needs 15 more years to meet the sustainable development goal (SDG) target (FMOH, 2015b). \r\nMore than 85% of the deaths are attributed to three clinical conditions: prematurity, asphyxia and infection (Desalew A, Sintayehu Y, Teferi N, Amare F, Geda B, Worku T, 2020; Tekelab T, Akibu M, Tagesse N, Tilhaun T, Yohanes Y, 2019; WHO, 2022; Woday Tadesse A, Mekuria Negussie Y, 2021). These clinical conditions can be prevented or treated with an appropriate intervention (Lawn et al., 2014). Greater emphasis on wide-scale implementation of proven, cost-effective measures is needed to save newborns’ lives. Ethiopia has designed and implemented thirty-six cost-effective and high impact interventions (FMOH, 2015b). The interventions could prevent roughly two-thirds of all neonatal deaths (Darmstadt G, Bhutta Z, 2005).\r\nEvidences suggest that most neonatal deaths can be prevented even in settings with high neonatal mortality and weak health systems (Darmstadt G, Bhutta Z, 2005; Lawn et al., 2014). While health coverage increased, the rates of antenatal visit and proportion of deliveries attended by skilled providers have also increased, progress on neonatal health outcomes is stagnant (FMOH, 2019). A recent meeting summoned by the World Health Organization (WHO) acknowledged absence of research assessing the impact of neonatal interventions as a serious gap to achieving the SDGs.  \r\nResearchers have attempted to find an answer to the unfailing trend of neonatal death. Few studies in Ethiopia have assessed effectiveness of packages of community-based interventions and others the quality of specific interventions at facility level (Haws RA, Thomas AL, Bhutta ZA, 2007). Despite the effort the findings of these studies failed to give a full answer to the problem. One of the major gaps is that it lacks comprehensive evaluation of all the available thirty-six interventions, did not account the major clinical conditions leading to death, did not consider the preventive, diagnostic and therapeutic aspects of interventions and most importantly, the finding does not fill the knowledge gap existed about how well each intervention are saving lives. \r\nIn southwest Ethiopia, to the best of our knowledge we could not find a single study that attempted to evaluate the impact of available interventions on neonatal mortality considering the preventive, diagnostic and therapeutic aspects of interventions and the setting at which interventions are implemented.\r\nCognizant of the gap, we therefore, planned to estimate the impact of interventions in southwest Ethiopia on neonatal death using a mathematical model known as Maternal and Neonatal Directed Assessment of Technologies (MANDATE). The model assesses neonatal mortality rate, conditions associated with it and the potential impact of the interventions to save lives. Besides, it provides insights on potential bottlenecks that might prevent an intervention from saving the maximum number of neonatal lives (Jones-Hepler B, Moran K, Griffin J, McClure EM, Rouse D, Barbosa C, 2017). \r\nThis study is crucial for it aims to provide an insight in to the potential impact of available interventions with respect to the life saved by considering the preventive, diagnostic and therapeutic aspect of the interventions. The finding of this study will help to understand the interventions that have had a substantial effect on reducing neonatal mortality rates and to prioritize area of focus for better enhancement. Furthermore, this study is based on mathematical modeling, hence the finding could show a more valid gap in real context compared to randomized controlled trial.  Besides, this study considered the constraint compared to randomized controlled trials.','Study area and period\r\nThe study will be conducted in southwest Ethiopia, which includes Jimma zone, southwest Ethiopia region, Illu-Ababora zone, Badele zone and Gambella region. The total number of populations in the area is estimated to be more than 20 million. The southwest Ethiopia is known by its cash crops particularly coffee. There is one tertiary hospital (JUMC), 24 primary and general hospitals.  An estimated 3,500 health care providers are working in these facilities. The study will be conducted from June, 2022 to May, 2023.  \r\n	Study design\r\nWe will employ a facility based cross-sectional study and will use Maternal and Neonatal Directed Assessment of Technologies (MANDATE) model. \r\nMANDATE is mathematical model created to evaluate maternal, fetal and neonatal mortality rates and the impact of interventions on these outcomes. It provides insights on potential bottlenecks that might prevent an intervention from saving the maximum number of neonatal lives. \r\nWe will model neonatal mortality associated with asphyxia, preterm birth and bacterial infection. To develop the model, we will populate variables regarding penetration, utilization, and efficacy of preventive, diagnostic, and treatment interventions specific to prematurity, infection and asphyxia. \r\n	Population\r\n	Source population\r\nAll health care providers and neonates in southwest Ethiopia will be source population of the study.\r\n	Study population\r\nHealth care providers and neonates in selected health facilities in southwest Ethiopia will be the study population.\r\nEligibility criteria: Health care providers who are providing antenatal care (ANC), labor and delivery, postnatal care (PNC) and neonatal services and who have been working for at least six months will be included in the study.  Health care providers who are not available at health facilities due to different reasons such as maternity leave and serious illness will be excluded from the study. Neonates being referred to, delivered or admitted in selected facility will be included in the study. Those neonates with comorbidity to which available interventions are not directed will be excluded\r\n	Sample size and Sampling technique /Sampling procedures\r\n	Sample size\r\nWe calculated the sample size for the two study populations (i.e., neonates and health care providers) to meet the study objectives.\r\nAccordingly, we calculated the sample size required to determine magnitude of neonatal mortality and associated clinical condition using single population proportion formula:\r\n((〖Z_(α/2))〗^2*P*Q)/d^2 \r\nAssumptions were: prevalence of neonatal mortality= 50%, Z α/2 =1.96 corresponding to 95% confidence level and d=0.05, non-response rate=10%\r\n((〖1.96)〗^2*0.5*0.5)/〖0.05〗^2    =422(Neonates)\r\nAdditionally, to determine the penetration and utilization of each intervention we estimated the required sample size using single population proportion formula with an assumption: prevalence of penetration and utilization= 50%, Z α/2 =1.96 corresponding to 95% confidence level and d=0.05, non-response rate=10%\r\n((〖1.96)〗^2*0.5*0.5)/〖0.05〗^2    =422(health care providers)\r\n\r\n	Sampling procedure \r\nFirst, we will use stratified sampling techniques to select hospitals from each stratum(zone) in southwest Ethiopia. Next, we will go one year back and find a six-month data of neonates to estimate baseline data.  Then the calculated sample size will be proportionally allocated to selected hospitals. Finally, we will determine sampling interval and select study subjects using systematic random sampling technique. The first study subject will be randomly selected with lottery methods from the first interval and the subsequent samples will then be selected at every determined sampling interval. \r\nHealth care providers working on ANC, delivery, postnatal and NICU of selected hospitals will be sampled as follows: number of samples allocated to each selected hospital will be further allocated to each department by probability proportional to size allocation methods. Then, a fresh list of health workers in each department will be generated and selected either with simple random sampling technique or systematic random sampling techniques. \r\n	Data collection procedures (Instrument, personnel, data collection technique)\r\nWe will use two instruments for data collection; i) a structured questionnaire and ii) structured observational checklist. The first instrument will be used to identify neonatal mortality rate and clinical conditions associated with it. The instrument has three sections; socio-demography, neonatal condition and clinical conditions. It has been previously utilized for similar objectives. \r\nThe second instrument will be newly developed by the authors to identify the penetration and utilization of high impact interventions targeted to the common clinical condition attributing to neonatal death. The psychometric properties of the instrument will be determined before actual utilization.\r\nThe English version of the instruments will be translated in to local official working language (i.e. Afan oromo and Amharic) by experts in languages. Then the consistency will be ensured with back translation. \r\nThe principal investigator will recruit xxx data collector based on relevant data collection experiences. Then data collectors will be trained and oriented on how to use instrument and collect data by considering the ethical principles. The PI will also select XXX supervisors who will move in each site to assist and supervise the data collection process.\r\nData will be collected using interviewer administered questionnaire and participatory structured observation.\r\n	Operational definitions \r\nPenetration: the availability of interventions \r\nUtilization: the proper utilization of interventions\r\nEfficacy: the effect of intervention to save neonatal lives \r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n	Data analysis procedures\r\nData will be entered in to EpiData version 4.2.  The analysis will be performed using Statistical Package for the Social Sciences (SPSS window version 25) software.  Descriptive statistics such as frequency and percentage for categorical variables and mean, standard deviation and others for continuous variable will be done as appropriate.  \r\nPrediction of clinical condition to neonatal mortality will be determined using logistic regression.  Bivariate analysis will be done to identify candidate variables for multivariate logistic regression analysis at p-value < 0.25.  Adjusted odds ratio (AOR) with respective 95% confidence interval (CI) at p-value of < 0.05 will be used to declare a statistically significant variable. \r\nDecision about the impact of intervention will be made using mathematical modelling. The MANDATE models clinical conditions and sub conditions leading to neonatal death. The model calculates the number of neonatal deaths during the study period in southwest Ethiopia. The model is calibrated using several scenarios. First, the model\'s baseline inputs are calibrated using incidence rates before intervention. Next interventions are entered to prevent, diagnose, and treat neonatal conditions using baseline estimates of their penetration, utilization, impact. Lastly, MANDATE consider untreated case fatality rates to predict the probability of neonatal death without interventions (Figure 1).\r\n \r\nFigure 1: Schematic illustration of the mathematical modeling of interventions in MANDATE. \r\n	Data quality management\r\nThe quality of data will be assured by employing the following strategies. Three-day training will be provided for data collectors and supervisors. Pretest of the data collection tool will be conducted on 5% of sample size in a hospital that is not selected for actual study. Revision of the data collection tool will be done based on pretest finding. Close supervision of the data collection process will be done by supervisors and principal investigator. Completeness and consistency of data will be checked on daily basis by supervisors. Double entry of data will be employed during data entry.  \r\n	Dissemination plan\r\nThe findings of the study will be disseminated to the health facilities in southwest Ethiopia. The findings will be submitted to Jimma University, Institute of health research coordinating office. It will be presented at conferences and seminars, and publication of the findings on international reputable journal will be also considered. We will also use social media platforms to reach relevant concerned bodies.','<p style=\"margin-left:32px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">Darmstadt G, Bhutta Z, C. S. (2005). Evidence-based, cost-effective interventions: how many newborn babies can we save? <em>Lancet</em>.</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:32px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">Desalew A, Sintayehu Y, Teferi N, Amare F, Geda B, Worku T,&nbsp; et al. (2020). Cause and predictors of neonatal mortality among neonates admitted to neonatal intensive care units of public hospitals in eastern Ethiopia: A facility-based prospective follow-up study. <em>BMC Pediatrics</em>.</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:32px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">FMOH. (2015a). <em>Ethiopia Health Sector Transformation Plan</em>. Retrieved from https://www.globalfinancingfacility.org/ethiopia-health-sector-transformation-plan</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:32px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">FMOH. (2015b). <em>National Strategy for Newborn and Child Survival in Ethiopia &ndash; Healthy Newborn Network</em>.</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:32px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">FMOH. (2019). <em>Ethiopia - Mini Demographic and Health Survey</em>. Retrieved from https://microdata.worldbank.org/index.php/catalog</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:32px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">Haws RA, Thomas AL, Bhutta ZA, D. G. (2007). Impact of packaged interventions on neonatal health: a review of the evidence. Health Policy and Planning. <em>Health Policy and Planning</em>.</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:32px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">Jones-Hepler B, Moran K, Griffin J, McClure EM, Rouse D, Barbosa C,&nbsp; et al. (2017). Maternal and Neonatal Directed Assessment of Technologies (MANDATE): Methods and Assumptions for a Predictive Model for Maternal, Fetal, and Neonatal Mortality Interventions. <em>Global Health: Science and Practice</em>.</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:32px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">Lawn, J. E., Blencowe, H., Oza, S., You, D., Lee, A. C. C., Waiswa, P., &hellip; Cousens, S. N. (2014). Every newborn: Progress, priorities, and potential beyond survival. <em>The Lancet</em>, <em>384</em>(9938), 189&ndash;205. https://doi.org/10.1016/S0140-6736(14)60496-7</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:32px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">Masaba, B. B., &amp; Mmusi-Phetoe, R. M. (2020). Neonatal survival in sub-sahara: A review of kenya and south africa. <em>Journal of Multidisciplinary Healthcare</em>, <em>13</em>, 709&ndash;716. https://doi.org/10.2147/JMDH.S260058</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:32px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">Tekelab T, Akibu M, Tagesse N, Tilhaun T, Yohanes Y, N. S. (2019). Neonatal mortality in Ethiopia: A protocol for systematic review and meta-analysis. Systematic Reviews. <em>BMC Public Health</em>.</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:32px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">UN. (2015). <em>THE 17 GOALS Sustainable Development</em>. Retrieved from https://sdgs.un.org/goals</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:32px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">UNICEF. (2015). Levels and trends in child mortality. <em>Google Scholar</em>. Retrieved from https://scholar.google.com/scholar_lookup?title=Child+Mortality+2019&amp;publication_year=2019</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:32px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">WHO. (2022). Newborns: improving survival and well-being. Retrieved from https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/newborns-reducing-mortality</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:32px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">Woday Tadesse A, Mekuria Negussie Y, A. S. (2021). . Neonatal mortality and its associated factors among neonates admitted at public hospitals, pastoral region, Ethiopia: A health facility based study. <em>PLoS ONE</em>.</span></span></span></span></p>','To evaluate the impact of neonatal interventions in reducing neonatal death in southwest Ethiopia, 2022/23.',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,'Neonatal interventions, neonatal mortality, MANDETE, mathematical model',10,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'Proposal-627f8b63ce8fe873127135.docx'),(95,346,13,17,'Background: Sepsis is defined as a life-threatening organ dysfunction caused by a dysregulated host response to infection. One-third to half of ICU admitted patients are estimated to have sepsis or septic shock. However, the accessibility of high-quality data is still warranted to better estimate the burden of sepsis in LMICs. Data regarding the magnitude and outcomes of sepsis are not well known in LMICs, the area allegedly with the highest-burden. Current knowledge of bacterial profile and antibiotic resistance patterns in sepsis patients is also essential to designing and implementing an appropriate management approach. \r\nObjective: The aim of this study is to assess the clinical, microbiological, and molecular profile of sepsis among ICU admitted patients at Jimma Medical Center.\r\nMethods: A prospective longitudinal study design will be conducted at Jimma Medical Center (JMC)by consecutively recruiting 303 ICU admitted adult patients. Socio-demographic and clinical data will be collected using a checklist and questionnaire. Five to 10 milliliters of the Blood sample will be collected aseptically using a disposable syringe and needle on the day of admission into two blood culture bottles containing Tryptic Soy Broth (TSB) for microbiological analysis including identification and Antibiotic susceptibility testing. DNA will be extracted from bacterial isolates using the High Pure PCR Template Preparation Kit (Roche) according to the manufacturer’s protocol. Whole-genome sequencing (WGS) will be performed using the MiSeq instrument (Illumina) and will be assembled and analyzed for the presence of resistance and virulence genes, as well as relatedness between strains using an in-house bioinformatics pipeline. Blood samples will also be collected on day 1, day 3, and day 7 of admission using an EDTA tube (3ml) and serum separator tube (SST)(3ml) for complete blood count and chemistry analysis, respectively. Data will be analyzed by SPSS version 25. Multivariable stepwise logistic regression analysis will be used to explore predictors of ICU mortality in patients with sepsis or septic shock. Survival analysis will be performed using Cox proportional hazard regression analysis. The receiver operating characteristic (ROC) curve will be used to see the diagnostic and prognostic performances of the SOFA and qSOFA scores. \r\nWork plan: The study will be conducted from September 1, 2022, to August 30, 2023.\r\nBudget: With the detailed and reasonable budget breakdown, 2,238,687 ETB is proposed to undertake this study.',NULL,'Clinical, Microbiological and Molecular Characterization of Sepsis Among Intensive Care Unit Admitted Adult Patients at Jimma Medical Center','2022-05-25 14:37:17','The WHO declared sepsis a global health problem in 2017 and urged countries to prevent, detect, and treat this dreadful condition promptly and effectively. Early identification and appropriate and timely management are the key steps to mitigate the burden. Though the majority of sepsis cases and related deaths are assumed to occur in LMIC, data from this region is limited and the report is based on studies conducted mainly in hospitals in high-income countries. \r\nEstimation of the burden of sepsis is hampered by various challenges in developing countries. Failure to use appropriate diagnostic tools is one of the challenges that hugely affect the early identification and management of the disease. The current working diagnostic tool is SOFA score and qSOFA which are developed by the European Society of Intensive Care Medicine and the Society of Critical Care Medicine based on data from high-income countries. The present study will attempt to precisely determine the burden of sepsis by using these tools. This will also create an opportunity to see the performance of SOFA and qSOFA in developing countries. The result will inform policymakers and other stakeholders about the burden and prompt validation of appropriate diagnostic tool that best fits the local context.\r\nMoreover, identification of the etiologic agent and timely administration of antimicrobial therapy is the foundation of effective sepsis treatment. In line with this comes the requirement for antimicrobial stewardship as sepsis is a significant driver of antibiotic use. The threat of AMR is growing steadily which contributes to the persistent burden of infectious diseases. Understanding the AMR pattern and its genetic underpinnings is also the aim of this study which will aid in developing a targeted intervention for the good of the society.','60260731a4cf766c1be3a4bd7a1dee74','completed','Sepsis is defined as a life-threatening organ dysfunction caused by a deregulated host response to infection (1). The critical tenet of sepsis pathogenesis relies on inflammatory imbalance following pathogenic infection and subsequent production of pro-inflammatory cytokines (2). The recognition of pathogen-associated molecular patterns (PAMPS) or damage-associated molecular patterns (DAMPs) by pattern-recognition receptors (PRRs) on the surface of antigen-presenting cells and monocytes leads to the activation of signaling pathways and the production and release of inflammatory cytokines (3). The resulting inflammation in turn leads to progressive organ damage and multi-organ dysfunction through different pathophysiological processes including coagulopathy and complement cascade activation (4)(5). On the other hand, the concomitant immunosuppression as a compensatory anti-inflammatory response in sepsis can make the patient more prone to further infections (6).\r\nClinically, sepsis is operationalized as an increase in the Sequential Organ Failure Assessment (SOFA) score of 2 points or more while septic shock is a subset of sepsis identified by a vasopressor requirement to maintain a mean arterial pressure (MAP) of 65 mm Hg or greater and serum lactate level greater than 2 mmol/L (>18 mg/dL) in the absence of hypovolemia (1). The SOFA score encompasses various clinical and laboratory assessments including respiratory, coagulation, liver, cardiovascular, central nervous system, and renal components(7). \r\nComputing SOFA score at the bedside may be challenging as it includes biochemical tests to be done in the laboratory. Thus, a qSOFA screening tool is developed which is based on respiratory rate (> 22 breaths/min), systolic blood pressure (< 100 mmHg), and altered mental state (Glasgow Coma Scale < 13) (8).  Patients who meet at least two of these criteria are likely to have either longer ICU stay or at high risk of death, according to a retrospective analysis of a large database (9). \r\nHowever, there is limited applicability of these clinical tools to identify sepsis in low and middle-income countries (LMICs) which highlights the presence of unrecognized sepsis cases in the region (10). Improved accessibility of high-quality data is still warranted to better estimate the burden of sepsis in LMICs (11). World health organization (WHO) highlighted the difficulty of establishing the global burden of sepsis and urged the member states to reinforce efforts to identify, document, prevent, and treat sepsis (12). The main challenge in LMICs arises from a failure to early identify sepsis and implementation of targeted treatment.  Moreover, limited applicability of the clinical tools to identify sepsis such as SOFA score and quick SOFA (qSOFA) score hampers knowledge of the actual burden. In fact, these tools were developed based on data from high-income countries and not yet evaluated in low-income countries  (10). Thus, evaluating the diagnostic and prognostic performance of SOFA and qSOFA scores in LMICs would be highly valuable to understanding the burden of sepsis in a uniform manner.\r\nBacteria are the common cause of pathogenic sepsis though viruses and fungi can also cause a significant proportion (13). About 30% of intensive care unit (ICU) patients have sepsis and 2/3rd of the sepsis patients have positive isolates, of which gram-negative microorganisms dominate, according to a worldwide study in 2012 (14). Variability of bacterial profile across different regions has also been reported in this study whereby Methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus (MRSA) was more common in the Middle East (14.4%) and North America (12.8%), Klebsiella spp isolates were most commonly reported in Africa (31.3%), Eastern Europe (28.5%), and South America (24.7%), and Pseudomonas spp was most frequent in Eastern Europe (21.1%) and South America (20.4%) (14).\r\nMulti-drug resistance (MDR) is one of the reasons for the growing incidence of sepsis in ICU due to exposure to different invasive procedures, length of stay in ICU, and use of broad-spectrum antibiotics (15)(16). Antimicrobial resistance has been a growing threat in developing countries aggravated by abuse or irrational use of antimicrobials, making the commonly available antimicrobials ineffective (17). The prevalence of MDR bacteria in ICU varies across different geographical regions (16) (18) (19). Current knowledge of the bacterial profile and antibiotic resistance in sepsis patients is essential to designing and implementing an appropriate management approach. Furthermore, understanding the extent of high-risk clones associated with MDR at the study site is paramount to establishing appropriate surveillance and tracking mechanisms.\r\nData regarding the magnitude and outcomes of sepsis are not well known in LMICs, the area allegedly with the highest-burden. There is also a paucity of current knowledge regarding the bacterial profile and antibiotic resistance patterns. Therefore, this study aims to assess the prevalence and outcomes of sepsis, as well as clinical, microbiological, and molecular profiles of sepsis among ICU admitted patients.','3.1.	Study area and period\r\nThe study will be conducted at Jimma Medical Center (JMC). JMC is one of the oldest hospitals in Ethiopia and it is the only teaching and referral hospital in southwest Ethiopia with an 800-bed capacity and a catchment population of over 20 million people. JMC has a total of 12 beds in the medical and surgical ICUs, with an estimated annual admission of 400 patients. One-third to half of ICU admitted patients are estimated to have sepsis or septic shock. Sepsis is the 2nd common cause of admission and septic shock is the 1st common cause of death in JMC ICUs, according to a 2020 report from the unit. The study will be conducted from September 1, 2022, to August 30, 2023.\r\n3.2.	Study Design\r\nA prospective longitudinal study design will be conducted. The prevalence of sepsis and septic shock among ICU admitted adults will be determined and the same patients will also be followed for possible outcomes. \r\n3.3.	Study Population\r\nAll patients > 18 years of age who will be admitted to the ICU of JMC during the study period will be included to determine the prevalence and outcome of sepsis in the ICU. Positive isolates from sepsis patients will be used to assess bacterial profile and antimicrobial susceptibility patterns.\r\n3.4.	Eligibility criteria\r\nAll patients aged 18 years or older who are admitted to ICU and stay at least for 24hrs will be included.\r\n3.5.	Sample size estimation and sampling procedure\r\nThe sample size is computed based on a single population proportion sample size calculation using the following formula:\r\nn= (z α/2)2 (p) (1-p)/d2\r\nWhereby, \r\n            n=sample size \r\nP= prevalence of sepsis which is 26.5% according to a study in Addis Ababa, Ethiopia (20). \r\n            α =critical value at 95% confidence interval of certainty (1.96) \r\n            d=margin of sampling error to be tolerated which is 5% \r\nThe calculated sample size will be 303. A consecutive sampling technique will be carried out to select 303 adults admitted to ICU.\r\n3.6.	Variables\r\n3.6.1.	Dependent Variables\r\nSepsis \r\nOutcomes (Discharge, Death)\r\n3.6.2.	Independent variables\r\nSocio-demographic characteristics: Age, Sex, Educational status, Employment status, marital status, residence, monthly income, family size\r\nClinical characteristics: Etiologic agents, source of infection, comorbidities, medication, medical procedures (use of a mechanical ventilator, catheter, central line use), time of antibiotic administration, length of stay (LOS) in ICU, LOS in hospital\r\n3.7.	Data collection tools\r\nA structured checklist and questionnaire will be used to collect background information and clinical data on the patients. A Glasgow coma score (GCS) will be obtained by the physician. A blood pressure measurement will also be made to compute the mean arterial pressure (MAP).\r\n3.8.	Specimen collection and processing\r\nFive to 10 milliliters of the blood sample will be collected aseptically using a disposable syringe and needle on the day of admission into two blood culture bottles containing Tryptic Soy Broth (TSB) and transported to the microbiology laboratory. Blood samples will also be collected on day 1, day 3, and day 7 of admission using an EDTA tube and serum separator tube (SST) for complete blood count and chemistry analysis, respectively. Arterial blood samples (1 ml) for arterial blood gas (ABG) analysis will also be obtained these days. This repeated sample collection will aid in evaluating the diagnostic and prognostic performance of the SOFA and qSOFA scores.\r\n3.9.	Sample analysis\r\n3.9.1.	Sepsis diagnosis\r\nSepsis and septic shock diagnosis will be made based on the latest definition (1) ; SOFA > 2, and SOFA > 2 + Fluid refractory hypotension + serum lactate > 2mmol/L, respectively (Table 1). \r\nquick SOFA (qSOFA) score will also be computed which includes 1 point for each of 3 criteria: (1) respiratory rate ≥ 22 breaths/min, (2) altered mental status, or (3) systolic blood pressure (SBP) ≤ 100 mm Hg. A SOFA score ≥ 2 is suggestive of sepsis (9).\r\n3.9.2.	Isolation and identification of pathogens\r\nThe blood specimen will be inoculated into a BD BACTEC Peds Plus/F blood culture bottle and gently mixed. The bottle will be incubated in the BD BACTEC™ FX40 automated culture machine for a maximum of five days until an alert for growth is noticed. A bottle with a positive alert will be sub-cultured on Blood, Chocolate, and MacConkey agar (Oxoid, England). Plates will be incubated at 35-370C aerobically and anaerobically for 18-72 hrs and all the incubated plates will be inspected for growth and identification will be made based on the standard microbiological identification technique. Strains will be preserved for later molecular characterization.\r\n3.9.3.	Antimicrobial susceptibility testing\r\nAntibiotic susceptibility testing will be done using the Kirby Bauer disc diffusion technique on Muller Hinton agar (Oxoid, England). The selection of the antibiotic discs will be based on the Clinical Laboratory Standard Institute (CLSI) guideline, 30th edition. Penicillin (1µg), oxacillin (1µg), cefoxitin (30µg), ampicillin (10mg), erythromycin (15mg), clindamycin (2mg), trimethoprim-sulphamethoxazole (1.25µg), amoxicillin/clavulanic acid (10mg), ceftazidime (30µg), cefepime (30µg), ceftriaxone (30µg), gentamicin (10mg), ciprofloxacin (5mg), tetracycline (30mg), chloramphenicol (30mg), ampicillin-sulbactam (10mg), vancomycin (30µg), and meropenem (10mg) will be used. All the antibiotic discs for the study will be obtained from Oxoid Ltd., Basingstoke, Hampshire, UK. Broth culture method or colony suspension, equivalent to a 0.5 McFarland standard will be used. Reference strains of E. coli ATCC 25922 and S. aureus ATCC 25923 will be used to control the reliability of antimicrobial susceptibility tests.\r\n3.9.4.	Identification of resistance genes\r\nThe strains will be shipped to the Ethiopian public health institute (EPHI) for the molecular workup. DNA will be extracted from bacterial isolates using the High Pure PCR Template Preparation Kit (Roche) according to the manufacturer’s protocol. The DNA concentration and purity will be determined by NanoDrop 2000 spectrophotometer (Thermo Fisher Scientific). One nanogram (in 5 µl) of each DNA extract will be used for Nextera XT library preparation (Illumina, San Diego, CA) (21). Whole-genome sequencing (WGS) will be performed using the MiSeq instrument (Illumina) using the MiSeq V3 chemistry to produce 2 × 300 bp paired-end reads (22). The WGS will be assembled and analyzed for the presence of resistance and virulence genes using an in-house bioinformatics pipeline. This pipeline will also be used to investigate the relatedness between the strains of the same species. \r\n3.9.5.	Complete blood count\r\nThe platelet count will be determined as part of the CBC using impedance and light scatter-based hematology analyzer from EDTA anticoagulated blood sample. \r\n3.9.6.	Organ function tests\r\nNon-anticoagulated blood sample from SST will be used to analyze creatinine, bilirubin, and lactate using an automated clinical chemistry analyzer.\r\n3.10.	Data management and statistical analysis\r\nData will be checked for its completeness and entered into Epidata version 3.1 (Epidata Association, Odense Denmark) and exported to Statistical Package for Social Sciences (SPSS) version 25 software (IBM Corporation, USA) for analysis. Continuous variables will be presented as the means ± standard deviations (SDs) or medians [interquartile ranges (IQRs)], and categorical variables will be presented as percentages. Multivariable stepwise logistic regression analysis will be used to explore predictors of ICU mortality in patients with sepsis or septic shock. Survival analysis will be performed using Cox proportional hazard regression analysis. The receiver operating characteristic (ROC) curve will be used to see the diagnostic and prognostic performances of the SOFA and qSOFA scores. \r\n3.11.	Quality Assurance\r\nTo ensure the quality of the data, pre-analytical, analytical, and post-analytical precautions will be observed. The English version of the questionnaire will be translated into the local language, (Amharic and Oromifa) and re-translated to the English version for its accuracy and consistency. Training will be given to the data collectors by the principal investigator before the actual data collection started. Manufacturer instructions and standard operating procedures will be strictly followed during specimen collection and all other laboratory procedures.\r\n3.12.	Plan for dissemination\r\nThe finding of this study will be submitted to the respective schools of Jimma University. The findings of the study will also be published in peer-reviewed scientific journals. It will also be presented at different scientific conferences in Ethiopia and internationally.','<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">1. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Singer M, Deutschman CS, Seymour CW, Shankar-Hari M, Annane D, Bauer M, et al. The Third International Consensus Definitions for Sepsis and Septic Shock (Sepsis-3). Acta Med Okayama [Internet]. 2012 Feb 23 [cited 2021 Aug 3];66(4):299&ndash;305. Available from: https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/26903338/</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">2. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Huang M, Cai S, Su J. The pathogenesis of sepsis and potential therapeutic targets. Int J Mol Sci. 2019;20(21). </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">3. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Raymond SL, Holden DC, Mira JC, Stortz JA, Loftus TJ, Mohr AM, Moldawer L MF. Microbial recognition and danger signals in sepsis and trauma. Biochim Biophys Acta Mol basis Dis. 2017;1863(10):2564&ndash;73. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">4. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Jarczak D, Kluge S, Nierhaus A. Sepsis&mdash;Pathophysiology and Therapeutic Concepts. Vol. 8, Frontiers in Medicine. Frontiers Media S.A.; 2021. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">5. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; BG C, FK S, GF W. Cytokine storm and sepsis disease pathogenesis. Semin Immunopathol [Internet]. 2017 Jul 1 [cited 2021 Sep 17];39(5):517&ndash;28. Available from: https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/28555385/</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">6. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; E T, A F, R A, E C, M H, C L, et al. Pro- and anti-inflammatory responses are regulated simultaneously from the first moments of septic shock. Eur Cytokine Netw [Internet]. 2011 Jun [cited 2021 Oct 5];22(2):82&ndash;7. Available from: https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/21628135/</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">7. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Vincent J-L, Moreno R, Takala J, Willatts S, De Mendon&ccedil;a A, Bruining H, et al. The SOFA (Sepsis-related Organ Failure Assessment) score to describe organ dysfunction/failure. Intensive Care Med 1996 227 [Internet]. 96AD [cited 2021 Sep 18];22(7):707&ndash;10. Available from: https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/BF01709751</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">8. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Al.&nbsp; van der WS et. Classifying sepsis patients in the emergency department using SIRS, qSOFA, or MEWS. Neth J Med. 2018;76(4):158&ndash;66. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">9. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Seymour CW, Liu VX, Iwashyna TJ, et al. Assessment of clinical criteria for sepsis: for the Third International Consensus Definitions for Sepsis and Septic Shock (Sepsis-3). JAMA. 2016;315:762&ndash;74. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">10. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Rello J, Leblebicioglu H. Sepsis and septic shock in low-income and middle-income countries: the need for a different paradigm. Int J Infect Dis [Internet]. 2016 Jul 1 [cited 2021 Sep 18];48:120&ndash;2. Available from: http://www.ijidonline.com/article/S1201971216310311/fulltext</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">11. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Rudd KE, Johnson SC, Agesa KM, Shackelford KA, Tsoi D, Kievlan DR, Colombara DV, Ikuta KS, Kissoon N, Finfer S, Fleischmann-Struzek C, Machado FR, Reinhart KK, Rowan K, Seymour CW, Watson RS, West TE, Marinho F, Hay SI, Lozano R, Lopez AD, Angus DC, Murra NM, KE R, SC J, KM A, KA S, D T, et al. Global, regional, and national sepsis incidence and mortality, 1990-2017: analysis for the Global Burden of Disease Study. Lancet [Internet]. 2020 Jan 18 [cited 2021 Aug 3];18;395(10219):200&ndash;11. Available from: https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/31954465/</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">12. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; WHO. Sepsis [Internet]. 2020 [cited 2021 Sep 17]. Available from: https://www.who.int/news-room/factsheets/detail/sepsis?__cf_chl_captcha_tk__=pmd_kulzVSlRw5lPKCgTWghJW6kI6fErTbbJP0p7HTJ297E-1631864259-0-gqNtZGzNAuWjcnBszQmR</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">13. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Dolin HH, Papadimos TJ, Chen X, Pan ZK. Characterization of Pathogenic Sepsis Etiologies and Patient Profiles: A Novel Approach to Triage and Treatment. Microbiol Insights. 2019;12(Figure 1):117863611882508. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">14. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Sakr Y, Jaschinski U, Wittebole X, Szakmany T, Lipman J, &Ntilde;amendys-Silva SA, et al. Sepsis in Intensive Care Unit Patients: Worldwide Data From the Intensive Care over Nations Audit. Open Forum Infect Dis [Internet]. 2018 Dec 1 [cited 2021 Sep 12];5(12). Available from: https://academic.oup.com/ofid/article/5/12/ofy313/5193171</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">15. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Tosi M, Roat E, De Biasi S, Munari E, Venturelli S, Coloretti I, et al. Multidrug-resistant bacteria in critically ill patients: a step further antibiotic therapy. J Emerg Crit Care Med. 2018 Dec;2:103&ndash;103. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">16. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; I L, MJ D, A S, MH P, D H, KM O. A Multicenter, Prospective, Observational Study to Determine Predictive Factors for Multidrug-Resistant Pneumonia in Critically Ill Adults: The DEFINE study. Pharmacotherapy [Internet]. 2019 Mar 1 [cited 2021 Sep 18];39(3):253&ndash;60. Available from: https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/30101412/</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">17. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Ayukekbong, J.A., Ntemgwa, M. &amp; Atabe AN. The threat of antimicrobial resistance in developing countries: causes and control strategies. Antimicrob Resist Infect Control. 2017;6:47. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">18. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; A T, D K, K L, BM, J V, F B de C, et al. Characteristics and determinants of outcome of hospital-acquired bloodstream infections in intensive care units: the EUROBACT International Cohort Study. Intensive Care Med [Internet]. 2012 Dec [cited 2021 Sep 23];38(12):1930&ndash;45. Available from: https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/23011531/</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">19. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Pattanayak C, Patnaik SK, Datta PP, Panda P. A study on antibiotic sensitivity pattern of bacterial isolates in the intensive care unit of a tertiary care hospital in Eastern India. Int J Basic Clin Pharmacol [Internet]. 2017 Jan 30 [cited 2021 Sep 23];2(2):153&ndash;9. Available from: https://www.ijbcp.com/index.php/ijbcp/article/view/1227</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">20. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Mulatu HA, Bayisa T, Worku Y, Lazarus JJ, Woldeyes E, Bacha D, et al. Prevalence and outcome of sepsis and septic shock in intensive care units in Addis Ababa, Ethiopia: A prospective observational study. African J Emerg Med [Internet]. 2021;11(1):188&ndash;95. Available from: https://doi.org/10.1016/j.afjem.2020.10.001</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">21. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Illumina. Nextera XT DNA Library Preparation Kit [Internet]. 2022. Available from: https://emea.illumina.com/products/by-type/sequencing-kits/library-prep-kits/nextera-xt-dna.html</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">22. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Illumina. A wide breadth of sequencing applications. 2022; Available from: https://emea.illumina.com/systems/sequencing-platforms/miseq/applications.html</span></span></span></span></p>','To assess sepsis\'s clinical, microbiological, and molecular profile among ICU admitted patients at Jimma Medical Center',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,'Keywords: Sepsis, septic shock, and antimicrobial resistance',10,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'Proposal-627fcdad82e83228517996.docx');
INSERT INTO `submission` VALUES (96,114,13,20,'Background and rationale: The neonatal period is the most vulnerable time of human life for diseases. Most newborn deaths take place in low and middle-income countries. It is a significant contributor to under-five morbidity and mortality in sub-Saharan Africa including Ethiopia. \r\nObjective: To assess time to death and predictors among neonates admitted in Jimma zone hospitals of neonatal intensive care unit, southwest Ethiopia, 2022.\r\nMethods and Material: Institution-based prospective follow up study design will be conducted on neonates admitted to the neonatal intensive care unit of Jimma zone government Hospital from August 1, 2022 to January 30, 2023. Sample size for the study is calculated by using Epi-Info ver.7 for cohort study and 416 new-born will be included for the study. The sample size is proportionally allocated to jimma zone government hospitals which has neonatal intensive care unit. Interview based and chart review method of data collection will be used based on the structured questionnaires. Computer based data collection by using CSPro application will be undertaken and exported to R statistical software for analysis. Incidence of new-born mortality will be computed. Multilevel survival analysis will be applied on the hierarchical data to examine hospital variability (random effect) and to analyze covariates related to time to death of newborn. Adjusted hazard ratio with a 95% confidence interval and p-value < 0.05 will be used to declare a significant association.\r\nWork plan and Budget: the project start from July 2, 2022 to March 30, 2023 with total budget 366,779.',NULL,'Survival status and predictors of mortality among neonates admitted to neonatal intensive care unit of Jimma zone government hospitals, Ethiopia: Multilevel survival analysis','2022-05-17 18:19:51','Mainly, this study helps study participants to know the incidence of neonatal mortality and its predictors. And also, it will provide guides for policy makers and stakeholders with updated information about neonatal mortality for future planning and interventions. Hospitals with high incidence of neonatal mortality is encouraged to look in its own health care delivery system. The result will also give an insight on neonatal mortality and can be utilized as a benchmark for future researchers.','fc5c9eca1a6fd04141f9ad081ef186d1','completed','The neonatal period is defined as the first 28 days of life[1][2]. Neonatal mortality is defined as the death of newborn babies within the first four weeks of life regardless of gestational age (GA). It is classified as early neonatal mortality (ENM) which occurs before reaching the 7th day of life and late neonatal mortality (LNM) which occurs between the 7th and 28th days of life. The risk of dying is highest in the first four weeks of life and even more the closer to the birth date[3]. Despite this increased risk for all babies, babies with low birth weight (<2500 g) are at increased risk of early mortality[4].\r\nGlobally 2.4 million children died in the first month of life in 2020. There are approximately 6700 new-born deaths every day, amounting to 47% of all child deaths under the age of 5 years, up from 40% in 1990. The world has made substantial progress in child survival since 1990. Globally, the number of neonatal deaths declined from 5 million in 1990 to 2.4 million in 2020. However, the decline in neonatal mortality from 1990 to 2020 has been slower than that of post-neonatal under-5 mortality. With the increase in facility births (almost 80% globally), there is a great opportunity for providing essential newborn care and identifying and managing high risk newborns. [5][2].\r\nThe chances of survival from birth varies widely depending on where a child is born. Sub-Saharan Africa had the highest neonatal mortality rate in 2020 at 27 (25–¬¬32) deaths per 1000 live births, followed by central and southern Asia with 23 (21–¬¬25) deaths per 1000 live births. A child born in sub-Saharan Africa is 10 times more likely to die in the first month than a child born in a high-income country. Country-level neonatal mortality rates in 2020 ranged from 1 death per 1000 live births to 44 and the risk of dying before the 28th day of life for a child born in the highest-mortality country was approximately 56 times higher than the lowest-mortality country[5][6].\r\nLow birth weight has been defined by the world health organization (WHO) as weight at birth of Less than 2500 grams regardless of gestational age[7]. There are three mutually not exclusive categories: Low birth weight <2500 g, Very low birth weight <1500 g, and Extremely low birth weight <1000 g of a newborn measured within an hour after delivery and before significant extra uterine weight loss[8]. It is caused by intrauterine growth restriction, prematurity, or both[9]. The major health problems associated with low birth weight (LBW) neonatal mortality include feeding difficulties, hypoglycemia, hypothermia, pulmonary immaturity, susceptibility to infection, fluid and electrolyte imbalance, and poor immune system. Even the consequences of low birth weight continue into adulthood as increasing the risk of adult-onset chronic conditions, such as obesity and diabetes[10], [11]. \r\nAbout 71% of the neonatal deaths occurred within the first week after birth and, the cumulative death rate reached 79% in the second week. The estimated hazard ratios of mortality were higher for twins or multiple births, first order birth, male sex, birth interval shorter than 24 months, very small and vary large size neonates born to mothers younger than 20 years of age and above 34 years, and neonates whose mothers had a history of pregnancy complications compared to their respective counterparts. The risk of dying was lower for neonates whose mothers attended antenatal visits and neonates put to breast immediately upon birth[12][13].\r\nThe study conducted in Gondor comprehensive specialized hospital shows, the proportion of preterm neonatal death was 28.8%. And home delivery, neonatal respiratory distress syndrome, hyaline membrane disease, gestational age presence of jaundice and hypoglycemia at admission were found significant to neonate mortality[11][14]. \r\n1.2 Statement of the Problem\r\nChildren face the highest risk of dying in their first month of life at an average global rate of 17 deaths per 1,000 live births in 2020, down by 54 per cent from 37 deaths per 1,000 in 1990. In comparison, the probability of dying after the first month and before reaching age 1 was estimated at 11 deaths per 1,000 and the probability of dying after reaching age 1 and before reaching age 5 was estimated at 9 deaths per 1,000 in 2020. Globally, 2.4 million children died in the first month of life in 2020 – approximately 6,500 neonatal deaths every day – with about a third of all neonatal deaths occurring within the first day after birth, and close to three-quarters occurring within the first week of life[2][5].\r\nGlobally, every year 15 to 20% of newborns have LBW which counts about twenty million, of which over 96% of them were in developing countries. The risk of death was also seven times higher for LBW neonates. Low birth weight contributes to sixty to eighty percent of all neonatal deaths, annually[7], [10].\r\nThe burden of neonatal mortality in Ethiopia is 11% which is nearly half of that of SSA[15]. Another study also reported that the prevalence of neonatal death in Ethiopia was 10.4%[16]. This might be why Ethiopia was one of five countries those account for about half of all global neonatal deaths. According to the Ethiopian Demographic and health survey (EDHS) 2016, neonatal mortality showed a decline of 41% over the past two decades in Ethiopia[15], despite it was stagnated for the last 5 years and becoming 30 deaths per 1000 live births according to mini EDHS 2019[17]. The neonatal mortality was 37 per 1000 live births in the Oromia region[15].\r\nThe trend of neonatal mortality in Ethiopia was far from achieving the goals of newborn and child survival by 2030 in Ethiopia, aiming to reduce neonatal mortality to at least as low as 12 per 1000 live births. To accelerate the decline in neonatal mortality rate in Ethiopia, specific interventions would have to target key factors causing neonatal mortality like LBW[7][18].\r\nNeonatal death has mainly resulted from conditions and diseases associated with a lack of quality care at birth, or skilled care and treatment immediately after birth and in the first days of life. These are readily preventable or treatable with proven, cost-effective interventions[19]. It is possible to improve the survival and health of newborns by achieving high coverage of quality antenatal care, skilled care at birth, postnatal care for mother and baby, and care of small and sick newborns[20]. \r\nThe proportion of facility-based neonatal deaths was unacceptably high. The main cause of deaths were preventable and treatable. Hence, improving the timing and quality of antenatal care is essential for early detection, anticipating high risk newborns, and timely interventions. Furthermore, early initiation of feeding and better referral linkage to tertiary health facilities could lead to a reduction in neonatal death in this setting[14][21][22].\r\nThe proportion of facility-based neonatal mortality was 20% (95% CI: 16.7-23.8%). The causes of death were complications of preterm birth (28.58%), birth asphyxia (22.45%), neonatal infection (18.36%), meconium aspiration syndrome (7.14%), and congenital malformation (4.048%). Low birth weight, preterm births, length of stay of stay of the intensive care unit, hyperthermia, and initiation of feeding were predictors of neonatal of death among infants admitted to the neonatal intensive care units of public hospitals in eastern Ethiopia[18][22][14].\r\nThere is a paucity of information on survival status variablity among hospitals and predictors of neonatal mortality admitted NICU to inform programs and policies in Ethiopia, particularly in a study area. This significantly limits understanding of the extent and depth of the problem for evidence-based intervention. This study aimed to assess the variablity of neonate death among hospitals, survival status and predictors of mortality among neonates admitted to neonatal intensive care unit in jimma zone hospitals.','3.1. Study area and period \r\nThe study will be conducted from August 1, 2022 to December 30, 2023 among the neonates admitted intensive care units of jimma zone government hospitals. Jimma Zone has 21 districts and 2 urban administrative. There are 10 government hospitals in a zone. These are Jimma specialized hospital, Shenen Gibe primary hospital, Dedo primary hospital, Limmu Genet general hospital, Limmu Seka primary hospital, Omo Nada primary hospital, Agaro general hospital, Seka Chekorsa primary hospital, Setema primary hospital and Dimtu primary hospital. Among these three hospitals; Dimtu, Limu Seka and Seka Chekorsa primary hospitals have no neonatal intensive care centre unit. The zone has 2986957 total populations from this, 2794110 were rural dwellers and 192847 were urban dweller[23].\r\n 3.2. Study Design and Source Population\r\nInstitution-based prospective follow up study design will be conducted. All neonates admitted to intensive care units in all government hospitals of the zone will be source population.\r\n 3.2.1. Study Population\r\nAll neonates admitted to neonatal intensive care units of all government hospital in a jimma zone during data collection (five months).\r\n3.3. Inclusion and exclusion criteria \r\n3.3.1. Inclusion criteria\r\n	All neonates admitted to intensive care unit of jimma zone hospitals during study period.\r\n 3.3.2. Exclusion Criteria\r\n	Neonates who’s there family left hospital against medical advice before end of neonatal period(couldn’t get information whether survived or not within 28 complete days after birth)\r\n	Mother of neonates who are mentally or seriously ill and unable to communicate during the period of data collection and those not willing to participate in the study will be excluded.\r\n 3.4. Sample Size determination\r\nThe required sample size for this study will be determined by single population formula using neonates mortality among ICU admitted neonate proportion is 18.6 (2)% from study conducted in Amhara regional referral hospitals[24]. Using the single population formula n=  (z^2 pq)⁄d^2 , where z =1.96, d = 0.05 margin of error and considering 95% of CI, the sample size found to be 233(with 10 non-response rate, it will be 256). And sample size of neonate’s mortality due to potential predictors depend on different study conducted was calculated using double population formula as follow. \r\n\r\n\r\nVariable 	Study area	Proportion	n	n with 10% non-response rate	Reference\r\nPremature	Jimma University Medical Center	55.8	379	416	[21][25]\r\n\r\nRespiratory Distress Syndrome	Jimma University Medical Center	41	372	408	[21]\r\n\r\nPreterm neonatal death	University of Gondor comprehensive specialized hospital	28.8	315	347	[14]\r\n\r\nFinally, the sample size will be taken is 416. Even if neonate required from each hospital not admitted in study time period, all neonates admitted intensive care unit of the government hospitals in jimma zone satisfies inclusion criteria will be included to the study.  \r\n3.5. Sampling technique \r\nA stratified sampling technique will be used to take neonates in NICU for the study. We will use the 10 government hospitals in the Jimma zone as strata. Only seven hospitals have NICUs and three (Dimtu, Limu Seka and seka chekorsa) have no NICUs. The number of sample size for each hospitals was allocated proportionally based on the number of pas year admission.\r\n 3.6. Data Collection process\r\nData will be collected through chart review and face-to-face interview of the mother of a newborn admitted to the ICU. the survival status and covariates data which determine mortality of neonates(first level sampling unit) in hospital(second level), will be collected from the neonates that are nested within hospital. As the study design is prospective follow up study, data will be collected during admission to ICU and survival status of the neonates will be checked after 28 complete days.  The neonates lost to follow up from different reasons before end of neonatal period, to confirm whether they are survived or not, will be followed through phone of the family or the health extension workers. Neonatal deaths in the ICU and at home are recorded as events, if it occurred after completion of their treatment in the ICU of the neonate period. The neonates who\'s their survive status information is couldn’t confirmed after 28 complete days of birth were, will be considered as censored data. \r\nData will be collected by BSc degree holder nurse workers in ICU using questionnaires. The English version questionnaire will be adopted from WHO standard and DHS questionnaire. The data collection process will be supervised strictly by trained supervisors of the ward and principal investigators. The validity of the questionnaire is will be checked before actual data collection. To control the quality of data, in addition to training, pretest, supervision and use of suitable languages for respondent, the inter-item consistency of the indicators to measure the composite score of wealth index, BP and neonatal care practices will be checked by using Chronbach-alpha at 0.7 cut-off points. The demographic and socio-economic predictors will be collected through face-to-face interviews using an interviewer-administered questionnaire from the mother and reviewing medical record. \r\nData collection will be supported by computer assisted personal interview (CAPI) using CSPro application on the android tablet.\r\n	 Variables of the study \r\nDependent Variables\r\nThe dependent variables for this study is the time to death of neonate in ICU\r\nIndependent Variables \r\n	Independent variables are age, sex of neonate, religion, ethnicity, education status of mother, marital status, occupation type of mother, religion and residence, place of delivery, attendant of delivery, ANC frequency, birth order, low birth weight, Respiratory Distress Syndrome, birth asphyxia and birth trauma, congenital malformation, gestation age at birth, type of birth, complications during, hypothermia, hypoglycemia, family monthly income, distance to health facility, level of health facility for place of delivery, type of pregnancy, fetal presentation\r\nData Quality Assurance\r\nPrior to the start of the survey, data collectors will be updated on proper measurement and sample data collection. . A pretest will be conducted on 5 % of records to see usually recorded variables and possible amendments will be taken to the tool based on the finding. Data will be collected during the real data collection period, and regular supervisions will be carried out to monitor. \r\nData Processing and Analysis\r\nThe data collected using CSPro application will be brought to R statistical software version 4.3.0 for analysis. Descriptive statistics will be presented in tabular and graph form. Then, the findings will be presented in tables and texts. Cox proportional hazard regression model for multilevel survival data will be used to identify predictors of survival time to death of neonates. Difference in survival to death time among public hospitals in Jimma zone will be investigated. AIC also will be used for model selection. Adjusted hazard ratio (AHR) with a 95% confidence interval (CI) and p-value < 0.05 in the cox-proportional hazard regression model will be considered statistically significant.','<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">[1]&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; A. B. Post, R. Van Stolk, T. A. Broughan, and R. J. Tuthill, &ldquo;Crohn&rsquo;s disease of the gallbladder,&rdquo; <em>J. Clin. Gastroenterol.</em>, vol. 16, no. 2, pp. 139&ndash;142, 1993, doi: 10.1097/00004836-199303000-00012.</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">[2]&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; &ldquo;Neonatal mortality - UNICEF DATA.&rdquo; https://data.unicef.org/topic/child-survival/neonatal-mortality/ (accessed May 07, 2022).</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">[3]&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; A. G. A. Kolios, L. E. French, and A. A. Navarini, &ldquo;Detection of small changes in psoriasis intensity with PrecisePASI,&rdquo; <em>Dermatology</em>, vol. 230, no. 4, pp. 314&ndash;317, 2015, doi: 10.1159/000371811.</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">[4]&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; J. Katz, A. Lee, N. Kozuki, J. Lawn, S. C.-T. Lancet, and&nbsp; undefined 2013, &ldquo;Mortality risk in preterm and small-for-gestational-age infants in low-income and middle-income countries: a pooled country analysis,&rdquo; <em>Elsevier</em>, Accessed: Apr. 21, 2022. [Online]. Available: https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0140673613609939.</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">[5]&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; &ldquo;Newborn Mortality.&rdquo; https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/levels-and-trends-in-child-mortality-report-2021 (accessed May 07, 2022).</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">[6]&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; L. Alkema, F. Chao, D. You, J. Pedersen, and C. C. Sawyer, &ldquo;National, regional, and global sex ratios of infant, child, and under-5 mortality and identification of countries with outlying ratios: A systematic assessment,&rdquo; <em>Lancet Glob. Heal.</em>, vol. 2, no. 9, pp. e521&ndash;e530, 2014, doi: 10.1016/S2214-109X(14)70280-3.</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">[7]&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; H. Blencowe <em>et al.</em>, &ldquo;National, regional, and worldwide estimates of low birthweight in 2015, with trends from 2000: a systematic analysis,&rdquo; <em>Lancet Glob. Heal.</em>, vol. 7, no. 7, pp. e849&ndash;e860, Jul. 2019, doi: 10.1016/S2214-109X(18)30565-5.</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">[8]&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; R. Sutan, M. Mohtar, A. N. Mahat, and A. M. Tamil, &ldquo;Determinant of Low Birth Weight Infants: A Matched Case Control Study,&rdquo; <em>Open J. Prev. Med.</em>, vol. 04, no. 03, pp. 91&ndash;99, Mar. 2014, doi: 10.4236/ojpm.2014.43013.</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">[9]&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; R. Sutan, M. Mohtar, A. N. Mahat, and A. M. Tamil, &ldquo;Determinant of Low Birth Weight Infants: A Matched Case Control Study,&rdquo; <em>Open J. Prev. Med.</em>, vol. 04, no. 03, pp. 91&ndash;99, 2014, doi: 10.4236/ojpm.2014.43013.</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">[10]&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; S. Dessu, A. Habte, and M. Mesele, &ldquo;The Kaplan Meier estimates of mortality and its predictors among newborns admitted with low birth weight at public hospitals in Ethiopia,&rdquo; <em>PLoS One</em>, vol. 15, no. 9 September, pp. 1&ndash;17, 2020, doi: 10.1371/journal.pone.0238629.</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">[11]&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; T. A. Woelile <em>et al.</em>, &ldquo;Survival Status and Predictors of Mortality Among Low-Birth-Weight Neonates Admitted to the Neonatal Intensive Care Unit at Felege Hiwot Comprehensive Specialized Hospital, Bahir Dar, Ethiopia, 2020,&rdquo; <em>Pediatr. Heal. Med. Ther.</em>, vol. Volume 12, no. August, pp. 451&ndash;466, 2021, doi: 10.2147/phmt.s323526.</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">[12]&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; N. Wakgari and E. Wencheko, &ldquo;Risk factors of neonatal mortality in Ethiopia,&rdquo; <em>Ethiop. J. Heal. Dev.</em>, vol. 27, no. 3, pp. 192&ndash;199, Apr. 2015, doi: 10.4314/ejhd.v27i3.</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">[13]&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; &ldquo;Risk factors of neonatal mortality in Ethiopia | Ethiopian Journal of Health Development.&rdquo; https://www.ajol.info/index.php/ejhd/article/view/115319 (accessed May 07, 2022).</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">[14]&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; A. E. Yismaw, A. A. Gelagay, and M. M. Sisay, &ldquo;Survival and predictors among preterm neonates admitted at University of Gondar comprehensive specialized hospital neonatal intensive care unit , Northwest,&rdquo; vol. 3, pp. 1&ndash;11, 2019.</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">[15]&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Central Statistical Agency [Ethiopia] and ICF International, &ldquo;Ethiopia Demographic and Health Survey 2011,&rdquo; pp. 1&ndash;452, 2012.</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">[16]&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; A. Talie, M. Taddele, and M. Alemayehu, &ldquo;Magnitude of Low Birth Weight and Associated Factors among Newborns Delivered in Dangla Primary Hospital, Amhara Regional State, Northwest Ethiopia, 2017,&rdquo; <em>J. Pregnancy</em>, vol. 2019, 2019, doi: 10.1155/2019/3587239.</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">[17]&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Ethiopian Public Health Institute Addis Ababa, <em>Ethiopia Mini Demographic and Health Survey</em>, no. July. 2019.</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">[18]&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; S. Oza, J. E. Lawn, D. R. Hogan, C. Mathers, and S. N. Cousens, &ldquo;Neonatal cause-of-death estimates for the early and late neonatal periods for 194 countries: 2000&ndash;2013,&rdquo; <em>Bull World Heal. Organ</em>, vol. 93, no. 1, pp. 19&ndash;28, 2015, doi: 10.2471/blt.14.139790.</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">[19]&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; A. (Dir. . DAVID and E. (Dir. . MULLERBECK, &ldquo;Committing to Child Survival: A Promise Renewed,&rdquo; 2012.</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">[20]&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; W. Benjamin, <em>No Titיליle</em>, vol. 3. 2019.</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">[21]&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; G. A. F. Tura Koshe Haso, Ebrahim Yimam Reta, Sheka Shemsi Seid, Shemsedin Amme Ibro, Abdulwahid Awol Ahmed, Adugna Olani Akuma, &ldquo;Causes and factors associated with neonatal mortality in Neonatal Intensive Care Unit ( NICU ) of Jimma University Medical Center , Jimma , South,&rdquo; pp. 39&ndash;48, 2019.</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">[22]&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; A. Desalew <em>et al.</em>, &ldquo;Cause and predictors of neonatal mortality among neonates admitted to neonatal intensive care units of public hospitals in eastern Ethiopia: A facility-based prospective follow-up study,&rdquo; <em>BMC Pediatr.</em>, vol. 20, no. 1, pp. 1&ndash;11, Apr. 2020, doi: 10.1186/S12887-020-02051-7/TABLES/5.</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">[23]&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; A. Ababa, &ldquo;Federal Demographic Republic of Ethiopia Central Statistical Agency Population Projection of Ethiopia for All Regions At Wereda Level from 2014 &ndash; 2017,&rdquo; no. August 2013, 2017.</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">[24]&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; B. A. Mengistu, A. E. Yismaw, and Z. N. Azene, &ldquo;Incidence and predictors of neonatal mortality among neonates admitted in Amhara regional state referral hospitals , Ethiopia : prospective follow up study,&rdquo; pp. 1&ndash;14, 2020.</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">[25]&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; L. K. Ko <em>et al.</em>, &ldquo;&lsquo; We brought our culture here with us &rsquo;: A qualitative study of perceptions of HPV vaccine and vaccine uptake among East African immigrant mothers,&rdquo; <em>Papillomavirus Res.</em>, vol. 7, no. December 2018, pp. 21&ndash;25, 2019, doi: 10.1016/j.pvr.2018.12.003.</span></span></span></span></p>','To assess survival status and predictors of mortality among neonates admitted to neonatal intensive care unit of Jimma zone government hospitals, Ethiopia, 2022.',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,'neonatal mortality,predictors,NICU,Multilevel survival analysis',10,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'Proposal-6280f1242342a566099874.docx'),(97,354,13,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'23ff0ea593aed054e26d87e4e845bde6',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,NULL,0,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),(98,336,13,15,'Background: Globally, over 20% of 17.9 million cardiovascular diseases (CVDs) related mortality were attributable to smoking annually. In Ethiopia, smoking was responsible for nearly 15.5% of the approximately 81,000 CVD-related deaths recorded in 2017, and smoking prevalence varied from 5.3 to 17.5% among patients with CVD. Despite the reality that trials and implementation of various smoking cessation techniques involving smokers with CVDs in high-income countries have shown an increase in smoking abstinence rates and reduced the multidimensional burden of smoking, studies in low- and middle-income countries, including Ethiopia, are very limited. \r\nObjective: To evaluate the effect of telephone-aided counseling on smoking cessation in patients with cardiovascular disease at the cardiac follow-up clinic of Jimma medical center, Southwest Ethiopia.\r\nMethods: A two-arm unblinded randomized control trial will be conducted involving 146 (n=73 in each group) smokers with cardiovascular disease attending at cardiac follow-up clinic of Jimma medical center, from June 2022 to June 2023. Based on the eligibility criteria, participants will be recruited over a month and then, randomly assigned to the intervention or control group (IG/CG). Then, the IG will receive 5 sessions of telephone aided smoking cessation counseling, each session lasting 30-45 minutes, from trained counselors over 3 months, while the CG will receive standard cardiac follow-up care. To gather the required data, face-to-face interviews, patient chart review, telephone-based counseling and follow up-interview will be carried out. After 3 months of the intervention/standard cardiac follow-up care, data collectors will conduct structured telephone-based follow-up interviews for each of the participants in IG and CG at 7 and 30-day as well as 3 and 6-month to measure smoking status to record the 7 and 30-day self-reported prevalence of abstinence as well as 3 and 6-month self-reported prolonged abstinence, respectively. Data will be entered into Epi data version 4.2.0 and exported to STATA 15.0 for analysis. Intention-to-treat and complete-case analyses will be employed to evaluate the study hypotheses. Two-sided tests will be used in all analyses with a significance level of < 0.05.',NULL,'The effect of Telephone-aided counseling on smoking cessation in patients with cardiovascular disease at cardiac follow-up clinic of Jimma medical center: A randomized control trial','2022-05-28 20:19:56','Overall, quitting smoking is an important preventive health measure for both first-hand and second-hand smokers to reduce premature death, diseases, and healthcare costs (20, 22). Finding a solution is a critical national and global health priority as the burden of smoking-related diseases such as CVDs rises, impacting treatment outcomes both directly and indirectly. As a result, our findings will highlight a strategy for treating smoking in CVD patients (34) as well as a possible approach to meet a 2017 Ethiopian tobacco control plan (35) and WHO-FTC goal (24) through using mobile phones. Furthermore, the findings will be serve as an input to establish cigarette quitline programs at Jimma Medical Center and on national scale (20) by gathering important information that:\r\n•	Helps to know and compare the effect of telephone-aided counseling on 7-day self-reported point prevalence smoking abstinence compared with the standard cardiac follow-up care.\r\n•	Helps to know and compare the effect of telephone-aided counseling on 30-day self-reported point prevalence smoking abstinence compared with the standard cardiac follow-up care.\r\n•	Helps to know and compare the effect of telephone-aided counseling on 3-month self-reported prolonged smoking abstinence compared with the standard cardiac follow-up care.\r\n•	Helps to know and compare the effect of telephone-aided counseling on 6-month self-reported prolonged smoking abstinence compared with the standard cardiac follow-up care. \r\n•	It will give an insight on the effect of telephone-aided counseling on the number of cigarettes smoked per day at the 7th day, 30-day, 3-month, and 6-month follow-up.','4040c72161cd2a5a37f38eaf22db5339','completed','Tobacco smoking is the world\'s largest public health issue; by 2021, nearly 1.3 billion people used tobacco, killing over 8 million people and costing the global economy US$ 1.4 trillion (1-3). Tobacco contains more than 250 hazardous and addictive compounds (4), which are responsible for millions of people worldwide developing preventable diseases such as cancer, cardiovascular diseases (CVDs), respiratory diseases, mental illness, and so on (1, 3, 5-7). Low- and middle-income countries (LMICs), including Ethiopia, bear more than 80% of the global smoking burden (1). According to the 2016 Global Adult Tobacco Survey (GATS), over 3.4 million Ethiopian adults were smokers (8), resulting in over 17,000 deaths and costing the country 240 million Birrs per year (9). \r\nAccording to a World Health Organization (WHO) report in 2021, cigarette smoking is the leading risk factor for CVDs and related fatalities worldwide, accounting for nearly 20% of the 17.9 million CVD deaths annually (10, 11). The burden of smoking-related CVDs varies significantly among regions, with LMICs accounting for over 75% of the total (10). In Africa, a 2019 meta-analysis found that the hospital-based pooled prevalence of smoking among hypertension patients was 12.9%, with 11.8% and 11.6% in Sub-Saharan and East Africa, respectively (12). In Ethiopia, cross-sectional studies found that tobacco use among CVD patients ranged from 5.3% to 17.5% (13-16), which was higher than the general population prevalence (i.e.5.0%) (8). Studies from Jimma medical center revealed the highest smoking prevalence (17.5%) among stroke patients (14), while the total smoking rate among CVD patients was 15.8% (15). Overall, of over 81,000 CVDs attributable fatalities in Ethiopia in 2017, about 15.5% of them were smokers (17, 18). \r\nEven though successful smoking cessation in patients with CVD improves the quality of life, improves cardiovascular function, lowers the risk of stroke and coronary heart disease, lowers mortality, and reduces health expenditure (19-23), Nicotine, one of the chemicals in tobacco, has a dependency and addictive properties that make quitting smoking difficult (4). As a result, various smoking cessation supportive methods, such as nicotine replacement therapy (NRT), medication (bupropion/varenicline), psychotherapy/counseling and quitlines, telephone aid tobacco cessation programs, applications, and websites, have emerged and are available for smokers who intend to quit (24, 25). In the general population around the world, using these smoking cessations supportive techniques resulted in a 55% smoking quit rate, however, the success rate without any help is as low as 3 % after 6-12 months of follow-up (26, 27).  \r\nLikewise, the effectiveness of different smoking cessation strategies involving smokers with CVD has been reported in high-income nations. For instance, in a randomized clinical trial comprising 687 peripheral artery disease outpatients in America, patients receiving comprehensive advice/counseling reported a 21.3% 6-month self-reported abstinence rate, compared to 6.8 % in the control group (28). Another randomized clinical trial in America involving 100 smokers with myocardial infarction using telephone counseling resulted in a 67% abstinent rate compared with a 43% in the control group at a 6-month follow-up (29). A cohort study in Canada including 168 cardiovascular patients receiving inpatient and telephone support counseling found a 41.5 % 6-month smoking cessation rate, compared with 20% in the usual treatment group (30). A randomized control clinical trial encompassing 1860 cardiac patients in Hong Kong (China) found no significant difference in self-reported abstinence rates at 7 and 30 days after intervention employing three sessions of face-to-face counseling versus brief counseling (31). \r\nAlthough most African countries, including Ethiopia, have ratified the WHO Framework Convention on Tobacco Control (WHO-FCTC), which includes measures like raising tobacco prices, advertising and promotional bans, smoking restrictions, public education, and offering support to quit smoking (32), studies on the effectiveness of various smoking cessation supports are scarce in the continent. Furthermore, WHO-FCTC encourages countries to invest in developing and implementing smoking cessation programs to reduce tobacco use by 30% between 2010 and 2025, enabling 152 million tobacco users to successfully quit by 2030, saving millions of lives and contributing to long-term economic growth (24, 33). \r\nIn Ethiopia, despite the Ministry of Health declaring smoking cessation as a target for the management of CVD in 2016 (34) and increasing access to smoking cessation services was one of the national Tobacco Control Strategic Plan in 2017 (35), smoking cessation-supporting programs and services are severely limited (36). This is supported by a 2016 GATS finding that less than 2.0% of 1.3 million current smokers who attempted to quit smoking in the previous year succeeded without receiving major smoking cessation assistance (8). As a result, establishing and integrating cost-effective technology-aided smoking cessation techniques into healthcare system should be prioritized (37). \r\nThus as an opportunity for telephone owner expansion, telephone-based smoking cessation counseling is an emerging, less expensive, and widely accessible technology for reaching smokers (38). It will be a feasible and economical tool to help smokers quit and avoid relapse in low-income countries like Ethiopia (39). Taking advantage of this opportunity, and given the paucity of evidence on the effectiveness of smoking cessation programs in Ethiopia, testing and implementing telephone-based counseling could be an input to achieve a national tobacco control strategic plan (35) as well as to meet the WHO-FCTC target (20). \r\nThus, the purpose of the study is to evaluate the effectiveness of telephone-aided counseling on smoking cessation in patients with cardiovascular disease who are on cardiac follow-up at Jimma Medical Center. The study hypothesized that telephone-assisted smoking cessation counseling leads to higher rates of 7 and 30-day self-reported point prevalence of smoking abstinence, as well as 3 and 6-month self-reported prolonged smoking abstinence when compared to standard cardiac follow-up care.','Study setting and period \r\nThe study will be conducted at the cardiac follow-up clinic of Jimma Medical Center, Jimma zone, Oromia, Southwest Ethiopia. It is located 352 kilometers from Addis Ababa, the capital. The medical center provides services for more than 15 million populations. The cardiac follow-up clinic provides a service for over 5,000 patients (4000-adult, and 1000-children) with CVDs such as hypertension, heart failure, stroke, myocardial infarction, peripheral arterial disease, valvular heart disease, cardiomyopathy, deep vein thrombosis, etc. The study will be conducted from June 2022 to June 2023.\r\nStudy design 	\r\nA two-arm unblinded randomized control trial will be employed. The control group (CG) will receive standard cardiac follow-up care, whereas the intervention group (IG) will receive smoking cessation counseling via telephone call in addition to standard cardiac follow-up care. \r\nPopulation\r\nTarget population\r\nAll smokers with CVD and on cardiac follow-up at Jimma Medical Center.\r\nStudy population \r\nAll smokers with CVD and on cardiac follow-up fulfilling the inclusion criteria during the study period. \r\nEligibility Criteria\r\nInclusion criteria:\r\n	Age ≥18 years. \r\n	Patients who are current smokers, according to Center for Disease Control  definition, an individual who has smoked 100 cigarettes in his or her lifetime and who currently smokes cigarettes (40). \r\n	Patients with seeking cardiac follow-up care for her/his cardiovascular disease.    \r\n	Patients willing to participate in the study. \r\nExclusion criteria:\r\n	Patients currently using smoking cessation treatment including electronic cigarettes, nicotine replacement therapy (NRT), or other \r\n	Patients with comorbid-psychiatric diseases diagnosed by a responsible physician at a follow-up clinic \r\n	Does not own a mobile phone.\r\nSample size estimation and sampling techniques \r\nThe sample size is determined by using two independent sample size determination formula for the randomized clinical trial (41) with the following assumptions: a previous trial that reported the efficacy of telephone counseling for smoking cessation in adults with myocardial infarction (29) results in a 24% difference in 6-month prolonged smoking abstinence rates between the control and the intervention group at 6-months follow up assessment (43% versus 67%, respectively), with the assumption of 80% power, alpha of 5%, 1:1 enrollment ratio, the attrition rate of 10%. The final sample size will be 146 smoker adults (n = 73 in each study group) with CVD. A systematic sampling technique will be employed to enroll participants within the first month (since most cardiovascular patients have a regular follow-up every month at the cardiac clinic of JMC) of the study period. With the following consideration of a 15.8% proportion of current smokers among smoker patients with cardiovascular disease in a previous study at Jimma Medical Center (15), the estimated number of smoker patients with CVDs is 632 (15.8%*4000). Of this, to get 146 study participants, every 5th (632/146) smoker patients with CVDs seeking cardiac follow-up care will be recruited accordingly. \r\nRandomization, and allocation\r\nAn independent person will serially code each of the study participants, and the code will then be entered into the randomization software program, i.e. Random number generator (42). Then, participants will be randomly allocated to the interventional or control group (IG/CG) by the software in a 1:1 ratio.\r\nData collection tool and procedure \r\nThe data collection tool will be designed after reviewing relevant literatures. The tool will be comprised of participants\' socio-demographic and smoking-related data, medical condition and medication profile, interventional counseling topics, and the outcome measurements part. The data collection tool will be translated into the two dominant local languages (Afaan Oromo and Amharic). Eight-day training will be provided for the data collectors (nurses) and counselors (psychiatry nurses). During the recruitment (the 1st month of the study), the selected study participant’s baseline socio-demographic, smoking-related, medical condition, and medication profile data will be collected. Accordingly, face to face interviews using a structured questionnaire will be conducted to record the baseline socio-demographic and smoking-related data such as participant\'s phone number, sex, age, monthly income, educational level, occupation, alcohol consumption, Khat chewing, number of cigarettes smoked a day, age when the patient started smoking, years smoking, the stage of readiness to quit smoking, previous smoking quit attempts, and nicotine dependency, while the patient’s chart will be reviewed to assess the medical condition and medication profile. After each participant\'s allocation to the IG and CG, in the next 3 months, the interventional group will receive 5 sessions of telephone-based smoking cessation counseling in addition to standard cardiac follow-up care, while the control group will receive standard cardiac follow-up care. Then, in the next 6 months, data collectors (nurses) will conduct structured telephone-based follow-up interviews for each of the participants in IG and CG at 7-day, 30-day, 3-month, and 6-month to measure smoking status according to the 7-day self-reported prevalence of abstinence, 30-day self-reported prevalence of abstinence, 3-month self-reported prolonged abstinence, and 6-months self-reported prolonged abstinence, respectively.\r\nControl group\r\nFollowing a briefing about the study\'s objectives and a follow-up schedule, participants will only receive their standard cardiac follow-up care.\r\nIntervention group\r\nParticipants will receive telephone-based smoking cessation counseling in addition to their standard cardiac follow-up care after being briefed on the study\'s objectives, counseling sessions, and a follow-up schedule. Over three months, a trained psychiatric nurse will deliver a standardized 5-session telephone counseling on smoking cessation per participant (each session lasting 30-45 minutes) on the 1st day, 1 week, 15 days, and 1 and 3 months post-allocation (43). During telephone counseling, motivational Interviewing (MI) and a cognitive-behavioral skill-building approach will be employed (44). The MI approach is a flexible interview guide that adapts to the stages of motivation and preferences of the participants. The main goal of MIs is to encourage people to quit smoking by motivating them and making it easier for them to stick to their decision. Participants will be encouraged to establish a favorable environment for quitting smoking by learning about typical methods for overcoming cigarette cravings (45). Another way to help smokers handle their challenges is to use a skill-building strategy. Smokers will be encouraged to sort out primary barriers to quitting and keeping quit, to identify practical solutions, and to implement and assess these thoughts to improve their skills.\r\nThe counseling intervention will, in general, follow the 5A stages, a systematic approach currently recommended by the WHO (46) and included in the Ethiopian smoking cessation policy as well (47) which has 5 discrete elements: (i) Ask about smoking at every contact (ii) Advise the patient to quit smoking (iii) Assess readiness to quit (iv) Assist the patient in quitting, and (v) Arrange follow-up. A list of topics for counseling is set in each counseling session, in addition to considering each patient\'s needs (according to table 1) (48, 49). \r\nTable 1: An overview of tobacco cessation counseling topic during the telephone aided interventional period. \r\nSession of counseling during the interventional period	Counseling topics \r\nSession 1 (on the 1st day)	•	Reasons to quit tobacco use\r\n•	Discuss the risks of smoking (hypertension, heart attack, stroke, COPD, cancer, and other heart diseases) \r\n•	Benefits of quitting like saving money, improved health, taste, sense of smell and appearance, and being a positive role model for children/family.\r\n•	Reason for smoking and ways of quitting\r\n•	Identify barriers to stopping tobacco smoking and possible solutions \r\n•	Encouraged to set a quit date \r\nSession 2 (on the 1st  week)\r\n	•	If he/she set a quit date, counsel on the following point (if not, return to session 1)\r\n•	Withdrawal symptoms and social support\r\n•	Dealing with stress, anger, anxiety, and depression coping strategies\r\nSession 3 (on 2nd  week) 	•	Tobacco-free lifestyle and dealing with high-risk situations\r\n•	Maintenance counseling for smoking cessation\r\nSession 4 (on 1st  month) 	•	Participants’ feelings, cessation attempts, challenges experienced, and how they coped\r\n•	Maintenance counseling for smoking cessation\r\nSession 5 (on 3rd  month)  	•	Maintenance counseling for smoking cessation\r\n\r\nStudy Variables	\r\nCovariates/independent Variables\r\nBaseline socio-demographic, medical, medication, and smoking-related variables: sex, age, income, educational level, occupation, alcohol consumption, khat chewing, medical condition, number of medications taking, years on cardiac follow-up, number of cigarettes smoked a day, age when the patient started smoking, years smoking, the stage of readiness to quit according to readiness to quit ladder (RTQ), previous quit attempts, nicotine dependency according to Fagerström\'s test, and. During the recruitment session, participants\' motivation to quit smoking will be assessed using a Readiness to quit ladder (RTQ) (50). A 10-point RTQ Likert scale questionnaire will be used to measure motivation to change smoking behavior i.e. ranging from 1 = \"I have decided to continue smoking\" to 10 = \"I have already quit smoking.\" The tool was validated in the previous studies including from Dire Dawa, Ethiopia, and illustrated the ladder is associated with cognitive and behavioral indices of readiness to consider smoking cessation (51, 52). The scale will be divided into 2 subgroups, the low-motivated one including the pre-contemplation (not thinking about quitting) phases and the high-motivated one containing the “contemplation (thinking about quitting but not ready to quit), preparation (getting ready to quit), action (quitting), and maintenance (remaining a non-smoker) phases. The baseline nicotine dependency will be assessed by using the six items of Fagerström\'s test (53). The Fagerstrom Test questionnaire has four yes/no items that are scored from 0 to 1 and two multiple-choice items are scored from 0 to 3. The items are summed to yield a total score of 0-10. As the total Fagerström score increased, the patient\'s physical dependence on nicotine is more intense. The Fagerstrom test questionnaire was used and validated in a previous study in Ethiopia (52). \r\nOutcomes/dependent variables\r\n	Point prevalence of self-reported smoking abstinence (at 7 and 30-day follow-up). \r\n	Self-reported prolonged smoking abstinence (at 3 and 6-month follow-up).\r\n	Change in the number of cigarettes smoked per day in each follow-up interval.\r\n  Outcome measurements \r\nThe outcome will be measured using the most extensively used and approved outcome measures in clinical studies of smoking cessation, which are self-reported point prevalence abstinence and prolonged abstinence over a 6-month follow-up period after the intervention ends (54-56). \r\nAccordingly, the primary outcome will be the rate of 6-month self-reported prolonged smoking abstinence after the end of 3-month intervention (for the interventional group) and after 3-month standard cardiac follow-up care (for the control group). Prolonged abstinence is a more stable and better proxy for lifelong abstinence and health benefit, and it has a better temporal relationship to intervention than point prevalence abstinence (54). Whereas, the secondary outcomes will be the rate of (1) 7-day self-reported point prevalence smoking abstinence, (2) 30-day point prevalence smoking abstinence, (3) 3-month self-reported prolonged smoking abstinence, and (4) the reduction in the number of smoking between each follow-up interval. All of the outcomes will be measured through participants\' self-report by using structured follow-up telephone interviews (according to table 2). \r\nTable 2: Session of follow up and outcome data’s to be collected in both the interventional and control group \r\nSession of follow-up -\r\nafter 3 months of intervention/standard cardiac follow-up care 	Activities- interviewing by asking structured questions\r\n	Data’s to be collected \r\nSession 1 (at the 7th -day)	1.	Have you smoked cigarettes, even a puff, in the past 7 days?”\r\n2.	If the response is yes for Q1- have you made any attempt to quit smoking? If yes, No of attempt_________\r\n3.	If the response is yes for Q1-how many cigarettes you smoke daily____	•	7-day self-reported point prevalence abstinence\r\n•	No of quit attempts made in the past 7-days\r\n•	Amount of cigarettes  smoked daily \r\n\r\nSession 2 (at the 30th -day)	1.	Have you smoked cigarettes, even a puff, in the past 30- days?”\r\n2.	If the response is yes for Q1- have you made any attempt to quit smoking? If yes, no of attempt_________\r\n3.	If the response is yes for Q1-how many cigarettes you smoke daily____	•	30-day self-reported point prevalence abstinence\r\n•	No of quit attempts made in the past 30-days\r\n•	Amount of cigarettes  smoked daily \r\n\r\nSession 3 (at 3rd-month)	1.	Have you smoked cigarettes, even a puff, in the past 3-months?”\r\n2.	If the response is yes for Q1- have you made any attempt to quit smoking? If yes, no of attempt_________\r\n3.	If the response is yes for Q1-how many cigarettes do you smoke daily____	•	3-month self-reported prolonged abstinence\r\n•	No of quit attempts made in the past 3-months \r\n•	Amount of cigarettes  smoked daily \r\n\r\nSession 4 (at 6th -month )	1.	Have you smoked cigarettes, even a puff, in the past 6-months?”\r\n2.	If the response is yes for Q1- have you made any attempt to quit smoking? If yes, no of attempt_________\r\n3.	If the response is yes for Q1-how many cigarettes you smoked daily____	•	6-month self-reported prolonged abstinence\r\n•	No of quit attempts made in the past 6-months \r\n•	Amount of cigarettes  smoked daily \r\n\r\n	\r\nData management and statistical analysis\r\nData will be entered into Epi data version 4.2.0 and exported to STATA 15.0 for analysis. The categorical variables will be presented by using frequency and percentage, and parametric continuous variables will be described using mean and standard deviations (SD), whereas, for non-parametric continuous variables, median and percentiles will be used. The baseline participant’s characteristics will be analyzed across the two groups by using the chi-square/Fisher exact test for categorical variables and the Student t-test for continuous variables. Thus, the IG and the CG will be compared in different moments of the follow-up. In case of any significant differences at baseline, confounding variables will be included in subsequent analyses. In addition to randomization, we will use bivariate and multivariate logistic models to control for any confounding variables. To evaluate the study hypotheses, an intention-to-treat analysis will be undertaken, in which all subjects assigned to a condition will be included. In addition, a complete-case analysis will be performed, which will include only participants who have outcome data on all assessment. Two-sided tests will be used in all analyses with a significance level of < 0.05. \r\nData quality assurance\r\nThe following will be made to assure data quality\r\n•	Training for the data collectors and counselors will be provided\r\n•	Participants will be made familiar with the data collection tool and procedures.\r\n•	Before data collection, the questionnaire will be distributed to internal medicine specialists, psychologists, and psychiatrists for potential amendment.\r\n•	Data completeness and accuracy will be checked regularly.\r\nPlan for dissemination and ensuring utilization of findings\r\nThe finding of the study will be presented to the Institute of health, JMC, Ministry of Health, and as well to concerned professional associations, like the Ethiopian pharmaceutical association (EPA), the Ethiopian public health association, and others. The finding will be presented at national and international scientific conferences and will be published in peer-reviewed reputable journals.\r\nResearch project team \r\nThe project team is formed from multiple disciplines involving pharmacists, a psychologist, a psychiatrist, and an internist. The team will execute the research as described in this project.\r\nEthical Considerations\r\nThe ethical approval and clearance will be obtained from the Institutional Review Board (IRB) of Jimma University. Written informed consent will be requested from each study participant following explaining the objective of the study. Upon collection of data and completion of the study, all raw data will be kept strictly confidential. The patient will be informed about their full right not to participate and as well as leave the study at any time during the study period.','<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">1.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; WHO. Tobacco: WHO; 2021 [cited 2022 April 2]. Available from: https://<a href=\"http://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/tobacco\" style=\"color:#0563c1; text-decoration:underline\">www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/tobacco</a>.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">2.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Spohr SA, Nandy R, Gandhiraj D, Vemulapalli A, Anne S, Walters ST. Efficacy of SMS text message interventions for smoking cessation: a meta-analysis. Journal of Substance Abuse Treatment. 2015;56:1-10.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">3.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; WHO. The economics of tobacco: World Health Organization 2022 [cited 2022 April 2]. Available from: https://<a href=\"http://www.euro.who.int/en/health-topics/disease-prevention/tobacco/publications/the-economics-of-tobacco\" style=\"color:#0563c1; text-decoration:underline\">www.euro.who.int/en/health-topics/disease-prevention/tobacco/publications/the-economics-of-tobacco</a>.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">4.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Despain JSaL. Tobacco and Chronic Disease: Wyoming Prevention Depot; 2019 [cited 2022 April 2]. Available from: https://wysac.uwyo.edu/wyomingtobacco/2019/09/19/tobacco-and-chronic-disease-2/.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">5.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; WHO. WHO report on the global tobacco epidemic 2021: addressing new and emerging products: WHO; 2021 [cited 2022 April 2]. Available from: https://<a href=\"http://www.who.int/publications/i/item/9789240032095\" style=\"color:#0563c1; text-decoration:underline\">www.who.int/publications/i/item/9789240032095</a>.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">6.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Prabhat Jha MM, Frank J Chaloupka, Ayda Yurekli, Chintanie Ramasundarahettige, Krishna Palipudi, Witold Zatońksi, Samira Asma, and Prakash C Gupta. Global Hazards of Tobacco and the Benefits of Smoking Cessation and Tobacco Taxes: The International Bank for Reconstruction and Development / The World Bank; 2015. Available from: https://<a href=\"http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK343639/\" style=\"color:#0563c1; text-decoration:underline\">www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK343639/</a>.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<