 cigarette smoking in 2 and 6 months in Lebanon. Journal of Research in Health Sciences. 2017;17(2):379.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">52.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Girma E, Assefa T, Deribew A. Cigarette smokers&#39; intention to quit smoking in Dire Dawa town Ethiopia: an assessment using the Transtheoretical Model. BMC Public health. 2010;10(1):1-7.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">53.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Heatherton TF, Kozlowski LT, Frecker RC, Fagerstrom KO. The Fagerstr&ouml;m test for nicotine dependence: a revision of the Fagerstrom Tolerance Questionnaire. British journal of addiction. 1991;86(9):1119-27.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">54.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Hughes JR, Carpenter MJ, Naud S. Do point prevalence and prolonged abstinence measures produce similar results in smoking cessation studies? A systematic review. Nicotine &amp; Tobacco Research. 2010;12(7):756-62.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">55.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Hughes JR, Keely JP, Niaura RS, Ossip-Klein DJ, Richmond RL, Swan GE. Measures of abstinence in clinical trials: issues and recommendations. Nicotine &amp; Tobacco Research. 2003;5(1):13-25.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">56.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; NIHR. Glossary: Cochrane Tobacco Addiction; 2003 [cited 2022 April 21]. Available from: https://tobacco.cochrane.org/resources/glossary.</span></span></p>','To evaluate the effect of telephone-aided counseling on smoking cessation in patients with cardiovascular disease at cardiac follow-up clinic of Jimma medical center.',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,'cardiovascular disease,Smoking cessation,Telephone aid counseling,Randomized control trial',10,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'Proposal-62925921402f6170845085.docx'),(99,356,14,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'0b193e21e86ec4d55c791d364a97d94b',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,NULL,0,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),(100,357,13,19,'Background: To control microorganisms and the transmission of healthcare acquired infections, a variety of environmental cleaning agents are available for use in healthcare facilities. However, quaternary ammonium compounds, alcohol, sodium hypochlorite, and hydrogen peroxide are the most commonly used cleaning chemicals, especially in low- and middle-income countries. Because of the differences in each cleaning solution and the benefits and drawbacks of their prospective usage in healthcare, sodium hypochlorite is the cleaner of choice. This is because sodium hypochlorite has broad-spectrum antibacterial activity and is commercially available at a low cost.\r\nObjectives: To evaluate the quality and surface cleaning efficacy of different brands of sodium hypochlorite solutions used in health facilities in Jimma zone, Oromia Regional State, Ethiopia. \r\nMethods: The samples will be collected from all health facilities available in Jimma zone and the laboratory work will be conducted in Jimma University. To evaluate the quality cleansing agents, different quality control parameters of physical inspection, identification test, assay of the active ingredient, and pH will be performed. And to check microbial cleansing efficacy, P. aeruginosa, S. aureus, E. coli and candida albicans will be used. In addition, samples collected from health facilities will cultured and cleansing efficacy of sodium hypochlorite will be evaluated\r\nResults and discussion: Will be discussed depending on the output of the experimental work.\r\nBudget requested: 750,172.5 will required for undertaking of the study.',NULL,'Quality of cleansing agents used in health facilities and its role for the development of antimicrobial resistance: A case of Jimma zone','2022-05-15 18:08:48','Identification of poor and good quality sodium hypochlorite available in Jimma zone\r\n	Cleansing activity of sodium hypochlorite on cultured microorganism from hospitals\r\n	Cleansing Activity of sodium hypochlorite onlaboratory standard microorganism of pseudomonasaeruginosa, Escherichiacoli, Candidaalbicans and staphylococcusaureus','5a05bdd41a703e02a22e776b5684f2b0','completed','Sodium hypochlorite is the cleaning product of choice for controlling microorganism in healthcare facilities because it has broad-spectrum antibacterial activity and commercially availability at a low cost(1).Chlorine (Cl2) is the active ingredient, and it possesses oxidizing properties (17). Depending on temperature and pH, chlorine exists in solution in two forms: hypochlorite ion (OCl-) and hypochlorous acid (HOCl) (2).  Moreover, their presence in sodium hypochlorite solutions is critical for successful disinfection in healthcare facilities (3).\r\nHand hygiene, environmental worker training, and surface cleaning and disinfection are effective infection prevention and control (IPC) techniques that can prevent a high percentage of healthcare-associated infections (HAI) in healthcare facilities (4). Despite these efforts, HAIs remain a problem in clinical settings, especially in low- and middle-income nations (5). As a result, the approach to microorganism control in healthcare facilities may be called into question and evaluated (6).\r\nTo control microorganisms and the transmission of HAIs, a variety of environmental cleaning agents are available for use in healthcare facilities. However, quaternary ammonium compounds, alcohol, sodium hypochlorite, and hydrogen peroxide are the most commonly used cleaning chemicals, especially in low- and middle-income countries. Because of the differences in each cleaning solution and the benefits and drawbacks of their prospective usage in healthcare, sodium hypochlorite is the cleaner of choice. This is because sodium hypochlorite has broad-spectrum antibacterial activity and is commercially available at a low cost.\r\nDespite cleaning with sodium hypochlorite solution, research suggests that multidrug resistance (MDR) bacteria are prevalent in healthcare settings (5). The issue could be linked to low-quality sodium hypochlorite solutions used in clinical settings, which might exacerbate HAIs (3). Thus, the presence of low-quality sodium hypochlorite solution might explain the difficulty in controlling MDR bacteria in healthcare settings (7).\r\nSodium hypochlorite is the cleaning agent of choice in Ethiopia, as it is in many other low- and middle-income countries, to regulate a load of microorganisms in healthcare environments. Sodium hypochlorite solution is available in concentrations of 0.5 % (w/v) or more than 5 %(w/v). Furthermore, Ethiopian national infection prevention guidelines recommend using a 0.5 % (w/v) sodium hypochlorite solution to clean and disinfect healthcare facility surfaces (8).\r\nThe Ethiopian Food and Drug Administration (EFDA) is responsible for registering antiseptics and disinfectants and conducting regulatory assessments (9). A study conducted in Addis Ababa reported that Antiseptics failed to meet the country\'s regulatory requirements. Furthermore, the EFDA did not inspect manufacturers or evaluate the quality of antiseptics on the market regularly (10). As a result, cleansing agents like sodium hypochlorite solution might not be regulated strictly. \r\nThe amount of available chlorine in commercially available sodium hypochlorite solutions varied widely, according to studies evaluating the quality of sodium hypochlorite solutions, with the majority of samples having less available chlorine than the stated value (11)(12). As a result, sodium hypochlorite products on the market are either unregulated or are subject to less stringent rules.Similarly, previous research assessed the efficacy of sodium hypochlorite solution against microorganisms that cause HAIs. Accordingly, using sodium hypochlorite solution at lower-than-optimal concentrations can lead to the evolution of antibiotic-resistant microorganisms(13). Furthermore, routine cleaning using a random content of available chlorine in healthcare facilities has been demonstrated to be ineffectual in preventing and eradicating harmful microbes(14). \r\nThe availability of low-quality sodium hypochlorite solution might be due to non-stringent control of disinfection and antiseptic (11). In developing countries, disinfectants are subject to less stringent regulation because many resource-constrained national regulatory authorities place a higher priority on ensuring pharmaceutical quality. As a result, quality control systems and strict manufacturing standards are rarely implemented to ensure product quality and efficacy (11). A study conducted in Kenya(15) and Gambia revealed that in a quality test of commercially available bleaches, all of the samples failed to fulfil the acceptable standards for the contents (4-6 % w/v) (16).','3.	Material and methods \r\n3.1.	Study area and period\r\nThe sample will be collected from all health facilities available in Jimma zone.  Jimma is the capital town of Jimma zone. Jimma town is located in southwest Ethiopia, 352 kilometers from the capital Addis Ababa. Jimma Town is a commercial center for pharmaceutical transactions in the southwest region of Ethiopia. Samples will becollected from both government and privately owned health facilities, and the laboratory work will be conducted in Jimma University.\r\n3.2.	Sample collection\r\nThe samples will be collected from all hospitals (public and private) available in the Jimma zone. Health centers and private clinics will be selected using simple random sampling techniques, and at least one-third of health centers and private clinics available in the zone will be included. \r\nAll available brands of sodium hypochlorite will be collected purposively from all health facilities included in the study. After both health facilities and data collectors have given their agreement, the samples will be collected by trained data collectors. One brand will be sampled from a health facility. However, if the sample is only available in a single health facility, there is a chance of sampling two or more brands from such a facility.\r\nTo obtain appropriate bulk and laboratory samples, the protocol stated in the WHO and FAO pesticide requirements shall be followed. A bulk sample for end-user sodium hypochlorite solution containers of 0.8 L or 1L is obtained (200 mL from each sample per brand) and merged. However, each bulk sample from a 5L container should comprise material from the top, middle, and bottom of the container. After that, a laboratory sample (200 ml from each bulk sample) is taken for additional laboratory examination.\r\n3.3.	Chemical and reagents\r\nSodium bicarbonate, sodium thiosulphate pentahydrate, sodium carbonate, potassium dichromate, potassium iodide, and glacial acetic acid, hydrochloric acid, calcium hydroxide will be used. All chemicals used will be analytical grade chemicals.\r\n3.4.	Test microorganisms and media\r\nStaphylococcus aureus, pseudomonasaeruginosa, Escherichiacoli and candidaalbicans, and Bacto TM Trypticase soy Broth, Soybean Casein Digest Agar and phosphate buffer will be used.\r\n3.5.	Equipment\r\nIncubator plus Series, colony counter, Bante pH meter, Textile electronic balance, Olympus CX23 microscope, and Maxi Mix II sonicator will be used. \r\n3.6.	Quality evaluation parameters\r\nThe physicochemical quality of sodium hypochlorite was evaluated following Food and Agriculture Organization (FAO) and WHO 2015 guidelines and the US pharmacopeia methods. \r\n3.6.1.	Visual inspection\r\nAfter collection, all samples will be visually inspected for packaging, labeling information (i.e. product name and active ingredient content, net content of the pack, batch number, storage advice, manufacturing company, expiry date, and manufacturing date), and physical characteristics using a checklist based on 2015 Food and Agricultural Organization (FAO) and World Health Organization (WHO) guidelines on pesticide labeling practice.\r\n3.6.2.	Identification test\r\nRed litmus paper was immersed in a sodium hypochlorite solution (2 ml from each laboratory sample). The color change from red to blue, then white, was visually observed. \r\n3.6.3.	Determination of available chlorine\r\nPrimary standard potassium dichromate will be accurately weighed (210 mg), placed in a glass-stoppered flask, and then dissolved in water (100 ml). The stoppered will be removed, and quickly potassium iodide (3 gm), sodium bicarbonate (2 gm), and hydrochloric acid (5 ml) will be added to the flask. The flask will be allowed to stand in dark for 10 minutes. The iodine liberated from the reaction will be titrated with sodium thiosulphate solution (0.1 N). \r\nThe endpoint of the titration, the discharge of blue color, will be detected using a freshly prepared starch test solution. The factor, N, of 0.1N Na2S2O3 solution versus standardization will be calculated using the chemical reactions and formula given below.  \r\nK2Cr2O7 + 6KI + 14HCl ↔ 2CrCl3 + 8KCl + 7H2O + 3I2.............................................eq.1\r\nI3-+2S2O3-2 ↔ 3I- + S4O6-2…………………………………………...………………..eq.2\r\nN = mg of potassium dichromate / 49.033 * ml of sodium thiosulphate……..…...…..eq.3\r\n3.6.4.	Assay of available chlorine in samples\r\nThe sample solution will be weighed accurately (3 ml), placed in a glass-stoppered flask, and then will be diluted with distilled water (50 ml). Potassium iodide (2 gm) and 6 N acetic acid (10 ml) will be added to the flask, and the flask will be allowed to stand in dark for 10 minutes. The iodine liberated from the reaction will be titrated with sodium thiosulphate solution (0.1 N). The endpoint of the titration, the discharge of blue color, was detected using a freshly prepared starch test solution. \r\nThe overall reactions and formula for determining the content of available chlorine in samples are given in the equations below.\r\nNaOCl (aq) +2CH3COOH (l) + 2KI(s) → NaCl (aq) + 2CH3COOK (aq) + H2O (l) + I2….....eq.4\r\nI2(g) + 2Na2S2O3(aq) → 2NaI(aq) + Na2S4O6 (aq)................................................................eq.5\r\nConcentration of available chlorine (% w/v) = {(titer volume of sodium thiosulphate * Factor * Equivalence) / sample volume} * 100……………..….……….……………eq.6\r\nIn this reaction, two moles (n) of Na2S2O3 were reacted with one mole of iodine (I2) liberated from one mole of sodium hypochlorite solution. As a result, each ml of 0.1 N sodium thiosulphate consumed was equivalent to 3.723 mg sodium hypochlorite or 3.545 mg available chlorine.\r\n3.6.5.	Determination of the pH value of a sample\r\nA few parts of the sample solution will be used to rinse the pH and temperature sensors of the pH meter. The sample solution will be then precisely weighed (2 ml) and transferred to a beaker. After that, the pH and temperature sensors will be completely submerged in the sample solution. Finally, the mean values of the sample solution pH and temperature will be calculated using three readings.\r\n3.7.	Antimicrobial cleansing activity of sodium hypochlorite\r\nThe surface cleaning efficacy of sodium hypochlorite solution will be evaluated using the Organization for Economic Co-operation and Development (OECD) method. 10μL test organism (P. aeruginosa, S. aureus, E. coli and candida albicans) will be placed in the center of each carrier. The carriers will be then dried for 30 to 50 minutes at 22 ± 2°C in a desiccation unit. After drying, inoculated carriers will be placed in a sodium hypochlorite sample solution (10 ml) that will be equilibrated at 22 ±2 °C and exposed for 10 minutes.\r\nThe carriers will be removed and placed in neutralizing broth (10 ml) tubes, with the neutralized test tube containing the carrier being recorded as the 100 dilution. After 30 minutes of neutralization, a serial dilution of 10-1will be made and filtration with 0.2 μm membrane filters will be initiated. The membrane filters will then incubated on Trypticase soy agar (TSA) at 36 ± 1°C for a minimum of 72 ± 4 hours and the number of colonies will be counted. The above procedure will be employed for the control test also. A phosphate buffer solution will be used instead of a sodium hypochlorite solution.\r\n3.7.1.	Samples selection for testing antimicrobial cleansing efficacy\r\nBased on the results of the physicochemical quality of sodium hypochlorite samples, one brand with good quality and one brand with low quality will be chosen for evaluating the surface cleaning efficacy of sodium hypochlorite.','<h1 style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:14pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Cambria,&quot;serif&quot;\"><span style=\"color:#365f91\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">References </span></span></span></span></span></h1>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">1. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Estrela C, Estrela CRA, Barbin EL, Span&oacute; JCE, Marchesan MA, P&eacute;cora JD. Mechanism of action of sodium hypochlorite. Braz Dent J. 2002;13(2):113&ndash;7. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">2. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Dash T, Mohan R, Mannava Y, Thomas M, Srikanth N. Effect of storage temperature and heating on the concentration of available chlorine and pH of 2.5% sodium hypochlorite. Saudi Endod J. 2017;7(3):161&ndash;5. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">3. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Rutala WA, Cole EC, Thomann CA, Weber DJ. Stability and Bactericidal Activity of Chlorine Solutions. Infect Control Hosp Epidemiol. 1998;19(5):323&ndash;7. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">4. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; CDC. Best Practices for Environmental Cleaning in Healthcare Facilities : in Resource-Limited Settings. Div Healthc Qual Promot. 2019;1&ndash;91. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">5. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Almatroudi A, Gosbell IB, Hu H, Jensen SO, Espedido BA, Tahir S, et al. Staphylococcus aureus dry-surface biofilms are not killed by sodium hypochlorite: Implications for infection control. J Hosp Infect. 2016;93(3):263&ndash;70. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">6. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; K&ouml;hler AT, Rodloff AC, Labahn M, Reinhardt M, Truyen U, Speck S. Efficacy of sodium hypochlorite against multidrug-resistant Gram-negative bacteria. J Hosp Infect [Internet]. The Healthcare Infection Society; 2018;100(3):e40&ndash;6. Available from: https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jhin.2018.07.017</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">7. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Ali A, Elshimy A. The effect of exposure to sub-inhibitory concentrations of hypochlorite and quaternary ammonium compounds on antimicrobial susceptibility of Pseudomonas aeruginosa. AJIC Am J Infect Control. Elsevier Inc.; 2018; </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">8. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Federal Ministry of Health Ethiopia. Infection Prevention for Healthcare Facilities in Ethiopia: Disease Prevention and Control Department Addis Ababa, Ethiopia. 2004;(July). </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">9. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Ethiopian food and drug authority. Guideline for Registration of Antiseptics and Disinfectants. 2021;(March). </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">10. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Selam MN. Hand sanitizers marketed in the streets of addis ababa, ethiopia, in the era of covid-19: A quality concern. Risk Manag Healthc Policy. 2020;13:2483&ndash;7. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">11. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Lantagne DS. Viability of commercially available bleach for water treatment in developing countries. Am J Public Health. 2009;99(11):1975&ndash;8. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">12. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Ahmida N, Ahmida A, El-zwaey RS, Towier NH. Determination of Active Constituents of Some Household Cleaners Available in Benghazi Markets. J Appl Chem. 2021;10 (2)(April):179&ndash;88. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">13. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Christenson EC, Cronk R, Atkinson H, Bhatt A, Berdiel E, Cawley M, et al. Evidence map and systematic review of disinfection efficacy on environmental surfaces in healthcare facilities. Int J Environ Res Public Health. 2021;18(21). </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">14. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Pereira SSP, de Oliveira HM, Turrini RNT, Lacerda RA. Disinfection with sodium hypochlorite in hospital environmental surfaces in the reduction of contamination and infection prevention: A systematic review. Rev da Esc Enferm. 2015;49(4):675&ndash;81. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">15. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Wachira SN. Assessment of the Quality of Sodium Hypochlorite and Hydrogen Peroxide Products in Nairobi City County. 2019;(November). </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">16. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Marong F, Iyekowa O, Oyelakin O, Anthony A, Mendy M. Titrimetric Analysis of Commercial Bleach a Gambia Case Study. African J Chem Educ. 2020;10(January):146&ndash;52. </span></span></span></span></p>','To evaluate the quality and surface cleaning efficacy of different brands of sodium hypochlorite solutions used in health facilities in Jimma zone, Oromia Regional State, Ethiopia',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,'Quality, Sodium hypochlorite, Antimicrobial resistance, Cleansing agent, Jimma Zone, Ethiopia',10,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'Proposal-628116b6a7efd361458553.docx'),(101,149,13,15,'All test','Subtitles','Test submission of mega call','2022-05-22 11:09:19','Test2','3e6144de3d932261fd92ff5f26c62b52','completed','Tets','Test2','<p>Test2</p>','General',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,'Test,test12',5,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'Proposal-6290d686df162190240223.docx'),(102,150,13,18,'Executive Summary \r\nBackground: Diabetes is largely escalating and becoming burden of global health problem which is a common cause of morbidity, mortality and disability. Out of two adults living with diabetes, one is not aware of his/her condition and is at great risk of devastating health complications which can be prevented through diagnosis and appropriate disease management. Researches done in western countries reported the benefit and effectiveness of group mindfulness-based cognitive therapy (MBCT) in reducing depression, diabetic specific distress, and improved glycemic control, physical activity and dietary control. However, in Jimma University Medical Center, Ethiopia, despite several study results showed the prevalence of depression and diabetes specific distress and its adverse consequence among type 2 diabetic patients, there is no evidence about the applicability and effectiveness of MBCT among patients with type2 diabetes. Thus, research mega proposal is developed to address the aforementioned gap.\r\nObjective: the goal and objective this research mega project is to investigate effectiveness of mindfulness-based cognitive therapy (MBCT) in reducing depression and improving glycemic control among type 2 diabetic patients at Jimma University Medical Center.\r\nMethods: A two arm, randomized, controlled trial study design will be used to compare the MBCT group with control group. Eligible 344 of the study type 2 diabetic patients will be selected using simple random sampling technique from the eligible total screened population. Enrolment, randomization, and double blinding will be done. Outcome variables will be assessed three times at baseline, mid-line MBCT, and end-line MBCT phases. Collected data will be treated by epi data and SPSS and analyzed using paired t-test, repeated measures, ANCOVA, and linear regression. Significance will be declared at p-value ≤0.05. \r\nRequested money: a total 787,060.00 birr is needed to conduct the research project.',NULL,'Effectiveness of mindfulness-based cognitive therapy in reducing depression and improving glycemic control among type 2 diabetic patients at Jimma University Medical Center: Randomized controlled trial','2022-05-27 14:45:05','This research mega project is expected to show us whether or not the non-pharmacological intervention of Group Mindfulness-Based Cognitive Therapy (MBCT) is effective in helping type 2 diabetic patients’ master MBCT skills, improve their depression symptoms, blood sugar level, diabetic specific distress, diabetes medication adherence, physical activity, and dietary control. Besides, the study will show how structured non-pharmacological intervention could be helpful for type 2 diabetic outpatients','2eea7212913064ca7170f430b22fcdfe','completed','3.	Background and Rationale \r\nDiabetes is largely escalating and becoming burden of global health problem; which is a common cause of morbidity, mortality and disability. Globally, nowadays, more than 422 million people are living live with diabetes, the majority living in low-and middle-income countries, and 1.5 million deaths are directly attributed to diabetes each year (1). The number of people living with diabetes estimated to rise to 570 million by 2030 and to 700 million by 2045 (2). From two adults living with diabetes, one is not aware of his/her condition and is at great risk of devastating health complications which can be prevented through diagnosis and appropriate disease management (2). World Health Organization (WHO) reports that, as Agenda of Sustainable Development, by 2030, UN Member States outlined ambitious target downgrade premature mortality from diabetes by one third; achieve universal health coverage; and provide access to affordable essential medicines (3).\r\nIn Ethiopia, a meta-analysis study showed that the prevalence of depression among diabetic patients was 39.73%. Specifically, in Oromia, prevalence of depression among diabetes patients was 52.9% (4). \r\nSeveral studies were done in southwest Ethiopia reported significant adverse impact of depression, diabetic specific distress, and health related quality of life complications among type2 diabetic patients. For instance, in 2014, a study done in Jimma medical center reported that the prevalence of depression among diabetes patients was 43.6%. Besides, in terms of gender, it reported that 52.7% and 36% prevalence of depression among females and males, respectively. Presence of at least one diabetes complication and older age > 55 years were found to be independent predictor of depression among people with diabetes (5). Another study done in 2019 at Jimma medical center reported that presence of depression symptoms led complicated health related quality of life (6) and deteriorated treatment adherence (7)  of type 2 diabetic patients.\r\nFurthermore, having depression symptoms as well as diabetic specific distress exposed diabetic patients into lower quality of life (8) and worsened their negative appraisals of insulin therapy (9).  Particularly, ample studies reported such adverse consequences of depression symptoms such as poor self-care behaviors (10), lower glycemic control (11), adverse cardiovascular outcomes, and higher mortality rates (12, 13). \r\nAvailable randomized control trial documents in developed world reported group mindfulness-based cognitive therapy (MBCT) effectiveness among type2 diabetic patients. For a stance a study done in Netherlands 2013 showed that MBCT more significantly reduced depressive symptoms, diabetic specific distress, and improved quality of life among type diabetic patients in MBCT group than to control group (14). Another meta-analysis, in eight random controlled studies, reported that MBCT significantly improved glycosylated hemoglobin (HbA1c), blood sugar level, dietary control, physical activity, and reduced diabetes-related distress and depression symptoms (15). Another randomized control trials reported that MBCT among women type2 diabetes lowered the ruminative thoughts, difficulties in emotion regulation and perceived stress (16). Other studies also showed that MBCT had significant positive impact glycemic control in diabetic patients that led them to have better mental well-being, improved quality of life (17), attention regulation skills and psychological functioning (18). However, in Ethiopia, little is known about the applicability and effectiveness of MBCT in patients with type2 diabetes.\r\nThe rationale behind this research project is that, in Ethiopia, although ample study results reported that type 2 diabetes, depression, and diabetic specific distress are highly co-existed chronic public health problem that is adversely complicating type 2 diabetic patient’s physical, mental, social and spiritual life, there is no clinical trial study on effectiveness of alternative non-pharmacological interventions such as group Mindfulness-Based Cognitive Therapy (MBCT) done among type2 diabetic patients with depression, diabetic-specific distress, dietary control and physical activity problems. However, several studies done in western countries reported the significance effectiveness of MBCT among type2 diabetic patients. Thus, in return, this research project will help us to evaluate the outcome of effectiveness of MBCT intervention among type2 diabetic patient status change in Ethiopian context. Besides, this research mega project will further strengthens the visions of national, and WHO and UN goals on reducing diabetes premature mortality by one third by 2030 (3) by introducing non-pharmacological intervention MBCT and promoting adaptive coping skills to depression and diabetic specific distress among type 2 diabetic patients.','4.	Methodology (Methods and Materials)\r\n4.1.	Study setting and period \r\nThe study will be conducted at Jimma University Medical Center (JUMC), the largest provider of outpatient Chronic Clinic in Jimma Zone, Oromia regional state, from September 3rd, 2022 to May 30th 2023. Jimma town is found at 352 km from Addis Ababa, capital city of Ethiopia in the southwestern part of the country. JUMC is the only teaching and referral hospital in southwestern part of the country and providing specialized clinical services to about 15 million people in the catchment area.  Jimma University has made relentless efforts in extensive renovation and expansion work to make the hospital conducive for service, teaching and research activities. According the recent report by Jimma University Medical Center (JUMC) Chronic Clinic, from April 2013 Ec. April 2014 Ec., around 470 and 5,640 type 2 diabetes patients attended JUMC chronic clinic outpatient department monthly and yearly, respectively.\r\n4.2.	Study design\r\nA two arm, randomized, controlled trial will be conducted to compare the MBCT group with non-MBCT group.\r\n4.3.	Population\r\n4.3.1.	Source population:\r\nAll type 2 diabetic patients who are on follow up at Jimma University Medical Center.\r\n4.3.2.	Study population\r\nSelected type 2 diabetic patients who are on follow up that fulfilled eligibility criteria during the study period\r\n4.4.	Inclusion Criteria \r\n•	Age 18 and above\r\n•	Type 2 diabetic patients who are on follow up treatment\r\n•	Mild to moderate depression symptom  (Beck Depression Inventory score of 14-28)\r\n•	Type 2 diabetic patients who are on follow up for at least six months \r\nRandomization to MBCT group and control group will be done after the patient pass study eligibility criteria and baseline assessment.\r\n4.5.	Exclusion criteria\r\n•	Severe depression symptoms (Beck Depression Inventory score of 29-63)\r\n•	Below 18 years age \r\n•	those seriously ill and unable to communicate, participate in the study\r\n•	Diabetic patients type 1\r\n•	 with history of major diagnosis of psychotic disorders, bipolar disorder, substance abuse/ dependence, organic brain syndrome (based on self-report and chart review) that would interfere with study participation or consent, \r\n•	Diabetic patients  type 2 who are not on medication follow up\r\n4.6.	Sample size Determination and Sampling Technique\r\nThe study sample size is determined at α error probability= 0.05, effect size d=0.5, Power (1-β err prob) = 0.80 (19, 20), which is 143 for each group. By adding 20% non-response rates, the final sample size is 344. Thus, 344 eligible type 2 diabetes patients will be included in the study. These patients will be selected using simple random sampling technique whereby study participants will be randomly selected from the total screened and eligible type 2 diabetic patients.\r\n4.7.	Screening procedures\r\nIn this phase, two trained BSc nurses will screen study participants based on eligible criteria at JUMC Chronic clinic. Study criteria fulfilling type 2 diabetic patients will be selected using cconsecutive method from total source population of 5,772 and will be screened based on depression symptom (using Beck Depression Inventory (BDI-II)) (21) level from September 10th 2022 to December 10th 2022. Daily, on average 15-21 type2 diabetic patients are supposed to attend JUMC. Thus, those type 2 diabetic patients with BDI-II score of 14 to 28 will have the chance to take part in the study if they are selected based on simple random sampling technique. However, those diabetic with severe depression (BDI-II score of 29 to 63), seriously ill and unable to communicate,  having major diagnosis of psychotic disorders, bipolar disorder, substance abuse/ dependence, organic brain syndrome (which will be extracted from patient card)  will be excluded in the study.\r\n4.8.	Enrolment\r\nFrom the total screened eligible type2 diabetic population, 344 type 2 diabetic study participants with mild to moderate depression (i.e. BDI-II score of 14-28) will be selected using simple random sampling technique and, then, they will have baseline assessments, and random enrolment into either MBCT group or control group.\r\nRandomization: those 344 diabetes patients with mild to moderate depression (or BDI-II score of 14 to 28) will be randomly assigned, in blocks of two by one senior psychiatrist and one senior Clinical psychologist (who will not involve in MBCT intervention study), stratified by severity of depression symptom and blood sugar level. Enrolment will be done between December 11th and 12th 2022.\r\nBlinding: double blind will be employed. During enrolling type 2 diabetic patients in to the MBCT group or control group, the person doing the allocation will be hidden. Study participants and data collectors will remain blind to study assignment during the baseline, and two post MBCT intervention visits. \r\n4.9.	 Data collection procedures and Outcome Assessments and \r\nOutcome assessments: the research mega project questionnaire will consist socio-economic-demographic factors, clinical factors, substance related items, and standard scales on such outcome variables as level of depression, blood sugar level, diabetic specific distress, diabetes medication adherence, physical activity, dietary control. The assessments will be done three times: at baseline, 4 weeks of mid-line MBCT intervention and 8 weeks of end-line MBCT intervention. \r\nBaseline assessment (Time 0, T0): will be done from September 10th 2022 to December 10th 2022. Eligible type 2 diabetic patients will be assessed by study questionnaire and blood sample colleting procedures. In this phase, study populations will be selected using simple random technique from source population. Thus, within the subsequent 3 months stay, assigned professionals will screen eligible patients from the total type2 diabetic patients, and then data collectors assess and collect blood samples from those randomly selected study populations.\r\nData will be collected by face-to-face interview technique. Data collectors will be trained by project teams for 8 days on study tools, assessment procedures, study ethical aspects and relationship with study participants. Data will be collected by 8 trained BSc nurses, who had experience on data collection and those who know the Afaan Oromo and Amharic languages and culture of the study participants. Data collection procedure will be supervised by 2 supervisors.\r\nMid-line MBCT outcome (Time 1, T1) assessments: will be done from January 15th 2023 to February 7th 2023 after 4 weeks of MBCT intervention. Type2 diabetic patients in both MBCT group and control group will be assessed their depression level, blood sugar level, and diabetic specific distress. In this phase, assessment will focus on such changes that the study intervention mid-line outcome variables as depression symptom level and diabetic specific distress.\r\nEnd-line MBCT outcome (Time 2, T2) assessments: will be done from March 10th 2023- April 3rd 2023 after the total 8 weeks of MBCT intervention. Type2 diabetic patients in both MBCT group and control group will be assessed by same study questionnaire as used during baseline assessment phase. In this phase, both primary outcome and secondary outcome variables together with independent factors will be assessed. Besides, same data collection procedures and supervision method will be used.\r\n4.10.	Description of Mindfulness-Based Cognitive Therapy (MBCT)\r\nStandard MBCT treatment manual will be used based on the protocol developed by Segal, Teasdale and Williams, 2002 (22).\r\nMBCT Type 2 diabetic patients group will receive 8 sessions MBCT. Each session will last for 2 hours long and will be conducted once a week.  The treatment manual is prepared in English, will be translated into culturally feasible such as Afaan Oromo and Amharic. The linguistic adaptation of the intervention is to be fully described in a separate report; including adjustments in both content and process.\r\nDuring the final session, an evaluation of the therapy experience will be carried out with the participant to identify strengths and successes achieved. Recommendations related to follow up and areas to continue working on will be discussed with the participant.\r\nMBCT providers will be those experienced and MBCT certified two clinical psychologists with knowledge of study participant’s Afaan Oromo and Amharic languages and cultures. All therapists will be trained by licensed MBCT therapist and study personnel for 8 days. MBCT providers will have daily clinical debriefings with project personnel.\r\nEach type2 diabetic patient in each MBCT group will receive 8 subsequent MBCT sessions.\r\nSupervision will focus both on maintaining fidelity and ensuring flexibility to address the cultural issues and problems.\r\nDetails of the SOP of MBCT are to be attached later.\r\nUsual Care\r\nAll study participants will continue to receive type 2 diabetes medical care at JUMC Chronic Clinic as usual.\r\nType2 diabetic patients who report other psychiatric symptoms, in between MBCT intervention, will typically be first evaluated by the primary care provider or psychiatrists; and reports will also be documented. Patients who are judged to pose risk to themselves or to others due to psychiatric symptomatology will be immediately referred to emergency care.\r\n4.11.	Variables and measurements \r\n4.11.1.	Primary outcome\r\n4.11.1.1.	Level of depression\r\nIn this research project, level of depression will be measured by Beck Depression Inventory (BDI-II). Beck Depression Inventory (BDI-II) will be used to screen the presence and the severity of depressive symptoms among type 2 diabetic patients. The BDI-II was developed in 1996 and was derived from the BDI. The 21-item is scored on a scale of 0-3 in a list of four statements arranged in increasing severity about a particular symptom of depression, bringing the BDI–II into alignment with DSM–V criteria. A score of 0 to 13 is considered as normal, 14 to 19=mild depression, 20 to 28= moderate depression, and 29 to 63=severe depression. The internal consistency was described as around 0.9 and the retest reliability ranged from 0.73 to 0.96 (21).\r\n The BDI-II is a sufficiently reliable and valid measure for assessing depression among type2 diabetic patients with reliability (α = 0.89) (5). \r\nIn this study, BDI-II score of 14 to 28 or mild to moderate level of depression will be considered for group mindfulness-based cognitive therapy intervention.\r\n4.11.1.2.	Diabetic specific distress\r\nDiabetes-specific distress will be measured by Problem Areas In Diabetes (PAID) survey. This scale contains 20 items about common negative feelings related to living with diabetes (e.g., “Feeling depressed when you think about living with diabetes,” “feeling discouraged with your diabetes regimen”) (23). The items will be rated on a 6-point Likert scale (1 “not a problem”; 6 “a serious problem”).  PAID scores will be organized to 0-100 scale (24) indicating a higher score as more distress type 2 diabetic patients, and with cutoff score of 40 indicates seriously elevated diabetes distress (25).\r\n4.11.2.	Secondary outcome\r\n4.11.2.1.	Diabetes Medication Adherence\r\nIn this research project, diabetes medication adherence will be measured by Morisky Medication Adherence Scale (MMAS-8). This scale is rated on yes/no responses. It is widely used self-administered and validated tool among type 2 diabetes patients (26). This scale also widely used in Ethiopia among type2 diabetic patients (27). \r\nAdherence is defined as the extent to which a type 2 diabetes patient’s behavior— taking medication, following a diet, and/or executing lifestyle changes—corresponds with agreed recommendations from a health care provider. \r\nAdherent: patients who score 0 based on Morisky Medication Adherence Scale-8 (MMAS-8) (26).\r\nNon adherent: patients who score 1–8 based on Morisky Medication Adherence Scale-8 (MMAS-8) (26).\r\n4.11.2.2.	Physical activity\r\n\r\nPhysical activity will be measured based on the global recommendation of 150 minutes or more of moderate-to-vigorous intensity aerobic activity per week or at least 30 min/day (28). Typ2 diabetic patient will be regarded as non-adherent to physical activity if he/she exercises less than 150 minutes per week.\r\n4.11.2.3.	Dietary control \r\nDietary Control of study participants will be measured by 10 close-ended items before the study period. Each item will be rated in either of the three point scale, Likert scale, (always=2, sometimes=1 or never=0). Scores will be summed, arranged 0 to 20; good adherence if the score is >15, partial adherence if the score is 10-15, and non-adherence if the score is <10.\r\n4.11.2.4.	Blood sugar level\r\nThe study participant’s blood sugar level (glycemic control) will be measured using Fasting Blood Sugar (FBS) and HbA1c.\r\nHbA1c test: HbA1c will be used to measure average blood glucose (29, 30). The effect of MBCT intervention and better control of blood sugar will be noted with decrease of HbA1c. For HbA1c, glycemic status will be considered as good if HbA1c level is <7% or poor if its ≥7% (31).\r\nFasting Blood Sugar (FBS) will be considered as controlled if its level is ≤140 mg/dl or uncontrolled if its level is >140 mg/dl (32).\r\nSamples will be collected and analyzed according to the standards.\r\nBody mass index (BMI): Body mass index (BMI) will be categorized as normal weight if BMI is 18.5–24.9, Underweight if BMI is < 18.5, overweight if BMI is 25–29.9 kg/m2, and obese if BMI is ≥ 30 kg/m2 based on the World Health Organization criteria (33).\r\n\r\n\r\n4.11.2.5.	Serum Serotonin level\r\nAll 172 MBCT group type2 diabetic patients’ serum serotonin will be measured two times during the study period.\r\nSerotonin analysis will be performed using venous blood (with a sample volume of at least 10 mL of serum), collected in a vacutainer without anticoagulant. After harvesting, the serum will be separated by centrifugation. The processing will be done by high-pressure liquid chromatography (34). \r\nThus, serotonin level will be determined using chromatographic technique. Further semi quantitative values will be enumerated using electro blotting. \r\nAliquots will be separated into Nunc tubes. More laboratory procedures will be conducted according to SOP.\r\n4.11.3.	Independent variables\r\n4.11.3.1.	Socio-demographic variables: include age, sex, educational status, religion, occupation etc, which will be extracted using closed-ended questions. \r\n4.11.3.2.	Clinical variables: include vital signs, diabetes characteristics,  duration and treatment, co-morbid medical and mental conditions\r\n4.11.3.3.	Substance Use Variables \r\nThree month substance use status on Khat, Alcohol, Shisha or Ganga, etc\r\n4.12.	Operational Definition:\r\n•	Depression disorder: Beck Depression Inventory (BDI-II) score of 14 to 28 is to be considered as having depression (which is mild to moderate) and supposed to get involved in the MBCT intervention (21).\r\n•	Blood sugar level: glycemic status will be considered as good if HbA1c level is <7% or poor if its ≥7% (31).\r\n•	Diabetic specific distress: a higher Problem Areas In Diabetes (PAID) score indicates as more distress among type 2 diabetic patients, and with cutoff score of 40 indicates seriously elevated diabetes distress (25)\r\n•	Diabetes medication adherence: Adherent: patients who score 0 based on Morisky Medication Adherence Scale-8 (MMAS-8) (26). Non adherent: patients who score 1–8 based on Morisky Medication Adherence Scale-8 (MMAS-8) (26).\r\n•	Physical activity: Typ2 diabetic patient will be regarded as non-adherent to physical activity if he/she exercises less than 150 minutes per week (28).\r\n•	Dietary control: good adherence if the score is >15, partial adherence if the score is 10-15, and non-adherence if the score is <10.\r\n4.13.	Data management and statistical analysis \r\nFilled questionnaires will be checked for completeness and consistency of the responses. Then, data will be entered into Epi-Data version 3.1, exported to SPSS version 20.0 software for analysis. Data will be analyzed using ANCOVA, paired T-test and repeated measure of analysis. \r\nBivariate and multivariate linear regression will be employed to examine the predicting factors of outcome variables. A variable with p-value less than ≤0.05 will be used as cutoff point for statistical significance\r\n6.	Capacity building Plan\r\nTwo master ICCMH students will be involved in the research project\r\n8.	Ethical considerations\r\nEthical approval will be obtained from Jimma University Institute of Health Research Ethical Review Board. Formal letters will be written to Jimma University Medical Center (JUMC).  Study participants will be informed about the details of the study and written consent will be obtained. Participation in the study will be completely voluntary and refusal to respond to some of the questions or therapy session or interruption from the study will be possible at any time when the study participant wants to have it. All the information obtained from the study participants will remain confidential and anonymous by using code numbers. Participants will be informed that there will not be any financial benefit to be given for taking part in the study. \r\nDuring the screening phase, type 2 diabetic patients with severe depression symptoms in BDI-II score of 29-63 will be linked to psychiatry clinic for further psychiatric assessments and treatment.  All study participants will continue to receive diabetic mellitus medical care and treatment at Jimma Medical Center Chronic Clinic as usually is done.','<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><strong><span style=\"font-size:14.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">References</span></span></strong></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<ol>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><a href=\"https://www.who.int/health-topics/diabetes#tab=tab_1\" style=\"color:blue; text-decoration:underline\"><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">https://www.who.int/health-topics/diabetes#tab=tab_1</span></span></a> </span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Saeedi P, et al. IDF Diabetes Atlas Committee. Global and regional diabetes prevalence estimates for 2019 and projections for 2030 and 2045: Results from the International Diabetes Federation Diabetes Atlas, 9th edition. Diabetes Res Clin Pract 2019;157:107843</span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">WHO. Global report on diabetes, 2016.</span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Henok Mulugeta Teshome, et al. The Prevalence of Depression among Diabetic Patients in Ethiopia: A Systematic Review and Meta-Analysis, 2018: </span></span><em><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Hindawi Depression Research and Treatment; Volume 2018, Article ID 6135460, 8 pages. </span></span></em><a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1155/2018/6135460\" style=\"color:blue; text-decoration:underline\"><em><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">https://doi.org/10.1155/2018/6135460</span></span></em></a> </span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Dejene S, Negash A, Tesfay K, Jobset A, Abera M (2014) Depression and Diabetes in Jimma University Specialized Hospital, Southwest Ethiopia. <em>J Psychiatry 17: 126 doi: 10.4172/1994-8220.1000126</em>.</span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Muze M, Hailu E, Woldemichael K, Fekecha B (2017) Health Related Quality of Life And its Associated Factors Among Diabetic Patients Attending Diabetes Clinic in Jimma University Teaching Hospital, Ethiopia,2014. <em>J Diabetes Metab 8: 751. </em></span></span><em><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">doi:10.4172/2155-6156.1000751</span></span></em></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Gebrehiwot Teklay, Jemal Hussien and Dawit Tesfaye. Non-adherence and associated factors among type 2 diabetic patients at Jimma University Specialized Hospital, Southwest Ethiopia, 1<sup>st</sup> October 2013. <em>J.Med.Sci., 13(7):578-584</em></span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Schram MT, Baan CA, Pouwer F. Depression and quality of life in patients with diabetes: a systematic review from the European Depression in Diabetes (EDID) research consortium.<em> Curr Diabetes Rev 2009;5:112&ndash;119</em></span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Makine C, Karsida_g C, Kadio_glu P, et al. Symptoms of depression and diabetes specific emotional distress are associated with a negative appraisal of insulin therapy in insulin-na&iuml;ve patients with type 2 diabetes mellitus. A study from the European Depression in Diabetes [EDID] Research Consortium.<em> Diabet Med 2009; 26:28&ndash;33</em></span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Gonzalez JS, Peyrot M, McCarl LA, et al. Depression and diabetes treatment nonadherence: a meta-analysis.<em> Diabetes Care</em> <em>2008;31:2398&ndash;2403</em></span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Lustman PJ, Anderson RJ, Freedland KE, de Groot M, Carney RM, Clouse RE. Depression and poor glycemic control: a meta-analytic review of the literature<em> Diabetes Care 2000;23:934&ndash;942</em></span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Egede LE, Nietert PJ, Zheng D. Depression and all-cause and coronary heart disease mortality among adults with and without diabetes.<em> Diabetes Care 2005;28:1339&ndash;1345</em></span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Bogner HR, Morales KH, Post EP, Bruce ML. Diabetes, depression, and death: a randomized controlled trial of a depression treatment programfor older adults based in primary care (PROSPECT). <em>Diabetes Care 2007;30:3005&ndash;3010</em></span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Jenny Van Son, Ivan Nyklicek, Victor J. Pop, Marion C. Blonk, Ronald J. Erdtsieck, Pieter F. Spooren, Arno W. Toorians, And Fran&ccedil;ois Pouwer. The Effects of a Mindfulness-Based Intervention on Emotional Distress, Quality of Life, and HbA1c in Outpatients With Diabetes (DiaMind): A randomized controlled trial. <em>Diabetes Care 36:823&ndash;830, 2013</em></span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Yun-xia Ni, Lin Ma, and Ji-ping Li. Effects of mindfulness-based intervention on glycemic control and psychological outcomes in people with diabetes: A systematic review and meta-analysis. <em>J Diabetes Investig 2021; 12: 1092&ndash;1103</em>.</span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Razavizadeh Tabadkan BZ, Jajarmi M, Vakili Y. [The Effectiveness of Mindfulness-based Cognitive Therapy on Ruminative Thoughts, Perceived Stress and Difficulties in Emotion Regulation of Women with Type 2 Diabetes (Persian)].<em> Iranian Journal of Psychiatry and Clinical Psychology. 2019; 24(4):370-383. </em></span></span><a href=\"http://dx.doi.org/10.32598/ijpcp.24.4.370\" style=\"color:blue; text-decoration:underline\"><em><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">http://dx.doi.org/10.32598/ijpcp.24.4.370</span></span></em></a> </span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Arab sheibani KH,Alipor A, Zare H, Poursharifi H, . The impact of mindfulness-based cognitive therapy (mbct) on mental and physical well-being indicators in patients with type2 diabetes. <em>Zahedan J Res Med Sci (ZJRMS) 2013; 15</em></span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Maya J. Schroevers,&nbsp; K. Annika Tovote &amp; Joost C. Keers, Thera P. Links,&nbsp; Robbert Sanderman, and&nbsp; Joke Fleer Individual Mindfulness-Based Cognitive Therapy for People with Diabetes: a Pilot Randomized Controlled Trial. <em>Mindfulness, 14 August 2013. DOI 10.1007/s12671-013-0235-5</em></span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Cohen, J. (1988). Statistical power analysis for the behavioral sciences. <em>Hillsdale, New Jersey: Lawrence Erlbaum Associates.</em></span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Armitage, P., Berry, G., &amp; Matthews, J. (2002). Statistical methods i</span></span><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">n medical research (4th edition </span></span><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">ed.). <em>Blackwell Science Ltd.</em></span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Wang Y-p, Gorenstein C. Psychometric properties of the Beck Depression Inventory-II: a comprehensive review. <em>Revista Brasileira de Psiquiatria2013; 35:416-31</em></span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Segal ZV, Williams MG, Teasdale JD. Mindfulness-based cognitive therapy for depression: a new approach to preventing relapse. <em>New York: Guilford Press; 2002</em></span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Polonsky WH, Anderson BJ, Lohrer PA, et al. Assessment of diabetes-related distress.<em> Diabetes Care 1995; 18:754&ndash;760</em></span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Snoek FJ, Pouwer F, Welch GW, Polonsky WH. Diabetes-related emotional distress in Dutch and U.S. diabetic patients: cross cultural validity of the problem areas in diabetes scale.<em> Diabetes Care 2000;23:1305&ndash;1309</em></span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Hermanns N, Kulzer B, Krichbaum M, Kubiak T, Haak T. How to screen for depression and emotional problems in patients with diabetes: comparison of screening characteristics of depression questionnaires, measurement of diabetes specific emotional problems and standard clinical assessment.<em> Diabetologia 2006; 49:469&ndash;477</em></span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Morisky DE1, Ang A, Krousel-Wood M, Ward HJ. Predictive validity of a medication adherence measure in an outpatient setting.<em> J Clin Hypertens (Greenwich). 2008 May; 10(5):348&ndash;54</em></span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Muhammed Ali,&nbsp; Tigestu Alemu and Oumer Sada. Medication adherence and its associated factors among diabetic patients at Zewditu Memorial Hospital, Addis Ababa, Ethiopia.<em> Ali et al. BMC Res Notes (2017) 10:676</em></span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">American Diabetes Association,&nbsp;Clinical Diabetes, vol. 37, no. 1, American Diabetes Association<em>, Richmond, VA, USA, 2019,&nbsp;</em></span></span><a href=\"https://clinical.diabetesjournals.org/content/37/1/11\" style=\"color:blue; text-decoration:underline\" target=\"_blank\"><em><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">https://clinical.diabetesjournals.org/content/37/1/11</span></span></em></a></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">American Diabetes Association (ADA). National standards for diabetes self-management education<em>. Diab Care 2005; 28(l1): 72-79.</em></span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Snoek FJ. Management of diabetes: Psychological aspects of diabetes management<em>. Medicine 2002; 30(1): 14-15.</em></span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">American Diabetes Association. Standards</span></span> <span style=\"font-size:11.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">of medical care in diabetes&ndash;</span></span><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">2015 abridged </span></span><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">for primary care providers. <em>Clin. Diabetes.</em></span></span> <span style=\"font-size:11.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">33(2), 97</span></span><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">-</span></span><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">111 (2015)</span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">American Diabetes Association. Nutrition</span></span> <span style=\"font-size:11.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">recommendations and interventions</span></span> <span style=\"font-size:11.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">for diabetes. <em>Diabetes Care. </em></span></span><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">33(8), 1911 </span></span><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">(2010)</span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">World Health Organization. Physical status: The use and interpretation of anthropometry: report of a WHO Expert committee. <em>Technical report series 854. Geneva:</em> <em>World Health Organization; 1995</em></span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Ghidul Serviciilor de Laborator. Referin&cedil;tele Specifice Tehnologiei de Lucru Utilizate. 2010. Available online:<em> </em></span></span><a href=\"https://www\" style=\"color:blue; text-decoration:underline\"><em><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">https://www</span></span></em></a><em><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">.synevo.ro/hemograma/ (accessed on13 May 2022</span></span></em></span></span></li>\r\n</ol>','The overall objective of this research mega project is to investigate effectiveness of mindfulness-based cognitive therapy (MBCT) in reducing depression and improving glycemic control among type 2 diabetic patients at Jimma University Medical Center: Randomized controlled trial',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,'Mindfulness-Based Cognitive Therapy, type2 diabetes, Jimma University medical center, Jimma',10,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'Proposal-6284bec9760c4618129382.docx'),(103,339,13,21,'Background:  Substance use among adolescents is a cause of concern in Ethiopia, as it is linked with behavioral and academic problems, and infectious diseases like HIV/AIDS and hepatitis. Unlike the traditional ways of imparting health education, the interactive software application on \'substance use prevention’ can be a novel, innovative and interesting learning aid for students, focusing on  self-efficacy and self-directedness towards a healthy lifestyle.\r\nObjective: To assess the effects of an interactive software application on substance use prevention through improved knowledge, attitude and self-efficacy among high school students in Jimma Town, Oromia, Ethiopia 2022.\r\nMethods: A pre-test - post-test with control group experimental research will be conducted from September to November 2022. Two high schools from Jimma town will be randomly selected and then the intervention will be randomly assigned to either of the schools.  An interactive software application on ‘prevention of substance use’ will be developed and installed into the school computers of the experimental group, comprising 103 randomly selected students of grades 9 to 12. Students will have daily 45 minutes exposure to the computer games on substance use prevention  for sixty days. The control group of 103 randomly selected will be exposed to the conventional health  education available in the school. Structured questionnaire will be used to gather data on effect of the interactive software application in terms of magnitude, knowledge, attitude and self-efficacy of prevention of substance use. Data will be entered to Epi-data version 4.2 and analyzed using SPSS version 23. Testing of hypothesis will be done by analyzing difference in pre and post test scores using paired t-test, and independent t-test with statistical significance at P-value < 0.05. Descriptive statistics, binary and multivariate logistic regression analysis will be used.  \r\nWork plan and budget: The study will be conducted starting from preparation of proposal till completion of the project from April 2022 to January2023 with a total of 405,635 Ethiopian Birr.\r\nStart date:  15 July /2022     End date: 31 January 2023',NULL,'Effects of an interactive software application on substance use prevention through improved knowledge, attitude and self-efficacy among high school students in Jimma Town, Oromia, Ethiopia 2022: Randomised Controlled Trial.','2022-05-25 15:16:45','7.	Research output/outcome\r\n\r\nSpecific objectives	Expected goal(outcomes)\r\n1.	To develop an interactive software application Substance use prevention	-	Interactive software developed based on the concept of Gamification: using gaming techniques to motivate user participation while providing a useful method in supporting learning and problem solving. Use of \r\n-	comics, cartoons on psychoactive substances, somatic and psychological effects, and stories on recovery.\r\n-	Quiz game on  Knowledge & attitude  regarding substance use\r\n-	Role play game with an assumed acquaintance on practice to prevent, treatment, rehabilitation and referral services available.\r\n2. To test the efficacy of the interactive software application among the high school students	 The opinion on the device to stimulate interest, motivate self-directed learning and the level of acceptance among the students will be revealed before the actual study.[pretest]\r\n3. To identify the magnitude of substance use and it’s statistically associated factors among high school students.	The life time and past 30 days magnitude of substance use and the common types of addictive substances used by the high schools students from the control and experimental groups will be known.\r\n4. To identify factors associated with the knowledge, attitude, and self-efficacy of substance use among high school students	 The factors associated with the knowledge attitude, and self-efficacy of substance use among high school students in both the groups will be revealed.\r\n5. To assess the effect of interactive software application in decreasing use of substance among high school students 	-The effectiveness of the interactive software application on substance use prevention will be demonstrated after 60 days of use of the device by the students in the experimental group, in terms of decrease in substance use in the past 60 days, by comparing within groups and among groups.\r\n6. To assess the effect of interactive software application on knowledge and attitude towards substance use prevention and treatment among high school students 	The effectiveness of the interactive software application on substance use prevention in improving knowledge, and attitude in preventing substance use will be demonstrated, by comparing within groups and among groups of high school students.\r\n7. To assess the effect of interactive software application on self-efficacy in preventing substance use among high school students 	The effectiveness of the interactive software application on substance use prevention among high school students  in improving self-efficacy for preventing substance use will be demonstrated ,by comparing within groups and among groups of high school students','e03be84991b21bfc9ec93ba8bd449649','completed','Substance abuse is the harmful use of psychoactive substances, including alcohol and illicit drugs[1], for the purpose of creating pleasurable effects on the brain which leads to addiction.[2] The frequently used substances are prescription medications and over the counter medicines such as pain killers, benzodiazepines, stimulants , and sedatives,  other substances like alcohol, tobacco , inhalants, and illegal substances like cocaine, heroin, inhalants, marijuana, club drugs, steroids, and methamphetamines.[3] \r\nThese substances or drugs have intoxicating effects that lead to misuse. The harmful effects are associated with either a single occasion of drug use like overdose or an injury resulting from intoxication, or chronic use associated with dependence causing health problems and death.[4 ] It burdens the society with the cost of health care and law enforcement, and connected with a range of social issues including discrimination, unemployment, homelessness, poverty and family breakdown. [5]    \r\n When young people abuse substances the complications are serious as it increases the odds of engaging in risky sexual behavior, personality disorders, sexual violence, criminal tendencies and drug dependence among others.[6] The risk for drug use is high during the period of transition. While events like divorce, loss of job may increase the risk of drug use among adults, situations that put teenagers at risk are change in location, family divorce or changing schools. Many adolescents are likely to have their maiden experiment with cigarettes and alcohol when faced with new and challenging social, family and academic and peer pressures as they progress from elementary through middle-school. [7,8]\r\nDrug or substance abuse among adolescents needs serious attention because, during this stage of life significant neural development takes place, characterised by changes in gray and white matter regions of the brain. The gray matter of the brain decreases but the white matter increases, beginning primarily in posterior regions of the brain and progressing to more anterior regions, with neural changes taking place well into the mid-to-late 20s. Moreover, there is a variation in the manner of the brain development, with mesolimbic and reward systems maturing before the prefrontal and cognitive control areas (areas responsible for assessing situations, making sound decisions, and controlling our emotions and impulses). These differences in the non-linear development of the two neural systems are thought to make adolescents more vulnerable to engage in risk-taking behaviors like alcohol and other drug use. [9] Thus, they are highly motivated to pursue  pleasurable rewards and avoid pain, but their judgment and decision-making skills are still limited, which affects their ability to evaluate risks and make sound decisions, including decisions about using drugs. [7]\r\n Rationale:\r\nGlobally, more than 275 million people were reported to have used drugs, and over 36 million people suffered from drug use disorders.[10] According to report by Centers For Disease Control and Prevention (CDC), 15% of high school students had used selected illicit or injection drugs  such as cocaine, inhalants, heroin, methamphetamines, hallucinogens, or ecstasy,14% had misused prescription opioids.[11] In Sub-Saharan Africa , the prevalence of substance use was estimated at 42%  among adolescents. [12] Ethiopia has also confirmed an increased use of these substances in urban population, even among young people. In addition to alcohol and tobacco, which are legal substances, khat, is legally grown and purchased in Ethiopia. Some studies have appreciated the magnitude of use and their risk factors in the Ethiopian context. According to these studies, adolescent youths who have parents or friends who use these substances are more likely to engage in the same behavior; and those who use substance are likely to have more friends. Nowadays adolescent youths perceive girls who use tobacco as modern and consider them to be attractive. [13] A meta-analysis on substance use among students in Ethiopia pointed to the lifetime prevalence at 22.5%. [14] Specifically, in Ambo town the prevalence of substance abuse among high school students was 16%. Khat abuse was the most common substance (10.5%), followed by smoking cigarettes (3.8%); 2.6% and 1.01% students used alcohol and shisha, respectively. The main reasons for substance use were peer pressure (33%) and a desire for acceptance in the friend circle (32.8%). [15] Similarly another study in a sub city of Addis Ababa found 26.5% lifetime prevalence of substance use among preparatory students. Further, 16% students reported consumption of alcohol, smoking cigarettes (9.6%), and Khat use by 9.4% of the respondents. [16]  Inadequate knowledge about substance use, their perceived immediate benefits despite awareness of the long term harm determine substance use behaviors  [17,18]  but self-efficacy behavioral skills of individuals have been found to best predict substance-use prevention. [ 19] \r\nInjectable drug use is known to place youth at direct risk for HIV, and drug use among youths is generally known to increase their risk of overdose. Broadly, drug use ups their susceptibility to sexual risk behavior, experience of violence, and mental illness and suicide risks. [11]Substance use has been associated with behavioral and academic problems, and infectious diseases like HIV/AIDS and hepatitis [20]. Besides, the initiation of substance use early in life contributes to higher levels of use and abuse later in life. [8] \r\nStrong school policies against substance use, providing education and support, and the students’ participation in school mini media clubs , have been hailed as a protective factors in the school environment for reducing the risk of early exposure to substance use.[13-16,20]  \r\nPrevention and early intervention strategies assume that when initiated at an early stage, averts worsening of the problem and offers enhanced cost-effective outcomes for the community.  By instituting substance use prevention programs that will benefit adolescents in the secondary school stage, the disease burden can be minimised as it delays the start of use, reduces problematic use and lessens the harms associated with use of alcohol and illicit drugs. Therefore, providing education and support to build up student’s protective factors against drug and alcohol abuse is a responsibility of the community, government, health services and individuals for delaying the age of onset of substance use and forestalling long term harms  and costs to the community. [5,13,20]\r\nThe Ethiopia School Health Program Framework (August,2017), initiated by the Ethiopian Ministry of Health  proposed the need for school health package on mental health including psychosocial support needed to be provided by interventions like content of information and  mobile applications, web sites for key message dissemination.[21] However, the traditional  method of  implementation and dissemination of the existing psycho-educational programs in schools present serious challenges as they require large resources in terms of money and time. In contrast, training and counselling via software has been found advantageous economically and matches with the lifestyle and communication habits of the adolescents.  These days, most of the students are familiar with the use of  mobile phones and typically use them on a daily basis for texting, taking  photos, playing games etc. [22] Hence, in this age of advanced technology and pervasive use of digitalisation even in school education, the Ethiopian education ministry’s encouragement for the use of information and communication technology (ICT)  is obvious as it supports  the  contemporary  constructivist  approach to learning thus augmenting the flexibility of delivery of education in terms of accessibility of  knowledge and  above all making the process of learning as learner driven. [23] Moreover, unlike personal, face-to-face interventions programs, these software applications have distinct advantages, as they save space, are accessible any time, are more cost effective in the long run, are better in providing tailored feedback, and are able to reach more members of the target population. Empirical evidence support the effectiveness of web-based interventions  and mobile phone apps that provide personalized feedback for reducing the intake of psychoactive substances. For example,an interactive  drug prevention app ‘Once Upon A High ‘to draw attention to the importance of prevention was found to be a useful tool for use in schools to assist preventive intervention programs by increasing knowledge and self-efficacy of the students.[24]  A systematic review on the ‘Effectiveness of Mobile App-Based Psychological Interventions for College Students’, concluded that mobile applications addressing psychological interventions are effective for addressing  stress, anxiety, depression and risky behaviors such as alcohol and tobacco abuse and sexual knowledge  among college students. Hence, mhealth interventions could benefit universities, especially college counselling services to handle students\' mental health and overcome human resource difficulties. [25] Yet, despite the availability of mobile apps and the usage by young people, limited research is available on apps in health promotion for adolescents and students. [26] Scientific evidence supports strong knowledge base, negative attitudes toward substance use are correlated with higher levels of self-confidence to resist substance use. [19, 27-29]\r\nIn Jimma, there are 6 schools with students studying in grades 9-12 in the higher secondary level. Health education is given by traditional methods through school clubs formed within the schools. But in some schools these clubs are not actively functioning. Moreover, health information imparted by the traditional ways, may be boring for students. On the other hand, interactive digital teaching /learning applications for providing psycho-education on prevention of substance use, although non-existent in Jimma , could be interesting for students. Therefore, if  a  software application is installed in the computers provided in the  schools , it can be an innovative and interesting learning aid for students  as it will be learner driven and focus on self-efficacy and self-directedness towards a healthy lifestyle. Moreover, installing software application in the computers of the schools instead of downloading in individual mobile phones, will prevent excessive use of mobile phones by students and jeopardizing study time with mobile phone games.\r\nTherefore, the aim of the current study is to determine the effect of an interactive software application on substance use prevention, for improving knowledge, attitudes and self-efficacy among high school students in Jimma town, Oromia, Ethiopia.\r\nSignificance of the Project:\r\nThe importance of this study proceeds from the absolute benefits it will bring to students, teachers and administrators, concerned with protection of students against substance abuse.\r\n1	The findings will reveal the prevalence of substance use among high school students in Jimma town\r\n2	Findings of this project will shed light on the knowledge levels of students on substance use, their  attitude, and  perceived self-efficacy towards substance use.\r\n3	It would benefit for carrying on large scale studies using this application to tests its effectiveness in preventing / reducing substance use among high school students and adolescents in the community.\r\n4	The software application can be installed in the school computers and used directly by students during the specified school time, thereby reducing the need for trained human resources. It would keep students motivated and encourage self –directed learning, it would be easily accessible to them.\r\n5	The potential efficacy of this application would be beneficial to individual students who otherwise might not seek or receive professional help.\r\n6	The findings from this project on the efficacy of using interactive software application as a means for health education on prevention of substance use can be extended to develop similar applications on health and testing the effectiveness in other areas of health and lifestyle.  \r\n7	Findings of this project will benefit the ministry of education to popularize software applications for purpose of psycho-education on prevention of substance use among high school students.','Methodology\r\n4.1 Study setting and period \r\nThis study will be conducted among the high school students of public schools which are located in Jimma Town, 352 km away from Addis Ababa to southwest direction. There are six higher secondary public schools in Jimma Town. Grades 9-12 are considered as high school sections. Altogether in the six schools there are approximately 15,000 students studying in the higher secondary sections. The study is proposed to be conducted in two high schools. Mole Mandara High school with 847 males and 1065 female students. The Geda secondary school has 1864 males and 814 female students. The study will be conducted from September 2022 to November 2022.       \r\n4.2 Study design\r\nA pre-test - post-test with control group experimental research will be conducted to assess  the effects of an interactive software application on  substance use prevention for high school students . This design is appropriate as two high schools in Jimma Town will be randomly selected. Further there will be random assignment of these schools into control and experimental groups. The effects of the intervention will be tested by comparing the difference in pre-test and post-test scores  both within and between groups.\r\n                                                   (T0)                              60 days                  (T1)\r\nExperimental Group : R        Pre-test(o1 )   Intervention          Post-test (o2)\r\nControl Group         :  R         Pretest  (o3)       -----------              Post-test(o4)\r\n*R= Random Assignment\r\n\r\nFig 2.  Pre- Test-Post-Test Control Group Design for Effectiveness of an Interactive Software Application On Substance Use Prevention For High School Students through improved knowledge, attitude and self-efficacy among high school students in  Jimma town, Oromia, Ethiopia 2022\r\n4.3 Population\r\n4.3.1 Source population: \r\nAll students studying in grades 9 to 12 in the selected schools of Jimma Town.\r\n4.3.2 Study population: \r\n All sampled students of grades 9to 12 in the selected schools.\r\n4.3.3 Inclusion criteria:\r\n-	 Male and female students studying in grades 9 to 12.\r\n-	Those who are willing to participate.\r\n4.3.4 Exclusion criteria:\r\n-	Students who are not present during the study period. \r\n4.4 Sample size determination and sampling technique\r\nThe sample size will be determined using double population proportion formula using STATCAL of EPI info software based on the following assumption for the objectives. \r\n•	n (for each group) = [(Zα + Zβ)2* (P1 (1 - P1) + P2 (1 - P2)) /(P2 - P1)2]      where,\r\n•	n = the number of individuals in each group.\r\n•	P1 = proportion of high school students with substance use assumed to be 50%\r\n•	P2 = proportion of substance use will be decreased by 10% (40%) in the intervention area \r\n•	Zα = the standard normal value at 5% level of significance(alpha)--- (Type I error)\r\n•	Z1-β = the standard normal value at 80 %( power) --- Type II error.\r\nTo estimate the sample size for evaluating the outcome variables of the intervention package, “the decline in the prevalence of substance use was 50%.   Therefore, the calculation of the sample size is based on the aforementioned assumptions. Then the final sample size is come up by adding a non-retrieval rate of 10% to each arm.\r\n•	for control(P1) group = (93 *10/100) +93 = 103 \r\n•	for Intervention = (P2) group = (93 *10/100) +93 = 103\r\nSo, the final sample size for each study site is 206. Accordingly, 206 students are expected to participate from the intervention and control arms of the study areas.\r\n\r\nSampling technique \r\nA simple random sampling using lottery method will be used to select two schools from the six high schools in Jimma town, followed by random assignment of one school to control group and the other to experimental group. \r\nProportional allocation of students to each grade from 9-12 in each of the groups.  A Sampling frame will be developed for each class (section) in every grade.  Students will be selected randomly from this sampling frame until the required sample size is obtained.\r\n\r\n	\r\n\r\n\r\n \r\n                                                                                \r\n \r\nFig 3. SCHEMATIC REPRESENTATION OF SAMPLING TECHNIQUE 	\r\n4.5 Data collection tools and data collection procedures\r\n  Data collection Tools:\r\nA self-administered structured questionnaire will be developed in English/ Amharic / AfanOromo and administered to the students of 2 high schools. The structured questionnaire to meet the objectives of the project comprises of:\r\nI. a) Socio-demographic characteristics of the participants: age, sex, grade of study, religion, ethnicity, living situation, educational status of the parents, occupational status of the parents, Income of the parents; source of info on subs use.\r\nb) Students’ substance use related factors: Items pertaining to use of alcohol and substances by the students in lifetime, during  past 30 days prior to the commencement of the study, and in past 60 days after the intervention, will be   measured by items adapted from the Brief Screener for Tobacco, Alcohol, and other Drugs. [30]\r\nc) Parent related factors: to identify substance use by parents and their attitude towards substance use.\r\nd) School factors: policy & guideline& rules on substance use; training for teachers and students; adolescent and youth club; mass media; availability of substances near the school.\r\nII: Knowledge of alcohol, and other drugs:  Consists of 10 items to measure knowledge, health perspective, types of addictive substances, and consequences of substance use, adapted by reviewing various literature.  [31-33] \r\nIII. Attitude/ beliefs towards substance use and attitude towards substance user. A 5-point Likert scale (strongly agree to disagree) with four categories (i.e., personal opinions, society values, government responsibility, and health consequences of alcohol and/or substance use) and 12 items on attitude towards substance use by parents/ peers [24,31]\r\nIV. Perceived self-efficacy in substance use: measured by Drug Avoidance Self-Efficacy Scale, DASES, has 16 items specific to avoidance of drug use. Items consist of descriptions of situations in which drug users may find themselves tempted to take drugs or alcohol. Respondents have to rate their confidence in their ability to avoid drug use in those situations, using a 7-point scale ranging from 1 (certainly no) to 7 (certainly yes). The scale consists of questions pertaining to 9 situations of risk: negative emotional states, boredom, social anxiety, celebration, unpleasant physical states, interpersonal conflict, interpersonal influence, negative thoughts, and the immediate availability of drugs. [34]\r\n II. A software will be developed on the concept of gaming using gaming techniques such as 1. Comics, cartoons on : common psychoactive substances, somatic and psychological effects, and stories on recovery. 2. Quiz game on Knowledge, attitude & beliefs regarding substance use and attitude towards substance user. 3. Role play game with an assumed acquaintance on self-efficacy to guard against substance use, information on treatment, rehabilitation and referral services available.\r\nData collection procedure: \r\nThe data will be collected by trained data collectors.\r\n 1. The two groups of students, belonging to control and experimental groups will be administered the questionnaire at baseline (TO) by the data collectors.\r\n2. For  the experimental group students, the interactive  software application for prevention of substance use  will be installed in the school computers, available in the school health clubs .The students in the experimental group will be given a demonstration on the use of the software application by the  software developer . \r\n3. Experimental group students of grades 9-12 will have access to the software during their allotted time slots, for 45 minutes daily for 60 days.   The students will be followed daily by the trained supervisors for providing support and troubleshooting issues that may arise while using the software. Any difficulty faced by the student will be attended to immediately.  The students in the control group will have the conventional health education programs that are given in the school.\r\n4. At the end of two months (60 days) (T1), data will be collected from both the groups. [35]\r\n 5. The Effects will be measured by difference between pre-test of experimental and control groups with their corresponding post- test scores (O2 &O1) - (O4 & O3).\r\n4.6 Study variables	\r\nDependent variable: \r\n•	Decreased substance use. \r\n•	Increased knowledge in prevention of substance use.\r\n•	Improved attitude towards preventing substance use.\r\n•	Increased self-efficacy in preventing substance use.\r\nIndependent variables\r\n•	Socio-demographic characteristics: age, sex, grade of study, religion, ethnicity, living situation, parents’ educational level, parents’ occupational status, parents’ income; Source Of information on  substance use. \r\n•	Student’s factors:  substance use, type of substance, use of addictive substance by friends & type.\r\n•	Parent related factors :  history of substance use, type of substance,  attitude towards substance use \r\n•	School related factors: policy & guideline& rules on substance use, training for teachers and students, adolescent and youth club, mass media, availability of substances near the school. \r\n4.7	Operational definition\r\n•	Effectiveness on substance use prevention: refers to the improvement in the scores obtained by students in the aspects of Knowledge, attitudes and self-efficacy on prevention of substance use.\r\n•	Effects of Interactive software application on substance use prevention: refers to improved post-test scores  among students following use of the interactive software on substance use prevention when  compared to their pre-test scores, as well as comparing the post test scores of students in both the groups.\r\n•	Substance use: refers to response from students regarding their substance use status,  the reasons for use of addictive / psychoactive substances  like khat, alcohol, tobacco, marijuana, masthish, shisha, prescription drugs, non-prescription drugs  etc. and  the current status of the risk as measured by Brief Screener for Tobacco, Alcohol, and other Drugs [30] and substance use disorder will be diagnosed as per the DSMV criteria for substance use disorder.\r\n•	Knowledge of substance use: measured as total scores obtained by students regarding addictive substances, types, the symptoms, consequences, referral services higher scores include better knowledge. Poor knowledge <50% ; Satisfactory Knowledge 50-75%; Good knowledge >75% [33]\r\n•	Attitudes towards substance use: measured as total scores obtained by students regarding, beliefs values about substance use, attitude towards substance user. Total scores ranging 18to 36 considered positive attitude ; less than 18 considered negative attitude[33]\r\n•	Self –efficacy for prevention of substance use: measured by Drug Avoidance Self-Efficacy Scale. The higher the score the higher the self-efficacy for prevention of substance use. [34]\r\n \r\nProject process description including interventions\r\nN	Intervention activity 	Purpose 	Participants 	Frequency 	Duration 	Location \r\n1	Baseline questionnaire development \r\n\r\nTraining data collectors and supervisors 	To understand the level and factors associated with substance use\r\n\r\n	Researcher \r\n\r\n\r\nData collectors and supervisors 	\r\nonce\r\n\r\nOnce	3days\r\n\r\n\r\n3days 	Jimma\r\n\r\n\r\nJimma\r\n2	Development of Interactive software application	Preventing substance use and improving knowledge, attitudes, and self-efficacy on prevention of substance use through self- motivated and self-directed learning	Software developer	once	60days\r\n(2months)	Jimma\r\n3.	Pre-intervention data collection	 To know pre –intervention, magnitude of substance use, knowledge, attitude, and self-efficacy on substance use 	Data collectors	once	One day	Jimma\r\n4	Training  for teachers on use of the interactive software application	 To train students on the use of the interactive software.	Software developer	twice	 Two days	Jimma\r\n5.		Implementing the intervention\r\n	To improve student’s knowledge, attitude, and self-efficacy	Trained teachers and research team	45 minutes per student daily	60 days	Jimma\r\n	5.1 comics, cartoons ,stories  	To improve knowledge on common psychoactive substances, somatic and psychological effects. Knowledge on rehabilitation. Attitude towards substance use and substance user				\r\n	5.2 Quiz game 	 To improve knowledge ,attitude & beliefs regarding substance use and attitude towards substance user				\r\n	5.3 Role play	Game with an assumed acquaintance on self-efficacy to guard against substance use, information on treatment, rehabilitation and referral services available.				\r\n6.		Post-intervention data collection	Assess Post –intervention: magnitude, knowledge, attitude and self-efficacy on substance use of students 	Data collectors	once	One day	Jimma\r\n \r\n4.8	Data management and statistical analysis\r\nThe pre-test and post-test data will be checked for completeness, coded and entered into Epi-data software version 4.2 and will be exported to Statistical package for Social Science (SPSS) version 23 for analysis. Descriptive analysis will be computed. Testing of hypothesis will be done by analysing difference in pre and post-test score using paired t-test - for within group comparison. Independent samples t-test – for making comparison between groups (Control and experimental) with statistical significance at P-value < 0.05. The results will be presented in tables, graphs and narrative. Objectives 3 to 7, binary and multivariate logistic regression analysis will be used.\r\n4.9 Data Quality assurance \r\nTo ensure quality of data, pretested and standardized questionnaires will be used for the pre and post-tests. The questionnaire will be translated into Amharic and AfanOromo languages by language experts, then back-translated by similar language experts. The pre-test will be carried out for consistency and reliability of the questionnaire. The interactive software application for prevention of substance use will also be pre-tested before the actual study.  For the  study, trained data collectors and supervisors will be used to collect the data and support the students during the intervention.  The selected teachers and students will be trained on the use of the interactive software application. Training will be given for the teachers for scheduling the students to use the computers, and observing students during their time slot. Filled questionnaires will be checked for completeness and questionnaires with incomplete part will be excluded from analysis. During data entry, Epi-data version 4.2 will be used to minimize error. And SPSS software will be used for analysis. \r\n4.10	Plan for dissemination and ensuring utilization of findings\r\nThe findings of this project will be disseminated to office of Health Research and Postgraduate Director, administrator of JMC, and a minimum of four publications will arise from this project.    \r\n	Ethical considerations \r\nThis study will be conducted after receiving ethical approval from the Institutional Review Board of Jimma University.  Permission will be obtained from the school authorities before conducting the study. Students will be clearly informed that their participation in the study is voluntary .Anonymity and confidentiality will be respected and maintained in the study. Students’ consent (parents ‘consent) will be obtained as per the policies of the school). The participants will be explained about the purpose of conducting the pre and post-tests, as well as the purpose of the study. Confidentiality of the participants will be assured by coding and they need not indicate their names on the response sheets of both pre and post-tests, they will be explained the risks and benefits of the study, right to withdraw without penalty, and contact information for the investigators. \r\nDuring the screening for prevalence of substance use, students who are found to be at risk of substance use disorder  will be suggested  for further assessment to determine how  best to manage their care and for therapy as needed.','<ol>\r\n	<li><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Substance Abuse. (2017). WHO | Regional Office for Africa. </span><a href=\"https://www.afro.who.int/health-topics/substance-abuse\" style=\"color:#0563c1; text-decoration:underline\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\"><span style=\"color:#0563c1\">https://www.afro.who.int/health-topics/substance-abuse</span></span></a></li>\r\n	<li><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Ananya Mandal. (2019, February 27). What is Drug Abuse? News-Medical.net. </span><a href=\"https://www.news-medical.net/health/What-is-Drug-Abuse.aspx\" style=\"color:#0563c1; text-decoration:underline\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\"><span style=\"color:#0563c1\">https://www.news-medical.net/health/What-is-Drug-Abuse.aspx</span></span></a><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">.</span></li>\r\n	<li><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">The Top 10 Most Used Drugs. (2021, August 5). Steps to Recovery. </span><a href=\"https://www.stepstorecovery.com/rehab-blog/the-top-10-most-used-drugs/\" style=\"color:#0563c1; text-decoration:underline\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\"><span style=\"color:#0563c1\">https://www.stepstorecovery.com/rehab-blog/the-top-10-most-used-drugs/</span></span></a></li>\r\n	<li><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">scheme=AGLSTERMS. AglsAgent; corporate Name=Department for Health and Wellbeing; address=11 Hindmarsh Square, A. (n.d.). Harmful drug use. Www.sahealth.sa.gov.au. </span><a href=\"https://www.sahealth.sa.gov.au/wps/wcm/connect/public+content/sa+health+internet/conditions/drugs/harmful+drug+use\" style=\"color:#0563c1; text-decoration:underline\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\"><span style=\"color:#0563c1\">https://www.sahealth.sa.gov.au/wps/wcm/connect/public+content/sa+health+internet/conditions/drugs/harmful+drug+use</span></span></a></li>\r\n	<li><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Deshayes, M. (2017) Why is drug and alcohol prevention important?. Youth Solutions - Promoting Health. Preventing Harm. </span><a href=\"https://youthsolutions.com.au/submenu/news/ys-comment-drug-alcohol-prevention-important/\" style=\"color:#0563c1; text-decoration:underline\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\"><span style=\"color:#0563c1\">https://youthsolutions.com.au/submenu/news/ys-comment-drug-alcohol-prevention-important/</span></span></a></li>\r\n	<li><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Idowu, A., Aremu, A. O., Olumide, A., &amp; Ogunlaja, A. O. (2018). Substance abuse among students in selected secondary schools of an urban community of Oyo-state, South West Nigeria: implication for policy action. African health sciences, 18(3), 776&ndash;785. </span><a href=\"https://doi.org/10.4314/ahs.v18i3.36\" style=\"color:#0563c1; text-decoration:underline\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\"><span style=\"color:#0563c1\">https://doi.org/10.4314/ahs.v18i3.36</span></span></a><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\"> [6]</span></li>\r\n	<li><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">NIDA. 2020, July 10. Preventing Drug Misuse and Addiction: The Best Strategy. Retrieved from </span><a href=\"https://nida.nih.gov/publications/drugs-brains-behavior-science-addiction/preventing-drug-misuse-addiction-best-strategy\" style=\"color:#0563c1; text-decoration:underline\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\"><span style=\"color:#0563c1\">https://nida.nih.gov/publications/drugs-brains-behavior-science-addiction/preventing-drug-misuse-addiction-best-strategy</span></span></a></li>\r\n	<li><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Griffin, K. W., &amp; Botvin, G. J. (2010). Evidence-based interventions for preventing substance use disorders in adolescents. Child and adolescent psychiatric clinics of North America, 19(3), 505&ndash;526. </span><a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.chc.2010.03.005\" style=\"color:#0563c1; text-decoration:underline\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\"><span style=\"color:#0563c1\">https://doi.org/10.1016/j.chc.2010.03.005</span></span></a></li>\r\n	<li><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Squeglia, L. M., &amp; Gray, K. M. (2016). Alcohol and Drug Use and the Developing Brain. Current psychiatry reports, 18(5), 46. </span><a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1007/s11920-016-0689-y\" style=\"color:#0563c1; text-decoration:underline\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\"><span style=\"color:#0563c1\">https://doi.org/10.1007/s11920-016-0689-y</span></span></a></li>\r\n	<li><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Substance Abuse | Healthy People 2020. (2008). Healthypeople.gov. </span><a href=\"https://www.healthypeople.gov/2020/topics-objectives/topic/substance-abuse\" style=\"color:#0563c1; text-decoration:underline\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\"><span style=\"color:#0563c1\">https://www.healthypeople.gov/2020/topics-objectives/topic/substance-abuse</span></span></a><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">.</span></li>\r\n	<li><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Centers for Disease Control and Prevention. (2019<strong>). </strong></span><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">High Risk Substance Use in Youth</span><strong><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">. </span></strong><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">CDC. </span><a href=\"https://www.cdc.gov/healthyyouth/substance-use/index.htm\" style=\"color:#0563c1; text-decoration:underline\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\"><span style=\"color:#0563c1\">https://www.cdc.gov/healthyyouth/substance-use/index.htm</span></span></a></li>\r\n	<li><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Mekuria, M., Girma, T., &amp; Birhanu, A. (2019). Assessment of Substance Abuse and Associated Factors among Secondary School Students in Ambo Town, Ethiopia, 2018. Journal of Addiction Research &amp; Therapy, 10(3). </span><a href=\"https://doi.org/10.4172/2155-6105.1000385\" style=\"color:#0563c1; text-decoration:underline\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\"><span style=\"color:#0563c1\">https://doi.org/10.4172/2155-6105.1000385</span></span></a></li>\r\n	<li><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Roba, H. S., Beyene, A. S., Irenso, A. A., &amp; Gebremichael, B. (2019). Prevalence of lifetime substances use among students in Ethiopia: a systematic review and meta-analysis. Systematic Reviews, 8(1). </span><a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1186/s13643-019-1217-z\" style=\"color:#0563c1; text-decoration:underline\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\"><span style=\"color:#0563c1\">https://doi.org/10.1186/s13643-019-1217-z</span></span></a></li>\r\n	<li><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Truebwasser, U. (2017). Landscape Analysis of Adolescent Health and Nutrition in Ethiopia [Review of Landscape Analysis of Adolescent Health and Nutrition in Ethiopia]. Https://Www.aliveandthrive.org/Sites/Default/Files/Attachments/Ethiopia-Adolescent-Report-9-17.Pdf.</span></li>\r\n	<li><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Olawole-Isaac, A., Ogundipe, O., Amoo, E. O., &amp; Adeloye, D. (2018). Substance use among adolescents in sub-Saharan Africa: a systematic review and meta-analysis. S Afri J Child Health 12 (2b): 79&ndash;84.</span></li>\r\n	<li><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Seid, L., Gintamo, B., Mekuria, Z. N., Hassen, H. S., &amp; Gizaw, Z. (2021). Substance use and associated factors among preparatory school students in Kolfe-Keranyo sub-city of Addis Ababa, Ethiopia. Environmental Health and Preventive Medicine, 26(1). </span><a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1186/s12199-021-01032-1\" style=\"color:#0563c1; text-decoration:underline\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\"><span style=\"color:#0563c1\">https://doi.org/10.1186/s12199-021-01032-1</span></span></a></li>\r\n	<li><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Liranso GS, Eliyas KA, Israel FF (2017) Drug and Substance Abuse Knowledge and Attitudes among Youth in Addis Ababa Ethiopia. J Subst Abuse Alcohol 5(2): 1056.</span></li>\r\n	<li><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Faizi, N., Alvi, Y., Saraaswat, A., Yasir, M. (2021).</span> <span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Knowledge, attitude, practice, and pattern of substance use among adolescents and young adults from Aligarh, India.</span> <span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Indian J Comm Health. 2021;33(4):615-620. </span><a href=\"https://doi.org/10.47203/IJCH.2021.v33i04.013\" style=\"color:#0563c1; text-decoration:underline\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">https://doi.org/10.47203/IJCH.2021.v33i04.013</span></a></li>\r\n	<li><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Ikonta, P. C. (2020). Information-Adequacy, Motivation and Behavioral-Skills as Determinants of Substance-Use Prevention among Undergraduates in Babcock University, Ilishan &ndash; Remo, Nigeria. TEXILA INTERNATIONAL JOURNAL of PUBLIC HEALTH, 8(1), 204&ndash;215. </span><a href=\"https://doi.org/10.21522/tijph.2013.08.01.art022\" style=\"color:#0563c1; text-decoration:underline\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">https://doi.org/10.21522/tijph.2013.08.01.art022</span></a></li>\r\n	<li><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Taremian, F., Yaghubi, H., Pairavi, H., Hosseini, S. R., Zafar, M., &amp; Moloodi, R. (2018). Risk and protective factors for substance use among Iranian university students: a national study. Substance Abuse Treatment, Prevention, and Policy, 13(1). </span><a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1186/s13011-018-0181-2\" style=\"color:#0563c1; text-decoration:underline\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\"><span style=\"color:#0563c1\">https://doi.org/10.1186/s13011-018-0181-2</span></span></a></li>\r\n	<li><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Ababa, A. (2017). Ethiopia School Health Program Framework Federal Democratic Republic of Ethiopia Ministry of Health SCHOOL HEALTH PROGRAM FRAMEWORK Be Healthy to Learn and Learn to be Healthy. </span><a href=\"https://www.prb.org/wp-content/uploads/2020/06/Ethiopia-School-Health-Program-Framework-August-2017_FINAL.pdf\" style=\"color:#0563c1; text-decoration:underline\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\"><span style=\"color:#0563c1\">https://www.prb.org/wp-content/uploads/2020/06/Ethiopia-School-Health-Program-Framework-August-2017_FINAL.pdf</span></span></a></li>\r\n	<li><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Ellickson, PL.(2014) You&rsquo;ve shown the program is effective. Now what? In: Weichold K, Giannotta F, editors. Theory-Based Approaches to Substance Misuse and Abuse Prevention in School. San Francisco: Jossey-Bass . p. 95&ndash;106.</span></li>\r\n	<li><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Alemu,M. B.( 2016). Transforming educational practices of Ethiopia into development and the knowledge society through information and communication technology. African Educational Research Journal, 5(1), 1&ndash;17. </span><a href=\"https://eric.ed.gov/?id=EJ1216159\" style=\"color:#0563c1; text-decoration:underline\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\"><span style=\"color:#0563c1\">https://eric.ed.gov/?id=EJ1216159</span></span></a></li>\r\n	<li><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Kapit&aacute;ny-F&ouml;v&eacute;ny, M., Vagdalt, E., Ruttkay, Z., Urb&aacute;n, R., Richman, M. J., &amp; Demetrovics, Z. (2018). Potential of an Interactive Drug Prevention Mobile Phone App (Once Upon a High): Questionnaire Study among Students. JMIR serious games, 6(4), e19. </span><a href=\"https://doi.org/10.2196/games.9944\" style=\"color:#0563c1; text-decoration:underline\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\"><span style=\"color:#0563c1\">https://doi.org/10.2196/games.9944</span></span></a></li>\r\n	<li><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Oliveira, C., Pereira, A., Vagos, P., N&oacute;brega, C., Gon&ccedil;alves, J., &amp; Afonso, B. (2021). Effectiveness of Mobile App-Based Psychological Interventions for College Students: A Systematic Review of the Literature. Frontiers in Psychology, 12. </span><a href=\"https://doi.org/10.3389/fpsyg.2021.647606\" style=\"color:#0563c1; text-decoration:underline\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\"><span style=\"color:#0563c1\">https://doi.org/10.3389/fpsyg.2021.647606</span></span></a></li>\r\n	<li><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Dute, D. J., Bemelmans, W. J., &amp; Breda, J. (2016). Using Mobile Apps to Promote a Healthy Lifestyle Among Adolescents and Students: A Review of the Theoretical Basis and Lessons Learned. JMIR mHealth and uHealth, 4(2), e39. </span><a href=\"https://doi.org/10.2196/mhealth.3559\" style=\"color:#0563c1; text-decoration:underline\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\"><span style=\"color:#0563c1\">https://doi.org/10.2196/mhealth.3559</span></span></a></li>\r\n	<li><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Chueh, K.-H., Ding, G.-Y., Yao, K.-W., Huang, Y.-J., &amp; Hung, C.-C. (2013). Relationships among risk knowledge, attitudes and ability to resist substance abuse in adolescents.Hu Li Za Zhi the Journal of Nursing, 60(1), 60&ndash;68. </span><a href=\"https://doi.org/10.6224/JN.60.1.60\" style=\"color:#0563c1; text-decoration:underline\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\"><span style=\"color:#0563c1\">https://doi.org/10.6224/JN.60.1.60</span></span></a></li>\r\n	<li><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Bonyani, A., Safaeian, L., Chehrazi, M., Etedali, A., Zaghian, M., &amp; Mashhadian, F. (2018). A high school-based education concerning drug abuse prevention. Journal of education and health promotion, 7, 88. </span><a href=\"https://doi.org/10.4103/jehp.jehp_122_17\" style=\"color:#0563c1; text-decoration:underline\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\"><span style=\"color:#0563c1\">https://doi.org/10.4103/jehp.jehp_122_17</span></span></a></li>\r\n	<li><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Heckman, C. J., Dykstra, J. L., &amp; Collins, B. N. (2011). Substance-Related Knowledge, Attitude, and Behavior among College Students: Opportunities for Health Education. Health education journal, 70(4), 383&ndash;399. </span><a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1177/0017896910379694\" style=\"color:#0563c1; text-decoration:underline\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\"><span style=\"color:#0563c1\">https://doi.org/10.1177/0017896910379694</span></span></a></li>\r\n	<li><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Teens Screening Tool: Brief Screener for Tobacco, Alcohol, and other Drugs. (n.d.). Nida.nih.gov. https://nida.nih.gov/ast/bstad/#/</span></li>\r\n	<li><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Ajzoon, M. (n.d.). Retrieved May 3, 2022, from </span><a href=\"https://digitalcommons.odu.edu/cgi/viewcontent.cgi?article=1010&amp;context=healthservices_etd\" style=\"color:#0563c1; text-decoration:underline\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">https://digitalcommons.odu.edu/cgi/viewcontent.cgi?article=1010&amp;context=healthservices_etd</span></a></li>\r\n	<li><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">PhenX Toolkit: Protocols. (n.d.). Retrieved from www.phenxtoolkit.org website: </span><a href=\"https://www.phenxtoolkit.org/protocols/view/510202\" style=\"color:#0563c1; text-decoration:underline\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">https://www.phenxtoolkit.org/protocols/view/510202</span></a></li>\r\n	<li><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Mohammed, O. H., E. Hossein, Y., &amp; H. Mohamed, A. (2021). Assessment of Knowledge and Attitudes of Secondary School Students Regarding Drug Abuse at Minia City. Minia Scientific Nursing Journal, 009(1), 2&ndash;8. https://doi.org/10.21608/msnj.2021.188058</span></li>\r\n	<li><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Drug Avoidance Self-Efficacy Scale | www.emcdda.europa.eu. (n.d.). Www.emcdda.europa.eu. Retrieved May 2, 2022, from https://www.emcdda.europa.eu/drugs-library/drug-avoidance-self-efficacy-scale_ro#:~:text=The%20DASES%20is%20a%2016.</span></li>\r\n	<li><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">How Long Does Addiction Treatment Take? - Addiction Center. (2017). Addiction Center. </span></span><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><a href=\"https://www.addictioncenter.com/rehab-questions/how-long-does-treatment-take/\" style=\"color:#0563c1; text-decoration:underline\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\"><span style=\"color:#0563c1\">https://www.addictioncenter.com/rehab-questions/how-long-does-treatment-take/</span></span></a></span></span></li>\r\n</ol>','To assess the effect of an interactive software application on substance use prevention through improved knowledge, attitude and self-efficacy among high school students in Jimma Town, Oromia, Ethiopia 2022.',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,'Adolescents, Substance Use, Interactive Software Application',10,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'Proposal-628e1783e82d2066412809.docx'),(105,1,13,15,'fhlkjhlfdk','dfnmbfd','test2','2022-05-24 11:42:22','fgkjfg','7284f21dd30f70b838e2344646f57f3e','completed','fj','ffk','<p>jkghkjhgkj</p>','gh',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,'gkjfgh,hgjh,khkuyi',10,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'Proposal-628c9a4ea96af947628882.docx'),(106,149,14,17,'nbsa','test','ytest','2022-05-25 14:34:50','test','5a49c1d99ae8b7491f24a19492d65292','completed','test2','test','<p>testas</p>','test',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,'sdfsdf,sd,sdfsd,dsfsdf,fsdfsdfd,dfsd',10,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'Proposal-628e145a6450b400869392.docx'),(107,90,13,15,'The child mortality rate is a key indicator of socioeconomic advancement and life quality.  Ethiopia, for example, is one of the top-ten countries that account for half of all under-five deaths globally, and Ethiopia\'s child mortality rate in 2020 was 48.7% deaths per 1000 live births. Reducing neonatal mortality has been prioritized by the ministry and is one of the Sustainable Development Goals targets. Even though asphyxia, respiratory distress syndrome, and sepsis have been recognized as key contributing factors, child health problems in Ethiopia are considerably contributing to the unprecedented neonatal and under-five deaths. In addition to the various interventions and follow-ups that occur during routine prenatal care, the Ministry of Health has advocated the expansion of neonatal intensive care units as a successful strategy for reducing infant mortality. However, to mitigate the challenge, the system still requires early prediction of outcomes, which is crucial for prioritizing preventative care. This indicates that a system for generating compelling evidence, as well as a continuous monitoring system, are essential to assist decision-makers and policymakers. The purpose of this research project is to create data-driven systems for infant health continuous risk monitoring in order to predict events and identify risks. The design will be used as a social good mechanism to link pregnant patients with the appropriate medical care providers (clinic or hospital) depending on the predicted events and risks, while respecting their personal self-interest in taking timely action.',NULL,'Development of AI based data driven continuous infant health monitoring, disease prediction, and risk identification system','2022-05-25 15:21:57','Three major outputs are expected after successful completion of the proposed project (1) health monitoring system, (2) disease prediction system (3) sequential disease prediction and risk identification system. This also demonstrated in Figure 2.','321721a05b654f63db990eb94837a68c','completed','Infectious diseases account for a substantial share of the annual burden of 10 million deaths among children under the age of five. Sepsis, pneumonia, diarrhea, and tetanus account for 36% of the 4 million infant deaths. Estimates and uncertainty bound for post neonatal mortality attributable to infections include 22% of deaths due to diarrhea (14–30%), 21% of deaths due to pneumonia (14–24%), 9% of deaths due to malaria (6–13%), and 1% of deaths due to measles (1–9%). Only 42 countries, most from the sub-Saharan countries, are responsible for about 90% of the worldwide burden of child mortality [1].\r\nEthiopia has one of the highest under-five mortality rates with more than 321,000 children under the age of five dying every year. More than 70% of these child deaths are due to five diseases, namely pneumonia, diarrhea, malaria, measles, and malnutrition, and often to a combination of these conditions [2]. Further, Harvard T.H. CHAN School of Public Health released a news with the heading \"addressing maternal and child health in Ethiopia\" and begin by highlighting that \"every minute, 21 children under the age of five die throughout the world, and a woman dies in childbirth every two minutes\" [3]. \r\nTo address those challenge, various initiatives have been launched in Ethiopia to reduce child mortality rates in order to meet the SDGs\' global under-five deaths target. Some are led by the government, while others are led by NGOs or individual researcher studies. Those initiatives provide progressive results [4-8]. They attempted to discover potential risk factors for child death in Ethiopia, either collectively or individually. Of the existing initiatives, we observed programs like Child Health and Mortality Prevention Surveillance, the study of sickness in pre-terms, performance monitoring for action, and many other child health projects at the national level. Various policies and guidelines were developed based on the findings, with the goal of encouraging and strengthening health extension workers through educational programs aimed at educating mothers on the benefits of having an antenatal checkup before their first delivery, spacing their birth intervals, and so on. \r\nIn addition, the Ethiopia Ministry of Health in collaboration with UNICEF has implemented Integrated Management of Newborn and Childhood Illness. With this implementation report, they outpointed that, infectious diseases have a higher account for the death of infants and children. In addition, the Ethiopian Ministry of Health has developed a strategic plan for child survival for reducing the death rate of infants from 44/1000 to 22/1000. To accomplish all these, the use of technological assistants is a great option.\r\nThe current manual method of disease diagnosis requires skilled professionals. According to Karen et.al [4], Ethiopia is one of the countries in the world with a low health workforce density of 0.7/1000 population, which is far below the minimum threshold density of 2.3 health workers per 1000 population for countries to achieve essential services. In addition to that, humans are usually prone to biases in which there are possibilities where different professionals can give different diagnosis predictions for a single patient. This could be due to experiences, skills, or perceptions. All this made Ethiopia have one of the highest under-five mortality rates with more than 321,000 children under the age of five dying every year. More than 70% of these child deaths are due to infectious diseases [4]. \r\nAll these diseases are treatable if detected at their earlier stage. To do that, parents are supposed to know the symptoms caused by these diseases. By knowing the symptoms experienced by the child, parents can immediately find out the disease that is caused and the prevention, so that the disease contracted by the child can be treated immediately. Which seems impossible for parents who live in countries like Ethiopia. In Ethiopia, the majority of the parents are illiterate and used to exercise cultural medication techniques that are not scientifically approved. The only alternative is to immediately visit pediatricians, but this often collides with the facts such as the existence of the limited number of pediatricians while those that need to be handled are quite a lot; they need very experienced professionals to identify the diseases is far beyond the total number of professionals available.\r\nVarious initiatives have been launched in Ethiopia to reduce child mortality rates in order to meet the SDGs\' global under-five deaths target. Some are led by the government, while others are led by NGOs or individual researcher studies. Those initiatives provide progressive results. They attempted to discover potential risk factors for child death in Ethiopia, either collectively or individually. Of the existing initiatives, we observed programs like Child Health and Mortality Prevention Surveillance, the study of sickness in pre-terms, performance monitoring for action, and many other child health projects at the national level. Various policies and guidelines were developed based on the findings, with the goal of encouraging and strengthening health extension workers through educational programs aimed at educating mothers on the benefits of having an antenatal checkup before their first delivery, spacing their birth intervals, and so on. \r\nHowever, there were few to no initiatives aimed at developing a system for continuous and data-driven monitoring. Ethiopian ministry of 2021 health maturity assessment document also indicated there are \"decision support tools exist in some settings and are based on alerts and reminders to the program managers, care providers, and patients\". However, it lacks the most crucial components, such as decision support tools that combine the program and clinical guidelines; well-defined condition-specific order sets and documentation templates to aid decisions; and knowledge management systems.  Such systems are common in the developed world, but many are commercially and legally prohibitive for developing countries. \r\nThis implies that there is a recognized need and desire to develop standard methods, as well as intelligent and active learning decision support tools, to aid in decision-making. Therefore; to bridge that gap, we devised methodologies for designing data-driven systems for continuous risk monitoring of child health in order to predict events and identify dangers, which would be made available as open-source tools for comparable contexts. We wanted to use machine learning techniques to introduce and design a continuous infant health risk monitoring system. We also intend to leverage the design for a social good mechanism to connect pregnant patients with the proper medical service providers (clinic or hospital).','Methodology\r\n3.1.	General Method framework \r\nFigure 1 demonstrates the general method framework of the proposed system. In the first phase of the project a preliminary study will be conducted in order to understand the extent of the issue, identify indicator parameters, and previous proposed state-of-the-art techniques of solutions made so far (if any). This will be followed by requirement gathering. A human-centered design approach will be followed in all development activities. User needs and requirements will be collected, analysed and selected to develop the user interface of the proposed systems. In the remaining phases of the project, three modules including health monitoring system, disease prediction system and disease risk identification will be developed and tested after collected the required data from different health facilities.','<ol>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"background-color:white\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">Bhutta, Z. A., &amp; Saeed, M. A. (2008). Childhood Infectious Diseases: Overview. International Encyclopedia of Public Health, 620&ndash;640.</span></span></span><a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1016/B978-012373960-5.00568-2\"> </a><a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1016/B978-012373960-5.00568-2\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"background-color:white\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">https://doi.org/10.1016/B978-012373960-5.00568-2</span></span></span></a></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"background-color:white\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">Integrated Management of Newborn and Childhood Illness, Part 1 Blended Learning Module for the Health Extension Programme, Federal Democratic Republic of Ethiopia Ministry of Health, Addis Ababa, Ethiopia</span></span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"background-color:white\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">Aurora Saibene, Michela Assale, Marta Giltri, Expert systems: Definitions, advantages and issues in medical field</span></span></span><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"> applications, Volume 177, 2021, 114900, ISSN 0957-4174,</span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"background-color:white\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">Karen Feldscher</span></span></span><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"background-color:white\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"> (2022). </span></span></span><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"background-color:white\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">Addressing maternal and child health in Ethiopia. Retervied from: </span></span></span><a href=\"https://www.hsph.harvard.edu/news/features/addressing-maternal-and-child-health-in-ethiopia/\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"background-color:white\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">https://www.hsph.harvard.edu/news/features/addressing-maternal-and-child-health-in-ethiopia/</span></span></span></a><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"background-color:white\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">.&nbsp; Access date: April 2022.</span></span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"background-color:white\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">Madewell, Z. J., Whitney, C. G., Assefa, N., Bassat, Q., El Arifeen, S., Gurley, E. S., ... &amp; Blau, D. M. (2022). Prioritising health-care strategies to reduce childhood mortality, insights from Child Health and Mortality Prevention Surveillance (CHAMPS): a longitudinal study. <em>The Lancet Global Health</em>, <em>10</em>, S8.</span></span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"background-color:white\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">Seale, A. C., Assefa, N., Madrid, L., Wittmann, S., Abdurahman, H., Teferi, N., ... &amp; Scott, J. A. G. (2022). Setting up child health and mortality prevention surveillance in Ethiopia. <em>Gates Open Research</em>, <em>5</em>, 173.</span></span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"background-color:white\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">Muhe, L. M., McClure, E. M., Mekasha, A., Worku, B., Worku, A., Dimtse, A., ... &amp; Goldenberg, R. L. (2018). A prospective study of causes of illness and death in preterm infants in Ethiopia: the SIP study protocol. <em>Reproductive Health</em>, <em>15</em>(1), 1-9.</span></span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"background-color:white\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">Zimmerman, L., Desta, S., Yihdego, M., Rogers, A., Amogne, A., Karp, C., ... &amp; Shiferaw, S. (2020). Protocol for PMA-Ethiopia: a new data source for cross-sectional and longitudinal data of reproductive, maternal, and newborn health. <em>Gates Open Research</em>, <em>4</em>.</span></span></span></span></span></li>\r\n</ol>','To create data-driven systems for infant health continuous risk monitoring in order to predict events and identify risks.',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,'Artificial Intelligence,infant disease,health monitoring,disease diagnosis,risk monitoring',10,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'Proposal-628e19668a739090600609.docx'),(108,200,13,17,'Background: Resistance to antimalarial drugs become a great challenge in the prevention, control, and elimination of malaria. Thus, searching for a new and effective antimalarial drug is of paramount importance. Traditionally used medicinal plants are used as a major source for the development of novel antimalarial drugs.  The root of P. dodecandra is used to treat malaria in traditional folk medicine. However, the antimalarial activity of the root is not yet studied scientifically. Thus, this project aims to scientifically evaluate the antimalarial activities of crude extract and solvent fractions of the root of P. dodecandra in mice.\r\nMethod: The fresh root of the plant will be collected, washed, dried at room temperature, and made as coarse powder. The powder will be subjected to cold maceration using hydromethanol to obtain the crude extract. The fractionation of the crude extract will be done using chloroform, ethyl acetate, and distilled water. The antimalarial activity of the crude extract and solvent fractions will be tested in 4-day suppressive, curative, and prophylactic test models.  The data will be analyzed using one-way ANOVA followed by Post hoc Tuky’s test in SPSS version 21 within a 95 % confidence interval. The result will be expressed in terms of mean ± standard error of the mean.\r\nTime frame and budget: the project will be conducted in 8 months (May 2022 to Jan 2023) and a total of 674,680 ETB is needed.',NULL,'Evaluating antimalarial activities of crude extract and solvent fractions of the root of phytolaca dodecandra L herit (Phytolaccaceae) against Plasmodium berghei infected mice: Experimental study','2022-05-25 15:40:19','The findings of the project will evaluate the safety and antimalarial efficacy of the crude extract and solvent fractions of the root of P. dodecandra. Because of the widespread of antimalarial drug resistance searching for the novel antimalarial drugs become paramount important.  Thus, the finding of this project could assist the scientific community in the screening and development of novel and effective antimalarial drugs.','9c238fd95e0215af8dcfc0c7c7187d15','completed','Although several strategies have been taken against malaria, it remains a global public health concern. According to the world health organization (WHO) 2021 report, 241 million malarial cases and 627, 000 deaths were recorded globally in 2020 which showed an increment from 2019, 227 million malaria cases and 558, 000 deaths. Most of the increase in malarial cases in 2020 comes from African countries which accounted for about 95%, 220 million malaria cases (Organization, 2021). In Ethiopia in 2021, the pooled prevalence of malaria among adults was 13.61% (Kendie, Nibret Semegn, & Ferede, 2021).  In addition, the prevalence of malaria in malaria elimination targeted areas in Ethiopia was 1.17 % (Nega et al., 2021).\r\nResistance to antimalarial drugs becomes a great challenge in the prevention, control, and elimination of malaria. Particularly Plasmodium falciparum develops resistance to nearly all available antimalarial drugs. Plasmodium vivax also develops resistance to chloroquine and primaquine. Resistance to sulfadoxine-pyrimethamine is also frequently reported.  Resistance to artemisinins and artemisinin-based combination therapies (ACT) become a major-wake up call for global public health sectors (Bloland & Organization, 2001; Conrad & Rosenthal, 2019).\r\nSeveral traditional medicinal plants are used as an alternative and /or the primary source in the management of malaria. In addition, traditional medicinal plants are used as the major source in the development of conventional antimalarial drugs (Manandhar, Luitel, & Dahal, 2019).  For instance, the commonly used antimalarial drugs; artemisinins (Tu, 2011) and quinine (Renslo, 2013) are developed from traditionally used medicinal plants.\r\nPhytolaca dodecandra L’Hert (Phytolaccaceae) is also called an ‘endod’ in Ethiopia and African soapberry (English). It is a sprawling woody climber with a stem length of 5 to 8 meters with erect, racemic, dioecious flowering stalks and red berries (fruit).  It is widely distributed in tropical Africa and Madagascar. The fruits have been used as detergents or soap for a long period in Africa. In 1970, Lemma, the field researcher, discovered that the endod berry killed snails (Adams, Neisess, Parkhurs, Makhubu, & Yohannes, 1989).\r\nP. dodecandra is abundantly used in traditional medicine to treat a wide variety of human ailments in Ethiopia.   These include but do not exclude, treatment of anthrax, scabies, jaundice, rabies (Temam, 2016; Wubetu, Abula, & Dejenu, 2017), wound (Tefera & Kim, 2019; Teklehaymanot & Giday, 2007), and intestinal parasites (Temam, 2016; Tuasha, Petros, & Asfaw, 2018). In addition, the root and leaves of P. dodecandra are used to treat malaria in traditional folk medicine (Bekele & Reddy, 2015; Berhan, Asfaw, & Kelbessa, 2006; Chekole, 2017). However, the antimalarial activity of root extract of P. dodecandra is didn’t yet studied scientifically. Thus, this project aims to scientifically evaluate the antimalarial activity of the crude extract and solvent fractions of the root of P .dodecandra.','3.1. Plant material\r\nThe fresh root of the plant will be collected. A sample of the collected root will be identified and authenticated by botanists.\r\n3.2. Plant material extraction and fractionation \r\nThe collected root will be washed with clean water to remove dust particles and dried at room temperature for a week.  Then, the dried root will be broken into small pieces and milled with a grinding mill to a coarse powder.  The resulting powder will be subject to cold maceration using 80 % methanol (hydromethanol) in a macerating flask for a few days with occasional shaking.  The macerate will be filtered with gauze followed by Whatman filter paper No.1.  The residue will be re-macerated to get enough amount of filtrate.  Finally, the filtrates will be combined and concentrated in a 40 0C hot air oven.  Furthermore, the aqueous content of the concentrated filtrate will be removed by a lyophilizer.  In the end, the extract will be packed and put in the refrigerator until use.\r\n A measured amount of the crude extract will be fractionated using chloroform, ethyl acetate, and distilled water, in the order of increasing polarity. First, the crude extract will be dissolved in distilled water. This bulk solution will be suspended in a separatory funnel. Then, chloroform at a volume equal to the distilled water will be added to the bulk solution and left to stand for a few hours under occasional stirring until a clear layer will be formed. After two clear layers are formed, the chloroform content will be filtered out. The residue will be re-macerated twice with the same volume of chloroform to obtain enough chloroform fraction. Then after, the same volume of ethyl acetate will be added to the residue and done in the same manner as chloroform. The residue after the ethylacetate fraction will be used as was an aqueous fraction. The chloroform and ethyl acetate fractions will be concentrated and dried in a hot at 40 °C and the AF will be lyophilized. Finally, the fractions will be kept in the refrigerator until the time of use.\r\n3.3. Animals \r\nEither sex of Swiss albino mice aged 6 to 8 weeks will be bought from Ethiopia public health institution (EPHI).  The mice will be kept in a cage in a room of 12 hours light and dark cycle.  The animals will feed on standard pellets and water ad libtum. The mice will be acclimatized in the study room for a week before the experiment. \r\n 3.4. Acute oral toxicity study\r\nThe test will be conducted in compliance with the organization for economic cooperation and development (OECD) 425 guidelines (Toxicity–Up, 2001). Healthy 5 female mice will be used for the test. The mice will fast for food for 4 hours before the test and 2 hours after the test. A sighting study will be conducted by administering 2000mg/kg of the extract via oral gavage to one mouse and will be observed for 24 hours.  If the mouse didn’t die within 24 hours, the rest of 4 mice will be treated with the same dose of the extract.  Then, the treated mice will be observed every 30 minutes for the first 4 hours and every day for 2 weeks for the occurrence of common signs and symptoms of toxicity including death, change in fur, and behavior. \r\n3.5. Preliminary phytochemical screening \r\nThe medicinal activity of plants relies on several secondary metabolites like alkaloids, flavonoids, saponins, tannins, glycosides, steroids, and others.  The presence of alkaloids, flavonoids, tannins, phenols,  and saponins in the extract will be screened based on standards (Mojab, Kamalinejad, Ghaderi, & Vahidipour, 2010; Pandey & Tripathi, 2014). \r\n3.6. Grouping and dosing of the animals\r\nThe animals will be grouped randomly into five groups each with six mice.  Each group will be treated with different agents as follows.\r\nGroup I (negative control), will be treated with 10 ml/kg of distilled water for the crude extract and aqueous fraction and 20 % tween 80 for chloroform and ethyl acetate fractions.\r\nGroup II (positive control), will be treated with chloroquine 25 mg/kg.\r\nGroup III, will be treated with a low dose of the extract\r\nGroup IV, will be treated with a medium dose of the extract\r\nGroup V, will be treated with a large dose of the extract\r\nThe different doses of the extract will be determined based on the acute oral toxicity test result.\r\n\r\n3.7. Antimalarial activity studies  \r\n3.7.1. Parasite inoculation\r\nP. berghei is the commonly used rodent malaria to investigate the in vivo antimalarial activity of medicinal plant extracts (Peters, Portus, & Robinson, 1975).   Some P. berghei infected mice will be bought from EPHI. Then the parasite will be maintained by blood transfusion from the donor mice (with parasitemia level of 30-37%) to the healthy one.  The donor mice will be euthanized with anesthesia and infected drops of blood will be collected through a cardiac puncture into the heparinized tube. Then, the standard inoculum which contains 1x107 of the infected RBCs in 0.2 ml of blood will be prepared. Finally, 0.2 ml of blood will be inoculated into the healthy mice intraperitoneally (Nureye, Assefa, Nedi, & Engidawork, 2018).\r\n3.7.2. Four–day suppressive test \r\nThe activity of the extract on early malaria infection will be tested based on Peter’s 4-day suppressive test(Peters et al., 1975).  On the first day of the study (D0), the animals will be grouped as described earlier and inoculated with 0.2 ml of the standard inoculum of parasitized erythrocytes intraperitoneally.   After 3 hours of inoculation, each group will be treated as described under the grouping and dosing section.  The treatment will proceed for 4 consecutive days (D0-D3) in a once-daily dosage.  On the 5th day (D4) of the test, blood will be taken from the tail vein of each mouse to determine the level of parasitemia and percentage of parasitemia suppression. And also, change in body weight, rectal temperature, and packed cell volume (PCV) of the infected mice will be determined.\r\n3.7.3. Rane’s test / Curative\r\nThe activity of the extract against established infection will be determined using Ryley and Peters procedure(Ryley & Peters, 1970). On the first day of the study (D0) animals will be grouped as mentioned earlier and inoculated with 0.2 ml of the standard inoculum of the parasitized erythrocytes intraperitoneally and left untreated for 72 hours or until the 4th day (D3) of the study. On D3 blood will be taken from the tail vein of the mouse to determine the level of the parasite before the treatment. After taking blood, the mice will be treated daily for 4 days (D3-D6) as described earlier under the grouping and dosing section. On the 8th day (D7) blood will be taken from the tail vein of the mouse to determine post-treatment parasitemia level and percentage parasitemia suppression.  In, addition changes in body weight, rectal temperature, and PCV will be determined.  \r\n3.7.4. Prophylactic/repository test \r\nThe prophylactic activity of the extract against the residual infection will be evaluated using the method applied by Peters (Peters, 1965).  On the first day (D0) of the study, the animals will be grouped and treated daily as described under the section on grouping and dosing and left uninfected until the 5th day (D4). On the D4 each mouse will be inoculated intraperitoneally with 0.2 ml of the standard inoculum of the infected red blood cells.  On the 8th day (D7), 72 hours post-infection,   blood will be taken from the tail vein of the mouse to determine the level of parasitemia and percentage of parasitemia suppression.\r\n3.8. Determination of percent parasitemia and parasitemia suppression \r\nThe level of the parasite in the blood (parasitemia) will be determined by taking blood from the tail vein onto the microscope slide. Then, a thin blood smear will be made on the microscope slide to be seen under the microscope in which infected red blood cells will be seen.  The percent parasitemia and parasitemia suppression activity of the extract will be determined in terms of the negative controls using the following formulas (Nureye et al., 2018).\r\n % parasitemia= number of infected RBCs    x100\r\n                                             Total number of RBC count\r\n% parasitemia suppression = mean parasitemia (negative control-treated)   x 100\r\n                                                        Mean parasitemia of negative control\r\n\r\n3.9. Determination of change in body weight and rectal temperature\r\nMalarial infection in rodents causes a decrease in body weight and loss of temperature.  Thus, the body weight and rectal temperature of the mice will be measured using a digital balance and rectal thermometer, respectively, just before the treatment and post-treatment.  Changes in body weight and temperature will be determined by measuring the difference between before and after the treatment as follows:\r\n% Change in body weight/ temperature = body weight/temperature (Di-Dj) x 100\r\n                                                                        Bodyweight/temperature at Di\r\n       Where; Di= day just before the treatment, Dj= day just after the treatment.\r\n\r\n3.10. Determination of mean survival time of the mice\r\nExtracts with antimalarial activity could prolong the survival time of the infected mice.  Thus, the mean survival time (MST) of the mice will be determined by monitoring the infected mice for a month.  Mean survival time will be determined using the following formula (Ayalew & Bekele, 2021).\r\n          MST = Total survival days of the mice in a group\r\n                        Total number of mice in that group\r\n\r\n3.11. Determination of packed cell volume \r\nThe activity of the extract against the parasite-induced hemolysis could be determined by measuring the packed cell volume (PCV).  It will be measured by taking blood from the tail vein of the mouse into a heparinized capillary tube until 3/4th of its height. Then the tubes will be sealed tightly and put on the microhematocrit centrifuge with the sealed end facing outward and will be centrifuged at 11,000 rpm for 5 minutes.  At the end of centrifugation, the tubes will be taken out of the centrifuge and PCV will be determined using the standard hematocrit reader. Finally, PCV will be calculated using the following formula (Misganaw, Engidawork, & Nedi, 2019).\r\n PCV= Volume of RBCs in a given volume of blood x 100\r\n             Total blood volume  \r\n\r\n3.12. Data analysis \r\nThe result will be expressed in terms of mean ± standard error of the mean (SEM). Data will be analyzed using one-way analysis of variance (ANOVA) followed by post hoc Tukey\'s test in version 21 SPSS within a confidence interval of 95%. \r\n\r\n3.13. Ethical approval \r\nFor the first approval of the study protocol, Jimma University Institutional Review Board will be requested.  Further approval of the study will be done by all concerned offices or institutions.','<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Adams, R., Neisess, K., Parkhurs, R., Makhubu, L., &amp; Yohannes, L. W. (1989). Phytolacca dodecandra (Phytolaccaceae) in Africa: geographical variation in morphology. <em>Taxon, 38</em>(1), 17-26. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Ayalew, M., &amp; Bekele, A. (2021). Evaluation of the Antimalarial Activity of Crude Extract of Eucalyptus globulus Labill. Leaf against Plasmodium berghei in Mice. <em>Evidence-Based Complementary and Alternative Medicine, 2021</em>. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Bekele, G., &amp; Reddy, P. R. (2015). Ethnobotanical study of medicinal plants used to treat human ailments by Guji Oromo tribes in Abaya District, Borana, Oromia, Ethiopia. <em>Universal Journal of Plant Science, 3</em>(1), 1-8. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Berhan, A., Asfaw, Z., &amp; Kelbessa, E. (2006). Ethnobotany of plants used as insecticides, repellents and antimalarial agents in Jabitehnan district, West Gojjam. <em>SINET: Ethiopian Journal of Science, 29</em>(1), 87-92. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Bloland, P. B., &amp; Organization, W. H. (2001). <em>Drug resistance in malaria</em>. Retrieved from </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Chekole, G. (2017). Ethnobotanical study of medicinal plants used against human ailments in Gubalafto District, Northern Ethiopia. <em>Journal of ethnobiology and ethnomedicine, 13</em>(1), 1-29. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Conrad, M. D., &amp; Rosenthal, P. J. (2019). Antimalarial drug resistance in Africa: the calm before the storm? <em>The Lancet infectious diseases, 19</em>(10), e338-e351. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Kendie, F. A., Nibret Semegn, E., &amp; Ferede, M. W. (2021). Prevalence of malaria among adults in Ethiopia: a systematic review and meta-analysis. <em>Journal of tropical medicine, 2021</em>. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Manandhar, S., Luitel, S., &amp; Dahal, R. K. (2019). In vitro antimicrobial activity of some medicinal plants against human pathogenic bacteria. <em>Journal of tropical medicine, 2019</em>. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Misganaw, D., Engidawork, E., &amp; Nedi, T. (2019). Evaluation of the anti-malarial activity of crude extract and solvent fractions of the leaves of Olea europaea (Oleaceae) in mice. <em>BMC complementary and alternative medicine, 19</em>(1), 171. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Mojab, F., Kamalinejad, M., Ghaderi, N., &amp; Vahidipour, H. R. (2010). Phytochemical screening of some species of Iranian plants. <em>Iranian Journal of Pharmaceutical Research</em>(2), 77-82. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Nega, D., Abera, A., Gidey, B., Mekasha, S., Abebe, A., Dillu, D., . . . Haile, M. (2021). Baseline malaria prevalence at the targeted pre-elimination districts in Ethiopia. <em>BMC Public Health, 21</em>(1), 1-11. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Nureye, D., Assefa, S., Nedi, T., &amp; Engidawork, E. (2018). In vivo antimalarial activity of the 80 Methanolic root bark extract and solvent fractions of Gardenia ternifolia Schumach. &amp; Thonn.(Rubiaceae) against Plasmodium berghei. <em>Evidence-Based Complementary and Alternative Medicine, 2018</em>. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Organization, W. H. (2021). World malaria report 2021. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Pandey, A., &amp; Tripathi, S. (2014). Concept of standardization, extraction and pre phytochemical screening strategies for herbal drug. <em>Journal of pharmacognosy and phytochemistry, 2</em>(5). </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Peters, W. (1965). Drug resistance in Plasmodium berghei. I. Chloroquine resistance. <em>Experimental parasitology, 17</em>(1), 80-89. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Peters, W., Portus, J., &amp; Robinson, B. (1975). The chemotherapy of rodent malaria, XXII: the value of drug-resistant strains of P. berghei in screening for blood schizontocidal activity. <em>Annals of Tropical Medicine &amp; Parasitology, 69</em>(2), 155-171. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Renslo, A. R. (2013). Antimalarial drug discovery: from quinine to the dream of eradication. In (Vol. 4, pp. 1126-1128): ACS Publications.</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Ryley, J., &amp; Peters, W. (1970). The antimalarial activity of some quinolone esters. <em>Annals of Tropical Medicine &amp; Parasitology, 64</em>(2), 209-222. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Tefera, B. N., &amp; Kim, Y.-D. (2019). Ethnobotanical study of medicinal plants in the Hawassa Zuria District, Sidama zone, Southern Ethiopia. <em>Journal of ethnobiology and ethnomedicine, 15</em>(1), 1-21. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Teklehaymanot, T., &amp; Giday, M. (2007). Ethnobotanical study of medicinal plants used by people in Zegie Peninsula, Northwestern Ethiopia. <em>Journal of ethnobiology and ethnomedicine, 3</em>(1), 1-11. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Temam, T. D. (2016). Ethnobotanical study of medicinal plants of Mirab-Badwacho district, Ethiopia. <em>Journal of BioScience and Biotechnology, 5</em>(2), 151-158. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Toxicity&ndash;Up, A. O. (2001). OECD GUIDELINE FOR TESTING OF CHEMICALS. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Tu, Y. (2011). The discovery of artemisinin (qinghaosu) and gifts from Chinese medicine. <em>Nature medicine, 17</em>(10), 1217-1220. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Tuasha, N., Petros, B., &amp; Asfaw, Z. (2018). Medicinal plants used by traditional healers to treat malignancies and other human ailments in Dalle District, Sidama Zone, Ethiopia. <em>Journal of ethnobiology and ethnomedicine, 14</em>(1), 1-21. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Wubetu, M., Abula, T., &amp; Dejenu, G. (2017). Ethnopharmacologic survey of medicinal plants used to treat human diseases by traditional medical practitioners in Dega Damot district, Amhara, Northwestern Ethiopia. <em>BMC research notes, 10</em>(1), 157. </span></span></span></span></p>','To evaluate the antimalarial activities of the crude extract and solvent fractions of the root of P. dodecandra against P. berghei infected mice',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,'Phytolaca dodecandra, Root, Antimalarial activity, Plasmodium berghei, Mice',10,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'Proposal-628e22c6e88c5217730922.docx'),(109,329,14,16,'This study aims to: determine the risks to surgical site infection (SSI) and explore determinants of time to SSI after cesarean section (CS) at Jimma Medical Center (JMC), identify the risk and determinants of time to contract nosocomial infection after neonatal hospital admission, and  analyze the risk and determinants of Intensive care unit (ICU) death due to nosocomial infection at JMC. The data will be gathered from primary and secondary sources.  We will be prospectively gathered from all women who are experiencing elective and emergency C-sections, all neonates who are stayed at least 48h at JMC neonatal ward during the period, and all adult ICU patients who are waited more than 48 hours after admission at ICU select from January 2, 2022, to January 1, 2023. We will test the hypothesis that there is no significant predictor related to time to SSI after CS. The analysis will be done on the time of events using a log-rank test. Mann-Whitney U-test will be utilized to compare the characteristics of with and without NIs of neonatal patients. The work will also use statistical model analysis, such as the survival model, the multistate model, competing for risk model, and joint modeling.','1.	Analysis of Surgical site infection after cesarean section  2.	Assessment of neonatal nosocomial infection 3.	 Determinants of  ICU death due to nosocomial infection','Risk analysis on nosocomial infection and associated mortality in Jimma Medical Center','2022-05-27 15:42:55','Identification of predictors on time to surgical site infection after cesarean section \r\n	Differentiation of predictors on time  to contract nosocomial infection after neonatal hospital admission   \r\n	Determining the risk of  NI associated mortality in patients admitted to adult ICU','266fd5680ea22332ecd06c03e9644ba7','completed','Nosocomial infections are a critical cause of sickness and death, and they can have to destroy emotional, financial, and medical results. The frequencies of NIs are varying in their sorts, estimates, and other traits of the clinics and the seriousness are high in patients at seriously care units. The most methods of reasoning for avoiding NIs are to diminish the load and supply utilization by dragging out the stay of patients in hospitals. If this wealth includes the value in elective utilization at that point, disease control programs can be qualifying with creating fetched ventures; these contamination control programs are expensive themselves, so the cost of contamination control should be comparing to the savings. Thus improving the adequacy of clinic administrations by a preventive approach for unseemly clinic confirmations and stay can coordinate to an increment in the efficiency of the hospitals, diminish the waiting list, and the resultant ideal utilize of existing wellbeing care offices without participating the quality of care. Therefore this consider will provide data on the prevalence of NI, time to infection, change of status of patients, and length of time to contract nosocomial infection for stakeholders.','The study will be conducted at JMC. The data will be gathered from primary and secondary sources. We will be prospectively gathered from all women who are experiencing elective and emergency C-sections, all neonates who are stayed at least 48h at JMC neonatal ward during the period, and all adult ICU patients who are waited more than 48 hours after admission at ICU select from January 2, 2022, to January 1, 2023. We will test the hypothesis that there is no significant predictor related to time to SSI after CS. The analysis will be done on the time of events using a log-rank test. Mann-Whitney U-test will be utilized to compare the characteristics of with and without NIs of neonatal patients. The work will also use statistical model analysis, such as the survival model, the multistate model, competing for risk model, and joint modeling.',NULL,'To examine  risks on nosocomial infection and associated  mortality in Jimma Medical Center',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,'nosocomial infection multistate model survival model',10,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'Proposal-6290c74f521ce865042575.doc'),(110,320,14,16,'This study aims to: determine the risks to surgical site infection (SSI) and explore determinants of time to SSI after cesarean section (CS) at Jimma Medical Center (JMC), identify the risk and determinants of time to contract nosocomial infection after neonatal hospital admission, and  analyze the risk and determinants of Intensive care unit (ICU) death due to nosocomial infection at JMC. The data will be gathered from primary and secondary sources.  We will be prospectively gathered from all women who are experiencing elective and emergency C-sections, all neonates who are stayed at least 48h at JMC neonatal ward during the period, and all adult ICU patients who are waited more than 48 hours after admission at ICU select from January 2, 2022, to January 1, 2023. We will test the hypothesis that there is no significant predictor related to time to SSI after CS. The analysis will be done on the time of events using a log-rank test. Mann-Whitney U-test will be utilized to compare the characteristics of with and without NIs of neonatal patients. The work will also use statistical model analysis, such as the survival model, the multistate model, competing for risk model, and joint modeling.','1.	Analysis of Surgical site infection after cesarean section  2.	Assessment of neonatal nosocomial infection 3.	 Determinants of  ICU death due to nosocomial infection','Risk analysis on nosocomial infection and associated mortality in Jimma Medical Center','2022-05-27 15:48:35','The expected outcome of the study will be \r\n	Identification of predictors on time to surgical site infection after cesarean section \r\n	Differentiation of predictors on time  to contract nosocomial infection after neonatal hospital admission   \r\n	Determining the risk of  NI associated mortality in patients admitted to adult ICU','4d4bc8c5cbb84f634e452438925f3298','completed','Nosocomial infections are a critical cause of sickness and death, and they can have to destroy emotional, financial, and medical results. The frequencies of NIs are varying in their sorts, estimates, and other traits of the clinics and the seriousness are high in patients at seriously care units.\r\nThe most methods of reasoning for avoiding NIs are to diminish the load and supply utilization by dragging out the stay of patients in hospitals. If this wealth includes the value in elective utilization at that point, disease control programs can be qualifying with creating fetched ventures; these contamination control programs are expensive themselves, so the cost of contamination control should be comparing to the savings. Thus improving the adequacy of clinic administrations by a preventive approach for unseemly clinic confirmations and stay can coordinate to an increment in the efficiency of the hospitals, diminish the waiting list, and the resultant ideal utilize of existing wellbeing care offices without participating the quality of care. Therefore this consider will provide data on the prevalence of NI, time to infection, change of status of patients, and length of time to contract nosocomial infection for stakeholders.','The study will be conducted at JMC. The data will be gathered from primary and secondary sources.  We will be prospectively gathered from all women who are experiencing elective and emergency C-sections, all neonates who are stayed at least 48h at JMC neonatal ward during the period, and all adult ICU patients who are waited more than 48 hours after admission at ICU select from January 2, 2022, to January 1, 2023. We will test the hypothesis that there is no significant predictor related to time to SSI after CS. The analysis will be done on the time of events using a log-rank test. Mann-Whitney U-test will be utilized to compare the characteristics of with and without NIs of neonatal patients.\r\nThe work will also use statistical model analysis, such as the survival model, the multistate model, competing for risk model, and joint modeling.',NULL,'To examine  risks on nosocomial infection and associated  mortality in Jimma Medical Center',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,'nosocomial infection multistate model survival model',10,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'Proposal-6290ba854a0af476179270.doc'),(111,314,13,15,'Background; One of the major components of the HSTP is strengthening the health management information system (HMIS) as information revolution of the country. HMIS represents a combination of health services based data source. The primary aim of HMIS is to support informed strategic decision-making by providing quality data that help managers and health workers plan and manage the health service system. Hence, the general objective of this study was to implement evidence based intervention in improving data quality and utilization of routine health information at a point of care whereby to determine the effect of technical and behavioral intervention through identifying factors affecting information utilization. The aim of this study is to evaluate the effectiveness of continuous data quality Improvement intervention implemented at health facility to improve quality of care in providing essential health services.  \r\n\r\nMethods: This implementation study will be conducted in Jimma town public health facilities from Sep 01, 2022-Feb30, 2023. Quasi-experimental study with pre-posttest design will be employed whereby 5 pubic health care facilities will be assessed at baseline and all of them will be included for the intervention based mean score of the data quality index identified at baseline. Minimum of 76 health professionals will be included in the intervention as per our sample size calculation. Among all health professionals who will be participated in the intervention, at least 50% of them will be sampled for mentorship/observation, follow up and demonstration to build their capacity.  Expected outcomes/outputs of the intervention will be  generated evidence using the baseline assessment on data quality, generated evidence on endline data to analyze the change on outcome variables, prioritized data quality gaps in health care delivery process, developed process and outcome indicators for the designed intervention, generated evidence on routine information utilization, improved data quality and utilization and improved knowledge, skill and practice. Self-administered structured questionnaire will be distributed for selected health professionals and observational checklist will be used by investigators to assess study units and departments to identify how data and information is generated such as observation of registration books, monthly and annual reports, and graph, charts and maps from health institutions and town health office. The data quality will be measured based on  different dimension and utilization will be measured at individual levels. Appropriate ways of data analysis will be done using SPSS version 25.0, and effect analysis will be also done to determine the effectiveness of the  technical and behavioral intervention.  \r\nWork and budget plan: the total time that needed to finalize this study will be 10 months with a total budget of 510,00ETB.','HMIS','Technical and Behavioral Intervention to Improve Data Quality and Utilization of Routine Health Information at a Point of Care in Public Health Facilities of Jimma Town, Southwest Ethiopia: a Quasi-Experimental Study','2022-05-26 16:35:25','Expected outcomes/outputs \r\n•	Generated evidence using the baseline assessment on data quality\r\n•	Generated evidence on endline data to analyze the change on outcome variables \r\n•	Prioritized data quality gaps in health care delivery process\r\n•	Developed process and outcome indicators for the designed intervention \r\n•	Generated evidence on routine information utilization \r\n•	Improved data quality and utilization\r\n•	Improved knowledge, skill and practice','3989de6f2a4126640ff08bb58be847d4','completed','Ethiopia is currently implementing Health Sector Transformation Plan (HSTP). The ongoing HSTP (2015/16-2019/2020) is aligned with and time frame of the wider National transformation plan  and  makes the health chapter  of  the  five  year  Plan  for ―ENVISIONING Ethiopia’s Path towards Universal Health Coverage through Strengthening Primary Health Care (1).\r\nOne of the major components of the HSTP is strengthening the health management information system (HMIS) as information revolution of the country. HMIS represents a combination of health services based data source. The primary aim of HMIS is to support informed strategic decision-making by providing quality data that help managers and health workers plan and manage the health service system. There have been repeated efforts to reform the HMIS in Ethiopia. The FMOH, considering the crucial role that HMIS would play in the successful implementation of HSTP’s strategic plan; has put information revolution as one among the four components of the health sector transformation agendas since HSTP in 2009 (2).\r\nToday\'s health care system in developing countries has undergone many structural changes over the years in response to prevailing health problems and in recognition of weakness in the existing health delivery system (1). Global infectious threats, scrutiny of progress towards the millennium development Goals, and performance-based release of donor funding have all contributed to this increased awareness of the need for evidence. Decentralization and delegation of budgetary controls have shifted much of this growing burden to the periphery, requiring districts to provide local health statistics as a basis for decision-making.\r\nCurrently, in Ethiopia, the emphasis of health systems development aims at the district level. Based on primary health care the district health system is supposed to be self-contained segment of the national health system and it comprises a well-defined population within a clearly delineated administrative and geographical area (3). The district health offices are coordinating the diversified spectrum of health services at the district level and the system contains a network of primary health units such as health center and health posts and district hospital in some areas. According to WHO, a district health system includes all the relevant health care activities such as promotive, preventive, curative and rehabilitative health services (1).\r\nRecognizing the manifold responsibilities of the district health team and the need to allocate scarce resources, the necessity of health management based on adequate information use become obvious and crucial. The main constraint to implement the primary health care approach as reported by most countries is inadequate information for the managerial process (1). As commitment to health sector reform has strengthened, the realization of a good information system is necessary to support reform. Many important operational decisions were made at district level. Increased decentralization and increased focus on preventive and primary health care will increase the importance of decisions made at districts, moreover; a good management system at a district level increases efficiency and effectiveness of woreda level decision makers (4).\r\nThe needs of and the expectations in information systems in a district health system in a developing country must be seen in the context of the involved health systems. These are different from health systems in industrialized countries mainly due to the available resources. The historical background of a health system and the cultural set up also has an impact on the structure of health systems as they are today.\r\nIn literature, the terms \"Health Information System\" and \"Health Management Information System\" are used alternatively for information systems in district health systems, while the latter is becoming more popular. Both terms basically refer to the same thing. The term \"Health Information System\" tends to be used with more impact on the nationally organized health statistics. The term \"Health Management Information System\" is often used with the intention, to broaden the view towards the management impact of an information system. This term also values the need for resource management, logistics and staff information. Since in this particular thesis the focus is on information processing directly related to health, the term \"Health Information System\" shall be applied. But this does not exclude the integration into the managerial process (WHO, 1987).\r\nThe complex nature of district health system brings a complex nature of the involved information system. An information system in a district health system can be defined as the partial system of a district health system, which dealing with, data generation, sharing, analysis and complete information processing and storing of the district health system. The information system has to make available: - the right information, the right knowledge, to the right persons and institutions, in the right form, at the right time, and in the right place. This broad definition comprises the various expectations in information systems for district health systems. The scope of the needed information ranges from mortality and morbidity data over stock control data to knowledge on how to prevent certain diseases. The right persons and institutions include for example the members of the district health team, the ministry of health, and the donor agencies which promote vertical programs and also the communities and individual clients of a health service.\r\n\r\nThe right form for a morbidity report to the ministry of health might be just a computer printed list, while donor agencies require a much more complex impact evaluation. On the other hand, the right form for health education requires a very informal, but culturally embedded and educational sound presentation of health issues. The right time for information about an upcoming cholera epidemic differs from the right time for sporadic oral health education seminars. Looking at the right places, it is evident that transport plays an essential role in information systems (3, 4).\r\nResearch done in most parts of the world reported that information collected using top- down system would have little value for health care action at the site of data generation because it was supposed to be inappropriate and erroneous (4,5). This does not only make accurate data collection difficult but considered as not useful for decision- making (5). A similar study done in Ghana and Uganda about the utilization rate of information suggested that not more than 10% and 20% of the information enter in a register is ever used to improve management in any meaningful way respectively (6-8). The importance of Health information systems have increased from the existence of primary health care to create better opportunities for community oriented decision making rather than political and bureaucratic supervision (5).\r\nAccording to 2002/03-2004/5 Ministry of Health HSDP II report there is lack of coordination in leadership, strategy, policy, and of having skilled human resources and of guidelines, timeliness, and completeness of health information system. Health information system remains poor and these problems contribute to the failure to use data as the basis for informed decision-making in planning and other management functions. This situation is observed in most parts of the health sector where much has to be done to develop strategies to know the magnitude of the disease and to develop intervention activities like to request drugs, develop appropriate IEC programs and any relevant/timely decision making. Parallel reporting mechanism resulted in multiple reporting formats and increased administrative workload (1, 10). Survey done by Essential Services of Health for Ethiopia (ESHE) in some part of Amhara Region, reported that utilization of information at district and health facilities level was partial and uneven. More systematic, long term monitoring and data based planning were not inherent at district and regional level (11).\r\nTherefore, this implementation study will measure the quality (Inputs, accuracy/validity, completeness, timeliness) and utilization of data and associated factors at a point of primary health care level at baseline and endline to determine the effect of intervention packages in Jimma town, Southwest Ethiopia. The findings are believed to contribute to strengthen the health information system in Ethiopia in general and, in the intervention area in particular.','Intervention area and period \r\nThis implementation study will be conducted in Jimma town public health facilities from Sep 01, 2022-Feb30, 2023. Jimma Town is one of the towns in Oromia regional states and it has 17 Kebeles, four health centers, one public primary hospital, and 17 working sites for urban health extension professionals.  In those health facilities there are 259 health workers (professionals and supportive staff) and 59 urban health extension professionals.  From the record of Jimma Town health office, there were 210,908 (M=106044, F=104863) total population living within total household of 43939. Shenan Gibe Hospital serves 25% of Jimma town populations as the catchment population. It runs an annual governmental budget of 25,296380 Birr with bed capacity of 63 and had 172 staff. It was established in 2003 by Oromia regional state and give service under four major areas, 22680 inpatients, 29,940 outpatient attendants, 10,873 births attended and 12,963 emergency services. \r\nHence, our intervention facility will be one primary hospital (Shenen gibe hospital) and 4 health centers (Jimma HC, Mendera Kochi HC, Hermata Mentina HC and Bocho bore HC)  in the town. \r\nIntervention/study design\r\nQuasi-experimental study with pre-posttest design will be employed whereby 5 pubic health care facilities will be assessed at baseline and all of them will be included for the intervention based mean score of the data quality index identified at baseline. The aim of this study is to evaluate the effectiveness of continuous data quality Improvement intervention implemented at health facility to improve quality of care in providing essential health services.  \r\nSample size Estimation  \r\nFor baseline and end-line sample (Health professionals) \r\nThe sample size was calculated using single population proportion formulae with the following assumptions.\r\n•	P = 50% assumed that the proportion of information utilization at health facilities level among health professionals.  \r\n•	D = 5% degree of precision. 95%\r\n•	confidence interval, á=0.05 \r\n•	n = Z á/2p (1-P)/d2\r\n•	By adding 10% non-response rate\r\nBased on these assumptions, the total calculated sample size =423 health professionals and health managers. However, due to the source population(190) is less than 10,000, we apllied finite population correction formula and the final sample size become 76. However, based on the budget, we may include all health professionals working in the selected health facilities. Among all health professionals who will be participated in the intervention, at least 50% of them will be sampled for mentorship/observation, follow up and demonstration to build their capacity. \r\nIntervention approach\r\nThe intervention will follow pre-post test approach with three steps such as baseline assessment, intervention and finally end-line assessment. The intervention time will be for 6 months (because to test the quality of data and to see improvement on utilization, it needs reasanable time) between the baseline and end-line assessment. For the knowledge, attitude and practice gaps to be indentified, at least three times training will be provided intensively and then follow up and mentoring will be conducted.   \r\nIntervention activities to improve data quality and utilization (intervention outcome) \r\nFor technical/competency (knowledge and skill) \r\nTo improve Knowledge and attitude \r\n•	training on data generation and handling process \r\n•	provision of adapted 1st line data generating standard tool \r\n•	Follow up/ supportive supervision  \r\nTo improve Skill \r\n•	mentoring digitalization process (demonstration)\r\n•	identifying skill gaps through observation and taking corrective measures  \r\nFor behavioral intervention (training and follow up/mentorship)  \r\n•	To improve:-data ownership, commitment, simplicity of data processing, information utilization    \r\nOutcomes of intervention \r\n-Primary outcome:-To improve data quality and utilization \r\n-Secondary outcomes:- Knowledge, skill and behavioral improvement \r\nExpected outcomes/outputs \r\n•	Generated evidence using the baseline assessment on data quality\r\n•	Generated evidence on endline data to analyze the change on outcome variables \r\n•	Prioritized data quality gaps in health care delivery process\r\n•	Developed process and outcome indicators for the designed intervention \r\n•	Generated evidence on routine information utilization \r\n•	Improved data quality and utilization\r\n•	Improved knowledge, skill and practice \r\n\r\nMeasuring tool (baseline and end-line) \r\nEnglish version of questionnaire will be developed after extensive revision of relevant literature. The questions and statement will be grouped and arranged according to the particular objectives. Quantitative data will be collected using per-tested and standardized questionnaire using same items at baseline and endline. The questionnaire will be pre-tested before the final work and appropriate adjustment will be done. Qualitative data will be collected from town health offices and facility level by observation of each departments and units using observational checklist to supplement quantitative data to verify events. \r\nSelf-administered structured questionnaire will be distributed for selected health professionals and observational checklist will be used by investigators to assess study units and departments to identify how data and information is generated such as observation of registration books, monthly and annual reports, and graph, charts and maps from health institutions and town health office. The data quality will be measured based on  different dimesion and utilizatin will be measured at individual levels. For the assessment of data quality we will use LQAS approach using the following indicators: Maternal health: Antenatal care first visit, Fourth visit, Cach up/missed visit and institutional deliveries, Immunization: BCG, Pentavalent/DTP third doses and Measel in children under one year, HIV indicators: PMTCT coverage, Family planning: Contraceptive users were included for data quality and health information utilization assessment.\r\n\r\nOperational definitions\r\nUtilization of information for decision making: ‘1’ for planning, ‘2’ for budget preparation/allocation, ‘3’ for quality improvement, ‘4’ for monitoring activities, and ‘5’ for presence of key indicators with charts or tables (indicators were not expected to be same, that is, they may vary from one unit to another). So health workers working in health facilities and woreda health office will be considered as utilizing health information when they practice at least three of these five criteria.\r\nData quality - the degree to which the data or statistics measure what was intended to be measured when the data collection system was designed in terms of completeness, accuracy, and timeliness.\r\nHealth information - is health care data that have been organized in to a meaningful format, aggregate information about all patients and other related activities important for patients/clients and for overall services improvement. \r\nHealth information system – interrelated components for acquiring and analysis of the data and providing information for the management of health system and monitoring of health activities.\r\nQuality assurance  \r\nAppropriate Training will be given to data collectors and supervisors. Pre-test will be carried out as found appropriate. Close supervision of data collection and checking for accuracy and completeness of data will be carried out. The questionnaire initially prepared in English will be translated into Amharic then back translated into English to ensure consistency. The questionnaire will be pretested on 5 % of the actual sample size in Agaro Town administration before the actual data collection starts to make correction on tool accordingly. Training will be provided to both data collectors and supervisors for one day by the principal investigator and some members of the core research team. There will be supervision on daily basis, and checking on 10% of the collected questionnaire will be conducted by the investigator and after entry to Epidata, verification will be done to check error report.\r\nMethod of Data analysis\r\nData will be entered, cleaned and analyzed using EPI data 3.1 and will be analyzed using SPSS version 21 statistical packages. Descriptive analysis for utilization rate of information by sex, age, yeas of services, supervision and training and determinate factors will be analyzed. Relative risk (risk of generating poor data quality that reduced due to the intervention and use of information) and confidence interval will be calculated to show the association between the utilization of information and exposure variables including intervention variable. Multivariable Logistic regression analysis will be employed using identified candidate variable from Bivariate analysis (p<0.25)to avoid the confounding effect of the exposure variables such as sex, educational status, training, supervision, year of services, salary and some other factors one over the other. With 95% CI, COR and AOR will be used for final interpretation using p<0.05.','<p>1. FMOH, Health sector transformation plan: Federal democratic republic of Ethiopia ministry of health; FMOH, 2009. And WHO, regional office for Africa, Health information systems development and strengthening, guidance on needs assessment for national health information systems development, 2000. 2. Department, Ethiopian health sector strategic plan (HSDPIII) 2005/6-2009/2010.Addis Ababa: Federal Ministry of Health; pal 2005 and Bulletin of the world health organization August 2005, 83 (8). 3. MOH, Ethiopian health sector development program II, 2002/03-2004/05. 4. WHO, Design and implementation of health information systems, 2000. 5. World health forum, how should information on health care can be generated and used;1987,8:408- 409. 6. Maria G.N.Musokeetal; information and its value to health workers in rural Uganda: aqualitative perspective, Black well science Ltd, health bibaries review, 2000,17, pp 194-202 7. B.Campbell,S.Adjei,A.H.eywood:From data to decision making in health, the evaluation of a health management information system; Royal tropical Institute, Amesterdam theNetherlands,1996. 8. BASICS II, JSC; The status of referrals in the three Districts in Ghana. Analysis of Referral pathways for children under five Centers for child health, 1996. 9. JGLADWIN, RADIXON and TDWILSON, implementing a new health management information system in Uganda. health policy planning, Oxford university press 2003;18(2):214-224, 10. MOH, Ethiopian health sector development program. Final evaluation of HSDP II 2005. 11. ESHE II, Report of the baseline needs assessment for performance improvement, Amhara Regional state, August 9, 2004. 12. R.E.Cibulskis, and G. Hiawalyer, Information system for health sector monitoring inPapua New Guinea;Bulletin of the world health organization vol. 80, no 9 Genebra,2002.bulletin@who.int. 13. World Health Forum, Appropriate information: new products and services 1993,VOL.14, NO.4. 14. Natalie Kenney, survey researcher, Alison MacFarlane, reader in perinatal and public health statistics, Identifying problem with data collection at a local level: survey of NHSmaternity in England. BMJ, 1999 September 4; 319(7210):619-622. 15. MOH, Ethiopia, report of a health system baseline survey, April/may 1995. 16. ESHE, health information system, a reference guide line for health workers in the SNNPR RHB,ZHD, Hospitals, WHOf, HC, HS, and health posts. June 2002. 17. World Health Forum, Evangeline E.Suva, Gathering information for health 1986,VOL.7, NO.4. 18. Bwalya .C.Njelesani, Zambia health management information system (HMIS).http://www.vaccinealliance.org/print/print.php?page id=1023 and language id=4. 19. WHO, regional office for western pacific, Improving data quality, a guide for developing countries, 2000. 20. Peter Kintuetal. Development of HMIS in poor countries: Uganda as a case study. Health policy and Development 2005, vol.3, no.1. 21. Management information systems and microcomputer in primary health care, report of an international workshop organized by the Aga Khan foundation, the Agar Khan University, and the National School of Public Health, Ministry of Health ,Portugal conducted at the Gulbenkian foundation conference center Lisbon, Portugal, November,1987.</p>','To implement evidence based intervention in improving data quality and utilization of routine health information at a point of care whereby to detemine the effect of technical and behavioral intervention through identifying factors affecting information utilization.',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,'HMIS, data quality, routine information utilization, technical and behavioral intervention,  primary health care, Jimma',10,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'Proposal-628f808ec2998261884259.docx'),(112,185,13,18,'Background: The comorbidity of HIV with other chronic illnesses, especially non-communicable diseases (NCDs) is a serious concern among people living with HIV/AIDS. The advancement of antiretroviral therapy (ART) has significantly changed the outcome of HIV from a death sentence into a chronic condition because of people living with HIV/AIDS live longer. Advanced age brings a risk of NCD comorbidity from the traditional risk factors. The impacts of NCDs-HIV coexistence in sub-Saharan Africa, including Ethiopia, are immense. Determining the burden of the commonest NCDs comorbidity on HIV patients and their risk factors has a significant contribution to the management of these comorbid conditions.\r\nObjective: To identify the hidden burden of chronic non-communicable disease among HIV-infected people on ART in Jimma town health facilities, Ethiopia.\r\nMethods and materials: A facility-based cross-sectional study design will be employed among 740 adult people living with HIV/AIDS who are getting ART treatment in Jimma town health facilities. Study respondents will be proportionally allocated based on the health facilities, and a systematic random sampling technique will be used to recruit a study unit. Data will be obtained using the WHO STEPS Questionnaire, Anthropometric measurements, blood pressure measurements, and biochemical measurements. Descriptive and Logistic regression analysis will be computed to identify the magnitude and risk factors and predict the associated factors.\r\nTimeline and budget request: The study will be carried out from From August, 2022 to December, 2022 and the total budget will be 438088  ETB.',NULL,'The hidden burden of chronic non-communicable disease among HIV infected people on ART in Jimma town health facilities, Ethiopia','2022-05-28 16:47:22','Expected outcomes (the impact or changes in the field that the project will have contributed to)\r\nThe generated pieces of evidence are expected to contribute to the early recognition of hypertension among HIV-infected people, and the designing of effective prevention and control management for it.\r\nEvidence-based promotive/preventive interventions will be designed to minimize the risk of hypertension. \r\nThe generated pieces of evidence are expected to contribute to early recognition of DM, and the designing of effective prevention and control management among HIV infected people\r\nEvidence-based promotive/ preventive interventions will be designed to minimize the risk of diabetic mellitus.\r\nEarly recognition of common mental disorders among HIV-infected people will be made, and evidence-based effective prevention and control management will be designed.','05a81a99afeb526389bc0a4eeea0d59e','completed','Background \r\nHuman Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV) and Acquired Immune Deficiency Syndrome (AIDS) is continued to be serious public health concerns. It disproportionately affects low- and middle-income countries. In 2020, there were nearly 37.7 million people across the world with HIV/AIDS. Among these, around 36 million were adults. Over two-thirds of all people living with HIV live in Africa and 680,000 people lost their lives from AIDS-associated diseases in 2020 (1). In Ethiopia, 665,723 people were estimated to live with HIV (PLHIV) in 2018 and more than three-quarter (79 %) of patients with HIV adults (aged between15&64 years) know their HIV status. Nearly all (97 %) of the HIV-positive adults were on ART (2).  \r\nPLHIV raises concern about the coexistence of HIV with other chronic illnesses, particularly non-communicable diseases (NCDs) (3). Enhanced access to anti-retroviral treatment has changed HIV into a chronic disorder because HIV-positive people live longer. Advanced age brings an increased risk of NCD comorbidity from the traditional risk factors. Importantly, patient with HIV/AIDS is associated with a higher likelihood of NCDs (4). The 2017 WHO progress monitor revealed that NCDs kill 41 million people annually, 71% of all deaths across the globe. Each year, 15 million people who lost their lives from an NCD are between the ages of 30 and 69 years; over 85% of these \"early\" deaths occur in developing countries. Cardiovascular diseases are a reason for most NCD deaths, followed by cancers, respiratory diseases, and diabetes (5). \r\nThe epidemiologic intersection between NCDs and HIV; the impact of NCDs-HIV comorbidity in low- and middle-income countries, including Ethiopia, are likely to be very huge (6, 7). Patients with HIV/AIDS and NCDs share many common risk factors. These mostly include unhealthy diets, sedentary lifestyles, tobacco use, and harmful use of alcohol (8). Furthermore, evidence on the underlying mechanisms showed that there is a multifaceted interface among HIV infection, ART, and NCDs risk factors. NCDs in people living with HIV/AIDS are occurring due to a combination of chronic immune activation, medication side effects, co-infections, and the aging process itself. Moreover, they may get deteriorated by HIV and some antiretroviral drugs (9). However, it is only relatively recently that NCDs have received due attention in developing countries. Subsequently, undiagnosed NCDs were found to be key problems as the individuals are unaware of their status in this regard (10).\r\nAccording to 2020 WHO progress monitors, 39% of the total death that occurred in Ethiopia is secondary to non-communicable diseases (11). The finding of the research conducted in Bahir Dar city, Ethiopia among patients who were receiving ART showed that the magnitude of NCDs (hypertension and DM) was 19.6 %. Among these, 14.1 % of them had hypertension and 8.6 % had diabetes mellitus (12). \r\nCommon mental disorders (Depression and anxiety) are extremely prevalent among patients who are living with HIV/AIDS. There is an interdependence and vicious circularity between mental health and HIV/AIDS. Research findings from the high-income countries revealed that around half of all PLHIV have a diagnosable mental disorder and in some cases a threefold higher rate of mental disorder. When mental illness and HIV co-occur; there is growing evidence that the progression of the virus is greater and poor adherence to medication (13, 14). Patients with HIV/AIDS have a higher chance of developing common mental disorders than healthy individuals (15). HIV/AIDS presents a major emotional and cognitive burden to the infected individuals. PLHIV are often affected by common mental disorders, as they adjust to the effect of the diagnosis of being infected and face the challenges of living with a chronic life-threatening disease (16). \r\nMany studies have found that PLHIV acquires higher rates of mental health problems than the general community (17). The finding from Hawassa city, Ethiopia among patients attending antiretroviral therapy clinics revealed that the magnitude of common mental disorders was 32.7% (18). Likely, another study conducted in Alert Hospital, Addis Ababa, Ethiopia showed that the prevalence of depression and anxiety among HIV patients was 41.2 % and 32.4 % respectively (19).\r\nThe rationale of the study\r\nManagement of HIV-NCD comorbidity is much more complex and challenging for the health system, patients, and their families compared to those without the disorders (20). Various research works and reports from health care facilities revealed that NCDs currently have a higher burden on the health of the people and health system in low-income countries. Pieces of evidence from various studies forecasted that NCDs will rise in the future, thus, an imperative way to mitigate NCDs is to give a due emphasis to reducing the risk factors (21). Evidence on the burden of HIV and the common NCDs comorbidity, determining factors, and searching how well the health system and patients are responding to this striking epidemic have paramount importance to plan and strengthen the prevailing prevention and management strategies. Hence, this study intended to assess the hidden burden of common non-communicable diseases among HIV-infected people on ART in Jimma town health facilities, south-west Ethiopia.','4.	Design and methods \r\na)	Study area and period: The study will be done in Jimma Town, from February 1-30/2022 in HIV-infected adults people aged ≥ 18 years on ART in Jimma Town health facilities, Ethiopia. The town is located in Oromia regional state, southwest Ethiopia, 355 km away from the capital city Addis Ababa. According to Central Statistical Agency (CSA, 2007) report, the estimated total population of the town for the year 2020/21 is 207 573 with a 1:1 male to female ratio. \r\nb)	Study design: Institution based cross-sectional study \r\nc)	Population\r\ni.	Source population: All adult people living with HIV/AIDS who are attending Jimma town health facilities. \r\nii.	Study population: Study participants will be randomly selected from adult people living with HIV who are on ART follow-up at the health facilities of Jimma Town.\r\niii.	Inclusion and exclusion criteria: All adult people living with HIV aged ≥ 18 years will be included in the current study. Nonetheless, adults with physical deformities (kyphosis, sclerosis), pregnant women, and adults with known NCDs will be excluded from this study.\r\nd)	Sampling procedure and sample size estimation; \r\nA stratified random sampling technique will be used to select study participants from the source population. First strata will be formed based on the health facilities with ART services, then study participants will be selected using systematic random sampling techniques based on the proportion to the size of ART followers.\r\nThe sample size will be calculated using single population proportion formula, by considering, prevalence of NCDs 19.6% (12), 95% CI, 3% margin of error, 10% non-response rate; the final sample size will be 673 + (0.1*673)= 740 .\r\nN = =  \r\nData collection      \r\nData will be collected using WHO STEPS Questionnaire (22) adapted to the local context based on the study objectives.  A stepwise approach will be conducted to collect socio-demographic data, behavioral, depression, and clinical measurements. For measuring the mood, anxiety, eating, alcohol, and somatoform disorders status of the study participants, PRIME-MD Patient Health Questionnaire (PHQ) will be used. WHO Alcohol, Smoking and Substance Involvement Screening Test (ASSIST) tool will be used to assess the study participant\'s alcohol, Khat, and tobacco use.\r\nAnthropometric measurements: The height of the study participants will be measured to the nearest 0.1 cm using a Stadiometer (seca Germany) with the participants positioned at the Frankfurt Plane and the four points (heel, calf, buttocks, and shoulder) touching the vertical stand and their shoes are taken off. Before starting the measurements, the stadiometer will be checked using calibration rods. Weight will be measured using a digital weight scale to the nearest 0.1 kg with the subjects wearing light closes and shoes taken off. The validity of the scale will be checked using an object of a known weight every morning and between the measurements. Waist circumferences will be measured at the midway between the lowest costal margin at the midclavicular line and the anterior superior iliac spine using fixed tension tape. All anthropometric measurements will be done in triplicate and the average value will be used for further analyses. Standardization exercises will be done to reduce inter-observer error. Body mass index (BMI) will be calculated as the weight in kg divided by height in meters squared (kg/ m2).\r\nBlood pressure: Blood pressure will be measured in triplicate using an Aneroid Sphygmomanometer with small, medium, and large cuff sizes (23), as fit to the subjects, after 5 min of rest. The subsequent measurements will be done 5 min apart. Following the WHO recommendation, the mean systolic and diastolic blood pressures will be considered for analysis.\r\nLipid profile and blood glucose level: Body composition and laboratory analyses of lipid profile and random blood sugar (RBS) level will be done. The laboratory parameters will be determined according to the standard operating procedures. Five 5 ml venous blood will be collected to determine participants\' RBS levels and lipid profiles. The subjects will be instructed to wait for a laboratory examination. The serum will be carried out in ABX Pentra 400 Automated Chemistry Machine (Horiba ABX SAS, 34184 Montpellier, France) at JMC Clinical chemistry core laboratory using to determine RBS and lipid profile including total cholesterol (TC), high-density lipoprotein (HDL), and triacylglycerol (TAG). The low-density lipoprotein (LDL) level will be calculated by using the Freidwald formula (24). Measurement of physical and biochemical factors will be carried out based on the standard operating procedures.\r\nA laboratory technologist, who does not know the participants\' history or other measurements, will determine all laboratory values. Lipid profiles and RBS concentrations will be reported as mg/dl.e)	Study  variables\r\nDependent Variables \r\n•	Diabetic Mellitus \r\n•	Hypertension\r\n•	Mental Disorders \r\nIndependent Variables \r\n•	Socio-economic and demographic (age, sex, education, income/wealth index.) factors;\r\n•	Behavioral related risk factors (cigarette smoking, alcohol drinking, dietary practice, Khat chewing, and level of physical activity)\r\n•	Disease-related factors (CD4 count, stage of disease, duration of knowing the status)\r\n•	KAP of NCDs\r\n•	Anthropometric measurements (Ratio of waist circumference to hip circumference, and BMI)\r\n•	Biochemical tests (lipid profile, Blood Glucose)\r\n•	Blood pressure\r\nf)	Operational definition\r\n1.	Undiagnosed NCD: Participants who are not aware of their status regarding hypertension, diabetes mellitus, depression, anxiety, and substance use disorder.  \r\n2.	Knowledge of NCDs:  will be measured by validated tools for developing countries. It consists of 39 questions related to the community\'s knowledge about NCDs and their risk factors. Correct answers will be coded 1 and coded by 0. The total possible score will be ranged from 0 to 39 and it will be expressed as a percentage. At a cut-off level ≥ 60%, the median value of the individual percentage scores will be selected as an indicator of \"good\" knowledge.\r\n3.	Cutoff points for BP: After the participants are allowed to take rest for 5 minutes, blood pressure will be measured. Based on WHO classification systolic blood pressure (SBP) will be stratified into four levels: <140 mmHg (normal), 140-159 mmHg (mildly raised), and 160-179 mmHg (moderately raised), and 180+ mmHg (severely raised).  Similarly, diastolic blood pressure (DBP) will be stratified into four levels: < 90 mmHg (normal), 90-99 mmHg (mildly raised), 100-109 mmHg (moderately raised), and 110+ mmHg (severely raised). A participant will be classified as having normal blood pressure if both SBP and DBP are normal.  Otherwise, a participant will be classified as having mild hypertension when SBP and/or DBP are mildly raised; moderate hypertension when SBP and/or DBP are moderately raised; and severe hypertension when SBP and/or DBP are severely raised (25).\r\n4.	Waist circumference (WC): measurement will be taken at the midpoint between the lower margin of the last rib and the iliac crest using a stretch-resistant measurement tape. Just before taking the measurement, participants will be requested to stand with their feet together, place their arms at the side of their body with the palms of their hands facing inwards, and breathe out gently. The cut of values is 102 cm for male and 88 cm for females and the values greater than this number indicates a risk factor for obesity.\r\n5.	Waist to hip ratio: It will be determined by dividing waist to hip in centimeters. The cut-off values are 0.90 cm for males and 0.85cm for females and values greater than the cut-off value will be considered as at risk for obesity. \r\n6.	Cutoff points for blood glucose level: participants will be considered as having normal blood glucose levels if serum glucose is between (<200 mg/dL). Otherwise, abnormal.\r\n7.	Cutoff points for lipid profile: Lipid profiles such as LDL, TC, TGC, and HDL will be measured by Horiba ABX SAS, 34184 Montpellier, France.  Lipids levels will be classified according to WHO classification. In this study, the desirable level of TC will be 240 mg/dl. Similarly, the optimal level of LDL will be 190 mg/dl otherwise it will be defined as very high. Triglycerides level will be considered normal when it is 500 mg/dl. HDL will be considered desirable if it is >40 mg/dl.\r\n5.	Data management and statistical analysis \r\n	First, the data will be checked for completeness and consistency. The data will be cleaned by checking outliers and missing values. Data will be entered in Epi Data version 3.1and exported into SPSS version 20 for further analysis. Descriptive analysis of the background characters will be performed. Normality will be checked for continuous variables. Binary logistic regression analysis will be used to measure the possible association between dependent and independent factors. Variance inflation factor will be used to check multicollinearity and Before drawing conclusions based on the logistic regression model, the Hosmer-Lemeshow goodness of fit test will be checked to check the goodness of fit (P- value> 0.05). Bivariate logistic regression at p-value ≤ 0.25 will be used for selecting candidate variables for multiple logistic regressions. Variables with a P-value ≤0.05 on multiple logistic regression analysis will be considered as independent predictors of the outcome variables at a 95% confidence interval.\r\n6.	Quality assurance \r\n	To ensure the data quality, and appropriately designed data collection instrument will be used. Trained BSc nurses, who will be recruited, based on their qualifications and prior experience in data collection will collect the data. The data collectors will be trained for two days before the actual data collection on interviewing approach, sampling taking, anthropometric measurement, and data recording. Calibration of instruments will be done to increase the reliability and validity of the measurements. All the measurements and interviews will be conducted under the close supervision of the research team. The data collectors will be selected depending on their abilities to speak the local language and experience in the clinical setup as well as on the data collection. A questionnaire for the survey will be first prepared in English language, then translated into Afaan-Oromo and Amharic, then translated back into English to check for consistency. Then, the questionnaire will be pre-tested on 5% of the sample size in Agaro Town for its clarity and time required. Based on a pretest, the additional adjustment will be made to terminologies and the formats of the questionnaire.\r\n7.	Plan for dissemination and ensure utilization of findings \r\n	The findings of the study will be disseminated through a presentation at a scientific conference and publication in peer-reviewed reputable journals. The findings will be also communicated to the local health planners and other relevant stakeholders at the national, regional, zonal, and town health bureau levels to enable them to take and apply research recommendations for improving the quality of services.\r\n8.	Ethical and environmental considerations \r\n	Before commencing the actual study, ethical clearance will be obtained from Jimma University, Institute of Health Ethical Review Committee. The supportive letter will be secured from Jimma town and respective health institutions. In addition, written informed consent will be obtained from the study participants. All the participants\' information will be kept confidential using a coding system and no direct benefit will be provided for the participants except cost-free lipid profiles, blood glucose and hypertension will be performed for each participant. Five ml venous blood collection will be performed by experienced laboratory technologists following the standard operating procedure. Those who are found to be positive for the measurements of this study will be advised and linked to a chronic care unit for further evaluation.  All materials and equipment used for the study will be safely managed and disposed of accordingly.','<p><span style=\"font-size:14px\"><span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman,Times,serif\"><strong>References&nbsp;</strong></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<ol>\r\n	<li><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">UNAIDS. Global HIV and AIDS Statistics. FACT SHEET [Internet]. 2021; Available from:</span></span> <u><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\"><span style=\"color:blue\"><a href=\"https://www.unaids.org/en/resources/fact-sheet\"><span style=\"color:blue\">https://www.unaids.org/en/resources/fact-sheet</span></a></span></span></span></u></li>\r\n	<li><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">PEPFAR-E PE. Ethiopia Country Operational Plan COP2020/FY2021 Strategic Direction Summary. Pepfar-report, March 2020; 3&ndash;12.</span></span> <span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Available from:</span></span> <u><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\"><span style=\"color:blue\"><a href=\"ht