ont-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Oni T, Youngblood E, Boulle A, McGrath N, Wilkinson RJ, Levitt NS. Patterns of HIV, TB, and non-communicable disease multi-morbidity in peri-urban South Africa- a cross-sectional study. BMC Infect Dis. 2015;15(1):1&ndash;8. </span></span></li>\r\n	<li><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Hemkens LG, Bucher HC. HIV infection and cardiovascular disease. Eur Heart J. 2014;35(21):1373&ndash;81. </span></span></li>\r\n	<li><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Kavishe B, Biraro S, Baisley K, Vanobberghen F, Kapiga S, Mundari P, et al. High prevalence of hypertension and risk factors for non-communicable diseases (NCDs): A population-based cross-sectional survey of NCDS and HIV infection in Northwestern Tanzania and Southern Uganda. BMC Med [Internet]. 2015;13(1):1&ndash;21. Available from: <u><span style=\"color:blue\"><a href=\"http://dx.doi.org/10.1186/s12916-015-0357-9\"><span style=\"color:blue\">http://dx.doi.org/10.1186/s12916-015-0357-9</span></a></span></u> </span></span></li>\r\n	<li><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">&nbsp;WHO. Noncommunicable Diseases: progress monitor. 2020. 69 p. Available from <u><span style=\"color:blue\"><a href=\"../../user/Downloads/9789240000490-eng%20(3).pdf\"><span style=\"color:blue\">file:///C:/Users/user/Downloads/9789240000490-eng%20(3).pdf</span></a></span></u> </span></span></li>\r\n	<li><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Getahun Z, Azage M, Abuhay T, Abebe F. Comorbidity of HIV, hypertension, and diabetes and associated factors among people receiving antiretroviral therapy in Bahir Dar city, Ethiopia. J Comorbidity. 2020;10:1&ndash;12. </span></span></li>\r\n	<li><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Chibanda D, Cowan F, Gibson L, Weiss HA, Lund C. Prevalence and correlates of probable common mental disorders in a population with a high prevalence of HIV in Zimbabwe. BMC Psychiatry [Internet]. 2016;16(1). Available from: <u><span style=\"color:blue\"><a href=\"http://dx.doi.org/10.1186/s12888-016-0764-2\"><span style=\"color:blue\">http://dx.doi.org/10.1186/s12888-016-0764-2</span></a></span></u> </span></span></li>\r\n	<li><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Ford N, Vitoria M, Penazzato M, Doherty M, Shubber Z, Meintjes G, et al. Causes of hospital admission among people living with HIV worldwide: A systematic review and meta-analysis. Lancet HIV [Internet]. 2015;2(10):e438&ndash;44. Available from: <u><span style=\"color:blue\"><a href=\"http://dx.doi.org/10.1016/S2352-3018(15)00137-X\"><span style=\"color:blue\">http://dx.doi.org/10.1016/S2352-3018(15)00137-X</span></a></span></u> </span></span></li>\r\n	<li><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Brandt R. The mental health of people living with HIV/AIDS in Africa: A systematic review. African J AIDS Res. 2009;8(2):123&ndash;33. </span></span></li>\r\n	<li><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">WHO. HIV / AIDS and Mental Health: Report by the Secretariat [Internet]. 2008. Available from: <u><span style=\"color:blue\"><a href=\"http://www.who.int/iris/handle/10665/2107\"><span style=\"color:blue\">http://www.who.int/iris/handle/10665/2107</span></a></span></u> </span></span></li>\r\n	<li><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Remien RH, Stirratt MJ, Nguyen N, Robbins RN, Pala AN, Mellins CA. Mental health and HIV/AIDS: The need for an integrated response. Aids. 2019;33(9):1411&ndash;20. </span></span></li>\r\n	<li><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Duko B, Toma A, Abraham Y. Prevalence and correlates of common mental disorder among HIV patients attending antiretroviral therapy clinics in Hawassa City, Ethiopia. Ann Gen Psychiatry. 2019;18(1):1&ndash;6. </span></span></li>\r\n	<li><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Tesfaw G, Ayano G, Awoke T, Assefa D, Birhanu Z, Miheretie G, et al. Prevalence and correlates of depression and anxiety among patients with HIV on-follow up at Alert Hospital, Addis Ababa, Ethiopia. BMC Psychiatry. 2016;16(1):1&ndash;7. </span></span></li>\r\n	<li><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">George S, McGrath N, Oni T. The association between a detectable HIV viral load and non-communicable diseases comorbidity in HIV positive adults on antiretroviral therapy in Western Cape, South Africa. BMC Infect Dis. 2019;19(1):1&ndash;11. </span></span></li>\r\n	<li><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Jacob N, Coetzee D. Mental illness in the Western Cape Province, South Africa: A review of the burden of disease and healthcare interventions. South African Med J. 2018;108(3):176&ndash;80. </span></span></li>\r\n	<li><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">World Health Organization. The WHO STEPwise approach to Surveillance of non-communicable diseases (STEPS): Non-communicable Diseases and Mental Health 20 Avenue Appia, 1211 Geneva 27, Switzerland; 2003</span></span></li>\r\n	<li><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Frese EM, Fick A, Sadowsky HS. Blood pressure measurement guidelines for physical therapists. Cardiopulm Phys Ther J. 2011;22(2):5&ndash;12. doi: 10.1097/01823246-201122020-00002&nbsp; </span></span></li>\r\n	<li><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Hata Y, Nakajima K. Application of Friedewald&#39;s LDL-cholesterol estimation formula to serum lipids in the Japanese population. Jpn Circ J. 1986; 50(12):1191&ndash;200. doi: 10.1253/jcj.50.1191.</span></span></li>\r\n	<li><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">WHO. International society of hypertension (ISH) statement on management of hypertension. Geneva, 2003.</span></span></li>\r\n</ol>','To determine the hidden burden of chronic non-communicable disease among HIV-infected people on ART in Jimma town, Ethiopia.',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,'Undiagnosed NCDs, HIV/AIDS, hidden burden, health facilities, Jimma Town',10,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'Proposal-62922701e30e8554921047.doc'),(113,10,13,24,'Summary \r\nThe chewing of khat releases chemicals structurally similar to amphetamines, which give the consumer a mild high that some say is equivalent to drinking concentrated coffee. Khat contains more than forty alkaloids, glycosides, tannins, amino acids, vitamins and minerals. Khat has active ingredient like cathinone which is chemically similar to damphetamine, cathine and cathinone. The chemical contents of khat directly affect digestive tract due to the presence of tannins in the khat. Toxicity has been assessed in animal laboratory, and khat extracts have been reported to contain mutagenic factors. The preconception of this study is the fact that khat consumption affects the dietary need of khat consumers; indirectly it can also affect the health of newborn infants among pregnant women. As the pregnant women are highly vulnerable to malnutrition; khat consumption can affect the nutritional status of newborn infants. However, there is limited evidence that khat chewing may have an impact on foetal growth and development and birth weight. Therefore, this study aimed to clarify the impact of Khat chewing on dietary intake, breast milk quality and birth outcome of new-born infants.  \r\nProspective Cohort study will be conducted in Jimma and East Wollega zones. Prospective cohort study design will be employed in selected hospitals of the two zones. A between-group comparison design will be used to measure the effects of khat chewing on the birth outcome and milk composition among selected pregnant women.  Pregnant women of 7 months or more gestational age will be enrolled at ANC clinic and followed until 6 weeks of postpartum. A total of 1,034 study participants will be included into the study. The data will be collected through interviewer administered structured questionnaire, taking breast milk sample and anthropometric measurements. All the participants will be interviewed for their socio demographic information, dietary intakes and health risky behaviours. Breast milk will be collected and analyzed at 6 weeks of postpartum. Anthropometric measurements of new-born, weight and length, will be taken at birth and 6 weeks of postpartum. Descriptive statistics will be used to examine the frequency distributions of pregnant women demographic and socio-economic characteristics and dietary intake practices. Logistic regression analysis will be done to identify independent risk factors of outcome variables using odd ratio risk and 95% of confidence intervals.',NULL,'Effects of Khat Consumption on Birth Outcomes and milk composition among Pregnant and lactating Women in Jimma and East Wollega Zone, Ethiopia','2022-05-27 10:38:03','Research output/outcome\r\nThis project will assess Effects of Khat Consumption on Birth Outcomes and milk composition among Pregnant and lactating Women in Jimma and East Wollega Zone, Ethiopia and will result in identifying the effects of khat chewing on birth outcome and milk quality.  The research result will also provide evidences for researchers to conduct further studies.','ddae0504e9ad8ca4837d43b5933262b2','completed','Khat defined as a fresh leaves and shoots of the plant Catha Edulis, a large shrub of Celastraceae family. Khat grows at altitudes of 1500-2500 meters. Khat consumption is a practice that dates back thousands of years in the East of African countries like Ethiopia and the Arabian Peninsula like Yemen where the khat plant is widely cultivated and known by a variety of names, including qaat in Somalia and chat in Ethiopia. Khat mainly grows in Ethiopia, Somalia, Kenya, Yemen, Saudi Arabia and in some areas of South Africa and Madagascar. Majority of khat user think that it increases energy, alertness, happiness, better thinking capacity and creativity(Hassan & Gunaid, 2002; Jansson et al., 1988; Sewasew & Mengesha, 2014)\r\nA few animal and epidemiologic studies reported a number of negative consequences on reproductive health and poor pregnancy outcomes. These results were found to extend in seriousness with the expanded recurrence and length of khat chewing. Neonates of moms who chewed khat amid pregnancy had a noteworthy diminish in all neonatal parameters such as birth weight, length, head circumference and Apgar score in comparison with those of moms who did not chew khat amid pregnancy. The possible mechanisms are vasoconstriction within the utero-placental vascular bed and insufficient maternal weight pick up since of the smothered craving caused by khat fixings. Khat chewing amid pregnancy is suspected to cause disappointment to flourish amid childhood as a long-term impact(Abderrahman & Modallal, 2008; Ageely, 2008; Jansson et al., 1988; N. T. Wabe, 2011)\r\nThe euphoric impacts are fast due to its retention through the oral mucosa. Khat chewing is accepted to upgrade social interaction and more often than not utilized by people to make strides their capacity of work basically by students, drivers, military work force, pastoralists and agriculturists(N. T. Wabe, 2011). Khat has different physiologic and hormonal related with diminished appetite. Various animals and lab studies have appeared that khat chewing diminish appetite and food intake. Additionally, epidemiological ponders have detailed the anorexic impact of khat chewing. Anorexia, the well-known side impact, likely contributes to ailing health detailed to win among chewer. Chewing does undoubtedly delay gastric purging of a semi-solid supper. Anorexia as often as possible takes after a khat session and chewers rarely eat an advance noteworthy supper on the same day(Abderrahman & Modallal, 2008; Ageely, 2008; Hassan & Gunaid, 2002; Jansson et al., 1988; N. T. Wabe, 2011).\r\nThe chewing of khat discharges chemicals basically comparative to amphetamines, which deliver the customer a gentle tall that a few say is proportionate to drinking concentrated coffee. Khat mainly contains alkaloids, glycosides, tannins, amino acids, vitamins and minerals(N. Wabe & Mohammed, 2012). Khat has dynamic fixing like cathinone which is chemically comparable to amphetamine, cathine and cathinone. Cathinone produces sympathomimetic and central anxious framework incitement closely resembling to the impact of amphetamine(Bookheimer et al., 1995). Cathinone actuates an increment within the action of the dopaminergic pathways within the central anxious framework. The impacts of khat chemicals can remain for the greatest of 3-4 hours after chewing and they were continuously supplanted by mellow dysphoria, sedation, uneasiness, discouragement, a sleeping disorder, anorexia and other psychiatric disorders(Hassan & Gunaid, 2002).\r\nThe chemical contents of khat directly affect digestive tract due to the presence of tannins substance in the khat and that may also an effect on the central and peripheral nervous system, and on the oro-gastro-intestinal system(Alshoabi et al., 2022; N. Wabe & Mohammed, 2012). Some of the most digestive tract consequences of khat chewing were gastritis and constipation(Alshoabi et al., 2022), had mutagenic effect of toxicity when assessed in animal laboratory(Abderrahman & Modallal, 2008). Studies has also showed that prolonged exposure to khat may leads to increased blood pressure, tachycardia, insomnia, fatigue, irritability, migraine headache, anorexia and impotency in men(Alshoabi et al., 2022; Ewenat et al., 2014; Nakajima et al., 2017).\r\nThe drugs and legal substances taken during pregnancy can escalate the danger of health-related problems of the mother and the infant. Socio-economically underprivileged women were prone to chew khat than their counterparts. Studies also showed that children born of substance-using mothers are mainly exposed to intrauterine growth, low birth weight, prematurity and experience respiratory depression. Other physiological effects include increased respiratory rate, hyperthermia, sweating, pupil dilation and decreased intraocular pressure(Al-aghbari et al., 2015; Nakajima et al., 2017; Yadassa Tesso et al., 2017). Khat chewing has an effect on the cardiovascular framework is or maybe less emotional, but expanded heart rate & blood pressure are common side-effects, making khat exceptionally destructive for hypertensive patients. Khat is additionally known chemical to be excreted in breast milk, but no research has been done so distant on how this influences nursing babies(Fetene et al., 2021; Hassen et al., 2012; N.A.G.M.Hassan, A.A. Gunaid, 2007).\r\n\r\nResearch done in Yemen revealed that those women chewing khat were having a high risk for preterm labor, induction of labor, malpresentation, low birth weight, and measurably inconsequential chance for preeclampsia, blood transfusion, fetal distress, PROM, PPH & perineal tears, IUFD, perinatal mortality and congenital malformation (Al-aghbari et al., 2015). Another study showed that because of cathine substance in khat chewing lactating mother frequently complain poor lactation(Luqman & Danowski, 1976), and cathine compound was detected in urine of breastfed infant(Kristiansson et al., 1987).\r\nSeveral studies done in Ethiopia showed that the overall khat chewing prevalence was 38.6% in Jimma (Alemseged et al., 2012), 48.6% in Nekemte (Wondemagegn et al., 2017), in pregnant women were 65.8% in Jimma (Yadassa Tesso et al., 2017), 19.6% in Harar and Dire Dawa (Fetene et al., 2021), 35.8% in Butajira (Alamneh et al., 2020) and 9.9% in Gedeo (Mekuriaw et al., 2020). Evidence indicates that neonates of mothers who chewed khat during pregnancy had a significant decrease in all neonatal parameters such as birth weight, length, head circumference and Apgar score at 1 and 5 minutes when compare with counterpart (Abd el-Aziz & Ahmed, 1998).\r\n\r\nKhat chewing is becoming a highly prevalent practice and a growing public health concern in Ethiopia. There is a significant increment of khat chewers over the years in Ethiopia which previously cultivated more in the eastern parts of Ethiopia. Currently, khat consumption is more common in the central and eastern parts of Ethiopia when compared to northern and western part of Ethiopia(Ewenat et al., 2014; Gebissa, 2010; Yitayih & van Os, 2021).  \r\nThe preconception of this study is the fact that khat consumption affects the dietary need of khat consumers; indirectly it can also affect the health of newborn infants among pregnant women. As the pregnant women are highly vulnerable to malnutrition, khat consumption can affect the nutritional status of newborn infants. Additionally, studies also showed that khat consumption affects hormonal production and the activities of enzyme(Al-aghbari et al., 2015). However, there is limited evidence that chewing khat during pregnancy may have an impact on foetal growth, development, birth weight and also impact on infant behavior because of contents in milk composition. Therefore, this study aimed to assess the impact of Khat chewing on breast milk quality and birth weight of new-born infants in Jimma and East Wollega Zones.','Jimma University\r\nInstitute of Health\r\nHealth Research and Postgraduate Director Office\r\n\r\nTheme: Nutrition, Malnutrition and Dietetics\r\n\r\n\r\nEffects of Khat Consumption on Birth Outcomes and milk composition among Pregnant and lactating Women in Jimma and East Wollega Zone, Ethiopia\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n1.	Desalegn Tamiru (PhD)\r\n2.	Yohannes Kebede (PhD)\r\n3.	Henok Gulilat (PhD)\r\n4.	Tadele Amente (PhD candidate)\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n \r\n\r\nJimma, Ethiopia \r\nMay, 2022\r\n1.	Background and Rationale\r\nKhat defined as a fresh leaves and shoots of the plant Catha Edulis, a large shrub of Celastraceae family. Khat grows at altitudes of 1500-2500 meters. Khat consumption is a practice that dates back thousands of years in the East of African countries like Ethiopia and the Arabian Peninsula like Yemen where the khat plant is widely cultivated and known by a variety of names, including qaat in Somalia and chat in Ethiopia. Khat mainly grows in Ethiopia, Somalia, Kenya, Yemen, Saudi Arabia and in some areas of South Africa and Madagascar. Majority of khat user think that it increases energy, alertness, happiness, better thinking capacity and creativity(Hassan & Gunaid, 2002; Jansson et al., 1988; Sewasew & Mengesha, 2014)\r\nA few animal and epidemiologic studies reported a number of negative consequences on reproductive health and poor pregnancy outcomes. These results were found to extend in seriousness with the expanded recurrence and length of khat chewing. Neonates of moms who chewed khat amid pregnancy had a noteworthy diminish in all neonatal parameters such as birth weight, length, head circumference and Apgar score in comparison with those of moms who did not chew khat amid pregnancy. The possible mechanisms are vasoconstriction within the utero-placental vascular bed and insufficient maternal weight pick up since of the smothered craving caused by khat fixings. Khat chewing amid pregnancy is suspected to cause disappointment to flourish amid childhood as a long-term impact(Abderrahman & Modallal, 2008; Ageely, 2008; Jansson et al., 1988; N. T. Wabe, 2011)\r\nThe euphoric impacts are fast due to its retention through the oral mucosa. Khat chewing is accepted to upgrade social interaction and more often than not utilized by people to make strides their capacity of work basically by students, drivers, military work force, pastoralists and agriculturists(N. T. Wabe, 2011). Khat has different physiologic and hormonal related with diminished appetite. Various animals and lab studies have appeared that khat chewing diminish appetite and food intake. Additionally, epidemiological ponders have detailed the anorexic impact of khat chewing. Anorexia, the well-known side impact, likely contributes to ailing health detailed to win among chewer. Chewing does undoubtedly delay gastric purging of a semi-solid supper. Anorexia as often as possible takes after a khat session and chewers rarely eat an advance noteworthy supper on the same day(Abderrahman & Modallal, 2008; Ageely, 2008; Hassan & Gunaid, 2002; Jansson et al., 1988; N. T. Wabe, 2011).\r\nThe chewing of khat discharges chemicals basically comparative to amphetamines, which deliver the customer a gentle tall that a few say is proportionate to drinking concentrated coffee. Khat mainly contains alkaloids, glycosides, tannins, amino acids, vitamins and minerals(N. Wabe & Mohammed, 2012). Khat has dynamic fixing like cathinone which is chemically comparable to amphetamine, cathine and cathinone. Cathinone produces sympathomimetic and central anxious framework incitement closely resembling to the impact of amphetamine(Bookheimer et al., 1995). Cathinone actuates an increment within the action of the dopaminergic pathways within the central anxious framework. The impacts of khat chemicals can remain for the greatest of 3-4 hours after chewing and they were continuously supplanted by mellow dysphoria, sedation, uneasiness, discouragement, a sleeping disorder, anorexia and other psychiatric disorders(Hassan & Gunaid, 2002).\r\nThe chemical contents of khat directly affect digestive tract due to the presence of tannins substance in the khat and that may also an effect on the central and peripheral nervous system, and on the oro-gastro-intestinal system(Alshoabi et al., 2022; N. Wabe & Mohammed, 2012). Some of the most digestive tract consequences of khat chewing were gastritis and constipation(Alshoabi et al., 2022), had mutagenic effect of toxicity when assessed in animal laboratory(Abderrahman & Modallal, 2008). Studies has also showed that prolonged exposure to khat may leads to increased blood pressure, tachycardia, insomnia, fatigue, irritability, migraine headache, anorexia and impotency in men(Alshoabi et al., 2022; Ewenat et al., 2014; Nakajima et al., 2017).\r\nThe drugs and legal substances taken during pregnancy can escalate the danger of health-related problems of the mother and the infant. Socio-economically underprivileged women were prone to chew khat than their counterparts. Studies also showed that children born of substance-using mothers are mainly exposed to intrauterine growth, low birth weight, prematurity and experience respiratory depression. Other physiological effects include increased respiratory rate, hyperthermia, sweating, pupil dilation and decreased intraocular pressure(Al-aghbari et al., 2015; Nakajima et al., 2017; Yadassa Tesso et al., 2017). Khat chewing has an effect on the cardiovascular framework is or maybe less emotional, but expanded heart rate & blood pressure are common side-effects, making khat exceptionally destructive for hypertensive patients. Khat is additionally known chemical to be excreted in breast milk, but no research has been done so distant on how this influences nursing babies(Fetene et al., 2021; Hassen et al., 2012; N.A.G.M.Hassan, A.A. Gunaid, 2007).\r\n\r\nResearch done in Yemen revealed that those women chewing khat were having a high risk for preterm labor, induction of labor, malpresentation, low birth weight, and measurably inconsequential chance for preeclampsia, blood transfusion, fetal distress, PROM, PPH & perineal tears, IUFD, perinatal mortality and congenital malformation (Al-aghbari et al., 2015). Another study showed that because of cathine substance in khat chewing lactating mother frequently complain poor lactation(Luqman & Danowski, 1976), and cathine compound was detected in urine of breastfed infant(Kristiansson et al., 1987).\r\nSeveral studies done in Ethiopia showed that the overall khat chewing prevalence was 38.6% in Jimma (Alemseged et al., 2012), 48.6% in Nekemte (Wondemagegn et al., 2017), in pregnant women were 65.8% in Jimma (Yadassa Tesso et al., 2017), 19.6% in Harar and Dire Dawa (Fetene et al., 2021), 35.8% in Butajira (Alamneh et al., 2020) and 9.9% in Gedeo (Mekuriaw et al., 2020). Evidence indicates that neonates of mothers who chewed khat during pregnancy had a significant decrease in all neonatal parameters such as birth weight, length, head circumference and Apgar score at 1 and 5 minutes when compare with counterpart (Abd el-Aziz & Ahmed, 1998).\r\n\r\nKhat chewing is becoming a highly prevalent practice and a growing public health concern in Ethiopia. There is a significant increment of khat chewers over the years in Ethiopia which previously cultivated more in the eastern parts of Ethiopia. Currently, khat consumption is more common in the central and eastern parts of Ethiopia when compared to northern and western part of Ethiopia(Ewenat et al., 2014; Gebissa, 2010; Yitayih & van Os, 2021).  \r\nThe preconception of this study is the fact that khat consumption affects the dietary need of khat consumers; indirectly it can also affect the health of newborn infants among pregnant women. As the pregnant women are highly vulnerable to malnutrition, khat consumption can affect the nutritional status of newborn infants. Additionally, studies also showed that khat consumption affects hormonal production and the activities of enzyme(Al-aghbari et al., 2015). However, there is limited evidence that chewing khat during pregnancy may have an impact on foetal growth, development, birth weight and also impact on infant behavior because of contents in milk composition. Therefore, this study aimed to assess the impact of Khat chewing on breast milk quality and birth weight of new-born infants in Jimma and East Wollega Zones.  \r\n2.	Hypothesis   \r\n-	Since khat consumption affects the dietary intakes of pregnant women, the birth weights of newborn infants are below the average value of normal children born of non-khat consumers.   \r\n-	Since khat consumption affects the dietary intakes of pregnant women, the milk composition from khat chewing mothers are below the average value compared to non-khat chewers.   \r\n3.	Research questions \r\n-	Is there association between khat consumption during pregnancy and birth weight of newborn infants in Jimma and East Wollega zones?\r\n-	Is there relationship between khat consumption and occurrence of eclampsia among pregnant women in Jimma and East Wollega Zones?\r\n-	Does khat chewing affects breast milk composition among lactating mothers in Jimma and East Wollega Zones?\r\n4.	Project Goals and Objectives\r\n4.1. Project Goals\r\no	The goal of this project is to generate evidences on the effects of khat chewing on birth outcome and breast milk composition and giving direction for both researchers and policy makers. \r\n4.2. Project Objectives \r\no	To determine the impacts of khat chewing on birth outcomes among the pregnant women in Jimma and East Wollega zones, Ethiopia, 2022/23\r\no	To identify socio economic and health effect of khat chewing among pregnant women in Jimma and East Wollega zones, 2022/23\r\no	To search the effects of khat chewing on breast milk composition among women who gave birth in Jimma and East Wollega zones, Ethiopia, 2022/23 \r\n5. Methods and Materials\r\n5.1. Description of Study Area and period\r\nA study will be conducted in Jimma and East Wollega zones. The two zones are located in Oromia regional states and far away from Addis Ababa in South west at 354 km (Jimma) and west of Addis Ababa at 328 km (East Wollega/Nekemte). The distance between the capital city of the two zones are 250 km via Bedele Town. The total population of the two zones for 2022 are estimated to be 3,803,824, whom 51.1% are females in Jimma and 1,799,134, whom 50.26 % are females in East Wollega. A rural population in the two zones accounts around 85% of the total populations(CSA (Central Statistics Agency), 2019). The two zones have shared geographical locations, and weather condition. Jimma is known in producing cash crops like khat and coffee. Khat production and consumption is currently common practice in Jimma zone(Nakajima et al., 2017; Yadassa Tesso et al., 2017). In east Wollega zone, agriculture is the dominant livelihood of the population. Most of their agricultural activities cash crop like maize, teff, sorghum and other pulses. So far, well known by food secure zone from Oromia region zones(Bureau, 2021). The study will be conducted from April 01, 2022 to June 30, 2023 in selected health institutions of the two zones. \r\n5.2. Study Design and Sampling\r\nProspective cohort study design will be employed in selected hospitals of the two zones. A between-group comparison design will be used to measure the effects of khat chewing on the birth outcome and milk composition among selected pregnant women. Two hospitals with similar levels from each zone will be selected by lottery methods. Shanan Gibe and Limmu hospitals from Jimma Zone, and Arjo and Gida Ayana hospitals from East Wollega zone will be included in the study. Multistage sampling technique will be employed to select hospitals and sampling frame. \r\n5.3. Source and Study population\r\nThe source population will be all khat consumers and non-consumers pregnant women living in Jimma and East Wollega zones. The study population will be all randomly selected pregnant and lactating women and those fulfil the inclusion criteria.  \r\n5.4. Inclusion criteria\r\n	Women who are consuming khat at least once in a day and who are constantly don’t consume khat.\r\n	 Otherwise, women those who rarely consume khat will not be included.  \r\n	Those pregnant women who have no known chronic non communicable disease that interfere the normal progress of gestational weight\r\n	Able to communicate and no plan to leave the area till the end of the study \r\n	Those pregnant women had at least two ANC visit at selected hospitals or 3rd trimester ?\r\n5.5. Sample size Determination\r\nThe sample will be calculated using Gpower 3.0 with the following assumptions: 50% an expected prevalence of low-birth-weight infants among those whose mothers consume khat, 0.50 expected odds ratio of low dietary intake among khat consumers(Al-aghbari et al., 2015), margin of error of 5% and power of 90%. This gives a total sample size 470 (235 for each group) and multiplied with a design effect of two. Then, 10%non-response rate will be added to give a final sample size of 1,034. Finally, equal numbers of pregnant women will be allotted into khat consumers and non-consumers groups in selected hospitals. \r\n5.6. Study variable \r\nDependent variable \r\n-	Birth weight outcome\r\n-	Milk composition \r\n-	Occurrence of eclampsia\r\nIndependent variable \r\n-	Socio-demographic variables: Age, Residence, Religion, Educational status, Economic Status, Marital Status, Number of Birth, ANC follow up\r\n-	Khat chewing: Frequency, amount, duration, type\r\n-	Dietary intake pattern: Type, amount, frequency \r\n-	Health condition of mothers \r\n5.7. Data Collection &Analysis\r\nThe data will be collected through interviewer administered structured questionnaire, taking blood sample and anthropometric measurements. All the participants will be interviewed for their socio demographic information, dietary intakes and health risky behaviours. Anthropometric measurements will be taken at the end of the interview. \r\nData will be collected two times during registration time and when the women come for delivery. Data on socio-demographic and economic variables will be gathered using structured questionnaire by using local language (Afan Oromo and Amharic). Socio-economic status will be assessed using the items used to assess wealth status in the Ethiopian Demographic and Health Survey(Ethiopian Public Health Institute (EPHI) & ICF, 2021). \r\nA Qualitative food frequency questionnaire modified from a WHO-STEP wise approach will be used to gather information on the frequency of consumption of different food groups from each participant. This technique will be considered to obtain qualitative descriptive data on typical consumption of food and groups of food over extended periods of time. Finally, data will be used to categorize participants for detailed characteristics of food and dietary diversity. Individual dietary intake questionnaires will be developed based on FAO criteria. Individual dietary intake will be assessed using a food frequency questionnaire containing 30 food items that are commonly consumed in the study area. Participants will be asked the amount of food they consumed, the frequency of consumption of each food group per day, per week or per months. Pregnant women will be coded as a consumer of a food item if they have consumed the food item at least once per week from the listed food which is locally available. Consumption of a food item over the reference period will be given a score of “1” while non-consumption will be given a score “0”. Then, food items will be categorized into starch staples/ dark green leafy vegetable/ other vitamin A rich fruits and vegetables/ other fruits and vegetables/ organ meat/ meat and fish/ eggs/legumes, nuts and seeds/ milk and milk products (Kedir et al., 2013; Kennedy et al., 2010; WHO, 2003). \r\nBlood sample will be taken to measure the blood status of chemicals in khat. Blood sample will be taken before and after consumption of khat. A blood sample will be taken two times from first day registration and during delivery time at ANC clinic of the selected hospitals. Appetite test will be done by asking the participants to rate their hunger, desire to eat, fullness, future consumption and thirst according to visual analogue scales.\r\nNormally breast milk composition is dynamic, and varies within a feeding, diurnal, duration of lactation, and different between lactating mothers. Human milk compositions are macronutrients (i.e. protein, fat, lactose and energy), vitamins and minerals in different amount for preterm and term mothers(Olivia Ballard & Morrow, 2013). Urine sample from infant and milk expression will be collected from mother at multiple occasion from the same client.  Breast milk composition will be measured using MIRIS  Human Milk Analyzer (HMA)(McLeod et al., 2016; Olga et al., 2021).\r\nNutritional status of the infants will be generated using WHO Anthro plus program, version 1.4.0. Height, weight of each of the infant will be measured using standardized and calibrated equipment. Weight will be measured with light clothing and no shoes and recorded to nearest 0.1 kg using UNICEF seca digital weighing scale (Germany). Height will be measured using a portable Stadiometer (Seca, Germany) and recorded to the nearest 0.1cm. During height measurement shoes, bulky clothing, pins and braids from the hair that could affect the measurement will be removed. Height will be measured with the head of participants at the Frankfurt plane, knees straight and the heels buttocks and the shoulders blades touching the vertical surface of the stadiometer. \r\nThe data will be checked for appropriate coding, missing values, outliers and cleaned for consistency and completeness. The data entry will be done using EpiData version 3.1 software to minimize error and exported to SPSS software version 25 for further analysis. WHO Anthro-plus version 1.0.4.0 software will be used to analyse nutritional status on babies.  A one-sample Kolmogorov-Smirnov test will be used to assess normality of the data. Principal components analysis will be used to generate composite score for wealth, knowledge, and attitude variable based on individual questionnaire items. \r\nDescriptive statistics will be used to examine the frequency distributions of pregnant women demographic and socio-economic characteristics and dietary intake practices. The general chi square test for independence and fisher exact test will be used to investigate the association between the nutritional status of infants and nominal explanatory variables. Chi square test for trend will be used to measure association between nutritional status and ordinal scale independent variables. Multicollinearity will be checked based on the observation of the value of variance inflation factor (VIF<10) and standard error (SE<2), then managed based on number of variables scored greater in VIF and SE. The model creation for model fit test will be stepwise regression and the goodness of fit will be tested through Hosmer and lemeshow test. After conducting bivariate analysis, those variables with p-values ≤ 0.25 will be included into multiple logistic regression analysis. In order to identify independent risk factors for khat chewing and birth outcomes, a multivariate logistic regression analysis will be carried out using odds ratios and 95% of confidence intervals. The Backward stepwise likelihood ratio method of the multiple logistic regressions will be used to isolate an independent effect of the predictor of birth outcomes.\r\n5.8. Data Quality Assurance\r\nTo avoid the possibility of measurement bias, data collectors will be blinded of the study objective and hypothesis. Data quality will be ensured during tool development, data collection, cleaning, entry and analysis. All collected data will be checked every day for their completeness, clarity and consistency by supervisor and principal investigators. The questionnaire will be prepared in English then translated to Amharic and Afan-oromo language and retranslated to English before data collection. Any unclear and ambiguous data will be corrected by recollecting the data from actual study population by going back to the place while minor error will be corrected by the principal investigators as deemed. Three days training will be given for the data collectors and the tool will be tested on 5% of sample size in Agaro hospital to prevent data contamination prior to the study. \r\nThe anthropometric measurements (height, weight) will be taken using standardized and calibrated equipment in duplicate for each participant. Weighing scales will be checked against zero reading after weighing every child and will be calibrated with known weight object regularly. \r\n6. Capacity building (inclusion of PhD and/or MSc students)\r\nOne PhD students will participate in this project for his final dissertation  \r\n7. Ethical considerations\r\nAfter approval of the proposal, Ethical clearance and formal letter will be obtained from research ethics committee of Jimma University. The necessary permission will be also obtained from respective zone Health Office. Prior to the first interview both pregnant women and their families will be informed about objective of the study and privacy during the interview. Informed verbal consent will be also obtained from both participants and their families.','<p style=\"margin-left:32px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">Abderrahman, S., &amp; Modallal, N. (2008). Genotoxic effects of Catha edulis (Khat) extract on mice bone marrow cells. <em>Jordan Journal of Biological Sciences</em>, <em>1</em>(4), 165&ndash;172.</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:32px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">Ageely, H. M. A. (2008). Review Article HEALTH AND SOCIO-ECONOMIC HAZARDS ASSOCIATED WITH. <em>J Family Community Med</em>, <em>15</em>(1), 3&ndash;11.</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:32px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">Al-aghbari, A., Mustafa, M., Nasr, A. A., &amp; Assad, A. (2015). Khat Chewing During Pregnancy: an Insight on an Ancient Problem. Impact of Chewing Khat on Maternal and Fetal Outcome among Yemeni Pregnant Women. <em>Journal of Gynecology and Neonatal Biology</em>, <em>1</em>(2), 28&ndash;31.</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:32px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">Alshoabi, S. A., Hamid, A. M., Gameraddin, M. B., Suliman, A. G., Omer, A. M., Alsultan, K. D., Binmodied, A. A., &amp; Alhamadi, M. A. (2022). Risks of khat chewing on the cardiovascular, nervous, gastrointestinal, and genitourinary systems: A narrative review. <em>Journal of Family Medicine and Primary Care</em>, <em>6</em>(2), 169&ndash;170. https://doi.org/10.4103/jfmpc.jfmpc</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:32px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">Bookheimer, S. Y., Zeffiro, T. A., Blaxton, T., Gaillard, W., &amp; Theodore, W. (1995). Khat chewing delays gastric emptying of a semi-solid meal. <em>Altment Pharmacol Ther</em>, <em>9</em>, 81&ndash;83.</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:32px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">Bureau, E. W. zone H. (2021). <em>General Working plan of Health Bureau</em>.</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:32px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">CSA (Central Statistics Agency). (2019). <em>Projected Population of Ethiopia-2011(2019)</em>. <em>July</em>.</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:32px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">Ethiopian Public Health Institute (EPHI), &amp; ICF. (2021). Ethiopia Mini Demographic and Health Survey 2019: Final Report. In <em>FEDERAL DEMOCRATIC REPUBLIC OF ETHIOPIA</em> (Issue July). https://dhsprogram.com/pubs/pdf/FR363/FR363.pdf</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:32px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">Ewenat, G., Yemane, B., &amp; Alemayehu, W. (2014). Khat chewing among Ethiopian University students - a growing concern. <em>BMC Public Health</em>, <em>14</em>(1198), (21 November 2014)-(21 November 2014).</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:32px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">Fetene, M. T., Teji, K., Assefa, N., Bayih, W. A., Tsehaye, G., &amp; Hailemeskel, H. S. (2021). Magnitude and associated factors of substance use among pregnant women attending antenatal care in public hospitals of eastern Ethiopia. <em>BMC Psychiatry</em>, <em>21</em>(1), 1&ndash;12. https://doi.org/10.1186/s12888-021-03078-5</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:32px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">Gebissa, E. (2010). Khat in the Horn of Africa: Historical perspectives and current trends. <em>Journal of Ethnopharmacology</em>, <em>132</em>(3), 607&ndash;614. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jep.2010.01.063</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:32px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">Hassan, N. A., &amp; Gunaid, A. A. (2002). The effect of chewing Khat leaves on human mood. <em>Saudi Medical Journal</em>, <em>23</em>(7), 850&ndash;853.</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:32px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">Hassen, K., Abdulahi, M., Kerie, M. W., Sudhakar, M., &amp; Dejene, T. (2012). Khat as risk factor for hypertension: A systematic review. <em>JBI Library of Systematic Reviews</em>, <em>10</em>(28), 1&ndash;12. https://doi.org/10.11124/jbisrir-2012-303</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:32px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">Jansson, T., Kristiansson, B., &amp; Qirbi, A. (1988). EFFECT OF KHAT ON MATERNAL FOOD INTAKE, MATERNAL WEIGHT GAIN AND FETAL GROWTH IN THE LATE-PREGNANT GUINEA PIG. <em>Ethnopharmacology</em>, <em>23</em>, 11&ndash;17.</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:32px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">Kristiansson, B., Ghani, N. A., Eriksson, M., Garle, M., &amp; Qirbi, A. (1987). Use of khat in lactating women: A pilot study on breast-milk secretion. <em>Journal of Ethnopharmacology</em>, <em>21</em>(1), 85&ndash;90. https://doi.org/10.1016/0378-8741(87)90097-3</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:32px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">Luqman, W., &amp; Danowski, T. S. (1976). The use of khat (Catha edulis) in Yemen. Social and medical observations. <em>Annals of Internal Medicine</em>, <em>85</em>(2), 246&ndash;249. https://doi.org/10.7326/0003-4819-85-2-246</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:32px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">McLeod, G., Sherriff, J., Hartmann, P. E., Nathan, E., Geddes, D., &amp; Simmer, K. (2016). Comparing different methods of human breast milk fortification using measured v. assumed macronutrient composition to target reference growth a randomised controlled trial. <em>British Journal of Nutrition</em>, <em>115</em>(3), 431&ndash;439. https://doi.org/10.1017/S0007114515004614</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:32px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">N.A.G.M.Hassan, A.A. Gunaid, I. M. M.-L. (2007). Khat(Chata edulis): Health Aspect of Khat Chewing. <em>Eastern Meditrranean Health Journal</em>, <em>13</em>, 706&ndash;717.</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:32px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">Nakajima, M., Jebena, M. G., Taha, M., Tesfaye, M., Gudina, E., Lemieux, A., Hoffman, R., &amp; al&rsquo;Absi, M. (2017). Correlates of khat use during pregnancy: A cross-sectional study. <em>Addictive Behaviors</em>, <em>73</em>(January), 178&ndash;184. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.addbeh.2017.05.008</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:32px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">Olga, L., Petry, C. J., van Diepen, J. A., Prentice, P. M., Hughes, I. A., Vervoort, J., Boekhorst, J., Chichlowski, M., Gross, G., Dunger, D. B., &amp; Ong, K. K. (2021). Extensive study of breast milk and infant growth: Protocol of the Cambridge baby growth and breastfeeding study (CBGS-BF). <em>Nutrients</em>, <em>13</em>(8). https://doi.org/10.3390/nu13082879</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:32px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">Olivia Ballard, J., &amp; Morrow, A. L. (2013). Human Milk Composition: Nutrients and Bioactive Factors. <em>Pediatr Clinical North America</em>, <em>78</em>(2), 711&ndash;716. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.pcl.2012.10.002.Human</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:32px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">Sewasew, D. T., &amp; Mengesha, T. Z. (2014). KHAT USE PREVALENCE , CAUSES AND ITS EFFECT ON MENTAL HEALTH , BAHIR-DAR , NORTH WEST ETHIOPIA. <em>European Scientific Journal</em>, <em>10</em>(23), 234&ndash;253.</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:32px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">Wabe, N., &amp; Mohammed, M. (2012). What science says about khat (Catha edulis Forsk)? Overview of chemistry, toxicology and pharmacology. <em>Journal of Experimental and Integrative Medicine</em>, <em>2</em>(1), 29. https://doi.org/10.5455/jeim.221211.rw.005</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:32px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">Wabe, N. T. (2011). Chemistry, pharmacology, and toxicology of khat (catha edulis forsk): a review. <em>Addiction &amp; Health</em>, <em>3</em>(3&ndash;4), 137&ndash;149. http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/24494129%0Ahttp://www.pubmedcentral.nih.gov/articlerender.fcgi?artid=PMC3905534</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:32px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">Yadassa Tesso, F., Woldesemayat, L. A., &amp; Birhanu Kebede, D. (2017). Magnitude of Substance Use and Associated Factors among Pregnant Women Attending Jimma Town Public Health Facilities, Jimma Zone, Oromia Regional State Southwest Ethiopia. <em>Clinics in Mother and Child Health</em>, <em>14</em>(4), 1&ndash;5. https://doi.org/10.4172/2090-7214.1000275</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:32px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">Yitayih, Y., &amp; van Os, J. (2021). Prevalence and determinants of chewing khat among women in Ethiopia: data from Ethiopian demographic and health survey 2016. <em>BMC Psychiatry</em>, <em>21</em>(1), 1&ndash;8. https://doi.org/10.1186/s12888-021-03136-y</span></span></span></span></p>','o	The goal of this project is to generate evidences on the effects of khat chewing on birth outcome and breast milk composition and giving direction for both researchers and policy makers.',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,'Khat Chewing, Birth Outcome, Milk Quality, Dietary Intake',10,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'Proposal-62907e0dd2c9f990902739.doc'),(114,131,13,18,'Proposal Summary\r\nBackground: The total number of people with diabetes in the world is projected to rise from 366 million in 2011 to an estimated 552 million by 2030. Every ten seconds a person dies from diabetes related causes. In Ethiopia, the number of people aged 20-79 years living with diabetes was estimated to be 1.3 million adults with diabetes, and the prevalence was 2.9%. This figure is projected to reach 1.8 million by 2030. According to the World Health Organization (WHO), about 65-80% of the world’s population in developing countries depends essentially on plants for their primary healthcare due to poverty and lack of access to modern medicine. Different researches reveal that, the use of medicinal plants for treatment of diabetes mellitus in Ethiopia is a common practice. However the safety and efficacy as well as isolation and characterization of the active compounds of these plants need thorough investigation by scientific communities. Therefore the current research project will focus on evaluating of the safety and in vivo antidiabetic efficacy of selected medicinal plants traditionally used for treatment of Diabetes mellitus. Furthermore, isolation and structural elucidation of the active compounds with promising antidiabetic activity will be carried out. \r\nMethods: This project employed literature reviewing of medicinal plants traditionally used in Ethiopia for treatment of diabetes mellitus. Then, the top five prevalent medicinal plants will be investigated for safety and in vivo anti diabetic activity. Finally further isolation of the active compounds and their structural elucidation will be carried out for those showing promising in- vivo anti diabetic activity. \r\nWork and budget plan: The entire project will be conducted from September 2022 to June 2023 and the total budget of the project is 676,105 ETB.',NULL,'Antidiabetic Activity Evaluation and Phytochemical characterization of Selected Medicinal Plants Traditionally Used for Treatment of Diabetes Mellitus','2022-05-27 10:45:07','Significance of the Study\r\nThe current research which is focusing on medicinal plants benefits the Ethiopian population who rely on herbal medicine for treatment of diabetes by three folds besides contributing to the existing body of knowledge by generating current information. First if the plants proved to be safe and effective it is easily accessible and affordable to the communities and hence they can be benefited by using them from their localities as phytomedicines. Secondly, if the plants are not safe or effective or both it will help to draw the attention of the public and the authorities to the ineffective use of medicinal plants which in such a serious case like the management of diabetes may adversely affect the health of the consumer and complicate the user’s health condition without any clear benefit.\r\n Thirdly, the study involves the search for bioactive phytochemicals. The success of such study will contribute for discovery of lead compounds which will be further developed to new anti-diabetic drugs through synthetic chemistry. On the other hand, the effectiveness of in vivo antidiabetic activity of the investigational crude plants may also be used as a base for the development of medicinal plants in to phytomedicines to be used clinically after thorough clinical investigation about its safety and effectiveness. In either of these cases, the success of the research project will contribute for the health and economic development of the country.','190bced654bdc86fa9ec9e8d0f90e685','completed','1. Introduction\r\nDiabetes mellitus (DM) is a group of metabolic disorders characterized by hyperglycemia, hypertriglyceridemia and hypercholesterolemia resulting from defects in insulin secretion or action or both [1]. It is initially characterized by a loss of glucose homeostasis with disturbances of carbohydrate, fat and protein metabolism [2]. Diabetes is a known major risk factor for cardiovascular disease, metabolic syndrome, dyslipidemia, and end-stage renal disease [3]. Optimal glycemic control is a primary requirement for improvement of the quality of life of diabetic patients as uncontrolled glucose level leads to different diabetic complications which is classified as micro and macro vascular complications [4]. \r\nDiabetes is one of the major causes of premature death worldwide. Every ten second a person dies from diabetes related causes mainly from cardiovascular complications [5]. A few years ago, the concern of the “diabetes epidemic” was restricted to the developed countries, while the other parts of the world were not considered as threatened. Unfortunately, the picture has moved and nowadays no country escapes the diabetes invasion [6]. The total number of people with diabetes in the world is projected to rise from 366 million in 2011 to an estimated 552 million by 2030 [7]. This is mainly due to a tremendous increase of 171% in developing countries, while it will increase only of 41% in developed countries [6]. This clearly shows that diabetes is becoming the major public health problem of developing countries.\r\n In Ethiopia, the number of people aged 20-79 years living with diabetes was estimated to be 1.3 million adults with diabetes, and the prevalence was 2.9%. This figure is projected to reach 1.8 million by 2030 [8].  A community based study available from one of the urban center in the southwestern part of Ethiopia has indicated the prevalence of T2DM to be 5.3% in those older than 40 years of age. The prevalence of participants with impaired glucose regulation, i.e. impaired fasting glucose and impaired glucose tolerance, was found to be 14.8% which was further indicative of the impending problem [9].  \r\nMedicinal plants have been used in traditional health care systems since prehistoric times and are still the most important health care source for the vast majority of the population around the world [10].  According to the World Health Organization (WHO), about 65-80% of the world’s population in developing countries depends essentially on plants for their primary healthcare due to poverty and lack of access to modern medicine [11]. \r\nEthiopia is a country characterized by a wide range of climatic and ecological conditions possessing enormous diversity of flora and fauna, including wide range of potentially useful medicinal plants. Traditional medical practices are common in Ethiopia in which about 80% of the population in the country use plant-based traditional medicine as their major primary healthcare system [12]. Generally, the majority of Ethiopians rely on indigenous remedies for a numerous socio-cultural and economic reasons.\r\n\r\nStatement of the Problem\r\nPatients with diabetes require optimal glycemic control and prevention of diabetes complications to improve their quality of life. Pharmacological therapy, as monotherapy or combination therapy is often necessary to achieve glycemic control in the management of diabetes. However, it has been reported that neither sulfonylureas nor biguanide can significantly alter the rate of progression of hyperglycemia in type 2 diabetic patients [13]. This means that currently available drugs for the treatment of diabetes are not effective enough to control blood glucose level and thereby diabetic complications. Furthermore, the currently used antidiabetic drugs have several side effects. For example, sulfonylureas are known for hypoglycemia and weight gain as the main side effects [14]. Weight gain, edema, anemia, pulmonary edema, and congestive heart failure are among the risk of the PPARγ analogue, thiazolidinedione’s [15]. Thus, the management of diabetes without any side effects is still a challenge. Therefore, development of new antihyperglycemic agents with safety and effectiveness is an urgent need [16].\r\n Studies have been demonstrated that oxidative stress, mediated mainly by hyperglycemia-induced generation of free radicals, contributes to the development and progression of diabetes and its complications [17, 18]. On the other hand, although insulin resistance is crucial to the pathogenesis of the disease, the associated atherogenic lipoprotein phenotype considerably enhances the risk [19]. Additionally, lipid accumulation in the liver has been shown to play a key role in insulin resistance and alleviation of this condition was suggested to be one mechanism to improve insulin sensitivity [20]. Hence, searching for medicinal plants having antidiabetic, lipid modifying ability and antioxidant activity is very useful as it is to mean shooting two birds with a single bullet.','5. Methods and Materials \r\n5.1 Chemicals and Reagents\r\nThe following solvents (ethanol, petroleum ether, ethyl acetate, n-butanol, methanol, chloroform) and chemicals (streptozotocin, citric acid, sodium citrate, anhydrous sodium sulphate, silica, tween 80) all of analytical grade will be purchased from the local vendors. The standard antidiabetic drugs namely, glibenclamide and metformin, will be purchased from local pharmacies. Insulin ELISA kit, triglycerides determination kit, total cholesterol levels determination kit, glucometer with glucose oxidase kit will also be purchased from local vendors.  \r\n5.2 Plant Material\r\nThe plant materials will be collected from areas where the plants traditionally used for the treatment of diabetes. Taxonomical identification of the plants specimens will be confirmed and voucher specimens will be deposited at Jimma University Herbarium.\r\n5.3 Experimental Animals\r\nSprague Dewelly rats of male sex weighing 200–250g will be used for the study. The animals will be maintained at room temperature and12-h light–dark cycles. The animals will be randomized into experimental and control groups and housed in group of three in sanitized polypropylene cages containing sterile paddy husk as bedding. They will have free access to standard pellets as basal diet and water ad libitum. Animals will be habituated to laboratory conditions for 72 h prior to experimental protocol to minimize if any of non-specific stress. \r\n5.4	Method of Extraction and Purifications\r\n5.4.1 Crude Extract\r\nThe plant materials will be shade dried and powdered. The powder will be extracted three times by Ultrasonic-assisted extraction with 95% ethanol at room temperature for 1hour. The extracts will be filtered using Whatman filter paper. The filtrates will be combined and concentrated under reduced pressure at 40 oC using rotary evaporator and stored in a refrigerator for use in subsequent experiments. The dried plant material supposed to have essential oil will be subjected to steam distillation using Clevenger-type apparatus[21]  to obtain the essential oil. The essential oils will be dried over anhydrous Na2SO4 and stored at 4 oC in the dark.\r\n\r\n5.4.2 Fractionation \r\nThe fractionation of ethanolic extract will be done according to the method described by Chakrabarti et al. (2002) [22]. Briefly, to the concentrated ethanolic extract a volume of water (500 ml) will be added and fractionated with petroleum ether (4x600 ml), chloroform (4x/600 ml), ethyl acetate (4x/600 ml) and n-butyl alcohol (4x/600 ml). The percentage yields of petroleum ether, chloroform, ethyl acetate and n-butanol extracts will be calculated. The anti-diabetic activity of each fraction will be evaluated. \r\n5.4.3 Chromatographic Isolation of Active Fractions\r\nThe solvent fractions with greater activity will be subjected to fractionation and purification using a range of chromatographic techniques such as Column Chromatography (CC) over silica gel, Preparative Thin Layer Chromatography (prep-TLC) and gel filtration over sephadex, to obtain pure compounds. \r\n5.5 Structural Determination \r\nThe structure of pure compounds will be established using a combination of spectroscopic methods such as Infra-Red (IR), Ultra Violet (UV), 1H and 13C Nuclear Magnetic Resonance (NMR), and Mass Spectrometry (MS). The isolated compounds will also be screened for their in vivo activity. The compounds with moderate activity will be then chemically modified so as to enhance their activities. Furthermore, standardization and formulation including their toxicity will be performed following appropriate regulatory pathways for plant-based medicines.\r\n5.6 Acute Toxicity Study\r\nThe acute toxicity study will be carried out according to OECD guidelines–425. Healthy adult Wistar albino rats of either sex, starved overnight will be divided into two groups, each consisting of five rats and one group will be orally fed with 2000mg/kg of the extracts while the other fed with vehicle. The animals will be observed continuously for 2 h under the following profiles [23].\r\n	Behavioral profile: Alertness, restlessness, irritability, and fearfulness.\r\n	Neurological profile: Spontaneous activities, reactivity, touch response, pain response and gait.\r\n	Autonomic profile: Defecation and urination.\r\n	After a period of 24 hours, 72 hours and 14 days they will be observed for any lethality or death.\r\n5.7 Induction of Experimental Diabetes\r\nDiabetes will be induced by intraperitoneal administration of streptozotocin dissolved in 0.1M citrate buffer, pH 4.5, in a dose of 65 mg/kg [24].  Three days after the injection, the blood glucose levels will be measured and the animals with blood glucose levels higher than 300 mg/dl will be considered as diabetic. To overcome the hypoglycemia which occurred during the first 24 h following the STZ administration, diabetic rats will be orally given 5% glucose solution [25]. \r\n5.8 Determination of Hypoglycaemic Effect on Acute Administration\r\nDiabetic animals will be fasted for 12 h (water ad libitum). Test samples will be given orally using oral gastric gavages. The blood glucose concentrations will be measured in all animals at the beginning of the study and the measurements will be repeated 1/2, 1, 2, 4 h and 6 h after the initial of the experiment  [26]. \r\n5.9 Prophylactic Activity against STZ Challenge\r\nFor determination of prophylactic activity, normal animals will be treated with test extracts at a dose of 500 mg/kg (p.o.) for 30 days. After monitoring blood glucose levels on day 30, animals will be challenged to streptozotocin at a dose of 75 mg/kg (i.p.). Blood glucose levels after 48 h will be monitored for induction of hyperglycemia along with untreated control animals (fed with distilled water) challenged with STZ [27].\r\n5.10 Evaluation of Chronic Effects of Extracts in Streptozotocin-induced Diabetic Rats\r\n The experimentally diabetic induced animals will be randomly assigned into six groups of six animals each and will receive the following treatments: \r\n	Group I: Normal control + distilled water\r\n	Group II: Diabetic + distilled water\r\n	Group III: Diabetic + different doses of plant extracts\r\n	Group IV: Diabetic + Standard anti diabetic drug\r\n The freshly prepared solutions of the extracts will be orally administered daily for 30days. Body weights and blood glucose level analysis will be done weekly on overnight fasted animals [28, 29].  After 30 days of treatment, rats will be anesthetized by ether, their blood will be collected from heart in ordinary tubes for serum total cholesterol, triglycerides and high-density lipoprotein analysis [30].\r\n5.10	Biochemical Measurement \r\n5.10.1Determination of Blood Glucose Levels\r\n Blood glucose concentration (mg/dl) will be determined using a Glucometer-elite commercial test, based on the Glucose oxidase method. Blood samples will be collected from the tip of tail at the defined time patterns [31].\r\n5.10.2 Determination of Insulin Concentration\r\nSerum insulin levels will be measured by the microplate ELISA method using a commercial kit [32].\r\n5.10.3 Determination of Plasma Lipid Levels\r\nPlasma triglycerides and total cholesterol levels will be measured using colorimetric method with commercially available kits. Absorbance will be measured at 500 nm [33] Blood will be collected from heart in ordinary tubes and will be allowed to clot, and then the clotted blood will be centrifuged at 2500rpm for 5min. Serum total cholesterol, triglycerides and high-density lipoprotein will be estimated using commercial kits. Low-density lipoprotein and very low-density lipoprotein will be calculated using the following formula [30].\r\n5.10.4 Insulin Resistance Calculation\r\nFasting plasma insulin–glucose ratio (FIGR) and insulin resistance indices (FIRI) will be calculated according to the formulae earlier used by Harati et al. and Shalam et al. (2006) [34]:\r\n	FIGR = fasting insulin/fasting glucose\r\n	FIRI = (fasting insulin X fasting glucose)/25.','<h1><span style=\"font-size:14pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri Light&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"color:#2e74b5\"><a name=\"_Toc103515631\">9. References</a></span></span></span></h1>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">&nbsp;<a name=\"_ENREF_1\">1.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Muhit, M.A., et al., <em>Cardiovascular disease prevalence and prescription patterns at a tertiary level hospital in Bangladesh.</em> Journal of Applied Pharmaceutical Science, 2012. <strong>2</strong>(3): p. 8.</a></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><a name=\"_ENREF_2\">2.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Tripathi, B.K. and A.K. Srivastava, <em>Diabetes mellitus: complications and therapeutics.</em> Medical science monitor, 2006. <strong>12</strong>(7): p. RA130-RA147.</a></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><a name=\"_ENREF_3\">3.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Nishizawa, Y., et al. <em>Roles of metabolic and endocrinological alterations in atherosclerosis and cardiovascular disease in renal failure: another form of metabolic syndrome</em>. in <em>Seminars in nephrology</em>. 2004. Elsevier.</a></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><a name=\"_ENREF_4\">4.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Fowler, M.J., <em>Microvascular and macrovascular complications of diabetes.</em> Clinical diabetes, 2011. <strong>29</strong>(3): p. 116-122.</a></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><a name=\"_ENREF_5\">5.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Susan van, D., et al., <em>The global burden of diabetes and its complications: an emerging pandemic.</em> European Journal of Cardiovascular Prevention &amp; Rehabilitation, 2010. <strong>17</strong>(1_suppl): p. s3-s8.</a></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><a name=\"_ENREF_6\">6.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Virally, M., et al., <em>Type 2 diabetes mellitus: epidemiology, pathophysiology, unmet needs and therapeutical perspectives.</em> Diabetes &amp; metabolism, 2007. <strong>33</strong>(4): p. 231-244.</a></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><a name=\"_ENREF_7\">7.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Whiting, D.R., et al., <em>IDF diabetes atlas: global estimates of the prevalence of diabetes for 2011 and 2030.</em> Diabetes research and clinical practice, 2011. <strong>94</strong>(3): p. 311-321.</a></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><a name=\"_ENREF_8\">8.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Sicree, R., et al., <em>The global burden.</em> Diabetes and impaired glucose tolerance Baker IDI Heart and Diabetes Institute, 2010.</a></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><a name=\"_ENREF_9\">9.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Zekewos, A., et al., <em>Prevalence of diabetes mellitus and associated factors in Southern Ethiopia: a community based study.</em> Ethiopian journal of health sciences, 2018. <strong>28</strong>(4).</a></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><a name=\"_ENREF_10\">10.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Uprety, Y., et al., <em>Traditional use of medicinal plants in the boreal forest of Canada: review and perspectives.</em> Journal of ethnobiology and ethnomedicine, 2012. <strong>8</strong>(1): p. 1-14.</a></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><a name=\"_ENREF_11\">11.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Xavier, T.F., M. Kannan, and A. Auxilia, <em>Traditional Medicinal Plants Used in the treatment of different skin diseases.</em> International Journal of Current Microbiology and Applied Sciences, 2015. <strong>4</strong>(5): p. 1043-1053.</a></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><a name=\"_ENREF_12\">12.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Seid, M.A. and B.A. Tsegay, <em>Ethnobotanical survey of traditional medicinal plants in Tehuledere district, South Wollo, Ethiopia.</em> Journal of Medicinal Plants Research, 2011. <strong>5</strong>(26): p. 6233-6242.</a></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><a name=\"_ENREF_13\">13.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Krentz, A.J. and C.J. Bailey, <em>Oral antidiabetic agents.</em> Drugs, 2005. <strong>65</strong>(3): p. 385-411.</a></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><a name=\"_ENREF_14\">14.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Cheng, A.Y. and I.G. Fantus, <em>Oral antihyperglycemic therapy for type 2 diabetes mellitus.</em> Cmaj, 2005. <strong>172</strong>(2): p. 213-226.</a></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><a name=\"_ENREF_15\">15.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Cheng, A.Y. and I.G. Fantus, <em>Effect of thiazolidinediones on lipid profile.</em> Cmaj, 2005. <strong>173</strong>(4): p. 344-345.</a></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><a name=\"_ENREF_16\">16.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Ray, A., <em>Beyond debacle and debate: developing solutions in drug safety.</em> Nature Reviews Drug Discovery, 2009. <strong>8</strong>(10): p. 775-779.</a></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><a name=\"_ENREF_17\">17.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Rahimi, R., et al., <em>A review on the role of antioxidants in the management of diabetes and its complications.</em> Biomedicine &amp; Pharmacotherapy, 2005. <strong>59</strong>(7): p. 365-373.</a></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><a name=\"_ENREF_18\">18.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Khan, A.N., et al., <em>Role of antioxidant in oxidative stress and diabetes mellitus.</em> Journal of pharmacognosy and phytochemistry, 2015. <strong>3</strong>(6): p. 217-220.</a></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><a name=\"_ENREF_19\">19.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Naskar, S., et al., <em>Evaluation of antihyperglycemic activity of Cocos nucifera Linn. on streptozotocin induced type 2 diabetic rats.</em> Journal of ethnopharmacology, 2011. <strong>138</strong>(3): p. 769-773.</a></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><a name=\"_ENREF_20\">20.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Xu, M., et al., <em>MicroRNA‐190b regulates lipid metabolism and insulin sensitivity by targeting IGF‐1 and ADAMTS9 in non‐alcoholic fatty liver disease.</em> Journal of cellular biochemistry, 2018. <strong>119</strong>(7): p. 5864-5874.</a></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><a name=\"_ENREF_21\">21.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Eidi, M., A. Eidi, and H. Zamanizadeh, <em>Effect of Salvia officinalis L. leaves on serum glucose and insulin in healthy and streptozotocin-induced diabetic rats.</em> Journal of ethnopharmacology, 2005. <strong>100</strong>(3): p. 310-313.</a></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><a name=\"_ENREF_22\">22.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Chakrabarti, R., et al., <em>Antidiabetic and hypolipidemic activity of Helicteres isora in animal models.</em> Journal of Ethnopharmacology, 2002. <strong>81</strong>(3): p. 343-349.</a></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><a name=\"_ENREF_23\">23.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Dandekar, P., et al., <em>Toxicological evaluation of pH-sensitive nanoparticles of curcumin: acute, sub-acute and genotoxicity studies.</em> Food and Chemical Toxicology, 2010. <strong>48</strong>(8-9): p. 2073-2089.</a></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><a name=\"_ENREF_24\">24.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Orhan, G., et al., <em>Synergy tests by E test and checkerboard methods of antimicrobial combinations against Brucella melitensis.</em> Journal of clinical microbiology, 2005. <strong>43</strong>(1): p. 140-143.</a></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><a name=\"_ENREF_25\">25.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Orhan, E., et al., <em>Effects of plant growth promoting rhizobacteria (PGPR) on yield, growth and nutrient contents in organically growing raspberry.</em> Scientia Horticulturae, 2006. <strong>111</strong>(1): p. 38-43.</a></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><a name=\"_ENREF_26\">26.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Orhan, I.E., <em>Centella asiatica (L.) Urban: from traditional medicine to modern medicine with neuroprotective potential.</em> Evidence-based complementary and alternative medicine, 2012. <strong>2012</strong>.</a></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><a name=\"_ENREF_27\">27.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Singh, S.N., et al., <em>Effect of an antidiabetic extract of Catharanthus roseus on enzymic activities in streptozotocin induced diabetic rats.</em> Journal of Ethnopharmacology, 2001. <strong>76</strong>(3): p. 269-277.</a></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><a name=\"_ENREF_28\">28.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Pandit, R., A. Phadke, and A. Jagtap, <em>Antidiabetic effect of Ficus religiosa extract in streptozotocin-induced diabetic rats.</em> Journal of ethnopharmacology, 2010. <strong>128</strong>(2): p. 462-466.</a></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><a name=\"_ENREF_29\">29.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Ojewole, J., <em>Antiinflammatory and analgesic effects of Psidium guajava Linn.(Myrtaceae) leaf aqueous extract in rats and mice.</em> Methods and findings in experimental and clinical pharmacology, 2006. <strong>28</strong>(7): p. 441-446.</a></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><a name=\"_ENREF_30\">30.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Feshani, A.M., S.M. Kouhsari, and S. Mohammadi, <em>Vaccinium arctostaphylos, a common herbal medicine in Iran: Molecular and biochemical study of its antidiabetic effects on alloxan-diabetic Wistar rats.</em> Journal of ethnopharmacology, 2011. <strong>133</strong>(1): p. 67-74.</a></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><a name=\"_ENREF_31\">31.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Orhan, D.D., et al., <em>Evaluation of the hypoglycemic effect and antioxidant activity of three Viscum album subspecies (European mistletoe) in streptozotocin-diabetic rats.</em> Journal of ethnopharmacology, 2005. <strong>98</strong>(1-2): p. 95-102.</a></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><a name=\"_ENREF_32\">32.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Irudayaraj, S.S., et al., <em>Antidiabetic and antioxidant activities of Toddalia asiatica (L.) Lam. leaves in streptozotocin induced diabetic rats.</em> Journal of Ethnopharmacology, 2012. <strong>143</strong>(2): p. 515-523.</a></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><a name=\"_ENREF_33\">33.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Zeggwagh, N.-A., et al., <em>Study of hypoglycaemic and hypolipidemic effects of Inula viscosa L. aqueous extract in normal and diabetic rats.</em> Journal of ethnopharmacology, 2006. <strong>108</strong>(2): p. 223-227.</a></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><a name=\"_ENREF_34\">34.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Adeneye, A.A., <em>The leaf and seed aqueous extract of Phyllanthus amarus improves insulin resistance diabetes in experimental animal studies.</em> Journal of Ethnopharmacology, 2012. <strong>144</strong>(3): p. 705-711.</a></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\">&nbsp;</p>','This research project will focus on evaluation of in vivo anti-diabetic activity of selected medicinal plants traditionally used for treatment of diabetes mellitus as well as isolation and structural elucidation of the active compounds',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,'Diabetes, medicinal plants, in vivo antidiabetic activity, isolation, structural elucidation',10,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'Proposal-6290815db51eb517405092.docx'),(115,199,13,12,'Background: effective leadership, management, and governance (LMG) have a pivotal role in a well-performing health system. In the health system of Africa, primary health care has been brought a significant improvement in the health status of population though challenges of health problems and inequity of health services are still prevalent. Yet, poor leadership and management, and scarcity of resources mainly provoke these obstacles at district health system. Similarly, government of Ethiopia has a strategic plan for 20 years to achieve universal health cover for all citizens, mainly through the primary health care approach. Moreover, Ethiopia has delineated realizing excellence in leadership as one pillar of strategic plan to build robust LMG in health system and having capable managers in the district health system is unquestionable. Despite obvious importance of strong LMG in health system, yet people on the position have little preparation to succeed. Evidences has shown that leadership development intervention was associated to improved health system outcomes in different African countries. The aim of this leadership challenge will be to provide action-based leadership, management and governance training as an intervention to empower district health teams to improve quality of care and health outcomes in Jimma districts, Oromia region, Southwest Ethiopia.\r\nMethods: Prospective implementation of this project will consider 30% of districts based on WHO guideline facility sampling and all primary hospitals, health centers and health posts in the selected districts of Jimma zone will be included. Quasi-experimental study design will be used to measure changes due to implementation of this project. Quantitative data will be collected using Kobo collect and qualitative data through face-to-face interview. Descriptive statistics and linear regression will be done using STATA V.14 for quantitative data. Pre-post sample t-test and analysis of variance will be implemented to check whether there will be significant differences in mean values by individual and organizational performances. Multivariable linear regressions will be conducted to identify determinants of LMG intervention effectiveness. For qualitative data, thematic analysis will applied using MAXQDA Version 20.\r\nProject duration and Budget: Action-based LMG training intervention is an approximately six-month program with a total budget of 1,340,640.00 ETB.',NULL,'Fostering leadership, management and governance practices in the district health system of Jimma zone, Southwest Ethiopia','2022-05-27 13:27:25','The impact of will be foster leadership and management practices and enhance aim to meet health service quality improvement, population health outcome, and creating responsive health system for community health needs. The implementation of the project also contribute to enhance good working climate and increase employee’s satisfaction, which will have an impact on employee motivation and retention in primary health care. Moreover, the findings of this implementation project will help policymakers, program managers, and implementers to make informed decisions in the area of performance improvement. Similarly, it will help to further strengthen academia-health authorities, which is align to community based education approach of Jimma University.','51e7426ac8aaccfac8056f03e868e12b','completed','Despite significance of leadership, management and governance for meeting health system goals globally, application of management science to the field is a very recent phenomenon. Advancement in technical skills and medical technologies enable to halt million deaths, yet the missing is good L&M not boost in the low-income settings (1). Effective LMG has a pivotal role for well performing health system (2).  \r\nIn Africa, primary health care (PHC) has been brought a significant improvement on health status of population though challenges of health problems and inequity of health services still prevalent. Poor leadership and management, and scarcity of resources mainly provoke these impediments at district health system (DHS) (3). \r\nRecently Ethiopia has been implementing the first phase health sector transformation plan (HSTP), which is a part of “envisioning Ethiopian to achieving universal health coverage through strengthening PHC” by end of 2035. The government of Ethiopia has been established three-tier health system: primary healthcare units, general hospital and tertiary level specialized hospital. PHC is accessible for society extensively and serve as entry point for health service in Ethiopia. District health office oversees health performances and manage primary health care unit. Thus, having capable managers in the district health team (DHMT) is unquestionable. As a result, Ethiopia has delineated realizing excellence in leadership as a priority area in HSTP (4,5). This urges that the need to strengthen health L&M among DHMTs.\r\nDespite obvious importance of strong L&M in health system, yet people on the position have little preparation to succeed (1). Evidences has shown that leadership development intervention was associated to improved health system outcomes in different African countries (6–9). The aim of this leadership challenge will be to provide action based leadership and management training (LMT) as an intervention to empower DHMTs to improve quality of care and health outcomes in Jimma districts, Oromia region. The project will apply LMG framework for results, and challenge model developed by Management Sciences for Health to ensure practicability of the intervention. The project will help to develop competencies and skills of DHMTs for leading and managing district health system modestly. The evidences from the project will be used as benchmark to expand leadership development intervention in other districts. \r\nDirect beneficiaries of the project: DHMTs, health professionals and zonal health department of Jimma, and primarily community at large. Developmental partners and health researchers will be indirect beneficiaries of the project.','This project will be implemented of Jimma zone, which is located 350 km from Addis Ababa in southwest direction. This zone has 21 districts with a total projected population of 3,090,112 of which 89.69% are rural inhabitants. There are one tertiary hospital, 4 primary hospitals, 114 health centers, and 459 health posts (10). For this prospective implementation project, we will consider 30% of districts based on WHO guideline and all primary hospitals, health centers and health posts in the selected districts of Jimma zone. \r\nQuasi-experimental study design will be used to measure changes due to implementation of this project. Action-based LMG training intervention is an approximately six-month program that uses a team-based approach to develop LMG skills among health managers and workers in all districts of Jimma zone. Strategies for implementation include development of modules, conduct baseline assessment to identify LMG challenges in the district health system of the zone, advance training, mentorship for field level capacity building, and evaluation of trained DHMTs performance at facility level and end-line survey. Consultative meeting and launching of the project will be made with regional, zonal and woreda health offices managers, and developmental partners working on leadership development in Jimma zone. Action-based LMT will be given following hierarchical management system from region to district level for core supervisory teams. Core supervisory team will cascade the training to all coordinators at zone, health offices and primary health care units (PHCUs). Core supervisory teams based on the challenges selected by DHMTs will do face-to-face and virtual mentorships every month. PHCU will review and evaluate health challenge performances monthly. Governing body of health facility will also receive basic training on how to steer an organization in a shared strategic direction. Two phases evaluation on effectiveness of LMG training outcomes will be done. Pre-post evaluation of immediate/intermediate outcomes (knowledge, skills and competencies) of each trainee and measure improvement on intermediate outcomes (working climate and management system) will be conducted after three months of training. Long-term action based LMT outcomes (quality of care and population level health-outcomes) will be evaluated at the end of six months. Training manual of LMT, guideline of district health system management, mentorship checklist and evaluation tools will be produced to facilitate implementation of the project.\r\nAll training profiles and data generated through evaluation of the project will be stored in electronic version in MS excel sheet, data collection using Kobo collect and analyzed. Descriptive statistics and linear regression will be done using STATA V.14 for quantitative data. Pre-post sample t-test and analysis of variance will be implemented to check whether there will be significant differences in mean values by individual and organizational performances. Multivariable linear regressions will be conducted to identify determinants of LMG intervention effectiveness. For qualitative data, thematic analysis will applied using MAXQDA Version 20.\r\n The findings of the study will be presented in scientific work and published them on reputable journals. Based on the best experiences identified from the project implementation, scale up of the project to all districts of the zone and beyond will be considered. \r\nEthical clearance will be obtained from the Institutional Review Broad (IRB) of Institute of Health, Jimma University. Letter of permission will be secured from Oromia Regional health Bureaus to zonal health department and respective districts. Confidentiality of information will be assured using coding system and by removing any personal identifiers.','<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">1. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Management Science for Health. An Urgent Call to Professionalize Leadership and Management in Health Care Worldwide. 2006;4(4). </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">2. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Lemay N, Ellis A. Leadership Can Be Learned , But How Is It Measured ? 2008;8(8). </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">3. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Chatora R, Tumusiime P. Management , Leadership and Partnership for District Health. 2004; </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">4. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Federal Democratic Republic of Ethiopia Ministry of Health. Health Sector Transformation plan. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">5. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; World Health Organization. Health workforce governance and leadership capacity in the African region review of human resources for health units in the ministries of health;2012. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">6. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Martineau T, Raven J, Aikins M, Alonso-garbayo A, Baine S, Huss R, et al. Strengthening health district management competencies in Ghana , Tanzania and Uganda : lessons from using action research to improve health workforce performance. 2018; </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">7. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Fetene N, Canavan ME, Megentta A, Id EL, Tan AX, Nadew K, et al. District-level health management and health system performance. 2019;1&ndash;9. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">8. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Mutale W, Kachemba A, Mukendi R, Clarke K, Mulenga D. Leadership and management training as a catalyst to health system strengthening in low-income settings : Evidence from implementation of the Zambia Management and Leadership course for district health managers in Zambia. 2017;1&ndash;24. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">9. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Seims LRK, Alegre JC, Murei L, Bragar J, Thatte N, Kibunga P. Strengthening management and leadership practices to increase health-service delivery in Kenya : an evidence-based approach. 2012;1&ndash;7. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">10. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Zonal Health Department. Annual Health Report; Unpublished Work 2017. </span></span></span></span></p>','To foster leadership, management, and governance practices in the district health system of Jimma zone, Southwest Ethiopia',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,'Leadership, Management, Governance, District health system',10,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'Proposal-6290a78374c7d484909825.doc'),(116,370,13,18,'Background: Cancer and diabetes are two of the most common non-communicable diseases (NCDs) with increasing incidence worldwide, in all age groups and accounting for the vast majority of global mortality. According to the WHO, four disorders (cancer, diabetes, cardiovascular diseases and chronic respiratory disorders) account for over 80% of premature deaths attributable to NCDs worldwide. In Ethiopia, the prevalence of both cancer and diabetes is on the rise, although a systematic nationwide registry is not readily available. Global efforts to control NCDs have been disrupted by the COVID-19 pandemic and the two share risk factors, which are linked to adverse COVID-19 outcomes. Therefore, a renewed effort is required to monitor and control NCDs and their associated factors.\r\nTNFα is a pleiotropic, proinflammatory cytokine that is implicated in a variety of cellular and systemic regulatory activities. It has a dual role in the development of cancer. It normally inhibits tumor progression and metastasis but may also act as a tumor promoter. The contradictory functions might be related to the dose and/or the type of target tissue involved. In either case, it can serve as a biomarker for monitoring disease progression. As a pro-inflammatory cytokine, TNFα induces insulin resistance and hyperglycemia leading to the pathogenesis of type 2 diabetes. It contributes to insulin resistance in part by reducing the expression of glucose transporter type 4.\r\nDespite such direct links between TNFα and the two emerging epidemics, no notable research in this area is available in Ethiopia. We think that such a study can serve as a cost-effective means of monitoring the emergence, progression and/or response to treatment of these diseases. Our study will contribute to ways for monitoring, early detection of and intervention against these and related NCDs and improving an evaluation of treatment outcomes.\r\nObjective: To investigate the change in the concentrations of serum TNFα in cancer and type 2 diabetes patients attending Jimma Medical Center at the time of diagnosis and during a course of treatment.\r\nMethods: A hospital-based case study design will be used to collect blood samples, clinical data and anthropometric information from consenting cancer and diabetes patients attending the JMC from the time of diagnosis. The cancer treatment unit and diabetes consultation ward will be the primary sites of sample collection. The collection of data and samples will be guided by a structured questionnaire and a checklist. Serum levels of TNFα will be determined by human TNFα ELISA.',NULL,'Serum Tumor Necrosis Factor α (TNFα) as Novel Pleiotropic Biomarker for Cancer and Diabetes Mellitus','2022-05-29 09:01:47','6. Research Output / Expected Output\r\n1. To evaluate the serum TNFα levels in newly diagnosed cancer patients attending JMC.\r\n•	The results will enable us to know changes in circulating levels of the cytokine and how it correlates with the onset/stage of cancer.\r\n•	It will reveal whether any change in serum TNFα levels show variations with the type or stage of cancers and as response to treatments. \r\n•	The results will also show whether changes in the levels of the biomarker are affected by other variables such age, sex and residence situation.\r\n\r\n2. To evaluate the evolution of serum TNFα levels in cancer patients attending JMC during a prolonged treatment period.\r\n•	The result will enable us to monitor the mode of changes in serum level of TNFα during treatment. \r\n•	The result will help to determine whether the cytokine level in serum could be used to monitor response to treatment and/or can be correlated with changes in the severity of the disease. \r\n\r\n3. To evaluate the serum TNFα levels in newly diagnosed diabetic patients attending JMC.\r\n•	The result will help to know the change in serum TNFα levels in relation to the onset of diabetes. \r\n•	It helps to determine whether this changes with time as a response to prescribed treatment course.\r\n\r\n4. To evaluate the serum TNFα levels in established diabetic patients attending JMC.\r\n•	The results will help to establish relative TNFα levels in patients who have lived with the disease for some years of their lives.\r\n\r\n5. To evaluate the serum TNFα levels in normal control population and first-degree relatives of diabetic patients attending JMC.\r\n•	The result will help us know whether any changes in serum levels of TNFα in diabetic patients have genetic basis or are heritable. \r\n•	The data can be used; in this case there is a genetic basis, to determine the patterns of inheritance. \r\n•	This data can have predictive value and form the basis for establishing preventive and follow-up measures.','67c0ac6c3b80726a5321222c343a1793','completed','Although the overall global health trends show an improvement over the past 30 or so years, several independent studies indicate that the prevalence of chronic non-communicable diseases (NCDs) is steadily increasing around the world. The phenomenon is observed globally in all age groups, irrespective of the socio-economic status of the population under observation and has implications for expenditure on health care. \r\nNCDs remain to be the leading global causes of death, being responsible for over 70% of all deaths worldwide[21]. More than three-quarters of NCD deaths occur in low- and middle-income countries, as these tend to be relatively resource-poor and treatment and monitoring facilities are inadequate or inaccessible. Cancer and diabetes, together with cardiovascular and chronic respiratory disease, are the leading causes of death from NCDs on a global scale [31]. These chronic disorders have several behavioral risk factors in common, including tobacco use, unhealthy dietary habits, lack of physical activity and alcohol abuse [31]. \r\nThe global response to the control the increasing prevalence of NCDs over the past couple of decades has been encouraging. For example, the WHO had published an agenda for research on the prevention and control of NCDs in 2011 [4, 25], which was followed by the endorsement of a Global Action Plan that aimed at encouraging countries to take coordinated actions towards the goal of a quantifiable reduction in premature death attributable to NCDs [22].\r\nNevertheless, it has now been predicted that the burden of NCDs will show an ominous and steady increase, especially in resource-poor countries, to the extent that by 2030, it leads to eight times more mortality in such countries as compared to those in more affluent nations [30]. This may even prove to be an optimistic estimate because of the unforeseen and devastating effects of the COVID-19 pandemic which has caused major disruptions to critical health services in almost all countries around the world. The pandemic might even have caused a considerable setback to the efforts and achievements in the control of NCDs during the preceding decade.\r\nA recent WHO report on the statistics of world health indicates that diabetes and cancer are among the diseases that have considerably contributed to the substantial increase in the number of years lived with disability around the globe [23]. The mortality caused by the four major NCDs (cancer, diabetes, cardiovascular diseases and chronic respiratory diseases) combined was 33.2 million people in 2019 alone, which represents an increase of about 28% as compared to the numbers in the year 2000 [23]. Specifically, the deaths caused by cancer grew by 37% to 9.3 million people while those caused by diabetes increased by an alarming 72% to 2.0 million people during this same period. Although these may have been worsened by population growth and increased life expectancy and show regional variabilities, the overall data suggest that the situation is deteriorating, especially in low-income countries. The situation, if not confronted by more cost-effective monitoring and intervention strategies can, in combination with the COVID-19 pandemic, derail efforts to achieve health-related Sustainable Development Goals that have been set up by the UN. \r\nIn the case of Ethiopia, the percentage of deaths attributable to NCDs is reported to be 39% with a total of 275,000 NCD deaths [21]. This is higher than the WHO estimates (from 2008) of 34% of annual deaths due to NCDs [4]. Although Ethiopia has developed a national strategic action plan for the prevention and control of NCDs and their risk factors [20], there is no current, representative survey at the national level that suggest a progress towards the achievement of the set goals.\r\nTherefore, in addition to the national action plan for the prevention and control of NCDs, high-quality research is needed to better understand the induction and progression of these diseases within the country context. These types of data not only fill in the knowledge gap where appropriate but also identify new methods and approaches for improved disease prevention and patient care as well as inform the relevant bodies on better ways of developing and maintaining an optimal health care system.\r\nTNFα is a multifunctional, pro-inflammatory cytokine that plays important roles in various cellular events. It is a member of a large family of TNF superfamily of cytokines that regulates cell differentiation, proliferation, survival and death[12]. It was discovered in the late 1970s as a cytokine with a promising role in suppressing tumor cell proliferation and inducing tumor regression [8]. However, research data over the following decades revealed that the cytokine possesses roles as diverse as involvement in the development of immunity and in responses such as tumor growth, inflammation and transplant rejection [1]. The studies also discovered several members of the TNF superfamily of ligands and receptors have been linked to a wide array of disorders including cancer and cardiovascular and metabolic diseases. \r\nThe role of TNF-induced signaling pathways in cancer has become paradoxical [28] – being anti-tumor by stimulating cytotoxicity in cancer cells or a potent tumor promoter by inducing the proliferation, survival and migration of cancerous cells. While the anti-cancer properties of TNF can be harnessed for the treatment of cancer, its mitogenic and tumor-promoting activities can serve as a therapeutic target in certain circumstances [5].\r\nSignaling pathways induced by TNFα are mediated by two types of receptors - TNFR1 and TNFR2 [10]. While TNFR1 is widely expressed in almost all cells, the expression of TNFR2 is much more restricted. Ligand binding to these receptors induces three major signaling pathways: the nuclear factor kappa B (NF-κB), the mitogen-activated protein kinase (MAPK) and apoptosis signaling pathways [5]. In addition to the kind of target tissue, the crosstalk between these diverse signaling pathways probably determines the outcomes of TNFα treatment either as inducer of cancer cell proliferation or destruction. It appears that at physiologic doses, TNFα acts as a tumor-promoting cytokine [9], by inducing cellular transformation, proliferation and angiogenesis, while at therapeutic dosage it can induce cancer cell death and disruption of tumor vasculature [17]. Therefore, a detailed study is required to use circulating serum level of TNFα as a reliable biomarker and a prognosis factor in cancer patients.\r\nThe pro-inflammatory effects of TNFα contibute to the development of insulin resistance and pathogenesis of type 2 diabetes [14, 29]. It has been demonstrated that high levels of TNFα are expressed in and secreted by adipose tissue of obese humans and experimental animal models [15]. Furthermore, such elevated level in adipocytes and other peripheral tissues has been liked with obesity and the development of insulin resistance, both pathophysiological hallmarks of type 2 diabetes [3]. The correlation of between serum TNFα concentrations and insulin resistance has been evidenced by observations that while the administration of TNFα to animals and neutralization of TNFα can induce and improve insulin sensitivity, respectively, the development of obesity-induced insulin resistance is reduced in knockout mice that are deficient in TNFα or TNFR [2, 16, 29]. Data from human studies in this area are highly variable, in part influenced by population history and environmental factors. \r\n\r\n2. STATEMENT OF THE PROBLEM\r\nAccording to the 2021 WHO report, 41 million people die each year due to NCDs, equivalent to 71% of all global deaths [11]. Specifically, in people aged between 30 and 69 years, more than 15 million die from a NCD every year. The same comprehensive report indicated that the vast majority (85%) of these \"premature\" deaths occur in low- and middle-income countries. As already indicated, cancers (with 9.3 million people) and diabetes (1.5 million people) are two of the four leading NCDs that account for over 80% of all “premature” deaths annually, the others being cardiovascular diseases (17.9 million people) and respiratory diseases (4.1 million people). A recent report by the International Diabetes Federation (IDF) showed that a total of 537 million people aged 20-79 years live with diabetes around the world and this is predicted to increase to 643 million by the year 2030 [26]. According to this report, in 2021 alone, 6.7 million people died of diabetes. In addition, the report suggested low- and middle-income countries are affected the most by this global problem as 3 in 4 adults with diabetes live in such countries, which includes Ethiopia. The situation in the African continent is equally dramatic with 24 million (one in 22) people living with diabetes, which killed 416,000 people in 2021 [26]. Moreover, 54% of the people live with undiagnosed diabetes. \r\nIn Ethiopia, cancer has been noted as an emerging public health problem, accounting for about 7% of total national mortality [24]. Population-based overall data for the country as a whole is not available but estimates from the Addis Ababa population-based cancer registry indicate that 64,000 new cases of cancer occur annually [27].\r\nDiabetes, on the other hand, is an emerging public health problem in Ethiopia that is responsible for about 2% of total deaths in 2016 [13]. It follows the continued global increase in diabetes prevalence, rapidly emerging as a considerable challenge to individual quality of life and the economic well-being and a burden to the healthcare system of the country. An estimate by the IDF [26] suggested that nearly 2 million Ethiopians of 20-79 years of age lived with diabetes in 2021 (a 35% increase from the 2011 number of 1.4 million) and more than 26,000 people of the same age category died. This is most likely an underestimation since many (44%) of Ethiopians of the same age category live with undiagnosed diabetes. In fact, for this age category, Ethiopia is ranked in the top five African countries for the number of people living with diabetes. \r\nEthiopia is rapidly undergoing a significant level of mostly unplanned urbanization which is likely to bring with it changes in dietary habits as well as other unhealthy lifestyles such as exposure to tobacco smoke, increasingly sedentary life and increases alcohol abuse, all of which are well-recognized risk factors for the emergence of NCDs. With the health care facilities not expanding or specializing at a matching pace, the burden of these diseases on the community is a looming catastrophe. Therefore, if we are to avoid a major health care crisis, the national NCD control plans must be supported by a robust clinical and biomedical research data that can improve mitigation strategies.\r\n\r\n3. SIGNIFICANCE OF THE STUDY\r\nStudies that aimed to discover reliable and easy to detect biomarkers for NCDs in Ethiopia are unavailable to the best of our knowledge. However, the development of cost-effective strategies to identify individuals at increased risk of the diseases is important to target preventive measures and treatment options before the onset of full-blown disease. Patients under care would also benefit from a relatively simple marker that can monitor disease prognosis and response to treatment. Therefore, this project will produce a groundbreaking data in the biochemical study of two of the most common NCDs (cancer and diabetes) by using serum TNF levels as a biological marker for the onset, progression and response to treatment over a prolonged period of time. By validating this cytokine as a biomarker in a readily available biological sample, the project will provide added critical data underpinning NCDs monitoring, treatment and surveillance strategies at a regional and national level. \r\nOur project will not be limited to producing an original research data and contributing to the training of future generation of researchers but will also lay a foundation for institutional capacity building. We think this pioneering project can be the beginning of even more fundamental research because we will request the necessary institutional permission to store the collected biological samples and anonymous donor data in a nascent Biobank for future analysis, when advanced proteomics and genomics tools become available. Separate funding will be sought for this additional part of the work, which promises to be the first of its kind in the county and the basis for national and international collaboration in advanced biomedical research.','3.1 Study Design and period:  \r\nA hospital facility-based comparative prospective cohort study design will be used in cancer and diabetes treatment centers in JMC from September 1, 2022 to August 31, 2023.\r\n\r\n3.2 Population \r\n\r\n3.2.1 Source population: confirmed cancer and diabetes patients attending JMC.\r\n3.2.2 Study population: patients that are newly diagnosed with cancer or type 2 diabetes (T2D) attending JMC. \r\n3.2.2 Exclusion criteria: patients taking drugs that might influence TNF levels, those with (advanced) complications of T2D and recent recipients of donor blood will be excluded from the study.\r\n\r\n3.3 Sample Size Determination and Sampling Procedures\r\n\r\n3.3.1 Sample Size Determination\r\nThe sample size (n) required for the study will be calculated using the formula to estimate a single population proportion formula [7], by considering the following assumptions. Zα/2=critical value for normal distribution at 95% confidence level which equals 1.96 (Z value at alpha=0.05), d=margin of error 0.05, and P=50 % because estimations of the levels of serum TNFα as a biomarker for cancer and diabetes mellitus were not yet reported in the context of Ethiopia as well as Africa. \r\nBased on the formula, the representative sample size will 384. And a 10% nonrespondent rate will be added to 384. The final sample size will be 423. \r\n\r\n3.3.2   Sampling Procedure \r\nJMC has a recently inaugurated state-of-the-art cancer treatment center and follow-up ward. All patients attending this center and the existing diabetes treatment facility will be sampled. Sampling will be on the basis of informed consent. \r\n\r\n3.4. Variables\r\n\r\n3.4.1 Dependent variable \r\nSerum levels of TNFα in cancer and diabetes patients\r\n\r\n3.4.2. Independent Variables \r\nSocio-demographic factors: sex, age, residence, ethnicity, occupation, family income and family history.\r\nComorbidities: obesity, BMI, fat mass.\r\nClinical and related variables: clinical-stage, hospital stay and physical examinations\r\nBiochemical Parameters: Hyperglycemia and HbA1c (cytokine measurements primarily TNFα, as indicated but also interleukins if relevant reagents become available).\r\n\r\n3. 5 Data collection tool and procedure\r\n\r\n3.5.1 Data collection tool: \r\nThe data will be collected by using a structured questionnaire and checklist. The checklist will be adapted from the WHO tool for data extraction and developed after an extensive review of the literature and similar studies [6, 18, 19].\r\n\r\n3.5.2 Data collection procedure: \r\nTwo independent, dedicated and well-briefed team of data and sample collectors will be recruited for the two disease treatment sections. The data and sample collectors will be selected and trained for their roles by the principal and co-investigators. We will make sure that each team of collectors is composed of at least one specialized nurse and a laboratory technologist.\r\n\r\n3.6 Quality Control\r\nData and biological sample collectors will be trained on how to retrieve anonymous patient data from treatment archives, contact patients for a recall or clarification when needed, and also collect additional biological samples, when necessary. Facilitators and supervisors will be assigned to control and guide the data and sample collections in order to make sure that systematic and consistent data collection will be completed in the allocated time period. The data will be checked and assessed by the investigators for completeness and consistency before entry into Epi-data 4.1. \r\n\r\n3.7 Data Analysis Plan\r\nThe cleaned and entered data from Epi-data 4.1 will be exported to SPSS version 25. Descriptive statistics will be used to pronounce the frequency with percentage mean with standard deviation and displayed in graphs, charts and tables. Dependent and independent T-test will be applied to compare the average levels of TNF alpha profiles of patients. Linear and binary logistic regression will be done to select candidate variables for multivariate analysis and to see the significant co-relation of the independent variables. The independent variable found to have association with p-value less than 0.25 will be a candidate for multivariate logistic regression model and variables with p-value less than 0.05 will be used for declaring statistical significance.\r\n\r\n4. RESEARCH PROJECT TEAM\r\nWe, all the investigators listed below will take the responsibility of executing the research as described in this proposal and the methodology it entails, obey to ethical clearance regulations, whenever applicable, commit to regular monitoring of activities and financial conduct by the Institute of Health represented by the Health Research and Postgraduate Director Office, make available publications, regular reports or any product of the research to the same office and build on the development of research culture at Jimma University. Above all, we will be governed by the JU Research Policy.\r\n\r\n5. Capacity Building\r\nThis project will contribute to the development of highly trained researchers at the university since four postgraduate students will be recruited to participate in the work either as part of the preparation of their final year graduation paper or as an additional research experience. \r\nMoreover, we are planning for a significant institutional capacity-building effort. That is, on condition that our proposal is granted, we will seek the necessary ethical approval to store anonymous patient data and biological samples at the facilities of the Biomedical Sciences department. The facilities will be founded now and serve as a starting material for the establishment of a Biobank using funds from other sources.\r\n\r\n7. ETHICAL CONSIDERATION\r\nEthical clearance for this project will be obtained from Jimma University Institutional Review Board. The directors of the treatment centers will be communicated with a formal letter requesting their cooperation in conducting this research. The findings of this research will be communicated the collaborating centers in an aggregated form where patients will remain anonymous and personal information will remain confidential.\r\n\r\n8. PLAN FOR DISSEMINATION\r\nThe report of the results of this research will be submitted to Jimma University, Institute of Health Research and Innovation Office in due time. The result will be communicated to other relevant bodies as per the regulation of the university. Equally as important, every effort will be made to disseminate the results of this work through posters, seminar presentations, and publications in reputable international journals.','<p><strong>10. REFERENCES&nbsp;</strong></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman,Times,serif\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">Aggarwal, Charu C, S Yu Philip, Jiawei Han, and Jianyong Wang. <em>A framework for clustering evolving data streams</em>. in <em>Proceedings 2003 VLDB conference</em>. 2003. Elsevier.</span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman,Times,serif\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">Akash, Muhammad Sajid Hamid, Kanwal Rehman, and Aamira J Journal of cellular biochemistry Liaqat, <em>Tumor necrosis factor‐alpha: Role in development of insulin resistance and pathogenesis of type 2 diabetes mellitus.</em> 2018. <strong>119</strong>(1): p. 105-110.</span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman,Times,serif\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">Akbar, Muhammad Usman, Kanwal Rehman, Khalid Mahmood Zia, Muhammad Imran Qadir, Muhammad Sajid Hamid Akash, and Muhammad J Critical Reviews&trade; in Eukaryotic Gene Expression Ibrahim, <em>Critical review on curcumin as a therapeutic agent: From traditional herbal medicine to an ideal therapeutic agent.</em> 2018. <strong>28</strong>(1).</span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman,Times,serif\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">Alwan, Ala J Global status report on noncommunicable diseases . <em>Global status report on noncommunicable diseases 2010.</em> 2011.</span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman,Times,serif\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">Cai, Weibo, Ting Gao, Hao Hong, Jiangtao J Nanotechnology Sun, science, and applications, <em>Applications of gold nanoparticles in cancer nanotechnology.</em> 2008. <strong>1</strong>: p. 17.</span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman,Times,serif\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">Dalamaga, Maria J World journal of experimental medicine, <em>Obesity, insulin resistance, adipocytokines and breast cancer: New biomarkers and attractive therapeutic targets.</em> 2013. <strong>3</strong>(3): p. 34.</span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman,Times,serif\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">Daniel, W J New York, <em>Biostatistics: A foundation for analysis in the health sciences, 7th edr wiley.</em> 1999: p. 141-142.</span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman,Times,serif\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">Dickinson, William R, Walter S Snyder, and V Matthews, <em>Plate tectonics of the laramide orogeny</em>. Vol. 3. 1978: Matthews.</span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman,Times,serif\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">Dobrzycka, Bozena, Sławomir J Terlikowski, Andrzej Mazurek, Oksana Kowalczuk, Wiesława Niklińska, Lech Chyczewski, and Marek J Folia histochemica et cytobiologica Kulikowski, <em>Mutations of the kras oncogene in endometrial hyperplasia and carcinoma.</em> 2009. <strong>47</strong>(1): p. 65-68.</span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman,Times,serif\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">Dostert, Catherine, Melanie Grusdat, Elisabeth Letellier, and Dirk J Physiological reviews Brenner, <em>The tnf family of ligands and receptors: Communication modules in the immune system and beyond.</em> 2019. <strong>99</strong>(1): p. 115-160.</span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman,Times,serif\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">Ellapen, TJ, M Barnard, GL Strydom, KM Masime, and Y J International Quarterly of Community Health Education Paul, <em>A comparison between selected noncommunicable disease mortality rates between 2010 and 2016 among selected southern african countries.</em> 2021. <strong>41</strong>(2): p. 119-123.</span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman,Times,serif\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">Gaur, Upasna and Bharat B J Biochemical pharmacology Aggarwal, <em>Regulation of proliferation, survival and apoptosis by members of the tnf superfamily.</em> 2003. <strong>66</strong>(8): p. 1403-1408.</span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman,Times,serif\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">Habte, Bruck M, Tedla Kebede, Teferi G Fenta, and Heather J BMC research notes Boon, <em>Explanatory models of adult patients with type 2 diabetes mellitus from urban centers of central ethiopia.</em> 2016. <strong>9</strong>(1): p. 1-11.</span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman,Times,serif\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">Hotamisligil, GS J Journal of internal medicine, <em>The role of tnf&alpha; and tnf receptors in obesity and insulin resistance.</em> 1999. <strong>245</strong>(6): p. 621-625.</span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman,Times,serif\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">Jung, Kyu-Won, Young-Joo Won, Hyun-Joo Kong, Chang-Mo Oh, Duk Hyoung Lee, Jin Soo J Cancer research Lee, and treatment: official journal of Korean Cancer Association, <em>Prediction of cancer incidence and mortality in korea, 2014.</em>2014. <strong>46</strong>(2): p. 124.</span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman,Times,serif\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">Keister, Lisa A and Stephanie J Annual Review of Sociology Moller, <em>Wealth inequality in the united states.</em> 2000. <strong>26</strong>(1): p. 63-81.</span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman,Times,serif\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">Lejeune, Diane, Laure Dumoutier, Stefan Constantinescu, Wiebe Kruijer, Jan Jacob Schuringa, and Jean-Christophe J Journal of Biological Chemistry Renauld, <em>Interleukin-22 (il-22) activates the jak/stat, erk, jnk, and p38 map kinase pathways in a rat hepatoma cell line: Pathways that are shared with and distinct from il-10.</em> 2002. <strong>277</strong>(37): p. 33676-33682.</span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman,Times,serif\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">Lin, Wan-Wan and Shie-Liang J Biochemical pharmacology Hsieh, <em>Decoy receptor 3: A pleiotropic immunomodulator and biomarker for inflammatory diseases, autoimmune diseases and cancer.</em> 2011. <strong>81</strong>(7): p. 838-847.</span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman,Times,serif\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">Nishimura, Fusanori, Yoshihiro Iwamoto, Junji Mineshiba, Akemi Shimizu, Yoshihiko Soga, and Yoji J Journal of periodontology Murayama, <em>Periodontal disease and diabetes mellitus: The role of tumor necrosis factor‐&alpha; in a 2‐way relationship.</em> 2003. <strong>74</strong>(1): p. 97-102.</span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p><span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman,Times,serif\">&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Federal Democratic Republic of Ethiopia,&nbsp;Ministry of Health (MOH)<span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">. <em>National strategic action plan (NSAP) for prevention &amp; control of non-communicable diseases in Ethiopia</em>, 2016.&nbsp;</span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman,Times,serif\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">World Health Organisation (WHO). <em>Noncommunicable diseases progress monitor 2020</em>. 2020, World Health Organisation Geneva, Switzerland.</span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman,Times,serif\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">World Health Organisation (WHO). <em>Global action plan for the prevention and control of noncommunicable diseases 2013-2020</em>. 2013: World Health Organization.</span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman,Times,serif\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">World Health Organisation (WHO). <em>Global progress report on hiv, viral hepatitis and sexually transmitted infections, 2021: Accountability for the global health sector strategies 2016&ndash;2021: Actions for impact: Web annex 2: Data methods.</em> 2021.</span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman,Times,serif\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">World Health Organisation (WHO). <em>Noncommunicable diseases country profiles 2018.</em> 2018.</span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman,Times,serif\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">&nbsp;World Health Organisation (WHO). <em>A prioritized research agenda for prevention and control of noncommunicable diseases</em>. 2011: World Health Organization.</span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman,Times,serif\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">Sun, Hong, Pouya Saeedi, Suvi Karuranga, Moritz Pinkepank, Katherine Ogurtsova, Bruce B Duncan, Caroline Stein, et al., <em>Idf diabetes atlas: Global, regional and country-level diabetes prevalence estimates for 2021 and projections for 2045.</em> 2022. <strong>183</strong>: p. 109119.</span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman,Times,serif\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">Tigeneh, Wondemagegnhu, Abera Molla, Ayenalem Abreha, and Mathwose J Int J Cancer Res Mol Mech Assefa, <em>Pattern of cancer in tikur anbessa specialized hospital oncology center in ethiopia from 1998 to 2010.</em> 2015. <strong>1</strong>(1): p. 1-5.</span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman,Times,serif\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">Tse, Brian WC, Kieran F Scott, and Pamela J J Prostate cancer Russell, <em>Paradoxical roles of tumour necrosis factor-alpha in prostate cancer biology.</em> 2012. <strong>2012</strong>.</span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman,Times,serif\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">Tzanavari, T, P Giannogonas, and Katia P J TNF Pathophysiology Karalis, <em>Tnf-&alpha; and obesity.</em> 2010. <strong>11</strong>: p. 145-156.</span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman,Times,serif\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">Wei, Changli, Shafic El Hindi, Jing Li, Alessia Fornoni, Nelson Goes, Junichiro Sageshima,</span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman,Times,serif\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">Dony Maiguel, et al., <em>Circulating urokinase receptor as a cause of focal segmental glomerulosclerosis.</em> 2011. <strong>17</strong>(8): p. 952-960.</span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p><span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman,Times,serif\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">World Health Organization (WHO). <em>Noncommunicable diseases: Progress monitor 2020</em>. 2020.</span></span></span></p>','To determine serum levels of tumor necrosis factor α (TNFα) as a novel pleiotropy biomarker for cancer and diabetes mellitus.',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,'Tumor necrosis factor-alpha,Diabetes,Cancer,Biochemical,Ethiopia',10,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'Proposal-62922ed97c47f232001442.docx'),(117,371,13,23,'Abstract/ Executive summary\r\nBackground: - Conjunctival masses are comprised of a broad range of diagnoses. The 3 most important malignant tumors include ocular surface squamous neoplasia, melanoma, and lymphoma. The guidelines for diagnosis and management of conjunctival mass are based on the literatures and books written in the Westerns and developed countries. It is important to study the clinical, histopathological characteristics and associated factors of conjunctival mass in our population for best managent.\r\nObjective: - To assess the magnitude, clinical, histopathological characteristics and associated factors among patients with conjunctival mass who attend in Jimma university medical center, department of Ophthalmology South West Ethiopia.\r\nMethods: - To assess the magnitude of conjunctival mass; institutional based cross sectional study will be conducted on 384 patients from July 2022 up to June 2023 among randomly selected patients that came to Jimma university department of ophthalmology (JUDO). To identify the determinants for conjunctival mass; a case control  study design will be used to do on 276 patients from anterior segment clinic in JUDO (138 cases and 138 controls) over the one year period. Pretested, structured questionnaire will be used to collect data. The data collected will be cleaned and entered into Epi data version 3.1 and exported to SPSS version 20.0 for analysis. Descriptive statistics (frequencies and percentages) will be computed to show the clinical and hist-opathological features. Kappa statistics and other validity measures will be used to determine validity of clinical diagnosis of conjunctival mass. Bivariate analysis will be computed to select the candidate variables for multivariable logistic regression model. Then variables with a P-value ≤ 0.25 in the bivariate analysis will be entered into the multivariable logistic regression model to identify independent predictors. Then the predictors with a p-value ≤ 0.05 will be taken as a significant variable.\r\nWork plan and budget: The study will be conducted from July 2022 up to July 2023 and the total estimated budget is 533,170 ETB.',NULL,'Magnitude, Clinical, histopathological features and associated factors among patients with conjunctival mass who visit Jimma university medical center, department of Ophthalmology South West Ethiopia',NULL,'RESEARCH OUTPUT/OUTCOME\r\nThe overall reason to do this research is because, knowing the appropriate underlying pathologic characteristics is the gold standard investigation modality that guides to the proper management. Because of resource limitation, we have, majority of our diagnosis and management is based on clinical features. The validity study that we are going to undertake will let us know how much our clinical diagnosis is fruitful and identifies the determinants for conjunctival mass that we should control. Conjunctival mass has a significant impact on the appearance the eye and also the cosmetics of patients especially for younger groups of populations. In the malignant types of conjunctival masses, metastasis can occur which could be a cause for death. As there is deficiency in literatures in our study area related to this topic, this study will be used as a baseline research for future similar studies.','aa7cfeb17c873c805ae2bc14f783299d',NULL,'Background and Rationale\r\n\r\nConjunctival mass consist of a wide range of diagnoses; which include the conjunctival malignant tumors like; ocular surface squamous neoplasia (OSSN) (14%), melanoma (12%) and lymphoma (7%). But in children, conjunctival malignancies are not common (1).\r\nOther classification of conjunctival tumors is congenital and acquired lesion (2, 3). Conjunctiva can also be a site for systemic disease to manifest mimicking like tumor and in this case surgical excision and biopsy is not the primary management (4).\r\nPremalignant or malignant squamous epithelial lesions, lipomatous, leukemic, and secondary tumors are usually diagnosed in the older patient while choristomatous, vascular and fibrous lesions are often seen in younger individuals (5). These all information is from books written and studies conducted in western countries and may not be similar with features in Africa specifically Ethiopia, or our study population. So we need to study the features and associated factors of conjunctival mass for our population.\r\nInvasive squamous cell carcinoma and intraepithelial squamous carcinoma of the conjunctiva are histologically distinguished based on their invasion of epithelial basement membrane and substantia propria (6). The growths have different features like nodular, gelatinous, flat superficial leukoplakia, or diffuse invasive. These tumors may present as localized slowly growing lesions that have similar picture as benign conjunctival degenerations and sometimes they may present with pinguecula and pterygium (6, 7). Cornea or scleral invasion and intraocular spread are among complications of invasive squamous carcinoma (8).\r\nOcular surface squamous neoplasia (OSSN) is treated either surgically or by topical or injectable chemotherapy. Metastasis occurs in < 8% of cases which could be a cause for death. When it involves the orbit, it will require orbital exenteration as a management which has disfigurements on cosmetic appearance of patients (9, 11). But, in our country we do not have the topical chemotherapy or injectable immunotherapy. So, we do surgery both for diagnosis and treatment purpose.\r\nThe most frequent malignant tumor of the ocular surface is squamous cell carcinoma of the conjunctiva. Globally, it has an incidence of 0.13 – 1.9 per 100,000 persons, affecting mainly individuals between 50 and 70 years old and mostly men. The predisposing conditions for ocular surface squamous neoplasia generaly include; chronic solar radiation, immune deficiency, ocular surface injury, vitamin A deficiency, organ transplant, autoimmune conditions, xeroderma pigmentosum, and chronic exposure to cigarette smoke (10).\r\nStudies show that conjunctival squamous cell carcinoma usually affects male, elderly who are above 60 years of age. Most of the lesions occur at the limbus and patients could suffer from recurrent ocular surface squamous neoplasia (11).\r\nAfrica has the highest incidence of OSSN in the world with equal male and female preponderance. The high prevalence in these countries has been accredited to their proximity to the equator that exposes to longer sunlight exposure and the high prevalence of human imuno deficiency virus (HIV) (12). Similar to this, according to a study in Southern Ethiopia, Hawassa, squamoid conjunctival growth was found to be the second most common ocular manifestation (3.8%) in HIV patients preceded by Seborrheic blepharitis (5%) (13). But the study was done only in retroviral infected adult patients and it may not be generalized for all population.\r\nOn the view of clinical and histo pathological features of conjunctival masses different researches have been done. According to the result of a research in US, the most common presenting symptoms were red eye (68%) and ocular irritation (57%). Sclera was involved in 37% of cases, intraocular involvement in 13% and the orbit was invaded in 11% of cases with invasive squamous cell carcinoma (14). This study has showed the different features of conjunctival masses but it didn’t indicate how much the clinical diagnosis is similar with the histopathological result.  \r\nIn Asia, result of a retrospective study showed that 36.7% of tumors in the corneal and conjunctival epithelium were squamous cell carcinoma (malignant) and 26.7% were benign (carcinoma in situ) (15). In Taiwan, the clinical and histologic characteristics of ocular surface squamous neoplasia (OSSN) was investigated in 36 patients and the most common histologic type was papilliform followed by gelatinous, leukoplakic, and corneal intraepithelial neoplasia (16). The drawback of this study is that, it was retrospective study.\r\nIn Kenya there was a prospective study in 496 participants to describe the presentation of OSSN and identify clinical features which distinguish it from benign lesions. According to the histological diagnosis, OSSN was the most common taking 38% finding, followed by pterygium (36%) and actinic keratosis (19%).   Females were predominated; HIV-infection was common among OSSN cases (74%); most common signs more frequent in OSSN included; feeder vessels,  moderate inflammation, corneal involvement, leukoplakia, pigmentation and papilliform surface  (17).\r\nIn NorthWest Ethiopia there was a study done on the clinical and histopathological characteristics of ocular surface squamous neoplasia from a tertiary eye care center on a series of 50 cases over 16 months of period. The findings of the study showed leukoplakic appearance to be the commonest type (68%) and nasal side of the conjunctiva was the commonest location of the lesions (72%).  The histopathological examination has indicated that 96% of the participants had ocular surface squamous neoplasia. Among this ocular surface squamous neoplasia cases, 36% had carcinoma in situ, 36% invasive squamous cell carcinoma, 18% conjunctival intraepithelial neoplasia and 10% had associated pterygium clinically and on histology as well. Although the duration of data collection is longer than our study period, their sample size is minimal compared to our planned research. \r\nConjunctival mass is a common compliant that patients and it has a significant impact on the appearance of the eye and affects the cosmetics of patients especially for younger and productive groups of populations. This indirectly has a great consequence on the economy and income of the person, the family and the society as in general. In the malignant types of conjunctival masses, metastasis can occur which could be a cause for death. Knowing the appropriate underlying pathologic characteristics is the gold standard investigation modality that guides to the proper management. \r\nAfrica has the highest number of OSSN in the world. But, the guidelines for diagnosis and management are based on the literatures and books written in the Westerns and developed countries. Researches done in Ethiopia are very minimal and they focused on patients with immune compromised one and most of them didn’t include the histopathology studies and undermines the burden of benign conjunctival masses. It is important to study the clinical, histopathological characteristics, and associated factors of conjunctival mass in our population for best management.\r\nSo, this research will try to address the gaps and can be used as baseline data for future studies.','METHODS AND MATERIALS \r\nStudy area and period\r\nThis study will be done in Jimma University Department of Ophthalmology, located in Jimma town, 345 km south-west of Addis Ababa, the capital of Ethiopia. JUDO is the only tertiary eye-care center in south-western part of Ethiopia with a catchment area of over 15 million populations. In addition to patient’s service, the department is engaged in academic activities and researches. Currently, there are 11ophthalmologists, 22 ophthalmology residents, 5 optometrists, one cataract surgeon, 3 ophthalmic nurses, and 25 general nurses in JUDO. The anterior segment clinic is run by a fellowship trained cornea surgeon and general ophthalmologists and ophthalmology residents. The study will be conducted prospectively from July 2022 –June 2023.\r\nStudy design \r\nInstitution-based cross-sectional and case control study will be conducted. \r\nPopulation\r\nSource population \r\nAll new patients who will visit JUDO triage during the study period for the magnitude and those patients who are going to come the anterior segment clinic patients for the case control study.\r\nStudy population\r\nFor determining the magnitude of conjunctival mass: - Randomly selected patients among those who visit JUDO during the study period and who fulfill the inclusion criteria.\r\nTo identify determinants for conjunctival masses: - Patients from anterior segment clinic segregated into cases and control based on their clinical finding. \r\nTo assess the validity of clinical diagnosis; and to describe the clinical and histopathological features of conjunctival mass: - Patients with the diagnosis of conjuctival mass and in whom biopsy is going to be sent to histopathology and who fulfill the inclusion criteria. \r\nStudy unit \r\nIndividual from the selected patients fulfilling the inclusion criteria, and who will actually participate in the study.\r\n\r\nEligibility criteria \r\nInclusion Criteria\r\nAll patients who will visit JUDO during the study period the magnitude study\r\nAll patients at anterior segment clinic during the study period; for case control study\r\nAll patients with the diagnosis of conjuctival mass in whom excisional or incisional biopsy is going to be sent to histopathology examination.\r\nExclusion Criteria\r\nKnown ocular surface disease, previous pathology test, superimposed ocular infection, hearing and mental health problems.\r\nSample Size and Sampling Techniques\r\nSample size will be determined using single population proportion formula as stated below.\r\n              n = (Z 1-α/2) 2 p (1-p)  \r\n                        d2\r\n n = minimum sample size, \r\nZ 1-α/2 = significance level at α = 0.05 (1.96)\r\n d = margin of error (5%)\r\n              P = prevalence of conjunctival mass in the study area= 50%\r\n	 n = 384 \r\nK = N/n while N = 15,400   K = 40\r\n\r\nFor the case control study sample size is calculated by using epi info 7 software programs assuming, Power of 80%, a confidence level of 95%, ratio of control to cases of 1, percent of control exposed of 50% and odds ratio of 2. The total sample size has come to 276 (138 cases and 138 controls).\r\n\r\nVariables of the study\r\nDependent variables:\r\nMagnitude of conjunctival mass\r\nValidity of clinical diagnosis of types of conjunctival mass\r\nHistopathological features of conjunctival mass\r\nIndependent variables  \r\nSociodemographic factors (age, sex, marital status, place of residence, occupation, educational status, income, family size) family history, sunlight exposure, ocular trauma(chemical, mechanical, radiation), traditional medication use, traditional mal practice,  comorbidity (HIV AIDS,TB, diabetes mellitus, hypertension), history of Prior steroid use, behavioral factors (smoking cigarrate, alcohol, chat chewing, use of IV drugs) use of eye drops, previous eye surgery, presence of inflammation, feeder vessel, pigmentation, size of the mass, site of the mass, lymph node swelling. \r\nTable 1. Methodological summary\r\nObjective	Study Design	Population	Sample size	Sampling Method	Major outcome variable	Model of analyses\r\n1.	Determine magnitude of  conjunctiva mass	Cross-sectional	All patients in JUDO triage room	384	Systematic sampling	Magnitude of conjunctival mass	Proportion\r\n2.	Identify the Determinants  of Conjunctiva mass	Case control	All patient at anterior segment clinic of JUDO	276(138 cases &138 control)	Systematic sampling (all cases)	Determinants of Conjunctiva mass	Logistic Regression Model\r\n3.	Determine the Validity of clinical Dx of Conjunctiva mass	Cross-sectional	All patient in JUDO	138	Systematic sampling	Validity Measures	KAPPA statistics, and other validity measures\r\n4.	Describe the clinical and hist-opathological features	Cross-sectional	All patient in JUDO	138	Systematic sampling	Clinical and histopathological characteristics of conjunctival mass	Proportion & descriptive statistics\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nData collection procedures\r\nA two days training for data collectors will be given about the research and how the data is going to be collected. \r\nPretested, structured questionnaire will be used to collect data. For the magnitude study, 384 patients will be randomly selected by their card number. The first card will be selected by lottery method and based on the K value every 40 cards will be selected and the data will be filled. \r\nIn the case control study; 276 patients (138 those with conjunctival mass and 138 with no conjunctival mass) from anterior segment referral clinic will be consecutively enrolled into the study. \r\nTo determine the validity of clinical diagnosis, to describe the clinical and histopathological features of conjunctival mass; all patients that are diagnosed with conjunctival mass during the study period and in whom excision or incisional biopsy has been done will participate in the study. \r\nPatients will be informed about the research and those who are going to participate in the magnitude study; socio demographic information (age, sex, address, marital status, literacy status) will be filled with ophthalmic nurse and the presence of conjunctival mass will be assessed documented with ophthalmic resident and they are referred to anterior segment clinic. Risk factors for conjunctival mass (duration of sunlight exposure, immune status, trauma history, previous surgery, alcohol use, cigarrate smoking, intra venous drug abuse) will be assessed in both cases (patients with conjuntical mass) and control groups (anterior segment patients with no conjunctival mass). By using snellen chart their visual acuity will be measured. All patients will undergo a complete slit-lamp and fundus examinations at the initial presentation. On those patients with conjunctival mass, further clinical data including laterality, mass location, growth pattern and invasion of adjacent structures and lymph nodes will be recorded by ophthalmic resident. Diagnostic excisional or incisional biopsy will be obtained for all patients included in this study will be done by senior ophthalmologist. Excisional biopsy will be performed respecting a 4-mm safety margin. If there is corneal extension, involved corneal epithelium with 1-mm clear epithelium beyond the tumor margin will be removed. Other intra operative findings associated with the mass like; size, pigmentation, exact location, presence of feeding vessel, will be recorded. Finally, the excised tissue will be immersed formalin and submitted for histopathology test. Tissue sectioning and staining will be done pathologic resident and the final histologic feature differentiation and diagnosis will be done by senior pathologist.\r\nData analysis\r\nThe data collected entered into Epi data version 3.1.  It will be cleaned and coded using SPSS version 20.0 for analysis. Descriptive statistics (frequencies and percentages) will be computed to show the picture of the data. Bivariate analysis will be computed to select the candidate variables for multivariable logistic regression model. Then variables with a P-value of less than or equal to 0.25 in the bivariate analysis will be entered into the multivariable logistic regression model to identify independent predictors. Then the predictors with a p-value less than or equal to 0.05 will be taken as a significant variable.\r\nData quality control\r\nTrained lab technician, pathologic resident and ophthalmologic resident, will be involved in the collection of data from the patients. Two-day training will be given for the data collector and lab technician regarding study objective, interview techniques, measurements and ethical issues during data collection. Pretest will be done among 5% of the sample before three days of the actual data collection in order to assess its clarity, length, completeness and consistency, language barriers and contextual gaps on the structured questionnaires. The questionnaires will be also translated in to Amharic /Afan Oromo to facilitate understanding of the respondents. Questionnaires will be checked daily for accuracy, consistency and completeness by using electronics means of communication. The instruments will be standardized and checked about their functionality.\r\nOperational definitions \r\nControl group: - Consecutively selected patients that visit anterior segment clinic during the study period and those who do not have conjunctival mass.\r\nCase group: - Consecutively selected patients that visit anterior segment clinic with the diagnosis of conjuctival mass.\r\nSquamouss papillomas: - Papillary fibrovascular cores covered by hyperplastic squamous epithelium (8).\r\nConjunctival intraepithelial neoplasia (CIN): - Atypical neoplastic cells which involves the epithelium only, with the basement membrane intact. \r\n	Mild: - involvement of 1/3 of the epithelium \r\n	Moderate: - involvement of 2/3 of the epithelium\r\nSevere (Squamous carcinoma in situ): - severe atypia, full -thickness involvement of the epithelium with squamous eddies or keratin whorls/pearls (8).\r\nGranuloma: - Smooth, red-purple, sessile or pedunculated lesions (8).\r\nInvasive SCC: - Implies that neoplastic cells have broken through the basement membrane and invaded the underlying stroma (8). \r\nInclusion cyst: - Benign cysts filled with serous fluid and slimy mucous secretions (19). \r\nNevus: - A discrete lesion on the conjunctiva, dark brown to yellow color, and will contain clear cystic components (20).\r\nPterygium: - A raised, wedge-shaped fibrovascular growth of the conjunctiva that extends onto the cornea (8).\r\nEthical consideration\r\nEthical clearance will be obtained from the institutional review board of Jimma University Institute of Health. Official support letter will be obtained from Jimma Universitydepartment of ophthalmology and pathology. All study-related information will be kept confidential. Written consent from patients will be obtained. Name of the patients will not be included in the data to be collected and the result of this study. According to the declaration of Helsinki Ethical Principles for Medical Research, patients will be counseled and referred for further management according to their pathological findings. \r\nDissemination plan\r\nThe results will be disseminated through national ophthalmic association, training organized by the Ministry of Health and through a publication in international reputable journals.','<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><strong><span style=\"font-size:14.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Cambria&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\"><span style=\"color:#365f91\">REFERENCES </span></span></span></strong></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<ol>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"background-color:white\"><span style=\"color:#333333\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Shields CL, Chien JL, Surakiatchanukul T, et al. Conjunctival tumors: review of clinical features, risks, biomarkers, and outcomes. The 2017 J. Donald M. Gass Lecture. Asia Pac J Ophthalmol. 2017;6:109-20.</span></span></span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">&nbsp;Shields CL, Alset AE, Boal NS, et al. Conjunctival tumors in 5002 cases. Comparative analysis of benign versus malignant counterparts. The 2016 James D. Allen Lecture. Am J Ophthalmol. 2017;173:106-33.</span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Shields CL, Shields JA. Tumors of the conjunctiva and cornea. Surv Ophthalmol. 2004;49:3-24.</span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Grossniklaus HE, Green WR, Luckenbach M, et al. Conjunctival lesions in adults. A clinical and histopathologic review. Cornea. 1987;6:78-116.</span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Shields CL, Sioufi K, Alset AE, et al. Conjunctival tumors in children. Features differentiating benign from malignant tumors. JAMA Ophthalmol. 2017;135:215-24.</span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Lee GA, Hirst LW. Ocular surface squamous neoplasia. <em>Surv Ophthalmol </em>1995;<strong>39</strong>:429&ndash;49.</span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Grossniklaus HE, Green WR, Luckenbach M, <em>et al</em>. Conjunctival lesions in adults. <em>Cornea </em>1987;<strong>6</strong>:87&ndash;116.</span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Robert H. Rosa, jr, MD, Ronald Buggage, MD,&hellip;etal, Ophthalmic Pathology and Intraocular Tumors, 2011-2012, 47 &ndash; 75</span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Roland Ho &uml;llhumerID1, Susan WilliamsID1, Pamela Michelow; Observational study of ocular surface squamous neoplasia: Risk factors, diagnosis, management and outcomes at a tertiary eye hospital in South Africa, plos one, 2020, 1 -12 </span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Go&ntilde;i Espildora I, Jans J, Petric Guajardo M, Manzor Veliz M, Flores P&eacute;rez JC, Varas Marchant G, Mu&ntilde;oz-Schuffenegger P, Zoroquiain P. Squamous cell carcinoma of the conjunctiva with extraocular involvement: case report and literature review. Medwave. 2016 May 26;16(04).</span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">McKelvie PA, Daniell M, McNab A, Loughnan M, Santamaria JD. Squamous cell carcinoma of the conjunctiva: a series of 26 cases. Br J Ophthalmol. 2002 Feb;86(2):168-73. doi: 10.1136/bjo.86.2.168. PMID: 11815342; PMCID: PMC1770993.</span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Gichuhi S, Sagoo MS, Weiss HA, Burton MJ. Epidemiology of ocular surface squamous neoplasia in Africa. Trop Med Int Health. 2013 Dec; 18(12):1424&ndash;43. https://doi.org/10.1111/tmi.12203 PMID: 24237784</span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Amsalu A, Desta K, Nigussie D, Delelegne D. Ocular manifestation and their associated factors among HIV/AIDS patients receiving highly active antiretroviral therapy in Southern Ethiopia. International journal of ophthalmology. 2017;10(5):776.</span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Tunc M, Char DH, Crawford B, Miller T. Intraepithelial and invasive squamous cell carcinoma of the conjunctiva: analysis of 60 cases. British Journal of Ophthalmology. 1999 Jan 1;83(1):98-103.</span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Kim BH, Kim MK, Wee WR, Oh JY. Clinical and pathological characteristics of ocular surface squamous neoplasia in an Asian population. Graefe&#39;s Archive for Clinical and Experimental Ophthalmology. 2013 Nov;251(11):2569-73.</span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Ma IH, Hu FR, Wang IJ, Chen WL, Hsu YJ, Chu HS, Yuan CT, Hou YC. Clinicopathologic correlation of ocular surface squamous neoplasia from a university hospital in North Taiwan 1994 to 2014. Journal of the Formosan Medical Association. 2019 Apr 1;118(4):776-82.</span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Gichuhi S, Macharia E, Kabiru J, Zindamoyen AM, Rono H, Ollando E, Wanyonyi L, Wachira J, Munene R, Onyuma T, Sagoo MS, Weiss HA, Burton MJ. Clinical Presentation of Ocular Surface Squamous Neoplasia in Kenya. JAMA Ophthalmol. 2015 Nov;133(11):1305-13. doi: 10.1001/jamaophthalmol.2015.3335. PMID: 26378395; PMCID: PMC5321496.</span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Tesfalem Hagos Gebremariam1, Yared Assefa Wolde1*,&hellip;etal Clinical and histopathological characteristics of ocular surface squamous neoplasia: Evidence from a tertiary eye care center, northwest Ethiopia, Trends Med,</span></span> <span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">October</span></span> <span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">2018, 18(6): 1-4</span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Deshmukh S, Bhattacharjee K, Hawaibam S, et al. Conjunctival inclusion cyst, an uncommon complication of a common surgery: a case report and review of literature. Adv Ophthalmol Vis Syst. 2018;8(6):298-299. DOI: 10.15406/aovs.2018.08.00325</span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Shields CL, Fasiudden A, Mashayekhi A, Shields JA. Conjunctival Nevi: Clinical Features and Natural Course in 410 Consecutive Patients. Arch Ophthalmol. 2004;122(2):167&ndash;175. doi:10.1001/archopht.122.2.167</span></span></span></span></li>\r\n</ol>','To assess the magnitude, clinical, histopathological characteristics, and associated factors among patients with conjunctival mass who attend Jimma university medical center, department of Ophthalmology South West Ethiopia.',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,'conjunctiva, mass, histopathology, surgery',1,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),(118,373,13,18,'Summary\r\nBackground: Acute coronary syndrome (ACS) is the most common coronary artery diseases and the leading cause of morbidity and mortality in the globe. ACS accounts for the first presentation of coronary vessel diseases and is a broader term used for describing signs and symptoms of cardiac ischemia. There are numerous ACS biomarkers that are currently available and that have clinical use as diagnostic, prognostic or predictive indicators. Most commonly used ACS biomarkers are high sensitive troponin T or I, creatinine phosphokinase-MB, and natriuretic peptides. Limited studies have demonstrated a strong association between haematological indices and the risk of adverse outcomes in patients with ACS due to the inflammation and systemic hypoxemia implicated in the pathophysiology mechanisms. This study will put a baseline data to come up with a better plan to deal with hematologic parameters on the follow up of ACS patients.\r\nObjectives: The aim of this study is to evaluate the role of hematological markers as potential diagnostic and prognostic indicator of all causes of mortality and morbidity during hospitalization of patients with acute coronary syndrome.\r\nMethods: Institutional based longitudinal cohort study will be conducted in Jimma Medical Center (JMC), South West Ethiopia from August 1st 2022 to April 30th, 2023. All consecutive patients admitted with confirmed ACS to cardiovascular unit of JMC who fulfill the inclusion criteria, during the study period will be included. Data will be collected through a face-to-face interviewing of study participants and retrieving patients’ medical records. Complete blood counts and serum biochemical analysis will be performed by following procedures recommended by the manufactures. After checking for completeness of the data, it will be entered into Epi-data software version 3.1 and then will be transferred to SPSS version 25 for analysis. All appropriate statistical analysis will be carried out. To quantify the discriminating ability of hematological markers, measures of accuracy such as sensitivity, specificity, likelihood ratio, and receiver operating characteristic (ROC) curve analysis will be carried out.','1.	To evaluate the pattern of hematologic markers of acute coronary syndrome patients compared with apparently healthy controls.  2.	To investigate the role of hematological indices in risk stratification of acute coronary syndrome. 3.	To evaluate the pro','Prognostic Efficacy of Hematological Markers in Patients with Acute Coronary Syndrome',NULL,'7.	Research Outcomes/ Impact\r\nNo studies were conducted on hematologic indices of acute coronary syndrome patients and its role as a biomarker and prognostic indicator in Africa and particularly in Ethiopia; thus, this study partly contribute for the build-up of functional value of hematological and biochemical parameters and their potential role for grading the severity and predicting the course of morbidity and mortality of acute coronary syndrome. Compared with known cardiac biomarkers those hematologic indices are part of routine laboratory investigation that can be done easily, existing in every clinical setup, less expensive and consequent wide availability in daily clinical practice for screening, early diagnosis, and follow up of the disease to minimize the complication and worsening of the disease, especially in resource limited settings.','525e783d4bfd78f337af4d0e390d2e5f',NULL,'Background and Rational\r\nAcute coronary syndrome (ACS) is the most common coronary artery diseases (CAD) and the leading cause of morbidity and mortality in the globe. ACS accounts for the first presentation of coronary vessel diseases and is a broader term used for describing signs and symptoms of cardiac ischemia(1).It is characterized by ruptured, vulnerable plaque and subsequent intraluminal thrombus formation, resulting in the termination of blood flow and possible myocardial infarction. There is a persistent low-grade inflammation that contributes to the initiation and progression of the atherosclerotic process. Unstable angina (UA), non-ST-segment elevation myocardial infarction (NSTEMI), and ST-segment elevation myocardial infarction (STEMI) are sub types of ACS(2).Though there are lists of identified risk factors; age, sex, tobacco use, physical inactivity, unhealthy diet, obesity, genetic predisposition, raised blood pressure, raised blood sugar, raised blood cholesterol, psychosocial factors, poverty, air pollution, and low educational status are some of well-known risk factors of ACSs. While the individual contribution of each risk factor varies between different communities or ethnic groups, the overall contribution of these risk factors is very consistent(3). ACS is suspected when a person presents with symptoms, particularly chest pain, and especially when they also have known risk factors like high blood pressure, being overweight or a family history. Rapid and accurate diagnosis is critical because ACS requires immediate intervention and prognosis improves significantly with rapid treatment(4).As was stated in a previous study, only one-fifth of the patients presenting with the complaint of acute chest pain required immediate treatment and hospital admission. However, a lack of a proper approach to patient stratification at the time of presentation leads to physicians’ over-admitting such patients(5).Biomarkers are able to reflect pathophysiologic process of ACS, and also provide meaningful information about prognosis and support clinical decision making without duplicating any information that is already available clinically. There are numerous ACS biomarkers that are currently available and that have clinical use as diagnostic, prognostic or predictive indicators(6).Most commonly, ACS biomarkers are high sensitive troponin T or I (hs-cTn), creatinine phosphokinase-MB (CK-MB), natriuretic peptides (BNP, and NT-proBNP), copeptin, galectin-3, and coronary calcium. Additionally, inflammatory biomarkers such as C-reactive protein (CRP), interleukin 6, fibrinogen, and tumor necrosis factor alpha (TNF-α) are used(7).Advances in biomarker research and developments related to ACS over the past three decades have led to more sensitive screening methods and improved treatments resulting in more favorable clinical outcomes in the community. However, the use of biomarkers for different purposes in ACS remains an important area of research that has been explored by scientists over the years and many new developments are still underway(8). Considerable advances in the clinical laboratory testing combined with improvement in analytical techniques over the past decade have greatly improve the role of clinical biochemistry in the management of ACS(9). \r\nAssessing prognosis of ACS is an important aspect in clinical decision making and setting a therapeutic strategy, and the use of hematological biomarkers may improve this(10, 11).Several studies have demonstrated a strong association between hematological parameters and the risk of adverse outcomes in patients with ACS due to the inflammation and systemic hypoxemia implicated in the pathophysiology mechanisms, and depending on the extent of the injury, causing circulatory failure and, consequently, organ dysfunction(12). Apart from hematological and oncological diseases, the inflammatory and hypoxemic processes are the main stimuli for the greater production by the bone marrow of the constituents of the blood. The presence of immature young cells in the peripheral circulation is directly associated with the intensity of systemic inflammation and hypoxemia and, consequently, with a poorer prognosis of ACS. An increased systemic and local inflammation plays a key role in the pathophysiology of ACS(13, 14).\r\nThe appearance of nucleated red blood cells (NRBC) and increases in red blood cell distribution width (RDW), mean platelet volume (MPV), neutrophil to lymphocyte ratio (NLR), and immature granulocytes (IG) in peripheral blood are directly associated with the intensity of systemic inflammation and hypoxemia in patients with ACS(15). RDW is a coefficient of variability in erythrocyte volume and size. The relationship between elevated RDW levels and increased risk of mortality in these patients is incompletely understood. It has been proposed that inﬂammation induces changes in erythrocyte maturation, resulting in the release of immature cells into the circulation and increased RDW(16). As was demonstrated in previous studies, neurohormonal and adrenergic activation might inﬂuence erythropoiesis, raising RDW levels and making patients more prone to adverse clinical outcomes such as re-infarction and cardiovascular death(17).There are studies showed that RDW level was significantly higher in ACS patient compared to non-ACS control group and the sensitivity of ACS diagnosis could be improved by measuring cardiac troponin T and RDW simultaneously. Compared with patients with a normal RDW on admission, patients with increased RDW had higher in-hospital mortality rate (18, 19). Neutrophil to lymphocyte ratio (NLR) is an inexpensive, easy to obtain, widely available marker of inflammation, which can aid in the risk stratification of patients with various cardiovascular diseases in addition to the traditionally used markers(20). Platelet size has been demonstrated to reflect platelet activity and seems to be a useful predictive and prognostic biomarker of cardiovascular events. It is also associated with a variety of pro-thrombotic and pro-inflammatory diseases. Literature indicate that mean platelet volume (MPV) can provide important information on the course and prognosis in many pathological conditions, such as cardiovascular diseases(21).Many studies have reported that low platelet counts in patients with ACS.Chu and his colleagues (22) reported MPV to be an early and independent diagnostic marker of ACS in patients presenting with acute chest pain. A meta-analysis reported that raised MPV was independently associated with the incidence of acute MI, mortality following MI,and restenosis following coronary intervention(23).It has been suggested that the presence of serum NRBCs in hospitalized patients with cardiovascular diseases is associated with increased mortality and the need for these patients to stay in the intensive care unit even with clinical improvement(24).\r\nDespite substantial declines in cardiovascular disease mortality during the last three decades in developed countries, it is becoming common in many developing countries. It resulted for 17.9 million deaths (32.1%) in 2015, up from 12.3 million (25.8%) in 1990 worldwide. Over 80% of CVD associated deaths were in low-and middle-income countries. The disease has a high rate in Eastern and Central Sub-Saharan Africa compared to Western and Southern Sub-Saharan Africa(25). Deaths, at a given age, from CVD are more common and have been increasing in much of the developing world. Coronary artery disease (CAD) and stroke account for 80% of CVD deaths in males and 75% of CVD deaths in females(26). Estimating the global prevalence of CVD is notoriously challenging due to multiple countries that are reporting the prevalence ascertained with a varying methodology which renders interpretation difficultand it requires information about those who do not visit the health facility(27). Likewise, estimating the burden of CVD is a challenging task in sub-Saharan Africa countries including Ethiopia(28).However, the World Health Organization has predicted 11.1 million deathsonly from coronary artery disease in 2020(29). In 2011, WHO estimated that 34% of Ethiopian population is dying from non-communicable diseases, with a national cardiovascular disease prevalence of 15%(30). A systematic review conducted in Ethiopia found that the pooled prevalence of CVD was 5%. The prevalence was higher in the population who visits hospitals (8%) compared to the general population (2%). There was no significant difference in the overall prevalence of CVD between males and females in Ethiopia(31). A study conducted in Ethiopia also showed that among cardiovascular-related deaths, 72.6% were diagnosed with ACS and in-hospital mortality rate was 27.4%(32).\r\nRapid and accurate diagnosis is critical because ACS requires immediate intervention and prognosis improves significantly with rapid treatment(4). Measurement of cardiac troponin (cTn) levels plays an essential role in the management of patients with suspicion of an ACS. Higher cTn levels correlate with a larger MI size and, accordingly, greater mortality risk(33). For this reasons cardiac troponin is a gold standard biomarker used for both short and long term risk assessment and its elevation is a commonly used clue for the selection of patients to beneficial therapies. However, the prognostic value of cTn is not absolute. For example, high-risk patients with unstable angina usually do not have cTn elevation, and in STEMI cTn levels may be affected by reperfusion modalities. Moreover, cTn levels do not cover all pathobiologic spectrums being relevant in ACS, like left ventricular dysfunction, hemodynamic stress, inflammation, and/or renal dysfunction(34). Concurrently, advances in our understanding of the pathogenesis and consequences of acute coronary athero-thrombosis have stimulated the development of new biomarkers and created the opportunity for an expanded role of multiple biomarkers, some old and others new, in the classification and individualization of treatment for ACS(35). A study report indicate that substantially to the accumulating evidence that a multi-marker strategy, employing a pathophysiological diverse set of biomarkers, is likely to add importantly to cardiac-specific troponin alone in the risk assessment of patients with ACS(36). \r\nThis has generated interest in prognostic biomarkers that can be used in ACS together with cTn for improvement of risk prediction. The past decades have seen a constant proliferation of such candidate biomarkers. There are proteins responsible for the induction of cell multiplication and other proteins are responsible for cellular differentiation, which are controlled by factors external to the bone marrow. Erythropoietin is produced to stimulate the bone marrow in the presence of hypoxemia(37). An increased systemic and local inflammation plays a key role in the pathophysiology of ACS. It is responsible for specific cellular multiplication and differentiation of progenitor cell. In recent years, a strong interest has been drawn to hematological indices given that they may provide independent information on pathophysiology, risk stratification, and optimal management of ACS(38). Furthermore, many studies have pointed at their effective prognostic value in all-cause mortality in terms of acute myocardial infarction and unstable angina. The most recent study also emphasizes their significant value in the combined analysis with other markers, such as troponin, or with GRACE, HEART, and TIMI scores, which improve risk stratification and diagnosis in ACS patients(11).\r\nDespite an overwhelming number of studies on hematologic biomarkers and cardiovascular disease, there is paucity of published comprehensive studies equating the relevance and implication of hematological parameters for ACS in low income settings, in Africa, particularly where anemia and infection due to parasites, bacteria, mycobacteria, and viruses are common and that affect hematologic biomarkers. This study will put a baseline data to come up with a better plan todeal with hematologic indices on the follow up of ACS patients. Those hematologic parameters are part of routine laboratory investigation that can be done easily, existing in every clinical setup, less expensive and consequent wide availability in daily clinical practice for screening, early diagnosis, and follow up of the disease to minimize the complication and worsening of the disease, especially in resource limited settings made them very popular in the laboratory testing.This study will also help to assess the impact of racial, ecological, lifestyle, and economic difference on hematologic parameters of ACS patients in accordance with most other studies done in developed countries. Besides, this study will be used as a baseline for upcoming studies with similar motives to explore in this study area.','4	Methods and Materials\r\n4.1	Study area and Period\r\nThis study will be conducted in cardiovascular unit of Jimma medical center (JMC). JMC is one of the oldest hospitals in Ethiopia and it is the only teaching and referral hospital in southwest Ethiopia with 800 bed capacity and a catchment population of over 15 million people. The hospital is located in Jimma City (356 Km South-West of Addis Ababa). JMC cardiovascular treatment unit is one of the designated health facilities for CVD treatment. The study will be conducted from August 1st 2022 to April 30th, 2023.\r\n4.2	Study Design\r\nOverall an institutional based longitudinal cohort study will be conducted in Jimma Medical Center (JMC), South West Ethiopia; to assess the relevance and implication of hematologic indices on the prognosis and outcome of acute coronary syndrome.\r\nFor specific objective #1, a comparative cross-sectional study design will be implemented to see the pattern of hematological indices among ACS patients and apparently healthy comparative group.  \r\n4.3	Source Population\r\n	For all objectives\r\nACS study group: All ACS patients attending JMC during the study period.\r\n	For specific objective #1\r\nNon-ACS comparative apparently healthy group: All Jimma community, Jimma university workers, patient visitors, and patient attendants from JMC available during study periods.\r\n\r\n4.4	Study Population\r\n	For all objectives\r\nACS study group: All consecutive patients admitted with confirmed acute coronary syndrome to cardiovascular unit of JMC who fulfill the inclusion criteria, during the study period will be included. \r\n\r\n	For specific objective #1\r\nNon-ACS comparative apparently healthy group: Age, sex and place of residency matched apparently healthy individuals from Jimma community, Jimma university workers, patient visitors and patient attendants will be recruited purposively in a 1 to 1 ratio.\r\n\r\n4.5	Inclusion & Exclusion Criteria\r\n4.5.1	Inclusion Criteria\r\nAll patients admitted with confirmed acute coronary syndrome to medical wards of JMC will be included. The diagnosis of acute coronary syndrome will be carried out as per the protocol of WHO using clinical history, electrocardiography, and laboratory abnormality during admission. \r\n\r\n4.5.2	Exclusion Criteria\r\nPatients aged less than 18 years and those who have been diagnosed with hematological disease, known malignancy, systemic inflammatory or autoimmune disease, and thrombocytopenia before admission, secondary anemia, chronic liver disease, renal failure, and those on immune-suppressive therapy, use of anticoagulant agents, and readmitted after discharge will be excluded from the present study.\r\n\r\n4.6	Sampling Technique\r\nEvidence is as good as the research it is based on, which in turn depends on the statistical soundness of the claims it make. It is very important to understand that method of sample size calculation is different for different study designs and one blanket formula for sample size calculation cannot be used for all study designs. Accordingly in this mega research each specific objective depends on its respective statistical model.  \r\n	For specific objective # 1\r\nTo determine the minimum sample size for comparative cross-sectional study design epi info sample size calculation formula for comparing two means and standard deviation is employed. Since an equal ratio of cases and controls, confidence interval of 95% , power of 80%,and the Mean ± SD of RDW for case and control 17.2 (± 2.89) and 15.7 (±2.7) respectively will be used(39). Accordingly the final sample size calculated is 55 for case and 55 for control.\r\n	For specific objective # 2 and 3\r\nIt is defined as a longitudinal research study that includes a category of people who share the same characteristic, typically those who experienced a common event in a selected period, such as disease. There is no control group, and no intervention or treatment is given to patient that is why it is different from randomized control trials. For such study independent cohort sample size estimation formula is used. Hence, study design for specific objective # 2 and #3 is longitudinal cohort the following formula is applied to determine the minimum sample size.\r\n\r\n \r\nWhere\r\nn = Total number of desired study subjects (case) to identify true relative risk with two-sided Type-I error \r\nm = Number of subjects (control) per experimental subject\r\nZ 1-β = It is the desired power (0.84 for 80% power) \r\nZ1-α/2 = Critical value and a standard value for the corresponding level of confidence. (At 95% CI it is 1.96)\r\np0 = Possibility of event in controls (30%)\r\np1 = Possibility of event in experimental (20%)\r\np = p1+m p0 /m+1\r\n\r\n	Accordingly the final sample size calculated is 184.\r\n\r\n	For specific objective #4\r\nThis specific objective uses time to event analysis. The minimum sample size is determined to assess a mortality hazard ratio between patients with ACS and hematological indices; assuming α-error of 5% and a statistical power of 80%(15). Accordingly minimum sample size is 163 patients. \r\n	For specific objective #5  \r\nFor this specific objective there will be computing a new diagnostic algorithm that doesn’t need sample size determination.  \r\nHence, for all specific objectives of this study the study participants are the same ACS patients admitted to JMC during the study period, it is preferable to take the formula that gives greater number cases. Consequently the greater minimum sample size of the study will be 184 ACS patients. By considering 10% of drop out the final minimum sample size will be 203. The same number of age, sex, and places of residency matched apparently healthy individuals will be recruited purposively in a 1 to 1 ratio for the non-ACS comparative group. Therefore; the number of participants in this study will be 406. The level of statistical significance adopted is p <0.05. \r\n4.7	Data Collection Procedure\r\nAfter having a baseline data within 24 hours of ACS patient admission and checked for all inclusion/exclusion criteria patients will be classified in to sub-types of acute coronary syndrome as STEMI, NSTEMI, and UA to formulate sub sets of study groups. Then after patients will be on follow-up to evaluate the prognostic effect of hematologic parameters until discharge or deceased. A face to face interview will be conducted for ACS and non-ACS comparative group using structured questionnaire adopted from different literatures with imperative modification. \r\n\r\nProcedure\r\nEight data collectors (four nurse and four senior laboratory professional) and four supervisors will be recruited to collect and manage the data collection processes respectively. All study participants (their family member or care giver, in case if the patient is unable to communicate and give consent) will be informed about the purpose and the procedure undertaken through the data collection to enhance maximum participation and then asked for their willing to sign a written informed consent form to take part in the study. After obtaining informed written consent, the participants’ medical record will be assessed for the possibility of exclusion criteria; then the study subjects will be subjected to face-to-face interview and laboratory investigations.\r\nData will be collected through a face-to-face interviewing of study participants and retrieving patients’ medical records by the four trained nurse data collectors. The questionnaire comprises different parts that deals on socio-demographic characteristics, behavioural variables, ACS risk factors, health related factors, mortality risk assessment tools, and RAND-36 measure of health-related quality of life.\r\nLaboratory Analysis\r\nComplete blood counts and serum biochemical analysis will be performed by four well-trained laboratory professionals. Four ml of venous blood sample will be collected under aseptic conditions using disposable syringe then 2ml of it will emptied to ethylene diamine tetra acetate (EDTA) tube for complete blood count analysis and the remaining 2 ml will emptied in to vacuum tube(organ tube) for serum biochemical analysis. Blood sample will be collected in the morning starting from admission date to the date of discharge in every 120 hours interval. For the non-ACS comparative group, complete blood counts and serum biochemical analysis will be performed only once. All measurements will be performed 30 to 60 minutes after blood collection made.\r\nComplete blood counts comprising of haemoglobin, RBC, RDW,  NRBC, WBC, IG,WBC differential counts (neutrophil, lymphocyte, eosinophil, basophile, and monocyte) and percentages will be analysed using Uni-CelDxH 800 Coulter Cellular Analysis System. The mean platelet volume (MPV) and other haematological parameters, such as mean corpuscular volume (MCV), platelet distribution width (PDW), and plateletcrit (PCT) will also be measured. Neutrophil to lymphocyte ratio (NLR), platelet to lymphocyte ratio (PLR), MPVLR, WBC to MPV (WMR), and immature platelet fraction (IPF) will be calculated. The DxH 800 hematology analyzer, can utilize accurate data about individual cell size, shape and structure to provide very fast, high quality, and first-pass results with greater confidence. This heavy duty and advanced hematologic analyzer is accessible at JMC hematology laboratory.\r\nImportant serum biochemical measurements such as, high sensitive troponin I and T, BNP, NT-proBNP, CK-MB, CRP, Inl.6, TNFα, and fibrinogen will be measured using a Roche Cobas Integra 400 analyzer. The COBAS INTEGRA 400 plus analyzer is the perfect solution for laboratories running up to 400 samples per day. Its broad test menu comprises over 120 assays and applications that consolidate clinical chemistry with specific proteins, therapeutic drug monitoring and drug abuse testing. This compact tabletop analyzer offers maximum versatility to improve efficiency and reduce costs. It uses the convenient cobas c pack reagent format, which standardizes patient results across integrated laboratory networks. JMC clinical chemistry laboratory uses this machines for serum biochemical analysis.\r\n4.8	Variables	\r\n4.8.1	Dependent Variables\r\n	For specific objective #1\r\n	Hematological indices\r\n	For the rest specific objectives\r\n	Outcomes of ACS\r\n\r\n4.8.2	Independent Variables\r\nSocio-demographic characteristics, serum cardiac biomarkers, behavioural variables, established ACS risk factors, health related factors, mortality risk assessment tools, and RAND-36 measure of health-related quality of life and length of hospital stay.\r\n\r\n4.9	Operational Definition\r\n	Confirmed ACS case: refers to a patients with any group of clinical symptoms compatible with acute myocardial ischemia and includes UA, NSTEMI, and STEMI(40).\r\n	Non-ST-segment elevation myocardial infarction (NSTEMI), defined by elevated cardiac ischemic markers (CK-MB or troponin) with typical chest pain and/or ECG changes indicating myocardial ischemia; \r\n	ST-segment elevation myocardial infarction (STEMI), defined by typical chest pain lasting more than 20 minutes and associated with at least 1 of these characteristics: an increase greater than 1 ng/mL in troponin I and a new ST-segment elevation as measured from the J-point in 2 or more contiguous leads from leads V1, V2, and V3 measuring at least 0.2 mV or at least 0.1 mV in the remaining leads during the first 12 hours after symptom onset,\r\n	Unstable angina (UA), defined by typical chest pain and/or ECG changes indicating myocardial ischemia with negative cardiac ischemic markers.\r\n	Non-ACS comparative apparently healthy person: refers to a person without sign and symptoms of ACS and free from any chronic diseases listed in the exclusion criteria.\r\n	Serum biochemical measurements:  The troponin samples, as well as other biochemical measurements will measured using a Roche Cobas Integra 400 analyzer with a reference value of 0.014 μg / L.\r\n	A positive NRBC:  is defined as any value of NRBC above zero.\r\n	High MPV: MPV cut-off level is ≥10.4 fL\r\n	NLR: is calculated by dividing the neutrophil count with the lymphocyte count with, high cut-off level is ≥ 3.7.\r\n\r\n4.10	Data Analysis\r\nAfter checking for completeness of the data, it will be entered into Epi-data software version 3.1 and then will be transferred to SPSS version 25 for analysis. All appropriate statistical analysis will be carried out to address each specific objectives of this study. In general continuous variables will be express as mean ±SD or median and quartiles, and categorical variables present as absolute values and percent (%). Categorical variables will be compared using two-tailed Pearson\'s chi-squared (X2) test. The comparison of means, to establish the normality of the distribution, will be carry out using the Kolmogov-Smirnov test, followed by Student\'s t test for normal distribution variables or Mann-Whitney\'s non-parametric test for non-normal distribution variables. The relative morbidity and mortality risk will be calculated for clinical and laboratory variables, with confidence intervals of 95%. Logistic univariate regressions will be performed to evaluate predictive and prognostic predictors. A multivariate logistic regression model will be used to identify independent prognostic predictors. To quantify the discriminating ability of haematological markers; measures of diagnostic accuracy such as sensitivity, specificity, likelihood ratio, and Receiver Operating Characteristic (ROC) curve analysis will be carried outand the optimum cutoff of hematologic indices in predicting mortality. The area under curve with the ranges of 0.90 to 1, 0.80 to 0.90, 0.70 to 0.80, 0.60 to 0.70, and 0.50 to 0.60 is defined as excellent, good, fair, poor, and failing, respectively. Survival curves were made using the Kaplan-Meier analysis and compared via the log-rank test. All the variables whose p values were less than 0.25 in the univariate analysis were included in a multivariate Cox regression analysis. The backward stepwise likelihood ratio method will applied to identify the independent predictors of 30 days’ mortality. A 2-tailed p value of less than 0.05 will considered statistically significant(39).\r\n4.11	Data Quality Assurance\r\nData quality will be assured by proper designing of the questionnaire. A week before the actual data collection period, training will be given for both data collectors and supervisors; on data collection techniques and procedures, laboratory investigation and confidentiality of information. Pretest will be conducted on 5% of admitted ACS patients before the actual data collection period in order to get feedback and take the necessary corrective measures. The principal investigator will assume the supervisory role along with other supervisor throughout the data collection procedure. The data collection procedure will be checked frequently through supervision for its consistency on each day by the principal investigator and supervisors. Additionally, principal investigator will reviewing the collected data daily for its completeness. \r\nBlood sample quality will be ensured during pre-analytical, analytical, and post-analytical stages by following the standard operating procedures. In pre-analytical stage, appropriate volume of blood will be collected, labeled by a code, standard operating procedures (SOPs) and manufacturers’ instruction will strictly followed, and all laboratory reagents expiry date will be checked. Before running the samples daily start up procedure and daily calibration will be performed. Senior laboratory personnel will performed all the tests. The results of the investigation will be recorded in caution to avoid patient misidentification. For each laboratory investigation, the principal investigator will check report form daily, for completeness and confidentiality of the results recorded, and reported.','<h1><span style=\"font-size:16pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Cambria,&quot;serif&quot;\"><span style=\"color:#365f91\"><strong>Reference </strong></span></span></span></h1>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">1.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Grove EL, Hvas A-M, Kristensen SD. Immature platelets in patients with acute coronary syndromes. Thrombosis and haemostasis. 2009;101(01):151-3.</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">2.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Babes EE, Zaha DC, Tit DM, Nechifor AC, Bungau S, Andronie-Cioara FL, et al. Value of Hematological and Coagulation Parameters as Prognostic Factors in Acute Coronary Syndromes. Diagnostics. 2021;11(5):850.</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">3.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; D&rsquo;Adamo E, Guardamagna O, Chiarelli F, Bartuli A, Liccardo D, Ferrari F, et al. Atherogenic dyslipidemia and cardiovascular risk factors in obese children. International journal of endocrinology. 2015;2015.</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">4.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Nallamothu BK, Bradley EH, Krumholz HM. Time to treatment in primary percutaneous coronary intervention. New England Journal of Medicine. 2007;357(16):1631-8.</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">5.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Lippi G, Montagnana M, Salvagno GL, Guidi GC. Potential value for new diagnostic markers in the early recognition of acute coronary syndromes. Canadian Journal of Emergency Medicine. 2006;8(1):27-31.</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">6.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Dhingra R, Vasan RS. Biomarkers in cardiovascular disease: Statistical assessment and section on key novel heart failure biomarkers. Trends in cardiovascular medicine. 2017;27(2):123-33.</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">7.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Braunwald E. Biomarkers in heart failure. New England Journal of Medicine. 2008;358(20):2148-59.</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">8.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Goff DC, Lloyd-Jones DM, Bennett G, Coady S, D&rsquo;agostino RB, Gibbons R, et al. 2013 ACC/AHA guideline on the assessment of cardiovascular risk: a report of the American College of Cardiology/American Heart Association Task Force on Practice Guidelines. Journal of the American College of Cardiology. 2014;63(25 Part B):2935-59.</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">9.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Lippi G. Novel troponin immunoassay for early ACS rule-out. Nature Reviews Cardiology. 2016;13(1):9-10.</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">10.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; McNeal CJ, Dajani T, Wilson D, Cassidy-Bushrow AE, Dickerson JB, Ory M. Hypercholesterolemia in youth: opportunities and obstacles to prevent premature atherosclerotic cardiovascular disease. Current atherosclerosis reports. 2010;12(1):20-8.</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">11.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Monteiro J&uacute;nior JGdM, de Oliveira Cipriano Torres D. Hematological parameters as prognostic biomarkers in patients with cardiovascular diseases. Current Cardiology Reviews. 2019;15(4):274-82.</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">12.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Vis J, Huisman A. Verification and quality control of routine hematology analyzers. International journal of laboratory hematology. 2016;38:100-9.</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">13.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Uysal HB, Dağlı B, Akg&uuml;ll&uuml; C, Avcil M, Zencir C, Ayhan M, et al. Blood count parameters can predict the severity of coronary artery disease. The Korean journal of internal medicine. 2016;31(6):1093.</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">14.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Budzianowski J, Pieszko K, Burchardt P, Rzeźniczak J, Hiczkiewicz J. The role of hematological indices in patients with acute coronary syndrome. Disease Markers. 2017;2017.</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">15.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Monteiro J&uacute;nior JGdM, Torres DdOC, da Silva MCFC, Martins CMdH, da Silva IK, do Nascimento MEM, et al. Prognostic value of hematological parameters in patients with acute myocardial infarction: Intrahospital outcomes. PloS one. 2018;13(4):e0194897.</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">16.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Weiss G, Goodnough LT. Anemia of chronic disease. New England Journal of Medicine. 2005;352(10):1011-23.</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">17.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Kato H, Ishida J, Imagawa S, Saito T, Suzuki N, Matsuoka T, et al. Enhanced erythropoiesis mediated by activation of the renin‐angiotensin system via angiotensin II type 1a receptor. The FASEB journal. 2005;19(14):2023-5.</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">18.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Lippi G, Filippozzi L, Montagnana M, Salvagno GL, Franchini M, Guidi GC, et al. Clinical usefulness of measuring red blood cell distribution width on admission in patients with acute coronary syndromes. Clinical chemistry and laboratory medicine. 2009;47(3):353-7.</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">19.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Uyarel H, Ergelen M, Cicek G, Kaya MG, Ayhan E, Turkkan C, et al. Red cell distribution width as a novel prognostic marker in patients undergoing primary angioplasty for acute myocardial infarction. Coronary artery disease. 2011;22(3):138-44.</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">20.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Bhat T, Teli S, Rijal J, Bhat H, Raza M, Khoueiry G, et al. Neutrophil to lymphocyte ratio and cardiovascular diseases: a review. Expert review of cardiovascular therapy. 2013;11(1):55-9.</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">21.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Korniluk A, Koper-Lenkiewicz OM, Kamińska J, Kemona H, Dymicka-Piekarska V. Mean platelet volume (MPV): new perspectives for an old marker in the course and prognosis of inflammatory conditions. Mediators of inflammation. 2019;2019.</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">22.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Chu H, Chen W-L, Huang C-C, Chang H-Y, Kuo H-Y, Gau C-M, et al. Diagnostic performance of mean platelet volume for patients with acute coronary syndrome visiting an emergency department with acute chest pain: the Chinese scenario. Emergency Medicine Journal. 2011;28(7):569-74.</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">23.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Chu S, Becker R, Berger P, Bhatt D, Eikelboom J, Konkle B, et al. Mean platelet volume as a predictor of cardiovascular risk: a systematic review and meta‐analysis. Journal of Thrombosis and Haemostasis. 2010;8(1):148-56.</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">24.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Monteiro J&uacute;nior JGdM, Torres DdOC, da Silva MCFC, Ramos TMdB, Alves ML, Filho WJN, et al. Nucleated red blood cells as predictors of all-cause mortality in cardiac intensive care unit patients: a prospective cohort study. PLoS One. 2015;10(12):e0144259.</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">25.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Wang H, Naghavi M, Allen C, Barber R, Bhutta Z, Carter A. A systematic analysis for the Global Burden of Disease Study 2015. Lancet. 2016;388(10053):1459-544.</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">26.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Murray CJ, Barber RM, Foreman KJ, Ozgoren AA, Abd-Allah F, Abera SF, et al. Global, regional, and national disability-adjusted life years (DALYs) for 306 diseases and injuries and healthy life expectancy (HALE) for 188 countries, 1990&ndash;2013: quantifying the epidemiological transition. The Lancet. 2015;386(10009):2145-91.</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">27.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Nason E. An overview of cardiovascular disease and research. WR-467-RS. 2007.</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">28.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Gemechu T, Mahmoud H, Parry EH, Phillips DI, Yacoub MH. Community-based prevalence study of rheumatic heart disease in rural Ethiopia. European journal of preventive cardiology. 2017;24(7):717-23.</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">29.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Clark AM, Hartling L, Vandermeer B, McAlister FA. Meta-analysis: secondary prevention programs for patients with coronary artery disease. Annals of internal medicine. 2005;143(9):659-72.</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">30.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Organization WH. Noncommunicable diseases country profiles 2018. 2018.</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">31.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Angaw DA, Ali R, Tadele A, Shumet S. The prevalence of cardiovascular disease in Ethiopia: a systematic review and meta-analysis of institutional and community-based studies. BMC Cardiovascular Disorders. 2021;21(1):1-9.</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">32.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Bogale K, Mekonnen D, Nedi T, Woldu MA. Treatment Outcomes of Patients with Acute Coronary Syndrome Admitted to Tikur Anbessa Specialized Hospital, Addis Ababa, Ethiopia. Clinical Medicine Insights: Cardiology. 2019;13:1179546819839417.</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">33.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Hall&eacute;n J. Troponin for the estimation of infarct size: what have we learned? Cardiology. 2012;121(3):204-12.</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">34.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; James SK, Lindb&auml;ck J, Tilly J, Siegbahn A, Venge P, Armstrong P, et al. Troponin-T and N-terminal pro-B-type natriuretic peptide predict mortality benefit from coronary revascularization in acute coronary syndromes: a GUSTO-IV substudy. Journal of the American College of Cardiology. 2006;48(6):1146-54.</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">35.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Morrow DA, de Lemos JA, Sabatine MS, Murphy SA, Demopoulos LA, DiBattiste PM, et al. Evaluation of B-type natriuretic peptide for risk assessment in unstable angina/non&ndash;ST-elevation myocardial infarction: B-type natriuretic peptide and prognosis in TACTICS-TIMI 18. Journal of the American College of Cardiology. 2003;41(8):1264-72.</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">36.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Sabatine MS, Morrow DA, de Lemos JA, Gibson CM, Murphy SA, Rifai N, et al. Multimarker approach to risk stratification in non-ST elevation acute coronary syndromes: simultaneous assessment of troponin I, C-reactive protein, and B-type natriuretic peptide. Circulation. 2002;105(15):1760-3.</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">37.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Machlus KR, Italiano JE. The incredible journey: From megakaryocyte development to platelet formation. Journal of Cell Biology. 2013;201(6):785-96.</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">38.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Musher DM, Abers MS, Corrales-Medina VF. Acute infection and myocardial infarction. New England Journal of Medicine. 2019;380(2):171-6.</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">39.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Adam AM, Ali MA, Shah AA, Rizvi AH, Rehan A, Godil A, et al. Efficacy of hematological and coagulation parameters in the diagnosis and prognosis of patients with acute coronary syndrome. The Journal of Tehran University Heart Center. 2018;13(3):115.</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">40.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Kumar A, Cannon CP, editors. Acute coronary syndromes: diagnosis and management, part I. Mayo Clinic Proceedings; 2009: Elsevier.</span></span></span></span></p>','The aim of this study is to evaluate the role of hematological markers as potential diagnostic and prognostic indicator of all causes of mortality and morbidity during hospitalization of patients with acute coronary syndrome.',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,'Acute coronary syndrome, hematological indices, ACS prognosis, and mortality risk, Jimma medical Center.',5,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'Proposal-6290bfe3dcfae822972540.docx'),(119,137,14,24,'Abstract\r\nBackground:-Globally, more than 340 million children aged 5 to 19 years were overweight or obese in 2016 according to World Health Organization, 2020. As childhood overweight and obesity is likely to track into adulthood, more comprehensive and contextualized studies are paramount as there are few studies conducted in low and Middle income countries. Majority of the studies did not evaluate the effect on both proximal and distal out comes, the outcome measure in most studies were limited to body mass index, they did not include waist circumference and body composition as an outcome measure. And also there is no school based interventions focusing on double burden of malnutrition, body composition and growth of adolescents in Jimma zone Ethiopia. Hence, \r\nObjective of this study is to assess the effect of a school-based life style modification on double burden of malnutrition and body composition among adolescents’ in Jimma town, Ethiopia. A school-based randomized control trial study supported by qualitative findings will be conducted from July -September 2022 among school aged adolescents at Jimma secondary school. There will be 584 study participants (control and interventional group).  Half of these study participants will receive life style modification interventions through Behavioral change communication. Data will be collected by face to face interview using structured questionnaire, anthropometric measurement and skin pinch. Binary logistic regression will be used for data analysis for body composition while ordinal logistic regression will be used for analysis of outcome. \r\nTimeline and budget:-The study will be conducted within one year period with a total budget of 423,428.5ETB.','Double burden malnutrion','EFFECT OF A SCHOOL-BASED LIFE STYLE MODIFICATION ON DOUBLE BURDON MALNUTRITION AND BODY COMPOSTION AMONG ADOLENCENTS IN JIMMA TOWN, ETHIOPIA Randomized Controlled Trial','2022-05-28 18:08:30','1, Improved nutritional status through   life style modification intervention among adolescents’ in Jimma town, Ethiopia.\r\n2. Change in better dietary habit through   life style modification intervention among adolescents’ in Jimma town, Ethiopia\r\n3. Change in knowledge,attitude and practice of healthy life style behavior among adolescents’ in Jimma town, Ethiopia','39aeac4b49a25f09e318f562d24684ec','completed','Adolescence is a crucial time for lifelong nutrition, with rapid physical growth and transition between childhoods to adulthood (1). During this time, adolescents will achieve 15%-20% of their adult height, up to 60% of their skeletal mass, and half their adult body weight (2). It is also characterized by changes in body composition, behavioral changes in diet, and physical activity. There are around 1.2 billion adolescents (10 -19 years) in the world, of which 90 percent live in low and middle- income countries (3).It is expected that Sub-Saharan Africa is to have more adolescents than any other region by 2050 (4). In Ethiopia, the number of adolescents constitutes quarter of the total population (5).\r\nOptimal nutrition is important for both the current and future wellbeing of adolescents (6). A healthy diet has also significant effect on adolescents in maintaining weight, improving physical performance and, optimizing growth. However, most of the healthy diets of adolescents are replaced by modern and unhealthy diet, which contain greater amounts of cheap, energy dense, nutrient-poor foods that are high in sugar, sodium and fat (including processed and ultra processed foods) (7). In addition, sugar-sweetened soft drinks (fizzy drinks) are the main source of free sugars in children and adolescents’ diets (8). The effect of dietary transition is raising overweight and obesity, but modern diets have also implication in underweight.\r\nGlobally, more than 340 million children aged 5 to 19 years were overweight or obese in 2016. As childhood overweight and obesity is likely to track into adulthood (8).\r\nAs childhood overweight and obesity is likely to continue into adulthood (9) excessive weight gain during childhood can substantially increase the risk of developing other nutrition related non-communicable diseases in later years (9). Unhealthy lifestyle behaviors, including unhealthy diet and inadequate physical activity, are the primary modifiable factors of excessive weight gain (10).\r\nMany developing countries, nutritional and epidemiological transitions are leading to increase over\r\nnutrition, which, together with prevalent under nutrition, is resulting in the double burden of malnutrition  (DBM) among adolescents (9). The overall prevalence of stunting, thinness, overweight and obesity in adolescents in LMICs was 10%, 6% and 21%, respectively (10). Recent studies in Ethiopia also revealed the coexistence of double burden of malnutrition among in-school adolescents (11)(12).\r\nIt is important to; highlight the need for early intervention taking both aspects into account to fill the identified gap. Addressing the DBM can be considered as “dual nutrition challenge” or an opportunity for “double returns”. However, Interventions that have the potentials to simultaneously reduce the risk burden of both under nutrition and overweight can be given at school level by encouraging healthy diet and life style modification which will be, cost effective and successful. An intervention on life style modification habits of adolescents has profound impact on current and future health of adolescents. This will provide adolescence as the second window of opportunity for growth and development in the lifecycle. It also offers a unique chance to address nutritional problems and develop healthy and long lasting dietary and lifestyle practices. A sustainable healthy diet and healthy eating practices during adolescence have the potential to limit any nutritional deficits and linear-growth faltering accrued during the first decade of life, and may limit harmful behaviors contributing to the epidemic of diet related non communicable diseases in adulthood. In addition, it will bring better health for adolescents ,improved well-being and productivity for their future adult life and reduced health risk for their children(13).This provides for a high return on investment on nutritional interventions done in early childhood in turns of reaping high demographic dividend. Since schools are ideal places to work with children and adolescents, they are the best place for health promotion and lifestyle modification and for implementing favorable environmental changes through the availability of healthy foods and physical exercise (14). \r\nHowever, there was a lack of literatureon obesity management and prevention from LMICs, despite the well documented rise of obesity in the region. From few studies conducted in LMICs, majority of them were low methodologicalquality, several studies did not evaluate the effect on both proximal and distal out comes, the outcomemeasure in most studies were limited to BMI they did not include waist circumference and bodycomposition as an outcome measure. In addition most studies were focused on nutrition and physical activity, but this study includes variables like modifiable risk factors (17) (18) (19). \r\nA systematic review targeting school based interventions on Double burden of malnutrition suggest, although multi-component school-based nutrition interventions are promising, there are several gaps in the available evidence. First, most of studies comes from high-income counties has limited generalizability, they also focused on general health and wellbeing, healthy eating, and physical activity and lacked attention to multiple forms of malnutrition. In addition they did not focus only on adolescents and included child-adolescent together (15). From literature review done on DBM also revealed that there is a scarcity of studies reporting interventions simultaneously tackling under nutrition and overweight and obesity (DBM) (16). Unfortunately, there was no school based interventions focusing on DBM, Body composition and growth of adolescents in Jimma zone Ethiopia.\r\n\r\nIt will be necessary to have this study, which contribute to identify adolescents at risk of both overweight and underweight during base line, determine predictors related to diet, lifestyle ,nutrition knowledge,attitude towards nutrition and socio-demographic factors which influence improper body composition. It also provides interventions for preventive care to promote normal growth and development of school adolescent population by early shaping adequate dietary and healthy life style behavior.','3.Methods and materials\r\n3.1 Study area and period\r\n\r\nThe study will be conducted at secondary school adolescents in jimma town, Ethiopia, among school aged adolescents. There are primary schools and 4 high schools in the town. The study will be conducted from July15- 2022 to September 2022\r\n3.2 Study design \r\nA school-based randomized control trial study design will be used in selected  secondary school adolescents at Jimma town.\r\n3.3 Source Population\r\n Secondary school students aged 14-19 years in Jimma, town.\r\n3.4 Study Population\r\nAll selected adolescents aged 14-19 years from randomly selected secondary schools\r\n3.5 Sample Size Determination \r\nThe sample size was calculated using GPOWER 3.0 for the difference between two independent means with the following assumptions:\r\nt tests – For Difference between two independent means (two groups)\r\nAnalysis: A priori: Compute required sample size\r\nInput: Tail(s) = One\r\nEffect size d = 0.3\r\nα err prob = 0.05\r\nPower (1-β err prob) = 0.8\r\nAllocation ratio N2/N1 = 1\r\nOutput: Noncentrality parameter δ = 2.5009998\r\nCritical t = 1.6503932\r\nDf = 276\r\nSample size group 1 = 139\r\nSample size group 2 = 139\r\nTotal sample size = 278\r\nActual power = 0.8023399\r\nConsidering the design effect and 5%non response rate total sample size become 584\r\n3.6. Sampling Technique and Procedure\r\nMulti-stage stratified random sampling technique will be used to select the sample. In the first stage, simple random sampling procedure will used to select the schools. The selected schools will be randomly assigned to the two study arms (intervention & control groups).Similarly, at the second stage, simple random sampling will be employed to select classes from each grade and students from each class for both interventional and control arms (figure1).Selected students from each classes who fulfill illegibility criteria will be invited to participate in the study.\r\n \r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n3.7. Intervention/implementation procedure\r\nThe interventional group will receive life style modification interventions through BCC on healthy dietary habits, physical activity and risky behavior (substance use, cigarette smoking, Khat chewing, and Alcohol use) will be given for students and their family. While the control group will not receive any interventions unless the routine care. The school adolescents on the intervention group will be followed for 6 months starting from recruitment period. \r\nQualitative study will be used to explore and gain deeper understanding of the awareness, perceptions and attitudes towards, healthy dietary habits physical activity and risky behavior (substance use, cigarette smoking, Khat chewing, and Alcohol use).\r\n\r\n3.7. Eligibility criteria\r\n3.7.1 Inclusion Criteria\r\nPrimary and secondary school children aged 14-19 years, who will not leave the schools during the intervention period, will be included.\r\n3.7.2 Exclusion Criteria\r\nSchool children with obvious congenital, Physical disability including deformity (Kyphosis, Scoliosis), impaired feeding, edema, and severe illness or clinical complications will be excluded.\r\n3.8 Study variables\r\n•	Dependent Variables\r\n	Nutritional status, body composition\r\n•	Independent Variables\r\n	Social demographic variables such as sex, age, level of education/ grade \r\n	Anthropometric measurements like Height, weight, waist circumference, MUAC, skin fold thickness.\r\n	Dietary intake\r\n	Physical activity \r\n	Unhealthy life style behaviors( alcohol intake, cigarette smoking, khat chewing and substance abuse).\r\n3.9 Operational definitions and definition of terms\r\n	BMI The body mass index (BMI) BMI calculation divides an adult\'s weight in kilograms by their height in meters squared.\r\n	Unhealthy lifestyle: - living a sedentary lifestyle and not getting enough physical exercise. Eating an unhealthy diet that\'s high in fat proteins, Tran’s fats, sugary foods, and sodium. Smoking. Excessive drinking.\r\n	Healthy lifestyle: This means eating a balanced diet, getting regular exercise, avoiding tobacco and drugs and getting plenty of rest. \r\n	Physical activity \r\n–	Inactive\'(mostly sitting, or walking and cycling less than two hours per week), \r\n–	‘Walking/cycling\' (walking and cycling at least two hours per week or more, no exercise), \r\n–	\'Exercise\' (30 minutes per week or more), and\' \r\n–	More exercise\' (90 minutes per week or more) Double burden of malnutrition.\r\n	Over nutrition: - Over nutrition is defined as the overconsumption of nutrients and food to the point at which health is adversely affected .reflected by overweight and obesity. BMI >18.5kg/m2\r\n	Under nutrition:- defined as the body mass index less than 18.5kg/m2\r\n3.10 Data Collection Tool and Procedure\r\nQuantitative data collection tools will be developed after reviewing various relevant literature and the Guidelines. Data will be collected by face to face interview of the study participants. For the qualitative study part, a semi-structured guide will be developed considering the research questions or objectives. Data will be collected by trained and experienced health professionals.\r\nAnthropometric variables included body weight, height and waist circumference (WC). Measurements will be performed in the morning by trained researchers using standardized procedures. Body weight will bemeasured to the nearest 100 g, with minimal clothing and without shoes, using a calibrated portable scale. \r\nHeight will be measured to the nearest cm with the subject in the full standing position without shoes using calibrated portable measuring rod. Body mass index (BMI) will be calculated as body weight in kg divided by height squared in meters. The International Obesity Task Force (IOTF) age- and sex-specific BMI reference values  used to define overweight and obesity in adolescents aged 14–17 years [33]. For participants aged ≥18 years, we used the cut-off points for adults (overweight, 25–29.9 kg/m2; obesity ≥30 kg/m2). WC will be measured horizontally at navel level and at the end of gentle expiration to the nearest to 0.1 cm using a non-stretchable measuring tape .Waist hip ratio (WHR) will be calculated as the ratio between WC in cm and hip in cm. Body fat mass, fat free mass Body fat mass, fat free mass and percentage of body fat (% body fat) were evaluated using skin fold thickness.\r\n3.11 Data analysis plan\r\nData will be checked for accuracy and entered into Epi-data 4.6before being exported to SPSS version 25for analysis. Participants\' socio-demography and other variables will be presented using descriptive statistics such as mean, percentage, and standard deviation. Bivariate analysis will be done to check a relationship between independent variables and the outcome variables with a p-value of less than 0.25 will be regarded for multivariate logistic regression. Generalized Estimating Equation will be used to see the effect of life style modification on double  burden malnutrition.  Finally, for independent predictors of the outcome variables, we will apply multivariate logistic regression, with a p-value of 0.05 considered statistically significant. The qualitative data will be analyzed using the atlas ti.7.1.A thematic analysis approach through which the codes, categories, and themes can be developed from the data will be employed. The data will be transcribed verbatim, translated to English; the language through which the analysis will be carried out. Reading and re-reading of the data will be carried out to understand and extract the important concepts from the data, and line-byline coding will be done. The codes will be merged to develop the categories, and the categories will be in turn merged to form themes .Then, the findings will be presented with themes, categories, and quotations from the data.\r\n3.12 Data quality management\r\nData quality management to ensure uniformity, the questionnaire will be translated from English to Afan Oromo and Amharic, and then back to English to check consistency. Data collectors and supervisors will get two days of training on the study\'s purpose and data collection processes. Prior to data collection, 5% of the total sample will be pretested to validate the data collecting tool\'s clarity and integrity. Grammar, sequence, and timing modifications and changes will be made based on the results of pretests before the actual data collection began. The data collected will be checked daily for integrity, accuracy, clarity and consistency. The trustworthiness of the qualitative findings will be ensured through different  techniques. First, a semi structured questionnaire will be developed concerning the research questions. Second, a diversified group of individuals (from each selected grades) will be recruited from different (different schools).Third, peer debriefing will be done daily. For anthropometric measurements all standard pre questions will be performed.','<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\"><span style=\"color:black\">1. Keeley B, Chief E, Little C, Vrolijk K, Analyst D, Wauchope S, et al. Children , food and nutrition. 2019.<br />\r\n2. Spear BA. adolescent growth and development [Internet]. PubMed; Available from: April 2002Journal of<br />\r\nthe American Dietetic Association 102(3 Suppl):S23-9<br />\r\n3. Report WY. World youth report [Internet]. NEW YORK, 2018 UNITED; Available from:<br />\r\nwww.un.org/development/desa/youth<br />\r\n4. Progress for Children [Internet].A report c. United Nations Children&rsquo;s Fund (UNICEF); 2012. Available<br />\r\nfrom: &lt;www.childinfo.org&gt;.<br />\r\n5. Federal Democratic Republic of Ethiopia Popula- tion Census Commission. . [Internet]. Available from:<br />\r\nhttp://www.csa .gov.et/<br />\r\n6. GUIDELINE: Implementing effective actions for improving ADOLESCENT NUTRITION [Internet].<br />\r\nAvailable from: ISBN 978-92-4-151370-8<br />\r\n7. Roberto CA. Public health policies to encourage healthy eating habits : recent perspectives. 2015;81&ndash;90.<br />\r\n8. Health B. Soda Consumption Among Adolescents : Implications for Low- and Middle-Income Countries.<br />\r\n2017;107(7):1025&ndash;7.<br />\r\n9. Reality CN. HHS Public Access.Dyn Double Burd Malnutrition Chang Nutr Real. 2020;395(10217):65&ndash;74.<br />\r\n10. Caleyachetty R, Thomas GN, Kengne AP, Echouffo-tcheugui JB, Schilsky S. The double burden of<br />\r\nmalnutrition among adolescents : analysis of data from the Global School-Based Student Health and Health<br />\r\nBehavior in School-Aged Children surveys in 57 low- and middle-income countries. 2018;414&ndash;24.<br />\r\nAvailable from: Am J ClinNutr 2018;108:414?424<br />\r\n11. Berbada DA, Haidar J, Gebremichael G, Haftu D. Magnitude of double burden of malnutrition and its<br />\r\nassociated factors among selected in-school adolescents : Evidence from South Ethiopia. 2017;9(May):30&ndash;7.<br />\r\n12. Hassen K, Gizaw G. Dual Burden of Malnutrition Among Adolescents of Smallholder Coffee Farming<br />\r\nHouseholds of Jimma Zone , Southwest Ethiopia. 2017;38(2):196&ndash;208.<br />\r\n13. GUIDELINE: Implementing effective actions for improving ADOLESCENT NUTRITION [Internet].<br />\r\nAvailable from: ; https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/3.0/igo)<br />\r\n14. Paper D. Schools as a System to Improve Nutrition.2017;(September).<br />\r\n15. Shinde S, Wang D. School-Based Interventions Targeting Double Burden of Malnutrition and Educational<br />\r\nOutcomes of Adolescents in Low- and Middle-Income Countries : Protocol for a Systematic Review. :1&ndash;17.<br />\r\n16. Pe&ntilde;alvo SM 1 and JL. Actions Targeting the Double Burden of Malnutrition : A Scoping Review. :1&ndash;14.<br />\r\nAvailable from: https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/31892187/<br />\r\n17. Chawla N, Panza A, Sirikulchayanonta C, Kumar R, Taneepanichskul S. ORIGINAL ARTICLE<br />\r\neffectiveness of a school-based multicomponent intervention onnutritional status among primary school children in bangkok , thailand.<br />\r\n2017;29(1):13&ndash;20. Available from: http://www.jamc.ayubmed.edu.pk<br />\r\n18. Nutrition A, Quality D, Gutkowska K. Effect of an Education Program on Nutrition and Methodology.<br />\r\n19. Verstraeten R, Roberfroid D, Lachat C, Leroy JL, Holdsworth M, Maes L, et al. Effectiveness of preventive<br />\r\nschool-based obesity interventions in low- and middle-income countries : A systematic review Effectiveness<br />\r\nof preventive school-based obesity interventions in low- and middle-income countries : a systematic review<br />\r\n1 &ndash; 3. 2012;(July).</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">20. <span style=\"color:black\">N, Bellocco R, Bottai M, et al. Reproducibility of the past year and historical self-administered total physical activity questionnaire among older women. Eur J Epidemiol2007;22:363-8.</span>].</span></span></span></p>','To assess the effect of a school-based life style modification on double burden of malnutrition and body composition among adolescents’ in Jimma town, Ethiopia',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,'Double burden malnutrition, physical actity, dieatery habits, Ethiophia',10,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'Proposal-62923aeed963d231203222.docx'),(120,172,13,12,'Executive summary\r\nThe need for palliative care now a day is on exponentiation as a result of increased prevalence of non-communicable chronic illness including cancer. Palliative care is centered on the relief of symptoms and stress reduction on account of the disease, thereby improving quality of life for both the patient as well as the family. It begins from the point of diagnosis and continues through the course of the disease until cure or death and in to family bereavement. Currently, the World Health Organization, Ethiopian ministry of Health, and other organization such as Hospice Ethiopia are recommending home-based palliative care as best model for best way of palliative care delivery method. Thus, this study will be aimed to determine the feasibility of establishing Home-based palliative care model through giving training for health professionals in the community, Jimma, Ethiopia, 2022. Both quantitative and qualitative methods will be used to undertake this study. For the quantitative part, all category of health care professionals including health extension workers, nurses, midwives, medical doctors, and other health workers who work in Jimma town public health facilities (17 health posts, 4 health centers, 1 general hospital, and 1 teaching hospital) will be included in the study. This mean that a total of 314 health care professionals will be selected to be included in this study by stratified sampling technique. For the qualitative part, the study participants will be selected purposively until the data saturation meet. Data will be collected both before and after providing training. Data will be entered by Epi-data 4.6 and analyzed by SPSS version 23. Pre-experimental pre and posttest will be used to see the effectiveness of the training program. The qualitative part will be analyzed thematically by using Atlas.ti 8.1 software. To undertake this study, a total of 541,466.20 ETB is required.',NULL,'Determining the feasibility of establishing Home -Based Palliative Care model through giving training program for health professionals of public health facilities in Jimma town, Ethiopia, 2022: A mixed study design','2022-05-27 20:06:39','The baseline knowledge, perception, and practice of health care providers regarding the provision of home-based palliative care services in the community will be revealed. The knowledge, perception, and practice of health professionals regarding the provision of home-based palliative care services in the community, after the training, will be revealed. The training program to improve knowledge, practice, and perception of health professionals for providing home-based palliative care services in the community can be evaluated. The facilitators and   barriers   in providing home-based palliative care by health professionals in the community will be identified. The status of the existing palliative care services at Jimma Medical Center will be useful to develop a home-based palliative care Model. And the home-based palliative care model will be implemented in Jimma town.','e242ba9a52fca28d30458b068adfd371','completed','1.    Background and Rationale\r\n\r\n1.1	Background\r\nBackground\r\nPalliative care is a kind of medical care provided for people living with a terminal illness, such as cancer, dementia, or heart failure. The care is centered on the relief of symptoms and stress reduction on account of the disease, thereby improving quality of life for both the patient as well as the family (1). It begins from the point of diagnosis and continues through the course of the disease until cure or death and in to family bereavement (2). \r\nAlthough both palliative care and hospice provide comfort, palliative care is aimed at assisting patients and their loved ones transit from treatment meant to cure or control the disease to hospice care. It can be given at any point during the cancer care, from diagnosis to the end of life.  Hospice care has no curative intent, but focuses on the comfort and quality of life of a seriously ill individual who is approaching the end of life (2,3).\r\nPalliative care gives patients the opportunity for pain and symptom management and offers a better quality of life for patient by improving physical, psychological, social (financial, legal, employment) or spiritual aspects and that of the family by addressing caregiver needs (4). Most often palliative care is provided in the hospital. But it can be offered in other settings too, including assisted living facilities, nursing homes, outpatient clinics, and hospices (5), by specialized multidisciplinary team comprising physicians, nurses, support workers, paramedics, pharmacists, physiotherapists and volunteers (3). \r\nThe increasing ageing population and the rising burden of non-communicable diseases and some communicable diseases	 in the world have escalated the demand for palliative care. Approximately 40 million people are in need of palliative care every year. Only 14% of them, mostly restricted to high-income countries, have access to it, even though 78% of the world\'s population in need of palliative care belong to low- and middle-income countries. It is accepted that early provision of palliative care reduces unnecessary hospitalization and use of medical services. But, in 2018, the International Narcotics Control Board found out only 14% of morphine was used by 79% of the world’s populace, basically from low –middle income nations, for the management of pain and suffering (6).\r\nPalliative care is a human right. In spite of the growing burden of cancer in Ethiopia, palliative care needs of the people remains largely unmet (7,8), because the palliative programs focus on treatment of symptoms, with limited psychosocial, emotional, spiritual and economic support (8). This results in psychosocial distress and financial crisis (7).\r\nRationale: It is estimated that there are more than 150,000 people diagnosed with cancer each year in Ethiopia (9). As the world population ages and prevalence of cancer and other non-communicable diseases including life-threatening diseases continue to rise in low- and middle-income countries, the global, regional, and national needs for palliative care are increasing.\r\nWorld Health Organization target for Sustainable Development Goal 3 (2016-2030) is to develop palliative care policies for 40 million people.  The slogan for the Program is ‘Leave no one behind. With just nine years left to achieve these goals, all countries are targeting to use technology, resources, and tools. However, only 14% of the population in the world is receiving palliative care (9.)\r\nMany study showed that integrating palliative care early in the disease trajectory can result not only in a good control of such symptoms and better quality of life for those patients and their families but also in their illness perception, goals of care discussion, acceptance of advanced care planning, and overall survival (9,10).\r\nGlobally, in 2017, over 29 million (29,063,194) people died from diseases requiring palliative care. The estimated number of people in need of palliative care at the end of life was 20.4 million. The biggest proportion, 94%, corresponds to adults of which 69% were over 60 years old and 25% were 15 to 59 years old. Only 14% of all people in need of palliative care were received it. The great majority of adults in need of palliative care died from cardiovascular diseases (38.5%), and cancer (34%), followed by chronic respiratory diseases (10.3%), HIV/AIDS (5.7%), diabetes (4.5%) and drug-resistant TB (0.8%) (11). \r\nThe main focus of palliative care provision in Africa is through home based and community-based models. The  model should encompasses both community and hospital based care, besides the growing  development palliative care in sub-Saharan Africa is designed and structured in different ways but all can be tied by public health approach and had similar practices (12). \r\nDue to limited development of PC across Africa many patients have not received formal palliative care services (12). These challenges are exacerbated by poor health and social care infrastructures as well as limited health financing in many African countries. In addition, there is lack of understanding of what PC is and what its benefits are (13,).\r\nBesides most of the African governments have no awareness on importance and integration of palliative care to health care system and give the priority for curative services. Lack palliative care decentralization from the central hospitals to the community or patient home in which the key services were given at hospital levels (14).\r\nEthiopia is attempting to move forward in its policy development process to establish palliative care. However, most people who are suffering from cancers, human immune deficient virus / acquired immune deficient syndrome (HIV/AIDS), and other chronic illnesses are often diagnosed late stage. This creates a huge burden of suffering for the Ethiopian people with extremely limited access to pain medications and other Palliative care interventions(154).of the important factors influencing a successful delivery of palliative care, one is the health care professionals’ knowledge, perception and practice in addressing physical, psychosocial, and spiritual needs (15,16). \r\nThis research will try to answer the question what is the knowledge, perception and practice of health professionals to wards palliative care services in Jimma town in order to establish home based palliative care model.\r\nSignificance of the Project: \r\nIn sub-Saharan Africa, providing palliative care training for health professionals at district may increase access of palliative care and patients can prefer the site of palliative care provisions. However, lack of support from professionals and extended families causes the patients and care to prefer institutional care than home based care. Despite, study showed that home based care programs are an excellent way of delivering holistic care if families, volunteers are taught how to give basic care and get support from health professionals.\r\nThis research is based on the fact that there is an unmet need of palliative care services in the country. In order to fill these gaps, health care professionals need to have the knowledge, good perception and practice of delivering palliative care for those in need of it and  for integrating the palliative care services in to primary health care system in order to deliver home-based palliative care services tothe patients and their families.\r\nThus, to establish home based palliative care model, it is desirable to assess the knowledge, perception and practice regarding palliative care services among health professionals who are working in public health facilities in Jimma town. Besides, the facilitators and barriers need to be explored to find out the existing resources and challenges faced by health professionals in this regard. This study will help in devising appropriate strategies to improve the knowledge, perception and practice of healthcare professionals regarding home- based palliative care delivery model in study area. The study can be used as a baseline data for other similar studies and also to policy makers and health care planners to expand their understanding of current practice and inform future policy development.','4. Methods\r\n4.1 Study setting and period\r\nThis study will be conducted among health professionals working in public health facilities of Jimma town. Thus, the public health facilities of Jimma town to be considered for this study will be the health posts, health centers, and hospitals hosted by the government and found in the town. In Jimma town, there are 17 health posts with 59 health extension workers; 4 Health centers with total of 204 health professionals where each health center had 46 -56 health professionals; 1 General hospital with 220 health professionals; and 1 teaching hospital with 1246 health professionals. The study will be conducted from August 2022 to December 30/ 2022. \r\n4.2 Study design\r\n A mixed method research design, which includes both quantitative and qualitative research (Triangulation design), will be used to evaluate the feasibility of establishing Home -Based Palliative Care model by providing five days training program for health professionals in the public health facilities in Jimma town. A pre-experimental, One-group pretest-posttest design will be used to evaluate the effectiveness of the training program on the health professionals’ knowledge, perception, and practice of providing home-based palliative care services. The qualitative research will be conducted for in-depth exploration into the facilitators and barriers   in providing home-based palliative care by health professionals in the community. \r\n4.3 Population\r\n4.3.1 Source population\r\nAll the health professionals working in the Public health facilities of Jimma town.\r\n4.3.2 Study population\r\nThe study population will be the randomly selected health professionals of Jimma  town public health facilities. \r\n4.3.3 Inclusion criteria\r\nHealth professionals who had at least six months work experience in the health facilities will be included.\r\n4.3.4 Exclusion criteria\r\nThe health professionals who may have severe illness and not present during the study period will be excluded.  \r\n	\r\n4.4 Sample size determination and sampling technique\r\nFor the pre-experimental, One-group pretest-posttest design, the sample size is determined by using single population proportion formula (n=zα/22*p(1-p/d2)  with the assumption of  ‘zα/2’ a critical value on normal distribution curve at 95% confidence interval, thus 1.96; ‘p’ proportion of feasibility of Home-Based palliative care provision taken as 50% because of lack of relevant previous study; and ‘d’ a margin of error taken as 5%. Thus, the sample size n=1.962*0.5(1-0.5)/0.052 resulting in 384. Since our source population is only 1729, which is less than 10,000 we can use correction formula to gain representative sample size for our source population. Thus, nf=N*n/N+n; nf= 1729*384/1729+384; nf=663986/2113; nf=314. Finally, the sample size will be proportionally allocated for each health facility followed by stratified sampling technique to select the study participants from each category of the health profession as represented below (Figure 2).\r\nFor the qualitative study from each category of health profession in each health facility, the participants will be purposively selected for in-depth exploration of facilitators and barriers in providing home-based palliative care by health professionals in the community until data saturation meet.\r\n\r\n\r\n	\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nFigure 2. Schematic representation of sampling procedure of health professionals of Jimma town health facilities for a study to be conducted on feasibility of Home-Based palliative care, 2022. \r\n4.5 Data collection tools and data collection procedures\r\nFor the quantitative part of this study, structured self-administered questionnaire developed after reviewing relevant related literatures will be used for the data collection. For the qualitative part of this study, in-depth interview guide will be used for the data collection.  For the quantitative part, data will be collected both before starting the training as pre-test and after finishing the training as post-test. For the qualitative section, data will be collected after the training will be provided for the study participants.  The training on Home-based palliative care will be provided for the health professionals of Jimma town public health facilities for five days. Thus, since the total sample size is 314, the training will be provided by 12 rounds training 26/27 participants on each round.  \r\n\r\nThe data collectors will be recruited for the quantitative part based on merit of having previous data collection experience, and at least qualified to the level of first degree in health science field. Also, there will be supervisors of the data collection procedure to present any necessary facilitation of the data collection procedure. The data collectors will be given two days training on the objectives of the study, data collection tool, approaches of the data collection and how to intervene if they phased challenges in the process of the data collection. \r\n\r\nQualitative method will be used to gather in-depth views of participants pertaining to the facilitators and barriers/challenges faced by the health professionals in providing home –based palliation as it exists currently and their richer insights into establishing home-based palliative care model.   \r\n A semi-structured interview guide comprising open-ended questions probing questions that will be developed and validated by experts prior to data collection, will be used in this study. \r\nInterviews will be conducted until saturation is reached. Data will be collected through semi-structured interviews and audio-recorded with prior consent from the participants. .Data collection and analysis will be aimed at the following :\r\n1)	Perception of  delivery of home-based palliative care services\r\n2)	 Experience sharing on rendering palliative care at home.\r\n3)	The factors that facilitate the establishment of home based palliative care service\r\n4)	Factors that hinder the establishment of home based palliative care service\r\n\r\nThe data collection method for the qualitative method will be in-depth interview will conducted to explore the views of the participants and probing question will be used. During data collection voice recorders and field-notes will be used to capture the information obtained from the interview. The interview will take 35- 60 minutes. Two assistant interviewers having BSC degree and MSC degree & having an experience on qualitative data collection will be selected. The principal investigator will give the training course prior to data collection. The session of the training will include purpose and objective of the study, meaning of each question and techniques of interview. As well as the role and responsibility of assistant interviewer will be described.\r\nFor both the quantitative and the qualitative part, English version questionnaire and in-depth interview guide will be used since both the data collectors and the study participants are considered to understand English as they are all passed through educational program provided in English starting from Grade 9 to University level.\r\n4.6 Study variables\r\nDependent variable\r\n The health professionals’ Knowledge, Perception, and Practice of providing home-based palliative care services.\r\nIndependent variables:\r\nSocio-demographic characteristics of health professionals:	Age, Sex, Marital status,Religion, Ethnicity,Professional type, Profession qualification, Work experience, Monthly income.\r\nPersonal Factors of health professionals:-Knowledge  on palliative care, Perception towards palliative care, Practice of Palliative care\r\nOrganisational factors: -Type of Health facilty,Palliative care  service,Palliative Care training,Policies ( guidelines & protocol, medication availability) on palliative care,Budget  allocated,Palliative care focal person, Multidisciplinary team & services, Ambulance.\r\n4.7 Operational definition\r\nHome-based palliative care:   Provision of palliative care service at the patient’s home which includes care for patient and family.\r\n Feasibility of  establishing Home -Based Palliative Care model : In this study feasibility  refers to the gain in Knowledge , improved perception and  practice of the health professionals   and the facilitators and barriers  in establishing  Home -Based Palliative Care model\r\nKnowledge of home -based palliative care service :  will be measured by correct responses of health professionals to items on a self-administered questionnaire on information and awareness of home -based palliative care service. Knowledge will be expressed in scores and categorized as ‘good knowledge’ for above mean scores; ‘poor knowledge’ for score below the mean.\r\nPerception of home-based palliative care service: will be measured by the responses on the 5 –points likert scale for items on attitude, opinions , beliefs about providing home-based palliative care . The sum of the scores will be categorized as ‘favourable perception’ ( above mean scores) and ‘unfavourable perception’ (below mean scores).\r\nPractice of home -based palliative care service: will be measured  by  responses on a questionnaire  to assess the  awareness on practice of home-based palliative care, expressed in scores and categorized as ‘good practice’  for above mean scores; ‘poor practice’ for scores below the mean.\r\nFacilitators of home -based palliative care service: refers to factors that promote establishment of home-based palliative care \r\nBarriers of home -based palliative care service: refers to factors that hinder the establishment of home-based palliative care.\r\n4.8	Data management and statistical analysis\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nFor the quantitative part, pre-test and post-test data will be checked for completeness, coded and entered into Epi-data software version 4.6 and will be exported to Statistical package for Social Science (SPSS) version 23 for analysis. Descriptive analysis will be computed. Testing of hypothesis will be done by analyzing difference in pre and post-test average score using paired sample t-test with statistical significance at P-value < 0.05. Eta squared effect size statistics will be used to quantify the magnitude of the intervention. \r\nFor the qualitative data analysis, before the interview, the bracketing will be done to avoid the preconceived ideas of the data collector from creeping in to the research data.\r\n Braun and Clarke approach for thematic analysis with deductive approach will be done to identify semantic themes, in the data.\r\nOn completion of interviewing the participants of each day audio recordings will be listened carefully; then data will be transcribed word-by-word into plain text\r\nThe following steps will be followed for thematic analysis:\r\n1.	Familiarization:  After transcribing the audio by verbatim, verifying with the respondent, the transcribed text will be read through to get familiar with it. \r\n2.	Coding: By highlighting sections of the text and labeling to describe the content.\r\n3.	Combining similar codes to create families.\r\n4.	Similar families will become themes.\r\n5.	Generating themes: \r\n6.	Finally the themes will be analyzed and described with presentation of selected representative data extracts.\r\n\r\nData will be analyzed using Atlas.ti 8.1 software. Finally the data from the quantitative method and the qualitative method will be triangulated.\r\n  \r\n4.9 Data Quality assurance \r\nTo ensure quality of data, the questionnaire will be appropriately designed after review of related literatures.  Filled questionnaires will be checked for completeness and questionnaires with incomplete part will be excluded from analysis. During data entry, Epi-data version 4.6 will be used to minimize error. And SPSS software will be used for analysis. \r\nFor Qualitative data: The trustworthiness of the qualitative research will be guided by the following criteria: Credibility, Dependability, conformability, and transferability. Member checking will be done by sharing the data (transcripts), interpretations, and conclusions with the research participants for clarification of interpretations, conclusions and providing additional information. A peer researcher other than data collector and data analyst will perform an external audit for the purpose of examining the data collection processes, data analysis regarding a clear description of coding tree, and examining the results of the research study to confirm the accuracy of the findings and ensuring that the findings are supported by the data that will be collected.  Finally the results will be used as baseline for other studies.\r\nTriangulation method will be used in this study to supplement qualitative findings with quantitative data collection methods for checking the consistency of the findings.  \r\nThe COREQ checklist will be used as a guide to report important aspects of the research team, study methods, context of the study, findings, analysis and interpretation (17).\r\n4.10	Plan for dissemination and ensuring utilization of findings\r\nThe findings of this project will be disseminated to office of Jimma University Health Research and Postgraduate Director, to Jimma Zone Health Department, and to administrator of each public health facilities to enable them well informed on the finding and took necessary action.\r\n4.11 Ethical considerations \r\n    This study will be conducted after receiving ethical approval from the Institutional Review Board of Jimma University, Institute of Health. The participants will be explained the purpose of the data collection. Confidentiality of the participants’ information will be assured by coding and not indicating specifying names on the response sheets and using the collected data the research purpose only. Also, the anonymity of the participants’ information will be assured throughout.	\r\n\r\nFor the qualitative part, Consent of participants, confidentiality of audio taped interviews will be ensured. Privacy will be provided during the interview.  The transcripts will be shared with the participants for clarification regarding interpretations, and conclusions.\r\n\r\n4.12. Dissemination plan\r\nThe result of this study will be disseminated to Jimma University Institute of Health research and innovation director office, and to all public health facilities of Jimma town. Also, a minimum of four publications will arise from this project.','<h2 style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><a name=\"_Toc104565246\">References:</a></span></span></h2>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\">&nbsp;</p>\r\n\r\n<ol>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\">Palliative Care Australia. (2022). What is palliative care? - Palliative Care Australia. Palliative Care Australia. <a href=\"https://palliativecare.org.au/resource/what-is-palliative-care/\" style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;; color:blue; text-decoration:underline\">https://palliativecare.org.au/resource/what-is-palliative-care/</a></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\">Difference Between Hospice and Palliative Care. (2017, November 29). Compassus. <a href=\"https://www.compassus.com/for-caregivers/hospice-vs-palliative-care\" style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;; color:blue; text-decoration:underline\">https://www.compassus.com/for-caregivers/hospice-vs-palliative-care</a></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\">National Cancer Institute. (2010). Palliative Care in Cancer. Retrieved from National Cancer Institute website: <a href=\"https://www.cancer.gov/about-cancer/advanced-cancer/care-choices/palliative-care-fact-sheet\" style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;; color:blue; text-decoration:underline\">https://www.cancer.gov/about-cancer/advanced-cancer/care-choices/palliative-care-fact-sheet</a></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\">Why Is Palliative Care Important? (n.d.). Retrieved from www.crossroadshospice.com website: <a href=\"https://www.crossroadshospice.com/hospice-palliative-care-blog/2019/march/14/why-is-palliative-care-important/#:~:text=Palliative%20care%20is%20important%20because\" style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;; color:blue; text-decoration:underline\">https://www.crossroadshospice.com/hospice-palliative-care-blog/2019/march/14/why-is-palliative-care-important/#:~:text=Palliative%20care%20is%20important%20because</a>..</span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\">Kam, K. (n.d.). How to Find Palliative Care. WebMD. Retrieved May 4, 2022, from <a href=\"https://www.webmd.com/palliative-care/how-to-find-palliative-care\" style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;; color:blue; text-decoration:underline\">https://www.webmd.com/palliative-care/how-to-find-palliative-care</a></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\">Palliative Care. (n.d.). Www.who.int. <a href=\"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/palliative-care#:~:text=Key%20facts\" style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;; color:blue; text-decoration:underline\">https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/palliative-care#:~:text=Key%20facts</a></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\">Reid, E. A., Gudina, E. K., Ayers, N., Tigineh, W., &amp; Azmera, Y. M. (2018). Caring for Life-Limiting Illness in Ethiopia: A Mixed-Methods Assessment of Outpatient Palliative Care Needs. Journal of Palliative Medicine, 21(5), 622&ndash;630. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1089/jpm.2017.0419\" style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;; color:blue; text-decoration:underline\">https://doi.org/10.1089/jpm.2017.0419</a></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\">Kaba, M., de Fouw, M., Deribe, K. S., Abathun, E., Peters, A. A. W., &amp; Beltman, J. J. (2021). Palliative care needs and preferences of female patients and their caregivers in Ethiopia: A rapid program evaluation in Addis Ababa and Sidama zone. PLOS ONE, 16(4), e0248738. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0248738\" style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;; color:blue; text-decoration:underline\">https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0248738</a></span></span></span></li>\r\n</ol>\r\n\r\n<ol start=\"9\">\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\">Lakew, S., Musema, H., Shimeles, T., &amp; Challinor, J. (2015). Assessment of knowledge, accessibility and utilization of palliative care services among adult cancer patients at Tikur Anbesa Specialized Hospital, Addis Ababa, Ethiopia, 2014: a cross-sectional institution based study. BMC Research Notes, 8(1). <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1186/s13104-015-1630-x\" style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;; color:blue; text-decoration:underline\">https://doi.org/10.1186/s13104-015-1630-x</a></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\">Kaba, M., de Fouw, M., Deribe, K. S., Abathun, E., Peters, A. A. W., &amp; Beltman, J. J. (2021). Palliative care needs and preferences of female patients and their caregivers in Ethiopia: A rapid program evaluation in Addis Ababa and Sidama zone. PLOS ONE, 16(4), e0248738. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0248738\" style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;; color:blue; text-decoration:underline\">https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0248738</a></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\">Lakew, S., Musema, H., Shimeles, T., &amp; Challinor, J. (2015). Assessment of knowledge, accessibility and utilization of palliative care services among adult cancer patients at Tikur Anbesa Specialized Hospital, Addis Ababa, Ethiopia, 2014: a cross-sectional institution based study. BMC Research Notes, 8(1). <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1186/s13104-015-1630-x\" style=\"font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;; color:blue; text-decoration:underline\">https://doi.org/10.1186/s13104-015-1630-x</a></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"color:black\">Meier DE. Palliative Care Facts and Stats :Palliative Care Frequently Asked Questions. New York City: Center to Advance Palliative Care; 2014</span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"color:black\">World Health Organization. Integrating palliative care nd symptom relief into primary health care: A WHO guide for planners, implementers and managers [Internet].Vol. 1, Journal of Materials Processing Technology. 2018. 1&ndash;88 p. </span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"color:black\">&nbsp;Connor SR, Bermedo MCS. Global Atlas of Palliative Care at the End of Life. London: Worldwide Palliative Care Alliance; 2014.</span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"color:black\">Downing J, Atieno M, Debere S, Mwangi-Powell F, Kiyange F, editors. A Handbook of PalliativeCare in Africa. Kampala, Uganda: African Palliative Care Association; 2010. 08-09 p.</span></span></span></span></li>\r\n	<li style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"color:black\">World Palliative Care Alliance. Global atlas of palliative care at the end of life [Internet]. 2014. 111 p. Available from: http://www.who.int/cancer/publications/palliative-care-atlas/en/</span></span></span></span></li>\r\n</ol>\r\n\r\n<p><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">17.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Booth, A., Hannes, K., Harden, A., Noyes, J., Harris, J., &amp; Tong, A. (2014). COREQ (Consolidated Criteria for Reporting Qualitative Studies). Guidelines for Reporting Health Research: A User&rsquo;s Manual, 214&ndash;226. https://doi.org/10.1002/9781118715598.ch21</span></span></p>','To determine the feasibility of establishing Home-based palliative care model through giving training for health professionals in the community, Jimma, Ethiopia, 2022',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,'Key words: Home-based palliative care, public health facilities, Jimma town, Ethiopia',10,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'Proposal-6291049cc96ca723635145.doc'),(121,65,13,25,'Background: Hypertension (High blood pressure is also known as \"the silent killer and a major risk factor for cardiovascular diseases (CVDs). Even though medical advances, it continues to have high rate of morbidity and mortality. Treatment of hypertension is a challenge for medical practitioners due to its multifactorial and variation individually. Currently, the practice of treating chronic hypertension is by targeting the Angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE). However, the existing ACE inhibitors have side effects so associated with which require alternate approaches for effective management of hypertension from medicinal plants. Natural product research remains a potential source in the production of novel candidates for the treatment of hypertension without any side effect. Therefore, Ethiopian endemic medicinal plants i.e. Thymus schimperi and Moringa stenopetala are  well known traditional medicinal plants used in the treatment of various diseases since a century .In this study, Methanol leaves extracts of Thymus schimperi and Moringa stenopetala may have outstanding ACE inhibitors on Aldosterone-salt induced hypertension Wistar albino rats.\r\n\r\nObjective: The aim of this study is to Investigate and evaluate ACE inhibition potential of Ethiopia\'s endemic Medicinal Plants “Thymus schimperi and Moringa stenopetala ‘leaves extracts on Aldosterone-salt induced hypertension wistar albino rats.\r\n\r\nMethodology: The study will be conducted using laboratory breed healthy, male Wistar albino rats. The rats will be randomly selected from the population and randomly allocated in to 2 main treatment groups (n=6). Group 1 served as normal control, group 2 served as diseased control treated with aldosterone--salt hypertension control, Group 3 as Lisinopril injection (48 mg/kg), groups 4, 5 and 6, will be hypertensive rats which will be received different doses of methanol extracts of ( lower dose medium dose and Higher dose in mg/kg,  of Thymus schmpiri and Moringa stenopetala independently the extract will administered orally  for 6 weeks after  the LD50 of the extract will be determined. At the end of 6th week, all the rats will be anesthetized with intramuscular injection of ketamine and sacrificed by cervical dislocation. Blood samples  will be  collected in two different tubes, one with anticoagulant for the separation of plasma and another without anticoagulant for the serum. Plasma and serum will be separated by centrifugation; then serum samples will be collected from rats of each group for biochemical assay. After data collection and cleaning, data will be entered in to SPSS version 25 for statistical analysis, results will be expressed as means ± SD. Statistical data analysis will be done using two -way ANOVA. The significant variables can be considered with a p-value≤ 0.05. \r\nBudget Plan: 562,553.794   Ethiopian birr will be required for this study',NULL,'Investigation of Novel Angiotensin-Converting Enzyme Inhibitory and Assessment of Nutritional composition of Ethiopian Endemic Medicinal Plants extracts to treat Hypertension','2022-05-28 11:55:25','1. To investigate Angiotensin-Converting Enzyme (ACE) inhibitory potential of leave extract of Thymus schimperi\r\n•	To know the novel biomolecules found in  Thymus schimperi extract used to inhibit ACE activities.\r\n•	The result will enable to motivate researchers and drug developers to find out novel natural derivative drugs  from Ethiopian Medicinal plants\r\n2. To investigate Angiotensin-Converting Enzyme (ACE) inhibitory potential of leaves extract of Moringa stenopetala \r\n•	To know the novel biomolecules found in   Moringa stenopetala extract used to inhibit ACE activities.\r\n•	The result will enable to motivate researchers and drug developers to find out novel natural derivative drugs  from Ethiopian Medicinal plants \r\n3. To assess the nutritional composition of Thymus schimperi and Moringa stenopetala extract\r\n•	To know the nutritional composition of Thymus schimperi and Moringa stenopetala extract.and will enhance researchers and other concerned bodies to develop special attention for this Endemic wild edible  plants \r\n4. To compare Angiotensin-Converting Enzyme (ACE) inhibitory potential of leaves extract of Thymus schimperi and Moringa stenopetala \r\n•	The result enables us to identify the potential ACE inhibitors and Antihypertensive Medicinal plants which are traditionally been used by Ethiopian Communities since a  century. \r\n5. To examine the histopathological effect of plant extracts on the histopathology status of lungs of treated rats.\r\n•	The result will enable us to know the impacts of extracts on soft tissue organs; so taking Thymus schimperi and Moringa stenopetala as a supplementary diet will have a protective effect on the lungs and concerned bodies will use the information about the Ethiopian wild edible plants on their respective service providers and policy formulation activities.\r\n6.To identify the novel bioactive molecules which inhibit ACE found in Thymus schimperi and Moringa stenopetala leaves extracts\r\n•	The result able to know the responsible bioactive molecules which is responsible to inhibit ACE, and helps to develop novel anti-hypertensive drugs.','2fe1cf9dabc060daa47626691a3ab908','completed','1. RATIONALE  OF THE STUDY \r\n\r\nHypertension (HTN) or high blood pressure, sometimes called arterial hypertension, is a\r\nchronic medical condition in which the blood pressure in the arteries is elevated. This leads  the heart to work harder than normal to circulate blood through the blood vessel(1). Where having a systolic blood pressure (SBP) of ≥140 mmHg and a diastolic blood pressure (DBP) of ≥90 mmHg, every 20/10 (SBP/DBP) mmHg increase indicates a higher risk stage of hypertension (2). Hypertension is most common cardiovascular disease and is a major worldwide public health challenge because of its high frequency and concomitant risks of cardiovascular and kidney disease. It has been identified as the leading risk factor for mortality, and is ranked third as a cause of disability-adjusted life-years and the leading cause of death in developed countries It has become a major public health issue in most developing countries also (3). The prevalence of hypertension in various regions of the world has been widely reported. however, no information has been compiled for its prevalence and absolute burden around the world. Accurate estimates of the worldwide prevalence of this condition are essential as a source of primary information and for rational planning of health services. Measurement of the global burden of hypertension would allow international public-health policy-makers to assign sufficient priority and resources to its management and prevention(4).\r\nHypertension is one of well-known common disease in the world as a major risk factor for various cardiovascular and renal disease including coronary artery disease, heart failure, brain stroke and peripheral vascular disease, it accounts for an estimated 54 percent of all strokes and 47 percent of all ischemic heart disease globally(5). Hypertension affects more than a quarter of all adults and is the leading attributable cause of death worldwide. It is estimated that over 1 billion people worldwide have hypertension.\r\n In 2000, 26.4% of the world’s populations suffered hypertension and it is predicted that this rate would increase by 60% in 2025.Since the proportion of hypertensive people will increase rapidly. Approximately 10% of all global healthcare spending is attributable to high blood pressure (6).  \r\nHypertension is of a multifactorial nature meaning there are several factors whose combined effects produce hypertension and it is caused by mutual interactions between genetic and environmental factors. The pathogenesis of more than 90% cases of this common disease remains unknown. This type of hypertension is often named essential hypertension which may be a genetic disorder(6). It has been shown that blood pressure is a phenotype-dependent parameter and its regulation is associated with the activity of several genes. The genes act indirectly through enzymes, receptor and other tissue mechanisms. The cause of the remaining 10% of hypertension cases is well-known: underlying disease, injury toxicity and others factors and they are classified as secondary hypertension. Nevertheless, genetic as well as non-genetic factors play an important role in the progression and manifestation of the disease(6). Blood pressure(hypertension) is regulated and maintained by cascade of hormones namely RAAS (renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system).The activation of renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system (RAAS) is one of the established pathogenesis of blood pressure (hypertension), and blocking agents of renin, angiotensin converting enzyme (ACE), angiotensin II ( Ang II) type 1(AT1) receptor, and aldosterone of RAAS are useful medications in the clinical settings When blood volume or sodium levels in the body are low, or blood potassium is high, cells in the kidney release the enzyme, renin. Renin converts angiotensinogen, which is produced in the liver, to the hormone angiotensin I (inactive decapeptide)(7). \r\nThe key enzyme in the RAAS is angiotensin converting enzyme (EC 3.415.1) is a cell membrane zinc metallopeptidase which is mainly found in the kidney, lung ,on the surface of the endothelium and epithelium which  metabolizes angiotensin I into angiotensin II (a potent vasoconstrictor) , bradykinin (a hypotensive peptide)   and affects cardiovascular homeostasis Apart from the role in vasoconstriction, Angiotensin II stimulates the release of the hormone aldosterone in the adrenal glands, which causes the renal tubules to retain sodium and water and excrete potassium and facilities sympathetic activity and has other potentially harmful effects on the cardiovascular system(8). Together, angiotensin II and aldosterone work to raise blood volume, blood pressure and sodium levels in the blood to restore the balance of sodium, potassium, and fluids. If the renin-angiotensin system becomes overactive, consistently leads to blood pressure (hypertension)(8, 9). High ACE activity leads to increased concentration of angiotensin II which is causes blood vessels to constrict and blood pressure to increase. \r\nTherefore, development of agents that inhibit the conversion of angiotensin I to angiotensin II, and bradykinin to inactive components began as a therapeutic strategy to treat hypertension.Several drugs  ranging from ACE inhibitors (captopril, Enalapril, Fosinopril, Perindopril, Quinapril, Ramipril, Trandolapril) and Lisinopril)(10), renin inhibitor (Aliskirenb)(11), angiotensin receptors blockers (ARBs—Losartan, Irbesartan, Olmesartan)(12),calcium channel blockers (Nifedipine, Verapamil, Diltiazem)(13),diuretics (Indapamide, Furosemide, Amiloride)(14), sympathoplegic agents (clonidine, reserpine)(15) α- adrenergic blockers (Prazosin, Doxazosin) (16), β-adrenergic blockers (Nebivolol, Atenolol)(17) to vasodilators (Minoxidil, sodium nitroprusside)(18), are  clinically uses for treatment and   manage blood pressure levels  and also used for  maintaining  of the electrolyte balance in hypertensive patients , but in  reality that high blood pressure is managed in only 34% of hypertensive patients(19).So a point of interest to physicians and health-care practitioners is the alarming, The major concerns that often delay treatment allude to higher costs of antihypertensive drugs, their availability  and accessibility (20), also  these drugs produced  several side effects   after stopping the medication like a dry, irritating, nonproductive cough, headache, dizziness and they may sometimes cause elevated amounts of potassium in the blood(19) .So taking this into account search for natural ACE inhibitors for treatment of hypertension is safe and economical use. This is due to several factors, principal of which is that herbal medicine is a cheaper alternative with fewer undesired side effects.\r\nvarious phytochemical compounds has been identified to possess in vitro ACE inhibitory activity, including hydrolysable tannins, phenylpropanes, proanthocyanidins, flavonoids, xanthones, fatty acids, terpenoids, alkaloids oligosaccharides and peptide amino acids, among others(21) . Nevertheless, only a few works on bioprospecting plant species for this molecular target has been reported(21, 22) .\r\n In this Study Ethiopian Endemic medicinal plants (Thymus schimperi and Moringa stenopetala species may represent an outstanding opportunity of finding new leads for the development of ACE inhibitor drug since these medicinal plants has been used as treatment of hypertension for a century in Ethiopia (23).\r\nThymus schimperi locally known as ‘Tossign’ (Amharic) Thymus schimperi   Ronniger is one of the 350 plant species in the genus Thymus that belongs to the Lamiaceae (i.e., Labiatae or mint) family(24) .  It have been wildly practiced in folk medicine, the different parts of Thymus schimperi  used for treatments of various health ailments such as cold, fever, and cough, asthma, hypertension  diabetic mellitus(24, 25). The leaves, characterized by their pleasant aroma, are largely used as tea served after decoction. In some areas, the leaf is smoked like a cigarette. It is also used as an additive to common food spices by local people  T. schimperi was found to have antifungal, diuretic and antihypertensive activity (25).\r\nThe other commonly used medicinal plant is Moringa (Moringa stenopetala, Bac.) locally known as shiferaw , a tree that belongs to the family Moringaceae is  cultivated in Ethiopia mainly in the Zones and Special districts such as South Omo, Gamo Gofa, Kaffa, Sheka, Bench Maji, Wolaita, Dawaro, Bale, Borena, Sidama, Burji, Amaro, Konso and Derashe(26). Apart from being consumed as a vegetable M. stenopetala is also used to treat malaria, hypertension, stomach disorder and dysentery, asthma, diabetes, epilepsy and visceral leishmaniasis by local communities(26). The M. stenopetala extract is rich in antioxidants including rutin and has potent antiodxidative effect(27). In vitro studies shows that Moringa stenopetala have\r\ndiuretic, hypotensive, antihypertensive and in vitro vasodilatory effects(27, 28).\r\n\r\n1.2. Statement of the problem\r\n   Globally, nearly one billion people have high blood pressure (hypertension); of these, two-thirds are in developing countries. Hypertension is one of the most important causes of premature death worldwide and the problem is growing; in 2025, an estimated 1.56 billion adults will be living with hypertension. Hypertension kills nearly 8 million people every year, worldwide(29).Hypertension is the most common cardiovascular disease and is a major public health issue in developed as well as developing countries. Although it is common and readily detectable, but if left untreated it can often lead to lethal complications. Hypertension is the most prevalent treatable risk factor for stroke, coronary artery dis- ease, and renal disease(29, 30).Hypertension is one of the most common diseases in the world and also an important risk factor for heart failure, renal failure and brain stroke. Hypertension is of a multifactorial nature and it is caused by mutual interactions between genetic and environmental factors. The pathogenesis of more than 90% cases of this common disease remains unknown(32). The cause of the remaining 10% of hypertension cases is well-studied(24). The renin–angiotensin system is a powerful mechanism in the pathogenesis of hypertension. The renin-angiotensin system or RAS regulates blood pressure and fluid balance in the body. When blood volume or sodium levels in the body are low, or blood potassium is high, cells in the kidney release the enzyme, renin. Renin converts angiotensinogen, which is produced in the liver, to the hormone angiotensin I(24, 31). An enzyme known as ACE or angiotensin-converting enzyme found in the lungs metabolizes angiotensin I into angiotensin II and stimulates the degradation important vasodilator molecules bradykinin. Angiotensin II causes blood vessels to constrict and blood pressure to increase. Angiotensin II stimulates the release of the hormone aldosterone in the adrenal glands, which causes the renal tubules to retain sodium and water and excrete potassium. Together, angiotensin II and aldosterone work to raise blood volume, blood pressure and sodium levels in the blood to restore the balance of sodium, potassium, and fluids(32). If the renin-angiotensin system becomes overactive, consistently high blood pressure results(31, 32), for controlling hypertension  angiotensin converting enzyme inhibitors are most frequently used group of antihypertensive drugs (33). In 2014, the Eighth Joint National Commission (JNC8) published evidence-based guidelines for the treatment of high blood pressure in adults which recommended that ACE inhibitors are one of four drug classes recommended for initial therapy for adults with elevated blood pressure(33, 34). The mechanism of action of angiotensin converting enzyme inhibitors has an impact on modulating the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system (RAAS) by blocking the angiotensin converting enzyme actions(35). The exact mechanism of ACE inhibitors is not fully known. They do interfere with the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system but their effect is not directly related to renin levels in the blood(36).Hence angiotensin converting enzyme inhibitors significantly block the conversion of angiotensin I to angiotensin II and reduce the degradation of bradykinin. Hence lowers blood pressure by reducing the formation of strong vasoconstrictors (angiotensin II), and reducing the degradation of the strong vasodilators (bradykinin)(22, 36).Various classes of drugs and regimens have been proposed for the control of hypertension. In spite of its significance, hypertension remains poorly controlled(37). All Hypertensive  drugs cause dizziness, ankle swelling, headache, fatigue, hyperkalemia, nausea, hypotension, chest discomfort  and renal impairment was been reported(38)  . In such cases, use of herbal drugs as therapeutic agents is helped to prevent hypertension and there will be enhancement of metabolic health(31).A large number of natural ACE inhibitors are well known for their hypotensive action. Some of these include Gooseberry, Gokshura, Rose petal jam, Turmeric, Ginger, Cinnamon, and Cardamom Allium sativum, Soy Sauce, Tropaeolum majus, Artemisia scoparia(31, 39).\r\nThymus schimperi (Endemic to Ethiopian) and Moringa stenopetala, are  well known medicinal plants which  have high medicnal values used since ancient time to cure different disease conditions in humans(24, 40).The aim of the present study investigate Novel ACE Inhibitor molecules and micronutrients from Ethiopian Endemic wild edible and antihypertensive Medicinal Plants plants (Thymus schimperi and Moringa stenopetal) leaves extracts on Wistar albino rats. Up to my knowledge the ACE inhibition potential and responsible bioactive compounds in Thymus schimperi and Moringa stenopetala leaves extracts have not been investigated and this is first study of its kind in this respect. Thus, this study will be contributed more to the scientific community to develop evidence based strategies to tackle the huge impact of hypertension and also will generate scientific information to affected communities on optional ways to minimize the adverse effects of hypertension.      \r\n\r\n1.3 Significance of the study \r\n  The finding of this research is expected to be baseline for further studies on Ethiopian endemic medicinal plants  not only for the treatment of hypertension but also for the treatment of next frontier  diseases  such as cancer, diabetic mellitus, Alzheimer’s ,Tuberculosis bacillus ,Parkinson’s diseases ,schizophrians  etc., have been treating by Ethiopia  tradition medicinal plants  more than a century, so this research project work is  going to disintegration the closed story of Ethiopian uniqueness and  hotspotness  by natural biodiversity to  other countries researchers who are interested to conduct intense laboratory based  research in the same or related  areas will be invited .Other researchers who have been conducting targeting on phytochemical derivatives ACE inhibitors and their pharmaceutical properties and Nutritional composition  will be added the existing data and initiate nationwide measure to address the issue. The Ethiopian government and higher institute will become aware of about the use of Ethiopian traditional medical plants for battling hypertension and associated complications and they may be given priority to conservation, sustainable utilization hence. The Ethiopian government and higher institute should give high attention to practical based education police in the country. Hypertension is very pandemic public issue of the globe and hence the significance of   the result of this research project work is not limited only in the specific target group.  All developed and developing countries will be beneficiary. Communities who are suffer from hypertension and associated complications will be highly benefited from the fruit of this project by curing themselves without any side effect unlike synthetic drugs .And the other  significance of this research project is for a  number of students trained at Masters and PhD levels in the area of, drug discovery, Medical biochemistry, pharmacology and clinical nutrition, identification of anti-hypertensive  responsible bioactive compounds  from medicinal plants will be benefited and also a number of publications in reputable journals.','3.1 Study area and period: The study will be conducted in laboratory of Biochemistry, Department of Biomedical Sciences, Faculty of Medical Sciences, Jimma University Ethiopia from August 30  ,2022 to    July 30   2023.\r\n3.2 Study design:    Laboratory based randomized control experimental trial study design will be conducted.\r\n3.3 Population\r\n3.3.1   Source population:  Wistar albino rats \r\n3.3.2 Study population: 60 male Wistar albino rats will be used in the study.\r\n3.4 Inclusion and Exclusion Criteria \r\n3.4.1 Inclusion Criteria: Healthy male adult Wistar albino rats will be included.\r\n3.4.2 Exclusion Criteria: Unhealthy, female wistar albino rats will be excluded in this study. \r\n3.5 sample size estimation\r\n The adequacy sample size will be determined by using resource equation method as we have multiple endpoints measurements of variables in our study. According to resource equation E is measured as follow:\r\n   E=Total number of animals – Total number of groups. \r\nWhere E is the degree of freedom of analysis of variance (ANOVA). E between 10 and 20 is considered as adequate. E larger than 20 implicate more than adequate animals are used (41).\r\nIn our study we intended to use 60 Wistar albino male rats per experiment, we will be conducted 2 main treatment groups in each experiment groups will have six groups(n=5) that will be assigned to 12 groups(6 Groups for each Medicinal Plant). \r\nE = 60 –12; E= 48. This implicates as we intended to utilize more than adequate animals, but this is justifiable as the sample of blood that can be obtained from samples is limited and increasing the number of animals used is obligatory for this study. Moreover, some procedures during data collection necessitate avoiding of the rat from other procedures to be done, so this also influence to double the number of animals that will be used in the study.  \r\n3.6 Study variables\r\n3.6.1 Independent variable\r\n   Concentration of aldosterone, Salt concentration levels, concentration Phytochemicals \r\n Nutritional Composion \r\n3.6.2 Dependent variables\r\n Levels of serum creatinine (Cre), serum urea nitrogen (UN) Blood urea nitrogen(BUN) Serum Calcium, Serum Potassium. The concentration of angiotensin II (Ang II) indicates the inhibition of ACE.\r\n3.7   Plant collection and authentication\r\nFresh plant leaves of Thymus schimperi and Moringa stenopetala will be collected from the natural habitat based on the months of work plan.  Thymus schimperi will be collected around Debre Birhane, North shewa, Amhara National State, Ethiopia whereas fresh leaves of Moringa stenopetala will be collected from Kaffa, SNNP, Ethiopia. The plant will be identified and authenticated and voucher specimens Number will be given by the faculty member of Department of studies in Botany, Jimma University, Ethiopia.\r\n3.8 Required chemical and Equipment  \r\n3.8.1 Required chemicals\r\nThe following solvents (ethanol, petroleum ether, ethyl acetate, n-butanol, methanol, chloroform) and chemicals (ACE   substrate HHL, Lisinopril, Sucrose, aldosterone citric acid, sodium citrate, anhydrous sodium sulphate, silica, tween 80) and other required chemicals for achievement of these study will be analytically graded and purchasing from local drug vendors or abroad.\r\n3.8.2 Required Equipment \r\nIn this study Soxhlet Extraction Apparatus(Garg,500ml), Extraction thimbles 30x100, Pyrex Erlenmeyer Flasks (50ml,100ml,250ml ,200ml, 500ml), volumetric Flask(50ml,100ml,100ml,2000ml), Ferret Cage, rat oral gavage deep freezer (-80oC), test tubes with graduations, centrifuge, Centrifuge test tube with gradations, Incubator, shaker, Micropipettes (p10.p50. p100, p200, p1000, p5000), Spectrophotometer, tissue homogenizer, scissors, Pyrex Beakers (50ml,100ml,200ml,250ml, 400ml,500ml,1000m), light microscope, Colored camera, Thin layer chromatography, HPLC.  \r\n3.9 Preliminary phytochemical evaluation \r\n3.9.1   Preparation of the crude extract \r\n The collected and authenticated Matured plant leaves of “Thymus schimperi and Moringa stenopetala leaves will be washed with tap water to remove soil and unwanted dust particles. Then the leaves will be shade dried at room temperature and powdered using mechanical mixer and stored in air tight bottle. The adequate amount of powder materials will be extracted in hot sohxlet apparatus sequential using Hexane, methanol, ethanol and distilled w. The volume of each solvent will be 500ml for 24 cycle during extraction for each medicinal plants. The extracted will be concentrated by evaporation (complete removal of solvent) and will be weighed and used for experiment.\r\n 3.9.2 Phytochemical screening \r\n  Both qualitative and quantitative analysis of the important phytochemical compounds and secondary metabolites such as alkaloids, saponin, tannin, phlobatannin, phenol, anthraquinoes, flavonoids, glycosides, steroids, terpenes, cardenolides, flavonoid, glycosides and steroids will be determined and tested by using standard test tube procedures and thin layer chromatography.The solvent extract with greater activity will be subjected to fractionation and purification using a range of chromatographic techniques such as Column Chromatography (CC) over silica gel, Preparative Thin Layer Chromatography (prep-TLC) and gel filtration over sephadex, to obtain pure compounds.\r\n3.10 Nutritional Evaluation\r\n3.10.1 Proximate Analysis\r\nProximate analysis will be carried out on the leave extract Thymus schimperi and Moringa stenopetala to determine its nutritional values with regards to protein, carbohydrate, moisture, ash contents, crude fiber, minerals and crude fat contents using standard methods described by (42).\r\n3.10. 2 Determination of Plasma ACE Activity\r\n ACE activity in serum will be  measured by using HHL as a substrate(39). The serum samples (50 µL) will be mixed with 0.1 M sodium borate buffer (pH 8.3, 100 µL) containing 300 mM NaCl and 5 mM HHL (50 µL). The mixture will be incubated at 37 ˝C for 45 min, and then the enzymatic reaction will be stopped with the addition of 2 N HCl  solution (200 µL). The reaction mixture will be separated with ethyl acetate (2.0 mL). The upper layer (ethyl acetate layer, 1.5 mL) will be concentrated and dissolved with 1 M NaCl solution (1 mL). The absorbance will be measured at 228 nm and the hippuric acid content in the serum will be calculated using calibration curve of hippuric acid standard.\r\n3.10.3 Assay for ACE Inhibitory Activity\r\n  The ACE inhibitory activities extracts  of  Both Thymus schimperi and Moringa stenopetala  phytochemical compounds will be  measured according to the methods described by(39), with slight modification. The samples (20 µL) will be mixed with 0.1 M sodium borate buffer (pH 8.3, 100 µL) containing 300 mM NaCl and 5 mM HHL (50 µL). The mixture will be then pre-incubated at 37 ˝C for 10 min. After pre-incubation, the mixture will be added to 100 µL which purchased from Sigma-aldrich laboratory reagent and chemical distributor India. The mixture will be incubated at 37 ˝C for 90 min and then the enzymatic reaction will be stopped by the addition of 2 N HCl solutions (200 µL). The reaction mixture will be separated with ethyl acetate (2 mL). The upper layer (ethyl acetate layer, 1.5 mL) will be concentrated and the hippuric acid fraction will be dissolved with 1 M NaCl solution (1 mL). The absorbance (A) will be measured at 228 nm. Will be used as a positive control. The absorbance (B) of Q (quercetin) and the samples will be measured by the same procedure as described above except for ACE extract. The ACE inhibitory activities of the samples will be determined as percentage decrease in absorbance compared to that of a control test. The dose-response curve of each compound at the concentration of ď1 mM will be a linear for ACE inhibitory activity. The isolated compounds will be assayed at the concentration of 1 mM.\r\nACE inhibitory activity (%) = [(control-A-B)/Control] ×100\r\n3.10.4    Structural Determination \r\nThe structure of pure compounds will be established using a combination of spectroscopic methods such as Infra-Red (IR), Ultra Violet (UV), 1H and 13C Nuclear Magnetic Resonance (NMR), and Mass Spectrometry (MS). The isolated compounds will also be screened for their in vivo activity. The compounds with moderate activity will be then chemically modified so as to enhance their activities.\r\n3.10.5 Experimental animals\r\nNormal male Wistar albino rats, bred in the animal house of Jimma University laboratory age of 6 to 8 weeks and weighing 220 to 300 g will be used for the experiment. They will be kept for a week in a controlled environment (25±1°C and 12 h/12 h light/dark cycle). The animals will be allowed free access to tap water and standard laboratory animal food. The care and handling of the animals will be in accordance with the internationally accepted standard guidelines for use of animals (43). \r\n3.10.6 Aldosterone-salt induction of hypertension on wistar albino rats\r\nAnimals will be given, weekly twice, subcutaneous injections of aldosterone (300 mg/kg BW) in in dimethyl formamide (vehicle) solution, and salt will be administered by substitution of 2% NaCl solution for drinking water ad libitum before one week of the actual experimental periods.\r\n3. 11 Experimental Groups\r\n3.7.1 The effect of Thymus schimperi leaves extract on the activity of Angiotensin-Converting Enzyme (ACE) in induced hypertensive Wistar albino rats.\r\nGroup I: Normal control+ distilled water  \r\nGroup II:  Hypertensive with distilled water \r\nGroup III: Hypertensive rats with standard ant-hypertensive drug (Lisinopril)\r\nGroup IV: Hypertensive rats   with the lower dose of Thymus schimperi leaf extract \r\nGroup V: Hypertensive rats with medium dose of Thymus schimperi leaf extract \r\nGroup VI: Hypertensive rats with Higher dose of Thymus schimperi leaf extract \r\n3.7.2 The effect of Moringa stenopetala leaves extract on the activity of Angiotensin-Converting Enzyme (ACE) in induced hypertensive Wistar albino rats.\r\nGroup I: Normal control+ distilled water  \r\nGroup II:  Hypertensive with distilled water \r\nGroup III: Hypertensive rats with standard ant-hypertensive drug (Lisinopril)\r\nGroup IV: Hypertensive rats   with the lower dose of Moringa stenopetala leaf extract \r\nGroup V: Hypertensive rats with medium dose of Moringa stenopetala leaf extract \r\nGroup VI: Hypertensive rats with Higher dose of Moringa stenopetala leaf extract	\r\n3.11.1 Blood pressure measurements\r\nSystolic and diastolic blood pressure will be determined by the tail-cuff method (IITC, model 31, Woodland Hills, CA, USA) from 0th day to 6 weeks. The tail cuff approach to determine arterial blood pressure requires certain precautions such as reduction of stress of the animals, appropriate training of rats over multiple days and adequate pre warming to dilate the tail artery. The rats will be placed in a heated chamber at an ambient temperature of 30–34°C for 15 min, and from each rat, 1–10 blood pressure values will be recorded. The lowest three readings averaged to obtain a mean blood pressure.\r\n3.11.2   Data Collection Techniques and Procedures \r\nAt the end of the experiment, all rats (both treated and control groups) rats will be sacrificed using an overdose of ether anesthesia and the blood will be collected in vacutainer tube by cardiac puncture from night fasted cervical dislocated rats, Blood samples will be collected in two different tubes, one with anticoagulant for the separation of plasma and another without anticoagulant for the serum. Plasma and serum will be separated by centrifugation; then serum sample will be collected from the rat of each group for biochemical assay. The chest will be opened and the lungs will be rapidly removed and placed in isopentane chilled in liquid N2. The tissue will be stored at - 70°C until analyzed, before analyses, frozen tissue will be divided into two sections for histopathology study and ACE Assay. For histopathology study the tissue will be fixed in neutral formalin at room temperature for more than 24 h, will be dehydrated with a graded alcohol series and embedded in paraffin at 52°C. Sections will be prepared and stained with hematoxylin and eosin for histological examinations. While the remaining sections will be milled with a stainless-steel mortar and pestle, homogenized in cold trizma-HCl buffer (pH 7.8), defatted using acetone and will be decanted the acetone and dry the homogenate. The homogenate will be centrifuged at 40000xg at 4°C for 15 min repeatedly until pelleting will be stopped. Supernatants will be stored at -80°C, until required. Then ACE activity will be determined by a modification of the methods described by (44), whereas the Protein content of the sample will be determined by Biuret method using bovine serum albumin (BSA) as standard. \r\n3.11.3 Biochemical Assays \r\nAfter scarified by cervical dislocation blood sample will be collected. Blood samples from rat in the same experimental group will be pooled together to get the required blood volume for sample analysis. Serum creatinine (Cre), and serum urea nitrogen (UN)or Blood Urea Nitrogen (BUN), Serum Calcium, Serum Potassium, will be determined to assess renal function. Whereas the concentration of angiotensin II (Ang II) shows that inhibition of ACE by plant extract. Serum creatinine will be measured by using a kinetic method using alkaline picrate ( Jaffé method) or(45). Serum Calcium will be determined   the mothed describe by(46), Serum potassium will be measured by (47), serum urea nitrogen (UN)or Blood Urea Nitrogen (BUN),will be determine by the protocol(48).After the animal experiment will be conducted  he concentration of angiotensin II (Ang II)   will be  measure the method describe(49)\r\n3.11.4   Quality Assurance\r\nData quality will be maintained by using trained personnel for laboratory animal handling, sample collection and sample processing. Moreover, by adherence to scientific procedures and kit supplier companies’ guidelines the quality of the research and its outcome will be maintained.\r\n3.11.5   Data Management and Statistical Analysis \r\nData will be entered into computer using Epi-Data version 4.1 and exported to the Statistical package for Social Science (SPSS) version 25.0 for analysis. Differences between mean values will be evaluated using Student’s t test while proportions will be compared using the Pearson’s Chi-square test. Multivariable logistic regression analyses will be used to assess the crude and adjusted effect of seemingly significant predictors of the target outcome. Categorical and continuous data will be expressed as percentages and mean ± standard deviation respectively. Descriptive statistics will be applied for the analysis of patient characteristics, including means, standard deviations (SD), medians, and percentiles and categorical variables will be analyzed by using the chi-square test. A p-value of <0.05 will be considered to be statistically significant.\r\n3.13 Plan for dissemination and ensuring utilization of findings\r\n Progress report and final report will be submitted to Health Research and Innovation Directorate, Institute of Health Research, Jimma University. The Summary report of the finding will be submitted to Ethiopian Biodiversity Institutes to recall attention for the conservation and sustainable utilization of these Endemic wild edible and Medicinal plants. The finding of this study will be tested through different phases of clinical trial in Ethiopian Public Health Institute and other institutes which have been organized laboratories in Ethiopia as well as abroad according to the guide line and rule and regulation of Ethiopia. The research results also will be disseminated by publishing on reputable journals, through poster and seminar presentations.','<p><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">1.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Dornas WC, Silva MEJJob. Animal models for the study of arterial hypertension. 2011;36(4):731-7.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">2.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Chobanian AV, Bakris GL, Black HR, Cushman WC, Green LA, Izzo Jr JL, et al. Seventh report of the joint national committee on prevention, detection, evaluation, and treatment of high blood pressure. 2003;42(6):1206-52.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">3.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Kearney PM, Whelton M, Reynolds K, Muntner P, Whelton PK, He JJTl. Global burden of hypertension: analysis of worldwide data. 2005;365(9455):217-23.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">4.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Saputri F, Mun&rsquo;im A, Lukmanto D, Aisyah S, Rinandy JJIJPSR. Inhibition of angiotensin converting enzyme (ACE) activity by some Indonesia edible plants. 2015;6(3):1054-9.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">5.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Sharifi N, Souri E, Ziai SA, Amin G, Amanlou MJDJoPS. Discovery of new angiotensin converting enzyme (ACE) inhibitors from medicinal plants to treat hypertension using an in vitro assay. 2013;21(1):1-8.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">6.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Raźny K, Bednarski M, Sapa J, Gdula-Argasińska J, Tyszka-Czochara M, Librowski T, et al. Animal models for hypertension research. 2014;55.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">7.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Baltatzi M, Savopoulos C, Hatzitoli disease. Plant flavonoids as angiotensin converting enzyme inhibitors in regulation of hypertension. 2011;1(5):172-88.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">9.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Sica DAJTJoCH. Angiotensin<span style=\"font-family:&quot;Cambria Math&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">‐</span>Converting Enzyme Inhibitors&#39; Side Effects&mdash;Physiologic and Non<span style=\"font-family:&quot;Cambria Math&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">‐</span>Physiologic Considerations. 2005;7:17-23.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">10.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Mignat C, Unger TJDs. ACE inhibitors. 1995;12(5):334-47.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">11.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Greenlee WJJMrr. Renin inhibitors. 1990;10(2):173-236.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">12.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Heran BS, Musini VM, Bassett K, Taylor RS, Wright JMJCDoSR. Angiotensin receptor blockers for heart failure. 2012(4).</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">13.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Eisenberg MJ, Brox A, Bestawros ANJTAjom. Calcium channel blockers: an update. 2004;116(1):35-43.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">14.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Ernst ME, Moser MJNEJoM. Use of diuretics in patients with hypertension. 2009;361(22):2153-64.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">15.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Chester AE, Meyers FHJPotSfEB, Medicine. Central sympathoplegic and norepinephrine-depleting effects of antioxidants. 1988;187(1):62-8.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">16.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; EL-ETR AA, GLISSON SNJIAC. Alpha-adrenergic blocking agents. 1978;16(2):239-60.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">17.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Frishman WH, Saunders EJTJoCH. &beta;<span style=\"font-family:&quot;Cambria Math&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">‐</span>adrenergic blockers. 2011;13(9):649.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">18.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Singh A, Laribi S, Teerlink JR, Mebazaa AJEhj. Agents with vasodilator properties in acute heart failure. 2017;38(5):317-25.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">19.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Wong RH, Garg ML, Wood LG, Howe PRJN. Antihypertensive potential of combined extracts of olive leaf, green coffee bean and beetroot: a randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled crossover trial. 2014;6(11):4881-94.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">20.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Al Disi SS, Anwar MA, Eid AHJFip. Anti-hypertensive herbs and their mechanisms of action: part I. 2016;6:323.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">21.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Frishman WH, Beravol P, Carosella CJD-a-M. Alternative and complementary medicine for preventing and treating cardiovascular disease. 2009;3(55):121-92.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">22.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Biswas D, Uddin M, Dizdarevic LL, J&oslash;rgensen A, Duttaroy AKJEjon. Inhibition of angiotensin-converting enzyme by aqueous extract of tomato. 2014;53(8):1699-706.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">23.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; d&rsquo;Avigdor E, Wohlmuth H, Asfaw Z, Awas TJJoE, ethnomedicine. The current status of knowledge of herbal medicine and medicinal plants in Fiche, Ethiopia. 2014;10(1):1-33.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">24.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Haji H, Makonnen E, Debella A, Geleta BJBJoP, Toxicology. Evaluation of diuretic and antihypertensive activity of leaf extracts of Thymus schimperi in rats. 2016;7(1):1-8.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">25.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Asressu KH. Antimicrobial activity and phytochemical screening of crude extracts of medicinal plants grown in eastern Ethiopia. 2013.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">26.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Habtemariam SJAPJoTB. The African Moringa is to change the lives of millions in Ethiopia and far beyond. 2016;6(4):355-6.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">27.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Geleta B, Makonnen E, Debella A, Tadele AJFip. In vivo antihypertensive and antihyperlipidemic effects of the crude extracts and fractions of Moringa stenopetala (Baker f.) Cufod. leaves in rats. 2016;7:97.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">28.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Geleta B, Eyasu M, Kebamo S, Debella A, Makonnen E, Abebe AJAPJoTB. In vitro vasodilatory effect of aqueous leaf extract of Thymus serrulatus on thoracic aorta of Guinea pigs. 2015;5(1):15-8.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">29.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Martiniuk AL, Lee CM, Lawes CM, Ueshima H, Suh I, Lam TH, et al. Hypertension: its prevalence and population-attributable fraction for mortality from cardiovascular disease in the Asia-Pacific region. 2007;25(1):73-9.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">30.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Kaur M, Rana A, Kumar S. Induction of hypertension by various animal models. 2011.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">31.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Ranjini H, Udupa PE, Thomas JMJSJoAMS. Angiotensin converting enzyme (ACE): inhibition of sheep kidney and lung ACE in vitro by Rauwolfia serpentina and allium sativum. 2015;3(5):1936-40.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">32.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Chou R, Helfand M, Carson S. Drug class review on angiotensin converting enzyme inhibitors: Oregon Evidence-based Practice Center, Oregon Health &amp; Science University; 2004.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">33.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Rikken F. Adverse drug reactions in a different context. State University Groningen, Groningen; 1998.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">34.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Alibasic E, Ljuca F, Ramic E, Bajraktarevic A, Batic-Mujanovic O, Dzafic F, et al. ADVANTAGES AND LIMITATIONS OF THE USE OF ACE INHIBITORS.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">35.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Berl TJJotASoN. Angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitors versus AT1 receptor antagonist in cardiovascular and renal protection: the case for AT1 receptor antagonist. 2004;15(1 suppl):S71-S6.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">36.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Herman LL, Padala SA, Annamaraju P, Bashir KJS. Angiotensin converting enzyme inhibitors (ACEI). 2021.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">37.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Guerrero L, Castillo J, Qui&ntilde;ones M, Garcia-Vallv&eacute; S, Arola L, Pujadas G, et al. Inhibition of angiotensin-converting enzyme activity by flavonoids: structure-activity relationship studies. 2012;7(11):e49493.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">38.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Joshi VD, Dahake AP, Suthar APJIJPTR. Adverse effects associated with the use of antihypertensive drugs: An overview. 2010;2:10-3.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">39.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Cho J-Y, Park K-H, Hwang DY, Chanmuang S, Jaiswal L, Park Y-K, et al. Antihypertensive effects of Artemisia scoparia Waldst in spontaneously hypertensive rats and identification of angiotensin I converting enzyme inhibitors. 2015;20(11):19789-804.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">40.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Habtemariam S, Varghese GKJB. Extractability of rutin in herbal tea preparations of Moringa stenopetala leaves. 2015;1(3):169-82.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">41.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Charan J, Kantharia NJJop, pharmacotherapeutics. How to calculate sample size in animal studies? 2013;4(4):303.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">42.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Basumatary S, Nath N. Assessment of chemical compositions and in vitro antioxidant properties of Musa balbisiana Colla inflorescence. International Journal of Pharmaceutical Research. 2018;10(1):80-5.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">43.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Council NR. Guide for the care and use of laboratory animals. 2010.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">44.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Cushman D, Cheung HJBp. Spectrophotometric assay and properties of the angiotensin-converting enzyme of rabbit lung. 1971;20(7):1637-48.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">45.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Husdan H, Rapoport AJCc. Estimation of creatinine by the Jaffe reaction: a comparison of three methods. 1968;14(3):222-38.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">46.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Husdan H, Leung M, Oreopoulos D, Rapoport AJCc. Measurement of serum and plasma ionic calcium with the&quot; Space-Stat 20 Ionized Calcium Analyzer&quot;. 1977;23(9):1775-7.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">47.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Gunter EW, Lewis BG, Koncikowski SM. Laboratory procedures used for the third National Health and Nutrition Examination Survey (NHANES III), 1988-1994. 1996.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">48.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Chua K, Tan IJJoCP. Serum urea analysis using the Beckman BUN Analyzer. 1976;29(6):517-9.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">49.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Nussberger J, Brunner DB, Waeber B, Brunner HRJH. Specific measurement of angiotensin metabolites and in vitro generated angiotensin II in plasma. 1986;8(6):476-82.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p><span style=\"font-size:9pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">50. os AJH. Role of angiotensin converting enzyme inhibitors and angiotensin receptor blockers in hypertension of chronic kidney disease and renoprotection. Study results. 2011;15(Suppl 1):27.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p>&nbsp;</p>','•	To investigate Novel ACE Inhibitor molecules and micronutrients from Ethiopian Endemic wild edible and antihypertensive Medicinal Plants plants (Thymus schimperi and Moringa stenopetal) leaves extracts on Wistar albino rats',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,'Hypertension,Endemic Medical plants,Angiotensin converting enzyme,Ethiopia,Thymus schimperi,Moringa stenopetala',10,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'Proposal-6291e3295aa4a943031158.doc'),(122,14,14,25,'Background:\r\nReproduction is fundamental to species survival and it is strongly intimated with many factors including, nutrition, hormones and genetic and other idiopathic factors. Nutritional   have a significant effect on both male and female reproductive performance and fertility rates. Taking of Sufiecnet and adequate composition of nutrient is determinant for well development of both primary and secondary sexual characteristics, and also the secretion of sexual hormones of both male and female. Unfortunately, due to Insufficient intake of nutrients both human and animals causes infertility which is a biological disease that impairs a couple’s ability to achieve a viable pregnancy, the problem   can result from male or female factors. The female factors stem from ovulation problems and complex in female it is multifactorial nature. Therefore, searching natural derivative molecules used for treatment of Sexual dysfunction and infertility in female, will be the golden standard, surprisingly Ethiopian wild, edible medicinal plant Ferula communis s. has been used for enhancement of Reproductive performance and Fertility rates, Since a century.\r\nObjective:\r\nTo study Nutritional Composition of Ferula communis s. extracts and its effect on, Sex hormones, sexual desire, Lipid profiles, Electrolytes, and Histopathology of Reproductive organs in female Wistar rats to enhance fertility and reproductive rates.\r\nMethodology:\r\nThe study will be conducted using thirty female Wistar albino rats. The rates will be and ran allocated in to 5 groups in each groups (n=6). Group 1 served as normal control with distilled water,, group 2 served as a positive control , rats will be fed orally 0.6 mg/kg Clomiphene citrate (CC) ,Group -3 , Group -4 and Group -5 will be   rats  fed orally with lowest dose (D1) ,medium doses (D2)  and  highest dose (D3) of ferula communis s.  stem extract for 6 weeks after determining of LD50 of the extract. On the end of 6th weeks, the experiment rats will be fasted overnight and sacrificed by cervical all the rats will be anesthetized with intramuscular injection of ketamine and sacrificed by cervical dislocation. Then blood will be taken by cardiac puncture, from each rat of each group for biochemical and hormonal assay. Ovary, uterus, hypothalamus, pituitary, thyroid and Mammary gland will be also taken from each experimental rats for histopathological evaluation. Data will be coded and entered Epi data version 4.1 and will be exported to SPSS version 25 for analysis.  The results will be expressed as means ± SD. Statistical data analysis will be done using two -way ANOVA. The significant variables can be considering with p-value≤ 0.05. \r\nBudget:    634,814.994 Ethiopian birr  will be required',NULL,'Study on Nutritional compositionof  Ferula communis s. extracts and its Effects  on, Sex hormones, sexual desire, Lipid profiles, Electrolytes and Histopathology of Reproductive organs on female Wistar rats','2022-05-28 14:01:15','1.To asses phytochemical constitute of   ferula communis s. stem extract	\r\n•	The result will be helps to know the phyto constitutes of the plants extract in both quantitative and qualitative\r\n2. To determine Nutritional composition of ferula communis s. stem extract 	\r\n•	To know the nutritional composition of ferula communis s. extract  and will enhance researchers and other concerned bodies to develop special attention for this Endemic wild edible  plants\r\n3. To observe the effect of extract on female Sexual desires in female Wistar rats	\r\n•	The results enable to know the effect of the plants extract on Sexual desires in female Wistar rats\r\n4. To determine the effect of  ferula communis s.  extract on sex hormones (estrogen, progesterone, FSH, LH, and Testosterone)	\r\n•	The result helps to the effect of the ferula communis s.  stem extract on the secretion of  l sex hormones ((estrogen, progesterone, FSH, LH, and Testosterone)\r\n5. To examine the histopathological effect   ferula communis s. extract on histopathology status of ovary, uterus, hypothalamus, pituitary, thyroid and Mammary gland\r\n	•	The results enable to know the pertinent (positive and negative) effect of the ferula communis s. extract on histopathology status of ovary, uterus, hypothalamus, pituitary, thyroid and Mammary gland\r\n6. To analyze the total lipid profile of treated female Wistar rats by ferula communis s. extract\r\n	•	The result helps  to know the pertinent(positive and negative) effect of the ferula communis s.extract on lipid profile of treated female Wistar rats\r\n7. To assess effect of ferula communis s. extract on Serum electrolytes (K+, Na+, Cl-, Ca+2) of female Wistar rats\r\n	•	The results enable to know the pertinent (positive and negative) effect of ferula communis s. extract on extract on Serum electrolytes (K+, Na+, Cl-, Ca+2) in female Wistar rats','a5da4c79478774dd98d8d983d8133b2b','completed','Reproduction is fundamental to species survival of all living systems. In mammalian species,  thousands of essential ‘sex’ genes and hormones are conserved through evolution by means of reproduction(1), understanding of reproduction, fertilities mechanism and intimated factors is highly needed and required prior attention , to maintain Population dynamic , functioning  and continuity of the species .It is well established that there are enormous differences in the specifics of how each species reproduces(2). Reproductive professionals have been studying wildlife benefits from advances in the human infertility and livestock production fields to overcome infertility (humans), more efficient/higher quality food production (livestock) and retention of all gene diversity (wildlife). Nonetheless, these three groups share aligned interests in ‘ensuring reproductive health and preserving fertility.\r\nThere is strong interest in extending the reproductive longevity of a valuable wild animal indefinitely into the future, with the occasional re-infusion of its genes into the contemporary population such an approach contributes by avoiding (or mitigating) genetic drift and the tendency for inbreeding in small populations(3, 4).However, due to various factors the longevity of wildlife  animals  have been under question and leads infertility including human beings. Infertility is the diminished ability or the inability to conceive and have offspring. It leads distressing issue to both the couple and their populations. The World Health Organization(WHO) has classified it as a global health problem(5). As recognized by the American Society of Reproductive Medicine, infertility is a biological disease that impairs a couple’s ability to achieve a viable pregnancy. It can be caused by hormonal, ovarian, uterine, urological, and other medical factors. Known risk factors include advanced age, being over-or underweight, lack of exercise, smoking, alcohol and substance abuse, sexually transmitted diseases, and poor nutrition. \r\nAccording to the American Society of Reproductive Medicine, a medical infertility cause can be identified, or perhaps only indefinitely suggested, in approximately 90% of cases and may be multifactorial in 25% of cases, Up to 10% of infertility cannot be explained medically(6). \r\n The problem of reproduction and infertility are   key process can result from male or female factors can exist in isolations or combination and fertility investigations, diagnosis and treatment should be always being considered in the context of the couples. \r\nMale infertility has many causes--from hormonal imbalances, to physical problems, to psychological and/or behavioral problems.  Moreover, fertility reflects a man’s “overall” health.  Male who live a healthy lifestyle are more likely to produce healthy sperm. Male factors include low sperm count and sperm abnormalities, such as altered morphology and low motility(7).\r\n   Female factors stem from ovulation problems such as premature ovarian failure (early menopause), thyroid irregularities, polycystic ovarian syndrome, and fallopian tube obstruction. Female infertility is a complex disorder and can be caused by a number of factors including genetic, environmental diets and behavioral(8). Hormones also have significance for fertility and reproductive in human and animals. \r\nHormones are also regulate many bodily processes, such as appetite, sleep, and growth. Sex hormones are those that play an essential role in sexual development and reproduction. The main glands that produce sex hormones are the adrenal glands and the gonads, which include the ovaries in females and testes in males(9).The sex hormones produced by the testes or ovaries, are basically the steroid hormones which plays important functions in animal and human  body(10).. They are known by different names in both male and female, in female they are called the estrogen (ER) and  progesterone (PR) while in males they are called the androgen (AR)(11)  .LH acts on specialized cells within the testes and stimulates release of the male sex hormone, testosterone. FSH acts on a separate population of cells within the testes to stimulate the production of sperm. During puberty testosterone is essential for growth, increasing muscle bulk, development of the penis and for male pattern hair growth. \r\nLack of the male sex hormone, testosterone, is termed Hypogonadism. If the pituitary gland does not produce enough or any gonadotrophin hormones, this can affect fertility(12). The sex steroid hormones (estrogens, progesterone, and androgens) affect gene expression through direct interaction with nuclear DNA and transcription machinery by means of steroid hormone receptors(12).Sex hormones act throughout the entire brain of both males and females via both genomic and nongenomic receptors. Many neural and behavioral functions are affected by estrogens, including mood, cognitive function, blood pressure regulation, motor coordination, pain, and opioid sensitivity(13). Estrogen (ER), progesterone (PR), and testosterone are hormones that affect sexual desire and functioning. \r\nThe brain is a target for sex steroid hormones began with studies of reproductive hormone actions on the hypothalamus, regulating not only gonadotropin secretion and ovulation in females (14). With advances understanding of the neuroendocrine control of reproduction is crucial. Hypothalamic gonadotropin releasing hormone (GnRH) is at the top of the axis and has effects in the pituitary gland, where peripheral and central signals are integrated to control the output of gonadotropins, luteinizing hormone (LH) and follicles stimulating hormone (FSH). \r\nIn turn, LH and FSH act on the gonads to stimulate gametogenesis and the production of gonadal sex steroids and peptides, including estrogens, progesterone and testosterone, as well as inhibins, hence  a disorder characterized by the absence of spontaneous pubertal maturation, infertility and low levels of sex steroid hormones in association with inappropriately levels of LH and FSH(15).\r\nInfertility considered reproductive problem and has been negative effect and consequences for the individual or couple, very different, it incurs wrecking impact on the life of that individual for not full filling the biological role of parenthood (19). In   human it has always been considered as a social stigma and has often been treated as socially, mentally and physically damaging experience for the childless women rather than man (19). And the global deterioration of reproduction and fertility is a major concern and the modern therapeutic approaches to fight poor reproductive performance and infertility are expensive, less accessible, have long term treatment tenure and possess various side effects. Whereas, the herbal therapies, are better positioned to offer more holistic approaches to enhance reproductive performance and fertility rate (24). Ethiopia is endowed with a diverse biological resources including about 6, 500 species of higher plants, with approximately 12% endemic, hence making it one of the six plant biodiversity rich regions(25). Where most of the medicinal plants are confined and and have been used as a source of traditional medicine to treat different human and livestock ailments (27).In Ethiopia there is special herb traditional medicinal plants known as ferula communis s. belongs to Apiaceae family locally know as ‘Dog” have several medicinal and nutritional values in North Part of Ethiopia. Several studies shown that  ferula communis s  were used as tranquilizers and for the treatment of digestive disorders, rheumatism, headache, arthritis, dizziness and toothache, (29, 30), anti-hysteria (31), skin infections (32), and have different Biological activity including antimicrobial(33), anticancer(34),Cytotoxic and antiproliferative activity(35),and antioxidant activity(36). Due to the   presence of various bioactive molecules such as daucane derivatives (28), phytoestrogens (32, 38),ferutinin ,coumarin ferulenol, lapiferin, myrcene ,aristolene , monoterpenes (39-41).This plants has been used  to  enhance reproductive performance, fertility rate, nourishes and stimulates sexual organs and tissues. However, the potential of this plant in the regulation of reproduction fertility and nutritional values has not been studied. Therefore, this   study will be studied Nutritional Composition of ferula communis s. and its effects on Sex hormones, sexual desire Lipid profiles, Electrolytes and Histopathology of Reproductive organs in animal models to enhance fertility and reproductive rates\r\n1.2  Significance of the study \r\nThe finding of this research is expected to be baseline for further studies on  this traditional medicinal plant ,traditionally this plants is   not only for the treatment of reproduction and fertility problem, but also for the treatment of  material morbidity during birth due to excessive bleeding (locally  called it \'serakian\') additionally it have highly  nutritional value  , so this research project work is  going to  open  new   research area  in Ethiopia because Ethiopia  is rich in immense  traditional knowledge to  solve all health and nutritional problems by using natural traditional medicinal plants.  Researchers who are interested to conduct intense laboratory based research in this will be invited. \r\nOther researchers who have been conducting targeting on phytochemical derivatives drugs for the treatment of infertility and reproductive problems   will be added the existing data and initiate nationwide measure to address the issue. \r\nThe Ethiopian government and higher institute will become aware of about the use of Ethiopian traditional medical plants for battling of different diseases and enhance reproductive and fertility rates of both in human and livestock’s (Cattles). And they may be given priority to conservation, sustainable utilization to Ethiopian traditional medicinal plants and traditional knowledge’s. They should be giving high attention to practical based education police in the country.\r\n Poor reproduction and infertility of both human and animals is very serious public issue of the globe and hence the significance of   the result of this research project work is not limited only in the specific target group.  All developed and developing countries will be beneficiary. Human who are suffer from infertility and reproduction problem and countries or individual whose income depend on farming animals (husbandry) will be highly benefited from the fruit of this project. And the other significance of this research project is for a number of students trained at Masters and PhD levels in the area of, drug discovery, Reproductive health, Medical biochemistry, pharmacology, clinical nutrition, identification of responsible bioactive compounds from medicinal plants will be benefited and also a number of publications in reputable journals.','3.1 Study setting and period: The study will be conducted at Jimma University, Department of Biomedical Sciences, Biochemistry laboratory, from August,15,2022 to  Jun  30, 2023  \r\n3.2 Study design:  Experimental (Randomized Controlled study).\r\n3.3  Population: Wistar albino rats \r\n3.3.1  Source population: Healthy laboratory strain rat\r\n 3.3.2  Study population:  Female Wistar albino rats will be used in this  study\r\n3.4   Inclusion and exclusion criteria:\r\n3.4.1 Inclusion Criteria : Adult female wistar albino rats will be included in this study \r\n3.4.2 Exclusion Criteria: Unhealthy female wistar albino rats and male wistar albino rats will be excluded in this study. \r\n3.5   Sample Size Estimation and Sampling Procedures \r\nThe adequacy sample size will be determined by using resource equation method as we have multiple endpoints measurements of variables in our study. According to resource equation E is measured as follow:\r\nE=Total number of animals – Total number of groups. \r\nWhere E is the degree of freedom of analysis of variance (ANOVA). E between 10 and 20 is considered as adequate. E larger than 20 implicate more than adequate animals are used (23)\r\nIn our study  we intended to use  30  Wistar albino female rats per experiment, we will be conducted  6 groups  (3 treatment   and 2 control groups) in each  groups we will have 6  female wistar albino rats(n=6). \r\nE = 30–5= 25; E= 30\r\nThis implicates as we intended to utilize more than adequate animals, but this is justifiable as the sample of organ and tissue that can be obtained from samples is limited and increasing the number of animals used is obligatory for this study. Moreover, some procedures during data collection necessitate avoiding of the rat from other procedures to be done, so this also influence to double the number of animals that will be used in the study.  \r\n3.6 Plant collection and authentication\r\nWhole parts of plant will be collected in Alem-Sag forest South Gondar, Amhara regional state, Ethiopia on the month of Augusts 2022. The plant will be identified, authenticated and voucher specimen number will be given by the faculty member of Department of studies in Botany, Jimma University, Ethiopia and deposited as herbarium in the Biomedical Sciences, Department laboratory, Jimma University, Ethiopia.\r\n3.7 Chemicals and Instruments  \r\n3.7.1 Chemicals and reagents\r\nPhytochemical extraction reagents (water, methanol, ethanol, n-hexane ,)\r\nLipid profile (TG, LDL HDL, Cholesterol) reagents\r\nSex organs and tissues (uterus, ovary, hypothalamus, pituitary, thyroid and Mammary gland histopathology reagents \r\nFemale sex hormones (estrogen and progesterone, Testosterone. FSH, LH and inhibin) estimation reagents Ethers, Clomiphene citrate,\r\n3.7.2 Materials and Equipment\r\nIn this study Soxhlet Extraction Apparatus (Garg,500ml), Extraction thimbles 30x100,  Pyrex Erlenmeyer Flasks (50ml,100ml,250ml ,200ml, 500ml), volumetric Flask(50ml,100ml,100ml,2000ml), Ferret Cage, rat oral gavage deep freezer (-80oC), test tubes with graduations, centrifuge, Centrifuge test tube with gradations, Incubator, shaker, Micropipettes (p10.p50. p100, p200, p1000, p5000), Spectrophotometer, tissue homogenizer, scissors, Pyrex Beakers (50ml,100ml,200ml,250ml, 400ml,500ml,1000m), light microscope, Colored camera, Thin layer chromatography, HPLC.  Laboratory kits (FSH, LH).\r\nProximate Analysis reagents (Forced-air drying oven at 103 - 105oC, capable of maintaining temperature at ±1oC, Glass dish with lid  Desiccator  ,Top loading electronic balance, accurate to 0.5 mg.\r\n3.8  Preliminary Phytochemical Evaluation\r\n3.8.1. Preparation of the crude extract \r\n The collected and authenticated Matured stem of dog will be washed with tap water to remove soil and unwanted dust particles. Then the stem will be shade dried at room temperature to remove residual moisture and powdered using mechanical mixer and stored in air tight bottle. The adequate amount of powder materials   will be extracted in hot sohxlet apparatus by using preferred solvent. The volume of the solvents will be 500ml for 24 cycle during extraction. The extracted will be concentrated by evaporation (complete removal of solvent) and will be weighed and used for experiment.\r\n3.8.2. Phytochemical Screening \r\nBoth qualitative and quantitative analysis of the crude suit solvent extract of the stem of ferula communis   will be tested by using standard test tube procedures and thin layer chromatography. The presences of alkaloids, saponin, tannin, flavonoids, glycosides, steroids and terpenes. The qualitative results will be expressed as (+) for the presence and (-) for absent of the photochemical(37).Whereas quantitative results will be expressed by standard Units.\r\n3.9 Nutritional Evaluation\r\n3.9. 1 Proximate Analysis\r\nProximate analysis will be carried out on the stems of  ferula communis s to determine its nutritional values with regards to protein, carbohydrate, moisture, ash contents, crude fiber, minerals and crude fat contents using standard methods described by (16).\r\n3.10   In-Vitro Antioxidant Assay\r\n The antioxidant activity of  MP extracts will be measured using a DPPH free radical scavenging assay according to the modified method described by(38). Briefly, 0.2mM of DPPH-methanol solution will be added to equal volume of extract dissolved in methanol at various concentrations (5μg–500μg/ml). The mixture will be vortexed vigorously and incubated for 30 min at RT. The absorbance will be spectrophotometrically measured at 517 nm. Ascorbic acid will be used as positive control. The scavenging activity will be calculated using the following equation: \r\nDPPH Scavenger activity (%)= (AC-AS/AC) x100, where Ac is the absorbance of reaction without samples and As is the absorbance of tested extract. The antioxidant activity will be expressed as median effective concentration (EC50) where the concentration caused 50% reduction of DPPH.\r\n3.10.1 Toxicity assays	\r\nAcute toxicity test of the plant extract will be performed on Twenty-four female Wistar albino rats weighing about 190 g and 250g. The rats will be selected for the test because they are frequently more sensitive to the toxicity of test compounds than males. The rats will be randomly grouped in four groups (n=6). Group I-III will be given in the doses of 150 mg/kg, 300 mg/kg and 600 mg/kg of body weight orally respectively , by  using the ‘Up-and- Down’ protocols(17), accordance with the Organization for Economic Development (OECD ) guideline no. 425(4(18) .For  2, 4, 6, 8, 12 and 24 h. The number of animals that survived after 24 and 48 h will be also noted.  Group IV serve as a normal control will be  received  tap water and normal diet  ad  libitum(19). All rats will be individually observed daily for general behavioral and body weight changes, hazardous symptoms and mortality for a period of 28 days’ post treatment. Then LD50 will be predicted.\r\n3.11 Experimental Animals\r\n The study will be conducted using 30 adult female  Wistar rats, aged at least 3 months and weighing between 190 g and 250 g. The animals will be purchased from School of veterinary medicine, College of Agriculture, Jimma University, Ethiopia and housed in the animal house of Department of Biochemistry, Jimma University, Ethiopia. The rat will be randomly allocated in to 6 groups (3 experimental and 2 control groups). In each group there will be 6 female rats. The rats will be acclimated to the laboratory environment for 7 days prior to the study. Animal house will be well maintained under standard hygienic condition at (26 ± 2) °C, relative humidity of 44%–55%, 12 h day and night cycle, and with food and water ad libitum. The care and handling of the animals will be in accordance with the internationally accepted standard guidelines for use of animals (20). \r\nThe rats will be orally gavaged with methanol stem extract (MSE) of plant in the following manner after the LD50 of the plant will be determined.\r\nGroup -1: Normal control with Distilled water \r\nGroup -2:Rats will be fed orally 0.6 mg/kg Clomiphene citrate (CC) as positive control \r\nGroup -3: Rats will be fed orally with lowest dose (D1) plant extract\r\nGroup -4: Rats will be fed orally with medium doses (D2)  of plant extract\r\nGroup -5: Rats will be fed orally with the highest dose (D3) of plant extract. During the treatment period the sexual desire, behavioral changes, physical change will be observed; body weight changes also will be recorded. \r\n3.12 Data collection tools\r\n3.12.1 Behavioral observations\r\nThroughout the experimental periods ,the sexual behaviors of experimental   rats will be observed and  recorded using video camera by customizing the methods used by(21). All experimental groups will be exposed to the T-maze and Passive avoidance tests to assess spatial learning and memory, respectively. The T-maze test will include the spontaneous alternation test and rewarded alternation test. The T-maze test will be done according to the protocol described by(22) . Passive avoidance test will be done according to procedure described by(23) .\r\n3.12.1 Tissue sampling, processing, and histological observation   \r\nAt the end of experimental rats from each group (both treated and control groups) will be sacrificed using an overdose of ether anesthesia and blood samples will be taken for hormone assay and   lipid profiles, and Uterus, brain, ovary and mammary gland will be   dissected and fixed in neutral formalin at room temperature for 24 h. After dehydration in ethanol, blocks will be embedded in paraffin. Serial sections will be cut (6 µm) and stained with toluidine blue (1% aqueous solution) and Alcian blue-safranin (pH: 1.0, buffered by using 0.1 N HCl) (24), and will be examined under light microscope for assessment of  sex hormones secretion and physiological changes.  \r\n3.13 Biochemical assays\r\n3.13.1 Blood Samples Collection, Serum Preparation, Hormonal assay, Lipid profile and electrolytes \r\n  To perform the blood biochemical tests, blood sampling will be done for 10 days. Blood sample will be collected by cardiac puncture method after sacrificed of rats using an overdose of ether anesthesia. The blood samples will be centrifuged and the sera of samples will be separated at 3000 RPM enabled 15 minutes. The concentrations of LH, FSH, estrogen ,progesterone estradiol, an Testosterone  in 100 µl Serum will be determined by(25). The kit is a competitive inhibition enzyme immunoassay technique for the in vitro quantitative measurement of rat FSH in serum, plasma and other biological fluids. For research use only, the sensitivity of the kit is dose of FSH is typically less than 1.11ng/ml. Results of LH, FSH and progesterone and Testosterone estrogen will be expressed as ng/m/l, while estradiol as pg/m/l(26).Whereas the lipid profile (Serum triacylglycerol level will be  estimated using (27) test kit and VLDL-cholesterol will be  calculated using the formula TG/2.2 mmol/l. Low-density lipoprotein (LDL) cholesterol will be  determined by differential subtraction of the sum of the cholesterol fractions from the total cholesterol. Na+ K+ and Cl- were determined by flame photometry using(28). The absorbance of all the tests were determined using spectrophotometer.\r\n3.14 Study Variables \r\n     3.14.1       Dependent t variables \r\nSerum lipid profile level (Triglyceride, LDL, HLDL, Cholesterol), Body weight, Histopathology of Tissue and organs, Concentration of sex hormones (LH, FSH, estrogen, progesterone of Testosterone)\r\n3.14.2 Independent variables    Sex, Age, Concentration of extract, Standard d\r\n\r\nFigure-1: Proposed procedures to investigate the effects of Plant Ferula communis s. extracts on, Sex hormones, sexual desire, Lipid profiles, Electrolytes and Histopathology of Reproductive organs and tissues in female Wistar to enhance fertility and reproductive rates,2022 (see the attached proposal).\r\n\r\n3.14 Quality Assurance\r\nData quality will be maintained by using trained personnel for laboratory animal handling, sample collection and sample processing. Moreover, by adherence to scientific procedures and kit supplier companies’ guidelines the quality of the research and its outcome will be maintained.\r\n3.15 Data Management and Statistical Analysis\r\n  Data will be checked, cleaned, and entered in to Epi-data software version 3.1, and then it will be exported to SPSS version 24.0 software for analysis. Differences between groups will be assessed by one-way analysis of variance (ANOVA). Differences between pair of means will be assessed by the Least Significant Difference (LSD) test. When variance will not homogeneous a nonparametric analysis will be performed. Results will be presented by tables, graphs and charts, and considered as statistically significant if P≤ 0.05. \r\n3.16 Plan for dissemination and ensuring utilization of findings\r\nThe research result report will be submitted to the college of health sciences research and postgraduate coordinating office.  The research results will be disseminated by publishing on reputable journals, through poster and seminar presentations.','<h1><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">1<strong>.</strong>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Matzuk MM, Lamb DJ. The biology of infertility: research advances and clinical challenges. Nature medicine. 2008;14(11):1197-213.</span></span></h1>\r\n\r\n<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">2.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Comizzoli P, Songsasen N, Wildt DE. Protecting and extending fertility for females of wild and endangered mammals.&nbsp; Oncofertility: Springer; 2010. p. 87-100.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">3.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Songsasen N, Comizzoli P. Protecting and extending fertility for females of wild and endangered mammals.&nbsp; Textbook of oncofertility research and practice: Springer; 2019. p. 401-12.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">4.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Wildt DE, Wemmer C. Sex and wildlife: the role of reproductive science in conservation. Biodiversity &amp; Conservation. 1999;8(7):965-76.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">5.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Vayena E, Rowe PJ, Griffin PD. Current practices and controversies in assisted reproduction: report of a meeting on medical, ethical and social aspects of assisted reproduction, held at WHO Headquarters in Geneva, Switzerland: World Health Organization; 2002.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">6.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Barrett JR. Fertile grounds of inquiry: environmental effects on human reproduction. National Institute of Environmental Health Sciences; 2006.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">7.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Miyamoto T, Tsujimura A, Miyagawa Y, Koh E, Namiki M, Sengoku K. Male infertility and its causes in human. Advances in urology. 2011;2012.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">8.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Huo X, Chen D, He Y, Zhu W, Zhou W, Zhang J. Bisphenol-A and female infertility: a possible role of gene-environment interactions. International journal of environmental research and public health. 2015;12(9):11101-16.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">9.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Bancroft J. The endocrinology of sexual arousal. Journal of endocrinology. 2005;186(3):411-27.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">10.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Jost A, Vigier B, Pr&Eacute;Pin J, Perchellet JP, editors. Studies on sex differentiation in mammals. Proceedings of the 1972 Laurentian Hormone Conference; 1973: Elsevier.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">11.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; McMichael AJ, Potter JD. Reproduction, endogenous and exogenous sex hormones, and colon cancer: a review and hypothesis. Journal of the National Cancer Institute. 1980;65(6):1201-7.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">12.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Tapanainen JS, Aittom&auml;ki K, Min J, Vaskivuo T, Huhtaniemi IT. Men homozygous for an inactivating mutation of the follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH) receptor gene present variable suppression of spermatogenesis and fertility. Nature genetics. 1997;15(2):205-6.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">13.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Marrocco J, McEwen BS. Sex in the brain: hormones and sex differences. Dialogues in clinical neuroscience. 2016;18(4):373.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">14.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Hiemke C, Banger M, Kohsik R, Hundt M, Ghraf R. Actions of sex hormones on the brain. Progress in Neuro-Psychopharmacology and Biological Psychiatry. 1992;16(3):377-88.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">15.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Kaiser UB. Understanding reproductive endocrine disorders. Nature Reviews Endocrinology. 2015;11(11):640-1.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">16.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Basumatary S, Nath N. Assessment of chemical compositions and in vitro antioxidant properties of Musa balbisiana Colla inflorescence. International Journal of Pharmaceutical Research. 2018;10(1):80-5.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">17.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Co-operation OfE, Development. Test No. 425: acute oral toxicity: up-and-down procedure: OECD Publishing; 2008.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">18.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Doak S, Manshian B, Jenkins G, Singh N. In vitro genotoxicity testing strategy for nanomaterials and the adaptation of current OECD guidelines. Mutation Research/Genetic Toxicology and Environmental Mutagenesis. 2012;745(1-2):104-11.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">19.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Mothana RA, Mentel R, Reiss C, Lindequist U. Phytochemical screening and antiviral activity of some medicinal plants from the island Soqotra. Phytotherapy Research: An International Journal Devoted to Pharmacological and Toxicological Evaluation of Natural Product Derivatives. 2006;20(4):298-302.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">20.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Health NIo. Guide for the care and use of laboratory animals: National Academies; 1985.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">21.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Boissy A, Aubert A, D&eacute;sir&eacute; L, Greiveldinger L, Delval E, Veissier IJAW. Cognitive sciences to relate ear postures to emotions in sheep. 2011;20(1):47.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">22.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; De-Mello N, Carobrez AJBP. Elevated T-maze as an animal model of memory: effects of scopolamine. 2002;13(2):139-48.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">23.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Sadegzadeh F, Sakhaie N, Isazadehfar K, Saadati HJNL. Effects of exposure to enriched environment during adolescence on passive avoidance memory, nociception, and prefrontal BDNF level in adult male and female rats. 2020;732:135133.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">24.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Aydın Y, Tun&ccedil;el N, G&uuml;rer F, Tun&ccedil;el M, Koşar M, Oflaz G. Ovarian, uterine and brain mast cells in female rats: cyclic changes and contribution to tissue histamine. Comparative Biochemistry and Physiology Part A: Molecular &amp; Integrative Physiology. 1998;120(2):255-62.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">25.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Steelman SL, Pohley FM. Assay of the follicle stimulating hormone based on the augmentation with human chorionic gonadotropin. Endocrinology. 1953;53(6):604-16.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">26.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Faccio L, Da Silva AS, Tonin AA, Fran&ccedil;a RT, Gressler LT, Copetti MM, et al. Serum levels of LH, FSH, estradiol and progesterone in female rats experimentally infected by Trypanosoma evansi. Experimental parasitology. 2013;135(1):110-5.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">27.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Eveland SS, Milot DP, Guo Q, Chen Y, Hyland SA, Peterson LB, et al. A high-precision fluorogenic cholesteryl ester transfer protein assay compatible with animal serum and 3456-well assay technology. Analytical biochemistry. 2007;368(2):239-49.</span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\">28.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Crawford RA. Determination of Sodium and Potassium by Flame Photometry: University of Illinois at Urbana-Champaign; 1951.</span></span></p>','.To study Nutritional Composition of Ferula communis s. extracts and its effect on, Sex hormones, sexual desire, Lipid profiles, Electrolytes, and Histopathology of Reproductive organs in female Wistar rats to enhance fertility and reproductive rates.',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,'Ferula communis s. nutritional composition, reproductive, fertilities',10,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'Proposal-6291ff62acd25560324760.docx'),(123,286,13,19,'Executive Summary \r\nBackground:  Antimicrobial resistance (AMR) has been posed greatest challenges to human and animal health worldwide. Human deaths due to AMR are projected to be 10 million worldwide, surpassing casualties resulting from diabetes and cancer combined with an estimated economic output loss of $100 trillion by 2050. The major consequence of resistance is the escalating difficulty of successfully treating bacterial infections with the currently available limited array of antimicrobials. There were about 480,000 new global cases of multidrug-resistant tuberculosis in only 2013. Moreover, infection of AMR pathogens will lead to an increased risk of mortality, longer treatment and stays in hospital, and the use of more expensive and more toxic second-line therapies which increases medical costs to the patients and health care facilities. This makes AMR one of the pertinent public health problem and need evidence to design policy and strategies to mitigate and contain health and economic burden of it. Hence, the aim of this study will be to measure magnitude of AMR and its impact on health-related quality of life and economic burden among admitted patients and their families in Jimma University Medical Center, Southwest Ethiopia.\r\nMethods: A longitudinal study design will be used to collect data on the magnitude of AMR, health related quality of life and economic burden of it among patients and their family. Both quantitative and qualitative data will be gathered and presented in triangulation. Systematic review of literatures will be done to evaluate previous trend of AMR and its economic burden and health related quality of life in the Ethiopia. For all objectives descriptive statistics will be used to summarize the characteristics of the study population. Data normality will be checked using the Kolmogorov–Smirnov test. Continuous variables will be compared between groups using the Wilcoxon rank-sum test. ANCOVA models, adjusted for age and sex, will be used to compare continuous variables across the study groups. Variables will also be log-transformed to achieve a normal distribution. Categorical variables will be compared between groups using Fisher’s exact test. Spearman’s rho coefficient will be used to assess the correlation between continuous variables.  During the process of management, frequencies & percentages will be calculated to describe the data by tables or figures using appropriate commands in SPSS version 25. During the process of analysis, a receiver operating characteristic (ROC) curve analysis (to estimate the predictive capacity of the logistic regression) and Hosmer-Lemeshow goodness of fit test (examine how well each of the models fit the data) will be executed for each regression model (142). Based on the result of the two tests, the variables which have statistically significant (a p-value of < 0.25) associations with the AMR in the bivariate analysis will further be a candidate for stepwise multiple logistic regression model to control the effect of confounding variables. Finally, the Variables which have statistically significant (a p-value of < 0.05) associations with the AMR will be considered as potential risk factors for the dependent variable.  General linear model (GLM) repeated measures ANOVA was used to determine a possible significant interaction effect and variation in the pattern of subjects over time, as well as which independent variables were predictive of changes in summary scores. MAXQDA version 20 will used for the qualitative data analysis.\r\nProject duration and Budget: The project will be completed within 2015 budget year and a total of 525,205.00 ETB will be required to successfully accomplish this project.',NULL,'Measuring magnitude, economic burden and health related quality of life for patients diagnosed with antimicrobial resistant bacteria, Jimma University Medical Center, Southwest Ethiopia.','2022-05-28 16:33:18','Identifying magnitude, economic burden and health related quality of life among patients with antimicrobial resistance will be helpful to design evidence based policy and containment strategies by health policy makers, planners and managers. Furthermore, it will enhance attention given to prevention and control of AMR by government and development partners to produce applicable interventions to reduce burden of it and improve welfare of patients and their families.','0275e9e6d79e8983eab1d2a776cdbc13','completed','Microorganisms are among the best friends of the mankind and the worst enemies. Apart from their useful role, their effect as a threat factor to humans, animals and plants persists in the form of many human and animal infections and food spoilage, which severely burdens the individual and global economy, with bacteria taking the lead. The growth of human populations and economic development has led to an increase in municipal solid waste, which further exposes people to more bacteria(2) (3). In order to combat the worst outcome that can happen during exposure to bacteria, humans have developed and used several anti-microbial agents. However, people often misuse antimicrobial agents, either intentionally or unintentionally, and contribute to the emergence of antimicrobial resistance (AMR) in bacteria, with potentially adverse consequences for human health. This condition particularly affects hospital-based treatments, where people are constantly and extensively based on antibiotic treatment. The burden of AMR worldwide is extensive and likely to spread. The resistance swiftly dynamical, with some resistance carrying the possibility of encoded on a transferable gene giving a chance of resistance spread among bacteria of the same strain (10). \r\nThe potential risks from AMR organisms in the water environment include infection by AMR pathogens like resistant Campylobacter spp., Shigella spp., Salmonella typhi and other Salmonella spp present in the water; colonization and subsequent infection with opportunistic pathogens, such as Escherichia coli  and enterococci; and transient colonization with AMR organisms that can then be a source of AMR genes for the normal flora through horizontal gene transfer; should an endogenous infection occur, it may be with an AMR strain.\r\nEven if AMR affects every country irrespective of its level of income (12), LMIC bear the greatest burden jeopardising the achievement of the Sustainable Development Goals (SDG). Poor waste disposal coupled with inadequate infection control stimulates the spread of AMR, which can become endemic in the community (11). \r\nAntimicrobial resistance is among the most common priority areas identified by both national and international agencies, is mushrooming as a silent pandemic (16).  \r\nTackling the threat to human health presented by antimicrobial resistance remains a challenge for health care systems across the world (17). In addition to the intrinsic characteristic of micro-organisms, the prevalence of clinically relevant antimicrobial resistance (AMR) has been accelerated by the inappropriate use of antimicrobial agents, in turn driven by several factors including suboptimal prescribing and inadequate public adherence to recommended behavior such as completion of prescribed antibiotics (18).\r\nGlobally, infection due to AMR strains kills at least 75,000 people every year worldwide, and causes extended hospital stays. Antimicrobial resistance results mainly from indiscriminate antibiotic usage in humans, animals and agriculture, and from the rapid emergence and dissemination of resistant pathogens (19). \r\nAnnual human deaths due to AMR are projected to be 10 million worldwide, surpassing casualties resulting from diabetes and cancer combined with an estimated economic output loss of $100 trillion by 2050. The major consequence of resistance is the escalating difficulty of successfully treating bacterial infections with the currently available limited array of antimicrobials. In 2013 alone, there were about 480,000 new global cases of multidrug-resistant tuberculosis (20).\r\nInfection with AMR pathogens will lead to an increased risk of mortality, longer treatment and stays in hospital, and the use of more expensive and more toxic second-line therapies which increases medical costs to the patients and health care facilities (10). \r\nGenerally, the subject area needs a sensible and continuous research activity. However, to the reach of this review, studies haven’t been conducted continuously while some limitations were identified within the studies that are conducted elsewhere, including the Ethiopian case. To highlight some of the limitation; First, most of the performed studies aren’t interleaved with different methodologies, and are typically conducted out of the present study setting. Secondly, some studies have thoroughly based on clinical data or based on preliminary diagnostic laboratory tests causing overwhelmed laboratory inputs. Thirdly, some findings are ambitious because of several reasons like non-random sampling, unclear inclusion criteria, very wide OR, cumbersome reports or unideal component of authors. Therefore, this study is expected to address the limitations of previous studies and capitalize information about the magnitude of AMR and its impact on health-related quality of life and economic burden among admitted patients and their families at JUMC.','Microorganisms are among the best friends of the mankind and the worst enemies. Apart from their useful role, their effect as a threat factor to humans, animals and plants persists in the form of many human and animal infections and food spoilage, which severely burdens the individual and global economy, with bacteria taking the lead. The growth of human populations and economic development has led to an increase in municipal solid waste, which further exposes people to more bacteria(2) (3). In order to combat the worst outcome that can happen during exposure to bacteria, humans have developed and used several anti-microbial agents. However, people often misuse antimicrobial agents, either intentionally or unintentionally, and contribute to the emergence of antimicrobial resistance (AMR) in bacteria, with potentially adverse consequences for human health. This condition particularly affects hospital-based treatments, where people are constantly and extensively based on antibiotic treatment. The burden of AMR worldwide is extensive and likely to spread. The resistance swiftly dynamical, with some resistance carrying the possibility of encoded on a transferable gene giving a chance of resistance spread among bacteria of the same strain (10). \r\nThe potential risks from AMR organisms in the water environment include infection by AMR pathogens like resistant Campylobacter spp., Shigella spp., Salmonella typhi and other Salmonella spp present in the water; colonization and subsequent infection with opportunistic pathogens, such as Escherichia coli  and enterococci; and transient colonization with AMR organisms that can then be a source of AMR genes for the normal flora through horizontal gene transfer; should an endogenous infection occur, it may be with an AMR strain.\r\nEven if AMR affects every country irrespective of its level of income (12), LMIC bear the greatest burden jeopardising the achievement of the Sustainable Development Goals (SDG). Poor waste disposal coupled with inadequate infection control stimulates the spread of AMR, which can become endemic in the community (11). \r\nAntimicrobial resistance is among the most common priority areas identified by both national and international agencies, is mushrooming as a silent pandemic (16).  \r\nTackling the threat to human health presented by antimicrobial resistance remains a challenge for health care systems across the world (17). In addition to the intrinsic characteristic of micro-organisms, the prevalence of clinically relevant antimicrobial resistance (AMR) has been accelerated by the inappropriate use of antimicrobial agents, in turn driven by several factors including suboptimal prescribing and inadequate public adherence to recommended behavior such as completion of prescribed antibiotics (18).\r\nGlobally, infection due to AMR strains kills at least 75,000 people every year worldwide, and causes extended hospital stays. Antimicrobial resistance results mainly from indiscriminate antibiotic usage in humans, animals and agriculture, and from the rapid emergence and dissemination of resistant pathogens (19). \r\nAnnual human deaths due to AMR are projected to be 10 million worldwide, surpassing casualties resulting from diabetes and cancer combined with an estimated economic output loss of $100 trillion by 2050. The major consequence of resistance is the escalating difficulty of successfully treating bacterial infections with the currently available limited array of antimicrobials. In 2013 alone, there were about 480,000 new global cases of multidrug-resistant tuberculosis (20).\r\nInfection with AMR pathogens will lead to an increased risk of mortality, longer treatment and stays in hospital, and the use of more expensive and more toxic second-line therapies which increases medical costs to the patients and health care facilities (10). \r\nGenerally, the subject area needs a sensible and continuous research activity. However, to the reach of this review, studies haven’t been conducted continuously while some limitations were identified within the studies that are conducted elsewhere, including the Ethiopian case. To highlight some of the limitation; First, most of the performed studies aren’t interleaved with different methodologies, and are typically conducted out of the present study setting. Secondly, some studies have thoroughly based on clinical data or based on preliminary diagnostic laboratory tests causing overwhelmed laboratory inputs. Thirdly, some findings are ambitious because of several reasons like non-random sampling, unclear inclusion criteria, very wide OR, cumbersome reports or unideal component of authors. Therefore, this study is expected to address the limitations of previous studies and capitalize information about the magnitude of AMR and its impact on health-related quality of life and economic burden among admitted patients and their families at JUMC.','<p style=\"margin-left:43px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">1. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; World Health Organization; Resistance F factsheet on antimicrobial. oNE DHEALTH. 2021. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">2. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Sharma C, Rokana N, Chandra M, Singh BP, Gulhane RD, Gill JPS, et al. Antimicrobial resistance: Its surveillance, impact, and alternative management strategies in dairy animals. Front Vet Sci. 2018;4(JAN):1&ndash;27. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">3. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Abdel-Shafy HI, Mansour MSM. Solid waste issue: Sources, composition, disposal, recycling, and valorization. Egypt J Pet [Internet]. 2018;27(4):1275&ndash;90. Available from: https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ejpe.2018.07.003</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">4. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Perry B, Grace D. The impacts of livestock diseases and their control on growth and development&nbsp; processes that are pro-poor. Philos Trans R Soc London Ser B, Biol&nbsp; Sci. 2009 Sep;364(1530):2643&ndash;55. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">5. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Lima T, Domingues S, Silva GJ Da. Manure as a potential hotspot for antibiotic resistance dissemination by horizontal gene transfer events. Vet Sci. 2020;7(3). </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">6. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Bernal MP, Alburquerque JA, Moral R. Composting of animal manures and chemical criteria for compost maturity assessment.&nbsp; A review. Bioresour Technol. 2009 Nov;100(22):5444&ndash;53. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">7. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Savin M, Alexander J, Bierbaum G, Hammerl JA, Hembach N, Schwartz T, et al. Antibiotic-resistant bacteria, antibiotic resistance genes, and antibiotic residues in wastewater from a poultry slaughterhouse after conventional and advanced treatments. Sci Rep [Internet]. 2021;11(1):1&ndash;11. Available from: https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-021-96169-y</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">8. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Udikovic-Kolic N, Wichmann F, Broderick NA, Handelsman J. Bloom of resident antibiotic-resistant bacteria in soil following manure&nbsp; fertilization. Proc Natl Acad Sci U S A. 2014 Oct;111(42):15202&ndash;7. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">9. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Friedman ND, Temkin E, Carmeli Y. The negative impact of antibiotic resistance. Clin Microbiol Infect&nbsp; Off Publ Eur&nbsp; Soc Clin Microbiol Infect Dis. 2016 May;22(5):416&ndash;22. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">10. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Water AE, Issue H. Editorial. Food Addit Contam. 1984;1(2):72. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">11. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Marschang, Sascha E de S. No Title [Internet]. Improper waste disposal in healthcare facilities and other hotspots can contribute to the global proliferation of antibiotic resistant bacteria. 2016 [cited 2022 Jan 5]. Available from: https://hospitalhealthcare.com/latest-issue-2016/waste-disposal-in-healthcare-and-effects-on-amr/</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">12. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Tayler L. AMR and the Global Action Plan. </span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">13. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Oyebode O, Kandala N-B, Chilton PJ, Lilford RJ. Use of traditional medicine in middle-income countries: a WHO-SAGE study. Health Policy Plan [Internet]. 2016 Oct 1;31(8):984&ndash;91. Available from: https://doi.org/10.1093/heapol/czw022</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:43px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">14. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Nascimenta, GGF, Locatelli, J., Freitas, PC and Silva, GL, &ldquo;Antibacterial activity of plant extracts and phytochemicals on antibiotic-resistant bacteria&rdquo;, Brazilian Journal of Microbiology, Vol. 31, 2000. pp. 247-256.</span></span></span></span></p>','To determine the magnitude, economic burden and health related quality of life for patients diagnosed with antimicrobial resistant bacteria, Jimma University Medical Center, Southwest Ethiopia.',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,'Antimicrobial resistance, economic burden, quality of life',10,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'Proposal-62922468453aa080406548.doc'),(124,362,14,18,'Executive Summary\r\nBackground:  Cancer is a leading cause of morbidity and mortality worldwide. This results in considerable mental, physical, and emotional stress in patients. Quality can be defined as the degree to which health services for individuals and populations increase the likelihood of desired health outcomes and are consistent with current the professional knowledge.  Because of the nature and impact of the disease, and its treatment, measurement of the quality of cancer care is important to bring to the attention of healthcare providers to improve the quality of service.\r\nObjective: This study aimed to assess the quality of cancer care among cancer patients from the perspective of patients, health care providers, and services in the adult oncology unit of Jimma medical center, Jimma town, southwest Ethiopia 2022.\r\nMethods: Facility-based mixed cross-sectional and pre-experimental study designs will be conducted at Jimma Medical Center (JMC) from August to November 2022. All sampled patients who have follow up at the oncologic unit of Jimma medical center and purposively selected healthcare professionals who provide oncologic services for cancer patients during the study period will be included. A pretest will be done to ensure the validity of the questionnaires. The data will be entered into Epidata version 4.6. Then the data will be exported to SPSS version 23.0 for statistical analysis. Bivariate and multivariate logistic regression analyses will be conducted. Variables with a p-value of < 0.05 will be considered statistically significant. Paired t-test analysis will be performed to assess the effect of training to improve the knowledge, attitude and skill of health care providers.     \r\nBudget: The total budget required for the present study is 394,187.2 ETB',NULL,'QUALITY OF CANCER CARE AMONG CANCER PATIENTS AT ADULT ONCOLOGIC UNIT OF JIMMA MEDICAL CENTER, JIMMA TOWN, SOUTHWEST ETHIOPIA 2022','2022-05-28 17:22:55','The outcomes of the present study are patient satisfaction, barriers to the provision of oncologic service, and improvement in the knowledge, attitude, and skill of health care providers in the adult oncology unit of Jimma medical center.','87caa2f10dadf38fbdc8072026fb5c80','completed','1. Background and rationale\r\nCancer is a group of diseases characterized by the uncontrolled growth and spread of abnormal cells in the body (Grabowski, Aagaard-Hansen, Willaing, & Jensen, 2017). Cancer cells exhibit abnormalities in the regulation of cell division and survival. DNA defects in mismatch repair pathway ignore growth-regulating signals in the environment surrounding that cell invade tissue and metastasize  through blood or lymphatics to distant organs (Boyle & Levin, 2008; Nam et al., 2017).\r\nCancer ranks as a leading cause of death and an important barrier to decrease life expectancy in every country of the world (Bray, Laversanne, Weiderpass, & Soerjomataram, 2021). According to estimates from the World Health Organization (WHO), cancer is the first or second leading cause of death before the age of 70 in 112 of 183 countries and ranks third or fourth in 23 countries (Yu, 2021). Overall, the burden of cancer incidence and mortality is rapidly growing worldwide; this reflecting both aging and population growth as well as changes in the prevalence and distribution of the main risk factors for cancer, several of which are associated with socioeconomic development (Gersten & Wilmoth, 2002). While evidence shows that cancer in Ethiopia are highly prevalent in which peoples are diagnosed made at the later stage of the disease (Islam, Billah, Hossain, & Oldroyd, 2017).\r\nCancer is becoming an increasingly a chronic disease. Patient care is shifting from a model that was focused on the immediate need to treat the tumor to a more holistic approach in the care of the patient to ensure both quantity and quality of life. Consideration of these needs begins before the active treatment phase and continues after the transition to long-term survivorship. This shift from acute cancer care to treating cancer as a chronic disease has corresponded to a more recent focus on quality cancer care (Hess & Pohl, 2013). \r\nCancer is a major public health cocern worldwide. The global burden of cancer is estimated to have risen to 18.1 million new cases and 9.6 million deaths. Globally, the total number of people who survive 5 years after a cancer diagnosis is estimated to be 43.8 million. On the other hand in Europe, cancer accounts for 23.4% of the global cancer cases and 20.3% of deaths. While in America, cancer accounts for 21.0% of incidence and 14.4% of mortality. In contrast to cancer deaths in Asia (57.3%) and Africa (7.3%) were higher than new cases (48.4% and 5.8%, respectively), in the same manner in Ethiopia, it is the second leading cause of death among the adult population ( (Siegel, Miller, & Jemal, 2020). This might be due to the alarmingly growing population together with lifestyle changes that will designate the rising liability for cancer. It accounts for approximately 5.8% of total national mortality. Although population-based data do not exist in the country except for Addis Ababa, it is estimated that the annual cancer incidence is around 60,960 cases and the annual mortality is over 44,000. For people under the age of 75 years, the risk of being diagnosed with cancer is 11.3% and the risk of dying from the disease is 9.4%. Factors such as Stage of cancer, type of cancer, length of hospital stay, comorbid condition, reason of admission, past medical history,  sociodemographic factors, attention to care, timeliness of care, social support system, availability of information, availability of communication, electronic recording systems, equitability of  access, Patient social support programs, Policies, staff workload, working experience, training or awareness of new research, multidisciplinary teams, coordination of care, responsibility for care, communication, trust, caring behavior, comfort, social support, health cost, coordinated care, treatment guidelines and policies were identified to be associated with quality of  cancer care (Colosia et al., 2011). \r\nThe quality of health care can be precisely defined and accurately measured, but there are many different perspectives on quality to consider. Patients tend to evaluate care in terms of its responsiveness to their individual needs and may expect and value access to, and choice of services, doctors, and treatments that maximize their ability to work and enjoy life. Physicians may view quality in terms of their ability to exercise medical judgment to optimize patient outcomes. From a health plan\'s perspective, quality might mean efficiency, appropriate use of diagnostic and therapeutic technologies, and maintenance of high levels of patient satisfaction with care. From a public health perspective, quality may be reflected in high levels of access to primary care, effective prevention, and low morbidity and mortality rates (McGlynn, 1997). \r\nThe Institute of Medicine defines quality as the degree to which health services for individuals and populations increase the likelihood of desired health outcomes and are consistent with current professional knowledge (Larson, 1991). In practical terms, poor quality can mean too much care (e.g., unnecessary tests, medications, or procedures, with associated risks and side effects); too little care (e.g., not receiving a lifesaving surgical procedure); or wrong care (e.g., medicines that should not be given together, poor surgical techniques). Good quality means providing patients with appropriate services in a technically competent manner, with good communication, shared decision making, and cultural sensitivity (Citation, 1999).\r\nEfforts to measure the quality of cancer care in Ethiopia are still an early stage. National organizations conducting quality assessments have focused primarily on prevention and screening, although they are now moving toward assessing the quality of diagnosis and management. Research studies on the quality of cancer care have been limited, with most concentrating on cancers with comparatively higher prevalence and more evidence supporting clinical practice. Much of the literature and popular beliefs have focused on the perspective of the patient but do not cover variables from the perspective of health care providers and services which might have greatly contribute to the improvement of the quality of the service. The majority of the previous studies were not interventional.\r\n Furthermore, oncology services provided in our country are few and wholly inadequate, not well-prepared to bear the growing burden of cancer. In addition, there are hardly any fully functioning public radiotherapy palliative and rehabilitative center in the entire country (Haileselassie, Mulugeta, Tigeneh, Kaba, & Labisso, 2019). Thus the quality of cancer care can enhance the chance of early detection and treatment by attracting and retaining patients, which might reduce the risk of poor prognosis and high cancer-related morbidity and mortality rates.\r\n To the best our knowledge, this kind of study is limited to Ethiopia in general and in the study area in particular. Therefore, this study aimed to assess the quality of cancer care among cancer patients from the perspectives of patients, health care providers and services in the adult oncology unit of Jimma medical center, Jimma town, southwest Ethiopia 2022.\r\nThe findings of this study may help provide information regarding the quality of cancer care and initiation of healthcare-seeking behaviors of patients since the improvement of quality will have a positive effect on attracting and retaining patients which can make them aware of the risk factors, for early detection and diagnosis. The findings of this study can be used by health care planners, policymakers, stakeholders, and non-governmental organizations to revise and develop guidelines and implementation activities regarding the quality of cancer care. Health professionals will be provided health education and which might have a great contribution to improving the quality of cancer care since it is directly associated with patient satisfaction and provision of care additionally the findings of this study will contribute to the researchers as baseline data for further studies on quality of cancer care in adult oncology and can serve as a starting point for future initiatives designed to improve quality cancer care and help identify quality measures that are important to patients and providers.','Study area, period and design\r\nThis study is conducted in Jimma Medical Center in Jimma town, Jimma zone, Oromiya region, Southwest Ethiopia.  Jimma town is located 352 km Southwest of Addis Ababa. JMC is one of the oldest hospitals in Ethiopia and was established in 1930 G.C. Besides playing the role as of university teaching center, it is a referral institution for the south-west sub-region of the country that is allegedly responsible for 15-20 million people with a very wide catchment area that covers 17,500 km2. The hospital provides inpatient, outpatients, emergency, delivery and pharmaceutical services. It is a teaching tertiary hospital with fifteen units, and four major clinical departments including internal medicine, surgery, pediatrics and gynecology/obstetrics. Other clinical departments such as oncology, dentistry, ophthalmology, psychiatry, dermatology and follow-up clinics, and more than eight hundred beds.  It provides clinical services to the hospital for approximately 87, 280 outpatients and 18, 776 emergency visit attendances annually. An average of 428 outpatients having to follow up at the oncologic unit in JMC for two months. Currently the oncologic unit of JMC is run by three senior oncologist specialists, three general practitioners (GP), 6 oncology nurses (two MSc in oncology nursing and four BSc nurses) and a medical physicist (from the Health Management Information System (HMIS) of JMC in 2014 E.c). It has 14 beds used for outpatient follow-up services. This study will be conducted from August to November 2022. An institution-based mixed cross-sectional study design will be employed to answer research objectives 1, 2, 3 and 4. To address the research objective 5 pre-experimental study designs will be implemented.\r\nStudy population\r\nAll patients who have follow up at the oncologic unit of Jimma medical center will be the source population. All sampled patients who have follow up at oncologic unit of Jimma medical center and who will fulfil the inclusion criteria and purposively selected healthcare professionals who provide oncologic services for cancer patients will be the study population.\r\nEligibility criterion\r\nPatients age 15 and above years, histologically confirmed for the diagnosis of cancer, who had already started treatment (i.e., greater than or equal to one cycle of chemotherapy, 2–6 weeks of radiotherapy, at least two cycles of targeted therapy, or follow-up care in the past 3–24 months) will be eligible for the study (Brédart et al., 2018). Patients with severe illness at the time of data collection, known psychiatric disorders, speaking and hearing problems and readmitted patients during the study period will be excluded from the study.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nSample size determination and sampling technique \r\nTo answer research objectives 1 and 2 the sample size will be determined by using single population proportion formula by considering 44.8 % prevalence for the overall satisfaction of patients from the previous study on the topic of interest and factors associated (Abate, Aman, Nasir, Gebremariam, & Fentie, 2020). With margin error of 5% between the sample and the population at 95% confidence level \r\nThe formula for the single population proportion is:\r\n \r\nn = 380\r\nWhere: -\r\nn = sample size\r\nZα/2 = Confidence level at 95% which is 1.96\r\np = estimated prevalence level 44.8% \r\nd = margin of error of 5%\r\nBy adding a non-response rate of 10%, the final sample size will be 418. All eligible study participants will be enrolled consecutively until the final sample size is reach. To answer research objective 3 a purposive sampling technique will be applied.\r\nData collection tools \r\nData will be collected through face-to-face interviews with the study participants using data abstraction from the newly updated European Organization for Research and Treatment of Cancer (EORTC) PATSAT-C33 questionnaire. The data abstraction format was designed to extract information from a medical chart or directly from the patient, such as socio-demographic characteristics (age, sex, marital status, educational level, region, place of residence, source of treatment, and employment status), as well as clinical characteristics, including primary cancer diagnosis, type of cancer treatment (i.e., chemotherapy, radiotherapy, surgery, or a combination of these), and patient performance status documented by treating physician using the Eastern Cooperative Oncology Group (ECOG) scale. Clinical characteristics were extracted from the patients’ medical records. The EORTC PATSAT-C33 is a 33-item questionnaire that is reliable, validated internationally, and widely used to assess patients’ appraisal of services provided by hospital doctors, nurses, and radiotherapy technicians. It is also used to assess specific organizational and structural aspects of service delivery and to highlight aspects of care that need improvement in the outpatient oncology setting. Each item is rated on a 5-point Likert scale (1=“poor,” 2=“fair,” 3=“good,” 4=“very good,” and 5= “excellent”). The satisfaction responses were categorized into satisfaction with doctors\' technical skills (items 1–3), information exchange (items 4–6) and affective behavior (items 7–10); overall satisfaction with nurses or radiotherapy technologists (items 11–17), coordination (items 18–21), interaction with all hospital staff (items 22–28) and hospital organization and environment (items 18–32); and overall satisfaction with the hospital (items 1–33). The mean score for each subcategory will then calculated and transformed into a 0–100 scale using 100/ (maximum score – no. of items) (the sum of means – no. of items). Higher scores indicate a higher satisfaction level and vice versa (Brédart et al., 2018). For qualitative data, an observational checklist will be used to assess the service process which is adapted from Kenya\'s quality model for health (Healthcare, n.d.). Additionally, in-depth interviews will be conducted with purposively selected health care providers using supplementary informants with an interview guide. \r\nInterviewers will be trained data collectors who will receive brief training. This questionnaire will first be translated from its original English language version into Amharic and Oromic by a person who speaks Amharic, Oromic and English fluently. A second translator who will not be seen the original English version back-translated the instrument from the Amharic and Oromic versions into English. The two English translation versions will be then compared for equivalence. If the back-translated items and the originals did not agree, this process will be repeated until the back-translated and the original versions were congruous. Subsequently, pre-testing will be 10 selected patients treated for cancer. During data collection, COVID- 19 preventive practices like wearing a face masks will be strictly applied by data collectors.\r\nData analysis\r\nData will be coded, cleaned compiled and entered into Epi-data 4.6, and exported to SPSS version 23.0. Chi-squared test will be performed to check the adequacy of cells before performing binary logistic regression. The bivariate analysis will be conducted and variables having a p-value less than 0.25 will consider for multi-variable logistic regression analysis, variables with a p-value of < 0.05 will be considered statistically significant. For qualitative, the data will be transcribed verbatim and translated into English by the language experts. Then the data will be analyzed using thematic content analysis Assisted by Atlas.ti 8. Codes will be identified for key concepts that represent recurring themes which will then be categorized, grouped and organized to draw out key themes. While for objective 5 paired t-test analysis method will be used.\r\nQuality control\r\nA pretest will be done to ensure the validity of the survey tools. Training is will be arranged for data collectors on the objective of the study, each item of the questionnaire, their responsibility, and obligations. During data collection, the investigators will tightly supervise whether the data collectors adhere to the research protocol or not and make an immediate correction. The collected data will also be double-checked for completeness. \r\nOperational definition and definition of terms \r\nPatient satisfaction: patients who will have an above mean score of EORTC Satisfaction with cancer care will be nominated as satisfied and below the mean score not satisfied (Brédart et al., 2018).\r\nPerceived Social support: The total sum score ranges from 3 to 14, a person with 3–8 has poor social support, 9–11 has moderate social support and 12–14 has strong social support (Bøen, Dalgard, & Bjertness, 2012).\r\nElectronic recording systems: information recorded by a computer that is produced or received in the initiation, conduct or completion of an agency or individual activity.\r\nEquitability of the access: Equal access and utilization to health care for those in equal need of health care. Equal (or, rather, equitable) health outcomes (as measured by, for example, quality-adjusted life expectancy) (Oliver & Mossialos, 2004).\r\nTimeliness of care: system’s capacity to provide care quickly after a need is recognized','<p>Abate, D., Aman, M. A., Nasir, B. B., Gebremariam, G. T., &amp; Fentie, A. M. (2020). Assessment of quality of care using information on patient satisfaction at adult oncology center of Tikur Anbessa specialized hospital, Ethiopia: A cross-sectional study. Patient Preference and Adherence, 14, 847&ndash;858. https://doi.org/10.2147/PPA.S253027 B&oslash;en, H., Dalgard, O. S., &amp; Bjertness, E. (2012). The importance of social support in the associations between psychological distress and somatic health problems and socio-economic factors among older adults living at home: a cross sectional study. BMC Geriatrics, 12, 27. https://doi.org/10.1186/1471-2318-12-27 Boyle, P., &amp; Levin, B. (2008). World CanCer report 2008. In Cancer Control (Vol. 199). https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cma.2010.02.010 Bray, F., Laversanne, M., Weiderpass, E., &amp; Soerjomataram, I. (2021). The ever-increasing importance of cancer as a leading cause of premature death worldwide. Cancer, 127(16), 3029&ndash;3030. https://doi.org/10.1002/cncr.33587 Br&eacute;dart, A., Anota, A., Young, T., Tomaszewski, K. A., Arraras, J. I., Moura De Albuquerque Melo, H., &hellip; Aaronson, N. K. (2018). Phase III study of the European Organisation for Research and Treatment of Cancer satisfaction with cancer care core questionnaire (EORTC PATSAT-C33) and specific complementary outpatient module (EORTC OUT-PATSAT7). European Journal of Cancer Care, 27(1), 1&ndash;11. https://doi.org/10.1111/ecc.12786 Citation, S. (1999). Ensuring Quality Cancer Care. In Ensuring Quality Cancer Care. https://doi.org/10.17226/6467 Colosia, A. D., Peltz, G., Pohl, G., Liu, E., Copley-Merriman, K., Khan, S., &amp; Kaye, J. A. (2011). A review and characterization of the various perceptions of quality cancer care. Cancer, 117(5), 884&ndash;896. https://doi.org/10.1002/cncr.25644 Gersten, O., &amp; Wilmoth, J. R. (2002). The cancer transition in Japan since 1951. Demographic Research, 7(December), 271&ndash;306. https://doi.org/10.4054/DemRes.2002.7.5 Grabowski, D., Aagaard-Hansen, J., Willaing, I., &amp; Jensen, B. (2017). Principled Promotion of Health: Implementing Five Guiding Health Promotion Principles for Research-Based Prevention and Management of Diabetes. Societies, 7(2), 10. https://doi.org/10.3390/soc7020010 Haileselassie, W., Mulugeta, T., Tigeneh, W., Kaba, M., &amp; Labisso, W. L. (2019). The Situation of Cancer Treatment in Ethiopia: Challenges and Opportunities. Journal of Cancer Prevention, 24(1), 33&ndash;42. https://doi.org/10.15430/jcp.2019.24.1.33 Healthcare, Q. O. F. (n.d.). Checklist for Assessing Cultural Significance. Hess, L. M., &amp; Pohl, G. (2013). Perspectives of quality care in cancer treatment: A review of the literature. American Health and Drug Benefits, 6(6), 321&ndash;329. Islam, R. M., Billah, B., Hossain, M. N., &amp; Oldroyd, J. (2017). Barriers to cervical cancer and breast cancer screening uptake in low-income and middle-income countries: A systematic review. Asian Pacific Journal of Cancer Prevention, 18(7), 1751&ndash;1763. https://doi.org/10.22034/APJCP.2017.18.7.1751 Larson, E. (1991). Medicare: A Strategy for Quality Assurance. In Journal of Nursing Care Quality (Vol. 5). https://doi.org/10.1097/00001786-199107000-00013 McGlynn, E. A. (1997). Six Challenges in Measuring the Quality of Health Care. Health Affairs, 16(3), 7&ndash;21. https://doi.org/10.1377/hlthaff.16.3.7 Nam, M., Akie, T. E., Sanosaka, M., Craige, S. M., Kant, S., &amp; ... (2017). Let us know how access to this document benefits you. 0&ndash;11. https://doi.org/10.7191/cancer Oliver, A., &amp; Mossialos, E. (2004). Equity of access to health care: Outlining the foundations for action. Journal of Epidemiology and Community Health, 58(8), 655&ndash;658. https://doi.org/10.1136/jech.2003.017731 Siegel, R. L., Miller, K. D., &amp; Jemal, A. (2020). Cancer statistics, 2020. CA: A Cancer Journal for Clinicians, 70(1), 7&ndash;30. https://doi.org/10.3322/caac.21590 Yu, J. (2021). CEA and ADA in Pleural Fluid for Differential Malignant Pleural Effusion and Tuberculous Pleural Effusion. 1&ndash;12.</p>','This study aimed to assess the quality of cancer care among cancer patients from the perspective of patients, health care providers, and services in the adult oncology unit of Jimma medical center, Jimma town, southwest Ethiopia 2022.',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,'Quality of care, cancer patients, health care providers, oncology unit, Jimma medical center, Ethiopia.',10,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'Proposal-6292300b3c808090145978.docx');
